"polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Vomitos", "similarity": 0.934}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Vomitings", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "vomilo", "similarity": 0.929}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Vomitings", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "vomitos", "similarity": 0.97}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Vomitings", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Vomitos", "similarity": 0.982}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "vomilo", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "vomitos", "similarity": 0.956}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "vomilo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Vomitos", "similarity": 0.935}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "vomitos", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Vomitos", "similarity": 0.988}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "v\u00f3mitos", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "v\u00f3mitos", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 9, "stable_key": "clu_94625bf3401b28aa", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Vomiting", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "The act of expelling stomach contents; a symptom or illness.", "note": "Can refer to the symptom or a specific illness characterized by vomiting.", "wikipedia_extract": "Vomiting (scientifically known as emesis, and otherwise known as puking, and throwing up) is the forceful expulsion of the contents of one's stomach through the mouth and sometimes the nose. The substance so produced (the erstwhile contents of the stomach, now expelled) is also known as vomit or vomitus. Vomiting can be the result of ailments like food poisoning, gastroenteritis, pregnancy, motion sickness, or hangover; or it can be an after effect of diseases such as brain tumors, elevated intracranial pressure, or overexposure to ionizing radiation. The feeling that one is about to vomit is called nausea; it often precedes, but does not always lead to vomiting. Impairment due to alcohol or anesthesia can cause inhalation of vomit. In severe cases, where dehydration develops, intravenous fluid may be required."}}, {"canonical_name": "cold", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "book_count": 7, "total_mentions": 165, "members": [{"entity_id": "cold", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "cold", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 5, "variants": ["cold"], "contexts": ["low temperature, a climatic factor", "environmental quality affecting plant growth"]}, {"entity_id": "cold", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "cold", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 10, "variants": ["cold"], "contexts": ["thermal stimulus", "low temperature quality"]}, {"entity_id": "cold", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "cold", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["cold"], "contexts": ["temperature quality", "low temperature"]}, {"entity_id": "cold", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "cold", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["cold"], "contexts": ["central quality discussed regarding snow"]}, {"entity_id": "cold", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "cold", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 116, "variants": ["cold", "Cold", "coldness"], "contexts": ["a fundamental quality in early modern medicine", "medical quality"]}, {"entity_id": "cold", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Cold", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 29, "variants": ["Cold", "cold", "cold caus"], "contexts": ["quality causing pains and torments", "quality associated with Angelica's virtues"]}, {"entity_id": "cold", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Cold", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cold"], "contexts": ["quality of temperature"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "cold", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "cold", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "cold", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "cold", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "cold", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "cold", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "cold", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cold", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "cold", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "cold", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "cold", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "cold", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "cold", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cold", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "cold", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "cold", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "cold", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cold", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "cold", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cold", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Cold", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Cold", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 10, "stable_key": "clu_06ad6c064ed8b34c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "cold", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q199804", "wikidata_description": "lung disease involving long-term poor airflow", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chronic%20obstructive%20pulmonary%20disease", "note": "Refers to the physical sensation and thermodynamic state of low temperature, as well as its perceived qualities in historical medical contexts.", "wikipedia_extract": "Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) is a type of progressive lung disease characterized by chronic respiratory symptoms and airflow limitation. GOLD defines COPD as a heterogeneous lung condition characterized by chronic respiratory symptoms (shortness of breath, cough, sputum production or exacerbations) due to abnormalities of the airways (bronchitis, bronchiolitis) or alveoli (emphysema) that cause persistent, often progressive, airflow obstruction. The main symptoms of COPD include shortness of breath and a cough, which may or may not produce mucus. COPD progressively worsens, with everyday activities such as walking or dressing becoming difficult. While COPD is incurable, it is preventable and treatable. The two most common types of COPD are emphysema and chronic bronchitis, and have been the two classic COPD phenotypes."}}, {"canonical_name": "temperature", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 7, "total_mentions": 128, "members": [{"entity_id": "temperature", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "temperature", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["temperature"], "contexts": ["degree of hotness or coldness", "quality of heat or cold"]}, {"entity_id": "temperature", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "temperature", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 33, "variants": ["temperature"], "contexts": ["measure of heat", "degree of hotness or coldness"]}, {"entity_id": "temperature", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "temperature", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 45, "variants": ["Temperature", "temperature"], "contexts": ["degree of heat or cold", "measure of hotness or coldness"]}, {"entity_id": "temperature", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "temperature", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 10, "variants": ["temperature"], "contexts": ["degree of heat in a substance", "degree of hotness or coldness"]}, {"entity_id": "temp\u00e9rature", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "temp\u00e9rature", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["temp\u00e9rature"], "contexts": ["degree of heat", "temperature"]}, {"entity_id": "temp\u00e9rature", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "temp\u00e9rature", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 5, "variants": ["temp\u00e9rature"], "contexts": ["degree of heat or cold", "degree of heat"]}, {"entity_id": "temp\u00e9rature", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "temp\u00e9rature", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 27, "variants": ["temp\u00e9rature", "Temp. duB.", "tableau de la temp\u00e9rature", "temperature", "temp\u00e9rature atmosph\u00e9rique", "temp\u00e9rature de l'eau", "temp\u00e9rature moyenne", "temp\u00e9ratures", "temp\u00e9ratures de la surface de l'eau", "temp\u00e9ratures mensuelles", "temp\u00e9ratures moyennes", "temp\u00e9ratures moyennes annuelles"], "contexts": ["temperature", "climatic condition"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "temperature", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "temperature", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "temperature", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "temperature", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "temperature", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "temperature", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "temperature", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "temperature", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "temperature", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "temperature", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "temperature", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "temperature", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "temp\u00e9rature", "target_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "target_name": "temp\u00e9rature", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "temp\u00e9rature", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "temp\u00e9rature", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "temp\u00e9rature", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "temp\u00e9rature", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 11, "stable_key": "clu_c640958ace25ff19", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Temperature", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q11466", "wikidata_description": "physical property of matter that quantitatively expresses the common notions of hot and cold", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temperature", "wikipedia_extract": "Temperature quantitatively expresses the attribute of hotness or coldness. Temperature is measured with a thermometer. It reflects the average kinetic energy of the vibrating and colliding atoms making up a substance. In classical thermodynamics and kinetic theory, temperature reflects the average kinetic energy of the particles in a system, providing a quantitative measure of how energy is distributed among microscopic degrees of freedom. Thermometers are calibrated in various temperature scales that historically have relied on various reference points and thermometric substances for definition. The most common scales are the Celsius scale with the unit symbol \u00b0C (formerly called centigrade), the Fahrenheit scale (\u00b0F), and the Kelvin scale (K), with the third being used predominantly for scientific purposes."}}, {"canonical_name": "Wine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 7, "total_mentions": 113, "members": [{"entity_id": "wine", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Wine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 101, "variants": ["Wine", "wine"], "contexts": ["used in decoction for serpent bites", "liquid used with seeds"]}, {"entity_id": "vino", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "vino", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["vino"], "contexts": ["drink to take root with in winter"]}, {"entity_id": "wine", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "wine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 2, "variants": ["wine"], "contexts": ["generous, mixed with pepper"]}, {"entity_id": "wine", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Wine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 5, "variants": ["wine", "Wine"], "contexts": ["alcoholic beverage", "mixed with ashes for pain relief"]}, {"entity_id": "wine", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "wine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["wine"], "contexts": ["intoxicant affecting reaction time"]}, {"entity_id": "wine", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "wine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["wine"], "contexts": ["product made from grapes", "alcoholic beverage from grapes"]}, {"entity_id": "wine", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Wine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Wine"], "contexts": ["liquid drawn up in glass"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Wine", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "vino", "similarity": 0.98}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Wine", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "wine", "similarity": 0.993}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "vino", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "wine", "similarity": 0.986}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Wine", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "wine", "similarity": 0.992}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Wine", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "wine", "similarity": 0.992}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Wine", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Wine", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "wine", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "wine", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "wine", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Wine", "similarity": 0.992}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "wine", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Wine", "similarity": 0.992}], "id": 12, "stable_key": "clu_6110a1f26657a473", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Wine", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "note": "Both 'vino' and 'Vino' are clearly the Italian and Spanish/Portuguese words for wine, a common medicinal ingredient.", "wikidata_id": "Q282", "wikidata_description": "alcoholic drink typically made from grapes through the fermentation process", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wine", "wikipedia_extract": "Wine is an alcoholic drink made from fermented grape juice. It is produced and consumed in many regions around the world, in a wide variety of styles which are influenced by different varieties of grapes, growing environments, viticulture methods, and production techniques. Wine has been produced for thousands of years, the earliest evidence dating from c. 6000 BCE in present-day Georgia. Its popularity spread around the Mediterranean during Classical antiquity, and was sustained in Western Europe by winemaking monks and a secular trade for general drinking. New World wine was established by settler colonies from the 16th century onwards, and the wine trade increased dramatically up to the latter half of the 19th century, when European vineyards were largely destroyed by the invasive pest phylloxera.", "description": "An alcoholic beverage made from fermented grapes."}}, {"canonical_name": "Europa", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 7, "total_mentions": 103, "members": [{"entity_id": "western_europe", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Western Europe", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Western Europe"], "contexts": ["region with paleozoic formations"]}, {"entity_id": "western_europe", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Western Europe", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 5, "variants": ["Western Europe"], "contexts": ["maritime nations", "region with less isobarometric oscillations"]}, {"entity_id": "europa", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Europa", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Europa"], "cont