 Indian subcontinent from the Tibetan Plateau. The range has some of the Earth's highest peaks, including the highest, Mount Everest. More than 100 peaks exceeding elevations of 7,200 m (23,600 ft) above sea level lie in the Himalayas. The Himalayas abut on or cross territories of five countries: Nepal, India, China, Bhutan and Pakistan. The sovereignty of the range in the Kashmir region is disputed among India, Pakistan, and China. The Himalayan range is bordered on the northwest by the Karakoram and Hindu Kush ranges, on the north by the Tibetan Plateau, and on the south by the Indo-Gangetic Plain."}}, {"canonical_name": "animals", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 6, "total_mentions": 77, "members": [{"entity_id": "animals", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "animals", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["animals"], "contexts": ["living organisms that ingest food", "living organisms distinct from plants"]}, {"entity_id": "animals", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "animals", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 14, "variants": ["animals", "Animals"], "contexts": ["organic forms", "vital organisms"]}, {"entity_id": "animals", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "animals", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 23, "variants": ["animals", "Animals"], "contexts": ["living organisms", "correspondences become extremely complex"]}, {"entity_id": "animals", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Animals", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["Animals", "animals"], "contexts": ["living organisms with sympathies", "living organisms"]}, {"entity_id": "animals", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Animals", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 30, "variants": ["Animals", "animals"], "contexts": ["living organisms that are not plants", "marked effect of habit"]}, {"entity_id": "animals", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Animals", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["animals", "Animals"], "contexts": ["voice of animals produces sound", "specific diversity and distribution laws"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "animals", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "animals", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "animals", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "animals", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "animals", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "animals", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Animals", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Animals", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Animals", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Animals", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Animals", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Animals", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 36, "stable_key": "clu_9f77ad4004c8b35d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Animals", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q431", "wikidata_description": "scientific study of animals", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Animal", "note": "The term 'beast' in early modern texts is a general term for any non-human animal, often used in contrast to humans or for specific examples of fauna.", "wikipedia_extract": "Animals are multicellular, eukaryotic organisms belonging to the biological kingdom Animalia (). With few exceptions, animals consume organic material, breathe oxygen, have myocytes and are able to move, can reproduce sexually, and grow from a hollow sphere of cells, the blastula, during embryonic development. Animals form a clade, meaning that they arose from a single common ancestor. Over 1.5 million living animal species have been described, of which around 1.05 million are insects, over 85,000 are molluscs, and around 65,000 are vertebrates. It has been estimated there are as many as 7.77 million animal species on Earth. Animal body lengths range from 8.5 \u03bcm (0.00033 in) to 33.6 m (110 ft).", "description": "Living creatures, sources of bezoars, subject to washing."}}, {"canonical_name": "America", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 6, "total_mentions": 77, "members": [{"entity_id": "america", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "America", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 21, "variants": ["America"], "contexts": ["location used for distance comparison", "origin of plants in India"]}, {"entity_id": "america", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "America", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 15, "variants": ["America"], "contexts": ["location of Mr. Bond's discovery", "continent with western coast"]}, {"entity_id": "america", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "America", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 9, "variants": ["America"], "contexts": ["metaphor for unexplored territory", "continent influencing compass variation"]}, {"entity_id": "american", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "American", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["American"], "contexts": ["related to America", "relating to America"]}, {"entity_id": "amer_", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Amer.", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Amer."], 