.org/wiki/Limestone", "wikipedia_extract": "Limestone is a type of carbonate sedimentary rock which is the main source of the material lime. It is composed mostly of the minerals calcite and aragonite, which are different crystal forms of calcium carbonate CaCO3. Limestone forms when these minerals precipitate out of water containing dissolved calcium. This can take place through both biological and nonbiological processes, though biological processes, such as the accumulation of corals and shells in the sea, have likely been more important for the last 540 million years. Limestone often contains fossils which provide scientists with information on ancient environments and on the evolution of life. About 20% to 25% of sedimentary rock is carbonate rock, and most of this is limestone."}}, {"canonical_name": "Glass", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "VESSEL", "book_count": 6, "total_mentions": 38, "members": [{"entity_id": "glass", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Glass", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "VESSEL", "count": 19, "variants": ["Glass", "Glass Vessel", "Glasses", "glassed Pots", "jarr Glasses"], "contexts": ["material for distilling Angelica", "container for Elm-Tree water"]}, {"entity_id": "vidro", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "vidro", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "VESSEL", "count": 2, "variants": ["vidro"], "contexts": ["glass, a material", "container for storing liquid"]}, {"entity_id": "vidros", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "vidros", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "VESSEL", "count": 1, "variants": ["vidros"], "contexts": ["glass bottles"]}, {"entity_id": "glass_vessel", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "glass vessel", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "VESSEL", "count": 1, "variants": ["glass vessel"], "contexts": ["container made of glass"]}, {"entity_id": "glass", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Glass", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": 