vement and force"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "muscle", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "muscle", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "muscle", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "muscle", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "muscle", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "animal muscle", "similarity": 0.964}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "muscle", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "muscle", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "muscle", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "muscle", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "muscle", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "animal muscle", "similarity": 0.964}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "muscle", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "muscle", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "muscle", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "muscle", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "animal muscle", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "muscle", "similarity": 0.964}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "organes musculaires", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Muscle", "similarity": 0.902}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Muscle", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "muscle", "similarity": 0.947}], "id": 43, "stable_key": "clu_df97001aeee63633", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Muscles", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q7365", "wikidata_description": "contractile soft tissue of animals", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muscle", "note": "The early modern term 'mufculos' (or 'mufculofus') is a Latinate form referring to muscles, especially in anatomical contexts. The OCR variant 'mucous' is likely a misreading or a contextually distinct usage if it refers to a bodily fluid, but the overwhelming number of occurrences point to anatomical muscles.", "wikipedia_extract": "Muscle is a specialised soft tissue, one of the four basic types of animal tissues. There are three types of muscle tissues in vertebrates:- skeletal muscle tissue, cardiac muscle tissue, and smooth muscle tissue. Muscle tissue gives skeletal muscles the ability to contract and relax. Muscle tissue contains special contractile proteins called actin and myosin which interact to cause movement. Among many other muscle proteins present are two regulatory proteins, troponin and tropomyosin. Muscle is formed during embryonic development, in a process known as myogenesis."}}, {"canonical_name": "knife", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "TOOL", "book_count": 6, "total_mentions": 21, "members": [{"entity_id": "knife", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "knife", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "TOOL", "count": 1, "variants": ["knife"], "contexts": ["cutting tool with a blade"]}, {"entity_id": "knife", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "knife", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "TOOL", "count": 16, "variants": ["Knife", "knife"], "contexts": ["a cutting instrument", "cutting tool"]}, {"entity_id": "knife", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "knife", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "TOOL", "count": 1, "variants": ["knife"], "contexts": ["cutting instrument"]}, {"entity_id": "knife", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Knife", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "TOOL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Knife"], "contexts": ["steel object attracted by needle"]}, {"entity_id": "knife", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Knife", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "TOOL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Knife"], "contexts": ["cutting instrument"]}, {"entity_id": "knife", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Knife", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "TOOL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Knife"], "contexts": ["cutting tool"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "knife", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "knife", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "knife", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "knife", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "knife", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "knife", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Knife", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Knife", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Knife", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Knife", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Knife", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Knife", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 44, "stable_key": "clu_f24684c271950071", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Knife", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "Q32489", "wikidata_description": "tool with a cutting edge or blade", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knife", "wikipedia_extract": "A knife (pl.: knives; from Old Norse knifr 'knife, dirk') is a tool or weapon with a cutting edge or blade, usually attached to a handle or hilt. One of the earliest tools used by humanity, knives appeared at least 2.5 million years ago, as evidenced by the Oldowan tools. Originally made of wood, bone, and stone (such as flint and obsidian), over the centuries, in step with improvements in both metallurgy and manufacturing, knife blades have been made from copper, bronze, iron, steel, ceramic, and titanium. Most modern knives have fixed or folding blades, with styles varying by maker and country. Knives can serve various purposes. Hunters use a hunting knife, soldiers use the combat knife, scouts, campers, and hikers carry a pocketknife; there are kitchen knives for preparing foods (the chef's knife, the paring knife, bread knife, cleaver), table knife (butter knives and steak knives), weapons (daggers or switchblades), knives for throwing or juggling, and knives for religious ceremony or display (the kirpan)."}}, {"canonical_name": "Occidente", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 6, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "west", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "West", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["West"], "contexts": ["direction", "direction to look when digging Mandrake"]}, {"entity_id": "occident", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "occident", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["occident"], "contexts": ["West"]}, {"entity_id": "west", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "west", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["west"], "contexts": ["direction of Sun's apparent movement"]}, {"entity_id": "occidentales", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Occidentales", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Occidentales"], "contexts": ["referring to the West Indies"]}, {"entity_id": "occidente", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_