arget_name": "calor", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 51, "stable_key": "clu_186efa40b7f472bf", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Heat", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q28128222", "wikidata_description": "state of high relative temperature, often said about body temperature, weather or objects", "note": "Refers to the Aristotelian/Galenic quality of heat, a fundamental concept in early modern humoral theory and philosophy of nature."}}, {"canonical_name": "Dioscorides", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 201, "members": [{"entity_id": "dioscorides", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Dioscorides", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 14, "variants": ["Dioscorides"], "contexts": ["source for Plinie's borrowings", "ancient medical writer"]}, {"entity_id": "dioscorides", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Dioscorides", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 37, "variants": ["Dioscorides", "Diascoride", "Diascorides"], "contexts": ["author of a book on herbs", "ancient Greek physician and botanist"]}, {"entity_id": "dioscorides", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Dioscorides", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Dioscorides"], "contexts": ["ancient medical authority"]}, {"entity_id": "dioscorides", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Dioscorides", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 14, "variants": ["Dioscorides", "Diofcorides", "Dioscoridos", "Dioscortes"], "contexts": ["ancient authority on herbs and medicines", "ancient authority on medicinal plants"]}, {"entity_id": "dioscorides", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Dioscorides", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 135, "variants": ["Dioscorides", "Diascor\u00eddes", "Diosc", "Diosc.", "DioscondQs", "Dioscor", "Dioscorid.", "Dioscor\u00eddes", "Dioseorides", "D\u00edoscorides"], "contexts": ["ancient author on materia medica", "ancient authority"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Dioscorides", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Dioscorides", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Dioscorides", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Dioscorides", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Dioscorides", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Dioscorides", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Dioscorides", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Dioscorides", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Dioscorides", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Dioscorides", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Dioscorides", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Dioscorides", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Dioscorides", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Dioscorides", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Dioscorides", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Dioscorides", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Dioscorides", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Dioscorides", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Dioscorides", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Dioscorides", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 52, "stable_key": "clu_436167f729fd3013", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Dioscorides", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Ancient Greek authority on materia medica and medicinal plants.", "wikipedia_extract": "Pedanius Dioscorides (Ancient Greek: \u03a0\u03b5\u03b4\u03ac\u03bd\u03b9\u03bf\u03c2 \u0394\u03b9\u03bf\u03c3\u03ba\u03bf\u03c5\u03c1\u03af\u03b4\u03b7\u03c2, Ped\u00e1nios Dioskour\u00edd\u0113s; c. 40\u201390 AD),  \"the father of pharmacognosy\", was a Greek physician, pharmacologist, botanist, and author of De materia medica (in the original Ancient Greek: \u03a0\u03b5\u03c1\u1f76 \u1f55\u03bb\u03b7\u03c2 \u1f30\u03b1\u03c4\u03c1\u03b9\u03ba\u1fc6\u03c2, Peri hul\u0113s iatrik\u0113s, both meaning \"On Medical Material\"), a 5-volume Greek encyclopedic pharmacopeia on herbal medicine and related medicinal substances, that was widely read for more than 1,500 years. For almost two millennia Dioscorides was regarded as the most prominent writer on plants and plant drugs. A native of Anazarbus, Cilicia, Asia Minor, Dioscorides likely studied medicine nearby at the school in Tarsus, which had a pharmacological emphasis, and he dedicated his medical books to Laecanius Arius, a medical practitioner there. Though he writes he lived a \"soldier's life\" or \"soldier-like life\", his pharmacopeia refers almost solely to plants found in the Greek-speaking eastern Mediterranean, making it likely that he served in campaigns, or travelled in a civilian capacity, less widely than supposed. The name Pedanius is Roman, suggesting that an aristocrat of that name sponsored him to become a Roman citizen. Between 50 and 70 AD Dioscorides wrote a five-volume book in his native Greek, \u03a0\u03b5\u03c1\u1f76 \u1f55\u03bb\u03b7\u03c2 \u1f30\u03b1\u03c4\u03c1\u03b9\u03ba\u1fc6\u03c2 (Per\u00ec hyl\u0113s \u00edatrik\u0113s), known in Western Europe more often by its Latin title De materia medica (\"On Medical Material\"), which became the precursor to all modern pharmacopeias.", "birth_year": 40, "death_year": 90}}, {"canonical_name": "light", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 188, "members": [{"entity_id": "light", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "light", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 4, "variants": ["light"], "contexts": ["environmental condition affecting variation", "influences color"]}, {"entity_id": "light", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "light", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 10, "variants": ["light", "Light"], "contexts": ["visual stimulus", "stimulus for reaction-time"]}, {"entity_id": "light", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "light", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 26, "variants": ["Light", "light"], "contexts": ["electromagnetic radiation", "phenomenon of electromagnetic activity"]}, {"entity_id": "light", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "light", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 116, "variants": ["light", "Light"], "contexts": ["phenomenon of refraction and reflection", "action under same circumstances"]}, {"entity_id": "light", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Light", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 32, "variants": ["Light", "light"], "contexts": ["electromagnetic radiation", "physical phenomenon acting through space"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "light", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "light", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "light", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "light", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "light", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "light", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "light", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Light", "similarity": 0.996}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "light", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "light", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "light", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "light", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "light", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Light", "similarity": 0.996}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "light", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "light", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "light", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Light", "similarity": 0.996}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "light", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Light", "similarity": 0.996}], "id": 53, "stable_key": "clu_18df288f8b60139e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Light", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The contexts indicate discussion of light as a physical phenomenon (environmental, visual stimulus, electromagnetic radiation) rather than a substance in the chemical sense.", "wikidata_id": "Q542", "wikidata_description": "sports involving running, jumping, throwing and walking", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sport%20of%20athletics", "wikipedia_extract": "Athletics is a group of sporting events that involves competitive running, jumping and throwing. The most common types of athletics competitions are track and field, road running, cross-country running, and race walking. The results of racing events are decided by finishing position (or time, where measured), while the jumps and throws are won by the athlete that achieves the highest or furthest measurement from a series of attempts. The simplicity of the competitions, and the lack of a need for expensive equipment, makes athletics one of the most common types of sports in the world. Athletics is mostly an individual sport, with the exception of relay races and competitions which combine athletes' performances for a team score, such as cross country. Organized athletics are traced back to the ancient Olympic Games from 776 BC."}}, {"canonical_name": "memory", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 173, "members": [{"entity_id": "memory", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Memory", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["Memory"], "contexts": ["mental faculty improved by treatment", "faculty of retaining and recalling information"]}, {"entity_id": "memory", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "memory", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 143, "variants": ["memory", "MEMORY", "Memory"], "contexts": ["ability to recall past experiences", "Soul's faculty"]}, {"entity_id": "memoria", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "memoria", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["memoria"], "contexts": ["faculty Ambar can improve"]}, {"entity_id": "memoria", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "memoria", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 22, "variants": ["Memoria", "memoria"], "contexts": ["memory", "faculty of the soul for retention"]}, {"entity_id": "memory", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "memory", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["memory"], "contexts": ["faculty of recalling past experiences"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Memory", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "memory", "similarity": 0.965}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Memory", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "memoria", "similarity": 0.92}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Memory", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "memoria", "similarity": 0.92}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Memory", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "memory", "similarity": 0.965}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "memory", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "memoria", "similarity": 0.949}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "memory", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "memoria", "similarity": 0.949}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "memory", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "memory", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "memoria", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "memoria", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "memoria", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "memory", "similarity": 0.949}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "memoria", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "memory", "similarity": 0.949}], "id": 54, "stable_key": "clu_cf67b7333f0fc9b6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Memory", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q492", "wikidata_description": "mental faculties and processes involved in storing and retrieving information", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Memory", "wikipedia_extract": "Memory is the faculty of the mind by which data or information is encoded, stored, and retrieved when needed. It is the retention of information over time for the purpose of influencing future action. If past events could not be remembered, it would be impossible for language, relationships, or personal identity to develop. Memory loss is usually described as forgetfulness or amnesia. Memory is often understood as an informational processing system with explicit and implicit functioning that is made up of a sensory processor, short-term (or working) memory, and long-term memory. This can be related to the neuron."}}, {"canonical_name": "vinagre", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 154, "members": [{"entity_id": "vinagre", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "vinagre", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 10, "variants": ["vinagre"], "contexts": ["acidic liquid used in preparation", "ingredient in a preparation"]}, {"entity_id": "vinagre", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "vinagre", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 10, "variants": ["vinagre"], "contexts": ["Product made from fruit peel", "acidic liquid"]}, {"entity_id": "vinagre", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "vinagre", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 127, "variants": ["vinagre", "Vinacearum", "Vinagre", "vina", "vinacea", "vinagre Ro\u017fado", "vinagre aguado", "vinagre branco forte", "vinagre forte", "vinagre fortiffimo", "vinagre fortissimo", "vinagre rofado", "vinagre rolado", "vinagre rosado", "vinagre ro\u017fado", "vinagreRofado", "vinagreRo\u017fado", "vinagrebem forte", "vinagreforte"], "contexts": ["vinegar, an acidic liquid", "added to remedy"]}, {"entity_id": "vinaigre", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "vinaigre", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["vinaigre"], "contexts": ["acidic liquid used as an excitation"]}, {"entity_id": "vinagre", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "vinagre", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 6, "variants": ["vinagre"], "contexts": ["vinegar", "acidic liquid derived from wine"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "vinagre", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "vinagre", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "vinagre", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "vinagre", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "vinagre", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "vinagre", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "vinaigre", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "vinagre", "similarity": 0.972}], "id": 55, "stable_key": "clu_7213cc4f6e604a6e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "vinegar", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "wikidata_id": "Q407153", "wikidata_description": "chemical compound", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ethyl%20acetate", "wikipedia_extract": "Ethyl acetate (commonly abbreviated EtOAc, ETAC or EA) is the organic compound with the formula CH3CO2CH2CH3, simplified to C4H8O2. This flammable, colorless liquid has a characteristic sweet smell (similar to pear drops) and is used in glues, nail polish removers, and the decaffeination process of tea and coffee. Ethyl acetate is the ester of ethanol and acetic acid; it is manufactured on a large scale for use as a solvent. Ethyl acetate was first synthesized by the Count de Lauraguais in 1759 by distilling a mixture of ethanol and acetic acid. In 2004, an estimated 1.3 million tonnes were produced worldwide. The combined annual production in 1985 of Japan, North America, and Europe was about 400,000 tonnes.", "description": "An acidic liquid produced by fermentation of ethanol."}}, {"canonical_name": "Medicina", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 143, "members": [{"entity_id": "medicina", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "medicina", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["medicina", "Medicina"], "contexts": ["practice of healing and treatment", "medicine"]}, {"entity_id": "medicina", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "medicina", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 20, "variants": ["medicina", "Medicina"], "contexts": ["art and use of healing", "mild and easy medicine for purging"]}, {"entity_id": "medecina", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "medecina", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 23, "variants": ["medecina", "MEDECINAES", "Medicina", "medecinal", "medicina", "medicina hindu", "medicinaes", "medicinais"], "contexts": ["practice of healing", "the practice of healing"]}, {"entity_id": "medicina", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Medicina", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 8, "variants": ["Medicina", "Medicma", "medicina"], "contexts": ["Medicine", "practice of healing and remedies"]}, {"entity_id": "medicina", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Medicina", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 88, "variants": ["Medicina", "Medic. Legal.", "Medic.theo", "Medica", "Medicina dia\u017ftatica", "Medicina moderna", "Medicinalium", "Medicine", "Medicin\u00e6", "Medic\u00e6", "medicina", "medicine"], "contexts": ["the subject of Hippocrates' and Celsus' writings", "the subject of Hippocrates' writings"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "medicina", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "medicina", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "medicina", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Medicina", "similarity": 0.968}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "medicina", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Medicina", "similarity": 0.968}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "medicina", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Medicina", "similarity": 0.968}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "medicina", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Medicina", "similarity": 0.968}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "medecina", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Medicina", "similarity": 0.929}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "medecina", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Medicina", "similarity": 0.929}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Medicina", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Medicina", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 56, "stable_key": "clu_e874316946eb152a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Medicine", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This cluster refers to the broad concept of medical practice and healing, as indicated by the various languages and contexts provided.", "wikidata_id": "Q12140", "wikidata_description": "substance used to diagnose, cure, treat, or prevent disease", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Medication", "wikipedia_extract": "Medication (also called medicament, medicine, pharmaceutical drug, medicinal product, medicinal drug, or simply drug) is a drug used to diagnose, cure, treat, or prevent disease. Drug therapy (pharmacotherapy) is an important part of the medical field and relies on the science of pharmacology for continual advancement and on pharmacy for appropriate management. Drugs are classified in many ways. One of the key divisions is by level of control, which distinguishes prescription drugs (those that a pharmacist dispenses only on the medical prescription) from over-the-counter drugs (those that consumers can order for themselves). Medicines may be classified by mode of action, route of administration, biological system affected, or therapeutic effects. The World Health Organization keeps a list of essential medicines."}}, {"canonical_name": "Plinio", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 142, "members": [{"entity_id": "plinio", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "Plinio", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Plinio"], "contexts": ["ancient Latin author cited", "ancient author mentioned for sugar cane growth"]}, {"entity_id": "plinio", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Plinio", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 9, "variants": ["Plinio"], "contexts": ["ancient author on natural history", "ancient authority on natural history"]}, {"entity_id": "plinio", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Plinio", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 112, "variants": ["Plinio", "Plinio*", "Plinius", "Plin\u00edo", "Pl\u00ednio"], "contexts": ["ancient authority", "ancient authority praised in the text"]}, {"entity_id": "plinio", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Plinio", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 5, "variants": ["Plinio"], "contexts": ["Ancient authority on natural history", "ancient author whose writing is discussed"]}, {"entity_id": "plinio", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Plinio", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 13, "variants": ["Plinio", "Plinius"], "contexts": ["ancient authority", "cited for a description of a work"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturale