e_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "ideas", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "ideas", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "ideas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "ideias", "similarity": 0.979}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "ideas", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "ideas", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "ideas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "ideias", "similarity": 0.979}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "ideas", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "ideas", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "ideias", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "ideas", "similarity": 0.979}], "id": 82, "stable_key": "clu_a0170198e146432d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ideas", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q131841", "wikidata_description": "mental image or concept", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Idea", "wikipedia_extract": "In philosophy and in common usage, an idea (from the Greek word: \u1f30\u03b4\u03ad\u03b1 (idea), meaning 'a form, or a pattern') is the result of thought. Also in philosophy, ideas can also be mental representational images of some object. Many philosophers have considered ideas to be a fundamental ontological category of being. The capacity to create  and understand the meaning of ideas is considered to be an essential and defining feature of human beings. An idea arises in a reflexive, spontaneous manner, even without thinking or serious reflection, for example, when we talk about the idea of a person or a place. A new or an original idea can often lead to innovation."}}, {"canonical_name": "sensations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 69, "members": [{"entity_id": "sensations", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "sensations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["sensations"], "contexts": ["sensory perceptions", "feelings experienced by organisms"]}, {"entity_id": "sensations", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "sensations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 57, "variants": ["sensations", "Sensations"], "contexts": ["raw input from senses", "raw sensory input"]}, {"entity_id": "sensations", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "sensations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sensations"], "contexts": ["perceptions from the senses"]}, {"entity_id": "sensations", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "sensations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sensations"], "contexts": ["awareness of stimuli"]}, {"entity_id": "sensations", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "sensations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 8, "variants": ["sensations"], "contexts": ["basic elements of thought", "basic units of experience"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "sensations", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "sensations", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "sensations", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "sensations", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "sensations", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "sensations", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "sensations", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "sensations", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "sensations", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "sensations", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "sensations", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "sensations", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "sensations", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "sensations", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "sensations", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "sensations", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "sensations", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "sensations", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "sensations", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "sensations", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 83, "stable_key": "clu_3a348fff959c6b82", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sensation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q173253", "wikidata_description": "physiological capacity of organisms that provides data for perception", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sense", "wikipedia_extract": "A sense is a biological system used by an organism for sensation, the process of  gathering information about the surroundings through the detection of stimuli. Although, in some cultures, five human senses were traditionally identified as such (namely sight, smell, touch, taste, and hearing), many more are now recognized. Senses used by non-human organisms are even greater in variety and number. During sensation, sense organs collect various stimuli (such as a sound or smell) for transduction, meaning transformation into a form that can be understood by the brain. Sensation and perception are fundamental to nearly every aspect of an organism's cognition, behavior and thought. In organisms, a sensory organ consists of a group of interrelated sensory cells that respond to a specific type of physical stimulus."}}, {"canonical_name": "Aristotle", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 69, "members": [{"entity_id": "aristotle", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Aristotle", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 36, "variants": ["Aristotle"], "contexts": ["philosopher who classified fallacies", "ancient Greek philosopher"]}, {"entity_id": "aristotle", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Aristotle", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Aristotle", "ARISTOTLE"], "contexts": ["ancient philosopher", "received writings from Alexander"]}, {"entity_id": "aristotle", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Aristotle", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 6, "variants": ["Aristotle"], "contexts": ["ancient philosopher, cited for avoiding category errors", "argues heart is sole seat of soul"]}, {"entity_id": "aristotle", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Aristotle", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 22, "variants": ["Aristotle"], "contexts": ["ancient philosopher and scientist", "ancient Greek philosopher and scientist"]}, {"entity_id": "aristotle", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Aristotle", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Aristotle"], "contexts": ["ancient Greek philosopher and scientist"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Aristotle", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Aristotle", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodox