us and could refer to multiple classical or medieval authorities. However, given the context of early modern medical and scientific texts, Aristotle and Avicenna are strong candidates. 'Arr.' is less common for a major figure. 'Amat.' is not a standard abbreviation for a major philosopher or physician."}}, {"canonical_name": "thermom\u00e8tre", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 65, "members": [{"entity_id": "thermom\u00e8tre", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "thermom\u00e8tre", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 2, "variants": ["thermom\u00e8tre"], "contexts": ["instrument to measure temperature", "instrument for measuring temperature"]}, {"entity_id": "thermom\u00e8tre", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "thermom\u00e8tre", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 46, "variants": ["thermom\u00e8tre", "THERMOM\u00c8TRE", "Thermom.", "thermo-m\u00e8tre", "thermometer", "thermometre", "thermom\u00e8tre centesimal", "thermom\u00e8tre centigrade", "thermom\u00e8tre cent\u00e9simal", "thermom\u00e8tre cent\u00e9s\u00f9nal", "thermom\u00e8tre dcRcaumur", "thermom\u00e8tre de Fabr.", "thermom\u00e8tres"], "contexts": ["instrument for measuring temperature", "thermometer"]}, {"entity_id": "thermometer", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Thermometer", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 6, "variants": ["thermometer", "Thermometer"], "contexts": ["instrument for measuring temperature", "instrument used to measure temperature"]}, {"entity_id": "thermometer", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Thermometer", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 10, "variants": ["Thermometer", "thermometer"], "contexts": ["measures temperature", "instrument for measuring temperature"]}, {"entity_id": "thermometer", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "thermometer", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 1, "variants": ["thermometer"], "contexts": ["instrument for measuring temperature"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "thermom\u00e8tre", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "thermom\u00e8tre", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "thermom\u00e8tre", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Thermometer", "similarity": 0.991}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "thermom\u00e8tre", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Thermometer", "similarity": 0.991}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "thermom\u00e8tre", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "thermometer", "similarity": 0.994}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "thermom\u00e8tre", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Thermometer", "similarity": 0.991}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "thermom\u00e8tre", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Thermometer", "similarity": 0.991}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "thermom\u00e8tre", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "thermometer", "similarity": 0.994}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Thermometer", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Thermometer", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Thermometer", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "thermometer", "similarity": 0.994}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Thermometer", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "thermometer", "similarity": 0.994}], "id": 85, "stable_key": "clu_61149a37ba4a7483", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "thermometer", "confidence": "high", "type": "OBJECT", "description": "An instrument used to measure temperature.", "wikipedia_extract": "A thermometer is a device that measures temperature (the hotness or coldness of an object) or temperature gradient (the rates of change of temperature in space). A thermometer has two important elements: (1) a temperature sensor (e.g. the bulb of a mercury-in-glass thermometer or the pyrometric sensor in an infrared thermometer) in which some change occurs with a change in temperature; and (2) some means of converting this change into a numerical value (e.g. the visible scale that is marked on a mercury-in-glass thermometer or the digital readout on an infrared model). Thermometers are widely used in technology and industry to monitor processes, in meteorology, in medicine (medical thermometer), and in scientific research. While an individual thermometer is able to measure degrees of hotness, the readings on two thermometers cannot be compared unless they conform to an agreed scale. Today there is an absolute thermodynamic temperature scale. Internationally agreed temperature scales are designed to approximate this closely, based on fixed points and interpolating thermometers."}}, {"canonical_name": "Tamarindos", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 65, "members": [{"entity_id": "tamarindos", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "tamarindos", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["tamarindos"], "contexts": ["tree with pods used as medicine"]}, {"entity_id": "tamarins", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Tamarins", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Tamarins"], "contexts": ["plant that likes dry, open places"]}, {"entity_id": "tamarindos", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Tamarindos", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 33, "variants": ["Tamarindos", "Tamarindo", "Tamarindus indica", "Tamarix", "tamar", "tamar-hindi", "tamargueira", "tamargueiras", "tamarindi", "tamarindo", "tamarindos", "tamarinho", "tamarinhos", "tamarisco"], "contexts": ["medicinal and pleasant tasting fruit", "variant spelling of tamarind"]}, {"entity_id": "tamarindi", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Tamarindi", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 9, "variants": ["Tamarindi", "Tamarindi frefchi", "tamarindi"], "contexts": ["tamarind fruit", "tamarinds passed through sieve"]}, {"entity_id": "tamarindos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Tamarindos", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 21, "variants": ["Tamarindos", "Tamarindis", "tamaragran", "tamargueyra", "tamarindi", "tamarindos"], "contexts": ["fruit used in preparations", "fruit used in medicine"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "tamarindos", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Tamarins", "similarity": 0.905}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "tamarindos", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Tamarindos", "similarity": 0.992}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "tamarindos", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Tamarindi", "similarity": 0.912}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "tamarindos", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Tamarindos", "similarity": 0.992}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Tamarins", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Tamarindos", "similarity": 0.923}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Tamarins", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Tamarindi", "similarity": 0.884}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Tamarins", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Tamarindos", "similarity": 0.923}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Tamarindos", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Tamarindi", "similarity": 0.917}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Tamarindos", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Tamarindos", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Tamarindi", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Tamarindos", "similarity": 0.917}], "id": 86, "stable_key": "clu_88b3e52641c4996e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tamarind", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Tamarindus indica", "family": "Fabaceae", "description": "A tropical tree valued for its edible fruit.", "note": "Refers to the fruit and tree of the Tamarindus indica.", "wikipedia_extract": "Tamarind (Tamarindus indica) is a leguminous tree bearing edible fruit that is indigenous to tropical Africa and naturalized in Asia. The genus Tamarindus is monotypic, meaning that it contains only this species. It belongs to the family Fabaceae. The tamarind tree produces brown, pod-like fruits that contain a sweet, tangy pulp, which is used in cuisines around the world. The pulp is also used in traditional medicine and as a dye. The wood can be used for woodworking, and tamarind seed oil can be extracted from the seeds."}}, {"canonical_name": "sang", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 64, "members": [{"entity_id": "sang", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "sang", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 36, "variants": ["sang"], "contexts": ["circulating bodily fluid", "body fluid related to organs"]}, {"entity_id": "blood", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "blood", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 4, "variants": ["blood"], "contexts": ["metaphorical term for ancestral influence", "fluid for propulsion"]}, {"entity_id": "sangra", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "sangra", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["sangra"], "contexts": ["blood"]}, {"entity_id": "blood", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "blood", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 11, "variants": ["blood"], "contexts": ["fluid whose subtraction can affect consciousness", "circulating fluid in the body"]}, {"entity_id": "blood", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "blood", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 12, "variants": ["blood"], "contexts": ["fluid found on feathers", "circulating bodily fluid"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "sang", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "blood", "similarity": 0.972}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "sang", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "sangra", "similarity": 0.978}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "sang", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "blood", "similarity": 0.972}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "sang", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "blood", "similarity": 0.972}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "blood", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "sangra", "similarity": 0.954}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "blood", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "blood", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "blood", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "blood", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "sangra", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "blood", "similarity": 0.954}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "sangra", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "blood", "similarity": 0.954}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "blood", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "blood", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 87, "stable_key": "clu_3321e843161b68eb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Blood", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q7873", "wikidata_description": "organic fluid which transports nutrients throughout the organism", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blood", "wikipedia_extract": "Blood is a body fluid in the circulatory system of humans and other vertebrates that delivers necessary substances such as nutrients and oxygen to the cells, and transports metabolic waste products away from those same cells. Blood is composed of blood cells suspended in plasma. Plasma, which constitutes 55% of blood fluid, is mostly water (92% by volume), and contains proteins, glucose, mineral ions, and hormones. The blood cells are mainly red blood cells (erythrocytes), white blood cells (leukocytes), and (in mammals) platelets (thrombocytes). The most abundant cells are red blood cells. These contain hemoglobin, which facilitates oxygen transport by reversibly binding to it, increasing its solubility.", "description": "The red bodily fluid circulating in humans and animals."}}, {"canonical_name": "Floride", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 63, "members": [{"entity_id": "florida", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "florida", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 5, "variants": ["florida", "Florida"], "contexts": ["coastal region where plant is found", "province where experience took place"]}, {"entity_id": "floride", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Floride", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 42, "variants": ["Floride", "Floride occidentale", "Florides"], "contexts": ["geographical region", "northern extremity connected to South America"]}, {"entity_id": "florida", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Florida", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 12, "variants": ["Florida"], "contexts": ["land in the Western Indies", "region where Saissasfras tree is found"]}, {"entity_id": "floride", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "Floride", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Floride"], "contexts": ["geographic location in North America", "southern extent of North American mountain range"]}, {"entity_id": "florida", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Florida", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Florida"], "contexts": ["region mentioned in relation to Gulf-stream"]}, {"entity_id": "florida", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Florida", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Florida"], "contexts": ["region affected by storm curve"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "florida", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Floride", "similarity": 0.932}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "florida", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Florida", "similarity": 0.878}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Floride", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Florida", "similarity": 0.9}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Floride", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Florida", "similarity": 0.924}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Floride", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Florida", "similarity": 0.924}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Florida", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Florida", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 88, "stable_key": "clu_e069c91ac1a0251b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Florida", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A region in the southeastern United States, historically explored.", "note": "The spelling 'Floride' is a historical variant.", "wikipedia_extract": "Florida (  FLORR-ih-d\u0259; Spanish: [flo\u02c8\u027ei\u00f0a] ) is a state in the Southeastern and South Atlantic regions of the United States. It borders the Gulf of Mexico to the west, Alabama to the northwest, Georgia to the north, the Atlantic Ocean to the east, the Straits of Florida to the south, and The Bahamas to the southeast. About two-thirds of Florida occupies a peninsula between the Gulf of Mexico and the Atlantic Ocean. It has the longest coastline in the contiguous United States, spanning approximately 1,350 miles (2,170 km), not including its many barrier islands. It is the only state that borders both the Gulf of Mexico and the Atlantic Ocean. With a population of over 23 million, it is the third-most populous state in the United States and ranks seventh in population density as of 2020."}}, {"canonical_name": "observation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 62, "members": [{"entity_id": "observation", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "observation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 40, "variants": ["observation"], "contexts": ["act of noticing and recording phenomena", "perceiving phenomena as nature offers them"]}, {"entity_id": "observation", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "observation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["observation"], "contexts": ["act of watching carefully"]}, {"entity_id": "observation", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "observation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["observation"], "contexts": ["act of watching or noticing carefully", "act of noticing or perceiving"]}, {"entity_id": "observation", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "observation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["observation"], "contexts": ["act of watching and noting phenomena", "gathering data about phenomena"]}, {"entity_id": "observation", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "observation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 11, "variants": ["observation"], "contexts": ["act of watching or noting phenomena", "gathering information through senses"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "observation", "target_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "target_name": "observation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "observation", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "observation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "observation", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "observation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "observation", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "observation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "observation", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "observation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "observation", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "observation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "observation", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "observation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "observation", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "observation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "observation", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "observation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "observation", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "observation", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 89, "stable_key": "clu_8e1f2e83b1916a93", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Observations", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "Systematic recording of data or phenomena in scientific study.", "note": "Refers to the practice of scientific observation and data collection.", "wikipedia_extract": "Observation in the natural sciences refers to the active acquisition of information from a primary source. It involves the act of noticing or perceiving phenomena and gathering data based on direct engagement with the subject of study. In living organisms, observation typically occurs through the senses. In science, it often extends beyond unaided perception, involving the use of scientific instruments to detect, measure, and record data. This enables the observation of phenomena not accessible to human senses alone. Observations in science are typically categorized as either qualitative or quantitative: Qualitative observations describe characteristics that are not expressed numerically, such as color, texture, or behavior."}}, {"canonical_name": "Space", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 55, "members": [{"entity_id": "espace", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "espace", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["espace"], "contexts": ["dimension of the universe"]}, {"entity_id": "space", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "space", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["space"], "contexts": ["dimension of existence"]}, {"entity_id": "space", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "space", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 17, "variants": ["SPACE", "space", "Space"], "contexts": ["three-dimensional extent", "dimension related to consciousness"]}, {"entity_id": "space", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "space", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["Space", "space"], "contexts": ["vast expanse beyond Earth", "region where celestial bodies exist"]}, {"entity_id": "space", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Space", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 30, "variants": ["SPACE", "Space", "space"], "contexts": ["fundamental concept of physics", "three-dimensional extent"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "espace", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "space", "similarity": 0.95}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "espace", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "space", "similarity": 0.95}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "espace", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "space", "similarity": 0.95}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "espace", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Space", "similarity": 0.934}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "space", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "space", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "space", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "space", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "space", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Space", "similarity": 0.981}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "space", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "space", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "space", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Space", "similarity": 0.981}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "space", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Space", "similarity": 0.981}], "id": 90, "stable_key": "clu_82da87fcf7ac989f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "space", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q1", "wikidata_description": "totality consisting of space, time, matter and energy", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Universe", "wikipedia_extract": "The universe is all of space and time and their contents. It comprises all of existence, any fundamental interaction, physical process and physical constant, and therefore all forms of matter and energy, and the structures they form, from sub-atomic particles to entire galactic filaments. Since the early 20th century, the field of cosmology establishes that space and time emerged together at the Big Bang 13.787\u00b10.020 billion years ago and that the universe has been expanding since then. The portion of the universe that can be seen by humans is approximately 93 billion light-years in diameter at present, but the total size of the universe is not known. Some of the earliest cosmological models of the universe were geocentric, placing Earth at the center. During the European Scientific Revolution, astronomical observations led to a heliocentric model."}}, {"canonical_name": "Lungs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 55, "members": [{"entity_id": "lungs", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Lungs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 44, "variants": ["lungs", "Lungs"], "contexts": ["body part treated by the herb", "organs of respiration"]}, {"entity_id": "lungs", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "lungs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 3, "variants": ["lungs"], "contexts": ["organs for respiration in vertebrates", "respiratory organs in air-breathing vertebrates"]}, {"entity_id": "lungs", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "lungs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 3, "variants": ["lungs"], "contexts": ["organ producing feelings", "assigned seat of the soul"]}, {"entity_id": "lungs", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Lungs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lungs"], "contexts": ["organs of respiration"]}, {"entity_id": "lungs", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "lungs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 4, "variants": ["lungs"], "contexts": ["organs for breathing", "organs for respiration"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Lungs", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "lungs", "similarity": 0.974}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Lungs", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "lungs", "similarity": 0.974}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Lungs", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Lungs", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Lungs", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "lungs", "similarity": 0.974}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "lungs", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "lungs", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "lungs", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Lungs", "similarity": 0.974}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "lungs", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "lungs", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "lungs", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Lungs", "similarity": 0.974}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "lungs", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "lungs", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Lungs", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "lungs", "similarity": 0.974}], "id": 91, "stable_key": "clu_bcbc715f48f885c1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lungs", "confidence": "high", "type": "anatomy", "description": "The primary organs of respiration in humans and other vertebrates.", "wikipedia_extract": "The lungs are the primary organs of the respiratory system in many animals, including humans. In mammals and most other tetrapods, two lungs are located near the backbone on either side of the heart. Their function in the respiratory system is to extract oxygen from the atmosphere and transfer it into the bloodstream, and to release carbon dioxide from the bloodstream into the atmosphere, in a process of gas exchange. Respiration is driven by different muscular systems in different species. Mammals, reptiles and birds use their musculoskeletal systems to support and foster breathing. In early tetrapods, air was driven into the lungs by the pharyngeal muscles via buccal pumping, a mechanism still seen in amphibians."}}, {"canonical_name": "Peru", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 54, "members": [{"entity_id": "peru", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Peru", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Peru"], "contexts": ["country in Western South America"]}, {"entity_id": "peru", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Peru", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 13, "variants": ["Peru"], "contexts": ["South American country", "location for magnetic observations"]}, {"entity_id": "peru", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Peru", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 24, "variants": ["Peru", "Per\u00fa"], "contexts": ["region in the New World", "region where a plant is used as a purgative"]}, {"entity_id": "peru", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Peru", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 14, "variants": ["Peru"], "contexts": ["place where figs are found", "region in America"]}, {"entity_id": "peru", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Peru", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Peru"], "contexts": ["location of arc measurement", "country where meridian arcs were measured"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Peru", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Peru", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Peru", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Peru", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Peru", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Peru", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Peru", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Peru", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Peru", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Peru", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Peru", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Peru", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Peru", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Peru", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Peru", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Peru", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Peru", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Peru", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Peru", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Peru", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 92, "stable_key": "clu_11fad535247756b3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Peru", "confidence": "low", "type": "place", "description": "A country in South America, also historically associated with Persia.", "note": "The cluster mixes references to Peru and Persia.", "wikipedia_extract": "Peru, officially the Republic of Peru, is a country in western South America. It is bordered to the north by Ecuador and Colombia, to the east by Brazil, to the southeast by Bolivia, to the south by Chile, and to the south and west by the Pacific Ocean. Peru is a megadiverse country, with habitats ranging from the arid plains of the Pacific coastal region in the west, to the peaks of the Andes mountains extending from the north to the southeast of the country, to the tropical Amazon basin rainforest in the east with the Amazon River. Peru has a population of over 32 million, and its capital and largest city is Lima. At 1,285,216 km2 (496,225 sq mi), Peru is the 19th largest country in the world, and the third largest in South America. Peruvian territory was home to several cultures during the ancient and medieval periods, and has one of the longest histories of civilization of any country, tracing its heritage back to the 10th millennium BCE Caral\u2013Supe civilization, the earliest civilization in the Americas and considered one of the cradles of civilization."}}, {"canonical_name": "Intelligence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 51, "members": [{"entity_id": "intelligence", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "intelligence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["intelligence"], "contexts": ["capacity for learning and reasoning"]}, {"entity_id": "intelligence", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "intelligence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["intelligence"], "contexts": ["human faculty for discovery and creation", "faculty for understanding and reasoning"]}, {"entity_id": "intelligence", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Intelligence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 26, "variants": ["Intelligence", "intelligence"], "contexts": ["capacity for reasoning and understanding", "ability to learn and reason"]}, {"entity_id": "intelligence", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "intelligence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["intelligence"], "contexts": ["capacity for learning and reasoning"]}, {"entity_id": "intelligence", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "intelligence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 20, "variants": ["Intelligence", "intelligence"], "contexts": ["human capacity for learning and reasoning", "capacity for learning and reasoning"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "intelligence", "target_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "target_name": "intelligence", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "intelligence", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Intelligence", "similarity": 0.965}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "intelligence", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "intelligence", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "intelligence", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "intelligence", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "intelligence", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Intelligence", "similarity": 0.965}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "intelligence", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "intelligence", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "intelligence", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "intelligence", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Intelligence", "target_book": "kosmos_hu