is a Latinized form of Greek 'ethos' or 'ethikos', often referring to habit or custom. In a medical context, it could describe chronic or habitual fevers or a consumptive state.", "wikidata_id": "Q12204", "wikidata_description": "infectious disease caused by the bacterium Mycobacterium tuberculosis", "wikipedia_url": "", "wikipedia_extract": ""}}, {"canonical_name": "Kidneys", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 47, "members": [{"entity_id": "kidneys", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Kidneys", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Kidneys"], "contexts": ["organ where stones can form"]}, {"entity_id": "kidneys", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Kidneys", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 42, "variants": ["Kidneys", "Kidnies", "kidneys"], "contexts": ["organs for filtering blood", "organ where stones can form"]}, {"entity_id": "kidneys", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "kidneys", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["kidneys"], "contexts": ["organ that secretes urine", "assigned seat of the soul"]}, {"entity_id": "kidneys", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "kidneys", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["kidneys"], "contexts": ["organs whose shape varies with pelvis"]}, {"entity_id": "kidneys", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "kidneys", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["kidneys"], "contexts": ["organs that filter blood"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Kidneys", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Kidneys", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Kidneys", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "kidneys", "similarity": 0.919}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Kidneys", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "kidneys", "similarity": 0.919}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "kidneys", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "kidneys", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 103, "stable_key": "clu_72d581f56dfa2e6d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Kidneys", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "Organs of the urinary system responsible for filtering blood.", "wikipedia_extract": "In humans, the kidneys are two reddish-brown bean-shaped blood-filtering organs that are a multilobar, multipapillary form of mammalian kidneys, usually without signs of external lobulation. They are located on the left and right in the retroperitoneal space, and in adult humans are about 12 centimetres (4+1\u20442 inches) in length. They receive blood from the paired renal arteries; blood exits into the paired renal veins. Each kidney is attached to a ureter, a tube that carries excreted urine to the bladder. The kidney participates in the control of the volume of various body fluids, fluid osmolality, acid\u2013base balance, various electrolyte concentrations, and removal of toxins. Filtration occurs in the glomerulus: one-fifth of the blood volume that enters the kidneys is filtered.", "wikidata_id": "Q50811406", "wikidata_description": "Academic journal published by Publishing House Zaslavsky, covering the subjects: Medicine: Internal medicine: Specialties of internal medicine: Diseases of the genitourinary system. Urology"}}, {"canonical_name": "Newton", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 46, "members": [{"entity_id": "newton", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "Newton", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Newton"], "contexts": ["famous physicist a