heart", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "heart", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "heart", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "heart", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "heart", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "heart", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "heart", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "heart", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "heart", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "heart", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 131, "stable_key": "clu_ce5504eeaeae4722", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "heart", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "Early modern texts treat the heart as a central organ for circulation and emotion, reflecting a blend of anatomical understanding and philosophical concepts.", "wikidata_id": "Q1072", "wikidata_description": "inner organ for the circulation of blood", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heart", "wikipedia_extract": "The heart is a muscular organ found in humans and other animals. This organ pumps blood through the blood vessels. The heart and blood vessels together make up the circulatory system. The pumped blood carries oxygen and nutrients to the tissue, while carrying metabolic waste such as carbon dioxide to the lungs. In humans, the heart is approximately the size of a closed fist and is located between the lungs, in the middle compartment of the chest, called the mediastinum. In humans, the heart is divided into four chambers: upper left and right atria and lower left and right ventricles.", "description": "The central organ of the circulatory system."}}, {"canonical_name": "comet", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 35, "members": [{"entity_id": "com\u00e8tes", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "com\u00e8tes", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 6, "variants": ["com\u00e8tes"], "contexts": ["corps c\u00e9lestes circulant autour du soleil", "celestial bodies with impulse motion"]}, {"entity_id": "planetary_comets", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "planetary comets", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["planetary comets"], "contexts": ["comets within planetary orbits"]}, {"entity_id": "cometa", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Cometa", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cometa"], "contexts": ["comet"]}, {"entity_id": "comet", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "comet", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 24, "variants": ["comet"], "contexts": ["icy celestial body", "celestial body, not animal"]}, {"entity_id": "comets", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "comets", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 3, "variants": ["comets"], "contexts": ["celestial bodies with tails", "celestial phenomena regarded as supernatural"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "com\u00e8tes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "planetary comets", "similarity": 0.922}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "com\u00e8tes", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "comet", "similarity": 0.888}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "com\u00e8tes", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "comets", "similarity": 0.935}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "planetary comets", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Cometa", "similarity": 0.9}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "planetary comets", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "comet", "similarity": 0.909}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "planetary comets", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "comets", "similarity": 0.961}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Cometa", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "comet", "similarity": 0.979}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Cometa", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "comets", "similarity": 0.927}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "comet", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "comets", "similarity": 0.943}], "id": 132, "stable_key": "clu_4814e58460378bd5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": null, "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "Q3559", "wikidata_description": "icy small astronomical object", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comet", "wikipedia_extract": "A comet is an icy, small Solar System body or interstellar object that warms and begins to release gases when passing close to the Sun, a process called outgassing. This produces an extended, gravitationally unbound atmosphere or coma surrounding the nucleus, and sometimes a tail of gas and dust gas blown out from the coma. These phenomena are due to the effects of solar radiation and the outstreaming solar wind plasma acting upon the nucleus of the comet. Comet nuclei range from a few hundred meters to tens of kilometers across and are composed of loose collections of ice, dust, and small rocky particles. The coma may be up to 15 times Earth's diameter, while the tail may stretch beyond one astronomical unit. If sufficiently close and bright, a comet may be seen from Earth without the aid of a telescope and can subtend an arc of up to 30\u00b0 (60 Moons) across the sky."}}, {"canonical_name": "Rome", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 35, "members": [{"entity_id": "rome", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Rome", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 8, "variants": ["Rome"], "contexts": ["city with a secret name", "ancient city"]}, {"entity_id": "rome", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Rome", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 9, "variants": ["Rome"], "contexts": ["city on the Mohawk river", "city with latitude and temperature data"]}, {"entity_id": "rome", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Rome", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 5, "variants": ["Rome"], "contexts": ["location where Vico saw satellites", "city where Chigi Library is located"]}, {"entity_id": "rome", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Rome", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 9, "variants": ["Rome"], "contexts": ["location of Professor Secchi's observations", "city whose year count is referenced"]}, {"entity_id": "rome", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "Rome", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Rome"], "contexts": ["city whose foundation is mentioned", "empire influencing Europe's civil existence"]}, {"entity_id": "roman", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Roman", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Roman"], "contexts": ["referring to ancient adage", "writers who used the word 'cosmos'"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Rome", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Rome", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Rome", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Rome", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Rome", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Rome", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Rome", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Rome", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Rome", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Rome", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Rome", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Rome", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Rome", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Roman", "similarity": 0.9}], "id": 133, "stable_key": "clu_955afb03b0773db9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Rome", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Ancient city, center of the Roman Empire and a significant historical location.", "note": "Includes references to the Roman Empire itself.", "wikipedia_extract": "Rome is the capital city and most populated comune (municipality) of Italy. It is also the administrative centre of the Lazio region and of the Metropolitan City of Rome. A special comune named Roma Capitale with 2,746,984 residents in 1,287.36 km2 (497.1 mi2), Rome is the third most populous city in the European Union by population within city limits. The Metropolitan City of Rome Capital, with a population of 4,223,885 residents, is the most populous metropolitan city in Italy. Its metropolitan area is the third-most populous within Italy. Rome is located in the central-western portion of the Italian Peninsula, within Lazio (Latium), along the shores of the Tiber Valley."}}, {"canonical_name": "Variation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 34, "members": [{"entity_id": "variation", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "variation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["variation", "Variation"], "contexts": ["magnetic variation", "deviation of magnetic needle from true north"]}, {"entity_id": "variation", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Variation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 26, "variants": ["Variation", "variation"], "contexts": ["change in species over time", "differences in inherited traits"]}, {"entity_id": "variation", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "variation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["variation"], "contexts": ["magnetic declination"]}, {"entity_id": "variation", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "variation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Variation", "variation"], "contexts": ["perturbation of lunar orbit", "perturbation in lunar orbit"]}, {"entity_id": "variation", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "variation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["variation"], "contexts": ["difference or change"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "variation", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Variation", "similarity": 0.951}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "variation", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "variation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "variation", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "variation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "variation", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "variation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Variation", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "variation", "similarity": 0.951}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Variation", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "variation", "similarity": 0.951}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Variation", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "variation", "similarity": 0.951}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "variation", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "variation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "variation", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "variation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "variation", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "variation", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 134, "stable_key": "clu_7c462733575d98cb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Variation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The context 'magnetic variation' points to magnetic declination, but 'change in species over time' and 'magnetic variation' indicate the broader concept of change or deviation.", "wikidata_id": "Q1130761", "wikidata_description": "concept in linguistics, more than one way of saying the same thing", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Variation%20%28linguistics%29", "wikipedia_extract": "Variation is a characteristic of language: there is more than one way of saying the same thing in a given language. Variation can exist in domains such as pronunciation (e.g., more than one way of pronouncing the same phoneme or the same word), lexicon (e.g., multiple words with the same meaning), grammar (e.g., different syntactic constructions expressing the same grammatical function), and other features. Different communities or individuals speaking the same language may differ from each other in their choices of which of the available linguistic features to use, and how often (inter-speaker variation), and the same speaker may make different choices on different occasions (intra-speaker variation). While diversity of variation exists, there are also some general boundaries on variation. For instance, speakers across distinct dialects of a language tend to preserve the same word order or fit new sounds into the language's established inventory of phonemes (the study of such restrictions known as phonotactics, morphotactics, etc.); however, exceptions to these restrictions are possible too. Linguistic variation does not equate to ungrammatical usage of the language, but speakers are still (often unconsciously) sensitive to what is and is not possible in their native lect."}}, {"canonical_name": "air", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 33, "members": [{"entity_id": "air", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "air", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 4, "variants": ["air"], "contexts": ["gaseous mixture exerting pressure", "composition of the cosmic or external medium"]}, {"entity_id": "air", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "air", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 12, "variants": ["air"], "contexts": ["volatile element", "air"]}, {"entity_id": "air", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "air", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["air"], "contexts": ["gaseous mixture surrounding Earth"]}, {"entity_id": "air", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "air", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 5, "variants": ["air"], "contexts": ["substance to be purged from instruments", "gaseous atmosphere"]}, {"entity_id": "air", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "air", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 11, "variants": ["air"], "contexts": ["gaseous element involved in decomposition", "medium for shining in example phrase"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "air", "target_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "target_name": "air", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "air", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "air", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "air", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "air", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "air", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "air", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "air", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "air", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "air", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "air", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "air", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "air", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "air", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "air", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "air", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "air", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "air", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "air", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 135, "stable_key": "clu_ef5cd6384b5e1753", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Air", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "wikidata_id": "Q25299932", "wikidata_description": "island in Padang City, West Sumatra Province, Indonesia"}}, {"canonical_name": "metal", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 33, "members": [{"entity_id": "metal", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Metal", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Metal"], "contexts": ["molten substance in which crystal swims", "lustrous, conductive element"]}, {"entity_id": "metal", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "metal", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["metal"], "contexts": ["processed iron", "material for espodio"]}, {"entity_id": "metal", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "metal", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 20, "variants": ["metal", "metallicos", "metallo", "metals"], "contexts": ["material of the basin", "metal, a natural material"]}, {"entity_id": "metal", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "metal", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 6, "variants": ["metal"], "contexts": ["material of plates", "highly polished plate material"]}, {"entity_id": "metal", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "metal", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["metal"], "contexts": ["elemental substance", "fragment exposed to air or water"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Metal", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "metal", "similarity": 0.996}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Metal", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "metal", "similarity": 0.996}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Metal", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "metal", "similarity": 0.996}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Metal", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "metal", "similarity": 0.996}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "metal", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "metal", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "metal", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "metal", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "metal", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "metal", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "metal", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "metal", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "metal", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "metal", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "metal", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "metal", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 136, "stable_key": "clu_7907da80da20d2e2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Metal", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q11426", "wikidata_description": "element, compound, or alloy that is a good conductor of both electricity and heat", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metal", "note": "The Latin 'rerum metallicarum' directly translates to 'of metallic things' or 'metallic substances'. The context of electrical conductivity reinforces this.", "wikipedia_extract": "A metal (from Ancient Greek  \u03bc\u03ad\u03c4\u03b1\u03bb\u03bb\u03bf\u03bd (m\u00e9tallon) 'mine, quarry, metal') is a material that, when polished or fractured, shows a lustrous appearance, and conducts electricity and heat relatively well. These properties are all associated with having electrons available at the Fermi level, as opposed to nonmetallic materials which do not. Metals are typically ductile (can be drawn into a wire) and malleable (can be shaped via hammering or pressing). A metal may be a chemical element, such as iron, an alloy, such as stainless steel, or a molecular compound, such as polymeric sulfur nitride. The general science of metals is called metallurgy, a subtopic of materials science; aspects of their electronic and thermal properties are also within the scope of condensed matter physics and solid-state chemistry, as it is a multidisciplinary topic. In colloquial use, materials such as steel alloys are referred to as metals, while others, such as polymers, wood, or ceramics are nonmetallic materials."}}, {"canonical_name": "Syria", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 33, "members": [{"entity_id": "syria", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "Syria", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Syria"], "contexts": ["region with a similar balsam"]}, {"entity_id": "syria", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Syria", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 6, "variants": ["Syria"], "contexts": ["country with a popular belief about a\u00ebrolites", "country, center of earthquake circle"]}, {"entity_id": "siria", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Siria", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Siria"], "contexts": ["land where mineral is found"]}, {"entity_id": "syria", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Syria", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 24, "variants": ["Syria", "Siria", "Sir\u00eda", "Syr\u00eda", "S\u00edria"], "contexts": ["region where cubebs were questioned", "region where 'Musa' was used"]}, {"entity_id": "syria", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Syria", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Syria"], "contexts": ["coast with different marine plants"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Syria", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Syria", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Syria", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Siria", "similarity": 0.965}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Syria", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Syria", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Syria", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Syria", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Syria", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Siria", "similarity": 0.965}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Syria", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Syria", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Syria", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Syria", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Siria", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Syria", "similarity": 0.965}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Siria", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Syria", "similarity": 0.965}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Syria", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Syria", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 137, "stable_key": "clu_ed3ea152c5b4a69b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Syria", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A region in the Middle East.", "wikipedia_extract": "Syria, officially the Syrian Arab Republic, is a country in West Asia located in the Eastern Mediterranean and the Levant. It borders the Mediterranean Sea to the west, Turkey to the north and northwest, Iraq to the east and southeast, Jordan to the south, and Israel and Lebanon to the southwest. It is a republic under a provisional government and comprises 14 governorates. Damascus is the capital and largest city. With a population of 26 million across an area of 185,180 square kilometres (71,500 sq mi), it is the 56th-most populous and 87th-largest country. The name \"Syria\" historically referred to a wider region."}}, {"canonical_name": "planet", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 32, "members": [{"entity_id": "planet", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "planet", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["planet"], "contexts": ["cosmic conditions for existence"]}, {"entity_id": "planet", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "planet", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["planet"], "contexts": ["Earth as a celestial body"]}, {"entity_id": "plan\u00e8te", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "plan\u00e8te", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 5, "variants": ["plan\u00e8te"], "contexts": ["Earth", "Earth's crust"]}, {"entity_id": "planet", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "planet", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 19, "variants": ["planet"], "contexts": ["celestial body orbiting a star", "our world"]}, {"entity_id": "planet", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "planet", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 6, "variants": ["planet"], "contexts": ["celestial body orbiting a star", "celestial body observed in motion"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "planet", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "planet", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "planet", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "plan\u00e8te", "similarity": 0.981}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "planet", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "planet", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "planet", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "planet", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "planet", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "plan\u00e8te", "similarity": 0.981}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "planet", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "planet", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "planet", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "planet", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "plan\u00e8te", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "planet", "similarity": 0.981}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "plan\u00e8te", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "planet", "similarity": 0.981}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "planet", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "planet", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 138, "stable_key": "clu_39e8d10aeba6c9c5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Planet", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "wikidata_id": "Q634", "wikidata_description": "celestial body directly orbiting a star or stellar remnant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Planet", "wikipedia_extract": "A planet is a large, rounded astronomical body that is generally required to be in orbit around a star, stellar remnant, or brown dwarf, and is not one itself. The Solar System has eight planets by the most restrictive definition of the term: the terrestrial planets Mercury, Venus, Earth, and Mars, and the giant planets Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, and Neptune. The best available theory of planet formation is the nebular hypothesis, which posits that an interstellar cloud collapses out of a nebula to create a young protostar orbited by a protoplanetary disk. Planets grow in this disk by the gradual accumulation of material driven by gravity, a process called accretion. The word planet comes from the Greek \u03c0\u03bb\u03b1\u03bd\u03ae\u03c4\u03b1\u03b9 (plan\u1e17tai) 'wanderers'. In antiquity, this word referred to the Sun, Moon, and five points of light visible to the naked eye that moved across the background of the stars\u2014namely, Mercury, Venus, Mars, Jupiter, and Saturn."}}, {"canonical_name": "licor", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 32, "members": [{"entity_id": "drink", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "drink", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["drink"], "contexts": ["effects similar to poison"]}, {"entity_id": "drink", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Drink", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Drink"], "contexts": ["liquid clarified by herb", "used for wounds, containing daisies"]}, {"entity_id": "licor", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "licor", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 12, "variants": ["licor"], "contexts": ["alcoholic beverage", "liquid from the tree"]}, {"entity_id": "boissons", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "boissons", "category