fer to a specific type of liquor (alcohol) or more broadly to any liquid or drink. Given the contexts, 'beverage' is the most encompassing modern referent.", "wikidata_id": "Q50816708", "wikidata_description": "Academic journal published by MDPI AG , covering the subjects: Technology: Home economics: Nutrition. Foods and food supply | Medicine: Internal medicine: Specialties of internal medicine: Nutritional diseases. Deficiency diseases"}}, {"canonical_name": "Summer", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 32, "members": [{"entity_id": "summer", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Summer", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 26, "variants": ["Summer", "Summer Months"], "contexts": ["season stirring up humors", "season associated with heat"]}, {"entity_id": "verano", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "verano", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["verano"], "contexts": ["season corresponding to September", "season when flowers appear"]}, {"entity_id": "verano", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "verano", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["verano"], "contexts": ["season for administering waters", "hot season"]}, {"entity_id": "verano", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "verano", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["verano"], "contexts": ["season of summer"]}, {"entity_id": "summer", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Summer", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Summer"], "contexts": ["season with specific temperature and electricity"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Summer", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "verano", "similarity": 0.961}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Summer", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "verano", "similarity": 0.961}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Summer", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "verano", "similarity": 0.961}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Summer", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Summer", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "verano", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "verano", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "verano", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "verano", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "verano", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Summer", "similarity": 0.961}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "verano", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "verano", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "verano", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Summer", "similarity": 0.961}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "verano", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Summer", "similarity": 0.961}], "id": 140, "stable_key": "clu_c2f15b5488e1b951", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Summer", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "The warmest season of the year, between spring and autumn.", "note": "Includes references to Midsummer and abstract concepts of summer.", "wikipedia_extract": "Summer or summertime is the hottest and brightest of the four temperate seasons, occurring after spring and before autumn. At or centred on the summer solstice, daylight hours are the longest and darkness hours are the shortest, with day length decreasing as the season progresses after the solstice. The earliest sunrises and latest sunsets also occur near the date of the solstice. The date of the beginning of summer varies according to definition, climate, tradition, and culture. When it is summer in the Northern Hemisphere, it is winter in the Southern Hemisphere, and vice versa. The modern English summer derives from the Middle English somer, via the Old English sumor."}}, {"canonical_name": "imagination", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 31, "members": [{"entity_id": "imagination", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "imagination", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["imagination"], "contexts": ["faculty for forming new ideas", "faculty of forming new ideas"]}, {"entity_id": "imagination", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "imagination", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["imagination"], "contexts": ["power of the mind"]}, {"entity_id": "imagination", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "imagination", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["imagination"], "contexts": ["faculty of forming mental images"]}, {"entity_id": "imagination", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "imagination", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 25, "variants": ["imagination", "Imagination"], "contexts": ["Soul's faculty", "mental faculty of forming images"]}, {"entity_id": "imagination", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "imagination", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["imagination"], "contexts": ["faculty for forming mental images", "powerful influence on optical impressions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "imagination", "target_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "target_name": "imagination", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "imagination", "target_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "target_name": "imagination", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "imagination", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "imagination", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "imagination", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "imagination", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "imagination", "target_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "target_name": "imagination", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "imagination", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "imagination", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "imagination", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "imagination", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "imagination", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "imagination", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "imagination", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "imagination", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "imagination", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "imagination", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 141, "stable_key": "clu_ee42482851d1cba5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "imagination", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q91048696", "wikidata_description": "vocal track by Bob Dylan; 2017 studio recording; cover version", "wikipedia_extract": "Sociological imagination is a term used in the field of sociology to describe a framework for understanding social reality that places personal experiences within a broader social and historical context. It was coined by American sociologist C. Wright Mills in his 1959 book The Sociological Imagination to describe the type of insight offered by the discipline of sociology. Today, the term is used in many sociology textbooks to explain the nature of sociology and its relevance in daily life. In The Sociological Imagination, Mills attempts to reconcile two different and abstract concepts of social reality: the \"individual\" and the \"society.\" Accordingly, Mills defined sociological imagination as \"the awareness of the relationship between personal experience and the wider society.\" In exercising one's sociological imagination, one seeks to understand situations in one's life by looking at situations in broader society. For example, a single student who fails to keep up with the academic demands of college and ends up dropping out may be perceived to have faced personal difficulties or faults; however, when one considers that around 50% of college students in the United States fail to graduate, we can understand this one student's trajectory as part of a larger social issue.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sociological_imagination"}}, {"canonical_name": "Zinc", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 31, "members": [{"entity_id": "zinc", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "zinc", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["zinc"], "contexts": ["metallic element"]}, {"entity_id": "zinc", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "zinc", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["zinc"], "contexts": ["metal used in galvanic irritation", "metal used for electrical irritation"]}, {"entity_id": "zinc", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "zinc", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["zinc"], "contexts": ["chemical element in meteoric masses", "element in battery"]}, {"entity_id": "zinco", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "zinco", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["zinco"], "contexts": ["metal do qual espodio \u00e9 oxydo", "metal from which tutia is derived"]}, {"entity_id": "zinc", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Zinc", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 24, "variants": ["Zinc", "zinc"], "contexts": ["prismatic crystals changed by heat", "metal in protoxides"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "zinc", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "zinc", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "zinc", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "zinc", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "zinc", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "zinco", "similarity": 0.944}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "zinc", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Zinc", "similarity": 0.955}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "zinc", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "zinc", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "zinc", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "zinco", "similarity": 0.944}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "zinc", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Zinc", "similarity": 0.955}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "zinc", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "zinco", "similarity": 0.944}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "zinc", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Zinc", "similarity": 0.955}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "zinco", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Zinc", "similarity": 0.95}], "id": 142, "stable_key": "clu_5398ea1759e4e0ab", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": null, "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q758", "wikidata_description": "chemical element with symbol Zn and atomic number 30", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zinc", "wikipedia_extract": "Zinc is a chemical element; it has symbol Zn and atomic number 30. It is a slightly brittle metal at room temperature and has a shiny blue whitish appearance when surface oxidation is removed. It is the first element in group 12 (IIB) of the periodic table. Zinc is the 24th most abundant element in Earth's crust, with an average concentration of 70 grams per ton. Zinc also has five stable isotopes; the most abundant of which, Zn-64, comprises nearly half of zinc's total abundance. In some respects, zinc is chemically similar to magnesium: both elements exhibit only one normal oxidation state (+2), and the Zn2+ and Mg2+ ions are of similar size."}}, {"canonical_name": "Fish", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "FISH", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 31, "members": [{"entity_id": "fish", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Fish", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "FISH", "count": 15, "variants": ["fish", "Fish"], "contexts": ["aquatic prey of Mustela vison", "aquatic vertebrate with gills"]}, {"entity_id": "fish", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "fish", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "FISH", "count": 2, "variants": ["fish"], "contexts": ["aquatic vertebrate animal", "food source containing phosphorus"]}, {"entity_id": "fish", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "fish", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "FISH", "count": 3, "variants": ["fish"], "contexts": ["fossil impression found in rocks", "coprolites of fish found"]}, {"entity_id": "fish", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "fish", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "FISH", "count": 7, "variants": ["fish", "Fish"], "contexts": ["peculiar tribe inhabiting ocean depths", "decaying substances emitting light"]}, {"entity_id": "fish", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "fish", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "FISH", "count": 4, "variants": ["fish"], "contexts": ["aquatic vertebrates", "food source from the sea"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Fish", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "fish", "similarity": 0.985}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Fish", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "fish", "similarity": 0.985}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Fish", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "fish", "similarity": 0.985}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Fish", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "fish", "similarity": 0.985}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "fish", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "fish", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "fish", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "fish", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "fish", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "fish", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "fish", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "fish", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "fish", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "fish", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "fish", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "fish", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 143, "stable_key": "clu_70c23abb92c6d4a5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Fish", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "description": "Aquatic vertebrate animals with gills, fins, and typically scales.", "wikipedia_extract": "A fish is an aquatic, anamniotic, gill-bearing vertebrate animal with swimming fins and a tough cranium to protect the brain, but lacking limbs with digits. Fish can be grouped into the more basal jawless fish and the more common jawed fish, the latter including all living cartilaginous and bony fish, as well as the extinct placoderms and acanthodians. In a break from the long tradition of grouping all fish into a single class (Pisces), modern phylogenetics views fish as a paraphyletic group that includes all vertebrates except tetrapods. In English, the plural of \"fish\" is fish when referring to individuals and fishes when referring to species. Most fish are cold-blooded, their body temperature varying with the surrounding water, though some large, active swimmers like the white shark and tuna can maintain a higher core temperature. Many fish can communicate acoustically with each other, such as during courtship displays."}}, {"canonical_name": "fishes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "FISH", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 30, "members": [{"entity_id": "fishes", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Fishes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "FISH", "count": 3, "variants": ["Fishes"], "contexts": ["aquatic animals with fins", "category of aquatic animals"]}, {"entity_id": "fishes", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "fishes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "FISH", "count": 9, "variants": ["fishes", "Fishes"], "contexts": ["aquatic vertebrates with swimbladders", "electric organs of fishes"]}, {"entity_id": "fishes", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "fishes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "FISH", "count": 6, "variants": ["fishes"], "contexts": ["aquatic animals with automatic actions", "animals where vision persists after hemisphere removal"]}, {"entity_id": "fishes", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "fishes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "FISH", "count": 7, "variants": ["fishes"], "contexts": ["aquatic vertebrates", "aquatic vertebrate animals"]}, {"entity_id": "fishes", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "fishes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "FISH", "count": 5, "variants": ["Fishes", "fishes"], "contexts": ["larger division of animals", "earliest known vertebrate remains"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Fishes", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "fishes", "similarity": 0.975}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Fishes", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "fishes", "similarity": 0.975}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Fishes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "fishes", "similarity": 0.975}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Fishes", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "fishes", "similarity": 0.975}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "fi