: "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "ganado", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "cattle", "similarity": 0.951}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "cattle", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "cattle", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "cattle", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "cattle", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "cattle", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "cattle", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 155, "stable_key": "clu_af75f1d6049fe0e9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cattle", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "The Spanish 'ganado' directly translates to 'livestock' or 'cattle' in this context.", "wikidata_id": "Q133733", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cattleya", "wikipedia_extract": "Cattleya () is a genus of orchids from Costa Rica south to Argentina. The genus is abbreviated C in trade journals. Epiphytic or terrestrial orchids with cylindrical rhizome from which the fleshy noodle-like roots grow. Pseudobulbs can be conical, spindle-shaped or cylindrical; with upright growth; one or two leaves growing from the top of them. The leaves can be oblong, lanceolate or elliptical, somewhat fleshy, with smooth margin. The inflorescence is a terminal raceme with few or several flowers."}}, {"canonical_name": "country", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 29, "members": [{"entity_id": "nation", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Nation", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Nation"], "contexts": ["political entity"]}, {"entity_id": "country", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "country", "category": "PLACE", "subcateg