a_medicinal", "target_name": "headaches", "similarity": 0.973}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Headach", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "headache", "similarity": 0.978}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "headaches", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "headaches", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "headaches", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "headache", "similarity": 0.975}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "headaches", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "headache", "similarity": 0.975}], "id": 159, "stable_key": "clu_4cb14193bd996043", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Headache", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "A common symptom characterized by pain in the head.", "wikipedia_extract": "A headache, also known as cephalalgia, is the symptom of pain in the face, head, or neck. It can occur as a migraine, tension-type headache, or cluster headache. There is an increased risk of depression in those with severe headaches. Headaches can occur as a result of many conditions. There are a number of different classification systems for headaches. The most well-recognized is that of the International Headache Society, which classifies it into more than 150 types of primary and secondary headaches.", "modern_term": "Headache", "note": "The terms refer to the symptom of pain in the head, commonly known as headache.", "wikidata_id": "Q67841409", "wikidata_description": "various conditions with the symptom of headache"}}, {"canonical_name": "sympathia", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 28, "members": [{"entity_id": "sympathie", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "sympathie", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "coun