: "Crystal", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "cristal", "similarity": 0.938}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Crystal", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "crystal", "similarity": 0.966}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Crystal", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "crystal", "similarity": 0.966}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "cristal", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "cristal", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "cristal", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "crystal", "similarity": 0.94}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "crystal", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "crystal", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 167, "stable_key": "clu_17cf70a2c0a02b21", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Crystal", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "female given name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crystal%20%28name%29", "wikipedia_extract": "Crystal () is a common English language female given name. Variant forms of the name include Kristal,  Krystal,  Cristal,  Kristel,  Krystle and Kristol. As a feminine name, it is a 19th-century coinage, derived from crystal, a transparent quartz gemstone, usually colorless, that can be cut to reflect brilliant light, whose name comes from Ancient Greek \u03ba\u03c1\u03cd\u03c3\u03c4\u03b1\u03bb\u03bb\u03bf\u03c2 krystallos \"ice\". It increased in usage along with other names suggestive of gemstones. As a masculine name, it is a Scottish diminutive of Christopher. Cristal is a Spanish variant."}}, {"canonical_name": "Teeth", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 25, "members": [{"entity_id": "teeth", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "teeth", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["teeth"], "contexts": ["structures in the mouth for chewing", "part of deer used to judge age"]}, {"entity_id": "teeth", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Teeth", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 16, "variants": ["Teeth"], "contexts": ["part whose pains are eased by Angelica", "aching teeth treated by Balm"]}, {"entity_id": "teeth", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "teeth", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 5, "variants": ["teeth"], "contexts": ["structures for biting and chewing", "correlated with hair in dogs"]}, {"entity_id": "teeth", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "teeth", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["teeth"], "contexts": ["structures in the mouth"]}, {"entity_id": "teeth", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "teeth", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["teeth"], "contexts": ["structures in the mouth for biting"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "teeth", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Teeth", "similarity": 0.998}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "teeth", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "teeth", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "teeth", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "teeth", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "teeth", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "teeth", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Teeth", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "teeth", "similarity": 0.998}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Teeth", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "teeth", "similarity": 0.998}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Teeth", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "teeth", "similarity": 0.998}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "teeth", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "teeth", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "teeth", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "teeth", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "teeth", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "teeth", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 168, "stable_key": "clu_86aa0aee237aa795", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "teeth", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "While 'dentes' can refer to animal tusks, the context of medicinal texts and general biological discussion points to human teeth as the primary referent.", "wikidata_id": "Q1545761", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grevillea", "wikipedia_extract": "Grevillea (), commonly known as spider flowers, is a genus of about 360 species of evergreen flowering plants in the family Proteaceae. Plants in the genus Grevillea are shrubs, rarely trees, with the leaves arranged alternately along the branches, the flowers zygomorphic, arranged in racemes at the ends of branchlets, and the fruit a follicle that splits down one side only, releasing one or two seeds. Plants in the genus Grevillea are shrubs, rarely small trees with simple or compound leaves arranged alternately along the branchlets. The flowers are zygomorphic and typically arranged in pairs along a sometimes branched raceme at the ends of branchlets. The flowers are bisexual, usually with four tepals in a single whorl. There are four stamens and the gynoecium has a single carpel."}}, {"canonical_name": "Ganges", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 25, "members": [{"entity_id": "ganges", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Ganges", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 5, "variants": ["Ganges", "Gange"], "contexts": ["river", "river in India"]}, {"entity_id": "ganges", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Ganges", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Ganges"], "contexts": ["river in India", "major river in India"]}, {"entity_id": "ganges", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Ganges", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ganges"], "contexts": ["river in India"]}, {"entity_id": "ganges", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Ganges", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 14, "variants": ["Ganges"], "contexts": ["river in India", "valley in India"]}, {"entity_id": "ganges", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Ganges", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Ganges"], "contexts": ["river with mouths causing bore", "river with tidal wave at mouths"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Ganges", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Ganges", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Ganges", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Ganges", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Ganges", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Ganges", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Ganges", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Ganges", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Ganges", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Ganges", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Ganges", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Ganges", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Ganges", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Ganges", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Ganges", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Ganges", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Ganges", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Ganges", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Ganges", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Ganges", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 169, "stable_key": "clu_2884614e5d3dcc2c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ganges River", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Major river in India, important geographical and historical feature.", "wikipedia_extract": "The Ganges ( GAN-jeez; in India: Ganga,  GUNG-ah; in Bangladesh: Padma,  PUD-m\u0259) is a trans-boundary river of Asia which flows through India and Bangladesh. The 2,525-kilometre-long (1,569 mi) river rises in the western Himalayas in the Indian state of Uttarakhand. It flows south and east through the Gangetic plain of North India, receiving the right-bank tributary, the Yamuna, which also rises in the western Indian Himalayas, and several left-bank tributaries from Nepal that account for the bulk of its flow. In West Bengal, India, a feeder canal taking off from its right bank diverts 50% of its flow southwards, artificially connecting it to the Hooghly River. The Ganges continues into Bangladesh, its name changing to the Padma. It is then joined by the Jamuna, the lower stream of the Brahmaputra, and eventually the Meghna, forming the major estuary of the Ganges Delta, and emptying into the Bay of Bengal."}}, {"canonical_name": "plantas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 24, "members": [{"entity_id": "plantes", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "plantes", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["plantes"], "contexts": ["plants"]}, {"entity_id": "plantes", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "plantes", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 5, "variants": ["plantes"], "contexts": ["aquatic plants living in hot waters", "vegetable organisms"]}, {"entity_id": "plantes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "plantes", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["plantes", "plantes cultiv\u00e9es"], "contexts": ["organisms needing nutrition", "general term for botanical life"]}, {"entity_id": "plantas", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "plantas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 8, "variants": ["plantas", "Plantas", "planta"], "contexts": ["plants from the Indies with medicinal virtues", "plants from the Indies"]}, {"entity_id": "plantas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "plantas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 7, "variants": ["plantas"], "contexts": ["plants with medicinal virtues", "plants used in medicine"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "plantes", "target_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "target_name": "plantes", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "plantes", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "plantes", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "plantes", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "plantas", "similarity": 0.995}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "plantes", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "plantas", "similarity": 0.995}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "plantes", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "plantes", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "plantes", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "plantas", "similarity": 0.995}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "plantes", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "plantas", "similarity": 0.995}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "plantes", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "plantas", "similarity": 0.995}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "plantes", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "plantas", "similarity": 0.995}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "plantas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "plantas", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 170, "stable_key": "clu_755bbcf416011c08-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Plants", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "note": "This cluster refers to plants in general, not a specific species or genus.", "wikidata_id": "Q164686", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plant", "wikipedia_extract": "Plants are the eukaryotic organisms that constitute the kingdom Plantae. They are predominantly photosynthetic, meaning that they obtain their energy from sunlight. They do that using the green pigment chlorophyll in their chloroplasts to produce sugars from carbon dioxide and water. Exceptions are parasitic plants that have lost the genes for chlorophyll and photosynthesis, and obtain their energy from other plants or fungi. Most plants are multicellular, except for some green algae. Historically, as in Aristotle's biology, the plant kingdom encompassed all living things that were not animals, and included algae and fungi."}}, {"canonical_name": "Plato", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 24, "members": [{"entity_id": "plato", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Plato", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 6, "variants": ["Plato"], "contexts": ["ancient philosopher, knowledge as remembrance", "ancient writer"]}, {"entity_id": "plato", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Plato", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Plato"], "contexts": ["assigned parts of body to soul faculties", "ancient philosopher of soul substantiality"]}, {"entity_id": "plato", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Plato", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 10, "variants": ["Plato"], "contexts": ["ancient philosopher cited in footnote", "ancient Greek philosopher cited in footnote"]}, {"entity_id": "plato", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Plato", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Plato", "Platonis", "Plat\u00e3o"], "contexts": ["ancient philosopher", "ancient philosopher cited for virtues of wine"]}, {"entity_id": "plato", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Plato", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Plato"], "contexts": ["ancient philosopher"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Plato", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Plato", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Plato", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Plato", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Plato", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Plato", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Plato", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Plato", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Plato", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Plato", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Plato", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Plato", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Plato", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Plato", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Plato", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Plato", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Plato", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Plato", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Plato", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Plato", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 171, "stable_key": "clu_93d668d18e3fbd8a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Plato", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": -428, "death_year": -348, "description": "Ancient Greek philosopher, founder of the Academy.", "wikidata_id": "Q859", "wikidata_description": "4th-century BCE Greek philosopher", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plato", "wikipedia_extract": "Plato ( PLAY-toe; Greek: \u03a0\u03bb\u03ac\u03c4\u03c9\u03bd, Pl\u00e1t\u014dn; born c. 428\u2013423 BC, died 348/347 BC) was an ancient Greek philosopher of Classical Athens who is most commonly considered the foundational thinker of the Western philosophical tradition. An innovator of the literary dialogue and dialectic forms, Plato influenced all the major areas of theoretical philosophy and practical philosophy, and was the founder of the Platonic Academy, a philosophical school in Athens where Plato taught the collection of philosophical theories that would later become known as Platonism. Plato's most famous contribution is his Theory of Forms (or Ideas), which aims to solve what is now known as the problem of universals. He was influenced by the pre-Socratic thinkers Pythagoras, Heraclitus, and Parmenides, although much of what is known about them is derived from Plato himself. Along with his teacher Socrates, and his student Aristotle, Plato is a central figure in the history of Western philosophy."}}, {"canonical_name": "Longitude", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 24, "members": [{"entity_id": "longitude", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Longitude", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Longitude"], "contexts": ["angular distance east or west of prime meridian"]}, {"entity_id": "longitudes", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "longitudes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["longitudes"], "contexts": ["geographic coordinate related to temperature"]}, {"entity_id": "longitude", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "longitude", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["longitude"], "contexts": ["geographic divisions affecting temperature", "angular distance east or west of prime meridian"]}, {"entity_id": "longitudes", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "longitudes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["longitudes", "latitudes", "longitude"], "contexts": ["measurement of east-west position", "measurement of north-south position"]}, {"entity_id": "longitude", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Longitude", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 16, "variants": ["Longitude", "longitude"], "contexts": ["celestial coordinate", "angular distance east or west of ecliptic"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Longitude", "target_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "target_name": "longitudes", "similarity": 0.852}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Longitude", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "longitude", "similarity": 0.903}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Longitude", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "longitudes", "similarity": 0.852}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Longitude", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Longitude", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "longitudes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "longitude", "similarity": 0.948}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "longitudes", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "longitudes", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "longitudes", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Longitude", "similarity": 0.852}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "longitude", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "longitudes", "similarity": 0.948}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "longitude", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Longitude", "similarity": 0.903}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "longitudes", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Longitude", "similarity": 0.852}], "id": 172, "stable_key": "clu_31e5c2012249eeee", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Longitude", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q36477", "wikidata_description": "geographic coordinate that specifies the east-west position of a point on the Earth's surface", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Longitude", "wikipedia_extract": "Longitude (, AU and UK also ) is a geographic coordinate that specifies the east-west position of a point on the surface of the Earth, or another celestial body. It is an angular measurement, usually expressed in degrees and denoted by the Greek letter lambda (\u03bb). Meridians are imaginary semicircular lines running from pole to pole that connect points with the same longitude. The prime meridian defines 0\u00b0 longitude; by convention the International Reference Meridian for the Earth passes near the Royal Observatory in Greenwich, south-east London on the island of Great Britain. Positive longitudes are east of the prime meridian, and negative ones are west. Because of the Earth's rotation, there is a close connection between longitude and time measurement."}}, {"canonical_name": "Italia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 24, "members": [{"entity_id": "italia", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Italia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Italia"], "contexts": ["region where wolves are believed harmful"]}, {"entity_id": "italia", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "Italia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Italia"], "contexts": ["Italy"]}, {"entity_id": "italia", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Italia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 6, "variants": ["Italia"], "contexts": ["country where oil of Ben is made", "region where Mechoacan was used"]}, {"entity_id": "italia", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Italia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 9, "variants": ["Italia"], "contexts": ["country in Europe", "continent where a fish is not found"]}, {"entity_id": "italia", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Italia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 7, "variants": ["Italia"], "contexts": ["country where book was received", "Italy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Italia", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "Italia", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Italia", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Italia", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Italia", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Italia", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Italia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Italia", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Italia", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Italia", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Italia", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Italia", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Italia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Italia", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Italia", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Italia", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Italia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Italia", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Italia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Italia", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 173, "stable_key": "clu_0af750baac0f4da7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Italy", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "European country associated with specific plants and historical references.", "wikipedia_extract": "Italy, officially the Italian Republic, is a country in Southern and Western Europe. It consists of a peninsula that extends into the Mediterranean Sea, with the Alps on its northern land border, as well as nearly 800 islands, notably Sicily and Sardinia. Italy shares land borders with France to the west; Switzerland and Austria to the north; Slovenia to the east; and the two enclaves of Vatican City and San Marino. It is the tenth-largest country in Europe by area, covering 301,340 km2 (116,350 sq mi), and the third-most populous member state of the European Union, with nearly 59 million inhabitants. Italy's capital and largest city is Rome; other major cities include Milan, Naples, Turin, Palermo, Bologna, Florence, Genoa, and Venice. The history of Italy goes back to numerous Italic peoples, notably including the ancient Romans, who conquered the Mediterranean world during the Roman Republic and ruled it for centuries during the Roman Empire."}}, {"canonical_name": "argile", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 24, "members": [{"entity_id": "argile", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "argile", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 8, "variants": ["argile"], "contexts": ["clay", "clay material"]}, {"entity_id": "argile", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "argile", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 12, "variants": ["argile"], "contexts": ["component of the soil", "clay found in layers"]}, {"entity_id": "argilla", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "argilla", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["argilla", "argilla ferruginosa"], "contexts": ["synonym for clay", "type of earth used in medicine"]}, {"entity_id": "argilla", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "argilla", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["argilla"], "contexts": ["clay"]}, {"entity_id": "argilla", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "argilla", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["argilla"], "contexts": ["clay"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "argile", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "argile", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "argile", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "argilla", "similarity": 0.981}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "argile", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "argilla", "similarity": 0.981}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "argile", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "argilla", "similarity": 0.981}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "argile", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "argilla", "similarity": 0.981}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "argile", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "argilla", "similarity": 0.981}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "argile", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "argilla", "similarity": 0.981}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "argilla", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "argilla", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "argilla", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "argilla", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "argilla", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "argilla", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 174, "stable_key": "clu_7ff1143f6fac33cf", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Clay", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q164192", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claytonia", "note": "Argile and argilla are historical terms for clay.", "wikipedia_extract": "Claytonia (spring beauty) is a genus of flowering plants native to Asia, North America, and Central America. The vitamin-rich leaves can be eaten raw or cooked, and the tubers can be prepared like potatoes. The plants are somewhat fleshy and only a few centimeters in height. The flower heads are about 2.5 cm (1 inch) in diameter. The genus was formerly included in the purslane family (Portulacaceae), but with the adoption of the APG IV system, in 2009 it was moved to the family Montiaceae. A number of the species were formerly treated in the related genus Montia."}}, {"canonical_name": "rotation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 24, "members": [{"entity_id": "rotation", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "rotation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["rotation"], "contexts": ["spinning around an axis"]}, {"entity_id": "rotation", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "rotation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["rotation"], "contexts": ["movement of the eyeball"]}, {"entity_id": "rotation", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "rotation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["rotation"], "contexts": ["spinning of a celestial body", "spinning of celestial body"]}, {"entity_id": "rotation", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "rotation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 18, "variants": ["rotation", "Rotation"], "contexts": ["spinning motion", "spinning motion of the earth"]}, {"entity_id": "rotation", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "rotation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["rotation"], "contexts": ["spinning motion around an axis", "spinning motion"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "rotation", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "rotation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "rotation", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "rotation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "rotation", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "rotation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "rotation", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "rotation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "rotation", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "rotation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "rotation", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "rotation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "rotation", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "rotation", "similarity": 1