"kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "horse", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["horse"], "contexts": ["extant animal found as fossil"]}, {"entity_id": "horse", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "horse", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["horse"], "contexts": ["animal used as a source of power", "example of a labouring force"]}, {"entity_id": "horse", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "horse", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["horse"], "contexts": ["animal providing force for plough"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "horse", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "horse", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "horse", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "horse", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "horse", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "horse", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "horse", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "horse", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "horse", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "horse", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "horse", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "horse", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "horse", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "horse", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "horse", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "horse", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "horse", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "horse", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "horse", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "horse", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 176, "stable_key": "clu_d4797be5c7ad162b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Horse", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "The phrase 'agua do mar' (sea water) in the context of 'animal listed with numbers' and 'water used for healing soldiers' is a misinterpretation or OCR error. The surrounding English texts and the specific mention of 'Mare' (a mare, or female horse) strongly indicate the referent is 'horse'. The 'animal listed with numbers' context might refer to breeds or classifications, and 'female horse used in a cross' is a direct biological reference.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "domesticated four-footed mammal from the equine family", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horse", "wikipedia_extract": "The horse (Equus ferus caballus) is a domesticated, one-toed, hoofed mammal. It belongs to the taxonomic family Equidae and is one of two extant subspecies of Equus ferus. The horse has evolved over the past 45 to 55 million years from a small multi-toed creature, Eohippus, into the large single-toed animal of today, originally in North America. Humans began domesticating horses around 4000 BCE in Central Asia, and their domestication is believed to have been widespread by 3000 BCE. Horses in the subspecies caballus are domesticated, although some domesticated populations live in the wild as feral horses. These feral populations are not true wild horses, which are horses that have never been domesticated.", "linnaean": "Equus caballus", "family": "Equidae", "description": "A domesticated mammal with hooves, widely used by humans."}}, {"canonical_name": "digestion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 23, "members": [{"entity_id": "digestion", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "digestion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["digestion"], "contexts": ["process of breaking down food"]}, {"entity_id": "digestion", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "digestion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["digestion"], "contexts": ["biological process of breaking down food"]}, {"entity_id": "digestion", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "digestion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 8, "variants": ["digestion", "Digestion", "digesting"], "contexts": ["process of breaking down food", "aided by Balm"]}, {"entity_id": "digestion", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "digestion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 8, "variants": ["digestion", "digesta", "digestione", "digest\u00e3o", "digetao"], "contexts": ["process affected by eating habits", "process of breaking down food"]}, {"entity_id": "digestion", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "digestion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["digestion"], "contexts": ["process of breaking down food"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "digestion", "target_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "target_name": "digestion", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "digestion", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "digestion", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "digestion", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "digestion", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "digestion", "targ