an effect"]}, {"entity_id": "cause", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Cause", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Cause", "cause"], "contexts": ["reason for an effect", "reason for an event or phenomenon"]}, {"entity_id": "causation", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Causation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["causation", "Causation"], "contexts": ["hypothesis of causation", "process of causing an effect"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "cause", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "causa", "similarity": 0.986}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "cause", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "causes", "similarity": 0.954}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "cause", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "causa", "similarity": 0.986}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "cause", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "cause", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "causa", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "causes", "similarity": 0.931}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "causa", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "causa", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "causa", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "cause", "similarity": 0.986}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "causes", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "causa", "similarity": 0.931}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "causes", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "cause", "similarity": 0.954}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "causa", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "cause", "similarity": 0.986}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Cause", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Causation", "similarity": 0.933}], "id": 201, "stable_key": "clu_4c6b51045c026813", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Causality", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q1149766", "wikidata_description": "conceptual link between natural phenomena that temporally follow one another because one is the cause of the other", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Causality%20%28physics%29", "wikipedia_extract": "In physics,  causality, requires the cause of an event to be in the past light cone of the result and to be ultimately reducible to fundamental interactions. Similarly, a cause cannot have an effect outside its future light cone. Macroscopic vs microscopic causality Causality can be defined macroscopically, at the level of human observers, or microscopically, for fundamental events at the atomic level. The strong causality principle forbids information transfer faster than the speed of light; the weak causality principle operates at the microscopic level and need not lead to information transfer. Physical models can obey the weak principle without obeying the strong version. In the algebraic formulation of quantum field theory, microscopic causality is taken as an axiom, formulated technically but equivalent to the idea that two measurements in different places at the same time cannot affect each other."}}, {"canonical_name": "Appetite", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 19, "members": [{"entity_id": "appetite", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Appetite", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 10, "variants": ["Appetite", "appetit to meat", "appetite"], "contexts": ["provoked by cherry gum", "desire for food"]}, {"entity_id": "appetite", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Appetite", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Appetite"], "contexts": ["Soul's faculty"]}, {"entity_id": "appetite", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "appetite", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["appetite"], "contexts": ["strong desire or craving"]}, {"entity_id": "appetite", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "appetite", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["appetite", "appetite de comer"], "contexts": ["desire to eat", "appetite, a bodily desire for food"]}, {"entity_id": "appetite", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "appetite", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["appetite"], "contexts": ["desire to eat"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Appetite", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Appetite", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "appetite", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "appetite", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "appetite", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "appetite", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "appetite", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "appetite", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 202, "stable_key": "clu_5161e69bb6f8e146", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Appetite", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q254327", "wikidata_description": 