se 'Ouvidos' directly translates to 'ears' or 'hearing organs'. The English cognates 'Hearing', 'auditory organs', and 'auditory apparatus' all point to the ear as the anatomical structure responsible for hearing.", "wikidata_id": "Q115777984", "wikidata_description": "scientific article published on 26 January 2022"}}, {"canonical_name": "nerves", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "nerves", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "nerves", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["nerves"], "contexts": ["nervous system structures", "fibers transmitting impulses"]}, {"entity_id": "nerves", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "nerves", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 8, "variants": ["nerves"], "contexts": ["nervous system tissue", "conductors of nerve impulses"]}, {"entity_id": "nerves", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "nerves", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["nerves"], "contexts": ["structures transmitting electrical signals", "body tissues transmitting signals"]}, {"entity_id": "nerves", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "nerves", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["nerves"], "contexts": ["transmit sensory perceptions"]}, {"entity_id": "nerves", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "nerves", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 5, "variants": ["nerves"], "contexts": ["structures conveying impulses", "structures transmitting sensations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "nerves", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "nerves", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "nerves", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "nerves", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "nerves", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "nerves", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "nerves", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "nerves", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "nerves", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "nerves", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "nerves", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "nerves", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "nerves", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "nerves", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "nerves", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "nerves", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "nerves", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "nerves", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "nerves", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "nerves", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 207, "stable_key": "clu_05fd54596f87c8b3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "nerves", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The text mentions 'neruos' and 'nerari' in the context of nerves affected by cramps, pointing to the anatomical concept of nerves.", "wikidata_id": "Q3234179", "wikidata_description": "short story by Anton Chekhov", "wikipedia_extract": "A nerve is an enclosed, cable-like bundle of nerve fibers (called axons). Nerves have historically been considered the basic units of the peripheral nervous system. A nerve provides a common pathway for the electrochemical nerve impulses called action potentials that are transmitted along each of the axons to peripheral organs or, in the case of sensory nerves, from the periphery back to the central nervous system. Each axon is an extension of an individual neuron, along with other supportive cells such as some Schwann cells that coat the axons in myelin. Each axon is surrounded by a layer of connective tissue called the endoneurium. The axons are bundled together into groups called fascicles, and each fascicle is wrapped in a layer of connective tissue called the perineurium.", "wikipedia_url": "https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Les_Nerfs"}}, {"canonical_name": "Pigeon", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "pigeon", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Pigeon", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 2, "variants": ["Pigeon"], "contexts": ["synonym for Dove", "common bird species"]}, {"entity_id": "pigeon", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Pigeon", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 13, "variants": ["Pigeon", "pigeon"], "contexts": ["domesticated bird breeds", "domesticated bird species"]}, {"entity_id": "pigeon", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "pigeon", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["pigeon"], "contexts": ["common bird species"]}, {"entity_id": "pigeon", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "pigeon", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["pigeon"], "contexts": ["bird studied for brain function"]}, {"entity_id": "pigeon", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "pigeon", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["pigeon"], "contexts": ["animal dodo was proven to be"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Pigeon", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Pigeon", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "pigeon", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "pigeon", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "pigeon", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "pigeon", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "pigeon", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "pigeon", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 208, "stable_key": "clu_2b62f246b4c02c90", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pigeon", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q10856", "wikidata_description": "family of birds (Aves)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Columbidae", "wikipedia_extract": "Columbidae is a bird family consisting of doves and pigeons. It is the only family in the order Columbiformes. These are stout-bodied birds with small heads, relatively short necks and slender bills that in some species feature fleshy ceres. They feed largely on plant matter, feeding on seeds (granivory), fruit (frugivory), and foliage (folivory). In colloquial English, the smaller species tend to be called \"doves\", and the larger ones \"pigeons\", although the distinction is not consistent, and there is no scientific separation between them. Historically, the common names for these birds involve a great deal of variation."}}, {"canonical_name": "relation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 17, "members": [{"entity_id": "relation", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "relation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["relation"], "contexts": ["connection or link between things"]}, {"entity_id": "relation", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "relation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 7, "variants": ["relation", "Relation"], "contexts": ["connection to topic or interest", "connection or association between things"]}, {"entity_id": "relation", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "relation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["relation"], "contexts": ["connection or link"]}, {"entity_id": "rela\u00e7\u00e3o", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "rela\u00e7\u00e3o", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["rela\u00e7\u00e3o"], "contexts": ["empirically admitted relation for comparison"]}, {"entity_id": "relation", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "relation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 7, "variants": ["relation"], "contexts": ["philosophical concept of connection between entities", "connection or correspondence between entities"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "relation", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "relation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "relation", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "relation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "relation", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "rela\u00e7\u00e3o", "similarity": 0.963}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "relation", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "relation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "relation", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "relation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "relation", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "rela\u00e7\u00e3o", "similarity": 0.963}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "relation", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "relation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "relation", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "rela\u00e7\u00e3o", "similarity": 0.963}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "relation", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "relation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "rela\u00e7\u00e3o", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "relation", "similarity": 0.963}], "id": 209, "stable_key": "clu_d8fadd8c091a5b2f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Relationship", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Covers both literal relationships and abstract connections.", "wikidata_id": "Q930933", "wikidata_description": "general relation between different objects or individuals", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Relation%20%28philosophy%29", "wikipedia_extract": "Relations are ways in which several entities stand to each other. They usually connect distinct entities but some associate an entity with itself. The adicity of a relation is the number of entities it connects. The direction of a relation is the order in which the elements are related to each other. The converse of a relation carries the same information and has the opposite direction, like the contrast between \"two is less than five\" and \"five is greater than two\". Both relations and properties express features in reality with a key difference being that relations apply to several entities while properties belong to a single entity."}}, {"canonical_name": "Members", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 17, "members": [{"entity_id": "membres", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "membres", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["membres"], "contexts": ["limbs of animals"]}, {"entity_id": "members", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Members", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 6, "variants": ["Members", "Member"], "contexts": ["members out of joint strengthened", "body parts out of joint"]}, {"entity_id": "miembros", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "miembros", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 4, "variants": ["miembros"], "contexts": ["limbs of the body", "limbs"]}, {"entity_id": "miembros", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "miembros", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 3, "variants": ["miembros", "miembros interiores", "miembros mas principales"], "contexts": ["limbs", "internal body parts"]}, {"entity_id": "membros", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "membros", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 3, "variants": ["membros", "membros principaes"], "contexts": ["limbs, parts of the body", "body parts that wine can de-swell"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "membres", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Members", "similarity": 0.978}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "membres", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "miembros", "similarity": 0.996}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "membres", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "miembros", "similarity": 0.996}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "membres", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "membros", "similarity": 0.992}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Members", "targ