ilarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "arms", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "arm", "similarity": 0.918}], "id": 212, "stable_key": "clu_7fe687f12077013c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Arm", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "While 'arm' refers to a body part, in these early modern contexts, it's often used more broadly as a descriptor or in comparative statements, making 'concept' a more fitting type when discussing its usage across diverse texts rather than a specific anatomical instance.", "wikidata_id": "Q399", "wikidata_description": "sovereign state in the South Caucasus region of Eurasia", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armenia", "wikipedia_extract": "Armenia, officially the Republic of Armenia, is a landlocked country in the Armenian highlands of West Asia. It is a part of the Caucasus region and is bordered by Turkey to the west, Georgia to the north, Azerbaijan to the east, and Iran and the Azerbaijani exclave of Nakhchivan to the south. Yerevan is the capital, largest city and financial center. The Armenian highlands have been home to the Hayasa-Azzi, Shupria, and Nairi peoples. By at least 600 BC, an archaic form of Proto-Armenian, an Indo-European language, had diffused into the Armenian highlands. The first Armenian state of Urartu was established in 860 BC, and by the 6th century BC it was replaced by the Satrapy of Armenia."}}, {"canonical_name": "organs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 17, "members": [{"entity_id": "organs", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "organs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["organs"], "contexts": ["body parts for specific functions"]}, {"entity_id": "organs", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "organs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 4, "va