ns Letrados", "homens gottofos"], "contexts": ["male bodies", "men who also suffered suffocations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "hommes", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "hombres", "similarity": 0.988}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "hommes", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "hombres", "similarity": 0.988}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "hommes", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "homens", "similarity": 0.99}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "hommes", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "homens", "similarity": 0.99}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "hombres", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "hombres", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "hombres", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "homens", "similarity": 0.995}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "hombres", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "homens", "similarity": 0.995}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "hombres", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "homens", "similarity": 0.995}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "hombres", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "homens", "similarity": 0.995}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "homens", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "homens", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 214, "stable_key": "clu_605ec8cdf0359f79", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Individuals", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "While 'individuals' refers to distinct beings, in a scientific context, it often functions as a concept referring to distinct units of observation or analysis rather than a specific group of people.", "wikidata_id": "Q5", "wikidata_description": "any single member of Homo sapiens, unique extant species of the genus Homo", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human", "description": "General term for human beings, often in the context of medical treatment.", "wikipedia_extract": "Humans (Homo sapiens, meaning \"thinking man\" or \"wise man\") are the most common and widespread species of primate, and the only extant species of the genus Homo. They are great apes characterized by hairlessness, obligate bipedality, manual dexterity with opposable thumbs, precision grip, and high intelligence. Humans have large brains compared to body size (a high encephalization quotient), enabling more advanced cognitive skills that facilitate successful adaptation to varied environments, development of sophisticated tools, and formation of complex social structures and civilizations. Humans possess a disproportionately larger volume of both cerebral white matter and gray matter present in the prefrontal cortex (PFC) than any other primate species, which facilitated the expansion of higher-order executive functions. Humans are highly social, with individual humans tending to belong to a multi-layered network of distinct social groups \u2013 from families and peer groups to corporations and political states. As such, social interactions between humans have established a wide variety of values, social norms, languages, and traditions (collectively termed institutions), each of which bolsters human society."}}, {"canonical_name": "cabellos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 17, "members": [{"entity_id": "hairs", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "hairs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["hairs"], "contexts": ["clothed the style"]}, {"entity_id": "hair", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "hair", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["hair"], "contexts": ["filaments growing from skin", "body appendage"]}, {"entity_id": "hairs", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "hairs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["hairs"], "contexts": ["structures on land plants"]}, {"entity_id": "cabello", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "cabello", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["cabello"], "contexts": ["hair"]}, {"entity_id": "cabellos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "cabellos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 12, "variants": ["cabellos", "cabell", "cabello", "cabelo"], "contexts": ["hair, a bodily product", "hair used in remedies"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "hairs", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "hair", "similarity": 0.966}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "hairs", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "hairs", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "hairs", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "cabello", "similarity": 0.943}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "hairs", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cabellos", "similarity": 0.959}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "hair", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "hairs", "similarity": 0.966}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "hair", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "cabello", "similarity": 0.979}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "hair", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cabellos", "similarity": 0.947}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "hairs", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "cabello", "similarity": 0.943}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "hairs", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cabellos", "similarity": 0.959}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "cabello", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cabellos", "similarity": 0.972}], "id": 215, "stable_key": "clu_74baff7bb12c9252", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hair", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "Identified as animal hair based on 'filament from animal skin' and 'body part, plant helps shedding'.", "wikidata_id": "Q866324", "wikidata_description": "organ found in the dermal layer of the mammalian skin that regulates hair growth", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hair%20follicle", "wikipedia_extract": "Hair is a protein filament that grows from follicles found in the dermis. Hair is one of the defining characteristics of mammals. The human body, apart from areas of glabrous skin, is covered in follicles which produce thick terminal and fine vellus hair. Most common interest in hair is focused on hair growth, hair types, and hair care, but hair is also an important biomaterial primarily composed of protein, notably alpha-keratin. Attitudes towards different forms of hair, such as hairstyles and hair removal, vary widely across different cultures and historical periods, but it is often used to indicate a person's personal beliefs or social position, such as their age, gender, or religion. The word \"hair\" usually refers to two distinct structures: the part beneath the skin, called the hair follicle, or, when pulled from the skin, the bulb or root.", "description": "Filamentous growth from follicles in the skin, used in remedies."}}, {"canonical_name": "moisture", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 17, "members": [{"entity_id": "moisture", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "moisture", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 8, "variants": ["moisture", "moist humors", "moisture thereof"], "contexts": ["quality causing joint issues", "malignant quality of ulcers dried by fern"]}, {"entity_id": "humidades", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "humidades", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["humidades"], "contexts": ["bodily fluids"]}, {"entity_id": "humidades_nutritivas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "humidades nutritivas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["humidades nutritivas"], "contexts": ["nutritive fluids"]}, {"entity_id": "moisture", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "moisture", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["moisture"], "contexts": ["environmental condition affecting variation", "water in the atmosphere or soil"]}, {"entity_id": "humidad_quajada", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "humidad quajada", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["humidad quajada"], "contexts": ["proposed material for metal genesis"]}, {"entity_id": "moisture", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Moisture", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 4, "variants": ["moisture", "Moisture"], "contexts": ["water in the air", "water vapor in the air"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "moisture", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "humidades", "similarity": 0.935}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "moisture", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "humidades nutritivas", "similarity": 0.883}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "humidades", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "humidades nutritivas", "similarity": 0.916}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "moisture", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "humidad quajada", "similarity": 0.886}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "moisture", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Moisture", "similarity": 0.988}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "humidad quajada", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Moisture", "similarity": 0.884}], "id": 216, "stable_key": "clu_293acff9172e0a55", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bodily fluid", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "Liquid substances within the body, essential for its functions.", "note": "Includes concepts like humors or other bodily liquids.", "wikipedia_extract": "Body fluids, bodily fluids, or biofluids, sometimes body liquids, are liquids within the body of an organism. In lean healthy adult men, the total body water is about 60% (60\u201367%) of the total body weight; it is usually slightly lower in women (52\u201355%). The exact percentage of fluid relative to body weight is inversely proportional to the percentage of body fat. A lean 70 kg (150 lb) man, for example, has about 42 (42\u201347) liters of water in his body. The total body of water is divided into fluid compartments, between the intracellular fluid compartment (also called space, or volume) and the extracellular fluid (ECF) compartment (space, volume) in a two-to-one ratio: 28 (28\u201332) liters are inside cells and 14 (14\u201315) liters are outside cells. The ECF compartment is divided into the interstitial fluid volume \u2013 the fluid outside both the cells and the blood vessels \u2013 and the intravascular volume (also called the vascular volume and blood plasma volume) \u2013 the fluid inside the blood vessels \u2013 in a three-to-one ratio: the interstitial fluid volume is about 12 liters; the vascular volume is about 4 liters."}}, {"canonical_name": "observations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 16, "members": [{"entity_id": "observations", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "observations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 6, "variants": ["observations"], "contexts": ["scientific observations", "act of observing phenomena"]}, {"entity_id": "observations", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "observations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["observations"], "contexts": ["act of watching and noting phenomena", "act of watching carefully"]}, {"entity_id": "observations", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "observations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 5, "variants": ["observations", "d'observation.", "observation"], "contexts": ["scientific data collection", "recorded findings from study"]}, {"entity_id": "observations", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "observations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["observations"], "contexts": ["act of watching and recording", "act of watching carefully"]}, {"entity_id": "observations", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "observations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["observations"], "contexts": ["recordings of scientific data"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "observations", "target_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "target_name": "observations", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "observations", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "observations", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "observations", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "observations", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "observations", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "observations", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "observations", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "observations", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "observations", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "observations", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "observations", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "observations", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "observations", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "observations", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "observations", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "observations", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "observations", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "observations", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 217, "stable_key": "clu_8e1f2e83b1916a93-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Observations", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "Systematic recording of data or phenomena in scientific study.", "note": "Refers to the practice of scientific observation and data collection.", "wikipedia_extract": "Observation in the natural sciences refers to the active acquisition of information from a primary source. It involves the act of noticing or perceiving phenomena and gathering data based on direct engagement with the subject of study. In living organisms, observation typically occurs through the senses. In science, it often extends beyond unaided perception, involving the use of scientific instruments to detect, measure, and record data. This enables the observation of phenomena not accessible to human senses alone. Observations in science are typically categorized as either qualitative or quantitative: Qualitative observations describe characteristics that are not expressed numerically, such as color, texture, or behavior."}}, {"canonical_name": "bird", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 16, "members": [{"entity_id": "oiseau", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "oiseau", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["oiseau"], "contexts": ["animal poisoned by toad venom"]}, {"entity_id": "bird", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "bird", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 8, "variants": ["bird"], "contexts": ["animal with feathers", "animal that lays eggs"]}, {"entity_id": "bird", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "bird", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 4, "variants": ["bird"], "contexts": ["animal with more complex nervous system", "animal with fewer lower brain centers"]}, {"entity_id": "bird", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "bird", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["bird"], "contexts": ["each species has peculiar haunt"]}, {"entity_id": "bird", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "bird", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 2, "variants": ["bird"], "contexts": ["feathered, winged vertebrate", "flying creature"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "oiseau", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "bird", "similarity": 0.98}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "oiseau", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "bird", "similarity": 0.98}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "oiseau", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "bird", "similarity": 0.98}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "oiseau", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "bird", "similarity": 0.98}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "bird", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "bird", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "bird", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "bird", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "bird", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "bird", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "bird", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "bird", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "bird", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "bird", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "bird", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "bird", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 218, "stable_key": "clu_0256a7c1d393eff8-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Birds", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bird", "note": "General term for avian animals.", "wikipedia_extract": "Birds are a group of warm-blooded theropod dinosaurs constituting the class Aves, characterised by feathers, toothless beaked jaws, the laying of hard-shelled eggs, a high metabolic rate, a four-chambered heart, and a strong yet lightweight skeleton. Birds live worldwide and range in size from the 5.5 cm (2.2 in) bee hummingbird to the 2.8 m (9 ft 2 in) common ostrich. There are over 11,000 living species and they are split into 44 orders. More than half are passerine or \"perching\" birds. Birds have wings whose development varies according to species; the only known groups without wings are the extinct moa and elephant birds. Wings, which are modified forelimbs, gave birds the ability to fly, although further evolution has led to the loss of flight in some birds, including ratites, penguins, and diverse endemic island species."}}, {"canonical_name": "life", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 16, "members": [{"entity_id": "life", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "life", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["life"], "contexts": ["abstract concept related to existence"]}, {"entity_id": "life", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "life", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["life"], "contexts": ["attribute of celestial bodies", "state of being alive"]}, {"entity_id": "life", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "life", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["life"], "contexts": ["state of being alive"]}, {"entity_id": "life", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "life", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["life"], "contexts": ["state of existence", "existence"]}, {"entity_id": "life", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "life", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["life"], "contexts": ["existence and living beings", "animating force of the universe"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "life", "target_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "target_name": "life", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "life", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "life", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "life", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "life", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "life", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "life", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "life", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "life", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "life", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "life", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "life", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "life", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "life", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "life", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "life", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "life", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "life", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "life", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 219, "stable_key": "clu_251ca0c2be1ffe42", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Life", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}}, {"canonical_name": "Speech", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 16, "members": [{"entity_id": "speech", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "speech", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["speech"], "contexts": ["means of communication", "vocal communication"]}, {"entity_id": "speech", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Speech", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Speech"], "contexts": ["function aided by Marjerom"]}, {"entity_id": "speech", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Speech", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["speech", "Speech"], "contexts": ["faculty of verbal communication", "vocal communication"]}, {"entity_id": "speech", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "speech", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["speech"], "contexts": ["expression of ideas", "verbal communication"]}, {"entity_id": "speech", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "speech", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["speech"], "contexts": ["verbal communication", "of the peasant"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "speech", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Speech", "similarity": 0.986}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "speech", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Speech", "similarity": 0.986}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "speech", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "speech", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "speech", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "speech", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Speech", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Speech", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Speech", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "speech", "similarity": 0.986}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Speech", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "speech", "similarity": 0.986}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Speech", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "speech", "similarity": 0.986}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Speech", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "speech", "similarity": 0.986}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "speech", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "speech", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 220, "stable_key": "clu_a68948bee48980a0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Speech", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q18342738", "wikidata_description": "process and act of speaking or giving a lecture to a group of people in a structured, deliberate manner intended to inform, influence, or entertain a listening audience", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Public%20speaking", "wikipedia_extract": "Public speaking, or oratory, is the delivering of a speech to a live audience. Throughout history, public speaking has held significant cultural, religious, and political importance, emphasizing the necessity of effective rhetorical skills. It allows individuals to connect with a group of people to discuss any topic. The goal as a public speaker may be to educate, teach, or influence an audience. Public speakers often utilize visual aids like a slideshow, pictures, and short videos to get their point across... The ancient Chinese philosopher Confucius, a key figure in the study of public speaking, advocated for speeches that could profoundly affect individuals, including those not present in the audience."}}, {"canonical_name": "Russia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 16, "members": [{"entity_id": "russia", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Russia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Russia"], "contexts": ["country where Ridley was physician"]}, {"entity_id": "russia", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Russia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 5, "variants": ["Russia"], "contexts": ["vast country spanning Eastern Europe and Asia", "territory with Silurian deposits"]}, {"entity_id": "russia", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Russia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Russia"], "contexts": ["part of Europe with western declination", "country receiving aerial currents"]}, {"entity_id": "r\u00fassia", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "R\u00fassia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 3, "variants": ["R\u00fassia"], "contexts": ["European country", "where galanga minor is used as condiment"]}, {"entity_id": "russia", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Russia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Russia"], "contexts": ["empire with cold climate", "country where Kasan is located"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Russia", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Russia", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Russia", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Russia", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Russia", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "R\u00fassia", "similarity": 0.985}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Russia", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Russia", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Russia", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Russia", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Russia", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "R\u00fassia", "similarity": 0.985}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Russia", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Russia", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Russia", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "R\u00fassia", "similarity": 0.985}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Russia", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Russia", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "R\u00fassia", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Russia", "similarity": 0.985}], "id": 221, "stable_key": "clu_7c20bc649b89581b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Russia", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Vast country spanning Eastern Europe and Asia.", "wikipedia_extract": "Russia, or the Russian Federation, is a country in Eastern Europe and North Asia. It is the largest country in the world, spanning eleven time zones and sharing land borders with fourteen countries. With a population of over 140 million, Russia is the most populous country in Europe and the ninth-most populous in the world. It is a highly urbanised country, with sixteen of its urban areas having more than 1 million inhabitants. Moscow, the most populous metropolitan area in Europe, is the capital and largest city of Russia, while Saint Petersburg is its second-largest city and a major cultural centre. Human settlement on the territory of modern Russia dates back to the Lower Paleolithic."}}, {"canonical_name": "Oscillations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 16, "members": [{"entity_id": "oscillations", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "oscillations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["oscillations"], "contexts": ["wave-like movements"]}, {"entity_id": "oscillations", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "oscillations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["oscillations", "oscillation", "oscillations diurnes"], "contexts": ["measurement of magnetic forces", "measure of magnetic force"]}, {"entity_id": "oscillations", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "oscillations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["oscillations"], "contexts": ["back-and-forth movements", "fluctuations in accuracy and error"]}, {"entity_id": "oscillations", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Oscillations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["Oscillations", "oscillations"], "contexts": ["three kinds in the ocean", "wave-like movements in fluid"]}, {"entity_id": "oscillations", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "oscillations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["oscillations"], "contexts": ["regular fluctuations", "vertical and lateral, caused by ocean-swell"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "oscillations", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "oscillations", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "oscillations", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "oscillations", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "oscillations", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Oscillations", "similarity": 0.991}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "oscillations", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "oscillations", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "oscillations", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "oscillations", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "oscillations", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Oscillations", "similarity": 0.991}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "oscillations", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "oscillations", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "oscillations", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Oscillations", "similarity": 0.991}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "oscillations", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "oscillations", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Oscillations", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "oscillations", "similarity": 0.991}], "id": 222, "stable_key": "clu_a7bd35781961ad09", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Oscillation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q170475", "wikidata_description": "repetitive variation of some measure about a central value", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oscillation", "wikipedia_extract": "Oscillation is the repetitive or periodic variation, typically in time, of some measure about a central value (often a point of equilibrium) or between two or more different states. Familiar examples of oscillation include a swinging pendulum and alternating current. Oscillations can be used in physics to approximate complex interactions, such as those between atoms. Oscillations occur not only in mechanical systems but also in dynamic systems in virtually every area of science: for example the beating of the human heart (for circulation),  business cycles in economics, predator\u2013prey population cycles in ecology, geothermal geysers in geology, vibration of strings in guitar and other string instruments, periodic firing of nerve cells in the brain, and the periodic swelling of Cepheid variable stars in astronomy. The term vibration is precisely used to describe a mechanical oscillation. Oscillation, especially rapid oscillation, may be an undesirable phenomenon in process control and control theory (e.g."}}, {"canonical_name": "Indian Ocean", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 16, "members": [{"entity_id": "oc\u00e9an_indien", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "oc\u00e9an Indien", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["oc\u00e9an Indien"], "contexts": ["ocean with significant geological changes"]}, {"entity_id": "indian_ocean", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Indian Ocean", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Indian Ocean"], "contexts": ["ocean where fish range from Pacific", "ocean"]}, {"entity_id": "indian_ocean", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Indian Ocean", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 6, "variants": ["Indian Ocean"], "contexts": ["ocean navigated by Chinese vessels", "ocean"]}, {"entity_id": "oceano_indico", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "oceano Indico", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["oceano Indico"], "contexts": ["ocean for trade"]}, {"entity_id": "indian_ocean", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Indian Ocean", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 6, "variants": ["Indian Ocean"], "contexts": ["ocean entered by tidal wave", "ocean with periodic winds called monsoons"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "oc\u00e9an Indien", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Indian Ocean", "similarity": 0.96}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "oc\u00e9an Indien", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Indian Ocean", "similarity": 0.96}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "oc\u00e9an Indien", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "oceano Indico", "similarity": 0.961}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "oc\u00e9an Indien", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Indian Ocean", "similarity": 0.96}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Indian Ocean", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Indian Ocean", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Indian Ocean", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "oceano Indico", "similarity": 0.95}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Indian Ocean", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Indian Ocean", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Indian Ocean", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "oceano Indico", "similarity": 0.95}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Indian Ocean", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Indian Ocean", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "oceano Indico", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Indian Ocean", "similarity": 0.95}], "id": 223, "stable_key": "clu_36fc0c4d019078e2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Indian Ocean", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "wikidata_id": "Q370597", "wikidata_description": "species of mammal", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indo-Pacific%20bottlenose%20dolphin", "wikipedia_extract": "The Indo-Pacific bottlenose dolphin (Tursiops aduncus) is a species of bottlenose dolphin. This dolphin grows to 2.6 m (8.5 ft) long, and weighs up to 230 kg (510 lb). It lives in the waters around India, northern Australia, South China, the Red Sea, and the eastern coast of Africa. Its back is dark grey and its belly is lighter grey or nearly white with grey spots. The Indo-Pacific bottlenose dolphin is generally smaller than the common bottlenose dolphin, has a proportionately longer rostrum, and has spots on its belly and lower sides. It also has more teeth than the common bottlenose dolphin \u2014 23 to 29 teeth on each side of each jaw compared to 21 to 24 for the common bottlenose dolphin."}}, {"canonical_name": "ciudad", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 16, "members": [{"entity_id": "city", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "City", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["City"], "contexts": ["location of familiar dish", "location where Alex\u2020ander Porredg is familiar"]}, {"entity_id": "ciudad", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "Ciudad", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ciudad"], "contexts": ["location mentioned in the text"]}, {"entity_id": "ciudad", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Ciudad", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ciudad"], "contexts": ["designation for Trinidad"]}, {"entity_id": "ciudad", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "ciudad", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 10, "variants": ["ciudad", "Ciudad", "Ciudades"], "contexts": ["geographic location, city", "city"]}, {"entity_id": "city", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "city", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["city"], "contexts": ["central business district", "location where units cluster together"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "City", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "Ciudad", "similarity": 0.912}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "City", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Ciudad", "similarity": 0.912}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "City", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "ciudad", "similarity": 0.958}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "City", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "city", "similarity": 0.994}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Ciudad", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Ciudad", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Ciudad", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "ciudad", "similarity": 0.929}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Ciudad", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "city", "similarity": 0.903}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Ciudad", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "ciudad", "similarity": 0.929}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Ciudad", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "city", "similarity": 0.903}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "ciudad", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "city", "similarity": 0.962}], "id": 224, "stable_key": "clu_2708c5314bc0a588", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "City", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "capital and most populous city in France", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris", "wikipedia_extract": "Paris is the capital and largest city of France, with an estimated city population of 2,047,602 in an area of 105.4 km2 (40.7 sq mi), and a metropolitan population of 13,239,090 as of January 2026. Located on the river Seine in the centre of the \u00cele-de-France region, it is the largest metropolitan area and fourth-most populous city in the European Union (EU). Nicknamed the City of Light, partly because of its role in the Age of Enlightenment, Paris has been one of the world's major centres of finance, diplomacy, commerce, culture, fashion, and gastronomy since the 17th century. Administratively, Paris is divided into twenty arrondissements (districts), each having their own cultural identity. Haussmann's renovation of Paris, which created new boulevards, parks, and public works, gave birth to a modern city known as the \"capital of the 19th century\". Paris is a major railway, motorway, and air-transport hub; in 2024 Charles de Gaulle Airport was the EU's busiest airport.", "note": "This is a generic term. The specific city would depend on the context of the text."}}, {"canonical_name": "quality", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 16, "members": [{"entity_id": "quality", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "quality", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["quality"], "contexts": ["sharp & evil quality of humors", "malignant or pestilential quality"]}, {"entity_id": "quality", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "quality", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["quality"], "contexts": ["inherent characteristic"]}, {"entity_id": "quality", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "quality", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 11, "variants": ["quality"], "contexts": ["attribute or characteristic of something", "characteristic or attribute of thought"]}, {"entity_id": "quality", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "quality", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["quality"], "contexts": ["relation of specific properties and nature"]}, {"entity_id": "quality", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "quality", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["quality"], "contexts": ["characteristic of substances"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "quality", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "quality", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "quality", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "quality", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "quality", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "quality", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "quality", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "quality", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "quality", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "quality", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "quality", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "quality", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "quality", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "quality", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "quality", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "quality", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "quality", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "quality", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "quality", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "quality", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 225, "stable_key": "clu_0a53b908759d2edd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Quality", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q185957", "wikidata_description": "non-inferiority or superiority of something; a perceptual, conditional, and somewhat subjective attribute that may be understood differently by different people", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quality%20%28business%29", "wikipedia_extract": "In business, engineering, and manufacturing, quality \u2013 or high quality \u2013 has a pragmatic interpretation as the non-inferiority or superiority of something (goods or services); it is also defined as being suitable for the intended purpose (fitness for purpose) while satisfying customer expectations. Quality is a perceptual, conditional, and somewhat subjective attribute and may be understood differently by different people. Consumers may focus on the specification quality of a product/service, or how it compares to competitors in the marketplace. Producers might measure the conformance quality, or degree to which the product/service was produced correctly. Support personnel may measure quality in the degree that a product is reliable, maintainable, or sustainable. In such ways, the subjectivity of quality is rendered objective via operational definitions and measured with metrics such as proxy measures."}}, {"canonical_name": "flower", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 16, "members": [{"entity_id": "flower", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "flower", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 7, "variants": ["flower"], "contexts": ["plant part with petals and stamens", "reproductive part of a plant"]}, {"entity_id": "flower", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "flower", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["flower"], "contexts": ["object for observation in kindergarten"]}, {"entity_id": "flower", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "flower", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["flower"], "contexts": ["part of a plant", "reproductive part of a plant"]}, {"entity_id": "flor", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "flor", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 5, "variants": ["flor", "Flor de Borrajas", "Flores delangre", "flores"], "contexts": ["flower of the plant", "small white flower"]}, {"entity_id": "flower", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "flower", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["flower"], "contexts": ["part of a plant affected by weight"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "flower", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "flower", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "flower", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "flower", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "flower", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "flor", "similarity": 0.958}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "flower", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "flower", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "flower", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "flower", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "flower", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "flor", "similarity": 0.958}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "flower", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "flower", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "flower", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "flor", "similarity": 0.958}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "flower", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "flower", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "flor", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "flower", "similarity": 0.958}], "id": 226, "stable_key": "clu_cda2f6e3c507c90a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Flower", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "note": "Refers to the reproductive part of a plant in general, not a specific species.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "psychoactive herb from the Cannabis plant used for medical or recreational purposes", "wikipedia_url": "", "wikipedia_extract": ""}}, {"canonical_name": "Elephant", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 15, "members": [{"entity_id": "elephant", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Elephant", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["Elephant"], "contexts": ["animal with debated joint structure", "animal with unusual leg joints"]}, {"entity_id": "elephant", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "elephant", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["elephant"], "contexts": ["colossal animal compared to tapir"]}, {"entity_id": "elephant", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Elephant", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 7, "variants": ["elephant", "Elephant"], "contexts": ["slowest breeding animal", "large herbivorous mammal"]}, {"entity_id": "elephant", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "elephant", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["elephant"], "contexts": ["extant animal found as fossil"]}, {"entity_id": "elephant", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "elephant", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["elephant"], "contexts": ["large mammal from torrid zone", "animal inhabiting the torrid zone"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Elephant", "target_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "target_name": "elephant", "similarity": 0.992}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Elephant", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Elephant", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Elephant", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "elephant", "similarity": 0.992}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Elephant", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "elephant", "similarity": 0.992}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "elephant", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Elephant", "similarity": 0.992}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "elephant", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "elephant", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "elephant", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "elephant", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Elephant", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "elephant", "similarity": 0.992}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Elephant", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "elephant", "similarity": 0.992}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "elephant", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "elephant", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 227, "stable_key": "clu_3676de87c334ffdc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Elephants", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "description": "Large, herbivorous mammals known for their size and strength.", "wikipedia_extract": "Elephants are the largest living land animals. Three living species are currently recognised: the African bush elephant (Loxodonta africana), the African forest elephant (L. cyclotis), and the Asian elephant (Elephas maximus). They are the only surviving members of the family Elephantidae and the order Proboscidea; extinct relatives include mammoths and mastodons. Distinctive features of elephants include a long proboscis called a trunk, tusks, large ear flaps, pillar-like legs, and tough but sensitive grey skin. The trunk is prehensile, bringing food and water to the mouth and grasping objects. Tusks, which are derived from the incisor teeth, serve both as weapons and as tools for moving objects and digging."}}, {"canonical_name": "papel", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 15, "members": [{"entity_id": "paper", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Paper", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Paper"], "contexts": ["material used for draughts"]}, {"entity_id": "papier", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "papier", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["papier"], "contexts": ["paper imported and exported", "paper"]}, {"entity_id": "paper", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "paper", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["paper"], "contexts": ["material for writing or wrapping", "material for folding"]}, {"entity_id": "papel", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "papel", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 4, "variants": ["papel"], "contexts": ["used in remedies", "paper as a white object"]}, {"entity_id": "paper", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "paper", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 4, "variants": ["paper"], "contexts": ["chemically prepared for impressions", "bad conductor of heat used in experiments"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Paper", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "papier", "similarity": 0.982}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Paper", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "paper", "similarity": 0.992}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Paper", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "papel", "similarity": 0.978}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Paper", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "paper", "similarity": 0.992}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "papier", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "paper", "similarity": 0.971}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_