TANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Phosphorus", "phosphorus"], "contexts": ["chemical element linked to thought", "chemical element associated with thought"]}, {"entity_id": "phosphorus", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "phosphorus", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["phosphorus"], "contexts": ["chemical element with specific properties", "chemical compound causing luminosity"]}, {"entity_id": "phosphorus", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "phosphorus", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["phosphorus"], "contexts": ["chemical element in the brain"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Phosphorus", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "phosphore", "similarity": 0.944}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Phosphorus", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "phosphorus", "similarity": 0.951}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Phosphorus", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "phosphorus", "similarity": 0.951}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Phosphorus", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "phosphorus", "similarity": 0.951}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "phosphore", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "phosphorus", "similarity": 0.995}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "phosphore", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "phosphorus", "similarity": 0.995}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "phosphore", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "phosphorus", "similarity": 0.995}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "phosphorus", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "phosphorus", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "phosphorus", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "phosphorus", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "phosphorus", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "phosphorus", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 277, "stable_key": "clu_e7be48e0aa8f0bf9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Phosphorus", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "note": "Phosphorus was known as a chemical element with luminescent properties, and its association with thought relates to early ideas about its role in the nervous system.", "wikidata_id": "Q674", "wikidata_description": "chemical element with symbol P and atomic number 15", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phosphorus", "wikipedia_extract": "Phosphorus is a chemical element; it has symbol P and atomic number 15. All elemental forms of phosphorus are highly reactive and are therefore never found in nature. Elemental phosphorus can be prepared artificially, the two most common allotropes being white phosphorus and red phosphorus. With 31P as its only stable isotope, phosphorus has an occurrence in Earth's crust of about 0.1%, generally as phosphate rock. A member of the pnictogen family, phosphorus readily forms a wide variety of organic and inorganic compounds, with as its main oxidation states +5, +3 and \u22123. The isolation of white phosphorus in 1669 by Hennig Brand marked the scientific community's first discovery of an element since antiquity."}}, {"canonical_name": "humoral theory", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "humoral_theory", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "humoral theory", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["humoral theory"], "contexts": ["theory of bodily fluids"]}, {"entity_id": "humoral_theory", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "humoral theory", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["humoral theory"], "contexts": ["implied by physical and moral states"]}, {"entity_id": "humoral_theory", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "humoral theory", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Humoral theory", "humoral theory"], "contexts": ["theory of bodily fluids", "implied by qualities like cold/moist"]}, {"entity_id": "humoral_theory", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "humoral theory", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["humoral theory"], "contexts": ["theory of bodily fluids"]}, {"entity_id": "humoral_theory", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "humoral theory", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 3, "variants": ["humoral theory"], "contexts": ["implied medical theory", "theory of bodily fluids"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "humoral theory", "target_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "target_name": "humoral theory", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "humoral theory", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "humoral theory", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "humoral theory", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "humoral theory", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "humoral theory", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "humoral theory", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "humoral theory", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "humoral theory", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "humoral theory", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "humoral theory", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "humoral theory", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "humoral theory", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "humoral theory", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "humoral theory", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "humoral theory", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "humoral theory", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "humoral theory", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "humoral theory", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 278, "stable_key": "clu_8c3269375b02b91c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "humoral theory", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "A medical theory explaining health based on bodily fluids (humors).", "wikipedia_extract": "Humorism, the humoral theory, or humoralism, was a system of medicine detailing a supposed makeup and workings of the human body, adopted by Ancient Greek and Roman physicians and philosophers. Humorism began to fall out of favor in the 17th century and it was definitively disproved with the discovery of microbes. The concept of \"humors\" may have origins in Ancient Egyptian medicine, or Mesopotamia, though it was not systemized until ancient Greek thinkers. The word humor is a translation of Greek \u03c7\u03c5\u03bc\u03cc\u03c2, chymos (literally 'juice' or 'sap', metaphorically 'flavor'). Early texts on Indian Ayurveda medicine presented a theory of three or four humors (do\u1e63as), which they sometimes linked with the five elements (pa\u00f1ca-bh\u016bta): earth, water, fire, air, and space. The concept of \"humors\" (chemical systems regulating human behaviour) became more prominent from the writing of medical theorist Alcmaeon of Croton (c. 540\u2013500 BC)."}}, {"canonical_name": "age", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "age", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "age", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["age"], "contexts": ["duration of existence"]}, {"entity_id": "age", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "age", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["age", "Age"], "contexts": ["period of life", "factor influencing medicine dosage"]}, {"entity_id": "age", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "age", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["age"], "contexts": ["period of existence"]}, {"entity_id": "age", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "age", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["age"], "contexts": ["duration of existence", "passage of time in life"]}, {"entity_id": "age", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "age", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["age"], "contexts": ["synonym for period of time", "duration of existence"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "age", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "age", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "age", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "age", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "age", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "age", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "age", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "age", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "age", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "age", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "age", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "age", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "age", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "age", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "age", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "age", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "age", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "age", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "age", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "age", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 279, "stable_key": "clu_f5d8bbb91066446f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Age", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q4691836", "wikidata_description": "genus of insects", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Age%20%28moth%29", "wikipedia_extract": "Age is a genus of moths belonging to the subfamily Olethreutinae of the family Tortricidae."}}, {"canonical_name": "Pallas", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "pallas", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "Pallas", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Pallas"], "contexts": ["savant naturaliste"]}, {"entity_id": "pallas", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Pallas", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Pallas"], "contexts": ["mentioned koulan description", "naturalist who originated a doctrine"]}, {"entity_id": "pallas", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Pallas", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Pallas"], "contexts": ["encountered celestial spirits among Mongols", "described mud volcano"]}, {"entity_id": "palla", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Palla", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Palla"], "contexts": ["author who mentioned Bezoar stone"]}, {"entity_id": "platina", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Platina", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Platina"], "contexts": ["author of a text", "scholar"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Pallas", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Pallas", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Pallas", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Pallas", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Pallas", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Palla", "similarity": 0.972}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Pallas", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Platina", "similarity": 0.877}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Pallas", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Pallas", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Pallas", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Palla", "similarity": 0.972}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Pallas", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Platina", "similarity": 0.877}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Pallas", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Palla", "similarity": 0.972}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Pallas", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Platina", "similarity": 0.877}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Palla", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Platina", "similarity": 0.874}], "id": 280, "stable_key": "clu_7d1b713cb92220f2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Peter Simon Pallas", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1741, "death_year": 1811, "description": "German zoologist, botanist, and explorer.", "note": "The references to \"Pallas\" in Darwin's and Humboldt's works, especially concerning descriptions and encounters, align with Pallas's extensive travels and scientific contributions. 'Platina' is likely a misreading or variant spelling of Pallas.", "wikidata_id": "Q155914", "wikidata_description": "German zoologist and botanist (1741\u20131811)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter%20Simon%20Pallas", "wikipedia_extract": "Peter Simon Pallas FRS FRSE (22 September 1741 \u2013 8 September 1811) was a Prussian zoologist, botanist, ethnographer, explorer, geographer, geologist, natural historian, and taxonomist. He studied natural sciences at various universities in early modern Germany and worked primarily in the Russian Empire between 1767 and 1810. Peter Simon Pallas was born in Berlin, Kingdom of Prussia, the son of Professor of Surgery Simon Pallas. He studied with private tutors and took an interest in natural history, later attending the University of Halle and the University of G\u00f6ttingen. In 1760, he moved to the University of Leiden and passed his doctor's degree at the age of 19. Pallas travelled throughout the Dutch Republic and to London, improving his medical and surgical knowledge."}}, {"canonical_name": "Violets", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "violets", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Violets", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Violets", "Violet"], "contexts": ["flower with medicinal uses", "herb with similar operations to pansies"]}, {"entity_id": "flor_de_violetas", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "Flor de violetas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Flor de violetas"], "contexts": ["violet flowers used in syrup"]}, {"entity_id": "violet", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "violet", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["violet"], "contexts": ["primary color", "fragrant flower"]}, {"entity_id": "violetas", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Violetas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Violetas"], "contexts": ["flower ingredient in a decoction"]}, {"entity_id": "violet", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "violet", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["violet"], "contexts": ["flower used in sugar preparations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Violets", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "Flor de violetas", "similarity": 0.887}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Violets", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "violet", "similarity": 0.888}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Violets", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Violetas", "similarity": 0.987}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Violets", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "violet", "similarity": 0.888}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Flor de violetas", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "violet", "similarity": 0.912}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Flor de violetas", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Violetas", "similarity": 0.897}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Flor de violetas", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "violet", "similarity": 0.912}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "violet", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Violetas", "similarity": 0.883}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "violet", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "violet", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Violetas", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "violet", "similarity": 0.883}], "id": 281, "stable_key": "clu_768eb5353cf4f575", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Violet", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Viola L.", "family": "Violaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q146095", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants; violets", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viola%20%28plant%29", "note": "The genus 'Viola' encompasses various species commonly known as violets. The context 'primary color' refers to the color violet, derived from the flower.", "wikipedia_extract": "Viola, commonly known as the violets, is a genus of flowering plants in the family Violaceae. It is the largest genus in the family, containing over 680 species. Most species are found in the temperate Northern Hemisphere; however, some are also found in widely divergent areas such as Hawaii, Australasia, and the Andes. Some Viola species are perennial plants, some are annual plants, and a few are small shrubs. Many species, varieties and cultivars are grown in gardens for their ornamental flowers. In horticulture, the term pansy is normally used for those multi-colored large-flowered cultivars which are raised annually or biennially from seed and used extensively in bedding."}}, {"canonical_name": "archipelago", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "archipelago", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "archipelago", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["archipelago"], "contexts": ["group of islands where species live", "group of islands"]}, {"entity_id": "archipel", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Archipel", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Archipel"], "contexts": ["Archipelago"]}, {"entity_id": "archipelago", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Archipelago", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Archipelago"], "contexts": ["surrounding Jamaica", "region, center of earthquake circle"]}, {"entity_id": "archipelago", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "archipelago", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["archipelago"], "contexts": ["group of islands", "geographic region"]}, {"entity_id": "archipelago", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "archipelago", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["archipelago"], "contexts": ["group of islands"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "archipelago", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Archipel", "similarity": 0.937}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "archipelago", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Archipelago", "similarity": 0.983}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "archipelago", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "archipelago", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "archipelago", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "archipelago", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Archipel", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Archipelago", "similarity": 0.961}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Archipel", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "archipelago", "similarity": 0.937}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Archipel", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "archipelago", "similarity": 0.937}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Archipelago", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "archipelago", "similarity": 0.983}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Archipelago", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "archipelago", "similarity": 0.983}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "archipelago", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "archipelago", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 282, "stable_key": "clu_9c5245d83f6f2df7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Archipelago", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "wikidata_id": "Q33837", "wikidata_description": "collection of islands including the water around them; use island group (Q1402592) for a collection of islands excluding neighbouring water", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Archipelago", "wikipedia_extract": "An archipelago (  AR-k\u0259-PEL-\u0259-goh), sometimes called an island group or island chain, is a chain, cluster, or collection of islands. An archipelago may be in an ocean, a sea, or a smaller body of water. Examples of archipelagos include the Aegean Islands (the origin of the term), the Canadian Arctic Archipelago, the Stockholm Archipelago, the Malay Archipelago (which includes the Indonesian and Philippine Archipelagos), the Lucayan (Bahamian) Archipelago, the Japanese archipelago, and the Hawaiian Archipelago. The word archipelago is derived from the Italian arcipelago, used as a proper name for the Aegean Sea, itself perhaps a deformation of the Greek \u0391\u03b9\u03b3\u03b1\u03af\u03bf\u03bd \u03a0\u03ad\u03bb\u03b1\u03b3\u03bf\u03c2. Later, usage shifted to refer to the Aegean Islands (since the sea has a large number of islands). The erudite paretymology, deriving the word from Ancient Greek \u1f04\u03c1\u03c7\u03b9- (arkhi-, \"chief\") and \u03c0\u03ad\u03bb\u03b1\u03b3\u03bf\u03c2 (p\u00e9lagos, \"sea\"), proposed by Buondelmonti, can still be found."}}, {"canonical_name": "indien", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "indien", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "indien", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 4, "variants": ["indien", "Indien"], "contexts": ["indigenous person", "person from India"]}, {"entity_id": "indian", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Indian", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Indian"], "contexts": ["foreign personage in mediumship"]}, {"entity_id": "indiani", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Indiani", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Indiani"], "contexts": ["people who call Zeilan by a certain name"]}, {"entity_id": "indianos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Indianos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, 