ta_1563", "source_name": "hot, cold, moist, dry", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "hot, cold, moist, dry", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "hot, cold, moist, dry", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "hot, cold, moist, dry", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "hot, cold, moist, dry", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "hot, cold, moist, dry", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 309, "stable_key": "clu_4d881c8ef941bce7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Humoral qualities", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "These are the four fundamental qualities (hot, cold, moist, dry) central to ancient Greek and early modern European medical theories, particularly humoralism, describing the nature of elements, substances, and the body."}}, {"canonical_name": "armes", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "armes", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "armes", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["armes"], "contexts": ["weapons"]}, {"entity_id": "guns", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Guns", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Guns"], "contexts": ["weaponry"]}, {"entity_id": "weapons", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Weapons", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Weapons"], "contexts": ["source of invenomed wounds"]}, {"entity_id": "armas", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Armas", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Armas"], "contexts": ["weapons, defensive and offensive"]}, {"entity_id": "armas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "armas", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["armas"], "contexts": ["weapons on the ship"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "armes", "target_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "target_name": "Guns", "similarity": 0.937}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "armes", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Weapons", "similarity": 0.979}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "armes", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Armas", "similarity": 0.987}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "armes", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "armas", "similarity": 0.982}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Guns", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Weapons", "similarity": 0.932}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Guns", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Armas", "similarity": 0.965}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Guns", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "armas", "similarity": 0.943}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Weapons", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Armas", "similarity": 0.979}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Weapons", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "armas", "similarity": 0.96}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Armas", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "armas", "similarity": 0.984}], "id": 310, "stable_key": "clu_9ef97294cd67246d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "firearms", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "While 'guns' and 'weapons' are broader, the context of 'invenomed wounds' and 'bellicos' points towards firearms as a specific and relevant early modern referent for causing injury, even if edged weapons also fit. Firearms became increasingly prominent during this period.", "wikidata_id": "Q12796", "wikidata_description": "personal weapon using combustion or an explosive charge to propel a projectile", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Firearm", "wikipedia_extract": "A firearm is any type of gun that shoots projectiles using high explosive pressure generated from combustion (deflagration) of chemical propellant, most often black powder in antique firearms and smokeless powder in modern firearms. Small arms is a subset of light firearms that is designed to be readily carried and operated by an individual. The term \"firearm\" is however variably defined in both technically and legally in different countries (see legal definitions), and can be used colloquially (sometimes incorrectly) to refer to any type of guns. The first firearms originated in 10th-century Song dynasty China (see gunpowder weapons in the Song dynasty), when bamboo tubes containing gunpowder and pellet projectiles were mounted on spears to make the portable fire lance, which was operable by a single person and was later used effectively as a shock weapon in the siege of De'an in 1132. In the 13th century, fire lance barrels were replaced with metal tubes and transformed into the metal-barreled hand cannon, and the technology gradually spread throughout Eurasia during the 14th century. Older firearms typically used black powder as a propellant, but modern firearms use smokeless powder or other explosive propellants."}}, {"canonical_name": "Human Nature", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "human_nature", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Human Nature", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Human Nature"], "contexts": ["source of common error and infirmity"]}, {"entity_id": "nature_humaine", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "nature humaine", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["nature humaine"], "contexts": ["the human condition and its afflictions"]}, {"entity_id": "human_nature", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "human nature", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["human nature"], "contexts": ["abstract idea of man"]}, {"entity_id": "human_nature", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "human nature", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["human nature"], "contexts": ["essential qualities of humanity"]}, {"entity_id": "human_nature", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Human Nature", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Human Nature"], "contexts": ["fundamental characteristics of humans"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Human Nature", "target_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "target_name": "nature humaine", "similarity": 0.934}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Human Nature", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "human nature", "similarity": 0.949}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Human Nature", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "human nature", "similarity": 0.949}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Human Nature", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Human Nature", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "nature humaine", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "human nature", "similarity": 0.982}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "nature humaine", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "human nature", "similarity": 0.982}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "nature humaine", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Human Nature", "similarity": 0.934}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "human nature", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "human nature", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "human nature", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Human Nature", "similarity": 0.949}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "human nature", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Human Nature", "similarity": 0.949}], "id": 311, "stable_key": "clu_1c01ff3f4eee7101", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Human nature", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q5937288", "wikidata_description": "scientific journal", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_nature", "wikipedia_extract": "Human nature comprises the fundamental dispositions and characteristics\u2014including ways of thinking, feeling, and acting\u2014that humans are said to have naturally. The term is often used to denote the essence of humankind; however, this usage has proven to be controversial in that there is dispute as to whether or not such an essence actually exists. Arguments about human nature have been a central focus of philosophy for centuries and the concept continues to provoke lively philosophical debate. While both concepts are distinct from one another, discussions regarding human nature are typically related to those regarding the comparative importance of genes and environment in human development (i.e., nature versus nurture). Accordingly, the concept also continues to play a role in academic fields, such as both the natural and the social sciences, and philosophy, in which various theorists claim to have yielded insight into human nature. Human nature is traditionally contrasted with human attributes that vary among societies, such as those associated with specific cultures."}}, {"canonical_name": "Famine", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "famine", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Famine", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Famine"], "contexts": ["condition signified by maggots"]}, {"entity_id": "famine", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "famine", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["famine"], "contexts": ["widespread scarcity of food"]}, {"entity_id": "fame", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "fame", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["fame"], "contexts": ["hunger"]}, {"entity_id": "fame", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "fame", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["fame"], "contexts": ["hunger"]}, {"entity_id": "famine", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "famine", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["famine"], "contexts": ["widespread scarcity of food"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Famine", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "famine", "similarity": 0.998}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Famine", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "fame", "similarity": 0.942}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Famine", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "fame", "similarity": 0.942}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Famine", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "famine", "similarity": 0.998}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "famine", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "fame", "similarity": 0.945}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "famine", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "fame", "similarity": 0.945}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "famine", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "famine", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "fame", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "fame", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "fame", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "famine", "similarity": 0.945}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "fame", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "famine", "similarity": 0.945}], "id": 312, "stable_key": "clu_9afde59f126cb23e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hunger", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Starvation", "note": "While 'starvation' is the extreme form, 'hunger' is the direct translation and the concept itself.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_extract": "Muselmann (German plural Muselm\u00e4nner) was a term used amongst prisoners of German Nazi concentration camps during the Holocaust of World War II to refer to those suffering from a combination of starvation (known also as \"hunger disease\") and exhaustion, as well as those who were resigned to their impending death. The Muselmann prisoners exhibited severe emaciation and physical weakness, an apathetic listlessness regarding their own fate, and unresponsiveness to their surroundings owing to their barbaric treatment. Some scholars argue that the term possibly comes from the Muselmanns' inability to stand for any time due to the loss of leg muscle, thus leading them to spend much of their time in a prone position. Muselmann also literally means \"a Muslim\" in Yiddish and a number of other languages (albeit with spelling differences), and ultimately derives from the Old Turkish word for Muslim, \u0645\u0633\u0644\u0645\u0627\u0646 (m\u00fcsliman). \"Muselmann\" seemingly derives from the German: Muselman, a historical term for \"Muslim\" (literally 'mussulman') which is now considered derogatory. If this derivation is correct, \"Muselmann\" would literally mean \"Muslim man\" (Muselman + Mann); but how this term later came to be used to denote starving concentration camp prisoners is uncertain.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hunger"}}, {"canonical_name": "Regions", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "regions", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Regions", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Regions"], "contexts": ["geographic areas"]}, {"entity_id": "regiones", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "regiones", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["regiones"], "contexts": ["areas with varying climates and qualities"]}, {"entity_id": "region", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "region", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["region"], "contexts": ["a region"]}, {"entity_id": "regi\u00e3o", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "regi\u00e3o", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["regi\u00e3o"], "contexts": ["geographic region"]}, {"entity_id": "region", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "region", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["region"], "contexts": ["area of land"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Regions", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "regiones", "similarity": 0.962}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Regions", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "region", "similarity": 0.889}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Regions", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "region", "similarity": 0.889}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "regiones", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "region", "similarity": 0.9}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "regiones", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "regi\u00e3o", "similarity": 0.891}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "regiones", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "region", "similarity": 0.9}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "region", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "regi\u00e3o", "similarity": 0.977}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "region", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "region", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "regi\u00e3o", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "region", "similarity": 0.977}], "id": 313, "stable_key": "clu_66769b86e6285b78", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Regions", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}}, {"canonical_name": "origin", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "origin", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "origin", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["origin"], "contexts": ["beginning or source"]}, {"entity_id": "origin", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "origin", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["origin"], "contexts": ["point or cause of beginning"]}, {"entity_id": "origin", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "origin", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["origin"], "contexts": ["beginning or source of something"]}, {"entity_id": "origin", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "origin", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["origin"], "contexts": ["starting point for measurement"]}, {"entity_id": "origin", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "origin", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["origin"], "contexts": ["hypothetical connector in language analysis"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "origin", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "origin", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "origin", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "origin", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "origin", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "origin", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "origin", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "origin", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "origin", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "origin", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "origin", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "origin", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "origin", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "origin", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "origin", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "origin", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "origin", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "origin", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "origin", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "origin", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 314, "stable_key": "clu_578b2ca999b781a7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "origin", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q7376362", "wikidata_description": "start of a river or stream", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/River%20source", "wikipedia_extract": "The headwater of a river or stream is the geographical point of its beginning, specifically where surface runoff water begins to accumulate into a flowing channel of water. A river or stream into which one or many tributary rivers or streams flow has many headwaters, these being all of the individual headwaters of its tributaries. Each headwater is one of the river or stream's sources, as it is the place where surface runoffs from rainwater, meltwater, or spring water begin accumulating into a more substantial and consistent flow that becomes a first-order tributary of that river or stream. The tributary with the longest channel of all the tributaries to a river or stream, such length measured from that tributary's headwater to its mouth where it discharges into the river or stream, is the main stem of the river or stream in question. The United States Geological Survey (USGS) states that a river's \"length may be considered to be the distance from the mouth to the most distant headwater source (irrespective of stream name), or from the mouth to the headwaters of the stream commonly known as the source stream\". As an example of the second definition above, the USGS at times considers the Missouri River as a tributary of the Mississippi River."}}, {"canonical_name": "dryness", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "dryness", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "dryness", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["dryness"], "contexts": ["environmental quality affecting plant growth"]}, {"entity_id": "dryness", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "dryness", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["dryness"], "contexts": ["extreme quality in the dry direction"]}, {"entity_id": "dryness", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "dryness", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["dryness"], "contexts": ["observed in Northern Asia"]}, {"entity_id": "aridity", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "aridity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["aridity"], "contexts": ["quality of dryness"]}, {"entity_id": "dryness", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "dryness", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["dryness"], "contexts": ["quality of lacking moisture"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "dryness", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "dryness", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "dryness", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "dryness", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "dryness", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "aridity", "similarity": 0.899}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "dryness", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "dryness", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "dryness", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "dryness", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "dryness", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "aridity", "similarity": 0.899}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "dryness", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "dryness", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "dryness", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "aridity", "similarity": 0.899}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "dryness", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "dryness", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "aridity", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "dryness", "similarity": 0.899}], "id": 315, "stable_key": "clu_393c78794e6a7ea5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "dryness", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "In humoral theory, 'dryness' was considered a fundamental quality of the four elements and humors, impacting physiology and the environment.", "wikidata_id": "Q5309747", "wikidata_description": "medical condition in which there is a decrease in normal lubrication of the skin or mucous membranes", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dryness%20%28medical%29", "wikipedia_extract": "Dryness is a medical condition in which there is local or more generalized decrease in normal lubrication of the skin or mucous membranes. Examples of local dryness include dry mouth, dry eyes, dry skin (xeroderma), diabetic foot and vaginal dryness. These often have specific causes and treatments. It is possible to have dry eyes without any other signs or symptoms, but this usually causes a syndrome of eye symptoms called keratoconjunctivitis sicca. More generalized dryness can be caused by e.g. dehydration (that is, more general loss of body fluids), anticholinergic drugs and Sj\u00f6gren syndrome."}}, {"canonical_name": "agua", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 431, "members": [{"entity_id": "agua", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "agua", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 24, "variants": ["agua"], "contexts": ["liquid for drinking", "liquid for preparation"]}, {"entity_id": "agua", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "agua", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 73, "variants": ["agua", "AQUA", "Agua", "agua Herrada", "agua azefada", "agua cetr\u00edna", "agua de angeles", "agua de cadabagas", "agua de cevadas", "agua de chicoria", "agua de endivia", "agua de fuente", "agua de las dichas", "agua fuerte", "agua hierrada", "agua rofada", "aguas", "aguate"], "contexts": ["water, used in treatment", "water from the root, medicinal"]}, {"entity_id": "\u00e1gua", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "\u00e1gua", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["\u00e1gua"], "contexts": ["water"]}, {"entity_id": "agua", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "agua", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 333, "variants": ["agua", "Aagua", "Agua", "Agua fria", "Aguas mineraes", "agua aodoente", "agua bem quente", "agua clara", "agua corrente", "agua da chuva", "agua da fonte", "agua da fonte fanta", "agua dafonte", "agua de cifterna", "agua de cistena", "agua de cisterna", "agua de po\u00e7o", "agua debel", "agua decevada", "agua do Borratem", "agua do po\u00e7o", "agua do po\u00e7odo Borratem", "agua doce", "agua falobra", "agua ferrada", "agua fervendo", "agua fervente", "agua fria", "agua friada fonte", "agua morna", "agua muito fria", "agua muyto fria", "agua muyto quente", "agua natural", "agua ordinaria", "agua quente", "aguabem quente", "aguada fonte", "aguadafonte", "aguadafonte\u017fecoza", "aguade Afpar", "aguade alguma fonteboa", "aguade cifterna", "aguade cifterna fria", "aguade farelos", "aguadous", "aguafria", "aguas mineraes", "feyxo de agua corrente", "vapor da agua fervente", "\u00e1gua"], "contexts": ["Water invented by Fern\u00e3o Mendes", "water for urinary suppressions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "agua", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "agua", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "agua", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "\u00e1gua", "similarity": 0.995}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "agua", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "agua", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "agua", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "\u00e1gua", "similarity": 0.995}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "agua", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "agua", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "\u00e1gua", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "agua", "similarity": 0.995}], "id": 316, "stable_key": "clu_c530ca2f7a54bb50-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "water", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "A transparent liquid essential for life, used medicinally and as a medium.", "note": "Includes Portuguese ('agua'), French ('eau'), and generic terms ('fluid').", "wikipedia_extract": "Water is an inorganic compound with the chemical formula H2O. It is a transparent, tasteless, odorless, and nearly colorless chemical substance. It is the main constituent of Earth's streams, lakes, and oceans and the fluids of all known living organisms, in which it acts as a solvent. Water, being a polar molecule, undergoes strong intermolecular hydrogen bonding which is a large contributor to its physical and chemical properties. It is vital for all known forms of life, despite not providing food energy or being an organic micronutrient. Due to its presence in all organisms, its chemical stability, its worldwide abundance, and its strong polarity relative to its small molecular size, water is often referred to as the \"universal solvent\"."}}, {"canonical_name": "Hydrope\u017fia", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 274, "members": [{"entity_id": "hidropes\u00eda", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "hidropes\u00eda", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 1, "variants": ["hidropes\u00eda"], "contexts": ["condition of fluid accumulation"]}, {"entity_id": "hydrop\u00ed", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "hydrop\u00ed", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 1, "variants": ["hydrop\u00ed"], "contexts": ["dropsy caused by internal obstructions"]}, {"entity_id": "hydroforbia", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "hydroforbia", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 1, "variants": ["hydroforbia"], "contexts": ["disease name"]}, {"entity_id": "hydrope\u017fia", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Hydrope\u017fia", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 271, "variants": ["Hydrope\u017fia", "HyDropefia", "Hydro", "Hydroceph.", "Hydrocephalo", "Hydrocephalos", "Hydrocephalus", "Hydrop.", "Hydrop.pector.", "Hydrope", "Hydrope Ascite", "Hydrope Pectoris", "Hydrope Tympanite", "Hydrope-", "Hydropefia", "Hydropefia Afcitica", "Hydropefia Anasarca", "Hydropefia anazarca", "Hydropefia do bofe", "Hydropefia do peyto", "Hydropefia dop\u00e9yto", "Hydropefia uterina", "Hydropefiado", "Hydropefiado peito", "Hydropefias", "Hydropele", "Hydropem", "Hydropepectoris", "Hydropepsias", "Hydropepulmon.", "Hydropeques", "Hydropesia", "Hydrope\u017fia Afcitis", "Hydrope\u017fia Anafarca", "Hydrope\u017fia A\u017fcitica", "Hydrope\u017fia Timpanitis", "Hydrope\u017fia do Peyto", "Hydrope\u017fia he uterina", "Hydrope\u017fiada cabe\u00e7a", "Hydrope\u017fias", "Hydrope\u017fias Tympaniticas", "Hydrope\u017fias humoraes", "Hydrope\u017f\u017fa", "Hydrophobia", "Hydropi", "Hydropica", "Hydropico", "Hydropicobeber", "Hydropicos", "Hydropis Afcita", "Hydropoft", "Hydrops", "Hydropspectoris", "Hydroptico", "hydrocephalos", "hydrope", "hydropefia", "hydropefia capital", "hydropefiada cabe\u00e7a", "hydropefias", "hydropem", "hydrope\u017fia", "hydrope\u017fia do peito", "hydrope\u017fias", "hydrope\u017fias do peyto", "hydropica", "hydropico", "hydropicos", "hydrops", "hyrops"], "contexts": ["illness treated by Quintilio's powders", "dropsy, caused by watery humor"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "hidropes\u00eda", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "hydrop\u00ed", "similarity": 0.938}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "hidropes\u00eda", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "hydroforbia", "similarity": 0.886}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "hidropes\u00eda", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Hydrope\u017fia", "similarity": 0.939}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "hydrop\u00ed", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Hydrope\u017fia", "similarity": 0.918}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "hydroforbia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Hydrope\u017fia", "similarity": 0.895}], "id": 317, "stable_key": "clu_e730e9b9196c6805", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Dropsy", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "A condition characterized by abnormal accumulation of fluid in body tissues.", "note": "Includes variations like Hydrope\u017fia, Hydropick diseases, Hydropicos, hydrop\u00ed, hydroforbia, and hidropici.", "wikipedia_extract": "Edema (American English), also spelled oedema (Commonwealth English), and also known as fluid retention, swelling, dropsy and hydropsy, is the build-up of fluid in the body's tissue. Most commonly, the legs or arms are affected. Symptoms may include skin that feels tight, the area feeling heavy, and joint stiffness. Other symptoms depend on the underlying cause. Causes may include venous insufficiency, heart failure, kidney problems, low protein levels, liver problems, deep vein thrombosis, infections, kwashiorkor, angioedema, certain medications, and lymphedema. It may also occur in immobile patients (stroke, spinal cord injury, aging), or with temporary immobility such as prolonged sitting or standing, and during menstruation or pregnancy."}}, {"canonical_name": "Medicos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 269, "members": [{"entity_id": "m\u00e9decins", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "m\u00e9decins", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 12, "variants": ["m\u00e9decins"], "contexts": ["practitioners of medicine", "medical practitioners"]}, {"entity_id": "m\u00e9dicos", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "m\u00e9dicos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 4, "variants": ["m\u00e9dicos"], "contexts": ["Indian physicians using plant for diagnosis", "medical practitioners who named cachexia"]}, {"entity_id": "m\u00e9decins", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "m\u00e9decins", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 2, "variants": ["m\u00e9decins", "m\u00e9decins praticiens"], "contexts": ["physicians in Jamaica", "medical practitioners in Havana"]}, {"entity_id": "medicos", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "medicos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 1, "variants": ["medicos"], "contexts": ["medical practitioners in Havana"]}, {"entity_id": "medicos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Medicos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 250, "variants": ["Medicos", "Medi", "Medi.co", "Medic", "Medic.", "Medici", "Medicis", "Medico", "Medicode", "Medicol", "Medicomuyto", "Medicorum", "Medicos Catholicos", "Medicos letrados", "Medicos modernos", "Medicos principiantes", "Medicosgran", "Medicum", "Medicus", "hum Medico", "medico", "medicos", "medicum"], "contexts": ["physicians", "medical practitioners"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "m\u00e9decins", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "m\u00e9dicos", "similarity": 0.979}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "m\u00e9decins", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "m\u00e9decins", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "m\u00e9decins", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "medicos", "similarity": 0.988}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "m\u00e9decins", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Medicos", "similarity": 0.957}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "m\u00e9dicos", "target_book": "relati