", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 174, "members": [{"entity_id": "galeno", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "Galeno", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Galeno", "galeno"], "contexts": ["ancient medical authority"]}, {"entity_id": "galeno", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Galeno", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 33, "variants": ["Galeno", "Gale", "Galen", "Galenon"], "contexts": ["ancient medical authority", "ancient author on poisons"]}, {"entity_id": "galeno", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Galeno", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 77, "variants": ["Galeno", "Gale.", "Galen", "Galenas", "Galenus"], "contexts": ["Ancient Greek physician", "ancient medical authority"]}, {"entity_id": "galeno", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Galeno", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 62, "variants": ["Galeno", "Galen", "Galero", "galena"], "contexts": ["Ancient medical authority", "ancient authority on medicine"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Galeno", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Galeno", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Galeno", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Galeno", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Galeno", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Galeno", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Galeno", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Galeno", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Galeno", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Galeno", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Galeno", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Galeno", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 324, "stable_key": "clu_57c0f703d572d61a-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Galen", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Ancient Greek physician and highly influential medical authority.", "note": "Refers to the ancient physician, not the planet or musician.", "wikipedia_extract": "Aelius Galenus or Claudius Galenus (Greek: \u039a\u03bb\u03b1\u03cd\u03b4\u03b9\u03bf\u03c2 \u0393\u03b1\u03bb\u03b7\u03bd\u03cc\u03c2; September 129 \u2013 c.\u2009216 CE), often anglicized as Galen () or Galen of Pergamon, was a Roman and Greek physician, surgeon, and philosopher. Considered to be one of the most accomplished of all medical researchers of antiquity, Galen influenced the development of various scientific disciplines, including anatomy, physiology, pathology, pharmacology, and neurology, as well as philosophy and logic. The son of Aelius Nicon, a wealthy Greek architect with scholarly interests, Galen received a comprehensive education that prepared him for a successful career as a physician and philosopher. Born in the ancient city of Pergamon (present-day Bergama, Turkey), Galen traveled extensively, exposing himself to a wide variety of medical theories and discoveries before settling in Rome, where he served prominent members of Roman society and eventually was given the position of personal physician to several emperors. Galen's understanding of anatomy and medicine was principally influenced by the contemporary theory of the four humors: black bile, yellow bile, blood, and phlegm, as first advanced by the author of On the Nature of Man in the Hippocratic corpus. Galen's views dominated and influenced Western medical science for more than 1,300 years."}}, {"canonical_name": "attention", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 171, "members": [{"entity_id": "attention", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "attention", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 166, "variants": ["Attention", "attention", "ATTENTION"], "contexts": ["focusing mental resources", "focus of cognitive resources"]}, {"entity_id": "effort_of_attention", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "effort of attention", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["effort of attention"], "contexts": ["mental exertion of focus"]}, {"entity_id": "attention", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "attention", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["attention"], "contexts": ["focusing of mental resources", "focusing of mental awareness"]}, {"entity_id": "attention", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "attention", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["attention"], "contexts": ["focusing of the mind"]}, {"entity_id": "attention", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "attention", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["attention"], "contexts": ["mental faculty directed to facts"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "attention", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "attention", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "attention", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "attention", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "attention", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "attention", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "effort of attention", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "attention", "similarity": 0.882}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "effort of attention", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "attention", "similarity": 0.882}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "effort of attention", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "attention", "similarity": 0.882}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "attention", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "attention", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "attention", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "attention", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "attention", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "attention", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 325, "stable_key": "clu_dcb552a2355cd384", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Attention", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q6501338", "wikidata_description": "behavioral and cognitive process of selectively concentrating on a discrete aspect of information, whether deemed subjective or objective, while ignoring other perceivable information", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attention", "wikipedia_extract": "Attention is the concentration of awareness directed at some phenomenon while mostly excluding others. Across disciplines, the nature of this directedness is conceptualized in different ways. In cognitive psychology, attention is often described as the allocation of limited cognitive processing resources to a subset of information, thoughts, or tasks. In neuropsychology, attention is understood as a set of mechanisms by which sensory cues and internal goals modulate neuronal tuning and orient behavioral and cognitive processes. Attention is not a unitary phenomenon but an umbrella term for multiple related processes, including selective attention (prioritizing some stimuli over others), sustained attention (maintaining focus), divided attention (sharing resources across tasks), and orienting (shifting focus in space or time). These processes are supported by distributed neural networks in frontal, parietal, and subcortical regions and are closely linked to working memory, executive functions, and consciousness."}}, {"canonical_name": "electricity", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 165, "members": [{"entity_id": "electricity", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "electricity", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["electricity"], "contexts": ["form of energy", "phenomenon of electric charge"]}, {"entity_id": "electricity", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "electricity", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 19, "variants": ["Electricity", "electricity"], "contexts": ["atmospheric phenomenon", "fundamental force of nature"]}, {"entity_id": "electricity", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "electricity", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 128, "variants": ["Electricity", "electricity"], "contexts": ["fundamental force and phenomenon", "means of instantaneous time conveyance"]}, {"entity_id": "electricity", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Electricity", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 16, "variants": ["Electricity", "electricity"], "contexts": ["study of electric charge and fields", "fundamental force in nature"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "electricity", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "electricity", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "electricity", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "electricity", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "electricity", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Electricity", "similarity": 0.991}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "electricity", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "electricity", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "electricity", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Electricity", "similarity": 0.991}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "electricity", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Electricity", "similarity": 0.991}], "id": 326, "stable_key": "clu_d45a70567e33ad73", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "electricity", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the scientific understanding of electrical phenomena and the hypothetical 'electric fluid'.", "wikidata_id": "Q383973", "wikidata_description": "process of generating electrical power", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electricity%20generation", "wikipedia_extract": "Electricity generation is the process of generating electric power from sources of primary energy. For utilities in the electric power industry, it is the stage prior to its delivery (transmission, distribution, etc.) to end users or its storage, using for example, the pumped-storage method. Consumable electricity is not freely available in nature, so it must be \"produced\", transforming other forms of energy to electricity. Production is carried out in power stations, also called \"power plants\". Electricity is most often generated at a power plant by electromechanical generators, primarily driven by heat engines fueled by combustion or nuclear fission, but also by other means such as the kinetic energy of flowing water and wind. Other energy sources include solar photovoltaics and geothermal power."}}, {"canonical_name": "earth", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 159, "members": [{"entity_id": "earth", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "earth", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["earth"], "contexts": ["surface where beings exist"]}, {"entity_id": "earth", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "earth", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["earth"], "contexts": ["planet on which we live", "celestial body"]}, {"entity_id": "earth", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "earth", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 96, "variants": ["earth", "Earth"], "contexts": ["our planet, a subject of scientific study", "our planet"]}, {"entity_id": "earth", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Earth", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 58, "variants": ["Earth"], "contexts": ["celestial body, the planet we inhabit", "celestial body, subject of conception"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "earth", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "earth", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "earth", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "earth", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "earth", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Earth", "similarity": 0.983}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "earth", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "earth", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "earth", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Earth", "similarity": 0.983}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "earth", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Earth", "similarity": 0.983}], "id": 327, "stable_key": "clu_f522307c9c5c037e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Earth", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Global", "wikidata_id": "Q36133", "wikidata_description": "natural body consisting of layers that are primarily composed of minerals", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soil", "wikipedia_extract": "Soil, also commonly referred to as earth, is a mixture of organic matter, minerals, gases, water, and organisms that together support the life of plants and soil organisms. Some scientific definitions distinguish dirt from soil by restricting the former term specifically to displaced soil. Soil consists of a solid collection of minerals and organic matter (the soil matrix), as well as a porous phase that holds gases (the soil atmosphere) and a liquid phase that holds water and dissolved substances both organic and inorganic, in ionic or in molecular form (the soil solution). Accordingly, soil is a complex three-state system of solids, liquids, and gases. Soil is a product of several factors: the influence of climate, relief (elevation, orientation, and slope of terrain), organisms, and the soil's parent materials (original minerals) interacting over time. It continually undergoes development by way of numerous physical, chemical and biological processes, which include weathering with associated erosion.", "note": "The term 'terra' or 'terre' in early modern texts can refer to the planet Earth, or to mineral earths used in medicine or as impurities."}}, {"canonical_name": "Riverius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 151, "members": [{"entity_id": "rivero", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Rivero", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 28, "variants": ["Rivero", "M. Rivero", "Rivera"], "contexts": ["distinguished man establishing a school of mines", "traveler who leveled mountains"]}, {"entity_id": "de_la_rive", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "De la Rive", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["De la Rive"], "contexts": ["scientist, abstracted Poisson's work", "author of 'Essai Historique sur l'Electricite'"]}, {"entity_id": "riverius", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Riverius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 119, "variants": ["Riverius", "Rive", "River.", "Riverins", "Riverio", "Rivetio", "Rivetius"], "contexts": ["Author of 'febrifugio'", "early modern author"]}, {"entity_id": "de_la_rive", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "De la Rive", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["De la Rive"], "contexts": ["observed temperature increase with depth"]}, {"entity_id": "rive", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Rive", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rive"], "contexts": ["observed temperature increase in wells"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Rivero", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Riverius", "similarity": 0.864}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Rivero", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Rive", "similarity": 0.879}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "De la Rive", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "De la Rive", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "De la Rive", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Rive", "similarity": 0.9}], "id": 328, "stable_key": "clu_19c756f17dc318a9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Laurent Rivi\u00e8re", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1705, "death_year": 1781, "description": "French chemist and physician, noted for work on minerals and metallurgy", "note": "The primary referent seems to be the French chemist Laurent Rivi\u00e8re, known for his work in metallurgy and mineralogy, which aligns with the mention of establishing a school of mines. The mention of 'febrifugio' could refer to a work by a different Riverius, but the context of mines and scientific abstraction points strongly to Laurent Rivi\u00e8re."}}, {"canonical_name": "moist", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 146, "members": [{"entity_id": "moist", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "moist", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 15, "variants": ["moist", "Moist", "moistens"], "contexts": ["quality associated with Angelica's virtues", "quality of humidity"]}, {"entity_id": "moist", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "moist", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["moist"], "contexts": ["implied by discussion of 'grados de frialdad'"]}, {"entity_id": "moist", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "moist", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 17, "variants": ["moist"], "contexts": ["a quality of substances", "quality of substances"]}, {"entity_id": "moist", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "moist", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 113, "variants": ["Moist", "moist"], "contexts": ["a fundamental quality in early modern medicine", "medical quality"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "moist", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "moist", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "moist", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "moist", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "moist", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "moist", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "moist", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "moist", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "moist", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "moist", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "moist", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "moist", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 329, "stable_key": "clu_8ccec5d4fa693ca5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "moist", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "A quality of dampness or humidity, often discussed in medical contexts.", "note": "This refers to the quality of being moist or humid, rather than a specific substance.", "wikipedia_extract": "Humorism, the humoral theory, or humoralism, was a system of medicine detailing a supposed makeup and workings of the human body, adopted by Ancient Greek and Roman physicians and philosophers. Humorism began to fall out of favor in the 17th century and it was definitively disproved with the discovery of microbes. The concept of \"humors\" may have origins in Ancient Egyptian medicine, or Mesopotamia, though it was not systemized until ancient Greek thinkers. The word humor is a translation of Greek \u03c7\u03c5\u03bc\u03cc\u03c2, chymos (literally 'juice' or 'sap', metaphorically 'flavor'). Early texts on Indian Ayurveda medicine presented a theory of three or four humors (do\u1e63as), which they sometimes linked with the five elements (pa\u00f1ca-bh\u016bta): earth, water, fire, air, and space. The concept of \"humors\" (chemical systems regulating human behaviour) became more prominent from the writing of medical theorist Alcmaeon of Croton (c. 540\u2013500 BC)."}}, {"canonical_name": "Avicenna", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 141, "members": [{"entity_id": "avicen", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Avicen", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Avicen"], "contexts": ["physician, diverged from Galen", "Persian polymath, physician, and philosopher"]}, {"entity_id": "avicenna", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Avicenna", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Avicenna", "Avicen"], "contexts": ["labored in this study", "medieval medical authority"]}, {"entity_id": "avicenna", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Avicenna", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 8, "variants": ["Avicenna", "Avicena", "avicenna"], "contexts": ["Arabian scholar who wrote about Cardamomo", "mentioned in relation to zucchero candi"]}, {"entity_id": "avicenna", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Avicenna", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 128, "variants": ["Avicenna", "Avicen", "Avicen.", "Avicena", "Avicennas", "Avicenne", "Avicenn\u00e6"], "contexts": ["ancient medical authority on medicine canons", "medieval medical authority"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Avicen", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Avicenna", "similarity": 0.912}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Avicen", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Avicenna", "similarity": 0.912}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Avicen", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Avicenna", "similarity": 0.912}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Avicenna", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Avicenna", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Avicenna", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Avicenna", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Avicenna", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Avicenna", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 330, "stable_key": "clu_d77196501d6c33a4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Avicenna", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Influential Persian polymath and physician of the Islamic Golden Age.", "note": "Also known by variations like Avicena and Auicena.", "wikipedia_extract": "Ibn Sina (c.\u2009980 \u2013 22 June 1037), commonly known in the West as Avicenna ( A(H)V-ih-SEN-\u0259), was a preeminent philosopher and physician of the Muslim world. He was a seminal figure of the Islamic Golden Age, serving in the courts of various Iranian rulers, and was influential to medieval European medical and Scholastic thought. Often described as the father of early modern medicine, Avicenna's most famous works are The Book of Healing, a philosophical and scientific encyclopedia, and The Canon of Medicine, a medical encyclopedia that became a standard medical text at many medieval European universities and remained in use as late as 1650. Besides philosophy and medicine, Avicenna's corpus includes writings on astronomy, alchemy, geography and geology, psychology, Islamic theology, logic, mathematics, physics, and works of poetry. His philosophy was of the Peripatetic school derived from Aristotelianism, of which he is considered among the greatest proponents within the Muslim world. Avicenna wrote most of his philosophical and scientific works in Arabic but also wrote several key works in Persian; his poetry was written in both languages."}}, {"canonical_name": "Humboldt", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 133, "members": [{"entity_id": "humboldt", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Humboldt", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 80, "variants": ["Humboldt"], "contexts": ["author of 'Cosmos'", "cited for astronomical observations"]}, {"entity_id": "humboldt", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Humboldt", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Humboldt"], "contexts": ["authority on plant origins", "early modern scholar"]}, {"entity_id": "humboldt", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Humboldt", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 45, "variants": ["Humboldt", "A. de Humboldt", "Alexandre de Humboldt", "Guillaume de Humboldt", "HOMBOLDT", "Humboldl", "Humbuldt", "M. Guillaume de Humboldt", "M. de Humboldt", "M. de Humholdt", "Wilhelm von Humboldt", "Wilhelm, von Humboldt"], "contexts": ["author of scientific works", "astronomical observations"]}, {"entity_id": "humboldt", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Humboldt", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 5, "variants": ["Humboldt"], "contexts": ["naturalist and explorer", "observed mine temperatures, measured depth"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Humboldt", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Humboldt", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Humboldt", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Humboldt", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 331, "stable_key": "clu_baf9b5edab0f7714", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Alexander von Humboldt", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1769, "death_year": 1859, "description": "Prussian explorer and naturalist, key figure in biogeography", "wikidata_id": "Q6694", "wikidata_description": "Prussian geographer, naturalist and explorer (1769\u20131859)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander%20von%20Humboldt", "wikipedia_extract": "Friedrich Wilhelm Heinrich Alexander von Humboldt (14 September 1769 \u2013 6 May 1859) was a German polymath, geographer, naturalist, explorer, and proponent of Romantic philosophy and science. He was the younger brother of the Prussian minister, philosopher, and linguist Wilhelm von Humboldt (1767\u20131835). Humboldt's quantitative work on botanical geography laid the foundation for the field of biogeography, while his advocacy of long-term systematic geophysical measurement pioneered modern geomagnetic and meteorological monitoring. Humboldt and Carl Ritter are both regarded as the founders of modern geography as they established it as an independent scientific discipline. Between 1799 and 1804, Humboldt travelled extensively in the Americas, exploring and describing them for the first time from a non-Spanish European scientific point of view. On these travels, along with French explorer Aim\u00e9 Bonpland, he traversed thousands of miles through some of the most difficult and little-known places on Earth, to include identifying the source of the Orinoco River and in 1802 climbing the highest mountain in Ecuador to a height of 19,286 feet, at the time a world record altitude for a Westerner.", "note": "While 'Long' and 'Lange' are the exact OCR, context strongly suggests Humboldt's exploration of the Rocky Mountains and his scientific writings."}}, {"canonical_name": "bexiga", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 128, "members": [{"entity_id": "bexiga", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "bexiga", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 12, "variants": ["bexiga"], "contexts": ["organ for storing urine", "organ cleaned by the root's bark"]}, {"entity_id": "bexiga", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "bexiga", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 5, "variants": ["Bexiga", "bexiga"], "contexts": ["bladder, organ of the body", "bladder, where stones can cause burning"]}, {"entity_id": "bexiga", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "bexiga", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 3, "variants": ["bexiga"], "contexts": ["Bladder, affected by sores", "body part treated with aloes"]}, {"entity_id": "bexiga", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "bexiga", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 108, "variants": ["bexiga", "Bexiga", "Nabexiga", "abexiga", "abexiga do fel", "abexiga dofel", "bexiga do fei", "bexiga do fel", "be\u017fexiga", "nabexiga", "nabexigadofel"], "contexts": ["body part", "bladder"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "bexiga", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "bexiga", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "bexiga", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "bexiga", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "bexiga", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "bexiga", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "bexiga", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "bexiga", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "bexiga", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "bexiga", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "bexiga", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "bexiga", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 332, "stable_key": "clu_1123faaa49db51b8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bladder", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "An organ that stores urine.", "wikipedia_extract": "The bladder (from Old English  bl\u00e6dre 'bladder, blister, pimple') is a hollow organ in humans and other vertebrates that stores urine from the kidneys. In placental mammals, urine enters the bladder via the ureters and exits via the urethra during urination. In humans, the bladder is a distensible organ that sits on the pelvic floor. The typical adult human bladder will hold between 300 and 500 ml (10 and 17 fl oz) before the urge to empty occurs, but can hold considerably more. The Latin phrase for \"urinary bladder\" is vesica urinaria, and the term vesical or prefix vesico- appear in connection with associated structures such as vesical veins. The modern Latin word for \"bladder\" \u2013 cystis \u2013 appears in associated terms such as cystitis (inflammation of the bladder).", "wikidata_id": "Q9382", "wikidata_description": "internal organ of humans and vertebrates"}}, {"canonical_name": "Indios", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 122, "members": [{"entity_id": "indios", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "indios", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 7, "variants": ["indios"], "contexts": ["indigenous people", "indigenous people of the land"]}, {"entity_id": "indios", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Indios", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 3, "variants": ["Indios"], "contexts": ["people of all castes", "indigenous people"]}, {"entity_id": "indios", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Indios", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 76, "variants": ["Indios", "Indianos", "Indio", "Indios Caribes", "Ind\u00edos", "indios"], "contexts": ["indigenous people of the Americas", "indigenous people who make the oil"]}, {"entity_id": "\u00edndios", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "\u00edndios", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 36, "variants": ["\u00edndios", "Indianos", "indianos", "indios", "\u00cdndios", "\u00cdndios de Cuba"], "contexts": ["people of India", "people who value amber"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "indios", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Indios", "similarity": 0.981}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "indios", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Indios", "similarity": 0.981}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "indios", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "\u00edndios", "similarity": 0.976}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Indios", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Indios", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Indios", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "\u00edndios", "similarity": 0.956}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Indios", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "\u00edndios", "similarity": 0.956}], "id": 333, "stable_key": "clu_60f2f46691a9b6ea", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Indigenous peoples of the Americas", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Peoples native to the Americas, often described in early colonial texts.", "note": "The term 'Indios' was used by Europeans to refer to both the indigenous peoples of the Americas and the inhabitants of India.", "wikidata_id": "Q36747", "wikidata_description": "peoples indigenous to the Western Hemisphere", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indigenous%20peoples%20of%20the%20Americas", "wikipedia_extract": "The Indigenous peoples of the Americas are the peoples who are native to the Americas or the Western Hemisphere. Their ancestors are among the pre-Columbian population of South or North America, including Central America and the Caribbean. Indigenous peoples live throughout the Americas. While often minorities in their countries, Indigenous peoples are the majority in Greenland and close to a majority in Bolivia and Guatemala. There are at least 1,000 different Indigenous languages of the Americas. Some languages, including Quechua, Arawak, Aymara, Guaran\u00ed, Nahuatl, and some Mayan languages, have millions of speakers and are recognized as official by governments in Bolivia, Peru, Paraguay, and Greenland."}}, {"canonical_name": "Andes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 121, "members": [{"entity_id": "andes", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Andes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Andes"], "contexts": ["mountain range with moraine formation"]}, {"entity_id": "andes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Andes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 92, "variants": ["Andes"], "contexts": ["Andes of Peru", "mountain range in South America"]}, {"entity_id": "andes", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Andes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 26, "variants": ["Andes"], "contexts": ["mountain range containing Chimborazo", "mountain chain containing Chimborazo"]}, {"entity_id": "cordilleras_of_the_andes", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Cordilleras of the Andes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cordilleras of the Andes"], "contexts": ["mountain range with volcanoes"]}, {"entity_id": "cordilleras_of_the_andes", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Cordilleras of the Andes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cordilleras of the Andes"], "contexts": ["mountain range in South America"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Andes", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "And