"body", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 21, "variants": ["body"], "contexts": ["physical organism", "physical form to be shielded"]}, {"entity_id": "body", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "body", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 8, "variants": ["Body", "body"], "contexts": ["physical structure of an organism", "entire physical structure"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Body", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "body", "similarity": 0.997}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Body", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "body", "similarity": 0.997}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Body", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "body", "similarity": 0.997}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "body", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "body", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "body", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "body", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "body", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "body", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 352, "stable_key": "clu_15f8db1f57883a5a-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Body", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'Bodies' is used broadly in early modern texts to refer to physical entities, particularly human and animal bodies, in both scientific and philosophical contexts.", "wikidata_id": "Q5", "wikidata_description": "any single member of Homo sapiens, unique extant species of the genus Homo", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human", "wikipedia_extract": "Humans (Homo sapiens, meaning \"thinking man\" or \"wise man\") are the most common and widespread species of primate, and the only extant species of the genus Homo. They are great apes characterized by hairlessness, obligate bipedality, manual dexterity with opposable thumbs, precision grip, and high intelligence. Humans have large brains compared to body size (a high encephalization quotient), enabling more advanced cognitive skills that facilitate successful adaptation to varied environments, development of sophisticated tools, and formation of complex social structures and civilizations. Humans possess a disproportionately larger volume of both cerebral white matter and gray matter present in the prefrontal cortex (PFC) than any other primate species, which facilitated the expansion of higher-order executive functions. Humans are highly social, with individual humans tending to belong to a multi-layered network of distinct social groups \u2013 from families and peer groups to corporations and political states. As such, social interactions between humans have established a wide variety of values, social norms, languages, and traditions (collectively termed institutions), each of which bolsters human society.", "description": "The physical structure of a person or animal."}}, {"canonical_name": "Varieties", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 80, "members": [{"entity_id": "vari\u00e9t\u00e9s", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "vari\u00e9t\u00e9s", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["vari\u00e9t\u00e9s"], "contexts": ["subdivisions within a species"]}, {"entity_id": "vari\u00e9t\u00e9s", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "vari\u00e9t\u00e9s", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["vari\u00e9t\u00e9s"], "contexts": ["differences within a species"]}, {"entity_id": "varieties", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Varieties", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 66, "variants": ["varieties", "Varieties"], "contexts": ["subdivisions within a species", "subgroups within a species"]}, {"entity_id": "variety", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "variety", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 8, "variants": ["variety"], "contexts": ["subdivision within a species", "distinct group within a species"]}, {"entity_id": "varieties", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "varieties", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["varieties"], "contexts": ["different forms within a species", "distinct groups within a species"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "vari\u00e9t\u00e9s", "target_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "target_name": "vari\u00e9t\u00e9s", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "vari\u00e9t\u00e9s", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Varieties", "similarity": 0.937}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "vari\u00e9t\u00e9s", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "variety", "similarity": 0.918}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "vari\u00e9t\u00e9s", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "varieties", "similarity": 0.956}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "vari\u00e9t\u00e9s", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Varieties", "similarity": 0.937}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "vari\u00e9t\u00e9s", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "variety", "similarity": 0.918}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "vari\u00e9t\u00e9s", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "varieties", "similarity": 0.956}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Varieties", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "varieties", "similarity": 0.991}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "variety", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "varieties", "similarity": 0.891}], "id": 353, "stable_key": "clu_8ef8c323cd6fa300", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Varieties", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "In the context of Darwin, 'varieties' can refer to what are now called subspecies or distinct populations."}}, {"canonical_name": "Oc\u00e9an", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 79, "members": [{"entity_id": "oc\u00e9an", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "Oc\u00e9an", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 8, "variants": ["Oc\u00e9an", "oc\u00e9an"], "contexts": ["hypothetical ocean covering the globe", "large body of saltwater"]}, {"entity_id": "oc\u00e9an", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Oc\u00e9an", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 67, "variants": ["OC\u00c9AN", "oc\u00c9AN", "Oc\u00e9an"], "contexts": ["ocean causing erosions", "ocean name"]}, {"entity_id": "ocean", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "ocean", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["ocean"], "contexts": ["vast body of saltwater", "vast body of water for bird transportation"]}, {"entity_id": "ocean", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "ocean", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["ocean"], "contexts": ["large body of saltwater", "large bodies of saltwater on Earth"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Oc\u00e9an", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Oc\u00e9an", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "ocean", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "ocean", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 354, "stable_key": "clu_b1b20bdeba2b0163", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ocean", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "wikidata_id": "Q161419", "wikidata_description": "ancient Greek god of the earth-encircling river, Oceanos", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oceanus", "wikipedia_extract": "In Greek mythology, Oceanus or Okeanos was one of the Titans, the children of Uranus (Sky) and Gaia (Earth), the brother and husband of the Titaness Tethys, and the father of the river gods and the Oceanids, as well as being the great river which encircled the entire world. According to M. L. West, the etymology of Oceanus is \"obscure\" and \"cannot be explained from Greek\". The use by Pherecydes of Syros of the form \u014cgen\u00f3s (\u1f68\u03b3\u03b5\u03bd\u03cc\u03c2) for the name lends support for the name being a loanword. However, according to West, no \"very convincing\" foreign models have been found."}}, {"canonical_name": "Agarico", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 76, "members": [{"entity_id": "ag\u00e1rico", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "Ag\u00e1rico", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ag\u00e1rico"], "contexts": ["plant mentioned as dung"]}, {"entity_id": "agarico", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Agarico", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Agarico"], "contexts": ["plant mixed with the root", "agaric, a strong emetic"]}, {"entity_id": "agarico", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Agarico", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 21, "variants": ["Agarico", "Agarico buono", "Agarico trocifca", "agarico"], "contexts": ["purgative medicine", "herb used in trochisci for purging"]}, {"entity_id": "agarico", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Agarico", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 51, "variants": ["Agarico", "Agarici", "Agarico trocifcado", "Agarico trociscado", "agaric", "agarico", "agarico trocifcado", "agarico troci\u017fcado", "agaricotro", "agaricotrocifcado"], "contexts": ["purgative remedy for headaches", "agaric for pills"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Ag\u00e1rico", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Agarico", "similarity": 0.701}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Ag\u00e1rico", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Agarico", "similarity": 0.701}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Ag\u00e1rico", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Agarico", "similarity": 0.701}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Agarico", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Agarico", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Agarico", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Agarico", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Agarico", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Agarico", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 355, "stable_key": "clu_983e6990e2038789", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Agaric fungus", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "family": "Agaricaceae", "description": "A purgative fungus used in historical medicine.", "note": "Refers to the medicinal properties of various fungi, particularly from the genus Fomes.", "wikipedia_extract": "Laricifomes officinalis, also known as agarikon, eburiko, or the quinine conk, is a wood-decay fungus that grows in large conks on the trunks of trees. It causes brown heart rot on conifers in Eurasia, Morocco, and North America. This fungus is the only member of the genus Laricifomes. There has been a history of human use of the fungus, from textiles, to ritualistic masks, and medicinal use; the name \"quinine conk\" refers to its bitter taste. There is recent scientific evidence of it having potency against several viruses. This mushroom is also known as Fomitopsis officinalis."}}, {"canonical_name": "iron", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 75, "members": [{"entity_id": "iron", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "iron", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 14, "variants": ["iron"], "contexts": ["metal found in meteoric dust", "element found in dust"]}, {"entity_id": "iron", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "iron", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 35, "variants": ["iron", "Iron"], "contexts": ["common metal, often brittle or hard", "metal in protoxides"]}, {"entity_id": "iron", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Iron", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 23, "variants": ["Iron"], "contexts": ["common metal", "material exhibiting magnetism"]}, {"entity_id": "iron", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Iron", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["Iron", "iron"], "contexts": ["metal quenched in water for remedy", "mentioned as a substance"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "iron", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "iron", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Iron", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Iron", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 356, "stable_key": "clu_257cd6b5b944ec64-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "iron", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q677", "wikidata_description": "chemical element with symbol Fe and atomic number 26", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iron", "wikipedia_extract": "Iron is a chemical element; it has symbol Fe (from Latin  ferrum 'iron') and atomic number 26. It is a metal that belongs to the first transition series and group 8 of the periodic table. It is, by mass, the most common element on Earth, forming much of Earth's outer and inner core. It is the fourth most abundant element in the Earth's crust. In its metallic state it was mainly deposited by meteorites. Extracting usable metal from iron ores requires kilns or furnaces capable of reaching 1,500 \u00b0C (2,730 \u00b0F), about 500 \u00b0C (900 \u00b0F) higher than that required to smelt copper."}}, {"canonical_name": "barom\u00e8tre", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 74, "members": [{"entity_id": "barom\u00e8tre", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "barom\u00e8tre", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 2, "variants": ["barom\u00e8tre"], "contexts": ["instrument to measure atmospheric pressure", "instrument for measuring atmospheric pressure"]}, {"entity_id": "barom\u00e8tre", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "barom\u00e8tre", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 43, "variants": ["barom\u00e8tre", "BAROM\u00c8TRE", "barom\u00e8tres", "barom\u00e8tres portatifs", "barom\u00e9triques"], "contexts": ["instrument for measuring atmospheric pressure", "instrument used for measurement"]}, {"entity_id": "barometer", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "barometer", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 13, "variants": ["Barometer", "barometer"], "contexts": ["instrument measuring atmospheric pressure", "device for measuring atmospheric pressure"]}, {"entity_id": "barometrical", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "barometrical", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 1, "variants": ["barometrical"], "contexts": ["related to barometric pressure measurement"]}, {"entity_id": "barometer", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "barometer", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 15, "variants": ["barometer", "Barometer"], "contexts": ["measures atmospheric pressure", "instrument for measuring atmospheric pressure"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "barom\u00e8tre", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "barom\u00e8tre", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "barom\u00e8tre", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "barometer", "similarity": 0.913}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "barom\u00e8tre", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "barometrical", "similarity": 0.882}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "barom\u00e8tre", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "barometer", "similarity": 0.913}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "barom\u00e8tre", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "barometer", "similarity": 0.913}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "barom\u00e8tre", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "barometrical", "similarity": 0.882}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "barom\u00e8tre", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "barometer", "similarity": 0.913}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "barometer", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "barometer", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "barometrical", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "barometer", "similarity": 0.933}], "id": 357, "stable_key": "clu_6c2aa5405927bf2c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Barometer", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikipedia_extract": "An altimeter or an altitude meter is an instrument used to measure the altitude of an object above a fixed level. The measurement of altitude is called altimetry, which is related to the term bathymetry, the measurement of depth under water. In 1931, the US Army Air corps and General Electric together tested a sonic altimeter for aircraft, which was considered more reliable and accurate than one that relied on air pressure when heavy fog or rain was present. The new altimeter used a series of high-pitched sounds like those made by a bat to measure the distance from the aircraft to the surface, which on return to the aircraft was converted to feet shown on a gauge inside the aircraft cockpit. A radar altimeter measures altitude more directly, using the time taken for a radio signal to reflect from the surface back to the aircraft. Alternatively, Frequency Modulated Continuous-wave radar can be used.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Altimeter"}}, {"canonical_name": "time", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 74, "members": [{"entity_id": "time", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "time", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["time"], "contexts": ["measure of duration and change", "recurring at long intervals of time"]}, {"entity_id": "time", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "time", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 38, "variants": ["Time", "time"], "contexts": ["dimension of existence", "perceptual quality"]}, {"entity_id": "time", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Time", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 10, "variants": ["Time", "time"], "contexts": ["measurement of duration", "related to tidal wave propagation and pendulum"]}, {"entity_id": "time", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Time", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 22, "variants": ["TIME", "time", "Time"], "contexts": ["fundamental concept of physics", "continuum of existence"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "time", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "time", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "time", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Time", "similarity": 0.964}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "time", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Time", "similarity": 0.964}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "time", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Time", "similarity": 0.964}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "time", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Time", "similarity": 0.964}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Time", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Time", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 358, "stable_key": "clu_ad7e2fa36baeeb71", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Time", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "note": "The term 'hours' in the context of early modern texts invariably refers to units of time, fundamental to measurement and daily life."}}, {"canonical_name": "Lisboa", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 74, "members": [{"entity_id": "lisbonne", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Lisbonne", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 7, "variants": ["Lisbonne"], "contexts": ["city where Adrien Balbi resided", "city where maps were traced"]}, {"entity_id": "lisboa", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Lisboa", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Lisboa"], "contexts": ["city where academy is located", "location of 'Mem. de Lisboa'"]}, {"entity_id": "lisbon", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Lisbon", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 7, "variants": ["Lisbon"], "contexts": ["city destroyed by earthquake", "city affected by great earthquake"]}, {"entity_id": "lisbona", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Lisbona", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Lisbona", "Lisboa"], "contexts": ["city from which stone was brought", "city from which a stone was brought"]}, {"entity_id": "lisboa", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Lisboa", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 55, "variants": ["Lisboa", "LISBOA", "Lisboade", "Lisbonen\u017fe"], "contexts": ["city of the Archbishop", "Lisboa"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Lisbonne", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Lisbon", "similarity": 0.984}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Lisbonne", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Lisbona", "similarity": 0.988}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Lisbonne", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Lisboa", "similarity": 0.937}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Lisboa", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Lisbon", "similarity": 0.949}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Lisboa", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Lisbona", "similarity": 0.949}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Lisboa", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Lisboa", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Lisbon", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Lisbona", "similarity": 0.99}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Lisbon", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Lisboa", "similarity": 0.949}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Lisbona", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Lisboa", "similarity": 0.949}], "id": 359, "stable_key": "clu_5eb17dc61c6c70be", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lisbon", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Capital and largest city of Portugal.", "wikipedia_extract": "Lisbon, officially the Very Noble and Ever Loyal City of Lisbon, is the capital and most populous municipality of Portugal, with an estimated population of 575,739, as of 2024, within its administrative limits and 3,028,000 within the metropolis, as of 2025. Lisbon is mainland Europe's westernmost capital city (second overall after Reykjav\u00edk), and the only one along the Atlantic coast, the others (Reykjav\u00edk and Dublin) being on islands. The city lies in the western portion of the Iberian Peninsula, on the northern shore of the River Tagus. The western portion of its metro area, the Portuguese Riviera, hosts the westernmost point of Continental Europe, culminating at Cabo da Roca. Lisbon is one of the oldest cities in the world and the second-oldest European capital city (after Athens), predating other modern European capitals by centuries. Settled by pre-Celtic tribes and later founded and civilized by the Phoenicians, Julius Caesar made it a municipium called Felicitas Julia, adding the term to the name Olissipo."}}, {"canonical_name": "Cardamomo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 72, "members": [{"entity_id": "cardamomo_menor", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "cardamomo menor", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 5, "variants": ["cardamomo menor"], "contexts": ["cardamom menor", "minor type of cardamomo"]}, {"entity_id": "cardamomo", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Cardamomo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 28, "variants": ["Cardamomo", "Cardamo mag.", "Cardamo magg", "Cardamo mm.", "Cardamomo mag", "Cardamomo mag.", "Cardamomo min", "Cardamomoj", "Cardimomo"], "contexts": ["spice with debated origins and properties", "spice from seeds"]}, {"entity_id": "cardamomo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "cardamomo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 29, "variants": ["cardamomo", "CARDAMOMO", "Cardamomo", "Cardantamo", "cardamomo majytr", "cardamomo mayor", "cardamomo mayoy"], "contexts": ["a spice", "spice"]}, {"entity_id": "cardamomo_menor", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "cardamomo menor", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["cardamomo menor"], "contexts": ["plant for electuary"]}, {"entity_id": "cardamono", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "cardamono", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["cardamono"], "contexts": ["fruit used like cardamom"]}, {"entity_id": "cardamomo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "cardamomo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 8, "variants": ["cardamomo"], "contexts": ["spice used in compound medicine", "cardamom"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "cardamomo menor", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Cardamomo", "similarity": 0.88}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "cardamomo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cardamomo menor", "similarity": 0.888}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "cardamono", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cardamomo", "similarity": 0.916}], "id": 360, "stable_key": "clu_ab80595d4a7a83ba", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cardamom", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Elettaria cardamomum", "family": "Zingiberaceae", "note": "The term 'cardamomo menor' likely refers to a specific variety or preparation of cardamom, but the core referent is cardamom.", "wikidata_id": "Q33466", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elettaria%20cardamomum", "wikipedia_extract": "Elettaria cardamomum, commonly known as green cardamom or true cardamom, is a herbaceous, perennial plant in the ginger family, native to southern India. It is the most common of the species whose seeds are used as a spice called cardamom that has a strong aroma used in both savory and sweet cooking. It is cultivated widely in tropical regions and reportedly naturalized in R\u00e9union, Indochina, and Costa Rica. Elettaria cardamomum is a pungent, aromatic, herbaceous, perennial plant, growing to about 2\u20134 m (6 ft 7 in \u2013 13 ft 1 in) in height. The leaves are alternate in two ranks, linear-lanceolate, 40\u201360 cm (16\u201324 in) long, with a long pointed tip. The flowers are white to lilac or pale violet, produced in a loose spike 30\u201360 cm (12\u201324 in) long."}}, {"canonical_name": "Honey", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 70, "members": [{"entity_id": "honey", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Honey", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Honey"], "contexts": ["used in various remedies", "sweet substance used as vehicle"]}, {"entity_id": "honey", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Honey", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 65, "variants": ["Honey"], "contexts": ["ingredient added to decoction", "honey if it be to clens the womb"]}, {"entity_id": "honey", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "honey", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["honey"], "contexts": ["substance with specific density"]}, {"entity_id": "honey", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "honey", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 2, "variants": ["honey"], "contexts": ["doubled quantity mixed with remedy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Honey", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Honey", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Honey", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "honey", "similarity": 0.981}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Honey", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "honey", "similarity": 0.839}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Honey", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "honey", "similarity": 0.981}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Honey", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "honey", "similarity": 0.839}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "honey", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "honey", "similarity": 0.849}], "id": 361, "stable_key": "clu_d440bc24de0115fc", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Honey is a sweet and viscous substance made by several species of bees, the best-known of which are honey bees. Honey is made and stored to nourish bee colonies. Bees produce honey by gathering and then refining the sugary secretions of plants (primarily floral nectar) or the secretions of other insects, like the honeydew of aphids. This refinement takes place both within individual bees, through regurgitation and enzymatic activity, and during storage in the hive, through water evaporation that concentrates the honey's sugars until it is thick and viscous. Honey bees stockpile honey in the hive. Within the hive is a structure made from wax called honeycomb."}}, {"canonical_name": "freneticos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 69, "members": [{"entity_id": "frenes\u00edes", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "frenes\u00edes", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["frenes\u00edes"], "contexts": ["frenzies"]}, {"entity_id": "frenezia", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "frenezia", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 1, "variants": ["frenezia"], "contexts": ["frenzy, a mortal sign"]}, {"entity_id": "fren\u00e9tica", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "fren\u00e9tica", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["fren\u00e9tica"], "contexts": ["state of being frenzied"]}, {"entity_id": "freneticos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "freneticos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 66, "variants": ["freneticos", "Frenefi", "Frenefis", "Frenet", "Freneticos", "Frene\u017fi", "frene", "frenefis", "frenei", "frenesi", "frenesis", "frenes\u00ed", "frenetica", "frenetico", "frenetis", "frene\u017fi", "frene\u017fis", "fresne\u017fi", "phrenitis"], "contexts": ["frenetic individuals", "patients suffering from frenzy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "frenes\u00edes", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "fren\u00e9tica", "similarity": 0.923}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "frenes\u00edes", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "freneticos", "similarity": 0.909}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "frenezia", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "fren\u00e9tica", "similarity": 0.895}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "frenezia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "freneticos", "similarity": 0.888}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "fren\u00e9tica", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "freneticos", "similarity": 0.947}], "id": 362, "stable_key": "clu_d9d09f131e3d568d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Frenzy", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "A state of wild excitement or uncontrolled behavior.", "note": "Refers to historical medical concepts of mental agitation.", "wikipedia_extract": "Frenzy is a 1972 British neo-noir thriller film directed by Alfred Hitchcock in the penultimate feature film of his extensive career. The screenplay by Anthony Shaffer is based on the 1966 novel Goodbye Piccadilly, Farewell Leicester Square by Arthur La Bern. The film stars Jon Finch, Alec McCowen and Barry Foster and features Billie Whitelaw, Anna Massey, Barbara Leigh-Hunt, Bernard Cribbins and Vivien Merchant. The original music score was composed by Ron Goodwin. The plot centres on a serial killer in contemporary London and the ex-RAF serviceman he implicates. In a very early scene there is dialogue that mentions two actual London serial murder cases: the Christie murders in the 1940s\u20131950s and the Jack the Ripper murders in 1888."}}, {"canonical_name": "Boussingault", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 68, "members": [{"entity_id": "boussingault", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "Boussingault", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Boussingault"], "contexts": ["provided relations about curare"]}, {"entity_id": "boussingault", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Boussingault", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 50, "variants": ["Boussingault", "Boussiagault", "Boussingaidt", "Boussingault et Rivero", "Boussiogault", "Bouss\u00eengault", "Doussingault", "M. Boussineault", "M. Boussingault", "M. Bousslngault", "MM. Boussingault", "MM. Boussingault et Rivero", "MM. Rivero et Boussingault"], "contexts": ["measurement taker", "scientist who measured mountain height"]}, {"entity_id": "boussingault", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Boussingault", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 15, "variants": ["Boussingault"], "contexts": ["scientist who wrote a memoir on temperature", "determined invariable temperature stratum depth"]}, {"entity_id": "boussingault", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Boussingault", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Boussingault"], "contexts": ["scientist who studied earth's temperature", "calculated depth of underground stratum of constant heat"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "Boussingault", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Boussingault", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "Boussingault", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Boussingault", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "Boussingault", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Boussingault", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Boussingault", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Boussingault", "similarity":