 "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "sound", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "sound", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "sound", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "sound", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "sound", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "sound", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "sound", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "sound", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "sound", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "sound", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "sound", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "sound", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 399, "stable_key": "clu_3a66abcbc89364c3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sound", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q491713", "wikidata_description": "long, relatively wide body of water, connecting two larger parts of the sea", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sound%20%28geography%29", "note": "Context of auditory vibrations and phenomena, including those associated with natural events.", "wikipedia_extract": "In geography, a sound is a smaller body of water usually connected to a sea or an ocean. A sound may be an inlet that is deeper than a bight and wider than a fjord; or  a narrow sea channel or an ocean channel between two land masses, such as a strait; or also a lagoon between a barrier island and the mainland. A sound is often formed by the seas flooding a river valley. This produces a long inlet where the sloping valley hillsides descend to sea-level and continue beneath the water to form a sloping sea floor. These sounds are more appropriately called rias. The Marlborough Sounds in New Zealand are examples of this type of formation."}}, {"canonical_name": "Oro", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 44, "members": [{"entity_id": "oro", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "oro", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["oro"], "contexts": ["gold, a precious metal", "ornamental metal"]}, {"entity_id": "oro", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "oro", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["oro"], "contexts": ["Spanish for gold", "gold, traded in the region"]}, {"entity_id": "oro", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Oro", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 30, "variants": ["oro", "Oro"], "contexts": ["precious metal brought from the Indies", "gold"]}, {"entity_id": "oro", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "oro", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 9, "variants": ["oro"], "contexts": ["gold, a precious metal", "metal where borrace is found"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "oro", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "oro", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "oro", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Oro", "similarity": 0.993}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "oro", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "oro", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "oro", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Oro", "similarity": 0.993}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "oro", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "oro", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Oro", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "oro", "similarity": 0.993}], "id": 400, "stable_key": "clu_f40ee3060d273874", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Gold", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "The Spanish word 'oro' directly translates to 'gold'.", "wikidata_id": "Q897", "wikidata_description": "chemical element with symbol Au and atomic number 79", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold", "wikipedia_extract": "Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au (from Latin aurum) and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions. Gold often occurs as the free element (native state), as nuggets or grains, in rocks, veins, and alluvial deposits."}}, {"canonical_name": "cinamomo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 44, "members": [{"entity_id": "cinamomo", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "Cinamomo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["CINAMOMO", "Cinamomo"], "contexts": ["a plant, also called cinnamon", "spice ingredient in syrup"]}, {"entity_id": "ciuamomo", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "ciuamomo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["ciuamomo"], "contexts": ["plant, same origin as cinnamon"]}, {"entity_id": "cinamomo", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Cinamomo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cinamomo"], "contexts": ["type of cinnamon"]}, {"entity_id": "cinamomo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "cinamomo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 39, "variants": ["cinamomo", "CINAMOMO", "Cinamomo", "Cinnamo^um", "Cinnamomum", "Cirmamum", "cinamomo musilitico", "cinnamo mi", "cinnamomo", "cirmamomo", "citmamomo", "cynamomum"], "contexts": ["spice discussed in colloquy", "Spice discussed in the text"]}, {"entity_id": "cimiamomo", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Cimiamomo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cimiamomo"], "contexts": ["herb used in preparations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Cinamomo", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Cinamomo", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Cinamomo", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "cinamomo", "similarity": 0.941}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "ciuamomo", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "cinamomo", "similarity": 0.898}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "ciuamomo", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Cimiamomo", "similarity": 0.92}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Cinamomo", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "cinamomo", "similarity": 0.941}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "cinamomo", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Cimiamomo", "similarity": 0.901}], "id": 401, "stable_key": "clu_f3e6baaf0c14937a-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cinnamon", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Cinnamomum verum J.Presl", "family": "Lauraceae", "note": "While 'canela' can refer to both cinnamon and cassia, the context of Moluccas Islands strongly suggests cinnamon.", "wikidata_id": "Q28165", "wikidata_description": "spice obtained from the inner bark of several tree species from the genus Cinnamomum", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cinnamon", "wikipedia_extract": "Cinnamon is a spice obtained from the inner bark of several tree species from the genus Cinnamomum. Cinnamon is used mainly as an aromatic condiment and flavouring additive in a wide variety of cuisines, in particular sweet and savoury dishes such as biscuits, breakfast cereals, snack foods, bagels, teas, hot chocolate, and traditional foods. The aroma and flavour of cinnamon derive from its essential oil and principal component, cinnamaldehyde, as well as numerous other constituents, including eugenol. Cinnamon is the name for several species of trees and the commercial spice products that some of them produce. All are members of the genus Cinnamomum in the family Lauraceae. Only a few Cinnamomum species are grown commercially for spice."}}, {"canonical_name": "tropiques", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 42, "members": [{"entity_id": "tropiques", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "tropiques", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 17, "variants": ["tropiques", "tropicales"], "contexts": ["geographic region near the equator", "region where mountains rise above snow line"]}, {"entity_id": "tropics", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "tropics", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 13, "variants": ["tropics"], "contexts": ["regions receiving germs of civilization", "warm regions near the equator"]}, {"entity_id": "tropics", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "tropics", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 9, "variants": ["tropics"], "contexts": ["regions around the equator", "region around the equator"]}, {"entity_id": "tropic", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "tropic", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["tropic"], "contexts": ["geographic zone where storms change path", "line of latitude near equator"]}, {"entity_id": "tropics", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "tropics", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["tropics"], "contexts": ["region with luxuriant plant-life"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "tropiques", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "tropics", "similarity": 0.988}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "tropiques", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "tropics", "similarity": 0.988}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "tropiques", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "tropic", "similarity": 0.963}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "tropiques", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "tropics", "similarity": 0.988}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "tropics", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "tropics", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "tropics", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "tropic", "similarity": 0.978}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "tropics", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "tropics", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "tropics", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "tropics", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "tropic", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "tropics", "similarity": 0.978}], "id": 402, "stable_key": "clu_64eba7a2fd8d9004", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tropics", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Global", "wikidata_id": "Q42530", "wikidata_description": "region of the Earth surrounding the equator", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tropics", "wikipedia_extract": "The tropics are the region of Earth surrounding the equator, where the Sun may shine directly overhead. This contrasts with the temperate or polar regions of Earth, where the Sun can never be directly overhead. Because of Earth's axial tilt, the width of the tropics (in latitude) is twice the tilt. The tropics are also referred to as the tropical zone and the torrid zone (see geographical zone). Due to the sun's high angle throughout the year, the tropics receive the most solar energy over the course of the year, and consequently have the highest temperatures on the planet. Even when not directly overhead, the sun is still close to overhead throughout the year, therefore the tropics also have the lowest seasonal variation on the planet; \"winter\" and \"summer\" lose their temperature contrast."}}, {"canonical_name": "genus", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 41, "members": [{"entity_id": "genus", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "genus", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 36, "variants": ["genus"], "contexts": ["taxonomic rank above species", "taxonomic rank below family"]}, {"entity_id": "genus", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "genus", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["genus"], "contexts": ["classification based on resemblance"]}, {"entity_id": "genus", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "genus", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["genus"], "contexts": ["biological classification"]}, {"entity_id": "genus", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "genus", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["genus"], "contexts": ["classification of similar opinions", "taxonomic rank in classification"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "genus", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "genus", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "genus", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "genus", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "genus", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "genus", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "genus", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "genus", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "genus", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "genus", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "genus", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "genus", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 403, "stable_key": "clu_6f1713827cddb727", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Genus", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "volition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 41, "members": [{"entity_id": "voluntad", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "voluntad", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["voluntad"], "contexts": ["willpower"]}, {"entity_id": "volition", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "volition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 32, "variants": ["volition", "Volition"], "contexts": ["Soul's faculty", "power of conscious choice"]}, {"entity_id": "volition", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "volition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["volition"], "contexts": ["the power of using one's will"]}, {"entity_id": "volitional", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "volitional", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["volitional"], "contexts": ["related to the will or volition", "relating to the will"]}, {"entity_id": "volition", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "volition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["volition"], "contexts": ["the act of willing or choosing", "the power of using one's will"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "voluntad", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "volition", "similarity": 0.92}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "volition", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "volitional", "similarity": 0.9}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "volition", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "volition", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "volitional", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "volition", "similarity": 0.9}], "id": 404, "stable_key": "clu_8d9d4e0a1351aa68", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "volition", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q1441519", "wikidata_description": "cognitive process by which an individual decides on and commits to a particular course of action", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Volition%20%28psychology%29", "wikipedia_extract": "Volition, also known as will or conation, is the cognitive process by which an individual decides on and commits to a particular course of action. It is defined as purposive striving and is one of the primary human psychological functions. Others include affect (feeling or emotion), motivation (goals and expectations), and cognition (thinking). Volitional processes can be applied consciously or they can be automatized as habits over time. Most modern conceptions of volition address it as a process of conscious action control which becomes automatized (e.g. see Heckhausen and Kuhl; Gollwitzer; Boekaerts and Corno)."}}, {"canonical_name": "mammals", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 40, "members": [{"entity_id": "mammals", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "mammals", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 23, "variants": ["Mammals", "mammals"], "contexts": ["class of animals with mammary glands", "class of animals in geological treatises"]}, {"entity_id": "mammals", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "mammals", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["mammals"], "contexts": ["vertebrate animals with mammary glands", "mammalian animals"]}, {"entity_id": "mammalia", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "mammalia", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 5, "variants": ["mammalia"], "contexts": ["class of animals", "class of warm-blooded animals"]}, {"entity_id": "mammals", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "mammals", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 10, "variants": ["Mammals", "mammals"], "contexts": ["larger division of animals", "warm-blooded vertebrates"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "mammals", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "mammals", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "mammals", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "mammalia", "similarity": 0.985}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "mammals", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mammals", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "mammals", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "mammalia", "similarity": 0.985}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "mammals", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mammals", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "mammalia", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mammals", "similarity": 0.985}], "id": 405, "stable_key": "clu_216dd87a7239253f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mammals", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q7377", "wikidata_description": "class of animals, mostly terrestrial tetrapods, some aquatic", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mammal", "wikipedia_extract": "A mammal (from Latin  mamma 'breast') is a vertebrate animal of the class Mammalia (). Mammals are characterised by the presence of milk-producing mammary glands for feeding their young, a broad neocortex region of the brain, fur or hair, and three middle ear bones. These characteristics distinguish them from reptiles and birds, from which their ancestors diverged in the Carboniferous Period over 300 million years ago. Around 6,640 extant species of mammals have been described and divided into 27 orders. The study of mammals is called mammalogy. The largest orders of mammals, by number of species, are the rodents, bats, and eulipotyphlans (including hedgehogs, moles and shrews)."}}, {"canonical_name": "will", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 39, "members": [{"entity_id": "will", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778"