, "source_name": "SIR DAVID BREWSTER", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Sir David Brewster", "similarity": 0.923}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "M. Brewster", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Sir David Brewster", "similarity": 0.872}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "M. Brewster", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "David Brewster", "similarity": 0.874}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Sir David Brewster", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "David Brewster", "similarity": 0.943}], "id": 459, "stable_key": "clu_381574b0d2c9e245", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "David Brewster", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1781, "death_year": 1868, "description": "Scottish physicist, inventor, and writer on scientific subjects.", "wikidata_id": "Q15442692", "wikidata_description": "American journalist", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David%20Brewster%20%28journalist%29", "wikipedia_extract": "David Clark Brewster (born September 26, 1939) is an American journalist and the founder, editor and publisher of the Seattle Weekly and the Northwest news website Crosscut.com. He is also the founder, creator and former executive director of the nonprofit cultural center Town Hall Seattle. He was born on September 26, 1939, in Newark, New Jersey, the son of Gaylord Clark Brewster and Marjorie Jane Anderson. His father was a 1930 graduate of the University of Nebraska. He graduated in 1961, Phi Beta Kappa,  with a Bachelor of Arts from Yale University in New Haven, Connecticut, and he received  his master's degree from Yale University in 1963. In 1964, he married Joyce Skaggs, a 1961 graduate of Smith College."}}, {"canonical_name": "Paracelsus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 24, "members": [{"entity_id": "paracelse", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "Paracelse", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Paracelse"], "contexts": ["theorized drug action from astrological influences", "associated with heroic remedies and universal panaceas"]}, {"entity_id": "paracelsus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Paracelsus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 9, "variants": ["Paracelsus"], "contexts": ["singular figure, reviled past learning", "alchemist on wine extraction"]}, {"entity_id": "paracelsus", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Paracelsus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Paracelsus"], "contexts": ["Paracelsus extols it to the Skies", "renaissance alchemist and physician"]}, {"entity_id": "paracelsus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Paracelsus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 10, "variants": ["Paracelsus", "Paracelf.", "Paracelfi", "Paracelfo", "Paracelso"], "contexts": ["early modern scholar on Archidoxis", "early modern physician and alchemist"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "Paracelse", "target_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "target_name": "Paracelsus", "similarity": 0.84}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "Paracelse", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Paracelsus", "similarity": 0.84}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Paracelsus", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Paracelsus", "similarity": 0.77}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Paracelsus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Paracelsus", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Paracelsus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Paracelsus", "similarity": 0.77}], "id": 460, "stable_key": "clu_dc3d86c4249cfe10", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Paracelsus", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1493, "death_year": 1541, "description": "Influential alchemist, physician, and astrologer."}}, {"canonical_name": "exercicio", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 24, "members": [{"entity_id": "exercice", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "exercice", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["exercice"], "contexts": ["physical activity leading to wear"]}, {"entity_id": "exercise", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "exercise", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["exercise"], "contexts": ["physical activity influencing traits"]}, {"entity_id": "exercicio", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "exercicio", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["exercicio"], "contexts": ["physical activity"]}, {"entity_id": "exercicio", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "exercicio", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 21, "variants": ["exercicio", "exerciciodema", "exerciciolaborio\u017fo", "exercise", "exercitar"], "contexts": ["physical activity and regimen", "physical activity for health"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "exercice", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "exercise", "similarity": 0.956}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "exercice", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "exercicio", "similarity": 0.975}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "exercice", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "exercicio", "similarity": 0.975}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "exercise", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "exercicio", "similarity": 0.965}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "exercise", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "exercicio", "similarity": 0.965}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "exercicio", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "exercicio", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 461, "stable_key": "clu_8c3b02891e37f331", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Exercise", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "Physical activity undertaken for health or regimen.", "wikipedia_extract": "Exercise or working out is physical activity that enhances or maintains fitness and overall health. It is performed for various reasons, including weight loss or maintenance, to aid growth and improve strength, develop muscles and the cardiovascular system, hone athletic skills, improve health, or simply for enjoyment. Many people choose to exercise outdoors where they can congregate in groups, socialize, and improve well-being as well as mental health. In terms of health benefits, usually, 150 minutes (2 hours and 30 minutes) of moderate-intensity exercise per week is recommended for reducing the risk of health problems. At the same time, even doing a small amount of exercise is healthier than doing none. Only doing an hour and a quarter (11 minutes/day) of exercise could reduce the risk of early death, cardiovascular disease, stroke, and cancer."}}, {"canonical_name": "Anglois", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 24, "members": [{"entity_id": "anglicus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Anglicus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Anglicus"], "contexts": ["nickname for Bartholomeus Glanvil"]}, {"entity_id": "anglois", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Anglois", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 21, "variants": ["Anglois", "Angtois", "anglois"], "contexts": ["English people", "usage for classifying canals"]}, {"entity_id": "angli", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Angli", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Angli"], "contexts": ["English people who consume many sweets"]}, {"entity_id": "anglo-americans", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Anglo-Americans", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Anglo-Americans"], "contexts": ["people of American descent"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Anglicus", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Anglois", "similarity": 0.851}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Anglicus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Angli", "similarity": 0.841}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Anglois", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Angli", "similarity": 0.881}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Anglois", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Anglo-Americans", "similarity": 0.884}], "id": 462, "stable_key": "clu_9a1f21ae26974cb2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "English people", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "People from England, mentioned in medical and botanical texts.", "wikidata_id": "Q987355", "wikidata_description": "countries and regions where English is used", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/English-speaking%20world", "note": "The term 'Anglois' and 'Anglo-Americans' in the contexts provided refer to people of English origin or descent.", "wikipedia_extract": "The English-speaking world comprises the 88 countries and territories in which English is an official, administrative, or cultural language. In the early 2000s, between one and two billion people spoke English, making it the largest language by number of speakers, the third largest language by number of native speakers and the most widespread language geographically. The countries in which English is the native language of most people are sometimes termed the Anglosphere. Speakers of English are called Anglophones. Early Medieval England was the birthplace of the English language; the modern form of the language has been spread around the world since the 17th century, first by the worldwide influence of England and later the United Kingdom, and then by that of the United States. Through all types of printed and electronic media of these countries, English has become the leading language of international discourse and the lingua franca in many regions and professional fields, such as science, navigation and law."}}, {"canonical_name": "Sede", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 24, "members": [{"entity_id": "thirst", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Thirst", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 3, "variants": ["Thirst"], "contexts": ["immoderate desire for drink", "sensation of needing to drink"]}, {"entity_id": "thirst", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "thirst", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["thirst"], "contexts": ["physical sensation of needing drink"]}, {"entity_id": "sede", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Sede", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 19, "variants": ["Sede", "sede", "sedes"], "contexts": ["thirst", "sensation of thirst"]}, {"entity_id": "thirst", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "thirst", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["thirst"], "contexts": ["sensation of needing to drink"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Thirst", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "thirst", "similarity": 0.998}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Thirst", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Sede", "similarity": 0.958}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Thirst", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "thirst", "similarity": 0.998}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "thirst", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Sede", "similarity": 0.961}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "thirst", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "thirst", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Sede", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "thirst", "similarity": 0.961}], "id": 463, "stable_key": "clu_4716a23d5a4be142", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Thirst", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "An uncomfortable sensation indicating a need for fluids.", "wikipedia_extract": "Thirst is the craving for potable fluids, resulting in the basic instinct of animals to drink. It is an essential mechanism involved in fluid balance. It arises from a lack of fluids or an increase in the concentration of certain osmolites, such as sodium. If the water volume of the body falls below a certain threshold or the osmolite concentration becomes too high, structures in the brain detect changes in blood constituents and signal thirst. Continuous dehydration can cause acute and chronic diseases, but is most often associated with renal and neurological disorders. Excessive thirst, called polydipsia, along with excessive urination, known as polyuria, may be an indication of diabetes mellitus or diabetes insipidus."}}, {"canonical_name": "cobre", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 24, "members": [{"entity_id": "cobre", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "cobre", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["cobre"], "contexts": ["metal used for utensils"]}, {"entity_id": "cobre", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Cobre", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 5, "variants": ["Cobre"], "contexts": ["metal brought from the Indies", "metal discussed for its temperature"]}, {"entity_id": "cobre", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "cobre", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 11, "variants": ["cobre"], "contexts": ["metal for coinage", "metal used for sieves"]}, {"entity_id": "cobre", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "cobre", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 7, "variants": ["cobre"], "contexts": ["metal used for a pot", "material for a vessel"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "cobre", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Cobre", "similarity": 0.942}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "cobre", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "cobre", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "cobre", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cobre", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Cobre", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "cobre", "similarity": 0.942}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Cobre", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cobre", "similarity": 0.942}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "cobre", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cobre", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 464, "stable_key": "clu_1fa3ab7b274a70ee", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Copper", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q753", "wikidata_description": "chemical element with symbol Cu and atomic number 29", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copper", "wikipedia_extract": "Copper is a chemical element; it has symbol Cu (from Latin cuprum) and atomic number 29. It is a soft, malleable, and ductile metal with very high thermal and electrical conductivity. A freshly exposed surface of pure copper has a pinkish-orange color. Copper is used as a conductor of heat and electricity, as a building material, and as a constituent of various metal alloys, such as sterling silver used in jewelry, cupronickel used to make marine hardware and coins, and constantan used in strain gauges and thermocouples for temperature measurement. Copper is one of the few native metals, meaning metals that occur naturally in a directly usable, unalloyed metallic form. This led to very early human use in several regions, from c."}}, {"canonical_name": "Aloe", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 24, "members": [{"entity_id": "american_aloe", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "American aloe", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["American aloe"], "contexts": ["plant stem observed for rapid growth"]}, {"entity_id": "al\u00f5e", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "al\u00f5e", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["al\u00f5e"], "contexts": ["Greek and Latin name for aloe"]}, {"entity_id": "al\u00f4e", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Al\u00f4e", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Al\u00f4e"], "contexts": ["plantations on Socotra"]}, {"entity_id": "aloe", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Aloe", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 16, "variants": ["Aloe", "ALOE", "Aloe zoccoterino", "Alo\u00e8", "Alo\u00e9", "aloe"], "contexts": ["plant name", "juice of a plant"]}, {"entity_id": "aloe", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Aloe", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 5, "variants": ["Aloe", "aloe", "alo\u00e9"], "contexts": ["herb used for purging", "ingredient in a purgative"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "American aloe", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Aloe", "similarity": 0.9}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "American aloe", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Aloe", "similarity": 0.9}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "al\u00f5e", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Aloe", "similarity": 0.748}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "al\u00f5e", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Aloe", "similarity": 0.748}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Al\u00f4e", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Aloe", "similarity": 0.749}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Al\u00f4e", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Aloe", "similarity": 0.749}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Aloe", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Aloe", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 465, "stable_key": "clu_b542f197247a6194", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Aloes", "confidence": "high", "type": "PLANT", "description": "A genus of succulent plants, known for their medicinal properties.", "note": "Refers to the plant genus 'Aloe' and its derivatives like 'alo\u00e9s'.", "wikipedia_extract": "Agarwood, aloeswood, eaglewood, gaharuwood, commonly referred to as oud or oudh (from Arabic: \u0639\u0648\u062f, romanized: \u02bf\u016bd, pronounced [\u0295u\u02d0d]), is a fragrant, dark and resinous wood used in incense, perfume, and small hand carvings. It forms in the heartwood of Aquilaria trees after they become infected with a type of Phaeoacremonium mold, P. parasitica. The tree defensively secretes a resin to combat the fungal infestation. Prior to becoming infected, the heartwood mostly lacks scent, and is relatively light and pale in colouration. However, as the infection advances and the tree produces its fragrant resin as a final option of defense, the heartwood becomes very dense, dark, and saturated with resin. This product is harvested, and most famously referred to in cosmetics under the scent names of oud, oodh or aguru; however, it is also called aloes (not to be confused with the succulent plant genus Aloe), agar (this name, as well, is not to be confused with the edible, algae-derived thickening agent agar agar), as well as gaharu or jinko."}}, {"canonical_name": "Trees", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 24, "members": [{"entity_id": "trees", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Trees", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 13, "variants": ["Trees", "trees"], "contexts": ["habitat and food source for organisms", "large trees growing"]}, {"entity_id": "trees", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Trees", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Trees", "trees"], "contexts": ["woody perennial plants", "moisture turns to steam from electricity"]}, {"entity_id": "trees", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "trees", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 4, "variants": ["trees", "Trees"], "contexts": ["source of gums for concretion", "woody perennial plants"]}, {"entity_id": "trees", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "trees", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 3, "variants": ["trees"], "contexts": ["objects in tapestries", "plants with spiral branches"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Trees", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Trees", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "trees", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "trees", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 466, "stable_key": "clu_c3c188d2ab77cd00-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tree", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "note": "This cluster refers to the general category of 'tree' rather than a specific species. 'Arbusto' (shrub) is a related but distinct botanical term.", "wikidata_id": "Q10884", "wikidata_description": "perennial woody plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tree", "wikipedia_extract": "In botany, a tree is a perennial plant with an elongated stem, or trunk, usually supporting branches and leaves. In some usages, the definition of a tree may be narrower, e.g., including only woody plants with secondary growth, only plants that are usable as lumber, or only plants above a specified height. Wider definitions include taller palms, tree ferns, bananas, and bamboos. Trees are not a monophyletic taxonomic group but consist of a wide variety of plant species that have independently evolved a trunk and branches as a way to tower above other plants to compete for sunlight. The majority of tree species are angiosperms or hardwoods; of the rest, many are gymnosperms or softwoods. Trees tend to be long-lived, some trees reaching several thousand years old.", "description": "A woody perennial plant, typically having a single stem or trunk."}}, {"canonical_name": "Analysis", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 23, "members": [{"entity_id": "analyse", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "analyse", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["analyse"], "contexts": ["breaking down phenomena", "breaking down into components"]}, {"entity_id": "analysis", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Analysis", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 12, "variants": ["Analysis", "analysis"], "contexts": ["breaking down into parts", "title of a work"]}, {"entity_id": "analysis", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Analysis", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 7, "variants": ["analysis", "Analysis"], "contexts": ["skill required to follow scientific outlines", "breaking down into components"]}, {"entity_id": "analysis", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "analysis", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["analysis"], "contexts": ["breaking down into components"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "analyse", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Analysis", "similarity": 0.897}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "analyse", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Analysis", "similarity": 0.897}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "analyse", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "analysis", "similarity": 0.962}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Analysis", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Analysis", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Analysis", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "analysis", "similarity": 0.92}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Analysis", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "analysis", "similarity": 0.92}], "id": 467, "stable_key": "clu_7cd447547f79f028", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "analysis", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Both 'analyze' and 'analysis' in this context refer to the intellectual process of breaking down complex subjects into their constituent parts, a fundamental concept in scientific and philosophical inquiry.", "wikidata_id": "Q15751184", "wikidata_description": "mathematics journal", "wikipedia_extract": "A concept is an abstract idea that serves as a foundation for more concrete principles, thoughts, and beliefs. Concepts play an important role in all aspects of cognition. As such, concepts are studied within such disciplines as linguistics, psychology, and philosophy, and these disciplines are interested in the logical and psychological structure of concepts, and how they are put together to form thoughts and sentences. The study of concepts has served as an important flagship of an emerging interdisciplinary approach, cognitive science. In contemporary philosophy, three understandings of a concept prevail: mental representations, such that a concept is an entity that exists in the mind (a mental object) abilities peculiar to cognitive agents (mental states) Fregean senses, abstract objects rather than a mental object or a mental state Concepts are classified into a hierarchy, higher levels of which are termed \"superordinate\" and lower levels termed \"subordinate\". Additionally, there is the \"basic\" or \"middle\" level at which people will most readily categorize a concept.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "Scaligero", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 23, "members": [{"entity_id": "scaliger", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Scaliger", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 10, "variants": ["Scaliger"], "contexts": ["ancient authority, did not deny crystal is ice", "Renaissance scholar"]}, {"entity_id": "scaligero", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Scaligero", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 11, "variants": ["Scaligero"], "contexts": ["Defended Pliny's statement on aloes", "made notes on Orta's book"]}, {"entity_id": "scaligero", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Scaligero", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Scaligero"], "contexts": ["Julius Caesar Scaliger"]}, {"entity_id": "scaliger", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Scaliger", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Scaliger"], "contexts": ["Renaissance scholar"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Scaliger", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Scaliger", "similarity": 0.818}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Scaligero", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Scaligero", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Scaligero", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Scaliger", "similarity": 0.974}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Scaligero", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Scaliger", "similarity": 0.974}], "id": 468, "stable_key": "clu_b695b9595f229f6b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Julius Caesar Scaliger", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1484, "death_year": 1558, "description": "Italian physician, botanist, poet, and literary critic.", "note": "The cluster includes both Julius Scaliger and Joseph Scaliger. Julius Caesar Scaliger is the most prominent of the two for scholarship in the relevant period and context.", "wikidata_id": "Q441066", "wikidata_description": "Italian scholar, physician and philosopher (1484-1558)", "wikipedia_extract": "Julius Caesar Scaliger (; 23 April  or August 1484 \u2013 21 October 1558), or Giulio Cesare della Scala, was an Italian scholar and physician, who spent a major part of his career in France. He employed the techniques and discoveries of Renaissance humanism to defend Aristotelianism against the New Learning. In spite of his contentious disposition, his contemporary reputation was high. Jacques Auguste de Thou claimed that none of the ancients could be placed above him and that he had no equal in his own time. Scaliger's father, Benedetto Bordone, was a miniaturist and illuminator. Scaliger himself was known in his youth by the family name Bordone, but later insisted that he was a scion of the house of La Scala, for a hundred and fifty years lords of Verona."}}, {"canonical_name": "Cicero", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 23, "members": [{"entity_id": "cicero", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Cicero", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Cicero"], "contexts": ["Roman statesman and orator", "ancient writer"]}, {"entity_id": "cicero", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Cicero", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Cicero"], "contexts": ["ancient philosopher on insight into nature"]}, {"entity_id": "cicero", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Cicero", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cicero"], "contexts": ["Roman philosopher and orator"]}, {"entity_id": "cicero", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Cicero", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 17, "variants": ["Cicero", "Cicer", "Cicer.", "Ciceron", "Cicerone"], "contexts": ["Ancient Roman orator and philosopher", "Ancient authority, author of 'de Officijs'"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Cicero", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Cicero", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Cicero", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Cicero", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Cicero", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Cicero", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Cicero", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Cicero", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Cicero", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Cicero", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Cicero", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Cicero", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 469, "stable_key": "clu_ccb622fe0550f5ad", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cicero", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": -106, "death_year": -43, "description": "Roman statesman, lawyer, scholar, and orator.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cicero", "wikipedia_extract": "Marcus Tullius Cicero ( SISS-\u0259-roh; Latin: [\u02c8ma\u02d0rkus \u02c8tulli.us \u02c8kik\u025br\u0254\u02d0]; 3 January 106 BC \u2013 7 December 43 BC) was a Roman statesman, lawyer, scholar, philosopher, orator, writer and Academic skeptic, who tried to uphold optimate principles during the political crises that led to the establishment of the Roman Empire. His extensive writings include treatises on rhetoric, philosophy and politics. He is considered one of Rome's greatest orators and prose stylists and the innovator of what became known as \"Ciceronian rhetoric\". Cicero was educated in Rome and in Greece. He came from a wealthy municipal family of the Roman equestrian order, and served as consul in 63 BC. He greatly influenced both ancient and modern reception of the Latin language."}}, {"canonical_name": "Theriaca", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 23, "members": [{"entity_id": "theriaca", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Theriaca", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Theriaca"], "contexts": ["work by Nicander on venemous beasts and antidotes"]}, {"entity_id": "theriaca", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Theriaca", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 12, "variants": ["Theriaca", "Theriacas", "Therlaca", "Ther\u00edaca"], "contexts": ["medicine taken daily by Marco Antonio", "principal medicine against poison"]}, {"entity_id": "theriaca", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "theriaca", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 8, "variants": ["theriaca", "Thcriaca", "Theria", "Theriaca"], "contexts": ["compound medicine", "medicinal preparation for which calcite is burned"]}, {"entity_id": "theriac", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "theriac", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 2, "variants": ["theriac"], "contexts": ["remedy mixed with oximel"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Theriaca", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Theriaca", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Theriaca", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "theriaca", "similarity": 0.982}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Theriaca", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "theriac", "similarity": 0.933}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Theriaca", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "theriaca", "similarity": 0.982}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Theriaca", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "theriac", "similarity": 0.933}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "theriaca", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "theriac", "similarity": 0.958}], "id": 470, "stable_key": "clu_325116fd0d502a0e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Theriac", "confidence": "high", "type": "text", "note": "The text mentions 'Theriaca' as a compound medicine, and references Nicander's work on antidotes. This likely refers to Theriaca Andromachi, a complex polypharmaceutical preparation.", "wikidata_id": "Q1546149", "wikidata_description": "medieval medical concoction", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theriac", "wikipedia_extract": "Theriac or theriaca is a medical concoction originally labelled by the Greeks in the 1st century AD and widely adopted in the ancient world as far away as Persia, China and India via the trading links of the Silk Route. It was an alexipharmic, or antidote for a variety of poisons and diseases. It was also considered a panacea, a term for which it could be used interchangeably: in the 16th century Adam Lonicer wrote that garlic was the rustic's theriac or Heal-All. The word theriac comes from the Greek term \u03b8\u03b7\u03c1\u03b9\u03b1\u03ba\u03ae (th\u0113riak\u0113), a feminine adjective signifying \"pertaining to animals\", from \u03b8\u03b7\u03c1\u03af\u03bf\u03bd (th\u0113rion), \"wild animal, beast\". The ancient bestiaries included information\u2014often fanciful\u2014about dangerous beasts and their bites. When cane sugar was an exotic Eastern commodity, the English recommended the sugar-based treacle as an antidote against poison, originally applied as a salve.", "description": "A complex compound medicine or antidote, historically used as a panacea."}}, {"canonical_name": "Chest", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 23, "members": [{"entity_id": "chest", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Chest", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 16, "variants": ["Chest", "chest"], "contexts": ["part cleansed of phlegm by electuary", "cleansed of humors by decoction"]}, {"entity_id": "chest", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "chest", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["chest"], "contexts": ["size increased by breathing in high regions"]}, {"entity_id": "barriga", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "barriga", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["barriga"], "contexts": ["belly"]}, {"entity_id": "barriga", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "barriga", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 5, "variants": ["barriga", "barrigas das pernas"], "contexts": ["body part", "belly, body region"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Chest", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "chest", "similarity": 0.988}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Chest", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "barriga", "similarity": 0.906}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Chest", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "barriga", "similarity": 0.906}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "chest", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "barriga", "similarity": 0.894}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "chest", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "barriga", "similarity": 0.894}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "barriga", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "barriga", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 471, "stable_key": "clu_5fceb426535ab272", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Chest", "confidence": "high", "type": "anatomy", "description": "The part of the body between the neck and the abdomen; the thorax.", "wikipedia_extract": "The thorax (pl.: thoraces or thoraxes) or chest is a part of the anatomy of mammals and other tetrapod animals located between the neck and the abdomen. In insects, crustaceans, and the extinct trilobites, the thorax is one of the three main divisions of the body, each in turn composed of multiple segments. The human thorax includes the thoracic cavity and the thoracic wall. It contains organs including the heart, lungs, and thymus gland, as well as muscles and various other internal structures. The chest may be affected by many diseases, of which the most common symptom is chest pain. The word thorax comes from the Greek \u03b8\u03ce\u03c1\u03b1\u03be th\u1e53rax \"breastplate, cuirass, corslet\" via Latin: thorax."}}, {"canonical_name": "New-York", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 23, "members": [{"entity_id": "new-york", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "New-York", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 10, "variants": ["New-York", "New York"], "contexts": ["state for comparison", "state with small relative population"]}, {"entity_id": "new_york", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "New York", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 7, "variants": ["NEW YORK", "New York"], "contexts": ["city of publication", "city where translation was published"]}, {"entity_id": "new_york", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "New York", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 5, "variants": ["New York"], "contexts": ["location for hemisphere comparison", "location with magnetic intensity"]}, {"entity_id": "new_york", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "New York", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["New York"], "contexts": ["location of Professor Draper"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "New-York", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "New York", "similarity": 0.983}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "New-York", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "New York", "similarity": 0.983}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "New-York", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "New York", "similarity": 0.983}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "New York", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "New York", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "New York", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "New York", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "New York", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "New York", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 472, "stable_key": "clu_93f08e4af0a2f6ec", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "New York", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A major city and state in the United States.", "note": "Includes references to both the city and the state, as well as a nearby city (Newark).", "wikipedia_extract": "The geography of New York City is characterized by its coastal position at the meeting of the Hudson River and the Atlantic Ocean in a naturally sheltered harbor. The city's geography, with its scarce availability of land, is a contributing factor in making New York the most densely populated major city in the United States. Environmental issues are chiefly concerned with managing this density, which also explains why New York is among the most energy-efficient and least automobile-dependent cities in the United States. The city's climate is temperate. New York City is located on the coast of the Northeastern United States at the mouth of the Hudson River in southeastern New York state. It is located in the New York\u2013New Jersey Harbor Estuary, the centerpiece of which is the New York Harbor, whose deep waters and sheltered bays helped the city grow in significance as a trading city."}}, {"canonical_name": "Roma", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 23, "members": [{"entity_id": "roma", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Roma", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Roma"], "contexts": ["city where the resin was first brought", "city where water chilling is customary"]}, {"entity_id": "roma", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Roma", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 10, "variants": ["Roma"], "contexts": ["city, left to the Pope", "city where Mascarenhas was ambassador"]}, {"entity_id": "roma", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Roma", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Roma"], "contexts": ["city with good river water"]}, {"entity_id": "roma", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Roma", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 9, "variants": ["Roma", "Ro\u017fma", "roma"], "contexts": ["ancient empire and center of power", "city in Italy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Roma", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Roma", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Roma", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Roma", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Roma", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Roma", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Roma", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Roma", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Roma", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Roma", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Roma", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Roma", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 473, "stable_key": "clu_955afb03b0773db9-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Rome", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Ancient city, center of the Roman Empire and a significant historical location.", "note": "Includes references to the Roman Empire itself.", "wikipedia_extract": "Rome is the capital city and most populated comune (municipality) of Italy. It is also the administrative centre of the Lazio region and of the Metropolitan City of Rome. A special comune named Roma Capitale with 2,746,984 residents in 1,287.36 km2 (497.1 mi2), Rome is the third most populous city in the European Union by population within city limits. The Metropolitan City of Rome Capital, with a population of 4,223,885 residents, is the most populous metropolitan city in Italy. Its metropolitan area is the third-most populous within Italy. Rome is located in the central-western portion of the Italian Peninsula, within Lazio (Latium), along the shores of the Tiber Valley."}}, {"canonical_name": "Equator", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 23, "members": [{"entity_id": "equator", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Equator", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 7, "variants": ["Equator"], "contexts": ["Earth's equatorial line", "imaginary line dividing Earth into hemispheres"]}, {"entity_id": "equator", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Equator", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 13, "variants": ["Equator", "equator"], "contexts": ["imaginary line around Earth's middle", "line of zero latitude"]}, {"entity_id": "equator", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "equator", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["equator"], "contexts": ["lands of the equator"]}, {"entity_id": "equator", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "equator", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["equator"], "contexts": ["imaginary line around Earth's center", "imaginary line around Earth's middle"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Equator", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Equator", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "equator", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "equator", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 474, "stable_key": "clu_f43f4c2359bdca2b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Equator", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q983", "wikidata_description": "sovereign state in Africa", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Equatorial%20Guinea", "wikipedia_extract": "Equatorial Guinea, officially the Republic of Equatorial Guinea, is a country on the west coast of Central Africa. It has an area of 28,000 square kilometres (11,000 sq mi). Formerly the colony of Spanish Guinea, its post-independence name refers to its location both near the Equator and in the African region of Guinea. As of 2025, the country has a population of 1,853,559, over 85% of whom are members of the Fang people, the country's dominant ethnic group. The Bubi people, indigenous to Bioko, are the second largest group at approximately 6.5% of the population. Its capital is Ciudad de la Paz, replacing former capital Malabo in 2026, while its largest city is Bata."}}, {"canonical_name": "Chymico", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 22, "members": [{"entity_id": "chimique", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "chimique", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["chimique"], "contexts": ["relating to chemistry"]}, {"entity_id": "chemical", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "chemical", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["chemical"], "contexts": ["relating to chemistry"]}, {"entity_id": "chymico", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Chymico", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 12, "variants": ["Chymico", "Chimica", "Chimyca", "Chymic.", "Chymic\u00e6"], "contexts": ["alchemical", "alchemist preparing magistery"]}, {"entity_id": "chemical", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "chemical", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 8, "variants": ["chemical", "Chemical"], "contexts": ["facts about matter composition", "related to metallurgy and relations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "chimique", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "chemical", "similarity": 0.969}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "chimique", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Chymico", "similarity": 0.918}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "chimique", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "chemical", "similarity": 0.969}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "chemical", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Chymico", "similarity": 0.904}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "chemical", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "chemical", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Chymico", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "chemical", "similarity": 0.904}], "id": 475, "stable_key": "clu_152abd7203a874cc-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Chemistry", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Early modern usage of 'Chymistry' refers to the precursor of modern chemistry.", "wikidata_id": "Q898737", "wikidata_description": "scientific journal for chemistry in Europe", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chemistry%3A%20A%20European%20Journal", "wikipedia_extract": "Chemistry: A European Journal is a weekly peer-reviewed scientific journal that covers all areas of chemistry and related fields. It is published by Wiley-VCH on behalf of Chemistry Europe. The editor-in-chief is Haymo Ross. According to the Journal Citation Reports, the journal has a 2024 impact factor of 3.7."}}, {"canonical_name": "Sulphur", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 22, "members": [{"entity_id": "sulphur", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Sulphur", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 10, "variants": ["sulphur", "Sulphur"], "contexts": ["flammable part in crystal", "used to corrode steel"]}, {"entity_id": "sulphur", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "sulphur", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 9, "variants": ["sulphur"], "contexts": ["trace component of aerolite analysis", "chemical element in meteoric masses"]}, {"entity_id": "sulphur", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "sulphur", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["sulphur"], "contexts": ["element, part of sulphuretted hydrogen gas", "interposed dielectric material"]}, {"entity_id": "sulphur", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "sulphur", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["sulphur"], "contexts": ["element in Fibrine composition"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Sulphur", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "sulphur", "similarity": 0.979}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Sulphur", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "sulphur", "similarity": 0.979}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Sulphur", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "sulphur", "similarity": 0.979}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "sulphur", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "sulphur", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "sulphur", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "sulphur", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "sulphur", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "sulphur", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 476, "stable_key": "clu_2bd325ed697ebcd2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sulfur", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "note": "Brimstone is an archaic term for sulfur.", "wikidata_id": "Q1157552", "wikidata_description": "mineral (as opposed to the chemical element)", "wikipedia_extract": "Sulfuric acid (American spelling and the preferred IUPAC name) or sulphuric acid (Commonwealth spelling), known in antiquity as oil of vitriol, is a mineral acid composed of the elements sulfur, oxygen, and hydrogen, with the molecular formula H2SO4. It is a colorless, odorless, and viscous liquid that is miscible with water. Pure sulfuric acid does not occur naturally due to its strong affinity to water vapor; it is hygroscopic and readily absorbs water vapor from the air. Concentrated sulfuric acid is a strong oxidant with powerful dehydrating properties, making it highly corrosive towards other materials, from rocks to metals. Phosphorus pentoxide is a notable exception in that it is not dehydrated by sulfuric acid but, to the contrary, dehydrates sulfuric acid to sulfur trioxide. Upon addition of sulfuric acid to water, a considerable amount of heat is released; thus, the reverse procedure of adding water to the acid is generally avoided since the heat released may boil the solution, spraying droplets of hot acid during the process.", "wikipedia_url": "https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soufre_%28min%C3%A9ral%29"}}, {"canonical_name": "cells", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 22, "members": [{"entity_id": "sels", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "sels", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["sels"], "contexts": ["formed by water, air, earth, fire", "salts, chemical compounds"]}, {"entity_id": "cells", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "cells", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 14, "variants": ["cells"], "contexts": ["basic structural units of nervous tissue", "basic biological units"]}, {"entity_id": "cells", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "cells", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["cells"], "contexts": ["multiplication and transformation in organic development", "basic unit of organic development"]}, {"entity_id": "cells", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "cells", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["cells"], "contexts": ["aggregation forming the heart", "multiplication forming the liver"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "sels", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "cells", "similarity": 0.931}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "sels", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "cells", "similarity": 0.931}, {"source_b