: "Crystal () is a common English language female given name. Variant forms of the name include Kristal,  Krystal,  Cristal,  Kristel,  Krystle and Kristol. As a feminine name, it is a 19th-century coinage, derived from crystal, a transparent quartz gemstone, usually colorless, that can be cut to reflect brilliant light, whose name comes from Ancient Greek \u03ba\u03c1\u03cd\u03c3\u03c4\u03b1\u03bb\u03bb\u03bf\u03c2 krystallos \"ice\". It increased in usage along with other names suggestive of gemstones. As a masculine name, it is a Scottish diminutive of Christopher. Cristal is a Spanish variant."}}, {"canonical_name": "pigeons", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 22, "members": [{"entity_id": "pigeons", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "pigeons", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["pigeons"], "contexts": ["common bird"]}, {"entity_id": "pigeons", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "pigeons", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 14, "variants": ["Pigeons", "pigeons"], "contexts": ["domestic birds studied for breeds", "domesticated bird species"]}, {"entity_id": "pigeons", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "pigeons", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 6, "variants": ["pigeons"], "contexts": ["animals used in experiments", "birds studied for vision after hemisphere removal"]}, {"entity_id": "pigeons", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "pigeons", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["pigeons"], "contexts": ["animal"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "pigeons", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "pigeons", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "pigeons", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "pigeons", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "pigeons", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "pigeons", "similarity": 0.922}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "pigeons", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "pigeons", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "pigeons", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "pigeons", "similarity": 0.922}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "pigeons", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "pigeons", "similarity": 0.922}], "id": 479, "stable_key": "clu_2b62f246b4c02c90-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pigeon", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q10856", "wikidata_description": "family of birds (Aves)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Columbidae", "wikipedia_extract": "Columbidae is a bird family consisting of doves and pigeons. It is the only family in the order Columbiformes. These are stout-bodied birds with small heads, relatively short necks and slender bills that in some species feature fleshy ceres. They feed largely on plant matter, feeding on seeds (granivory), fruit (frugivory), and foliage (folivory). In colloquial English, the smaller species tend to be called \"doves\", and the larger ones \"pigeons\", although the distinction is not consistent, and there is no scientific separation between them. Historically, the common names for these birds involve a great deal of variation."}}, {"canonical_name": "mechanics", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 22, "members": [{"entity_id": "mechanical_science", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Mechanical Science", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mechanical Science"], "contexts": ["scientific study of mechanics"]}, {"entity_id": "mechanical", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Mechanical", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mechanical"], "contexts": ["relating to physical forces"]}, {"entity_id": "mechanics", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "mechanics", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["mechanics"], "contexts": ["branch of physics dealing with motion", "science applied to national prosperity"]}, {"entity_id": "mechanics", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "mechanics", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 10, "variants": ["mechanics"], "contexts": ["laws governing motion and forces", "study of motion and forces"]}, {"entity_id": "mechanics", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "mechanics", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["mechanics"], "contexts": ["study of scales and lever principles", "study of motion and forces"]}, {"entity_id": "mechanical", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "mechanical", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["mechanical", "Mechanical"], "contexts": ["branch of physics", "relating to forces and motion"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Mechanical Science", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "mechanics", "similarity": 0.919}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Mechanical Science", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "mechanics", "similarity": 0.919}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Mechanical Science", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mechanics", "similarity": 0.919}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Mechanical", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mechanical", "similarity": 0.96}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "mechanics", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "mechanics", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "mechanics", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mechanics", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "mechanics", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mechanical", "similarity": 0.899}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "mechanics", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mechanics", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "mechanics", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mechanical", "similarity": 0.899}], "id": 480, "stable_key": "clu_8e5da36904d49678", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mechanics", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q41217", "wikidata_description": "science concerned with physical bodies subjected to forces or displacements", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mechanics", "wikipedia_extract": "Mechanics (from Ancient Greek  \u03bc\u03b7\u03c7\u03b1\u03bd\u03b9\u03ba\u03ae (m\u0113khanik\u1e17) 'of machines') is the area of physics concerned with the relationships between force, matter, and motion among physical objects. Forces applied to objects may result in displacements, which are changes of an object's position relative to its environment. Theoretical expositions of this branch of physics have their origins in Ancient Greece, for instance, in the writings of Aristotle and Archimedes (see History of classical mechanics and Timeline of classical mechanics). During the early modern period, scientists such as Galileo Galilei, Johannes Kepler, Christiaan Huygens, and Isaac Newton laid the foundation for what is now known as classical mechanics. In the 20th century the concepts of classical mechanics were challenged by new discoveries, leading to fundamentally new approaches including relativistic mechanics and quantum mechanics. The ancient Greek philosophers were among the first to propose that abstract principles govern nature."}}, {"canonical_name": "anatomie", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 21, "members": [{"entity_id": "anatomie", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "anatomie", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 13, "variants": ["anatomie"], "contexts": ["study of the structure of living things", "field hindered by prejudice"]}, {"entity_id": "anatomique", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "anatomique", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["anatomique"], "contexts": ["viewpoint in biology concerning structure", "anatomical viewpoint"]}, {"entity_id": "anatomie", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "anatomie", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["anatomie"], "contexts": ["field of study for skull engravings"]}, {"entity_id": "anatomia", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "anatomia", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["anatomia"], "contexts": ["study of the body's structure"]}, {"entity_id": "anatomias", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "anatomias", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 4, "variants": ["anatomias", "Anatomicas"], "contexts": ["dissections performed", "dissections of bodies for study"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "anatomie", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "anatomie", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "anatomie", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "anatomia", "similarity": 0.996}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "anatomie", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "anatomias", "similarity": 0.979}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "anatomique", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "anatomie", "similarity": 0.979}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "anatomique", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "anatomia", "similarity": 0.979}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "anatomique", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "anatomias", "similarity": 0.958}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "anatomie", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "anatomia", "similarity": 0.996}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "anatomie", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "anatomias", "similarity": 0.979}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "anatomia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "anatomias", "similarity": 0.977}], "id": 481, "stable_key": "clu_4b12b43c7b1388e3-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Anatomy", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q514", "wikidata_description": "study of the internal structure of organisms and their parts", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anatomy", "wikipedia_extract": "Anatomy (from Ancient Greek  \u1f00\u03bd\u03b1\u03c4\u03bf\u03bc\u03ae (anatom\u1e17) 'dissection') is the branch of morphology concerned with the study of the internal and external structure of organisms and their parts. Anatomy is a branch of natural science that deals with the structural organization of living things. It is an old science, having its beginnings in prehistoric times. Anatomy is inherently tied to developmental biology, embryology, comparative anatomy, evolutionary biology, and phylogeny, as these are the processes by which anatomy is generated, both over immediate and long-term timescales. Anatomy and physiology, which study the structure and function of organisms and their parts respectively, make a natural pair of related disciplines, and are often studied together. Human anatomy is one of the essential basic sciences that are applied in medicine, and is often studied alongside physiology."}}, {"canonical_name": "Platinum", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 21, "members": [{"entity_id": "platine", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "platine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["platine"], "contexts": ["metal influencing chemical reactions", "metal used as a catalyst"]}, {"entity_id": "platine", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "platine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 7, "variants": ["platine", "Platine"], "contexts": ["found in abundance in Choco and Barbacoas", "metal found in Clioco and Barbacoas"]}, {"entity_id": "platinum", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "platinum", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["platinum"], "contexts": ["precious metal", "metal with high density"]}, {"entity_id": "platinum", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Platinum", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 10, "variants": ["platinum", "Platinum"], "contexts": ["metal used in chemical experiments", "precious metal element"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "platine", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "platine", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "platine", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "platinum", "similarity": 0.985}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "platine", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Platinum", "similarity": 0.929}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "platine", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "platinum", "similarity": 0.985}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "platine", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Platinum", "similarity": 0.929}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "platinum", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Platinum", "similarity": 0.946}], "id": 482, "stable_key": "clu_29bc88ddbd489182", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": null, "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "While 'Plata' can mean silver, the context of 'platine' and 'Platina' strongly indicates platinum, a distinct precious metal.", "wikidata_id": "Q880", "wikidata_description": "chemical element with symbol Pt and atomic number 78", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Platinum", "wikipedia_extract": "Platinum is a chemical element; it has symbol Pt and atomic number 78. It is a dense, malleable, ductile, highly unreactive, precious, silverish-white transition metal. Its name originates from Spanish platina, a diminutive of plata \"silver\". Platinum is a member of the platinum group of elements and group 10 of the periodic table of elements. It has six naturally occurring isotopes. It is one of the rarer elements in Earth's crust, with an average abundance of approximately 5 \u03bcg/kg."}}, {"canonical_name": "stars", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 21, "members": [{"entity_id": "\u00e9toiles", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "\u00e9toiles", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["\u00e9toiles"], "contexts": ["celestial bodies placed in the sky", "celestial bodies, fixed and planets"]}, {"entity_id": "groups_of_stars", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "groups of stars", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["groups of stars"], "contexts": ["application of 'cosmos'"]}, {"entity_id": "stars", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "stars", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 15, "variants": ["stars", "Stars"], "contexts": ["celestial bodies observed in the sky", "distant celestial bodies"]}, {"entity_id": "stars", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "stars", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["stars"], "contexts": ["celestial bodies", "celestial bodies, incandescent matter"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "\u00e9toiles", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "stars", "similarity": 0.953}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "\u00e9toiles", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "stars", "similarity": 0.953}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "groups of stars", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "stars", "similarity": 0.928}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "groups of stars", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "stars", "similarity": 0.928}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "stars", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "stars", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 483, "stable_key": "clu_6cab5530686afac8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Stars", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Universe", "note": "Refers to celestial bodies observed in the sky.", "wikidata_id": "Q37529922", "wikidata_description": "family name"}}, {"canonical_name": "eclipses", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 21, "members": [{"entity_id": "\u00e9clipses", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "\u00e9clipses", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["\u00e9clipses"], "contexts": ["celestial event obscuring a body", "astronomical events of obscuration"]}, {"entity_id": "eclipses_of_the_sun", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "eclipses of the Sun", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["eclipses of the Sun"], "contexts": ["celestial event blocking sunlight"]}, {"entity_id": "eclipses", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "eclipses", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["eclipses", "Eclipsis"], "contexts": ["astronomical events", "celestial events for measurement"]}, {"entity_id": "eclipses", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "eclipses", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 11, "variants": ["Eclipses", "eclipses"], "contexts": ["obscuring of celestial bodies", "astronomical events"]}, {"entity_id": "solar_eclipses", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "solar eclipses", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Solar Eclipses", "solar eclipses"], "contexts": ["Moon's shadow on the Earth", "shadowing of Sun by Moon"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "\u00e9clipses", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "eclipses of the Sun", "similarity": 0