he world's largest sponsored collection of Open Source projects and developers. The Foundation focuses on key services such as intellectual property (IP) management, ecosystem development, and IT infrastructure. The Eclipse Project was originally created by IBM in November 2001 and was supported by a consortium of software vendors. In 2004, the Eclipse Foundation was founded to lead and develop the Eclipse community. It was created to allow a vendor-neutral, open, and transparent community to be established around Eclipse."}}, {"canonical_name": "c\u00e1ssia", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 21, "members": [{"entity_id": "cassia", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Cassia", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cassia"], "contexts": ["dissolved with Rubarb powder"]}, {"entity_id": "cassia", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Cassia", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Cassia"], "contexts": ["plant that likes dry, open places", "species with fragrant leaves"]}, {"entity_id": "c\u00e1ssia", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "c\u00e1ssia", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 11, "variants": ["c\u00e1ssia", "C\u00e1ssia"], "contexts": ["name applied to bark and trunks of Cinnamomum", "genus of plants related to Cassia fistula"]}, {"entity_id": "cassia", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Cassia", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 7, "variants": ["Cassia", "Casia", "calsia", "cassia"], "contexts": ["spice related to cinnamon", "a plant, likely Cassia"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Cassia", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Cassia", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Cassia", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "c\u00e1ssia", "similarity": 0.731}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Cassia", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Cassia", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Cassia", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "c\u00e1ssia", "similarity": 0.731}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Cassia", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Cassia", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "c\u00e1ssia", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Cassia", "similarity": 0.731}], "id": 485, "stable_key": "clu_8eeac31f00fa8de6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cassia", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Cinnamomum aromaticum Nees", "family": "Lauraceae", "note": "The term 'casia' in early modern Portuguese and Spanish medical texts often referred to the bark of the cinnamon tree, specifically the species now known as Cinnamomum aromaticum or Cinnamomum verum. The context of bark and trunks confirms this identification.", "wikidata_id": "Q162882", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_extract": "Cassia, Kezia or Keziah, a female surname of Biblical origin the female gentilicium name of the Cassia gens in ancient Rome Cassia or Kassia, Byzantine abbess and music scholar C\u00e1ssia Eller (1962\u20132001), Brazilian musician C\u00e1ssia Kiss (born 1958), Brazilian actress Cassi\u00e0 Maria Just (1926\u20132008), Catalan abbot Cassia O'Reilly (born 1996), Irish singer-songwriter Cassia, a fictional playable character in Heroes of the Storm Cassia Maria Reyes, the protagonist of the Matched trilogy by Ally Condie", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cassia_%28genus%29"}}, {"canonical_name": "Atlantic", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 21, "members": [{"entity_id": "atlantic", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Atlantic", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Atlantic"], "contexts": ["ocean region with currents", "birds annually cross"]}, {"entity_id": "atlantic", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Atlantic", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 7, "variants": ["Atlantic"], "contexts": ["ocean", "ocean with no level difference"]}, {"entity_id": "atlantic", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Atlantic", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 11, "variants": ["Atlantic"], "contexts": ["ocean with raised tide", "ocean with a deep trough influencing tidal wave"]}, {"entity_id": "atlantic", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Atlantic", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Atlantic"], "contexts": ["ocean affected by subsidence"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Atlantic", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Atlantic", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Atlantic", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Atlantic", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Atlantic", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Atlantic", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Atlantic", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Atlantic", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Atlantic", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Atlantic", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Atlantic", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Atlantic", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 486, "stable_key": "clu_aee9c0b900591188", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Atlantic Ocean", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Multiple", "wikidata_id": "Q97", "wikidata_description": "ocean between Europe, Africa and the Americas", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantic%20Ocean", "wikipedia_extract": "The Atlantic Ocean is the second largest of the world's five oceanic divisions, with an area of about 85,133,000 km2 (32,870,000 sq mi). It covers approximately 17% of Earth's surface and about 24% of its water surface area. During the Age of Discovery, it was known for separating the New World of the Americas (North America and South America) from the Old World of Afro-Eurasia (Africa, Asia, and Europe). Through its separation of Afro-Eurasia from the Americas, the Atlantic Ocean has played a central role in the development of human society, globalization, and the histories of many nations. While the Norse were the first known humans to cross the Atlantic, it was the expedition of Christopher Columbus in 1492 that proved to be the most consequential. Columbus's expedition ushered in an age of exploration and colonization of the Americas by European powers, most notably Portugal, Spain, France, and the United Kingdom."}}, {"canonical_name": "Baltic", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 21, "members": [{"entity_id": "baltic", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Baltic", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Baltic"], "contexts": ["sea with brackish waters"]}, {"entity_id": "baltique", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Baltique", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 7, "variants": ["Baltique"], "contexts": ["sea south of which blocks are found", "sea south of sandy plains"]}, {"entity_id": "baltic", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Baltic", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 10, "variants": ["Baltic"], "contexts": ["shores where earthquake was felt", "sea bordering Northern Europe"]}, {"entity_id": "baltic", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Baltic", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Baltic"], "contexts": ["sea with imperceptible tides", "a tideless sea"]}, {"entity_id": "baltic_sea", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Baltic Sea", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Baltic Sea"], "contexts": ["sea with lower atmospheric pressure than France"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Baltic", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Baltique", "similarity": 0.925}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Baltic", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Baltic", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Baltic", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Baltic", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Baltic", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Baltic Sea", "similarity": 0.965}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Baltique", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Baltic", "similarity": 0.925}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Baltique", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Baltic", "similarity": 0.925}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Baltique", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Baltic Sea", "similarity": 0.894}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Baltic", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Baltic", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Baltic", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Baltic Sea", "similarity": 0.965}], "id": 487, "stable_key": "clu_8c9036fd0f6650f4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Baltic Sea", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Multiple", "wikidata_id": "Q579938", "wikidata_description": "geographic region in Northern Europe", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baltic%20region", "wikipedia_extract": "The Baltic Sea Region, alternatively the Baltic Rim countries (or simply the Baltic Rim), and the Baltic Sea countries/states, refers to the general area surrounding the Baltic Sea, including parts of Northern, Central and Eastern Europe. Unlike the \"Baltic states\", the Baltic region includes all countries that border the sea. The first to name it the Baltic Sea (Latin: Mare Balticum) was 11th century German chronicler Adam of Bremen. Depending on the context the Baltic Sea Region might stand for: The countries that have shorelines along the Baltic Sea: Denmark, Estonia, Finland, Germany, Latvia, Lithuania, Poland, Russia, and Sweden. The group of countries that are members of the inter-governmental Baltic Assembly and Baltic Council of Ministers, and generally referred to by the shorthand, Baltic states:  Estonia, Latvia, and Lithuania. Estonia, Latvia, Lithuania and Kaliningrad Oblast of Russia, exclaved from the remainder of Russia."}}, {"canonical_name": "Mirra", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 21, "members": [{"entity_id": "mirra", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "Mirra", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "RESIN", "count": 2, "variants": ["Mirra", "mirra"], "contexts": ["resin used as a substitute", "ingredient in Indian oil preparation"]}, {"entity_id": "mirra", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Mirra", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 17, "variants": ["Mirra", "mirra"], "contexts": ["plant product used in medicine", "used in embalming with aloes"]}, {"entity_id": "mirra", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Mirra", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "RESIN", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mirra"], "contexts": ["myrrh resin used in preparations"]}, {"entity_id": "mirra", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "mirra", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["mirra"], "contexts": ["ingredient in plaster"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Mirra", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Mirra", "similarity": 0.653}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Mirra", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Mirra", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Mirra", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "mirra", "similarity": 0.612}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Mirra", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Mirra", "similarity": 0.653}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Mirra", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "mirra", "similarity": 0.939}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Mirra", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "mirra", "similarity": 0.612}], "id": 488, "stable_key": "clu_7c2be69bf5e0581f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Myrrh", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Commiphora myrrha", "family": "Burseraceae", "note": "Although Myrrh is a resin, it is derived from plants.", "wikidata_id": "Q1137175", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_extract": "A resin is a solid or highly viscous liquid that can be converted into a polymer. Resins may be biological or synthetic in origin, but are typically harvested from plants. Resins are mixtures of organic compounds insoluble in water, predominantly terpenes. Technically, resins should not be confused with gums, which consist predominantly of water-soluble polysaccharides, although these two terms are often interchangeable in the less formal context. Common resins include pine oleoresins, amber, hashish, frankincense, myrrh and the animal-derived resin, shellac. Resins are used in varnishes, adhesives, food additives, incenses and perfumes.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cicely", "description": "A fragrant resinous substance from certain trees, used medicinally."}}, {"canonical_name": "ma\u00efs", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 20, "members": [{"entity_id": "maize", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "maize", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["maize"], "contexts": ["plant studied for reproductive system differences", "crop growing on moraines"]}, {"entity_id": "maiz", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "maiz", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 5, "variants": ["maiz"], "contexts": ["grain used in a beverage", "grain used in beverages"]}, {"entity_id": "ma\u00efs", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "ma\u00efs", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 12, "variants": ["ma\u00efs"], "contexts": ["foodstuff", "crop sown in farms"]}, {"entity_id": "maize", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "maize", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["maize"], "contexts": ["agricultural staple crop"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "maize", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "maiz", "similarity": 0.989}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "maize", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "maize", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "maiz", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "ma\u00efs", "similarity": 0.913}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "maiz", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "maize", "similarity": 0.989}], "id": 489, "stable_key": "clu_26c93a6bfe886fa6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Maize", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Zea mays Linnaeus", "family": "Poaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q11575", "wikidata_description": "species of grass cultivated as a food crop", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maize", "wikipedia_extract": "Maize (; Zea mays), also known as corn in North American English, is a tall stout grass that produces cereal grain. The leafy stalk of the plant gives rise to male inflorescences or tassels which produce pollen, and female inflorescences called ears. The ears yield grain, known as kernels or seeds. In modern commercial varieties, these are usually yellow or white; other varieties can be of many colors. Maize was domesticated by indigenous peoples in southern Mexico about 9,000 years ago from wild teosinte. Native Americans planted it alongside beans and squashes in the Three Sisters polyculture."}}, {"canonical_name": "cozimentos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 20, "members": [{"entity_id": "cozimiento", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "cozimiento", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 4, "variants": ["cozimiento"], "contexts": ["decoction, used for medicinal purposes", "decoction of a plant root"]}, {"entity_id": "cozimientos", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "cozimientos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["cozimientos"], "contexts": ["decoctions for enemas"]}, {"entity_id": "cozimento", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "cozimento", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 3, "variants": ["cozimento"], "contexts": ["Boiled mixture", "decoction of herbs"]}, {"entity_id": "cozimentos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "cozimentos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 12, "variants": ["cozimentos", "Cozimentos", "cozimento peytoral"], "contexts": ["decoctions", "digestive processes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "cozimiento", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "cozimientos", "similarity": 0.952}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "cozimiento", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "cozimento", "similarity": 0.894}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "cozimientos", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "cozimento", "similarity": 0.895}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "cozimientos", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cozimentos", "similarity": 0.919}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "cozimento", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cozimentos", "similarity": 0.973}], "id": 490, "stable_key": "clu_e85ea1c9459a9c5f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Decoctions", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "Preparations made by boiling plant material in water.", "wikipedia_extract": "Decoction is a method of extraction by boiling herbal or plant material (which may include stems, roots, bark and rhizomes) to dissolve the chemicals of the material. It is the most common preparation method in various herbal medicine systems. Decoction involves first drying the plant material; then mashing, slicing, or cutting the material to allow for maximum dissolution; and finally boiling in water to extract oils, volatile organic compounds and other various chemical substances. Occasionally, aqueous ethanol or glycerol may be used instead of water. Decoction can be used to make tisanes, tinctures and similar solutions. Decoctions and infusions may produce liquids with differing chemical properties, as the temperature or preparation difference may result in more oil-soluble chemicals in decoctions versus infusions."}}, {"canonical_name": "boticas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 20, "members": [{"entity_id": "botica", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "botica", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["botica"], "contexts": ["apothecary shop"]}, {"entity_id": "boticas", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Boticas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Boticas", "botica"], "contexts": ["places where medicines are compounded", "apothecary's shop"]}, {"entity_id": "boticas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "boticas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["boticas", "botyca"], "contexts": ["apothecaries' shops", "place where turbith is sold"]}, {"entity_id": "boticas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "boticas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 13, "variants": ["boticas", "botica"], "contexts": ["Pharmacies where medicines are sold", "pharmacies containing antidotes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "botica", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Boticas", "similarity": 0.912}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "botica", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "boticas", "similarity": 0.885}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "botica", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "boticas", "similarity": 0.885}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Boticas", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "boticas", "similarity": 0.985}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Boticas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "boticas", "similarity": 0.985}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "boticas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "boticas", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 491, "stable_key": "clu_42e4406e36d16aef", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pharmacy", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Establishment where medicines are prepared and sold.", "note": "'boticas' translates to pharmacies or apothecaries' shops.", "wikipedia_extract": "Pharmacy is the science and practice of discovering, producing, preparing, dispensing, reviewing and monitoring medications, aiming to ensure the safe, effective, and affordable use of medicines. It is a miscellaneous science as it links health sciences with pharmaceutical sciences and natural sciences. The professional practice is becoming more clinically oriented as most of the drugs are now manufactured by pharmaceutical industries. Based on the setting, pharmacy practice is either classified as community or institutional pharmacy. Providing direct patient care in the community of institutional pharmacies is considered clinical pharmacy. The scope of pharmacy practice includes more traditional roles such as compounding and dispensing of medications."}}, {"canonical_name": "Orinoco", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 20, "members": [{"entity_id": "orinoco", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Orinoco", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Orinoco", "Oritwco"], "contexts": ["river in South America", "river"]}, {"entity_id": "alto_orinoco", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Alto Orinoco", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Alto Orinoco", "Alto-Orinoco"], "contexts": ["upper part of the Orinoco River", "region of the Orinoco"]}, {"entity_id": "orinoco", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Orinoco", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 12, "variants": ["Orinoco"], "contexts": ["major river in South America", "woody district where natives use specific terms"]}, {"entity_id": "orinoco", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Orinoco", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Orinoco"], "contexts": ["river in South America", "river with higher part"]}, {"entity_id": "orinoco", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Orinoco", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Orinoco"], "contexts": ["river with cataracts"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Orinoco", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Orinoco", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Orinoco", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Orinoco", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Orinoco", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Orinoco", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Alto Orinoco", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Orinoco", "similarity": 0.919}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Alto Orinoco", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Orinoco", "similarity": 0.919}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Alto Orinoco", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Orinoco", "similarity": 0.919}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Orinoco", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Orinoco", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Orinoco", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Orinoco", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Orinoco", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Orinoco", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 492, "stable_key": "clu_899b6531614420cd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Orinoco River", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Venezuela, Colombia", "wikidata_id": "Q131792", "wikidata_description": "river in South America", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orinoco", "wikipedia_extract": "The Orinoco (Spanish pronunciation: [o\u027ei\u02c8noko]) is one of the longest rivers in South America at 2,140 km (1,330 mi). Its drainage basin, sometimes known as the Orinoquia, covers approximately 1,000,000 km2 (390,000 sq mi), with 65% of it in Venezuela and 35% in Colombia. It is the fourth largest river in the world by discharge volume of water (39,000 m3/s (1,400,000 cu ft/s) at delta) due to the high precipitation throughout its catchment area (2,300 millimetres per are [0.084 in/sq ft]). The Orinoco River and its tributaries are the major transportation system for eastern and interior Venezuela and the Llanos of Colombia. The environment and wildlife in the Orinoco's basin are extremely diverse. The river's name is derived from the Warao term for \"a place to paddle\", derived from the terms g\u00fciri (paddle) and noko (place) i.e."}}, {"canonical_name": "jar", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "VESSEL", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 20, "members": [{"entity_id": "vase", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "vase", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "VESSEL", "count": 1, "variants": ["vase"], "contexts": ["container for collecting water"]}, {"entity_id": "jar", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "jar", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "VESSEL", "count": 1, "variants": ["jar"], "contexts": ["container with a wide mouth"]}, {"entity_id": "jar", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Jar", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "VESSEL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Jar"], "contexts": ["container"]}, {"entity_id": "jar", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "jar", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "VESSEL", "count": 17, "variants": ["jar", "Jar"], "contexts": ["a container for substances", "container for substances"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "vase", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "jar", "similarity": 0.888}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "vase", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "jar", "similarity": 0.888}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "jar", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Jar", "similarity": 0.976}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "jar", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "jar", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Jar", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "jar", "similarity": 0.976}], "id": 493, "stable_key": "clu_9b3c3cfea8f48f49", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jar", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "'Urna boccia' and 'urnas' both refer to containers. 'Urna' implies an urn or jar, and 'boccia' can mean bottle or pot. The context 'container for preparations | containers for ashes' reinforces this, suggesting vessels used for storing or preparing substances.", "wikidata_id": "Q999961", "wikidata_description": "town in N\u00e1chod District, Hradec Kr\u00e1lov\u00e9 Region, Czech Republic", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jarom%C4%9B%C5%99", "wikipedia_extract": "Jarom\u011b\u0159 (Czech pronunciation: [\u02c8jarom\u0272\u025br\u031d\u030a]; German: Jermer) is a town in N\u00e1chod District in the Hradec Kr\u00e1lov\u00e9 Region of the Czech Republic. It has about 12,000 inhabitants. It is known for the Josefov Fortress. Josefov is well preserved and is protected as an urban monument reservation, the historic town centre of Jarom\u011b\u0159 is procected as an urban monument zone. Jarom\u011b\u0159 consists of nine municipal parts (in brackets population according to the 2021 census): Jarom\u011b\u0159 is located about 18 kilometres (11 mi) west of N\u00e1chod and 16 km (10 mi) northeast of Hradec Kr\u00e1lov\u00e9. It lies mostly in a flat agricultural landscape of the East Elbe Table."}}, {"canonical_name": "Philosophy", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 20, "members": [{"entity_id": "philosophy", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Philosophy", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 9, "variants": ["Philosophy"], "contexts": ["study of fundamental questions", "study of fundamental nature of knowledge"]}, {"entity_id": "philosophy", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Philosophy", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 9, "variants": ["Philosophy", "philosophy"], "contexts": ["study of fundamental questions about existence", "study of fundamental questions"]}, {"entity_id": "philosophy", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "philosophy", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["philosophy"], "contexts": ["study of the fundamental nature of knowledge, reality, and existence"]}, {"entity_id": "philosophy", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "philosophy", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["philosophy"], "contexts": ["study of knowledge and existence"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Philosophy", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Philosophy", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "philosophy", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "philosophy", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 494, "stable_key": "clu_a29efb545be80340", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Philosophy", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q5891", "wikidata_description": "study of the truths and principles of being, schools of thought, knowledge, or conduct", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philosophy", "note": "Used in the context of academic disciplines and the study of fundamental questions.", "wikipedia_extract": "Philosophy (from Ancient Greek philosoph\u00eda lit. 'love of wisdom') is a systematic study of general and fundamental questions concerning topics like existence, knowledge, mind, reason, language, and value. It is a rational and critical inquiry that reflects on its methods and assumptions. Historically, many of the individual sciences, such as physics and psychology, formed part of philosophy. However, they are considered separate academic disciplines in the modern sense of the term. Influential traditions in the history of philosophy include Western, Arabic\u2013Persian, Indian, and Chinese philosophy."}}, {"canonical_name": "philosophers", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 19, "members": [{"entity_id": "philosophes", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "philosophes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 7, "variants": ["philosophes"], "contexts": ["thinkers who study fundamental questions", "thinkers who distinguish reasoning forms"]}, {"entity_id": "philosophers", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Philosophers", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Philosophers"], "contexts": ["sensible philosophers conceive of generation"]}, {"entity_id": "philosophers", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "philosophers", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 10, "variants": ["philosophers"], "contexts": ["thinkers and scholars", "thinkers who proposed elementary facts"]}, {"entity_id": "philosophers", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "philosophers", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["philosophers"], "contexts": ["thinkers who study fundamental questions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "philosophes", "target_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "target_name": "Philosophers", "similarity": 0.968}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "philosophes", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "philosophers", "similarity": 0.991}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "philosophes", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "philosophers", "similarity": 0.991}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Philosophers", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": 