et_name": "vision", "similarity": 0.976}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "vis\u00e3o", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "vision", "similarity": 0.976}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "vision", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "vision", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 524, "stable_key": "clu_f483f1c0235035bb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Vision", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q162668", "wikidata_description": "ability to interpret the surrounding environment using light in the visible spectrum", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Visual%20perception", "wikipedia_extract": "Visual perception is the ability to detect light and use it to form an image of the surrounding environment. Photodetection without image formation is classified as light sensing. In most vertebrates, visual perception can be enabled by photopic vision (daytime vision) or scotopic vision (night vision), with most vertebrates having both. Visual perception detects light (photons) in the visible spectrum reflected by objects in the environment or emitted by light sources. The visible range of light is defined by what is readily perceptible to humans, though the visual perception of non-humans often extends beyond the visual spectrum. The resulting perception is also known as vision, sight, or eyesight (adjectives visual, optical, and ocular, respectively)."}}, {"canonical_name": "Authority", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 16, "members": [{"entity_id": "authority", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Authority", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 8, "variants": ["authority", "Authority"], "contexts": ["power or influence of established ideas", "reliance on established sources of knowledge"]}, {"entity_id": "authority", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Authority", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Authority"], "contexts": ["power or permission granted by an official"]}, {"entity_id": "autoridade", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "autoridade", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["autoridade", "authoridade", "authoridades"], "contexts": ["reliance on learned opinions", "authority"]}, {"entity_id": "authority", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "authority", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["authority"], "contexts": ["basis for receiving beliefs without verification", "power or right to give orders"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Authority", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Authority", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Authority", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "autoridade", "similarity": 0.903}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Authority", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "authority", "similarity": 0.953}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Authority", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "autoridade", "similarity": 0.903}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Authority", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "authority", "similarity": 0.953}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "autoridade", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "authority", "similarity": 0.946}], "id": 525, "stable_key": "clu_f59e0e924ffdb23d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Authority", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q174834", "wikidata_description": "legitimate power to decide or authorize", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Authority", "wikipedia_extract": "Authority is commonly understood as the legitimate power of a person or group over other people. In a civil state, authority may be practiced by legislative, executive, and judicial branches of government, each of which has authority and is an authority. The term \"authority\" has multiple nuances and distinctions within various academic fields ranging from sociology to political science. The term authority identifies the political legitimacy, which grants and justifies rulers' right to exercise the power of government; and the term power identifies the ability to accomplish an authorized goal, either by compliance or by obedience; hence, authority is the power to make decisions and the legitimacy to make such legal decisions and order their execution. Ancient understandings of authority trace back to Rome and draw later from Catholic (Thomistic) thought and other traditional understandings. In more modern terms, forms of authority include transitional authority (exhibited in, for example, Cambodia), public authority in the form of popular power, and, in more administrative terms, bureaucratic or managerial techniques."}}, {"canonical_name": "Abdomen", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 16, "members": [{"entity_id": "cavit\u00e9_abdominale", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "cavit\u00e9 abdominale", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["cavit\u00e9 abdominale"], "contexts": ["location of secretory organ"]}, {"entity_id": "abdomen", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "abdomen", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["abdomen"], "contexts": ["posterior part of insect body"]}, {"entity_id": "ventre", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "ventre", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["ventre"], "contexts": ["body part"]}, {"entity_id": "abdomen", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Abdomen", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 12, "variants": ["Abdomen", "abdomem", "abdomen"], "contexts": ["abdominal region", "body cavity connected to stomach"]}, {"entity_id": "abdominis", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "abdominis", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["abdominis"], "contexts": ["part of the body"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "cavit\u00e9 abdominale", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "abdomen", "similarity": 0.928}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "cavit\u00e9 abdominale", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Abdomen", "similarity": 0.922}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "cavit\u00e9 abdominale", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "abdominis", "similarity": 0.903}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "abdomen", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "ventre", "similarity": 0.926}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "abdomen", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Abdomen", "similarity": 0.996}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "abdomen", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "abdominis", "similarity": 0.96}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "ventre", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Abdomen", "similarity": 0.922}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "ventre", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "abdominis", "similarity": 0.916}], "id": 526, "stable_key": "clu_043f5ac8c3082676", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Abdomen", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "While 'pelvis' is mentioned, the dominant term and context clearly point to the general abdominal region as the referent.", "wikidata_id": "Q9597", "wikidata_description": "in humans, region of the body between the thorax at the top and the pelvis at the bottom; in insects, segment at the caudal end of the body, following on from the head and thorax", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abdomen", "wikipedia_extract": "An abdomen (also belly or stomach in vertebrates, or metasoma in arthropods)  is the front part of the torso between the thorax (chest) and pelvis in humans and in other vertebrates. The area occupied by the abdomen is called the abdominal cavity. In arthropods, it is the posterior tagma of the body; it follows the thorax or cephalothorax. In humans, the abdomen stretches from the thorax at the thoracic diaphragm to the pelvis at the pelvic brim. The pelvic brim stretches from the lumbosacral joint (the intervertebral disc between L5 and S1) to the pubic symphysis and is the edge of the pelvic inlet. The space above this inlet and under the thoracic diaphragm is termed the abdominal cavity."}}, {"canonical_name": "Reality", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 16, "members": [{"entity_id": "r\u00e9alit\u00e9", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "r\u00e9alit\u00e9", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["r\u00e9alit\u00e9"], "contexts": ["the state of things as they actually exist"]}, {"entity_id": "reality", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Reality", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 7, "variants": ["reality", "Reality"], "contexts": ["philosophical concept of existence", "the state of being real"]}, {"entity_id": "realities", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "realities", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["realities"], "contexts": ["objective existence", "things that exist"]}, {"entity_id": "reality", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "reality", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["reality"], "contexts": ["the state of things as they actually exist"]}, {"entity_id": "reality", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "reality", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["reality", "Reality"], "contexts": ["the state of things as they actually exist", "the true nature of existence"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "r\u00e9alit\u00e9", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Reality", "similarity": 0.973}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "r\u00e9alit\u00e9", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "realities", "similarity": 0.912}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "r\u00e9alit\u00e9", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "reality", "similarity": 0.976}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "r\u00e9alit\u00e9", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "reality", "similarity": 0.976}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Reality", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "reality", "similarity": 0.98}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Reality", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "reality", "similarity": 0.98}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "realities", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "reality", "similarity": 0.919}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "realities", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "reality", "similarity": 0.919}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "reality", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "reality", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 527, "stable_key": "clu_b0569ee7f305ce84", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "reality", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q667661", "wikidata_description": "literary movement", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Literary%20realism", "wikipedia_extract": "Literary realism is a movement and genre of literature that attempts to represent mundane and ordinary subject-matter in a faithful and straightforward way, avoiding grandiose or exotic subject-matter, exaggerated portrayals, and speculative elements such as supernatural events and alternative worlds. It encompasses both fiction (realistic fiction) and nonfiction writing. Literary realism is a subset of the broader realist art movement that began with mid-nineteenth-century French literature (Stendhal) and Russian literature (Alexander Pushkin). It  attempts to represent familiar things, including everyday activities and experiences, as they truly are. Broadly defined as \"the representation of reality\", realism in the arts is the attempt to represent subject matter truthfully, without artificiality and avoiding artistic conventions, as well as implausible, exotic and supernatural elements. Realism has been prevalent in the arts at many periods, and is in large part a matter of technique and training, and the avoidance of stylization."}}, {"canonical_name": "Pythagoras", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 16, "members": [{"entity_id": "pythagoras", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Pythagoras", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 9, "variants": ["Pythagoras"], "contexts": ["ancient philosopher with allegorical precepts", "philosopher whose doctrine was perverted"]}, {"entity_id": "pythagoras", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Pythagoras", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Pythagoras"], "contexts": ["ancient Greek philosopher", "first used 'Cosmos' for universe order"]}, {"entity_id": "pythagoras", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Pythagoras", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Pythagoras", "Pi-t\u00e1goras"], "contexts": ["ancient Greek philosopher", "ancient philosopher"]}, {"entity_id": "pitagora", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Pitagora", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Pitagora"], "contexts": ["philosopher, transmitted principles"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Pythagoras", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Pythagoras", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Pythagoras", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Pythagoras", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Pythagoras", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Pitagora", "similarity": 0.868}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Pythagoras", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Pythagoras", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Pythagoras", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Pitagora", "similarity": 0.868}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Pythagoras", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Pitagora", "similarity": 0.868}], "id": 528, "stable_key": "clu_59ac505d087e06b8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pythagoras", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": -570, "death_year": -495, "description": "Ancient Greek philosopher and mathematician", "wikidata_id": "Q10261", "wikidata_description": "Greek philosopher and mathematician (c. 570 \u2013 c. 495 BC)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pythagoras", "wikipedia_extract": "Pythagoras of Samos (Ancient Greek: \u03a0\u03c5\u03b8\u03b1\u03b3\u03cc\u03c1\u03b1\u03c2; c. 570 \u2013 c. 495 BC) was an ancient Ionian Greek philosopher, polymath, and the eponymous founder of Pythagoreanism. His political and religious teachings were well known in Magna Graecia and influenced the philosophies of Plato, Aristotle, and, through them, Western philosophy. Modern scholars disagree regarding Pythagoras's education and influences, but most agree that he travelled to Croton in southern Italy around 530 BC, where he founded a school in which initiates were allegedly sworn to secrecy and lived a communal, ascetic lifestyle. In antiquity, Pythagoras was credited with mathematical and scientific discoveries, such as the Pythagorean theorem, Pythagorean tuning, the five regular solids, the theory of proportions, the sphericity of the Earth, the identity of the morning and evening stars as the planet Venus, and the division of the globe into five climatic zones."}}, {"canonical_name": "spirits of Wine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 16, "members": [{"entity_id": "spirits_of_wine", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "spirits of Wine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 8, "variants": ["spirits of Wine", "spirits of wine"], "contexts": ["distilled alcohol from wine", "liquid in which ice does not long continue fixation"]}, {"entity_id": "esprit-de-vin", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "esprit-de-vin", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["esprit-de-vin", "l'esprit-de-vin"], "contexts": ["alcohol", "spirit of wine, likely alcohol"]}, {"entity_id": "espiritos_do_vinho", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "espiritos do vinho", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["espiritos do vinho"], "contexts": ["spirits of wine, rise and affect the head"]}, {"entity_id": "spirit_of_wine", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "spirit of wine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 5, "variants": ["spirit of wine"], "contexts": ["liquid used in capillary experiments", "used in experiment with saline solution"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "spirits of Wine", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "esprit-de-vin", "similarity": 0.896}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "spirits of Wine", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "espiritos do vinho", "similarity": 0.951}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "spirits of Wine", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "spirit of wine", "similarity": 0.966}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "esprit-de-vin", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "espiritos do vinho", "similarity": 0.933}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "esprit-de-vin", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "spirit of wine", "similarity": 0.935}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "espiritos do vinho", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "spirit of wine", "similarity": 0.943}], "id": 529, "stable_key": "clu_67b6f1fe94c5d738", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Alcohol", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "wikidata_id": "Q153", "wikidata_description": "chemical compound", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ethanol", "wikipedia_extract": "Ethanol (also called ethyl alcohol, grain alcohol, drinking alcohol, or simply alcohol) is an organic compound with the chemical formula CH3CH2OH. It is an alcohol, with its formula also written as C2H5OH, C2H6O or EtOH, where Et is the pseudoelement symbol for ethyl. Ethanol is a volatile, flammable, colorless liquid with a pungent taste. As a psychoactive depressant, it is the active ingredient in alcoholic beverages, and the second most consumed drug globally behind caffeine. Ethanol is naturally produced by the fermentation process of sugars by yeasts or via petrochemical processes such as ethylene hydration. Historically it was used as a general anesthetic, and has modern medical applications as an antiseptic, disinfectant, solvent for some medications, and antidote for methanol poisoning and ethylene glycol poisoning.", "note": "Aqua vitae (Latin for 'water of life') is an archaic term for distilled spirits, most commonly referring to ethanol."}}, {"canonical_name": "Judeus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 16, "members": [{"entity_id": "jews", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Jews", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 4, "variants": ["Jews"], "contexts": ["people who misapprehended Messias prophecies", "accused the Saviour to Pilate"]}, {"entity_id": "juifs", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Juifs", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Juifs", "juifs"], "contexts": ["Jews", "effigies burned in processions"]}, {"entity_id": "judeus", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Judeus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 8, "variants": ["Judeus", "Judeos", "judeos", "judeu", "judeus"], "contexts": ["people from Jerusalem", "Portuguese term for certain Gentile merchants"]}, {"entity_id": "jews", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Jews", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Jews"], "contexts": ["people with ancient seven-day calendars", "denominations of time in their calendars"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Jews", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Juifs", "similarity": 0.974}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Jews", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Judeus", "similarity": 0.947}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Jews", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Jews", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Juifs", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Judeus", "similarity": 0.935}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Juifs", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Jews", "similarity": 0.974}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Judeus", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Jews", "similarity": 0.947}], "id": 530, "stable_key": "clu_401b8ba4c1080063", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jewish people", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Religious and ethnic group often referenced in historical texts.", "wikidata_id": "Q7325", "wikidata_description": "ethnoreligious group and nation from the Levant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jews", "wikipedia_extract": "Jews (Hebrew: \u05d9\u05b0\u05d4\u05d5\u05bc\u05d3\u05b4\u05d9\u05dd\u200e, ISO 259-2: Yehudim, Israeli pronunciation: [jehu\u02c8dim]), or the Jewish people, are an ethnoreligious group and nation, originating from the Israelites of ancient Israel and Judah. They traditionally adhere to Judaism. Jewish ethnicity, religion, and community are highly interrelated, as Judaism is an ethnic religion, though many ethnic Jews do not practice it. Religious Jews regard converts to Judaism as members of the Jewish nation, pursuant to the long-standing conversion process. The Israelites emerged from the pre-existing Canaanite peoples to establish Israel and Judah in the Southern Levant during the Iron Age. Originally, Jews referred to the inhabitants of the kingdom of Judah and were distinguished from the gentiles and the Samaritans."}}, {"canonical_name": "Horses", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 16, "members": [{"entity_id": "horses", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Horses", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["Horses"], "contexts": ["equine animals", "quadrupedes with different leg joints"]}, {"entity_id": "horses", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Horses", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 10, "variants": ["Horses", "horses"], "contexts": ["domesticated animal", "animal subject to size restrictions"]}, {"entity_id": "horses", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "horses", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["horses"], "contexts": ["animal symbol", "domesticated equine animals"]}, {"entity_id": "horses", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "horses", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["horses"], "contexts": ["living objects in tapestries"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Horses", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Horses", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Horses", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "horses", "similarity": 0.987}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Horses", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "horses", "similarity": 0.987}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Horses", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "horses", "similarity": 0.987}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Horses", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "horses", "similarity": 0.987}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "horses", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "horses", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 531, "stable_key": "clu_d4797be5c7ad162b-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Horse", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "The phrase 'agua do mar' (sea water) in the context of 'animal listed with numbers' and 'water used for healing soldiers' is a misinterpretation or OCR error. The surrounding English texts and the specific mention of 'Mare' (a mare, or female horse) strongly indicate the referent is 'horse'. The 'animal listed with numbers' context might refer to breeds or classifications, and 'female horse used in a cross' is a direct biological reference.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "domesticated four-footed mammal from the equine family", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horse", "wikipedia_extract": "The horse (Equus ferus caballus) is a domesticated, one-toed, hoofed mammal. It belongs to the taxonomic family Equidae and is one of two extant subspecies of Equus ferus. The horse has evolved over the past 45 to 55 million years from a small multi-toed creature, Eohippus, into the large single-toed animal of today, originally in North America. Humans began domesticating horses around 4000 BCE in Central Asia, and their domestication is believed to have been widespread by 3000 BCE. Horses in the subspecies caballus are domesticated, although some domesticated populations live in the wild as feral horses. These feral populations are not true wild horses, which are horses that have never been domesticated.", "linnaean": "Equus caballus", "family": "Equidae", "description": "A domesticated mammal with hooves, widely used by humans."}}, {"canonical_name": "magn\u00e9sie", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 16, "members": [{"entity_id": "magnes", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "magnes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["magnes"], "contexts": ["Latin for loadstone", "expression in the word magnes"]}, {"entity_id": "magnesian_stone", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "magnesian stone", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["magnesian stone"], "contexts": ["mineral substance, possibly Loadstone"]}, {"entity_id": "magn\u00e9sie", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "magn\u00e9sie", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 9, "variants": ["magn\u00e9sie", "magan\u00e8se", "mangan\u00e8se"], "contexts": ["chemical element or compound", "magnesia, a base"]}, {"entity_id": "magnesia", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "magnesia", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["magnesia"], "contexts": ["component of basalt and meteoric stones", "chemical compound found in rocks"]}, {"entity_id": "magnesia", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "magnesia", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["magnesia"], "contexts": ["earth decomposed by electricity"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "magnes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "magnesia", "similarity": 0.95}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epid