", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["fertility"], "contexts": ["ability to produce abundant crops"]}, {"entity_id": "fecundidade", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "fecundidade", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["fecundidade"], "contexts": ["fertility"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Fertility", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "fecundity", "similarity": 0.915}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Fertility", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "fertility", "similarity": 0.908}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Fertility", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "fecundidade", "similarity": 0.911}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "fecundity", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "fertility", "similarity": 0.934}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "fecundity", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "fecundidade", "similarity": 0.995}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "fertility", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "fecundidade", "similarity": 0.944}], "id": 541, "stable_key": "clu_199c45fe9f48e871", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "fertility", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q351870", "wikidata_description": "medicine dealing with prevention, diagnosis and management of reproductive problems", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reproductive%20medicine", "wikipedia_extract": "Reproductive medicine is a branch of medicine concerning the male and female reproductive systems. It encompasses a variety of reproductive conditions, their prevention and assessment, as well as their subsequent treatment and prognosis. Reproductive medicine has allowed the development of artificial reproductive techniques (ARTs) which have allowed advances in overcoming human infertility, as well as being used in agriculture and in wildlife conservation. Some examples of ARTs include IVF, artificial insemination (AI) and embryo transfer, as well as genome resource banking. The study of reproductive medicine is thought to date back to Aristotle, where he came up with the \u201cHaematogenous Reproduction Theory\u201d. However, evidence-based reproductive medicine is traceable back to the 1970s."}}, {"canonical_name": "Alemanha", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 16, "members": [{"entity_id": "alemania", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "Alemania", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Alemania"], "contexts": ["country where chocolate is known"]}, {"entity_id": "alemania", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Alemania", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 6, "variants": ["Alemania", "Aleman\u00eda"], "contexts": ["country with medicinal baths", "European country"]}, {"entity_id": "alemanha", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Alemanha", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 7, "variants": ["Alemanha", "Allemanha"], "contexts": ["Germany", "Germany, a country"]}, {"entity_id": "alemanha", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Alemanha", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Alemanha"], "contexts": ["country where physicians used Stibium", "country mentioned with hair entanglement"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Alemania", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Alemania", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Alemania", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Alemanha", "similarity": 0.99}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Alemania", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Alemanha", "similarity": 0.99}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Alemania", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Alemanha", "similarity": 0.99}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Alemania", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Alemanha", "similarity": 0.99}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Alemanha", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Alemanha", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 542, "stable_key": "clu_b8b6e936d75a95ce-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Germany", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A European country, associated with philosophical movements and scientific debates.", "note": "Includes references to both the country and its nationality.", "wikipedia_extract": "Germany, officially the Federal Republic of Germany, is a country in Western and Central Europe. It lies between the Baltic Sea and the North Sea to the north with the Alps to the south. Its sixteen constituent states have a total population of over 82 million, making it the most populous member state of the European Union. Germany borders Denmark to the north; Poland and the Czech Republic to the east; Austria and Switzerland to the south; and France, Luxembourg, Belgium, and the Netherlands to the west. The nation's capital and most populous city is Berlin and its main financial centre is Frankfurt; the largest urban area is the Ruhr. Settlement in the territory of modern Germany began in the Lower Paleolithic, with various tribes inhabiting it from the Neolithic onward, chiefly the Celts, and Germanic tribes inhabiting the north."}}, {"canonical_name": "Rheumatismos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 16, "members": [{"entity_id": "reuma", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "reuma", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 1, "variants": ["reuma"], "contexts": ["rheumatism or fluid flow"]}, {"entity_id": "rheumatic", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "rheumatic", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 1, "variants": ["rheumatic"], "contexts": ["chronic condition causing joint pain"]}, {"entity_id": "rheumatism", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "rheumatism", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 1, "variants": ["rheumatism"], "contexts": ["inflammatory condition affecting joints"]}, {"entity_id": "rheumatismo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "rheumatismo", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 3, "variants": ["rheumatismo", "rheumatica"], "contexts": ["condition treated by negundo", "disease treated with seed oil"]}, {"entity_id": "rheumatismos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Rheumatismos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 10, "variants": ["Rheumatismos", "Reumatismo", "Reumatismos", "Reumati\u017fmos", "Rheumatismo", "reumati\u017fmos", "rheumatismos"], "contexts": ["rheumatism", "rheumatic conditions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "reuma", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "rheumatism", "similarity": 0.885}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "reuma", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Rheumatismos", "similarity": 0.882}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "rheumatic", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "rheumatismo", "similarity": 0.952}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "rheumatic", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Rheumatismos", "similarity": 0.937}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "rheumatism", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "rheumatismo", "similarity": 0.969}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "rheumatism", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Rheumatismos", "similarity": 0.965}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "rheumatismo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Rheumatismos", "similarity": 0.943}], "id": 543, "stable_key": "clu_21f31330dae8802f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Rheumatism", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Rheumatism", "wikidata_id": "Q684924", "wikidata_description": "human disease", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rheumatism", "wikipedia_extract": "Rheumatism  (from the Ancient Greek \u1fe5\u03b5\u1fe6\u03bc\u03b1, rhe\u00fbma) or rheumatic disorders are conditions causing chronic, often intermittent pain affecting the joints or connective tissue. Rheumatism does not designate any specific disorder, but covers at least 200 different conditions, including arthritis and  \"non-articular rheumatism\", also known as \"regional pain syndrome\" or \"soft tissue rheumatism\". There is a close overlap between the term soft tissue disorder and rheumatism. Sometimes the term \"soft tissue rheumatic disorders\" is used to describe these conditions. The term \"Rheumatic Diseases\" is used in MeSH to refer to connective tissue disorders. The branch of medicine devoted to the diagnosis and therapy of rheumatism is called rheumatology.", "description": "Inflammatory condition causing pain and stiffness in joints."}}, {"canonical_name": "Boston", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 16, "members": [{"entity_id": "boston", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Boston", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 9, "variants": ["Boston"], "contexts": ["city in Massachusetts, USA", "city for sea voyages"]}, {"entity_id": "boston", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Boston", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Boston"], "contexts": ["city where a volume was published", "city visited by the subject"]}, {"entity_id": "boston", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Boston", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Boston"], "contexts": ["location where meteors were observed", "city where book was published"]}, {"entity_id": "boston", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Boston", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Boston"], "contexts": ["city on isothermal line of 50\u00b0 Fahrenheit"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Boston", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Boston", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Boston", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Boston", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Boston", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Boston", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Boston", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Boston", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Boston", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Boston", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Boston", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Boston", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 544, "stable_key": "clu_ba8dacdbe096573b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Boston", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A major city in Massachusetts, USA, a significant port and publication center.", "wikipedia_extract": "Boston is the capital and most populous city of the U.S. state of Massachusetts. It serves as the cultural and financial center of New England, a region of the Northeastern United States. Boston has an area of 48.4 sq mi (125 km2) and a population of 675,647 as of the 2020 census, making it the third-most populous city in the Northeastern United States after New York City and Philadelphia. The larger Greater Boston metropolitan statistical area had a population of 4.9 million in 2023, making it the largest metropolitan area in New England and the eleventh-largest in the United States. Boston was founded on Shawmut Peninsula in 1630 by English Puritan settlers, who named the city after the market town of Boston, Lincolnshire in England. During the American Revolution and Revolutionary War, Boston was home to several seminal events, including the Boston Massacre (1770), the Boston Tea Party (1773), Paul Revere's midnight ride (1775), the Battle of Bunker Hill (1775), and the Siege of Boston (1775\u20131776)."}}, {"canonical_name": "molecules", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 16, "members": [{"entity_id": "molecules", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "molecules", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 5, "variants": ["molecules"], "contexts": ["tiny particles of matter", "basic unit of chemical matter"]}, {"entity_id": "molecules", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "molecules", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["molecules"], "contexts": ["particles acted upon by chemical affinity", "hypothetical construction of matter"]}, {"entity_id": "molecules", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "molecules", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 5, "variants": ["molecules"], "contexts": ["smallest unit of a substance", "constituent particles of bodies"]}, {"entity_id": "molecules", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "molecules", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["molecules"], "contexts": ["smallest unit of a substance", "infinitesimal vibrations of infinitesimal molecules"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "molecules", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "molecules", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "molecules", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "molecules", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "molecules", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "molecules", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "molecules", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "molecules", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "molecules", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "molecules", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "molecules", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "molecules", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 545, "stable_key": "clu_3c30126c22acdd43", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "molecules", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q11369", "wikidata_description": "smallest possibile amount of a chemical substance", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Molecule", "note": "The descriptions 'basic unit of matter' and 'ultimate portion of matter' are consistent with the modern understanding of a molecule.", "wikipedia_extract": "A molecule is a group of two or more atoms that are held together by attractive forces known as chemical bonds; depending on context, the term may or may not include ions that satisfy this criterion. In quantum physics, organic chemistry, and biochemistry, the distinction from ions is dropped and molecule is often used when referring to polyatomic ions. A molecule may be homonuclear, that is, it consists of atoms of one chemical element, e.g. two atoms in the oxygen molecule (O2); or it may be heteronuclear, a chemical compound composed of more than one element, e.g. water (two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom; H2O). In the kinetic theory of gases, the term molecule is often used for any gaseous particle regardless of its composition."}}, {"canonical_name": "Diaphragma", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 16, "members": [{"entity_id": "diaphragm", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "diaphragm", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 3, "variants": ["diaphragm"], "contexts": ["innervated bilaterally for breathing", "muscle for breathing"]}, {"entity_id": "diaphragm", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "diaphragm", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["diaphragm"], "contexts": ["anatomical structure"]}, {"entity_id": "diaphragma", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Diaphragma", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 11, "variants": ["Diaphragma", "Diafragma", "diafragma"], "contexts": ["diaphragm, affected by tumors", "muscle for breathing"]}, {"entity_id": "diaphragm", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "diaphragm", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["diaphragm"], "contexts": ["muscle separating chest and abdomen"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "diaphragm", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "diaphragm", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "diaphragm", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Diaphragma", "similarity": 0.991}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "diaphragm", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "diaphragm", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "diaphragm", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Diaphragma", "similarity": 0.991}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "diaphragm", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "diaphragm", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Diaphragma", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "diaphragm", "similarity": 0.991}], "id": 546, "stable_key": "clu_7b897f5ab5b84a31", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Diaphragm", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q2163245", "wikidata_description": "diaphragm disease characterized by the lack of development of all or part of the diaphragm, which results in an abnormal opening that allows the stomach and intestines to move into the chest cavity and crowd the heart and lungs", "wikipedia_url": "", "wikipedia_extract": ""}}, {"canonical_name": "Pacific", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 16, "members": [{"entity_id": "pacific", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Pacific", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 6, "variants": ["Pacific"], "contexts": ["ocean location", "ocean where density maxima were found"]}, {"entity_id": "pacifico", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Pacifico", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Pacifico"], "contexts": ["ocean crossed b