onites"], "contexts": ["group that went extinct suddenly", "extinct marine invertebrates"]}, {"entity_id": "ammonites", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "ammonites", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["ammonites", "Ammonites nodosus"], "contexts": ["fossil found in jurassic limestone", "quelques ammonites"]}, {"entity_id": "ammonites", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Ammonites", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["Ammonites"], "contexts": ["fossil cephalopods", "extinct marine mollusks"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "ammonites", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Ammonites", "similarity": 0.955}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "ammonites", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "ammonites", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "ammonites", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Ammonites", "similarity": 0.955}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Ammonites", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "ammonites", "similarity": 0.955}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Ammonites", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Ammonites", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "ammonites", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Ammonites", "similarity": 0.955}], "id": 566, "stable_key": "clu_8d32ba7f7b8c3878", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "ammonites", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "family": "Ammonitida", "description": "Extinct marine molluscs with coiled shells, found as fossils.", "note": "Ammonites are extinct cephalopods. While historically sometimes classified differently, they are now recognized as an extinct group of molluscs.", "wikipedia_extract": "Ammonoids are extinct, typically coiled-shelled cephalopods composing the subclass Ammonoidea. They are more closely related to living octopuses, squid, and cuttlefish (which compose the clade Coleoidea) than they are to nautiluses (family Nautilidae), which they resemble. The earliest ammonoids appeared during the Emsian stage of the Early Devonian, around 410-408 million years ago, with the last species vanishing during or soon after the Cretaceous\u2013Paleogene extinction event approximately 66 million years ago. They are often called ammonites, which is most frequently used for members of the order Ammonitida, the only remaining group of ammonoids from the Jurassic up until their extinction. Ammonoids exhibited considerable diversity over their evolutionary history, with over 10,000 species having been described. Ammonoids are excellent index fossils, and they have been frequently used to link rock layers in which a particular species or genus is found to specific geologic time periods."}}, {"canonical_name": "reproduction", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "reproduction", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "reproduction", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["reproduction"], "contexts": ["process of creating offspring", "biological process of creating offspring"]}, {"entity_id": "procreation", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Procreation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Procreation"], "contexts": ["natural process of reproduction"]}, {"entity_id": "reproduction", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Reproduction", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["reproduction", "Reproduction"], "contexts": ["biological process of creating offspring"]}, {"entity_id": "reproduction", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "reproduction", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 7, "variants": ["reproduction"], "contexts": ["ability to recall information", "process of creating new tissue"]}, {"entity_id": "reproductions", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "reproductions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["reproductions"], "contexts": ["copies of past sensations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "reproduction", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Procreation", "similarity": 0.91}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "reproduction", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Reproduction", "similarity": 0.955}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "reproduction", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "reproduction", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "reproduction", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "reproductions", "similarity": 0.876}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Procreation", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Reproduction", "similarity": 0.925}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Procreation", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "reproduction", "similarity": 0.91}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Reproduction", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "reproduction", "similarity": 0.955}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Reproduction", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "reproductions", "similarity": 0.821}], "id": 567, "stable_key": "clu_a7a05dc9ac32a2f7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "reproduction", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q11990", "wikidata_description": "production of new individuals that contain some portion of genetic material inherited from one or more parent organisms", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reproduction", "wikipedia_extract": "Reproduction (or procreation or breeding) is the biological process by which new individual organisms \u2013 \"offspring\" \u2013 are produced from their \"parent\" or parents. There are two forms of reproduction: asexual and sexual. In asexual reproduction, an organism can reproduce without the involvement of another organism. Asexual reproduction is not limited to single-celled organisms. The cloning of an organism is a form of asexual reproduction. By asexual reproduction, an organism creates a genetically similar or identical copy of itself."}}, {"canonical_name": "Back", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "back", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Back", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 10, "variants": ["Back", "Backbone", "Backs"], "contexts": ["body part anointed to relieve pain", "body part affected by wind"]}, {"entity_id": "espaldas", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "espaldas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["espaldas"], "contexts": ["back"]}, {"entity_id": "back", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "back", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["back"], "contexts": ["posterior part of the torso"]}, {"entity_id": "dorso", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "dorso", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["dorso"], "contexts": ["body part"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Back", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "espaldas", "similarity": 0.882}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Back", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "back", "similarity": 0.989}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Back", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "dorso", "similarity": 0.902}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "espaldas", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "back", "similarity": 0.895}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "espaldas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "dorso", "similarity": 0.9}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "back", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "dorso", "similarity": 0.909}], "id": 568, "stable_key": "clu_27bdd872881dd456", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Back", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The presence of 'dorso' (Latin for back) strongly indicates anatomical context."}}, {"canonical_name": "Scotland", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "scotland", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Scotland", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Scotland"], "contexts": ["location where the plant grows", "country where Winter-Green grows"]}, {"entity_id": "scotland", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Scotland", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Scotland"], "contexts": ["country with scored mountains from ice", "mountains with scored flanks and polished surfaces"]}, {"entity_id": "scotland", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Scotland", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Scotland"], "contexts": ["country with coal beds", "Northern hemisphere location"]}, {"entity_id": "scotland", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Scotland", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 6, "variants": ["Scotland"], "contexts": ["location of trigonometrical survey", "country around which wave goes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Scotland", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Scotland", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Scotland", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Scotland", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Scotland", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Scotland", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Scotland", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Scotland", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Scotland", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Scotland", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Scotland", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Scotland", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 569, "stable_key": "clu_3a6b1c1fdf6bb9b1", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Scotland is a country that is part of the United Kingdom. It contains nearly one-third of the United Kingdom's land area, consisting of the northern part of the island of Great Britain and more than 790 adjacent islands, principally in the archipelagos of the Hebrides and the Northern Isles. In 2022, the country's population was about 5.4 million. Its capital city is Edinburgh, whilst Glasgow is the largest city and the most populous of the cities of Scotland. To the south-east, Scotland has its only land border with England; otherwise it is surrounded by the Atlantic Ocean to the north and west, the North Sea to the north-east and east, and the Irish Sea to the south. The legislature, the Scottish Parliament, elects 129 members to represent 73 constituencies."}}, {"canonical_name": "montagnes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "mountain", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "mountain", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["mountain"], "contexts": ["large natural elevation of the earth's surface", "high-altitude terrain"]}, {"entity_id": "montagnes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "montagnes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 10, "variants": ["montagnes"], "contexts": ["elevated landforms", "natural elevations of land"]}, {"entity_id": "montagna", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Montagna", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Montagna"], "contexts": ["Mountainous region"]}, {"entity_id": "mountain_peaks", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "mountain peaks", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["mountain peaks"], "contexts": ["highest points of mountains"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "mountain", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "montagnes", "similarity": 0.911}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "mountain", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Montagna", "similarity": 0.894}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "mountain", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mountain peaks", "similarity": 0.903}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "montagnes", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Montagna", "similarity": 0.928}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "montagnes", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mountain peaks", "similarity": 0.893}], "id": 570, "stable_key": "clu_08c6e71274484b0e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": null, "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "note": "The cluster includes 'montagnes' (French for mountains), 'mountain', 'Montagna' (likely related to mountain), and 'mountain peaks'. The general concept of a mountain or mountainous region is clear.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mountain", "wikipedia_extract": "A mountain is an elevated portion of the surface of a planet, generally with steep sides that show significant exposed bedrock. Although definitions vary, a mountain may differ from a plateau in having a limited summit area, and is usually higher than a hill, typically rising at least several hundred metres (a few thousand feet) above the surrounding land. A few mountains are isolated summits, but most occur in mountain ranges. Mountains are formed through tectonic forces, erosion, or volcanism, which act on time scales of up to tens of millions of years. Once mountain building ceases, mountains are slowly leveled through the action of weathering, through slumping and other forms of mass wasting, as well as through erosion by rivers and glaciers. High elevations on mountains produce colder climates than at sea level at similar latitude."}}, {"canonical_name": "peras", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "pera", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "pera", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["pera"], "contexts": ["tree wood comparison for palo", "pear-shaped root of the plant"]}, {"entity_id": "peras", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "peras", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["peras"], "contexts": ["pears, producing flowers and fruit", "pears"]}, {"entity_id": "pera", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Pera", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Pera"], "contexts": ["region from which fruit was sent", "fruit that grows underground"]}, {"entity_id": "p\u00eara", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "p\u00eara", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["p\u00eara", "pera", "peras"], "contexts": ["fruit used to temper food with sourness", "fruit lacking in India"]}, {"entity_id": "peras", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "peras", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 4, "variants": ["peras", "pera"], "contexts": ["pears", "type of fruit, possibly pear"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "pera", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Pera", "similarity": 0.962}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "pera", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "p\u00eara", "similarity": 0.778}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "pera", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "peras", "similarity": 0.908}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "peras", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "peras", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Pera", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "p\u00eara", "similarity": 0.72}], "id": 571, "stable_key": "clu_1b9546da219d5934", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pear", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Pyrus communis", "family": "Rosaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q434", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pear", "note": "The specific variety of 'P\u00eara' is not identified.", "wikipedia_extract": "Pears are fruits produced and consumed around the world, growing on a tree and are harvested in late summer into mid-autumn. The pear tree and shrub are a species of genus Pyrus , in the family Rosaceae, bearing the pomaceous fruit of the same name. Several species of pears are valued for their edible fruit and juices, while others are cultivated as trees. The tree is medium-sized and native to coastal and mildly temperate regions of Europe, North Africa, and Asia. Pear wood is one of the preferred materials in the manufacture of high-quality woodwind instruments and furniture. About 3,000 known varieties of pears are grown worldwide, which vary in both shape and taste."}}, {"canonical_name": "cereijas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "cerezas", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "cerezas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["cerezas"], "contexts": ["fruit resembling cherries"]}, {"entity_id": "cerezas", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Cerezas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Cerezas", "Cerez"], "contexts": ["fruit of Charranfol\u00ed, cherry-shaped", "fruit recommended for cooling"]}, {"entity_id": "cerejas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "cerejas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["cerejas", "cereaes"], "contexts": ["fruit that rots and causes fevers", "fruit, likely cherries"]}, {"entity_id": "cereijas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "cereijas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 8, "variants": ["cereijas", "Cereijas", "cereijasnegras", "cerejas"], "contexts": ["fruit used to wrap medicines", "eaten with cold water, caused hiccups"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "cerezas", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Cerezas", "similarity": 0.827}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "cerezas", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "cerejas", "similarity": 0.885}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "cerejas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cereijas", "similarity": 0.911}], "id": 572, "stable_key": "clu_24a2723e9ceb2c9e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cherries", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Prunus avium", "family": "Rosaceae", "description": "Sweet, red fruit of the cherry tree, used in food and medicine.", "wikipedia_extract": "Prunus avium, commonly called wild cherry, sweet cherry or gean, is a species of cherry, a flowering plant in the rose family, Rosaceae. It is native to Eurasia and naturalized elsewhere. It is an ancestor of P. cerasus (sour cherry). All parts of the plant except for the ripe fruit are slightly toxic, containing cyanogenic glycosides. The species is often cultivated as an ornamental tree. Prunus avium is a deciduous tree growing to 5\u201325 metres (16\u201382 feet) tall, with a trunk up to 1.5 m (5 ft) in diameter."}}, {"canonical_name": "coco", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "coco-mecaxihuitl", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "coco-mecaxihuitl", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["coco-mecaxihuitl"], "contexts": ["alternative name for cocomecatl"]}, {"entity_id": "cocoa-nut", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "cocoa-nut", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["cocoa-nut"], "contexts": ["plant requiring hot climate"]}, {"entity_id": "coco", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "coco", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 11, "variants": ["coco", "Coco", "Coco de mer", "Cocos", "coco do mar"], "contexts": ["fruit of the coconut tree", "fruit of the palm tree"]}, {"entity_id": "cocom\u00e9", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "cocom\u00e9", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["cocom\u00e9"], "contexts": ["ingredient in decoction"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "coco-mecaxihuitl", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "coco", "similarity": 0.881}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "cocoa-nut", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "coco", "similarity": 0.902}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "coco", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "cocom\u00e9", "similarity": 0.919}], "id": 573, "stable_key": "clu_e0f565f1574f0bcd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Coconut", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "family": "Arecaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q13187", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_extract": "The coconut (Cocos nucifera) is a member of the palm family (Arecaceae) and the only living species of the genus Cocos. The term \"coconut\" (or the archaic \"cocoanut\") can denote the whole coconut palm tree or the large hard fruit. Originally native to Central Indo-Pacific, they are ubiquitous in coastal tropical regions. The coconut tree provides food, fuel, cosmetics, folk medicine and building materials. The inner flesh of the mature fruit forms a regular part of the diets of many people in the tropics and subtropics. Coconut endosperm contains a large quantity of a liquid, \"coconut water\".", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coconut", "linnaean": "Cocos nucifera Linnaeus"}}, {"canonical_name": "commerce", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "commerce", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "commerce", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 8, "variants": ["commerce", "Commercf", "commerce de la France avec les colonies de l'Am\u00e9rique", "commerce fran\u00e7ois", "commerce int\u00e9rieur", "commerce libre"], "contexts": ["economic activity", "trade"]}, {"entity_id": "commerce", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "commerce", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["commerce"], "contexts": ["exchange of goods and services"]}, {"entity_id": "commercio", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "commercio", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["commercio", "commercio oriental"], "contexts": ["trade", "trade and commerce"]}, {"entity_id": "commerce", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "commerce", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 2, "variants": ["commerce", "Commerce"], "contexts": ["development from simple to complex", "activity of buying and selling"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "commerce", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "commerce", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "commerce", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "commercio", "similarity": 0.953}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "commerce", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "commerce", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "commerce", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "commercio", "sim