", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "thunder clouds", "similarity": 0.891}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Clouds", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "clouds", "similarity": 0.72}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Clouds", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "cloud", "similarity": 0.955}], "id": 575, "stable_key": "clu_36dda7feff676a31", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Clouds", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The context 'atmospheric formations' and mentions in scientific texts strongly indicate meteorological clouds, not anatomical ones.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}}, {"canonical_name": "chimistes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "chimistes", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "chimistes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 7, "variants": ["chimistes"], "contexts": ["practitioners dealing with simple bodies", "practitioners of chemistry"]}, {"entity_id": "chymists", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Chymists", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 2, "variants": ["Chymists"], "contexts": ["Practitioners of alchemy and chemistry", "practitioners who used Crystal for the Stone"]}, {"entity_id": "chemists", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "chemists", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 1, "variants": ["chemists"], "contexts": ["scientists studying chemical composition"]}, {"entity_id": "chemists", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "chemists", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["chemists"], "contexts": ["scientists studying chemical combinations", "practitioners who study chemical substances"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "chimistes", "target_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "target_name": "Chymists", "similarity": 0.982}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "chimistes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "chemists", "similarity": 0.983}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Chymists", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "chemists", "similarity": 0.982}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Chymists", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "chemists", "similarity": 0.889}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "chemists", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "chemists", "similarity": 0.901}], "id": 576, "stable_key": "clu_ee4bf6d3daae08ab", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Chemists", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Practitioners of alchemy, chemistry, and chemical sciences.", "wikidata_id": "Q13107184", "wikidata_description": "health facility where medicines are sold and medical advice is given", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pharmacy%20%28shop%29", "wikipedia_extract": "A pharmacy (also called drugstore in North American English or community pharmacy or chemist in Commonwealth English and both in Australian English) is a premises which provides pharmaceutical drugs, among other products. At the pharmacy, a pharmacist oversees the fulfillment of medical prescriptions and is available to counsel patients about prescription and over-the-counter drugs or about health problems and wellness issues. A typical pharmacy would be in the commercial area of a community. In most countries, a premises for prescription drugs is subject to legislation; with requirements for storage conditions, staff qualifications, equipment, record keeping (especially of controlled drugs) and other matters, all specified in legislation. It was once the case that pharmacists stayed within the premises compounding/dispensing medications, but there has been an increasing trend towards the use of trained pharmacy technicians, with the pharmacist spending more time communicating with patients. Pharmacy technicians are now more dependent upon automation to assist them in their new role dealing with patients' prescriptions and patient safety issues."}}, {"canonical_name": "laboratoire", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "laboratoire", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "laboratoire", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["laboratoire"], "contexts": ["place for scientific experiments", "place for scientific experimentation"]}, {"entity_id": "laboratory", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "laboratory", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["laboratory"], "contexts": ["place for scientific experiments"]}, {"entity_id": "laboratories", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "laboratories", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["laboratories"], "contexts": ["places for scientific experimentation"]}, {"entity_id": "laboratories", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "laboratories", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["laboratories"], "contexts": ["places for scientific experiments"]}, {"entity_id": "laboratory", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "laboratory", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["laboratory"], "contexts": ["place for scientific experiments", "place for artificial production of phenomena"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "laboratoire", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "laboratory", "similarity": 0.959}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "laboratoire", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "laboratory", "similarity": 0.959}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "laboratory", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "laboratories", "similarity": 0.941}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "laboratory", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "laboratory", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "laboratories", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "laboratories", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "laboratories", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "laboratory", "similarity": 0.941}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "laboratories", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "laboratory", "similarity": 0.941}], "id": 577, "stable_key": "clu_db3a8ec8c7bb7828", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "laboratory", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q483242", "wikidata_description": "facility that provides controlled conditions in which scientific or technological research, experiments, and measurement may be performed", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laboratory", "wikipedia_extract": "A laboratory (UK: ; US: ; colloquially lab) is a facility that provides controlled conditions in which scientific or technological research, experiments, and measurement may be performed. Laboratories are found in a variety of settings such as schools, universities, privately owned research institutions, corporate research and testing facilities, government regulatory and forensic investigation centers,  physicians' offices, clinics, hospitals, regional and national referral centers, and even occasionally personal residences. The organisation and contents of laboratories are determined by the differing requirements of the specialists working within. A physics laboratory might contain a particle accelerator or vacuum chamber, while a metallurgy laboratory could have apparatus for casting or refining metals or for testing their strength. A chemist or biologist might use a wet laboratory, while a psychologist's laboratory might be a room with one-way mirrors and hidden cameras in which to observe behavior. In some laboratories, such as those commonly used by computer scientists, computers (sometimes supercomputers) are used for either simulations or the analysis of data."}}, {"canonical_name": "Goethe", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "goethe", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "Goethe", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 5, "variants": ["Goethe"], "contexts": ["quoted on experience correcting man", "author and scientist"]}, {"entity_id": "goethe", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Goethe", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Goethe"], "contexts": ["proposed law of compensation of growth", "on compensation of growth"]}, {"entity_id": "goethe", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Goethe", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 5, "variants": ["Goethe"], "contexts": ["author who advised on enterprise", "author, significant in books"]}, {"entity_id": "goethe", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Goethe", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Goethe"], "contexts": ["established truth of organismal development"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "Goethe", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Goethe", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "Goethe", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Goethe", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "Goethe", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Goethe", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Goethe", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Goethe", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Goethe", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Goethe", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Goethe", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Goethe", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 578, "stable_key": "clu_377298077e368be9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Johann Wolfgang von Goethe", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1749, "death_year": 1832, "description": "Poet, playwright, and natural philosopher who studied morphology.", "note": "Cited for his contributions to morphology and the concept of 'Urpflanze' (primordial plant), relating to organismal development.", "wikidata_id": "Q5879", "wikidata_description": "German writer, artist, natural scientist and politician (1749\u20131832)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johann%20Wolfgang%20von%20Goethe", "wikipedia_extract": "Johann Wolfgang von Goethe (28 August 1749 \u2013 22 March 1832) was a German polymath who is widely regarded as the most influential writer in the German language. His work has had a wide-ranging influence on literary, political, and philosophical thought in the Western world from the late 18th century to the present. A poet, playwright, novelist, scientist, statesman, theatre-director, and critic, Goethe wrote a wide range of works, including plays, poetry and aesthetic criticism, as well as treatises on botany, anatomy, and colour. Goethe took up residence in Weimar in 1775 following the success of his first novel, The Sorrows of Young Werther (1774), and joined a thriving intellectual and cultural environment under the patronage of Duchess Anna Amalia that formed the basis of Weimar Classicism. He was ennobled by Karl August, Duke of Saxe-Weimar, in 1782. Goethe was an early participant in the Sturm und Drang literary movement."}}, {"canonical_name": "Error", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "erreur", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "erreur", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["erreur"], "contexts": ["state of being wrong or incorrect", "a mistake in scientific observation or reasoning"]}, {"entity_id": "error", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Error", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["error", "Error"], "contexts": ["opposite of truth and infallibility", "false judgment of things, assent unto falsity"]}, {"entity_id": "error", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "error", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["error", "ERROR"], "contexts": ["mistake or deviation from correctness", "a mistake or inaccuracy"]}, {"entity_id": "error", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "error", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["error"], "contexts": ["absence of accurate correspondence leading to failure"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "erreur", "target_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "target_name": "Error", "similarity": 0.889}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "erreur", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "error", "similarity": 0.901}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "erreur", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "error", "similarity": 0.901}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Error", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "error", "similarity": 0.974}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Error", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "error", "similarity": 0.974}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "error", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "error", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 579, "stable_key": "clu_d3cd58b526ff0628", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Error", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q29485", "wikidata_description": "action or proposition which is inaccurate or incorrect, that could result in failure or deviation from the intended performance or behaviour", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Error", "wikipedia_extract": "An error (from the Latin err\u0101re, meaning 'to wander') is an inaccurate or incorrect action, thought, or judgement. In statistics, \"error\" refers to the difference between the value which has been computed and the correct value. An error could result in failure or in a deviation from the intended performance or behavior. One reference differentiates between \"error\" and \"mistake\" as follows: An 'error' is a deviation from accuracy or correctness. A 'mistake' is an error caused by a fault: the fault being misjudgment, carelessness, or forgetfulness. Now, say that I run a stop sign because I was in a hurry, and wasn't concentrating, and the police stop me, that is a mistake."}}, {"canonical_name": "physics", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "physics", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "physics", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["physics"], "contexts": ["laws governing phenomena"]}, {"entity_id": "physic", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "physic", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["physic"], "contexts": ["term applied to medicine"]}, {"entity_id": "physics", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "physics", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["physics"], "contexts": ["study of matter and energy", "field of science where conservation of force applies"]}, {"entity_id": "physics", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "physics", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 9, "variants": ["physics", "Physics"], "contexts": ["branch of science", "science regulating social life"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "physics", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "physic", "similarity": 0.969}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "physics", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "physics", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "physics", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "physics", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "physic", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "physics", "similarity": 0.969}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "physic", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "physics", "similarity": 0.969}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "physics", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "physics", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 580, "stable_key": "clu_4612f0da1650226c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Physics", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q413", "wikidata_description": "study of matter and its motion, along with related concepts such as energy and force", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Physics", "wikipedia_extract": "Physics is the scientific study of matter, its fundamental constituents, its motion and behavior through space and time, and the related entities of energy and force. It is one of the most fundamental scientific disciplines. A scientist who specializes in the field of physics is called a physicist. Physics is one of the oldest academic disciplines. Over much of the past two millennia, physics, chemistry, biology, and certain branches of mathematics were a part of natural philosophy, but during the Scientific Revolution in the 17th century, these natural sciences branched into separate research endeavors. Physics intersects with many interdisciplinary areas of research, such as biophysics and quantum chemistry, and the boundaries of physics are not rigidly defined."}}, {"canonical_name": "Inverno", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "hiver", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "hiver", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["hiver"], "contexts": ["season"]}, {"entity_id": "hiver", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "hiver", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["hiver"], "contexts": ["season of winter"]}, {"entity_id": "invierno", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Invierno", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["invierno", "Invierno"], "contexts": ["season for taking medicine", "cold season"]}, {"entity_id": "inverno", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Inverno", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 8, "variants": ["inverno", "Inverno"], "contexts": ["season of winter", "winter season"]}, {"entity_id": "invierno", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "invierno", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["invierno"], "contexts": ["season with little heat"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "hiver", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "hiver", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "hiver", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Inverno", "similarity": 0.955}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "hiver", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "invierno", "similarity": 0.962}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "hiver", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Inverno", "similarity": 0.955}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "hiver", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "invierno", "similarity": 0.962}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Invierno", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Inverno", "similarity": 0.964}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Invierno", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "invierno", "similarity": 0.932}], "id": 581, "stable_key": "clu_6832f9e0358510b9-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Winter", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q852660", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Withania%20somnifera", "wikipedia_extract": "Withania somnifera, known commonly as ashwagandha, is an evergreen shrub in the Solanaceae family that is native to the Middle East and North Africa, other African regions, southern Europe, Indian subcontinent, and across Southeast Asia. Several other species in the genus Withania are morphologically similar. Other common names include Indian ginseng and winter cherry. W. somnifera is a short shrub 35\u201375 cm (14\u201330 in) tall with tomentose branches, dull green elliptic leaves up to 10\u201312 cm (3.9\u20134.7 in) long, small green bell-shaped flowers, and orange-red ripe fruit. It is affected by various pests and diseases in India, which can damage plant health and reduce its secondary metabolite content."}}, {"canonical_name": "combustion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "combustion", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "combustion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["combustion"], "contexts": ["chemical process of burning"]}, {"entity_id": "combustion", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "combustion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["combustion"], "contexts": ["process of burning"]}, {"entity_id": "combustion", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "combustion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 10, "variants": ["combustion", "Combustion"], "contexts": ["process of burning", "rapid chemical reaction producing heat"]}, {"entity_id": "combustion", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "combustion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["combustion"], "contexts": ["rapid chemical reaction with heat and light"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "combustion", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "combustion", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "combustion", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "combustion", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "combustion", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "combustion", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "combustion", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "combustion", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "combustion", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "combustion", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "combustion", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "combustion", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 582, "stable_key": "clu_19bdf3faee73007f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Combustion", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q133235", "wikidata_description": "high-temperature exothermic redox chemical reaction between a fuel (the reductant) and an oxidant, usually atmospheric oxygen, that produces oxidized, often gaseous products, in a mixture termed as smoke", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Combustion", "wikipedia_extract": "Combustion, or burning, is a high-temperature exothermic redox chemical reaction between a fuel (the reductant) and an oxidant, usually atmospheric oxygen, that produces oxidized, often gaseous products, in a mixture termed as smoke. Combustion does not always result in fire, because a flame is only visible when substances undergoing combustion vaporize, but when it does, a flame is a characteristic indicator of the reaction. While activation energy must be supplied to initiate combustion (e.g., using a lit match to light a fire), the heat from a flame may provide enough energy to make the reaction self-sustaining. The study of combustion is known as combustion science. Combustion is often a complicated sequence of elementary radical reactions. Solid fuels, such as wood and coal, first undergo endothermic pyrolysis to produce gaseous fuels whose combustion then supplies the heat required to produce more of them."}}, {"canonical_name": "law", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "law", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Law", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Law"], "contexts": ["system of rules enforced by society", "religious or legal statutes"]}, {"entity_id": "law", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Law", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Law"], "contexts": ["contrasted with Gospel"]}, {"entity_id": "law", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "law", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 3, "variants": ["law"], "contexts": ["abstract concept in spiritualist philosophy", "relationship between sensation and stimulus"]}, {"entity_id": "law", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "law", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 7, "variants": ["Law", "law"], "contexts": ["constant course of procedure in nature", "constant course of procedure"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Law", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Law", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Law", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "law", "similarity": 0.956}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Law", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "law", "similarity": 0.956}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Law", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "law", "similarity": 0.956}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Law", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "law", "similarity": 0.956}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "law", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "law", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 583, "stable_key": "clu_d517562011a8ae32", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Law of nature", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The context 'constant course of procedure in nature' points to natural law, rather than legal statutes.", "wikidata_id": "Q29524", "wikidata_description": "system of law that is purportedly determined by nature, and is thus universal", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natural_law", "wikipedia_extract": "Rights of nature or Earth rights is a legal and jurisprudential theory that describes inherent rights as associated with ecosystems and species, similar to the concept of fundamental human rights. The rights of nature concept challenges twentieth-century laws as generally grounded in a flawed frame of nature as \"resource\" to be owned, used, and degraded. Proponents argue that laws grounded in rights of nature direct humanity to act appropriately and in a way consistent with modern, system-based science, which demonstrates that humans and the natural world are fundamentally interconnected. This school of thought is underpinned by two basic lines of reasoning. First, since the recognition of human rights is based in part on the philosophical belief that those rights emanate from humanity's own existence, logically, so too do inherent rights of the natural world arise from the natural world's own existence. A second and more pragmatic argument asserts that the survival of humans depends on healthy ecosystems, and so protection of nature's rights in turn, advances human rights and well-being."}}, {"canonical_name": "minerals", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "minerals", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Minerals", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Minerals"], "contexts": ["inorganic solid substances", "non-living materials"]}, {"entity_id": "min\u00e9raux", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "min\u00e9raux", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["min\u00e9raux"], "contexts": ["naturally occurring inorganic substances", "non-living materials found in mountains"]}, {"entity_id": "minerals", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "minerals", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 5, "variants": ["Minerals", "minerals"], "contexts": ["artificial imitation of substances", "materials composing the earth's crust"]}, {"entity_id": "minerals", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Minerals", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["Minerals", "minerals"], "contexts": ["semi-opaque materials that become transparent in water", "crystallized substances with optical properties"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Minerals", "target_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "target_name": "min\u00e9raux", "similarity": 0.978}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Minerals", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "minerals", "similarity": 0.987}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Minerals", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Minerals", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "min\u00e9raux", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Minerals", "similarity": 0.978}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "minerals", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Minerals", "similarity": 0.987}], "id": 584, "stable_key": "clu_684025f2f518eba0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Minerals", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "This cluster clearly refers to inorganic solid substances studied in natural philosophy and medicine, aligning directly with the modern scientific definition of minerals.", "wikidata_id": "Q7946", "wikidata_description": "naturally occurring usually inorganic substance that has a (more or less) definite chemical composition and a crystal structure", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mineral", "wikipedia_extract": "In geology and mineralogy, a mineral or mineral species is, broadly speaking, a solid substance with a fairly well-defined chemical composition and a specific crystal structure that occurs naturally in pure form. The geological definition of mineral normally excludes compounds that occur only in living organisms. However, some minerals are often biogenic (such as calcite) or chemically organic compounds (such as mellite). Moreover, living organisms often synthesize inorganic minerals (such as hydroxylapatite) that also occur in rocks. The concept of mineral is distinct from rock, which is any bulk solid geologic material that is relatively homogeneous at a large enough scale. A rock may consist of one type of mineral or may be an aggregate of two or more different types of minerals, spacially segregated into distinct phases."}}, {"canonical_name": "testiculos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "testicles", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "testicles", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["testicles", "Testicles"], "contexts": ["male reproductive organs"]}, {"entity_id": "testicles", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Testicles", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Testicles"], "contexts": ["comparison for seed shape", "male reproductive organs"]}, {"entity_id": "test\u00edculos", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "test\u00edculos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["test\u00edculos"], "contexts": ["body part"]}, {"entity_id": "testiculos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "testiculos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 5, "variants": ["testiculos", "testiculo"], "contexts": ["testicles", "body part of goat"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "testicles", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Testicles", "similarity": 0.952}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "testicles", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "test\u00edculos", "similarity": 0.967}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "testicles", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "testiculos", "similarity": 0.985}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Testicles", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "test\u00edculos", "similarity": 0.97}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Testicles", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "testiculos", "similarity": 0.934}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "test\u00edculos", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "testiculos", "similarity": 0.967}], "id": 585, "stable_key": "clu_1864f7b1a25ffeab", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "testicles", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "Male reproductive organs.", "wikipedia_extract": "A testicle, also called testis (pl. testes) is the male gonad in all gonochoric animals, including humans, and is homologous to the ovary, which is the female gonad. Its primary functions are the production of sperm and the secretion of androgens, primarily testosterone. The release of testosterone is regulated by luteinizing hormone (LH) from the anterior pituitary gland. Sperm production is controlled by follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) from the anterior pituitary gland and by testosterone produced within the gonads. Male humans have two testicles of similar size contained within the scrotum, which is an extension of the abdominal wall. Scrotal asymmetry, in which one testicle extends farther down into the scrotum than the other, is common."}}, {"canonical_name": "Beast", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "beast", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "beast", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["beast"], "contexts": ["animal recipient of poison", "animal, non-human"]}, {"entity_id": "beasts", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Beasts", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Beasts"], "contexts": ["animals with hollow mouths and vocal spirit"]}, {"entity_id": "beast", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Beast", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 7, "variants": ["Beast", "Beasts"], "contexts": ["contrasted with man by reason", "used to refer to animals in general"]}, {"entity_id": "beasts", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "beasts", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["beasts"], "contexts": ["animals, especially large ones", "large animals"]}, {"entity_id": "beasts", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "beasts", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["beasts"], "contexts": ["living objects in tapestries"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "beast", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Beast", "similarity": 0.994}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Beasts", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Beast", "similarity": 0.95}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Beasts", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "beasts", "similarity": 0.993}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Beasts", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "beasts", "similarity": 0.993}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "beasts", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "beasts", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 586, "stable_key": "clu_9f77ad4004c8b35d-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Animals", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q431", "wikidata_description": "scientific study of animals", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Animal", "note": "The term 'beast' in early modern texts is a general term for any non-human animal, often used in contrast to humans or for specific examples of fauna.", "wikipedia_extract": "Animals are multicellular, eukaryotic organisms belonging to the biological kingdom Animalia (). With few exceptions, animals consume organic material, breathe oxygen, have myocytes and are able to move, can reproduce sexually, and grow from a hollow sphere of cells, the blastula, during embryonic development. Animals form a clade, meaning that they arose from a single common ancestor. Over 1.5 million living animal species have been described, of which around 1.05 million are insects, over 85,000 are molluscs, and around 65,000 are vertebrates. It has been estimated there are as many as 7.77 million animal species on Earth. Animal body lengths range from 8.5 \u03bcm (0.00033 in) to 33.6 m (110 ft).", "description": "Living creatures, sources of bezoars, subject to washing."}}, {"canonical_name": "jaspe", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "jasper", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Jasper", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Jasper"], "contexts": ["darker stone"]}, {"entity_id": "jaspe", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "jaspe", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 5, "variants": ["jaspe"], "contexts": ["jasper stone", "jasper mineral"]}, {"entity_id": "jasper", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "jasper", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 5, "variants": ["Jasper", "jasper"], "contexts": ["rock formed by contact", "gemstone formed by volcanic action"]}, {"entity_id": "jaspe", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "jaspe", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["jaspe"], "contexts": ["gemstone", "green stone, possibly a large stone"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Jasper", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "jasper", "similarity": 0.983}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "jaspe", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "jasper", "similarity": 0.901}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "jaspe", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "jaspe", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "jasper", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "jaspe", "similarity": 0.901}], "id": 587, "stable_key": "clu_fe2727470b703c67", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jasper", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "Multiple references to 'jaspe'/'Jasper' in contexts of stones and geological formations confirm this identification.", "wikidata_id": "Q978937", "wikidata_description": "city in and county sear of Jasper, Texas, United States"}}, {"canonical_name": "Dieu", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "dieu", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "Dieu", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 7, "variants": ["Dieu"], "contexts": ["God", "divine creator of heaven and earth"]}, {"entity_id": "dios", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "Dios", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Dios"], "contexts": ["used in an expression of waking up"]}, {"entity_id": "dios", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Dios", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 4, "variants": ["Dios"], "contexts": ["divine intervention", "divine aid in healing"]}, {"entity_id": "dio", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Dio", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Dio"], "contexts": ["God"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Dieu", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "Dios", "similarity": 0.989}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Dieu", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Dios", "similarity": 0.989}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Dieu", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Dio", "similarity": 0.964}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Dios", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_m