tegory": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["affinities"], "contexts": ["tendency of substances to combine", "source of steam power"]}, {"entity_id": "affinities", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "affinities", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["affinities"], "contexts": ["tendency of substances to combine", "tendency of elements to combine"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Affinities", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "affinities", "similarity": 0.989}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Affinities", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "affinities", "similarity": 0.989}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Affinities", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "affinities", "similarity": 0.989}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "affinities", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "affinities", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "affinities", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "affinities", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "affinities", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "affinities", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 601, "stable_key": "clu_90524b26a7e712ae", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "affinity", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q382497", "wikidata_description": "automorphism of an affine space", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Affine%20transformation", "wikipedia_extract": "In Euclidean geometry, an affine transformation or affinity (from the Latin, affinis, \"connected with\") is a geometric transformation that preserves lines and parallelism, but not necessarily Euclidean distances and angles. More generally, an affine transformation is an automorphism of an affine space (Euclidean spaces are specific affine spaces), that is, a function which maps an affine space onto itself while preserving both the dimension of any affine subspaces (meaning that it sends points to points, lines to lines, planes to planes, and so on) and the ratios of the lengths of parallel line segments. Consequently, sets of parallel affine subspaces remain parallel after an affine transformation. An affine transformation does not necessarily preserve angles between lines or distances between points, though it does preserve ratios of distances between points lying on a straight line. If X is the point set of an affine space, then every affine transformation on X can be represented as the composition of a linear transformation on X and a translation of X. Unlike a purely linear transformation, an affine transformation need not preserve the origin of the affine space."}}, {"canonical_name": "dedo pulgar", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "dedo_pulgar", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "dedo pulgar", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 5, "variants": ["dedo pulgar"], "contexts": ["body part for size comparison", "size reference for root pieces"]}, {"entity_id": "dedo", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "dedo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["dedo"], "contexts": ["finger"]}, {"entity_id": "dedo", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "dedo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["dedo"], "contexts": ["finger, where rings are worn"]}, {"entity_id": "dedo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "dedo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["dedo"], "contexts": ["part of the body"]}, {"entity_id": "dedo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "dedo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 4, "variants": ["dedo", "dedo annular"], "contexts": ["used to stir water", "part of the hand"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "dedo pulgar", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "dedo", "similarity": 0.935}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "dedo pulgar", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "dedo", "similarity": 0.935}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "dedo pulgar", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "dedo", "similarity": 0.935}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "dedo", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "dedo", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "dedo", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "dedo", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "dedo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "dedo", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "dedo", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "dedo", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "dedo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "dedo", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "dedo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "dedo", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 602, "stable_key": "clu_a51a1857989dc47f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "finger", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "description": "Part of the hand, used for manipulation and ornamentation.", "wikipedia_extract": "A finger is a prominent digit on the forelimbs of most tetrapod vertebrate animals, especially those with prehensile extremities (i.e. hands) such as humans and other primates. Most tetrapods have five digits (pentadactyly), and short digits (i.e. significantly shorter than the metacarpal/metatarsals) are typically referred to as toes, while those that are notably elongated are called fingers. In humans, the fingers are flexibly articulated and opposable, serving as an important organ of tactile sensation and fine movements, which are crucial to the dexterity of the hands and the ability to grasp and manipulate objects. Usually humans have five digits, the bones of which are termed phalanges, on each hand, although some people have more or fewer than five due to congenital disorders such as polydactyly or oligodactyly, or accidental or intentional amputations. The first digit is the thumb, followed by the index finger, middle finger, ring finger, and little finger or pinkie. According to different definitions, the thumb can be called a finger, or not."}}, {"canonical_name": "Florence", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "florence", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Florence", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Florence"], "contexts": ["location of observation", "city in Italy"]}, {"entity_id": "florence", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Florence", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Florence"], "contexts": ["city in Italy"]}, {"entity_id": "florence", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Florence", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Florence"], "contexts": ["city with mild winter temperature", "city with mean winter temperature"]}, {"entity_id": "florence", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Florence", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 6, "variants": ["Florence"], "contexts": ["location of dedication", "city on isothermal line of 59\u00b0 Fahrenheit"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Florence", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Florence", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Florence", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Florence", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Florence", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Florence", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Florence", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Florence", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Florence", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Florence", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Florence", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Florence", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 603, "stable_key": "clu_69f623903ab7c674", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Florence", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A major city in Tuscany, Italy, a center of Renaissance art and commerce.", "note": "Refers to the city and its historical significance.", "wikipedia_extract": "Florence (Italian: Firenze) is the capital and most populous city of the Italian region of Tuscany, with 362,353 inhabitants as of 2025. It is also the capital of the eponymous metropolitan province, which counts 989,460 inhabitants. Florence was a centre of medieval European trade and finance and one of the wealthiest cities of that era. It is considered by many academics to have been the birthplace of the Renaissance, becoming a major artistic, cultural, commercial, political, economic and financial center. During this time, Florence rose to a position of enormous influence in Italy, Europe, and beyond. Its turbulent political history includes periods of rule by the powerful Medici family and numerous religious and republican revolutions."}}, {"canonical_name": "Boodt", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "bowne", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Bowne", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Bowne"], "contexts": ["philosopher of metaphysics", "Professor discussing inner construction of thought"]}, {"entity_id": "bode", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Bode", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Bode"], "contexts": ["astronomical yearbook editor", "astronomer associated with Jahrbuch"]}, {"entity_id": "boodt", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Boodt", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 6, "variants": ["Boodt", "A. Boetii de Boodt", "Boedo de Boodt", "Boetius de Boodt"], "contexts": ["author of Gemmarum et lapidarum historia", "attributed salutary effect to diamond"]}, {"entity_id": "bode", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Bode", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bode"], "contexts": ["law assumed in computing Neptune\u2019s position"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Bowne", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Bode", "similarity": 0.875}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Bowne", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Bode", "similarity": 0.875}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Bode", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Boodt", "similarity": 0.853}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Bode", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Bode", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Boodt", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Bode", "similarity": 0.853}], "id": 604, "stable_key": "clu_7341e207ad07f35e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Arnold Geulincx", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1624, "death_year": 1669, "description": "Flemish philosopher associated with Cartesianism and Spinozism.", "note": "The references to 'Boodt' in 'coloquios_da_orta_1563' likely refer to the mineralogist Anselmus Bo\u00ebtius de Boodt. However, the references to 'Bode' and 'Bowne' in other texts suggest a potential confusion with philosophers like Arnold Geulincx, who also discussed metaphysics. The specific spelling 'Boodt' in the context of a 1563 text points strongly to Bo\u00ebtius de Boodt. However, given the other references, there might be multiple distinct individuals or significant OCR variability. Given the prompt's emphasis on early modern texts across multiple languages, the 'Boodt' of 'coloquios_da_orta' is most likely Anselmus Bo\u00ebtius de Boodt. The others ('Bode', 'Bowne') are harder to place definitively without more context, but 'Bode' appearing with Humboldt and Somerville suggests a scientific or geographical association, while 'Bowne' with James might be philosopher Borden Bowne. The mention of 'author of Gemmarum et lapidarum historia' strongly points to Anselmus Bo\u00ebtius de Boodt. Therefore, this cluster appears to conflate at least two, possibly three, individuals. Focusing on the most concrete identifier within the cluster as presented, and acknowledging the ambiguity.", "wikidata_id": "Q316559", "wikidata_description": "Flemish Cartesian philosopher", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arnold%20Geulincx", "wikipedia_extract": "Arnold Geulincx (Dutch pronunciation: [\u02c8\u0251rn\u0254lt \u02c8x\u00f8\u02d0l\u026a\u014bks]; 31 January 1624 \u2013 November 1669), also known by his pseudonym Philaretus, was a Flemish philosopher, metaphysician, and logician. He was one of the followers of Ren\u00e9 Descartes who tried to work out more detailed versions of a generally Cartesian philosophy. Samuel Beckett cited Geulincx as a key influence and interlocutor because of Geulincx's emphasis on the powerlessness and ignorance of the human condition. Geulincx was born in Antwerp. He studied philosophy and theology at the University of Leuven and was made professor of philosophy there in 1646. He lost his post in 1658, possibly for religious reasons, or (as has been suggested) a combination of unpopular views and his marriage in that year."}}, {"canonical_name": "Plater.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "plateau", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Plateau", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Plateau"], "contexts": ["studied shades of gray", "determined shade of gray judgments"]}, {"entity_id": "plateario", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Plateario", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Plateario", "Platea"], "contexts": ["Platerius in the chapter on Iron says", "mentioned regarding iron slag"]}, {"entity_id": "plater_", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Plater.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 6, "variants": ["Plater."], "contexts": ["author of a text", "author of tomo 3."]}, {"entity_id": "plateau", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Plateau", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Plateau"], "contexts": ["performed experiments on accidental colours"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Plateau", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Plateario", "similarity": 0.92}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Plateau", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Plateau", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Plateario", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Plater.", "similarity": 0.883}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Plateario", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Plateau", "similarity": 0.92}], "id": 605, "stable_key": "clu_650cc6e87454a5a3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Plater", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1581, "death_year": 1650, "description": "Swiss physician and anatomist, son of Felix Platter.", "note": "The reference to 'Plater.' in 'polyanthea_medicinal' and 'Plateario' in 'historia_medicinal_monardes_1574' strongly suggests variations of the name Plater or Plat\u00e6rus, referring to the renowned physician Felix Plater or his son, Plater. The contexts 'studied shades of gray' and 'Platerius in the chapter on Iron' also align with medical and scientific writings. 'Plateau' in James's 'principles_of_psychology' is likely a misattribution or refers to a different, unrelated Plateau.", "wikidata_id": "Q14927887", "wikidata_description": "genus of insects", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plateros", "wikipedia_extract": "Plateros is a genus of net-winged beetles in the family Lycidae and the tribe Platerodini. Species are recorded from the Americas, Africa and Asia, including Sri Lanka, Indochina, Korea and Japan."}}, {"canonical_name": "aqueous vapor", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "vapor", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "vapor", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["vapor"], "contexts": ["gaseous state of matter"]}, {"entity_id": "aqueous_vapor", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "aqueous vapor", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 6, "variants": ["aqueous vapor"], "contexts": ["condensed by terrestrial magnetism", "volcanic emission"]}, {"entity_id": "vapor", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "vapor", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["vapor"], "contexts": ["gaseous water in atmosphere", "gaseous state of matter in space"]}, {"entity_id": "vapor", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "vapor", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["vapor", "vapores"], "contexts": ["Aristotle's theory of metal origin", "vapors"]}, {"entity_id": "vaporem", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "vaporem", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["vaporem"], "contexts": ["vapor"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "vapor", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "aqueous vapor", "similarity": 0.955}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "vapor", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "vapor", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "vapor", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "vapor", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "vapor", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "vaporem", "similarity": 0.966}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "aqueous vapor", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "vapor", "similarity": 0.955}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "vapor", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "vapor", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "vapor", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "vaporem", "similarity": 0.966}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "vapor", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "vaporem", "similarity": 0.966}], "id": 606, "stable_key": "clu_e770353762c0d79a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Steam", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "wikidata_id": "Q190120", "wikidata_description": "gaseous phase of water; unlike other forms of water, water vapor is invisible", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Water%20vapor", "wikipedia_extract": "Water vapor, water vapour, or aqueous vapor is the gaseous phase of water. It is one state of water within the hydrosphere. Water vapor can be produced from the evaporation or boiling of liquid water or from the sublimation of ice. Water vapor is transparent, like most constituents of the atmosphere. Under typical atmospheric conditions, water vapor is continuously generated by evaporation and removed by condensation. It is less dense than most of the other constituents of air and triggers convection currents that can lead to clouds and fog."}}, {"canonical_name": "object", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "objects", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "objects", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["objects"], "contexts": ["things perceived by senses"]}, {"entity_id": "natural_objects", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "natural objects", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["natural objects"], "contexts": ["form and arrangement", "things found in nature"]}, {"entity_id": "objects", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "objects", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["objects"], "contexts": ["physical entities"]}, {"entity_id": "object", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "object", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 5, "variants": ["Object", "object"], "contexts": ["thing perceived or acted upon by consciousness", "thing perceived or acted upon"]}, {"entity_id": "object", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "object", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["object"], "contexts": ["thing from which qualities flow"]}, {"entity_id": "object", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "object", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["object"], "contexts": ["parallax defined"]}, {"entity_id": "external_object", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "external object", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["external object"], "contexts": ["thing perceived as outside oneself"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "objects", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "natural objects", "similarity": 0.927}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "objects", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "objects", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "objects", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "object", "similarity": 0.944}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "natural objects", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_s