xford"], "contexts": ["location of Professor Powell", "university associated with Professor Powell"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Oxford", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Oxford", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Oxford", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Oxford", "similarity": 0.873}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Oxford", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Oxford", "similarity": 0.873}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Oxford", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Oxford", "similarity": 0.873}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Oxford", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Oxford", "similarity": 0.873}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Oxford", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Oxford", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 635, "stable_key": "clu_23fd66d5dfb2348d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Oxford", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United Kingdom", "note": "While Oxford is primarily known as a city in the UK, the context mentions \"city in New York,\" suggesting a potential reference to Oxford, New York. However, the overwhelming majority of references are to the UK university city, which is a significant early modern intellectual hub.", "wikidata_id": "Q34217", "wikidata_description": "city in Oxfordshire, England", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oxford", "wikipedia_extract": "Oxford ( ) is a cathedral city and non-metropolitan district in Oxfordshire, England, of which it is the county town. The city is home to the University of Oxford, the oldest university in the English-speaking world; it has buildings in every style of English architecture since late Anglo-Saxon. Oxford's industries include motor manufacturing, education, publishing, science, and information technologies. Founded in the 8th century, it was granted city status in 1542. The city is located at the confluence of the rivers Thames (locally known as the Isis) and Cherwell. It had a population of 166,034 in 2024."}}, {"canonical_name": "Magellan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "magellan", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Magellan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Magellan"], "contexts": ["straits with compass variation", "strait at the southern tip of South America"]}, {"entity_id": "straits_of_magellan", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "straits of Magellan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["straits of Magellan"], "contexts": ["location with needle declination"]}, {"entity_id": "magellan", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "Magellan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Magellan"], "contexts": ["strait cutting through the Cordilleras"]}, {"entity_id": "magellan", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Magellan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Magellan"], "contexts": ["Straits"]}, {"entity_id": "magellan", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Magellan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 5, "variants": ["Magellan"], "contexts": ["strait in South America", "Explorer whose voyage influenced demarcation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Magellan", "target_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "target_name": "Magellan", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Magellan", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Magellan", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Magellan", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Magellan", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "straits of Magellan", "target_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "target_name": "Magellan", "similarity": 0.886}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "straits of Magellan", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Magellan", "similarity": 0.886}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "straits of Magellan", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Magellan", "similarity": 0.886}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Magellan", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Magellan", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Magellan", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Magellan", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Magellan", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Magellan", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 636, "stable_key": "clu_4eb56315da32c146", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Strait of Magellan", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A sea passage in South America named after an explorer.", "note": "Refers to both the geographical strait and the explorer Ferdinand Magellan.", "wikipedia_extract": "The Strait of Magellan (Spanish: Estrecho de Magallanes), also called the Straits of Magellan, is a navigable sea route in southern Chile separating mainland South America to the north and the Tierra del Fuego archipelago to the south. Considered the most important natural passage between the Atlantic and Pacific oceans, the strait is approximately 570 km (310 nmi; 350 mi) long and 2 km (1.1 nmi; 1.2 mi) wide at its narrowest point. In 1520, the Spanish expedition of the Portuguese navigator Ferdinand Magellan, after whom the strait is named, became the first Europeans to discover it. Magellan's original name for the strait was Estrecho de Todos los Santos (\"Strait of All Saints\"). The King of Spain, Emperor Charles V, who had sponsored the Magellan-Elcano expedition, changed the name to the Strait of Magellan in honor of Magellan. The route is difficult to navigate due to frequent narrows and unpredictable winds and currents."}}, {"canonical_name": "coal", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "coal", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Coal", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Coal"], "contexts": ["component of gunpowder for color and ignition", "component of gunpowder"]}, {"entity_id": "coals", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "coals", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["coals"], "contexts": ["fuel for heating"]}, {"entity_id": "black_coal", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "black coal", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["black coal"], "contexts": ["fossil fuel"]}, {"entity_id": "coal", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "coal", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 6, "variants": ["coal"], "contexts": ["mineral used in high temperature processes", "source of heat through oxidation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Coal", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "coals", "similarity": 0.912}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Coal", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "coal", "similarity": 0.989}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "coals", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "coal", "similarity": 0.911}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "black coal", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "coal", "similarity": 0.887}], "id": 637, "stable_key": "clu_015f7cd9871db30c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Coal", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "Includes related terms referring to coal and coal-bearing formations.", "wikidata_id": "Q268335", "wikidata_description": "left tributary of the Liard River in Canada", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coal", "wikipedia_extract": "Coal is a combustible black or brownish-black sedimentary rock, formed as layers called coal seams. Coal is mostly carbon with variable amounts of other elements, chiefly hydrogen, sulfur, oxygen, and nitrogen. It is a fossil fuel, formed when plants decay into peat which is converted into coal by the heat and pressure of deep burial over millions of years. Vast deposits formed from wetlands called coal forests that covered much of the tropics during the late Carboniferous and early Permian. Coal is used primarily as a fuel. While coal has been known and used for thousands of years, its usage was limited until the Industrial Revolution."}}, {"canonical_name": "Knees", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "knees", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "knees", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["knees"], "contexts": ["joint in the leg", "joint between thigh and lower leg"]}, {"entity_id": "knee", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Knee", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Knee"], "contexts": ["joint between thigh and lower leg"]}, {"entity_id": "knees", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Knees", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Knees"], "contexts": ["body part affected by gout", "joints affected by gout, treated by bathing"]}, {"entity_id": "knee", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "knee", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["knee"], "contexts": ["joint between thigh and lower leg"]}, {"entity_id": "joelhos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "joelhos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["joelhos"], "contexts": ["knees", "body parts"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "knees", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Knees", "similarity": 0.993}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "knees", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "knee", "similarity": 0.96}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "knees", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "joelhos", "similarity": 0.901}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Knee", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Knees", "similarity": 0.959}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Knee", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "knee", "similarity": 0.993}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Knees", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "knee", "similarity": 0.954}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Knees", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "joelhos", "similarity": 0.906}], "id": 638, "stable_key": "clu_c8b32df73f87cb27", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Knee", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Although anatomically a 'body part', the cluster refers to the concept of the knee as a joint and its relation to diseases like gout, rather than a specific anatomical specimen. Hence, 'concept' is the most fitting type.", "wikidata_id": "Q37425", "wikidata_description": "region around the kneecap"}}, {"canonical_name": "Bodies", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "bodies", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "bodies", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["bodies"], "contexts": ["general term for physical entities"]}, {"entity_id": "bodies", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Bodies", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 5, "variants": ["Bodies", "Bodie"], "contexts": ["human beings", "human physical form"]}, {"entity_id": "corpos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "corpos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["corpos"], "contexts": ["human bodies", "human bodies susceptible to contagion"]}, {"entity_id": "bodies", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "bodies", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["bodies"], "contexts": ["physical entities", "physical form of organisms"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "bodies", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Bodies", "similarity": 0.991}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "bodies", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "corpos", "similarity": 0.988}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "bodies", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "bodies", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Bodies", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "bodies", "similarity": 0.991}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "corpos", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "bodies", "similarity": 0.988}], "id": 639, "stable_key": "clu_15f8db1f57883a5a-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Body", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'Bodies' is used broadly in early modern texts to refer to physical entities, particularly human and animal bodies, in both scientific and philosophical contexts.", "wikidata_id": "Q5", "wikidata_description": "any single member of Homo sapiens, unique extant species of the genus Homo", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human", "wikipedia_extract": "Humans (Homo sapiens, meaning \"thinking man\" or \"wise man\") are the most common and widespread species of primate, and the only extant species of the genus Homo. They are great apes characterized by hairlessness, obligate bipedality, manual dexterity with opposable thumbs, precision grip, and high intelligence. Humans have large brains compared to body size (a high encephalization quotient), enabling more advanced cognitive skills that facilitate successful adaptation to varied environments, development of sophisticated tools, and formation of complex social structures and civilizations. Humans possess a disproportionately larger volume of both cerebral white matter and gray matter present in the prefrontal cortex (PFC) than any other primate species, which facilitated the expansion of higher-order executive functions. Humans are highly social, with individual humans tending to belong to a multi-layered network of distinct social groups \u2013 from families and peer groups to corporations and political states. As such, social interactions between humans have established a wide variety of values, social norms, languages, and traditions (collectively termed institutions), each of which bolsters human society.", "description": "The physical structure of a person or animal."}}, {"canonical_name": "weight", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "weight", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "weight", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["weight"], "contexts": ["ponderous quality of a body"]}, {"entity_id": "weight", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "weight", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["weight"], "contexts": ["perceptual quality", "perceived quality of mass under gravity"]}, {"entity_id": "weight", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "weight", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["weight"], "contexts": ["force due to gravity", "force of gravity on an object"]}, {"entity_id": "weight", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "weight", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["weight"], "contexts": ["physical property of matter", "force of gravity on an object"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "weight", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "weight", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "weight", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "weight", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "weight", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "weight", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "weight", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "weight", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "weight", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "weight", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "weight", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "weight", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 640, "stable_key": "clu_7ccbd899da364cd7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Weight", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q83462", "wikidata_description": "sport"}}, {"canonical_name": "paralysis", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "paralisis", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Paralisis", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 1, "variants": ["Paralisis"], "contexts": ["medical condition involving loss of function"]}, {"entity_id": "paralysis", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "paralysis", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 6, "variants": ["paralysis"], "contexts": ["loss of muscle function", "inability to move body parts"]}, {"entity_id": "progressive_paralysis", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "progressive paralysis", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 1, "variants": ["progressive paralysis"], "contexts": ["neurological disorder"]}, {"entity_id": "paralesia", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "paralesia", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 1, "variants": ["paralesia"], "contexts": ["paralysis, treated with coconut"]}, {"entity_id": "paralysis", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "paralysis", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 1, "variants": ["paralysis"], "contexts": ["loss of muscle function"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Paralisis", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "paralysis", "similarity": 0.99}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Paralisis", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "progressive paralysis", "similarity": 0.919}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Paralisis", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "paralesia", "similarity": 0.924}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Paralisis", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "paralysis", "similarity": 0.99}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "paralysis", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "paralesia", "similarity": 0.924}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "paralysis", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "paralysis", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "progressive paralysis", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "paralysis", "similarity": 0.924}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "paralesia", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "paralysis", "similarity": 0.924}], "id": 641, "stable_key": "clu_6ddf0b9468255db3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "paralysis", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "Loss of muscle function in one or more parts of the body.", "wikipedia_extract": "Paralysis (pl.: paralyses; also known as plegia) is a loss of motor function in one or more muscles. Paralysis can also be accompanied by a loss of feeling (sensory loss) in the affected area if there is sensory damage. In the United States, roughly 1 in 50 people have been diagnosed with some form of permanent or transient paralysis. The word \"paralysis\" derives from the Greek \u03c0\u03b1\u03c1\u03ac\u03bb\u03c5\u03c3\u03b9\u03c2, meaning \"disabling of the nerves\" from \u03c0\u03b1\u03c1\u03ac (para) meaning \"beside, by\" and \u03bb\u03cd\u03c3\u03b9\u03c2 (lysis) meaning \"making loose\". A paralysis accompanied by involuntary tremors is usually called \"palsy\". Paralysis is most often caused by damage in the nervous system, especially the spinal cord.", "note": "Covers general paralysis and related conditions like paralytic dementia.", "modern_term": "Paralysis", "wikidata_id": "Q1788314", "wikidata_description": "human disease"}}, {"canonical_name": "Gibraltar", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "gibraltar", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "Gibraltar", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Gibraltar"], "contexts": ["strait connecting Atlantic Ocean and Mediterranean Sea", "strait connecting Atlantic and Mediterranean"]}, {"entity_id": "gibraltar", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Gibraltar", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Gibraltar"], "contexts": ["city in Maracaybo province", "city in Venezuela"]}, {"entity_id": "straits_of_gibraltar", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Straits of Gibraltar", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Straits of Gibraltar"], "contexts": ["strait connecting Atlantic and Mediterranean"]}, {"entity_id": "straits_of_gibraltar", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Straits of Gibraltar", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Straits of Gibraltar"], "contexts": ["narrow strait influencing tides"]}, {"entity_id": "gibraltar,_the_straits_of", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Gibraltar, the Straits of", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Gibraltar, the Straits of"], "contexts": ["turning aside the tidal wave"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Gibraltar", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Gibraltar", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Gibraltar", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Straits of Gibraltar", "similarity": 0.95}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Gibraltar", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Straits of Gibraltar", "similarity": 0.95}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Gibraltar", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Gibraltar, the Straits of", "similarity": 0.938}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Gibraltar", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Straits of Gibraltar", "similarity": 0.95}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Gibraltar", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Straits of Gibraltar", "similarity": 0.95}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Gibraltar", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Gibraltar, the Straits of", "similarity": 0.938}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Straits of Gibraltar", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Straits of Gibraltar", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Straits of Gibraltar", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Gibraltar, the Straits of", "similarity": 0.972}], "id": 642, "stable_key": "clu_2cd30d4501ba1c88", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Strait of Gibraltar", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Spain", "wikidata_id": "Q36124", "wikidata_description": "strait that connects the Atlantic Ocean to the Mediterranean Sea", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strait%20of%20Gibraltar", "wikipedia_extract": "The Strait of Gibraltar, also known as the Straits of Gibraltar, is a narrow strait that connects the Atlantic Ocean to the Mediterranean Sea and separates Europe from Africa. The two continents are separated by 7.7 nautical miles (14.2 kilometers, 8.9 miles) at its narrowest point. Ferries cross between the two continents every day in as little as 35 minutes. The Strait's depth ranges between 300 and 900 metres (980 and 2,950 feet; 160 and 490 fathoms). The strait lies in the territorial waters of Morocco, Spain, and the British overseas territory of Gibraltar. Under the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea, foreign vessels and aircraft have the freedom of navigation and overflight to cross the strait of Gibraltar in case of continuous transit."}}, {"canonical_name": "New Holland", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "nouvelle-hollande", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "Nouvelle-Hollande", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Nouvelle-Hollande"], "contexts": ["historical name for Australia"]}, {"entity_id": "holland", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Holland", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Holland"], "contexts": ["country of Leyden university", "region in Lincolnshire"]}, {"entity_id": "new_holland", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "New Holland", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 5, "variants": ["New Holland"], "contexts": ["location of comet observation", "location for hemisphere comparison"]}, {"entity_id": "new_holland", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "New Holland", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["New Holland"], "contexts": ["shores with distinct species"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Nouvelle-Hollande", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Holland", "similarity": 0.903}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Nouvelle-Hollande", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "New Holland", "similarity": 0.943}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Nouvelle-Hollande", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "New Holland", "similarity": 0.943}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Holland", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "New Holland", "similarity": 0.892}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Holland", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "New Holland", "similarity": 0.892}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "New Holland", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "New Holland", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 643, "stable_key": "clu_a55e5c4431554e67-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Australia", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Australia", "note": " 'New Holland' was the historical name given to the western part of Australia. The context of distinct species and shores confirms this identification.", "wikidata_id": "Q408", "wikidata_description": "country in Oceania", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Australia", "wikipedia_extract": "Australia, officially the Commonwealth of Australia, is a country comprising the mainland of the Australian continent, the island of Tasmania and numerous smaller islands. It has a total area of 7,688,287 km2 (2,968,464 sq mi), making it the sixth-largest country in the world and the largest in Oceania. Australia is the world's flattest and driest inhabited continent. It is a megadiverse country, and its size gives it a wide variety of landscapes and climates including deserts in the interior and tropical rainforests along the coast. The ancestors of Aboriginal Australians began arriving from Southeast Asia 50,000 to 65,000 years ago, during the Last Glacial Period. By the time of British settlement, Aboriginal Australians spoke more than 250 distinct languages and had one of the oldest living cultures in the world.", "description": "Continent and country in the Southern Hemisphere, known for unique wildlife."}}, {"canonical_name": "arctic", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "arctic", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "arctic", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 4, "variants": ["arctic"], "contexts": ["cold polar region", "polar region around North Pole"]}, {"entity_id": "arctic", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Arctic", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Arctic"], "contexts": ["polar region"]}, {"entity_id": "arctic", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Arctic", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 3, "variants": ["Arctic"], "contexts": ["geographic region"]}, {"entity_id": "arctic", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Arctic", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Arctic"], "contexts": ["northern polar region", "region with mosses and lichens"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "arctic", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Arctic", "similarity": 0.951}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "arctic", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Arctic", "similarity": 0.951}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "arctic", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Arctic", "similarity": 0.951}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Arctic", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Arctic", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Arctic", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Arctic", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Arctic", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Arctic", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 644, "stable_key": "clu_8690a9b3c9e21de8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Arctic", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "International", "wikidata_id": "Q213141", "wikidata_description": "species of mammal in the family Viverridae, native to South and Southeast Asia", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binturong", "wikipedia_extract": "The binturong (Arctictis binturong) (; bin-TURE-ong, BIN-ture-ong), also known as the bearcat, is a viverrid native to South and Southeast Asia. It is uncommon in much of its range, and has been assessed as Vulnerable on the IUCN Red List because of a declining population. It is estimated to have declined at least 30% since the mid-1980s. The binturong is the only species in the genus Arctictis. The name \"binturong\" is related to the words benturong and tenturun used in Riau. In the Western Malayo-Polynesian languages spoken in Borneo, the name is related to the root ma-tuRun."}}, {"canonical_name": "climate", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "climates", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "climates", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["climates"], "contexts": ["different environments for animals", "prevailing weather conditions"]}, {"entity_id": "climas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "climas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["climas"], "contexts": ["regions with specific weather patterns"]}, {"entity_id": "climates", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "climates", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["climates"], "contexts": ["vicissitudes caused by oscillations"]}, {"entity_id": "climate", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "climate", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 5, "variants": ["climate"], "contexts": ["prevailing weather conditions of a region", "average weather conditions"]}, {"entity_id": "temperate_climates", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "temperate climates", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["temperate climates"], "contexts": ["climate where life decreases"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "climates", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "climas", "similarity": 0.98}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "climates", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "climates", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "climates", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "climate", "similarity": 0.966}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "climates", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "temperate climates", "similarity": 0.882}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "climas", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "climates", "similarity": 0.98}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "climas", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "climate", "similarity": 0.964}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "climates", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "climate", "similarity": 0.966}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "climates", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "temperate climates", "similarity": 0.882}], "id": 645, "stable_key": "clu_0eaf11413b467232", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Climate", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q7937", "wikidata_description": "long-term statistical characterization of atmospheric conditions in a region, typically averaged over periods of 30 years or more", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Climate", "wikipedia_extract": "Climate is the long-term weather pattern in a region, typically averaged over 30 years. More rigorously, it is the mean and variability of meteorological variables over a time spanning from months to millions of years. Some of the meteorological variables that are commonly measured are temperature, humidity, atmospheric pressure, wind, and precipitation. In a broader sense, climate is the state of the components of the climate system, including the atmosphere, hydrosphere, cryosphere, lithosphere and biosphere and the interactions between them. The climate of a location is affected by its latitude, longitude, terrain, altitude, land use and nearby water bodies and their currents. Climates can be classified according to the average and typical variables, most commonly temperature and precipitation."}}, {"canonical_name": "St. Helena", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "st__helena", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "St. Helena", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["St. Helena"], "contexts": ["island where naturalised plants/animals exterminated natives", "oceanic island with native productions exterminated"]}, {"entity_id": "saint_helena", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Saint Helena", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Saint Helena"], "contexts": ["island associated with Napoleon's exile"]}, {"entity_id": "st__helena", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "St. Helena", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["St. Helena"], "contexts": ["volcanic island with augmented gravitation", "island in magnetic zone"]}, {"entity_id": "st__helena", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "St. Helena", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["St. Helena"], "contexts": ["island in the Atlantic Ocean", "location for magnetic observations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "St. Helena", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Saint Helena", "similarity": 0.979}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "St. Helena", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "St. Helena", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "St. Helena", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "St. Helena", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Saint Helena", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "St. Helena", "similarity": 0.979}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Saint Helena", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "St. Helena", "similarity": 0.979}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "St. Helena", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "St. Helena", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 646, "stable_key": "clu_33c6e9f0b1f0d5db", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Saint Helena (, US: ; US:  ) is a volcanic and tropical island, located in the South Atlantic Ocean, some 1,874 km (1,165 miles) west of the mainland of the continent of Africa, with the Southern African nations of Angola and Namibia on its southeastern coast being the closest nations geographically. The island is around 1,950 km (1,210 mi) west of the coast of southwestern Angola, and 4,000 km (2,500 mi) east of the major seaport city of Rio de Janeiro, Brazil in South America. It is one of the three constituent parts of Saint Helena, Ascension and Tristan da Cunha, a British overseas territory. Saint Helena measures about 16 by 8 km (10 by 5 mi) and had a population of 4,439 in the 2021 census. It was named after Saint Helena (c. AD 246/248\u2013330), the mother of the Roman emperor Constantine I (AD 272\u2013337, reigned 306\u2013337), also known as Constantine the Great."}}, {"canonical_name": "August", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "agosto", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "Agosto", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Agosto"], "contexts": ["month of flowering"]}, {"entity_id": "ao\u00fbt", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Ao\u00fbt", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Ao\u00fbt"], "contexts": ["month, August", "month"]}, {"entity_id": "august", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "August", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["August"], "contexts": ["month", "developed psychrometer for atmospheric moisture"]}, {"entity_id": "agosto", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Agosto", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Agosto"], "contexts": ["month of a letter"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Agosto", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Ao\u00fbt", "similarity": 0.91}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Agosto", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "August", "similarity": 0.953}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Agosto", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Agosto", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Ao\u00fbt", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "August", "similarity": 0.89}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Ao\u00fbt", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Agosto", "similarity": 0.91}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "August", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Agosto", "similarity": 0.953}], "id": 647, "stable_key": "clu_09babe4d6b94512c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "August", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "olio rosato", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "azeyte_rosado", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "azeyte rosado", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["azeyte rosado"], "contexts": ["rose oil preparation"]}, {"entity_id": "olio_rosado", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "olio rosado", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["olio rosado"], "contexts": ["rose oil, an ingredient"]}, {"entity_id": "olio_rosato", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "olio rosato", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 5, "variants": ["olio rosato", "OLIO ROSATO", "OLIO ROSATO CO"], "contexts": ["rose oil preparation", "preparation made from roses"]}, {"entity_id": "azeyte_rosado", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "azeyte rosado", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 3, "variants": ["azeyte rosado", "azeyte rofado"], "contexts": ["rose oil", "rose oil, medicinal preparation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "azeyte rosado", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "azeyte rosado", "similarity": 0.857}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "olio rosado", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "olio rosato", "similarity": 0.984}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "olio rosado", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "azeyte rosado", "similarity": 0.9}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "olio rosato", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "azeyte rosado", "similarity": 0.897}], "id": 648, "stable_key": "clu_a704a005f00d10b7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Rose oil", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "wikidata_id": "Q83054", "wikidata_description": "essential oil", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rose%20oil", "wikipedia_extract": "Rose oil (rose otto, attar of rose, attar of roses, or rose essence) is an essential oil that is extracted from the petals of various types of rose. Rose ottos are extracted through steam distillation, while rose absolutes are obtained through solvent extraction, the absolute being used more commonly in perfumery. The production technique originated in Iran. Even with their high price and the advent of organic synthesis, rose oils are still perhaps the most widely used essential oil in perfumery. Two major species of rose are cultivated for the production of rose oil: Rosa damascena production today is dominated by 3 producers account for over 70% of the Rose oil market share: It is also grown on a smaller scale in Afghanistan, Armenia, Azerbaijan, Bosnia, Croatia, Cyprus, Ethiopia, Georgia, Greece, Jordan, Lebanon, India, Iran, Iraq, Israel, Moldova, North Macedonia, Oman, Serbia, Syria, Tajikistan, Turkmenistan, Pakistan, Romania, Russia, Ukraine, United Arab Emirates and Yemen. Rosa centifolia, the cabbage rose, which is more commonly grown in Morocco, Egypt and France."}}, {"canonical_name": "Guadalupe", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "guadalupe", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "Guadalupe", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Guadalupe"], "contexts": ["island in the Caribbean"]}, {"entity_id": "guadalupe", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Guadalupe", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Guadalupe"], "contexts": ["suburb of Havana"]}, {"entity_id": "guadaloupe", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Guadaloupe", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Guadaloupe"], "contexts": ["island with human skeletons", "location of fossil human remains"]}, {"entity_id": "guadalupe", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Guadalupe", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 5, "variants": ["Guadalupe", "Guadalup"], "contexts": ["river name", "word with debated etymology"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Guadalupe", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Guadalupe", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Guadalupe", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Guadaloupe", "similarity": 0.94}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Guadalupe", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Guadalupe", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Guadalupe", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Guadaloupe", "similarity": 0.94}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Guadalupe", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Guadalupe", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Guadaloupe", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Guadalupe", "similarity": 0.94}], "id": 649, "stable_key": "clu_8186f853632db836", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Guadeloupe", "confidence": "medium", "type": "place", "country": "France", "note": "While 'Guadalupe' can refer to other places, the context of Humboldt and 'coloquios_da_orta' suggests the Caribbean island or a related locale. The mention of a 'suburb of Havana' is ambiguous and might refer to a specific settlement or a misunderstanding.", "wikidata_id": "Q17012", "wikidata_description": "overseas region and department of France, and archipelago in the Caribbean"}}, {"canonical_name": "Gen\u00e8ve", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "gen\u00e8ve", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Gen\u00e8ve", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 5, "variants": ["Gen\u00e8ve"], "contexts": ["city in Switzerland", "location for barometric observations"]}, {"entity_id": "geneva", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Geneva", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Geneva"], "contexts": ["location with low meteor count", "city near Pregny"]}, {"entity_id": "geneve", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Geneve", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Geneve"], "contexts": ["city where Michael de Normandie was consul"]}, {"entity_id": "geneva", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Geneva", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Geneva"], "contexts": ["location of water boring experiment"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Gen\u00e8ve", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Geneva", "similarity": 0.896}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Gen\u00e8ve", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Geneve", "similarity": 0.865}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Gen\u00e8ve", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Geneva", "similarity": 0.896}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Geneva", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Geneve", "similarity": 0.971}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Geneva", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Geneva", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Geneve", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Geneva", "similarity": 0.971}], "id": 650, "stable_key": "clu_759134379de4304b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Genoa", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Major port city in Italy, historically significant for trade and exploration.", "note": "Refers to the city of Genoa, also known historically as G\u00eanes or Geneve in some texts.", "wikipedia_extract": "Genoa ( JEN-oh-\u0259; Italian: Genova [\u02c8d\u0292\u025b\u02d0nova] ; Ligurian: Z\u00eana [\u02c8ze\u02d0na]) is the sixth-largest city in Italy and the capital of the Italian region of Liguria. As of 2025, 565,301 people live within the city's administrative limits. While its metropolitan city has 818,629 inhabitants, more than 1.5 million people live in the wider metropolitan area stretching along the Italian Riviera. On the Gulf of Genoa in the Ligurian Sea, Genoa has historically been one of the most important ports on the Mediterranean: it is the busiest port in Italy and in the Mediterranean Sea and twelfth-busiest in the European Union. Genoa was the capital of one of the most powerful maritime republics for over seven centuries, from the 11th century to 1797. Particularly from the 12th century to the 15th century, the city played a leading role in the history of commerce and trade in Europe, becoming one of the largest naval powers of the continent and considered among the wealthiest cities in the world."}}, {"canonical_name": "Tartares", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "tartares", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Tartares", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 4, "variants": ["Tartares"], "contexts": ["ethnic group designation", "people using quippos for arithmetic"]}, {"entity_id": "tartars", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Tartars", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Tartars"], "contexts": ["term falsely given to Turkish tribes"]}, {"entity_id": "t\u00e1rtaros", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "t\u00e1rtaros", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 4, "variants": ["t\u00e1rtaros", "T\u00e1rtaros", "t\u00e1rtaros Uzbeks"], "contexts": ["alternative name for Mogores", "people who have 'ham' as king"]}, {"entity_id": "tartaros", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Tartaros", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Tartaros"], "contexts": ["people who name Ch\u00e0"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Tartares", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Tartars", "similarity": 0.964}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Tartares", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Tartaros", "similarity": 0.96}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Tartars", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Tartaros", "similarity": 0.949}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "t\u00e1rtaros", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Tartaros", "similarity": 0.788}], "id": 651, "stable_key": "clu_e0f34a6d93d591f0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tatars", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Turkic ethnic group, historically associated with Mongol conquests", "note": "The term was sometimes used broadly and inaccurately.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}}, {"canonical_name": "Suez", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "suez", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Suez", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Suez"], "contexts": ["location of another isthmus", "canal project"]}, {"entity_id": "suez", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Suez", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Suez"], "contexts": ["location of bitter lakes"]}, {"entity_id": "isthmus_of_suez", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Isthmus of Suez", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Isthmus of Suez"], "contexts": ["location with different water levels"]}, {"entity_id": "suez", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Suez", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Suez"], "contexts": ["port city", "destination from Red Sea"]}, {"entity_id": "suez", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Suez", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Suez"], "contexts": ["location with different marine plants"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Suez", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Suez", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Suez", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Isthmus of Suez", "similarity": 0.887}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Suez", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Suez", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Suez", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Suez", "similarity": 0.892}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Suez", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Suez", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Suez", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Suez", "similarity": 0.892}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Isthmus of Suez", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Suez", "similarity": 0.887}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Suez", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Suez", "similarity": 0.892}], "id": 652, "stable_key": "clu_8a33d0dab68a2b4a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Suez", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Port city and location of an isthmus and canal project.", "wikipedia_extract": "Suez is a seaport city in north-eastern Egypt, located on the north coast of the Gulf of Suez on the Red Sea, near the southern terminus of the Suez Canal. The city is the capital and largest city of the Suez Governorate. The modern city of Suez is a successor of the ancient Egyptian city of Clysma, a major Red Sea port and a center of monasticism. Railway lines and highways connect the city with Cairo, Port Said, and Ismailia. Suez has a petrochemical plant, and its oil refineries have pipelines carrying the finished product to Cairo. These are represented in the flag of the governorate: the blue background refers to the sea, the gear refers to Suez's status as an industrial governorate, and the flame refers to the petroleum firms of Suez."}}, {"canonical_name": "machine", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "machine", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "machine", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["machine"], "contexts": ["mechanical device", "machine"]}, {"entity_id": "machine_humaine", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "machine humaine", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["machine humaine"], "contexts": ["human machine"]}, {"entity_id": "appareil", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "appareil", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["appareil"], "contexts": ["scientific apparatus"]}, {"entity_id": "machine", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "machine", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 3, "variants": ["machine"], "contexts": ["mechanical entity with predictable actions", "device performing a task"]}, {"entity_id": "machinery", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Machinery", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Machinery"], "contexts": ["enables advantage from forces", "relations to force"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "machine", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "appareil", "similarity"