: "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "East", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "sud-est", "similarity": 0.919}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "East", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Est", "similarity": 0.911}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "East", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "de l'Est", "similarity": 0.911}], "id": 669, "stable_key": "clu_4f5bb71e43383dd3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "East", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "note": "Refers to the cardinal direction and associated regions/cultures, not a specific modern country.", "wikidata_id": "Q176081", "wikidata_description": "province of Iran"}}, {"canonical_name": "Simples", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "simples", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Simples", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Simples"], "contexts": ["basic medicinal ingredients"]}, {"entity_id": "simples", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Simples", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Simples"], "contexts": ["nature of single medicinal substances", "natural materials gathered for medicinal use"]}, {"entity_id": "simple", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "simple", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["simple"], "contexts": ["simple substance, like a plant"]}, {"entity_id": "semplici", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Semplici", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Semplici", "semplici"], "contexts": ["simple substances", "simple medicinal ingredients"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Simples", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Simples", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Simples", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "simple", "similarity": 0.966}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Simples", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Semplici", "similarity": 0.976}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Simples", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "simple", "similarity": 0.966}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Simples", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Semplici", "similarity": 0.976}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "simple", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Semplici", "similarity": 0.941}], "id": 670, "stable_key": "clu_9142e61da60c7466", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Simple Medicines", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "Single medicinal substances derived directly from nature, often plants.", "note": "Refers to the uncompounded ingredients used in traditional medicine.", "wikipedia_extract": "Ascorbic acid is an organic compound with formula C6H8O6, originally called hexuronic acid. It is a white solid, but impure samples can appear yellowish. It dissolves freely in water to give mildly acidic solutions. It is a mild reducing agent. Ascorbic acid exists as two enantiomers (mirror-image isomers), commonly denoted \"l\" (for \"levo\") and \"d\" (for \"dextro\"). The l isomer is the one most often encountered: it occurs naturally in many foods, and is one form (\"vitamer\") of vitamin C, an essential nutrient for humans and many animals."}}, {"canonical_name": "digesti\u00f3n", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "syst\u00e8me_digestif", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "syst\u00e8me digestif", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["syst\u00e8me digestif"], "contexts": ["body system for digestion"]}, {"entity_id": "digesti\u00f3n", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "digesti\u00f3n", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 4, "variants": ["digesti\u00f3n"], "contexts": ["process of breaking down food", "process aided by Guayabo fruit"]}, {"entity_id": "digestive_organs", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "digestive organs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["digestive organs"], "contexts": ["organs for processing food"]}, {"entity_id": "digestive_apparatus", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "digestive apparatus", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["digestive apparatus"], "contexts": ["for reducing materials for assimilation"]}, {"entity_id": "digestive_organs", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "digestive organs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["digestive organs"], "contexts": ["organs involved in digestion"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "syst\u00e8me digestif", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "digesti\u00f3n", "similarity": 0.943}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "syst\u00e8me digestif", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "digestive organs", "similarity": 0.948}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "syst\u00e8me digestif", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "digestive apparatus", "similarity": 0.956}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "syst\u00e8me digestif", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "digestive organs", "similarity": 0.948}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "digesti\u00f3n", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "digestive organs", "similarity": 0.906}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "digesti\u00f3n", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "digestive apparatus", "similarity": 0.915}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "digesti\u00f3n", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "digestive organs", "similarity": 0.906}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "digestive organs", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "digestive apparatus", "similarity": 0.976}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "digestive organs", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "digestive organs", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 671, "stable_key": "clu_190599d239958141", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Digestive system", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q2300099", "wikidata_description": "disease involving the gastrointestinal tract", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gastrointestinal%20disease", "wikipedia_extract": "Gastrointestinal diseases (abbrev. GI diseases or GI illnesses) refer to diseases involving the gastrointestinal tract, namely the esophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine and rectum; and the accessory organs of digestion, the liver, gallbladder, and pancreas. The oral cavity is part of the gastrointestinal system and as such the presence of alterations in this district can be the first sign of both systemic and gastrointestinal diseases. By far the most common oral conditions are plaque-induced diseases (e.g., gingivitis, periodontitis, dental caries). Oral symptoms can be similar to lesions occurring elsewhere in the digestive tract, with a pattern of swelling, inflammation, ulcers, and fissures. If these signs are present, then patients are more likely to also have anal and esophageal lesions and experience other extra-intestinal disease manifestations.", "note": "Refers to the anatomical and functional system responsible for digestion."}}, {"canonical_name": "serpentes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "serpent", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "serpent", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["serpent"], "contexts": ["emblematic of life's cycle"]}, {"entity_id": "serpentes", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "serpentes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["serpentes", "serpentes venenosas"], "contexts": ["creatures whose venom is treated", "guardians of diamonds in T\u00e2mara's account"]}, {"entity_id": "serpenti", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "serpenti", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "REPTILE", "count": 1, "variants": ["serpenti"], "contexts": ["serpents"]}, {"entity_id": "serpentes", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "serpentes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["Serpentes", "serpentes"], "contexts": ["animal used in medicine", "type of animal used in medicine"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "serpent", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "serpentes", "similarity": 0.932}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "serpent", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "serpentes", "similarity": 0.932}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "serpentes", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "serpenti", "similarity": 0.935}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "serpentes", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "serpentes", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "serpenti", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "serpentes", "similarity": 0.935}], "id": 672, "stable_key": "clu_22a686330488aa12", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Snakes", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q2102", "wikidata_description": "limbless, scaly, elongate reptile", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snake", "wikipedia_extract": "Snakes are elongated limbless reptiles of the suborder Serpentes. Cladistically squamates, snakes are ectothermic, amniote vertebrates covered in overlapping scales much like other members of the group. Many species of snakes have skulls with several more joints than their lizard ancestors and relatives, enabling them to swallow prey much larger than their heads (cranial kinesis). To accommodate their narrow bodies, snakes' paired organs (such as kidneys) appear one in front of the other instead of side by side, and most only have one functional lung. Some species retain a pelvic girdle with a pair of vestigial claws on either side of the cloaca. Lizards have independently evolved elongate bodies without limbs or with greatly reduced limbs at least twenty-five times via convergent evolution, leading to many lineages of legless lizards."}}, {"canonical_name": "death", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "death", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "death", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["death"], "contexts": ["abstract concept related to cessation of life"]}, {"entity_id": "death", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "death", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["death", "Death"], "contexts": ["consequence of transgression", "cessation of life"]}, {"entity_id": "death", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Death", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Death"], "contexts": ["final end for man", "state of being cold"]}, {"entity_id": "death", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "death", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["death"], "contexts": ["cessation of life"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "death", "target_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "target_name": "death", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "death", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Death", "similarity": 0.969}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "death", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "death", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "death", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Death", "similarity": 0.969}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "death", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "death", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Death", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "death", "similarity": 0.969}], "id": 673, "stable_key": "clu_fee707210695beac", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "death", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "The irreversible cessation of all biological functions that sustain a living organism.", "wikipedia_extract": "Death is the end of life; it is the irreversible cessation of biological functions that sustain a living organism. Death is thought to eventually and inevitably occur in all organisms; though some organisms, such as the immortal jellyfish, are biologically immortal, they can however still die from means other than the effects of aging. Something that is not considered an organism may be said \"to die\" in a figurative sense when it reaches an inactive state, such as when a star runs out of fuel. As of the early 21st century, 56 million people die per year. As of 2022, an estimated total of almost 110 billion humans have died, or roughly 94% of all humans to have ever lived. The cause of death is usually considered important, and an autopsy can be done to determine it."}}, {"canonical_name": "Quinine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "quinine", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "quinine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["quinine"], "contexts": ["drug used to treat fever"]}, {"entity_id": "quinine", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "quinine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["quinine"], "contexts": ["supposed substance in Byrsonima Moureila"]}, {"entity_id": "quinine", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "quinine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["quinine"], "contexts": ["substance applied to tongue"]}, {"entity_id": "quinine", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Quinine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 5, "variants": ["quinine", "Quinine"], "contexts": ["source of fluorescent light", "solution producing fluorescence and dispersion"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "quinine", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "quinine", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "quinine", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "quinine", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "quinine", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Quinine", "similarity": 0.795}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "quinine", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "quinine", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "quinine", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Quinine", "similarity": 0.795}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "quinine", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Quinine", "similarity": 0.795}], "id": 674, "stable_key": "clu_e04e05e36242d39b", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Quinine is a medication used to treat malaria and babesiosis. This includes the treatment of malaria due to Plasmodium falciparum that is resistant to chloroquine when artesunate is not available. While sometimes used for nocturnal leg cramps, quinine is not recommended for this purpose due to the risk of serious side effects. It can be taken by mouth or intravenously. Malaria resistance to quinine occurs in certain areas of the world. Quinine is also used as an ingredient in tonic water and other beverages to impart a bitter taste."}}, {"canonical_name": "Latines", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "latines", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Latines", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Latines"], "contexts": ["people speaking Latin", "people who transcribed Greek texts"]}, {"entity_id": "latin", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "Latin", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Latin"], "contexts": ["language of scholarly writing"]}, {"entity_id": "latin", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Latin", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Latin"], "contexts": ["ancient Roman language", "language"]}, {"entity_id": "latin", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Latin", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Latin"], "contexts": ["ancient language and people", "language with dwindling inflexions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Latines", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "Latin", "similarity": 0.942}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Latines", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Latin", "similarity": 0.942}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Latines", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Latin", "similarity": 0.942}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Latin", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Latin", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Latin", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Latin", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Latin", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Latin", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 675, "stable_key": "clu_9abd4d618c616dd5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Latin language", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q397", "wikidata_description": "Indo-European language of the Italic branch", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Latin", "wikipedia_extract": "Latin (lingua Latina or Latinum) is a classical language belonging to the Italic branch of the Indo-European languages. Latin was originally spoken by the Latins in Latium (now known as Lazio), the lower Tiber area around Rome, Italy. Through the expansion of the Roman Republic, it became the dominant language in the Italian Peninsula and subsequently throughout the Roman Empire. It has greatly influenced many languages, including English, having contributed many words to the English lexicon, particularly after the Christianisation of the Anglo-Saxons and the Norman Conquest. Latin roots appear frequently in the technical vocabulary used by fields such as theology, the sciences, medicine, and law. By the late Roman Republic, Old Latin had evolved into standardised Classical Latin."}}, {"canonical_name": "Mary", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "mary", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Mary", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 3, "variants": ["Mary"], "contexts": ["mother of Jesus", "mother of Jesus, associated with womb mystery"]}, {"entity_id": "mary", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Mary", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Mary"], "contexts": ["deceased individual whose spirit allegedly controlled Lurancy", "entity assumed control of Lurancy"]}, {"entity_id": "marie", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Marie", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Marie"], "contexts": ["subject of hypnotic trance study"]}, {"entity_id": "mariana", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Mariana", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mariana"], "contexts": ["wife of Maestro Juan Plutino"]}, {"entity_id": "martha", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Martha", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Martha"], "contexts": ["godchild with a dry cough"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Mary", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Mary", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Mary", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Marie", "similarity": 0.931}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Mary", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Mariana", "similarity": 0.891}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Mary", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Martha", "similarity": 0.863}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Mary", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Mariana", "similarity": 0.891}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Mary", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Martha", "similarity": 0.863}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Marie", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Mariana", "similarity": 0.888}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Marie", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Martha", "similarity": 0.855}], "id": 676, "stable_key": "clu_5d29d7f503261b91", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mary", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "Figure of religious significance, subject of psychological study, and historical individual.", "note": "The term 'Mary' appears in multiple contexts, some religious (mother of Jesus), some psychological (subject of study), and potentially historical. Distinguishing a single referent is difficult without more context for each instance.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "female given name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mary%20%28name%29", "wikipedia_extract": "Mary  is a feminine given name, the English form of the name Maria, which was in turn a Latin form of the Greek name \u039c\u03b1\u03c1\u03af\u03b1, Mar\u00eda or \u039c\u03b1\u03c1\u03b9\u03ac\u03bc, Mariam, found in the Septuagint and New Testament. The latter reflects the original Hebrew pronunciation of the name \u05de\u05e8\u05d9\u05dd (Masoretic pronunciation Miryam), as attested by the Septuagint. The vowel \"a\" in a closed unaccented syllable later became \"i\", as seen in other names such as \"Bil'am\" (Balaam) and \"Shimshon\" (Samson). The English name Mary arises by adoption of French Marie into Middle English. Wycliffe's Bible still has Marie, with the modern spelling current from the 16th century, found in the Tyndale Bible (1525), Coverdale Bible (1535) and later translations. The name Mary may have originated from the Egyptian language; it is likely derivative of the root mr, meaning \"love; beloved\" (compare mry.t-ymn, \"Merit-Amun\", i.e."}}, {"canonical_name": "Brass", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "brass", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Brass", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Brass"], "contexts": ["metal used for dial needles", "material for dial needles"]}, {"entity_id": "bronzo", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "bronzo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["bronzo"], "contexts": ["material for cooking vessels", "material of the mortar"]}, {"entity_id": "brass", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "brass", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["brass"], "contexts": ["material used in pendulums", "alloy made with zinc"]}, {"entity_id": "bronze", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "bronze", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["bronze"], "contexts": ["metal alloy used in impressions on copper plates"]}, {"entity_id": "bronze", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "bronze", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["bronze"], "contexts": ["material replacing stone in tools and weapons"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Brass", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "bronzo", "similarity": 0.88}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Brass", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "brass", "similarity": 0.937}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "bronzo", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "brass", "similarity": 0.906}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "bronzo", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "bronze", "similarity": 0.994}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "bronzo", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "bronze", "similarity": 0.994}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "brass", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "bronze", "similarity": 0.905}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "bronze", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "bronze", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 677, "stable_key": "clu_553a795ad1235519", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Brass", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "note": "Includes 'bronzo' and 'bronze', which are often used interchangeably or closely related to brass in historical texts, especially when referring to alloys.", "wikidata_id": "Q177932", "wikidata_description": "plant species grown for its oil-rich seed"}}, {"canonical_name": "Porcellana", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "porcellane", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Porcellane", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Porcellane"], "contexts": ["type of dish"]}, {"entity_id": "porcelaines", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "porcelaines", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["porcelaines", "porcelaine"], "contexts": ["ceramic goods from China", "porcelain"]}, {"entity_id": "porcellana", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Porcellana", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 4, "variants": ["porcellana", "Porcellana"], "contexts": ["herb with viscous juice", "herb that is viscous with humidity"]}, {"entity_id": "porcelain", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "porcelain", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["porcelain"], "contexts": ["ceramic material for manufacture"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Porcellane", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "porcelaines", "similarity": 0.92}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Porcellane", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Porcellana", "similarity": 0.994}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Porcellane", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "porcelain", "similarity": 0.957}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "porcelaines", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Porcellana", "similarity": 0.921}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "porcelaines", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "porcelain", "similarity": 0.941}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Porcellana", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "porcelain", "similarity": 0.948}], "id": 678, "stable_key": "clu_850f77d43f60df51", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Porcelain", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "description": "A hard, white, ceramic material, often decorated, originating from China.", "note": "Also refers to a plant with a viscous juice.", "wikipedia_extract": "Porcelain (), also called china, is a ceramic material made by heating raw materials, generally including kaolinite, in a kiln to temperatures between 1,200 and 1,400 \u00b0C (2,200 and 2,600 \u00b0F). The greater strength and translucence of porcelain, relative to other types of pottery, arise mainly from vitrification and the formation of the mineral mullite within the body at these high temperatures. End applications include tableware, decorative ware such as figurines, and products in technology and industry such as electrical insulators and laboratory ware. The manufacturing process used for porcelain is similar to that used for earthenware and stoneware, the two other main types of pottery, although it can be more challenging to produce. It has usually been regarded as the most prestigious type of pottery due to its delicacy, strength, and high degree of whiteness. It is frequently both glazed and decorated."}}, {"canonical_name": "M. James", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "james", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "James", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["James"], "contexts": ["saint associated with a plant name"]}, {"entity_id": "m__james", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "M. James", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 3, "variants": ["M. James"], "contexts": ["scientist", "traveler who found salt springs"]}, {"entity_id": "james", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "James", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["James"], "contexts": ["doctor who made observations"]}, {"entity_id": "james", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "James", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["James"], "contexts": ["cited observer of hypnotic trance"]}, {"entity_id": "james", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "James", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["James"], "contexts": ["measured for Great Survey, determined earth's density", "measurements in General Survey of Great Britain"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "James", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "M. James", "similarity": 0.912}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "James", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "James", "similarity": 0.684}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "James", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "James", "similarity": 0.684}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "James", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "James", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "M. James", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "James", "similarity": 0.912}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "James", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "James", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "James", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "James", "similarity": 0.684}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "James", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "James", "similarity": 0.684}], "id": 679, "stable_key": "clu_2d03c92ef984823c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "William James", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1842, "death_year": 1910, "description": "American philosopher and psychologist, influential in functionalism.", "note": "The mention of 'M. James' in Humboldt's work likely refers to William James, given the context of scientific discussions and his later prominence. 'Saint associated with a plant name' is a less likely context for William James himself, but could refer to a plant named after a saint that he discussed.", "wikidata_id": "Q125249", "wikidata_description": "American philosopher, psychologist, and pragmatist (1842\u20131910)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William%20James", "wikipedia_extract": "William James (January 11, 1842 \u2013 August 26, 1910) was an American philosopher and psychologist. The first educator to offer a psychology course in the United States, he is considered to be one of the leading thinkers of the late 19th century, one of the most influential philosophers and is often dubbed the \"father of American psychology.\" A Review of General Psychology analysis, published in 2002, ranked James as the 14th most eminent psychologist of the 20th century. A survey published in American Psychologist in 1991 ranked James's reputation in second place, after Wilhelm Wundt, who is widely regarded as the founder of experimental psychology. In an empirical study by Haggbloom et al. using six criteria such as citations and recognition, James was found to be the 14th most eminent psychologist of the 20th century. James wrote widely on many topics, including epistemology, education, metaphysics, psychology, religion, and mysticism."}}, {"canonical_name": "Sexes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "sexes", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "sexes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sexes"], "contexts": ["male and female reproductive roles"]}, {"entity_id": "sexes", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "sexes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sexes"], "contexts": ["biological categories for reproduction"]}, {"entity_id": "sexes", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Sexes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["sexes", "Sexes"], "contexts": ["biological distinction for reproduction", "biological distinction between male and female"]}, {"entity_id": "sexos", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "sexos", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sexos"], "contexts": ["biological categories"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "sexes", "target_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "target_name": "sexes", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "sexes", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Sexes", "similarity": 0.988}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "sexes", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "sexos", "similarity": 0.967}, {"source_book": "epo