d with trees"]}, {"entity_id": "forests", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "forests", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["forests"], "contexts": ["areas with tall trees", "densely wooded areas"]}, {"entity_id": "forests", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Forests", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["forests", "Forests"], "contexts": ["areas dominated by trees", "area with trees affecting atmosphere"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Forrests", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "forests", "similarity": 0.956}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Forrests", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "forests", "similarity": 0.956}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Forrests", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Forests", "similarity": 0.953}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "forests", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "forests", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "forests", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Forests", "similarity": 0.981}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "forests", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Forests", "similarity": 0.981}], "id": 715, "stable_key": "clu_f7bf8b5791a60bd3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Forest", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "note": "The term 'forests' is general and not specific to a particular geographical location.", "wikidata_id": "Q4421", "wikidata_description": "dense collection of trees covering a relatively large area", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forest", "wikipedia_extract": "A forest is an ecosystem characterized by a dense community of trees. Hundreds of definitions of forest are used throughout the world, incorporating factors such as tree density, tree height, land use, legal standing, and ecological function. The United Nations' Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) defines a forest as, \"Land spanning more than 0.5 hectares with trees higher than 5 meters and a canopy cover of more than 10 percent, or trees able to reach these thresholds in situ. It does not include land that is predominantly under agricultural or urban use.\" Using this definition, Global Forest Resources Assessment 2020 found that forests covered 4.06 billion hectares (10.0 billion acres; 40.6 million square kilometres; 15.7 million square miles), or approximately 31 percent of the world's land area in 2020. Forests are the largest terrestrial ecosystems of Earth by area, and are found around the globe. 45 percent of forest land is in the tropical latitudes."}}, {"canonical_name": "parts", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "parts", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Parts", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["parts", "Parts"], "contexts": ["cleansed after delivery", "privy parts affected by ulcers"]}, {"entity_id": "parts", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Parts", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Parts"], "contexts": ["anatomical features discussed"]}, {"entity_id": "parts", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "parts", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 3, "variants": ["parts"], "contexts": ["components of the body", "components that make up a whole"]}, {"entity_id": "parts", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "parts", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["parts"], "contexts": ["components of a whole"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Parts", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Parts", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Parts", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "parts", "similarity": 0.991}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Parts", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "parts", "similarity": 0.991}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Parts", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "parts", "similarity": 0.991}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Parts", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "parts", "similarity": 0.991}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "parts", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "parts", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 716, "stable_key": "clu_77425efe83e083af", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Body Part", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'parts' in the context of anatomical features and delivery is very general. It refers to components of the body, which falls under the broader concept of 'body part'.", "wikidata_id": "Q17781690", "wikidata_description": "subdivision of a whole organism, consisting of components of multiple anatomical systems"}}, {"canonical_name": "nerve", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "nerve", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "nerve", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["nerve"], "contexts": ["sensitive tissue transmitting impulses", "tissue sensitive to stimuli"]}, {"entity_id": "nerve", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "nerve", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["nerve"], "contexts": ["tissue that transmits signals", "tissue transmitting nerve impulses"]}, {"entity_id": "nerve", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "nerve", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["nerve"], "contexts": ["nerve and muscle of a frog"]}, {"entity_id": "nerve", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "nerve", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["nerve"], "contexts": ["tissue transmitting signals", "part of the nervous system"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "nerve", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "nerve", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "nerve", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "nerve", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "nerve", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "nerve", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "nerve", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "nerve", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "nerve", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "nerve", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "nerve", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "nerve", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 717, "stable_key": "clu_a43ca0bde66f6496-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "nerve", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q9620", "wikidata_description": "enclosed, cable-like bundle of axons in the peripheral nervous system", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nerve", "wikipedia_extract": "A nerve is an enclosed, cable-like bundle of nerve fibers (called axons). Nerves have historically been considered the basic units of the peripheral nervous system. A nerve provides a common pathway for the electrochemical nerve impulses called action potentials that are transmitted along each of the axons to peripheral organs or, in the case of sensory nerves, from the periphery back to the central nervous system. Each axon is an extension of an individual neuron, along with other supportive cells such as some Schwann cells that coat the axons in myelin. Each axon is surrounded by a layer of connective tissue called the endoneurium. The axons are bundled together into groups called fascicles, and each fascicle is wrapped in a layer of connective tissue called the perineurium.", "description": "Bundles of fibers transmitting nerve impulses."}}, {"canonical_name": "Polar Circle", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "polar_circle", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Polar Circle", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Polar Circle"], "contexts": ["latitude circle around the North Pole", "arctic region"]}, {"entity_id": "cercle_polaire", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "cercle polaire", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["cercle polaire"], "contexts": ["geographic latitude"]}, {"entity_id": "polar_circle", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "polar circle", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["polar circle"], "contexts": ["arctic region"]}, {"entity_id": "arctic_circle", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "arctic circle", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["arctic circle"], "contexts": ["circle of latitude in northern hemisphere", "geographic region with specific temperature characteristics"]}, {"entity_id": "polar_circles", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "polar circles", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["polar circles"], "contexts": ["frozen shores with distinct flora"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Polar Circle", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "cercle polaire", "similarity": 0.937}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Polar Circle", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "polar circle", "similarity": 0.842}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Polar Circle", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "arctic circle", "similarity": 0.913}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Polar Circle", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "polar circles", "similarity": 0.911}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "cercle polaire", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "arctic circle", "similarity": 0.892}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "cercle polaire", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "polar circles", "similarity": 0.924}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "polar circle", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "polar circles", "similarity": 0.885}], "id": 718, "stable_key": "clu_878197585ee4a4d7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Arctic Circle", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "International", "note": "While 'Polar Circle' can refer to both Arctic and Antarctic, the context of migration and latitude suggests the Arctic Circle is the primary referent, especially with 'arctic circle' also present.", "wikidata_id": "Q17013058", "wikidata_description": "international non-profit organization related to Arctic issues based in Reykjav\u00edk, Iceland", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arctic%20Circle%20%28organization%29", "wikipedia_extract": "Arctic Circle is an organization founded by former President of Iceland \u00d3lafur Ragnar Gr\u00edmsson, former Alaska Dispatch Publisher Alice Rogoff, former Premier of Greenland Kuupik Kleist and other partners. The organization's mission is to facilitate dialogue among political and business leaders, environmental experts, scientists, indigenous representatives, and other international stakeholders to address issues facing the Arctic as a result of climate change and melting sea ice. The Arctic Circle styles itself as an international organization with a Secretariat based in Reykjav\u00edk, Iceland. At his introduction of Arctic Circle organization on April 15, 2013, at the National Press Club in Washington, D.C., Iceland President \u00d3lafur Ragnar Gr\u00edmsson said that \"the Arctic has suffered from a lack of global awareness and, as a result, a lack of effective governance. In the past, the region did not matter to the world's decision-makers and was largely forgotten. Now, with sea-ice levels at their lowest point in recorded history, the world is waking up to the challenges and opportunities the Arctic presents for its citizens as well as those who live in lower latitudes.\" Participants in Arctic Circle's events since its inaugural Assembly in October 2013 have included institutional and governmental representatives, political and policy leaders, scientists and experts, activists, and indigenous people from the Arctic countries, as well as Asia, Europe, and elsewhere."}}, {"canonical_name": "temperate zones", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "temperate_zones", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "temperate zones", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["temperate zones"], "contexts": ["climatic zones for migration"]}, {"entity_id": "temperate_zones", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "temperate zones", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["temperate zones"], "contexts": ["regions with moderate temperatures", "geographic climate zones"]}, {"entity_id": "temperate_zones", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "temperate zones", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 3, "variants": ["temperate zones"], "contexts": ["regions between tropics and polar circles", "regions with wider atmospheric pressure changes"]}, {"entity_id": "temperate_zones", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "temperate zones", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["temperate zones"], "contexts": ["regions between tropics and polar circles"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "temperate zones", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "temperate zones", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "temperate zones", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "temperate zones", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "temperate zones", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "temperate zones", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "temperate zones", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "temperate zones", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "temperate zones", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "temperate zones", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "temperate zones", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "temperate zones", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 719, "stable_key": "clu_79a1a3dd383fddb8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "temperate zones", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "arbustes", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "bushes", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "bushes", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["bushes"], "contexts": ["shrubby plants on islands"]}, {"entity_id": "arbustes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "arbustes", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["arbustes"], "contexts": ["vegetation to be increased", "shrubs"]}, {"entity_id": "shrubs", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "shrubs", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["shrubs"], "contexts": ["small branching plants"]}, {"entity_id": "bushes", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "bushes", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["bushes"], "contexts": ["woody plants smaller than trees"]}, {"entity_id": "arboledas", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "arboledas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["arboledas"], "contexts": ["groves for recreation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "bushes", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "arbustes", "similarity": 0.942}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "bushes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "shrubs", "similarity": 0.915}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "bushes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "bushes", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "bushes", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "arboledas", "similarity": 0.934}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "arbustes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "shrubs", "similarity": 0.94}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "arbustes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "bushes", "similarity": 0.942}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "arbustes", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "arboledas", "similarity": 0.975}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "shrubs", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "arboledas", "similarity": 0.93}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "bushes", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "arboledas", "similarity": 0.934}], "id": 720, "stable_key": "clu_ccdfa099ead58c59", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "shrubs", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "wikidata_id": "Q1096635", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lantana", "wikipedia_extract": "Lantana () is a genus of about 150 species of perennial flowering plants in the verbena family, Verbenaceae. They are native to tropical regions of the Americas and Africa but exist as an introduced species in numerous areas, especially in the Australian-Pacific region, South and Northeastern part of India. The genus includes both herbaceous plants and shrubs growing to 0.5\u20132 m (1.6\u20136.6 ft) tall. Their common names are shrub verbenas or lantanas. The generic name originated in Late Latin, where it refers to the unrelated Viburnum lantana. Lantana's aromatic flower clusters (called umbels) are a mix of red, orange, yellow, or blue and white florets."}}, {"canonical_name": "respira\u00e7ao", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "respiraci\u00f3n", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "respiraci\u00f3n", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["respiraci\u00f3n"], "contexts": ["process of breathing"]}, {"entity_id": "respiration", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "respiration", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["respiration"], "contexts": ["process of breathing"]}, {"entity_id": "respira\u00e7ao", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "respira\u00e7ao", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 3, "variants": ["respira\u00e7ao", "respira\u00e7a\u00f3"], "contexts": ["process of breathing", "respiration"]}, {"entity_id": "respiration", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "respiration", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["respiration"], "contexts": ["process of breathing"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "respiraci\u00f3n", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "respiration", "similarity": 0.997}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "respiraci\u00f3n", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "respira\u00e7ao", "similarity": 0.987}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "respiraci\u00f3n", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "respiration", "similarity": 0.997}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "respiration", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "respira\u00e7ao", "similarity": 0.987}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "respiration", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "respiration", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "respira\u00e7ao", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "respiration", "similarity": 0.987}], "id": 721, "stable_key": "clu_e9566bae2a0ef15b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Respiration", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'Breath' can refer to both the substance exhaled and the process.", "wikidata_id": "Q9530", "wikidata_description": "process that moves air in and out of the lungs", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breathing", "wikipedia_extract": "Breathing (respiration or ventilation) is the rhythmic process of moving air into (inhalation) and out of (exhalation) the lungs to enable gas exchange with the internal environment, primarily to remove carbon dioxide and take in oxygen. All aerobic organisms require oxygen for cellular respiration, which extracts energy from food and produces carbon dioxide as a waste product. External respiration (breathing) brings air to the alveoli where gases move by diffusion; the circulatory system then transports oxygen and carbon dioxide between the lungs and the tissues. In vertebrates with lungs, breathing consists of repeated cycles of inhalation and exhalation through a branched system of airways that conduct air from the nose or mouth to the alveoli. The number of respiratory cycles per minute \u2014 the respiratory or breathing rate \u2014 is a primary vital sign. Under normal conditions, depth and rate of breathing are controlled unconsciously by homeostatic mechanisms that maintain arterial partial pressures of carbon dioxide and oxygen."}}, {"canonical_name": "Sangue di drago", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "RESIN", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "sangre_de_drago_nuestra", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "sangre de drago nuestra", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "RESIN", "count": 1, "variants": ["sangre de drago nuestra"], "contexts": ["European dragon's blood resin"]}, {"entity_id": "sang_de_dragin", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "sang de Dragin", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "RESIN", "count": 1, "variants": ["sang de Dragin"], "contexts": ["red resin"]}, {"entity_id": "fangre_de_drago", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "fangre de Drago", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "RESIN", "count": 1, "variants": ["fangre de Drago"], "contexts": ["dragon's blood resin"]}, {"entity_id": "sangue_di_drago", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Sangue di drago", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "RESIN", "count": 4, "variants": ["Sangue di drago", "sangue di drago", "Sangue di Drago"], "contexts": ["resin from a tree, used in medicine", "dragon's blood resin"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "sangre de drago nuestra", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Sangue di drago", "similarity": 0.906}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "sang de Dragin", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Sangue di drago", "similarity": 0.882}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "fangre de Drago", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Sangue di drago", "similarity": 0.905}], "id": 722, "stable_key": "clu_5e72e3c57e501e1f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Dragon's blood", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "Resinous substance from certain plants, used in medicine and dyes.", "note": "Refers to the resin collected from various species of Daemonorops, Dracaena, and Croton trees.", "wikipedia_extract": "Dragon's blood is a bright red resin that is obtained from different species of a number of distinct plant genera: Calamus spp. (previously Daemonorops) also including Calamus rotang, Croton, Dracaena and Pterocarpus. The red resin has been in continuous use since ancient times as varnish, medicine, incense, pigment, and dye. A great  degree of confusion existed for the ancients in regard to the source and identity of dragon's blood. Some medieval encyclopedias claimed its source as the literal blood of elephants and dragons who had perished in mortal combat. The resin of Dracaena species, \"true\" dragon's blood, and the very poisonous mineral cinnabar (mercury sulfide) were often confused by the ancient Romans. In ancient China, little or no distinction was made among the types of dragon's blood from the different species."}}, {"canonical_name": "Madagascar", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 4, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "madagascar", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Madagascar", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Madagascar"], "contexts": ["island nation", "island off the coast of Africa"]}, {"entity_id": "madagascar", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Madagascar", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Madagascar"], "contexts": ["island with a certain areal surface"]}, {"entity_id": "madagascar", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Madagascar", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Madagascar", "Madag\u00e1scar"], "contexts": ["Island off the coast of Africa", "northeast of this island"]}, {"entity_id": "madagascar", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Madagascar", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Madagascar"], "contexts": ["island country off Africa"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name