led by Yztacpatli"]}, {"entity_id": "lombrigas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "lombrigas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 5, "variants": ["lombrigas"], "contexts": ["Worms, treated with medicine", "treated with anacardo"]}, {"entity_id": "lombrigas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "lombrigas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 56, "variants": ["lombrigas", "Lombrigas", "as lombricas", "lombri-", "lombrias", "lombricagas", "lombriga", "lombrigas Afcaridas", "lonmbrigas"], "contexts": ["condition treated by remedy", "worms"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "lombrices", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "lombrigas", "similarity": 0.915}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "lombrices", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "lombrigas", "similarity": 0.915}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "lombrigas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "lombrigas", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 849, "stable_key": "clu_8ed780be880167de", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "worms", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "Intestinal parasites causing ailments.", "note": "The terms 'lombrigas' and 'lumbr\u00edgas' are Portuguese for worms.", "wikipedia_extract": "Parasitic worms, also known as helminths, are a polyphyletic group of large macroparasites; adults can generally be seen with the naked eye. Many are intestinal worms that are soil-transmitted and infect the gastrointestinal tract. Other parasitic worms such as  schistosomes reside in blood vessels. Some parasitic worms, including leeches and monogeneans, are ectoparasites \u2013  thus, they are not classified as helminths, which are endoparasites. Parasitic worms live in and feed in living hosts. They receive nourishment and protection while disrupting their hosts' ability to absorb nutrients."}}, {"canonical_name": "August", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 63, "members": [{"entity_id": "august", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "August", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 50, "variants": ["August"], "contexts": ["month for flowering", "month of the year"]}, {"entity_id": "agosto", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Agosto", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 12, "variants": ["Agosto"], "contexts": ["herb used in remedies", "month for harvesting jarro root"]}, {"entity_id": "august", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "August", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["August"], "contexts": ["month of observation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "August", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Agosto", "similarity": 0.933}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "August", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "August", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Agosto", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "August", "similarity": 0.933}], "id": 850, "stable_key": "clu_4d14b9ee89ebd428", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "August", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "The eighth month of the Gregorian calendar, associated with agricultural cycles.", "note": "Both terms refer to the month of August, with contexts related to agricultural and medicinal practices.", "wikipedia_extract": "August is the eighth month of the year in the Julian and Gregorian calendars. Its length is 31 days. In the Southern Hemisphere, August is the seasonal equivalent of February in the Northern Hemisphere. In the Northern Hemisphere, August falls in summer. In the Southern Hemisphere, the month falls during winter. In many European countries, August is the holiday month for most workers."}}, {"canonical_name": "Quinaquina", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 62, "members": [{"entity_id": "quinquina", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "quinquina", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["quinquina"], "contexts": ["plant used to treat fever"]}, {"entity_id": "quinquina", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "quinquina", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 13, "variants": ["quinquina", "Quinquina", "quinquas", "quinquinas"], "contexts": ["bark exported from the New World", "fever-reducing plant, also called cascarillas"]}, {"entity_id": "quinaquina", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Quinaquina", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 48, "variants": ["Quinaquina", "AQuinaquina", "Aquinaquin", "Qui-naquina", "Quinaqui", "quinaquina"], "contexts": ["cinchona bark", "remedy for fevers"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "quinquina", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "quinquina", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "quinquina", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Quinaquina", "similarity": 0.924}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "quinquina", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Quinaquina", "similarity": 0.924}], "id": 851, "stable_key": "clu_cb55afc3a3ed2559", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Cinchona (pronounced  or ) is a genus of flowering plants in the family Rubiaceae containing at least 23 species of trees and shrubs. All are native to the tropical Andean forests of western South America. A few species are reportedly naturalized in Central America, Jamaica, French Polynesia, Sulawesi, Saint Helena in the South Atlantic, and S\u00e3o Tom\u00e9 and Pr\u00edncipe off the coast of tropical Africa, and others have been cultivated in India and Java, where they have formed hybrids. Cinchona has been historically sought after for its medicinal value, as the bark of several species yields quinine and other alkaloids. These were the only effective treatments against malaria during the height of European colonialism, which made them of great economic and political importance. Trees in the genus are also known as fever trees because of their antimalarial properties."}}, {"canonical_name": "Rondeletius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 62, "members": [{"entity_id": "rondeletius", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Rondeletius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Rondeletius"], "contexts": ["physician questioning gold's efficacy", "author cited for experimental testimony"]}, {"entity_id": "rondellitius", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Rondellitius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rondellitius"], "contexts": ["early modern scholar"]}, {"entity_id": "rondeletius", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Rondeletius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 58, "variants": ["Rondeletius", "Rondel", "Rondel.", "Rondele", "Rondelecio", "Rondeletins", "Rondeletio."], "contexts": ["author of Methodus Curandi Morbi", "author who wrote about Gotta Coral"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Rondeletius", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Rondellitius", "similarity": 0.981}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Rondeletius", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Rondeletius", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Rondellitius", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Rondeletius", "similarity": 0.981}], "id": 852, "stable_key": "clu_1b7ece8cfe34cbac", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Guillaume Rondelet", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1507, "death_year": 1566, "description": "French physician, naturalist, and anatomist.", "wikidata_id": "Q960658", "wikidata_description": "French physician (1507-1566)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guillaume%20Rondelet", "wikipedia_extract": "Guillaume Rondelet (27 September 1507 \u2013 30 July 1566), also known as Rondeletus/Rondeletius, was Regius professor of medicine at the University of Montpellier in southern France and Chancellor of the University between 1556 and his death in 1566. He achieved renown as an anatomist and a naturalist with a particular interest in botany and ichthyology. His major work was a lengthy treatise on marine animals, which took two years to write and became a standard reference work for about a century afterwards, but his lasting impact lay in his education of a roster of star pupils who became leading figures in the world of late-16th century science. Rondelet was born in Montpellier in 1507. His father was an aromatarius, a combination of pharmacist, grocer and druggist. His father died while he was a child and he was brought up in the care of his elder brother."}}, {"canonical_name": "soul", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 60, "members": [{"entity_id": "soul", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "soul", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["soul", "Soul"], "contexts": ["immaterial essence of a living being", "attribute of celestial bodies"]}, {"entity_id": "soul", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "soul", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 54, "variants": ["soul", "Soul"], "contexts": ["metaphysical entity", "immaterial essence of a person"]}, {"entity_id": "souls", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Souls", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Souls"], "contexts": ["immaterial entities"]}, {"entity_id": "soul", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "soul", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["soul"], "contexts": ["recipient of inspiration about laws", "animating principle"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "soul", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "soul", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "soul", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Souls", "similarity": 0.846}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "soul", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "soul", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "soul", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "soul", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Souls", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "soul", "similarity": 0.846}], "id": 853, "stable_key": "clu_92c98b200557678d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Soul", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "thought", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 60, "members": [{"entity_id": "thinking", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "thinking", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 21, "variants": ["thinking"], "contexts": ["mental process of forming thoughts", "mental activity"]}, {"entity_id": "thought", "book_id": "first_p