1574", "source_name": "quebradura", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "quebraduras", "similarity": 0.955}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "quebradura", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "quebraduras", "similarity": 0.955}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "quebraduras", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "quebraduras", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1129, "stable_key": "clu_d1f5c3bb962a6d58", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Fracture", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Fracture", "note": "The term 'quebradura' in Portuguese and Spanish can refer to both fractures (broken bones) and hernias. Given the contexts mentioning 'broken bones or injuries' and 'fractures or breaks', 'fracture' is the primary referent. However, 'hernia' should be considered a secondary possibility depending on the specific text."}}, {"canonical_name": "metaes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 15, "members": [{"entity_id": "metaes", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "metaes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["metaes"], "contexts": ["metals", "frequently used in Indian medicine"]}, {"entity_id": "meta\u00ech", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "meta\u00ech", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["meta\u00ech"], "contexts": ["metals, processed by alchemists"]}, {"entity_id": "metaes", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "metaes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 12, "variants": ["metaes"], "contexts": ["metals", "metals, natural materials"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "metaes", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "meta\u00ech", "similarity": 0.884}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "metaes", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "metaes", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "meta\u00ech", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "metaes", "similarity": 0.884}], "id": 1130, "stable_key": "clu_7907da80da20d2e2-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Metal", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q11426", "wikidata_description": "element, compound, or alloy that is a good conductor of both electricity and heat", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metal", "note": "The Latin 'rerum metallicarum' directly translates to 'of metallic things' or 'metallic substances'. The context of electrical conductivity reinforces this.", "wikipedia_extract": "A metal (from Ancient Greek  \u03bc\u03ad\u03c4\u03b1\u03bb\u03bb\u03bf\u03bd (m\u00e9tallon) 'mine, quarry, metal') is a material that, when polished or fractured, shows a lustrous appearance, and conducts electricity and heat relatively well. These properties are all associated with having electrons available at the Fermi level, as opposed to nonmetallic materials which do not. Metals are typically ductile (can be drawn into a wire) and malleable (can be shaped via hammering or pressing). A metal may be a chemical element, such as iron, an alloy, such as stainless steel, or a molecular compound, such as polymeric sulfur nitride. The general science of metals is called metallurgy, a subtopic of materials science; aspects of their electronic and thermal properties are also within the scope of condensed matter physics and solid-state chemistry, as it is a multidisciplinary topic. In colloquial use, materials such as steel alloys are referred to as metals, while others, such as polymers, wood, or ceramics are nonmetallic materials."}}, {"canonical_name": "\u00eatres vivants", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "\u00eatres_vivants", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "\u00eatres vivants", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 10, "variants": ["\u00eatres vivants"], "contexts": ["living beings", "organisms that are alive"]}, {"entity_id": "\u00eatres_vivants", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "\u00eatres vivants", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["\u00eatres vivants"], "contexts": ["living organisms", "living beings, including humans"]}, {"entity_id": "viventes", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "viventes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["viventes"], "contexts": ["living beings", "Latin adjective meaning 'living'"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "\u00eatres vivants", "target_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "target_name": "\u00eatres vivants", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "\u00eatres vivants", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "viventes", "similarity": 0.934}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "\u00eatres vivants", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "viventes", "similarity": 0.934}], "id": 1131, "stable_key": "clu_18b221ab26d29042", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Organism", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q7239", "wikidata_description": "any individual living being or physical living system", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Organism", "wikipedia_extract": "An organism is any living thing that functions as an individual. Such a definition raises more problems than it solves, not least because the concept of an individual is also difficult. Several criteria, few of which are widely accepted, have been proposed to define what constitutes an organism. Among the most common is that an organism has autonomous reproduction, growth, and metabolism. This would exclude viruses, even though they evolve like organisms. Other problematic cases include colonial organisms; a colony of eusocial insects is organised adaptively, and has germ-soma specialisation, with some insects reproducing, others not, like cells in an animal's body."}}, {"canonical_name": "hydrog\u00e8ne", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "hydrog\u00e8ne", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "hydrog\u00e8ne", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 8, "variants": ["hydrog\u00e8ne"], "contexts": ["element that combines with oxygen", "element in water formation"]}, {"entity_id": "hydrogen_gas", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "hydrogen gas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["hydrogen gas"], "contexts": ["gas potentially involved in combustion"]}, {"entity_id": "hydrogen", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "hydrogen", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 5, "variants": ["hydrogen"], "contexts": ["chemical element found in candles", "element assimilated by plants"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "hydrog\u00e8ne", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "hydrogen gas", "similarity": 0.924}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "hydrog\u00e8ne", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "hydrogen", "similarity": 0.981}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "hydrogen gas", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "hydrogen", "similarity": 0.926}], "id": 1132, "stable_key": "clu_3b569751cb7e591c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hydrogen", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "wikidata_id": "Q556", "wikidata_description": "chemical element with symbol H and atomic number 1", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydrogen", "wikipedia_extract": "Hydrogen is a chemical element; it has the symbol H and atomic number 1. It is the lightest and most abundant chemical element in the universe, constituting about 75% of all normal matter. Under standard conditions, hydrogen is a gas of diatomic molecules with the formula H2, called dihydrogen, or sometimes hydrogen gas, molecular hydrogen, or simply hydrogen. Dihydrogen is colorless, odorless, non-toxic, and highly combustible. Stars, including the Sun, mainly consist of hydrogen in a plasma state, while on Earth, hydrogen is found as the gas H2 (dihydrogen) and in molecules, such as in water and organic compounds. The most common isotope of hydrogen, 1H, consists of one proton, one electron, and no neutrons."}}, {"canonical_name": "Philosophische Studien", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "\u00e9tudes_philosophiques", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "\u00e9tudes philosophiques", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["\u00e9tudes philosophiques"], "contexts": ["studies focusing on philosophical aspects"]}, {"entity_id": "philosophische_studien", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Philosophische Studien", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 12, "variants": ["Philosophische Studien"], "contexts": ["Wundt's periodical", "journal cited for Lange's hypothesis"]}, {"entity_id": "philosophical_research", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "philosophical research", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["philosophical research"], "contexts": ["study of general laws"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "\u00e9tudes philosophiques", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Philosophische Studien", "similarity": 0.966}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "\u00e9tudes philosophiques", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "philosophical research", "similarity": 0.893}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Philosophische Studien", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "philosophical research", "similarity": 0.893}], "id": 1133, "stable_key": "clu_dba027af02b7c877", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Philosophische Studien", "confidence": "high", "type": "text"}}, {"canonical_name": "Ancients", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "ancients", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Ancients", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 12, "variants": ["Ancients"], "contexts": ["historical figures promoting learning", "ancient thinkers and their ideas"]}, {"entity_id": "ancients", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "ancients", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["ancients"], "contexts": ["ancient thinkers and philosophers"]}, {"entity_id": "ancients", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "ancients", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["ancients"], "contexts": ["knew of electric light phenomenon as omen"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Ancients", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "ancients", "similarity": 0.978}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Ancients", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "ancients", "similarity": 0.978}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "ancients", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "ancients", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1134, "stable_key": "clu_d42612f908cb2f23", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ancients", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Ancient thinkers and authorities from historical past", "wikidata_id": "Q57288780", "wikidata_description": "scientific article published on 09 October 2018"}}, {"canonical_name": "Deer", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "deer", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Deer", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 7, "variants": ["Deer"], "contexts": ["quadrupedes with different leg joints", "animal discussed in chapter"]}, {"entity_id": "deer", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Deer", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Deer"], "contexts": ["animal fed by Beech in forests", "animals fed in parks, forrests, and chases"]}, {"entity_id": "deer", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "deer", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["deer"], "contexts": ["herbivorous mammal", "ungulate prey"]}, {"entity_id": "rein-deer", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "rein-deer", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["rein-deer"], "contexts": ["domesticated cervid species"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Deer", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Deer", "similarity": 0.812}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Deer", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "deer", "similarity": 0.991}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Deer", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "rein-deer", "similarity": 0.91}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Deer", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "deer", "similarity": 0.799}], "id": 1135, "stable_key": "clu_f42af70998c827f2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Deer", "confidence": "high", "type": "ANIMAL", "family": "Cervidae", "description": "Hoofed mammals hunted for meat and other products.", "wikipedia_extract": "A deer (pl.: deer) or true deer is a hoofed ruminant ungulate of the family Cervidae (informally the deer family). Cervidae is divided into subfamilies Cervinae (which includes, among others, muntjac, elk (wapiti), red deer, and fallow deer) and Capreolinae (which includes, among others reindeer (caribou), white-tailed deer, roe deer, and moose). Male deer of almost all species (except the water deer), as well as female reindeer, grow and shed new antlers each year. These antlers are bony extensions of the skull and are often used for combat between males. The musk deer (Moschidae) of Asia and chevrotains (Tragulidae) of tropical African and Asian forests are separate families that are also in the ruminant clade Ruminantia; they are not especially closely related to Cervidae. Deer appear in art from Paleolithic cave paintings onwards, and they have played a role in mythology, religion, and literature throughout history, as well as in heraldry, such as red deer that appear in the coat of arms of \u00c5land."}}, {"canonical_name": "Phrensie", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "phrensies", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Phrensies", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Phrensies"], "contexts": ["state of madness or delirium", "mental state treated with Opium"]}, {"entity_id": "phrensie", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Phrensie", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Phrensie", "Phrenetick"], "contexts": ["feverish delirium", "condition from eating Hemlock"]}, {"entity_id": "phrensies", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Phrensies", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Phrensies"], "contexts": ["mental derangement", "disturbed mental state"]}, {"entity_id": "phrene\u017fis", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Phrene\u017fis", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Phrene\u017fis", "Phrenes.", "Phrenitid", "phreneticorum"], "contexts": ["Condition of phrenitis", "illness of the frenetic"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Phrensies", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Phrensie", "similarity": 0.947}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Phrensies", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Phrensies", "similarity": 0.805}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Phrensie", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Phrene\u017fis", "similarity": 0.887}], "id": 1136, "stable_key": "clu_b841739754f498fe-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Delirium", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Delirium", "wikidata_id": "Q160796", "wikidata_description": "severe confusion that develops quickly, and often fluctuates in intensity", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delirium", "note": "Historically, 'phrensy' could encompass a broader range of mental disturbances, but in these contexts, it strongly points to delirium or a state of acute mental derangement, often associated with fever.", "wikipedia_extract": "Delirium (formerly acute confusional state, an ambiguous term that is now discouraged) is a specific state of acute confusion attributable to the direct physiological consequence of a medical condition, effects of a psychoactive substance, or multiple causes, which usually develops over the course of hours to days. As a syndrome, delirium presents with disturbances in attention, awareness, and higher-order cognition. People with delirium may experience other neuropsychiatric disturbances including changes in psychomotor activity (e.g., hyperactive, hypoactive, or mixed level of activity), disrupted sleep-wake cycle, emotional disturbances, disturbances of consciousness, or altered state of consciousness, as well as perceptual disturbances (e.g., hallucinations and delusions), although these features are not required for diagnosis. Diagnostically, delirium encompasses both the syndrome of acute confusion and its underlying organic process known as an acute encephalopathy. The cause of delirium may be either a disease process inside the brain or a process outside the brain that nonetheless affects the brain. Delirium may be the result of an underlying medical condition (e.g., infection or hypoxia), side effect of a medication such as diphenhydramine, promethazine, and dicyclomine, substance intoxication (e.g., opioids or hallucinogenic deliriants), substance withdrawal (e.g., alcohol or sedatives), or from multiple factors affecting one's overall health (e.g., malnutrition, pain, etc.)."}}, {"canonical_name": "South pole", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "north_pole", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "North Pole", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["North Pole"], "contexts": ["northernmost point of Earth's axis"]}, {"entity_id": "south_pole", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "South Pole", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["south pole", "South Pole"], "contexts": ["region of geographical discoveries", "location of magnetic observations"]}, {"entity_id": "south_pole", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "South pole", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 11, "variants": ["South pole", "south pole", "South Pole"], "contexts": ["southernmost point of Earth", "southernmost point of Earth's axis"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "North Pole", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "South Pole", "similarity": 0.941}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "North Pole", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "South pole", "similarity": 0.915}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "South Pole", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "South pole", "similarity": 0.961}], "id": 1137, "stable_key": "clu_d5a47245113a97b7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Earth's poles", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Antarctica/Arctic", "note": "The cluster includes both 'North Pole' and 'South Pole', referring to the geographic poles of the Earth."}}, {"canonical_name": "Hair", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "hair", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Hair", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hair"], "contexts": ["extimulating part used to kill dragon"]}, {"entity_id": "hair", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Hair", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 11, "variants": ["Hair", "hair"], "contexts": ["body part, plant helps shedding", "body part that can be made black"]}, {"entity_id": "hair", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "hair", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["hair"], "contexts": ["filament from animal skin", "thin filament used for comparison"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Hair", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Hair", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Hair", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "hair", "similarity": 0.996}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Hair", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "hair", "similarity": 0.996}], "id": 1138, "stable_key": "clu_333ed6d580e1f655", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hair", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "While 'hair' can refer to plant structures, the context 'filament from animal skin' and its use in killing dragons strongly suggests animal hair.", "wikidata_id": "Q866324", "wikidata_description": "organ found in the dermal layer of the mammalian skin that regulates hair growth", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hair%20follicle"}}, {"canonical_name": "Spitzbergen", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "spitzberg", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "Spitzberg", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Spitzberg"], "contexts": ["continuity of eastern Greenland coast", "archipelago in Arctic Ocean"]}, {"entity_id": "spitzbergen", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Spitzbergen", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Spitzbergen"], "contexts": ["location visited by Captain Sabine"]}, {"entity_id": "spitzbergen", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Spitzbergen", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 10, "variants": ["Spitzbergen"], "contexts": ["Arctic archipelago", "location with slow magnetic declination change"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Spitzberg", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Spitzbergen", "similarity": 0.928}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Spitzberg", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Spitzbergen", "similarity": 0.928}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Spitzbergen", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Spitzbergen", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1139, "stable_key": "clu_92c6a64d9251636d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Svalbard", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Norway", "note": "Arctic archipelago, historically known as Spitsbergen.", "wikidata_id": "Q21162", "wikidata_description": "largest island of the Svalbard archipelago in northern Norway", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spitsbergen", "wikipedia_extract": "Spitsbergen (Urban East Norwegian: [\u02c8sp\u026a\u0300ts\u02ccb\u00e6r\u0261n\u0329]; formerly known as West Spitsbergen; Norwegian: Vest Spitsbergen or Vestspitsbergen [\u02c8v\u025b\u0302st\u02ccsp\u026atsb\u00e6r\u0261n\u0329], also sometimes spelled Spitzbergen) is the largest and the only permanently populated island of the Svalbard archipelago in northern Norway in the Arctic Ocean. Constituting the westernmost bulk of the archipelago, it borders the Arctic Ocean, the Norwegian Sea and the Greenland Sea. Spitsbergen covers an area of 37,673 km2 (14,546 sq mi), making it the largest island in Norway and the 36th largest in the world. The administrative centre is Longyearbyen. Other settlements, in addition to research outposts, are the mining community of Barentsburg, the research community of Ny-\u00c5lesund, and the mining outpost of Sveagruva. Spitsbergen was covered in 21,977 km2 (8,485 sq mi) of ice in 1999, which was approximately 58.5% of the island's total area."}}, {"canonical_name": "organic being", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "organic_being", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "organic being", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 11, "variants": ["organic being"], "contexts": ["general term for living organism", "living organism"]}, {"entity_id": "organic_beings", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "organic beings", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["organic beings"], "contexts": ["living entities in the natural world", "living organisms"]}, {"entity_id": "organic_beings", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "organic beings", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["organic beings"], "contexts": ["living organisms"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "organic being", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "organic beings", "similarity": 0.93}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "organic being", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "organic beings", "similarity": 0.93}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "organic beings", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "organic beings", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1140, "stable_key": "clu_18b221ab26d29042-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Organism", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q7239", "wikidata_description": "any individual living being or physical living system", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Organism", "wikipedia_extract": "An organism is any living thing that functions as an individual. Such a definition raises more problems than it solves, not least because the concept of an individual is also difficult. Several criteria, few of which are widely accepted, have been proposed to define what constitutes an organism. Among the most common is that an organism has autonomous reproduction, growth, and metabolism. This would exclude viruses, even though they evolve like organisms. Other problematic cases include colonial organisms; a colony of eusocial insects is organised adaptively, and has germ-soma specialisation, with some insects reproducing, others not, like cells in an animal's body."}}, {"canonical_name": "migration", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "migration", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "migration", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 10, "variants": ["migration"], "contexts": ["movement of species from one region to another", "movement of organisms to new regions"]}, {"entity_id": "migrations", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "migrations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["migrations"], "contexts": ["movement of organisms from one place to another"]}, {"entity_id": "migration", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "migration", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["migration"], "contexts": ["false hypothesis in botanical language", "seasonal movement of animals"]}, {"entity_id": "migration", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "migration", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["migration"], "contexts": ["movement of people"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "migration", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "migration", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "migration", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "migration", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "migrations", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "migration", "similarity": 0.961}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "migrations", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "migration", "similarity": 0.961}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "migration", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "migration", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1141, "stable_key": "clu_a38a8727d15da4b4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Migration", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q193145", "wikidata_description": "movement of animals from place to place, usually seasonal", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Animal%20migration", "wikipedia_extract": "Animal migration is the relatively long-distance movement of individual animals, usually on a seasonal basis. It is the most common form of migration in ecology. It is found in all major animal groups, including birds, mammals, fish, reptiles, amphibians, insects, and crustaceans. The cause of migration may be local climate, local availability of food, the season of the year or for mating. To be counted as a true migration, and not just a local dispersal or irruption, the movement of the animals should be an annual or seasonal occurrence, or a major habitat change as part of their life. An annual event could include Northern Hemisphere birds migrating south for the winter, or wildebeest migrating annually for seasonal grazing."}}, {"canonical_name": "fauna", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "fauna", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "fauna", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["fauna"], "contexts": ["animal life of a particular region or period", "animal life of a region/period"]}, {"entity_id": "faunas", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "faunas", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["faunas"], "contexts": ["assemblages of animals from earlier world", "animal life of a region/period"]}, {"entity_id": "fauna", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "fauna", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["fauna"], "contexts": ["animal life of a region"]}, {"entity_id": "fauna", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "fauna", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 7, "variants": ["Fauna", "fauna"], "contexts": ["animal life of a region", "collective term for animal life"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "fauna", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "faunas", "similarity": 0.973}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "fauna", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "fauna", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "fauna", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "fauna", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "faunas", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "fauna", "similarity": 0.973}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "fauna", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "fauna", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1142, "stable_key": "clu_facbe551df5453bc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Fauna", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q104021", "wikidata_description": "set of animal species in any particular region and time", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fauna"}}, {"canonical_name": "Scandinave", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "scandinavia", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Scandinavia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Scandinavia"], "contexts": ["region with Silurian deposits", "northern region with arctic plants"]}, {"entity_id": "scandinave", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Scandinave", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 7, "variants": ["Scandinave", "Scandinaves", "Scandinavie"], "contexts": ["origin of granite blocks", "granite covering sandy plains"]}, {"entity_id": "p\u00e9ninsule_scandinave", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "p\u00e9ninsule Scandinave", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["p\u00e9ninsule Scandinave"], "contexts": ["origin of scattered rocks"]}, {"entity_id": "scandinavian_peninsula", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Scandinavian peninsula", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 3, "variants": ["Scandinavian peninsula"], "contexts": ["region experiencing geological uplift", "peninsula north of Europe"]}, {"entity_id": "scandinavia", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Scandinavia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Scandinavia"], "contexts": ["northern extremity climate ameliorated"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Scandinavia", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Scandinave", "similarity": 0.986}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Scandinavia", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "p\u00e9ninsule Scandinave", "similarity": 0.925}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Scandinavia", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Scandinavian peninsula", "similarity": 0.919}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Scandinavia", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Scandinavia", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Scandinave", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Scandinavian peninsula", "similarity": 0.913}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Scandinave", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Scandinavia", "similarity": 0.986}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "p\u00e9ninsule Scandinave", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Scandinavian peninsula", "similarity": 0.959}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "p\u00e9ninsule Scandinave", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Scandinavia", "similarity": 0.925}], "id": 1143, "stable_key": "clu_3a3fe3ebc80ec32d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Scandinavia", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A peninsula and region in Northern Europe with specific geological deposits.", "wikipedia_extract": "Scandinavia is a subregion of northern Europe, with strong historical, cultural, and linguistic ties between its constituent peoples. Scandinavia most commonly refers to Denmark, Norway, and Sweden. It can sometimes also refer to the Scandinavian Peninsula (which excludes Denmark but includes a part of northern Finland). In English usage, Scandinavia is sometimes used as a synonym for Nordic countries. Iceland and the Faroe Islands are sometimes included in Scandinavia for their ethnolinguistic relations with Sweden, Norway and Denmark. While Finland differs from other Nordic countries in this respect, some authors call it Scandinavian due to its economic and cultural similarities."}}, {"canonical_name": "melancolia", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "melancol\u00eda", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "melancol\u00eda", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 3, "variants": ["melancol\u00eda"], "contexts": ["melancholy, treated by root powder", "condition treated by the plant"]}, {"entity_id": "melancolia", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "melancolia", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 4, "variants": ["melancolia", "melancholias", "melancholicos", "melanchol\u00edas"], "contexts": ["melancholy treated by the water", "people suffering from melancholy"]}, {"entity_id": "melancolia", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "melancolia", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 7, "variants": ["melancolia"], "contexts": ["treated by Damasquo", "humor associated with illness"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "melancol\u00eda", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "melancolia", "similarity": 0.983}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "melancol\u00eda", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "melancolia", "similarity": 0.983}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "melancolia", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "melancolia", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1144, "stable_key": "clu_5e6ae3a64d00eeee-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Melancholy", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Melancholy", "note": "While 'sadness' is a direct translation, in the context of early modern medicine, 'tristeza' often referred to a more profound, melancholic state akin to what was historically understood as melancholy, a recognized condition with potential physical and mental symptoms.", "wikidata_id": "Q192077", "wikidata_description": "mood disorder of non-specific depression", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melancholia", "wikipedia_extract": "Melancholia or melancholy (Ancient Greek: \u03bc\u03b5\u03bb\u03b1\u03b3\u03c7\u03bf\u03bb\u03af\u03b1, romanized: melanchol\u00eda; from \u03bc\u03ad\u03bb\u03b1\u03b9\u03bd\u03b1 \u03c7\u03bf\u03bb\u03ae, m\u00e9laina chol\u1e17, 'black bile') is a concept found throughout ancient, medieval, and premodern medicine in Europe that describes a condition characterized by markedly depressed mood, bodily complaints, and sometimes hallucinations and delusions. Besides a pathological condition, melancholy could also refer to a mood or temperament and at times it was even used as a description of the human condition in general. Melancholy (or more precisely the 'black bile', from which melancholy derives its name) was regarded as one of the four temperaments matching the four humours. Until the 18th century, doctors and other scholars classified melancholic conditions as such by their perceived common cause \u2013 an excess of a notional fluid known as \"black bile\", which was commonly linked to the spleen. Hippocrates and other ancient physicians described melancholia as a distinct disease with mental and physical symptoms, including persistent fears and despondencies, poor appetite, abulia, sleeplessness, irritability, and agitation. Later, fixed delusions were added by Galen and other physicians to the list of symptoms.", "description": "A state of prolonged sadness, low spirits, or humors."}}, {"canonical_name": "palms", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "palmas", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "palmas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["PALMAS", "palmas"], "contexts": ["palms", "fronds of the palm tree"]}, {"entity_id": "palms", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "palms", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 9, "variants": ["palms"], "contexts": ["majestic tropical trees", "trees found in certain climates"]}, {"entity_id": "palms", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "palms", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["palms"], "contexts": ["tropical trees", "plants with one seed-lobe"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "palmas", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "palms", "similarity": 0.919}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "palmas", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "palms", "similarity": 0.919}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "palms", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "palms", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1145, "stable_key": "clu_8ea497f2b80d2e9f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Palm trees", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "family": "Arecaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q14080", "wikidata_description": "family of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arecaceae"}}, {"canonical_name": "Chingualas", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "chichimecos", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "chichimecos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["chichimecos"], "contexts": ["indigenous people of Mexico"]}, {"entity_id": "chingualas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Chingualas", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 8, "variants": ["Chingualas", "Chin", "Chinas", "Chingalas", "Chingallas", "Chtngalas", "chin"], "contexts": ["people of Ceylon", "language"]}, {"entity_id": "chenticos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Chenticos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 5, "variants": ["Chenticos"], "contexts": ["a group referred to by authors", "physicians or learned men"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "chichimecos", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Chingualas", "similarity": 0.868}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Chingualas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Chenticos", "similarity": 0.859}], "id": 1146, "stable_key": "clu_0a319f64d25d293d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sinhalese people", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "The majority ethnic group of Sri Lanka (formerly Ceylon)", "note": "The OCR likely mistranscribed 'Sinhalese' as 'Chingualas' and 'Chenticos'.", "wikidata_id": "Q932244", "wikidata_description": "ethnic group of Sri Lanka"}}, {"canonical_name": "Mont-Blanc", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "mont-blanc", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Mont-Blanc", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 6, "variants": ["Mont-Blanc", "Montblanc"], "contexts": ["mountain peak in France/Italy", "mountain peak"]}, {"entity_id": "mont_blanc", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Mont Blanc", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 6, "variants": ["Mont Blanc"], "contexts": ["mountain for comparison of height", "mountain, reference for height comparison"]}, {"entity_id": "mount_blanc", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Mount Blanc", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mount Blanc"], "contexts": ["mountain for elevation comparison"]}, {"entity_id": "mont_blanc", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Mont Blanc", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mont Blanc"], "contexts": ["mountain height for comparison"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Mont-Blanc", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Mont Blanc", "similarity": 0.904}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Mont-Blanc", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Mount Blanc", "similarity": 0.885}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Mont-Blanc", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Mont Blanc", "similarity": 0.904}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Mont Blanc", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Mont Blanc", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Mount Blanc", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Mont Blanc", "similarity": 0.983}], "id": 1147, "stable_key": "clu_c85d8d95128ef58f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mont Blanc", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "France", "wikidata_id": "Q19350231", "wikidata_description": "street in Amersfoort, the Netherlands"}}, {"canonical_name": "Companhia de JESUS", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "compania_de_j\u00e9sus", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Compania de J\u00e9sus", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Compania de J\u00e9sus"], "contexts": ["religious order"]}, {"entity_id": "compailia_de_j\u00e9sus", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Compailia de J\u00e9sus", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Compailia de J\u00e9sus"], "contexts": ["religious order"]}, {"entity_id": "companhia_de_jesus", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Companhia de Jesus", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["Companhia de Jesus", "companhia de Jesus"], "contexts": ["religious order", "religious order involved in pearl fishery"]}, {"entity_id": "companhia_de_jesus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Companhia de JESUS", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 7, "variants": ["Companhia de JESUS", "Companhiade JESU"], "contexts": ["religious order"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Compania de J\u00e9sus", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Companhia de Jesus", "similarity": 0.962}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Compania de J\u00e9sus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Companhia de JESUS", "similarity": 0.941}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Compailia de J\u00e9sus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Companhia de JESUS", "similarity": 0.884}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Companhia de Jesus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Companhia de JESUS", "similarity": 0.961}], "id": 1148, "stable_key": "clu_6ff0a69482aa81d6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Society of Jesus", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "A Catholic religious order of pontifical right.", "note": "Also known as the Jesuits.", "wikipedia_extract": "The Society of Jesus (Latin: Societas Iesu; abbreviation: S.J. or SJ), also known as the Jesuit Order or the Jesuits ( JEZH-oo-its, JEZ-ew-; Latin: Iesuitae), is a religious order of clerics regular of pontifical right for men in the Catholic Church. Headquartered in Rome, it was founded in 1540 by Ignatius of Loyola and six companions, with the approval of Pope Paul III. The Society of Jesus is the largest Catholic religious order and has played a significant role in education, charity, humanitarian acts and global policies. Jesuits are engaged in evangelization and apostolic ministry in 112 countries, including education, research, and cultural pursuits. They also conduct retreats, minister in hospitals and parishes, sponsor direct social and humanitarian works, and promote ecumenical dialogue. The Society of Jesus is consecrated under the patronage of Madonna della Strada, a title of the Blessed Virgin Mary, and it is led by a superior general."}}, {"canonical_name": "anacardo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "anacardo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "anacardo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 11, "variants": ["anacardo", "ANACARDO", "Anacardiace", "Anacardo", "anacardus"], "contexts": ["subject of discussion, Greek name for heart", "fruit used for various medicinal purposes"]}, {"entity_id": "anacardi", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "anacardi", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["anacardi"], "contexts": ["nuts used to extract mucilage"]}, {"entity_id": "anacardo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Anacardo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Anacardo", "Anacardos"], "contexts": ["ingredient in compound remedy", "cashews prepared for confection"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "anacardo", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "anacardi", "similarity": 0.915}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "anacardo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Anacardo", "similarity": 0.975}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "anacardi", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Anacardo", "similarity": 0.912}], "id": 1149, "stable_key": "clu_c0a406c1a8f78e38", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "cashew", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Anacardium occidentale", "family": "Anacardiaceae", "description": "A tropical tree and its kidney-shaped nut, used medicinally.", "note": "The Greek name for heart (anacardion) is also referenced, linking to the shape of the nut.", "wikipedia_extract": "Cashew is the common name of a tropical evergreen tree Anacardium occidentale, in the family Anacardiaceae. It is the source of the cashew nut (often simply called a 'cashew') and the cashew apple. The tree can grow as tall as 14 meters (46 feet). The species is native to South America. The dwarf cultivars, growing up to 6 m (20 ft), are the most profitable, maturing sooner and producing greater yields. In 2023, 3.9 million tons of cashew nuts were harvested globally, led by the Ivory Coast and India."}}, {"canonical_name": "elements", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "elements", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "elements", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 9, "variants": ["elements", "Elements"], "contexts": ["elements of philosophy", "fundamental parts"]}, {"entity_id": "elements", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "elements", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["elements"], "contexts": ["strife of the elements", "fundamental natural substances"]}, {"entity_id": "elements", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "elements", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["elements"], "contexts": ["fundamental chemical constituents", "simplest form of matter"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "elements", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "elements", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "elements", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "elements", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "elements", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "elements", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1150, "stable_key": "clu_81cb6dc9e94eee41", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Elements", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'elements' can refer to fundamental components in various contexts (philosophy, chemistry, psychology). The search term 'chemical element' is chosen to cover the most common scientific interpretation, but the context 'fundamental chemical constituents' strongly supports this. 'Elements of philosophy' could also suggest philosophical elements, but the presence of 'fundamental chemical constituents' leans towards a scientific interpretation.", "wikidata_id": "Q11344", "wikidata_description": "species of atoms having the same number of protons in the atomic nucleus and the same chemical properties, but not necessarily the same mass, or the same stability (or half-lifetime if they are unstable)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chemical%20element", "wikipedia_extract": "A chemical element is a species of atom defined by its number of protons. The number of protons is called the atomic number of that element. For example, oxygen has an atomic number of 8: each oxygen atom has 8 protons in its nucleus. Atoms of the same element can have different numbers of neutrons in their nuclei, known as isotopes of the element. Atoms of one element can be transformed into atoms of a different element in nuclear reactions, which change an atom's atomic number. Almost all baryonic matter in the universe is composed of elements (among rare exceptions are neutron stars)."}}, {"canonical_name": "words", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "words", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "words", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 9, "variants": ["words"], "contexts": ["units of language", "symbols for things, contract relation by vicinity"]}, {"entity_id": "word", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "word", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["word"], "contexts": ["unit of language", "linguistic unit of expression"]}, {"entity_id": "words", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "words", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["words"], "contexts": ["units of language"]}, {"entity_id": "words", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "words", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["words"], "contexts": ["conveying ideas"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "words", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "words", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "words", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "words", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "word", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "words", "similarity": 0.848}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "word", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "words", "similarity": 0.848}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "words", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "words", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1151, "stable_key": "clu_f2123da602d35eb8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Word", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This refers to the linguistic unit, not the concept of 'speaking' or 'utterance'.", "wikidata_id": "Q8171", "wikidata_description": "smallest linguistic element that will be said in isolation with semantic or pragmatic content", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Word", "wikipedia_extract": "A word is a basic element of language that carries meaning, can be used on its own, and is uninterruptible. Despite the fact that language speakers often have an intuitive grasp of what a word is, there is no consensus among linguists on its definition and numerous attempts to find specific criteria of the concept remain controversial. Different standards have been proposed, depending on the theoretical background and descriptive context; these do not converge on a single definition. Some specific definitions of the term \"word\" are employed to convey its different meanings at different levels of description, for example based on phonological, grammatical or orthographic basis. Others suggest that the concept is simply a convention used in everyday situations. The concept of \"word\" is distinguished from that of a morpheme, which is the smallest unit of language that has a meaning, even if it cannot stand on its own."}}, {"canonical_name": "alcohol", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "alcohol", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "alcohol", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["alcohol"], "contexts": ["chemical that can affect consciousness", "intoxicant affecting reaction time"]}, {"entity_id": "alcohol", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "alcohol", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 9, "variants": ["alcohol"], "contexts": ["solvent used in heating experiments", "liquid in capillary action"]}, {"entity_id": "alcohol", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "alcohol", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["alcohol"], "contexts": ["intoxicating liquid", "chemical compound separated by distillation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "alcohol", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "alcohol", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "alcohol", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "alcohol", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "alcohol", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "alcohol", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1152, "stable_key": "clu_67b6f1fe94c5d738-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Alcohol", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "wikidata_id": "Q153", "wikidata_description": "chemical compound", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ethanol", "wikipedia_extract": "Ethanol (also called ethyl alcohol, grain alcohol, drinking alcohol, or simply alcohol) is an organic compound with the chemical formula CH3CH2OH. It is an alcohol, with its formula also written as C2H5OH, C2H6O or EtOH, where Et is the pseudoelement symbol for ethyl. Ethanol is a volatile, flammable, colorless liquid with a pungent taste. As a psychoactive depressant, it is the active ingredient in alcoholic beverages, and the second most consumed drug globally behind caffeine. Ethanol is naturally produced by the fermentation process of sugars by yeasts or via petrochemical processes such as ethylene hydration. Historically it was used as a general anesthetic, and has modern medical applications as an antiseptic, disinfectant, solvent for some medications, and antidote for methanol poisoning and ethylene glycol poisoning.", "note": "Aqua vitae (Latin for 'water of life') is an archaic term for distilled spirits, most commonly referring to ethanol."}}, {"canonical_name": "terra", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "soil", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "soil", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["soil"], "contexts": ["upper layer of earth", "earthy material"]}, {"entity_id": "terra", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "terra", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 9, "variants": ["terra"], "contexts": ["earth", "material to cover wood pile"]}, {"entity_id": "soil", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Soil", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Soil", "soil"], "contexts": ["earthy material of land", "temperature dependence on its nature"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "soil", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "terra", "similarity": 0.923}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "soil", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Soil", "similarity": 0.996}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "terra", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Soil", "similarity": 0.923}], "id": 1153, "stable_key": "clu_359477f10a3a8db6-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Earth", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "The term 'terra' (Spanish/Portuguese) and 'earth' (English) refers to soil, land, or the element earth in classical and early modern contexts.", "wikidata_id": "Q36133", "wikidata_description": "natural body consisting of layers that are primarily composed of minerals", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soil", "country": "Global", "wikipedia_extract": "Soil, also commonly referred to as earth, is a mixture of organic matter, minerals, gases, water, and organisms that together support the life of plants and soil organisms. Some scientific definitions distinguish dirt from soil by restricting the former term specifically to displaced soil. Soil consists of a solid collection of minerals and organic matter (the soil matrix), as well as a porous phase that holds gases (the soil atmosphere) and a liquid phase that holds water and dissolved substances both organic and inorganic, in ionic or in molecular form (the soil solution). Accordingly, soil is a complex three-state system of solids, liquids, and gases. Soil is a product of several factors: the influence of climate, relief (elevation, orientation, and slope of terrain), organisms, and the soil's parent materials (original minerals) interacting over time. It continually undergoes development by way of numerous physical, chemical and biological processes, which include weathering with associated erosion."}}, {"canonical_name": "Nicolaus Monardes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "nicolai_monardis", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Nicolai Monardis", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Nicolai Monardis"], "contexts": ["physician and author"]}, {"entity_id": "nicolau_monardes", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Nicolau Monardes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 5, "variants": ["Nicolau Monardes", "Nicolaus Blancardus"], "contexts": ["provided information on cassia fistula origin", "cited by Clusius"]}, {"entity_id": "nicolaus_monardes", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Nicolaus Monardes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 8, "variants": ["Nicolaus Monardes", "Nicolaus Fontawus", "Nicolaus Maffa", "Nicolaus Mafla", "Nicolaus Malla", "Nicolus Florentinus"], "contexts": ["wrote about simple medicines", "author cited for medical writings"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Nicolai Monardis", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Nicolau Monardes", "similarity": 0.922}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Nicolai Monardis", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Nicolaus Monardes", "similarity": 0.925}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Nicolau Monardes", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Nicolaus Monardes", "similarity": 0.982}], "id": 1154, "stable_key": "clu_5827f161925d2d6e-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Nicol\u00e1s Monardes", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1512, "death_year": 1588, "description": "Spanish physician and botanist, author on American medicinal plants.", "wikidata_id": "Q950531", "wikidata_description": "Spanish botanist (1508-1588)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nicol%C3%A1s%20Monardes", "wikipedia_extract": "Nicol\u00e1s Bautista Monardes (1493 \u2013 10 October 1588) was a Spanish physician and botanist. Monardes published several books of varying importance. In Di\u00e1logo llamado pharmacodilosis (1536), he examines humanism and suggests studying several classical authors, principally Pedanius Dioscorides. He discusses the importance of Greek and Arab medicine in De Secanda Vena in pleuriti Inter Grecos et Arabes Concordia (1539). De Rosa et partibus eius (1540) is about roses and citrus fruits. It is known that Monardes also believed that tobacco smoke was an infallible panacea."}}, {"canonical_name": "Optalmia", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "obtalmia", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "obtalmia", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["obtalmia", "ophtalmias"], "contexts": ["medical condition discussed", "eye disease treated with lycium"]}, {"entity_id": "optalmia", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Optalmia", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 9, "variants": ["Optalmia"], "contexts": ["chapter title about eye inflammation", "inflammation of the eye's tunic"]}, {"entity_id": "ophthalmia", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Ophthalmia", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ophthalmia"], "contexts": ["inflammation of the eye"]}, {"entity_id": "ophthalmiis", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Ophthalmiis", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ophthalmiis"], "contexts": ["eye disease"]}, {"entity_id": "ophthalmia", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "ophthalmia", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["ophthalmia"], "contexts": ["inflammation of the eye"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "obtalmia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Optalmia", "similarity": 0.921}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "obtalmia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Ophthalmia", "similarity": 0.889}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "obtalmia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Ophthalmiis", "similarity": 0.883}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Optalmia", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "ophthalmia", "similarity": 0.962}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Ophthalmia", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "ophthalmia", "similarity": 0.998}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Ophthalmiis", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "ophthalmia", "similarity": 0.983}], "id": 1155, "stable_key": "clu_6904ce66884e60a1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ophthalmia", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Ophthalmia", "wikidata_id": "Q18558194", "wikidata_description": "Human disease", "note": "Both 'Ophthalmiis' and 'ophthalmia' directly refer to inflammation of the eye."}}, {"canonica