orption", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "absorption", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "absorption", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "absorption", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "absorption", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Absorptions", "similarity": 0.951}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "absorption", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "absorption", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "absorption", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Absorptions", "similarity": 0.951}], "id": 1189, "stable_key": "clu_f8678c45afb04067", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Absorption", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikipedia_extract": "The emissivity of the surface of a material is its effectiveness in emitting energy as thermal radiation. Thermal radiation is electromagnetic radiation that most commonly includes both visible radiation (light) and infrared radiation, which is not visible to human eyes. A portion of the thermal radiation from very hot objects (see photograph) is easily visible to the eye. The emissivity of a surface depends on its chemical composition and geometrical structure. Quantitatively, it is the ratio of the thermal radiation from a surface to the radiation from an ideal black surface at the same temperature as given by the Stefan\u2013Boltzmann law. (A comparison with Planck's law is used if one is concerned with particular wavelengths of thermal radiation.) The ratio varies from 0 to 1 for ordinary surfaces.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emissivity"}}, {"canonical_name": "sulphuric acid", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "acide_sulfurique", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "acide sulfurique", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["acide sulfurique"], "contexts": ["chemical agent"]}, {"entity_id": "sulphuric_acid", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "sulphuric acid", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["sulphuric acid"], "contexts": ["acid produced from sulphur", "acid vapor causing mineral transformation"]}, {"entity_id": "sulphurous_acids", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "sulphurous acids", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["sulphurous acids"], "contexts": ["acids issuing from the ground"]}, {"entity_id": "sulphurous_acid", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "sulphurous acid", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["sulphurous acid"], "contexts": ["gaseous compound of sulphur"]}, {"entity_id": "sulphuric_acid", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "sulphuric acid", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 6, "variants": ["sulphuric acid"], "contexts": ["acid used to clean platinum", "acid used to fix chrysotype"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "acide sulfurique", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "sulphuric acid", "similarity": 0.968}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "acide sulfurique", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "sulphurous acids", "similarity": 0.913}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "acide sulfurique", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "sulphurous acid", "similarity": 0.959}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "acide sulfurique", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "sulphuric acid", "similarity": 0.968}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "sulphuric acid", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "sulphuric acid", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "sulphurous acids", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "sulphuric acid", "similarity": 0.925}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "sulphurous acid", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "sulphuric acid", "similarity": 0.985}], "id": 1190, "stable_key": "clu_0b950bd0e8ac7725-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sulfurous acid", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "note": "While 'sulfuric acid' is also mentioned, the context of 'gaseous compound' and 'exhalations' points more strongly to sulfurous acid (H2SO3) as it is a weaker acid and can exist in gaseous form, whereas sulfuric acid (H2SO4) is a stronger acid typically encountered in liquid form or as a mist when concentrated.", "wikidata_id": "Q5282", "wikidata_description": "chemical compound", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sulfur%20dioxide", "wikipedia_extract": "Sulfur dioxide (IUPAC-recommended spelling) or sulphur dioxide (traditional Commonwealth English) is the chemical compound with the formula SO2. It is a colorless gas with a pungent smell that is responsible for the odor of burnt matches. It is released naturally by volcanic activity and is produced as a by-product of metals refining and the burning of sulfur-bearing hydrocarbon fuels. Sulfur dioxide is somewhat toxic to humans, although only when inhaled in relatively large quantities for a period of several minutes or more. It was known to medieval alchemists as \"volatile spirit of sulfur\". SO2 is a bent molecule with C2v symmetry point group."}}, {"canonical_name": "salgema", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "sal_gemma", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Sal Gemma", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Sal Gemma"], "contexts": ["salt attracts weakly", "mineral substance with electrical properties"]}, {"entity_id": "salgemma", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Salgemma", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["Salgemma"], "contexts": ["rock salt, used in pills", "rock salt"]}, {"entity_id": "salgema", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "salgema", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 7, "variants": ["Salgema", "salgema"], "contexts": ["salt", "added to decoction for gargles"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Sal Gemma", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Salgemma", "similarity": 0.512}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Salgemma", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "salgema", "similarity": 0.911}], "id": 1191, "stable_key": "clu_96314c24dbe24596", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Rock salt", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q5314", "wikidata_description": "mineral form of sodium chloride", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halite", "wikipedia_extract": "Halite ( HAL-yte, HAY-lyte), commonly known as rock salt, is a type of salt, the mineral (natural) form of sodium chloride (NaCl). Halite forms isometric crystals. The mineral is typically colorless or white, but may also be light blue, dark blue, purple, pink, red, orange, yellow or gray depending on inclusion of other materials, impurities, and structural or isotopic abnormalities in the crystals. It commonly occurs with  other evaporite deposit minerals such as several of the sulfates, halides, and borates. The name halite is derived from the Ancient Greek word for \"salt\", \u1f05\u03bb\u03c2 (h\u00e1ls). Halite dominantly occurs within sedimentary rocks where it has formed from the evaporation of seawater or salty lake water."}}, {"canonical_name": "Apollo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "apollonius", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Apollonius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Apollonius"], "contexts": ["subject of Philostratus's life"]}, {"entity_id": "apollodorus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Apollodorus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Apollodorus"], "contexts": ["related loadstone attraction at night"]}, {"entity_id": "apollo", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Apollo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 5, "variants": ["Apollo"], "contexts": ["offered to teach a prince", "offered to teach arts"]}, {"entity_id": "apollo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Apollo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 5, "variants": ["Apollo", "Apollonij", "Apolo"], "contexts": ["god of medicine", "god, temple associated with plague"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Apollonius", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Apollo", "similarity": 0.927}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Apollonius", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Apollo", "similarity": 0.927}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Apollodorus", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Apollo", "similarity": 0.864}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_n