oul described by Ulrici"]}, {"entity_id": "fluid", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "fluid", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 6, "variants": ["fluid"], "contexts": ["highly elastic substance in mineral cavities", "substance that flows, like air or water"]}, {"entity_id": "fluid", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "fluid", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["fluid"], "contexts": ["hypothetical medium between atoms", "substance that flows"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "fluid", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "fluid", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "fluid", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "fluid", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "fluid", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "fluid", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1259, "stable_key": "clu_70a36ec51f5abcfd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "water", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "A transparent liquid essential for life, used medicinally and as a medium.", "note": "Includes Portuguese ('agua'), French ('eau'), and generic terms ('fluid').", "wikipedia_extract": "Water is an inorganic compound with the chemical formula H2O. It is a transparent, tasteless, odorless, and nearly colorless chemical substance. It is the main constituent of Earth's streams, lakes, and oceans and the fluids of all known living organisms, in which it acts as a solvent. Water, being a polar molecule, undergoes strong intermolecular hydrogen bonding which is a large contributor to its physical and chemical properties. It is vital for all known forms of life, despite not providing food energy or being an organic micronutrient. Due to its presence in all organisms, its chemical stability, its worldwide abundance, and its strong polarity relative to its small molecular size, water is often referred to as the \"universal solvent\"."}}, {"canonical_name": "Encke\u2019s comet", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "encke's_comet", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Encke's comet", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Encke's comet"], "contexts": ["specific comet with short orbit", "comet with diminishing eccentricity"]}, {"entity_id": "encke\u2019s_comet", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Encke\u2019s comet", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 6, "variants": ["Encke\u2019s Comet", "Encke\u2019s comet"], "contexts": ["a specific comet", "comet with a short period"]}, {"entity_id": "encke\u2019s_comet", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Encke\u2019s comet", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Encke\u2019s comet"], "contexts": ["comet with diminishing period"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Encke's comet", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Encke\u2019s comet", "similarity": 0.997}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Encke's comet", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Encke\u2019s comet", "similarity": 0.997}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Encke\u2019s comet", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Encke\u2019s comet", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1260, "stable_key": "clu_91e27432b2267510", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Encke's Comet", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "Q166940", "wikidata_description": "periodic comet"}}, {"canonical_name": "compression", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "compression", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "compression", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["compression"], "contexts": ["flattening of the Earth", "flattening of Earth at poles"]}, {"entity_id": "compression", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "compression", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 7, "variants": ["Compression", "compression"], "contexts": ["flattening of a spheroid", "flattening of a spherical body"]}, {"entity_id": "compression", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "compression", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["compression"], "contexts": ["act of pressing or squeezing"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "compression", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "compression", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "compression", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "compression", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "compression", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "compression", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1261, "stable_key": "clu_48468517ebb7c76a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "compression", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q5157032", "wikidata_description": "astronomy concept", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Compression%20%28astronomy%29"}}, {"canonical_name": "Astronomers", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "astronomers", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "astronomers", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["astronomers"], "contexts": ["scientists who study celestial bodies", "scientific professionals"]}, {"entity_id": "astronomers", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Astronomers", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 7, "variants": ["Astronomers", "astronomers"], "contexts": ["scientists who study celestial bodies", "observers of celestial bodies"]}, {"entity_id": "astronomers", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "astronomers", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["astronomers"], "contexts": ["scientists studying celestial bodies"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "astronomers", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Astronomers", "similarity": 0.851}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "astronomers", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "astronomers", "similarity": 0.882}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Astronomers", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "astronomers", "similarity": 0.965}], "id": 1262, "stable_key": "clu_7bfbb3b67a9f4503", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "astronomers", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Scientists who study celestial bodies and the universe.", "wikidata_id": "Q11063", "wikidata_description": "scientist who studies celestial bodies", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Astronomer", "wikipedia_extract": "An astronomer is a scientist in the field of astronomy who focuses on a specific question or field outside the scope of Earth. Astronomers study astronomical objects, such as stars, planets, moons, comets and galaxies, by some combination of observation and the application of astrophysical models. Examples of topics or fields astronomers study include planetary science, solar astronomy, the origin or evolution of stars, or the formation of galaxies. A related but distinct subject is physical cosmology, which studies the universe as a whole. Astronomers cannot typically perform experiments: the objects of interest in the field are far away. Consequently they rely primarily on observations, collecting electromagnetic radiation or particles such as cosmic rays."}}, {"canonical_name": "Amat.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "arist_", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Arist.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Arist."], "contexts": ["ancient Greek philosopher"]}, {"entity_id": "arr_", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Arr.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Arr."], "contexts": ["ancient author cited for information"]}, {"entity_id": "amat_", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Amat.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 6, "variants": ["Amat.", "Amat", "Amat. Cent.", "Amat.Cent."], "contexts": ["author of Cent. 2. curat. 44.", "ancient medical authority"]}, {"entity_id": "avic_", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Avic.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Avic."], "contexts": ["ancient medical authority", "medical authority"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Arist.", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Arr.", "similarity": 0.905}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Arist.", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Amat.", "similarity": 0.906}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Arist.", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Avic.", "similarity": 0.874}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Arr.", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Amat.", "similarity": 0.895}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Arr.", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Avic.", "similarity": 0.857}], "id": 1263, "stable_key": "clu_0304e42f00d328e8-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Aristotle", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": -384, "death_year": -322, "description": "Ancient Greek philosopher and polymath, influential in many fields.", "wikidata_id": "Q868", "wikidata_description": "4th-century BCE Classical Greek philosopher and polymath", "wikipedia_url": "", "wikipedia_extract": "", "note": "The abbreviations 'Arist.', 'Arr.', 'Amat.', and 'Avic.' are ambiguous and could refer to multiple classical or medieval authorities. However, given the context of early modern medical and scientific texts, Aristotle and Avicenna are strong candidates. 'Arr.' is less common for a major figure. 'Amat.' is not a standard abbreviation for a major philosopher or physician."}}, {"canonical_name": "Tintura", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "tinta", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "tinta", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["tinta"], "contexts": ["liquid substance"]}, {"entity_id": "tinta", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "tinta", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["tinta"], "contexts": ["substance from trees or bushes"]}, {"entity_id": "tintura", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Tintura", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 8, "variants": ["Tintura", "tinctura", "tintura", "tintura de alambre", "tintura de a\u00e7o azul", "tinturado"], "contexts": ["made of silver for drowsiness", "extract from plants"]}, {"entity_id": "tinta", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Tinta", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Tinta"], "contexts": ["type of wine favored by vintners"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "tinta", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "tinta", "similarity": 0.825}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "tinta", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Tinta", "similarity": 0.785}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "tinta", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Tintura", "similarity": 0.921}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "tinta", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Tinta", "similarity": 0.963}], "id": 1264, "stable_key": "clu_db8c6f501bab656b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tincture", "confidence": "high", "type": "SUBSTANCE", "description": "An alcoholic extract of plant material or other substances.", "wikipedia_extract": "A tincture is typically an extract of plant or animal material dissolved in ethanol (ethyl alcohol). Solvent concentrations of 25\u201360% are common, but may run as high as 90%. In chemistry, a tincture is a solution that has ethanol as its solvent. In herbal medicine, alcoholic tinctures are made with various ethanol concentrations, which should be at least 20% alcohol for preservation purposes. Other solvents for producing tinctures include vinegar, glycerol (also called glycerine), diethyl ether and propylene glycol, not all of which can be used for internal consumption. Ethanol has the advantage of being an excellent solvent for both acidic and basic (alkaline) constituents."}}, {"canonical_name": "steam-engine", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "steam-engine", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "steam-engine", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 3, "variants": ["steam-engine"], "contexts": ["example of an extra-mental reality", "machine with moving power"]}, {"entity_id": "steam-engine", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "steam-engine", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["steam-engine"], "contexts": ["engine powered by steam", "example of a labouring force"]}, {"entity_id": "steam-engine", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "steam-engine", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["steam-engine"], "contexts": ["engine powered by steam", "machine powered by steam"]}, {"entity_id": "steam_engine", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "steam engine", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 3, "variants": ["steam engine"], "contexts": ["machine powered by steam", "engine powered by steam"]}, {"entity_id": "steam_engine", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "steam engine", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["steam engine"], "contexts": ["machine powered by steam"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "steam-engine", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "steam-engine", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "steam-engine", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "steam-engine", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "steam-engine", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "steam-engine", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "steam engine", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "steam engine", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1265, "stable_key": "clu_72e9bc9d27c69f20", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "steam engine", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "Q12760", "wikidata_description": "heat engine that performs mechanical work using steam as its working fluid", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steam%20engine", "wikipedia_extract": "A steam engine is a heat engine that performs mechanical work using steam as its working fluid. The steam engine uses the force produced by steam pressure to push a piston back and forth inside a cylinder. This pushing force can be transformed by a connecting rod and crank into rotational force for work. The term \"steam engine\" is normally applied to reciprocating engines, although some authorities have also referred to the steam turbine and devices such as Hero's aeolipile as \"steam engines\". The essential feature of steam engines is that they are external combustion engines, where the working fluid is separated from the combustion products. The ideal thermodynamic cycle used to analyze this process is called the Rankine cycle."}}, {"canonical_name": "Puerto rico", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "porto_rico", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Porto Rico", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Porto Rico"], "contexts": ["location where thermometer was observed", "Spanish island"]}, {"entity_id": "puerto_rico", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Puerto rico", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 5, "variants": ["Puerto rico", "Puerto Rico", "Puertorico"], "contexts": ["location where another tree was found", "location of a Guayaci tree"]}, {"entity_id": "puerto_rico", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "Puerto Rico", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["puerto Rico", "Puerto Rico"], "contexts": ["island where china plant grows", "Puerto Rico"]}, {"entity_id": "puerto-rico", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Puerto-Rico", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Puerto-Rico"], "contexts": ["location discussed in a manuscript"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Porto Rico", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Puerto rico", "similarity": 0.938}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Puerto Rico", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Puerto-Rico", "similarity": 0.835}], "id": 1266, "stable_key": "clu_4f7aa2c258558acd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Puerto Rico", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United States", "wikidata_id": "Q1183", "wikidata_description": "unincorporated territory of the United States of America"}}, {"canonical_name": "anatomiste", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "anatomiste", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "anatomiste", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["anatomiste"], "contexts": ["practitioner studying anatomy", "one who studies anatomy"]}, {"entity_id": "anatomistes", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "anatomistes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 3, "variants": ["anatomistes"], "contexts": ["practitioners of anatomy", "anatomists"]}, {"entity_id": "anatomicos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Anatomicos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 2, "variants": ["Anatomicos"], "contexts": ["scholars who study anatomy", "physicians who perform anatomy"]}, {"entity_id": "anatomist", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "anatomist", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 1, "variants": ["anatomist"], "contexts": ["expert in anatomy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "anatomiste", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "anatomist", "similarity": 0.901}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "anatomistes", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Anatomicos", "similarity": 0.92}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "anatomistes", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "anatomist", "similarity": 0.923}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Anatomicos", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "anatomist", "similarity": 0.88}], "id": 1267, "stable_key": "clu_b351794f8e374e6d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Anatomist", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "A scholar or expert who studies and practices anatomy.", "wikidata_id": "Q10872101", "wikidata_description": "scientist with a specialty in anatomy", "wikipedia_extract": "Anatomy (from Ancient Greek  \u1f00\u03bd\u03b1\u03c4\u03bf\u03bc\u03ae (anatom\u1e17) 'dissection') is the branch of morphology concerned with the study of the internal and external structure of organisms and their parts. Anatomy is a branch of natural science that deals with the structural organization of living things. It is an old science, having its beginnings in prehistoric times. Anatomy is inherently tied to developmental biology, embryology, comparative anatomy, evolutionary biology, and phylogeny, as these are the processes by which anatomy is generated, both over immediate and long-term timescales. Anatomy and physiology, which study the structure and function of organisms and their parts respectively, make a natural pair of related disciplines, and are often studied together. Human anatomy is one of the essential basic sciences that are applied in medicine, and is often studied alongside physiology.", "wikipedia_url": "https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anatomiste"}}, {"canonical_name": "Leipzig", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "leipzig", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "Leipzig", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Leipzig"], "contexts": ["location of publication", "city where Gall's book was published"]}, {"entity_id": "leipzig", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Leipzig", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 6, "variants": ["Leipzig"], "contexts": ["location of publication", "city where book was published"]}, {"entity_id": "leipzig", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Leipzig", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Leipzig"], "contexts": ["city of publication"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "Leipzig", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Leipzig", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "Leipzig", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Leipzig", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Leipzig", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Leipzig", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1268, "stable_key": "clu_07a055294c9dcf7e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Leipzig", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "City in Germany, significant for publication.", "note": "Refers to the city of Leipzig, Germany, a known center for printing and academic work.", "wikipedia_extract": "Leipzig is the most populous city in the German state of Saxony. The city has a population of 628,718 inhabitants as of 2023. It is the eighth-largest city in Germany and is part of the Central German Metropolitan Region. Leipzig is located about 150 km (90 mi) southwest of Berlin, in the southernmost part of the North German Plain (the Leipzig Bay), at the confluence of the White Elster and its tributaries Plei\u00dfe and Parthe. Leipzig has been a trade city since at least the time of the Holy Roman Empire. Via Regia and the Via Imperii, two important medieval trade routes, intersected here, marking the city's economic importance."}}, {"canonical_name": "conditions of life", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "conditions_d'existence", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "conditions d'existence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["conditions d'existence"], "contexts": ["circumstances necessary for life", "circumstances necessary for survival"]}, {"entity_id": "conditions_of_life", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "conditions of life", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 7, "variants": ["conditions of life"], "contexts": ["environmental factors affecting organisms", "environmental factors affecting survival"]}, {"entity_id": "condi\u00e7\u00f5es_da_vida", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "condi\u00e7\u00f5es da vida", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["condi\u00e7\u00f5es da vida"], "contexts": ["factors varying in Eastern lands"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "conditions d'existence", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "conditions of life", "similarity": 0.892}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "conditions d'existence", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "condi\u00e7\u00f5es da vida", "similarity": 0.9}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "conditions of life", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "condi\u00e7\u00f5es da vida", "similarity": 0.979}], "id": 1269, "stable_key": "clu_4855f5626096c542-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "environmental conditions", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q77189777", "wikidata_description": "book (work)"}}, {"canonical_name": "os", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "os", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "os", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["os"], "contexts": ["bones"]}, {"entity_id": "os", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "os", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["os"], "contexts": ["bones found in geological layers"]}, {"entity_id": "os", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "os", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 8, "variants": ["os", "OS"], "contexts": ["mouth", "bone"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "os", "target_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "target_name": "os", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "os", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "os", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "os", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "os", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1270, "stable_key": "clu_94e2225d475c1591", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bones", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "The Italian 'os' and its plural 'ossa' (implied by context) refer to bones. The contexts of geological layers and anatomy strongly support this identification.", "wikidata_id": "Q265868", "wikidata_description": "rigid organ that constitutes part of the endoskeleton of vertebrates", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bone"}}, {"canonical_name": "morphology", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "morphologiques", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "morphologiques", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["morphologiques"], "contexts": ["relating to form and structure"]}, {"entity_id": "morphology", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "morphology", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["MORPHOLOGY", "morphology", "Morphology"], "contexts": ["study of form and structure", "study of the form and structure of organisms"]}, {"entity_id": "morphology", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "morphology", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["morphology"], "contexts": ["study of form and structure"]}, {"entity_id": "organic_morphology", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "organic morphology", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["organic morphology"], "contexts": ["study of the form of living organisms"]}, {"entity_id": "morphological", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "morphological", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["morphological"], "contexts": ["study of form and structure"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "morphologiques", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "morphology", "similarity": 0.932}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "morphologiques", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "morphology", "similarity": 0.932}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "morphologiques", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "morphological", "similarity": 0.954}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "morphology", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "morphology", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "morphology", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "organic morphology", "similarity": 0.892}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "morphology", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "morphological", "similarity": 0.947}], "id": 1271, "stable_key": "clu_41a2b65ff8d778a4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Morphology", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q183252", "wikidata_description": "study of external forms and structures of organisms"}}, {"canonical_name": "Atmospheric air", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "air_ambiant", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "air ambiant", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["air ambiant"], "contexts": ["the surrounding atmosphere"]}, {"entity_id": "atmospheric_air", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "atmospheric air", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["atmospheric air"], "contexts": ["air entering volcanic crater"]}, {"entity_id": "atmospheric_air", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Atmospheric air", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 8, "variants": ["atmospheric air", "Atmospheric air"], "contexts": ["gaseous mixture of Earth's atmosphere", "air in the atmosphere"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "air ambiant", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "atmospheric air", "similarity": 0.95}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "air ambiant", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Atmospheric air", "similarity": 0.95}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "atmospheric air", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Atmospheric air", "similarity": 0.999}], "id": 1272, "stable_key": "clu_9b253729126d16c3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "atmospheric air", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "wikidata_id": "Q57085973", "wikidata_description": ""}}, {"canonical_name": "experiment", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "experiment", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "experiment", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 6, "variants": ["experiment"], "contexts": ["scientific investigation", "scientific procedure to test hypothesis"]}, {"entity_id": "experiment", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "experiment", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Experiment", "experiment"], "contexts": ["scientific procedure to test hypothesis", "scientific procedure to test a hypothesis"]}, {"entity_id": "experiment", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "experiment", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["experiment"], "contexts": ["scientific method of testing hypotheses"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "experiment", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "experiment", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "experiment", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "experiment", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "experiment", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "experiment", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1273, "stable_key": "clu_275d933ddcfa49f7-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "scientific experiment", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This cluster refers to the general concept and practice of conducting experiments for scientific inquiry.", "wikidata_id": "Q101965", "wikidata_description": "scientific procedure carried out to support, refute, or validate a hypothesis", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Experiment", "wikipedia_extract": "An experiment is a procedure carried out to support or refute a hypothesis, or determine the efficacy or likelihood of something previously untried. Experiments provide insight into cause-and-effect by demonstrating what outcome occurs when a particular factor is manipulated. Experiments vary greatly in goal and scale but always rely on repeatable procedure and logical analysis of the results. There also exist  natural experimental studies. A child may carry out basic experiments to understand how things fall to the ground, while teams of scientists may take years of systematic investigation to advance their understanding of a phenomenon. Experiments and other types of hands-on activities are very important to student learning in the science classroom."}}, {"canonical_name": "Agricola", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "agricola", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Agricola", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Agricola"], "contexts": ["author of 'de natura fossilium'", "ancient Roman author"]}, {"entity_id": "agr\u00edcola", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Agr\u00edcola", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Agr\u00edcola"], "contexts": ["physician praising bezoars", "early modern scholar"]}, {"entity_id": "agricola", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Agricola", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 5, "variants": ["Agricola", "Agricol"], "contexts": ["early modern author", "author of 'Comment. in Popp.tract.deAntimonio'"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Agricola", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Agr\u00edcola", "similarity": 0.952}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Agricola", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Agricola", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Agr\u00edcola", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Agricola", "similarity": 0.952}], "id": 1274, "stable_key": "clu_018b67f6f57a1125", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Georgius Agricola", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Early modern German scholar, physician, and author.", "note": "Refers to Georgius Agricola, a prominent figure in early modern natural knowledge.", "wikipedia_extract": "Georgius Agricola (; born Georg Bauer; 24 March 1494 \u2013 21 November 1555) was a German Humanist scholar, mineralogist and metallurgist. Born in the small town of Glauchau, in the Electorate of Saxony of the Holy Roman Empire, he was broadly educated, but took a particular interest in the mining and refining of metals. He was the first to drop the Arabic definite article al-, exclusively writing chymia and chymista in describing activity that we today would characterize as chemical or alchemical, giving chemistry its modern name. For his groundbreaking work De Natura Fossilium published in 1546, he is generally referred to as the father of mineralogy and the founder of geology as a scientific discipline. He is well known for his pioneering work De re metallica libri XII, that was published in 1556, one year after his death. This 12-chapter work is a comprehensive and systematic study, classification and methodical guide on all available factual and practical aspects that are of concern for mining, the mining sciences and metallurgy, investigated and researched in its natural environment by means of direct observation."}}, {"canonical_name": "insect", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "insect", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Insect", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Insect"], "contexts": ["general term for small arthropod creatures"]}, {"entity_id": "insect", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "insect", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 8, "variants": ["insect"], "contexts": ["arthropod with segmented body", "arthropod with six legs and wings"]}, {"entity_id": "insect", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "insect", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["insect"], "contexts": ["metamorphoses of an insect"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Insect", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "insect", "similarity": 0.963}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Insect", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "insect", "similarity": 0.963}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "insect", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "insect", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1275, "stable_key": "clu_a2a599c7566ec124-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Insects", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q181322", "wikidata_description": "pesticide used against insects", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Insect", "wikipedia_extract": "Insects (from Latin insectum) are hexapod invertebrates of the class Insecta. They are the largest group within the arthropod phylum. Insects have a chitinous exoskeleton, a three-part body (head, thorax and abdomen), three pairs of jointed legs, compound eyes, and a pair of antennae. Insects are the most diverse group of animals, with more than a million described species; they represent more than half of all animal species. The insect nervous system consists of a brain and a ventral nerve cord. Most insects reproduce by laying eggs."}}, {"canonical_name": "frogs", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "REPTILE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "frogs", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Frogs", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Frogs"], "contexts": ["oviparous quadruped with analogous joints"]}, {"entity_id": "frogs", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Frogs", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["Frogs", "frogs"], "contexts": ["amphibian species", "animals that cannot cross wide ocean spaces"]}, {"entity_id": "frogs", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "frogs", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "REPTILE", "count": 6, "variants": ["frogs"], "contexts": ["creatures for vivisection study", "animal studied for reflex movements"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Frogs", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Frogs", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Frogs", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "frogs", "similarity": 0.894}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Frogs", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "frogs", "similarity": 0.894}], "id": 1276, "stable_key": "clu_662718af8ba9edd4-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "frog", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "description": "Amphibious animal commonly used in scientific experiments.", "wikipedia_extract": "A frog is any member of a diverse and largely semiaquatic group of short-bodied, tailless amphibian vertebrates composing the order Anura (coming from the Ancient Greek \u1f00\u03bd\u03bf\u03cd\u03c1\u03b1, literally 'without tail'). Frog species with rough skin texture due to wart-like parotoid glands tend to be called toads, but the distinction between frogs and toads is informal and purely cosmetic, and does not reflect taxonomy or evolutionary history. Frogs are widely distributed, ranging from the tropics to subarctic regions, but the greatest concentration of species diversity is in tropical rainforest and associated wetlands. They account for around 88% of extant amphibian species, and are one of the five most diverse vertebrate orders. The oldest fossil \"proto-frog\" Triadobatrachus is known from the Early Triassic of Madagascar (250 million years ago), but molecular clock dating suggests their divergence from other amphibians may extend further back to the Permian, 265 million years ago. Adult frogs have a stout body, protruding eyes, anteriorly-attached tongue, limbs folded underneath, and no tail (the \"tail\" of tailed frogs is an extension of the male cloaca)."}}, {"canonical_name": "carne", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "meat", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "meat", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["meat"], "contexts": ["flesh of animals"]}, {"entity_id": "carne", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "carne", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 6, "variants": ["carne", "carne nuena", "carne podrida"], "contexts": ["meat, animal flesh", "flesh that is regenerated in wounds"]}, {"entity_id": "carno\u017fa", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "carno\u017fa", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 3, "variants": ["carno\u017fa", "carnofas", "carnofidade"], "contexts": ["fleshy", "fleshiness or corpulence"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "meat", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "carne", "similarity": 0.974}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "meat", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "carno\u017fa", "similarity": 0.921}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "carne", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "carno\u017fa", "similarity": 0.927}], "id": 1277, "stable_key": "clu_e22fdaf165d1bf67-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Meat", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q10990", "wikidata_description": "culinary name for animal flesh eaten as food", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meat", "note": "Refers to the muscular tissue of animals, both as food ('meat') and in an anatomical context.", "wikipedia_extract": "Meat is animal tissue, mostly muscle, that is eaten as food. Humans have hunted and farmed other animals for meat since prehistory. The Neolithic Revolution allowed the domestication of vertebrates, including chickens, sheep, goats, pigs, horses, and cattle, starting around 11,000 years ago. Since then, selective breeding has enabled farmers to produce meat with the qualities desired by producers and consumers. Meat is important to economies and cultures around the world. Meat is mainly composed of water, protein, and fat."}}, {"canonical_name": "Hipparchus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "hipparrchus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Hipparrchus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hipparrchus"], "contexts": ["ancient astronomer on sight"]}, {"entity_id": "hipparchus", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Hipparchus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Hipparchus"], "contexts": ["ancient astronomer"]}, {"entity_id": "hipparchus", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Hipparchus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 7, "variants": ["Hipparchus"], "contexts": ["ancient astronomer", "ancient astronomer who discovered precession"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Hipparrchus", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Hipparchus", "similarity": 0.961}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Hipparrchus", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Hipparchus", "similarity": 0.961}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Hipparchus", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Hipparchus", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1278, "stable_key": "clu_b3e6eaa5e7ac42ff", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hipparchus", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": -190, "death_year": -120, "description": "Ancient Greek astronomer and mathematician, known for star catalogs.", "wikidata_id": "Q1619936", "wikidata_description": "Pythagorean philosopher", "note": "The text also mentions 'Hippias', a different ancient figure, but 'Hipparque' primarily refers to Hipparchus in scientific contexts."}}, {"canonical_name": "European", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "europa", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Europa", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Europa"], "contexts": ["figure in Greek mythology"]}, {"entity_id": "european", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "European", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 8, "variants": ["European"], "contexts": ["vegetable forms in Indian Mountains", "geographic origin in botanical language"]}, {"entity_id": "europeu", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "europeu", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["europeu"], "contexts": ["habit of a European"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Europa", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "European", "similarity": 0.927}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Europa", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "europeu", "similarity": 0.911}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "European", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "europeu", "similarity": 0.975}], "id": 1279, "stable_key": "clu_b020b1d1613d9848", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Europe", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Multiple", "note": "Refers to the continent in general, not a specific person or group of people.", "wikidata_id": "Q5412157", "wikidata_description": "database of biomedical research", "wikipedia_url": "", "wikipedia_extract": ""}}, {"canonical_name": "ossements", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "ossements", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "ossements", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 6, "variants": ["ossements"], "contexts": ["bones of elephants found in the North", "bones found in northern lands"]}, {"entity_id": "ossemens", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "ossemens", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 3, "variants": ["ossemens", "ossemens fossiles"], "contexts": ["bones found in caves", "bones of mastodontes"]}, {"entity_id": "osseous_ones", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "osseous ones", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["osseous ones"], "contexts": ["connections turned into bone"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "ossements", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "ossemens", "similarity": 0.964}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "ossements", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "osseous ones", "similarity": 0.93}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "ossemens", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "osseous ones", "similarity": 0.925}], "id": 1280, "stable_key": "clu_107b6d38dc64ea4c-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bones", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "Q265868", "wikidata_description": "rigid organ that constitutes part of the endoskeleton of vertebrates", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bone", "wikipedia_extract": "A bone is a rigid organ that constitutes part of the skeleton in most vertebrate animals. Bones protect the organs of the body, produce red and white blood cells, store minerals, help regulate acid-base homeostasis, provide structure and support for the body, and enable mobility and hearing. Bones come in a variety of shapes and sizes and have complex internal and external structures. Bone tissue (also known as osseous tissue or bone in the uncountable) is a form of hard tissue, specialised connective tissue that is mineralized and has an intercellular honeycomb-like matrix, which helps to give the bone rigidity. Bone tissue is made up of different types of bone cells: osteoblasts and osteocytes (bone formation and mineralisation); osteoclasts (bone resorption); modified or flattened osteoblasts (lining cells that form a protective layer on the bone surface). The mineralised matrix of bone tissue has an organic component of mainly ossein, a form of collagen, and an inorganic component of bone mineral, made up of various salts.", "note": "The context indicates the anatomical meaning of bones, not their mineral composition as a substance."}}, {"canonical_name": "volume", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "volumes", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "volumes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["volumes"], "contexts": ["sizes, volumes"]}, {"entity_id": "volume", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "volume", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["volume"], "contexts": ["three-dimensional space occupied", "physical size of celestial bodies"]}, {"entity_id": "volume", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "volume", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["volume"], "contexts": ["amount of space occupied", "space occupied"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "volumes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "volume", "similarity": 0.82}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "volumes", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "volume", "similarity": 0.82}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "volume", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "volume", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1281, "stable_key": "clu_b77d0fe112ed886d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "volume", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q39297", "wikidata_description": "quantity of three-dimensional space"}}, {"canonical_name": "peixe", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "fish", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Fish", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Fish"], "contexts": ["smells like old rotten fish", "food that is often fatty"]}, {"entity_id": "peixe", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "peixe", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["peixe", "peixes"], "contexts": ["food item", "animal product"]}, {"entity_id": "peixe", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "peixe", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["peixe"], "contexts": ["fish to be avoided", "animal product"]}, {"entity_id": "peixe_de_co\u017ftra", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "peixe de co\u017ftra", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["peixe de co\u017ftra"], "contexts": ["shelled fish to avoid"]}, {"entity_id": "pescada", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Pescada", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Pescada"], "contexts": ["fish"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Fish", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "peixe", "similarity": 0.969}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Fish", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "peixe", "similarity": 0.969}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "peixe", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "peixe", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "peixe", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "peixe de co\u017ftra", "similarity": 0.892}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "peixe", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Pescada", "similarity": 0.918}], "id": 1282, "stable_key": "clu_70c23abb92c6d4a5-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Fish", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "description": "Aquatic vertebrate animals with gills, fins, and typically scales.", "wikipedia_extract": "A fish is an aquatic, anamniotic, gill-bearing vertebrate animal with swimming fins and a tough cranium to protect the brain, but lacking limbs with digits. Fish can be grouped into the more basal jawless fish and the more common jawed fish, the latter including all living cartilaginous and bony fish, as well as the extinct placoderms and acanthodians. In a break from the long tradition of grouping all fish into a single class (Pisces), modern phylogenetics views fish as a paraphyletic group that includes all vertebrates except tetrapods. In English, the plural of \"fish\" is fish when referring to individuals and fishes when referring to species. Most fish are cold-blooded, their body temperature varying with the surrounding water, though some large, active swimmers like the white shark and tuna can maintain a higher core temperature. Many fish can communicate acoustically with each other, such as during courtship displays."}}, {"canonical_name": "finger", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "finger", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Finger", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Finger"], "contexts": ["size of winter cherry root"]}, {"entity_id": "finger", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "finger", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 8, "variants": ["finger"], "contexts": ["digit on the hand", "part of hand used to depress piano key"]}, {"entity_id": "finger", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "finger", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["finger"], "contexts": ["digit of the hand"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Finger", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "finger", "similarity": 0.99}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Finger", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "finger", "similarity": 0.99}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "finger", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "finger", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1283, "stable_key": "clu_d85b593fdf3e0390-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "fingers", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "While fingers are anatomical parts, the context here discusses them as general physical entities, not specific medical diagnoses or treatments, aligning more with a conceptual use within broader scientific and philosophical discourse.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finger", "wikipedia_extract": "A finger is a prominent digit on the forelimbs of most tetrapod vertebrate animals, especially those with prehensile extremities (i.e. hands) such as humans and other primates. Most tetrapods have five digits (pentadactyly), and short digits (i.e. significantly shorter than the metacarpal/metatarsals) are typically referred to as toes, while those that are notably elongated are called fingers. In humans, the fingers are flexibly articulated and opposable, serving as an important organ of tactile sensation and fine movements, which are crucial to the dexterity of the hands and the ability to grasp and manipulate objects. Usually humans have five digits, the bones of which are termed phalanges, on each hand, although some people have more or fewer than five due to congenital disorders such as polydactyly or oligodactyly, or accidental or intentional amputations."}}, {"canonical_name": "Turbith", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "turbith", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Turbith", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Turbith"], "contexts": ["used for phlegm"]}, {"entity_id": "turbith", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "turbith", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["turbith"], "contexts": ["plant from the East Indies"]}, {"entity_id": "turbith", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Turbith", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 8, "variants": ["Turbith", "Turbithi gommoso"], "contexts": ["root of a milky plant", "purgative root"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Turbith", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "turbith", "similarity": 0.964}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Turbith", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Turbith", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "turbith", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Turbith", "similarity": 0.964}], "id": 1284, "stable_key": "clu_41b3ef87a3ac307a-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Turpeth", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Ipomoea turpethum", "family": "Convolvulaceae"}}, {"canonical_name": "Forbes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "forbes", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Forbes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 5, "variants": ["Forbes"], "contexts": ["naturalist and geologist", "E. Forbes independently arrived at a similar conclusion"]}, {"entity_id": "forbes", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Forbes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Forbes"], "contexts": ["author of a treatise on chess"]}, {"entity_id": "forbes", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Forbes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Forbes"], "contexts": ["ascertained boiling point variation with height", "professor proving moon's light effect on thermometer"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Forbes", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Forbes", "similarity": 0.756}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Forbes", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Forbes", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Forbes", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Forbes", "similarity": 0.756}], "id": 1285, "stable_key": "clu_0263d31e3ab78655", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Edward Forbes", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "A naturalist and geologist, known for his conclusions on biogeography.", "note": "Distinguished from Forbes Winslow, a different author.", "wikipedia_extract": "Edward Forbes FRS, FGS (12 February 1815 \u2013 18 November 1854) was a Manx naturalist. In 1846, he proposed that the distributions of montane plants and animals had been compressed downslope, and some oceanic islands connected to the mainland, during the recent ice age. This mechanism, which was the first natural explanation to explain the distributions of the same species on now-isolated islands and mountain tops, was discovered independently by Charles Darwin, who credited Forbes with the idea. He also incorrectly deduced the so-called azoic hypothesis, that life under the sea would decline to the point that no life forms could exist below a certain depth. Forbes was born at Douglas in the Isle of Man. His father was a well-to-do banker.", "birth_year": 1815, "death_year": 1854}}, {"canonical_name": "Verbasco", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "verbascum", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Verbascum", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Verbascum"], "contexts": ["plant genus mentioned in fertilization experiments", "plant species experimented on"]}, {"entity_id": "verbasco", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "verbasco", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["verbasco"], "contexts": ["leaves of the plant are like verbasco", "plant with similar leaves"]}, {"entity_id": "verbasco", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Verbasco", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 5, "variants": ["verbasco", "Verbasco"], "contexts": ["herb used in remedies", "herb used in a medicinal preparation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Verbascum", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Verbasco", "similarity": 0.882}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "verbasco", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Verbasco", "similarity": 0.809}], "id": 1286, "stable_key": "clu_377f063d2a145615", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mullein", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Verbascum", "family": "Scrophulariaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q160095", "wikidata_description": "species of the plant genus Verbascum"}}, {"canonical_name": "Leguminosa", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "leguminos\u00e6", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Leguminos\u00e6", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Leguminos\u00e6"], "contexts": ["plant family with glands at stipule base", "plant family with manipulation difficulties"]}, {"entity_id": "legumbre", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "legumbre", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["legumbre"], "contexts": ["plant that resembles a legume"]}, {"entity_id": "leguminosa", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Leguminosa", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 7, "variants": ["Leguminosa", "Leguminosae", "Leguminosas", "Leguminosce", "Leguminosie", "Leguminos\u00e9", "leguminosa"], "contexts": ["seed of a leguminous plant", "Family of plants"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Leguminos\u00e6", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "legumbre", "similarity": 0.895}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Leguminos\u00e6", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Leguminosa", "similarity": 0.98}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "legumbre", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Leguminosa", "similarity": 0.905}], "id": 1287, "stable_key": "clu_49aa26d540dd9bc9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Fabaceae", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "family": "Fabaceae", "description": "A large and economically important family of flowering plants.", "note": "Also known as Leguminosae.", "wikipedia_extract": "Fabaceae or Leguminosae, commonly known as the legume, pea, or bean family, is a large and agriculturally important family of flowering plants. It includes trees, shrubs, and perennial or annual herbaceous plants, which are easily recognized by their fruit (legume) and their compound, stipulate leaves. The family is widely distributed, and is the third-largest land plant family in number of species, behind only the Orchidaceae and Asteraceae, with about 765 genera and nearly 20,000 known species. The five largest genera of the family are Astragalus (over 3,000 species), Acacia (over 1,000 species), Indigofera (around 700 species), Crotalaria (around 700 species), and Mimosa (around 400 species), which constitute about a quarter of all legume species. The c.\u200919,000 known legume species amount to about 7% of flowering plant species. Fabaceae is the most common family found in tropical rainforests and dry forests of the Americas and Africa."}}, {"canonical_name": "mastodontes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "mastodon", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Mastodon", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Mastodon"], "contexts": ["extinct mammal species", "extinct elephant-like mammal"]}, {"entity_id": "mastodons", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "mastodons", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["mastodons"], "contexts": ["extinct elephant-like mammals", "extinct mammal name"]}, {"entity_id": "mastodontes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "mastodontes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["Mastodontes", "mastodontes"], "contexts": ["bones of Mastodontes", "extinct mammal bones found"]}, {"entity_id": "mastodon", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "mastodon", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["mastodon", "Mastodon"], "contexts": ["extinct mammal genus", "extinct prehistoric mammal"]}, {"entity_id": "mastodons", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "mastodons", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["mastodons"], "contexts": ["extinct elephant-like mammal"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Mastodon", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "mastodon", "similarity": 0.746}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "mastodons", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "mastodontes", "similarity": 0.93}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "mastodons", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "mastodon", "similarity": 0.931}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "mastodons", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "mastodons", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "mastodontes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "mastodons", "similarity": 0.93}], "id": 1288, "stable_key": "clu_e7dab61e3503cda8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mastodon", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "linnaean": "Mammut americanum", "family": "Mammutidae", "description": "An extinct genus of elephant-like mammals.", "wikipedia_extract": "A mastodon (from Ancient Greek  \u03bc\u03b1\u03c3\u03c4\u03cc\u03c2 (mast\u00f3s) 'breast' and  \u1f40\u03b4\u03bf\u03cd\u03c2 (odo\u00fas) 'tooth') is a member of the genus Mammut (German for 'mammoth'), which was endemic to North America and lived from the late Miocene to the early Holocene. Mastodons belong to the order Proboscidea, the same order as elephants and mammoths (which belong to the family Elephantidae). Mammut is the type genus of the extinct family Mammutidae, which diverged from the ancestors of modern elephants at least 28 million years ago, during the Oligocene. Like other members of Mammutidae, the molar teeth of mastodons have zygodont morphology (where parallel pairs of cusps are merged into sharp ridges), which strongly differ from those of elephantids. In comparison to its likely ancestor Zygolophodon, Mammut is characterized by particularly long and upward curving upper tusks, reduced or absent tusks on the lower jaw, as well as the shortening of the mandibular symphysis (the frontmost part of the lower jaw), the latter two traits also having evolved in parallel separately in elephantids. Mastodons had an overall stockier skeletal build, a lower-domed skull, and a longer tail compared to elephantids."}}, {"canonical_name": "grasses", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "grasses", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Grasses", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Grasses"], "contexts": ["family of monocotyledonous plants", "plant family"]}, {"entity_id": "grasses", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "grasses", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 5, "variants": ["grasses"], "contexts": ["plants found in uniform plains", "vegetation covering mountain plateaux"]}, {"entity_id": "grasses", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "grasses", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["grasses"], "contexts": ["social plants covering large tracts", "plants with one seed-lobe"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Grasses", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "grasses", "similarity": 0.96}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Grasses", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "grasses", "similarity": 0.96}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "grasses", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "grasses", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1289, "stable_key": "clu_d55e20b1a87c8d12", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Poaceae", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Poaceae", "family": "Poaceae", "note": "The term 'grasses' in these contexts refers to the plant family Poaceae, commonly known as grasses.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "psychoactive herb from the Cannabis plant used for medical or recreational purposes", "wikipedia_url": "", "wikipedia_extract": ""}}, {"canonical_name": "Hunt", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "hunter", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Hunter", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hunter"], "contexts": ["coined term secondary sexual characters"]}, {"entity_id": "hunter", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Hunter", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Hunter"], "contexts": ["cited work", "name cited for elephant name"]}, {"entity_id": "hunt", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Hunt", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 7, "variants": ["Hunt"], "contexts": ["experimenter with solar spectrum", "experiments on solar spectrum"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Hunter", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Hunter", "similarity": 0.838}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Hunter", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Hunt", "similarity": 0.889}], "id": 1290, "stable_key": "clu_2ad91bf0f3500c85", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "John Hunter", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1728, "death_year": 1793, "description": "Pioneering surgeon and anatomist, influential in medical education.", "note": "The context 'coined term secondary sexual characters' strongly points to Hunter, as does the general significance in anatomy and medicine from the period."}}, {"canonical_name": "skull", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "skull", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "skull", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["skull"], "contexts": ["bone structure of the head"]}, {"entity_id": "skull", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "skull", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 6, "variants": ["skull"], "contexts": ["bony structure of the head", "bony framework of the head"]}, {"entity_id": "skull", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "skull", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 3, "variants": ["skull"], "contexts": ["bony framework of the head", "bony structure of the head"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "skull", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "skull", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "skull", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "skull", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "skull", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "skull", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1291, "stable_key": "clu_74248dcfe2ba8492", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "skull", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "note": "The term 'skulls' in a medical/scientific context refers to the bony framework of the head. 'Part of pigeon anatomy' and 'part of bird skeletons' reinforce this."}}, {"canonical_name": "cholula", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "cholula", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "cholula", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 7, "variants": ["cholula"], "contexts": ["city of Cholula", "location of plant growth"]}, {"entity_id": "cholula", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Cholula", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Cholula"], "contexts": ["pyramid of Cholula", "city with a pyramid"]}, {"entity_id": "cholula", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Cholula", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cholula"], "contexts": ["location where an a\u00ebrolite was seen"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "cholula", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Cholula", "similarity": 0.713}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "cholula", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Cholula", "similarity": 0.858}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Cholula", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Cholula", "similarity": 0.871}], "id": 1292, "stable_key": "clu_8efc4409a23e54ed", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cholula", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Mexico", "note": "Cholula is a well-known archaeological site and city in Mexico, famous for its pyramid and historical significance. Both spellings refer to the same location.", "wikidata_id": "Q7380613", "wikidata_description": "important city of pre-Columbian Mesoamerica", "wikipedia_extract": "The Great Pyramid of Cholula, also known as Tlachihualtepetl (Nahuatl for \"constructed mountain\"), is a complex located in Cholula, Puebla, Mexico. It is the largest archaeological site of a pyramid (temple) in the world, as well as the largest pyramid by volume known to exist in the world today. The adobe brick pyramid stands 25 metres (82 ft) above the surrounding plain, which is significantly shorter than the Great Pyramid of Giza's height of 146.6 metres (481 ft), but much wider, measuring 300 by 315 metres (984 by 1,033 ft) in its final form, compared to the Great Pyramid's base dimensions of 230.3 by 230.3 metres (756 by 756 ft). The pyramid is a temple that traditionally has been viewed as having been dedicated to the god Quetzalcoatl. The architectural style of the building was linked closely to that of Teotihuacan in the Valley of Mexico, although influence from the Gulf Coast is evident as well, especially from El Taj\u00edn. The Cholula archaeological zone is situated 6.4 kilometres (4 mi) west of the city of Puebla, in the city of Cholula.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cholula_%28Mesoamerican_site%29"}}, {"canonical_name": "substance", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "sustancia", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "sustancia", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["sustancia"], "contexts": ["substance", "material composition of maize"]}, {"entity_id": "substance", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Substance", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Substance", "substance"], "contexts": ["material that can be reduced in bulk", "hard material for impact"]}, {"entity_id": "material_substance", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "material substance", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["material substance"], "contexts": ["nature of light according to early theory"]}, {"entity_id": "substance", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "substance", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 4, "variants": ["substance"], "contexts": ["fundamental nature of matter and its properties", "fundamental nature of things"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "sustancia", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Substance", "similarity": 0.976}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "sustancia", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "material substance", "similarity": 0.962}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "sustancia", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "substance", "similarity": 0.99}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Substance", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "substance", "similarity": 0.978}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "material substance", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "substance", "similarity": 0.974}], "id": 1293, "stable_key": "clu_337b34319257e325-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Substance", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q378078", "wikidata_description": "basic ontological concept", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Substance%20theory", "wikipedia_extract": "Substance theory, or substance\u2013attribute theory, is an ontological theory positing that objects are constituted each by a substance and properties borne by the substance but distinct from it. In this role, a substance can be referred to as a substratum or a thing-in-itself. Substances are particulars that are ontologically independent: they are able to exist all by themselves. Another defining feature often attributed to substances is their ability to undergo changes. Changes involve something existing before, during and after the change. They can be described in terms of a persisting substance gaining or losing properties."}}, {"canonical_name": "on\u00e7as", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "onca", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "onca", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["onca"], "contexts": ["ounce, unit of measurement for dosage"]}, {"entity_id": "on\u00e7a", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "on\u00e7a", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 3, "variants": ["on\u00e7a"], "contexts": ["unit of weight for amber", "unit of weight for the root"]}, {"entity_id": "on\u00e7as", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "on\u00e7as", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 6, "variants": ["on\u00e7as"], "contexts": ["unit of weight", "unit of measurement"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "onca", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "on\u00e7a", "similarity": 0.661}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "on\u00e7a", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "on\u00e7as", "similarity": 0.923}], "id": 1294, "stable_key": "clu_4bcf51c886dc9625", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ounce (unit of weight)", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "The early modern 'on\u00e7a' was a unit of weight, equivalent to the modern ounce."}}, {"canonical_name": "Auicena", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "auicen", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "Auicen", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Auicen"], "contexts": ["ancient medical authority"]}, {"entity_id": "auicena", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Auicena", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 6, "variants": ["Auicena", "Auice"], "contexts": ["authority on the complexion of Iron", "authority cited for opinion"]}, {"entity_id": "auicenna", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Auicenna", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Auicenna", "Auicenn"], "contexts": ["authority who compared cassia's flavor to rue", "medical authority on preservation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Auicen", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Auicena", "similarity": 0.846}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Auicen", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Auicenna", "similarity": 0.898}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Auicena", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Auicenna", "similarity": 0.915}], "id": 1295, "stable_key": "clu_d77196501d6c33a4-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Avicenna", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Influential Persian polymath and physician of the Islamic Golden Age.", "note": "Also known by variations like Avicena and Auicena.", "wikipedia_extract": "Ibn Sina (c.\u2009980 \u2013 22 June 1037), commonly known in the West as Avicenna ( A(H)V-ih-SEN-\u0259), was a preeminent philosopher and physician of the Muslim world. He was a seminal figure of the Islamic Golden Age, serving in the courts of various Iranian rulers, and was influential to medieval European medical and Scholastic thought. Often described as the father of early modern medicine, Avicenna's most famous works are The Book of Healing, a philosophical and scientific encyclopedia, and The Canon of Medicine, a medical encyclopedia that became a standard medical text at many medieval European universities and remained in use as late as 1650. Besides philosophy and medicine, Avicenna's corpus includes writings on astronomy, alchemy, geography and geology, psychology, Islamic theology, logic, mathematics, physics, and works of poetry. His philosophy was of the Peripatetic school derived from Aristotelianism, of which he is considered among the greatest proponents within the Muslim world. Avicenna wrote most of his philosophical and scientific works in Arabic but also wrote several key works in Persian; his poetry was written in both languages."}}, {"canonical_name": "Actuarius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "actuarius", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "Actuarius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Actuarius"], "contexts": ["ancient medical authority"]}, {"entity_id": "actuario", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Actuario", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Actuario"], "contexts": ["mentioned tryphera in 13th century"]}, {"entity_id": "actuarius", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Actuarius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 8, "variants": ["Actuarius", "Actuario"], "contexts": ["medical writer", "authority on methods of healing"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Actuarius", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Actuarius", "similarity": 0.683}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Actuario", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Actuarius", "similarity": 0.899}], "id": 1296, "stable_key": "clu_d22da9c52fd0fdc8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Johannes Actuarius", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Byzantine physician and medical writer of the 13th-14th century.", "wikidata_id": "Q2410364", "wikidata_description": "Byzantine physician"}}, {"canonical_name": "fonte", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "fuentes", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "fuentes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["fuentes"], "contexts": ["natural springs"]}, {"entity_id": "fonti", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "fonti", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["fonti"], "contexts": ["water from springs", "source of water"]}, {"entity_id": "fonte", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "fonte", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 7, "variants": ["fonte", "da fonte"], "contexts": ["opening a vein for evacuation", "medical procedure"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "fuentes", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "fonti", "similarity": 0.962}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "fuentes", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "fonte", "similarity": 0.93}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "fonti", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "fonte", "similarity": 0.934}], "id": 1297, "stable_key": "clu_ded853f50da531aa", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Spring", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A source of water, often from underground.", "note": "Encompasses natural water sources and the concept of a source.", "wikipedia_extract": "Geography (from Ancient Greek \u03b3\u03b5\u03c9\u03b3\u03c1\u03b1\u03c6\u03af\u03b1 ge\u014dgraph\u00eda; combining g\u00ea 'Earth' and gr\u00e1ph\u014d 'write', literally 'Earth writing') is the study of the lands, features, inhabitants, and phenomena of Earth. Geography is an all-encompassing discipline that seeks an understanding of Earth and its human and natural complexities\u2014not merely where objects are, but also how they have changed and come to be. While geography is specific to Earth, many concepts can be applied more broadly to other celestial bodies in the field of planetary science. Geography has been called \"a bridge between natural science and social science disciplines.\" The history of geography as a discipline spans cultures and millennia, being independently developed by multiple groups, and cross-pollinated by trade between these groups. Geography as a discipline dates back to the earliest attempts to understand the world spatially, with the earliest example of an attempted world map dating to the 9th century BC in ancient Babylon. Origins of many of the concepts in geography can be traced to Greek Eratosthenes of Cyrene, who may have coined the term \"geographia\" (c.\u2009276 BC \u2013 c.\u2009195/194 BC)."}}, {"canonical_name": "Bahama", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "bahama", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Bahama", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 8, "variants": ["Bahama", "Bahame", "Bahames"], "contexts": ["channel name", "location of a canal"]}, {"entity_id": "bahamas", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Bahamas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bahamas"], "contexts": ["Straits of the Bahamas"]}, {"entity_id": "bahama_islands", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Bahama Islands", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bahama Islands"], "contexts": ["islands west of sargassa sea"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Bahama", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Bahamas", "similarity": 0.987}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Bahama", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Bahama Islands", "similarity": 0.967}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Bahamas", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Bahama Islands", "similarity": 0.968}], "id": 1298, "stable_key": "clu_3f5f69b872b24092", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bahamas", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Bahamas", "wikidata_id": "Q778", "wikidata_description": "island sovereign state in the West Indies", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The%20Bahamas", "wikipedia_extract": "The Bahamas, officially the Commonwealth of the Bahamas, is an island country located within the Lucayan Archipelago in the Atlantic Ocean. The country comprises more than 3,000 islands, cays and islets in the Atlantic Ocean, located north of Cuba and north-west of the island of Hispaniola (split between the Dominican Republic and Haiti) and the Turks and Caicos Islands, southeast of the US state of Florida and east of the Florida Keys. The capital and largest city is Nassau on the island of New Providence. The Royal Bahamas Defence Force describes the Bahamas' territory as encompassing 470,000 km2 (180,000 sq mi) of ocean space. The Bahama islands were inhabited by the Arawak and Lucayans, a branch of the Arawakan-speaking Ta\u00edno, for many centuries. Christopher Columbus was the first European to see the islands, making his first landfall in the \"New World\" in 1492 when he landed on the island of San Salvador."}}, {"canonical_name": "Philadelphie", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "philadelphie", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Philadelphie", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 6, "variants": ["Philadelphie"], "contexts": ["city where a voyage was published", "city where a voyage appeared"]}, {"entity_id": "philadelphia", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Philadelphia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Philadelphia"], "contexts": ["city where Dr. Shoemaker is from", "city visited by 'Brown'"]}, {"entity_id": "philadelphia", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Philadelphia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Philadelphia"], "contexts": ["latitude where Aurora is frequent"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Philadelphie", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Philadelphia", "similarity": 0.917}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Philadelphie", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Philadelphia", "similarity": 0.917}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Philadelphia", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Philadelphia", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1299, "stable_key": "clu_97f32d7ee1e02f9e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Philadelphia", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A major city in Pennsylvania, USA.", "wikipedia_extract": "Philadelphia (  FIL-\u0259-DEL-fee-\u0259), colloquially referred to as Philly, is the most populous city in the U.S. state of Pennsylvania. It is the sixth-most populous city in the United States with a population of 1.6 million at the 2020 census, while the Philadelphia metropolitan area (sometimes called the Delaware Valley) with 6.33 million residents is the nation's ninth-largest metropolitan area. Philadelphia is known for its culture, cuisine, and history, maintaining contemporary influence in business and technology, sports, and music. Philadelphia was founded in 1682 by William Penn, an English Quaker and advocate of religious freedom, and served as the capital of the colonial era Province of Pennsylvania. It then played a vital role during the American Revolution and Revolutionary War. It served as the central meeting place for the nation's Founding Fathers in hosting the First Continental Congress (1774) and the Second Continental Congress, during which the Founders formed the Continental Army, elected George Washington as its commander, and adopted the Declaration of Independence on July 4, 1776."}}, {"canonical_name": "ammoniaque", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "ammoniaque", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "ammoniaque", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 4, "variants": ["ammoniaque", "ammonite"], "contexts": ["substance that does not precipitate plant sap", "ammonia"]}, {"entity_id": "nitrate_of_ammonia", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "nitrate of ammonia", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["nitrate of ammonia"], "contexts": ["atmospheric compound"]}, {"entity_id": "carbonate_of_ammonia", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "carbonate of ammonia", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["carbonate of ammonia"], "contexts": ["atmospheric compound"]}, {"entity_id": "ammoniacal_compounds", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "ammoniacal compounds", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["ammoniacal compounds"], "contexts": ["compounds containing ammonia"]}, {"entity_id": "ammonia", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "ammonia", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["ammonia"], "contexts": ["chemical for photographic spectrum", "compound in atmosphere consumed by plants"]}, {"entity_id": "muriate_of_ammonia", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "muriate of ammonia", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["muriate of ammonia"], "contexts": ["chemical used in paper preparation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "ammoniaque", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "nitrate of ammonia", "similarity": 0.907}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "ammoniaque", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "carbonate of ammonia", "similarity": 0.939}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "ammoniaque", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "ammoniacal compounds", "similarity": 0.909}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "ammoniaque", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "ammonia", "similarity": 0.967}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "nitrate of ammonia", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "ammonia", "similarity": 0.919}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "nitrate of ammonia", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "muriate of ammonia", "similarity": 0.895}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "carbonate of ammonia", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "ammonia", "similarity": 0.941}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "carbonate of ammonia", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "muriate of ammonia", "similarity": 0.918}], "id": 1300, "stable_key": "clu_e23eaef60284a52b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ammonia", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "wikidata_id": "Q4087", "wikidata_description": "chemical compound, NH\u2083", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ammonia", "wikipedia_extract": "Ammonia is an inorganic chemical compound of nitrogen and hydrogen with the formula NH3. A stable binary hydride and the simplest pnictogen hydride, ammonia is a colourless gas with a distinctive pungent smell. It is widely used in fertilizers, refrigerants, explosives, cleaning agents, and is a precursor for numerous chemicals. Biologically, it is a common nitrogenous waste, and it contributes significantly to the nutritional needs of terrestrial organisms by serving as a precursor to fertilisers. Around 70% of ammonia produced industrially is used to make fertilisers in various forms and composition, such as urea and diammonium phosphate. Ammonia in pure form is also applied directly into the soil."}}, {"canonical_name": "Hall", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "hall", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Hall", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 3, "variants": ["Hall", "Hall et Poster"], "contexts": ["observer's name", "Associated with astronomical observations"]}, {"entity_id": "hall", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Hall", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 6, "variants": ["Hall"], "contexts": ["cited observer of hypnotic trance", "cited physiologist"]}, {"entity_id": "hall", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Hall", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hall"], "contexts": ["constructed achromatic telescope"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Hall", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Hall", "similarity": 0.744}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Hall", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Hall", "similarity": 0.744}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Hall", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Hall", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1301, "stable_key": "clu_b4bfb852c3967cac", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Granville Stanley Hall", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1844, "death_year": 1924, "description": "American psychologist and educator, founder of child psychology.", "note": "Multiple 'Hall' individuals could fit, but Granville Stanley Hall is prominent in psychology texts of the period.", "wikidata_id": "Q309675", "wikidata_description": "American psychologist and educator (1846\u20131924)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/G.%20Stanley%20Hall", "wikipedia_extract": "Granville Stanley Hall (February 1, 1844 \u2013 April 24, 1924) was an American psychologist and educator who earned the first doctorate in psychology awarded in the United States of America at Harvard University in the nineteenth century. His interests focused on human life span development and evolutionary theory. Hall was the first president of the American Psychological Association and the first president of Clark University. A 2002 survey by Review of General Psychology ranked Hall as the 72nd most cited psychologist of the 20th century, in a tie with Lewis Terman. Born in Ashfield, Massachusetts, Hall grew up on a farm with his parents, Granville Bascom Hall, who served on the Massachusetts legislature, and Abigail Beals, who attended school at Albany Female Seminary and went on to become a teacher herself. As a child, Hall spent much of his time reading and taking advantage of the educational opportunities he could gain from his parents and the local schools."}}, {"canonical_name": "Erman", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "erman", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Erman", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Erman"], "contexts": ["M. Adolphe Erman observed", "collaborator in magnetic inclination measurement"]}, {"entity_id": "erman", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Erman", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 6, "variants": ["Erman"], "contexts": ["cited for magnetic declination data", "author of Physikal. Beob."]}, {"entity_id": "compart_erman", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Compart Erman", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Compart Erman"], "contexts": ["author of 'Archiv f\u00fcr wissenschaftliche Kunde von Russland'"]}, {"entity_id": "erman", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Erman", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Erman"], "contexts": ["observed barometer depression in Ochotzk Sea"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Erman", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Erman", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Erman", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Compart Erman", "similarity": 0.862}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Erman", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Erman", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Erman", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Erman", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Compart Erman", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Erman", "similarity": 0.862}], "id": 1302, "stable_key": "clu_6e557ee7fe7b69c1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Adolf Erman", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1806, "death_year": 1877, "description": "German geographer and physicist, known for geomagnetic studies.", "note": "The text mentions 'M. Adolphe Erman' and 'Adolph Erman', likely referring to the same person.", "wikidata_id": "Q61421", "wikidata_description": "German Egyptologist and lexicographer (1854-1937)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adolf%20Erman", "wikipedia_extract": "Johann Peter Adolf Erman (German: [\u025b\u0250\u032f\u02c8m\u00e3\u02d0]; 31 October 1854 \u2013 26 June 1937) was a German Egyptologist and lexicographer. Born in Berlin, he was the son of the physicist Georg Adolf Erman and grandson of the physicist Paul Erman and the astronomer Friedrich Wilhelm Bessel. Educated at Leipzig and Berlin, he became associate professor of Egyptology at the University of Berlin in 1883 and full professor in 1892. In 1885 he was appointed director of the Egyptian department at the royal museum. In 1934 he was excluded from the faculty of the university because he was, according to the Nazi ideology, one quarter Jewish. As his family had converted to Protestantism in 1902, he and his family were not persecuted by the Nazi Party, but they all lost their positions."}}, {"canonical_name": "desire", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "desire", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "desire", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["desire"], "contexts": ["preference for certain sensations", "a wish or longing"]}, {"entity_id": "desejo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "desejo", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["desejo"], "contexts": ["strong desire"]}, {"entity_id": "desire", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "desire", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["desire"], "contexts": ["a strong wish or longing", "a strong feeling of wanting something"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "desire", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "desejo", "similarity": 0.956}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "desire", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "desire", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "desejo", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "desire", "similarity": 0.956}], "id": 1303, "stable_key": "clu_7888562ffdbc5b3d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Desire", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "note": "The cluster explicitly mentions 'desires' and 'man's desires', directly referring to the psychological concept of desire."}}, {"canonical_name": "nervous shock", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "nervous_shock", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "nervous shock", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 5, "variants": ["nervous shock"], "contexts": ["mental shock or trauma", "sudden physical or mental impact"]}, {"entity_id": "shock", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "shock", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["shock"], "contexts": ["condition causing flaccidity in frog"]}, {"entity_id": "shock", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "shock", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["shock"], "contexts": ["sudden impact"]}, {"entity_id": "shock", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "shock", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 3, "variants": ["shock"], "contexts": ["sensation from electric discharge", "disagreeable sensation from electricity"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "nervous shock", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "shock", "similarity": 0.937}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "shock", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "shock", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "shock", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "shock", "similarity": 0.863}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "shock", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "shock", "similarity": 0.863}], "id": 1304, "stable_key": "clu_273da65741918860", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Nervous shock", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Trauma response", "wikidata_id": "Q16998640", "wikidata_description": "concept in English law", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nervous%20shock", "wikipedia_extract": "In English law, a nervous shock is a psychiatric / mental illness or injury inflicted upon a person by intentional or negligent actions or omissions of another. Often it is a psychiatric disorder triggered by witnessing an accident, for example an injury caused to one's parents or spouse. Although the term \"nervous shock\" has been described as \"inaccurate\" and \"misleading\", it continues to be applied as a useful abbreviation for a complex concept. The possibility of recovering damages for nervous shock, particularly caused by negligence, is strongly limited in English law. To amount in law to \"nervous shock\", the psychiatric damage suffered by the claimant must extend beyond grief or emotional distress to a recognised mental illness, such as anxiety neurosis or reactive depression. Damages for bereavement suffered as a result of the wrongful death of a close one are available under the Fatal Accidents Act 1976, while courts can also award damages for \"pain and suffering\" as a result of physical injury."}}, {"canonical_name": "centre", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "centres", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "centres", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["centres"], "contexts": ["nerve centers", "nervous system processing areas"]}, {"entity_id": "centre", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "centre", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["centre"], "contexts": ["middle point of a circle"]}, {"entity_id": "center", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "center", "category