 4, "variants": ["nucleus"], "contexts": ["solid core of the sun", "central part of a comet"]}, {"entity_id": "nuclei", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "nuclei", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["nuclei"], "contexts": ["central core of a nebula"]}, {"entity_id": "nucleus", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "nucleus", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 3, "variants": ["nucleus"], "contexts": ["central part of a comet"]}, {"entity_id": "solid_nucleus", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "solid nucleus", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["solid nucleus"], "contexts": ["dense central part of the earth"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "nucleus", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "nucleus", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "nucleus", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "nuclei", "similarity": 0.972}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "nucleus", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "nucleus", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "nucleus", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "solid nucleus", "similarity": 0.885}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "nucleus", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "nucleus", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "nucleus", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "solid nucleus", "similarity": 0.885}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "nuclei", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "nucleus", "similarity": 0.972}], "id": 1307, "stable_key": "clu_2295e757c09b4f4e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "nucleus", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'nucleus' in early modern texts referred to a central core or essential part, applicable across various scientific fields like astronomy (core of a sun or nebula) and psychology (central part of a thought). It is not a specific anatomical structure in the modern biological sense but a general concept of centrality.", "wikidata_id": "Q40260", "wikidata_description": "membrane-bounded organelle of eukaryotic cells in which chromosomes are housed and replicated", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cell%20nucleus", "wikipedia_extract": "The cell nucleus (from Latin  nucleus or nuculeus 'kernel, seed'; pl.: nuclei) is a membrane-bound organelle found in eukaryotic cells. Eukaryotic cells usually have a single nucleus, but a few cell types, such as mammalian red blood cells, have no nuclei, and a few others including osteoclasts have many. The main structures making up the nucleus are the nuclear envelope, a double membrane that encloses the entire organelle and isolates its contents from the cellular cytoplasm; and the nuclear matrix, a network within the nucleus that adds mechanical support. The cell nucleus contains nearly all of the cell's genome. Nuclear DNA is often organized into multiple chromosomes \u2013 long strands of DNA dotted with various proteins, such as histones, that protect and organize the DNA. The genes within these chromosomes are structured in such a way to promote cell function."}}, {"canonical_name": "Galileo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "galileo", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Galileo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 7, "variants": ["Galileo"], "contexts": ["Italian astronomer and physicist", "observed pendulum vibrations as a boy"]}, {"entity_id": "galileo", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Galileo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Galileo"], "contexts": ["first to notice points of rest and motion", "proposed method for finding star distances"]}, {"entity_id": "galileo", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Galileo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Galileo"], "contexts": ["scientist who challenged notion of inherent stopping"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Galileo", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Galileo", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Galileo", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Galileo", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Galileo", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Galileo", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1308, "stable_key": "clu_9834594c5cdeb823", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Galileo Galilei", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1564, "death_year": 1642, "description": "Italian astronomer, physicist, and engineer, key figure in the scientific revolution.", "wikidata_id": "Q48810529", "wikidata_description": "classical lyceum in Pisa in the province of Pisa (Italy) [school id: PIPC00701T]"}}, {"canonical_name": "Humidity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "humidity", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Humidity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 5, "variants": ["humidity", "Humidity"], "contexts": ["moisture content of atmosphere", "measure of water vapor in air"]}, {"entity_id": "humidity", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "humidity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["humidity"], "contexts": ["measure of water vapor in the air", "water vapor in the air"]}, {"entity_id": "humidity", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "humidity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["humidity"], "contexts": ["moisture in the air"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Humidity", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "humidity", "similarity": 0.969}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Humidity", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "humidity", "similarity": 0.969}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "humidity", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "humidity", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1309, "stable_key": "clu_7c50c9966182ad91-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Humidity", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q180600", "wikidata_description": "amount of water vapor in the air", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Humidity", "wikipedia_extract": "Humidity is the concentration of water vapor present in the air. Water vapor, the gaseous state of water, is generally invisible to the naked eye. Humidity indicates the likelihood for precipitation, dew, or fog to be present. Humidity depends on the temperature and pressure of the system of interest. The same amount of water vapor results in higher relative humidity in cool air than warm air. A related parameter is the dew point."}}, {"canonical_name": "solar rays", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "solar_rays", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "solar rays", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["solar rays"], "contexts": ["radiation from the sun", "source of heat absorbed by Earth"]}, {"entity_id": "solar_rays", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "solar rays", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 5, "variants": ["solar rays"], "contexts": ["rays from the sun", "power augmented by glass plate"]}, {"entity_id": "solar_rays", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "solar rays", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["solar rays"], "contexts": ["affect atomic arrangements of crystals", "primary cause of geological processes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "solar rays", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "solar rays", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "solar rays", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "solar rays", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "solar rays", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "solar rays", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1310, "stable_key": "clu_eb4ee00d1a588d24-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Solar Radiation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q17996169", "wikidata_description": "electromagnetic radiation received from the Sun", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solar%20radiation", "note": "While 'solar rays' literally refers to rays from the sun, in the context of early modern science, it encompasses the broader concept of electromagnetic radiation and its effects."}}, {"canonical_name": "Expansion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "expanse", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "expanse", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["expanse"], "contexts": ["quality of nature"]}, {"entity_id": "expansion", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "expansion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["expansion"], "contexts": ["force of matter to increase in volume"]}, {"entity_id": "expansion", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Expansion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["expansion", "Expansion"], "contexts": ["increase in volume", "process of becoming larger"]}, {"entity_id": "expansion", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "expansion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["expansion"], "contexts": ["increase in volume of heated bodies", "increase in volume"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "expanse", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Expansion", "similarity": 0.935}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "expanse", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "expansion", "similarity": 0.939}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "expansion", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Expansion", "similarity": 0.981}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "expansion", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "expansion", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Expansion", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "expansion", "similarity": 0.981}], "id": 1311, "stable_key": "clu_53152bcbf971cc3d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Expansion", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q19841649", "wikidata_description": "act or process of expansion of a structure"}}, {"canonical_name": "mal", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "mal", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "mal", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 3, "variants": ["mal"], "contexts": ["illness of the Cacique", "illness or sickness"]}, {"entity_id": "mal", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "mal", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 3, "variants": ["mal", "Mal"], "contexts": ["proper name of the largest island", "general term for illness"]}, {"entity_id": "mal", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "mal", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 4, "variants": ["mal"], "contexts": ["condition related to vapors", "illness, general term"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "mal", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "mal", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "mal", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "mal", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "mal", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "mal", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1312, "stable_key": "clu_c476c5ce353e2187", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Malaria", "confidence": "medium", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Malaria", "note": "The term 'mal' in early modern Portuguese and Spanish texts could refer to various ailments, but in contexts of island diseases and vapors, malaria is a strong candidate. It also appears to be used as a proper noun for an island, which is not a medical referent.", "wikidata_id": "Q12156", "wikidata_description": "mosquito-borne infectious disease", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malaria", "wikipedia_extract": "Malaria is a mosquito-borne infectious disease which is transmitted by the bite of Anopheles mosquitoes. Human malaria causes symptoms that typically include fever, fatigue, vomiting, and headaches. In severe cases, it can cause jaundice, seizures, coma, or death. Symptoms usually begin 10 to 15 days after being bitten by an infected Anopheles mosquito. If not properly treated, people may have recurrences of the disease months later. In those who have recently survived an infection, reinfection usually causes milder symptoms."}}, {"canonical_name": "battery", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "battery", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "battery", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 7, "variants": ["battery"], "contexts": ["device producing electrical current", "device producing electric current"]}, {"entity_id": "battery", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "battery", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["battery"], "contexts": ["produces electric current from chemical affinities"]}, {"entity_id": "battery", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "battery", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 1, "variants": ["battery"], "contexts": ["device producing electric current"]}, {"entity_id": "battery,_magnetic", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Battery, magnetic", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Battery, magnetic"], "contexts": ["device for magnetic electricity"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "battery", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "battery", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "battery", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Battery, magnetic", "similarity": 0.89}], "id": 1313, "stable_key": "clu_7