 have been assigned common names in addition to their taxon name", "note": "The phrase 'organic whole' in this context refers to a living organism or the interconnectedness of its parts."}}, {"canonical_name": "liquides", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "liquides", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "liquides", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 4, "variants": ["liquides"], "contexts": ["body fluids", "bodily fluids examined in experiments"]}, {"entity_id": "liquids", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "liquids", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["liquids"], "contexts": ["state of matter"]}, {"entity_id": "liquids", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Liquids", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 4, "variants": ["liquids", "Liquids"], "contexts": ["state of matter", "state of matter with fixed volume but not shape"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "liquides", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "liquids", "similarity": 0.998}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "liquides", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Liquids", "similarity": 0.991}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "liquids", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Liquids", "similarity": 0.994}], "id": 1317, "stable_key": "clu_eece0e86af87a552", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Liquid", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to any fluid, including bodily fluids, solutions, or extracts. The Italian 'liquori' also specifically points to medicinal preparations.", "wikidata_id": "Q469652", "wikidata_description": "tree species", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liquidambar%20styraciflua", "wikipedia_extract": "Liquidambar styraciflua, commonly known as the American sweetgum among other names, is a deciduous tree in the genus Liquidambar native to warm temperate areas of eastern North America and tropical montane regions of Mexico and Central America. Sweetgum is one of the main valuable forest trees in the southeastern United States, and is a popular ornamental tree in temperate climates. It is recognizable by the combination of its five-pointed star-shaped leaves (similar to maple leaves) and its hard, spiked fruits. It is currently classified in the plant family Altingiaceae, but was formerly considered a member of the Hamamelidaceae. This plant's genus name Liquidambar was first given by Linnaeus in 1753 from the Latin liquidus ('fluid') and the Arabic ambar ('amber'), in allusion to the fragrant terebinthine juice or gum which exudes from the tree. Its specific epithet styraciflua is an old generic name meaning 'flowing with storax' (a plant resin)."}}, {"canonical_name": "raz\u00e3o", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "raisonne", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "raisonne", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["raisonne"], "contexts": ["to think logically", "to think logically and form conclusions"]}, {"entity_id": "raz\u00e3o", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "raz\u00e3o", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["raz\u00e3o", "raz\u00e3o natural"], "contexts": ["faculty of the mind", "faculty of reason"]}, {"entity_id": "rationale", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "rationale", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["rationale"], "contexts": ["explanation of reasons"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "raisonne", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "raz\u00e3o", "similarity": 0.903}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "raisonne", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "rationale", "similarity": 0.906}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "raz\u00e3o", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "rationale", "similarity": 0.91}], "id": 1318, "stable_key": "clu_64d8a64bd668f3ab", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "rationality", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q938185", "wikidata_description": "quality of being agreeable to reason"}}, {"canonical_name": "\u00e0 priori", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "a_priori", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "a priori", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["a priori"], "contexts": ["ideas that are starting points of experimental reasoning", "from theory rather than observation"]}, {"entity_id": "a_priori", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "a priori", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["a priori"], "contexts": ["reasoning from theory to observation", "relations between data fixed by mind"]}, {"entity_id": "\u00e0_priori", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "\u00e0 priori", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["\u00e0 priori"], "contexts": ["reasoning from theory to reality", "truths independent of experience"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "a priori", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "a priori", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "a priori", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "\u00e0 priori", "similarity": 0.967}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "a priori", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "\u00e0 priori", "similarity": 0.967}], "id": 1319, "stable_key": "clu_7b2ae84bc6625de6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "a priori reasoning", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q12078874", "wikidata_description": "knowledge or justification independent of experience"}}, {"canonical_name": "synthesis", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "synth\u00e8se", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "synth\u00e8se", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["synth\u00e8se"], "contexts": ["reconstituting from components", "combining parts into a whole"]}, {"entity_id": "synthesis", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "synthesis", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["synthesis"], "contexts": ["combining elements into a whole", "function of combining elements"]}, {"entity_id": "synthesis", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "synthesis", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["synthesis"], "contexts": ["combining components into a whole"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "synth\u00e8se", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "synthesis", "similarity": 0.924}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "synth\u00e8se", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "synthesis", "similarity": 0.924}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "synthesis", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "synthesis", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1320, "stable_key": "clu_a863b3c397d40319", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "synthesis", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q851162", "wikidata_description": "multi-step, enzyme-catalyzed process where substrates are converted into more complex products in living organisms. In biosynthesis, simple compounds are modified, converted into other compounds, or joined to form macromolecules", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biosynthesis", "wikipedia_extract": "Biosynthesis, i.e., chemical synthesis occurring in biological contexts, is a term most often referring to multi-step, enzyme-catalyzed processes where chemical substances absorbed as nutrients (or previously converted through biosynthesis) serve as enzyme substrates, with conversion by the living organism either into simpler or more complex products. Examples of biosynthetic pathways include those for the production of amino acids, lipid membrane components, and nucleotides, but also for the production of all classes of biological macromolecules, and of acetyl-coenzyme A, adenosine triphosphate, nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide and other key intermediate and transactional molecules needed for metabolism. Thus, in biosynthesis, any of an array of compounds, from simple to complex, are converted into other compounds, and so it includes both the anabolism and catabolism (building up and breaking down) of complex molecules (including macromolecules). Biosynthetic processes are often represented via charts of metabolic pathways. A particular biosynthetic pathway may be located within a single cellular organelle (e.g., mitochondrial fatty acid synthesis pathways), while others involve enzymes that are located across an array of cellular organelles and structures (e.g., the biosynthesis of glycosylated cell surface proteins). Elements of biosynthesis include: precursor compounds, chemical energy (e.g."}}, {"canonical_name": "oiseaux", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "oiseaux", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "oiseaux", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 2, "variants": ["oiseaux"], "contexts": ["animals studied for nerve properties", "birds used in poisoning experiments"]}, {"entity_id": "oiseaux", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "oiseaux", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 3, "variants": ["oiseaux"], "contexts": ["creatures produced by upper waters", "birds whose feathers aid seed transport"]}, {"entity_id": "oiseaux", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "oiseaux", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 4, "variants": ["oiseaux", "oiseaux de terre", "oiseaux nocturnes", "oiseaux p\u00eacheurs"], "contexts": ["birds attracted by fruit and foliage", "nocturnal birds"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "oiseaux", "target_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "target_name": "oiseaux", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "oiseaux", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "oiseaux", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "oiseaux", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "oiseaux", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1321, "stable_key": "clu_389380fec5c7dba3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Birds", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "The French term 'oiseaux' unambiguously refers to birds. The contexts mentioning them in relation to nerve properties, upper waters, and fruit/foliage align with general observations of avian life.", "wikidata_id": "Q14915264", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bird%20%28surname%29"}}, {"canonical_name": "mathematics", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "mathematics", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "mathematics", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["mathematics"], "contexts": ["study of numbers, quantity, space, and relation"]}, {"entity_id": "math\u00e9matique", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "math\u00e9matique", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["math\u00e9matique"], "contexts": ["relating to mathematics and its principles"]}, {"entity_id": "mathematics", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Mathematics", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Mathematics"], "contexts": ["teaches laws of number and quantity", "use in the study of astronomy"]}, {"entity_id": "mathematics", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "mathematics", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["mathematics"], "contexts": ["abstract science of number, quantity, space", "study of numbers, quantity, space, structure"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "mathematics", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Mathematics", "similarity": 0.977}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "mathematics", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mathematics", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "math\u00e9matique", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Mathematics", "similarity": 0.958}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "math\u00e9matique", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mathematics", "similarity": 0.949}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Mathematics", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mathematics", "similarity": 0.977}], "id": 1322, "stable_key": "clu_aa7b75e3c263398b-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mathematics", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q853077", "wikidata_description": "mathematics teaching, learning and scholarly research", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mathematics%20education", "wikipedia_extract": "In contemporary education, mathematics education (known in Europe as the didactics or pedagogy of mathematics) is the practice of teaching, learning, and carrying out scholarly research into the transfer of mathematical knowledge. Although research into mathematics education is primarily concerned with the tools, methods, and approaches that facilitate practice or the study of practice, it also covers an extensive field of study encompassing a variety of different concepts, theories and methods. National and international organisations regularly hold conferences and publish literature in order to improve mathematics education. Elementary mathematics were a core part of education in many ancient civilisations, including ancient Egypt, ancient Babylonia, ancient Greece, ancient Rome, and Vedic India. In most cases, formal education was only available to male children with sufficiently high status, wealth, or caste. The oldest known mathematics textbook is the Rhind papyrus, dated from circa 1650 BCE."}}, {"canonical_name": "reactions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "r\u00e9actions", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "r\u00e9actions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["r\u00e9actions"], "contexts": ["responses to stimuli"]}, {"entity_id": "reactions", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "reactions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["reactions"], "contexts": ["responses to stimuli", "response to a stimulus"]}, {"entity_id": "reaction", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "reaction", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["reaction"], "contexts": ["response to stimulus", "response to a stimulus"]}, {"entity_id": "re-action", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "re-action", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["re-action"], "contexts": ["force exerted in response"]}, {"entity_id": "reactions", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "reactions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["reactions"], "contexts": ["responses to stimuli or conditions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "r\u00e9actions", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "reactions", "similarity": 0.911}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "r\u00e9actions", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "reaction", "similarity": 0.902}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "r\u00e9actions", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "re-action", "similarity": 0.894}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "r\u00e9actions", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "reactions", "similarity": 0.911}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "reactions", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "re-action", "similarity": 0.863}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "reactions", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "reactions", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "reaction", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "re-action", "similarity": 0.88}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "reaction", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "reactions", "similarity": 0.933}], "id": 1323, "stable_key": "clu_c3028f2e5096914d-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Chemical reactions", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q36534", "wikidata_description": "process that results in the interconversion of chemical species", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chemical%20reaction", "wikipedia_extract": "A chemical reaction is a process that leads to the chemical transformation of one set of chemical substances to another. When chemical reactions occur, the atoms are rearranged and the reaction is accompanied by an energy change as new products are generated. Classically, chemical reactions encompass changes that only involve the positions of electrons in the forming and breaking of chemical bonds between atoms, with no change to the nuclei (no change to the elements present), and can often be described by a chemical equation. Nuclear chemistry is a sub-discipline of chemistry that involves the chemical reactions of unstable and radioactive elements where both electronic and nuclear changes can occur. The substance (or substances) initially involved in a chemical reaction are called reactants or reagents. Chemical reactions are usually characterized by a chemical change, and they yield one or more products, which usually have properties different from the reactants."}}, {"canonical_name": "vital activity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "activit\u00e9_vitale", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "activit\u00e9 vitale", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["activit\u00e9 vitale"], "contexts": ["vital activity of organic elements"]}, {"entity_id": "vital_activity", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "vital activity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["vital activity"], "contexts": ["life force within a planet", "life force in the material world"]}, {"entity_id": "vital_actions", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "vital actions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["vital actions"], "contexts": ["processes constituting life", "forces derived similarly"]}, {"entity_id": "vital_activity", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "vital activity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["vital activity"], "contexts": ["activity characteristic of living beings"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "activit\u00e9 vitale", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "vital activity", "similarity": 0.971}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "activit\u00e9 vitale", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "vital actions", "similarity": 0.887}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "activit\u00e9 vitale", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "vital activity", "similarity": 0.971}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "vital activity", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "vital actions", "similarity": 0.925}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "vital activity", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "vital activity", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1324, "stable_key": "clu_4a6b4cb816813879", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Vitalism", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The phrases 'vital activity', 'vital actions', and 'life force' are hallmarks of the philosophical concept of vitalism.", "wikidata_id": "Q137271198", "wikidata_description": "nonprofit organisation from the Netherlands"}}, {"canonical_name": "instinctive", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "instinctive", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "instinctive", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["instinctive"], "contexts": ["innate, unlearned behavior or drive"]}, {"entity_id": "instinctive", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "instinctive", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["instinctive"], "contexts": ["behavior not learned by experience"]}, {"entity_id": "instinctive", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "instinctive", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 7, "variants": ["instinctive"], "contexts": ["innate, unlearned behavior", "innate behavioral patterns"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "instinctive", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "instinctive", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "instinctive", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "instinctive", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "instinctive", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "instinctive", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1325, "stable_key": "clu_a76d4c90e69b981a-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Instinct", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q18237", "wikidata_description": "inherent inclination of a living organism"}}, {"canonical_name": "Serpent", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "REPTILE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "serpent", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Serpent", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "REPTILE", "count": 6, "variants": ["Serpent"], "contexts": ["form of Satan's apparition", "initiated deceit"]}, {"entity_id": "serpiente", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "serpiente", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "REPTILE", "count": 1, "variants": ["serpiente"], "contexts": ["animal with venomous properties"]}, {"entity_id": "serpentarius", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Serpentarius", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Serpentarius"], "contexts": ["constellation where a star appeared", "star in, vanishing"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Serpent", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "serpiente", "similarity": 0.939}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Serpent", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Serpentarius", "similarity": 0.9}], "id": 1326, "stable_key": "clu_22a686330488aa12-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Snakes", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q2102", "wikidata_description": "limbless, scaly, elongate reptile", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snake", "wikipedia_extract": "Snakes are elongated limbless reptiles of the suborder Serpentes. Cladistically squamates, snakes are ectothermic, amniote vertebrates covered in overlapping scales much like other members of the group. Many species of snakes have skulls with several more joints than their lizard ancestors and relatives, enabling them to swallow prey much larger than their heads (cranial kinesis). To accommodate their narrow bodies, snakes' paired organs (such as kidneys) appear one in front of the other instead of side by side, and most only have one functional lung. Some species retain a pelvic girdle with a pair of vestigial claws on either side of the cloaca. Lizards have independently evolved elongate bodies without limbs or with greatly reduced limbs at least twenty-five times via convergent evolution, leading to many lineages of legless lizards."}}, {"canonical_name": "Bear", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "bear", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Bear", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 5, "variants": ["Bear"], "contexts": ["animal with a frequent oratorical tradition", "large mammal discussed in chapter"]}, {"entity_id": "bear", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Bear", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["bear", "Bear"], "contexts": ["large mammal", "catching water-insects"]}, {"entity_id": "bears", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "bears", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["bears"], "contexts": ["large carnivorous mammals"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Bear", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Bear", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Bear", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "bears", "similarity": 0.925}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Bear", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "bears", "similarity": 0.925}], "id": 1327, "stable_key": "clu_72278e86951327b8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bear", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q11788", "wikidata_description": "family of mammals", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bear", "wikipedia_extract": "Bears are carnivoran mammals of the family Ursidae (). They are classified as caniforms, or doglike carnivorans. Although only eight species of bears are extant, they are widespread, appearing in a wide variety of habitats throughout most of the Northern Hemisphere and partially in the Southern Hemisphere. Bears are found on the continents of North America, South America, and Eurasia. Common characteristics of modern bears include large bodies with stocky legs, long snouts, small rounded ears, shaggy hair, plantigrade paws with five nonretractile claws, and short tails. While the polar bear is mostly carnivorous, and the giant panda is mostly herbivorous, the remaining six species are omnivorous with varying diets."}}, {"canonical_name": "Isidore", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "isidore", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Isidore", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 5, "variants": ["Isidore"], "contexts": ["Church Father, wrote Etymologies", "ancient scholar"]}, {"entity_id": "isidor", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Isidor", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Isidor"], "contexts": ["Bishop of Sevil, author of De Originibus"]}, {"entity_id": "isidore", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Isidore", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Isidore"], "contexts": ["medieval scholar"]}, {"entity_id": "isidorus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Isidorus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Isidorus"], "contexts": ["ancient medical authority", "ancient authority cited"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "s