rity": 0.833}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "heavens", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "heavens", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1331, "stable_key": "clu_7297c94f67f8f5e6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cosmos", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'heavens' in early modern texts encompasses both the religious concept of an afterlife destination and the physical realm of celestial bodies and space. 'Cosmos' best captures the latter, scientific sense, while the religious sense is more theological. Given the context with Humboldt, 'Cosmos' is the most fitting modern referent for the scientific interpretation.", "wikidata_id": "Q476937", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cosmos%20%28plant%29", "wikipedia_extract": "Cosmos is a genus, with the same common name of cosmos, consisting of flowering plants in the daisy family. The generic name Cosmos derives either from the Greek \u03ba\u03cc\u03c3\u03bc\u03bf\u03c2 (cosmos) '(ordered) world' \u2013 in reference to the neat, orderly arrangement of the floral structures \u2013 or the Greek \u03ba\u03cc\u03c3\u03bc\u03b7\u03bc\u03b1 (k\u00f3smima) 'jewel' \u2013 in reference to the jewel-like colors of the capitula (composite flowers). Cosmos are herbaceous perennial plants or annual plants growing 0.3\u20132 metres (1\u20136+1\u20442 ft) tall. The leaves are simple, pinnate, or bipinnate, and arranged in opposite pairs. The flowers are produced in a capitulum with a ring of broad ray florets and a center of disc florets; flower color varies noticeably between the different species. The genus includes several ornamental plants popular in gardens."}}, {"canonical_name": "JESUS", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "jesus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Jesus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Jesus"], "contexts": ["Biblical figure, answered Peter's question", "suffered"]}, {"entity_id": "jesus", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Jesus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Jesus"], "contexts": ["body to be embalmed"]}, {"entity_id": "jesus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "JESUS", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 6, "variants": ["JESUS", "JESU", "Jesu"], "contexts": ["religious order", "arch name"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Jesus", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Jesus", "similarity": 0.836}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Jesus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "JESUS", "similarity": 0.824}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Jesus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "JESUS", "similarity": 0.982}], "id": 1332, "stable_key": "clu_4c123329a35134ab-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jesus Christ", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Central figure of Christianity, associated with religious healing and theology.", "note": "While 'Christ' can refer to the title of an anointed one, in these contexts, particularly with religious and priestly associations, it overwhelmingly refers to Jesus Christ.", "wikidata_id": "Q105742579", "wikidata_description": "American Internet personality", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jesus", "wikipedia_extract": "Jesus (c. 6 to 4 BC \u2013 AD 30 or 33), also referred to as Jesus Christ, Jesus of Nazareth, and by various other names and titles, was a 1st-century Jewish preacher and religious leader in the Roman province of Judaea. He is the central figure of Christianity, the world's largest religion. Most Christians consider Jesus to be the incarnation of God the Son and the awaited messiah, or Christ, a descendant of the Davidic line prophesied in the Old Testament. Virtually all modern scholars of antiquity agree that Jesus existed historically. Accounts of Jesus's life are contained in the Gospels, especially the four canonical Gospels of the New Testament."}}, {"canonical_name": "anime", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "anime", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Anime", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "RESIN", "count": 2, "variants": ["Anime"], "contexts": ["resinous body attracting vigorously", "electrick material used in experiments"]}, {"entity_id": "anime", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "anime", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "RESIN", "count": 3, "variants": ["anime"], "contexts": ["yellow gum compared to anime", "substance similar to the yellow and golden gum"]}, {"entity_id": "anime", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "anime", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 4, "variants": ["anime"], "contexts": ["substance for scent and perfumes", "resin identified with cancamo"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Anime", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "anime", "similarity": 0.979}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Anime", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "anime", "similarity": 0.663}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "anime", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "anime", "similarity": 0.692}], "id": 1333, "stable_key": "clu_e495df137065d128", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Anise", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Pimpinella anisum Linnaeus", "family": "Apiaceae", "note": "The context of 'resinous body attracting vigorously' and 'substance for scent and perfumes' points to anise, though it might also relate to anime resin, it is less likely given the other context.", "wikidata_id": "Q28692", "wikidata_description": "species of plant"}}, {"canonical_name": "Profetas", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "prophet", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Prophet", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Prophet"], "contexts": ["source of doctrine on earthquakes"]}, {"entity_id": "propheta", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Propheta", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Propheta"], "contexts": ["Prophet Muhammad"]}, {"entity_id": "profetas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Profetas", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 7, "variants": ["Profetas", "Profeffores", "Profeta"], "contexts": ["called 'Videntes' in scripture", "Prophets"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Prophet", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Propheta", "similarity": 0.98}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Prophet", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Profetas", "similarity": 0.886}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Propheta", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Profetas", "similarity": 0.884}], "id": 1334, "stable_key": "clu_6f3075ae38130e5f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Prophets", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Individuals believed to speak for God or divine inspiration.", "wikidata_id": "Q51236403", "wikidata_description": "5th episode of the 4th season of Person of Interest"}}, {"canonical_name": "Greek", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "greek", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Greek", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Greek"], "contexts": ["ancient Greek people and language"]}, {"entity_id": "greek", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Greek", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Greek"], "contexts": ["language of origin for the herb's name", "language"]}, {"entity_id": "greek", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Greek", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["Greek"], "contexts": ["language used for nomenclature", "ancient language"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Greek", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Greek", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Greek", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Greek", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Greek", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Greek", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1335, "stable_key": "clu_9e5ff38345857305", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Greek", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the ancient Greek people, culture, and language, often used in contexts of classical scholarship and the origin of scientific/medical terminology.", "wikidata_id": "Q7798", "wikidata_description": "name give in ancient Greece to their region"}}, {"canonical_name": "liquori", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "liquor", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "liquor", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["liquor"], "contexts": ["fluid absorbed by bodies"]}, {"entity_id": "liquor", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Liquor", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Liquor"], "contexts": ["liquid from decoction", "liquid from boiling used for syrup"]}, {"entity_id": "liquori", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "liquori", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 6, "variants": ["liquori", "liquore"], "contexts": ["substance derived from plants, liquors", "liquids used for medicine"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "liquor", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Liquor", "similarity": 0.995}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "liquor", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "liquori", "similarity": 0.945}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Liquor", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "liquori", "similarity": 0.925}], "id": 1336, "stable_key": "clu_eece0e86af87a552-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Liquid", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to any fluid, including bodily fluids, solutions, or extracts. The Italian 'liquori' also specifically points to medicinal preparations.", "wikidata_id": "Q469652", "wikidata_description": "tree species", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liquidambar%20styraciflua", "wikipedia_extract": "Liquidambar styraciflua, commonly known as the American sweetgum among other names, is a deciduous tree in the genus Liquidambar native to warm temperate areas of eastern North America and tropical montane regions of Mexico and Central America. Sweetgum is one of the main valuable forest trees in the southeastern United States, and is a popular ornamental tree in temperate climates. It is recognizable by the combination of its five-pointed star-shaped leaves (similar to maple leaves) and its hard, spiked fruits. It is currently classified in the plant family Altingiaceae, but was formerly considered a member of the Hamamelidaceae. This plant's genus name Liquidambar was first given by Linnaeus in 1753 from the Latin liquidus ('fluid') and the Arabic ambar ('amber'), in allusion to the fragrant terebinthine juice or gum which exudes from the tree. Its specific epithet styraciflua is an old generic name meaning 'flowing with storax' (a plant resin)."}}, {"canonical_name": "Bdellio", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "RESIN", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "bdellium", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Bdellium", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "RESIN", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bdellium"], "contexts": ["aromatic resin"]}, {"entity_id": "bdellio", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Bdellio", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "RESIN", "count": 5, "variants": ["BDELLIO", "Bdellio", "bdellio"], "contexts": ["resin, tear of a Serracenic tree", "resin from a Serracenian tree"]}, {"entity_id": "bdellio", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "bdellio", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["bdellio"], "contexts": ["ingredient in a fomentation", "resin used in a liniment"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Bdellium", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Bdellio", "similarity": 0.963}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Bdellio", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "bdellio", "similarity": 0.476}], "id": 1337, "stable_key": "clu_b1a40bfa72f21a8a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bdellium", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "note": "Bdellium is a known aromatic resin from various Commiphora species, fitting the context of an aromatic resin from a tree.", "wikipedia_extract": "Commiphora wightii, with common names Indian bdellium-tree, gugal, guggal, guggul, gugul, or mukul myrrh tree, is a flowering plant in the family Burseraceae, which produces a fragrant resin called gugal, guggul or gugul, that is used in incense and vedic medicine (or ayurveda). The species is native to western India, from where it was introduced westward to southern Pakistan and the middle-east. It prefers arid and semi-arid climates and is tolerant of poor soil. Commiphora wightii grows as a shrub or small tree, reaching a maximum height of 4 m (13 ft), with thin papery bark. The branches are thorny. The leaves are simple or trifoliate, the leaflets ovate, 1\u20135 cm (0.39\u20131.97 in) long, 0.5\u20132.5 cm (0.20\u20130.98 in) broad, and irregularly toothed.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Commiphora_wightii"}}, {"canonical_name": "marbres", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "marbles", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Marbles", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Marbles"], "contexts": ["hard bodies that do not readily strike fire"]}, {"entity_id": "marbres", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "marbres", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 7, "variants": ["marbres"], "contexts": ["metamorphic rock", "marbles showing shell traces"]}, {"entity_id": "marbles", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "marbles", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["marbles"], "contexts": ["small spheres used in experiments"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Marbles", "target_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "target_name": "marbres", "similarity": 0.967}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Marbles", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "marbles", "similarity": 0.993}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "marbres", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "marbles", "similarity": 0.965}], "id": 1338, "stable_key": "clu_aba4f89f40d67b54", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Marble", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "Contexts suggest both the rock and potentially a salt derived from it.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marble", "wikipedia_extract": "Marble is a metamorphic rock consisting of carbonate minerals (most commonly calcite  (CaCO3) or dolomite (CaMg(CO3)2) that have recrystallized under the influence of heat and pressure. It has a crystalline texture, and is typically not foliated (layered), although there are exceptions. In geology, the term marble refers to metamorphosed limestone, but its use in stonemasonry more broadly encompasses unmetamorphosed limestone. Pure white marble arises from very clean limestone or rock also called dolomite. However, its signature veins and swirls result from impurities like clay, sand, iron oxides, or chert originally present in the protolith\u2014magnesium-rich varieties may show green tones due to serpentine. These impurities are redistributed and recrystallized during metamorphism."}}, {"canonical_name": "Red Sea", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "red_sea", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Red sea", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Red sea"], "contexts": ["sea between Africa and Asia"]}, {"entity_id": "red_sea", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Red Sea", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Red Sea"], "contexts": ["sea", "geographic body of water"]}, {"entity_id": "red_sea", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Red Sea", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Red Sea"], "contexts": ["sea where tides are perceptible", "sea with different marine plants"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Red sea", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Red Sea", "similarity": 0.982}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Red sea", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Red Sea", "similarity": 0.982}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Red Sea", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Red Sea", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1339, "stable_key": "clu_8297bc8005964d5d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Red Sea", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A sea route connecting East and West.", "wikipedia_extract": "The Red Sea is a sea inlet of the Indian Ocean, lying between Africa and Asia. Its connection to the ocean is in the south, through the Bab-el-Mandeb Strait and the Gulf of Aden. To the north of the Red Sea lies the Sinai Peninsula, the Gulf of Aqaba, and the Gulf of Suez, which leads to the Suez Canal. It is underlain by the Red Sea Rift, which is part of the Great Rift Valley. The Red Sea has a surface area of roughly 438,000 km2 (169,000 sq mi), is about 2,250 km (1,400 mi) long, and 355 km (221 mi) across at its widest point. It has an average depth of 490 m (1,610 ft), and in the central Suakin Trough, it reaches its maximum depth of 2,730 m (8,960 ft)."}}, {"canonical_name": "granada", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "granate", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Granate", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Granate"], "contexts": ["gemstone"]}, {"entity_id": "granada", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "granada", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 6, "variants": ["granada", "GRANADA", "Granada", "Granatorum", "granadaf"], "contexts": ["location of publication", "precious stone (garnet)"]}, {"entity_id": "granato", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "granato", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["granato", "Granati"], "contexts": ["precious stone", "garnet stones"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Granate", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "granada", "similarity": 0.905}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "granada", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "granato", "similarity": 0.888}], "id": 1340, "stable_key": "clu_cc9163ff2fb61e0f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Garnet", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "Refers to the gemstone, but also can be a place name ('Granada') and a color. Given the contexts, gemstone is the most likely primary referent.", "wikidata_id": "Q105368", "wikidata_description": "family of semi-precious minerals"}}, {"canonical_name": "hernias", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "hernias", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Hernias", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hernias"], "contexts": ["a medical condition"]}, {"entity_id": "herniam", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Herniam", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Herniam"], "contexts": ["plaster for hernia with attractive power"]}, {"entity_id": "hernias", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "hernias", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["hernias"], "contexts": ["ruptures or hernias", "medical condition involving protrusions"]}, {"entity_id": "hernias", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "hernias", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 5, "variants": ["hernias", "Hernias", "Hernias inte\u017ftinaes", "hernias carnofas"], "contexts": ["condition affecting the throat", "hernias"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Hernias", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "hernias", "similarity": 0.991}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Hernias", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "hernias", "similarity": 0.991}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Herniam", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "hernias", "similarity": 0.903}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Herniam", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "hernias", "similarity": 0.903}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "hernias", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "hernias", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1341, "stable_key": "clu_e94a9c6f55e7ef24", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "hernias", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "Protrusion of an organ or tissue through an abnormal opening.", "note": "Includes specific types of ruptures and general hernias.", "wikipedia_extract": "A hernia (pl.: hernias or herniae, from Latin, meaning 'rupture') is the abnormal exit of tissue or an organ, such as the bowel, through the wall of the cavity in which it normally resides. The term is also used for the normal development of the intestinal tract, referring to the retraction of the intestine from the extra-embryonal navel coelom into the abdomen in the healthy embryo at about 71\u20442 weeks. Various types of hernias can occur, most commonly involving the abdomen, and specifically the groin. Groin hernias are most commonly inguinal hernias but may also be femoral hernias. Other types of hernias include hiatus, incisional, and umbilical hernias. Symptoms are present in about 66% of people with groin hernias."}}, {"canonical_name": "Thunder", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "thunder", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Thunder", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Thunder"], "contexts": ["sound caused by lightning"]}, {"entity_id": "thunder", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Thunder", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Thunder"], "contexts": ["natural phenomenon"]}, {"entity_id": "thunder", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Thunder", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 7, "variants": ["Thunder", "thunder"], "contexts": ["sound caused by lightning", "sound from lightning"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Thunder", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Thunder", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Thunder", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Thunder", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Thunder", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Thunder", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1342, "stable_key": "clu_136423f129fdc713", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Thunder", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'thunder' in this context refers to the natural phenomenon of sound, not an animal. The subcategory 'OTHER_ANIMAL' is likely an error.", "wikidata_id": "Q34116", "wikidata_description": "city in Thunder Bay District, province of Ontario, Canada", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thunder", "wikipedia_extract": "Thunder is the sound caused by lightning. Depending upon the distance from and nature of the lightning, it can range from a long, low rumble to a sudden, loud crack. The sudden increase in temperature and hence pressure caused by the lightning produces rapid expansion of the air in the path of a lightning bolt. In turn, this expansion of air creates a sonic shock wave, often referred to as a \"thunderclap\" or \"peal of thunder\". The scientific study of thunder is known as brontology and the irrational fear (phobia) of thunder is called brontophobia. The d in Modern English thunder (from earlier Old English \u00feunor) is epenthetic, and is now found as well in Modern Dutch donder (cf."}}, {"canonical_name": "internal heat", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "chaleur_int\u00e9rieure", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "chaleur int\u00e9rieure", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["chaleur int\u00e9rieure"], "contexts": ["heat originating from within the Earth", "abstract ideas, theories, mental faculties, and processes"]}, {"entity_id": "internal_heat", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "internal heat", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["internal heat"], "contexts": ["heat within the Earth", "heat generated within the Earth"]}, {"entity_id": "internal_heat", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "internal heat", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["internal heat"], "contexts": ["heat originating from within the Earth", "heat originating from within the earth"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "chaleur int\u00e9rieure", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "internal heat", "similarity": 0.929}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "chaleur int\u00e9rieure", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "internal heat", "similarity": 0.929}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "internal heat", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "internal heat", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1343, "stable_key": "clu_10c9b3101fd518f6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Internal heat", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q129655466", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article published in November 2018"}}, {"canonical_name": "bois", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "bois", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "bois", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 1, "variants": ["bois"], "contexts": ["wood pieces forming coal substance"]}, {"entity_id": "bois", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "bois", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 7, "variants": ["bois", "bois de charpente", "bois de construction", "bois de saule"], "contexts": ["material for bundles", "fragments found in sandstone"]}, {"entity_id": "woods", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "woods", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 1, "variants": ["woods"], "contexts": ["areas with many trees"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "bois", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "bois", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "bois", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "woods", "similarity": 0.94}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "bois", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "woods", "similarity": 0.94}], "id": 1344, "stable_key": "clu_8a1ea2407ba4f0ee-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Wood", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The context of 'timber', 'bois', and 'woods' consistently refers to the material derived from trees, used for construction, fuel, or as a habitat.", "wikidata_id": "Q105596644", "wikidata_description": "study of wood as a material and its physical, chemical, and biological characteristics", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wood%20science", "description": "The hard fibrous material forming the main substance of the trunk or branches of a tree or shrub.", "wikipedia_extract": "Wood is a structural tissue/material found as xylem in the stems and roots of trees and other woody plants. Being a natural material, it is characterized as an organic material \u2013 a natural composite of cellulosic fibers that are strong in tension and embedded in a matrix of lignin and hemicelluloses that resists compression. Wood is sometimes defined as only the secondary xylem in the stems of trees, or more broadly to include the same type of tissue elsewhere, such as in the roots of trees or shrubs. In a living tree, it performs a mechanical-support function, enabling woody plants to grow large or to stand up by themselves. It also conveys water and nutrients among the leaves, other growing tissues, and the roots. Wood may also refer to other plant materials with comparable properties, and to material engineered from wood, woodchips, or fibers."}}, {"canonical_name": "Discolourings", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "discolourings", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Discolourings", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 7, "variants": ["Discolourings", "discolorings", "discolourings", "discolourings of the Skin"], "contexts": ["skin imperfections removed by juice", "skin discoloration"]}, {"entity_id": "descoloridos", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "descoloridos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["descoloridos"], "contexts": ["pale appearance"]}, {"entity_id": "discoloratio-", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Discoloratio-", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Discoloratio-"], "contexts": ["skin discoloration"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Discolourings", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "descoloridos", "similarity": 0.938}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Discolourings", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Discoloratio-", "similarity": 0.93}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "descoloridos", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Discoloratio-", "similarity": 0.921}], "id": 1345, "stable_key": "clu_7ebb7ff79a4cf9ab", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Discoloration", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "Abnormal changes in the color of the skin or other tissues.", "note": "Refers to pale or otherwise altered skin tones.", "wikipedia_extract": "Jaundice, also known as icterus, is a yellowish or, less frequently, greenish pigmentation of the skin and sclera due to high bilirubin levels. Jaundice in adults typically indicates the presence of underlying diseases involving abnormal heme metabolism, liver dysfunction, or biliary-tract obstruction. The prevalence of jaundice in adults is rare, while jaundice in babies is common, with an estimated 80% affected during their first week of life. The most commonly associated symptoms of jaundice are itchiness, pale feces, and dark urine. Normal levels of bilirubin in blood are below 1.0 mg/dl (17 \u03bcmol/L), while levels over 2\u20133 mg/dl (34\u201351 \u03bcmol/L) typically result in jaundice. High blood bilirubin is divided into two types: unconjugated and conjugated bilirubin."}}, {"canonical_name": "Yorkshire", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "yorkshire", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Yorkshire", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 6, "variants": ["yorkshire", "Yorkshire"], "contexts": ["county where Red Bilberry grows", "region in the North parts of this Land"]}, {"entity_id": "yorkshire", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Yorkshire", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Yorkshire"], "contexts": ["district known for sheep farming", "English county"]}, {"entity_id": "yorkshire", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Yorkshire", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Yorkshire"], "contexts": ["cloth-districts"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Yorkshire", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Yorkshire", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Yorkshire", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Yorkshire", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Yorkshire", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Yorkshire", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1346, "stable_key": "clu_27e54ae73df0598c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Yorkshire", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A historic county in Northern England.", "wikipedia_extract": "Yorkshire ( YORK-sh\u0259r, -\u2060sheer) is an area of Northern England which was historically a county. Despite no longer being used for administration, Yorkshire retains a strong regional identity. The county was named after the city of York. The south-west of Yorkshire includes the cities of Leeds, Sheffield, Bradford, Doncaster and Wakefield. The north and east of the county includes the southern part of the Teesside conurbation; the port city of Hull is located in the south-east. York is positioned near the centre of the county."}}, {"canonical_name": "Averroes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "averrois", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Averrois", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Averrois", "Avenaris"], "contexts": ["medieval philosopher", "medieval medical authority"]}, {"entity_id": "averroes", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Averroes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 6, "variants": ["Averroes", "Avenzoar"], "contexts": ["medieval philosopher and physician", "ancient authority cited"]}, {"entity_id": "averroes", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Averroes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Averroes"], "contexts": ["medieval Islamic philosopher"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Averrois", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Averroes", "similarity": 0.967}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Averrois", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Averroes", "similarity": 0.967}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Averroes", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Averroes", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1347, "stable_key": "clu_7b18b0601727def9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Averroes", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1126, "death_year": 1198, "description": "Influential Islamic philosopher, jurist, and physician.", "wikidata_id": "Q39837", "wikidata_description": "Arab Andalusian Muslim writer and philosopher (1126\u20131198)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Averroes", "wikipedia_extract": "Ibn Rushd (14 April 1126 \u2013 11 December 1198), Latinized as Averroes, was an Andalusian polymath and jurist who was proficient in a variety of intellectual fields, including philosophy, theology, medicine, astronomy, physics, psychology, mathematics, neurology,  Islamic jurisprudence and law, and linguistics. The author of more than 100 books and treatises, his philosophical works include numerous commentaries on Aristotle, for which he was known in the Western world as \"The Commentator\" and \"Father of Rationalism\". Averroes was a strong proponent of Aristotelianism; he attempted to restore what he considered the original teachings of Aristotle and opposed the Neoplatonist tendencies of earlier Muslim thinkers, such as al-Farabi and Avicenna. He also defended the pursuit of philosophy against criticism by Ash'ari theologians such as Al-Ghazali. Averroes argued that philosophy was permissible in Islam and even compulsory among certain elites. He also argued scriptural text should be interpreted allegorically if it appeared to contradict conclusions reached by reason and philosophy."}}, {"canonical_name": "bagno Maria", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "balneo_mariae", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Balneo Mariae", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Balneo Mariae"], "contexts": ["apparatus for distillation", "water bath for distillation"]}, {"entity_id": "bagno_maria", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "bagno Maria", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 6, "variants": ["bagno Maria", "bagno Marie"], "contexts": ["water bath heating method", "Bain-marie, a double boiler for gentle heating"]}, {"entity_id": "banho_chamado_de_maria", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "banho chamado de Maria", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 1, "variants": ["banho chamado de Maria"], "contexts": ["method for extracting tincture"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Balneo Mariae", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "bagno Maria", "similarity": 0.896}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "bagno Maria", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "banho chamado de Maria", "similarity": 0.903}], "id": 1348, "stable_key": "clu_efd6efc02fac5ab3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bain-marie", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "description": "Double boiler used for gentle heating of substances.", "note": "Also known as a water bath.", "wikipedia_extract": "A bain-marie (English:  BAN-m\u0259-REE, French: [b\u025b\u0303 ma\u0281i]), also known as a water bath or double boiler, a type of heated bath, is a piece of equipment used in science, industry, and cooking to heat materials gently or to keep materials warm over a period of time. A bain-marie is also used to melt ingredients for cooking. The name comes from the French bain de Marie or bain-marie, in turn derived from the medieval Latin balneum Mariae, all meaning 'Mary's bath'. In his books, the 300 AD alchemist Zosimos of Panopolis credits for the invention of the device Mary the Jewess, an ancient alchemist. However, the water bath was known many centuries earlier (Hippocrates and Theophrastus), and the balneum Mariae attributed to Mary the Jewess was used to heat its contents above 100 \u00b0C, while the bain-marie that continues to be used today only heats its contents up to a gentle heat of less than 100 \u00b0C. The double boiler comes in a wide variety of shapes, sizes, and types, but traditionally is a wide, cylindrical, usually metal container made of three or four basic parts:  a handle, an outer (or lower) container that holds the working fluid, an inner (or upper), smaller container that fits inside the outer one and which holds the material to be heated or cooked, and sometimes a base underneath."}}, {"canonical_name": "Jerusal\u00e9m", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "jerusalem", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Jerusalem", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Jerusalem"], "contexts": ["Temple where writings were kept"]}, {"entity_id": "jerusalem", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Jerusalem", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Jerusalem"], "contexts": ["location of a king", "city in the Middle East"]}, {"entity_id": "jerusal\u00e9m", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Jerusal\u00e9m", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 6, "variants": ["Jerusal\u00e9m"], "contexts": ["city of origin for apothecary", "city where musay are found"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Jerusalem", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Jerusalem", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Jerusalem", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Jerusal\u00e9m", "similarity": 0.967}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Jerusalem", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Jerusal\u00e9m", "similarity": 0.967}], "id": 1349, "stable_key": "clu_3deaafe3e45de5e4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jerusalem", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Ancient and holy city, significant in religious and historical contexts.", "wikipedia_extract": "Jerusalem is a city in the Southern Levant, on a plateau in the Judaean Mountains between the Mediterranean and the Dead Sea. It is one of the oldest cities in the world and is considered holy to the three major Abrahamic religions\u2014Judaism, Christianity and Islam. Both Israel and Palestine claim Jerusalem as their capital city; Israel maintains its primary governmental institutions there, while Palestine ultimately foresees it as its seat of power. Neither claim is widely recognised internationally. Throughout its long history, Jerusalem has been destroyed at least twice, besieged 23 times, captured and recaptured 44 times, and attacked 52 times. The part of Jerusalem called the City of David shows first signs of settlement in the 4th millennium BCE, in the shape of encampments of nomadic shepherds."}}, {"canonical_name": "alg\u00e6", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "alg\u00e6", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "alg\u00e6", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 4, "variants": ["alg\u00e6", "Alg\u00e6"], "contexts": ["marine algae common to New Zealand and Europe", "diverse group of aquatic photosynthetic organisms"]}, {"entity_id": "alggae", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "alggae", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["alggae"], "contexts": ["found in calcareous sandstone"]}, {"entity_id": "alg\u00e6", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "alg\u00e6", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 4, "variants": ["alg\u00e6", "Alg\u00e6"], "contexts": ["marine plants", "marine plants with distinct species"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "alg\u00e6", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "alggae", "similarity": 0.917}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "alg\u00e6", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "alg\u00e6", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "alggae", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "alg\u00e6", "similarity": 0.917}], "id": 1350, "stable_key": "clu_1d54bb47eed602b5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Algae", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant"}}, {"canonical_name": "Mr. Blyth", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "mr__blyth", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Mr. Blyth", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Blyth", "Mr. Blyth"], "contexts": ["communicated facts on cattle", "judge of cross-bred geese fertility"]}, {"entity_id": "baily", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Baily", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Baily"], "contexts": ["reported on pendulum experiments"]}, {"entity_id": "baily", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Baily", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Baily"], "contexts": ["president of the Astronomical Society", "experimental philosopher"]}, {"entity_id": "mr__baily", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Mr. Baily", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Mr. Baily"], "contexts": ["obtained density of earth"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Mr. Blyth", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Mr. Baily", "similarity": 0.845}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Baily", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Baily", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Baily", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Mr. Baily", "similarity": 0.85}], "id": 1351, "stable_key": "clu_b720cdc1d51e550d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Edward Blyth", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1810, "death_year": 1873, "description": "Naturalist and zoologist, noted for work on animal behavior.", "wikidata_id": "Q358754", "wikidata_description": "English zoologist and pharmacist (1810\u20131873)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edward%20Blyth", "wikipedia_extract": "Edward Blyth (23 December 1810 \u2013 27 December 1873) was an English zoologist who worked for most of his life in India as a curator of zoology at the Royal Asiatic Society of Bengal in Calcutta (now Kolkata). He catalogued the specimens in the collection in his Catalogue of the Birds of the Asiatic Society in 1849. He did not collect specimens himself but received and described bird specimens from A.O. Hume, Samuel Tickell, Robert Swinhoe among others. His Natural History of the Cranes was published posthumously in 1881. On 23 December 1810, Blyth was born in London."}}, {"canonical_name": "Trilobites", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "trilobites", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Trilobites", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["trilobites", "Trilobites"], "contexts": ["ancient marine arthropods found in fossils", "extinct group of arthropods"]}, {"entity_id": "trilobites", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "trilobites", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["trilobites"], "contexts": ["fossil organism, first traces of organization", "fossil arthropod group"]}, {"entity_id": "trilobites", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Trilobites", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["Trilobites", "trilobites"], "contexts": ["fossil arthropod group", "fossil arthropods"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Trilobites", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "trilobites", "similarity": 0.991}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Trilobites", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Trilobites", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "trilobites", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Trilobites", "similarity": 0.991}], "id": 1352, "stable_key": "clu_83a5381cb8df5de2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Trilobites", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "description": "Extinct marine arthropods with a three-lobed body.", "note": "These are well-known fossil organisms.", "wikipedia_extract": "Trilobites (; meaning \"three-lobed entities\") are extinct marine arthropods that form the class Trilobita. One of the earliest groups of arthropods to appear in the fossil record, trilobites were among the most successful of all early animals, existing in oceans for almost 270 million years, with over 22,000 species having been described. Because trilobites had wide diversity and an easily fossilized mineralised exoskeleton made of calcite, they left an extensive fossil record. The study of their fossils has facilitated important contributions to biostratigraphy, paleontology, evolutionary biology, and plate tectonics. Trilobites are placed within the clade Artiopoda, which includes many organisms that are morphologically similar to trilobites, but are largely unmineralised. The relationship of Artiopoda to other arthropods is uncertain."}}, {"canonical_name": "development", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "development", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "development", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["development"], "contexts": ["process of change in inhabitants", "growth and evolution of organisms"]}, {"entity_id": "development", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "development", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["development"], "contexts": ["process of maturing or evolving", "abstract concept in spiritualist philosophy"]}, {"entity_id": "development", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "development", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["development"], "contexts": ["process of growth and change", "phenomena in plants and animals"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "development", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "development", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "development", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "development", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "development", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "development", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1353, "stable_key": "clu_fbbbc9fc3f8409e1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Development", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "Action and reaction", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "action_and_reaction", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "action and reaction", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["action and reaction"], "contexts": ["interplay between organisms and environment"]}, {"entity_id": "action_and_reaction", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Action and reaction", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Action and reaction", "action and reaction"], "contexts": ["Newton's third law of motion", "law of physics"]}, {"entity_id": "action_and_re-action", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "action and re-action", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["action and re-action"], "contexts": ["Newton's third law principle", "Newton's third law of motion"]}, {"entity_id": "action_and_reaction", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "action and reaction", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["action and reaction"], "contexts": ["equality of action and reaction", "Newton's third law of motion"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "action and reaction", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Action and reaction", "similarity": 0.996}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "action and reaction", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "action and re-action", "similarity": 0.918}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "action and reaction", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "action and reaction", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Action and reaction", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "action and re-action", "similarity": 0.917}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Action and reaction", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "action and reaction", "similarity": 0.996}], "id": 1354, "stable_key": "clu_aa4e42b0f9e4f762", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Action and reaction", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to Newton's third law of motion, though also used more broadly.", "wikidata_id": "Q3235565", "wikidata_description": "law of classical physics according to which if an object exerts a force upon another object, then that object exerts an equal and opposite force on the first object", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newton%27s%20third%20law"}}, {"canonical_name": "character", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "character", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "character", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["character"], "contexts": ["heritable trait or feature"]}, {"entity_id": "character", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "character", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["character"], "contexts": ["settles like plaster by age thirty", "fashioned will"]}, {"entity_id": "character", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "character", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["character"], "contexts": ["distinctive qualities of a person", "distinctive qualities of a person or group"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "character", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "character", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "character", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "character", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "character", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "character", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1355, "stable_key": "clu_01804cf88d6b3139", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Character", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "In biological texts, refers to heritable traits; in psychological contexts, to personality."}}, {"canonical_name": "Belgique", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "belgium", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Belgium", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Belgium"], "contexts": ["European country"]}, {"entity_id": "belgique", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Belgique", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 5, "variants": ["Belgique"], "contexts": ["country in Europe", "location where coal covers graunadc"]}, {"entity_id": "belgium", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Belgium", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Belgium"], "contexts": ["country in Western Europe", "location of geological observations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Belgium", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Belgique", "similarity": 0.963}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Belgium", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Belgium", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Belgique", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Belgium", "similarity": 0.963}], "id": 1356, "stable_key": "clu_ee68ffad6edf9477", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Belgium", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Belgium", "wikidata_id": "Q31", "wikidata_description": "country in western Europe", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgium", "wikipedia_extract": "Belgium, officially the Kingdom of Belgium, is a country in Northwestern Europe. Situated in a coastal lowland region known as the Low Countries, it is bordered by the Netherlands to the north, Germany to the east, Luxembourg to the southeast, France to the south, and the North Sea to the west. Belgium covers an area of 30,689 km2 (11,849 sq mi) and has a population of more than 11.8 million; its population density of 383/km2 (990/sq mi) ranks 22nd in the world and sixth in Europe. The capital and largest metropolitan region is Brussels; other major cities are Antwerp, Ghent, Charleroi, Li\u00e8ge, Bruges, Namur, and Leuven. Belgium is a parliamentary constitutional monarchy with a complex federal system structured on regional and linguistic grounds. The country is divided into three highly autonomous regions: the Flemish Region (Flanders) in the north, the Walloon Region (Wallonia) in the south, and the Brussels-Capital Region in the middle."}}, {"canonical_name": "vomito", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "vomito", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "vomito", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 7, "variants": ["vomito"], "contexts": ["symptom of vomiting", "expulsion of stomach contents"]}, {"entity_id": "vomito", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "vomito", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["vomito"], "contexts": ["symptom of illness"]}, {"entity_id": "vomiting", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "vomiting", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["vomiting"], "contexts": ["expulsion of stomach contents"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "vomito", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "vomito", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "vomito", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "vomiting", "similarity": 0.975}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "vomito", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "vomiting", "similarity": 0.975}], "id": 1357, "stable_key": "clu_94625bf3401b28aa-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Vomiting", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "The act of expelling stomach contents; a symptom or illness.", "note": "Can refer to the symptom or a specific illness characterized by vomiting.", "wikipedia_extract": "Vomiting (scientifically known as emesis, and otherwise known as puking, and throwing up) is the forceful expulsion of the contents of one's stomach through the mouth and sometimes the nose. The substance so produced (the erstwhile contents of the stomach, now expelled) is also known as vomit or vomitus. Vomiting can be the result of ailments like food poisoning, gastroenteritis, pregnancy, motion sickness, or hangover; or it can be an after effect of diseases such as brain tumors, elevated intracranial pressure, or overexposure to ionizing radiation. The feeling that one is about to vomit is called nausea; it often precedes, but does not always lead to vomiting. Impairment due to alcohol or anesthesia can cause inhalation of vomit. In severe cases, where dehydration develops, intravenous fluid may be required."}}, {"canonical_name": "sequedad", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "sequedad", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "sequedad", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 6, "variants": ["sequedad"], "contexts": ["quality of places where achiotl grows", "degree of dryness"]}, {"entity_id": "sequedad", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "sequedad", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["sequedad"], "contexts": ["quality of dryness", "dryness"]}, {"entity_id": "sequedad", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "sequedad", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["sequedad"], "contexts": ["quality of dryness"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "sequedad", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "sequedad", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "sequedad", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "sequedad", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "sequedad", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "sequedad", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1358, "stable_key": "clu_393c78794e6a7ea5-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "dryness", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "In humoral theory, 'dryness' was considered a fundamental quality of the four elements and humors, impacting physiology and the environment.", "wikidata_id": "Q5309747", "wikidata_description": "medical condition in which there is a decrease in normal lubrication of the skin or mucous membranes", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dryness%20%28medical%29", "wikipedia_extract": "Dryness is a medical condition in which there is local or more generalized decrease in normal lubrication of the skin or mucous membranes. Examples of local dryness include dry mouth, dry eyes, dry skin (xeroderma), diabetic foot and vaginal dryness. These often have specific causes and treatments. It is possible to have dry eyes without any other signs or symptoms, but this usually causes a syndrome of eye symptoms called keratoconjunctivitis sicca. More generalized dryness can be caused by e.g. dehydration (that is, more general loss of body fluids), anticholinergic drugs and Sj\u00f6gren syndrome."}}, {"canonical_name": "apetito", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "apetito", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "apetito", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["apetito"], "contexts": ["appetite", "stimulated by the plant's effects"]}, {"entity_id": "apetito", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "apetito", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["apetito", "apetito natural"], "contexts": ["provoked by Sulfur", "appetite"]}, {"entity_id": "apetite", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "apetite", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["apetite"], "contexts": ["desire for food"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "apetito", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "apetito", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "apetito", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "apetite", "similarity": 0.997}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "apetito", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "apetite", "similarity": 0.997}], "id": 1359, "stable_key": "clu_5161e69bb6f8e146-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Appetite", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q254327", "wikidata_description": "medical symptom"}}, {"canonical_name": "gallina", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "gallina", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "gallina", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 5, "variants": ["gallina"], "contexts": ["feather used for comparison of trunk thickness", "bird whose feathers are used medicinally"]}, {"entity_id": "galline", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "galline", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["galline"], "contexts": ["bird group previously thought to be dodo"]}, {"entity_id": "galli", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "galli", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 3, "variants": ["galli", "gallo"], "contexts": ["roosters", "birds used for food"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "gallina", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "galline", "similarity": 0.916}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "gallina", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "galli", "similarity": 0.936}], "id": 1360, "stable_key": "clu_cdd57097b4659cc6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Chicken", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "The Spanish 'gallina' and Italian 'gallina'/'galli' all point to chickens or domestic fowl. The context of feathers and comparison to dodos further supports this identification of birds, specifically chickens.", "wikidata_id": "Q780", "wikidata_description": "domesticated bird kept by humans primarily as a food source", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chicken"}}, {"canonical_name": "Scorpio", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "escorpi\u00f3n", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "escorpi\u00f3n", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["escorpi\u00f3n"], "contexts": ["animal with flowers resembling its tail", "name given by Spaniards to a lizard"]}, {"entity_id": "escorpiao", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Escorpiao", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Escorpiao"], "contexts": ["venomous scorpion"]}, {"entity_id": "scorpio", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Scorpio", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 6, "variants": ["Scorpio"], "contexts": ["constellation with starless patches", "constellation in zodiac"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "escorpi\u00f3n", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Escorpiao", "similarity": 0.934}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "escorpi\u00f3n", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Scorpio", "similarity": 0.917}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Escorpiao", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Scorpio", "similarity": 0.923}], "id": 1361, "stable_key": "clu_c2c1b272b2d168ee-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Scorpions", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "family": "Scorpiones", "description": "Arachnids known for their venomous sting, used in medicine and folklore.", "wikipedia_extract": "Scorpions (order Scorpiones) are predatory arachnids with eight legs, a pair of grasping pincers and a narrow, segmented tail, often carried in a characteristic forward curve over the back and always ending with a stinger. The evolutionary history of scorpions goes back 435 million years. They mainly live in deserts but have adapted to a wide range of environmental conditions, and can be found on all continents except Antarctica. There are over 2,500 described species, with 22 extant (living) families recognized to date. Their taxonomy is being revised to account for 21st-century genomic studies. Scorpions primarily prey on insects and other invertebrates, but some species hunt vertebrates."}}, {"canonical_name": "fluides \u00e9lastiques", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "fluides_\u00e9lastiques", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "fluides \u00e9lastiques", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 5, "variants": ["fluides \u00e9lastiques", "fluide gazeux", "fluides a\u00e9rilbrmes"], "contexts": ["gases causing geological phenomena", "elastic fluids"]}, {"entity_id": "elastic_fluids", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "elastic fluids", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["elastic fluids"], "contexts": ["cause of earth's trembling"]}, {"entity_id": "elastic_fluids", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "elastic fluids", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["elastic fluids"], "contexts": ["gases and vapors", "gases that expand"]}, {"entity_id": "elastic_bodies", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "elastic bodies", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["elastic bodies"], "contexts": ["materials that return to original shape after deformation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "fluides \u00e9lastiques", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "elastic fluids", "similarity": 0.955}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "fluides \u00e9lastiques", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "elastic fluids", "similarity": 0.955}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "fluides \u00e9lastiques", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "elastic bodies", "similarity": 0.884}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "elastic fluids", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "elastic fluids", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "elastic fluids", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "elastic bodies", "similarity": 0.942}], "id": 1362, "stable_key": "clu_b6df8406e5a3e11e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Elastic fluids", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to gases and vapors with elastic properties, often in the context of early theories of matter or geological forces.", "wikidata_id": "Q54878146", "wikidata_description": "scientific article"}}, {"canonical_name": "Mysore", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "mysore", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Mysore", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 4, "variants": ["Mysore", "Myso\u00efle"], "contexts": ["location with barometric heights", "location of Kater's observations"]}, {"entity_id": "mysore", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Mysore", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mysore"], "contexts": ["location with granite masses"]}, {"entity_id": "mysore", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Mysore", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 4, "variants": ["Mysore"], "contexts": ["region where cardamom is found", "region in India"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Mysore", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Mysore", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Mysore", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Mysore", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Mysore", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Mysore", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1363, "stable_key": "clu_72a517cf715c335f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mysore", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A historical region and city in Karnataka, India.", "wikipedia_extract": "Mysore (  my-SOR), officially Mysuru (Kannada: [\u02c8ma\u026a\u02c8su\u02d0\u027eu] ), is a city in the southern Indian state of Karnataka. It is the headquarters of Mysore district and Mysore division. As the traditional seat of the Wadiyar dynasty, the city functioned as the capital of the Kingdom of Mysore for almost six centuries (1399 to 1947). Known for its heritage structures, palaces (such as the famous Mysore Palace), and its culture, Mysore has been called the \"City of Palaces\", the \"Heritage City\", and the \"Cultural capital of Karnataka\". It is the second-most populous city in the state and one of the cleanest cities in India according to the Swachh Survekshan. Mysore is situated at the foothills of the Chamundi Hills."}}, {"canonical_name": "Caucase", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "caucase", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Caucase", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 4, "variants": ["Caucase"], "contexts": ["mountain range", "region of observations"]}, {"entity_id": "caucasus", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Caucasus", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Caucasus"], "contexts": ["geographic location", "mountain range and region"]}, {"entity_id": "c\u00e1ucaso", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "C\u00e1ucaso", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 3, "variants": ["C\u00e1ucaso"], "contexts": ["region included in 'Cora\u00e7one'", "mountain where a substance was seen"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Caucase", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Caucasus", "similarity": 0.958}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Caucase", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "C\u00e1ucaso", "similarity": 0.919}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Caucasus", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "C\u00e1ucaso", "similarity": 0.936}], "id": 1364, "stable_key": "clu_e3554f060de862d1-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Caucasus", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Mountain range and region of observations.", "wikipedia_extract": "The Caucasus ( KAW-k\u0259-s\u0259s) or Caucasia ( kaw-KAY-zh\u0259) is a region spanning Eastern Europe and Western Asia. It is situated between the Black Sea and the Caspian Sea, comprising parts of Southern Russia, Georgia, Armenia, and Azerbaijan. The Caucasus Mountains, including the Greater Caucasus range, have conventionally been considered as a natural barrier between Europe and Asia, bisecting the Eurasian landmass. Mount Elbrus, Europe's highest mountain, is situated in the Western Caucasus area of Russia. On the southern side, the Lesser Caucasus includes the Javakheti Plateau and the Armenian highlands. The Caucasus is divided into the North Caucasus and South Caucasus, although the Western Caucasus also exists as a distinct geographic space within the North Caucasus."}}, {"canonical_name": "Volta", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "volta", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Volta", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Volta"], "contexts": ["inventor of the electrometer", "inventor of eudiometer"]}, {"entity_id": "volta", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Volta", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Volta"], "contexts": ["inventor of the electrometer", "proposed method for electrometer"]}, {"entity_id": "volta", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Volta", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Volta"], "contexts": ["Professor at Pavia", "called into existence the fiery stream"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Volta", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Volta", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Volta", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Volta", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Volta", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Volta", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1365, "stable_key": "clu_eaad1b0044f20a1b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Alessandro Volta", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1745, "death_year": 1827, "description": "Italian physicist, inventor of the voltaic pile and electric battery.", "wikidata_id": "Q680", "wikidata_description": "Italian physicist, chemist, and pioneer of electricity and power (1745-1827)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alessandro%20Volta", "wikipedia_extract": "Alessandro Giuseppe Antonio Anastasio Volta (UK: , US: ; Italian: [ales\u02c8sandro d\u0292u\u02c8z\u025bppe an\u02c8t\u0254njo anas\u02c8ta\u02d0zjo \u02c8v\u0254lta]; 18 February 1745 \u2013 5 March 1827) was an Italian chemist and physicist who was a pioneer of electricity and power, and is credited as the inventor of the electric battery and the discoverer of methane. He invented the voltaic pile in 1799, and reported the results of his experiments in a two-part letter to the president of the Royal Society, which was published in 1800. With this invention, Volta proved that electricity could be generated chemically and debunked the prevalent theory that electricity was generated solely by living beings. Volta's invention sparked a great amount of scientific excitement and led others to conduct similar experiments, which eventually led to the development of the field of electrochemistry. Volta drew admiration from Napoleon Bonaparte for his invention, and was invited to the Institute of France to demonstrate his invention to the members of the institute. Throughout his life, Volta enjoyed a certain amount of closeness with the emperor who conferred upon him numerous honours."}}, {"canonical_name": "Bordeaux", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "bordeaux", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Bordeaux", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Bordeaux"], "contexts": ["city in France", "city between 30\u00b0 and 45\u00b0 latitude"]}, {"entity_id": "bordeaux", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Bordeaux", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bordeaux"], "contexts": ["city where Dr. Azam practiced"]}, {"entity_id": "bordeaux", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Bordeaux", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 6, "variants": ["Bordeaux"], "contexts": ["city in France", "location of Biot's pendulum observations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Bordeaux", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Bordeaux", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Bordeaux", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Bordeaux", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Bordeaux", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Bordeaux", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1366, "stable_key": "clu_dda34420e56bd721", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Bordeaux ( bor-DOH; French: [b\u0254rdo] ; Gascon Occitan: Bord\u00e8u [bu\u027e\u02c8\u00f0\u025bw]; Basque: Bordele) is a city on the river Garonne in the Gironde department, southwestern France. A port city, it is the capital of the Nouvelle-Aquitaine region, as well as the prefecture of the Gironde department. Its inhabitants are called \"Bordelais\" (masculine) or \"Bordelaises\" (feminine). The term \"Bordelais\" may also refer to the city and its surrounding region. The city of Bordeaux proper had a population of 259,809 in 2020 within its small municipal territory of 49 km2 (19 sq mi), but together with its suburbs and exurbs the Bordeaux metropolitan area had a population of 1,376,375 that same year (Jan. 2020 census), the sixth-most populated in France after Paris, Lyon, Marseille, Lille, and Toulouse. Bordeaux and 27 suburban municipalities form the Bordeaux Metropolis, an indirectly elected metropolitan authority now in charge of wider metropolitan issues."}}, {"canonical_name": "Bokhara", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "bokhara", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Bokhara", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Bokhara"], "contexts": ["city with a man-made mountain", "city in Asia"]}, {"entity_id": "bokhara", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Bokhara", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Bokhara"], "contexts": ["city with clear atmosphere for astronomy", "location with pure atmosphere"]}, {"entity_id": "bokh\u00e1ra", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Bokh\u00e1ra", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Bokh\u00e1ra", "Bokhara"], "contexts": ["birthplace of Avicenna", "city in Uzbeque"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Bokhara", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Bokhara", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Bokhara", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Bokh\u00e1ra", "similarity": 0.93}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Bokhara", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Bokh\u00e1ra", "similarity": 0.93}], "id": 1367, "stable_key": "clu_5a82aaf1160bad91", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bukhara", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Historic city in Uzbekistan, a Silk Road hub.", "wikipedia_extract": "Bukhara ( buu-KHAR-\u0259) is the seventh-largest city in Uzbekistan by population, with 280,187 residents as of 1 January 2020. It is the capital of Bukhara Region. The Bukhara region has been inhabited for at least five millennia, and the city has existed for half that time. Located on the Silk Road, the city has long served as a center of trade, scholarship, culture, and religion. Bukhara served as the capital of the Uzbek states such as the Khanate of Bukhara, the Emirate of Bukhara and later the Bukhara People\u2019s Soviet Republic. It was the birthplace of the scholar Imam Bukhari.", "country": "Uzbekistan", "wikidata_id": "Q5764", "wikidata_description": "city in Bukhara Province of Uzbekistan"}}, {"canonical_name": "Thermal springs", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "sources_thermales", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "sources thermales", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["sources thermales"], "contexts": ["geological feature"]}, {"entity_id": "thermal_springs", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Thermal springs", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 5, "variants": ["thermal springs", "Thermal springs"], "contexts": ["hot water springs", "springs with uniform temperature"]}, {"entity_id": "thermas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "thermas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["thermas", "thermae"], "contexts": ["location for baths"]}, {"entity_id": "balneis_thermalibus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "balneis thermalibus", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["balneis thermalibus"], "contexts": ["thermal baths"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "sources thermales", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Thermal springs", "similarity": 0.945}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "sources thermales", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "thermas", "similarity": 0.935}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Thermal springs", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "thermas", "similarity": 0.953}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Thermal springs", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "balneis thermalibus", "similarity": 0.913}], "id": 1368, "stable_key": "clu_34ba0afd5686a786", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "thermal springs", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "wikidata_id": "Q97726587", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article", "wikipedia_extract": "A hot spring, thermal spring, hydrothermal spring, or geothermal spring is a spring produced by the emergence of geothermally heated groundwater onto the surface of the Earth. The groundwater is heated either by shallow bodies of magma (molten rock) or by circulation through faults to hot rock deep in the Earth's crust. Hot spring water often contains large amounts of dissolved minerals. The chemistry of hot springs ranges from acid sulfate springs with a pH as low as 0.8, to alkaline chloride springs saturated with silica, to bicarbonate springs saturated with carbon dioxide and carbonate minerals. Some springs also contain abundant dissolved iron. The minerals brought to the surface in hot springs often feed communities of extremophiles, microorganisms adapted to extreme conditions, and it is possible that life on Earth had its origin in hot springs."}}, {"canonical_name": "Niebuhr", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "niebuhr", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Niebuhr", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Niebuhr"], "contexts": ["observer of continental atmosphere temperatures"]}, {"entity_id": "niebuhr", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Niebuhr", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Niebuhr"], "contexts": ["hypothesized Scythians were Mongolians"]}, {"entity_id": "niebuhr", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Niebuhr", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 7, "variants": ["Niebuhr"], "contexts": ["visited Djidda", "traveler who noted manna production"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Niebuhr", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Niebuhr", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Niebuhr", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Niebuhr", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Niebuhr", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Niebuhr", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1369, "stable_key": "clu_561980b0d945e981", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Carsten Niebuhr", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1733, "death_year": 1815, "description": "Danish geographer and explorer of Arabia", "note": "The context of visiting Djidda and observations aligns with Carsten Niebuhr's travels in Arabia.", "wikidata_id": "Q57420", "wikidata_description": "German mathematician, botanist, cartographer and explorer in Danish service (1733\u20131815)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carsten%20Niebuhr", "wikipedia_extract": "Carsten Niebuhr, or Karsten Niebuhr (17 March 1733 L\u00fcdingworth \u2013 26 April 1815 Meldorf, Dithmarschen), was a German mathematician, cartographer, and explorer in the service of Denmark-Norway. He is renowned for his participation in the Danish Arabia expedition (1761-1767). He was the father of the Danish-German statesman and historian Barthold Georg Niebuhr, who published an account of his father's life in 1817. Niebuhr was born in L\u00fcdingworth (now a part of Cuxhaven, Lower Saxony) in what was then Bremen-Verden. His father Barthold Niebuhr (1704-1749) was a successful farmer and owned his own property. Carsten and his sister were educated at home by a local school teacher, then he attended the Latin School in Otterndorf, near Cuxhaven."}}, {"canonical_name": "Reich", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "mm__reich", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "MM. Reich", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["MM. Reich"], "contexts": ["scholar who collaborated on a mine observation"]}, {"entity_id": "reich", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Reich", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 5, "variants": ["Reich"], "contexts": ["made observations on elevation", "performed torsion balance experiments"]}, {"entity_id": "reich", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Reich", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Reich"], "contexts": ["observed mine temperatures", "Professor who found mean temperature increase"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "MM. Reich", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Reich", "similarity": 0.875}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "MM. Reich", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Reich", "similarity": 0.875}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Reich", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Reich", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1370, "stable_key": "clu_d014d04207a6ee87", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Johann Christian Reich", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1741, "death_year": 1810, "description": "German metallurgist and mine inspector", "note": "The collaboration on mine observations and mine temperatures points to J.C. Reich, a notable figure in mining and metallurgy.", "wikidata_id": "Q21294804", "wikidata_description": "German medalist (1740\u20131814)"}}, {"canonical_name": "meaning", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "meaning", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "meaning", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["meaning"], "contexts": ["significance or interpretation", "significance or import of a thought"]}, {"entity_id": "meanings", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "meanings", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["meanings"], "contexts": ["significance or import of words and thoughts"]}, {"entity_id": "meaning", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "meaning", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["meaning"], "contexts": ["significance of a word"]}, {"entity_id": "meaning", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "meaning", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["meaning"], "contexts": ["fixed by processes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "meaning", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "meaning", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "meaning", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "meaning", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "meanings", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "meaning", "similarity": 0.922}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "meanings", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "meaning", "similarity": 0.922}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "meaning", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "meaning", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1371, "stable_key": "clu_e3a53e6a2d627225", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Meaning", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This refers to the general philosophical and linguistic concept of meaning, as discussed in works on psychology and general principles.", "wikidata_id": "Q16877783", "wikidata_description": "nebulously defined concept used in psychology as well as in other fields such as philosophy, linguistics, semiotics and sociology, in which an item, entity, or action represents, signals, or conveys", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meaning%20%28psychology%29", "wikipedia_extract": "Meaning is an epistemological concept used in multiple disciplines, such as psychology, philosophy, linguistics, semiotics, and sociology, with its definition depending upon the field of study by which it is being used. These multidisciplinary uses of the term are not independent and can more or less overlap; each construction of the term meaning can correspond with related constructions in other fields. The logical positivists, for example, associated meaning with scientific verification. n of idea. Like an idea, a meaning is said to be expressed or communicated by an utterance. A meaning explains the occurrence of a particular word in the sense that if there had been a different meaning to be expressed, a different word would probably have appeared."}}, {"canonical_name": "Bradley", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "bradley", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Bradley", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Bradley"], "contexts": ["philosopher quoted on attention", "calls abstract quality a 'floating adjective'"]}, {"entity_id": "bradley", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Bradley", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bradley"], "contexts": ["astronomer whose observations are mentioned"]}, {"entity_id": "bradley", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Bradley", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Bradley"], "contexts": ["astronomer who observed stars", "discovered nutation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Bradley", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Bradley", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Bradley", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Bradley", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Bradley", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Bradley", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1372, "stable_key": "clu_51122a60a2984f04", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "James Bradley", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1693, "death_year": 1762, "description": "English astronomer, discoverer of aberration of starlight", "note": "The mention of astronomical observations and stars strongly suggests James Bradley, a highly influential astronomer.", "wikidata_id": "Q111177518", "wikidata_description": "American politician"}}, {"canonical_name": "pitch", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "pitch", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "pitch", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["pitch"], "contexts": ["quality of tone that rises with rapidity", "highness or lowness of a tone"]}, {"entity_id": "pitch", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "pitch", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["pitch"], "contexts": ["perceived highness or lowness of a sound", "frequency of sound vibration"]}, {"entity_id": "pitch", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "pitch", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["pitch"], "contexts": ["highness or lowness of a sound"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "pitch", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "pitch", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "pitch", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "pitch", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "pitch", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "pitch", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1373, "stable_key": "clu_e92c3002daa07943", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "pitch (sound)", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The descriptions 'perceived highness or lowness of a sound' and 'quality of tone that rises with rapidity' unequivocally refer to the concept of pitch in acoustics and psychoacoustics.", "wikipedia_extract": "Pitch is the quality that makes it possible to judge sounds as \"higher\" and \"lower\" in the sense associated with musical melodies. Pitch is a perceptual property that allows sounds to be ordered on a frequency-related scale. Pitch is a major auditory attribute of musical tones, along with duration, loudness, and timbre. Pitch may be quantified as a frequency, but pitch is not a purely objective physical property; it is a subjective psychoacoustical attribute of sound. Historically, the study of pitch and pitch perception has been a central problem in psychoacoustics, and has been instrumental in forming and testing theories of sound representation, processing, and perception in the auditory system. Pitch is an auditory sensation in which a listener assigns musical tones to relative positions on a musical scale based primarily on their perception of the frequency of vibration (audio frequency)."}}, {"canonical_name": "Tyndall", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "tyndall", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Tyndall", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Tyndall"], "contexts": ["scientist discussing consciousness and brain activity", "scientist quoted on matter and life"]}, {"entity_id": "tyndall", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Tyndall", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 5, "variants": ["Tyndall"], "contexts": ["Professor who proved diamagnetic polarity", "scientist who conducted diamagnetic experiments"]}, {"entity_id": "tyndall", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Tyndall", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Tyndall"], "contexts": ["friend who held doctrine of rhythmical motion", "Professor who regards differences in heat absorption"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Tyndall", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Tyndall", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Tyndall", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Tyndall", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Tyndall", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Tyndall", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1374, "stable_key": "clu_6b4a267674519a67", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "John Tyndall", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1820, "death_year": 1893, "description": "Physicist and mountaineer, explored heat and magnetism.", "wikidata_id": "Q75927365", "wikidata_description": "Peerage person ID=408024"}}, {"canonical_name": "external world", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "external_world", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "external world", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["external world"], "contexts": ["reality outside of the mind"]}, {"entity_id": "external_world", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "external world", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["external world", "external World"], "contexts": ["the observable universe", "the reality outside the mind"]}, {"entity_id": "external_world", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "external world", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["external world"], "contexts": ["reality independent of the mind", "the world outside of one's mind"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "external world", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "external world", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "external world", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "external world", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "external world", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "external world", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1375, "stable_key": "clu_47cc38c6f479c8ee", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "External world", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q119532338", "wikidata_description": "US patent 11195349"}}, {"canonical_name": "Optic axis", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "optical_axes", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "optical axes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["optical axes"], "contexts": ["lines of sight of the eyes"]}, {"entity_id": "optic_axis", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Optic axis", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 7, "variants": ["optic axis", "Optic axis"], "contexts": ["direction of single refraction in crystals", "direction in crystals for optical effects"]}, {"entity_id": "optic_axes", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "optic axes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["optic axes"], "contexts": ["lines of sight of the eyes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "optical axes", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Optic axis", "similarity": 0.904}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "optical axes", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "optic axes", "similarity": 0.996}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Optic axis", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "optic axes", "similarity": 0.914}], "id": 1376, "stable_key": "clu_35b51e3cf398cdcd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Optical axis", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to both anatomical optics and crystallography."}}, {"canonical_name": "Amsterdam", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "amsterdam", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Amsterdam", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Amsterdam"], "contexts": ["location of publication"]}, {"entity_id": "amsterdam", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Amsterdam", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Amsterdam"], "contexts": ["city Varenius sought refuge in"]}, {"entity_id": "amsterdam", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Amsterdam", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 7, "variants": ["Amsterdam", "Amstelo-dami", "Amstelodami", "Amstelsedami"], "contexts": ["publication location", "publication location of Niebuhr's book"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Amsterdam", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Amsterdam", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Amsterdam", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Amsterdam", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Amsterdam", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Amsterdam", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1377, "stable_key": "clu_bc2da338395388ed", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Amsterdam", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Major city in the Netherlands, a hub for trade and publication.", "wikipedia_extract": "Amsterdam (Dutch: [\u02cc\u0251mst\u0259r\u02c8d\u0251m] ; lit.\u2009'Dam in the Amstel') is the capital and largest city of the Netherlands. It has a population of 933,680 in June 2024 within the city proper, 1,457,018 in the urban area and 2,480,394 in the metropolitan area. Located in the Dutch province of North Holland, Amsterdam is colloquially referred to as the \"Venice of the North\", for its large number of canals, now a UNESCO World Heritage Site. Amsterdam was founded at the mouth of the Amstel River, which was dammed to control flooding. Originally a small fishing village in the 12th century, Amsterdam became a major world port during the Dutch Golden Age of the 17th century, when the Netherlands was an economic powerhouse. Amsterdam was the leading centre for finance and trade, as well as a hub of secular art production."}}, {"canonical_name": "appetitus", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "lust", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "lust", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["lust"], "contexts": ["intense sexual desire"]}, {"entity_id": "gana_de_comer", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "gana de comer", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 2, "variants": ["gana de comer"], "contexts": ["loss of appetite", "appetite, a symptom"]}, {"entity_id": "appetitus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "appetitus", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 4, "variants": ["appetitus", "appetentia"], "contexts": ["appetite", "appetite or desire for food"]}, {"entity_id": "apetite", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "apetite", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 2, "variants": ["apetite"], "contexts": ["appetite"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "lust", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "gana de comer", "similarity": 0.902}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "lust", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "appetitus", "similarity": 0.917}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "lust", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "apetite", "similarity": 0.905}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "gana de comer", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "appetitus", "similarity": 0.956}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "gana de comer", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "apetite", "similarity": 0.956}], "id": 1378, "stable_key": "clu_6d285ea1128424d5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Appetite", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'appetitus' in early modern medical texts encompasses both normal hunger and abnormal appetites, including intense cravings and loss of appetite. 'Gana de comer' specifically means appetite for food.", "wikidata_id": "Q254327", "wikidata_description": "medical symptom", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anorexia%20%28symptom%29", "wikipedia_extract": "Anorexia is a medical term for a loss of appetite. While the term outside of the scientific literature is often used interchangeably with anorexia nervosa, many possible causes exist for a loss of appetite, some of which may be harmless, while others indicate a serious clinical condition or pose a significant risk. Anorexia in this usage is a symptom, not a diagnosis. The symptom also occurs in non-human animals, such as cats, dogs, cattle, goats, and sheep. In these species, anorexia may be referred to as inappetence. As in humans, loss of appetite can be due to a range of diseases and conditions, as well as environmental and psychological factors."}}, {"canonical_name": "Repulsion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "repulsion", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "repulsion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["repulsion"], "contexts": ["force pushing objects apart"]}, {"entity_id": "repulsion", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Repulsion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Repulsion", "repulsion"], "contexts": ["force between electric charges", "force pushing objects apart"]}, {"entity_id": "repulsions", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "repulsions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["repulsions"], "contexts": ["cause motion of thunder clouds"]}, {"entity_id": "repulsion", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "repulsion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["repulsion"], "contexts": ["forces of attraction and repulsion being universally co-existent", "force pushing objects apart"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "repulsion", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Repulsion", "similarity": 0.847}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "repulsion", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "repulsions", "similarity": 0.951}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "repulsion", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "repulsion", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Repulsion", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "repulsion", "similarity": 0.847}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "repulsions", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "repulsion", "similarity": 0.951}], "id": 1379, "stable_key": "clu_d545a961be32be1e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Repulsion", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Encompasses physical forces pushing objects apart, including electrostatic and possibly atmospheric phenomena.", "wikidata_id": "Q113644972", "wikidata_description": "German record label"}}, {"canonical_name": "Helvigius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "hevelius", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Hevelius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hevelius"], "contexts": ["observed comet nucleus changes"]}, {"entity_id": "helvigius", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Helvigius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Helvigius", "Helvvigius"], "contexts": ["observer of a strange phenomenon", "referent of Boneto de Dysenteria"]}, {"entity_id": "hevelius", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Hevelius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Hevelius"], "contexts": ["described similar appearance in 1682 comet", "observed comet head contraction phenomenon"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Hevelius", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Helvigius", "similarity": 0.851}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Hevelius", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Hevelius", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Helvigius", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Hevelius", "similarity": 0.851}], "id": 1380, "stable_key": "clu_3079067083b41d33", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Johannes Hevelius", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1611, "death_year": 1687, "description": "Polish astronomer and mayor of Danzig, renowned for comet observations.", "note": "The name 'Helvigius' appears to be an occasional variant or misspelling of 'Hevelius' in the texts.", "wikidata_id": "Q57963", "wikidata_description": "astronomer setting up the cartography of the Moon", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johannes%20Hevelius", "wikipedia_extract": "Johannes Hevelius (in German also known as Hevel; Polish: Jan Heweliusz; (1611-01-28)28 January 1611 \u2013 28 January 1687) was a  councillor and chairman of the city council of the Old Town, Gda\u0144sk (in the Polish\u2013Lithuanian Commonwealth). As an  astronomer, he gained a reputation as \"the founder of lunar topography\", and described ten new constellations, seven of which are still used by astronomers. According to the Polish Academy of Sciences (1975) the origin of the name goes back to the surname Hawke, a historical alternative spelling for the English word hawk, which changed into Hawelke or Hawelecke. In Poland he is known as Jan Heweliusz. Other versions of the name include Hewel, Hevel, Hevelke or Hoefel, H\u00f6welcke, H\u00f6felcke. According to Feliks Bentkowski (1814), during his early years he also signed as Hoefelius."}}, {"canonical_name": "crystallization", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "crystallization", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "crystallization", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["crystallization"], "contexts": ["formation of crystals"]}, {"entity_id": "crystallization", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "crystallization", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["Crystallization", "crystallization"], "contexts": ["process of forming crystals", "influence on transmissive power of bodies"]}, {"entity_id": "crystallization", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "crystallization", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["crystallization"], "contexts": ["process of forming crystals", "formation of crystals"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "crystallization", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "crystallization", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "crystallization", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "crystallization", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "crystallization", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "crystallization", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1381, "stable_key": "clu_13dbd9067519d293", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "crystallization", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q284256", "wikidata_description": "process by which a solid with a highly organised atomic or molecular structure forms", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crystallization", "wikipedia_extract": "Crystallization is a process that leads to solids with a uniform pattern of atoms or molecules, i.e. a crystal. The uniform nature of a crystalline solid can be contrasted with amorphous solids in which atoms or molecules lack regular organization. Crystallization can occur by various routes including precipitation from solution, freezing of a liquid, or deposition from a gas. Attributes of the resulting crystal can depend largely on factors such as temperature, air pressure, cooling rate, or solute concentration. Crystallization occurs in two main phases. The first is nucleation, the appearance of a crystalline phase from either a supercooled liquid or a supersaturated solvent."}}, {"canonical_name": "Dezembro", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "dezembro", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Dezembro", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Dezembro"], "contexts": ["month of a letter"]}, {"entity_id": "dezembro", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Dezembro", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 7, "variants": ["Dezembro"], "contexts": ["month of observation", "month of December"]}, {"entity_id": "december", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "December", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["December"], "contexts": ["month of the year"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Dezembro", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Dezembro", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Dezembro", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "December", "similarity": 0.976}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Dezembro", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somervil