"PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Herbert"], "contexts": ["cited in Asiat. Res. for measurement", "provided data on mountain elevations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "W. Herbert", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Herbert", "similarity": 0.874}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "W. Herbert", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Herbert", "similarity": 0.874}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Herbert", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Herbert", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Herbert", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Herbert", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Herbert", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Herbert", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1427, "stable_key": "clu_46761665ab038f55", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "William Herbert", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1778, "death_year": 1847, "description": "Botanist and author on plant hybridization and sexual systems.", "note": "Referenced for his work on plant hybridization and competition, aligning with botanical studies of the era."}}, {"canonical_name": "insecto", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "rodent", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Rodent", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Rodent", "rodent"], "contexts": ["wider range than other rodents", "animal order with specific habits"]}, {"entity_id": "rodent", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "rodent", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["rodent"], "contexts": ["mammal with complex nervous system", "animal with even fewer lower brain centers"]}, {"entity_id": "insecto", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "insecto", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["insecto"], "contexts": ["produces lacca on tree branches", "true generator of lacca"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Rodent", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "rodent", "similarity": 0.641}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Rodent", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "insecto", "similarity": 0.905}], "id": 1428, "stable_key": "clu_7441f014f524a8eb", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Rodents (from Latin r\u014dd\u0113ns, 'gnawing') are a group of mammals characterized by a single pair of continuously growing incisors in each of the upper and lower jaws. About 40% of all mammal species belong to the order Rodentia ( roh-DEN-sh\u0259 or roh-DEN-ch\u0259). They are native to all major land masses except for Antarctica, and several oceanic islands, though they have subsequently been introduced to most of these land masses by human activity. Rodents are extremely diverse in their ecology and lifestyles and can be found in almost every terrestrial habitat, including human-made environments. Species can be arboreal, fossorial (burrowing), saltatorial/ricochetal (leaping on their hind legs), or semiaquatic. However, all rodents share several morphological features, including having only a single upper and lower pair of ever-growing incisors."}}, {"canonical_name": "Chile", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "chile", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Chile", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Chile"], "contexts": ["country of origin for species", "location with glacial moraine"]}, {"entity_id": "chile", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Chile", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Chile"], "contexts": ["location where events occurred", "region inhabited by indigenous people"]}, {"entity_id": "chile", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Chile", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Chile"], "contexts": ["South American country", "country with land elevation due to earthquake"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Chile", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Chile", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Chile", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Chile", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Chile", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Chile", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1429, "stable_key": "clu_5a20c0ef22ae9577-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Chile", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A country in South America, known for its geography and inhabitants.", "wikipedia_extract": "Chile, officially the Republic of Chile, is a country in western South America. It is the southernmost country in the world and the closest to Antarctica, extending along a narrow strip of land between the Andes Mountains and the Pacific Ocean. According to the 2024 census, Chile had an enumerated population of 18.5 million. The country covers a territorial area of 756,102 square kilometers (291,933 sq mi), sharing borders with Peru to the north, Bolivia to the northeast, Argentina to the east, and the Drake Passage to the south. It also administers several Pacific islands, including Juan Fern\u00e1ndez, Isla Salas y G\u00f3mez, Desventuradas, and Easter Island, and claims about 1,250,000 square kilometers (480,000 sq mi) of Antarctica as the Chilean Antarctic Territory. The capital and largest city is Santiago, and the national language is Spanish."}}, {"canonical_name": "Pacific Ocean", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "pacific_ocean", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Pacific Ocean", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Pacific Ocean"], "contexts": ["bed of the Pacific Ocean", "ocean with discussed faunas"]}, {"entity_id": "pacific_ocean", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Pacific Ocean", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Pacific Ocean"], "contexts": ["eastern portion of the Pacific Ocean"]}, {"entity_id": "pacific_ocean", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Pacific Ocean", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 5, "variants": ["Pacific Ocean"], "contexts": ["large ocean on Earth", "ocean receiving tidal waves"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Pacific Ocean", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Pacific Ocean", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Pacific Ocean", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Pacific Ocean", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Pacific Ocean", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Pacific Ocean", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1430, "stable_key": "clu_a911490fe9a26789-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pacific Ocean", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "N/A", "note": "'Mer du Sud' and 'South Sea' are historical names for the Pacific Ocean.", "wikidata_id": "Q844857", "wikidata_description": "geographic region consisting of the islands of the Pacific Ocean", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pacific%20Islands"}}, {"canonical_name": "optic nerves", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "optic_nerve", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "optic nerve", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["optic nerve"], "contexts": ["nerve for vision, pigment-coated"]}, {"entity_id": "optic_nerves", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "optic nerves", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["optic nerves"], "contexts": ["nerves transmitting visual information", "nerves for vision"]}, {"entity_id": "optic_nerve-fibres", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "optic nerve-fibres", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["optic nerve-fibres"], "contexts": ["fibers in the eye for light perception"]}, {"entity_id": "optic_nerves", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "optic nerves", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["optic nerves"], "contexts": ["nerves transmitting visual information", "receives light vibrations to produce sensation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "optic nerve", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "optic nerves", "similarity": 0.972}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "optic nerve", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "optic nerve-fibres", "similarity": 0.927}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "optic nerve", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "optic nerves", "similarity": 0.972}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "optic nerves", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "optic nerves", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "optic nerve-fibres", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "optic nerves", "similarity": 0.934}], "id": 1431, "stable_key": "clu_d86dc082cf00838a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Optic Nerve", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "Q7098797", "wikidata_description": "human disease", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Optic%20nerve%20hypoplasia", "wikipedia_extract": "Optic nerve hypoplasia (ONH) is a medical condition arising from the underdevelopment (hypoplasia) of the optic nerve(s). This condition is the most common congenital optic nerve anomaly. The optic disc appears abnormally small because not all the optic nerve axons have developed properly. It is often associated with endocrinopathies (hormone deficiencies), developmental delay, and brain malformations. The optic nerve, responsible for transmitting visual signals from the retina to the brain, has approximately 1.2 million nerve fibers in the average person. In those diagnosed with ONH, however, there are noticeably fewer nerves."}}, {"canonical_name": "Mr. Adams", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "mr__smith", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Mr. Smith", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mr. Smith"], "contexts": ["described ant slave-making behavior"]}, {"entity_id": "mr__adams", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Mr. Adams", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Mr. Adams"], "contexts": ["predicted planet simultaneously", "discovered a planet"]}, {"entity_id": "mr__adams", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Mr. Adams", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 5, "variants": ["Mr. Adams"], "contexts": ["computed Neptune's perturbation on Uranus", "computed Neptune's perturbation of Uranus"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Mr. Smith", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Mr. Adams", "similarity": 0.859}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Mr. Smith", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Mr. Adams", "similarity": 0.859}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Mr. Adams", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Mr. Adams", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1432, "stable_key": "clu_bec505913f87c114", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "John Couch Adams", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1819, "death_year": 1892, "description": "Mathematician who predicted Neptune's existence", "note": "The context mentions predicting a planet and computing Neptune's perturbation, strongly suggesting John Couch Adams, though 'Mr. Adams' is generic. The mention of 'Mr. Smith' in the same cluster is likely a mistake or alternative reference in the source text and is ignored for the primary identification.", "wikidata_id": "Q20015", "wikidata_description": "English mathematician and astronomer (1819\u20131892)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John%20Couch%20Adams", "wikipedia_extract": "John Couch Adams ( KOOTCH; 5 June 1819 \u2013 21 January 1892) was a British mathematician and astronomer. He was born in Laneast, near Launceston, Cornwall, and died in Cambridge. His most famous achievement was predicting the existence and position of Neptune, using only mathematics. The calculations were made to explain discrepancies with Uranus's orbit and the laws of Kepler and Newton. At the same time, but unknown to each other, the same calculations were made by Urbain Le Verrier. Le Verrier sent his co-ordinates to Berlin Observatory astronomer Johann Gottfried Galle, who confirmed the existence of the planet on 23 September 1846, finding it within 1\u00b0 of Le Verrier's predicted location."}}, {"canonical_name": "social", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "social", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "social", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["social"], "contexts": ["living in communities"]}, {"entity_id": "social", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "social", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["SOCIAL", "social"], "contexts": ["social constituents of the 'Me'", "division of empirical life of self"]}, {"entity_id": "social", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "social", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["social"], "contexts": ["related to human society and its organization", "relating to society and its organization"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "social", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "social", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "social", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "social", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "social", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "social", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1433, "stable_key": "clu_f8967dca297c8fb6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Social", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "ocean-currents", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "marine_currents", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "marine currents", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["marine currents"], "contexts": ["local and temporary causes"]}, {"entity_id": "currents_of_the_sea", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "currents of the sea", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["currents of the sea"], "contexts": ["oceanic movements affecting dispersal"]}, {"entity_id": "currents_of_sea_water", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "currents of sea water", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["currents of sea water"], "contexts": ["movement of ocean water"]}, {"entity_id": "oceanic_currents", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Oceanic currents", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Oceanic currents"], "contexts": ["movement of water in oceans"]}, {"entity_id": "ocean-currents", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "ocean-currents", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["ocean-currents"], "contexts": ["increasing complication in arrangement", "movement of water in the ocean"]}, {"entity_id": "marine_currents", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Marine currents", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["marine currents", "Marine currents"], "contexts": ["movement of ocean water"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "marine currents", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "currents of sea water", "similarity": 0.955}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "marine currents", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Oceanic currents", "similarity": 0.953}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "marine current