itan disease, possibly syphilis"]}, {"entity_id": "napolitano", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "napolitano", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["napolitano"], "contexts": ["synonym for mal francos"]}, {"entity_id": "mal_napolitano", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "mal Napolitano", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["mal Napolitano"], "contexts": ["name for the disease"]}, {"entity_id": "morbo_napolitano", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "morbo napolitano", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 4, "variants": ["morbo napolitano"], "contexts": ["syphilis, also called sarna de Castella", "disease named after Naples"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "mal napolitano", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "mal Napolitano", "similarity": 0.895}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "mal napolitano", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "morbo napolitano", "similarity": 0.97}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "napolitano", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "morbo napolitano", "similarity": 0.938}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "mal Napolitano", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "morbo napolitano", "similarity": 0.887}], "id": 1441, "stable_key": "clu_e46c17123aad44b4-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Syphilis", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Syphilis", "note": "The term 'mala Franc^os' or 'mal Frances' was a common historical name for syphilis, meaning 'French evil'.", "wikidata_id": "Q18966640", "wikidata_description": "human disease", "description": "A sexually transmitted infection caused by the bacterium Treponema pallidum.", "wikipedia_extract": "Syphilis () is a sexually transmitted infection caused by the bacterium Treponema pallidum subspecies pallidum. The signs and symptoms depend on the stage it presents: primary, secondary, latent or tertiary. The primary stage classically presents with a single chancre (a firm, painless, non-itchy skin ulceration usually between 1 cm and 2 cm in diameter), though there may be multiple sores. In secondary syphilis, a diffuse rash occurs, which frequently involves the palms of the hands and soles of the feet. There may also be sores in the mouth or vagina. Latent syphilis has no symptoms and can last years."}}, {"canonical_name": "minerali", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "minerales", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "minerales", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["minerales"], "contexts": ["minerals discussed for medicine", "minerals"]}, {"entity_id": "minerales", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "minerales", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Minerales", "minerales"], "contexts": ["minerals mentioned by Dioscorides", "one of four things where poison is found"]}, {"entity_id": "minerali", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "minerali", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["minerali"], "contexts": ["minerals", "minerals in bath waters"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "minerales", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "minerales", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "minerales", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "minerali", "similarity": 0.981}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "minerales", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "minerali", "similarity": 0.981}], "id": 1442, "stable_key": "clu_684025f2f518eba0-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Minerals", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "This cluster clearly refers to inorganic solid substances studied in natural philosophy and medicine, aligning directly with the modern scientific definition of minerals.", "wikidata_id": "Q7946", "wikidata_description": "naturally occurring usually inorganic substance that has a (more or less) definite chemical composition and a crystal structure", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mineral", "wikipedia_extract": "In geology and mineralogy, a mineral or mineral species is, broadly speaking, a solid substance with a fairly well-defined chemical composition and a specific crystal structure that occurs naturally in pure form. The geological definition of mineral normally excludes compounds that occur only in living organisms. However, some minerals are often biogenic (such as calcite) or chemically organic compounds (such as mellite). Moreover, living organisms often synthesize inorganic minerals (such as hydroxylapatite) that also occur in rocks. The concept of mineral is distinct from rock, which is any bulk solid geologic material that is relatively homogeneous at a large enough scale. A rock may consist of one type of mineral or may be an aggregate of two or more different types of minerals, spacially segregated into distinct phases."}}, {"canonical_name": "Pino", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "pino", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "pino", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 1, "variants": ["pino"], "contexts": ["pine-like trees"]}, {"entity_id": "pino", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Pino", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Pino", "Pinos"], "contexts": ["tree from which some thought it came", "tree used for size comparison"]}, {"entity_id": "pino", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "pino", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Pino", "pino"], "contexts": ["mixed with mastice", "tree from which resin is distilled"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "pino", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Pino", "similarity": 0.901}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "pino", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "pino", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Pino", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "pino", "similarity": 0.901}], "id": 1443, "stable_key": "clu_39b28c9774d76de8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pine", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Pinus", "family": "Pinaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q1258277", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinellia%20ternata", "note": "'pine-apple' in Darwin's *Origin of Species* is an archaic term for the fruit of the pineapple plant, but the presence of 'pines' and 'pineta' referring to coniferous trees in other contexts makes 'Pine' the most encompassing modern referent.", "wikipedia_extract": "Pinellia ternata (Chinese: \u534a\u590f, Japanese: \u30ab\u30e9\u30b9\u30d3\u30b7\u30e3\u30af), crow-dipper, is a plant that is native to China, Japan, and Korea. However, it also grows as an invasive weed in parts of Europe (Austria, Germany) and in North America (California, Ontario, the northeastern United States). The leaves are trifoliate, and the flowers are of the spathe and spadix form that is typical of plants in the family Araceae. The plant spreads by rhizomes, and there are also small bulblets (also known as bulbils) at the base of each leaf. Flowers are borne in spring. This plant is toxic in raw form and must be processed."}}, {"canonical_name": "Filipinas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "islas_philippinas", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "Islas Philippinas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Islas Philippinas"], "contexts": ["location where trees grow"]}, {"entity_id": "filipinas", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Filipinas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 6, "variants": ["Filipinas"], "contexts": ["fertile district with Rio de Galafre", "jurisdiction mentioned in census data"]}, {"entity_id": "philippinas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Philippinas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Philippinas"], "contexts": ["archipelago where Magalh\u00e3es died"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Islas Philippinas", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Filipinas", "similarity": 0.897}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Islas Philippinas", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Philippinas", "similarity": 0.91}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Filipinas", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Philippinas", "similarity": 0.968}], "id": 1444, "stable_key": "clu_50aac63513850908-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Philippines", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Archipelago and fertile district, historically known as Filipinas.", "wikipedia_extract": "The Philippines, officially the Republic of the Philippines, is an archipelagic country in Southeast Asia. Located in the western Pacific Ocean, it consists of about 7,641 islands, with a total area of about 300,000 square kilometers, which are broadly categorized in three main geographical divisions from north to south: Luzon, Visayas, and Mindanao. With a population of over 112 million, it is the world's fourteenth-most-populous country. The Philippines is bounded by the South China Sea to the west, the Philippine Sea to the east, and the Celebes Sea to the south. It shares maritime borders with Taiwan to the north, Japan to the northeast, Palau to the east and southeast, Indonesia to the south, Malaysia to the southwest, Vietnam to the west, and China to the northwest. It has diverse ethnicities and a rich culture."}}, {"canonical_name": "dores de rins", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "dolores_de_ri\u00f1ones", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "dolores de ri\u00f1ones", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["dolores de ri\u00f1ones"], "contexts": ["kidney pains"]}, {"entity_id": "dolores_de_los_ri\u00f1ones", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "dolores de los ri\u00f1ones", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["dolores de los ri\u00f1ones"], "contexts": ["pain in the kidneys, relieved by the plant"]}, {"entity_id": "dolor_de_los_ri\u00f1ones", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "dolor de los ri\u00f1ones", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["dolor de los ri\u00f1ones"], "contexts": ["pain in the kidneys"]}, {"entity_id": "dolores_de_ri\u00f1ones", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "dolores de ri\u00f1ones", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["dolores de ri\u00f1ones"], "contexts": ["kidney pains"]}, {"entity_id": "dores_de_rins", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "dores de rins", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 4, "variants": ["dores de rins", "dor de rins", "dores dos rins"], "contexts": ["pain in kidneys", "kidney pains requiring aid"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "dolores de ri\u00f1ones", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "dolores de ri\u00f1ones", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "dolores de ri\u00f1ones", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "dores de rins", "similarity": 0.881}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "dolores de los ri\u00f1ones", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "dolores de ri\u00f1ones", "similarity": 0.994}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "dolores de los ri\u00f1ones", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "dores de rins", "similarity": 0.888}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "dolor de los ri\u00f1ones", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "dolores de ri\u00f1ones", "similarity": 0.987}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "dolor de los ri\u00f1ones", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "dores de rins", "similarity": 0.886}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "dolores de ri\u00f1ones", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "dores de rins", "similarity": 0.881}], "id": 1445, "stable_key": "clu_4f259bcf1bfc1bce", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "kidney pain", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "Pain or discomfort located in the region of the kidneys.", "wikipedia_extract": "Kidney failure, also known as renal failure or end-stage renal disease (ESRD), is a medical condition in which the kidneys can no longer adequately filter waste products from the blood, functioning at less than 15% of normal levels. Kidney failure is classified as either acute kidney failure, which develops rapidly and may resolve; and chronic kidney failure, which develops slowly and can often be irreversible. Symptoms may include leg swelling, feeling tired, vomiting, loss of appetite, and confusion. Complications of acute and chronic failure include uremia, hyperkalemia, and volume overload. Complications of chronic failure also include heart disease, high blood pressure, and anaemia. Causes of acute kidney failure include low blood pressure, blockage of the urinary tract, certain medications, muscle breakdown, and hemolytic uremic syndrome."}}, {"canonical_name": "enfermedades largas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "enfermedades_largas_e_yncurables", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "enfermedades largas e yncurables", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 1, "variants": ["enfermedades largas e yncurables"], "contexts": ["long and incurable illnesses"]}, {"entity_id": "enfermedades_largas", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "enfermedades largas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 4, "variants": ["enfermedades largas", "enfermedades largas y importunas"], "contexts": ["long illnesses treated with the root", "long diseases"]}, {"entity_id": "doen\u00e7as_prolongadas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "doen\u00e7as prolongadas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 2, "variants": ["doen\u00e7as prolongadas"], "contexts": ["long-lasting illnesses", "Long-lasting illnesses"]}, {"entity_id": "doen\u00e7as_cronicas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "doen\u00e7as cronicas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 1, "variants": ["doen\u00e7as cronicas"], "contexts": ["chronic illnesses"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "enfermedades largas e yncurables", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "enfermedades largas", "similarity": 0.923}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "enfermedades largas e yncurables", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "doen\u00e7as prolongadas", "similarity": 0.916}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "enfermedades largas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "doen\u00e7as prolongadas", "similarity": 0.974}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "enfermedades largas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "doen\u00e7as cronicas", "similarity": 0.91}], "id": 1446, "stable_key": "clu_b08a9d59cb8506c2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Chronic diseases", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Chronic diseases", "wikidata_id": "Q383126", "wikidata_description": "human health condition or disease that is persistent or otherwise long-lasting in its effects"}}, {"canonical_name": "enjoos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "comez\u00f3n", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "comez\u00f3n", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["comez\u00f3n"], "contexts": ["itching, treated by tzonpoton decoction"]}, {"entity_id": "comezon", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "comezon", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["comezon"], "contexts": ["symptom of itching"]}, {"entity_id": "enjoos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "enjoos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 6, "variants": ["enjoos"], "contexts": ["nausea", "symptom of sickness"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "comez\u00f3n", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "comezon", "similarity": 0.652}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "comez\u00f3n", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "enjoos", "similarity": 0.885}], "id": 1447, "stable_key": "clu_2f7b78e861a2773c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Itching", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Pruritus", "note": "The term 'enjoos' in Portuguese can refer to nausea, but in the context of the Spanish terms 'comez\u00f3n' and 'comezon', itching is the more likely consistent referent.", "wikidata_id": "Q5308818", "wikidata_description": "medical condition", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drug-induced%20pruritus"}}, {"canonical_name": "Santos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "santos", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "Santos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Santos"], "contexts": ["saints whose images are made"]}, {"entity_id": "santos", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Santos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 3, "variants": ["Santos"], "contexts": ["dictated notes", "friend of Rodriguez"]}, {"entity_id": "santos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Santos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 4, "variants": ["Santos"], "contexts": ["saints who speak of demonic diseases", "city where Maria Moreyra lived"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Santos", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Santos", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Santos", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Santos", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Santos", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Santos", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1448, "stable_key": "clu_168090440c9cad8f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "saints", "confidence": "medium", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'Santos' can refer to a person named Santos or 'saints' in Portuguese/Spanish. Given the context of 'saints who speak of demonic diseases', it's likely referring to saints in a general sense, possibly with specific examples being implied but not stated.", "wikidata_id": "Q7961292", "wikidata_description": "sainthood in Islamic religion"}}, {"canonical_name": "Plata", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "plata", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Plata", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 6, "variants": ["Plata", "Plata Vicja", "Platanar"], "contexts": ["viceroyalty with a large population", "river in South America"]}, {"entity_id": "plat\u00e6a", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Plat\u00e6a", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Plat\u00e6a"], "contexts": ["ancient Greek city"]}, {"entity_id": "platam", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Platam", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Platam"], "contexts": ["city associated with finding turbit"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Plata", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Plat\u00e6a", "similarity": 0.712}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Plata", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Platam", "similarity": 0.941}], "id": 1449, "stable_key": "clu_6e2fceaa37307c04", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "R\u00edo de la Plata", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Argentina/Uruguay", "note": "The text likely refers to the estuary, which is often called 'La Plata', and the surrounding regions.", "wikidata_id": "Q35827", "wikidata_description": "estuary formed by the confluence of the Uruguay River and the Paran\u00e1 River on the border between Argentina and Uruguay"}}, {"canonical_name": "Danemark", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "danemark", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Danemark", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Danemark", "Danemarck"], "contexts": ["country with sugar consumption", "maritime power"]}, {"entity_id": "danish", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Danish", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Danish"], "contexts": ["language and origin of 'gaard'"]}, {"entity_id": "denmark", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Denmark", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Denmark"], "contexts": ["country with opposite coasts", "European country mentioned in relation to tides"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Danemark", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Danish", "similarity": 0.928}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Danemark", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Denmark", "similarity": 0.94}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Danish", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Denmark", "similarity": 0.977}], "id": 1450, "stable_key": "clu_de6817e003ab2f31", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Denmark", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A Scandinavian country, known for maritime activity and sugar consumption.", "wikipedia_extract": "Denmark is a Nordic country in Northern Europe. It is the metropole and most populous constituent of the Kingdom of Denmark, also known as the Danish Realm, a constitutionally unitary state that includes the autonomous territories of the Faroe Islands and Greenland in the north Atlantic Ocean. Metropolitan Denmark, also called \"continental Denmark\" or \"Denmark proper\", consists of the northern Jutland peninsula and an archipelago of 406 islands. It is the southernmost of the Scandinavian countries, lying southwest of Sweden, south of Norway, and north of Germany, with which it shares a short border. Denmark proper is situated between the North Sea to the west and the Baltic Sea to the east. The Kingdom of Denmark, including the Faroe Islands and Greenland, has roughly 1,400 islands greater than 100 square metres (1,100 sq ft) in area; 443 have been named and 78 are inhabited."}}, {"canonical_name": "Prusse", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "prusse", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Prusse", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Prusse"], "contexts": ["country with ports", "country mentioned with population data"]}, {"entity_id": "prussia", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Prussia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Prussia"], "contexts": ["region of observation", "nation where Minden is located"]}, {"entity_id": "prussia", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Prussia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Prussia"], "contexts": ["location mentioned for a phenomenon"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Prusse", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Prussia", "similarity": 0.968}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Prusse", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Prussia", "similarity": 0.968}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Prussia", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Prussia", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1451, "stable_key": "clu_1f1b61c9561fc1db", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Prussia", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Historical region and former German state, known for its coast.", "wikipedia_extract": "Prussia (; German: Preu\u00dfen [\u02c8p\u0281\u0254\u028fsn\u0329] ; Old Prussian: Pr\u016bsija) was a German state centred on the North European Plain that originated from the 1525 secularization act of the Prussian part of the State of the Teutonic Order. For centuries, the House of Hohenzollern ruled Prussia, expanding its size with the Prussian Army. Prussia, with its capital at K\u00f6nigsberg and then, when it became the Kingdom of Prussia in 1701, Berlin, decisively shaped the history of Germany. Prussia formed the German Empire when it united the German states in 1871. It was de facto dissolved by an emergency decree transferring powers of the Prussian government to German Chancellor Franz von Papen in 1932 and de jure by an Allied decree in 1947. The name Prussia derives from the Old Prussians who were conquered by the Teutonic Knights \u2013 an organized Catholic medieval military order of German crusaders \u2013  in the 13th century."}}, {"canonical_name": "phosphorescence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "phosphorescence", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "phosphorescence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["phosphorescence", "phosphorescence de la mer"], "contexts": ["property of the insects", "emission of light"]}, {"entity_id": "phosphorescence", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "phosphorescence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Phosphorescence", "phosphorescence"], "contexts": ["sea phenomenon", "emission of light by sea"]}, {"entity_id": "phosphorescence", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "phosphorescence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Phosphorescence", "phosphorescence"], "contexts": ["light emission independent of external influence", "emission of light after excitation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "phosphorescence", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "phosphorescence", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "phosphorescence", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "phosphorescence", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "phosphorescence", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "phosphorescence", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1452, "stable_key": "clu_a4614d891cc770e4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "phosphorescence", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q192275", "wikidata_description": "process in which energy absorbed by a substance is released relatively slowly in the form of light", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phosphorescence", "wikipedia_extract": "Phosphorescence is a type of photoluminescence related to fluorescence. When exposed to light (radiation) of a shorter threshold wavelength, a phosphorescent substance will glow, absorbing the light and reemitting it at a longer wavelength. Unlike fluorescence, a phosphorescent material does not immediately reemit the radiation it absorbs. Instead, a phosphorescent material absorbs some of the radiation energy and reemits it for a much longer time after the radiation source is removed. In a general sense, there is no distinct boundary between the emission times of fluorescence and phosphorescence (i.e.: if a substance glows under a black light it is generally considered fluorescent, and if it glows in the dark it is often simply called phosphorescent). In a modern, scientific sense, the phenomena can usually be classified by the three different mechanisms that produce the light and the typical timescales at which they emit light: fluorescence, triplet phosphorescence, and persistent phosphorescence."}}, {"canonical_name": "Sir Wm. Hamilton", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "m__hamilton", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "M. Hamilton", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["M. Hamilton"], "contexts": ["gave an evaluation of the peninsula", "considered independent states"]}, {"entity_id": "sir_wm__hamilton", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Sir Wm. Hamilton", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 5, "variants": ["Sir Wm. Hamilton", "Wm. Hamilton"], "contexts": ["philosopher defending soul's presence in body", "author of Discussions"]}, {"entity_id": "sir_wm_hamilton", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Sir Wm Hamilton", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Sir Wm Hamilton"], "contexts": ["philosopher discussing consciousness and the Absolute"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "M. Hamilton", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Sir Wm. Hamilton", "similarity": 0.905}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "M. Hamilton", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Sir Wm Hamilton", "similarity": 0.893}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Sir Wm. Hamilton", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Sir Wm Hamilton", "similarity": 0.991}], "id": 1453, "stable_key": "clu_ee6f0134c7803bbf", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sir William Hamilton", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1788, "death_year": 1856, "description": "Scottish idealist philosopher and critic of knowledge"}}, {"canonical_name": "Kasan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "kasan", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Kasan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Kasan"], "contexts": ["university city", "city for observation"]}, {"entity_id": "kasan", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Kasan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Kasan"], "contexts": ["city used for longitude determination", "city of simultaneous observations"]}, {"entity_id": "kasan", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Kasan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Kasan"], "contexts": ["location of M. Kupffer's observations", "summer and winter mean temperature compared"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Kasan", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Kasan", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Kasan", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Kasan", "similarity": 0.852}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Kasan", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Kasan", "similarity": 0.852}], "id": 1454, "stable_key": "clu_d7f8c57b47ca8037", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Kazan", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Russia", "note": "The mentions of 'Kasan' as a city used for longitude determination and a university city, situated in European Russia, strongly point to Kazan.", "wikidata_id": "Q900", "wikidata_description": "city in the Republic of Tatarstan, Russia", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kazan", "wikipedia_extract": "Kazan is the largest city and capital of Tatarstan, Russia. The city lies at the confluence of the Volga and the Kazanka rivers, covering an area of 425.3 square kilometres (164.2 square miles), with a population of over 1.3 million residents, and up to nearly 2 million residents in the greater metropolitan area. Kazan is the fifth-largest city in Russia, being the most populous city on the Volga and within the Volga Federal District. Historically, Kazan was the capital of the Khanate of Kazan, and was conquered by Ivan the Terrible in the 16th century, at which point the city became a part of the Tsardom of Russia. The city was seized (and largely destroyed) during Pugachev's Rebellion (1773\u20131775), but was later rebuilt during the reign of Catherine the Great. In the following centuries, Kazan grew to become a major industrial, cultural and religious centre of Russia."}}, {"canonical_name": "Newfoundland", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "new-foundland", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "New-Foundland", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["New-Foundland"], "contexts": ["region with population count"]}, {"entity_id": "newfoundland", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Newfoundland", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Newfoundland"], "contexts": ["location with frequent lights", "Banks of Newfoundland"]}, {"entity_id": "newfoundland", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Newfoundland", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 5, "variants": ["Newfoundland"], "contexts": ["bank where hurricane was traced", "region near St. George's Bay on isothermal line"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "New-Foundland", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Newfoundland", "similarity": 0.722}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "New-Foundland", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Newfoundland", "similarity": 0.722}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Newfoundland", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Newfoundland", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1455, "stable_key": "clu_41ac68a675cc246d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Newfoundland and Labrador", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Canada", "wikidata_id": "Q48335", "wikidata_description": "island portion of Newfoundland and Labrador, Canada"}}, {"canonical_name": "Parry", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "parry", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Parry", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Parry"], "contexts": ["captain mentioned in reference"]}, {"entity_id": "parry", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Parry", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 6, "variants": ["Parry"], "contexts": ["saw northern light arch during day", "observed northern light"]}, {"entity_id": "parry", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Parry", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Parry"], "contexts": ["attempted to reach the north pole"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Parry", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Parry", "similarity": 0.726}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Parry", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Parry", "similarity": 0.726}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Parry", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Parry", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1456, "stable_key": "clu_67314860911a4588", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "William Parry", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1790, "death_year": 1855, "description": "British admiral and Arctic explorer who led expeditions to the North Pole.", "wikidata_id": "Q437310", "wikidata_description": "English naval officer and Arctic explorer (1790\u20131855)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edward%20Parry%20%28Royal%20Navy%20officer%2C%20born%201790%29", "wikipedia_extract": "Sir William Edward Parry  (19 December 1790 \u2013 8 July 1855) was a Royal Navy officer and explorer best known for his 1819\u20131820 expedition through the Parry Channel, probably the most successful in the long quest for the Northwest Passage, until it was finally negotiated by Roald Amundsen in 1906. In 1827, Parry attempted one of the earliest expeditions to the North Pole. He reached 82\u00b0 45' N, setting a record for human exploration Farthest North that stood for nearly five decades before being surpassed at 83\u00b0 20' N by Albert Hastings Markham in 1875. Parry was born in Bath, Somerset, the son of Caleb Hillier Parry and Sarah Rigby. He was educated at King Edward's School. At the age of thirteen he joined the flagship of Admiral Sir William Cornwallis in the Channel fleet as a first-class volunteer, in 1806 became a midshipman, and in 1810 received promotion to the rank of lieutenant in the frigate Alexander, which spent the next three years in the protection of the Spitsbergen whale fishery."}}, {"canonical_name": "English", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "english", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "English", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 4, "variants": ["English"], "contexts": ["Nationality of writers on psychology", "language spoken in England"]}, {"entity_id": "english", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "English", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["English"], "contexts": ["public receiving new translation", "public the interpreter addresses"]}, {"entity_id": "english", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "English", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["English"], "contexts": ["nationality associated with time and measures", "related to England"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "English", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "English", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "English", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "English", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "English", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "English", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1457, "stable_key": "clu_7740eb6cd24ff21e-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "England", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United Kingdom", "wikidata_id": "Q21", "wikidata_description": "country in north-west Europe, part of the United Kingdom", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/England", "wikipedia_extract": "England is a country that is part of the United Kingdom. It is located on the island of Great Britain, of which it covers about 62%, and more than 100 smaller adjacent islands. England shares a land border with Scotland to the north and another land border with Wales to the west, and is otherwise surrounded by the North Sea to the east, the English Channel to the south, the Celtic Sea to the south-west, and the Irish Sea to the west. Continental Europe lies to the south-east, and Ireland to the west. At the 2021 census, the population was 56,490,048. London is both the largest city and the capital."}}, {"canonical_name": "passion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "passion", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "passion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["passion"], "contexts": ["strong emotion", "strong emotion influencing thought"]}, {"entity_id": "passion", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "passion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["passion"], "contexts": ["storm calmed by nature"]}, {"entity_id": "passion", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "passion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["passion"], "contexts": ["strong emotion"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "passion", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "passion", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "passion", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "passion", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "passion", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "passion", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1458, "stable_key": "clu_532d0f1549cc9000", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "passion", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q623306", "wikidata_description": "final period in