r": 1723, "death_year": 1762, "description": "German astronomer, geographer, and mathematician, known for lunar theory.", "wikidata_id": "Q60406", "wikidata_description": "German astronomer", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tobias%20Mayer", "wikipedia_extract": "Tobias Mayer (17 February 1723 \u2013 20 February 1762) was a German astronomer famous for his studies of the Moon. He was born at Marbach, in W\u00fcrttemberg, and brought up at Esslingen in poor circumstances. A self-taught mathematician, he earned a living by teaching mathematics while still a youth. He had already published two original geometrical works when, in 1746, he entered J. B. Homann's cartographic establishment at Nuremberg."}}, {"canonical_name": "navigation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "navigation", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "navigation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 4, "variants": ["navigation"], "contexts": ["method of transport", "purpose of canal and rivers"]}, {"entity_id": "navigation", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "navigation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["navigation"], "contexts": ["act of guiding a ship or aircraft"]}, {"entity_id": "navigation", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Navigation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Navigation"], "contexts": ["importance of lunar motions, laws of storms"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "navigation", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "navigation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "navigation", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Navigation", "similarity": 0.969}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "navigation", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Navigation", "similarity": 0.969}], "id": 1636, "stable_key": "clu_f29a6059948cc098", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "navigation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q102066", "wikidata_description": "process of monitoring and directing the movement of a person, craft or vehicle from one place to another", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Navigation", "wikipedia_extract": "Navigation is a field of study that focuses on the process of monitoring and controlling the movement of a craft or vehicle from one place to another. The field of navigation includes four general categories: land navigation, marine navigation, aeronautic navigation, and space navigation. It is also the term of art used for the specialized knowledge used by navigators to perform navigation tasks. All navigational techniques involve locating the navigator's position compared to known locations or patterns. Navigation, in a broader sense, can refer to any skill or study that involves the determination of position and direction. In this sense, navigation includes orienteering and pedestrian navigation."}}, {"canonical_name": "Rodet", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "rodet", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Rodet", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Rodet"], "contexts": ["author of work on Paris warehouse", "source of information from Entrep\u00f4t de Paris"]}, {"entity_id": "rondot", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Rondot", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rondot"], "contexts": ["author of a study on China's commerce"]}, {"entity_id": "roget", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Roget", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Roget"], "contexts": ["author of articles on electro-magnetism"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Rodet", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Rondot", "similarity": 0.883}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Rodet", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Roget", "similarity": 0.897}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Rondot", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Roget", "similarity": 0.887}], "id": 1637, "stable_key": "clu_a94950117b5f6f72", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Fran\u00e7ois Rodet", "confidence": "low", "type": "person", "description": "French author, likely referring to a specific work or individual.", "note": "The names 'Rodet', 'Rondot', and 'Roget' are all present and attributed to different contexts, suggesting potential ambiguity or multiple individuals. Further context is needed for a definitive identification."}}, {"canonical_name": "Kotzebue", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "kotzebue", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Kotzebue", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 3, "variants": ["Kotzebue", "Kotzehue"], "contexts": ["author of 'Reise'", "person who made a measurement"]}, {"entity_id": "kotzebue", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Kotzebue", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Kotzebue"], "contexts": ["voyages mentioned for sea water temperature"]}, {"entity_id": "kotzebue", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Kotzebue", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Kotzebue"], "contexts": ["explorer who found temperature stratum", "discovered stratum in the ocean"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Kotzebue", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Kotzebue", "similarity": 0.699}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Kotzebue", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Kotzebue", "similarity": 0.699}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Kotzebue", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Kotzebue", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1638, "stable_key": "clu_6019401d6d99ff06", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Otto von Kotzebue", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1787, "death_year": 1846, "description": "Naval officer and explorer, known for voyages and scientific observations.", "wikidata_id": "Q58828", "wikidata_description": "Baltic German explorer (1787\u20131846)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Otto%20von%20Kotzebue", "wikipedia_extract": "Otto von Kotzebue (Russian: \u041e\u0301\u0442\u0442\u043e \u0415\u0432\u0441\u0442\u0430\u0301\u0444\u044c\u0435\u0432\u0438\u0447 \u041a\u043e\u0446\u0435\u0431\u0443\u0301; 30 December 1787 \u2013 15 February 1846) was a Baltic German naval officer in the Imperial Russian Navy. He commanded two naval expeditions into the Pacific for the purposes of exploration and scientific investigation. The first expedition explored Oceania and the western coast of North America and passed through the Bering Strait in search of a passage across the Arctic Ocean. His second voyage was intended as a military resupply mission to Kamchatka but again included significant explorations of the west coast of North America and Oceania. Kotzebue was born and raised in Reval (Tallinn), in the Governorate of Estonia of the Russian Empire. He was the second son of German writer and diplomat August von Kotzebue."}}, {"canonical_name": "Charles II", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "charles_ii", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Charles II", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 3, "variants": ["Charles II", "Charles III", "Roi Charles III"], "contexts": ["king during a period of Portuguese gains", "King Charles III"]}, {"entity_id": "charles_ii_of_england", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Charles II of England", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Charles II of England"], "contexts": ["monarch at whose court phenomenon was shown"]}, {"entity_id": "charles", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Charles", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Charles"], "contexts": ["King Charles", "king of France"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Charles II", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Charles II of England", "similarity": 0.939}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Charles II", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Charles", "similarity": 0.867}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Charles II of England", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Charles", "similarity": 0.877}], "id": 1639, "stable_key": "clu_9cc444f45f821dd3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Charles II of England", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1630, "death_year": 1685, "description": "King of England, Scotland, and Ireland, associated with scientific patronage.", "note": "The context 'King Charles' is ambiguous but strongly points to Charles II given the other references.", "wikidata_id": "Q122553", "wikidata_description": "King of England, Scotland, and Ireland from 1660 to 1685", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles%20II%20of%20England", "wikipedia_extract": "Charles II (29 May 1630 \u2013 6 February 1685) was King of Scotland from 1649 until 1651 and King of England, Scotland, and Ireland from the 1660 Restoration of the monarchy until his death in 1685. Charles II was the eldest surviving child of Charles I of England, Scotland and Ireland and Henrietta Maria of France. After Charles I's execution at Whitehall on 30 January 1649, at the climax of the English Civil War, the Parliament of Scotland proclaimed Charles II king on 5 February 1649. However, England entered the period known as the English Interregnum or the English Commonwealth with a republican government eventually led by Oliver Cromwell. Cromwell defeated Charles II at the Battle of Worcester on 3 September 1651, and Charles fled to mainland Europe. Cromwell became Lord Protector of England, Scotland and Ireland."}}, {"canonical_name": "perles", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "perles", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "perles", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["perles"], "contexts": ["production from Panama, Rio Hacha, Marguerite", "formerly famous pearl fisheries"]}, {"entity_id": "perl\u00e1s", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Perl\u00e1s", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Perl\u00e1s"], "contexts": ["prepared pearls, ingredient in a powder"]}, {"entity_id": "perla", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "perla", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["perla"], "contexts": ["Spanish name for pearl", "Latin name for gemstone"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "perles", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Perl\u00e1s", "similarity": 0.944}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "perles", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "perla", "similarity": 0.905}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Perl\u00e1s", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "perla", "similarity": 0.887}], "id": 1640, "stable_key": "clu_3eb7e2110968cc39-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "pearls", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "note": "The term 'perlas' is the Spanish plural for pearl, and 'perla' is singular. 'Perles' in French also means pearls."}}, {"canonical_name": "Charlestown", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "charlestown", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Charlestown", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Charlestown", "Charleston"], "contexts": ["city in South Carolina", "city in South Carolina, USA"]}, {"entity_id": "charleston", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Charleston", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Charleston"], "contexts": ["geographic location", "geographic location for spatial orientation"]}, {"entity_id": "charleston", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Charleston", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Charleston"], "contexts": ["city of Medical College", "city in Eastern America"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Charlestown", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Charleston", "similarity": 0.963}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Charlestown", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Charleston", "similarity": 0.963}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Charleston", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Charleston", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1641, "stable_key": "clu_b34e57d9fbe63731", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Charleston", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United States", "note": "The context of 'Medical College' strongly points to Charleston, South Carolina, a prominent historical location for medical education. While there are other Charlestowns, this is the most likely referent in an early modern scientific/medical context.", "wikidata_id": "Q47716", "wikidata_description": "largest city in the U.S. state of South Carolina", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charleston%2C_South_Carolina", "wikipedia_extract": "Charleston is the most populous city in the U.S. state of South Carolina. The city lies just south of the geographical midpoint of South Carolina's coastline on Charleston Harbor, an inlet of the Atlantic Ocean formed by the confluence of the Ashley, Cooper, and Wando rivers. Charleston had a population of 150,227 at the 2020 census, while the Charleston metropolitan area, comprising Berkeley, Charleston, and Dorchester counties, has an estimated 870,000 residents. It ranks as the third-most populous metropolitan area in the state and the 71st-most populous in the U.S. It is the county seat of Charleston County."}}, {"canonical_name": "Daniell", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "daniell", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Daniell", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Daniell"], "contexts": ["author of M\u00e9t\u00e9orologie", "scholar involved in sugar research"]}, {"entity_id": "daniell", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Daniell", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Daniell"], "contexts": ["views on atmospheric moisture measurement"]}, {"entity_id": "daniell", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Daniell", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Daniell"], "contexts": ["professor who improved Voltaic battery", "improved voltaic battery"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Daniell", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Daniell", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Daniell", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Daniell", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Daniell", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Daniell", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1642, "stable_key": "clu_da7ef193ec9c5313", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "John Frederic Daniell", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1790, "death_year": 1845, "description": "Meteorologist, chemist, and inventor of the Daniell cell.", "wikidata_id": "Q119047706", "wikidata_description": "painting by British (English) School, King's College London"}}, {"canonical_name": "latte", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "PRODUCT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "lait", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "lait", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "PRODUCT", "count": 2, "variants": ["lait"], "contexts": ["milk consumed in Havana", "animal product"]}, {"entity_id": "latte", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "latte", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "PRODUCT", "count": 3, "variants": ["latte", "latte della madre"], "contexts": ["milk, an improper part of animals", "milky substance"]}, {"entity_id": "latte", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Latte", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Latte"], "contexts": ["milk"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "lait", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "latte", "similarity": 0.895}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "latte", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Latte", "similarity": 0.689}], "id": 1643, "stable_key": "clu_c4aace0411a82c60", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Milk", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "Milk is a product derived from mammals.", "wikidata_id": "Q157184", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Astragalus%20%28plant%29"}}, {"canonical_name": "Hofmanus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "hofmann", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Hofmann", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Hofmann"], "contexts": ["scholar who executed leveling", "executed barometric leveling"]}, {"entity_id": "hofmann", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Hofmann", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hofmann"], "contexts": ["cited scholar"]}, {"entity_id": "hofmanus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Hofmanus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Hofmanus", "Hofman."], "contexts": ["author on memory loss", "Author of a medical method"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Hofmann", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Hofmann", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Hofmann", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Hofmanus", "similarity": 0.872}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Hofmann", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Hofmanus", "similarity": 0.872}], "id": 1644, "stable_key": "clu_81f655c4ecd05831", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "August Wilhelm von Hofmann", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1818, "death_year": 1892, "description": "German chemist known for his work on organic chemistry and aniline dyes.", "note": "The context mentions 'memory loss' which is not a primary association for A.W. von Hofmann. It is possible 'Hofmanus' refers to a different scholar or there's a misunderstanding in the source text or OCR."}}, {"canonical_name": "astronomical", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "astronomique", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "astronomique", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["astronomique"], "contexts": ["method of surveying"]}, {"entity_id": "astronomiques", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "astronomiques", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["astronomiques"], "contexts": ["operations used for mapping"]}, {"entity_id": "astronomical", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "astronomical", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["astronomical"], "contexts": ["related to celestial bodies", "sections on celestial bodies"]}, {"entity_id": "astronomic", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "astronomic", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["astronomic"], "contexts": ["related to periods and festivals", "related to celestial bodies and space"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "astronomique", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "astronomical", "similarity": 0.979}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "astronomique", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "astronomic", "similarity": 0.986}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "astronomiques", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "astronomical", "similarity": 0.969}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "astronomiques", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "astronomic", "similarity": 0.968}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "astronomical", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "astronomic", "similarity": 0.99}], "id": 1645, "stable_key": "clu_fd60ba24ea5dfe10-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Astronomy", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q333", "wikidata_description": "natural science studying celestial objects and phenomena in the cosmos", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Astronomy", "note": "While 'astronomical' can refer to the study of celestial bodies, in the context of surveying and mapping, it more broadly implies methods or operations related to celestial observation for measurement.", "wikipedia_extract": "Astronomy is a natural science that studies celestial objects and the phenomena that occur in the cosmos. It uses mathematics, physics, and chemistry to explain their origin and their overall evolution. Objects of interest include planets, moons, stars, nebulae, galaxies, meteoroids, asteroids, and comets. Relevant phenomena include supernova explosions, gamma ray bursts, quasars, blazars, pulsars, and cosmic microwave background radiation. More generally, astronomy studies everything that originates beyond Earth's atmosphere. Cosmology is the branch of astronomy that studies the universe as a whole."}}, {"canonical_name": "storms", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "temp\u00eates", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "temp\u00eates", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["temp\u00eates"], "contexts": ["severe weather events"]}, {"entity_id": "storms", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "storms", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 4, "variants": ["storms"], "contexts": ["geographical distribution of", "violent atmospheric disturbances"]}, {"entity_id": "thunderstorms", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "thunderstorms", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["thunderstorms"], "contexts": ["frequent near coasts due to electricity"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "temp\u00eates", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "storms", "similarity": 0.954}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "temp\u00eates", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "thunderstorms", "similarity": 0.932}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "storms", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "thunderstorms", "similarity": 0.939}], "id": 1646, "stable_key": "clu_aa1aafdeea694189-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "weather events", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'storms' in early modern texts, especially in contexts like Humboldt's 'Kosmos', refers to natural meteorological phenomena rather than diseases. 'Magnetic storms' refers to geomagnetic disturbances.", "wikidata_id": "Q130240049", "wikidata_description": "", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weather%20events%20during%20wars", "wikipedia_extract": "A storm is any disturbed state of the natural environment or the atmosphere of an astronomical body. It may be marked by significant disruptions to normal conditions such as strong wind, tornadoes, hail, thunder and lightning (a thunderstorm), heavy precipitation (snowstorm, rainstorm), heavy freezing rain (ice storm), strong winds (tropical cyclone, windstorm), wind transporting some substance through the atmosphere such as in a dust storm, among other forms of severe weather. Storms have the potential to harm lives and property via storm surge, heavy rain or snow causing flooding or road impassibility, lightning, wildfires, and vertical and horizontal wind shear. Systems with significant rainfall and duration help alleviate drought in places they move through. Heavy snowfall can allow special recreational activities to take place which would not be possible otherwise, such as skiing and snowmobiling. The English word comes from Proto-Germanic sturmaz meaning \"noise, tumult\"; a synonym is tempest, of French origin."}}, {"canonical_name": "Chypre", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "chypre", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Chypre", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Chypre"], "contexts": ["island with serpentine and diorite"]}, {"entity_id": "chypre", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Chypre", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Chypre"], "contexts": ["island country", "island where plants were seen"]}, {"entity_id": "chypre", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Chypre", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Chypre", "Chipre"], "contexts": ["origin of acaparo", "source of Caparrofa"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Chypre", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Chypre", "similarity": 0.903}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Chypre", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Chypre", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Chypre", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Chypre", "similarity": 0.903}], "id": 1647, "stable_key": "clu_d867f8cabe5b6a7d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cyprus", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Island country known for its plants and mineral resources.", "note": "Refers to the island of Cyprus.", "wikipedia_extract": "Cyprus, officially the Republic of Cyprus, is an island country in the eastern Mediterranean Sea, located off the coast of the Levant in West Asia. Cyprus\u2019 capital is Nicosia, while its largest municipality is Limassol. The northeast portion of the island is occupied by Turkey, which is separated from the Republic of Cyprus by the United Nations Buffer Zone. In the south of the island of Cyprus are the British sovereign military bases of Akrotiri and Dhekelia. The island is the third largest and third most populous in the Mediterranean, after Sicily and Sardinia. Cyprus was first settled by hunter-gatherers around 13,000 years ago, with farming communities emerging by 8500 BC."}}, {"canonical_name": "Bouvard", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "bouvard", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Bouvard", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bouvard"], "contexts": ["published meteorological variations"]}, {"entity_id": "bouvard", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Bouvard", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Bouvard"], "contexts": ["witness to comet light observation", "astronomer who made lunar observations"]}, {"entity_id": "bouvard", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Bouvard", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bouvard"], "contexts": ["estimated atmospheric undulations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Bouvard", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Bouvard", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Bouvard", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Bouvard", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Bouvard", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Bouvard", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1648, "stable_key": "clu_df6e17d281c70b47", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Fran\u00e7ois Bouvard", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1583, "death_year": 1650, "description": "French physician, mathematician, and astronomer.", "wikidata_id": "Q3084089", "wikidata_description": "French composer (c. 1684\u20131760)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fran%C3%A7ois%20Bouvard", "wikipedia_extract": "Fran\u00e7ois Bouvard (c. 1684\u20131760) was a French composer of the Baroque era. Originally from Lyon, Lyonnais, Bouvard began his career as a singer at the Paris Op\u00e9ra at the age of sixteen. When the quality of his voice deteriorated, he went to study in Rome and devoted himself to playing the violin and composition. His first opera, the trag\u00e9die en musique M\u00e9dus, appeared in Paris in 1702."}}, {"canonical_name": "Isle of France", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "\u00eeles_de_france", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "\u00eeles de France", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["\u00eeles de France"], "contexts": ["islands producing sugar"]}, {"entity_id": "isle_of_france", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Isle of France", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Isle of France"], "contexts": ["southern hemisphere observation location", "location of pendulum experiments"]}, {"entity_id": "ilha_de_fran\u00e7a", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "ilha de Fran\u00e7a", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["ilha de Fran\u00e7a"], "contexts": ["Island, likely Mauritius"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "\u00eeles de France", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Isle of France", "similarity": 0.923}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "\u00eeles de France", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "ilha de Fran\u00e7a", "similarity": 0.915}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Isle of France", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "ilha de Fran\u00e7a", "similarity": 0.951}], "id": 1649, "stable_key": "clu_fa1b52c906e98fcf", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "\u00cele-de-France", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A region in France or an island associated with French establishments and trade.", "note": "In the context of Humboldt, likely refers to an island, but the name itself denotes a French region.", "wikipedia_extract": "The \u00cele-de-France (; French: [il d\u0259 f\u0281\u0251\u0303s] ; lit.\u2009'Island of France') is the most populous of the eighteen regions of France, with an official estimated population of 12,271,794 residents on 1 January 2023. Containing the capital city of France, Paris, it is located in the north-central part of the country and often called the Paris Region (French: R\u00e9gion parisienne, pronounced [\u0281e\u0292j\u0254\u0303 pa\u0281izj\u025bn]). \u00cele-de-France is densely populated and retains a prime economic position on the national stage, and it covers 12,012 square kilometres (4,638 square miles), about 2% of metropolitan French territory. Its 2017 population was nearly one-fifth of the national total. The region is made up of eight administrative departments: Paris, Essonne, Hauts-de-Seine, Seine-Saint-Denis, Seine-et-Marne, Val-de-Marne, Val-d'Oise and Yvelines. It was created as the \"District of the Paris Region\" in 1961. In 1976, when its status was aligned with the French administrative regions created in 1972, it was renamed after the historic province of \u00cele-de-France."}}, {"canonical_name": "Bremen", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "bremen", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Bremen", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bremen"], "contexts": ["city of sugar importation"]}, {"entity_id": "bremen", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Bremen", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Bremen"], "contexts": ["city where meteor falls were observed", "city where meteoric fall was observed"]}, {"entity_id": "bremen", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Bremen", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bremen"], "contexts": ["location of Olbers"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Bremen", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Bremen", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Bremen", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Bremen", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Bremen", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Bremen", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1650, "stable_key": "clu_db9df04010eff022", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bremen", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Germany", "wikidata_id": "Q24879", "wikidata_description": "city in the Bremen federated state, Germany", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bremen", "wikipedia_extract": "Bremen (German pronunciation: [\u02c8b\u0281e\u02d0m\u0259n] ), officially the City Municipality of Bremen, is the capital of the German state of the Free Hanseatic City of Bremen (Freie Hansestadt Bremen) a two-city-state consisting of the cities of Bremen and Bremerhaven. It is an important port city with about 586,000 inhabitants, the Hanseatic city is the 11th-largest city of Germany and the second-largest city in Northern Germany after Hamburg. Bremen is the largest city on the River Weser, the longest river flowing entirely in Germany, lying some 60 km (37 mi) upstream from its mouth into the North Sea at Bremerhaven, and is completely surrounded by the state of Lower Saxony. Bremen is the centre of the Northwest Metropolitan Region, which also includes the cities of Oldenburg and Bremerhaven, and has a population of around 2.8 million people. Bremen is contiguous with the Lower Saxon towns of Delmenhorst, Stuhr, Achim, Weyhe, Schwanewede and Lilienthal. There is an exclave of Bremen in Bremerhaven, the \"Citybremian Overseas Port Area Bremerhaven\" (Stadtbremisches \u00dcberseehafengebiet Bremerhaven)."}}, {"canonical_name": "liquid", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "liquide", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "liquide", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["liquide"], "contexts": ["substance observed"]}, {"entity_id": "liquido", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Liquido", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Liquido"], "contexts": ["liquid obtained from tree branches", "liquid from a tree"]}, {"entity_id": "liquid", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "liquid", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["liquid"], "contexts": ["state of matter"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "liquide", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Liquido", "similarity": 0.987}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "liquide", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "liquid", "similarity": 0.993}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Liquido", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "liquid", "similarity": 0.985}], "id": 1651, "stable_key": "clu_eece0e86af87a552-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Liquid", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to any fluid, including bodily fluids, solutions, or extracts. The Italian 'liquori' also specifically points to medicinal preparations.", "wikidata_id": "Q469652", "wikidata_description": "tree species", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liquidambar%20styraciflua", "wikipedia_extract": "Liquidambar styraciflua, commonly known as the American sweetgum among other names, is a deciduous tree in the genus Liquidambar native to warm temperate areas of eastern North America and tropical montane regions of Mexico and Central America. Sweetgum is one of the main valuable forest trees in the southeastern United States, and is a popular ornamental tree in temperate climates. It is recognizable by the combination of its five-pointed star-shaped leaves (similar to maple leaves) and its hard, spiked fruits. It is currently classified in the plant family Altingiaceae, but was formerly considered a member of the Hamamelidaceae. This plant's genus name Liquidambar was first given by Linnaeus in 1753 from the Latin liquidus ('fluid') and the Arabic ambar ('amber'), in allusion to the fragrant terebinthine juice or gum which exudes from the tree. Its specific epithet styraciflua is an old generic name meaning 'flowing with storax' (a plant resin)."}}, {"canonical_name": "Port Bowen Harbour", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "port_bowen", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Port Bowen", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Port Bowen"], "contexts": ["and of the Port Bowen"]}, {"entity_id": "port_bowen", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Port Bowen", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Port Bowen"], "contexts": ["location of Parry's observations", "location of barometric observation"]}, {"entity_id": "port_bowen_harbour", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Port Bowen Harbour", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Port Bowen Harbour"], "contexts": ["location for sound transmission experiment", "transmission of sound across"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Port Bowen", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Port Bowen", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Port Bowen", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Port Bowen Harbour", "similarity": 0.939}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Port Bowen", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Port Bowen Harbour", "similarity": 0.939}], "id": 1652, "stable_key": "clu_6f094ff64213583c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Port Bowen Harbour", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Canada", "note": "Located in Nunavut, Canada.", "wikidata_id": "Q116744319", "wikidata_description": "bay in Nunavut, Canada", "wikipedia_extract": "Port Chalmers (M\u0101ori: K\u014dp\u016btai) is a town serving as the main port of the city of Dunedin, New Zealand. Port Chalmers lies 13 kilometres (8.1 mi) inside Otago Harbour, some 13 kilometres (8.1 mi) northeast of Dunedin's city centre. No archaeological sites relating to M\u0101ori have been discovered in Port Chalmers, although records from European settlers in the 1830s and 1840s note the presence of buildings on the now-reclaimed beach. By 1844 this settlement was fully abandoned. The M\u0101ori name for the bay at Port Chalmers is Koputai meaning \u2018full tide\u2019 and refers to an incident in which a group of warriors decided to spend the night in a cave that once existed at what was later known as Boiler Point and pulled their canoes well above the high tide mark. Overnight, the tide rose and the beached canoes were set adrift.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_Chalmers"}}, {"canonical_name": "Carolinas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "carolina", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Carolina", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Carolina"], "contexts": ["establishment and fort"]}, {"entity_id": "carolinas", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Carolinas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Carolinas"], "contexts": ["island group in the Pacific", "region in southeastern United States"]}, {"entity_id": "south_carolina", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "South Carolina", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["South Carolina"], "contexts": ["state of Charleston"]}, {"entity_id": "carolinas", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Carolinas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Carolinas"], "contexts": ["region affected by storm curve"]}, {"entity_id": "north_carolina", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "North Carolina", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["North Carolina"], "contexts": ["location on isothermal line of 59\u00b0 Fahrenheit"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Carolina", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "South Carolina", "similarity": 0.942}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Carolina", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Carolinas", "similarity": 0.968}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Carolinas", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Carolinas", "similarity": 0.839}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Carolinas", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "North Carolina", "similarity": 0.932}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "South Carolina", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Carolinas", "similarity": 0.916}], "id": 1653, "stable_key": "clu_8c55055b20fa29ea", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "North Carolina and South Carolina", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United States", "note": "The text mentions both North and South Carolina, and 'Carolinas' can refer to the combined region. The context of 'establishment and fort' and 'state of Charleston' also points to these US states.", "wikidata_id": "Q118350", "wikidata_description": "archipelago of tiny islands in the western Pacific Ocean, divided between the Federated States of Micronesia and Palau", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caroline%20Islands", "wikipedia_extract": "The Caroline Islands (or the Carolines) are a widely scattered archipelago of tiny islands in the western Pacific Ocean, to the north of New Guinea. Politically, they are divided between the Federated States of Micronesia (FSM) in the central and eastern parts of the group, and Palau at the extreme western end. Historically, this area was also called Nuevas Filipinas or New Philippines, because they were part of the Spanish East Indies and were governed from Manila in the Philippines. The Carolines are scattered across a distance of approximately 3,540 kilometres (2,200 mi; 1,910 nmi), from the westernmost island, Tobi, in Palau, to the easternmost island, Kosrae, a state of the FSM. The group consists of about 500 small coral islands, east of the Philippines, in the Pacific Ocean. The distance from Yap (one of the larger Caroline islands) to Manila is 1,200 mi (1,900 km)."}}, {"canonical_name": "Lloyd", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "lloyd", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Lloyd", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lloyd"], "contexts": ["executed geodetic leveling"]}, {"entity_id": "lloyd", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Lloyd", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 3, "variants": ["Lloyd"], "contexts": ["contributor to scientific proceedings", "contributor to science"]}, {"entity_id": "george_lloyd", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "George Lloyd", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["George Lloyd"], "contexts": ["editor of journey journal"]}, {"entity_id": "lloyd", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Lloyd", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lloyd"], "contexts": ["experiments in polarization of heat"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Lloyd", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Lloyd", "similarity": 0.719}, {"source_book": "r