disorder characterized by: frequent and recurrent episodes of binge eating; with associated negative psychological and social problems; but without subsequent episodes of purging"}}, {"canonical_name": "bile", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "bile", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "bile", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["bile"], "contexts": ["fluid secreted by the liver"]}, {"entity_id": "bile", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "bile", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 4, "variants": ["bile", "bilem"], "contexts": ["bodily fluid", "a humor"]}, {"entity_id": "bile", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "bile", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["bile"], "contexts": ["bodily fluid flowing through vessels"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "bile", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "bile", "similarity": 0.801}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "bile", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "bile", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "bile", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "bile", "similarity": 0.801}], "id": 1659, "stable_key": "clu_bf373224f813fc6b-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bile", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "note": "The term 'gall' in early modern texts often referred to bile, a fluid produced by the liver, but could also refer to the gallbladder itself or gallstones.", "wikidata_id": "Q208006", "wikidata_description": "central district and city in Bilecik Province, Turkey", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bilecik", "wikipedia_extract": "Bilecik is a city in northwestern Anatolia, Turkey. It is the seat of Bilecik Province and Bilecik District. Its population is 74,457 (2021). The mayor is Melek M\u0131zrak Suba\u015f\u0131 (CHP). The town is famous for its numerous restored Turkish houses. It is increasingly becoming more attractive to tourists.", "description": "A humor of the body, associated with illness and bodily functions."}}, {"canonical_name": "earths", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "earths", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "earths", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["earths"], "contexts": ["molten mineral matter from volcanoes", "component of volcanic fluid mixture"]}, {"entity_id": "earths", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "earths", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["earths"], "contexts": ["inorganic materials"]}, {"entity_id": "earths", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Earths", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["earths", "Earths"], "contexts": ["inorganic compounds", "decomposed by voltaic electricity"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "earths", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "earths", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "earths", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Earths", "similarity": 0.966}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "earths", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Earths", "similarity": 0.966}], "id": 1660, "stable_key": "clu_50553e34b4a9a6e9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Earths", "confidence": "medium", "type": "mineral", "note": "Refers to the classical element earth, or inorganic mineral matter, rather than the planet Earth.", "wikidata_id": "Q113462347", "wikidata_description": "fictional monster from Dungeons & Dragons"}}, {"canonical_name": "atmospheric currents", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "atmospheric_currents", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "atmospheric currents", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["atmospheric currents"], "contexts": ["equilibrium destroyed", "movement of air"]}, {"entity_id": "currents_of_air", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "currents of air", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["currents of air"], "contexts": ["flow of air"]}, {"entity_id": "currents_of_air", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "currents of air", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["currents of air"], "contexts": ["also concerned in cloud movements"]}, {"entity_id": "atmospheric_currents", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "atmospheric currents", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["atmospheric currents"], "contexts": ["varying in direction and intensity"]}, {"entity_id": "currents_of_air", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "currents of air", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["currents of air"], "contexts": ["natural and artificial, for separation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "atmospheric currents", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "currents of air", "similarity": 0.908}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "atmospheric currents", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "atmospheric currents", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "atmospheric currents", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "currents of air", "similarity": 0.908}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "currents of air", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "currents of air", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "currents of air", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "atmospheric currents", "similarity": 0.908}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "currents of air", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "currents of air", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "currents of air", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "atmospheric currents", "similarity": 0.908}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "currents of air", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "currents of air", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1661, "stable_key": "clu_c8865b679db0b4bf", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Atmospheric currents", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "Soda", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "soda", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "soda", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["soda"], "contexts": ["chemical element in meteoric masses", "chemical compound found in rocks"]}, {"entity_id": "soda", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Soda", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Soda"], "contexts": ["a substance used in medicine"]}, {"entity_id": "soda", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Soda", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["soda", "Soda"], "contexts": ["chemical used with gum guaiacum", "alkali decomposed by electricity"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "soda", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Soda", "similarity": 0.802}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "soda", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Soda", "similarity": 0.927}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Soda", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Soda", "similarity": 0.874}], "id": 1662, "stable_key": "clu_cf450bdc30b8d55e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sodium", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "note": "While 'soda' can refer to sodium carbonate or hydroxide, in early modern scientific contexts discussing meteoric masses and chemical uses, elemental sodium or its compounds are strongly implied. Given the chemical context and 'chemical element' mention, elemental sodium is the most appropriate modern referent.", "wikidata_id": "Q658", "wikidata_description": "chemical element with symbol Na and atomic number 11", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sodium", "wikipedia_extract": "Sodium is a chemical element; it has symbol Na (from Neo-Latin natrium) and atomic number 11. It is a soft, silvery-white, highly reactive metal. Sodium is an alkali metal, being in group 1 of the periodic table. Its only stable isotope is 23Na. The free metal does not occur in nature and must be prepared from compounds. Sodium is the sixth most abundant element in the Earth's crust and exists in numerous minerals such as feldspars, sodalite, and halite (NaCl)."}}, {"canonical_name": "Cephalopodes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "cephalopodes", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Cephalopodes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Cephalopodes"], "contexts": ["animal substance preserved in Oxford clay", "marine mollusks"]}, {"entity_id": "cephalopoda", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Cephalopoda", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cephalopoda"], "contexts": ["marine mollusc group"]}, {"entity_id": "cephalaspides", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Cephalaspides", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cephalaspides"], "contexts": ["family of fossil fish"]}, {"entity_id": "cephalopodes", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Cephalopodes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cephalopodes"], "contexts": ["animals with horny maxillae"]}, {"entity_id": "cephalopods", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Cephalopods", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cephalopods"], "contexts": ["shelled marine invertebrates"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Cephalopodes", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Cephalopodes", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Cephalopodes", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Cephalopods", "similarity": 0.941}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Cephalopoda", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Cephalopodes", "similarity": 0.988}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Cephalopoda", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Cephalopods", "similarity": 0.937}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Cephalaspides", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Cephalopodes", "similarity": 0.899}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Cephalaspides", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Cephalopods", "similarity": 0.913}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Cephalopodes", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Cephalopods", "similarity": 0.941}], "id": 1663, "stable_key": "clu_43bac36bad346c8a", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "A cephalopod  is any member of the molluscan class Cephalopoda  (Greek plural \u03ba\u03b5\u03c6\u03b1\u03bb\u03cc\u03c0\u03bf\u03b4\u03b5\u03c2, kephal\u00f3podes; \"head-feet\") such as a squid, octopus, cuttlefish, or nautilus. These exclusively marine animals are characterized by bilateral body symmetry, a prominent head, and a set of arms or tentacles (muscular hydrostats) modified from the primitive molluscan foot. Fisherman sometimes call cephalopods \"inkfish\", referring to their common ability to squirt ink. The study of cephalopods is a branch of malacology known as teuthology. Cephalopods became dominant during the Ordovician period, represented by primitive nautiloids. The class now contains two, only distantly related, extant subclasses: Coleoidea, which includes octopuses, squid, and cuttlefish; and Nautiloidea, represented by Nautilus and Allonautilus."}}, {"canonical_name": "Corpo Santo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "espiritu_santo", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Espiritu Santo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Espiritu Santo"], "contexts": ["coast of Brazil"]}, {"entity_id": "corpo_santo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Corpo Santo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Corpo Santo"], "contexts": ["island named after", "island name"]}, {"entity_id": "corpo_santo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Corpo Santo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Corpo Santo", "CorpoSanto"], "contexts": ["location of apothecary", "location where Samuel lived"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Espiritu Santo", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Corpo Santo", "similarity": 0.883}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Espiritu Santo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Corpo Santo", "similarity": 0.883}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Corpo Santo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Corpo Santo", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1664, "stable_key": "clu_b716f36da56d39ce", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Corpo Santo", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A place, island, or street, likely named after a saint.", "wikipedia_extract": "Corpo Santo is a Brazilian telenovela created by Jos\u00e9 Louzeiro for Rede Manchete, aired on March 30 and October 2, 1987, with 162 chapters. Written by Louzeiro, with supervised by Wilson Aguiar Filho, with the collaboration of Cl\u00e1udio MacDowell, Eliane Garcia, Leila M\u00edccolis and Maria Cl\u00e1udia Oliveira, under the direction of Ary Coslov and Walter Campos and general direction by Jos\u00e9 Wilker. It features Christiane Torloni, Reginaldo Faria, S\u00edlvia Buarque, Nathalia Timberg, L\u00eddia Brondi, Ot\u00e1vio Augusto, Jonas Bloch and S\u00e9rgio Viotti in the main roles. The beautiful widow Simone Reski moves with her family \u2013 her daughter Lucinha, her aunt Maria, and her maid Isaura \u2013 from the suburbs to downtown Rio de Janeiro. She immediately meets T\u00e9o, a friendly bachelor and bon vivant, unaware that he is involved with the video smuggling and pornographic film production mafia. T\u00e9o is interested in Simone because he sees Lucinha as a new nymphet star for his films."}}, {"canonical_name": "crystalline form", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "crystalline_state", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "crystalline state", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["crystalline state"], "contexts": ["state of matter with ordered atomic structure"]}, {"entity_id": "crystalline_form", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "crystalline form", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["crystalline form"], "contexts": ["external shape of crystals", "reason for difference in heat transmission"]}, {"entity_id": "crystalline_forms", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "crystalline forms", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["crystalline forms"], "contexts": ["geometric shapes of crystals"]}, {"entity_id": "crystalline_forms", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "crystalline forms", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["crystalline forms"], "contexts": ["shape of salt crystals"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "crystalline state", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "crystalline form", "similarity": 0.88}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "crystalline form", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "crystalline forms", "similarity": 0.95}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "crystalline forms", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "crystalline forms", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1665, "stable_key": "clu_13dbd9067519d293-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "crystallization", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q284256", "wikidata_description": "process by which a solid with a highly organised atomic or molecular structure forms", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crystallization", "wikipedia_extract": "Crystallization is a process that leads to solids with a uniform pattern of atoms or molecules, i.e. a crystal. The uniform nature of a crystalline solid can be contrasted with amorphous solids in which atoms or molecules lack regular organization. Crystallization can occur by various routes including precipitation from solution, freezing of a liquid, or deposition from a gas. Attributes of the resulting crystal can depend largely on factors such as temperature, air pressure, cooling rate, or solute concentration. Crystallization occurs in two main phases. The first is nucleation, the appearance of a crystalline phase from either a supercooled liquid or a supersaturated solvent."}}, {"canonical_name": "regimento", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "regimento", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "regimento", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["regimento", "regimiento"], "contexts": ["dietary regimen", "misspelling in errata"]}, {"entity_id": "regimento", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "regimento", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["regimento"], "contexts": ["royal decree or regulation"]}, {"entity_id": "regimento", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Regimento", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 2, "variants": ["regimento", "Regimento"], "contexts": ["diet or regimen", "guidelines for the sick"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "regimento", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "regimento", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "regimento", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Regimento", "similarity": 0.924}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "regimento", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Regimento", "similarity": 0.924}], "id": 1666, "stable_key": "clu_aaaa72027ac75dac", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "regimen", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The context 'dietary regimen' strongly suggests the medical/health meaning.", "wikidata_id": "Q10358588", "wikidata_description": "military formation of regiment size built around elements of the infantry branch"}}, {"canonical_name": "alambicco", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "alambique", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "alambique", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 2, "variants": ["alambique", "alambiques"], "contexts": ["distillation apparatus", "used in distillation"]}, {"entity_id": "alambique", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "alambique", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 1, "variants": ["alambique"], "contexts": ["vessel used for distillation"]}, {"entity_id": "alambicco", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "alambicco", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 3, "variants": ["alambicco", "alembicco", "alimbicco"], "contexts": ["apparatus for distillation", "distillation instrument"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "alambique", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "alambique", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "alambique", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "alambicco", "similarity": 0.935}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "alambique", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "alambicco", "similarity": 0.935}], "id": 1667, "stable_key": "clu_1acf142c10587a48", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Alambic", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "description": "A type of distillation apparatus used for separating liquids.", "wikipedia_extract": "An alembic (from Arabic: \u0627\u0644\u0625\u0646\u0628\u064a\u0642, romanized: al-inb\u012bq, originating from Ancient Greek: \u1f04\u03bc\u03b2\u03b9\u03be, romanized: ambix, 'cup, beaker') is an alchemical still consisting of two vessels connected by a tube, used for distillation of liquids. The complete distilling apparatus consists of three parts: the \"cucurbit\" (Arabic: \u1e33ar\u02bfa; Greek: \u03b2\u1fd6\u03ba\u03bf\u03c2, b\u00eekos), the still pot containing the liquid to be distilled, which is heated by a flame the \"head\" or \"cap\" (\u0625\u0650\u0646\u0652\u0628\u0650\u064a\u0642\u200e, \u02beinb\u012bq; Greek \u1f04\u03bc\u03b2\u03b9\u03be, ambix) which fits over the mouth of the cucurbit to receive the vapors, with an attached downward-sloping \"tube\" (\u03c3\u03c9\u03bb\u03ae\u03bd, s\u014dl\u0113n) the \"receiver\" (\u0642\u064e\u0627\u0628\u0650\u0644\u064e\u0629, q\u0101bila; \u1f04\u03b3\u03b3\u03bf\u03c2, angos or \u03c6\u03b9\u03ac\u03bb\u03b7, phial\u0113) container In the case of another distilling vessel, the retort, the \"cap\" and the \"cucurbit\" have been combined to form a single vessel. The anbik is also called the ra\u02bes (the Arabic word ra\u02bes means \"head\") of the cucurbit. The liquid in the cucurbit is heated or boiled; the vapour rises into the anbik, where it cools by contact with the walls and condenses, running down the spout into the receiver. A modern descendant of the alembic is the pot still, used to produce distilled beverages. Dioscorides's ambix, described in his De materia medica (c. 50 C.E.), is a helmet-shaped lid for gathering condensed mercury."}}, {"canonical_name": "Rosado", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "rosado", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "rosado", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["rosado"], "contexts": ["rosy substance"]}, {"entity_id": "rosada", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "rosada", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 2, "variants": ["rosada"], "contexts": ["rose water, an ingredient", "rose water"]}, {"entity_id": "rosado", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Rosado", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 3, "variants": ["Rosado", "rosado"], "contexts": ["rose-flavored ingredient in an electuary", "aromatic powder for stomach fomentation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "rosado", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Rosado", "similarity": 0.711}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "rosada", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Rosado", "similarity": 0.923}], "id": 1668, "stable_key": "clu_4ba8bd0e322ae9b7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Rosewater", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "description": "Fragrant liquid distilled from roses, used in medicine and cosmetics.", "wikipedia_extract": "Rose water or rosewater is a flavoured water created by steeping rose petals in water. It is typically made as a by-product during the distillation of rose petals to create rose oil for perfumes. Rose water is widely utilized to flavour culinary dishes and enhance cosmetic products, and it is significant in religious rituals throughout Eurasia. Iran is a major producer, supplying around 90% of the world's rose water demand. Central Iran is home to the annual Golabgiri festival each spring. Thousands of tourists visit the area to celebrate the rose harvest for the production of rosewater."}}, {"canonical_name": "vaso", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "VESSEL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "vaso", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "vaso", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "VESSEL", "count": 1, "variants": ["vaso"], "contexts": ["vessel for drinking goat's milk"]}, {"entity_id": "vaso_piccolo", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "vaso piccolo", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "VESSEL", "count": 1, "variants": ["vaso piccolo"], "contexts": ["small vessel for burning sulfur"]}, {"entity_id": "vaso", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "vaso", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "VESSEL", "count": 4, "variants": ["vaso"], "contexts": ["container for gargle", "container for water"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "vaso", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "vaso piccolo", "similarity": 0.888}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "vaso", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "vaso", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "vaso piccolo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "vaso", "similarity": 0.888}], "id": 1669, "stable_key": "clu_5f465ffe4b94643b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "vessel", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "The term 'vaso' can refer to various containers, but 'vessel' is the most general and accurate translation in this context.", "wikidata_id": "Q987767", "wikidata_description": "device creating a partially or fully enclosed space that can be used to contain, store, and transport objects or materials", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Container", "wikipedia_extract": "A container is any receptacle or enclosure for holding an item. Things kept inside of a container are protected on several sides by being inside of its structure. Humans have used containers for at least 100,000 years, and possibly for millions of years. Containers are used in storage, packaging, and transportation, including shipping. A container is any receptacle or enclosure for holding an item used in storage, packaging, and transportation, including shipping. A container can also be considered as a basic tool, consisting of any device creating a partially or fully enclosed space that can be used to contain, store, and transport objects or materials."}}, {"canonical_name": "meliloto", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "meliloto", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "meliloto", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["meliloto"], "contexts": ["plant used for scent comparison"]}, {"entity_id": "meliloto", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "meliloto", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["meliloto", "Meliloto"], "contexts": ["plant with yellow flowers", "melilot herb used in preparation"]}, {"entity_id": "meli_loto", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Meli loto", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Meli loto"], "contexts": ["Ingredient in a preparation"]}, {"entity_id": "meliloti", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "meliloti", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["meliloti"], "contexts": ["melilot ingredient"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "meliloto", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "meliloto", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "meliloto", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Meli loto", "similarity": 0.808}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "meliloto", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "meliloti", "similarity": 0.962}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "meliloto", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "meliloti", "similarity": 0.962}], "id": 1670, "stable_key": "clu_30c3452ef6f67533", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sweet clover", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Melilotus officinalis", "family": "Fabaceae", "note": "The description of yellow flowers points strongly to the genus Melilotus, commonly known as sweet clover."}}, {"canonical_name": "fluid mass", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "fluids", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "fluids", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["fluids"], "contexts": ["bodily liquids"]}, {"entity_id": "fluid_mass", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "fluid mass", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 4, "variants": ["fluid mass"], "contexts": ["matter in a liquid state", "rotating body of liquid"]}, {"entity_id": "mass", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "mass", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["mass"], "contexts": ["uniform body of matter"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "fluids", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "fluid mass", "similarity": 0.915}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "fluid mass", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mass", "similarity": 0.885}], "id": 1671, "stable_key": "clu_e8fbba8860766cbf", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "juices", "confidence": "medium", "type": "concept", "note": "This cluster includes both bodily fluids and plant extracts, a broader concept than a specific substance.", "wikidata_id": "Q1058795", "wikidata_description": "liquid originating from the inside of an organism, including fluids excreted or secreted, and body water that normally is not", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cold-pressed_juice", "wikipedia_extract": "Cold-pressed juice is juice that uses a hydraulic press to extract juice from fruit and vegetables, as opposed to other methods such as centrifugal or single auger. Without pasteurization or high-pressure processing (HPP), cold-pressed juices can be stored in a refrigerator for up to three days when phytochemical and micronutrient degradation occurs. Some juicers use technology that helps delay oxidation which can allow for slightly longer storage in refrigerators. This type of juice has been commercially produced for decades, but became more common in some countries since 2013. In general, these juices are more expensive than other types of juices, as they are made from 100% fruit and vegetables without any added ingredients. Although cold-pressed juices were produced over several decades, the products gained more common use during the fad of juice \"cleansing\", expanding into a wider industry in the early 21st century."}}, {"canonical_name": "pineta", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "pine-apple", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "pine-apple", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["pine-apple"], "contexts": ["plant variety mentioned in classification example"]}, {"entity_id": "pineta", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "pineta", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["pineta"], "contexts": ["term for pine trees", "plant associated with palmeta"]}, {"entity_id": "pine-apples", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "pine-apples", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["pine-apples"], "contexts": ["plant requiring hot climate"]}, {"entity_id": "pineta", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "pineta", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["pineta"], "contexts": ["pine groves mentioned by historians"]}, {"entity_id": "pinus", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Pinus", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Pinus"], "contexts": ["plant genus observed by Columbus"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "pine-apple", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "pineta", "similarity": 0.894}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "pine-apple", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "pine-apples", "similarity": 0.98}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "pineta", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Pinus", "similarity": 0.903}], "id": 1672, "stable_key": "clu_39b28c9774d76de8-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pine", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Pinus", "family": "Pinaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q1258277", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinellia%20ternata", "note": "'pine-apple' in Darwin's *Origin of Species* is an archaic term for the fruit of the pineapple plant, but the presence of 'pines' and 'pineta' referring to coniferous trees in other contexts makes 'Pine' the most encompassing modern referent.", "wikipedia_extract": "Pinellia ternata (Chinese: \u534a\u590f, Japanese: \u30ab\u30e9\u30b9\u30d3\u30b7\u30e3\u30af), crow-dipper, is a plant that is native to China, Japan, and Korea. However, it also grows as an invasive weed in parts of Europe (Austria, Germany) and in North America (California, Ontario, the northeastern United States). The leaves are trifoliate, and the flowers are of the spathe and spadix form that is typical of plants in the family Araceae. The plant spreads by rhizomes, and there are also small bulblets (also known as bulbils) at the base of each leaf. Flowers are borne in spring. This plant is toxic in raw form and must be processed."}}, {"canonical_name": "optical impressions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "optical_impression", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "optical impression", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["optical impression"], "contexts": ["visual sensory input"]}, {"entity_id": "optical_impressions", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "optical impressions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["optical impressions"], "contexts": ["perceptions received through vision", "visual perceptions influenced by imagination"]}, {"entity_id": "optical_impressions", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "optical impressions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["optical impressions"], "contexts": ["visual stimuli received by the eye", "sensory input from sight"]}, {"entity_id": "impressions_of_light", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "impressions of light", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["impressions of light"], "contexts": ["visual sensations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "optical impression", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "optical impressions", "similarity": 0.968}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "optical impressions", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "impressions of light", "similarity": 0.885}], "id": 1673, "stable_key": "clu_05b2675aa3670ae9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Visual Perception", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q113263364", "wikidata_description": "capacity of an insect to extract information from light"}}, {"canonical_name": "syst\u00e8mes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "syst\u00e8mes", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "syst\u00e8mes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["syst\u00e8mes"], "contexts": ["intellectual frameworks to avoid", "organized bodies of ideas"]}, {"entity_id": "system", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "system", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["system"], "contexts": ["natural classification"]}, {"entity_id": "system", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "system", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["system"], "contexts": ["referring to celestial arrangements"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "syst\u00e8mes", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "system", "similarity": 0.864}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "syst\u00e8mes", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "system", "similarity": 0.864}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "system", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "system", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1674, "stable_key": "clu_20511736e8bca049", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "system", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'system' is used broadly in early modern texts to refer to organized structures or arrangements, whether in nature (celestial, biological) or in abstract thought."}}, {"canonical_name": "Euler", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "euler", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "Euler", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Euler"], "contexts": ["great mathematician", "scientist associated with St. Petersburg Academy"]}, {"entity_id": "euler", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Euler", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Euler"], "contexts": ["theorist of magnetic inclination"]}, {"entity_id": "euler", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Euler", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Euler"], "contexts": ["proved conditions for solar system stability"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "Euler", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Euler", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "Euler", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Euler", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Euler", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Euler", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1675, "stable_key": "clu_5aea78bbfc11047e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Leonhard Euler", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1707, "death_year": 1783, "description": "Swiss mathematician and physicist, pioneer of calculus.", "wikidata_id": "Q751290", "wikidata_description": "three angles introduced by Leonhard Euler to describe the orientation of a rigid body with respect to a fixed coordinate system", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euler_angles", "wikipedia_extract": "The Euler angles are three angles introduced by Leonhard Euler to describe the orientation of a rigid body with respect to a fixed coordinate system. They can also represent the orientation of a mobile frame of reference in physics or the orientation of a general basis in  three dimensional linear algebra. Classic Euler angles usually take the inclination angle in such a way that zero degrees represent the vertical orientation. Alternative forms were later introduced by Peter Guthrie Tait and George H. Bryan intended for use in aeronautics and engineering in which zero degrees represent the horizontal position. Euler angles can be defined by elemental geometry or by composition of rotations (i.e."}}, {"canonical_name": "animaux vivants", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "animaux_vivants", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "animaux vivants", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["animaux vivants"], "contexts": ["living animals for experimentation", "organisms used for experimentation"]}, {"entity_id": "living_creatures", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "living creatures", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["living creatures"], "contexts": ["organisms that are alive"]}, {"entity_id": "living_organisms", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "living organisms", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["living organisms"], "contexts": ["general term for life"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "animaux vivants", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "living creatures", "similarity": 0.981}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "animaux vivants", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "living organisms", "similarity": 0.965}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "living creatures", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "living organisms", "similarity": 0.978}], "id": 1676, "stable_key": "clu_151e3f5926c6ea00",