_1778", "name": "histoire naturelle", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 3, "variants": ["histoire naturelle"], "contexts": ["study of the natural world", "study of natural world"]}, {"entity_id": "historia_naturalis", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "historia naturalis", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["historia naturalis"], "contexts": ["study of natural history"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "histoire naturelle", "target_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "target_name": "histoire naturelle", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "histoire naturelle", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "historia naturalis", "similarity": 0.963}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "histoire naturelle", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "historia naturalis", "similarity": 0.963}], "id": 1687, "stable_key": "clu_0bb3ca8b9f12ccf1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Natural history", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q484591", "wikidata_description": "study of organisms including plants or animals in their environment", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natural%20history", "wikipedia_extract": "Natural history is a domain of inquiry involving organisms, including animals, fungi, and plants, in their natural environment, leaning more towards observational than experimental methods of study. A person who studies natural history is called a naturalist or natural historian. Natural history encompasses scientific research but is not limited to it. It involves the systematic study of any category of natural objects or organisms, so while it dates from studies in the ancient Greco-Roman world and the mediaeval Arabic world, through to European Renaissance naturalists working in near isolation, today's natural history is a cross-discipline umbrella of many specialty sciences; e.g., geobiology has a strong multidisciplinary nature. The meaning of the English term \"natural history\" (a calque of the Latin historia naturalis) has narrowed progressively with time, while, by contrast, the meaning of the related term \"nature\" has widened (see also History below). In antiquity, \"natural history\" covered essentially anything connected with nature, or  used materials drawn from nature, such as Pliny the Elder's encyclopedia of this title, published c."}}, {"canonical_name": "produits", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "produits_organiques", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "produits organiques", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["produits organiques"], "contexts": ["substances derived from living organisms"]}, {"entity_id": "produits", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "produits", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["produits", "produit net"], "contexts": ["goods for trade", "agricultural yields or goods"]}, {"entity_id": "material_products", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "material products", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["material products"], "contexts": ["substances formed by geological processes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "produits organiques", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "produits", "similarity": 0.885}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "produits", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "material products", "similarity": 0.921}], "id": 1688, "stable_key": "clu_4f4cf55296b9f018", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "products", "confidence": "medium", "type": "concept", "note": "This is a very general term. In the geological context, it refers to natural substances formed. In the trade context, it refers to manufactured or gathered goods. The most general referent is 'products'.", "wikidata_id": "Q542929", "wikidata_description": "substance (species) formed from a chemical reaction"}}, {"canonical_name": "sulfate", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "sulfates", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "sulfates", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["sulfates"], "contexts": ["inorganic immediate principles"]}, {"entity_id": "sulfate", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "sulfate", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["sulfate"], "contexts": ["sulfate salt formed", "chemical compound"]}, {"entity_id": "sulfuris", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "sulfuris", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["sulfuris"], "contexts": ["sulfur"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "sulfates", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "sulfate", "similarity": 0.897}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "sulfates", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "sulfuris", "similarity": 0.896}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "sulfate", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "sulfuris", "similarity": 0.935}], "id": 1689, "stable_key": "clu_2bd325ed697ebcd2-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sulfur", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "note": "Brimstone is an archaic term for sulfur.", "wikidata_id": "Q1157552", "wikidata_description": "mineral (as opposed to the chemical element)", "wikipedia_extract": "Sulfuric acid (American spelling and the preferred IUPAC name) or sulphuric acid (Commonwealth spelling), known in antiquity as oil of vitriol, is a mineral acid composed of the elements sulfur, oxygen, and hydrogen, with the molecular formula H2SO4. It is a colorless, odorless, and viscous liquid that is miscible with water. Pure sulfuric acid does not occur naturally due to its strong affinity to water vapor; it is hygroscopic and readily absorbs water vapor from the air. Concentrated sulfuric acid is a strong oxidant with powerful dehydrating properties, making it highly corrosive towards other materials, from rocks to metals. Phosphorus pentoxide is a notable exception in that it is not dehydrated by sulfuric acid but, to the contrary, dehydrates sulfuric acid to sulfur trioxide. Upon addition of sulfuric acid to water, a considerable amount of heat is released; thus, the reverse procedure of adding water to the acid is generally avoided since the heat released may boil the solution, spraying droplets of hot acid during the process.", "wikipedia_url": "https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soufre_%28min%C3%A9ral%29"}}, {"canonical_name": "vascular system", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "vaisseaux_sanguins", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "vaisseaux sanguins", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["vaisseaux sanguins"], "contexts": ["tubes carrying blood"]}, {"entity_id": "blood-vessels", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "blood-vessels", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["blood-vessels"], "contexts": ["tubes carrying blood"]}, {"entity_id": "vascular_system", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "vascular system", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["vascular system"], "contexts": ["system of blood vessels", "network of blood vessels"]}, {"entity_id": "blood-vessels", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "blood-vessels", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["blood-vessels"], "contexts": ["vessels vaguely distinguishable from heart"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "vaisseaux sanguins", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "blood-vessels", "similarity": 0.921}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "vaisseaux sanguins", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "vascular system", "similarity": 0.909}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "vaisseaux sanguins", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "blood-vessels", "similarity": 0.921}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "blood-vessels", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "blood-vessels", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1690, "stable_key": "clu_e38b037de917f195", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Blood vessels", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The descriptions clearly point to the anatomical structures responsible for blood circulation.", "wikidata_id": "Q14864951", "wikidata_description": "The process in which the anatomical structures of blood vessels are generated and organized. The blood vessel is the vasculature carrying blood."}}, {"canonical_name": "modifications climat\u00e9riques", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "modifications_climat\u00e9riques", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "modifications climat\u00e9riques", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["modifications climat\u00e9riques"], "contexts": ["climatic modifications"]}, {"entity_id": "climatal_changes", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "climatal changes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["climatal changes"], "contexts": ["alterations in climate"]}, {"entity_id": "geographical_and_climatal_changes", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "geographical and climatal changes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["geographical and climatal changes"], "contexts": ["alterations in earth's surface and climate"]}, {"entity_id": "atmospheric_changes", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "atmospheric changes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["atmospheric changes"], "contexts": ["predetermination of"]}, {"entity_id": "meteorological_modifications", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "meteorological modifications", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["meteorological modifications"], "contexts": ["changes in atmospheric conditions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "modifications climat\u00e9riques", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "climatal changes", "similarity": 0.967}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "modifications climat\u00e9riques", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "geographical and climatal changes", "similarity": 0.907}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "modifications climat\u00e9riques", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "atmospheric changes", "similarity": 0.905}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "modifications climat\u00e9riques", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "meteorological modifications", "similarity": 0.935}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "climatal changes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "atmospheric changes", "similarity": 0.913}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "climatal changes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "meteorological modifications", "similarity": 0.898}], "id": 1691, "stable_key": "clu_7256d05bf2627635", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "climatic changes", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q260607", "wikidata_description": "process of adjustment to actual or expected climate change and its effects, seeking to moderate or avoid harm or exploit beneficial opportunities"}}, {"canonical_name": "Jussieu", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "de_jussieu", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "de Jussieu", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["de Jussieu"], "contexts": ["introduced natural classification in botany"]}, {"entity_id": "jussieu", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Jussieu", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Jussieu"], "contexts": ["observes M. Richard's sagacity", "on classification"]}, {"entity_id": "a__de_jussieu", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "A. de Jussieu", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["A. de Jussieu"], "contexts": ["remarked on disappearing characters"]}, {"entity_id": "jussieu", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Jussieu", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Jussieu"], "contexts": ["author of Traite de Botanique"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "de Jussieu", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Jussieu", "similarity": 0.947}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "de Jussieu", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "A. de Jussieu", "similarity": 0.923}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "de Jussieu", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Jussieu", "similarity": 0.947}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Jussieu", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Jussieu", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "A. de Jussieu", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Jussieu", "similarity": 0.885}], "id": 1692, "stable_key": "clu_77ccce8b81c05e6a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Antoine Laurent de Jussieu", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1748, "death_year": 1836, "description": "French botanist, established natural classification system", "note": "While 'Jussieu' could refer to other family members, Antoine Laurent is the most prominent botanist fitting the description 'author of Traite de Botanique'.", "wikidata_id": "Q223963", "wikidata_description": "French botanist (1748\u20131836)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antoine%20Laurent%20de%20Jussieu", "wikipedia_extract": "Antoine Laurent de Jussieu (French pronunciation: [\u0251\u0303twan lo\u0281\u0251\u0303 d\u0259 \u0292ysj\u00f8]; 12 April 1748 \u2013 17 September 1836) was a French botanist, notable as the first to publish a natural classification of flowering plants; much of his system remains in use today. His classification was based on an extended unpublished work by his uncle, the botanist Bernard de Jussieu. Jussieu was born in Lyon, France, in 1748, as one of 10 children, to Christophle de Jussieu, an amateur botanist. His father's three younger brothers were also botanists. He went to Paris in 1765 to be with his uncle Bernard and to study medicine, graduating with a doctorate in 1770, with a thesis on animal and vegetable physiology. His uncle introduced him to the Jardin du Roi, where he was appointed as a botany Demonstrator and deputy to L."}}, {"canonical_name": "saline contents", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "mati\u00e8res_salines", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "mati\u00e8res salines", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["mati\u00e8res salines"], "contexts": ["dissolved in organic aqueous media"]}, {"entity_id": "saline_contents", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "saline contents", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["saline contents"], "contexts": ["measure of water density", "salt dissolved in water"]}, {"entity_id": "saline_solution", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "saline solution", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["saline solution"], "contexts": ["solution used in Wollaston's experiment", "solution with salt and water"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "mati\u00e8res salines", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "saline contents", "similarity": 0.898}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "saline contents", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "saline solution", "similarity": 0.883}], "id": 1693, "stable_key": "clu_1be072ff5399e75e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "saline solution", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "wikidata_id": "Q275792", "wikidata_description": "saline water for medical purposes"}}, {"canonical_name": "parties du corps", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "parties_du_corps", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "parties du corps", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["parties du corps"], "contexts": ["general term for body parts"]}, {"entity_id": "bodily_parts", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "bodily parts", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["bodily parts"], "contexts": ["feelings pour in from"]}, {"entity_id": "body_parts", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "body parts", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["body parts"], "contexts": ["components of the human body"]}, {"entity_id": "partesdocorpo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "partesdocorpo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["partesdocorpo"], "contexts": ["parts of the body"]}, {"entity_id": "partes_do_corpo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "partes do corpo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["partes do corpo"], "contexts": ["parts of the body"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "parties du corps", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "bodily parts", "similarity": 0.975}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "parties du corps", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "body parts", "similarity": 0.972}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "parties du corps", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "partesdocorpo", "similarity": 0.961}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "parties du corps", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "partes do corpo", "similarity": 0.987}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "bodily parts", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "body parts", "similarity": 0.982}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "bodily parts", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "partes do corpo", "similarity": 0.971}], "id": 1694, "stable_key": "clu_15f8db1f57883a5a-4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Body", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'Bodies' is used broadly in early modern texts to refer to physical entities, particularly human and animal bodies, in both scientific and philosophical contexts.", "wikidata_id": "Q5", "wikidata_description": "any single member of Homo sapiens, unique extant species of the genus Homo", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human", "wikipedia_extract": "Humans (Homo sapiens, meaning \"thinking man\" or \"wise man\") are the most common and widespread species of primate, and the only extant species of the genus Homo. They are great apes characterized by hairlessness, obligate bipedality, manual dexterity with opposable thumbs, precision grip, and high intelligence. Humans have large brains compared to body size (a high encephalization quotient), enabling more advanced cognitive skills that facilitate successful adaptation to varied environments, development of sophisticated tools, and formation of complex social structures and civilizations. Humans possess a disproportionately larger volume of both cerebral white matter and gray matter present in the prefrontal cortex (PFC) than any other primate species, which facilitated the expansion of higher-order executive functions. Humans are highly social, with individual humans tending to belong to a multi-layered network of distinct social groups \u2013 from families and peer groups to corporations and political states. As such, social interactions between humans have established a wide variety of values, social norms, languages, and traditions (collectively termed institutions), each of which bolsters human society.", "description": "The physical structure of a person or animal."}}, {"canonical_name": "inflammatione", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "inflammation", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "inflammation", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["inflammation"], "contexts": ["medical term, meaning derived from phenomena"]}, {"entity_id": "inflammation", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "inflammation", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["inflammation"], "contexts": ["bodily response to injury"]}, {"entity_id": "inflammatione", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "inflammatione", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["inflammatione"], "contexts": ["a medical condition of swelling", "inflammation, medical condition"]}, {"entity_id": "inflammationes", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "inflammationes", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["inflammationes"], "contexts": ["inflammations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "inflammation", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "inflammation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "inflammation", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "inflammatione", "similarity": 0.992}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "inflammation", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "inflammationes", "similarity": 0.98}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "inflammation", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "inflammatione", "similarity": 0.992}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "inflammation", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "inflammationes", "similarity": 0.98}], "id": 1695, "stable_key": "clu_5375f765aba08d2d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Inflammation", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Inflammation", "wikidata_id": "Q14819849", "wikidata_description": "immediate defensive reaction (by vertebrate tissue) to infection or injury caused by chemical or physical agents, characterized by local vasodilation, extravasation of plasma into intercellular spaces and accumulation of white blood cells"}}, {"canonical_name": "Oak", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "oak", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Oak", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Oak"], "contexts": ["tree admired by Druids for mistletoe", "type of tree"]}, {"entity_id": "oak-tree", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "oak-tree", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 1, "variants": ["oak-tree"], "contexts": ["species of tree"]}, {"entity_id": "oak", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "oak", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 1, "variants": ["oak"], "contexts": ["broad-leaved tree"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Oak", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "oak-tree", "similarity": 0.915}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Oak", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "oak", "similarity": 0.946}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "oak-tree", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "oak", "similarity": 0.969}], "id": 1696, "stable_key": "clu_363000d95e00cbb0-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Oak", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "family": "Fagaceae", "note": "'pedunculated oaks' refers to Quercus robur.", "wikidata_id": "Q12004", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "linnaean": "Quercus", "description": "A large tree known for its wood and acorns, with medicinal uses.", "wikipedia_extract": "An oak is a hardwood tree or shrub in the genus Quercus of the beech family. They have spirally arranged leaves, often with lobed edges, and a nut called an acorn, borne within a cup. The genus is widely distributed in the Northern Hemisphere, with some 500 species, both deciduous and evergreen. Fossil oaks date back to the Middle Eocene. Molecular phylogeny shows that the genus is divided into Old World and New World clades, but many oak species hybridise freely, making the genus's history difficult to resolve. Ecologically, oaks are keystone species in habitats from Mediterranean semi-desert to subtropical rainforest."}}, {"canonical_name": "Etymologies", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "etymologies", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Etymologies", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Etymologies"], "contexts": ["work by Isidore on word origins", "study of word origins and meanings"]}, {"entity_id": "etymology", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "etymology", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["etymology"], "contexts": ["study of word origins"]}, {"entity_id": "etymologia", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "etymologia", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["etymologia"], "contexts": ["study of word origins"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Etymologies", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "etymology", "similarity": 0.925}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Etymologies", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "etymologia", "similarity": 0.941}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "etymology", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "etymologia", "similarity": 0.994}], "id": 1697, "stable_key": "clu_02fb5f301e5a46c3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Etymology", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q35245", "wikidata_description": "study of the history of words, their origins, and how their form and meaning have changed over time", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etymology", "wikipedia_extract": "Etymology ( ET-ih-MOL-\u0259-jee) is the study of the origin and evolution of words\u2014including their constituent units of sound and meaning\u2014across time. In the 21st century, as a subfield within linguistics, etymology has become an increasingly rigorous scientific field of study. It is most directly tied to historical linguistics, philology, and semiotics, and additionally draws upon comparative semantics, morphology, pragmatics, and phonetics in order to construct a comprehensive and chronological catalogue of all meanings that a word (and its related parts) has carried throughout its history. The origin of any particular word is also known as its etymology. For languages with a long written history, etymologists make use of texts, particularly texts about the language itself, to gather knowledge about how words were used during earlier periods, how they developed in meaning and form, or when and how they entered the language. Etymologists also apply the methods of comparative linguistics to reconstruct information about forms that are too old for any direct information to be available."}}, {"canonical_name": "visible rayes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "visible_rayes", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "visible rayes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["visible rayes"], "contexts": ["beams of light", "rays from eyes carrying poison"]}, {"entity_id": "rays_of_light", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "rays of light", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["rays of light"], "contexts": ["electromagnetic radiation visible to the eye"]}, {"entity_id": "visible_rays", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "visible rays", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["visible rays"], "contexts": ["rays detectable by sight", "part of the spectrum visible to the eye"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "visible rayes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "rays of light", "similarity": 0.913}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "visible rayes", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "visible rays", "similarity": 0.998}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "rays of light", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "visible rays", "similarity": 0.917}], "id": 1698, "stable_key": "clu_1409084ec56441ec", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Visible light", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q76299", "wikidata_description": "portion of the electromagnetic spectrum that is visible to the human eye", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Visible%20spectrum", "wikipedia_extract": "The visible spectrum is the band of the electromagnetic spectrum that is visible to the human eye. Electromagnetic radiation in this range of wavelengths is called visible light (or simply light). The optical spectrum is sometimes considered to be the same as the visible spectrum, but some authors define the term more broadly, to include the ultraviolet and infrared parts of the electromagnetic spectrum as well, known collectively as optical radiation. A typical human eye will respond to wavelengths from about 380 to about 750 nanometers. In terms of frequency, this corresponds to a band in the vicinity of 400\u2013790 terahertz. These boundaries are not sharply defined and may vary per individual."}}, {"canonical_name": "sulphureous", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "sulphureous", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "sulphureous", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["sulphureous"], "contexts": ["exhalations in clouds", "subterraneous veins"]}, {"entity_id": "sulphureous", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "sulphureous", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["sulphureous"], "contexts": ["describing turbid water"]}, {"entity_id": "sulphate", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "sulphate", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["sulphate"], "contexts": ["salt formed by acid and zinc", "salt of a metal"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "sulphureous", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "sulphureous", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "sulphureous", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "sulphate", "similarity": 0.919}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "sulphureous", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "sulphate", "similarity": 0.919}], "id": 1699, "stable_key": "clu_0b950bd0e8ac7725-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sulfurous acid", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "note": "While 'sulfuric acid' is also mentioned, the context of 'gaseous compound' and 'exhalations' points more strongly to sulfurous acid (H2SO3) as it is a weaker acid and can exist in gaseous form, whereas sulfuric acid (H2SO4) is a stronger acid typically encountered in liquid form or as a mist when concentrated.", "wikidata_id": "Q5282", "wikidata_description": "chemical compound", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sulfur%20dioxide", "wikipedia_extract": "Sulfur dioxide (IUPAC-recommended spelling) or sulphur dioxide (traditional Commonwealth English) is the chemical compound with the formula SO2. It is a colorless gas with a pungent smell that is responsible for the odor of burnt matches. It is released naturally by volcanic activity and is produced as a by-product of metals refining and the burning of sulfur-bearing hydrocarbon fuels. Sulfur dioxide is somewhat toxic to humans, although only when inhaled in relatively large quantities for a period of several minutes or more. It was known to medieval alchemists as \"volatile spirit of sulfur\". SO2 is a bent molecule with C2v symmetry point group."}}, {"canonical_name": "Johnson", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "johnson", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Johnson", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Johnson"], "contexts": ["named a stony plant", "names a plant"]}, {"entity_id": "johnson", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Johnson", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Johnson"], "contexts": ["English author on herbs", "author"]}, {"entity_id": "johnson", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Johnson", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Johnson"], "contexts": ["identified 'Mort-de-chien' with cholera"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Johnson", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Johnson", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Johnson", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Johnson", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Johnson", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Johnson", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1700, "stable_key": "clu_a8351ae4bfd681f4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "John Parkinson", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1567, "death_year": 1650, "description": "English botanist and apothecary, author of 'Theatrum Botanicum'", "note": "The 'Johnson' in 'pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646' likely refers to John Parkinson, given Browne's known interactions with him and Parkinson's botanical works. The 'English physician' context also aligns. The 'coloquios_da_orta_1563' entry is less certain but could refer to a different, lesser-known Johnson or a mistranscription.", "wikidata_id": "Q112659324", "wikidata_description": "British diplomat and plant collector (c. 1772-1847)"}}, {"canonical_name": "cubit", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "cubit", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "cubit", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["cubit"], "contexts": ["part of the arm", "forearm bone, measured from elbow"]}, {"entity_id": "cubiti", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "cubiti", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["cubiti"], "contexts": ["ancient unit of length"]}, {"entity_id": "cubito", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "cubito", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["cubito"], "contexts": ["elbow, site for bloodletting", "elbow"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "cubit", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "cubiti", "similarity": 0.915}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "cubit", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cubito", "similarity": 0.975}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "cubiti", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cubito", "similarity": 0.925}], "id": 1701, "stable_key": "clu_c7c0f724c04543bb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cubit", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers both to the anatomical part (forearm) and the ancient unit of length."}}, {"canonical_name": "Hieroglyphical", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "hieroglyphical", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Hieroglyphical", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Hieroglyphical"], "contexts": ["original form of the conceit", "ancient Egyptian writing system"]}, {"entity_id": "hieroglyphicks", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Hieroglyphicks", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hieroglyphicks"], "contexts": ["Egyptian picture writing system"]}, {"entity_id": "hieroglyphics", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Hieroglyphics", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hieroglyphics"], "contexts": ["interpreted by astronomy"]}, {"entity_id": "hieroglyphics", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "hieroglyphics", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["hieroglyphics"], "contexts": ["writing system of ancient Egypt"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Hieroglyphical", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Hieroglyphics", "similarity": 0.94}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Hieroglyphical", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "hieroglyphics", "similarity": 0.92}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Hieroglyphicks", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Hieroglyphics", "similarity": 0.955}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Hieroglyphicks", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "hieroglyphics", "similarity": 0.948}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Hieroglyphics", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "hieroglyphics", "similarity": 0.987}], "id": 1702, "stable_key": "clu_c13e29a82c8d6def", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hieroglyphics", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q846016", "wikidata_description": "American hip hop collective", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hieroglyphics%20%28group%29", "wikipedia_extract": "Hieroglyphics, also known as the Hieroglyphics Crew and Hiero, is an American underground hip hop collective based in Oakland, California. The group includes founder Del the Funky Homosapien, fellow rappers Casual, Pep Love, and all four members of the group Souls of Mischief\u2014Phesto, A-Plus, Opio, and Tajai\u2014as well as DJ Toure and producer/manager Domino. Since their inception, Hieroglyphics have found a following largely through their members, podcasts (\"Hierocasts\"), and promotion through their website. The collective uses a three-eyed, straight-lipped face logo that figures prominently on their albums, website, stickers, and clothing. As a collective, the Hieroglyphics have released three studio albums: 3rd Eye Vision in 1998, Full Circle in 2003, and The Kitchen in 2013. In 2005, the collective released a live DVD and accompanying CD of the Hiero's 2003 Full Circle Tour."}}, {"canonical_name": "delusions", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "delusion", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Delusion", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Delusion"], "contexts": ["state of being deceived or misled"]}, {"entity_id": "ilusos", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "ilusos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["ilusos"], "contexts": ["a type of flux or discharge"]}, {"entity_id": "delusions", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "delusions", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 3, "variants": ["delusions"], "contexts": ["false beliefs held despite evidence", "fixed false beliefs"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Delusion", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "ilusos", "similarity": 0.909}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Delusion", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "delusions", "similarity": 0.939}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "ilusos", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "delusions", "similarity": 0.895}], "id": 1703, "stable_key": "clu_5c7780e06fee8c76", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hallucination and Delusion", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Hallucination and Delusion", "note": "This cluster includes both perceiving something not present (hallucination) and false beliefs (delusion), which are distinct but often co-occurring psychiatric symptoms."}}, {"canonical_name": "diable", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "devill", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Devill", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Devill"], "contexts": ["biblical antagonist"]}, {"entity_id": "diable", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "diable", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Diable", "diable"], "contexts": ["name of a geological formation", "devil, mentioned as a flaw of the women"]}, {"entity_id": "diabo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "diabo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["diabo"], "contexts": ["figure associated with devil", "influence on name change"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Devill", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "diable", "similarity": 0.952}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "diable", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "diabo", "similarity": 0.956}], "id": 1704, "stable_key": "clu_e3b2bceffbf7104e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Devil", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This refers to the theological or mythological concept of the Devil, not a specific historical person.", "wikidata_id": "Q6674", "wikidata_description": "genus of flowering plants in the buttercup family Ranunculaceae"}}, {"canonical_name": "anger", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "anger", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "anger", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["anger"], "contexts": ["strong feeling of displeasure"]}, {"entity_id": "angry", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "angry", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["angry"], "contexts": ["feeling of strong displeasure"]}, {"entity_id": "anger", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "anger", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["anger"], "contexts": ["strong feeling of displeasure", "feeling of displeasure and hostility"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "anger", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "angry", "similarity": 0.925}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "anger", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "anger", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "angry", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "anger", "similarity": 0.925}], "id": 1705, "stable_key": "clu_6dde21a2760f44cc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Anger", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anger", "wikipedia_extract": "Anger is an intense emotional state involving a strong, uncomfortable and non-cooperative response to a perceived provocation, hurt, or threat. A person experiencing anger will often experience physical effects, such as increased heart rate, elevated blood pressure, and increased levels of the stress hormones adrenaline and noradrenaline. Some view anger as an emotion that triggers part of the fight or flight response. Anger becomes the predominant feeling behaviorally, cognitively, and physiologically when a person makes the conscious choice to take action to immediately stop the threatening behavior of another outside force. Anger can have many physical and mental consequences. The external expression of anger can be found in facial expressions, body language, physiological responses, and at times public acts of aggression."}}, {"canonical_name": "Arithmetica", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "arithmetick", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Arithmetick", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Arithmetick"], "contexts": ["branch of mathematics"]}, {"entity_id": "arithmetica", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Arithmetica", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Arithmetica", "Mathematica"], "contexts": ["arithmetic, used as a metaphor for counting", "art not necessary for healing"]}, {"entity_id": "arithmetic", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "arithmetic", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["arithmetic"], "contexts": ["rules of numerical relations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Arithmetick", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Arithmetica", "similarity": 0.96}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Arithmetick", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "arithmetic", "similarity": 0.976}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Arithmetica", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "arithmetic", "similarity": 0.979}], "id": 1706, "stable_key": "clu_e671e16d5debbba0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Arithmetic", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q11205", "wikidata_description": "elementary branch of mathematics"}}, {"canonical_name": "P\u00e9licans", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "pelican", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Pelican", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["Pelican"], "contexts": ["bird used in allegorical arguments"]}, {"entity_id": "pelicans", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "pelicans", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["pelicans"], "contexts": ["birds that ate fish containing seeds"]}, {"entity_id": "p\u00e9licans", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "P\u00e9licans", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 3, "variants": ["P\u00e9licans", "P\u00e9lican brun", "p\u00e9licans"], "contexts": ["birds harried by flamans", "pelican family"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Pelican", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "pelicans", "similarity": 0.894}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "pelicans", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "P\u00e9licans", "similarity": 0.835}], "id": 1707, "stable_key": "clu_ec372eb268f1350d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pelican", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q19413", "wikidata_description": "genus of birds", "wikipedia_extract": "Pelicans (genus Pelecanus) are a genus of large water birds that make up the family Pelecanidae. They are characterized by a long beak and a large throat pouch used for catching prey and draining water from the scooped-up contents before swallowing. They have predominantly pale plumage, except for the brown and Peruvian pelicans. The bills, pouches, and bare facial skin of all pelicans become brightly coloured before the breeding season. The eight living pelican species have a patchy, seasonally-dependent yet global distribution, ranging latitudinally from the tropics to the temperate zone. Pelicans are absent from interior Amazonian South America, from polar regions and the open ocean; at least one species is known to migrate to the inland desert of Australia's Red Centre, after heavy rains create temporary lakes.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pelican"}}, {"canonical_name": "discord", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "discord", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "discord", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["discord"], "contexts": ["negative quality, from evil principle"]}, {"entity_id": "discord", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "discord", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["discord"], "contexts": ["lack of harmony or agreement", "negative relation to topic"]}, {"entity_id": "discords", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "discords", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["discords"], "contexts": ["disagreeable sound from unrelated vibrations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "discord", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "discord", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "discord", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "discords", "similarity": 0.864}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "discord", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "discords", "similarity": 0.864}], "id": 1708, "stable_key": "clu_46581606f0e0902c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Discord", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q5281696", "wikidata_description": "genus of insects"}}, {"canonical_name": "winds", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "winds", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "winds", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["winds"], "contexts": ["moving air"]}, {"entity_id": "wind", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Wind", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Wind"], "contexts": ["word suggested by 'Sturm'"]}, {"entity_id": "winds", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "winds", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["winds"], "contexts": ["moving air with no regularity", "atmospheric currents transporting vapours"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "winds", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Wind", "similarity": 0.969}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "winds", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "winds", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Wind", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "winds", "similarity": 0.969}], "id": 1709, "stable_key": "clu_d93a3972abb3fc67", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Wind", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "References to moving air, with one instance potentially linking to the German word 'Sturm' (storm).", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind", "wikipedia_extract": "Wind is the natural movement of air or other gases relative to a planet's surface. Winds occur on a range of scales, from thunderstorm flows lasting tens of minutes, to local breezes generated by heating of land surfaces and lasting a few hours, to global winds resulting from the difference in absorption of solar energy between the climate zones on Earth. The study of wind is called anemology. The two main causes of large-scale atmospheric circulation are the differential heating between the equator and the poles, and the rotation of the planet, which is called the Coriolis effect. Within the tropics and subtropics, thermal low circulations over terrain and high plateaus can drive monsoon circulations. In coastal areas the sea breeze/land breeze cycle can define local winds; in areas that have variable terrain, mountain and valley breezes can prevail."}}, {"canonical_name": "p\u00e9trifications", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "petrifications", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "petrifications", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["petrifications"], "contexts": ["organic matter turned to stone"]}, {"entity_id": "p\u00e9trifications", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "p\u00e9trifications", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["p\u00e9trifications"], "contexts": ["fossilized remains found in mountains"]}, {"entity_id": "p\u00e9trifications", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "p\u00e9trifications", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["p\u00e9trifications"], "contexts": ["fossilized remains", "fossils"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "petrifications", "target_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "target_name": "p\u00e9trifications", "similarity": 0.925}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "petrifications", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "p\u00e9trifications", "similarity": 0.883}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "p\u00e9trifications", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "p\u00e9trifications", "similarity": 0.886}], "id": 1710, "stable_key": "clu_4ad4c516905284c5-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Fossils", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Early modern texts often used 'p\u00e9trifications' to refer to organic matter that had been mineralized or fossilized, including things like fossilized bones and shells.", "wikidata_id": "Q126956840", "wikidata_description": "fictional disease from Lies of P"}}, {"canonical_name": "Tropick", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "tropick", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Tropick", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Tropick"], "contexts": ["lines of latitude near equator"]}, {"entity_id": "tropiques", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "tropiques", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["tropiques"], "contexts": ["latitudinal lines near the equator"]}, {"entity_id": "tropique", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "tropique", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["tropique"], "contexts": ["tropical region"]}, {"entity_id": "oc\u00e9an_tropical", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Oc\u00e9an tropical", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Oc\u00e9an tropical"], "contexts": ["ocean region"]}, {"entity_id": "zones_tropicales", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "zones tropicales", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["zones tropicales"], "contexts": ["climatic regions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Tropick", "target_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "target_name": "tropiques", "similarity": 0.894}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Tropick", "target_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "target_name": "tropique", "similarity": 0.914}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "tropiques", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Oc\u00e