"similarity": 0.896}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Northern Ocean", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Southern Ocean", "similarity": 0.896}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Southern Ocean", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Southern Ocean", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1712, "stable_key": "clu_30c518faa181659e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Arctic Ocean", "confidence": "medium", "type": "place", "country": "International", "note": "The context mentions 'Northern Ocean' and then contrasts it with 'Southern Ocean'. While 'Northern Ocean' could refer to the Atlantic or Pacific in some historical contexts, the presence of 'Southern Ocean' strongly suggests the dichotomy of polar oceans. The mention of the 'Southern Ocean' south of the Pacific solidifies the interpretation of 'Northern Ocean' as the Arctic.", "wikidata_id": "Q7354", "wikidata_description": "polar region on the Earth's northern hemisphere"}}, {"canonical_name": "Poplar", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "poplar", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Poplar", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Poplar"], "contexts": ["tree whose gums form concretion"]}, {"entity_id": "poplar", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Poplar", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Poplar", "Poplar-Tree"], "contexts": ["tree dedicated to Hercules", "tree with two common sorts"]}, {"entity_id": "poplars", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "poplars", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 2, "variants": ["poplars"], "contexts": ["abundant in middle tertiary period", "tree species in ancient forests"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Poplar", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Poplar", "similarity": 0.878}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Poplar", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "poplars", "similarity": 0.919}], "id": 1713, "stable_key": "clu_c137951004a0b5e9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Poplar", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Populus spp.", "family": "Salicaceae", "wikipedia_extract": "Populus nigra, the black poplar, is a species of cottonwood poplar, the type species of section Aigeiros of the genus Populus, native to Europe, southwest and central Asia, and northwest Africa. Black poplars are medium- to large-sized deciduous trees, reaching 20\u201330 m, and rarely 40 m tall. Their leaves are diamond-shaped to triangular, 5\u20138 cm long and 6\u20138 cm broad, and green on both surfaces. Normally, their trunks achieve up to 1.5 m in diameter, but some unusual individual trees in France have grown old enough to have much larger trunks \u2013 more than 3 metres DBH (Diameter at Breast Height). The species is dioecious (male and female flowers are on different plants), with flowers in catkins and pollination achieved by the wind. The black poplar grows in low-lying areas of moist ground.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Populus_nigra"}}, {"canonical_name": "rubies", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "rubies", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Rubies", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rubies"], "contexts": ["precious red gemstone"]}, {"entity_id": "rubies", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "rubies", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["rubies"], "contexts": ["gem variety of corundum", "mineral rubies"]}, {"entity_id": "rubies", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "rubies", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["rubies", "Rubies"], "contexts": ["precious stones from mines", "diamonds, rubies, and other precious things"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Rubies", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "rubies", "similarity": 0.903}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Rubies", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "rubies", "similarity": 0.903}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "rubies", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "rubies", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1714, "stable_key": "clu_a90b385c48351aac", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ruby", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q43088", "wikidata_description": "variety of corundum, mineral, gemstone", "note": "'Rubim' is the Portuguese for ruby, and 'rame' (in this context, likely a misspelling or variant of 'rubi') and 'precious stone' point to the gem.  The metal context is less likely given the gem identification."}}, {"canonical_name": "medicinas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "medicine", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "medicine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["medicine"], "contexts": ["can intoxicate the head"]}, {"entity_id": "medicinas", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "medicinas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 2, "variants": ["medicinas", "medicinas compue\u017ftas"], "contexts": ["general term for medicinal preparations", "compound medicines"]}, {"entity_id": "medicine", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "medicine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 2, "variants": ["medicine"], "contexts": ["practice influenced by anatomy and physiology", "described by physician"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "medicine", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "medicinas", "similarity": 0.951}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "medicine", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "medicine", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "medicinas", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "medicine", "similarity": 0.951}], "id": 1715, "stable_key": "clu_3773fd7d57f753a1-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Medicine", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the practice of healing and remedies.", "wikidata_id": "Q12140", "wikidata_description": "substance used to diagnose, cure, treat, or prevent disease", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Substance_dependence", "wikipedia_extract": "Substance dependence, also known as drug dependence, is a biopsychological situation whereby an individual's functionality is dependent on the necessitated re-consumption of a psychoactive substance because of an adaptive state that has developed within the individual from psychoactive substance consumption that results in the experience of withdrawal and that necessitates the re-consumption of the drug. A drug addiction, a distinct concept from substance dependence, is defined as compulsive, out-of-control drug use, despite negative consequences. An addictive drug is a drug which is both rewarding and reinforcing. \u0394FosB, a gene transcription factor, which is now known to be a critical component and common factor in the development of virtually all forms of behavioral and drug addictions, but not dependence. The International Classification of Diseases classifies substance dependence as a mental and behavioural disorder. In the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (DSM-5) (released in 2013), substance abuse and substance dependence were eliminated and replaced with the single diagnosis of substance use disorders."}}, {"canonical_name": "trunk", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "trunk", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Trunk", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Trunk"], "contexts": ["main part of the body"]}, {"entity_id": "trunk", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "trunk", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 3, "variants": ["trunk"], "contexts": ["main part of the body", "central part of the body"]}, {"entity_id": "trunk", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "trunk", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["trunk"], "contexts": ["torso of the body"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Trunk", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "trunk", "similarity": 0.739}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Trunk", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "trunk", "similarity": 0.739}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "trunk", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "trunk", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1716, "stable_key": "clu_6dd21e4d6c6b79af", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "torso", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q160695", "wikidata_description": "central part of the living body", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Torso", "wikipedia_extract": "The torso or trunk is an anatomical term for the central part, or the core, of the body of many animals (including human beings), from which the head, neck, limbs, tail and other appendages extend. The tetrapod torso \u2014 including that of a human \u2014 can be divided into segments: The chest or upper torso. This section is also called the thorax or the thoracic region. This is where the forelimbs extend. The abdomen. The abdomenal section is also known as the \"mid-section\" or midriff."}}, {"canonical_name": "ardente", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "fiery", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "fiery", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["fiery"], "contexts": ["bodily quality"]}, {"entity_id": "ardente", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "ardente", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["ardente"], "contexts": ["quality of pepper"]}, {"entity_id": "fogo_brando", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "fogo brando", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["fogo brando"], "contexts": ["method of cooking decoction", "gentle fire"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "fiery", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "ardente", "similarity": 0.91}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "fiery", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "fogo brando", "similarity": 0.912}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "ardente", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "fogo brando", "similarity": 0.909}], "id": 1717, "stable_key": "clu_280c427c832a86a2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "mild heat", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Contexts suggest both a bodily quality and a method of preparation.", "wikidata_id": "Q42503243", "wikidata_description": "scientific article"}}, {"canonical_name": "vexiga", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 3, "