ecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "force d'attraction", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "attracting power", "similarity": 0.927}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "force d'attraction", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "chemical force of attraction", "similarity": 0.918}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "forces of attraction", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "chemical force of attraction", "similarity": 0.905}], "id": 1823, "stable_key": "clu_ed1433902402b67f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Forces of attraction", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This broadly encompasses forces like gravity and chemical affinity, as understood in early modern physics and chemistry.", "wikidata_id": "Q125851866", "wikidata_description": "episode of The Bureau of Magical Things (S1 E13)"}}, {"canonical_name": "materialists", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "mat\u00e9rialistes", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "mat\u00e9rialistes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["mat\u00e9rialistes"], "contexts": ["those who believe phenomena reducible to physical conditions"]}, {"entity_id": "materialists", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "materialists", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["materialists"], "contexts": ["philosophy that matter is fundamental reality", "philosophical group emphasizing material basis"]}, {"entity_id": "materialist", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Materialist", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Materialist"], "contexts": ["adherent of materialism"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "mat\u00e9rialistes", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "materialists", "similarity": 0.941}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "mat\u00e9rialistes", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Materialist", "similarity": 0.858}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "materialists", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Materialist", "similarity": 0.887}], "id": 1824, "stable_key": "clu_a3b5dce654d8e550-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "materialism", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q7081", "wikidata_description": "form of philosophical monism which holds matter to be the fundamental substance in nature", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Materialism", "wikipedia_extract": "In philosophy and metaphysics, materialism is a form of monism holding that matter is the fundamental substance of nature, so that all things, including mind and consciousness, arise from material interactions and depend on physical processes, including those of the human brain and nervous system. It contrasts with monistic idealism, which treats consciousness as fundamental, and is related to naturalism, the view that only natural laws and forces operate in the universe, and to physicalism, the view that all that exists is ultimately physical. Physicalism extends materialism by including forms of physicality beyond ordinary matter (e.g. spacetime, energy, forces, exotic matter), and some use the terms interchangeably. Alternative philosophies opposed or alternative to materialism or physicalism include idealism, pluralism, dualism, solipsism, panpsychism, and other forms of monism. Materialism is the philosophical doctrine that matter has a primary position in the nature of the world, with mind or consciousness emerging as a secondary, dependent reality or not existing at all."}}, {"canonical_name": "substances min\u00e9rales", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "mati\u00e8res_min\u00e9rales", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "mati\u00e8res min\u00e9rales", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["mati\u00e8res min\u00e9rales"], "contexts": ["non-living inorganic substances"]}, {"entity_id": "substances_min\u00e9rales", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "substances min\u00e9rales", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["substances min\u00e9rales"], "contexts": ["minerals separated during cooling", "minerals heated by earth's heat"]}, {"entity_id": "mineral_substances", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "mineral substances", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["mineral substances"], "contexts": ["substances formed by nature"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "mati\u00e8res min\u00e9rales", "target_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "target_name": "substances min\u00e9rales", "similarity": 0.978}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "mati\u00e8res min\u00e9rales", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "mineral substances", "similarity": 0.943}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "substances min\u00e9rales", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "mineral substances", "similarity": 0.969}], "id": 1825, "stable_key": "clu_684025f2f518eba0-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Minerals", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "This cluster clearly refers to inorganic solid substances studied in natural philosophy and medicine, aligning directly with the modern scientific definition of minerals.", "wikidata_id": "Q7946", "wikidata_description": "naturally occurring usually inorganic substance that has a (more or less) definite chemical composition and a crystal structure", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mineral", "wikipedia_extract": "In geology and mineralogy, a mineral or mineral species is, broadly speaking, a solid substance with a fairly well-defined chemical composition and a specific crystal structure that occurs naturally in pure form. The geological definition of mineral normally excludes compounds that occur only in living organisms. However, some minerals are often biogenic (such as calcite) or chemically organic compounds (such as mellite). Moreover, living organisms often synthesize inorganic minerals (such as hydroxylapatite) that also occur in rocks. The concept of mineral is distinct from rock, which is any bulk solid geologic material that is relatively homogeneous at a large enough scale. A rock may consist of one type of mineral or may be an aggregate of two or more different types of minerals, spacially segregated into distinct phases."}}, {"canonical_name": "law of nature", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "loi_de_la_nature", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "loi de la nature", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["loi de la nature"], "contexts": ["fundamental principles governing the universe"]}, {"entity_id": "law_of_nature", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "law of nature", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["law of nature"], "contexts": ["fundamental principle of the universe"]}, {"entity_id": "law_of_nature", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "law of nature", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Law of Nature", "law of nature"], "contexts": ["undulatory theory as", "fundamental principle of the universe"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "loi de la nature", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "law of nature", "similarity": 0.961}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "loi de la nature", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "law of nature", "similarity": 0.961}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "law of nature", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "law of nature", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1826, "stable_key": "clu_10550f3c1ecb86fb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Laws of nature", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q21191188", "wikidata_description": "1st episode of the 3rd season of Agents of S.H.I.E.L.D.", "wikipedia_extract": "Rights of nature or Earth rights is a legal and jurisprudential theory that describes inherent rights as associated with ecosystems and species, similar to the concept of fundamental human rights. The rights of nature concept challenges twentieth-century laws as generally grounded in a flawed frame of nature as \"resource\" to be owned, used, and degraded. Proponents argue that laws grounded in rights of nature direct humanity to act appropriately and in a way consistent with modern, system-based science, which demonstrates that humans and the natural world are fundamentally interconnected. This school of thought is underpinned by two basic lines of reasoning. First, since the recognition of human rights is based in part on the philosophical belief that those rights emanate from humanity's own existence, logically, so too do inherent rights of the natural world arise from the natural world's own existence. A second and more pragmatic argument asserts that the survival of humans depends on healthy ecosystems, and so protection of nature's rights in turn, advances human rights and well-being.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Physical_law"}}, {"canonical_name": "an\u00e6sthetics", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "anesth\u00e9siques", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "anesth\u00e9siques", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["anesth\u00e9siques"], "contexts": ["substances causing loss of sensation"]}, {"entity_id": "an\u00e6sthetics", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "an\u00e6sthetics", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["an\u00e6sthetics"], "contexts": ["substances causing loss of sensation"]}, {"entity_id": "an\u00e6sthetics", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "an\u00e6sthetics", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["an\u00e6sthetics"], "contexts": ["agents causing loss of sensation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "anesth\u00e9siques", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "an\u00e6sthetics", "similarity": 0.917}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "anesth\u00e9siques", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "an\u00e6sthetics", "similarity": 0.917}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "an\u00e6sthetics", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "an\u00e6sthetics", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 1827, "stable_key": "clu_7a363e2cbeaf7dee", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Anesthetics", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "wikidata_id": "Q50430271", "wikidata_description": "use of two or more substances simultaneously or sequentially to induce anesthesia.", "wikipedia_extract": "An anesthetic (American English) or anaesthetic (British English; see spelling differences) is a drug used to induce anesthesia\u2009\u2060\u2014\u2009\u2060in other words, to result in a temporary loss of sensation or awareness. They may be divided into two broad classes: general anesthetics, which result in a reversible loss of consciousness, and local anesthetics, which cause a reversible loss of sensation for a limited region of the body without necessarily affecting consciousness. A wide variety of drugs are used in modern anesthetic practice. Many are rarely used outside anesthesiology, but others are used commonly in various fields of healthcare. Combinations of anesthetics are sometimes used for their synergistic and additive therapeutic effects. Adverse effects, however, may also be increased."}}, {"canonical_name": "Carus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 3, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "carus", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "Carus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Carus"], "contexts": ["physician and philosopher"]}, {"entity_id": "carus", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Carus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Carus"], "contexts": ["author of 'Von den Urtheilen des Knochen und Schalen Gerustes'", "author of a scientific work"]}, {"entity_id": "carus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Carus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Carus"], "contexts": ["author on universal wisdom"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "Carus", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Carus", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "Carus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Carus", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_hum