rated and organized. Arteries are blood vessels that transport blood from the heart to the body and its organs."}}, {"canonical_name": "Nodes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 19, "members": [{"entity_id": "nodal_point", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "nodal point", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["nodal point"], "contexts": ["point of no vibration on a string"]}, {"entity_id": "nodes", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Nodes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 17, "variants": ["Nodes", "nodes"], "contexts": ["intersection points of orbits", "points where orbit intersects ecliptic"]}, {"entity_id": "nodal_points", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "nodal points", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["nodal points"], "contexts": ["points of rest on a vibrating string"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "nodal point", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Nodes", "similarity": 0.885}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "nodal point", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "nodal points", "similarity": 0.977}], "id": 2438, "stable_key": "clu_6fb8b7f1749025f3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Nodes", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This refers to points of zero amplitude in a vibrating system, or intersection points, particularly in physics and mathematics, not anatomical nodes."}}, {"canonical_name": "meteoric stones", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 19, "members": [{"entity_id": "meteoric_stones", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "meteoric stones", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 17, "variants": ["meteoric stones"], "contexts": ["stones from meteorites", "stones from space"]}, {"entity_id": "meteorites", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Meteorites", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Meteorites"], "contexts": ["rocks from space falling to Earth"]}, {"entity_id": "meteoric_stones", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Meteoric stones", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Meteoric stones"], "contexts": ["foreign origin proofs"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "meteoric stones", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Meteorites", "similarity": 0.919}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "meteoric stones", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Meteoric stones", "similarity": 0.979}], "id": 2439, "stable_key": "clu_1accce3775f1c3ef-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "meteorites", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "The term 'aerufegi' appears to be an OCR error or very obscure variant.", "wikidata_id": "Q18104262", "wikidata_description": "genus of insects"}}, {"canonical_name": "Halley", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 19, "members": [{"entity_id": "halley", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Halley", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 11, "variants": ["Halley"], "contexts": ["discovered comet with long period", "pronounced fire-ball of 1686 a cosmical body"]}, {"entity_id": "halley", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Halley", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 8, "variants": ["Halley"], "contexts": ["computed comet orbit elements", "associated with the comet's name"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Halley", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Halley", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2440, "stable_key": "clu_56641df13be70c95", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Edmond Halley", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1656, "death_year": 1742, "description": "English astronomer, mathematician, and physicist known for Halley's Comet.", "wikidata_id": "Q19059026", "wikidata_description": "street in Amsterdam, the Netherlands"}}, {"canonical_name": "Herschel", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 19, "members": [{"entity_id": "herschel", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Herschel", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 10, "variants": ["Herschel"], "contexts": ["traced a map of the world", "compared comets to nebulous stars"]}, {"entity_id": "herschel", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Herschel", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 8, "variants": ["Herschel"], "contexts": ["measured height of Etna by barometer", "assigned cause of atmospheric anomalies"]}, {"entity_id": "herchel", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Herchel", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Herchel"], "contexts": ["name correction in transcription notes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Herschel", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Herschel", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Herschel", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Herchel", "similarity": 0.946}], "id": 2441, "stable_key": "clu_7704807617099028-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "John Herschel", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1792, "death_year": 1871, "description": "Astronomer and mathematician, son of William Herschel.", "wikidata_id": "Q14278", "wikidata_description": "English polymath, mathematician, astronomer, chemist, inventor and photographer (*1792 \u2013 \u20201871)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John%20Herschel", "note": "The context of 'connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858' and discussions of solar phenomena strongly point to Sir John Herschel.", "wikipedia_extract": "Sir John Frederick William Herschel, 1st Baronet  (; 7 March 1792 \u2013 11 May 1871) was an English polymath active as a mathematician, astronomer, chemist, inventor and experimental photographer who invented the blueprint and did botanical work. Herschel originated the use of the Julian day system in astronomy. He named seven moons of Saturn and four moons of Uranus \u2013 the seventh planet, discovered by his father Sir William Herschel. He made many contributions to the science of photography, and investigated colour blindness and the chemical power of ultraviolet rays. His Preliminary Discourse (1831), which advocated an inductive approach to scientific experiment and theory-building, was an important contribution to the philosophy of science. Herschel was born in Slough, Buckinghamshire, the son of Mary Baldwin and astronomer Sir William Herschel."}}, {"canonical_name": "espicanardo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 19, "members": [{"entity_id": "espiquenardo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "espiquenardo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 8, "variants": ["espiquenardo", "ESPIQUENARDO", "Espiquernardo"], "contexts": ["plant with a strong smell", "plant with a strong scent, likely spikenard"]}, {"entity_id": "espicanardo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "espicanardo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 11, "variants": ["espicanardo", "E\u017fpicanardo", "efpicanardo", "e\u017fpicanardo"], "contexts": ["spikenard used in decoction", "spice used in a syrup"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "espiquenardo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "espicanardo", "similarity": 0.914}], "id": 2442, "stable_key": "clu_72e7c16131b83e26", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Spikenard", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "description": "An aromatic herb or shrub, used in perfumery and medicine.", "wikipedia_extract": "Spikenard, also called nard, nardin, and muskroot, is a class of aromatic amber-colored essential oil derived from Nardostachys jatamansi, a flowering plant in the honeysuckle family which grows in the Himalayas of Nepal, China, and India. The oil has been used over centuries as a perfume, a traditional medicine, or in religious ceremonies across a wide territory from India to Europe. Historically, the name nard has also referred to essential oils derived from other species including the closely related valerian genus, as well as Spanish lavender; these cheaper, more common plants have been used in perfume-making, and sometimes to adulterate true spikenard. The name nard is derived from Latin nardus, from Ancient Greek \u03bd\u03ac\u03c1\u03b4\u03bf\u03c2 (n\u00e1rdos), from  Hebrew: \u05e0\u05b5\u05e8\u05b0\u05d3\u05b0\u05bc (n\u0113rd). This word may ultimately derive either from Sanskrit \u0928\u0932\u0926 (n\u00e1lada 'Indian spikenard'), or from Naarda, an ancient Assyrian city (possibly the modern town of Dohuk, Iraq). The \"spike\" in the English name refers to the inflorescence or flowering stem of the plant."}}, {"canonical_name": "beldroegas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 19, "members": [{"entity_id": "beldroega", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "beldroega", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["beldroega", "beldroegas"], "contexts": ["plant name, purslane", "plant with leaves"]}, {"entity_id": "beldroegas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "beldroegas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 16, "variants": ["beldroegas"], "contexts": ["plant used in a syrup", "plant used in decoctions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "beldroega", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "beldroegas", "similarity": 0.975}], "id": 2443, "stable_key": "clu_e674fbdd5d53b87b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Purslane", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Portulaca oleracea", "family": "Portulacaceae", "description": "Edible plant with succulent leaves, used medicinally.", "wikipedia_extract": "Portulaca oleracea (common purslane, also known as little hogweed, or pursley) is a succulent plant in the family Portulacaceae. All parts of the plant are edible raw or cooked. Purslane grows from a taproot with fibrous secondary roots. The plant may reach 40 centimetres (16 inches) in height. It has smooth, reddish, mostly prostrate stems, and the leaves, which may be alternate or opposite, are clustered at stem joints and ends. The yellow flowers have five regular parts and are up to 6 millimetres (1\u20444 inch) wide."}}, {"canonical_name": "Espinhela", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 19, "members": [{"entity_id": "espinhela", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "espinhela", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["espinhela", "espinela"], "contexts": ["a type of ruby, reddish", "mineral species"]}, {"entity_id": "espinhela", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Espinhela", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 17, "variants": ["Espinhela", "Espinbela", "Espinillam", "espinbela", "espinhela"], "contexts": ["body part discussed", "cartilage or soft bone at the end of the chest"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "espinhela", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Espinhela", "similarity": 0.396}], "id": 2444, "stable_key": "clu_b8c7815bf77b57da", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Spinel", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "Early modern texts often used 'espinhela' or 'spinellane' to refer to spinel, a gemstone.", "wikidata_id": "Q191310", "wikidata_description": "oxide mineral", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spinel", "wikipedia_extract": "Spinel () is the magnesium/aluminium member of the larger spinel group of minerals. It has the formula MgAl2O4 in the cubic crystal system. Its name comes from the Latin word spinella, a diminutive form of spine, in reference to its pointed crystals. Spinel crystallizes in the isometric system; common crystal forms are octahedra, usually twinned. It has no true cleavage, but shows an octahedral parting and a conchoidal fracture. Its hardness is 8, its specific gravity is 3.5\u20134.1, and it is transparent to opaque with a vitreous to dull luster."}}, {"canonical_name": "incenfo macho", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 19, "members": [{"entity_id": "encen\u00e7o_macho", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "encen\u00e7o macho", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["encen\u00e7o macho"], "contexts": ["male incense", "a plant called male incense"]}, {"entity_id": "incenfo_macho", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "incenfo macho", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 17, "variants": ["incenfo macho", "Incenso macho", "Incen\u017fo macho", "incenso macho", "incen\u017fo macho", "p\u00f2de incen\u017fo macho"], "contexts": ["male incense", "male incense (likely frankincense)"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "encen\u00e7o macho", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "incenfo macho", "similarity": 0.949}], "id": 2445, "stable_key": "clu_b4c07ea1cafd6b2b-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Frankincense", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Boswellia sacra", "family": "Burseraceae", "note": " 'Incenso' in early modern Portuguese and Italian texts typically refers to frankincense, derived from Boswellia species.", "wikidata_id": "Q133964", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boswellia%20sacra", "wikipedia_extract": "Boswellia sacra, also known as Boswellia carteri and others, and commonly called the frankincense tree or the olibanum tree, is a tree in the genus Boswellia, in the Burseraceae family, from which frankincense, a resinous dried sap, is harvested. The olibanum tree is a plant native to the countries of Oman and Yemen, in the south of the Arabian Peninsula, and to Somalia and Ethiopia, in the Horn of Africa. In 2012, it was replanted in Israel in the Middle East, 1,500 years after dying out there. The Boswellia sacra species is a small deciduous tree that grows to a height of 2 to 8 m (6 ft 7 in to 26 ft 3 in), with one or more trunks. The bark has the texture of paper and can be easily removed. The tree has compound leaves and an odd number of leaflets that grow opposite to one another; the small, yellow-white flowers are gathered in axillary clusters composed of five petals, ten stamens, and a cup with five teeth; new leaves feature a downy covering.", "description": "Resinous tree producing aromatic incense, also used medicinally."}}, {"canonical_name": "Ventofas", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 19, "members": [{"entity_id": "ventosas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "ventosas", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 1, "variants": ["ventosas"], "contexts": ["medical cups for cupping"]}, {"entity_id": "ventofas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Ventofas", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 18, "variants": ["Ventofas", "Ventofas \u017farjadu", "ventofas", "ventofas farjadas"], "contexts": ["cupping glasses used for bleeding", "devices for drawing blood"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "ventosas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Ventofas", "similarity": 0.919}], "id": 2446, "stable_key": "clu_f646926eff6cec25", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cupping glasses", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "description": "Medical instruments used for cupping therapy.", "wikipedia_extract": "Cupping therapy is a pseudoscientific treatment method in which a local suction is created on the skin by using heated cups. As an alternative medicine practice, it is primarily used in Asia, but it is also used in Eastern Europe, the Middle East, and Latin America. There is no conclusive evidence supporting the claimed health benefits of cupping, and critics have characterized the practice as quackery. Cupping practitioners attempt to use cupping therapy for a wide array of medical conditions including fevers, chronic low back pain, poor appetite, indigestion, high blood pressure, acne, atopic dermatitis, psoriasis, anemia, stroke rehabilitation, nasal congestion, infertility, and menstrual period cramping. Despite the numerous ailments for which practitioners claim cupping therapy is useful, there is insufficient evidence demonstrating any health benefits. Cupping is generally not harmful for most people."}}, {"canonical_name": "sciences exp\u00e9rimentales", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "sciences_exp\u00e9rimentales", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "sciences exp\u00e9rimentales", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 17, "variants": ["sciences exp\u00e9rimentales"], "contexts": ["sciences based on experimentation", "experimental sciences"]}, {"entity_id": "experimental_sciences", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "experimental sciences", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["experimental sciences"], "contexts": ["sciences based on observation and experiment"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "sciences exp\u00e9rimentales", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "experimental sciences", "similarity": 0.959}], "id": 2447, "stable_key": "clu_15e28faa0830604c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "experimental science", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q129565731", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article"}}, {"canonical_name": "Pancreas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "pancr\u00e9as", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "pancr\u00e9as", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 3, "variants": ["pancr\u00e9as"], "contexts": ["gland behind stomach", "gland in the digestive system"]}, {"entity_id": "canal_pancr\u00e9atique", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "canal pancr\u00e9atique", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["canal pancr\u00e9atique"], "contexts": ["duct carrying pancreatic juice"]}, {"entity_id": "conduit_pancr\u00e9atique", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "conduit pancr\u00e9atique", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["conduit pancr\u00e9atique"], "contexts": ["pancreatic duct involved in ligature experiments"]}, {"entity_id": "pancreas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Pancreas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 13, "variants": ["Pancreas", "Pancreatico", "pancreas", "pancreatico"], "contexts": ["pancreas", "gland involved in digestion"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "pancr\u00e9as", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Pancreas", "similarity": 0.975}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "canal pancr\u00e9atique", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Pancreas", "similarity": 0.912}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "conduit pancr\u00e9atique", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Pancreas", "similarity": 0.906}], "id": 2448, "stable_key": "clu_d1eb74436e60721f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "pancreas", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "This refers to the organ and its associated structures.", "wikidata_id": "Q1601921", "wikidata_description": "pancreas disease characterized by autosomal dominant inheritance of the annular pancreas (head of the pancreas forming a ring around the second portion of the duodenum) and duodenal stenosis", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Annular%20pancreas"}}, {"canonical_name": "hot", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "hot", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "hot", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["hot"], "contexts": ["bodily quality"]}, {"entity_id": "hot", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "hot", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 17, "variants": ["hot"], "contexts": ["a quality of substances", "quality of substances"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "hot", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "hot", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2449, "stable_key": "clu_680dfb2f1cee461e-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "heat", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This refers to the concept of 'hot' as a fundamental quality in humoral theory and early chemistry."}}, {"canonical_name": "coquilles", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "coquilles", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "coquilles", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 12, "variants": ["coquilles"], "contexts": ["marine debris found on land", "marine debris forming terrestrial matter"]}, {"entity_id": "coquilles", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "coquilles", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 6, "variants": ["coquilles", "coquilles bivalves", "coquilles marines", "coquilles univalves"], "contexts": ["shell detritus", "shell fragments on rock"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "coquilles", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "coquilles", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2450, "stable_key": "clu_936584d83c83dbec", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Shells", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "The term 'coquilles' in the context of marine debris or shell detritus refers to shells, typically of mollusks or other marine organisms, rather than live animals.", "wikidata_id": "Q213096", "wikidata_description": "hard, protective outer layer created by marine animal", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seashell"}}, {"canonical_name": "Infection", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "infection", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Infection", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 17, "variants": ["Infection", "Infectious", "In\u2223fection", "infection"], "contexts": ["state of being infected, preserved from", "condition resisted by lovage"]}, {"entity_id": "infection", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "infection", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["infection"], "contexts": ["general term for bodily ailment"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Infection", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "infection", "similarity": 0.979}], "id": 2451, "stable_key": "clu_06ff02a80ff1b467", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Infection", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Infection", "wikidata_id": "Q166231", "wikidata_description": "invasion of and multiplication in a host by disease-causing pathogens or organisms, and the reaction of host tissues"}}, {"canonical_name": "Woods", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "woods", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Woods", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 17, "variants": ["Woods"], "contexts": ["habitat for Alder-Tree", "habitat for Yellow-Archangel"]}, {"entity_id": "woods", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "woods", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["woods"], "contexts": ["areas covered with trees"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Woods", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "woods", "similarity": 0.968}], "id": 2452, "stable_key": "clu_ade394937517251d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "woods", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "An area characterized by the presence of trees.", "wikipedia_extract": "A woodland ( ) is, in the broad sense, land covered with woody plants (trees and shrubs), or in a narrow sense, synonymous with wood (or in the U.S., the plurale tantum woods), a low-density forest forming open habitats with plenty of sunlight and limited shade (see differences between British, American and Australian English explained below). Some savannas may also be woodlands, such as savanna woodland, where trees and shrubs form a light canopy. Woodlands may support an understory of shrubs and herbaceous plants including grasses. Woodland may form a transition to shrubland under drier conditions or during early stages of primary or secondary succession. Higher-density areas of trees with a largely closed canopy that provides extensive and nearly continuous shade are often referred to as forests. Extensive efforts by conservationist groups have been made to preserve woodlands from urbanization and agriculture."}}, {"canonical_name": "Electuary", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "electuary", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Electuary", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 9, "variants": ["Electuary", "Electuaries"], "contexts": ["sweetened medicinal paste", "used for gout"]}, {"entity_id": "electuario", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "electuario", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 9, "variants": ["electuario", "electuario rofado", "electuarios"], "contexts": ["medicinal confection taken at night", "type of medicinal preparation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Electuary", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "electuario", "similarity": 0.893}], "id": 2453, "stable_key": "clu_88ecfbdedfda4168", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "electuary", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "A sweet, paste-like medicinal preparation mixed with honey or syrup.", "wikipedia_extract": "An electuary is a medicine consisting of a powder or other ingredient mixed with something sweet such as honey to make it more palatable. In German and Swiss cultures, electuary (German: Latwerge or Latwerg) is also more generally a thickened juice and honey preparation with a thick, viscous consistency that is used in for culinary purposes, such as a (bread) spread or as a sauce ingredient. In the Indian Ayurveda tradition, electuaries are called L\u0113hya (\u0932\u0947\u0939\u094d\u092f) (literally, \"lickable\"). There are several different types of electuary: laxative electuary, joyful electuary, etc. The fermentation of mixed herbs in honey and their effects on each other are said to increase the medical properties already present and to create new ones. Avicenna (1999)."}}, {"canonical_name": "Clusius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "clusius", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Clusius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Clusius"], "contexts": ["Renaissance botanist", "affirms pear-tree mistletoe prevalence"]}, {"entity_id": "clusius", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Clusius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 16, "variants": ["Clusius"], "contexts": ["notes on amber origin", "received amomo, drew Exoticorum"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Clusius", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Clusius", "similarity": 0.612}], "id": 2454, "stable_key": "clu_0ae8f3648411751c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Carolus Clusius", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1526, "death_year": 1609, "description": "Renaissance botanist and physician, explored flora of Iberian Peninsula.", "wikidata_id": "Q18983571", "wikidata_description": "street in Voorhout, the Netherlands"}}, {"canonical_name": "Divergence of Character", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "divergence_of_character", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Divergence of Character", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 17, "variants": ["Divergence of Character", "divergence of character"], "contexts": ["evolutionary splitting of lineages", "evolutionary process of species differentiation"]}, {"entity_id": "divergence_of_character", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "divergence of character", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["divergence of character"], "contexts": ["tendency for segregations to become more definite"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Divergence of Character", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "divergence of character", "similarity": 0.951}], "id": 2455, "stable_key": "clu_dfaa68a54e0ffd56", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Divergence of Character", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "raiz", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "ra\u00edz", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "ra\u00edz", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "ROOT", "count": 1, "variants": ["ra\u00edz"], "contexts": ["Mechoacan root used as an ointment"]}, {"entity_id": "raiz", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "raiz", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "ROOT", "count": 1, "variants": ["raiz"], "contexts": ["root of the plant"]}, {"entity_id": "raiz", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "raiz", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 16, "variants": ["raiz", "raizes", "ra\u00edz", "ra\u00edzes"], "contexts": ["part of a plant", "part of a plant, used as a spice"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "ra\u00edz", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "raiz", "similarity": 0.597}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "raiz", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "raiz", "similarity": 0.817}], "id": 2456, "stable_key": "clu_8d618ee24b6b2bd4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Root", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "note": "While specific medicinal roots like Mechoacan root are mentioned, the general term 'raiz' refers to the plant part.", "wikidata_id": "Q41500", "wikidata_description": "organ of a higher plant that anchors the rest of the plant in the ground, absorbs water and mineral salts from the soil, and does not bear leaves or buds", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root"}}, {"canonical_name": "cebola", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "cebada", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "cebada", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["cebada"], "contexts": ["grain used in water for fevers"]}, {"entity_id": "cebola", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "cebola", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 17, "variants": ["cebola", "Cebola", "Cebolas", "cebolas", "cebolla", "cebollas"], "contexts": ["plant, onion", "onion, pungent substance causing tears"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "cebada", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cebola", "similarity": 0.891}], "id": 2457, "stable_key": "clu_4258944386d586dd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "onion", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Allium cepa", "family": "Amaryllidaceae", "description": "A pungent, edible bulb vegetable.", "wikipedia_extract": "The onion (Allium cepa L.Tooltip Carl Linnaeus, from Latin cepa), also known as the bulb onion or common onion, is a vegetable that is the most widely cultivated species of the genus Allium. The shallot is a botanical variety of the onion which was classified as a separate species until 2011. The onion's close relatives include garlic, scallion, leek, and chives. The genus contains several other species variously called onions and cultivated for food, such as the Japanese bunching onion Allium fistulosum, the tree onion Allium \u00d7 proliferum, and the Canada onion Allium canadense. The name wild onion is applied to a number of Allium species, but A. cepa is exclusively known from cultivation. Its ancestral wild original form is not known, although escapes from cultivation have become established in some regions."}}, {"canonical_name": "amygdaloid", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "amygdaloid", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "amygdaloid", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 14, "variants": ["amygdaloid", "amygdaloicles", "amygdaloide", "amygdaloides", "amygdalo\u00efde", "amygdalo\u00efdes", "aniygdalo\u00efdes"], "contexts": ["geological formation described as masses", "geological formation, second type"]}, {"entity_id": "amygdaloid", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "amygdaloid", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["amygdaloid"], "contexts": ["igneous rock with cavities", "rock type that is shaken by earthquakes"]}, {"entity_id": "amygdaloids", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "amygdaloids", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["amygdaloids"], "contexts": ["rock texture"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "amygdaloid", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "amygdaloid", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "amygdaloid", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "amygdaloids", "similarity": 0.976}], "id": 2458, "stable_key": "clu_477451b72c9468c3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Amygdaloid", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "Amygdaloid refers to a volcanic rock texture with cavities, often filled with minerals. It's used in geology and mineralogy.", "wikipedia_extract": "The amygdala (; pl.: amygdalae  or amygdalas; also corpus amygdaloideum) is a paired  nuclear complex present in the cerebral hemispheres of vertebrates. It is considered part of the limbic system. In primates, it is located medially within the temporal lobes. It consists of many nuclei, each made up of further subnuclei. The subdivision most commonly made is into the basolateral, central, cortical, and medial nuclei together with the intercalated cell clusters. The amygdala has a primary role in the processing of memory, decision-making, and emotional responses (including fear, anxiety, and aggression).", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amygdala"}}, {"canonical_name": "Duperrey", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "duperrey", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Duperrey", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 13, "variants": ["Duperrey", "M. Duperrey"], "contexts": ["commander of French corvette", "early modern scholar"]}, {"entity_id": "duperrey", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Duperrey", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 5, "variants": ["Duperrey"], "contexts": ["provided pendulum experiment results", "investigator of magnetic equator changes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Duperrey", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Duperrey", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2459, "stable_key": "clu_52ef06e39bf6dbad", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Louis Isidore Duperrey", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1786, "death_year": 1865, "description": "French explorer and naval officer, commanded scientific expeditions."}}, {"canonical_name": "Serpentine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "serpentine", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Serpentine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 9, "variants": ["Serpentine", "Serpentine de Guanacoa", "serpentine"], "contexts": ["mineral found with weisstein", "serpentine rock, geological material"]}, {"entity_id": "serpentine", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "serpentine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 9, "variants": ["serpentine"], "contexts": ["igneous rock from earth's interior", "rock type"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Serpentine", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "serpentine", "similarity": 0.744}], "id": 2460, "stable_key": "clu_4ace5c181d3fec73", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Serpentine", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikipedia_extract": "A succubus (pl. succubi) is a female demon who is described in various European folklore as appearing in the dreams of male humans in order to seduce them. Repeated interactions between a succubus and a man will lead to sexual activity, a bond forming between them, and ultimately sexual intercourse, as she requires semen to survive. The establishment and perpetuation of such a relationship enables the production of a hybrid child known as a cambion, but at the expense of the man, whose mental and physical health will deteriorate rapidly, eventually resulting in his death if the succubus continues courting him for a protracted period. In modern representations, a succubus is often depicted as a beautiful woman seductress or charming, rather than as demonic or frightening, to attract people instead of repulsing them. The male counterpart of the succubus is the incubus.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Succubus"}}, {"canonical_name": "Mitscherlich", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "mitscherlich", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Mitscherlich", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 15, "variants": ["Mitscherlich"], "contexts": ["researcher on melting points", "author of 'Lehrbuch der Chemie'"]}, {"entity_id": "mitscherlich", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Mitscherlich", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Mitscherlich"], "contexts": ["experimenter on crystallisation and water", "scientist who studied crystal expansion"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Mitscherlich", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Mitscherlich", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2461, "stable_key": "clu_66883dfbdef5acc0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Eilhard Mitscherlich", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1794, "death_year": 1863, "description": "German chemist, discovered isomorphism and laws of chemical proportion.", "wikidata_id": "Q126219257", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article"}}, {"canonical_name": "electro-magnetism", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "electro-magnetism", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "electro-magnetism", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["electro-magnetism"], "contexts": ["branch of physics concerning electric and magnetic forces", "knowledge of electro-magnetism"]}, {"entity_id": "electro-magnetic", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "electro-magnetic", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["electro-magnetic"], "contexts": ["relating electricity and magnetism"]}, {"entity_id": "electro-magnetism", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "electro-magnetism", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 10, "variants": ["electro-magnetism", "Electro-Magnetism"], "contexts": ["magnetism produced by electric currents", "theory of electricity and magnetism interaction"]}, {"entity_id": "electro-magnetic", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "electro-magnetic", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["electro-magnetic"], "contexts": ["relating to electricity and magnetism"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "electro-magnetism", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "electro-magnetism", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "electro-magnetism", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "electro-magnetic", "similarity": 0.915}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "electro-magnetic", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "electro-magnetism", "similarity": 0.915}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "electro-magnetic", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "electro-magnetic", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2462, "stable_key": "clu_12955d7597e8937e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "electromagnetism", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q11406", "wikidata_description": "branch of science concerned with the phenomena of electricity and magnetism", "note": "The term 'Magneto-electricity' specifically refers to the generation of electricity from magnetic fields, a key component of electromagnetism."}}, {"canonical_name": "equinoxes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "equinoxes", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "equinoxes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["equinoxes"], "contexts": ["astronomical events", "astronomical periods"]}, {"entity_id": "equinoxes", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "equinoxes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 12, "variants": ["equinoxes", "Equinoxes"], "contexts": ["times of equal day and night", "points of equal day and night"]}, {"entity_id": "equinox", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "equinox", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["equinox"], "contexts": ["point where ecliptic and celestial equator intersect", "celestial point for measuring time"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "equinoxes", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "equinoxes", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "equinoxes", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "equinox", "similarity": 0.886}], "id": 2463, "stable_key": "clu_fb7583fb617493eb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "equinox", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q1347837", "wikidata_description": "implementation of the OSGi framework"}}, {"canonical_name": "Alepo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "aleppo", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Aleppo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Aleppo"], "contexts": ["major city in Syria", "Pachalik where earthquakes occurred"]}, {"entity_id": "alepo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Alepo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 16, "variants": ["Alepo", "Aleppo"], "contexts": ["principal city of Syria", "trading city"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Aleppo", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Alepo", "similarity": 0.943}], "id": 2464, "stable_key": "clu_44049b3f558b81e8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Aleppo", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Syria", "wikidata_id": "Q211457", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Aleppo_(2012\u20132016)", "wikipedia_extract": "The Battle of Aleppo (Arabic: \u0645\u064e\u0639\u0652\u0631\u064e\u0643\u064e\u0629\u064f \u062d\u064e\u0644\u064e\u0628\u064e, romanized: Ma\u02bfrakat \u1e24alab) was a major military confrontation in Aleppo, the largest city in Syria, between the Syrian opposition militias (such as the Free Syrian Army (FSA), the Levant Front and the al-Nusra Front) against the Ba'athist Syrian government, supported by Hezbollah, IRGC-backed Shia militant groups, Iran, and Russia, and against the Kurdish-majority People's Protection Units (YPG). The battle began on 19 July 2012 and was part of the Syrian Civil War. A stalemate that had been in place for four years finally ended in July 2016, when Ba'athist government troops closed the rebels' last supply line into Aleppo with the support of Russian airstrikes. In response, rebel forces launched unsuccessful counteroffensives in September and October that failed to break the siege; in November, government forces embarked on a decisive campaign that resulted in the recapture of all of Aleppo by December 2016. The Syrian government victory was widely seen as a turning point in Syria's civil war. The large-scale devastation of the battle and its importance led combatants to name it the \"mother of battles\" or \"Syria's Stalingrad\"."}}, {"canonical_name": "Forestus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "forster", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Forster", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Forster"], "contexts": ["provided pendulum experiment results"]}, {"entity_id": "forestus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Forestus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 17, "variants": ["Forestus", "Forest", "Forest.", "Foresto"], "contexts": ["early modern author cited", "early modern author"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Forster", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Forestus", "similarity": 0.901}], "id": 2465, "stable_key": "clu_83ba2728a9618621", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pieter van Foreest", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1521, "death_year": 1597, "description": "Dutch physician, 'father of Dutch medicine'.", "note": "The spelling 'Forestus' is a Latinization of his name. 'Forster' is likely an OCR error or variant.", "wikidata_id": "Q2838278", "wikidata_description": "Dutch classical scholar"}}, {"canonical_name": "Curcuma", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "curcuma", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Curcuma", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 17, "variants": ["Curcuma", "Curcuma Zedoaria", "Curcuma angusUfolia", "Curcuma longa", "curcuma", "curcumani", "curcumanit meimiram"], "contexts": ["plant related to a root", "plant used for medicine and dyeing"]}, {"entity_id": "curcuma", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Curcuma", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Curcuma"], "contexts": ["plant believed to be a type of Cippero"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Curcuma", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Curcuma", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2466, "stable_key": "clu_e8761bf833c6232e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Turmeric", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Curcuma longa", "family": "Zingiberaceae", "description": "Plant root used for medicine and dyeing, related to Cippero.", "wikipedia_extract": "Turmeric (), or Curcuma longa (), is a flowering plant in the ginger family Zingiberaceae. It is a perennial, rhizomatous, herbaceous plant native to the Indian subcontinent and Southeast Asia that requires temperatures between 20 and 30 \u00b0C (68 and 86 \u00b0F) and high annual rainfall to thrive. Plants are gathered each year for their rhizomes, some for propagation in the following season and some for consumption or dyeing. The rhizomes can be used fresh, but they are often boiled in water and dried, after which they are ground into a deep orange-yellow shelf-stable spice powder commonly used as a coloring and flavoring agent in many Asian cuisines, especially for curries (curry powder). Turmeric powder has a warm, bitter, black pepper-like flavor and earthy, mustard-like aroma. Although long used in Ayurvedic medicine, there is no high-quality clinical evidence that consuming turmeric or the principal turmeric constituent, curcumin, is effective for treating any disease."}}, {"canonical_name": "dry", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "dry", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "dry", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 17, "variants": ["dry"], "contexts": ["a quality of substances", "quality of substances"]}, {"entity_id": "dry", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "dry", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["dry"], "contexts": ["lacking moisture"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "dry", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "dry", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2467, "stable_key": "clu_fda2ad6be438df7b-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Dry", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "In early modern humoral theory, 'dry' (and its opposite 'moist') referred to fundamental qualities of substances and bodies, not just a lack of moisture."}}, {"canonical_name": "Deckan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "deckan", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Deckan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 16, "variants": ["Deckan", "Deckan interior"], "contexts": ["region name", "region conquered by a king"]}, {"entity_id": "deccan", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Deccan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Deccan"], "contexts": ["region in India with plant exceptions", "region in India"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Deckan", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Deccan", "similarity": 0.881}], "id": 2468, "stable_key": "clu_703f40ca741d8654", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Deccan", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "India", "wikidata_id": "Q8354327", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deccania", "wikipedia_extract": "Deccania is a monotypic genus of flowering plants in the family Rubiaceae. The genus contains only one species, viz. Deccania pubescens, which is endemic to India."}}, {"canonical_name": "ameixas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "ameixas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "ameixas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 8, "variants": ["ameixas", "ameixa", "ameixas redondas"], "contexts": ["fruit", "fruit that rots and causes fevers"]}, {"entity_id": "ameixas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "ameixas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 8, "variants": ["ameixas"], "contexts": ["fruit used in diet", "prunes used in a decoction"]}, {"entity_id": "ameixas_pafladas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "ameixas pafladas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["ameixas pafladas"], "contexts": ["dried plums used in remedies"]}, {"entity_id": "ameixas_calfacas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "ameixas calfacas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["ameixas calfacas"], "contexts": ["prunes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "ameixas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "ameixas", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "ameixas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "ameixas pafladas", "similarity": 0.921}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "ameixas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "ameixas calfacas", "similarity": 0.949}], "id": 2469, "stable_key": "clu_14a337cc8230d21e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Plums/Prunes", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "description": "Fruit used in diet, remedies, and known to cause fevers when rotting.", "note": "Includes dried plums ('ameixas pafladas', 'ameixas calfacas').", "wikipedia_extract": "A plum is a fruit of some species in Prunus subg. Prunus. Dried plums are usually called prunes. Plums are likely to have been one of the first fruits domesticated by humans, with origins in Eastern Europe, the Caucasus Mountains and China. They were brought to Britain from Asia, and their cultivation has been documented in Andalusia, southern Spain. Plums are a diverse group of species, with trees reaching a height of 5\u20136 metres (16\u201320 ft) when pruned."}}, {"canonical_name": "Arroz", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "aroz", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "aroz", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["aroz"], "contexts": ["used for cleaning pearls", "grain eaten in Balagate"]}, {"entity_id": "arroz", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Arroz", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 16, "variants": ["Arroz", "arroz"], "contexts": ["rice, an ingredient", "grain used as food"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "aroz", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Arroz", "similarity": 0.96}], "id": 2470, "stable_key": "clu_10ada8ed1aacd6f4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Rice", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Oryza sativa", "family": "Poaceae", "description": "A staple cereal grain, widely cultivated and consumed globally.", "wikipedia_extract": "Oryza sativa, having the common name Asian cultivated rice, is the much more common of the two rice species cultivated as a cereal, the other species being O. glaberrima, African rice. It was first domesticated in the Yangtze River basin in China 13,500 to 8,200 years ago. Oryza sativa belongs to the genus Oryza and the BOP clade in the grass family Poaceae. With a genome consisting of 430 Mbp across 12 chromosomes, it is renowned for being easy to genetically modify and is a model organism for the study of the biology of cereals and monocots. O. sativa has an erect stalk stem that grows 80\u2013120 cm (30\u201345 in) tall, with a smooth surface. The leaf is lanceolate, 15\u201330 cm (5+7\u20448\u201311+3\u20444 in) long, and grows from a ligule 10\u201320 mm (3\u20448\u20133\u20444 in) long."}}, {"canonical_name": "animaes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "animaes", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "animaes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["animaes"], "contexts": ["animals, source of bezoars", "animals that are washed"]}, {"entity_id": "animaes", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "animaes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 16, "variants": ["animaes", "animal", "animalcula", "animali"], "contexts": ["vital spirits", "animals"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "animaes", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "animaes", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2471, "stable_key": "clu_9f77ad4004c8b35d-4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Animals", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q431", "wikidata_description": "scientific study of animals", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Animal", "note": "The term 'beast' in early modern texts is a general term for any non-human animal, often used in contrast to humans or for specific examples of fauna.", "wikipedia_extract": "Animals are multicellular, eukaryotic organisms belonging to the biological kingdom Animalia (). With few exceptions, animals consume organic material, breathe oxygen, have myocytes and are able to move, can reproduce sexually, and grow from a hollow sphere of cells, the blastula, during embryonic development. Animals form a clade, meaning that they arose from a single common ancestor. Over 1.5 million living animal species have been described, of which around 1.05 million are insects, over 85,000 are molluscs, and around 65,000 are vertebrates. It has been estimated there are as many as 7.77 million animal species on Earth. Animal body lengths range from 8.5 \u03bcm (0.00033 in) to 33.6 m (110 ft).", "description": "Living creatures, sources of bezoars, subject to washing."}}, {"canonical_name": "Salva", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "salva", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "salva", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["salva"], "contexts": ["plant with a similar taste"]}, {"entity_id": "salva", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Salva", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 17, "variants": ["Salva", "salva", "salva verde"], "contexts": ["ingredient in fomentations", "herb for head wash"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "salva", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Salva", "similarity": 0.956}], "id": 2472, "stable_key": "clu_4f9937b90c9af231", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sage", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Salvia officinalis", "family": "Lamiaceae", "description": "Herb used in fomentations and for head washes, with a distinct taste.", "wikipedia_extract": "Salvia officinalis, common sage or sage, is a perennial, evergreen subshrub, with woody stems, grayish leaves, and blue to purplish flowers. It is a member of the mint family (Lamiaceae) and native to the Mediterranean region, though it has been naturalized in many places throughout the world. It has a long history of culinary use, and in modern times it has been used as an ornamental garden plant. The common name \"sage\" is also used for closely related species and cultivars. Cultivars are quite variable in size, leaf and flower color, and foliage pattern, with many variegated leaf types. The Old World type grows to approximately 60 cm (2 ft) tall and wide, with lavender flowers most common, though they can also be white, pink, or purple."}}, {"canonical_name": "ellipse", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "ellipse", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "ellipse", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 11, "variants": ["ellipse", "Ellipse"], "contexts": ["geometric shape of planetary orbits", "geometric shape of orbits"]}, {"entity_id": "ellipses", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "ellipses", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["ellipses"], "contexts": ["oval paths of planets", "shape of planetary orbits"]}, {"entity_id": "ellipse", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "ellipse", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["ellipse"], "contexts": ["geometric shape like a stretched circle", "eccentric curve of motion"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "ellipse", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "ellipse", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "ellipses", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "ellipse", "similarity": 0.907}], "id": 2473, "stable_key": "clu_f4c09badbb3b95c6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "ellipse", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q44224", "wikidata_description": "French animation studio"}}, {"canonical_name": "oxyde de carbone", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 17, "members": [{"entity_id": "oxyde_de_carbone", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "oxyde de carbone", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 14, "variants": ["oxyde de carbone"], "contexts": ["chemical compound causing poisoning", "toxic gas causing poisoning"]}, {"entity_id": "carbonic_oxide", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "carbonic oxide", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["carbonic oxide", "Carbonic oxide"], "contexts": ["compound of carbon and oxygen", "chemical compound"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "oxyde de carbone", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "carbonic oxide", "similarity": 0.942}], "id": 2474, "stable_key": "clu_e1f7d19400d2781e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Carbon monoxide", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical"}}, {"canonical_name": "membrana", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 17, "members": [{"entity_id": "membranes", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Membranes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Membranes"], "contexts": ["structures formed in embryonic development"]}, {"entity_id": "membrana", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "membrana", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 16, "variants": ["membrana", "membranas", "membranas do Abdo- men", "membranas do cerebro", "membranas docerebro"], "contexts": ["membrane", "tissue layer involved in cauterization"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Membranes", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "membrana", "similarity": 0.894}], "id": 2475, "stable_key": "clu_649fab5a90eef467", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Membranes", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "While 'membrane' can refer to anatomical structures, in this context, especially with 'embryonic development', it refers to the conceptual biological structure rather than a specific anatomical part.", "wikidata_id": "Q226697", "wikidata_description": "animal skin processed for writing or painting on", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parchment", "wikipedia_extract": "Parchment is a writing material made from specially prepared untanned skins of animals\u2014primarily sheep, calves and goats. It has been used as a writing medium in West Asia and Europe for more than two millennia. By 400 AD many of the written works intended for preservation in these regions had been transferred from papyrus to parchment. Vellum is a type of fine-quality parchment made from the skins of young animals such as lambs and young calves. The generic term animal membrane is sometimes used by libraries and museums that wish to avoid distinguishing between parchment and vellum. Today the term parchment is often used in non-technical contexts to refer to any animal skin, particularly goat, sheep or cow, that has been scraped or dried under tension."}}, {"canonical_name": "Yucatan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 17, "members": [{"entity_id": "yucatan", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "Yucatan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Yucatan"], "contexts": ["peninsula in Mexico"]}, {"entity_id": "yucatan", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Yucatan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 16, "variants": ["Yucatan"], "contexts": ["region included in Nouvelle-Espagne", "peninsula"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Yucatan", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Yucatan", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2476, "stable_key": "clu_f714ac096b842a11", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Yucatan", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Mexico", "wikidata_id": "Q20570420", "wikidata_description": "musical group from United Kingdom"}}, {"canonical_name": "toises", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 17, "members": [{"entity_id": "toises", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "toises", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["toises"], "contexts": ["unit of measurement for height"]}, {"entity_id": "toises", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "toises", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 16, "variants": ["toises", "TOISES", "toise", "toises carr\u00e9es"], "contexts": ["unit of length measurement", "unit of height measurement"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "toises", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "toises", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2477, "stable_key": "clu_af313acc9a7eb63b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Toise", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "A historical unit of length, particularly used in France.", "wikidata_id": "Q2784622", "wikidata_description": "unit of length", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toise"}}, {"canonical_name": "humores melancolicos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 17, "members": [{"entity_id": "melancholly_humors", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Melancholly humors", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 5, "variants": ["Melancholly humors", "Melanchollick Humors", "Melanchollick humor", "Melancholly Hu\u2223mors"], "contexts": ["humoral imbalance", "humor to be diminished"]}, {"entity_id": "humores_melancolicos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "humores melancolicos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 12, "variants": ["humores melancolicos"], "contexts": ["part of humoral theory", "body fluids associated with paralysis"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Melancholly humors", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "humores melancolicos", "similarity": 0.963}], "id": 2478, "stable_key": "clu_2c5dad17fbab5525", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Melancholic Humors", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "Body fluids associated with imbalance and melancholy in humoral theory.", "wikipedia_extract": "Humorism, the humoral theory, or humoralism, was a system of medicine detailing a supposed makeup and workings of the human body, adopted by Ancient Greek and Roman physicians and philosophers. Humorism began to fall out of favor in the 17th century and it was definitively disproved with the discovery of microbes. The concept of \"humors\" may have origins in Ancient Egyptian medicine, or Mesopotamia, though it was not systemized until ancient Greek thinkers. The word humor is a translation of Greek \u03c7\u03c5\u03bc\u03cc\u03c2, chymos (literally 'juice' or 'sap', metaphorically 'flavor'). Early texts on Indian Ayurveda medicine presented a theory of three or four humors (do\u1e63as), which they sometimes linked with the five elements (pa\u00f1ca-bh\u016bta): earth, water, fire, air, and space. The concept of \"humors\" (chemical systems regulating human behaviour) became more prominent from the writing of medical theorist Alcmaeon of Croton (c. 540\u2013500 BC)."}}, {"canonical_name": "Fumofterno", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 17, "members": [{"entity_id": "fumitory", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Fumitory", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Fumitory", "Fumitary", "Fu\u2223mitory"], "contexts": ["herb with medicinal properties", "herb"]}, {"entity_id": "fumofterno", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Fumofterno", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 13, "variants": ["Fumofterno", "Fumo", "Fumoft", "Fum\u00f2fterno", "fumo", "fumoflcrno", "fumofterno"], "contexts": ["fumitory herb used in syrup", "plant used in syrup"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Fumitory", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Fumofterno", "similarity": 0.888}], "id": 2479, "stable_key": "clu_12cba93709572ac4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Fumitory", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Fumaria officinalis L.", "family": "Papaveraceae", "note": "The Latin 'Fumofterno' and English 'Fumitory' both refer to the plant Fumaria officinalis."}}, {"canonical_name": "hearing", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 17, "members": [{"entity_id": "hear", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Hear", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hear"], "contexts": ["excessive heat in liver and stomach"]}, {"entity_id": "hearing", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "hearing", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 16, "variants": ["hearing", "Hearing"], "contexts": ["sense of auditory perception", "sense of sound"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Hear", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "hearing", "similarity": 0.883}], "id": 2480, "stable_key": "clu_d622be7b7e2b7336", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hearing", "confidence": "medium", "type": "concept", "note": "The context 'excessive heat in liver and stomach' suggests a different meaning, possibly related to bodily humors or a disease symptom, but the primary meaning and broader usage point to the sense of hearing.", "wikidata_id": "Q160289", "wikidata_description": "sensory perception of sound by living organisms"}}, {"canonical_name": "Copal", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "RESIN", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 17, "members": [{"entity_id": "copal", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "Copal", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "RESIN", "count": 14, "variants": ["COPAL", "Copal", "copal"], "contexts": ["resin from the tree", "type of resin"]}, {"entity_id": "copal", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Copal", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "RESIN", "count": 3, "variants": ["Copal"], "contexts": ["a white, lucid, and transparent resin", "resin burned in braziers"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Copal", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Copal", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2481, "stable_key": "clu_e18caa878ce94d24", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Copal", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "note": "Copal is a resin, often from trees of the Burseraceae family, but can also refer to other resins. Specific botanical identification can be difficult from historical texts.", "wikidata_id": "Q909436", "wikidata_description": "Tree resin long used as incense and for making varnish", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copal"}}, {"canonical_name": "balsamo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 17, "members": [{"entity_id": "balsamo", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "balsamo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 12, "variants": ["Balsamo", "balsamo"], "contexts": ["aromatic substance", "plant product used as medicine"]}, {"entity_id": "balsamo", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Balsamo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 5, "variants": ["BALSAMO", "Balsamo"], "contexts": ["precious liquid from New Spain", "a miraculous liquor with medicinal virtues"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "balsamo", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Balsamo", "similarity": 0.82}], "id": 2482, "stable_key": "clu_dc827dcb45f75dee", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Balsam", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "note": "Refers to aromatic resinous substances, often from trees, used medicinally and perfumerially.", "wikidata_id": "Q428750", "wikidata_description": "species of plant in the family Cucurbitaceae", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Momordica%20charantia"}}, {"canonical_name": "venenos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 17, "members": [{"entity_id": "venenos", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "venenos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 11, "variants": [