aceae", "note": "The term 'legumes' can refer to the plant family (Fabaceae) or the edible seeds/pods. Given the context of 'vegetables consumed', it likely refers to the edible parts but the family is the broader scientific classification."}}, {"canonical_name": "Timor", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "timor", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Timor", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Timor"], "contexts": ["island where petrified zoophytes inhabit"]}, {"entity_id": "timor", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Timor", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 13, "variants": ["Timor"], "contexts": ["island where amber is found", "island in volcanic chain"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Timor", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Timor", "similarity": 0.923}], "id": 2595, "stable_key": "clu_7fce16cd8b676c43", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Timor", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Indonesia / Timor-Leste", "wikidata_id": "Q26759680", "wikidata_description": "island in Central Bangka Regency, Bangka Belitung Islands Province, Indonesia"}}, {"canonical_name": "Sulfur", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "sulfure_de_fer", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "sulfure de fer", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["sulfure de fer"], "contexts": ["iron sulfide"]}, {"entity_id": "fer_sulfur\u00e9", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "fer sulfur\u00e9", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["fer sulfur\u00e9"], "contexts": ["sulfurated iron"]}, {"entity_id": "sulfur", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Sulfur", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 12, "variants": ["Sulfur", "Subphur", "Sulfur biuo", "Sulphur"], "contexts": ["substance flowing from springs", "principle of metals"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "sulfure de fer", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Sulfur", "similarity": 0.926}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "fer sulfur\u00e9", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Sulfur", "similarity": 0.905}], "id": 2596, "stable_key": "clu_2bd325ed697ebcd2-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sulfur", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "note": "Brimstone is an archaic term for sulfur.", "wikidata_id": "Q1157552", "wikidata_description": "mineral (as opposed to the chemical element)", "wikipedia_extract": "Sulfuric acid (American spelling and the preferred IUPAC name) or sulphuric acid (Commonwealth spelling), known in antiquity as oil of vitriol, is a mineral acid composed of the elements sulfur, oxygen, and hydrogen, with the molecular formula H2SO4. It is a colorless, odorless, and viscous liquid that is miscible with water. Pure sulfuric acid does not occur naturally due to its strong affinity to water vapor; it is hygroscopic and readily absorbs water vapor from the air. Concentrated sulfuric acid is a strong oxidant with powerful dehydrating properties, making it highly corrosive towards other materials, from rocks to metals. Phosphorus pentoxide is a notable exception in that it is not dehydrated by sulfuric acid but, to the contrary, dehydrates sulfuric acid to sulfur trioxide. Upon addition of sulfuric acid to water, a considerable amount of heat is released; thus, the reverse procedure of adding water to the acid is generally avoided since the heat released may boil the solution, spraying droplets of hot acid during the process.", "wikipedia_url": "https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soufre_%28min%C3%A9ral%29"}}, {"canonical_name": "porphyry", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "porphyrschiefer", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "porphyrschiefer", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["porphyrschiefer"], "contexts": ["another name for phonolithe"]}, {"entity_id": "porphyry", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "porphyry", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 13, "variants": ["porphyry"], "contexts": ["endogenous rock type", "type of rock"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "porphyrschiefer", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "porphyry", "similarity": 0.92}], "id": 2597, "stable_key": "clu_04593b67396f2838", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Porphyry", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q203445", "wikidata_description": "3rd-century Greek Neoplatonist philosopher", "note": "The Italian 'Porfido' directly translates to porphyry."}}, {"canonical_name": "mental life", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "mental_life", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "mental life", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 11, "variants": ["Mental Life", "mental life"], "contexts": ["phenomena and their conditions", "the nature of conscious experience"]}, {"entity_id": "mental_life", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "mental life", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["mental life"], "contexts": ["the sum of conscious experiences", "subjective experience and cognitive processes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "mental life", "t