natural water source", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artesian_Well_Park", "wikipedia_extract": "Olympia is the capital city of the U.S. state of Washington. The population was 55,605 at the 2020 census, while the Olympia metropolitan statistical area has an estimated 300,000 people. Olympia is the county seat of Thurston County and anchors the South Puget Sound region of western Washington, 50 miles (80 km) southwest of Seattle. The Squaxin and other Coast Salish peoples inhabited the southern Puget Sound region prior to the arrival of European and American settlers in the 19th century. The Treaty of Medicine Creek was signed in 1854 and followed by the Treaty of Olympia in 1856; these two treaties forced the Squaxin to relocate to an Indian reservation."}}, {"canonical_name": "celestial bodies", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "celestial_regions", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "celestial regions", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["celestial regions"], "contexts": ["regions of the sky", "regions of the sky or outer space"]}, {"entity_id": "celestial_bodies", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "celestial bodies", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 11, "variants": ["celestial bodies", "Celestial Bodies", "Celestial bodies"], "contexts": ["astronomical objects", "astronomical objects like planets and stars"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "celestial regions", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "celestial bodies", "similarity": 0.947}], "id": 2603, "stable_key": "clu_8992759cb5dc084c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Celestial objects", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q6999", "wikidata_description": "physical body of astronomically-significant size, mass, or role, naturally occurring in a universe"}}, {"canonical_name": "Sallafras", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "sallafras", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Sallafras", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 9, "variants": ["Sallafras", "Safras", "Saf\u00edafras", "Saissasfras", "Salfafras", "Sassafras"], "contexts": ["wood brought from Florida", "water source for treatment"]}, {"entity_id": "sallafr\u00e0s", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Sallafr\u00e0s", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 5, "variants": ["Sallafr\u00e0s", "Salfafras", "Sal\u017fafraz", "pao Salfafras", "sassafras"], "contexts": ["wood used in water preparation", "Sassafras bark used in medicine"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Sallafras", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Sallafr\u00e0s", "similarity": 0.792}], "id": 2604, "stable_key": "clu_4288edc21c34a20e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sassafras", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Sassafras albidum", "family": "Lauraceae", "wikidata_id": "Q131532", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants"}}, {"canonical_name": "epithimas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "epithimas", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Epithimas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Epithimas"], "contexts": ["substance to be prohibited", "cordial preparations"]}, {"entity_id": "epithimas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "epithimas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 12, "variants": ["epithimas", "epithema", "epithimos", "epitomas"], "contexts": ["cooling applications", "unguent for liver application"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Epithimas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "epithimas", "similarity": 0.839}], "id": 2605, "stable_key": "clu_11ff2cc9bec56c9c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Epithyme", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Cuscuta epithymum", "family": "Convolvulaceae", "note": "Early modern texts sometimes used plant names for specific preparations or compounds derived from them. 'Epithimas' likely refers to the plant itself, used medicinally.", "wikidata_id": "Q10273352", "wikidata_description": "genus of insects"}}, {"canonical_name": "Cananor", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "cananor", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Cananor", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 13, "variants": ["Cananor", "Canaoor"], "contexts": ["place with abundance of the anacardo", "city in India"]}, {"entity_id": "cananor", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Cananor", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cananor"], "contexts": ["a place, likely a spice source"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Cananor", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Cananor", "similarity": 0.831}], "id": 2606, "stable_key": "clu_8879b6a7ce13cb40", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Kannur", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "India", "wikidata_id": "Q4789666", "wikidata_description": "city in Kannur district in Kerala, India"}}, {"canonical_name": "antidoto", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "antidoto", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "antidoto", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["antidoto", "ant\u00eddoto", "ant\u00eddotos"], "contexts": ["remedy against poison", "considered function of stone"]}, {"entity_id": "antidoto", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "antidoto", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 9, "variants": ["antidoto", "Antidotum"], "contexts": ["antidote", "remedy against poison"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "antidoto", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "antidoto", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2607, "stable_key": "clu_c620e45a49338a35", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Antidote", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q194168", "wikidata_description": "substance that can counteract a form of poisoning"}}, {"canonical_name": "murtinhos", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "murtinhos", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "murtinhos", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 4, "variants": ["murtinhos", "murtinho"], "contexts": ["small myrtle plants", "plants not similar to turbit"]}, {"entity_id": "murtinhos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "murtinhos", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 10, "variants": ["murtinhos"], "contexts": ["myrtle, used in syrup", "kernels used in ointment"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "murtinhos", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "murtinhos", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2608, "stable_key": "clu_19bbd9cc8f233438-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Myrtle", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Myrtus communis Linnaeus", "family": "Myrtaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q165152", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Myrtus", "wikipedia_extract": "Myrtus (commonly called   myrtle) is a genus of flowering plants in the family Myrtaceae. It was first described by Swedish botanist Linnaeus in 1753. Over 600 names have been proposed in the genus, but nearly all have either been moved to other genera or been regarded as synonyms. Two species are currently accepted: Myrtus communis L. \u2013 Common myrtle; native to the Mediterranean region in southern Europe Myrtus nivellei Batt. & Trab.", "description": "A flowering shrub with aromatic leaves and berries."}}, {"canonical_name": "carbunculos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "carb\u00fanculos", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "carb\u00fanculos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 3, "variants": ["carb\u00fanculos"], "contexts": ["gemstones, mentioned by Pliny", "red or purple stones"]}, {"entity_id": "carbunculos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "carbunculos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 11, "variants": ["carbunculos", "Carbunculos", "carbunculos peftilenciaes", "carbunculus"], "contexts": ["carbuncles", "skin eruptions associated with malignant fevers"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "carb\u00fanculos", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "carbunculos", "similarity": 0.978}], "id": 2609, "stable_key": "clu_faaacdb21b967900", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lumps", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "Abnormal swellings or growths on or under the skin.", "note": "While 'carb\u00fanculos' can refer to gemstones, in a medicinal context, it refers to lumps or boils.", "wikipedia_extract": "Lyme disease, also known as Lyme borreliosis, is a tick-borne disease caused by species of Borrelia bacteria, transmitted by blood-feeding ticks in the genus Ixodes. It is the most common disease spread by ticks in the Northern Hemisphere. Infections are most common in the spring and early summer. Infection is treatable with antibiotics. Most treated patients experience a full recovery. For some patients recovery may not be immediate or complete, resulting in long-term effects."}}, {"canonical_name": "pombos", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "pombas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Pombas", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Pombas", "pombas"], "contexts": ["birds found in abundance", "many kinds of doves found"]}, {"entity_id": "pombos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "pombos", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 12, "variants": ["pombos", "Pombos", "pomba", "pombo"], "contexts": ["blood used in a remedy", "animal used as a remedy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Pombas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "pombos", "similarity": 0.929}], "id": 2610, "stable_key": "clu_4546e28d891a079c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Doves", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "linnaean": "Columba livia", "family": "Columbidae", "description": "Birds, often kept or used for medicinal purposes.", "note": "The Portuguese 'pombos' and 'pombas' both translate to doves or pigeons.", "wikipedia_extract": "The rock dove (Columba livia), also known as the common pigeon or \"rock pigeon\", is a member of the bird family Columbidae (doves and pigeons). In common usage, it is often simply referred to as the \"pigeon\", although the rock dove is the wild form of the bird; the pigeons familiar to most people are the domesticated forms of the wild rock dove. Wild rock doves are uniformly pale grey with two black bars on each wing, with few differences being seen between males and females; i.e. they are not strongly sexually dimorphic. The domestic pigeon, often, but invalidly, called \"Columba livia domestica\", which includes about 1,000 different breeds, is descended from this species. Escaped domestic pigeons are the origin of feral pigeons around the world. Both forms can vary widely in the colour and pattern of their plumage unlike their wild ancestor, being red, brown, checkered, uniformly coloured, or pied."}}, {"canonical_name": "sandalos citrinos", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "sandali_citrini", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Sandali citrini", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 6, "variants": ["Sandali citrini", "Sandali bianchO", "Sandati Citrini"], "contexts": ["yellow sandalwood used in preparation", "ingredient in a preparation"]}, {"entity_id": "sandalos_citrinos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "sandalos citrinos", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 8, "variants": ["sandalos citrinos", "Sandalos brancos", "Sandalos vermelhos", "sandalos", "sandalos vermelhos"], "contexts": ["ingredient in a decoction for fever", "citron sandalwood"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Sandali citrini", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "sandalos citrinos", "similarity": 0.925}], "id": 2611, "stable_key": "clu_0ae4dd2afd840132", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "yellow sandalwood", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "description": "A fragrant wood, likely a type of sandalwood, used medicinally.", "wikipedia_extract": "Sandalwood is a class of woods from trees in the genus Santalum. The woods are heavy, yellow, and fine-grained, and, unlike many other aromatic woods, they retain their fragrance for decades. Sandalwood oil is extracted from the woods. Sandalwood is often cited as one of the most expensive woods in the world. Both the wood and the oil produce a distinctive fragrance that has been highly valued for centuries. Consequently, some species of these slow-growing trees have suffered over-harvesting in the past."}}, {"canonical_name": "fel", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "felle", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "felle", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["felle"], "contexts": ["bile"]}, {"entity_id": "fel", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "fel", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 13, "variants": ["fel", "Fel"], "contexts": ["bile, a bodily fluid", "organ related to bile duct obstruction"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "felle", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "fel", "similarity": 0.906}], "id": 2612, "stable_key": "clu_bf373224f813fc6b-4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bile", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "note": "The term 'gall' in early modern texts often referred to bile, a fluid produced by the liver, but could also refer to the gallbladder itself or gallstones.", "wikidata_id": "Q208006", "wikidata_description": "central district and city in Bilecik Province, Turkey", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bilecik", "wikipedia_extract": "Bilecik is a city in northwestern Anatolia, Turkey. It is the seat of Bilecik Province and Bilecik District. Its population is 74,457 (2021). The mayor is Melek M\u0131zrak Suba\u015f\u0131 (CHP). The town is famous for its numerous restored Turkish houses. It is increasingly becoming more attractive to tourists.", "description": "A humor of the body, associated with illness and bodily functions."}}, {"canonical_name": "photography", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "photography", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "photography", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 13, "variants": ["photography"], "contexts": ["art of creating permanent pictures", "art of capturing images with light"]}, {"entity_id": "photography", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Photography", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Photography"], "contexts": ["art or practice of taking and processing photographs"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "photography", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Photography", "similarity": 0.95}], "id": 2613, "stable_key": "clu_990ef851832a8b81", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Photography", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q11633", "wikidata_description": "art, science, and practice of creating durable images by recording light or other electromagnetic radiation"}}, {"canonical_name": "Grove", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 14, "members": [{"entity_id": "grove", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Grove", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 11, "variants": ["Grove"], "contexts": ["scientist who studied electricity and radiation", "gives paper, snow, and lime as instances"]}, {"entity_id": "grove", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Grove", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Grove"], "contexts": ["credited with enunciation of doctrine", "suggested connection between vital and physical forces"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Grove", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Grove", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2614, "stable_key": "clu_87ff717cb4df3cf4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "William Grove", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1811, "death_year": 1896, "description": "Scientist who studied electricity and radiation", "wikidata_id": "Q75743296", "wikidata_description": "Peerage person ID=311924"}}, {"canonical_name": "chien", "category": "ANIMAL", "subc