on": "Roman writer, author of Collectanea Rerum Memorabilium", "wikidata_id": "Q3489494", "wikidata_description": "genus of arachnids"}}, {"canonical_name": "cholera", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "choler", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Choler", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 6, "variants": ["choler", "Choler"], "contexts": ["one of the four humors, associated with anger", "a humor, likely bile"]}, {"entity_id": "cholera", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "cholera", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 7, "variants": ["cholera"], "contexts": ["humor evacuated by Ca\u00f1afistola", "bodily fluid purged by Pinones"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Choler", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "cholera", "similarity": 0.931}], "id": 2617, "stable_key": "clu_dd458b9d58fb3644", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Choler", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This refers to the ancient concept of one of the four humors, not the modern disease 'cholera'.", "wikipedia_extract": "Anger is an intense emotional state involving a strong, uncomfortable and non-cooperative response to a perceived provocation, hurt, or threat. A person experiencing anger will often experience physical effects, such as increased heart rate, elevated blood pressure, and increased levels of the stress hormones adrenaline and noradrenaline. Some view anger as an emotion that triggers part of the fight or flight response. Anger becomes the predominant feeling behaviorally, cognitively, and physiologically when a person makes the conscious choice to take action to immediately stop the threatening behavior of another outside force. Anger can have many physical and mental consequences. The external expression of anger can be found in facial expressions, body language, physiological responses, and at times public acts of aggression."}}, {"canonical_name": "Meteor.", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "meteors", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Meteors", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Meteors"], "contexts": ["Aristotle's work on natural phenomena", "atmospheric phenomena"]}, {"entity_id": "meteor_", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Meteor.", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 9, "variants": ["Meteor."], "contexts": ["Aristotle's work on meteorology", "meteorological publication"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Meteors", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Meteor.", "similarity": 0.905}], "id": 2618, "stable_key": "clu_101df4218b7bae46-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Meteorology", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q25261", "wikidata_description": "branch of science dealing with the study of atmospheric phenomena and the short term variation of weather conditions including wind, precipitation, temperature, humidity, cloud cover, and air pressure", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meteorology", "note": "Refers to Aristotle's work on atmospheric phenomena.", "wikipedia_extract": "Meteorology is the scientific study of the Earth's atmosphere and short-term atmospheric phenomena (i.e., weather), with a focus on weather forecasting. It has applications in the military, aviation, energy production, transport, agriculture, construction, weather warnings, and disaster management. Along with climatology, atmospheric physics, atmospheric chemistry, and aeronomy, meteorology forms the broader field of the atmospheric sciences. The interactions between Earth's atmosphere and its oceans (notably El Ni\u00f1o and La Ni\u00f1a) are studied in the interdisciplinary field of hydrometeorology. Other interdisciplinary areas include biometeorology, space weather, and planetary meteorology. Marine weather forecasting relates meteorology to maritime and coastal safety, based on atmospheric interactions with large bodies of water."}}, {"canonical_name": "Quinsie", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "quinsies", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Quinsies", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Quinsies"], "contexts": ["throat inflammation treated by Jews ear", "medical condition"]}, {"entity_id": "quinsie", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Quinsie", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 11, "variants": ["Quinsie"], "contexts": ["condition of the throat", "condition cured by the herb"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Quinsies", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Quinsie", "similarity": 0.936}], "id": 2619, "stable_key": "clu_187287a14db474f1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "quinsy", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "quinsy", "wikidata_id": "Q1586014", "wikidata_description": "pus due to an infection behind the tonsil"}}, {"canonical_name": "homem", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "homme", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "homme", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 5, "variants": ["homme"], "contexts": ["humanity, mankind", "human species"]}, {"entity_id": "homem", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "homem", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 8, "variants": ["homem", "Homem", "homes", "hum homem"], "contexts": ["man suffering from itching", "human"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "homme", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "homem", "similarity": 0.975}], "id": 2620, "stable_key": "clu_39b405f3db4c3e00", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "human", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'homem' and its English cognates appear in contexts discussing the male gender, humans in general, and specific medical conditions affecting individuals."}}, {"canonical_name": "Groenland", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "gro\u00ebnland", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "Gro\u00ebnland", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 5, "variants": ["Gro\u00ebnland"], "contexts": ["landmass separated from Europe", "land possibly connected to America"]}, {"entity_id": "groenland", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "Groenland", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Groenland"], "contexts": ["large island north of North America", "large island nation"]}, {"entity_id": "groenland", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Groenland", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 6, "variants": ["Groenland", "Gr\u00f4nland"], "contexts": ["Greenland", "location mentioned in reference"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Gro\u00ebnland", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Groenland", "similarity": 0.793}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Groenland", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Groenland", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2621, "stable_key": "clu_7ad6acf13f371139-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Greenland", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Greenland", "wikidata_id": "Q223", "wikidata_description": "autonomous territory of the Kingdom of Denmark and world's largest island", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenland", "wikipedia_extract": "Greenland is an autonomous territory of the Kingdom of Denmark and is the largest of the kingdom's three constituent parts by land area, the others being Denmark proper and the Faroe Islands. Citizens of Greenland are citizens of Denmark. They are thus citizens of the European Union (EU), although Greenland is not part of the EU. It is the world's largest island and lies between the Arctic and Atlantic oceans, east of the Canadian Arctic Archipelago. It shares a small 1.2-kilometre (0.75 mi) border with Canada on Hans Island. The capital and largest city is Nuuk."}}, {"canonical_name": "Californie", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "californie", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "Californie", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Californie"], "contexts": ["peninsula in North America"]}, {"entity_id": "californie", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Californie", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 12, "variants": ["Californie"], "contexts": ["region in North America", "Former administrative division in Venezuela"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Californie", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Californie", "similarity": 0.877}], "id": 2622, "stable_key": "clu_73889f53e1801f40", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "California", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United States", "wikidata_id": "Q16047894", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants"}}, {"canonical_name": "Irlande", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "irlande", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "Irlande", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Irlande"], "contexts": ["island nation separated from England"]}, {"entity_id": "irlande", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Irlande", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 12, "variants": ["Irlande"], "contexts": ["Country", "country with a higher relative population"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Irlande", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Irlande", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2623, "stable_key": "clu_3d9767a1fab5626a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ireland", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "An island country with a notable population density.", "wikipedia_extract": "Ireland is an island in the North Atlantic Ocean, in Northwestern Europe. Geopolitically, the island is divided between the Republic of Ireland (officially named Ireland \u2013 a sovereign state covering five-sixths of the island) and Northern Ireland (part of the United Kingdom \u2013 covering the remaining sixth). It is separated from Great Britain to its east by the North Channel, the Irish Sea, and St George's Channel. Ireland is the second-largest island of the British Isles, the third-largest in Europe, and the twentieth-largest in the world. As of 2022, the population of the entire island is just over 7 million, with 5.1 million in the Republic of Ireland and 1.9 million in Northern Ireland, ranking it the second-most populous island in Europe after Great Britain. The geography of Ireland comprises relatively low-lying mountains surrounding a central plain, with several navigable rivers extending inland."}}, {"canonical_name": "tertiary formations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "tertiary_formations", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "tertiary formations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["tertiary formations"], "contexts": ["geological strata studied for species disappearance", "geological formations from the tertiary period"]}, {"entity_id": "tertiary_formation", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "tertiary formation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["tertiary formation"], "contexts": ["geological period"]}, {"entity_id": "tertiary_formations", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "tertiary formations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 10, "variants": ["tertiary formations", "Tertiary formations"], "contexts": ["geological period", "geological time period and rock layers"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "tertiary formations", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "tertiary formations", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "tertiary formation", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "tertiary formations", "similarity": 0.96}], "id": 2624, "stable_key": "clu_cd4609353a51c51f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tertiary Period", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q188645", "wikidata_description": "obsolete term for the geological period 66\u20132.58 million years ago", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tertiary%20period", "note": "Refers to the geological period, specifically the Neogene and Paleogene periods in modern classification.", "wikipedia_extract": "The Tertiary ( TUR-sh\u0259r-ee, US also  TUR-shee-err-ee) is an obsolete geologic period spanning 66 million to 2.6 or 1.8 million years ago. The period began with the extinction of the non-avian dinosaurs in the Cretaceous\u2013Paleogene extinction event, at the start of the Cenozoic Era, and extended to the beginning of the Quaternary glaciation at the end of the Pliocene Epoch. The Tertiary has not been recognised by the International Commission on Stratigraphy (ICS) since the late 1980s, with the timespan of the Tertiary now being split into the earlier Paleogene and the more recent Neogene periods, though the Tertiary continues to be used in some scientific publications. The term Tertiary was first used by Giovanni Arduino during the mid-18th century. He classified geologic time into primitive (or primary), secondary, and tertiary periods based on observations of geology in Northern Italy. Later a fourth period, the Quaternary, was applied."}}, {"canonical_name": "Resemblance", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "resemblance", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "resemblance", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["resemblance"], "contexts": ["similarity between individuals"]}, {"entity_id": "resemblance", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Resemblance", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 11, "variants": ["RESEMBLANCE", "Resemblance", "resemblance"], "contexts": ["similarity between things", "similarity in a fundamental respect"]}, {"entity_id": "resemblances", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "resemblances", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["resemblances"], "contexts": ["type of relation between ideas"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "resemblance", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Resemblance", "similarity": 0.952}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "resemblance", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "resemblances", "similarity": 0.937}], "id": 2625, "stable_key": "clu_4d3876a9fda938cd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Resemblance", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q105227892", "wikidata_description": "relation of resemblance between objects"}}, {"canonical_name": "plantes dicotyl\u00e9dones", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "dicotyledonous_plants", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "dicotyledonous plants", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["dicotyledonous plants"], "contexts": ["type of plants that germinated"]}, {"entity_id": "plantes_dicotyl\u00e9dones", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "plantes dicotyl\u00e9dones", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 12, "variants": ["plantes dicotyl\u00e9dones", "Monocotyd\u00e9lones", "Monocotyl\u00e9don\u00e9es", "dicotyl\u00e9don", "dicotyl\u00e9dones", "monocotyledones", "monocotyl\u00e9dones", "plantes monocolyl\u00e9dones arborescentes", "plantes monocotyl\u00e9dones", "plantes monocotyl\u00ebdones", "v\u00e9g\u00e9taux monocotyl\u00e9dons"], "contexts": ["dicotyledonous plants", "botanical family classification"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "dicotyledonous plants", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "plantes dicotyl\u00e9dones", "similarity": 0.949}], "id": 2626, "stable_key": "clu_083af715b32d8b78", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Dicotyledonous plants", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "note": "Dicotyledonous plants are now classified within the clade Angiosperms, but 'dicotyledonous' remains a valid descriptive term for a large group.", "wikidata_id": "Q8316", "wikidata_description": "historical grouping of flowering plants"}}, {"canonical_name": "chocolate", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "chocolate", "book_id": "quatro_libro