887348241abac", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "gum arabic", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Senegalia senegal (L.) Britton", "family": "Fabaceae", "note": "The term 'Gomma arabica' and 'gomas' in the context of resins clearly points to gum arabic, commonly derived from Acacia species, now often classified in Senegalia.", "wikidata_id": "Q1621617", "wikidata_description": "species of plant"}}, {"canonical_name": "temperamento", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "temperamento", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "temperamento", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["temperamento"], "contexts": ["temperament of the plant"]}, {"entity_id": "temperamento", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "temperamento", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 12, "variants": ["temperamento", "quentura do temperamento"], "contexts": ["bodily temperament", "bodily constitution and environmental influence"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "temperamento", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "temperamento", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2629, "stable_key": "clu_c5b0eb97bf380292", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "temperament", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the historical humoral theory of bodily temperament, as well as the inherent qualities of substances like plants.", "wikidata_id": "Q35548456", "wikidata_description": "scientific article published on January 1, 1992"}}, {"canonical_name": "Hist. de las Indias", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "hist__de_las_indias", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Hist. de las Indias", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 12, "variants": ["Hist. de las Indias", "Hist. de Ind.", "Hist. de Ind. occid.", "Hist. de las Jndias", "Hist. de ta Revo/.", "Hist. del Nuevo Mnndo", "Hist. des Antilles"], "contexts": ["historical text", "historical work by Gomara"]}, {"entity_id": "the_history_of_\u00edndia", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "The history of \u00cdndia", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["The history of \u00cdndia"], "contexts": ["book by Elphinstone"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Hist. de las Indias", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "The history of \u00cdndia", "similarity": 0.891}], "id": 2630, "stable_key": "clu_d74e0e842797d481", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "History of the Indies", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "description": "Historical text detailing the Americas, often attributed to Gomara.", "note": "The primary context refers to Gonzalo Fern\u00e1ndez de Oviedo y Vald\u00e9s's 'Historia general y natural de las Indias'.", "wikipedia_extract": "The Treaty of the Danish West Indies (Danish: Vestindiens traktat), officially the Convention between the United States and Denmark for cession of the Danish West Indies (Danish: Konventionen mellem USA og Danmark), was a 1916 treaty transferring sovereignty of the Danish West Indies from Denmark to the United States in exchange for a sum of US$25,000,000 in gold ($722 million in 2024), and agreement to cede US interest in Greenland. It is one of the most recent permanent expansions of United States territory. Two of the islands had been in Danish possession since the 17th century and St. Croix since 1733. The glory days of the colony had been from around 1750 to 1850, based on transit trade and the production of rum and sugar using African slaves as labour. By the second half of the 19th century, the sugar production was embattled by the cultivation of sugar beets, and although the slaves had been emancipated in 1848, the agricultural land and the trade was still controlled by the white population, and the living conditions of the descendants of the slaves were poor."}}, {"canonical_name": "Coro", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "coro", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Coro", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 12, "variants": ["Coro"], "contexts": ["province of Coro", "place name in Venezuela"]}, {"entity_id": "coro", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Coro", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Coro"], "contexts": ["location in arid plains"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Coro", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Coro", "similarity": 0.754}], "id": 2631, "stable_key": "clu_79a780c4fec522e6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Coro", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Venezuela", "wikidata_id": "Q1134454", "wikidata_description": "capital of Falc\u00f3n State and the oldest city in the west of Venezuela"}}, {"canonical_name": "Antilhas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "antilhas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Antilhas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Antilhas"], "contexts": ["region where guaiacam grows"]}, {"entity_id": "antilhas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Antilhas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 12, "variants": ["Antilhas"], "contexts": ["origin of 'p\u00e0o santo'", "source of false-santo"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Antilhas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Antilhas", "similarity": 0.886}], "id": 2632, "stable_key": "clu_64e293d18f9383a3-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Antilles", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Archipelago in the Caribbean Sea, comprising numerous islands.", "wikipedia_extract": "The Antilles is an archipelago within the West Indies in the Caribbean region of the Americas. It is bordered by the Caribbean Sea to the south and west, the Gulf of Mexico to the northwest, and the North Atlantic Ocean to the north and east. The Antillean islands are divided into two smaller archipelagos: the Greater Antilles and the Lesser Antilles. The Greater Antilles include the Cayman Islands and larger islands of Cuba, Hispaniola (subdivided into the nations of the Dominican Republic and Haiti), Navassa Island, Jamaica, and Puerto Rico. The Lesser Antilles contains the northerly Leeward Islands and the southeasterly Windward Islands, as well as the Leeward Antilles immediately north of Venezuela. The Lucayan Archipelago, consisting of The Bahamas and the Turks and Caicos Islands, though a part of the West Indies, is generally not included among the Antillean islands."}}, {"canonical_name": "Bauza", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "bauza", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Bauza", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 10, "variants": ["Bauza"], "contexts": ["contributor to data on Cuba", "scholar who made barometric measurements"]}, {"entity_id": "bayle", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Bayle", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Bayle"], "contexts": ["law of rarefaction", "law on atmospheric density in space"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Bauza", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Bayle", "similarity": 0.852}], "id": 2633, "stable_key": "clu_1ccc5c9fed57e3be", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Felipe Bauz\u00e1", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1764, "death_year": 1834, "description": "Spanish naval officer and geographer, involved with Cuban data", "note": "The 'Bayle' mention is likely a misspelling or confusion, as Bauz\u00e1 is strongly associated with the geographical data mentioned."}}, {"canonical_name": "grauwacke", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "grauwacke", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "grauwacke", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 9, "variants": ["grauwacke", "Graawakke", "grauvcackes", "grauwakke"], "contexts": ["type of sedimentary rock", "fragmentary rock type"]}, {"entity_id": "graywacke", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "graywacke", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["graywacke"], "contexts": ["material for building furnaces", "rock type"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "grauwacke", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "graywacke", "similarity": 0.885}], "id": 2634, "stable_key": "clu_21e80fba5938363d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Graywacke", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral"}}, {"canonical_name": "muschelkalk", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "muschelkalk", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "muschelkalk", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 7, "variants": ["muschelkalk", "mnachelkalk", "muscheikalk"], "contexts": ["geological formation name", "limestone type"]}, {"entity_id": "muschelkalk", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "muschelkalk", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 6, "variants": ["muschelkalk"], "contexts": ["geological formation", "geological rock formation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "muschelkalk", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "muschelkalk", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2635, "stable_key": "clu_7d032b3f39204c34", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Muschelkalk", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "This refers to a specific geological formation.", "wikidata_id": "Q60882191", "wikidata_description": "special protection area in the EU defined by the birds directive in Th\u00fcringen, Germany", "wikipedia_extract": "Nemertea is a phylum of animals also known as ribbon worms or proboscis worms, consisting of about 1300 known species. Most ribbon worms are very slim, usually only a few millimeters wide, although a few have relatively short but wide bodies. Many have patterns of yellow, orange, red and green coloration. The foregut, stomach and intestine run a little below the midline of the body, the anus is at the tip of the tail, and the mouth is under the front. A little above the gut is the rhynchocoel, a cavity which mostly runs above the midline and ends a little short of the rear of the body. All species have a proboscis which lies in the rhynchocoel when inactive but everts to emerge just above the mouth to capture the animal's prey with venom.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nemertea"}}, {"canonical_name": "Sy\u00e9nite", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "sy\u00e9nite", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Sy\u00e9nite", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 7, "variants": ["Sy\u00e9nite", "sy\u00e9nite"], "contexts": ["rock fragments found scattered", "rock type"]}, {"entity_id": "syenite", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "syenite", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 6, "variants": ["syenite"], "contexts": ["igneous rock", "igneous rock type"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Sy\u00e9nite", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "syenite", "similarity": 0.97}], "id": 2636, "stable_key": "clu_79f7f542450813b0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Syenite", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral"}}, {"canonical_name": "South Sea", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "south_sea", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "South Sea", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 12, "variants": ["South Sea"], "contexts": ["region where observer was in 1803", "ocean with oval configuration of isogonic lines"]}, {"entity_id": "south_sea_islands", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "South Sea islands", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["South Sea islands"], "contexts": ["islands with low tides"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "South Sea", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "South Sea islands", "similarity": 0.906}], "id": 2637, "stable_key": "clu_a911490fe9a26789-4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pacific Ocean", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "N/A", "note": "'Mer du Sud' and 'South Sea' are historical names for the Pacific Ocean.", "wikidata_id": "Q844857", "wikidata_description": "geographic region consisting of the islands of the Pacific Ocean", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pacific%20Islands"}}, {"canonical_name": "practice", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "practice", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "practice", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 11, "variants": ["practice", "PRACTICE", "Practice"], "contexts": ["repeated performance to improve skill", "reduces reaction time"]}, {"entity_id": "pratica", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "pratica", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 2, "variants": ["pratica"], "contexts": ["common usage or method", "custom or habit of eating betre"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "practice", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "pratica", "similarity": 0.948}], "id": 2638, "stable_key": "clu_9773e2a29c3a1548", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "practice", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This cluster spans both the English 'practice' and Portuguese 'pratica', referring to the concept of repeated action for improvement or common usage."}}, {"canonical_name": "recognition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "recognition", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "recognition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 11, "variants": ["Recognition", "recognition"], "contexts": ["identifying something previously encountered", "identifying something known"]}, {"entity_id": "recognition", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "recognition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["recognition"], "contexts": ["identifying something as familiar", "act of identifying something"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "recognition", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "recognition", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2639, "stable_key": "clu_cd9dc6e8bde90da8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Recognition", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q21142755", "wikidata_description": "mental process; the act of recognizing or the condition of being recognized"}}, {"canonical_name": "unconsciousness", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "unconsciousness", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "unconsciousness", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 10, "variants": ["Unconsciousness", "unconsciousness"], "contexts": ["lack of awareness", "symptom in hysterics"]}, {"entity_id": "unconsciousness", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "unconsciousness", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 3, "variants": ["unconsciousness"], "contexts": ["lack of awareness", "state of being unaware"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "unconsciousness", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "unconsciousness", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2640, "stable_key": "clu_441339b853e963a6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Unconsciousness", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Unconsciousness", "wikidata_id": "Q12829165", "wikidata_description": "the state, in a normally conscious being, of not being conscious", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unconsciousness", "wikipedia_extract": "Unconsciousness is a state in which a living individual exhibits a complete, or near-complete, inability to maintain an awareness of self and environment or to respond to any human or environmental stimulus. Unconsciousness may occur as the result of traumatic brain injury, brain hypoxia (inadequate oxygen, possibly due to a brain infarction or cardiac arrest), severe intoxication with drugs that depress the activity of the central nervous system (e.g., alcohol and other hypnotic or sedative drugs), severe fatigue, pain, anaesthesia, and other causes. Loss of consciousness should not be confused with the notion of the psychoanalytic unconscious, cognitive processes that take place outside awareness (e.g., implicit cognition), and with altered states of consciousness such as sleep, delirium, hypnosis, and other altered states in which the person responds to stimuli, including trance and psychedelic experiences. Heart failure (HF) (congestive heart failure (CHF)) Myocardial infarction (MI) (heart attack) Intracerebral hemorrhage (hemorrhagic stroke) Traumatic brain injury (TBI) (intracranial injury) Acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) In jurisprudence, unconsciousness may entitle the criminal defendant to the defense of automatism, i.e. a state without control of one's own actions, an excusing condition that allows a defendant to argue that they should not be held criminally liable for their actions or omissions. In most countries, courts must consider whether unconsciousness in a situation can be accepted as a defense; it can vary from case to case."}}, {"canonical_name": "Personality", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "personality", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Personality", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 10, "variants": ["Personality", "personality"], "contexts": ["quality of being a person", "individual character and identity"]}, {"entity_id": "personality", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "personality", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["personality"], "contexts": ["the enduring self or individual being", "quality of being a person"]}, {"entity_id": "personalities", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "personalities", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["personalities"], "contexts": ["distinctive qualities of individuals"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Personality", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "personality", "similarity": 0.989}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Personality", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "personalities", "similarity": 0.958}], "id": 2641, "stable_key": "clu_42de2b8f5646ee08", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Personality", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q641118", "wikidata_description": "psychological characteristics of an individual (usually referring to only humans)"}}, {"canonical_name": "psychological", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "psychological", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "psychological", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 7, "variants": ["psychological"], "contexts": ["relating to the mind and its functions", "relating to the mind"]}, {"entity_id": "psychologic", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "psychologic", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["psychologic"], "contexts": ["relating to the mind and its processes", "relating to psychology"]}, {"entity_id": "psychologically", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "psychologically", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["psychologically"], "contexts": ["relating to the mind"]}, {"entity_id": "psychological", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "psychological", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["psychological"], "contexts": ["relating to the mind and behavior"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "psychological", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "psychological", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "psychologic", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "psychological", "similarity": 0.977}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "psychologically", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "psychological", "similarity": 0.908}], "id": 2642, "stable_key": "clu_1e28969b7cac1141", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Psychological", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q7256364", "wikidata_description": "academic journal"}}, {"canonical_name": "Pressure", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "pressures", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "pressures", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["pressures"], "contexts": ["felt physical force"]}, {"entity_id": "pressure", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Pressure", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 12, "variants": ["pressure", "Pressure"], "contexts": ["force applied over an area", "force per unit area"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "pressures", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Pressure", "similarity": 0.94}], "id": 2643, "stable_key": "clu_7b74fed8b6427604", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pressure", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q123286893", "wikidata_description": "2013 videogame"}}, {"canonical_name": "Christian era", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "christian_era", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Christian era", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Christian era"], "contexts": ["historical period"]}, {"entity_id": "christian_era", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Christian era", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 12, "variants": ["Christian era", "Christian Era"], "contexts": ["calendar era based on Christ's birth", "historical time period"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Christian era", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Christian era", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2644, "stable_key": "clu_45e080e18e887488", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Christian Era", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q208141", "wikidata_description": "modern calendar era"}}, {"canonical_name": "physical geography", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "physical_geography", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "physical geography", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 11, "variants": ["Physical Geography", "physical geography"], "contexts": ["field of study on Asia", "study of Earth's natural phenomena"]}, {"entity_id": "physical_geography", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Physical Geography", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["PHYSICAL GEOGRAPHY", "Physical Geography"], "contexts": ["book by Mary Somerville", "book title"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "physical geography", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Physical Geography", "similarity": 0.988}], "id": 2645, "stable_key": "clu_c303cfb841566c71", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Physical Geography", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "feldspar", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "feldspar", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "feldspar", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 10, "variants": ["feldspar"], "contexts": ["mineral ejected from crater", "mineral decomposing in granites and porphyries"]}, {"entity_id": "feldspar", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "feldspar", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["feldspar"], "contexts": ["common rock-forming mineral", "mineral component of granite"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "feldspar", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "feldspar", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2646, "stable_key": "clu_1b13dce85cb7aed8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "feldspar", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "Includes 'Fluor-spar' which is a specific type of fluorspar (calcium fluoride) but also often discussed in relation to feldspars and general mineralogy of the time. However, 'feldspar' appears more frequently and broadly.", "wikidata_id": "Q170258", "wikidata_description": "mineral group", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Feldspar", "wikipedia_extract": "Feldspar ( FEL(D)-spar; sometimes spelled felspar) is a group of rock-forming aluminium tectosilicate minerals, also containing other cations such as sodium, calcium, potassium, or barium. The most common members of the feldspar group are the plagioclase (sodium-calcium) feldspars and the alkali (potassium-sodium) feldspars. Feldspars make up about 60% of the Earth's crust and 41% of the Earth's continental crust by weight. Feldspars crystallize from magma as both intrusive and extrusive igneous rocks and are also present in many types of metamorphic rock. Rock formed almost entirely of calcic plagioclase feldspar is known as anorthosite. Feldspars are also found in many types of sedimentary rocks."}}, {"canonical_name": "nickel", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "nickel", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "nickel", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 7, "variants": ["nickel"], "contexts": ["metal found in meteoric dust", "element found in dust"]}, {"entity_id": "nickel", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Nickel", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 6, "variants": ["Nickel", "nickel"], "contexts": ["metal in protoxides", "affected by magnet when at rest"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "nickel", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Nickel", "similarity": 0.939}], "id": 2647, "stable_key": "clu_40a6134ef5251595", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "nickel", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q744", "wikidata_description": "chemical element with symbol Ni and atomic number 28"}}, {"canonical_name": "heavenly bodies", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "heavenly_bodies", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "heavenly bodies", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 6, "variants": ["heavenly bodies"], "contexts": ["celestial objects in space", "celestial objects like stars and planets"]}, {"entity_id": "celestial_bodies", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "celestial bodies", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 4, "variants": ["celestial bodies"], "contexts": ["natural objects in space", "objects falling from space"]}, {"entity_id": "celestial_bodies", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "celestial bodies", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 3, "variants": ["celestial bodies"], "contexts": ["objects in space like planets and stars", "vast oscillations performed by vast celestial bodies"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "heavenly bodies", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "celestial bodies", "similarity": 0.971}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "celestial bodies", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "celestial bodies", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2648, "stable_key": "clu_62258e314ba30463", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "celestial bodies", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "Q1519541", "wikidata_description": "natural object visible in the sky to the naked eye"}}, {"canonical_name": "vapores acres", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "vapors", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "vapors", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 6, "variants": ["vapors"], "contexts": ["gaseous state of matter", "gaseous substances"]}, {"entity_id": "vapores_acres", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "vapores acres", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 7, "variants": ["vapores acres", "vapores corruptos", "vapores craffos", "vapores daterra", "vapores fetidos"], "contexts": ["acrid vapors", "acrid vapors, a type of vapor"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "vapors", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "vapores acres", "similarity": 0.907}], "id": 2649, "stable_key": "clu_5367e8a333e122bb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Acrid vapors", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "forces", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "forces", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "forces", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["forces"], "contexts": ["influences that cause change or motion", "pervading the universe"]}, {"entity_id": "forces", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "forces", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 8, "variants": ["forces"], "contexts": ["agents causing change in motion or state", "agents causing change in motion"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "forces", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "forces", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2650, "stable_key": "clu_1bfd4bdc25e283a2-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Force", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to physical force, a fundamental concept in mechanics and physics."}}, {"canonical_name": "Circular polarization", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "polarization", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "polarization", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["polarization"], "contexts": ["property of light", "property of light in atmosphere"]}, {"entity_id": "circular_polarization", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Circular polarization", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 11, "variants": ["circular polarization", "Circular polarization", "Circular Polarization"], "contexts": ["polarized light with circular waves", "polarization of light in a circle"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "polarization", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Circular polarization", "similarity": 0.923}], "id": 2651, "stable_key": "clu_fc49b79e9b4ff361", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Circular polarization", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "ellipsoid of revolution", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "ellipsoid_of_revolution", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "ellipsoid of revolution", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["ellipsoid of revolution"], "contexts": ["shape of a rotated ellipse"]}, {"entity_id": "elliptical_spheriod_of_revolution", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "elliptical spheriod of revolution", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["elliptical spheriod of revolution"], "contexts": ["mathematical shape of the Earth"]}, {"entity_id": "ellipsoid_of_revolution", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "ellipsoid of revolution", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 11, "variants": ["Ellipsoid of Revolution", "ellipsoid of revolution"], "contexts": ["spheroid formed by rotating an ellipse", "geometric shape of Earth"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "ellipsoid of revolution", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "ellipsoid of revolution", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "elliptical spheriod of revolution", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "ellipsoid of revolution", "similarity": 0.899}], "id": 2652, "stable_key": "clu_e469b110349810a4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ellipsoid of revolution", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q208395", "wikidata_description": "volume formed by rotating an ellipse around one of its axes; special case of ellipsoid"}}, {"canonical_name": "Aden", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "aden", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Aden", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Aden"], "contexts": ["location for monsoon naming"]}, {"entity_id": "aden", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Aden", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 12, "variants": ["Aden"], "contexts": ["port of call", "origin of goods to Diu"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Aden", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Aden", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2653, "stable_key": "clu_50e50dc7b3394572", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Aden", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Yemen", "wikidata_id": "Q131694", "wikidata_description": "port city and temporary capital of Yemen", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aden_Colony", "wikipedia_extract": "Aden Colony (Arabic: \u0645\u064f\u0633\u0652\u062a\u0652\u0639\u064e\u0645\u064e\u0631\u064e\u0629\u0652 \u0639\u064e\u062f\u064e\u0646\u0652, romanized: Musta'marat 'Adan) was a crown colony of the United Kingdom from 1937 to 1963 located in the southern part of modern-day Yemen. It consisted of the port city of Aden and also included the outlying islands of Kamaran, Perim and the Khuria Muria archipelago with a total area of 192 km2 (74 sq mi). Initially a key port for the British East India Company, it was annexed by the British in 1839 to secure maritime routes and prevent piracy in the Arabian Sea. Its strategic position at the entrance to the Red Sea made it a vital stopover for ships traveling between Europe, India, and the Far East, especially after the opening of the Suez Canal in 1869. Aden quickly became a major coaling station and transit hub for British shipping, and its significance to the British Empire grew throughout the 19th and early 20th centuries. Prior to 1937, Aden had been governed as part of British India (originally as the Aden Settlement subordinate to the Bombay Presidency, and then as a Chief Commissioner's province)."}}, {"canonical_name": "opilaciones", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "opilaciones", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "opilaciones", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 12, "variants": ["opilaciones", "Opilaciones", "opilaciones del bago", "opilaciones interi\u00f3res"], "contexts": ["obstructions", "obstructions treated by a substance"]}, {"entity_id": "opila\u00e7\u00f5es", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "opila\u00e7\u00f5es", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 1, "variants": ["opila\u00e7\u00f5es"], "contexts": ["condition for which remedies are given"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "opilaciones", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "opila\u00e7\u00f5es", "similarity": 0.985}], "id": 2654, "stable_key": "clu_9f4dde38de90902c-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Obstruction", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "Blockage or impediment in bodily passages or vessels.", "note": "Refers to the blockage of humors or vessels.", "wikipedia_extract": "Bowel obstruction, also known as intestinal obstruction, is a mechanical or functional obstruction of the intestines that prevents the normal movement of the products of digestion. Either the small bowel or large bowel may be affected. Signs and symptoms include abdominal pain, vomiting, bloating and not passing gas. Mechanical obstruction is the cause of about 5 to 15% of cases of severe abdominal pain of sudden onset requiring admission to hospital. Causes of bowel obstruction include adhesions, hernias, volvulus, endometriosis, inflammatory bowel disease, appendicitis, tumors, diverticulitis, ischemic bowel, tuberculosis and intussusception. Small bowel obstructions are most often due to adhesions and hernias, while large bowel obstructions are most often due to tumors and volvulus."}}, {"canonical_name": "purgar", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "purgas", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "purgas", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 2, "variants": ["purgas", "purgar"], "contexts": ["medical practice of cleansing the body", "medical practice for illness"]}, {"entity_id": "purgar", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "purgar", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 11, "variants": ["purgar", "purga", "purgando", "purgas", "purgativa", "purgativo", "purg\u00e3o"], "contexts": ["act of purging", "medical practice"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "purgas", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "purgar", "similarity": 0.916}], "id": 2655, "stable_key": "clu_9b7d1c8135f3f6b5-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Purging", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "Medical practice of cleansing the body, often with medicines.", "note": "Refers to inducing vomiting or bowel movements for medical purposes.", "wikipedia_extract": "In medicine, a cathartic is a substance that accelerates defecation. This is similar to a laxative, which is a substance that eases defecation, usually by softening feces. It is possible for a substance to be both a laxative and a cathartic. However, agents such as psyllium seed husks increase the bulk of the feces. Cathartics such as sorbitol, magnesium citrate, magnesium sulfate, or sodium sulfate were previously used as a form of gastrointestinal decontamination following poisoning via ingestion. They are no longer routinely recommended for poisonings."}}, {"canonical_name": "Mefue", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "mefue", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Mefue", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Mefue"], "contexts": ["ancient medical authority", "mentioned regarding ear ulcers"]}, {"entity_id": "mefue", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Mefue", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 11, "variants": ["Mefue"], "contexts": ["authority on plants", "medieval physician"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Mefue", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Mefue", "similarity": 0.662}], "id": 2656, "stable_key": "clu_3e58a3c171766280", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mefue", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "An early modern medical authority or physician.", "note": "The identity of 'Mefue' and 'Me' is uncertain; could refer to specific figures or generic terms for physicians."}}, {"canonical_name": "panaricio", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "panarizos", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "panarizos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["panarizos"], "contexts": ["ailment treated with iron powder"]}, {"entity_id": "panaricio", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "panaricio", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 12, "variants": ["panaricio", "Panaricio", "Panaricios", "panarici", "panariciopor", "panaricios"], "contexts": ["infection treated with cat's ear", "whitlow"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "panarizos", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "panaricio", "similarity": 0.883}], "id": 2657, "stable_key": "clu_bed4c7c2359ce0de", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "whitlow", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "A painful inflammation or infection of a finger or toe.", "wikipedia_extract": "A whitlow or felon is an infection of the tip of the finger. Herpetic whitlow and melanotic whitlow (subungual melanoma) are subtypes that are not synonymous with the term felon. A felon is an \"extremely painful abscess on the palmar aspect of the fingertip\". Whitlow usually refers to herpetic whitlow, though it can also refer to melanotic whitlow (subungual melanoma), which somewhat resembles acral lentiginous melanoma. The terms whitlow and felon are also sometimes misapplied to paronychia, which is an infection of the tissue at the side or base of the nail. Felon presents clinically with a throbbing pain."}}, {"canonical_name": "Sep\u00falveda", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "sep\u00falveda", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Sep\u00falveda", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 12, "variants": ["Sep\u00falveda"], "contexts": ["mentioned scholar", "author of 'Mandamus medicamentorum'"]}, {"entity_id": "sepulveda", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Sepulveda", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 1, "variants": ["Sepulveda"], "contexts": ["Physician of the Chamber of Philip III"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Sep\u00falveda", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Sepulveda", "similarity": 0.396}], "id": 2658, "stable_key": "clu_9ef98bf4b8e4e09f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Juan Gin\u00e9s de Sep\u00falveda", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1490, "death_year": 1573, "description": "Spanish philosopher and theologian, debated indigenous rights", "note": "The text mentions him as a scholar and physician to Philip III, which is incorrect. Juan Gin\u00e9s de Sep\u00falveda was primarily a philosopher and theologian, and though he lived in the 16th century, he was not known as a physician nor associated with Philip III."}}, {"canonical_name": "Espodio", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "espodio", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Espodio", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 11, "variants": ["Espodio", "espodio", "espodio metallico"], "contexts": ["preparation from burnt elephant bones", "substance discussed"]}, {"entity_id": "espodio", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "espodio", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["espodio"], "contexts": ["ingredient in a remedy for diabetes", "used in trociscos"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Espodio", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "espodio", "similarity": 0.672}], "id": 2659, "stable_key": "clu_4458a54f38aca1ed", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Spodium", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "A residue from furnaces or a substance derived from the East.", "note": "Likely a reference to a charcoal-like substance or mineral residue.", "wikipedia_extract": "Spodium, (Latin for ashes or soot) refers to burned bone (usually used for medical purposes), or the act of divination with ash. Spodium may also refer to other types of ash, such as the scrapings from the inside of a furnace. Spodium has a long history of medical usage, mentioned by Hippocrates and, for example, in the Medical Poem of Salerno \"...Who knows the cause why Spodium stancheth bleeding?...\" (in this case spodium referring to oxen bone Ashes)."}}, {"canonical_name": "triaga", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "triaga", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "triaga", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 11, "variants": ["triaga", "triacal", "triaga bebida", "triagua"], "contexts": ["theriac, compound medicine", "universal antidote"]}, {"entity_id": "triaca", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Triaca", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Triaca", "tria- ca"], "contexts": ["theriac (antidote)", "theriac, a complex antidote"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "triaga", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Triaca", "similarity": 0.906}], "id": 2660, "stable_key": "clu_325116fd0d502a0e-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Theriac", "confidence": "high", "type": "text", "note": "The text mentions 'Theriaca' as a compound medicine, and references Nicander's work on antidotes. This likely refers to Theriaca Andromachi, a complex polypharmaceutical preparation.", "wikidata_id": "Q1546149", "wikidata_description": "medieval medical concoction", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theriac", "wikipedia_extract": "Theriac or theriaca is a medical concoction originally labelled by the Greeks in the 1st century AD and widely adopted in the ancient world as far away as Persia, China and India via the trading links of the Silk Route. It was an alexipharmic, or antidote for a variety of poisons and diseases. It was also considered a panacea, a term for which it could be used interchangeably: in the 16th century Adam Lonicer wrote that garlic was the rustic's theriac or Heal-All. The word theriac comes from the Greek term \u03b8\u03b7\u03c1\u03b9\u03b1\u03ba\u03ae (th\u0113riak\u0113), a feminine adjective signifying \"pertaining to animals\", from \u03b8\u03b7\u03c1\u03af\u03bf\u03bd (th\u0113rion), \"wild animal, beast\". The ancient bestiaries included information\u2014often fanciful\u2014about dangerous beasts and their bites. When cane sugar was an exotic Eastern commodity, the English recommended the sugar-based treacle as an antidote against poison, originally applied as a salve.", "description": "A complex compound medicine or antidote, historically used as a panacea."}}, {"canonical_name": "melancia", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "melancia", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "melancia", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 4, "variants": ["melancia", "melancias"], "contexts": ["one of the four great cold seeds", "Portuguese name for water melon"]}, {"entity_id": "melancia", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "melancia", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 9, "variants": ["melancia", "Melancia", "melancias"], "contexts": ["seeds used for refreshing and cleansing kidneys", "watermelon used in remedy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "melancia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "melancia", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2661, "stable_key": "clu_c6d5e75ec4049478", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "watermelon", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Citrullus lanatus", "family": "Cucurbitaceae", "description": "A large, sweet fruit with a hard rind, often used for its cooling properties.", "wikipedia_extract": "The watermelon (Citrullus lanatus) is a species of flowering plant in the family Cucurbitaceae, that has a large, edible fruit. It is a scrambling and trailing vine-like plant, and is widely cultivated worldwide, with more than 1,000 varieties. Watermelons are grown in favorable climates from tropical to temperate regions worldwide for its large edible fruit, which is a berry with a hard rind and no internal divisions, and is botanically called a pepo. The sweet, juicy flesh is usually deep red to pink, with many seeds that are black when mature, white when immature. Seedless varieties also exist. The fruit can be eaten raw or pickled, and the rind is edible after cooking."}}, {"canonical_name": "azedas", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "azedas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "azedas", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["azedas"], "contexts": ["taste of the fruit", "taste quality of the fruits"]}, {"entity_id": "azedas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "azedas", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 11, "variants": ["Azedas", "azedas"], "contexts": ["sour", "sour grapes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "azedas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "azedas", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2662, "stable_key": "clu_2829259268b8c3dd-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "sour", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "Having a sharp, acidic taste or quality.", "note": "Refers to a taste quality.", "wikipedia_extract": "Sourness, also known as sour, is a basic taste sensation perceived through hydrogen ions from acidic compounds interacting with taste bud receptors, and it can also refer to an unpleasant or acidic quality in taste, disposition, or tone. It is a warning sign for potentially spoiled food, though it is balanced in some food products by sweetness, and it is associated with a low pH. The most common foods with natural sourness are fruits, such as lemon, lime, grape, orange, tamarind, and bitter melon. Fermented foods, such as wine, sour cream, vinegar or yogurt, may have sour taste. Children show a greater enjoyment of sour flavors than adults, and sour candy containing citric acid or malic acid is common."}}, {"canonical_name": "pernas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "pernas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "pernas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["pernas"], "contexts": ["third grade of camphor"]}, {"entity_id": "pernas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "pernas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 12, "variants": ["pernas", "perna"], "contexts": ["body part", "body part, for rubbing"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "pernas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "pernas", "similarity": 0.781}], "id": 2663, "stable_key": "clu_509549dd3d7f7b3b-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Legs", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "Q14856098", "wikidata_description": "family of plants", "note": "The context 'third grade of camphor' and 'body part' strongly suggests 'legs' as a translation of 'pernas', referring to a descriptive classification within herbalism or pharmacology, rather than anatomical legs."}}, {"canonical_name": "podagra", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "podagra", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "podagra", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 1, "variants": ["podagra"], "contexts": ["gout of the foot"]}, {"entity_id": "podagra", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "podagra", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 12, "variants": ["podagra", "Podagra", "podagre", "podagricos", "podagris"], "contexts": ["gout in the foot", "medical condition, likely gout"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "podagra", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "podagra", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2664, "stable_key": "clu_88100adab4afa6a2-4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Gout", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Gout", "wikidata_id": "Q133087", "wikidata_description": "medical condition that results in recurrent pain and swelling of joints", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gout", "wikipedia_extract": "Gout ( GOWT) is a form of inflammatory arthritis characterized by recurrent attacks of pain in a red, tender, hot, and swollen joint, caused by the deposition of needle-shaped crystals of the monosodium salt of uric acid. Pain typically comes on rapidly, reaching maximal intensity in less than 12 hours. In about half of cases, the joint at the base of the big toe is affected (Podagra). It may also result in tophi, kidney stones, or kidney damage. Gout is due to persistently elevated levels of uric acid (urate) in the blood (hyperuricemia). This occurs from a combination of diet, other health problems, and genetic factors.", "note": "The term 'gota coral' appears to be an early modern descriptor for gout, possibly with specific regional or contextual nuances, as suggested by the mention of 'coconut' in the context of treatment. However, the core disease is gout.", "description": "A metabolic disease causing joint inflammation."}}, {"canonical_name": "Coimbra", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "coimbra", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Coimbra", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Coimbra"], "contexts": ["university where Dimas Bosque completed studies"]}, {"entity_id": "coimbra", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Coimbra", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 12, "variants": ["Coimbra"], "contexts": ["location of university", "University city in Portugal"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Coimbra", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Coimbra", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2665, "stable_key": "clu_32aef4a13c9bc5bf", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Coimbra", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A historic university city in Portugal.", "note": "NuevaCoimbra is likely a reference to Coimbra or a settlement named after it.", "wikipedia_extract": "Coimbra, officially the City of Coimbra, is a city and a municipality in Portugal. The population of the municipality at the 2021 census was 140,796, in an area of 319.40 square kilometres (123.3 sq mi). It is the fourth-largest agglomerated urban area in Portugal after Lisbon, Porto, and Braga, and is the largest city of the district of Coimbra and the Central Region. About 460,000 people live in its intermunicipal community. Among the many archaeological structures dating back to the Roman era, when Coimbra was the settlement of Aeminium, are its well-preserved aqueduct and cryptoporticus. Similarly, buildings from the period when Coimbra was the capital of Portugal (from 1131 to 1255) still remain."}}, {"canonical_name": "red rays", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "red_rays", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "red rays", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 12, "variants": ["red rays"], "contexts": ["part of the solar spectrum", "part of the spectrum with least dispersion"]}, {"entity_id": "red_rays", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "red rays", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["red rays"], "contexts": ["light with longest undulations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "red rays", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "red rays", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2666, "stable_key": "clu_47a6010ac77913d1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Red Light", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q20266704", "wikidata_description": "painting by Francis Piatek"}}, {"canonical_name": "spheroid", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 13, "members": [{"entity_id": "spheroid", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "spheroid", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 10, "variants": ["Spheroid", "spheroid"], "contexts": ["nearly spherical shape", "shape resembling a sphere"]}, {"entity_id": "spheroid", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "spheroid", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 3, "variants": ["spheroid"], "contexts": ["imperfectly specific geometric figure", "celestial body shape"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "spheroid", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "spheroid", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2667, "stable_key": "clu_3bf50a66c94877de", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Spheroid", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "esprit", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "esprit", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "esprit", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 11, "variants": ["esprit"], "contexts": ["mind, intellect", "mind, intellect, or spirit"]}, {"entity_id": "spirit", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "spirit", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["spirit"], "contexts": ["wearied by external influences"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "esprit", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "spirit", "similarity": 0.971}], "id": 2668, "stable_key": "clu_828798a6efdb35db-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Vital spirits", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "In humoral theory, the animating essence of the body, responsible for life and function.", "note": "This refers to the ancient and medieval concept of vital spirits, a fundamental part of humoral medicine, distinct from modern understanding of biological processes.", "wikipedia_extract": "Vitalism is an idea that living organisms are differentiated from the non-living by the presence of forces, properties or powers including those which may not be physical  or chemical. Varied forms of vitalist theories were held in former times and they are now considered pseudoscientific concepts. Where vitalism explicitly invokes a vital principle, that element is often referred to as the \"vital spark\", \"energy\", \"\u00e9lan vital\" (coined by vitalist Henri Bergson), \"vital force\", or \"vis vitalis\", which some equate with the soul. In the 18th and 19th centuries, vitalism was discussed among biologists, between those belonging to the mechanistic school who felt that the known mechanics of physics would eventually explain the difference between life and non-life and vitalists who argued that the processes of life could not be reduced to a mechanistic process. Vitalist biologists such as Johannes Reinke proposed testable hypotheses meant to show inadequacies with mechanistic explanations, but their experiments failed to provide support for vitalism. Biologists now consider vitalism in this sense to have been refuted by empirical evidence, and hence regard it either as a superseded scientific theory, or as a pseudoscience since the mid-20th century."}}, {"canonical_name": "maladie", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "maladie", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "maladie", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 11, "variants": ["maladie"], "contexts": ["a disorder of structure or function", "medical condition being studied"]}, {"entity_id": "enfermedad", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "enfermedad", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["enfermedad"], "contexts": ["illness, singular cure"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "maladie", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "enfermedad", "similarity": 0.982}], "id": 2669, "stable_key": "clu_b94102ef122dc9b2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "disease", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "disease", "note": "This is a general term for 'illness' or 'malady' that applies broadly to any pathological condition.", "wikidata_id": "Q18123741", "wikidata_description": "disease caused by infection of pathogenic biological agents in a host organism", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infectious%20disease"}}, {"canonical_name": "force vitale", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "force_vitale", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "force vitale", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 11, "variants": ["force vitale"], "contexts": ["essential life force", "vital force theory"]}, {"entity_id": "living_force", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "living force", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["living force"], "contexts": ["synonym for vis viva or kinetic energy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "force vitale", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "living force", "similarity": 0.903}], "id": 2670, "stable_key": "clu_9ef8f7d2352aecf8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "vital force", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the historical philosophical and scientific concept of a vital force animating living organisms.", "wikidata_id": "Q137271198", "wikidata_description": "nonprofit organisation from the Netherlands"}}, {"canonical_name": "ph\u00e9nom\u00e8nes naturels", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "ph\u00e9nom\u00e8nes_naturels", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "ph\u00e9nom\u00e8nes naturels", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 7, "variants": ["ph\u00e9nom\u00e8nes naturels"], "contexts": ["natural phenomena", "observable occurrences in nature"]}, {"entity_id": "natural_phenomena", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "natural phenomena", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["natural phenomena", "Natural Phenomena"], "contexts": ["observable events in the natural world", "events in the natural world"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "ph\u00e9nom\u00e8nes naturels", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "natural phenomena", "similarity": 0.974}], "id": 2671, "stable_key": "clu_cb9e971a15875687-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "phenomena", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q16722960", "wikidata_description": "philosophical concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "Praxis Medica", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "pratique_m\u00e9dicale", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "pratique m\u00e9dicale", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 4, "variants": ["pratique m\u00e9dicale"], "contexts": ["application of medical knowledge", "medical practice"]}, {"entity_id": "praxis_medica", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Praxis Medica", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 8, "variants": ["Praxis Medica", "Prax Medic.", "Prax. Medic. admir.", "Praxis med"], "contexts": ["a medical practice text", "title of a medical work"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "pratique m\u00e9dicale", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Praxis Medica", "similarity": 0.897}], "id": 2672, "stable_key": "clu_4203021a915bea7f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "medical practice", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the application of medical knowledge and techniques, as well as potentially a text on the subject ('Praxis Medica').", "wikidata_id": "Q70488599", "wikidata_description": "organization and operation of the