u00e8ge", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "Norv\u00e8ge", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Norv\u00e8ge"], "contexts": ["landmass adjacent to Greenland", "land between Scotland and Greenland"]}, {"entity_id": "norw\u00e8ge", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Norw\u00e8ge", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 8, "variants": ["Norw\u00e8ge", "Nonv\u00e8ge"], "contexts": ["country where Tifi\u00e2rd is located", "country"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Norv\u00e8ge", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Norw\u00e8ge", "similarity": 0.99}], "id": 2682, "stable_key": "clu_6c1b17363b16ece0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Norway", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Norway", "wikidata_id": "Q184224", "wikidata_description": "species of mammal", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brown%20rat", "wikipedia_extract": "The brown rat (Rattus norvegicus), also known as the common rat, street rat, sewer rat, wharf rat, Hanover rat, Norway rat and Norwegian rat, is a widespread, common species of rat. One of the largest muroids, it is a brown or grey rodent with a body length of up to 28 cm (11 in) long, and a tail slightly shorter than that. It weighs between 140 and 500 g (4.9 and 17.6 oz). Thought to have originated in northern China and neighbouring areas, it has now spread to all continents except Antarctica, and is the dominant rat in Europe and much of North America, having become naturalised across the world. With rare exceptions, the brown rat lives wherever humans live, particularly in urban areas. They are omnivorous, reproduce rapidly, and can be a vector for several human diseases."}}, {"canonical_name": "Gr\u00e8ce", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "gr\u00e8ce", "book_id": "ep