on": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sulliman"}}, {"canonical_name": "Conquistadores", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "conquistadores", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Conquistadores", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 11, "variants": ["Conquistadores", "Conquistador", "Conquistador Diego deSicuessa", "conquistadores"], "contexts": ["Spanish conquerors of the Americas", "Spanish conquerors"]}, {"entity_id": "conqui\u017ftadores", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "conqui\u017ftadores", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["conqui\u017ftadores"], "contexts": ["conquerors"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Conquistadores", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "conqui\u017ftadores", "similarity": 0.845}], "id": 2706, "stable_key": "clu_e8c0d5435ef812ab", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Conquistadors", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Spanish conquerors in the Americas, especially during the colonial period."}}, {"canonical_name": "Payta", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "payta", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Payta", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 11, "variants": ["Payta", "Paita", "Paytas"], "contexts": ["port touched by Duperrey", "city on the coast of Peru"]}, {"entity_id": "payta", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Payta", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Payta"], "contexts": ["shore south of this location"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Payta", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Payta", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2707, "stable_key": "clu_a62d5b1e7962a3c1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Paita", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Peru", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_extract": "Paita is a city in northwestern Peru. It is the capital of the Paita Province which is in the Piura Region. It is a leading seaport in the region. Paita is located 1,089 km northwest of the country's capital Lima, and 57 km northwest of the regional capital of Piura. Starting in 2014, the city has considered ideas for separating from the Piura Region, proclaiming itself as the \"Miguel Grau Region\". Paita is located at 05\u00b005\u203228\u2033S 81\u00b006\u203223\u2033W on a small peninsula south of the mouth of the R\u00edo Chira on an area of 1,785 km2."}}, {"canonical_name": "Sahara", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "sahara", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Sahara", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 9, "variants": ["Sahara"], "contexts": ["Sahara desert", "large desert in North Africa"]}, {"entity_id": "sahara_desert", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Sahara desert", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Sahara desert"], "contexts": ["large desert in North Africa", "large desert in Africa"]}, {"entity_id": "sahara", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Sahara", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Sahara"], "contexts": ["causing monsoons"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Sahara", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Sahara desert", "similarity": 0.957}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Sahara", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Sahara", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2708, "stable_key": "clu_4e7d97462e650141", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sahara Desert", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Algeria, Chad, Egypt, Libya, Mali, Mauritania, Morocco, Niger, Sudan, Tunisia, Western Sahara", "wikidata_id": "Q1717126", "wikidata_description": "species of insect", "wikipedia_extract": "The Sahara (, ) is a desert spanning across North Africa. With an area of 9,200,000 square kilometres (3,600,000 sq mi), it is the largest hot desert in the world and the third-largest desert overall, smaller only than the deserts of Antarctica and the northern Arctic. The name \"Sahara\" is derived from Arabic: \u0635\u064e\u062d\u064e\u0627\u0631\u064e\u0649, romanized: \u1e63a\u1e25\u0101r\u0101 /s\u02e4a\u0127a\u02d0ra\u02d0/, a broken plural form of \u1e63a\u1e25r\u0101' (\u0635\u064e\u062d\u0652\u0631\u064e\u0627\u0621 /s\u02e4a\u0127ra\u02d0\u0294/), meaning \"desert\". The desert covers much of North Africa, excluding the fertile region on the Mediterranean Sea coast, the Atlas Mountains of the Maghreb, and the Nile Valley in Egypt and the Sudan. It stretches from the Red Sea in the east and the Mediterranean in the north to the Atlantic Ocean in the west, where the landscape gradually changes from desert to coastal plains. To the south it is bounded by the Sahel, a belt of semi-arid tropical savanna around the Niger River valley and the Sudan region of sub-Saharan Africa.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sahara_Desert_ant"}}, {"canonical_name": "Santa Rosa", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "santa_rosa", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Santa Rosa", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 8, "variants": ["Santa Rosa"], "contexts": ["valley in Antioquia with mercury", "city near mountain peak"]}, {"entity_id": "santa-rosa", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Santa-Rosa", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Santa-Rosa", "Santa Rosa de Cura"], "contexts": ["location in Colombia", "mine in Peru"]}, {"entity_id": "santa_rosa", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Santa Rosa", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Santa Rosa"], "contexts": ["location northwest of Guanaxuata"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Santa Rosa", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Santa Rosa", "similarity": 0.847}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Santa-Rosa", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Santa Rosa", "similarity": 0.937}], "id": 2709, "stable_key": "clu_b4ed77caf7481c16", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Santa Rosa", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Colombia", "note": "Context suggests a specific location in Antioquia, Colombia, possibly associated with mercury."}}, {"canonical_name": "Jura limestone", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "calcaire_du_jura", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Calcaire du Jura", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["Calcaire du Jura", "calcaire du Jura"], "contexts": ["Jurassic limestone", "Jura limestone"]}, {"entity_id": "jura_limestone", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Jura limestone", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 9, "variants": ["Jura limestone", "Jura Limestone"], "contexts": ["geological formation", "type of rock formation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Calcaire du Jura", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Jura limestone", "similarity": 0.913}], "id": 2710, "stable_key": "clu_ffc3aca171c61893", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jura limestone", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q530040", "wikidata_description": "Jura limestone was formed from sedimentation in an area of tropical lagoons some 140 to 220 million years ago. The result of this process is one of the defining features of the stone as we find a wide variety of fossilized creatures."}}, {"canonical_name": "comparison", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "compar\u00e9e", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "compar\u00e9e", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["compar\u00e9e", "comparaison"], "contexts": ["comparison", "comparison of intensities"]}, {"entity_id": "comparison", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "comparison", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 10, "variants": ["comparison", "Comparison", "COMPARISON"], "contexts": ["related to discrimination", "judging similarities and differences"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "compar\u00e9e", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "comparison", "similarity": 0.917}], "id": 2711, "stable_key": "clu_3d66bfbfaeb28762", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "comparison", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q1720648", "wikidata_description": "a method of examination or evaluation of two or more entities to deduce their similarities and differences"}}, {"canonical_name": "olivine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "olivine", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "olivine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["olivine"], "contexts": ["mineral found in rocks", "crystal not found in phonolithe"]}, {"entity_id": "olivine", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "olivine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 10, "variants": ["olivine"], "contexts": ["mineral found in meteoric stones", "mineral found in a\u00ebrolites"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "olivine", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "olivine", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2712, "stable_key": "clu_37b9a4e3a5d3983b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Olivine", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q7088210", "wikidata_description": "river in New Zealand", "wikipedia_extract": "The mineral olivine () is a magnesium iron silicate with the chemical formula (Mg,Fe)2SiO4. It is a type of nesosilicate or orthosilicate. The primary component of the Earth's upper mantle, it is a common mineral in Earth's subsurface, but weathers quickly on the surface. Olivine has many uses, such as the gemstone peridot (or chrysolite), as well as industrial applications like metalworking processes. The ratio of magnesium to iron varies between the two endmembers of the solid solution series: forsterite (Mg-endmember: Mg2SiO4) and fayalite (Fe-endmember: Fe2SiO4). Compositions of olivine are commonly expressed as molar percentages of forsterite (Fo) and/or fayalite (Fa) (e.g., Fo70Fa30, or just Fo70 with Fa30 implied).", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olivine"}}, {"canonical_name": "Venezaras", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "aherianas", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Aherianas", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Aherianas"], "contexts": ["tribal group"]}, {"entity_id": "venezaras", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Venezaras", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 11, "variants": ["Venezaras", "Venesars", "Venezianos", "venezianos"], "contexts": ["people living by theft", "people mentioned in passage"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Aherianas", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Venezaras", "similarity": 0.851}], "id": 2713, "stable_key": "clu_32f2a888d0b2e1f9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Venezaras", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "Tribal group associated with theft and living by it", "note": "The spelling 'Aherianas' appears to be a variant or misspelling of 'Venezaras'."}}, {"canonical_name": "interest", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "interest", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "interest", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 11, "variants": ["interest"], "contexts": ["concern or importance felt by consciousness", "feeling of engagement"]}, {"entity_id": "interesse", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "interesse", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["interesse"], "contexts": ["motive for judgment"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "interest", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "interesse", "similarity": 0.937}], "id": 2714, "stable_key": "clu_a7b81851f2eaddd5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "interest", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "association of ideas", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "association_of_ideas", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "association of ideas", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 9, "variants": ["Association of Ideas", "association of ideas"], "contexts": ["theory of how ideas connect", "linking of thoughts and memories"]}, {"entity_id": "associations_of_thought", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "associations of thought", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["associations of thought"], "contexts": ["linking of ideas in the mind", "linking of ideas and memories"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "association of ideas", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "associations of thought", "similarity": 0.92}], "id": 2715, "stable_key": "clu_d6ac65f9591dc756", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Association of Ideas", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q744778", "wikidata_description": "connection between ideas or mental states due to similarity or proximity in psychology", "note": "This is a foundational concept in psychology, referring to how thoughts and memories are linked."}}, {"canonical_name": "Subject", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "subject", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Subject", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 8, "variants": ["subject", "Subject"], "contexts": ["individual participating in experiment", "metaphysical entity of mental life"]}, {"entity_id": "subject", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Subject", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["subject", "Subject"], "contexts": ["entity experiencing consciousness", "the self or entity experiencing"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Subject", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Subject", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2716, "stable_key": "clu_5508ab0aa52a33f1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Subject", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "thinking", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "think", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "think", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["think"], "contexts": ["mental process of forming ideas", "intellectual activity of reasoning"]}, {"entity_id": "thinking", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "thinking", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 7, "variants": ["thinking", "Thinking"], "contexts": ["mental process of forming ideas", "the process of forming thoughts"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "think", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "thinking", "similarity": 0.919}], "id": 2717, "stable_key": "clu_c0d1b9ebe8d2c3ea", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Thinking", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q9420", "wikidata_description": "mental activity involving an individual's subjective consciousness", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thought", "wikipedia_extract": "In their most common sense, thought and thinking refer to cognitive processes that occur independently of direct sensory stimulation. Core forms include judging, reasoning, concept formation, problem solving, and deliberation. Other processes, such as entertaining an idea, memory, or imagination, are also frequently considered types of thought. Unlike perception, these activities can occur without immediate input from the sensory organs. In a broader sense, any mental event\u2014including perception and unconscious processes\u2014may be described as a form of thought. The term can also denote not the process itself, but the resulting mental states or systems of ideas."}}, {"canonical_name": "concepts", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "concepts", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "concepts", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["concepts"], "contexts": ["consciousness of complex objects, faint", "mental representations of objects or ideas"]}, {"entity_id": "concept", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "concept", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Concept", "concept"], "contexts": ["abstract idea or general notion", "mental representation of an idea"]}, {"entity_id": "concept", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "concept", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["concept"], "contexts": ["mental representation of objects", "mental representation of an idea"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "concepts", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "concept", "similarity": 0.857}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "concept", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "concept", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2718, "stable_key": "clu_134efc88a220e941", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Concept", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "falta de memoria", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "loss_of_memory", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "loss of memory", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["loss of memory"], "contexts": ["condition treated by Dr. Holbrook"]}, {"entity_id": "malady_of_memory", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "malady of memory", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["malady of memory"], "contexts": ["condition that oblivion is not, but a condition of memory"]}, {"entity_id": "falta_de_memoria", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "falta de memoria", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 10, "variants": ["falta de memoria", "falta de Memoria", "faltade memoria", "faltade memoriadizem", "faltasdememoria", "faltosde memoria"], "contexts": ["loss of memory", "a condition of memory loss"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "loss of memory", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "falta de memoria", "similarity": 0.962}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "malady of memory", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "falta de memoria", "similarity": 0.943}], "id": 2719, "stable_key": "clu_bc4581fc3c5427cf", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Amnesia", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Amnesia", "wikidata_id": "Q11072", "wikidata_description": "cognitive disorder where the memory is disturbed or lost and involves the loss of memories previously established, loss of the ability to create new memories, continuous high blood pressure and severe shock may also cause amnesia"}}, {"canonical_name": "mulheres hystericas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "hysterics", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "hysterics", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Hysterics", "hysterics"], "contexts": ["condition with emotional outbursts", "medical condition"]}, {"entity_id": "hysteric_patient", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "hysteric patient", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["hysteric patient"], "contexts": ["patient with abolished sensation and recollection"]}, {"entity_id": "hysteric_subjects", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "hysteric subjects", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["hysteric subjects"], "contexts": ["subjects experimented on by M. Janet"]}, {"entity_id": "hysteric_crisis", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "hysteric crisis", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["hysteric crisis"], "contexts": ["episode of psychological distress"]}, {"entity_id": "mulheres_hystericas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "mulheres hystericas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 5, "variants": ["mulheres hystericas", "hysterica"], "contexts": ["hysterical women patients", "female condition"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "hysterics", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "mulheres hystericas", "similarity": 0.905}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "hysteric patient", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "mulheres hystericas", "similarity": 0.883}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "hysteric subjects", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "mulheres hystericas", "similarity": 0.89}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "hysteric crisis", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "mulheres hystericas", "similarity": 0.899}], "id": 2720, "stable_key": "clu_ce2fd5c178d4fa14", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hysteria", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "A historical medical diagnosis for a range of psychological and physical symptoms in women.", "note": "A historical medical term encompassing a variety of psychological and physical symptoms, particularly in women.", "wikipedia_extract": "Hysteria is a term used to mean ungovernable emotional excess and can refer to a temporary state of mind or emotion. In the nineteenth century, female hysteria was considered a diagnosable physical illness in women. It is assumed that the basis for diagnosis operated under the belief that women are predisposed to mental and behavioral conditions; an interpretation of sex-related differences in stress responses. In the twentieth century, it shifted to being considered a mental illness. Influential physicians the likes of Sigmund Freud and Jean-Martin Charcot had dedicated research to hysteria patients. Currently, most physicians do not accept hysteria as a medical diagnosis."}}, {"canonical_name": "Unknowable", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "unknowable", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Unknowable", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Unknowable"], "contexts": ["principle for perplexities", "philosophical concept of the unknowable entity"]}, {"entity_id": "unknown", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "unknown", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["unknown"], "contexts": ["that which is not known"]}, {"entity_id": "unknowable", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Unknowable", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 9, "variants": ["Unknowable"], "contexts": ["philosophical concept of what cannot be known", "that which cannot be understood"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Unknowable", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Unknowable", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "unknown", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Unknowable", "similarity": 0.934}], "id": 2721, "stable_key": "clu_9615db5446b6b51c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Unknowable", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q61834404", "wikidata_description": "article by Richard Elwes published August 2010 in New Scientist"}}, {"canonical_name": "winds", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "winds", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "winds", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["winds"], "contexts": ["atmospheric movements", "movement of air"]}, {"entity_id": "winds", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Winds", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["winds", "Winds"], "contexts": ["movement of air", "extra-tropical"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "winds", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Winds", "similarity": 0.976}], "id": 2722, "stable_key": "clu_19d58f0aae4b17ba-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Wind", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind", "note": "The terms 'southerly winds' and 'spring east winds' describe specific types of winds. The most general modern referent is 'winds'.", "wikipedia_extract": "Wind is the natural movement of air or other gases relative to a planet's surface. Winds occur on a range of scales, from thunderstorm flows lasting tens of minutes, to local breezes generated by heating of land surfaces and lasting a few hours, to global winds resulting from the difference in absorption of solar energy between the climate zones on Earth. The study of wind is called anemology. The two main causes of large-scale atmospheric circulation are the differential heating between the equator and the poles, and the rotation of the planet, which is called the Coriolis effect. Within the tropics and subtropics, thermal low circulations over terrain and high plateaus can drive monsoon circulations. In coastal areas the sea breeze/land breeze cycle can define local winds; in areas that have variable terrain, mountain and valley breezes can prevail."}}, {"canonical_name": "volcanic action", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "volcanic_action", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "volcanic action", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["Volcanic action", "volcanic action"], "contexts": ["eruption of molten rock from Earth's interior", "geological process"]}, {"entity_id": "volcanic_action", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "volcanic action", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["volcanic action", "Volcanic Action"], "contexts": ["processes related to volcanoes", "geological process involving volcanoes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "volcanic action", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "volcanic action", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2723, "stable_key": "clu_8415cb4ad8108c86", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Volcanic activity", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q505748", "wikidata_description": "phenomena and processes associated with the action of volcanos, geysers and fumaroles", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Volcanism", "wikipedia_extract": "Volcanism, vulcanism, volcanicity, or volcanic activity is the phenomenon where solids, liquids, gases, and their mixtures erupt to the surface of a solid-surface astronomical body such as a planet or a moon. It is caused by the presence of a heat source, usually internally generated, inside the body; the heat is generated by various processes, such as radioactive decay or tidal heating. This heat partially melts solid material in the body or turns material into gas. The mobilized material rises through the body's interior and may break through the solid surface. For volcanism to occur, the temperature of the mantle must have risen to about half its melting point. At this point, the mantle's viscosity will have dropped to about 1021 Pascal-seconds."}}, {"canonical_name": "Lapland", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "lapland", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Lapland", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 6, "variants": ["Lapland"], "contexts": ["region where observations were made", "location of observations by Bravais and Lottin"]}, {"entity_id": "lapland", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Lapland", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 6, "variants": ["Lapland"], "contexts": ["location of arc measurement", "location of Wardhus observation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Lapland", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Lapland", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2724, "stable_key": "clu_fdcbac450223a15d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lapland", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Finland, Norway, Russia, Sweden", "wikidata_id": "Q5700", "wikidata_description": "region of Finland", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lapland_%28Finland%29", "wikipedia_extract": "Lapland is the largest and northernmost region of Finland. The 21 municipalities in the region cooperate in a Regional Council. Lapland borders the Finnish region of North Ostrobothnia in the south. It also borders the Gulf of Bothnia and the Norrbotten County in Sweden, Finnmark County and the Troms County in Norway, and Murmansk Oblast and the Republic of Karelia in Russia. The topography of Lapland varies from vast mires and forests in the south to fells in the north. The Arctic Circle crosses Lapland, so polar phenomena such as the midnight sun, the polar night and the northern lights can be viewed in this region."}}, {"canonical_name": "Messier", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "messier", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Messier", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 5, "variants": ["Messier"], "contexts": ["discovered comet in 1766", "discovered nebulous spot in 1774"]}, {"entity_id": "messier", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Messier", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 7, "variants": ["Messier"], "contexts": ["determined comet's orbit elements", "astronomer who first saw Encke's comet"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Messier", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Messier", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2725, "stable_key": "clu_d54f45a3acd90d84", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Charles Messier", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1730, "death_year": 1817, "description": "French astronomer who discovered numerous comets and compiled a famous catalog."}}, {"canonical_name": "carbonate of lime", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "carbonate_of_lime", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "carbonate of lime", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["Carbonate of lime", "carbonate of lime"], "contexts": ["chemical compound studied", "mineral transformed into limestone and dolomite"]}, {"entity_id": "carbonate_of_lime", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "carbonate of lime", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 8, "variants": ["carbonate of lime", "Carbonate of lime"], "contexts": ["mineral with many crystallization varieties", "mineral compound"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "carbonate of lime", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "carbonate of lime", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2726, "stable_key": "clu_f83ffd9e071702a0-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Calcium carbonate", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "Contexts suggest both calcium oxide (lime) and calcium carbonate (limestone).", "wikidata_id": "Q23767", "wikidata_description": "chemical compound", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calcium%20carbonate", "wikipedia_extract": "Calcium carbonate is a chemical compound with the chemical formula CaCO3. It is a common substance found in rocks as the minerals calcite and aragonite, most notably in chalk and limestone, eggshells, gastropod shells, shellfish skeletons and pearls. Materials containing much calcium carbonate or resembling it are described as calcareous. Calcium carbonate is the active ingredient in agricultural lime. Limescale is calcium carbonate produced when calcium ions in hard water react with carbonate ions. Calcium carbonate has medical use as a calcium supplement or as an antacid, but excessive consumption can be hazardous and cause hypercalcemia and digestive issues."}}, {"canonical_name": "magnetic storms", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "magnetic_storm", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "magnetic storm", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 3, "variants": ["magnetic storm"], "contexts": ["disturbance in Earth's magnetic field", "sudden disturbance in magnetic needle"]}, {"entity_id": "electric_storms", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "electric storms", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["electric storms"], "contexts": ["storms with noise and light", "storms with electrical phenomena"]}, {"entity_id": "storms,_magnetic", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Storms, magnetic", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Storms, magnetic"], "contexts": ["atmospheric disturbances"]}, {"entity_id": "magnetic_storms", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "magnetic storms", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 5, "variants": ["magnetic storms"], "contexts": ["sudden disturbances in Earth's magnetic field", "disturbances affecting magnetic elements"]}, {"entity_id": "magnetic_storm", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "magnetic storm", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["magnetic storm"], "contexts": ["disturbance in Earth's magnetic field"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "magnetic storm", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "magnetic storms", "similarity": 0.976}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "magnetic storm", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "magnetic storm", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "electric storms", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "magnetic storms", "similarity": 0.891}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Storms, magnetic", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "magnetic storms", "similarity": 0.952}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Storms, magnetic", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "magnetic storm", "similarity": 0.927}], "id": 2727, "stable_key": "clu_aa1aafdeea694189-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "weather events", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'storms' in early modern texts, especially in contexts like Humboldt's 'Kosmos', refers to natural meteorological phenomena rather than diseases. 'Magnetic storms' refers to geomagnetic disturbances.", "wikidata_id": "Q130240049", "wikidata_description": "", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weather%20events%20during%20wars", "wikipedia_extract": "A storm is any disturbed state of the natural environment or the atmosphere of an astronomical body. It may be marked by significant disruptions to normal conditions such as strong wind, tornadoes, hail, thunder and lightning (a thunderstorm), heavy precipitation (snowstorm, rainstorm), heavy freezing rain (ice storm), strong winds (tropical cyclone, windstorm), wind transporting some substance through the atmosphere such as in a dust storm, among other forms of severe weather. Storms have the potential to harm lives and property via storm surge, heavy rain or snow causing flooding or road impassibility, lightning, wildfires, and vertical and horizontal wind shear. Systems with significant rainfall and duration help alleviate drought in places they move through. Heavy snowfall can allow special recreational activities to take place which would not be possible otherwise, such as skiing and snowmobiling. The English word comes from Proto-Germanic sturmaz meaning \"noise, tumult\"; a synonym is tempest, of French origin."}}, {"canonical_name": "Infinite", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "infinite", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "infinite", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["infinite"], "contexts": ["boundless and endless concept"]}, {"entity_id": "infinite", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Infinite", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 11, "variants": ["infinite", "Infinite"], "contexts": ["First Cause as infinite", "philosophical concept of boundless existence"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "infinite", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Infinite", "similarity": 0.968}], "id": 2728, "stable_key": "clu_a411e8a20d0f54ed", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "infinity", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q205", "wikidata_description": "mathematical concept of something without any limit"}}, {"canonical_name": "vida", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "vida", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "vida", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["vida"], "contexts": ["life"]}, {"entity_id": "vida", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "vida", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 11, "variants": ["vida", "Vida", "Vidas"], "contexts": ["The state of being alive", "state of being conserved"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "vida", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "vida", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2729, "stable_key": "clu_251ca0c2be1ffe42-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Life", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}}, {"canonical_name": "Candia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "candia", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Candia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Candia"], "contexts": ["modern name for Heraklion, Crete"]}, {"entity_id": "candia", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Candia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 11, "variants": ["Candia"], "contexts": ["place known for praised herbs", "source of best ammi"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Candia", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Candia", "similarity": 0.819}], "id": 2730, "stable_key": "clu_15b8c7bf4a4cb7fc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Heraklion", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Greece", "note": "Candia is the historical name for Heraklion, the capital of Crete."}}, {"canonical_name": "Niccolao", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "nicolo", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Nicolo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Nicolo"], "contexts": ["mentioned in relation to Nicoto Florentino"]}, {"entity_id": "niccolao", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Niccolao", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 11, "variants": ["Niccolao", "Niccobo", "Niccoiaa", "Niccola", "Niccolag", "Niccolap Jlef", "Nicolao", "d'iNicco\u00ecao"], "contexts": ["associated with pills", "scholar"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Nicolo", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Niccolao", "similarity": 0.884}], "id": 2731, "stable_key": "clu_21fadb923d492f14", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Niccol\u00f2 Antinori", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "Florentine apothecary and physician, associated with pills.", "note": "Identification is based on association with Florentine apothecaries and pills, common in the Ricettario Fiorentino. 'Nicoto Florentino' likely refers to him."}}, {"canonical_name": "Ant\u00f3nio Nunes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "ant\u00f3nio_nunes", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Ant\u00f3nio Nunes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 11, "variants": ["Ant\u00f3nio Nunes", "Ant\u00f3nio Nimes", "Ant\u00f3nio Nunes de Carvalho"], "contexts": ["author of 'Lyyro dos pesos'", "author of Lyvro das Pesos"]}, {"entity_id": "antonio_nunes", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Antonio Nunes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Antonio Nunes"], "contexts": ["patient with dark urine"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Ant\u00f3nio Nunes", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Antonio Nunes", "similarity": 0.675}], "id": 2732, "stable_key": "clu_910d776533141fb0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ant\u00f3nio Nunes Ribeiro Sanches", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1700, "death_year": 1782, "description": "Portuguese physician and philosopher, author of medical texts.", "note": "While the cluster mentions 'Lyyro dos pesos' (likely a misprint or translation of 'Livro dos pesos') and a patient with dark urine, Ant\u00f3nio Nunes Ribeiro Sanches is a prominent Portuguese physician of the era with a significant body of work."}}, {"canonical_name": "amomo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "amomo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "amomo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 8, "variants": ["Amomo", "AMOMO", "amomo"], "contexts": ["substance discussed after amber", "plant with debated identity"]}, {"entity_id": "amomo", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Amomo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 4, "variants": ["AMOMO", "Amomo"], "contexts": ["mentioned as a topic", "plant not known in Italy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "amomo", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Amomo", "similarity": 0.992}], "id": 2733, "stable_key": "clu_ab80595d4a7a83ba-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cardamom", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Elettaria cardamomum", "family": "Zingiberaceae", "note": "The term 'cardamomo menor' likely refers to a specific variety or preparation of cardamom, but the core referent is cardamom.", "wikidata_id": "Q33466", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elettaria%20cardamomum", "wikipedia_extract": "Elettaria cardamomum, commonly known as green cardamom or true cardamom, is a herbaceous, perennial plant in the ginger family, native to southern India. It is the most common of the species whose seeds are used as a spice called cardamom that has a strong aroma used in both savory and sweet cooking. It is cultivated widely in tropical regions and reportedly naturalized in R\u00e9union, Indochina, and Costa Rica. Elettaria cardamomum is a pungent, aromatic, herbaceous, perennial plant, growing to about 2\u20134 m (6 ft 7 in \u2013 13 ft 1 in) in height. The leaves are alternate in two ranks, linear-lanceolate, 40\u201360 cm (16\u201324 in) long, with a long pointed tip. The flowers are white to lilac or pale violet, produced in a loose spike 30\u201360 cm (12\u201324 in) long."}}, {"canonical_name": "Salamanca", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "salamanca", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Salamanca", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 7, "variants": ["Salamanca"], "contexts": ["city where author and Ruano knew each other", "city where Ruano was educated"]}, {"entity_id": "salamanca", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Salamanca", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 5, "variants": ["Salamanca", "Salamnaca"], "contexts": ["city where Pedroza was a professor", "city where Luis Rodriguez Pedrofa was a physician"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Salamanca", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Salamanca", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2734, "stable_key": "clu_d5b8d2fe77c93ef7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Salamanca", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Historic university city in Spain.", "wikipedia_extract": "Salamanca (Spanish: [sala\u02c8ma\u014bka] ) is a municipality and city in the autonomous community of Castile and Le\u00f3n in Spain, and the capital of the province of Salamanca. Attached to the comarca of Campo Charro, the city lies on the northern half of the Meseta Central, in the western-central part of the Iberian Peninsula, straddling the Tormes river. As of 2024, with a population of 144,458, Salamanca is the 46th-largest municipality in Spain. The Iron Age hilltop site of Cerro de San Vicente on the right bank of the Tormes is considered as the first human settlement in the current-day city. By the 3rd century BCE, the urban settlement in the nearby Teso de las Catedrales had consolidated, under the influence of Vaccaei and Vettones. Following Roman subjugation, the indigenous oppidum gradually became the Roman civitas of Salmantica."}}, {"canonical_name": "Spodio", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "spodio", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "spodio", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["spodio", "spodo"], "contexts": ["ancient substance", "burnt ivory, also called spodium Arabum"]}, {"entity_id": "spodio", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Spodio", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 9, "variants": ["Spodio"], "contexts": ["substance found in furnaces", "a substance from the East"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "spodio", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Spodio", "similarity": 0.584}], "id": 2735, "stable_key": "clu_4458a54f38aca1ed-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Spodium", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "A residue from furnaces or a substance derived from the East.", "note": "Likely a reference to a charcoal-like substance or mineral residue.", "wikipedia_extract": "Spodium, (Latin for ashes or soot) refers to burned bone (usually used for medical purposes), or the act of divination with ash. Spodium may also refer to other types of ash, such as the scrapings from the inside of a furnace. Spodium has a long history of medical usage, mentioned by Hippocrates and, for example, in the Medical Poem of Salerno \"...Who knows the cause why Spodium stancheth bleeding?...\" (in this case spodium referring to oxen bone Ashes)."}}, {"canonical_name": "m\u00e3os", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "m\u00e3os", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "m\u00e3os", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["m\u00e3os"], "contexts": ["affected by black nails", "body part that swelled"]}, {"entity_id": "m\u00e3os", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "m\u00e3os", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 10, "variants": ["m\u00e3os"], "contexts": ["hands", "body part"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "m\u00e3os", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "m\u00e3os", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2736, "stable_key": "clu_23ff13c140e5c3b7-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hand", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "Q8418", "wikidata_description": "team sport played with a thrown ball and goals", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Handball", "note": "Referred to as a 'SUBSTANCE' but anatomically it functions as a body part/concept.", "wikipedia_extract": "Handball (also known as team handball, European handball, Olympic handball, or indoor handball) is a team sport in which two teams of seven players each (six outcourt players and a goalkeeper) pass a ball using their hands with the aim of throwing it into the goal of the opposing team. A standard match consists of two periods of 30 minutes, and the team that scores more goals wins. Modern handball is played on a court of 40 by 20 metres (131 by 66 ft), with a goal in the middle of each end. The goals are surrounded by a 6-metre (20 ft) zone where only the defending goalkeeper is allowed; goals must be scored by throwing the ball from outside the zone or while \"diving\" into it. The sport is usually played indoors, but outdoor variants exist in the forms of field handball, Czech handball (which were more common in the past) and beach handball. The game is fast and high-scoring: professional teams now typically score between 20 and 35 goals each, though lower scores were not uncommon until a few decades ago."}}, {"canonical_name": "narcotica", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "narc\u00f3tico", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "narc\u00f3tico", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["narc\u00f3tico"], "contexts": ["property of opium"]}, {"entity_id": "narcotica", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "narcotica", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 11, "variants": ["narcotica", "narcotico"], "contexts": ["numbing quality", "adverse quality affecting spirits/nerves"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "narc\u00f3tico", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "narcotica", "similarity": 0.959}], "id": 2737, "stable_key": "clu_7f4d6b52d52379f7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Narcotic", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to substances that induce drowsiness, stupor, or insensibility.", "wikidata_id": "Q10268802", "wikidata_description": "psychoactive drug whose possession, use or trade is outlawed"}}, {"canonical_name": "saliva", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "saliva", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "saliva", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["saliva"], "contexts": ["animal's saliva used for preparation"]}, {"entity_id": "saliva", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "saliva", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 11, "variants": ["saliva", "Saliva", "Saliva humana", "saliva\u00e7\u00e3o"], "contexts": ["bodily fluid", "bodily fluid involved in digestion"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "saliva", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "saliva", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2738, "stable_key": "clu_be3655d65f8a90d9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Saliva", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "The watery fluid secreted in the mouth by salivary glands.", "wikipedia_extract": "Saliva (commonly referred to as spit, drool or slobber) is an extracellular fluid produced and secreted by salivary glands in the mouth. In humans, saliva is around 99% water, plus electrolytes, mucus, white blood cells, epithelial cells (from which DNA can be extracted), enzymes (such as lingual lipase and amylase), and antimicrobial agents (such as secretory IgA, and lysozymes). The enzymes found in saliva are essential in beginning the process of digestion of dietary starches and fats. These enzymes also play a role in breaking down food particles trapped within dental crevices, thus protecting teeth from bacterial decay. Saliva also performs a lubricating function, wetting food and permitting the initiation of swallowing, and protecting the oral mucosa from drying out. Saliva has specialized purposes for a variety of animal species beyond predigestion."}}, {"canonical_name": "Thymo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "thymo", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Thymo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 11, "variants": ["Thymo", "THYMO", "thymo", "thymos", "thym\u00a9"], "contexts": ["plant Epithymo wraps around", "wild Hyssop, also called Thymo"]}, {"entity_id": "thymus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Thymus", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Thymus"], "contexts": ["plant mentioned in text"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Thymo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Thymus", "similarity": 0.92}], "id": 2739, "stable_key": "clu_f0f998ff9e1da016", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Thyme", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Thymus vulgaris", "family": "Lamiaceae", "description": "An aromatic herb with culinary and medicinal uses.", "note": "Likely refers to the genus Thymus, with Thyme vulgaris as the most common species.", "wikipedia_extract": "Thymus vulgaris (common thyme, German thyme, garden thyme or just thyme) is a species of flowering plant in the mint family Lamiaceae, native to southern Europe from the western Mediterranean to southern Italy. Growing to 15\u201330 cm (6\u201312 in) tall by 40 cm (16 in) wide, it is a bushy, woody-based evergreen subshrub with small, highly aromatic, grey-green leaves and clusters of purple or pink flowers in early summer. It is useful in the garden as groundcover, where it can be short-lived, but is easily propagated from cuttings. It is also the main source of thyme as an ingredient in cooking and as a herbal medicine. It is slightly spicier than oregano and sweeter than sage. The Latin specific epithet vulgaris means \u201ccommon\u201d in the sense of \u201cwidespread\u201d."}}, {"canonical_name": "liquid", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 12, "members": [{"entity_id": "liquidum", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "liquidum", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["liquidum"], "contexts": ["Latin adjective meaning 'liquid'"]}, {"entity_id": "fluido", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "fluido", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["fluido"], "contexts": ["state of bodily fluids"]}, {"entity_id": "liquid", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "liquid", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 10, "variants": ["liquid"], "contexts": ["state of matter with fixed volume", "state of matter between solid and gas"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "liquidum", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "liquid", "similarity": 0.959}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "fluido", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "liquid", "similarity": 0.901}], "id": 2740, "stable_key": "clu_930ee3e8699abe55", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Liquid", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the state of matter characterized by fixed volume but not fixed shape.", "wikidata_id": "Q26367132", "wikidata_description": "report of investigation published in 1973"}}, {"canonical_name": "expectation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "expectation", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "expectation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["expectation"], "contexts": ["medical treatment of waiting for nature to heal", "waiting for natural recovery"]}, {"entity_id": "expectation", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "expectation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["expectation", "Expectation"], "contexts": ["anticipation of signal", "anticipation of future events"]}, {"entity_id": "expectations", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "expectations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["expectations"], "contexts": ["anticipation of future events"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "expectation", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "expectation", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "expectation", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "expectations", "similarity": 0.913}], "id": 2741, "stable_key": "clu_775d8756b1812dd8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Expectation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q200125", "wikidata_description": "long-run average value of a random variable", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Expected%20value"}}, {"canonical_name": "spiritualistic", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "spiritualisme", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "spiritualisme", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["spiritualisme"], "contexts": ["philosophical doctrine about spirit", "philosophical view of spirit as primary"]}, {"entity_id": "spiritualistic", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "spiritualistic", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["spiritualistic"], "contexts": ["theory of scholasticism and common-sense", "philosophical view emphasizing spirit"]}, {"entity_id": "spiritualism", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "spiritualism", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["spiritualism"], "contexts": ["belief in spirits and afterlife", "belief in spiritual reality"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "spiritualisme", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "spiritualistic", "similarity": 0.889}, {"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "spiritualisme", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "spiritualism", "similarity": 0.993}], "id": 2742, "stable_key": "clu_f576b70526c15545-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Spiritualists", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to adherents of Spiritualism, a belief system focusing on communication with spirits.", "wikidata_id": "Q829348", "wikidata_description": "19th century religious movement based on the belief that one can communicate with the spirits of the dead"}}, {"canonical_name": "Phtisick", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "phthisie", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "phthisie", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 2, "variants": ["phthisie"], "contexts": ["consumption, a chronic lung disease", "illness treated by pulmonaria"]}, {"entity_id": "phtisick", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Phtisick", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 9, "variants": ["Phtisick", "Phthisick", "Phtisicks"], "contexts": ["lung disease", "consumption caused by salt humors"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "phthisie", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Phtisick", "similarity": 0.909}], "id": 2743, "stable_key": "clu_ed57e28f814ebf1e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Phthisis", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Tuberculosis", "wikidata_id": "Q12204", "wikidata_description": "infectious disease caused by the bacterium Mycobacterium tuberculosis", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tuberculosis"}}, {"canonical_name": "decomposition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "decomposition", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Decomposition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["decomposition", "Decomposition"], "contexts": ["breaking down into constituent parts", "breaking down substances"]}, {"entity_id": "decomposition", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "decomposition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 9, "variants": ["decomposition"], "contexts": ["process of decay in a dead body", "process of rotting or decay"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Decomposition", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "decomposition", "similarity": 0.983}], "id": 2744, "stable_key": "clu_31204712ff677fef", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Decomposition", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "\u00c6lian", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "\u00e6lian", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "\u00c6lian", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 8, "variants": ["\u00c6lian"], "contexts": ["Ancients followed by Philes for animal accounts", "ancient natural writer"]}, {"entity_id": "aelian", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Aelian", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Aelian"], "contexts": ["ancient writer", "ancient source cited in footnote"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "\u00c6lian", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Aelian", "similarity": 0.852}], "id": 2745, "stable_key": "clu_3775f8f72c5f788f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Aelian", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 170, "death_year": 230, "description": "Roman writer and teacher, known for 'On the Nature of Animals'", "note": "The Spanish 'Eliano' is a direct transliteration/cognate of the Latin 'Aelianus', referring to Claudius Aelianus.", "wikidata_id": "Q313782", "wikidata_description": "Roman author and teacher (c.175\u2013c.235)"}}, {"canonical_name": "Goat", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "goat", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Goat", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 6, "variants": ["Goat"], "contexts": ["domesticated mammal", "animal whose blood is discussed"]}, {"entity_id": "goats", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Goats", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Goats"], "contexts": ["have peculiar vermine", "animal discussed for gall presence"]}, {"entity_id": "goats", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "goats", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["goats"], "contexts": ["udders developed by milking", "domesticated animal"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Goat", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "goats", "similarity": 0.953}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Goats", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "goats", "similarity": 0.991}], "id": 2746, "stable_key": "clu_3329782a3e39bd73", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Goat", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q45320358", "wikidata_description": "species of mammal", "description": "An animal, specifically a goat, from which the bezoar stone was historically derived.", "wikipedia_extract": "The goat or domestic goat (Capra hircus) is a species of goat-antelope that is mostly kept as livestock. It was domesticated from the wild goat (C. aegagrus) of Southwest Asia and Eastern Europe. The goat is a member of the family Bovidae, meaning it is closely related to the sheep. It was one of the first animals to be domesticated, in Iran around 10,000 years ago. Goats have been used for milk, meat, wool, and skins across much of the world. Milk from goats is often turned into cheese."}}, {"canonical_name": "Turpentine", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "turpentine", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Turpentine", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "RESIN", "count": 3, "variants": ["Turpentine"], "contexts": ["resinous substance from pine trees", "resinous plant product"]}, {"entity_id": "turpentine", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Turpentine", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 8, "variants": ["Turpentine"], "contexts": ["substance dissolved in oil for wounds", "ingredient in Costmary salve"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Turpentine", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Turpentine", "similarity": 0.779}], "id": 2747, "stable_key": "clu_71946b7bbddd7ee4-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Turpentine", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "family": "Pinaceae", "note": "The term 'trementina' historically referred to turpentine, often derived from various coniferous trees.", "wikidata_id": "Q63045", "wikidata_description": "species of plant native to the Mediterranean region", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pistacia%20terebinthus", "wikipedia_extract": "Pistacia terebinthus, also called the terebinth,  is a deciduous shrub species of the genus Pistacia, native to the Mediterranean region from the western regions of Morocco and Portugal to Greece and western and southeastern Turkey. At one time terebinths growing on the eastern shores of the Mediterranean Sea  were regarded as a separate species, Pistacia palaestina, but these are now considered to be a synonym of P. terebinthus. The terebinth is a deciduous flowering plant belonging to the cashew family, Anacardiaceae; a small tree or large shrub, it grows to 10 m (33 ft) tall. The leaves are compound, 10\u201320 cm (3.9\u20137.9 in) long, odd pinnate with five to eleven opposite glossy oval leaflets, the leaflets 2\u20136 cm (0.79\u20132.36 in) long and 1\u20133 cm (0.39\u20131.18 in) broad. The flowers are reddish-purple, appearing with the new leaves in early spring.", "linnaean": "Pinus"}}, {"canonical_name": "Lucie", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "lucius", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Lucius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Lucius"], "contexts": ["book title", "cured of side pain with altar ashes and wine"]}, {"entity_id": "lucie", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Lucie", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 9, "variants": ["Lucie"], "contexts": ["patient studied by Janet", "subject of hypnotic experiments"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Lucius", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Lucie", "similarity": 0.887}], "id": 2748, "stable_key": "clu_708908d3a509da4a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lucius Annaeus Seneca", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "birth_year": 4, "death_year": 65, "description": "Roman Stoic philosopher, statesman, and dramatist.", "note": "The text mentions 'Lucius' in the context of pseudodoxia epidemica, which could refer to Lucius Apuleius or potentially another ancient figure. However, given the association with Browne and classical learning, Seneca is a possibility. The 'Lucie' in James's text is likely a different individual, but the cluster groups them. Without more context for 'Lucius', this identification remains tentative."}}, {"canonical_name": "declination", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "declination", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "declination", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Declination", "declination"], "contexts": ["magnetic declination", "state of decline or weakening"]}, {"entity_id": "declination", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "declination", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 9, "variants": ["declination"], "contexts": ["magnetic variation", "magnetic deviation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "declination", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "declination", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2749, "stable_key": "clu_3b8f69bf7ed480fd-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Magnetic declination", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q127448891", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article"}}, {"canonical_name": "Allum", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "allum", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Allum", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Allum"], "contexts": ["salt attracts weakly", "mineral substance with electrical properties"]}, {"entity_id": "roch-allum", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Roch-Allum", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Roch-Allum"], "contexts": ["mineral with attractive quality"]}, {"entity_id": "allum", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Allum", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 8, "variants": ["Allum"], "contexts": ["mineral added to plant preparation", "ingredient in a toothache remedy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Allum", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Allum", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Roch-Allum", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Allum", "similarity": 0.895}], "id": 2750, "stable_key": "clu_8e60ff9c186fcac6-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Alum", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q421857", "wikidata_description": "chemical compound", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aluminium%20sulfate", "wikipedia_extract": "Aluminium sulfate is a salt with the formula Al2(SO4)3. It is soluble in water and is mainly used as a coagulating agent (promoting particle collision by neutralizing charge) in the purification of drinking water and wastewater treatment plants, and also in paper manufacturing. The anhydrous form occurs naturally as a rare mineral millosevichite, found for example in volcanic environments and on burning coal-mining waste dumps. Aluminium sulfate is rarely, if ever, encountered as the anhydrous salt. It forms a number of different hydrates, of which the hexadecahydrate Al2(SO4)3\u00b716H2O and octadecahydrate Al2(SO4)3\u00b718H2O are the most common. The heptadecahydrate, whose formula can be written as [Al(H2O)6]2(SO4)3\u00b75H2O, occurs naturally as the mineral alunogen."}}, {"canonical_name": "Bees", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "bees", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Bees", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Bees"], "contexts": ["social flying insects", "emerge from corrupt Oxe"]}, {"entity_id": "bees", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Bees", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 9, "variants": ["Bees"], "contexts": ["insect with sting", "insects that gather nectar"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Bees", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Bees", "similarity": 0.904}], "id": 2751, "stable_key": "clu_70974a96653b749d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bees", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "description": "Social insects known for honey production and pollination.", "wikipedia_extract": "Bees are winged insects that form a monophyletic clade Anthophila within the superfamily Apoidea of the order Hymenoptera, with over 20,000 known species in seven recognized families. Some species \u2013 including honey bees, bumblebees, and stingless bees \u2013 are social insects living in highly hierarchical colonies, while over 90% of bee species \u2013 including mason bees, carpenter bees, leafcutter bees, and sweat bees \u2013 are solitary. Members of the most well-known bee genus, Apis (i.e. honey bees), are known to construct hexagonally celled waxy nests called hives. Unlike the closely related wasps and ants, who are carnivorous/omnivorous, bees are herbivores that specifically feed on nectar (nectarivory) and pollen (palynivory), the former primarily as a carbohydrate source for metabolic energy, and the latter primarily for protein and other nutrients for their larvae. They are found on every continent except Antarctica, and in every habitat on the planet that contains insect-pollinated flowering plants. The most common bees in the Northern Hemisphere are the Halictidae, or sweat bees, but they are small and often mistaken for wasps or flies."}}, {"canonical_name": "image", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "image", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Image", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Image"], "contexts": ["likeness or representation"]}, {"entity_id": "image", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "image", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 10, "variants": ["image"], "contexts": ["mental representation", "representation on the retina"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Image", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "image", "similarity": 0.959}], "id": 2752, "stable_key": "clu_1474e77ae3ada337", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Image", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This refers to both physical representations and mental constructs.", "wikidata_id": "Q860755", "wikidata_description": "extraction of information from images via digital image processing techniques"}}, {"canonical_name": "Crocus Martis", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "crocus_martis", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Crocus Martis", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Crocus Martis"], "contexts": ["steel corroded and reverberated"]}, {"entity_id": "crocus_martis", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Crocus Martis", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 10, "variants": ["Crocus Martis", "croco martis", "crocus martis"], "contexts": ["Crocus Martis, iron preparation", "aperitive substance"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Crocus Martis", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Crocus Martis", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2753, "stable_key": "clu_1a67cdca5c8931fe", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Iron preparations", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "note": "Refers to preparations of iron or steel, often corroded or reverberated, used medicinally."}}, {"canonical_name": "Banda", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "banda", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Banda", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Banda"], "contexts": ["isle where nutmeg tree fructifies"]}, {"entity_id": "banda", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Banda", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 10, "variants": ["Banda"], "contexts": ["region where clove is merchandise", "island group to be discovered"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Banda", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Banda", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2754, "stable_key": "clu_7e1c88b386c2c72f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Banda Islands", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Indonesia", "note": "Historically a major source of nutmeg and mace.", "wikidata_id": "Q739411", "wikidata_description": "island group in Indonesia", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Banda%20Islands", "wikipedia_extract": "The Banda Islands (Indonesian: Kepulauan Banda) are a volcanic group of ten small islands in the Banda Sea, about 140 km (87 mi) south of Seram Island and about 2,000 km (1,243 mi) east of Java, and constitute an administrative district (kecamatan) within the Central Maluku Regency in the Indonesian province of Maluku. The islands rise out of 4-to-6-kilometre (2.5 to 3.7 mi) deep ocean and have a total land area of approximately 172 square kilometres (66 sq mi); with associated maritime area this reaches 736.3 square kilometres (284.3 sq mi). They had a population of 18,544 at the 2010 Census and 20,924 at the 2020 Census; the official estimate as of mid-2023 was 21,902. Until the mid-19th century the Banda Islands were the world's only source of the spices nutmeg and mace, produced from the nutmeg tree. The islands are also popular destinations for scuba diving and snorkeling. The main town and administrative centre is Banda Neira, located on the island of the same name."}}, {"canonical_name": "Amomum", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "amomum", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Amomum", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Amomum"], "contexts": ["plant mistaken for Rose"]}, {"entity_id": "amomum", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Amomum", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 10, "variants": ["Amomum", "Amomum Korar\u00edma", "Amomum korarima", "amamum", "amomum"], "contexts": ["ingredient for theriac", "ingredient for compositions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Amomum", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Amomum", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2755, "stable_key": "clu_49fb8a02aa3e81be", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Amomum", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "description": "Aromatic plant genus, often used in traditional medicine and perfumery.", "wikipedia_extract": "Amomum is a genus of plants containing about 111 species native to China, the Indian subcontinent, Southeast Asia, New Guinea, and Queensland. It includes several species of cardamom. Plants of this genus are remarkable for their pungency and aromatic properties. Among ancient writers, the name amomum was ascribed to various odoriferous plants that cannot be positively identified today. The word derives from Latin amomum, which is the latinisation of the Greek \u1f04\u03bc\u03c9\u03bc\u03bf\u03bd (amomon), a kind of an Indian spice plant. Edmund Roberts noted on his 1834 trip to China that amomum was used as a spice to \"season sweet dishes\" in culinary practice."}}, {"canonical_name": "Saturne", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "saturne", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "Saturne", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 6, "variants": ["Saturne"], "contexts": ["planet with a ring", "a planet in our solar system"]}, {"entity_id": "saturn\u2019s_ring", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Saturn\u2019s ring", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Saturn\u2019s ring"], "contexts": ["celestial object orbiting Saturn", "celestial feature of Saturn"]}, {"entity_id": "saturn\u2019s", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Saturn\u2019s", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Saturn\u2019s"], "contexts": ["relating to the planet Saturn"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Saturne", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Saturn\u2019s ring", "similarity": 0.899}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Saturne", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Saturn\u2019s", "similarity": 0.967}], "id": 2756, "stable_key": "clu_5656476667f1291e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Saturn", "confidence": "high", "type": "planet", "note": "The term 'Saturne' and references to 'Saturn's ring' clearly point to the planet Saturn, a common celestial body of interest in early modern astronomy."}}, {"canonical_name": "Nil", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "nil", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "Nil", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Nil"], "contexts": ["river in Egypt", "major river in Africa"]}, {"entity_id": "nil", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Nil", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 7, "variants": ["Nil"], "contexts": ["river in Africa", "river from which Egyptians did not come"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Nil", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Nil", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2757, "stable_key": "clu_32d9aec44ab246b9-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Nile River", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "The major river of northeastern Africa, historically significant.", "wikipedia_extract": "The Nile River is a major north-flowing river in northeastern Africa which flows into the Mediterranean Sea. At 7,088 km (4,404 mi) long, it is the longest river in the world, although the volume of water it carries is much smaller than other major rivers such as the Amazon or Congo. Its drainage basin covers portions of eleven countries: the Democratic Republic of the Congo, Tanzania, Burundi, Rwanda, Uganda, Kenya, Ethiopia, Eritrea, South Sudan, Sudan, and Egypt. It plays an important economic role in the economy of these nations, and it is the primary water source for South Sudan, Sudan and Egypt. The Nile has two major tributaries: the White Nile and the Blue Nile. The White Nile, being longer, is considered to be the headwaters, although the Blue Nile contributes 80% of the water and silt below the confluence of the two."}}, {"canonical_name": "Hands", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "hands", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Hands", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 10, "variants": ["Hands"], "contexts": ["body part affected by gout", "stained by Bilberry juice"]}, {"entity_id": "hands", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "hands", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["hands"], "contexts": ["appendages for grasping"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Hands", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "hands", "similarity": 0.988}], "id": 2758, "stable_key": "clu_23ff13c140e5c3b7-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hand", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "Q8418", "wikidata_description": "team sport played with a thrown ball and goals", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Handball", "note": "Referred to as a 'SUBSTANCE' but anatomically it functions as a body part/concept.", "wikipedia_extract": "Handball (also known as team handball, European handball, Olympic handball, or indoor handball) is a team sport in which two teams of seven players each (six outcourt players and a goalkeeper) pass a ball using their hands with the aim of throwing it into the goal of the opposing team. A standard match consists of two periods of 30 minutes, and the team that scores more goals wins. Modern handball is played on a court of 40 by 20 metres (131 by 66 ft), with a goal in the middle of each end. The goals are surrounded by a 6-metre (20 ft) zone where only the defending goalkeeper is allowed; goals must be scored by throwing the ball from outside the zone or while \"diving\" into it. The sport is usually played indoors, but outdoor variants exist in the forms of field handball, Czech handball (which were more common in the past) and beach handball. The game is fast and high-scoring: professional teams now typically score between 20 and 35 goals each, though lower scores were not uncommon until a few decades ago."}}, {"canonical_name": "Neck", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "neck", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Neck", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 9, "variants": ["Neck", "Neck of the Bladder", "Necks"], "contexts": ["body part where herb is applied", "body part with swellings"]}, {"entity_id": "necks", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "necks", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["necks"], "contexts": ["part of runt pigeon anatomy"]}, {"entity_id": "neck", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "neck", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["neck"], "contexts": ["part connecting head and body"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Neck", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "necks", "similarity": 0.946}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Neck", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "neck", "similarity": 0.996}], "id": 2759, "stable_key": "clu_0cbd4f6cad08e83c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Neck", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "While 'Neck' refers to a body part, in this context, it's used as a concept of anatomical location rather than a specific substance or entity being treated.", "wikidata_id": "Q106138895", "wikidata_description": "clinical trial"}}, {"canonical_name": "china", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "HERB", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "china", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "China", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["China"], "contexts": ["origin of imported Rubarb", "herb used for French Pox"]}, {"entity_id": "china", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "china", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "HERB", "count": 8, "variants": ["china"], "contexts": ["plant used for its medicinal properties", "plant used for similar cures"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "China", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "china", "similarity": 0.661}], "id": 2760, "stable_key": "clu_2f7d54c5cb198107", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cinchona", "confidence": "medium", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Cinchona L.", "family": "Rubiaceae", "note": "While 'china' is often associated with the country China, in historical medical texts it commonly refers to Cinchona bark, also known as 'quina-quina', used to treat fevers, particularly malaria. The mention of 'origin of imported Rubarb' is somewhat misleading, as rhubarb is not from China and Cinchona is the more prevalent medicinal plant associated with this term. Further context from the specific texts would solidify this.", "wikidata_id": "Q160090", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants in the family Rubiaceae", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cinchona"}}, {"canonical_name": "Ants", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "ants", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Ants", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 10, "variants": ["ants", "Ants"], "contexts": ["social insects", "insects with notable instincts, slave-making"]}, {"entity_id": "ants", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "ants", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["ants"], "contexts": ["insects studied for reasoning"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Ants", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "ants", "similarity": 0.929}], "id": 2761, "stable_key": "clu_7c4923e9abd9a0b8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ants", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "linnaean": "Formicidae", "family": "Formicidae", "description": "Social insects known for complex behaviors and colonies.", "wikipedia_extract": "Ants are eusocial insects of the family Formicidae and, along with the related wasps and bees, belong to the order Hymenoptera. Ants evolved from vespoid wasp ancestors in the Cretaceous period. More than 13,800 of an estimated total of 22,000 species have been classified. They are easily identified by their geniculate (elbowed) antennae and the distinctive node-like structure that forms their slender waists. Ants form colonies that range in size from a few dozen individuals often living in small natural cavities to highly organised colonies that may occupy large territories with a sizeable nest (or nests) that consist of millions of individuals, in some cases they reach hundreds of millions of individuals in super colonies. Typical colonies consist of various castes of sterile, wingless females, most of which are workers (ergates), as well as soldiers (dinergates) and other specialised groups."}}, {"canonical_name": "progenitor", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "progenitor", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "progenitor", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 8, "variants": ["progenitor"], "contexts": ["ancestor of a group", "ancestor"]}, {"entity_id": "progenitors", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "progenitors", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["progenitors"], "contexts": ["ancestors", "ancestral organisms"]}, {"entity_id": "progenitors", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "progenitors", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["progenitors"], "contexts": ["ancestors or parents"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "progenitor", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "progenitors", "similarity": 0.846}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "progenitors", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "progenitors", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2762, "stable_key": "clu_aadd593165b7d791", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ancestor", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'progenitor' in these contexts directly refers to an ancestor or ancestral organism, particularly in evolutionary biology.", "wikidata_id": "Q402152", "wikidata_description": "person from whom another person is descended"}}, {"canonical_name": "Sir C. Lyell", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "sir_c__lyell", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Sir C. Lyell", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 6, "variants": ["Sir C. Lyell"], "contexts": ["discovered land shell exception", "geologist, author of Principles of Geology"]}, {"entity_id": "sir_charles_lyell", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Sir Charles Lyell", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 5, "variants": ["Sir Charles Lyell"], "contexts": ["attributed temperature difference to existing causes", "geologist and author of works on Geology"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Sir C. Lyell", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Sir Charles Lyell", "similarity": 0.953}], "id": 2763, "stable_key": "clu_9f197b248e0a368d-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Charles Lyell", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1797, "death_year": 1875, "description": "Scottish geologist, author of 'Principles of Geology'", "wikidata_id": "Q76126037", "wikidata_description": "Peerage person ID=549048", "note": "The text mentions both 'Mr. Lyell' and 'Mr. Lassell'. 'Mr. Lyell' is strongly associated with 'Principles of Geology', identifying Charles Lyell. 'Mr. Lassell' is likely William Lassell, an astronomer, as mentioned in the context of astronomy."}}, {"canonical_name": "Rabbit", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "rabbit", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Rabbit", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["Rabbit", "rabbit"], "contexts": ["animal that breeds freely under unnatural conditions", "ancestor of domestic rabbits"]}, {"entity_id": "rabbits", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "rabbits", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["rabbits"], "contexts": ["domesticated animal", "domesticated mammals with differing breeds"]}, {"entity_id": "rabbits", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "rabbits", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["rabbits"], "contexts": ["type of mammal", "mammals used in experiments"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Rabbit", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "rabbits", "similarity": 0.904}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "rabbits", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "rabbits", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2764, "stable_key": "clu_c140bd2626ee12b5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Rabbit", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal"}}, {"canonical_name": "H. C. Watson", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 11, "members": [{"entity_id": "h__c__watson", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "H. C. Watson", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 7, "variants": ["H. C. Watson", "Mr. H. C. Watson"], "contexts": ["naturalist who marked British plants", "provided advice on species variation"]}, {"entity_id": "watson", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Watson", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR