yl has a hardness of 7.5\u20138 on the Mohs scale. Most emeralds have many inclusions, so their toughness (resistance to breakage) is classified as generally poor. Emerald is a cyclosilicate. It occurs mainly in association with quartz, muscovite, albite, schorl, microcline, fluorite, smoky quartz and elbaite. The word \"emerald\" is derived (via Old French: esmeraude and Middle English: emeraude), from Vulgar Latin: esmaralda/esmaraldus, a variant of Latin smaragdus, which was via Ancient Greek: \u03c3\u03bc\u03ac\u03c1\u03b1\u03b3\u03b4\u03bf\u03c2 (sm\u00e1ragdos; \"green gem\")."}}, {"canonical_name": "mer Noire", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "mer_noire", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "mer Noire", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 7, "variants": ["mer Noire"], "contexts": ["sea between Europe and Asia", "sea between Eastern Europe and Western Asia"]}, {"entity_id": "mer_noire", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Mer Noire", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Mer Noire"], "contexts": ["sea with a specific surface area", "Black Sea"]}, {"entity_id": "mer-noire", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Mer-Noire", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mer-Noire"], "contexts": ["sea in Eastern Europe"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "mer Noire", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Mer Noire", "similarity": 0.989}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "mer Noire", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Mer-Noire", "similarity": 0.824}], "id": 2830, "stable_key": "clu_b5fd8c6aa5d94f13", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Black Sea", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "International", "wikidata_id": "Q166", "wikidata_description": "sea between Europe and Asia", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black%20Sea"}}, {"canonical_name": "Tartaria", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "tartarie", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "Tartarie", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 3, "variants": ["Tartarie"], "contexts": ["historical region in Central Asia", "region where hippopotamus teeth were found"]}, {"entity_id": "tartaria", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Tartaria", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 7, "variants": ["Tartaria"], "contexts": ["region of origin for Avicena", "historical region"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Tartarie", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Tartaria", "similarity": 0.941}], "id": 2831, "stable_key": "clu_c67bb2628f58afab", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tartary", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Historical region in Central and Northern Asia.", "note": "Refers to a vast historical region, often vaguely defined, in Central and Northern Asia.", "wikipedia_extract": "Tartary (Latin: Tartaria; French: Tartarie; German: Tartarei; Russian: \u0422\u0430\u0440\u0442\u0430\u0440\u0438\u044f, romanized: Tartariya) or Tatary (Russian: \u0422\u0430\u0442\u0430\u0440\u0438\u044f, romanized: Tatariya) was a blanket term used in Western European literature and cartography for a vast part of Asia bounded by the Caspian Sea, the Ural Mountains, the Pacific Ocean, and the northern borders of China, India, and Persia, at a time when this region was largely unknown to European geographers. The active use of the toponym (place name) can be traced from the 13th to the 19th centuries. In European sources, Tartary became the most common name for Central Asia that had no connection with the real polities or ethnic groups of the region; until the 19th century, European knowledge of the area remained extremely scarce and fragmentary. In modern English-speaking tradition, the region formerly known as Tartary is usually called Inner Asia or Central Eurasia. Much of this area consists of arid plains, the main nomadic population of which in the past was engaged in animal husbandry. Ignorance surrounding Tartary's use as a place name has spawned pseudohistoric conspiracy theories including ideas of a \"hidden past\" and \"mud floods\"."}}, {"canonical_name": "chaux", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "chaux", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "chaux", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["chaux"], "contexts": ["product of calcination of certain materials", "calcium oxide"]}, {"entity_id": "chaux", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "chaux", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 8, "variants": ["chaux"], "contexts": ["found by incineration", "substance found in water residue"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "chaux", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "chaux", "similarity": 0.829}], "id": 2832, "stable_key": "clu_f46528fd6dc25325", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lime", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "In early modern chemistry, 'chaux' referred to quicklime (calcium oxide) or slaked lime (calcium hydroxide), both derived from calcination of limestone.", "wikidata_id": "Q133315", "wikidata_description": "city in Ireland in the province of Munster", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Limerick"}}, {"canonical_name": "carbonate de chaux", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "carbonate_de_chaux_hydrat\u00e9", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "carbonate de chaux hydrat\u00e9", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["carbonate de chaux hydrat\u00e9"], "contexts": ["hydrated calcium carbonate from quarries"]}, {"entity_id": "carbonate_de_chaux", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "carbonate de chaux", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["carbonate de chaux"], "contexts": ["calcium carbonate agglutinating shells into rocks"]}, {"entity_id": "carbonate_de_chaux", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "carbonate de chaux", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 8, "variants": ["carbonate de chaux", "carbonate de ma-gn\u00e9sie", "carbonate de soude"], "contexts": ["calcium carbonate", "chemical compound"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "carbonate de chaux hydrat\u00e9", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "carbonate de chaux", "similarity": 0.902}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "carbonate de chaux", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "carbonate de chaux", "similarity": 0.86}], "id": 2833, "stable_key": "clu_eafc3e7257a6e8ec", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Calcium carbonate", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "Directly refers to calcium carbonate, which occurs naturally as limestone, marble, and chalk, and is also found in shells.", "wikidata_id": "Q23767", "wikidata_description": "chemical compound", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calcium%20carbonate"}}, {"canonical_name": "froment", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "froment", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "froment", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["froment"], "contexts": ["wheat, resists winter cold"]}, {"entity_id": "froment", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "froment", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 9, "variants": ["froment"], "contexts": ["grain cultivated in mountains", "wheat"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "froment", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "froment", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2834, "stable_key": "clu_4a7f525c853ba3c6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Wheat", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Triticum aestivum Linnaeus", "family": "Poaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q12106", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants (focus on taxonomy here, not on agriculture)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Triticum"}}, {"canonical_name": "privy Parts", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "privy_parts", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "privy Parts", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 9, "variants": ["privy Parts", "Privy parts", "privie parts", "privy parts"], "contexts": ["body part washed with decoction", "genital and anal areas"]}, {"entity_id": "partes_intimasdo_corpo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "partes intimasdo corpo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["partes intimasdo corpo"], "contexts": ["internal parts of the body"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "privy Parts", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "partes intimasdo corpo", "similarity": 0.92}], "id": 2835, "stable_key": "clu_d83a145826d4b5ed", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Genitals", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The phrase 'privy Parts' or 'partes intimasdo corpo' refers to the external genitalia and surrounding pelvic region, often with a focus on their medicinal or anatomical significance in the historical context.", "wikidata_id": "Q844342", "wikidata_description": "reproductive system of the human female, made up of internal and external organs"}}, {"canonical_name": "Womens Breasts", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "womens_breasts", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Womens Breasts", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 9, "variants": ["Womens Breasts", "Womens"], "contexts": ["body part where milk is increased", "body parts"]}, {"entity_id": "breasts", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "breasts", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["breasts"], "contexts": ["mammary glands on the chest"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Womens Breasts", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "breasts", "similarity": 0.96}], "id": 2836, "stable_key": "clu_78499e46de20c355", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Breasts", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Similar to 'Neck', 'Breasts' refers to an anatomical location and concept, not a distinct substance or entity."}}, {"canonical_name": "deformities", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "deformities", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "deformities", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 9, "variants": ["deformities", "Deformities", "Deformities in the Skin", "Deformity"], "contexts": ["abnormal physical characteristics", "in the face"]}, {"entity_id": "malconformations", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "malconformations", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["malconformations"], "contexts": ["abnormal physical developments"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "deformities", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "malconformations", "similarity": 0.928}], "id": 2837, "stable_key": "clu_9e675d4b82ee49ee", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Congenital malformations", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Congenital malformations", "wikidata_id": "Q6940403", "wikidata_description": "human disease"}}, {"canonical_name": "Wheesing", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "wheesing", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Wheesing", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 9, "variants": ["Wheesing", "Wheesings", "wheesing", "wheesings"], "contexts": ["difficulty breathing", "helped by fennel seed"]}, {"entity_id": "sneezing", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "sneezing", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["sneezing"], "contexts": ["involuntary expulsion of air"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Wheesing", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "sneezing", "similarity": 0.9}], "id": 2838, "stable_key": "clu_9b2c40a295c6e3de", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "A sneeze (also known as sternutation) is a semi-autonomous, convulsive expulsion of air from the lungs through the nose and mouth, usually caused by foreign particles irritating the nasal mucosa. A sneeze expels air forcibly from the mouth and nose in an explosive, spasmodic involuntary action. This action allows for mucus to escape through the nasal cavity and saliva to escape from the oral cavity. Sneezing is possibly linked to sudden exposure to bright light (known as photic sneeze reflex), sudden change (drop) in temperature, breeze of cold air, a particularly full stomach, exposure to allergens, or viral infection. Because sneezes can spread disease through infectious aerosol droplets, it is recommended to cover one's mouth and nose with the forearm, the inside of the elbow, a facial tissue or a handkerchief while sneezing. In addition to covering the mouth, looking down is also recommended to change the direction of the droplets spread and avoid high concentration in the human breathing heights."}}, {"canonical_name": "Astrologers", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "astrologers", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Astrologers", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 9, "variants": ["Astrologers", "Astrologer"], "contexts": ["attributed Henbane to Jupiter", "practitioners of astrology"]}, {"entity_id": "astr\u00f3logos", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "astr\u00f3logos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["astr\u00f3logos"], "contexts": ["Astrologers appointed by sovereigns"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Astrologers", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "astr\u00f3logos", "similarity": 0.967}], "id": 2839, "stable_key": "clu_d4cce850e4a97f0a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Astrologers", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "While 'astrologer' refers to a person, the cluster groups the general concept of 'astrologers' as a collective, often invoked for their influence on medicine and governance in early modern texts.  The modern referent is the field of astrology itself, or the practitioners of it collectively.", "wikidata_id": "Q34362", "wikidata_description": "ensemble of traditions, beliefs and divinations that interpret the positions of the planets and other celestial bodies; along with their correlation with terrestrial events"}}, {"canonical_name": "Pills", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "pills", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Pills", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 7, "variants": ["Pills", "Pill"], "contexts": ["medicinal form", "dosage form of medicine"]}, {"entity_id": "pillole", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "pillole", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 3, "variants": ["pillole"], "contexts": ["medicinal preparations softened with humors", "pills, a type of compound medicine"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Pills", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "pillole", "similarity": 0.883}], "id": 2840, "stable_key": "clu_d4f4f246345dee82", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pills", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "Dosage forms of medicine, often compounded.", "wikipedia_extract": "The combined oral contraceptive pill (COCP), often referred to as the birth control pill or colloquially as \"the pill\", is a type of birth control that is designed to be taken orally by women. It is the oral form of combined hormonal contraception. The pill contains two important hormones: a progestin (a synthetic form of the hormone progestogen/progesterone) and estrogen (usually ethinylestradiol or 17\u03b2 estradiol). When taken correctly, it alters the menstrual cycle to eliminate ovulation and prevent pregnancy. Combined oral contraceptive pills were first approved for contraceptive use in the United States in 1960, and remain a very popular form of birth control. They are used by more than 100 million women worldwide  including about 9 million women in the United States."}}, {"canonical_name": "Duque de Baviera", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "dukes_of_bavaria", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Dukes of Bavaria", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Dukes of Bavaria"], "contexts": ["supported Mathiolus' work"]}, {"entity_id": "duque_de_baviera", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Duque de Baviera", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 9, "variants": ["Duque de Baviera", "Duque de Cadaval", "Duque de Hetruria", "Duque do Cadaval", "Duque doCadaval", "Duquede Baviera", "DuquedeBaviera"], "contexts": ["employer of Martim Rulando", "Duke of Bavaria"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Dukes of Bavaria", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Duque de Baviera", "similarity": 0.854}], "id": 2841, "stable_key": "clu_bd1060778513d1c0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Duke of Bavaria", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "A noble patron, likely supporting scientific or medical endeavors.", "note": "Refers to historical rulers of Bavaria.", "wikipedia_extract": "The following is a list of rulers during the history of Bavaria. Bavaria was ruled by several dukes and kings, partitioned and reunited, under several dynasties. Since 1918, Bavaria has been under a republican form of government, and from 1949, Bavaria has been a democratic state in the Federal Republic of Germany. Ducal Bavaria (also known as the \"Old Stem duchy\") Around 548 the kings of the Franks placed the border region of Bavaria under the administration of a duke\u2014possibly Frankish or possibly chosen from amongst the local leading families\u2014who was supposed to act as a regional governor for the Frankish king. The first duke we know of, and likely the first, was Gariwald, or Garibald I, a member of the powerful Agilolfing family. This was the beginning of a series of Agilolfing dukes that was to last until 788."}}, {"canonical_name": "classes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "classes", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "classes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 8, "variants": ["classes"], "contexts": ["taxonomic rank above orders", "taxonomic rank above order"]}, {"entity_id": "classes", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "classes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["classes"], "contexts": ["social divisions and ranks", "social divisions within communities"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "classes", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "classes", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2842, "stable_key": "clu_59e01b739d40afe8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Class (taxonomy)", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The context of 'assign to a category' is general, but the presence of Darwin points strongly to taxonomic classification."}}, {"canonical_name": "E. Forbes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "e__forbes", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "E. Forbes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 7, "variants": ["E. Forbes"], "contexts": ["speaks confidently about shell coloration", "researches on marine life and depths"]}, {"entity_id": "j__forbes", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "J. Forbes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["J. Forbes"], "contexts": ["invented seismometer", "Professor who invented a self-registering apparatus"]}, {"entity_id": "e__forbes", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "E. Forbes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["E. Forbes"], "contexts": ["professor providing account of Santorino"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "E. Forbes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "J. Forbes", "similarity": 0.935}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "E. Forbes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "E. Forbes", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2843, "stable_key": "clu_0263d31e3ab78655-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Edward Forbes", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "A naturalist and geologist, known for his conclusions on biogeography.", "note": "Distinguished from Forbes Winslow, a different author.", "wikipedia_extract": "Edward Forbes FRS, FGS (12 February 1815 \u2013 18 November 1854) was a Manx naturalist. In 1846, he proposed that the distributions of montane plants and animals had been compressed downslope, and some oceanic islands connected to the mainland, during the recent ice age. This mechanism, which was the first natural explanation to explain the distributions of the same species on now-isolated islands and mountain tops, was discovered independently by Charles Darwin, who credited Forbes with the idea. He also incorrectly deduced the so-called azoic hypothesis, that life under the sea would decline to the point that no life forms could exist below a certain depth. Forbes was born at Douglas in the Isle of Man. His father was a well-to-do banker.", "birth_year": 1815, "death_year": 1854}}, {"canonical_name": "molluscs", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "molluscs", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "molluscs", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 7, "variants": ["Molluscs", "molluscs"], "contexts": ["extinct Silurian molluscs that changed greatly", "animal class"]}, {"entity_id": "mollusca", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "mollusca", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["mollusca"], "contexts": ["main division of muscle shells", "aquatic invertebrates"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "molluscs", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "mollusca", "similarity": 0.984}], "id": 2844, "stable_key": "clu_36288d7aba1ba529", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mollusks", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "description": "Invertebrate animals with soft bodies, often protected by a shell.", "wikipedia_extract": "Mollusca is a phylum of protostomic invertebrate animals, whose members are known as molluscs or mollusks (). 86,600 extant species of molluscs are recognized, making it the second-largest animal phylum after Arthropoda. The number of additional fossil species is estimated between 60,000 and 100,000, and the proportion of undescribed species is very high. Many taxa remain poorly studied. Molluscs are the largest marine phylum, comprising about 23% of all the named marine organisms. They are highly diverse, not just in size and anatomical structure, but also in behavior and habitat, as numerous groups are freshwater and even terrestrial species. The phylum is typically divided into 7 or 8 taxonomic classes, of which two are entirely extinct."}}, {"canonical_name": "Bermuda", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "bermuda", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Bermuda", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 6, "variants": ["Bermuda"], "contexts": ["island with peculiar soil and no endemic land birds", "island stocked by birds"]}, {"entity_id": "iles_bermudes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Iles Bermudes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Iles Bermudes", "Bermudes"], "contexts": ["group of islands", "archipelago location"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Bermuda", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Iles Bermudes", "similarity": 0.961}], "id": 2845, "stable_key": "clu_220d490d5daf5847", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bermuda", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "An archipelago and British Overseas Territory in the North Atlantic Ocean.", "wikipedia_extract": "Bermuda is a British Overseas Territory in the North Atlantic Ocean. The closest land outside the territory is in the U.S. state of North Carolina, about 1,035 km (643 mi) to the west-northwest. Bermuda is an archipelago consisting of 181 islands, although the most significant islands are connected by bridges and appear to form one landmass. It has a land area of 54 square kilometres (21 sq mi). Bermuda has a tropical climate, with warm winters and hot summers. Its climate also exhibits oceanic features similar to other coastal areas in the Northern Hemisphere with warm, moist air from the ocean ensuring relatively high humidity and stabilising temperatures."}}, {"canonical_name": "quadrupeds", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "quadrupeds", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "quadrupeds", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 5, "variants": ["quadrupeds"], "contexts": ["four-footed mammals", "animals with four feet"]}, {"entity_id": "quadrupeds", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Quadrupeds", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 5, "variants": ["Quadrupeds"], "contexts": ["four-footed mammals", "animals distributed geographically"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "quadrupeds", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Quadrupeds", "similarity": 0.95}], "id": 2846, "stable_key": "clu_6108ffa24179d595", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Quadruped", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "The terms 'quadrupedes', 'quadrumana', and 'quadrupeds' all refer to four-limbed animals, with 'quadrumana' being a specific subgroup (primates with grasping feet).", "wikipedia_extract": "A tripod stance is a behaviour in which quadruped animals rear up on their hind legs and use their tail to support this position. Several animals use this behaviour to improve observation or surveillance, and during feeding, grooming, thermoregulation, or fighting. The common dwarf mongoose (Helogale parvula) adopts a tripod stance when being vigilant for predators. In a similar mammal, the thirteen-lined ground squirrel (Ictidomys tridecemlineatus), vigilance behaviour includes four postures: (1) quadrupedal alert (all four feet on the ground with head above the horizontal); (2) semiupright alert (on hind feet with a distinctive slouch); (3) upright alert (on hind feet with back straight and mostly perpendicular to the ground); (4) extended upright alert (similar to upright alert except that the squirrel extends its hind legs, (see image of meerkats). In meerkats (Suricata suricatta), the tripod stance may be adopted relatively briefly during foraging, in which case it is termed guarding behaviour, or for substantially longer periods when the animal is not foraging, in which case it is termed sentinel behaviour. Macropods can stand erect on their hind legs, supported by their tail as the third leg of the tripod.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tripod_stance"}}, {"canonical_name": "Dr. Falconer", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "dr__falconer", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Dr. Falconer", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 8, "variants": ["Dr. Falconer", "Falconer"], "contexts": ["source of information on Indian plants", "informs about tropics' destructive damp and heat"]}, {"entity_id": "dr__falconer", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Dr. Falconer", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Dr. Falconer"], "contexts": ["investigator of Sewalik Hills"]}, {"entity_id": "falconer,_dr_", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Falconer, Dr.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Falconer, Dr."], "contexts": ["researcher of fossil remains"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Dr. Falconer", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Dr. Falconer", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Dr. Falconer", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Falconer, Dr.", "similarity": 0.919}], "id": 2847, "stable_key": "clu_0369536c150c790e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hugh Falconer", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1808, "death_year": 1865, "description": "Scottish surgeon, botanist, paleontologist, and geologist.", "wikidata_id": "Q76037335", "wikidata_description": "Peerage person ID=485474"}}, {"canonical_name": "Wallace", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "wallace", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Wallace", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 5, "variants": ["Mr. Wallace", "Wallace"], "contexts": ["naturalist studying Malay archipelago", "scientist who advanced theory of species origin"]}, {"entity_id": "wallace", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Wallace", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 5, "variants": ["Wallace", "Wallich"], "contexts": ["account of durian experience", "author"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Wallace", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Wallace", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2848, "stable_key": "clu_010d2823454686b7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Alfred Russel Wallace", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1823, "death_year": 1913, "description": "English naturalist, explorer, geographer, anthropologist, and biologist.", "wikidata_id": "Q160627", "wikidata_description": "British naturalist, explorer, geographer, anthropologist and biologist (1823-1913)"}}, {"canonical_name": "habits", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "habits", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Habits", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["habits", "Habits"], "contexts": ["learned behaviors of animals", "learned or ingrained patterns of behavior"]}, {"entity_id": "habits", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "habits", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 7, "variants": ["habits"], "contexts": ["repeated behaviors", "learned patterns of behavior"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Habits", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "habits", "similarity": 0.969}], "id": 2849, "stable_key": "clu_c8a62cedb3cb68be-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Habits", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to learned behaviors and ingrained patterns of action, both in animals and humans.", "wikidata_id": "Q52105", "wikidata_description": "ecological or environmental area inhabited by a particular species; natural environment in which an organism lives, or the physical environment that surrounds a species population"}}, {"canonical_name": "Abyssinia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "abyssinia", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Abyssinia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Abyssinia"], "contexts": ["historical name for Ethiopia", "location with southern vegetable forms"]}, {"entity_id": "abyssinia", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Abyssinia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 7, "variants": ["Abyssinia", "da Abyssinia"], "contexts": ["region of origin for cardamom", "region of origin for korar\u00edma"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Abyssinia", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Abyssinia", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2850, "stable_key": "clu_a6eecf18423b55ef", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ethiopia", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Ethiopia", "note": "The spelling '\u00c6thiopia' and 'Eti\u00f3pia' are historical variants of Ethiopia.", "wikidata_id": "Q115", "wikidata_description": "country in the Horn of Africa", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ethiopia", "wikipedia_extract": "Ethiopia, officially the Federal Democratic Republic of Ethiopia, is a landlocked country located in the Horn of Africa region of East Africa. It shares borders with Eritrea to the north, Djibouti to the northeast, Somalia to the east, Kenya to the south, South Sudan to the west, and Sudan to the northwest. Ethiopia covers a land area of 1,104,300 square kilometres (426,400 sq mi). As of 2025, it has around 135 million inhabitants, making it the 14th-most populous country. The national capital and largest city, Addis Ababa, lies several kilometres west of the East African Rift that splits the country into the African and Somali tectonic plates. Anatomically modern humans emerged from modern-day Ethiopia.", "description": "A country in Eastern Africa, historically known as Abyssinia."}}, {"canonical_name": "Berkeley", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "berkeley", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Berkeley", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Berkeley"], "contexts": ["assisted with experiments"]}, {"entity_id": "berkeley", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Berkeley", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 9, "variants": ["Berkeley"], "contexts": ["philosopher of definite images theory", "philosopher who compared thought to algebra"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Berkeley", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Berkeley", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2851, "stable_key": "clu_8cc2fe6082ab0404", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "George Berkeley", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1685, "death_year": 1753, "description": "Irish philosopher and bishop, known for idealism", "note": "The context 'philosopher of definite images theory' strongly points to George Berkeley. The reference in 'origin_of_species_darwin_1859' is likely a misattribution or a secondary reference to his philosophical ideas.", "wikidata_id": "Q82049", "wikidata_description": "Irish idealist philosopher and Anglican bishop (1685\u20131753)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George%20Berkeley", "wikipedia_extract": "George Berkeley ( BARK-lee; 12 March 1685 \u2013 14 January 1753), known as Bishop Berkeley (Bishop of Cloyne of the Anglican Church of Ireland), was an Anglo-Irish philosopher, writer, and clergyman who is regarded as the founder of \"immaterialism\", a philosophical theory he developed which later came to be known as subjective idealism. He has also been called \"the father of idealism\" by German philosopher Arthur Schopenhauer. Berkeley played a leading role in the empiricism movement and was one of its pioneers. He was among the most cited philosophers of 18th-century Europe, and his works deeply influenced later thinkers such as Immanuel Kant and David Hume. In 1709, Berkeley published his first major work An Essay Towards a New Theory of Vision, in which he discussed the limitations of human vision and advanced the theory that the proper objects of sight are not material objects, but light and colour. This foreshadowed his most well-known philosophical work A Treatise Concerning the Principles of Human Knowledge, published in 1710, which, after its poor reception, he rewrote in dialogue form and published under the title Three Dialogues Between Hylas and Philonous in 1713."}}, {"canonical_name": "ni\u00f1os", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "ni\u00f1os", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "ni\u00f1os", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 6, "variants": ["ni\u00f1os"], "contexts": ["dung of children used in remedy", "young human beings"]}, {"entity_id": "ni\u00f1os", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "ni\u00f1os", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 3, "variants": ["ni\u00f1os"], "contexts": ["children, for whom this preparation is useful", "young human beings"]}, {"entity_id": "ni\u00f1a", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "ni\u00f1a", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["ni\u00f1a"], "contexts": ["young female child"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "ni\u00f1os", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "ni\u00f1os", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "ni\u00f1os", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "ni\u00f1a", "similarity": 0.874}], "id": 2852, "stable_key": "clu_f73e3e9c843f42c8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Children", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to young human beings, often in the context of their excreta or ailments.", "wikidata_id": "Q10954511", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Child%20%28surname%29"}}, {"canonical_name": "Seuilla", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "seuilla", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "Seuilla", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Seuilla"], "contexts": ["city where a canon was from", "city where Monardez was from"]}, {"entity_id": "seuilla", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Seuilla", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 6, "variants": ["Seuilla"], "contexts": ["city where the physician was from", "city that is a port for the West Indies"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Seuilla", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Seuilla", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2853, "stable_key": "clu_ad4681fdfe986dab", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Seville", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Spain", "wikidata_id": "Q95088", "wikidata_description": "province of Spain", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Province%20of%20Seville"}}, {"canonical_name": "Montan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "montes", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "montes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["montes"], "contexts": ["mountains", "mountains where Tepetlachichicxihuitl grows"]}, {"entity_id": "montan", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Montan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 6, "variants": ["Montan", "Montau"], "contexts": ["location in the Andes", "location in Peru"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "montes", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Montan", "similarity": 0.923}], "id": 2854, "stable_key": "clu_d2709a74aa27d0c3-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Montane", "confidence": "medium", "type": "concept", "note": "The primary usage 'Montan' refers to 'montane' or mountainous regions, not a specific place. The 'mountain' reference further supports this interpretation."}}, {"canonical_name": "Almizque", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "al_mizque", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "al mizque", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["al mizque"], "contexts": ["plant scent comparison", "aromatic for syrup"]}, {"entity_id": "almizque", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Almizque", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 8, "variants": ["Almizque", "Almiogue", "almizque"], "contexts": ["animal product, musk", "musk"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "al mizque", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Almizque", "similarity": 0.924}], "id": 2855, "stable_key": "clu_3f44f6cdc91bc3c4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Musk", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "note": "Almizque is the Spanish word for musk, an animal product historically used in perfumery and medicine.", "wikidata_id": "Q206879", "wikidata_description": "central district and city in Nev\u015fehir Province, Turkey", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nev%C5%9Fehir"}}, {"canonical_name": "tigres", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "tigres", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "tigres", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["tigres"], "contexts": ["fierce animals killed by plant leaves", "fierce animals killed by the tree's gum"]}, {"entity_id": "tigres", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "tigres", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 8, "variants": ["tigres", "tigre"], "contexts": ["dangerous animals in Malaca", "dangerous animals in Malaca's forests"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "tigres", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "tigres", "similarity": 0.826}], "id": 2856, "stable_key": "clu_3e045ed774517d52", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tiger", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q19939", "wikidata_description": "species of big cat", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tiger"}}, {"canonical_name": "Cara\u00f1a", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "cara\u00f1a", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "cara\u00f1a", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "RESIN", "count": 2, "variants": ["cara\u00f1a"], "contexts": ["gum from tree, medicinal", "gum ingredient in ceroto"]}, {"entity_id": "cara\u00f1a", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Cara\u00f1a", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 8, "variants": ["Cara\u00f1a"], "contexts": ["resin from Tierra Firme", "resin used by indigenous people"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "cara\u00f1a", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Cara\u00f1a", "similarity": 0.579}], "id": 2857, "stable_key": "clu_fccad8f3208940f6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cara\u00f1a resin", "confidence": "medium", "type": "plant", "note": "Cara\u00f1a refers to a resin, likely from a tree. The specific species is not readily identifiable without more context."}}, {"canonical_name": "amargos", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "amargas", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "amargas", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["amargas"], "contexts": ["bitter quality of the plant", "taste quality of medicinal substances"]}, {"entity_id": "amargos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "amargos", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 8, "variants": ["amargos", "amargo", "amargor"], "contexts": ["quality that causes effervescence", "bitter qualities"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "amargas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "amargos", "similarity": 0.929}], "id": 2858, "stable_key": "clu_61ee8e160fb395ca-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "bitter taste", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q1517187", "wikidata_description": "basic taste", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bitterness", "wikipedia_extract": "Bitterness, also known as bitter, is one of the most sensitive of tastes, and many experience it as unpleasant, sharp, or off-putting, but it is sometimes desirable and intentionally added via various bittering agents. Common bitter foods and drinks include coffee, unsweetened cocoa, South American mate, coca tea, Momordica charantia, unripe olives, citrus peel, some varieties of cheese, many plants in the Brassicaceae family, Taraxacum greens, Marrubium vulgare, wild chicory, and escarole. The ethanol in alcoholic beverages tastes bitter, as do the additional bittering ingredients found in some alcoholic beverages, including hops in beer and gentian in bitters. Quinine is also known for its bitter taste and is found in tonic water. Bitterness is of interest to those studying evolution, as well as to various health researchers as a large number of naturally occurring bitter compounds are known to be toxic. The ability to detect bitter-tasting, toxic compounds at low thresholds is thought to serve an important protective function."}}, {"canonical_name": "anil", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "a\u00f1il", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "a\u00f1il", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["a\u00f1il"], "contexts": ["indigo plant for making color"]}, {"entity_id": "anil", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "anil", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 9, "variants": ["ANIL", "anil", "Anil"], "contexts": ["plant cultivated in Sicily", "plant to be discussed"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "a\u00f1il", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "anil", "similarity": 0.595}], "id": 2859, "stable_key": "clu_b70c87fa8642a1fb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Indigo", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Indigofera tinctoria Linnaeus", "family": "Fabaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q310648", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indigofera"}}, {"canonical_name": "orelhas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "orejas", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "orejas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["orejas"], "contexts": ["auditory organs"]}, {"entity_id": "orelhas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "orelhas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 9, "variants": ["orelhas", "orelha"], "contexts": ["body part", "parts of the head"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "orejas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "orelhas", "similarity": 0.98}], "id": 2860, "stable_key": "clu_48282be4b170a52b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ear", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "Context suggests anatomical part rather than the living organ, but 'ear' is the most appropriate modern referent for the object/part.", "wikidata_id": "Q7362", "wikidata_description": "organ that detects sound; organ of hearing and balance", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ear"}}, {"canonical_name": "Minas Geraes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "minas_geraes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Minas Geraes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 9, "variants": ["Minas Geraes", "Mina^ Geraes", "Minaes Geraes", "Minas-Geraes"], "contexts": ["region in Brazil", "state in Brazil"]}, {"entity_id": "minas_geraes", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Minas Geraes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Minas Geraes"], "contexts": ["diamond district in Brazil"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Minas Geraes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Minas Geraes", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2861, "stable_key": "clu_45eb2ebfe2e41d82", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Minas Gerais", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Brazil", "wikidata_id": "Q39109", "wikidata_description": "state in the Southeast Region of Brazil", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belo_Horizonte", "wikipedia_extract": "Belo Horizonte is the sixth-largest city in Brazil, with a population of around 2.4 million, and the third largest metropolitan area, containing a population of 6 million. It is the 13th-largest city in South America and the 18th-largest in the Americas. The metropolis is anchor to the Belo Horizonte metropolitan area, ranked as the third most populous metropolitan area in Brazil and the 17th most populous in the Americas. Belo Horizonte is the capital of the state of Minas Gerais, Brazil's second-most populous state. It is the first planned modern city in Brazil. The region was first settled in the early 18th century, but the city as it is known today was planned and constructed in the 1890s to replace Ouro Preto as the capital of Minas Gerais."}}, {"canonical_name": "San Carlos del Rio Negro", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "san_carlos_del_rio_negro", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "San Carlos del Rio Negro", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 9, "variants": ["San Carlos del Rio Negro"], "contexts": ["location linked astronomically", "settlement near Brazil"]}, {"entity_id": "st__carlos_del_rio_negro", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "St. Carlos del Rio Negro", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["St. Carlos del Rio Negro"], "contexts": ["location with low magnetic force"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "San Carlos del Rio Negro", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "St. Carlos del Rio Negro", "similarity": 0.976}], "id": 2862, "stable_key": "clu_5ba7413bd09fdbad", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "San Carlos de R\u00edo Negro", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Venezuela", "note": "A settlement and fort in Venezuela on the Rio Negro.", "wikidata_id": "Q1269422", "wikidata_description": "city", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San%20Carlos%20de%20R%C3%ADo%20Negro", "wikipedia_extract": "San Carlos de R\u00edo Negro is a city in Venezuela's Amazonas State, serving as the administrative capital of the municipal district of R\u00edo Negro. It is inhabited primarily by Amerindian people, in particular the Yanomami and Baniwa (Kurripako) peoples. It sits on the opposite side of the Rio Negro from the Colombian city of San Felipe. The city has approximately 1,200 inhabitants. The city of San Carlos de R\u00edo Negro was founded in 1759 as a camp set up by the expedition captained by Jos\u00e9 Solano y Bote. The expedition moved into the area to explore the extent of the limits of exploration as defined by the Treaty of Madrid between the Crowns of Portugal and Spain."}}, {"canonical_name": "Quevedo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "quevedo", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Quevedo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 9, "variants": ["Quevedo"], "contexts": ["captain of Spanish frigate", "recorded temperature maximum"]}, {"entity_id": "quevedo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Quevedo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Quevedo"], "contexts": ["Spanish writer"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Quevedo", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Quevedo", "similarity": 0.634}], "id": 2863, "stable_key": "clu_f6926189dee7894d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Francisco de Quevedo", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1580, "death_year": 1645, "description": "Spanish poet and writer of the Baroque period", "note": "The dual context of 'captain of Spanish frigate' and 'Spanish writer' is contradictory. Francisco de Quevedo was a prominent writer. The frigate captain reference might be a different person or an error.", "wikidata_id": "Q201315", "wikidata_description": "Spanish writer; (1580-1645)"}}, {"canonical_name": "Antigua", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "antigua", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Antigua", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 8, "variants": ["Antigua", "Aantigua", "V Antigua"], "contexts": ["island name", "island in the Caribbean"]}, {"entity_id": "antigua", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Antigua", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Antigua"], "contexts": ["island where earthquake was felt", "island in the Antilles"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Antigua", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Antigua", "similarity": 0.876}], "id": 2864, "stable_key": "clu_9bc5285a6eec889f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Antigua Guatemala", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Guatemala", "note": "Humboldt's 'Relation historique' often discusses volcanic and seismic activity in the Americas. Antigua Guatemala was a significant colonial capital and experienced earthquakes.", "wikidata_id": "Q212773", "wikidata_description": "city in the central highlands of Guatemala"}}, {"canonical_name": "Honduras", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "honduras", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Honduras", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 7, "variants": ["Honduras"], "contexts": ["gulf", "country in Central America"]}, {"entity_id": "honduras", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Honduras", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Honduras"], "contexts": ["region with a type of gargaparilla", "a region where medicinal plants are sourced"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Honduras", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Honduras", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2865, "stable_key": "clu_7721f3f6cb05e33f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Honduras", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Country in Central America, known for its gulf and medicinal plants.", "wikipedia_extract": "Honduras, officially the Republic of Honduras, is a country in Central America. It is bordered to the west by Guatemala, to the southwest by El Salvador, to the southeast by Nicaragua, to the south by the Pacific Ocean at the Gulf of Fonseca, and to the north by the Gulf of Honduras, a large inlet of the Caribbean Sea. Its capital and largest city is Tegucigalpa. Honduras was home to several important Mesoamerican cultures, most notably the Maya, before Spanish colonization in the sixteenth century. The Spanish introduced Catholicism and the now-predominant Spanish language, along with numerous customs that have blended with the indigenous culture. Honduras became independent in 1821 and has since been a republic, although it has consistently endured much social strife and political instability, and remains one of the poorest countries in the Western Hemisphere."}}, {"canonical_name": "Los Pastos", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "los_pastos", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Los Pastos", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 7, "variants": ["Los Pastos", "los Pastos"], "contexts": ["mountainous district in Spanish America", "province belonging to Quito"]}, {"entity_id": "los_pa_stos", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Los Pa.stos", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Los Pa.stos"], "contexts": ["plateau of a province", "province name"]}, {"entity_id": "los_pastos", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Los Pastos", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Los Pastos"], "contexts": ["elevated plateau"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Los Pastos", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Los Pastos", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Los Pa.stos", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Los Pastos", "similarity": 0.584}], "id": 2866, "stable_key": "clu_f5e258a0607f216f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Los Pastos", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Colombia", "note": "Los Pastos refers to a high-altitude plateau region in southwestern Colombia, known for its mountainous terrain.", "wikidata_id": "Q3897521", "wikidata_description": "", "wikipedia_extract": "Pasto, officially San Juan de Pasto (Spanish pronunciation: [sa\u014b \u02c8xwan de \u02c8pasto]; \"Saint John of Pasto\"), is the capital of the department of Nari\u00f1o, in southern Colombia. Pasto was founded in 1537 and named after indigenous people of the area. In the 2018 census, the municipality had a population of 392,930. Pasto is located in the Atriz Valley on the Andes cordillera, at the foot of the Galeras volcano. The etymology of the word Pasto can be traced to the indigenous people who inhabited the region at the arrival of the Spanish conquerors, the Pastos. However, the Atriz Valley itself was inhabited by the Quillacingas.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pasto,_Colombia"}}, {"canonical_name": "Lamanon", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "lamanon", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Lamanon", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 7, "variants": ["Lamanon"], "contexts": ["made observations", "work known to Cotte"]}, {"entity_id": "lamanon", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Lamanon", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Lamanon"], "contexts": ["made observations on expedition", "author of important letter"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Lamanon", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Lamanon", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2867, "stable_key": "clu_0970b8f8fa2ec061", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jean-Fran\u00e7ois de Galaup, comte de Lap\u00e9rouse", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1741, "death_year": 1788, "description": "French naval officer and explorer on Pacific expedition", "note": "The text mentions 'Lamanon' in the context of Humboldt's expedition accounts. Lamanon was a naturalist who accompanied Lap\u00e9rouse on his ill-fated voyage. Humboldt frequently referenced Lap\u00e9rouse's expedition.", "wikidata_id": "Q3436681", "wikidata_description": "French Navy officer and explorer (1741\u20131788)"}}, {"canonical_name": "P\u00e9ninsule", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "p\u00e9ninsule", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "P\u00e9ninsule", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 6, "variants": ["P\u00e9ninsule"], "contexts": ["Iberian Peninsula", "destination for tobacco"]}, {"entity_id": "pen\u00ednsula", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Pen\u00ednsula", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 4, "variants": ["Pen\u00ednsula", "pen\u00ednsula"], "contexts": ["where name reached", "Iberian Peninsula"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "P\u00e9ninsule", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Pen\u00ednsula", "similarity": 0.94}], "id": 2868, "stable_key": "clu_907fb6e3df65e146", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Peninsula", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A landmass surrounded by water on three sides, such as the Iberian Peninsula.", "note": "Refers to a geographical feature, often the Iberian Peninsula in the context of these texts.", "wikipedia_extract": "A peninsula is a landform that extends from a mainland, is connected to the mainland on only one side, and is mostly surrounded by water. Peninsulas exist on each continent. The largest peninsula in the world is the Arabian Peninsula. The word peninsula derives from Latin  paeninsula, from  paene 'almost' and  insula 'island'. The word entered English in the 16th century. A peninsula is generally defined as a piece of land surrounded on most sides by water."}}, {"canonical_name": "Charpentier", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "charpentier", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Charpentier", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 5, "variants": ["Charpentier", "Carpentier"], "contexts": ["author citation", "author on Pyrenean geology"]}, {"entity_id": "charpentier", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Charpentier", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Charpentier"], "contexts": ["author of 'Constitution Geologique des Pyrenes'", "author of geological work on Pyrenees"]}, {"entity_id": "m__charpentier", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "M. Charpentier", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["M. Charpentier"], "contexts": ["geologist"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Charpentier", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Charpentier", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Charpentier", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "M. Charpentier", "similarity": 0.871}], "id": 2869, "stable_key": "clu_cf9b925e354b41a5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "J\u00e9r\u00f4me-Charles-Alexandre Charpentier", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1797, "death_year": 1857, "description": "French geologist and author of geological works", "note": "Charpentier is a known geologist and author of works cited by Humboldt, fitting the context."}}, {"canonical_name": "Victoria", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "victoria", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Victoria", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 5, "variants": ["Victoria"], "contexts": ["place name in Venezuela", "city in Aragua valleys"]}, {"entity_id": "victoria_land", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Victoria Land", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 5, "variants": ["Victoria Land"], "contexts": ["area near magnetic south pole", "landmass extending from Cape Crozier"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Victoria", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Victoria Land", "similarity": 0.948}], "id": 2870, "stable_key": "clu_2b86c8eec43fc80b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Victoria", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Venezuela", "note": "Contexts suggest two distinct places named Victoria: one in Venezuela, and 'Victoria Land' near the South Pole.", "wikipedia_extract": "Victoria (Alexandrina Victoria; 24 May 1819 \u2013 22 January 1901) was Queen of the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland from 20 June 1837 until her death in 1901. Her reign of 63 years and 216 days, which was longer than those of any of her predecessors, constituted the Victorian era, a period of industrial, political, scientific, and military change within the United Kingdom marked by a great expansion of the British Empire. In 1876, the British parliament voted to grant her the additional title of Empress of India. Victoria was the daughter of Prince Edward, Duke of Kent and Strathearn (the fourth son of King George III), and Princess Victoria of Saxe-Coburg-Saalfeld. After the deaths of her father and grandfather in 1820, she was raised under close supervision by her mother and her comptroller, John Conroy. She inherited the throne aged 18 after her father's three elder brothers died without surviving legitimate issue.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Queen_Victoria"}}, {"canonical_name": "M. Adolphe Erman", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "m__adolphe_erman", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "M. Adolphe Erman", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["M. Adolphe Erman", "Adolphe Erman", "Adolphe Krman"], "contexts": ["scholar who traveled in Siberia", "scholar who determined magnetic equator"]}, {"entity_id": "adolph_erman", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Adolph Erman", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Adolph Erman"], "contexts": ["person with meteor observations", "investigated meteoric phenomena and their connections"]}, {"entity_id": "adolphus_erman", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Adolphus Erman", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Adolphus Erman"], "contexts": ["observer of declination lines in Northern Asia"]}, {"entity_id": "erman,_adolph", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Erman, Adolph", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Erman, Adolph"], "contexts": ["researcher on magnetic declination"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "M. Adolphe Erman", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Adolph Erman", "similarity": 0.931}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "M. Adolphe Erman", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Adolphus Erman", "similarity": 0.921}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "M. Adolphe Erman", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Erman, Adolph", "similarity": 0.87}], "id": 2871, "stable_key": "clu_6e557ee7fe7b69c1-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Adolf Erman", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1806, "death_year": 1877, "description": "German geographer and physicist, known for geomagnetic studies.", "note": "The text mentions 'M. Adolphe Erman' and 'Adolph Erman', likely referring to the same person.", "wikidata_id": "Q61421", "wikidata_description": "German Egyptologist and lexicographer (1854-1937)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adolf%20Erman", "wikipedia_extract": "Johann Peter Adolf Erman (German: [\u025b\u0250\u032f\u02c8m\u00e3\u02d0]; 31 October 1854 \u2013 26 June 1937) was a German Egyptologist and lexicographer. Born in Berlin, he was the son of the physicist Georg Adolf Erman and grandson of the physicist Paul Erman and the astronomer Friedrich Wilhelm Bessel. Educated at Leipzig and Berlin, he became associate professor of Egyptology at the University of Berlin in 1883 and full professor in 1892. In 1885 he was appointed director of the Egyptian department at the royal museum. In 1934 he was excluded from the faculty of the university because he was, according to the Nazi ideology, one quarter Jewish. As his family had converted to Protestantism in 1902, he and his family were not persecuted by the Nazi Party, but they all lost their positions."}}, {"canonical_name": "Pichincha", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "pichincha", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Pichincha", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Pichincha"], "contexts": ["mountain range", "province in Ecuador"]}, {"entity_id": "rucu-pichincha", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Rucu-Pichincha", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rucu-Pichincha"], "contexts": ["Volcano mentioned in the text"]}, {"entity_id": "pichincha", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Pichincha", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Pichincha"], "contexts": ["volcano near Quito", "mountain mentioned for its elongated form"]}, {"entity_id": "rucu_pichincha", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Rucu Pichincha", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Rucu Pichincha"], "contexts": ["active volcano near Quito", "volcano location"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Pichincha", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Pichincha", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Pichincha", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Rucu Pichincha", "similarity": 0.881}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Rucu-Pichincha", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Rucu Pichincha", "similarity": 0.875}], "id": 2872, "stable_key": "clu_21da2121fe86522f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pichincha", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Ecuador", "note": "Refers to the Pichincha volcano and surrounding region.", "wikidata_id": "Q742216", "wikidata_description": "volcano in Quito Metro, Ecuador", "wikipedia_extract": "Quito (Spanish pronunciation: [\u02c8kito] ; Quechua: Kitu), officially San Francisco de Quito, is the capital and second-largest city of Ecuador, with an estimated population of 2.8 million in its metropolitan area. It is also the capital of the province of Pichincha. Quito is in a valley on the eastern slopes of Pichincha, an active stratovolcano in the Andes. Quito's elevation of 2,850 m (9,350 ft) makes it either the highest or the second highest national capital city in the world. This varied standing is because Bolivia is a country with multiple capitals; if La Paz is considered the Bolivian national capital, it tops the list of highest capitals, but if Sucre is specified as the capital, then it is the second highest, behind Quito. Quito is the political and cultural center of Ecuador as the country's major governmental, administrative, and cultural institutions are within the city.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quito"}}, {"canonical_name": "Astr. Nachr.", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "astr_nachr_", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Astr.Nachr.", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Astr.Nachr."], "contexts": ["publication title"]}, {"entity_id": "astr__nachr_", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Astr. Nachr.", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 8, "variants": ["Astr. Nachr."], "contexts": ["astronomical publication", "astronomical journal"]}, {"entity_id": "astr__nachr", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Astr. Nachr", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Astr. Nachr"], "contexts": ["astronomical publication"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Astr.Nachr.", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Astr. Nachr.", "similarity": 0.826}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Astr.Nachr.", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Astr. Nachr", "similarity": 0.828}], "id": 2873, "stable_key": "clu_339c3b3e0b19f17b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Astronomische Nachrichten", "confidence": "high", "type": "text", "note": "'Astr. Nachr.' is a common abbreviation for this prominent astronomical journal."}}, {"canonical_name": "lib.", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "lib__lo", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "lib. lo", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["lib. lo"], "contexts": ["book reference"]}, {"entity_id": "lib_", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "lib.", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 9, "variants": ["lib.", "LIBERI", "LIBRI", "lib.de Pe\u017fte", "libr."], "contexts": ["book", "book chapter or section indicator"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "lib. lo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "lib.", "similarity": 0.89}], "id": 2874, "stable_key": "clu_54b9d61bd9f0fd5b-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Book", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Book", "note": "'lib.' is a common abbreviation for 'liber', the Latin word for book. 'lib. lo' likely refers to a specific book or section.", "wikipedia_extract": "A book is a written work of substantial length created by one or more authors. They can be distributed in various forms such as printed books, audiobooks, and electronic books (ebooks). Books are broadly classified into fiction, containing invented narrative or other imaginary content; and non-fiction, containing content intended as factual truth. The term may also refer to the physical or electronic object containing such a work. Modern books are typically printed in a codex format, composed of many pages bound together and protected by a cover. Before the wide adoption of the modern printing press, codices were used to create handwritten manuscripts."}}, {"canonical_name": "similarity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "similarity", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "similarity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 9, "variants": ["similarity", "Similarity"], "contexts": ["quality of being alike", "principle of association by likeness"]}, {"entity_id": "similes", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "similes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["similes"], "contexts": ["figurative expressions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "similarity", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "similes", "similarity": 0.889}], "id": 2875, "stable_key": "clu_c605b16acc288130-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Similarity", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q254465", "wikidata_description": "idea in geometry"}}, {"canonical_name": "muscular contraction", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "muscular_contraction", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "muscular contraction", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 5, "variants": ["muscular contraction"], "contexts": ["process of muscle shortening", "shortening of muscle fibers"]}, {"entity_id": "muscular_relaxation", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "muscular relaxation", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["muscular relaxation"], "contexts": ["physical sensation in muscles"]}, {"entity_id": "muscular_contraction", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "muscular contraction", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["muscular contraction"], "contexts": ["shortening of muscle fibers"]}, {"entity_id": "vascular_contraction", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "vascular contraction", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["vascular contraction"], "contexts": ["narrowing of blood vessels"]}, {"entity_id": "muscular_relaxation", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "muscular relaxation", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["muscular relaxation"], "contexts": ["loosening of muscle fibers"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "muscular contraction", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "muscular contraction", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "muscular contraction", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "vascular contraction", "similarity": 0.888}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "muscular relaxation", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "muscular contraction", "similarity": 0.91}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "muscular relaxation", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "muscular relaxation", "similarity": 0.816}], "id": 2876, "stable_key": "clu_a4b376d5de83c73a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Muscular contraction", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Includes 'muscular relaxation' as it's the inverse of contraction and related to the same physiological process.", "wikidata_id": "Q127006", "wikidata_description": "process in which force is generated within muscle tissue, resulting in a change in muscle geometry. Force generation involves a chemo-mechanical energy conversion step that is carried out by the actin/myosin complex activity"}}, {"canonical_name": "mental", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "mental", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "mental", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["mental"], "contexts": ["relating to the mind"]}, {"entity_id": "mental", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "mental", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["mental"], "contexts": ["forces which we distinguish as mental", "relating to the mind and intellect"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "mental", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mental", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2877, "stable_key": "clu_e5cacaff82f1d570", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mental", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "preparado", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "preparation", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "preparation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["preparation"], "contexts": ["readiness for action", "act of making ready"]}, {"entity_id": "preparado", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "preparado", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 8, "variants": ["preparado", "preparar", "prepararlhes", "prepara\u00e7\u00e3o", "prepar\u00e1o"], "contexts": ["prepared form of antimony", "prepared substance"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "preparation", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "preparado", "similarity": 0.904}], "id": 2878, "stable_key": "clu_e6aa006ef623ae53", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "preparation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The context points to both a general state of readiness and a specific medicinal preparation (antimony)."}}, {"canonical_name": "organic life", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "organic_life", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "organic life", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["organic life"], "contexts": ["bodily functions and existence"]}, {"entity_id": "organic_life", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "organic life", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 9, "variants": ["Organic Life", "organic life"], "contexts": ["life characterized by biological processes", "life forms including plants and animals"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "organic life", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "organic life", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2879, "stable_key": "clu_7a64d644ea1eddfc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "organic life", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q30403306", "wikidata_description": "scientific article"}}, {"canonical_name": "sedimentary rocks", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "sedimentary_rocks", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "sedimentary rocks", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 9, "variants": ["sedimentary rocks"], "contexts": ["rocks formed from sediment accumulation", "formed by continued generation"]}, {"entity_id": "sedimentary", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "sedimentary", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sedimentary"], "contexts": ["rock formed from sediment"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "sedimentary rocks", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "sedimentary", "similarity": 0.907}], "id": 2880, "stable_key": "clu_48d699bafea260f1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "sedimentary rocks", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q71059905", "wikidata_description": "Final Research Report, Grant 173"}}, {"canonical_name": "Brandes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "brandes", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Brandes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 8, "variants": ["Brandes"], "contexts": ["made observations on shooting star tails", "author of 'Unterhaltungen'"]}, {"entity_id": "brand", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Brand", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Brand"], "contexts": ["observed heights and velocities of falling stars", "observed meteors"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Brandes", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Brand", "similarity": 0.858}], "id": 2881, "stable_key": "clu_1fa06a02912ffc6d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Johann Christian Brandes", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1744, "death_year": 1804, "description": "German chemist and physician, observed meteors."}}, {"canonical_name": "Biela", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "biela", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Biela", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 6, "variants": ["Biela"], "contexts": ["comet with short period of revolution", "comet discoverer"]}, {"entity_id": "biela\u2019s", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Biela\u2019s", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Biela\u2019s"], "contexts": ["comet named after an individual", "a specific comet"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Biela", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Biela\u2019s", "similarity": 0.895}], "id": 2882, "stable_key": "clu_8c7c126100861a2a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Biela's Comet", "confidence": "medium", "type": "concept", "note": "The cluster contains references to 'Biela' and 'Biela\u2019s', which clearly refer to Biela's Comet. However, it also includes 'Beagle', the ship Darwin sailed on. The strong presence of 'Biela' and the context of celestial objects in Humboldt's 'Kosmos' point to the comet as the primary referent, with 'Beagle' being a misattribution or secondary mention within the texts analyzed.", "wikipedia_extract": "Biela's Comet or Comet Biela (official designation: 3D/Biela) was a periodic Jupiter-family comet first recorded in 1772 by Montaigne and Messier and finally identified as periodic in 1826 by Wilhelm von Biela. It was subsequently observed to split in two and has not been seen since 1852. As a result, it is currently considered to have been destroyed, although remnants have survived for some time as a meteor shower, the Andromedids. The comet was first recorded on 8 March 1772 by Jacques Leibax Montaigne; during the same apparition it was independently discovered by Charles Messier. It was also recorded in 1805 by Jean-Louis Pons, but was not recognized as the same object. Around 9 December 1805 comet Biela passed about 0.0366 AU (5.48 million km) from Earth."}}, {"canonical_name": "Chladni", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "chladni", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Chladni", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 6, "variants": ["Chladni"], "contexts": ["stated Italian physicist's hypothesis on meteoric stones", "scholar who attempted to explain shooting stars"]}, {"entity_id": "chladni", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Chladni", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Chladni"], "contexts": ["discovered symmetrical forms of nodal lines", "scientist known for sand figures"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Chladni", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Chladni", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2883, "stable_key": "clu_1dfbe608717b30d6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ernst Chladni", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1756, "death_year": 1827, "description": "German physicist, studied acoustics and meteoric stones.", "wikidata_id": "Q213579", "wikidata_description": "German physicist, mathematician and musician (1756 \u2013 1827)"}}, {"canonical_name": "Fourier", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "fourier", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Fourier", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 6, "variants": ["Fourier"], "contexts": ["scientist who studied heat", "scientist who conjectured solidification process"]}, {"entity_id": "fourier", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Fourier", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Fourier"], "contexts": ["theorist of space temperature", "computed central heat decrease"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Fourier", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Fourier", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2884, "stable_key": "clu_f907c390fce80749", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Joseph Fourier", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1768, "death_year": 1830, "description": "French mathematician and physicist known for work on heat and thermodynamics."}}, {"canonical_name": "meteors", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "meteors", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "meteors", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 5, "variants": ["Meteors", "meteors"], "contexts": ["celestial objects observed", "another name for aerolites"]}, {"entity_id": "meteor", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "meteor", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["meteor"], "contexts": ["celestial phenomenon"]}, {"entity_id": "meteors", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "meteors", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 3, "variants": ["meteors", "Meteors"], "contexts": ["celestial objects igniting in atmosphere", "celestial phenomena"]}, {"entity_id": "meteor", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Meteor", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Meteor"], "contexts": ["celestial phenomenon"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "meteors", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "meteors", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "meteors", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Meteor", "similarity": 0.938}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "meteor", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "meteors", "similarity": 0.955}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "meteor", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Meteor", "similarity": 0.979}], "id": 2885, "stable_key": "clu_50fe099109086b69", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "meteor", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q14502780", "wikidata_description": "genus of insects"}}, {"canonical_name": "Amp\u00e8re", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "ampere", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Ampere", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Ampere"], "contexts": ["intellectual who computed electro-dynamic forces", "proposed idea about Earth's temperature"]}, {"entity_id": "amp\u00e8re", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Amp\u00e8re", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Amp\u00e8re"], "contexts": ["author of 'Essai sur la Phil. des Sciences'"]}, {"entity_id": "amp\u00e8re", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Amp\u00e8re", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 6, "variants": ["Amp\u00e8re"], "contexts": ["discovered mutual action of electrical currents", "established theory of electro-magnetism"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Ampere", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Amp\u00e8re", "similarity": 0.754}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Amp\u00e8re", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Amp\u00e8re", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2886, "stable_key": "clu_9ad0792b577cba75", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Andr\u00e9-Marie Amp\u00e8re", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1775, "death_year": 1836, "description": "French physicist, father of electromagnetism.", "wikidata_id": "Q675", "wikidata_description": "French physicist and mathematician (1775\u20131836)"}}, {"canonical_name": "Maldiva", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "maldives", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Maldives", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Maldives"], "contexts": ["island nation", "coral islands"]}, {"entity_id": "maldiva", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Maldiva", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 8, "variants": ["Maldiva", "Maldha"], "contexts": ["island location", "islands mentioned in relation to amber"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Maldives", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Maldiva", "similarity": 0.984}], "id": 2887, "stable_key": "clu_b05628e3fe962292", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Maldives", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Maldives", "wikidata_id": "Q826", "wikidata_description": "sovereign state in South Asia, situated on an archipelago in the Arabian Sea"}}, {"canonical_name": "periodic time", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "periodic_times", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "periodic times", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["periodic times"], "contexts": ["time for celestial bodies to orbit"]}, {"entity_id": "periodic_time", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "periodic time", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 7, "variants": ["Periodic time", "periodic time"], "contexts": ["time for one complete orbit", "time for one complete revolution"]}, {"entity_id": "periodic_times", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "periodic times", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["periodic times", "Periodic Times"], "contexts": ["time for celestial orbits", "time for one revolution"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "periodic times", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "periodic time", "similarity": 0.887}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "periodic times", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "periodic times", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2888, "stable_key": "clu_826e127f16e5fbce-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Orbital Period", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q112118425", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article", "note": "In the context of celestial bodies, 'revolutions' refers to the time it takes for one body to complete an orbit around another. This is directly related to the concept of orbital period."}}, {"canonical_name": "north pole", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "north_pole", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "North Pole", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["North Pole"], "contexts": ["celestial pole"]}, {"entity_id": "north_pole", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "north pole", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 9, "variants": ["north pole"], "contexts": ["northernmost point of Earth's axis", "geographic location of exploration"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "North Pole", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "north pole", "similarity": 0.972}], "id": 2889, "stable_key": "clu_e79ebef6559a4722", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "North Pole", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q79494", "wikidata_description": "city in Alaska, USA"}}, {"canonical_name": "corrimientos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "corrimientos", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "corrimientos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 6, "variants": ["corrimientos", "corrimie"], "contexts": ["flow that resin prohibits", "affliction treated with gargaparrilla water"]}, {"entity_id": "corrimentos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "corrimentos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 4, "variants": ["corrimentos"], "contexts": ["discharges", "ailment treated by syrup"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "corrimientos", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "corrimentos", "similarity": 0.941}], "id": 2890, "stable_key": "clu_253df3f9e96e437a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Discharges", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "Abnormal bodily discharges, often indicating an ailment.", "note": "Refers to abnormal bodily flows or secretions, often treated as a medical condition.", "wikipedia_extract": "A vaginal disease is a pathological condition that affects part or all of the vagina. Sexually transmitted infections that affect the vagina include: Herpes genitalis. The herpes simplex virus (HSV) can infect the vulva, vagina, and cervix, and this may result in small, painful, recurring blisters and ulcers. It is also common for there to be an absence of any noticeable symptoms. Human papillomavirus (HPV), which may cause genital warts. Because of STIs, health authorities and other health outlets recommend safe sex practices when engaging in sexual activity."}}, {"canonical_name": "Bal\u017famo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "bal\u017famo", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Bal\u017famo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 6, "variants": ["Bal\u017famo", "Bal\u00edamo"], "contexts": ["precious liquid with marvelous effects", "imitation of true Balsamo"]}, {"entity_id": "bal\u017famo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Bal\u017famo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 4, "variants": ["Bal\u017famo", "bal\u017famo"], "contexts": ["precious medicinal part of the liquor", "balsam"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Bal\u017famo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Bal\u017famo", "similarity": 0.705}], "id": 2891, "stable_key": "clu_39f8b096b7372fad", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Balsam", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "A fragrant resinous substance obtained from certain trees, used medicinally.", "wikipedia_extract": "Balsam is the resinous exudate (or sap) which forms on certain kinds of trees and shrubs. Balsam (from Latin balsamum \"gum of the balsam tree,\" ultimately from a Semitic source such as Hebrew: \u05d1\u05b9\u05bc\u05e9\u05b6\u05c2\u05dd, romanized: bo\u015bem, lit.\u2009'spice, perfume') owes its name to the biblical Balm of Gilead. Balsams often contain benzoic or cinnamic acid or their esters. The Balsam of Matariyya was a substance famous as a panacea among physicians in the Middle East and Europe during the Antique and Medieval periods. The substance has long been used as a medicine, with early references to the substance recorded as far back as 285 BC. The Balsam of Matariyya was said to be derived from an Egyptian plant and is sometimes also referred to as the balm of Gilead or the balm of Mecca.", "wikidata_id": "Q428750", "wikidata_description": "species of plant in the family Cucurbitaceae"}}, {"canonical_name": "ro\u017fto", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "ro\u017ftro", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "ro\u017ftro", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["ro\u017ftro"], "contexts": ["face"]}, {"entity_id": "ro\u017fto", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "ro\u017fto", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 9, "variants": ["ro\u017fto"], "contexts": ["Part of the body affected by paralysis", "body part with color"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "ro\u017ftro", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "ro\u017fto", "similarity": 0.937}], "id": 2892, "stable_key": "clu_b2515a3339a96dc9-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Face", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "The front part of a person's head, from the forehead to the chin.", "wikipedia_extract": "The face is the front of the head in humans and many other animals that features most of the sense organs including the eyes, nose and mouth. Many animals may express emotions through their face. Sense organs in the faces of different animals are varied such as the snout, and the proboscis. Many animals are flat-faced (brachycephalic) such as the pug dog. The human face is crucial for identity, and damage such as scarring or developmental deformities may adversely affect the psyche. The front of the human head is called the face.", "wikidata_id": "Q355", "wikidata_description": "American online social media and social networking service"}}, {"canonical_name": "pez", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "pez", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "pez", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["pez"], "contexts": ["pitch"]}, {"entity_id": "pez", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "pez", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 9, "variants": ["pez"], "contexts": ["substance used for treatment", "pitch"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "pez", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "pez", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2893, "stable_key": "clu_1f2593cf666dfa86", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "pitch", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "A thick, sticky, dark substance obtained from resin.", "note": "'pezze' context might refer to pieces of cloth or material.", "wikipedia_extract": "Pez (English: , German: [pe\u02d0ts]; stylised as PEZ, officially Pfefferminz, German for peppermint) is the brand name of an Austrian candy and associated manual candy dispensers. The candy is a pressed, dry, straight-edged, curved-corner block 15 mm (5\u20448 inch) long, 8 mm (5\u204416 inch) wide and 5 mm (3\u204416 inch) high, with each Pez dispenser holding 12 candy pieces. Pez was invented in Austria and later exported worldwide. The all-uppercase spelling of the logo echoes the trademark's style on the packaging and the dispensers, with the logo drawn in perspective and giving the appearance that the letters are built out of 44 brick-like Pez mints (14 bricks in the P and 15 in each of the E and Z). Despite the widespread recognition and popularity of the Pez dispenser, the company considers itself to be primarily a candy company, stating that \"[t]oday, billions of PEZ candies are consumed annually in the U.S.A. alone\". Pez dispensers are a part of popular culture in many nations, an example being \"Soul Candy\" in the Japanese manga series Bleach.", "wikidata_id": "Q840692", "wikidata_description": "mixture of aromatic organic compounds, black, tar-like, super viscous liquid"}}, {"canonical_name": "oleos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "olios", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Olios", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Olios"], "contexts": ["preparations made with Ambar"]}, {"entity_id": "oleos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "oleos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 9, "variants": ["oleos", "Oleos varios", "oleos adftringentes"], "contexts": ["added to a mixture to thicken", "oils for this effect"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Olios", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "oleos", "similarity": 0.963}], "id": 2894, "stable_key": "clu_052280223146acb1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Oils", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "note": "The term 'oleos' in early modern Portuguese and Spanish medical texts generally refers to various types of oils, both plant-derived and animal-derived, used in preparations or as ingredients."}}, {"canonical_name": "especiarias", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "especiarias", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "especiarias", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 9, "variants": ["especiarias", "especia", "especiaria"], "contexts": ["aromatic plant products", "these spices"]}, {"entity_id": "especiarias", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "especiarias", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["especiarias"], "contexts": ["spices, plant-based flavorings"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "especiarias", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "especiarias", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2895, "stable_key": "clu_1333b297871c039d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Spices", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "description": "Aromatic plant products used for flavor and medicine.", "wikipedia_extract": "In the culinary arts, a spice is a seed, fruit, root, bark, or other plant substance primarily used for flavoring or coloring food. Spices are distinguished from herbs, which are the leaves, flowers, or stems of plants used for flavoring or as a garnish. Spices and herbs are both seasonings. Spices are sometimes used in medicine, religious rituals, cosmetics, or perfume production. They are usually classified into spices, spice seeds, and herbal categories. For example, vanilla is commonly used as an ingredient in fragrance manufacturing."}}, {"canonical_name": "Costa", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "costa", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Costa", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 9, "variants": ["costa", "Costa"], "contexts": ["geographic feature", "cited for practical materia medica"]}, {"entity_id": "costa", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Costa", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Costa"], "contexts": ["signer of official documents"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Costa", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Costa", "similarity": 0.665}], "id": 2896, "stable_key": "clu_84abd0b820134510", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Costa", "confidence": "low", "type": "person", "description": "A person named Costa, likely a scholar or official.", "note": "The term 'Costa' is extremely common as both a surname and a geographical feature (coast). Without further context from the texts, pinpointing a specific historical figure is impossible. The 'signer of official documents' and 'scholar' hints at a person, but it's too general.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}}, {"canonical_name": "Algarve", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "algarve", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Algarve", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 7, "variants": ["Algarve", "Algarby", "Algarves"], "contexts": ["region in the west", "region whose name derives from garb"]}, {"entity_id": "algarve", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Algarve", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 3, "variants": ["Algarve"], "contexts": ["region in southern Portugal", "region in Portugal"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Algarve", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Algarve", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2897, "stable_key": "clu_9c2e13d61dd53fea", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Algarve", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Southernmost region of Portugal, historically significant.", "wikipedia_extract": "The Algarve (UK: , US: , European Portuguese: [al\u02c8\u0263a\u027ev\u0268] ) is the southernmost NUTS II region of continental Portugal. It has an area of 4,997 km2 (1,929 sq mi) with 467,495 permanent inhabitants and incorporates 16 municipalities (concelhos or munic\u00edpios in Portuguese). The region has its administrative centre in the city of Faro, where both the region's international airport and public university, the University of Algarve, are located. The region is the same as the area included in the Faro District and is subdivided into two zones, one to the West (Barlavento) and another to the East (Sotavento). Tourism and related activities are extensive and make up the bulk of the Algarve's summer economy. Production of food which includes fish and other seafood, as well as different types of fruit and vegetables such as oranges, figs, plums, carob pods, almonds, avocados, tomatoes, cauliflowers, strawberries, and raspberries, are also economically important in the region."}}, {"canonical_name": "carabe", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "carabe", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "carabe", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 6, "variants": ["carabe"], "contexts": ["substance with similar virtues", "substance with glutinative and styptic properties"]}, {"entity_id": "carabe", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Carabe", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["Carabe", "carabe"], "contexts": ["Arabic name for amber", "substance used in preparations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "carabe", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Carabe", "similarity": 0.881}], "id": 2898, "stable_key": "clu_d1a4fb925e6e7925", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ambergris", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "note": "The Arabic name for ambergris is 'anbar', and the Portuguese 'carabe' is a likely transliteration or corruption of this, given the context of medicinal virtues and its use in substances.", "wikidata_id": "Q188774", "wikidata_description": "substance produced in the digestive system of sperm whales", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ambergris"}}, {"canonical_name": "enfermidades venenosas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "enfermidades_venenosas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "enfermidades venenosas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 4, "variants": ["enfermidades venenosas"], "contexts": ["poisonous illnesses", "state of being poisoned"]}, {"entity_id": "doen\u00e7as_veneno\u017fas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "doen\u00e7as veneno\u017fas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 3, "variants": ["doen\u00e7as veneno\u017fas"], "contexts": ["poisonous diseases", "Poisonous diseases where wine can be given"]}, {"entity_id": "doen\u00e7as_venenosas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "doen\u00e7as venenosas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["doen\u00e7as venenosas"], "contexts": ["poisonous diseases", "condition treated with Bezoartico"]}, {"entity_id": "doen\u00e7asvenenofas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "doen\u00e7asvenenofas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["doen\u00e7asvenenofas"], "contexts": ["poisonous diseases"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "enfermidades venenosas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "doen\u00e7as veneno\u017fas", "similarity": 0.973}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "enfermidades venenosas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "doen\u00e7as venenosas", "similarity": 0.992}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "enfermidades venenosas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "doen\u00e7asvenenofas", "similarity": 0.933}], "id": 2899, "stable_key": "clu_9349b37bd8b2514d-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Poisoning", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Poisoning", "note": "The term 'veneno' can refer to both the poison itself and the condition of being poisoned. 'Poisoning' encompasses both meanings.", "wikidata_id": "Q387175", "wikidata_description": "human disease"}}, {"canonical_name": "Toscana", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "toscana", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Toscana", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Toscana"], "contexts": ["region", "region where borax was extracted"]}, {"entity_id": "toscana", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Toscana", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 7, "variants": ["TOSCANA", "Toscana"], "contexts": ["region in Italy", "region name"]}, {"entity_id": "to(scana", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "To(scana", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["To(scana"], "contexts": ["region where ammi is found"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Toscana", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Toscana", "similarity": 0.931}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Toscana", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "To(scana", "similarity": 0.62}], "id": 2900, "stable_key": "clu_6a45e6459310abab", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tuscany", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Italy", "wikidata_id": "Q1273", "wikidata_description": "region in central Italy"}}, {"canonical_name": "rab\u00e3os", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "rab\u00e3os", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "rab\u00e3os", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["rab\u00e3os"], "contexts": ["vegetable, likely radishes", "vegetable sold"]}, {"entity_id": "rab\u00e3os", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "rab\u00e3os", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 8, "variants": ["rab\u00e3os", "rab\u00e3o"], "contexts": ["radishes", "flatulent food"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "rab\u00e3os", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "rab\u00e3os", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2901, "stable_key": "clu_186e2814b40188fe", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Radish", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Raphanus sativus", "family": "Brassicaceae", "description": "Edible root vegetable with a peppery flavor, commonly consumed raw.", "note": "The term 'rab\u00f4' in one context appears to refer to a specific type of pepper, but the overwhelming evidence points to 'radish' for the other instances.", "wikipedia_extract": "The radish (Raphanus sativus or Raphanus raphanistrum subsp. sativus) is a flowering plant in the mustard family, Brassicaceae. Its large taproot is commonly used as a root vegetable, although the entire plant is edible and its leaves are sometimes used as a leaf vegetable. Originally domesticated in Asia, radishes are now grown and consumed globally. The radish is sometimes considered to form a species complex with the wild radish and instead treated as the subspecies Raphanus raphanistrum subsp. sativus. Radishes are often used raw as a crunchy salad vegetable with a pungent, slightly spicy flavor, varying in intensity depending on its growing environment. There are numerous varieties varying in size, flavor, color, and length of time they take to mature.", "wikidata_id": "Q7224565", "wikidata_description": "species of plant"}}, {"canonical_name": "abobora", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "ab\u00f3bora", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "ab\u00f3bora", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["ab\u00f3bora"], "contexts": ["one of the four great cold seeds"]}, {"entity_id": "abobora", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "abobora", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 9, "variants": ["abobora", "abobaras", "aboboras"], "contexts": ["pumpkin", "pumpkin pulp used in a poultice"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "ab\u00f3bora", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "abobora", "similarity": 0.871}], "id": 2902, "stable_key": "clu_313a4a6b93609095", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pumpkin", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "description": "A large, round vegetable, often orange, used in cooking and medicine.", "wikipedia_extract": "A pumpkin is a cultivated winter squash in the genus Cucurbita. The term is most commonly applied to round, orange-colored squash varieties, but does not possess a scientific definition. It may be used in reference to many different squashes of varied appearance and belonging to multiple species in the Cucurbita genus. \"Pumpkin\" is sometimes used interchangeably with \"squash\" or \"winter squash\", and is commonly used for some cultivars of Cucurbita argyrosperma, Cucurbita ficifolia, Cucurbita maxima, Cucurbita moschata, and Cucurbita pepo. C. pepo pumpkins are among the oldest known domesticated plants, with evidence of their cultivation dating to between 7000 BCE and 5500 BCE in Mesoamerica. Wild species of Cucurbita and the earliest domesticated species are native to North America (parts of present-day northeastern Mexico and the southern United States), but cultivars are now grown globally for culinary, decorative, and other culturally-specific purposes. The pumpkin's thick shell encases edible seeds and pulp."}}, {"canonical_name": "c\u00e3o", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "c\u00e3o", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "c\u00e3o", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["c\u00e3o"], "contexts": ["animal used in poisoning test"]}, {"entity_id": "c\u00e3o", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "c\u00e3o", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 9, "variants": ["c\u00e3o"], "contexts": ["recipient of transplanted illness", "dog to whom urine was given"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "c\u00e3o", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "c\u00e3o", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2903, "stable_key": "clu_865d3f472d4055ea", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Dog", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal"}}, {"canonical_name": "Joule", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "joule", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Joule", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 9, "variants": ["Joule"], "contexts": ["ascertained heat from coal combustion", "provided mechanical equivalent of heat"]}, {"entity_id": "joule", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Joule", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Joule"], "contexts": ["Scientist who calculated thermal equivalent"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Joule", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Joule", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2904, "stable_key": "clu_bf50f0617c025f0a-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "James Prescott Joule", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1818, "death_year": 1889, "description": "English physicist, known for work on electricity and thermodynamics.", "wikidata_id": "Q8962", "wikidata_description": "English physicist and brewer (1818\u20131889)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James%20Prescott%20Joule", "wikipedia_extract": "James Prescott Joule (; 24 December 1818 \u2013  11 October 1889) was an English physicist. Joule studied the nature of heat and discovered its relationship to mechanical work. This led to the law of conservation of energy, which in turn led to the development of the first law of thermodynamics. The SI unit of energy, the joule (J), is named after him. He worked with Lord Kelvin to develop an absolute thermodynamic temperature scale, which came to be called the Kelvin scale. Joule also made observations of magnetostriction, and he found the relationship between the current through a resistor and the heat dissipated, which is also called Joule's first law."}}, {"canonical_name": "galvanic battery", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "galvanic_battery", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "galvanic battery", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 9, "variants": ["galvanic battery"], "contexts": ["device producing galvanic current", "source of electrical current"]}, {"entity_id": "galvanic_battery", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "galvanic battery", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["galvanic battery"], "contexts": ["produces electricity from chemical composition"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "galvanic battery", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "galvanic battery", "similarity": 0.838}], "id": 2905, "stable_key": "clu_74a95b0221913f80-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Galvanic battery", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "Q12714992", "wikidata_description": ""}}, {"canonical_name": "conservation of force", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "conservation_of_force", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "conservation of force", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 8, "variants": ["Conservation of Force", "conservation of force"], "contexts": ["principle of energy conservation", "principle of energy preservation"]}, {"entity_id": "conservation_of_force", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Conservation of Force", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Conservation of Force"], "contexts": ["a scientific generalization", "current expression for a scientific principle"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "conservation of force", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Conservation of Force", "similarity": 0.971}], "id": 2906, "stable_key": "clu_b245588b6b6261f9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Conservation of energy", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'Persistence of Force' is an early formulation of the concept later widely known as the conservation of energy.", "wikipedia_extract": "In physical science, mechanical energy is the sum of macroscopic potential and kinetic energies. The principle of conservation of mechanical energy states that if an isolated system or a closed system is subject only to conservative forces, then the mechanical energy is constant. If an object moves in the opposite direction of a conservative net force, the potential energy will increase; and if the speed (not the velocity) of the object changes, the kinetic energy of the object also changes. In all real systems, however, nonconservative forces, such as frictional forces, will be present, but if they are of negligible magnitude, the mechanical energy changes little and its conservation is a useful approximation. In elastic collisions, the kinetic energy is conserved, but in inelastic collisions some mechanical energy may be converted into thermal energy. The equivalence between lost mechanical energy  and an increase in temperature was discovered by James Prescott Joule.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mechanical_energy"}}, {"canonical_name": "oxidation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "oxidation", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Oxidation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["oxidation", "Oxidation"], "contexts": ["chemical process of reacting with oxygen", "chemical reaction with oxygen"]}, {"entity_id": "oxidation", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "oxidation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["oxidation"], "contexts": ["process of animal life", "process where absorbed nutriment gives out force"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Oxidation", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "oxidation", "similarity": 0.911}], "id": 2907, "stable_key": "clu_42d0bf48bc2ae1f9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Oxidation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q484152", "wikidata_description": "number that describes degree of oxidation of an atom in a chemical compound; the hypothetical charge that an atom would have if all bonds to atoms of different elements were fully ionic", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oxidation_state", "wikipedia_extract": "In chemistry, the oxidation state, or oxidation number, is the hypothetical charge of an atom if all of its bonds to other atoms are fully ionic. It describes the degree of oxidation (loss of electrons) of an atom in a chemical compound. Conceptually, the oxidation state may be positive, negative or zero. Beside nearly-pure ionic bonding, many covalent bonds exhibit a strong ionicity, making oxidation state a useful predictor of charge. The oxidation state of an atom does not represent the \"real\" charge on that atom, or any other actual atomic property. This is particularly true of high oxidation states, where the ionization energy required to produce a multiply positive ion is far greater than the energies available in chemical reactions."}}, {"canonical_name": "variable star", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 10, "members": [{"entity_id": "variable_star", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "variable star", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 4, "variants": ["variable star"], "contexts": ["star whose brightness changes", "Eta Aquil\u00e6"]}, {"entity_id": "variable_stars", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Variable stars", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Variable stars", "variable stars"], "contexts": ["stars with changing brightness", "position in Milky Way"]}, {"entity_id": "variable_stars", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "variable stars", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["variable stars"], "contexts": ["stars with changing brightness", "celestial bodies that brighten and fade"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "variable star", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "variable stars", "similarity": 0.939}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "Variable stars", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "variable stars", "similarity": 0.976}], "id": 2908, "stable_key": "clu_8eff494e3048efda", "ground_truth": {"m