distance measurement"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "milles", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "miles", "similarity": 0.918}], "id": 2970, "stable_key": "clu_913d38624b1e15e6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "mile", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "The context of 'miles', 'milles', and 'millimeter' suggests units of distance measurement. 'Miles' is the most common and broadest term, encompassing the others.", "wikidata_id": "Q253276", "wikidata_description": "unit of length (on ground)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mile", "wikipedia_extract": "The mile, sometimes the international mile or statute mile to distinguish it from other miles, is a British imperial unit and United States customary unit of length; both are based on the older English unit of length equal to 5,280 English feet, or 1,760 yards. The statute mile was standardised as a unit of length between the Commonwealth of Nations and the United States by an international agreement in 1959, when the yard was formally redefined with respect to SI units as 0.9144 metres, making the mile exactly 1609.344 metres (1.609344 kilometres). For everyday use, five miles equates roughly to eight kilometres. Mileage is the term used informally for a distance expressed in miles. With qualifiers, mile is also used to describe or translate a wide range of units derived from or roughly equivalent to the Roman mile (roughly 1.48 km), such as the nautical mile (now 1.852 km exactly), the Italian mile (roughly 1.852 km), and the Chinese mile (now 500 m exactly). The Romans divided their mile into 5,000 pedes (lit.\u2009'feet'), but the greater importance of furlongs in the Elizabethan-era England meant that the statute mile was made equivalent to 8 furlongs or 5,280 feet in 1593."}}, {"canonical_name": "Von Dechen", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "dechen", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Dechen", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Dechen"], "contexts": ["mentioned with Oeyhausen and la Roche"]}, {"entity_id": "von_dechen", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Von Dechen", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 8, "variants": ["Von Dechen", "von Dechen"], "contexts": ["made measurements of mine elevation", "geognosist providing geological observations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Dechen", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Von Dechen", "similarity": 0.94}], "id": 2971, "stable_key": "clu_eb5115198e94c0e6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Heinrich von Dechen", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1800, "death_year": 1889, "description": "German geologist and mining engineer", "wikidata_id": "Q78083", "wikidata_description": "German geologist (1800-1889)"}}, {"canonical_name": "Fresnel", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "fresnel", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Fresnel", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Fresnel"], "contexts": ["mentioned in relation to solar rays and heat"]}, {"entity_id": "fresnel", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Fresnel", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 8, "variants": ["Fresnel"], "contexts": ["authority on undulatory theory", "scientist who studied light refraction"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Fresnel", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Fresnel", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 2972, "stable_key": "clu_88154df088a68b3f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Augustin-Jean Fresnel", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1788, "death_year": 1827, "description": "French physicist, optics pioneer", "wikidata_id": "Q187241", "wikidata_description": "French engineer and physicist (1788-1827)"}}, {"canonical_name": "Burm\u00e1", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "birmans", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Birmans", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Birmans"], "contexts": ["land where salses were discovered"]}, {"entity_id": "burm\u00e1", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Burm\u00e1", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 8, "variants": ["Burm\u00e1", "Burma"], "contexts": ["country in Southeast Asia", "region discussed in dr. Mason's book"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Birmans", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Burm\u00e1", "similarity": 0.924}], "id": 2973, "stable_key": "clu_2f4cc83076127832", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Burma", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A country in Southeast Asia, now officially Myanmar.", "note": "Burma is the historical name for the country now officially known as Myanmar.", "wikipedia_extract": "Myanmar, officially the Republic of the Union of Myanmar and also referred to as Burma (the official English name until 1989), is a country in northwest Southeast Asia. It is the largest country by area in Mainland Southeast Asia and has a population of about 55 million. It is bordered by India and Bangladesh to the northwest, China to the northeast, Laos and Thailand to the east and southeast, and the Andaman Sea and the Bay of Bengal to the south and southwest. The country's capital city is Naypyidaw, while its largest city is Yangon (formerly Rangoon). Early civilisations in the area included the Tibeto-Burman-speaking Pyu city-states in Upper Myanmar and the Mon kingdoms in Lower Myanmar. In the 9th century, the Bamar people entered the upper Irrawaddy valley, and following the establishment of the Pagan Kingdom in the 1050s, the Burmese language and culture and Theravada Buddhism slowly became dominant in the country."}}, {"canonical_name": "Substance", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "substance", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Substance", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["Substance", "substance"], "contexts": ["pathological changes in the brain's substance", "self-existent being"]}, {"entity_id": "substancia", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "substancia", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["substancia"], "contexts": ["method of administering medicine", "quality of urine"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Substance", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "substancia", "similarity": 0.916}], "id": 2974, "stable_key": "clu_481605db57023d9b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "substance", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q378078", "wikidata_description": "basic ontological concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "reflex action", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 9, "members": [{"entity_id": "reflex_action", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "reflex action", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["reflex action"], "contexts": ["involuntary response to stimulus", "automatic response to stimulus"]}, {"entity_id": "reflex_actions", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "reflex actions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, 