for work on metals"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Aldrovandus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Aldrovandus", "similarity": 0.654}], "id": 3026, "stable_key": "clu_3add4684eaac4636", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ulisse Aldrovandi", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1522, "death_year": 1605, "description": "Italian naturalist, physician, and botanist, founder of Bologna's botanical garden.", "wikidata_id": "Q128671562", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article"}}, {"canonical_name": "Athens", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "athens", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Athens", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 5, "variants": ["Athens"], "contexts": ["city with a 'council of the bean'", "ancient Greek city"]}, {"entity_id": "athenas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Athenas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Athenas"], "contexts": ["city known for intelligent inhabitants", "city where plagues occurred"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Athens", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Athenas", "similarity": 0.99}], "id": 3027, "stable_key": "clu_e698c0565b26a20e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Athens", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Greece", "wikidata_id": "Q203263", "wikidata_description": "consolidated city-county and county seat of Clarke County, Georgia, United States", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Athens%2C%20Georgia", "wikipedia_extract": "Athens is a consolidated city-county in the U.S. state of Georgia. Downtown Athens lies about 70 miles (110 km) northeast of downtown Atlanta. The University of Georgia, the state's flagship public university and an R1 research institution, is in Athens and contributed to its initial growth. In 1991, after a vote the preceding year, the original City of Athens abandoned its charter to form a unified government with Clarke County, referred to jointly as Athens\u2013Clarke County, where it is the county seat. As of 2021, the Athens-Clarke County's official website's population of the consolidated city-county (all of Clarke County except Winterville and a portion of Bogart) was 128,711."}}, {"canonical_name": "horns", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "horns", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "horns", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Horns", "horns"], "contexts": ["animal keratinous growths", "bony protrusions on animal heads"]}, {"entity_id": "horns", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "horns", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 5, "variants": ["horns"], "contexts": ["bony outgrowths on heads of ungulates", "correlated with hair growth"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "horns", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "horns", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3028, "stable_key": "clu_aa155b8502e2eefe", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Horns", "confidence": "high", "type": "object"}}, {"canonical_name": "Philolaus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "philostratus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Philostratus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Philostratus"], "contexts": ["writer on Apollonius", "mentioned Pantarbes"]}, {"entity_id": "philolaus", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Philolaus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 6, "variants": ["Philolaus"], "contexts": ["Pythagorean philosopher", "ancient Greek philosopher"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Philostratus", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Philolaus", "similarity": 0.846}], "id": 3029, "stable_key": "clu_3826d9f7b1fbeb7e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Philolaus", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Ancient Greek Pythagorean philosopher and mathematician.", "note": "Browne's text might have an OCR error or misattribution for 'Philostratus'."}}, {"canonical_name": "Garlick", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "garlick", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Garlick", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Garlick"], "contexts": ["herb affecting loadstone attraction", "used to excite needles"]}, {"entity_id": "garlick", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Garlick", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 6, "variants": ["Garlick"], "contexts": ["herb", "used as remedy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Garlick", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Garlick", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3030, "stable_key": "clu_a3c70b05c715ad37-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Garlic", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Allium sativum Linnaeus", "family": "Amaryllidaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q23400", "wikidata_description": "species of plant (for the food, use Q21546392)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garlic", "wikipedia_extract": "Garlic (Allium sativum) is a species of bulbous flowering plants in the genus Allium. Its close relatives include the onion, shallot, leek, chives, Welsh onion, and Chinese onion. Garlic is native to central and western Asia, stretching from the Black Sea through the southern Caucasus, northeastern Iran, and the Hindu Kush. It has naturalized in many other parts of the world, including Mediterranean Europe and China. There are two subspecies and hundreds of varieties of garlic. Garlic has been used for thousands of years as a seasoning, culinary ingredient, and traditional medical remedy.", "description": "A pungent bulbous plant used as food and medicine."}}, {"canonical_name": "joints", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "joints", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "joints", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["joints"], "contexts": ["articulations in the body", "articulations in limbs"]}, {"entity_id": "joints", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "joints", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 6, "variants": ["joints"], "contexts": ["articulation between bones", "articulations between bones"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "joints", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "joints", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3031, "stable_key": "clu_1cdc076c550e474c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Joints", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q33652928", "wikidata_description": "scientific article", "note": "'Articulations' in this context refers to the anatomical structures of joints, where bones connect, allowing for movement. It can also refer to the manner in which things are joined or connected.", "description": "The points where two bones meet; connections in the body.", "wikipedia_extract": "A joint or articulation (or articular surface) is the connection made between bones, ossicles, or other hard structures in the body which link an animal's skeletal system into a functional whole. They are constructed to allow for different degrees and types of movement. Some joints, such as the knee, elbow, and shoulder, are self-lubricating, almost frictionless, and are able to withstand compression and maintain heavy loads while still executing smooth and precise movements. Other joints such as sutures between the bones of the skull permit very little movement (only during birth) in order to protect the brain and the sense organs. The connection between a tooth and the jawbone is also called a joint, and is described as a fibrous joint known as a gomphosis. Joints are classified both structurally and functionally."}}, {"canonical_name": "genius", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "genius", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Genius", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Genius"], "contexts": ["natural talent or inclination"]}, {"entity_id": "genius", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "genius", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 7, "variants": ["Genius", "genius"], "contexts": ["exceptional intellectual or creative power", "exceptional intellectual or creative ability"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Genius", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "genius", "similarity": 0.941}], "id": 3032, "stable_key": "clu_96fed2aef482d8b7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Genius", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q3419343", "wikidata_description": "American digital media company"}}, {"canonical_name": "Arriano", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "arrian", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Arrian", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Arrian"], "contexts": ["ancient historian"]}, {"entity_id": "arriano", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Arriano", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 7, "variants": ["Arriano", "Arriani", "\u00c2rriano"], "contexts": ["historian who wrote about Nearchus", "ancient writer"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Arrian", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Arriano", "similarity": 0.869}], "id": 3033, "stable_key": "clu_4850873fa1884861", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Arrian", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 86, "death_year": 160, "description": "Ancient Greek historian and philosopher, known for his histories of Alexander the Great.", "wikidata_id": "Q31845", "wikidata_description": "2nd century Greek historian, official and philosopher"}}, {"canonical_name": "polarization", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "polarity", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "polarity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["polarity"], "contexts": ["magnetic property"]}, {"entity_id": "polarization", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "polarization", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 7, "variants": ["polarization"], "contexts": ["orientation of light wave oscillations", "orientation of light waves"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "polarity", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "polarization", "similarity": 0.909}], "id": 3034, "stable_key": "clu_ab6186553504d37c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Polarization", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q85900110", "wikidata_description": "process of separation of a relatively homogeneous group into more distinct groups"}}, {"canonical_name": "Gilbert", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "d__gilbert", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "D. Gilbert", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["D. Gilbert"], "contexts": ["English physician and natural philosopher"]}, {"entity_id": "gilbert", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Gilbert", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 7, "variants": ["Gilbert"], "contexts": ["author of 'de Magnete'", "author of 'Physiologia Nova de Magnete'"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "D. Gilbert", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Gilbert", "similarity": 0.878}], "id": 3035, "stable_key": "clu_e4738cd2ad84916d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "William Gilbert", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1544, "death_year": 1603, "description": "English physician and natural philosopher, author of 'De Magnete'.", "wikidata_id": "Q192706", "wikidata_description": "English physician, physicist and natural philosopher (1544-1603)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William%20Gilbert%20%28physicist%29", "wikipedia_extract": "William Gilbert (; 24 May 1544? \u2013 30 November 1603), also known as Gilberd, was an English physician, physicist and natural philosopher. He passionately rejected both the prevailing Aristotelian philosophy and the Scholastic method of university teaching. He is remembered today largely for his book De Magnete (1600). A unit of magnetomotive force, also known as magnetic potential, was named the Gilbert in his honour; it has now been superseded by the Ampere-turn. Gilbert was born in Colchester to Jerome Gilberd, a borough recorder."}}, {"canonical_name": "Doleus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "duretus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Duretus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Duretus"], "contexts": ["physician questioning gold's efficacy"]}, {"entity_id": "doleus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Doleus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 7, "variants": ["Doleus", "Dolens"], "contexts": ["cited author on coughs", "medical author"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Duretus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Doleus", "similarity": 0.858}], "id": 3036, "stable_key": "clu_3eff428e76698a9c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Loys Delaunay", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1543, "death_year": 1627, "description": "French physician and author, known for his pharmacological works.", "note": "The name 'Doleus' is a Latinization of 'Delaunay'. 'Duretus' is likely a misspelling or alternative form."}}, {"canonical_name": "Lice", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "lice", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Lice", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lice"], "contexts": ["animated from corrupt humours in man"]}, {"entity_id": "lice", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Lice", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 7, "variants": ["Lice"], "contexts": ["condition treated by bark in vinegar", "parasitic insects"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Lice", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Lice", "similarity": 0.925}], "id": 3037, "stable_key": "clu_153d1018caff0106", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lice", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q6481228", "wikidata_description": "order of insects", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Head_lice_infestation", "wikipedia_extract": "Head lice infestation, also known as pediculosis capitis, is the infection of the head hair and scalp by the head louse (Pediculus humanus capitis). Itching from lice bites is common. During a person's first infection, the itch may not develop for up to six weeks. If a person is infected again, symptoms may begin much more quickly. The itch may cause problems with sleeping. Generally, however, it is not a serious condition."}}, {"canonical_name": "Venery", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "venery", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Venery", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Venery"], "contexts": ["impotency unto Venery"]}, {"entity_id": "venery", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Venery", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 7, "variants": ["Venery", "venery"], "contexts": ["provoketh to Venery", "sexual intercourse"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Venery", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Venery", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3038, "stable_key": "clu_76ad433a409b6ba8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Venus", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "Relates to sexual desire, intercourse, and the planet Venus.", "note": "Can refer to the act of sex ('Venery') or the planet 'Venus'.", "wikipedia_extract": "Venus is the second planet from the Sun. It is often called Earth's \"twin\" or \"sister\" among the planets of the Solar System for its orbit being the closest to Earth's, both being terrestrial planets, and having the most similar and nearly equal size, mass, and surface gravity. Venus, though, is significantly different, especially as it has no liquid water, and its atmosphere is far thicker and denser than that of any other rocky body in the Solar System. The atmosphere is composed mostly of carbon dioxide and has a thick cloud layer of sulfuric acid that spans the whole planet. At the mean surface level, the atmosphere reaches a temperature of 737 K (464 \u00b0C; 867 \u00b0F) and a pressure 92 times greater than Earth's at sea level, turning the lowest layer of the atmosphere into a supercritical fluid. From Earth, Venus is visible as a star-like point of light, appearing brighter than any other natural point of light in the sky, and as an inferior planet it is always relatively close to the Sun, either as the brightest \"morning star\" or \"evening star\"."}}, {"canonical_name": "argillaceous slate", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "argileuses", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "argileuses", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["argileuses"], "contexts": ["clayey materials", "clayey layers"]}, {"entity_id": "argillaceous_slate", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "argillaceous slate", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 5, "variants": ["argillaceous slate"], "contexts": ["old stratified rock", "sedimentary material suspended in liquids"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "argileuses", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "argillaceous slate", "similarity": 0.887}], "id": 3039, "stable_key": "clu_d32307ece9f8a7f8-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Argillaceous slate", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral"}}, {"canonical_name": "sable", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "sables", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "sables", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["sables"], "contexts": ["granular mineral material", "granular material from decomposed matter"]}, {"entity_id": "sable", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "sable", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["sable"], "contexts": ["granular mineral material"]}, {"entity_id": "sable", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "sable", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 5, "variants": ["sable"], "contexts": ["component of the soil", "granular material"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "sables", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "sable", "similarity": 0.931}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "sable", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "sable", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3040, "stable_key": "clu_7025982bd3f5842e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "sand", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "The context refers to 'granular mineral material' and 'component of the soil', strongly indicating sand.", "wikidata_id": "Q34679", "wikidata_description": "granular material composed of finely divided rock and mineral particles, sand particles range in diameter from 0.063 to 2 mm (per ISO 14688)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sand"}}, {"canonical_name": "aiguille aimant\u00e9e", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "aiguille_aimant\u00e9e", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "aiguille aimant\u00e9e", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 2, "variants": ["aiguille aimant\u00e9e"], "contexts": ["artifacts and instruments"]}, {"entity_id": "aiguille_aimant\u00e9e", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "aiguille aimant\u00e9e", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 6, "variants": ["aiguille aimant\u00e9e", "aiguille", "aiguille de d\u00e9clinaison"], "contexts": ["magnetized needle", "magnetic needle for compass"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "aiguille aimant\u00e9e", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "aiguille aimant\u00e9e", "similarity": 0.891}], "id": 3041, "stable_key": "clu_fd06c1511d47e1be", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "magnetic needle", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "Q129950755", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article published in April 2024"}}, {"canonical_name": "Languedoc", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "languedoc", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "Languedoc", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Languedoc"], "contexts": ["region in France with volcanoes", "French region with volcanic activity"]}, {"entity_id": "languedoc", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Languedoc", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 6, "variants": ["Languedoc"], "contexts": ["region with a canal", "region with a canal for large boats"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Languedoc", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Languedoc", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3042, "stable_key": "clu_d2463d5cb02cdcc5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Languedoc", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "France", "wikidata_id": "Q206221", "wikidata_description": "historical province of France", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Languedoc"}}, {"canonical_name": "Orient", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "orient", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "Orient", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Orient", "orient"], "contexts": ["East", "source of light and knowledge"]}, {"entity_id": "orient", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Orient", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 6, "variants": ["Orient", "l'Orient", "occidentale", "orientale"], "contexts": ["eastern regions", "o\u00f9 l'on trouve des mythes similaires"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Orient", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Orient", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3043, "stable_key": "clu_f68f1f0300b95682", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "East Asia", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "China, Japan, Korea", "note": "While 'Orient' can be a broad term, in the context of early modern natural philosophy and exploration, it most commonly refers to Asia, particularly East Asia.", "wikidata_id": "Q156895", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Betula%20pendula"}}, {"canonical_name": "craie", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "craie", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "craie", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["craie"], "contexts": ["material composed of debris"]}, {"entity_id": "craie", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "craie", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 7, "variants": ["craie", "craie chlorit\u00e9e"], "contexts": ["chalk rock", "chalk deposits"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "craie", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "craie", "similarity": 0.786}], "id": 3044, "stable_key": "clu_a92498d729e557d1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Chalk", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q200133", "wikidata_description": "Greek city in Central Greece region", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chalcis"}}, {"canonical_name": "Santorino", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "santorin", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "Santorin", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Santorin"], "contexts": ["an island near a new volcanic island"]}, {"entity_id": "santorino", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Santorino", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 7, "variants": ["Santorino"], "contexts": ["alternative name for Thera", "volcanic island"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Santorin", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Santorino", "similarity": 0.926}], "id": 3045, "stable_key": "clu_7986a16f09eecd5b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Santorini", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Greece", "note": "Santorino is an older spelling of Santorini, also known historically as Thera.", "wikidata_id": "Q129296", "wikidata_description": "Greek island in the Cyclades", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Santorini"}}, {"canonical_name": "mar\u00e9es atmosph\u00e9riques", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "mar\u00e9es", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "mar\u00e9es", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["mar\u00e9es"], "contexts": ["rise and fall of sea levels"]}, {"entity_id": "mar\u00e9es_atmosph\u00e9riques", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "mar\u00e9es atmosph\u00e9riques", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 7, "variants": ["mar\u00e9es atmosph\u00e9riques", "mar\u00e9es"], "contexts": ["atmospheric pressure variations", "atmospheric phenomenon observed for regularity"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "mar\u00e9es", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "mar\u00e9es atmosph\u00e9riques", "similarity": 0.89}], "id": 3046, "stable_key": "clu_d162a846be1f0d6d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Atmospheric Tides", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This refers to the concept of atmospheric pressure variations analogous to oceanic tides, explored by scientists like Laplace.", "wikidata_id": "Q55969335", "wikidata_description": ""}}, {"canonical_name": "volcanic eruptions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "\u00e9ruptions", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "\u00e9ruptions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["\u00e9ruptions"], "contexts": ["volcanic activity changing rocks"]}, {"entity_id": "volcanic_eruptions", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "volcanic eruptions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["volcanic eruptions"], "contexts": ["eruptions from planet's interior", "outbursts from volcanoes"]}, {"entity_id": "eruption", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "eruption", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["eruption"], "contexts": ["volcanic expulsion", "sudden release of material"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "\u00e9ruptions", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "volcanic eruptions", "similarity": 0.93}, {"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "\u00e9ruptions", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "eruption", "similarity": 0.954}], "id": 3047, "stable_key": "clu_b04bf0af5fe9a76f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Volcanic Eruptions", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q7692360", "wikidata_description": "natural phenomenon where lava, hot gases or other substances are emitted suddenly from a geological feature", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Volcanic%20eruption"}}, {"canonical_name": "d\u00e9troit de Magellan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "d\u00e9troits_de_magellan", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "d\u00e9troits de Magellan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["d\u00e9troits de Magellan"], "contexts": ["strait at the tip of America"]}, {"entity_id": "d\u00e9troit_de_magellan", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "d\u00e9troit de Magellan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 7, "variants": ["d\u00e9troit de Magellan"], "contexts": ["geographical location", "geographical location, strait"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "d\u00e9troits de Magellan", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "d\u00e9troit de Magellan", "similarity": 0.977}], "id": 3048, "stable_key": "clu_827fecc3c7c5ed6b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Strait of Magellan", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Chile, Argentina", "wikidata_id": "Q48365", "wikidata_description": "body of water in Chile connecting Atlantic and Pacific Oceans", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strait%20of%20Magellan"}}, {"canonical_name": "Sign", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "sign", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Sign", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 7, "variants": ["Sign", "Signs"], "contexts": ["zodiac sign influencing body parts", "astrological term"]}, {"entity_id": "sign", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "sign", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sign"], "contexts": ["indicator or cue"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Sign", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "sign", "similarity": 0.973}], "id": 3049, "stable_key": "clu_90f086348564f396-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Astrology", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q34362", "wikidata_description": "ensemble of traditions, beliefs and divinations that interpret the positions of the planets and other celestial bodies; along with their correlation with terrestrial events", "note": "The context 'zodiac sign influencing body parts' clearly points to astrological signs. The broader meaning 'indicator or cue' is secondary in this medical/scientific context."}}, {"canonical_name": "Faintings", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "faintings", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Faintings", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 7, "variants": ["Faintings", "Fainting", "fainting", "faintings"], "contexts": ["condition of the stomach", "after childbirth"]}, {"entity_id": "faintness", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "faintness", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["faintness"], "contexts": ["result of eating fungi"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Faintings", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "faintness", "similarity": 0.933}], "id": 3050, "stable_key": "clu_a908b26be286b990-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "fainting", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "syncope", "wikidata_id": "Q7659241", "wikidata_description": "religious art concept", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swoon%20of%20the%20Virgin", "wikipedia_extract": "The Swoon of the Virgin, in Italian Lo Spasimo della Vergine, or Fainting Virgin Mary was an idea developed in the late Middle Ages, that the Virgin Mary had fainted during the Passion of Christ, most often placed while she watched the Crucifixion of Jesus. It was based on mentions in later texts of the apocryphal Gospel of Nicodemus (Acta Pilati), which describe Mary swooning. It was popular in later medieval art and theological literature, but as it was not mentioned in the Canonical Gospels, it became controversial - Protestants rejecting it outright, and from the 16th century discouraged also by many senior Catholic churchmen. The swoon might be placed during the episode of Christ Carrying the Cross, as on the Via Dolorosa in Jerusalem, but very commonly also during the Crucifixion of Jesus; Nicholas Penny estimates that \"about half of the surviving paintings of the Crucifixion made between 1300 and 1500 will be found to include the Virgin fainting\". It also appeared in works showing the Deposition from the Cross and Entombment of Christ, as well as the 15th-century novelty of Christ taking leave of his Mother. A fainting Mary is sometimes shown in art as early as the 12th century, and becomes common by the middle of the 13th century.", "description": "A temporary loss of consciousness due to a sudden decrease in blood flow."}}, {"canonical_name": "Deafness", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "deafness", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Deafness", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 7, "variants": ["deafness", "Deafness"], "contexts": ["condition helped by Angelica juice/water", "condition helped by oil of seed"]}, {"entity_id": "deaf-mute", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "deaf-mute", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["deaf-mute"], "contexts": ["condition of being deaf and mute"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Deafness", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "deaf-mute", "similarity": 0.885}], "id": 3051, "stable_key": "clu_836f465504b61f09", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hearing Impairment", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Hearing Impairment"}}, {"canonical_name": "Wasps", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "wasps", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Wasps", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 6, "variants": ["Wasps", "Wasp"], "contexts": ["insect with sting", "insects whose stings are treated"]}, {"entity_id": "wasp", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Wasp", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["wasp", "Wasp"], "contexts": ["insect with a stinging tail", "sting of"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Wasps", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Wasp", "similarity": 0.901}], "id": 3052, "stable_key": "clu_ee1f2a4b635766eb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Wasps", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "family": "Vespidae", "description": "Flying insects characterized by their narrow waists and stinging ability.", "wikipedia_extract": "A wasp is any insect of the narrow-waisted suborder Apocrita of the order Hymenoptera which is neither a bee nor an ant; this excludes the broad-waisted sawflies (Symphyta), which look somewhat like wasps, but are in a separate suborder. The wasps do not constitute a clade, a complete natural group with a single ancestor, as  bees and ants are deeply nested within the wasps, having evolved from wasp ancestors. Wasps that are members of the clade Aculeata can sting their prey. The most commonly known wasps, such as yellowjackets and hornets, are in the family Vespidae and are eusocial, living together in a nest with an egg-laying queen and non-reproducing workers. Eusociality is favoured by the unusual haplodiploid system of sex determination in Hymenoptera, as it makes sisters exceptionally closely related to each other. However, the majority of wasp species are solitary, with each adult female living and breeding independently."}}, {"canonical_name": "Sol", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "sol", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Sol", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Sol"], "contexts": ["celestial body influencing herb gathering", "astrological influence on diseases"]}, {"entity_id": "sole", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "sole", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["sole"], "contexts": ["used for dissolving and heating substances", "sun used for drying/heating"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Sol", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "sole", "similarity": 0.886}], "id": 3053, "stable_key": "clu_51825864df35fe4e-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sun", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'Sol' refers to the Sun, both as a celestial body and in its influence on natural phenomena, as well as in a broader metaphorical sense of 'solar systems'.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_extract": "The Sun is the star at the centre of the Solar System. It is a massive, nearly perfect sphere of hot plasma, heated to incandescence by nuclear fusion reactions in its core, radiating the energy from its surface mainly as visible light and infrared radiation with 10% at ultraviolet energies. It is the main source of energy for life on Earth. The Sun has been an object of veneration in many cultures and a central subject for astronomical research since antiquity. The Sun orbits the Galactic Center at a distance of 24,000 to 28,000 light-years. Its mean distance from Earth is about 1.496\u00d7108 kilometres or about 8 light-minutes."}}, {"canonical_name": "malignas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "malignant", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "malignant", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 3, "variants": ["malignant", "Malignant", "malignant Ulcers"], "contexts": ["how foul or Malignant soever they be", "malignant or pestilential quality"]}, {"entity_id": "malignas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "malignas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 5, "variants": ["Malignas", "malignas"], "contexts": ["a type of ailment", "malignant fevers"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "malignant", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "malignas", "similarity": 0.93}], "id": 3054, "stable_key": "clu_33dbba4dcddb5f9e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Malignancy", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Malignancy", "wikidata_id": "Q55786988", "wikidata_description": "human disease"}}, {"canonical_name": "Dodon", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "dodoneus", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Dodoneus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Dodoneus"], "contexts": ["Renaissance botanist"]}, {"entity_id": "dodon", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Dodon", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Dodon", "Dodoneu"], "contexts": ["author of medical observations", "cited for observations on urine color"]}, {"entity_id": "dodoneus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Dodoneus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Dodoneus"], "contexts": ["ancient medical authority on blood tears", "cited for fevers"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Dodoneus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Dodon", "similarity": 0.891}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Dodoneus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Dodoneus", "similarity": 0.647}], "id": 3055, "stable_key": "clu_ea83d2d9fd67edea", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Rembert Dodoens", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1517, "death_year": 1585, "description": "Flemish physician and botanist, renowned for his herbal.", "wikidata_id": "Q437466", "wikidata_description": "Flemish physician and botanist (1517\u20131585)", "wikipedia_extract": "Rembert Dodoens (born Rembert van Joenckema, 29 June 1517 \u2013 10 March 1585) was a Flemish physician and botanist, also known under his Latinized name Rembertus Dodonaeus. He has been called the father of botany. The standard author abbreviation Dodoens is used to indicate this person as the author when citing a botanical name. Dodoens was born Rembert van Joenckema in Mechelen, then the capital of the Spanish Netherlands in 1517. His parents were Denis van Joenckema (d. 1533) and Ursula Roelants.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rembert_Dodoens"}}, {"canonical_name": "confey\u00e7\u00e3o Alchermes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "confectio_alkerimes", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Confectio Alkerimes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Confectio Alkerimes"], "contexts": ["compound medicine"]}, {"entity_id": "confey\u00e7\u00e3o_alchermes", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "confey\u00e7\u00e3o Alchermes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 7, "variants": ["confey\u00e7\u00e3o Alchermes", "Confey\u00e7\u00f5es de Alchermes", "confectionealchermes", "confey\u00e7ao de Alchermes", "confey\u00e7a\u00f3 de Alchermes", "confey\u00e7oensde Alchermes"], "contexts": ["confectionery medicine", "compound medicine Alchermes confection"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Confectio Alkerimes", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "confey\u00e7\u00e3o Alchermes", "similarity": 0.885}], "id": 3056, "stable_key": "clu_afc78c2b6ba8652f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Alchermes confection", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "A historical medicinal cordial, often red in color, containing many ingredients.", "wikipedia_extract": "Alchermes (, Italian: [al\u02c8k\u025brmes]; from the Arabic: \u0627\u0644\u0642\u0631\u0645\u0632, romanized: al-qirmiz, lit.\u2009'cochineal', from Persian: \u06a9\u0631\u0645\u0633\u062a, romanized: kermest, lit.\u2009'bloody, red, cochineal, carmine') is a type of Italian liqueur (especially in Tuscany, Emilia-Romagna, and Sicily) prepared by infusing neutral spirits with sugar, cinnamon, cloves, nutmeg, and vanilla, and other herbs and flavoring agents. Its most striking characteristic is its scarlet color, obtained by the addition of Kermes, a small scale insect from which the drink derives its name. Several proprietary variants are commercially available, where the coloring agent is a coal tar-derived dye such as E124 or E126, with alcoholic contents ranging from 21 to 32%. Its chief use is in coloring pastry, although a quick dessert is sometimes made by adding it to custard cream and sugar. In the Italian pudding zuppa inglese, sponge cake or ladyfingers soaked in this liqueur are a major ingredient. Alkermes was according to Ephraim Chamber's Cyclopaedia (1827), a modification of an 8th-century remedy namely Confectio Alkermes in the \"form and consistency of a confection\" (an electuary, which is thick and pasty)."}}, {"canonical_name": "Malay Archipelago", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "malay_archipelago", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Malay Archipelago", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 7, "variants": ["Malay Archipelago", "Malay archipelago"], "contexts": ["region studied by Wallace", "Malay Archipelago in the south"]}, {"entity_id": "malayalam", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "malayalam", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["malayalam"], "contexts": ["language in southern India"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Malay Archipelago", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "malayalam", "similarity": 0.933}], "id": 3057, "stable_key": "clu_246569a27a58f5e0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Malay Archipelago", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Indonesia; Malaysia; Singapore; Brunei; East Timor", "note": "The term 'malayalam' in the context of 'coloquios_da_orta_1563' refers to the Malayalam language, spoken in Kerala, India. However, the overwhelming majority of mentions and the explicit context of 'origin_of_species_darwin_1859' point to the geographical region. The primary identification is the archipelago.", "wikidata_id": "Q128232347", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article"}}, {"canonical_name": "structure", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "structure", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "structure", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 7, "variants": ["structure"], "contexts": ["physical makeup of organisms", "arrangement of parts in an organism"]}, {"entity_id": "organic_structure", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "organic structure", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["organic structure"], "contexts": ["living biological organization"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "structure", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "organic structure", "similarity": 0.947}], "id": 3058, "stable_key": "clu_d5a73f5c4107afba", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Biological structure", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "Growth", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "growth", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Growth", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["growth", "Growth"], "contexts": ["process of increasing in size", "process of increasing in size or complexity"]}, {"entity_id": "growth", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "growth", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["growth"], "contexts": ["process of increasing size", "process of increasing in size"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Growth", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "growth", "similarity": 0.982}], "id": 3059, "stable_key": "clu_c11274a25cca854d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Growth", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q1342838", "wikidata_description": "the increase in size or mass of an entire organism, a part of an organism or a cell"}}, {"canonical_name": "Bats", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "bats", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Bats", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 6, "variants": ["Bats", "bats"], "contexts": ["flying mammals with wings", "\u00e6rial mammals on islands"]}, {"entity_id": "bats", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "bats", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["bats"], "contexts": ["exception to indigenous distribution rule", "mammiferous animal indigenous to islands"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Bats", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "bats", "similarity": 0.981}], "id": 3060, "stable_key": "clu_be733a14869ea632-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bat", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q10103", "wikidata_description": "city in Riau Islands Province, Indonesia"}}, {"canonical_name": "theory of creation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "theory_of_creation", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "theory of creation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 6, "variants": ["theory of creation"], "contexts": ["explanation for species distribution", "explanation of life's origin by a creator"]}, {"entity_id": "theory_of_creation", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "theory of creation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 2, "variants": ["theory of creation"], "contexts": ["explanation of the origin of the universe", "explanation for the origin of the universe"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "theory of creation", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "theory of creation", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3061, "stable_key": "clu_e83e8757ff966dee-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Creationism", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Contexts suggest a theory of how species originated, contrasting with Darwinian evolution.", "wikidata_id": "Q1430216", "wikidata_description": "doctrine that God creates a soul for each body that is generated, as opposed to pre-existing before birth or being generated during conception", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Creationism%20%28soul%29", "wikipedia_extract": "Creationism is the religious belief that nature, and aspects such as the universe, Earth, life, and humans, originated with supernatural acts of divine creation, and is often pseudoscientific. In its broadest sense, creationism includes various religious views, which differ in their acceptance or rejection of modern scientific concepts, such as evolution, that describe the origin and development of natural phenomena. The term creationism most often refers to belief in special creation: the claim that the universe and lifeforms were created as they exist today by divine action, and that the only true explanations are those which are compatible with a Christian fundamentalist literal interpretation of the creation myth found in the Bible's Genesis creation narrative. Since the 1970s, the most common form of this has been Young Earth creationism which posits special creation of the universe and lifeforms within the last 10,000 years on the basis of flood geology, and promotes pseudoscientific creation science. From the 18th century onward, Old Earth creationism accepted geological time harmonized with Genesis through gap or day-age theory, while supporting anti-evolution. Modern old-Earth creationists support progressive creationism and continue to reject evolutionary explanations."}}, {"canonical_name": "Pictet", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "pictet", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Pictet", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 5, "variants": ["Pictet"], "contexts": ["pal\u00e6ontologist arguing against transmutation", "remarked on organic change and geological formations"]}, {"entity_id": "a__pictet", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "A. Pictet", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["A. Pictet", "Pictet"], "contexts": ["author of Les Orig. Indo-europ\u00e9ennes", "mentioned author on Indo-European origins"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Pictet", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "A. Pictet", "similarity": 0.892}], "id": 3062, "stable_key": "clu_e5a351e3435cb628", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "A. Pictet", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "Author of 'Les Origines Indo-europ\u00e9ennes', a linguist.", "note": "Multiple individuals named Pictet, context suggests a linguist or scholar, not a paleontologist."}}, {"canonical_name": "mammal", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "mammal", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "mammal", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["mammal"], "contexts": ["class of animals", "warm-blooded vertebrate"]}, {"entity_id": "mammalian", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "mammalian", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["mammalian"], "contexts": ["inhabitants of islands"]}, {"entity_id": "mammal", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "mammal", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["mammal"], "contexts": ["vertebrate with mammary glands", "class of animals undergoing development"]}, {"entity_id": "mammalian", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "mammalian", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["mammalian"], "contexts": ["relating to mammals"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "mammal", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mammal", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "mammal", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mammalian", "similarity": 0.967}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "mammalian", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mammal", "similarity": 0.967}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "mammalian", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mammalian", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3063, "stable_key": "clu_216dd87a7239253f-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mammals", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q7377", "wikidata_description": "class of animals, mostly terrestrial tetrapods, some aquatic", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mammal", "wikipedia_extract": "A mammal (from Latin  mamma 'breast') is a vertebrate animal of the class Mammalia (). Mammals are characterised by the presence of milk-producing mammary glands for feeding their young, a broad neocortex region of the brain, fur or hair, and three middle ear bones. These characteristics distinguish them from reptiles and birds, from which their ancestors diverged in the Carboniferous Period over 300 million years ago. Around 6,640 extant species of mammals have been described and divided into 27 orders. The study of mammals is called mammalogy. The largest orders of mammals, by number of species, are the rodents, bats, and eulipotyphlans (including hedgehogs, moles and shrews)."}}, {"canonical_name": "rhododendrons", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "rhododendrons", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "rhododendrons", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Rhododendrons", "rhododendrons"], "contexts": ["flowering shrubs", "genus of flowering plants"]}, {"entity_id": "rhododendron", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Rhododendron", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Rhododendron"], "contexts": ["genus of flowering plants", "plant with sterility issues"]}, {"entity_id": "rhododendron_ponticum", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Rhododendron Ponticum", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rhododendron Ponticum"], "contexts": ["species of Rhododendron"]}, {"entity_id": "rhododendron", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "Rhododendron", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rhododendron"], "contexts": ["plant called Rhododendron by Dioscorides"]}, {"entity_id": "rododendron", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "Rododendron", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rododendron"], "contexts": ["plant name given by Dioscorides"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "rhododendrons", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "Rhododendron", "similarity": 0.919}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "rhododendrons", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "Rododendron", "similarity": 0.914}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Rhododendron", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "Rhododendron", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Rhododendron", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "Rododendron", "similarity": 0.968}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Rhododendron Ponticum", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "Rhododendron", "similarity": 0.921}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Rhododendron Ponticum", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "Rododendron", "similarity": 0.896}], "id": 3064, "stable_key": "clu_835d52dc9ba29b0f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Rhododendron", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Rhododendron L.", "family": "Ericaceae", "note": "Includes references to the genus and specific species like Rhododendron Ponticum.", "wikidata_id": "Q189393", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhododendron"}}, {"canonical_name": "Coccus", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "coccus", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Coccus", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Coccus"], "contexts": ["insect studied for nerve variability"]}, {"entity_id": "coccus", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Coccus", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 7, "variants": ["Coccus", "Coccus lacca", "Coccus laooa", "coccus"], "contexts": ["Latin word for grain or berry", "genus of insect that generates lacca"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Coccus", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Coccus", "similarity": 0.822}], "id": 3065, "stable_key": "clu_22c7524a948e84b4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Firefly", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "The term 'cocuyos' refers to a type of bioluminescent beetle, commonly known as fireflies. 'Coccus' is likely a misreading or broader term for a beetle.", "wikidata_id": "Q25420", "wikidata_description": "family of beetles", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Firefly", "wikipedia_extract": "The Prince of Los Cocuyos is a memoir of Richard Blanco's childhood. The story takes place in Westchester, Miami during the late 1970s and '80s. Blanco is known for being the first Hispanic and openly gay poet of the United States. This book follows his early life as he faced cultural and identity differences within his own family and the hardships of coming of age. In the memoir, he substitutes the real names of his family and friends to tell his story. For example, \"Caco\" is Richard's older brother Carlos."}}, {"canonical_name": "monkeys", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "monkeys", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Monkeys", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Monkeys"], "contexts": ["fossil primates"]}, {"entity_id": "monkeys", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "monkeys", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 7, "variants": ["monkeys"], "contexts": ["primates for observation", "animal used in experiments"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Monkeys", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "monkeys", "similarity": 0.753}], "id": 3066, "stable_key": "clu_5eb9f3c4fac29adc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "monkeys", "confidence": "low", "type": "animal", "description": "Primates used in observation and experiments, offspring of mixed breeds.", "note": "Includes both 'monkeys' and 'mongrels' suggesting a broader category.", "wikipedia_extract": "Monkey is a common name that may refer to most mammals of the infraorder Simiiformes, also known as simians. Traditionally, all animals in the group now known as simians are counted as monkeys except the apes. Thus monkeys, in that sense, constitute an incomplete paraphyletic grouping; alternatively, if apes (Hominoidea) are included, monkeys and simians are synonyms. In 1812, \u00c9tienne Geoffroy grouped the apes and the Cercopithecidae group of monkeys together and established the name Catarrhini, \"Old World monkeys\" (\"singes de l'Ancien Monde\" in French). The extant sister of the Catarrhini in the monkey (\"singes\") group is the Platyrrhini (New World monkeys). Some nine million years before the divergence between the Cercopithecidae and the apes, the Platyrrhini emerged within \"monkeys\" by migration to South America likely by ocean."}}, {"canonical_name": "solano", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "solano", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "solano", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 7, "variants": ["solano"], "contexts": ["plant name", "plant with similar leaves to chilmecatl"]}, {"entity_id": "solani", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "solani", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["solani"], "contexts": ["used in preparations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "solano", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "solani", "similarity": 0.927}], "id": 3067, "stable_key": "clu_8f5762cdc3afeb2f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Solanum", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Solanum L.", "family": "Solanaceae", "note": "'Solano' and 'solani' refer to plants in the genus Solanum. The context of Hernandez suggests Solanum species, potentially those known in the Americas.", "wikidata_id": "Q146555", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solanum"}}, {"canonical_name": "ytericia", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 8, "members": [{"entity_id": "ytericia", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "ytericia", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 6, "variants": ["ytericia"], "contexts": ["jaundice", "jaundice, a condition treated by Axixpatli"]}, {"entity_id": "yetericia", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Yetericia", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 2, "variants": ["Yetericia", "\u017ftericia"], "contexts": ["jaundice treated by the water", "jaundice cured by sulphur"]}], "edges": [{"source