as' likely refers to grit or particulate matter, which could be sand-like or a mineral deposit.", "wikidata_id": "Q34679", "wikidata_description": "granular material composed of finely divided rock and mineral particles, sand particles range in diameter from 0.063 to 2 mm (per ISO 14688)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sand"}}, {"canonical_name": "aguila", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "aguila", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "Aguila", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 2, "variants": ["Aguila"], "contexts": ["Eagle", "eagle, a bird of prey"]}, {"entity_id": "aguila", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "aguila", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 5, "variants": ["aguila", "Aguila"], "contexts": ["substance that smells better when dry", "aloes wood, likely aquilaria"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Aguila", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "aguila", "similarity": 0.925}], "id": 3209, "stable_key": "clu_9e18fc4216e2e7f1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "eagle", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q2092297", "wikidata_description": "large carnivorous bird", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eagle"}}, {"canonical_name": "Gomma", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "RESIN", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "goma_de_liquidambar", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "Goma de Liquidambar", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "RESIN", "count": 1, "variants": ["Goma de Liquidambar"], "contexts": ["ingredient in the poultice"]}, {"entity_id": "gomma", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Gomma", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "RESIN", "count": 6, "variants": ["gomma", "Gomma"], "contexts": ["substance used to falsify opium", "resin used in preparations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Goma de Liquidambar", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Gomma", "similarity": 0.88}], "id": 3210, "stable_key": "clu_462b414699698af2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Plant gums", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "note": "Refers to exudates from plants, often sticky and viscous, used in medicine and industry.", "wikidata_id": "Q424275", "wikidata_description": "viscous substance produced by nearly all plants and some microorganisms; in terms of science, a polar glycoprotein and exopolysaccharide", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Resin", "wikipedia_extract": "A resin is a solid or highly viscous liquid that can be converted into a polymer. Resins may be biological or synthetic in origin, but are typically harvested from plants. Resins are mixtures of organic compounds insoluble in water, predominantly terpenes. Technically, resins should not be confused with gums, which consist predominantly of water-soluble polysaccharides, although these two terms are often interchangeable in the less formal context. Common resins include pine oleoresins, amber, hashish, frankincense, myrrh and the animal-derived resin, shellac. Resins are used in varnishes, adhesives, food additives, incenses and perfumes."}}, {"canonical_name": "Semi", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "SEED", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "semi", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "semi", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["semi"], "contexts": ["another name for buyo samo"]}, {"entity_id": "semi", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Semi", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "SEED", "count": 6, "variants": ["semi", "Semi"], "contexts": ["seeds from plants", "plant parts for extracting mucilage"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "semi", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Semi", "similarity": 0.858}], "id": 3211, "stable_key": "clu_603db056a06b39dd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "seed", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "wikidata_id": "Q40763", "wikidata_description": "embryonic plant enclosed in a protective outer covering (seed coat)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seed"}}, {"canonical_name": "licores", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "licores", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "licores", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["licores"], "contexts": ["liquids derived from plants"]}, {"entity_id": "licores", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "licores", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 6, "variants": ["licores", "Licores", "licor"], "contexts": ["liquids from the Indies with medicinal virtues", "liquids made with Ambar"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "licores", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "licores", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3212, "stable_key": "clu_e3cdcbd66fb9ebb6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "liquors", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "note": "Refers to distilled substances or potent liquid extracts, often of plant origin.", "wikidata_id": "Q257106", "wikidata_description": "plant species", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liquorice"}}, {"canonical_name": "coragon", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "coragon", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "coragon", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["coragon"], "contexts": ["heart-shaped leaves"]}, {"entity_id": "coragon", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "coragon", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 6, "variants": ["coragon", "corag\u00f3", "corason"], "contexts": ["heart, principal organ", "heart, organ"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "coragon", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "coragon", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3213, "stable_key": "clu_1ebfcc52ffcda1b2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "heart", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "The term 'coragon' appears to be a misspelling or variant referring to the heart, both anatomically and metaphorically (heart-shaped leaves).", "wikidata_id": "Q1072", "wikidata_description": "inner organ for the circulation of blood", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heart"}}, {"canonical_name": "enchimento de estomago", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "estomago_deuilitado", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "estomago deuilitado", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["estomago deuilitado"], "contexts": ["weakened stomach"]}, {"entity_id": "enchimento_de_estomago", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "enchimento de estomago", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 6, "variants": ["enchimento de estomago", "enchimentos", "enchimentos de estomago", "folu\u00e7os de enchimentode estomago"], "contexts": ["condition of stomach fullness", "cause of illness"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "estomago deuilitado", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "enchimento de estomago", "similarity": 0.896}], "id": 3214, "stable_key": "clu_48012be0b18f6e4b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Stomach fullness", "confidence": "medium", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Gastric distension", "note": "The terms 'estomago deuilitado' (weakened stomach) and 'enchimento de estomago' (stomach fullness) suggest a condition of gastric discomfort or distension, rather than a specific disease entity as understood today. 'Gastric distension' or 'dyspepsia' are plausible modern interpretations.", "wikidata_id": "Q47287435", "wikidata_description": "scientific article published on August 28, 2012"}}, {"canonical_name": "nabos", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "nabos", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "nabos", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["nabos"], "contexts": ["turnips, roots similar to"]}, {"entity_id": "nabos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "nabos", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 6, "variants": ["nabos"], "contexts": ["vegetable used in remedies", "turnips"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "nabos", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "nabos", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3215, "stable_key": "clu_54b9421c32e1cc33", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Turnip", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Brassica rapa", "family": "Brassicaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q3384", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brassica%20rapa"}}, {"canonical_name": "vipera", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "REPTILE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "vibora", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "vibora", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "REPTILE", "count": 1, "variants": ["vibora"], "contexts": ["snake whose head is used for throat ailments"]}, {"entity_id": "vipera", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "vipera", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "REPTILE", "count": 6, "variants": ["vipera", "Vipera", "vipere"], "contexts": ["animal whose venom is discussed", "viper used in trochisci composition"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "vibora", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "vipera", "similarity": 0.91}], "id": 3216, "stable_key": "clu_b036c27de1db0c17-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Adder", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q192056", "wikidata_description": "species of snake belonging to the viper family (Viperidae)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adder", "wikipedia_extract": "Vipera berus, commonly known as the common European adder and the common European viper, is a species of venomous snake in the family Viperidae. The species is extremely widespread and can be found throughout much of Europe, and as far as East Asia. There are three recognised subspecies. Known by a host of common names including common adder and common viper, the adder has been the subject of much folklore in Britain and other European countries. It is not regarded as especially dangerous; the snake is not aggressive and usually bites only when really provoked, stepped on, or picked up. Bites can be very painful, but are rarely fatal.", "note": "The term 'vipera' is the Latin genus for vipers, while 'Viper' and 'viper' are its English equivalents."}}, {"canonical_name": "Nova Espanha", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "new_spain", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "New Spain", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["New Spain"], "contexts": ["geographic location"]}, {"entity_id": "nova_espanha", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Nova Espanha", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 6, "variants": ["Nova Espanha", "Nova Hespanha"], "contexts": ["place where figs are found", "place where authors wrote"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "New Spain", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Nova Espanha", "similarity": 0.958}], "id": 3217, "stable_key": "clu_9c0ec9c3fc529b8b-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mexico", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Mexico", "wikidata_id": "Q170603", "wikidata_description": "viceroyalty of the Spanish Empire (1535-1821)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New%20Spain", "wikipedia_extract": "New Spain, officially the Viceroyalty of New Spain (Spanish: Virreinato de Nueva Espa\u00f1a [birej\u02c8nato \u00f0e \u02c8nwe\u03b2a es\u02c8pa\u0272a]; Nahuatl: Yankwik Kaxtillan Birreiyotl), originally the Kingdom of New Spain, was an integral territorial entity of the Spanish Empire, established by Habsburg Spain. It was one of several domains established during the Spanish conquest of the Americas, and had its capital in Mexico City. Its jurisdiction comprised a large area of the southern and western portions of North America, mainly what became Mexico and the Southwestern United States, but also California, Florida and Louisiana; Central America (as part of Mexico); the Caribbean like Hispaniola and Martinique, and northern parts of South America, even Colombia; several Pacific archipelagos, including the Philippines and Guam. Additional Asian colonies included \"Spanish Formosa\", on the island of Taiwan. After the 1521 Spanish conquest of the Aztec Empire, conqueror Hern\u00e1n Cort\u00e9s named the territory New Spain, and established the new capital, Mexico City, on the site of Tenochtitlan, the capital of the Aztec Empire. Central Mexico became the base of expeditions of exploration and conquest, expanding the territory claimed by the Spanish Empire."}}, {"canonical_name": "quadersandstein", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "quadersandstein", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "quadersandstein", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 6, "variants": ["quadersandstein", "quadersandstcin"], "contexts": ["rock type", "Quadensandstein formation"]}, {"entity_id": "quader_sanstein", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "quader sanstein", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["quader sanstein"], "contexts": ["geological rock formation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "quadersandstein", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "quader sanstein", "similarity": 0.89}], "id": 3218, "stable_key": "clu_5edc52424652dfe0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Quadersandstein", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q315298", "wikidata_description": "mountains in Germany and the Czech Republic"}}, {"canonical_name": "Mont Saint-Elie", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "mont_saint-elie", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Mont Saint-Elie", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 6, "variants": ["Mont Saint-Elie", "Mont Saint-Helen", "Saint-Elie"], "contexts": ["mountain in Russian America", "volcanic peak"]}, {"entity_id": "mount_st__elias", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Mount St. Elias", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mount St. Elias"], "contexts": ["limestone mass against which volcanic strata abut"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Mont Saint-Elie", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Mount St. Elias", "similarity": 0.893}], "id": 3219, "stable_key": "clu_100a7f11e67edda2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mount Saint Elias", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United States"}}, {"canonical_name": "Novembre", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "novembre", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Novembre", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["Novembre", "DECEMBRE", "D\u00e9cembre"], "contexts": ["month of meteorological observations", "month of observation"]}, {"entity_id": "novembre", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Novembre", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Novembre"], "contexts": ["month"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Novembre", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Novembre", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3220, "stable_key": "clu_1848f332223afed4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "November", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the month of November, used for meteorological and observational records.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}}, {"canonical_name": "variations horaires", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "variations_horaires", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "variations horaires", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 6, "variants": ["variations horaires", "variations", "variations du barom\u00e8tre", "variations horaires de la d\u00e9clinaison magn\u00e9tique"], "contexts": ["hourly changes in barometric height", "hourly changes"]}, {"entity_id": "horary_variations", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "horary variations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["horary variations"], "contexts": ["daily changes in magnetic declination"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "variations horaires", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "horary variations", "similarity": 0.936}], "id": 3221, "stable_key": "clu_bdd1a0b00914cc7d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hourly variations", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to changes in scientific measurements that occur on an hourly basis."}}, {"canonical_name": "Clermont-Ferrand", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "clermont-ferrand", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Clermont-Ferrand", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 6, "variants": ["Clermont-Ferrand", "Ciermont-Ftrrand", "Clermonl-Ferrand", "Clermont", "Clermont en Auvergne"], "contexts": ["city with barometric observations", "location of Ramond's observations"]}, {"entity_id": "clermont-ferrand", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Clermont-Ferrand", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Clermont-Ferrand"], "contexts": ["city in France"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Clermont-Ferrand", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Clermont-Ferrand", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3222, "stable_key": "clu_92bc050ce58ee5b5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Clermont-Ferrand", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "France", "wikidata_id": "Q42168", "wikidata_description": "commune in Puy-de-D\u00f4me, France", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clermont-Ferrand", "wikipedia_extract": "Clermont-Ferrand Cathedral, or the Cathedral of Our Lady of the Assumption of Clermont-Ferrand (French: Cath\u00e9drale Notre-Dame-de-l'Assomption de Clermont-Ferrand), is a Gothic cathedral and French national monument located in the city of Clermont-Ferrand in the Auvergne. It is the seat of the Archbishops of Clermont (bishops until 2002). It is built entirely in black lava stone, which makes it highly distinctive, and visible from a great distance. Its twin spires are 96.1 metres tall, and tower above the city's rooftops. It has been listed since 1862 as a monument historique by the French Ministry of Culture. In the 5th century, bishop Namatius laid the foundations of the city's first cathedral, allowing the Christian community to leave its ghetto, the \"vicus christianorum\"."}}, {"canonical_name": "Arica", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "arica", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Arica", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 6, "variants": ["Arica"], "contexts": ["city in Chile", "port city"]}, {"entity_id": "arica", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Arica", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Arica"], "contexts": ["Peruvian coast location"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Arica", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Arica", "similarity": 0.787}], "id": 3223, "stable_key": "clu_4160e67491b94628", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Arica", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A port city in northern Chile.", "note": "Acra is likely referring to Acre, a port city.", "wikipedia_extract": "Arica ( \u0259-REE-k\u0259; Spanish: [a\u02c8\u027eika]) is a commune and a port city with a population of 241,653 in the Arica Province of northern Chile's Arica y Parinacota Region. It is Chile's northernmost city, being located only 18 km (11 mi) south of the border with Peru. The city is the capital of both the Arica Province and the Arica and Parinacota Region. Arica is located at the bend of South America's western coast known as the Arica Bend or Arica Elbow. At the location of the city are two valleys that dissect the Atacama Desert converge: Azapa and Lluta. These valleys provide citrus and olives for export."}}, {"canonical_name": "Strasbourg", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "strasbourg", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Strasbourg", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 6, "variants": ["STRASBOURG", "Strasbourg"], "contexts": ["location with barometer data", "location with latitude 48\u00b0 34' 56\""]}, {"entity_id": "strasbourg", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Strasbourg", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Strasbourg"], "contexts": ["city in France"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Strasbourg", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Strasbourg", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3224, "stable_key": "clu_e6e03a124c01cc7c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Strasbourg", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "France", "wikidata_id": "Q6602", "wikidata_description": "capital and largest city of Grand Est, located in the historical region of Alsace", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strasbourg", "wikipedia_extract": "Strasbourg (UK:  STRAZ-burg, US:  STRAHSS-boorg, STRAHZ-, -\u2060burg; French: [st\u0281asbu\u0281] ; German: Stra\u00dfburg [\u02c8\u0283t\u0281a\u02d0sb\u028a\u0281k] ) is the prefecture and largest city of the Grand Est region of eastern France, in the historic region of Alsace. It is the prefecture of the Bas-Rhin department and the official seat of the European Parliament. The city has about three hundred thousand inhabitants, and together Greater Strasbourg and the arrondissement of Strasbourg have over five hundred thousand. Strasbourg's metropolitan area had a population of 860,744 in 2020, making it the eighth-largest metro area in France and home to 14% of the Grand Est region's inhabitants. The transnational Eurodistrict Strasbourg-Ortenau had a population of roughly 1,000,000 in 2022. Strasbourg is one of the de facto four main capitals of the European Union (alongside Brussels, Luxembourg and Frankfurt), as it is the seat of several European institutions, such as the European Parliament, the Eurocorps and the European Ombudsman of the European Union."}}, {"canonical_name": "Lindenau", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "lindenau", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Lindenau", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 5, "variants": ["Lindenau", "M. de Lindcnau", "M. de Lindenau", "M. de Lindeneau", "MM. de Lindenau et Bauza"], "contexts": ["person who made calculations", "calculated the surface area of Cuba"]}, {"entity_id": "lindenau", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Lindenau", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Lindenau"], "contexts": ["astronomer involved in stellar measurements", "associated with Pole Star and Struve"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Lindenau", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Lindenau", "similarity": 0.729}], "id": 3225, "stable_key": "clu_01a7122bd29882b8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bernhard von Lindenau", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1780, "death_year": 1854, "description": "German astronomer known for stellar measurements and calculations."}}, {"canonical_name": "\u00e9conomie politique", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "\u00e9conomie_politique", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "\u00e9conomie politique", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["\u00e9conomie politique"], "contexts": ["Science of governments", "field of study"]}, {"entity_id": "political_economy", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "political economy", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["political economy"], "contexts": ["science of social wealth and production", "field of study concerning production and distribution"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "\u00e9conomie politique", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "political economy", "similarity": 0.948}], "id": 3226, "stable_key": "clu_caf99be58b6ede7b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Political economy", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The study of the production, distribution, and consumption of wealth.", "wikidata_id": "Q28224630", "wikidata_description": "King's College, Cambridge club, founded 1909"}}, {"canonical_name": "Charcas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "charcas", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Charcas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 5, "variants": ["Charcas"], "contexts": ["region in Bolivia", "Charcas links Peru to Buenos-Ayres"]}, {"entity_id": "charcas", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Charcas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Charcas"], "contexts": ["origin of indigenous people", "region from which indigenous people came"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Charcas", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Charcas", "similarity": 0.651}], "id": 3227, "stable_key": "clu_c504c8d9e464dde4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sucre", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Bolivia"}}, {"canonical_name": "Saint-Thomas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "saint-thomas", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Saint-Thomas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 5, "variants": ["Saint-Thomas"], "contexts": ["Barbade, Martinique and Saint-Thomas", "island name"]}, {"entity_id": "st__thomas", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "St. Thomas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["St. Thomas"], "contexts": ["island near a magnetic equator node", "island on the western coast of Africa"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Saint-Thomas", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "St. Thomas", "similarity": 0.956}], "id": 3228, "stable_key": "clu_ff2d59a9453283d7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Saint Thomas", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United States Virgin Islands", "note": "Multiple references to 'Saint-Thomas' and 'St. Thomas' as an island, particularly in the context of Humboldt's works and its location near the magnetic equator, strongly indicate the Caribbean island.", "wikidata_id": "Q20851010", "wikidata_description": "census district of the United States Virgin Islands, includes the Island of Saint Thomas", "wikipedia_extract": "Thomas the Apostle also known as Didymus (Greek: \u0394\u03af\u03b4\u03c5\u03bc\u03bf\u03c2, romanized: D\u00eddymos 'twin'), was one of the Twelve Apostles of Jesus according to the New Testament. Thomas is commonly known as \"doubting Thomas\" because he initially doubted the resurrection of Jesus when he was told of it (as is related in the Gospel of John); he later confessed his faith (\"The lord of me and the God of me\") on seeing the places where the wounds appeared still fresh on the holy body of Jesus after the Crucifixion of Jesus. According to traditional accounts of the Saint Thomas Christians of Kerala, Thomas travelled outside the Roman Empire to preach the Gospel, travelling through southern India in the modern states of Kerala and Tamil Nadu, and eventually reached Muziris (modern-day North Paravur and Kodungalloor) in 52 AD. He started the Church of the East in the region around the Van Province, Edessa, and Hakkari regions of Upper Mesopotamia (modern-day southeastern Turkey), presumably sometime between mid-late 30s and 45, and spent many years evangelizing both Jews and non-christians in the area before heading further east in the late 40s. In 1258, some of the relics were brought to Ortona, in Abruzzo, Italy, where they have been held in the Church of Saint Thomas the Apostle. He is regarded as the patron saint of India among its Christian adherents, and the Feast of Saint Thomas on July 3 is celebrated as Indian Christians' Day.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_the_Apostle"}}, {"canonical_name": "Freiberg", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "freiberg", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Freiberg", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 5, "variants": ["Freiberg"], "contexts": ["location of mines", "city known for mining"]}, {"entity_id": "freiberg", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Freiberg", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Freiberg"], "contexts": ["latitude for centrifugal force calculation", "location of mines with magnetic disturbance"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Freiberg", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Freiberg", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3229, "stable_key": "clu_208cb6ad1fb9622e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Freiberg", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Germany", "note": "References to mines, mining schools, and latitude calculations in the context of physical sciences point to Freiberg, a city historically known for its mining and mineralogical significance.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_extract": "Freiberg ist ein deutscher Familienname. Freiberg ist ein Herkunftsname f\u00fcr diejenigen, die aus einem Ort namens Freiberg oder Freyberg stammen. Freibergs, Freyberg, Freiberger, Freyberger Bj\u00f6rn Freiberg (* 1970), deutscher Schauspieler Daniel Freiberg (* 1957), US-amerikanischer Jazzmusiker Dietrich von Freiberg (um 1240\u20131310), Philosoph, Mystiker, Dominikaner und Theologe Dov Freiberg (1927\u20132008), polnisch-israelischer Holocaust\u00fcberlebender und Buchautor Gottfried von Freiberg (1908\u20131962), \u00f6sterreichischer Hornist Hedwig Freiberg (1872\u20131945), deutsche Malerin und Schauspielerin, Ehefrau von Robert Koch Heinrich von Freiberg (13. Jh.), Dichter und Minnes\u00e4nger Henning Freiberg (* 1937), deutscher Kunstp\u00e4dagoge und Hochschullehrer im Ruhestand Kjell-B\u00f8rge Freiberg (* 1971), norwegischer Politiker Konrad Freiberg (* 1951), deutscher Polizist und Gewerkschaftsvorsitzender Lorenz III. von Freiberg (vor 1459\u20131487), Bischof von Gurk Ludwig von Freiberg (1442\u2013vor 1480), Bischof von Konstanz Marcos A. Freiberg (1911\u20131990), argentinischer Zoologe Margit Freiberg (* 1962), deutsche Tischtennisspielerin Michael Freiberg (Politiker) (* 1956), deutscher Politiker (CDU) Michael Freiberg (Radsportler) (* 1990), australischer Radrennfahrer Mona Freiberg (* 1950), deutsche Volksschauspielerin und S\u00e4ngerin Peter Freiberg (* 1954), deutscher Drehbuchautor, Radiomoderator und S\u00e4nger Siegfried Freiberg (1901\u20131985), \u00f6sterreichischer Schriftsteller und Bibliothekar Steffen Freiberg (* 1981), deutscher Politiker Walter Freiberg (* 1922), deutscher Oberst im Ministerium f\u00fcr Staatssicherheit (MfS) der DDR Freiberg.", "wikipedia_url": "https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Freiberg_%28Familienname%29"}}, {"canonical_name": "Humb.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "humb_", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Humb.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 5, "variants": ["Humb."], "contexts": ["author reference", "Abbreviation for Alexander von Humboldt"]}, {"entity_id": "humb_", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Humb.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Humb."], "contexts": ["cited author in footnote"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Humb.", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Humb.", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3230, "stable_key": "clu_baf9b5edab0f7714-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Alexander von Humboldt", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1769, "death_year": 1859, "description": "Prussian explorer and naturalist, key figure in biogeography", "wikidata_id": "Q6694", "wikidata_description": "Prussian geographer, naturalist and explorer (1769\u20131859)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander%20von%20Humboldt", "wikipedia_extract": "Friedrich Wilhelm Heinrich Alexander von Humboldt (14 September 1769 \u2013 6 May 1859) was a German polymath, geographer, naturalist, explorer, and proponent of Romantic philosophy and science. He was the younger brother of the Prussian minister, philosopher, and linguist Wilhelm von Humboldt (1767\u20131835). Humboldt's quantitative work on botanical geography laid the foundation for the field of biogeography, while his advocacy of long-term systematic geophysical measurement pioneered modern geomagnetic and meteorological monitoring. Humboldt and Carl Ritter are both regarded as the founders of modern geography as they established it as an independent scientific discipline. Between 1799 and 1804, Humboldt travelled extensively in the Americas, exploring and describing them for the first time from a non-Spanish European scientific point of view. On these travels, along with French explorer Aim\u00e9 Bonpland, he traversed thousands of miles through some of the most difficult and little-known places on Earth, to include identifying the source of the Orinoco River and in 1802 climbing the highest mountain in Ecuador to a height of 19,286 feet, at the time a world record altitude for a Westerner.", "note": "While 'Long' and 'Lange' are the exact OCR, context strongly suggests Humboldt's exploration of the Rocky Mountains and his scientific writings."}}, {"canonical_name": "Rennell", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "rennell", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Rennell", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 5, "variants": ["Rennell"], "contexts": ["hydrographic work author", "cited for geographical data"]}, {"entity_id": "rennell", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Rennell", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Rennell"], "contexts": ["termed Caribbean Sea a 'sea in motion'", "terms region 'sea in motion'"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Rennell", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Rennell", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3231, "stable_key": "clu_c66fea35fa2cc0ce", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "James Rennell", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1742, "death_year": 1830, "description": "English geographer and oceanographer, noted for his work on currents.", "wikidata_id": "Q965945", "wikidata_description": "English geographer, historian and oceanographer (1742\u20131830)"}}, {"canonical_name": "La P\u00e9rouse", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "la_p\u00e9rouse", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "La P\u00e9rouse", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["La P\u00e9rouse", "P\u00e9rouse", "la P\u00e9rouse"], "contexts": ["addressed by academy", "author of a voyage account"]}, {"entity_id": "laperouse", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Laperouse", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Laperouse"], "contexts": ["navigator and explorer"]}, {"entity_id": "la_perouse", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "La Perouse", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["La Perouse"], "contexts": ["leader of expedition", "explorer whose return was awaited"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "La P\u00e9rouse", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "La Perouse", "similarity": 0.805}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Laperouse", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "La Perouse", "similarity": 0.559}], "id": 3232, "stable_key": "clu_dc3ddf0f7bf042c9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jean-Fran\u00e7ois de Galaup, comte de Lap\u00e9rouse", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1741, "death_year": 1788, "description": "French naval officer and explorer leading a scientific expedition.", "wikidata_id": "Q294478", "wikidata_description": "French Navy officer and explorer (1741\u20131788)"}}, {"canonical_name": "capillarit\u00e9", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "capillarit\u00e9", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "capillarit\u00e9", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["capillarit\u00e9", "capillarity"], "contexts": ["effect on mercury in barometer tubes", "phenomenon in narrow tubes"]}, {"entity_id": "capillarity", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Capillarity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["capillarity", "Capillarity"], "contexts": ["universal action of surface tension", "force causing liquid surface changes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "capillarit\u00e9", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Capillarity", "similarity": 0.905}], "id": 3233, "stable_key": "clu_f4fa2ac54cced894", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "capillarity", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q188603", "wikidata_description": "ability of a liquid to flow in narrow spaces"}}, {"canonical_name": "Oby", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "oby", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Oby", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Oby", "Obi"], "contexts": ["river", "river banks for comparison"]}, {"entity_id": "oby", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Oby", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Oby"], "contexts": ["river in Northern Asia", "river where Barnaul is located"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Oby", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Oby", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3234, "stable_key": "clu_43bf16c9b756f2c6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Obidos", "confidence": "medium", "type": "place", "description": "A town in Portugal, also associated with a river or coastal area.", "note": "The context of 'Oby' as a river and 'Obidos' being a Portuguese town with coastal access makes this plausible.", "wikipedia_extract": "\u00d3bidos (Portuguese pronunciation: [\u02c8\u0254\u03b2i\u00f0u\u0283] ; Proto-Celtic: *Eburobrittium) is a town  and a municipality in the Oeste region, historical province of Estremadura and the Leiria district. The town proper has approximately 3,100 inhabitants. The municipality population in 2011 was 11,772 covering an area of 141.55 square kilometres (54.65 square miles). The name \"\u00d3bidos\" is a Latinised (oppidum, citadel) derivation of the older Celtic \"Eburobricio\". The municipality grew from a Roman settlement near the foothills of an elevated escarpment. The region of \u00d3bidos, extending from the Atlantic to the interior of Estremadura Province along the rivers and lakes has been inhabited since the late Paleolithic."}}, {"canonical_name": "Klaproth", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "klaproth", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Klaproth", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Klaproth", "Klaprolh", "Rlaproth"], "contexts": ["cited chemist for mineral analysis", "savante researcher of languages"]}, {"entity_id": "klaproth", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Klaproth", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Klaproth"], "contexts": ["scholar who wrote about the invention of the compass", "author of a letter on compass invention"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Klaproth", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Klaproth", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3235, "stable_key": "clu_5c53011083fb81fd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Martin Heinrich Klaproth", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1743, "death_year": 1817, "description": "German chemist known for mineral analysis and element discovery.", "wikidata_id": "Q983803", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants"}}, {"canonical_name": "cyanite", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "cyanite", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "cyanite", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["cyanite"], "contexts": ["mineral discovered in blocks", "mineral found in schist"]}, {"entity_id": "cyanite", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Cyanite", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["cyanite", "Cyanite"], "contexts": ["mineral that acts like a compass needle", "mineral that aligns with magnetism"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "cyanite", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Cyanite", "similarity": 0.992}], "id": 3236, "stable_key": "clu_c4d1e2eaa50343fd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Kyanite", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "The text mentions 'cyanite' which is a common misspelling of 'kyanite'.", "wikidata_id": "Q193450", "wikidata_description": "nesosilicate mineral"}}, {"canonical_name": "Lambert", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "lambert", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Lambert", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 4, "variants": ["Lambert"], "contexts": ["author of 'Illustration of the gems Cinchona'", "scholar cited for astronomical acts"]}, {"entity_id": "lambert", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Lambert", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Lambert"], "contexts": ["speculative conclusions about universe structure", "idea about wind direction and barometer"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Lambert", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Lambert", "similarity": 0.733}], "id": 3237, "stable_key": "clu_160f2c2c117f11c0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Johann Heinrich Lambert", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1728, "death_year": 1777, "description": "Swiss mathematician, physicist, and astronomer; developed cosmological theories.", "note": "The text mentions 'Illustration of the gems Cinchona' which might be a misattribution or a different Lambert. However, his cosmological theories align with the 'speculative conclusions about universe structure'.", "wikidata_id": "Q122999", "wikidata_description": "German mathematician, physicist and astronomer (1728-1777)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johann%20Heinrich%20Lambert", "wikipedia_extract": "Johann Heinrich Lambert (German: [\u02c8lamb\u025b\u0281t]; French: Jean-Henri Lambert; 26 or 28 August 1728 \u2013 25 September 1777) was a polymath from the Republic of Mulhouse, at that time allied to the Swiss Confederacy, who made important contributions to the subjects of mathematics, physics (particularly optics), philosophy, astronomy and map projections. Lambert was born in 1728 into a Huguenot family in the city of Mulhouse, nowadays in  Alsace, France, at that time a city-state allied to the Swiss Confederacy. Some sources give 26 August as his birth date and others 28 August. Leaving school at 12, he continued to study in his free time while undertaking a series of jobs. These included assistant to his father (a tailor), a clerk at a nearby iron works, a private tutor, secretary to the editor of Basler Zeitung and, at the age of 20, private tutor to the sons of Count Salis in Chur. Travelling Europe with his charges (1756\u20131758) allowed him to meet established mathematicians in the German states, The Netherlands, France and the Italian states."}}, {"canonical_name": "Chinese", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "chinois", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Chinois", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 3, "variants": ["chinois", "Chinois"], "contexts": ["people using quippos for arithmetic", "deserts"]}, {"entity_id": "chinese", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Chinese", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 4, "variants": ["Chinese"], "contexts": ["observers of planetary motions", "origin of ancient comet observations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Chinois", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Chinese", "similarity": 0.961}], "id": 3238, "stable_key": "clu_001dbccf66676c18", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Chinese people", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This refers to a group of people and their practices, not a specific individual."}}, {"canonical_name": "Maine", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "maine", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Maine", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Maine"], "contexts": ["state separated from Massachusets", "State in the United States"]}, {"entity_id": "maine", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Maine", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Maine"], "contexts": ["state where J. G. Clarke observed comet", "river region in Germany"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Maine", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Maine", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3239, "stable_key": "clu_f375b59fe6a0b97b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Maine", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United States", "wikipedia_extract": "Maine (  MAYN) is a state in the New England region of the United States, and the northeasternmost state in the contiguous United States. It borders New Hampshire to the west, the Gulf of Maine to the southeast, and the Canadian provinces of New Brunswick and Quebec to the northeast and northwest, and shares a maritime border with Nova Scotia. It is the only state to border only one other state, which is New Hampshire. Maine is the largest state in New England by total area, almost as large as the combined area of the remaining five states. Of the 50 U.S. states, it is the 12th-smallest by area, the 9th-least populous, the 13th-least densely populated, and the most rural. As of 2024, Maine's population stood at a Census-estimated 1,400,000, the state's highest-ever population estimate."}}, {"canonical_name": "fucus", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "fucus", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "fucus", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["fucus"], "contexts": ["possible source of filaments", "type of seaweed"]}, {"entity_id": "fucus", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "fucus", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["fucus"], "contexts": ["floating layers of fucus", "floating layers of seaweed"]}, {"entity_id": "fucus_natans", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Fucus natans", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Fucus natans"], "contexts": ["social plant of the sea"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "fucus", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "fucus", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "fucus", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Fucus natans", "similarity": 0.889}], "id": 3240, "stable_key": "clu_e94f0dba666e85bc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Fucus", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Fucus", "family": "Fucaceae", "note": "The mention of 'Fucus natans' confirms the genus. 'Fucus' is a widely recognized genus of marine algae.", "wikidata_id": "Q180597", "wikidata_description": "genus of algae"}}, {"canonical_name": "Lametherie", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "lametherie", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Lametherie", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Lametherie"], "contexts": ["published a geognostic sketch", "named bee genus"]}, {"entity_id": "lametherie", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Lametherie", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Lametherie"], "contexts": ["author of Journal de Physique", "publisher of Journal de Physique"]}, {"entity_id": "lam\u00e9therie", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Lam\u00e9therie", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Lam\u00e9therie"], "contexts": ["editor of Journal de Physique", "editor of scientific journal"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Lametherie", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Lametherie", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Lametherie", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Lam\u00e9therie", "similarity": 0.66}], "id": 3241, "stable_key": "clu_c29968fa2e4e3e1e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jean-Baptiste-Beno\u00eet Mathieu de Laumont", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1753, "death_year": 1834, "description": "French chemist and mineralogist, editor of Journal de Physique"}}, {"canonical_name": "Dhawalagiri", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "dhawalagiri", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Dhawalagiri", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Dhawalagiri", "Dbanalagiri"], "contexts": ["peak in the Himalayas"]}, {"entity_id": "dhawalagiri", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Dhawalagiri", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 5, "variants": ["Dhawalagiri"], "contexts": ["mountain elevation data", "highest point of the Himalaya"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Dhawalagiri", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Dhawalagiri", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3242, "stable_key": "clu_e993a5a35abaa594", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Dhaulagiri", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Nepal", "wikidata_id": "Q2564224", "wikidata_description": "genus of arachnids", "wikipedia_extract": "The eight-thousanders are the 14 mountains that rise more than 8,000 metres (26,247 ft) above sea level. They are all in the Himalayan and Karakoram mountain ranges. This is a list of mountaineers who have died on these mountains. Mount Everest, Earth's highest mountain at 8,848.86 metres (29,031.7 ft) above sea level, has been host to numerous tragedies. Deaths have occurred on the mountain every year since 1978, excluding 2020, when permits were not issued due to the COVID-19 pandemic. The most notable deadly events on Everest were the 1922 British Mount Everest expedition, 1970 Mount Everest disaster, 1974 French Mount Everest expedition, 1996 Mount Everest disaster, 2014 Mount Everest ice avalanche, 2015 Mount Everest avalanches and 2023 Mount Everest season.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_deaths_on_eight-thousanders"}}, {"canonical_name": "Voyages", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "voyages_and_descr_", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Voyages and Descr.", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Voyages and Descr.", "Voyages and Descriptions"], "contexts": ["title of Dampier's work", "publication title"]}, {"entity_id": "travels", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Travels", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Travels"], "contexts": ["book by M. Melish"]}, {"entity_id": "voyages", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Voyages", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Voyages"], "contexts": ["work cited", "Tavernier, Voyages"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Voyages and Descr.", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Voyages", "similarity": 0.914}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Travels", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Voyages", "similarity": 0.966}], "id": 3243, "stable_key": "clu_d5fc494def1c8cfe", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Travels", "confidence": "high", "type": "text", "note": "This cluster refers to the general concept or genre of travel writings, and specific titles like Dampier's 'Voyages' and M. Melish's 'Travels'."}}, {"canonical_name": "quintaes", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "quintaux", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "quintaux", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["quintaux"], "contexts": ["unit of weight for coffee", "unit of weight"]}, {"entity_id": "quintaes", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "quintaes", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 5, "variants": ["quintaes", "quintal", "quintall"], "contexts": ["unit of weight", "unit of measure for cloves"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "quintaux", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "quintaes", "similarity": 0.888}], "id": 3244, "stable_key": "clu_d02e928bbf0eabcc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Quintal", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "A historical unit of weight."}}, {"canonical_name": "Massachusetts", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "massachusetts", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Massachusetts", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Massachusetts"], "contexts": ["state in the United States", "origin of the author Abiel Abbot"]}, {"entity_id": "massachusetts", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Massachusetts", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 5, "variants": ["Massachusetts"], "contexts": ["location where translator saw comet", "state where fall of stars observed"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Massachusetts", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Massachusetts", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3245, "stable_key": "clu_981a29819d01d2f2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Massachusetts", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United States", "wikidata_id": "Q771", "wikidata_description": "state of the United States of America", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Massachusetts", "wikipedia_extract": "Massachusetts (   MASS-\u0259-CHOO-sits, -\u2060zits; Massachusett: Muhsachuweesut [m\u0259hswat\u0283\u0259wi\u02d0s\u0259t]), officially the Commonwealth of Massachusetts, is a state in the New England region of the Northeastern United States. It borders the Atlantic Ocean and the Gulf of Maine to its east, Connecticut and Rhode Island to its south, New Hampshire and Vermont to its north, and New York to its west. Massachusetts is the seventh-smallest state by land area. With an estimated population of over 7.1 million, it is the most populous state in New England, the 16th-most-populous in the United States, and the third-most densely populated U.S. state, after New Jersey and Rhode Island. Massachusetts was a site of early English colonization."}}, {"canonical_name": "Moser", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "moser", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Moser", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Moser"], "contexts": ["discovery of images produced at a distance", "discovered images produced at a distance"]}, {"entity_id": "moser", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Moser", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 5, "variants": ["Moser"], "contexts": ["experimenter with solar spectrum", "experiments on solar spectrum"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Moser", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Moser", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3246, "stable_key": "clu_75fd88b4219b85c8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ludwig Moser", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1765, "death_year": 1837, "description": "Austrian physician and physicist, early photography pioneer.", "wikidata_id": "Q90483856", "wikidata_description": "Austrian composer, organist and teacher"}}, {"canonical_name": "travertine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "travertin", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "travertin", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["travertin"], "contexts": ["stone enclosing human works"]}, {"entity_id": "travertine", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "travertine", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 5, "variants": ["Travertine", "travertine"], "contexts": ["horizontally ranged strata of limestone", "geological material forming before our eyes"]}, {"entity_id": "travertino", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "travertino", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["travertino"], "contexts": ["sedimentary rock type"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "travertin", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "travertine", "similarity": 0.993}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "travertin", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "travertino", "similarity": 0.983}], "id": 3247, "stable_key": "clu_6bc54ca36c22bae9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Travertine", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q187156", "wikidata_description": "form of terrestrial limestone deposited around mineral springs, especially hot springs"}}, {"canonical_name": "Neptune", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "neptune", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Neptune", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Neptune"], "contexts": ["Escalier de Neptune"]}, {"entity_id": "neptune", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Neptune", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 6, "variants": ["Neptune"], "contexts": ["primary planet in the solar system", "planet beyond Uranus"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Neptune", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Neptune", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3248, "stable_key": "clu_78508b11dff76cfc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Neptune", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "given name", "wikipedia_extract": "The Neptunes were an American record production duo composed of Pharrell Williams and Chad Hugo, formed in Virginia Beach, Virginia, in 1992. Williams often provided backing vocals and music video appearances on the duo's productions, while Hugo remained behind the scenes. Since their inception, the Neptunes have produced songs for several music industry artists, including Clipse, Jay-Z, Kelis, N.O.R.E., Britney Spears, Justin Timberlake, Nelly, Ludacris, T.I., Robin Thicke, Usher, Gwen Stefani, and Snoop Dogg, among others. In 2009, Billboard ranked the Neptunes number one on their list of the top 10 producers of the decade. In January 2020, the Neptunes were announced to be inducted into the Songwriters Hall of Fame as a part of the 2020 class, and in May 2021, Hugo and Williams received Honorary Doctorates from Berklee College of Music and Boston Conservatory at Berklee. Williams and Hugo met at a summer camp for the school of the Gifted and Talented in Virginia Beach, Virginia, where Williams played drums and Hugo played tenor saxophone.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Neptunes"}}, {"canonical_name": "nitrate of silver", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 7, "members": [{"entity_id": "nitrate_d'argent", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "nitrate d'argent", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["nitrate d'argent"], "contexts": ["chemical compound"]}, {"entity_id": "nitrate_of_silver", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "nitrate of silver", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 6, "variants": ["nitrate of silver"], "contexts": ["chemical for photographic surface", "chemical for photographic spectrum"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "nitrate d'argent", "target_book": "connexion_physical_s