, "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giambattista%20della%20Porta"}}, {"canonical_name": "Ctesias", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "ctesias", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Ctesias", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 5, "variants": ["Ctesias"], "contexts": ["physician and historian", "ancient writer of fabulous relations"]}, {"entity_id": "ctesias", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Ctesias", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ctesias"], "contexts": ["ancient Greek physician and historian"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Ctesias", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Ctesias", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3332, "stable_key": "clu_c250e6e7561301a0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ctesias", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Ancient Greek physician and historian, author of Indica.", "wikidata_id": "Q5191748", "wikidata_description": "genus of insects"}}, {"canonical_name": "\u00c6gyptians", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "\u00e6gyptians", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "\u00c6gyptians", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 5, "variants": ["\u00c6gyptians"], "contexts": ["ancient people with hieroglyphical doctrine", "ancient people of Egypt"]}, {"entity_id": "\u00e6gyptiorum", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "\u00c6gyptiorum", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["\u00c6gyptiorum"], "contexts": ["origin of secrets"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "\u00c6gyptians", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "\u00c6gyptiorum", "similarity": 0.959}], "id": 3333, "stable_key": "clu_e565c9eea0cfc369-4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Egypt", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "An ancient civilization and geographical location, relevant to medicinal plants and history.", "note": "Includes Portuguese ('Egypto'), French ('Egypte'), and English ('Egypt', 'Egyptian', 'AEgypt').", "wikipedia_extract": "Egypt, officially the Arab Republic of Egypt, is a country spanning the northeast corner of Africa and southwest corner of Asia via the Sinai Peninsula. It is bordered by the Mediterranean Sea to the north, Palestine and Israel to the northeast, the Red Sea to the east, Sudan to the south, and Libya to the west. The Gulf of Aqaba in the northeast separates Egypt from Jordan and Saudi Arabia. Cairo is the capital, largest city, and leading cultural centre, while Alexandria is the second-largest city and an important hub of industry and tourism. With over 107 million inhabitants, Egypt is the third-most populous country in Africa and 15th-most populated in the world. Egypt has one of the longest histories of any country, tracing its heritage along the Nile Delta back to the 6th\u20134th millennia BCE.", "country": "Egypt"}}, {"canonical_name": "Needles", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "needles", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Needles", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 5, "variants": ["Needles"], "contexts": ["pointed metal objects", "used for weighing and navigation"]}, {"entity_id": "needle", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "needle", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["needle"], "contexts": ["part of a compass or galvanometer"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Needles", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "needle", "similarity": 0.928}], "id": 3334, "stable_key": "clu_7200c00343efb8f4-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Needle", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "Q102004561", "wikidata_description": "stout, sharp-pointed piece of metal, wood, or other material, or sharp projection on an object", "note": "Contexts suggest both general needles and surgical needles."}}, {"canonical_name": "Rueus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "rueus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Rueus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 5, "variants": ["Rueus"], "contexts": ["writer on natural history", "author on occult miracles of nature"]}, {"entity_id": "ruelius", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Ruelius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ruelius"], "contexts": ["mentioned in a note by the author"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Rueus", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Ruelius", "similarity": 0.875}], "id": 3335, "stable_key": "clu_f0c2c826618178d4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jean Ruel", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1474, "death_year": 1537, "description": "French physician and botanist, wrote on materia medica.", "note": "The spelling 'Rueus'/'Ruelius' is an older Latinization of Jean Ruel.", "wikidata_id": "Q1592626", "wikidata_description": "French botanist (1474-1537)"}}, {"canonical_name": "Euclide", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "euclide", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Euclide", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Euclide"], "contexts": ["ancient mathematician", "mathematician, example of logical impossibility"]}, {"entity_id": "euclid", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Euclid", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Euclid"], "contexts": ["ancient mathematician"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Euclide", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Euclid", "similarity": 0.859}], "id": 3336, "stable_key": "clu_e0312d635207fcd3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Euclid", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": -300, "death_year": -295, "description": "Ancient Greek mathematician and geometer, author of Elements.", "wikidata_id": "Q1949136", "wikidata_description": "genus of insects", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euclidean_geometry", "wikipedia_extract": "Euclidean geometry is a mathematical system attributed to Euclid, an ancient Greek mathematician, which he described in his textbook on geometry, Elements. Euclid's approach consists in assuming a small set of intuitively appealing axioms (postulates) and deducing many other propositions (theorems) from these. One of those is the parallel postulate which relates to parallel lines on a Euclidean plane. Although many of Euclid's results had been stated earlier, Euclid was the first to organize these propositions into a logical system in which each result is proved from axioms and previously proved theorems. The Elements begins with plane geometry, still taught in secondary school (high school) as the first axiomatic system and the first examples of mathematical proofs. It goes on to the solid geometry of three dimensions."}}, {"canonical_name": "Centaures", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "centaures", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Centaures", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["Centaures"], "contexts": ["mythical creatures", "mythical creature, origin explained by mistake"]}, {"entity_id": "centaur", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Centaur", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["Centaur"], "contexts": ["constellation", "constellation and star for parallax"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Centaures", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Centaur", "similarity": 0.907}], "id": 3337, "stable_key": "clu_3f290b91f45d6a67", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Centaur", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "Refers to mythical creatures.", "wikidata_id": "Q156921", "wikidata_description": "species of flowering plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Centaurea%20cyanus"}}, {"canonical_name": "Ovid", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "ovid", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Ovid", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Ovid"], "contexts": ["Roman poet", "ancient writer"]}, {"entity_id": "ovid", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Ovid", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Ovid"], "contexts": ["Roman poet who described natural phenomenon", "ancient poet describing eruption"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Ovid", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Ovid", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3338, "stable_key": "clu_0d6da417cb9a92c9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ovid", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Roman poet, author of 'Metamorphoses' and known for classical mythology.", "wikipedia_extract": "Publius Ovidius Naso (Latin: [\u02c8pu\u02d0bli\u028as \u0254\u02c8w\u026adi\u028as \u02c8na\u02d0so\u02d0]; 20 March 43 BC \u2013 AD 17/18), known in English as Ovid ( OV-id), was a Roman poet who lived during the reign of Augustus. He was a younger contemporary of Virgil and Horace, with whom he is often ranked as one of the three canonical poets of Latin literature. The Imperial scholar Quintilian considered him the last of the Latin love elegists. Although Ovid enjoyed enormous popularity during his lifetime, the emperor Augustus exiled him to Tomis, the capital of the newly organised province of Moesia, on the Black Sea, where he remained for the last nine or ten years of his life. Ovid himself attributed his banishment to a carmen et error (\"poem and a mistake\"), but his reluctance to disclose specifics has resulted in much speculation among scholars. Ovid is most famous for the Metamorphoses, a continuous mythological narrative in fifteen books written in dactylic hexameters."}}, {"canonical_name": "Aarons", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "aarons", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Aarons", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Aarons"], "contexts": ["biblical figure associated with breast-plate", "brestplate stones"]}, {"entity_id": "aaron", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Aaron", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Aaron"], "contexts": ["biblical figure, wore a breast-plate", "biblical figure"]}, {"entity_id": "aaron", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Aaron", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Aaron"], "contexts": ["city near a battle", "mentioned in relation to death"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Aarons", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Aaron", "similarity": 0.893}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Aaron", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Aaron", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3339, "stable_key": "clu_8de0065e5505ddb9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Aaron", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Biblical high priest, brother of Moses.", "note": "References to 'Aaron' in early modern texts often allude to the biblical figure, sometimes in discussions of symbolic or religious symbolism, or as an authority."}}, {"canonical_name": "St. John", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "st__john", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "St. John", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["St. John"], "contexts": ["saint associated with a church", "church dedication"]}, {"entity_id": "st__john", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "St. John", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 4, "variants": ["St. John"], "contexts": ["saint to whom herb was dedicated", "associated with the herb"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "St. John", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "St. John", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3340, "stable_key": "clu_d3e154ccd527a884", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "John the Baptist", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Biblical figure, patron saint of several entities.", "note": "The term 'St. John' in early modern texts can refer to multiple saints, but in contexts of dedication (like to an herb or church), John the Baptist is the most common referent."}}, {"canonical_name": "Indian", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "indian", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Indian", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Indian"], "contexts": ["Origin of a shining stone", "region associated with cocks"]}, {"entity_id": "indian", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Indian", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Indian"], "contexts": ["related to Sanscrit", "indigenous inhabitant of the Americas"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Indian", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Indian", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3341, "stable_key": "clu_1919c0ea1c3a6d49", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "India", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "India", "wikidata_id": "Q668", "wikidata_description": "country in South Asia", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/India"}}, {"canonical_name": "hog", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "hogs", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Hogs", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Hogs"], "contexts": ["have peculiar vermine", "animal discussed for gall presence"]}, {"entity_id": "hog", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "hog", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["hog"], "contexts": ["domestic pig"]}, {"entity_id": "hogs", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "hogs", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["hogs"], "contexts": ["farm animals raised for meat"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Hogs", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "hog", "similarity": 0.932}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Hogs", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "hogs", "similarity": 0.991}], "id": 3342, "stable_key": "clu_e188b437ae9ba8b0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pig", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "Domesticated animal known for its meat and susceptible to vermin."}}, {"canonical_name": "Ash-tree", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "ash", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Ash", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Ash"], "contexts": ["type of tree", "tree whose shade snakes will not endure"]}, {"entity_id": "ash-tree", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Ash-tree", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Ash-tree", "Ash-Tree", "Ash-tree Tops"], "contexts": ["known for its virtues", "tree resembling Liquorice"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Ash", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Ash-tree", "similarity": 0.892}], "id": 3343, "stable_key": "clu_5cf6272d9c59e589", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ash", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Fraxinus excelsior Linnaeus", "family": "Oleaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q128887", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fraxinus", "wikipedia_extract": "Fraxinus (), commonly called ash, is a genus of plants in the olive and lilac family, Oleaceae, and comprises 45\u201365 species of usually medium-to-large trees, most of which are deciduous (dropping their leaves in autumn), although some subtropical species are evergreen. The genus is widespread throughout much of Europe, Asia, and North America. The leaves are usually opposite, and mostly pinnately compound (divided into leaflets in a feather-like arrangement). The seeds, known as \"keys\", are botanically fruits of the type called samara. Some species are dioecious, having male and female flowers on separate plants. Ash wood is strong and elastic, and used for the handles of tools."}}, {"canonical_name": "Beetles", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "beetles", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Beetles", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Beetles"], "contexts": ["worshipped insects"]}, {"entity_id": "beetles", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Beetles", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 5, "variants": ["Beetles", "beetles"], "contexts": ["insular insects with soldered elytra", "insects with hardened forewings"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Beetles", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Beetles", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3344, "stable_key": "clu_70974a96653b749d-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bees", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "description": "Social insects known for honey production and pollination.", "wikipedia_extract": "Bees are winged insects that form a monophyletic clade Anthophila within the superfamily Apoidea of the order Hymenoptera, with over 20,000 known species in seven recognized families. Some species \u2013 including honey bees, bumblebees, and stingless bees \u2013 are social insects living in highly hierarchical colonies, while over 90% of bee species \u2013 including mason bees, carpenter bees, leafcutter bees, and sweat bees \u2013 are solitary. Members of the most well-known bee genus, Apis (i.e. honey bees), are known to construct hexagonally celled waxy nests called hives. Unlike the closely related wasps and ants, who are carnivorous/omnivorous, bees are herbivores that specifically feed on nectar (nectarivory) and pollen (palynivory), the former primarily as a carbohydrate source for metabolic energy, and the latter primarily for protein and other nutrients for their larvae. They are found on every continent except Antarctica, and in every habitat on the planet that contains insect-pollinated flowering plants. The most common bees in the Northern Hemisphere are the Halictidae, or sweat bees, but they are small and often mistaken for wasps or flies."}}, {"canonical_name": "Trogus Pompeius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "trogus_pompeius", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Trogus Pompeius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Trogus Pompeius"], "contexts": ["ancient historian"]}, {"entity_id": "trogus_pompeius", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Trogus Pompeius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Trogus Pompeius"], "contexts": ["ancient source followed by Justinian", "ancient historian followed by author"]}, {"entity_id": "trogus,_pompeius", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Trogus, Pompeius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Trogus, Pompeius"], "contexts": ["ancient author on volcanoes and generation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Trogus Pompeius", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Trogus Pompeius", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Trogus Pompeius", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Trogus, Pompeius", "similarity": 0.944}], "id": 3345, "stable_key": "clu_23bd8a8c92edc0a0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Trogus Pompeius", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Roman historian whose work on ancient history was abridged by Justin.", "wikidata_id": "Q316119", "wikidata_description": "Gallo-Roman historian who flourished during the reign of the emperor Augustus", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gnaeus%20Pompeius%20Trogus", "wikipedia_extract": "Gnaeus Pompeius Trogus (fl. 1st century BC), also anglicized as Pompey Trogue, was a Gallo-Roman historian from the Celtic Vocontii tribe in Narbonese Gaul who lived during the reign of the emperor Augustus. He was nearly contemporary with Livy. Pompeius Trogus's grandfather served under Pompey in his war against Sertorius. Owing to Pompey's influence, he was able to obtain Roman citizenship and his family adopted their patron's praenomen and nomen Gnaeus Pompeius. Trogus's father served under Julius Caesar as his secretary and interpreter."}}, {"canonical_name": "Pythagoreans", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "pythagoreans", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Pythagoreans", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Pythagoreans"], "contexts": ["ancient philosophical school"]}, {"entity_id": "pythagoreans", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Pythagoreans", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Pythagoreans"], "contexts": ["ancient Greek philosophical school", "studied heavenly bodies and comets"]}, {"entity_id": "pythagorean", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Pythagorean", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Pythagorean"], "contexts": ["relating to Pythagorean philosophy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Pythagoreans", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Pythagoreans", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Pythagoreans", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Pythagorean", "similarity": 0.869}], "id": 3346, "stable_key": "clu_fe9d39b9284741cf", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pythagoreanism", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikipedia_extract": "Pythagoreanism originated in the 6th century BC, based on and around the teachings and beliefs held by Pythagoras and his followers, the Pythagoreans. Pythagoras established the first Pythagorean community in the ancient Greek colony of Croton, in modern Calabria (Italy) circa 530 BC. Early Pythagorean communities spread throughout Magna Graecia. Already during Pythagoras's life it is likely that the distinction between the akousmatikoi (\"those who listen\"), who is conventionally regarded as more concerned with religious, and ritual elements, and associated with the oral tradition, and the mathematikoi (\"those who learn\") existed. The ancient biographers of Pythagoras, Iamblichus (c.\u2009245 \u2013 c. AD 325) and his master Porphyry (c.\u2009234 \u2013 c."}}, {"canonical_name": "Storace", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "RESIN", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "storax", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Storax", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "RESIN", "count": 1, "variants": ["Storax"], "contexts": ["aromatic resin"]}, {"entity_id": "storace", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Storace", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "RESIN", "count": 5, "variants": ["Storace", "Storace calamita", "Storace liquida"], "contexts": ["a resinous substance", "ingredient in a recipe"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Storax", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Storace", "similarity": 0.888}], "id": 3347, "stable_key": "clu_780af9b9b8c44301-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Storax", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "family": "Styracaceae", "note": "Storax is a resin obtained from trees of the genus Styrax. 'Storace calami' likely refers to a specific preparation or source of storax.", "wikidata_id": "Q525062", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Styrax", "wikipedia_extract": "Storace is a surname. Notable people with the surname include: Bernardo Storace (fl.", "description": "Aromatic resin from trees, used in perfumery and medicine."}}, {"canonical_name": "turquesa", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "turchois", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Turchois", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Turchois"], "contexts": ["gemstone"]}, {"entity_id": "turquesa", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "turquesa", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 5, "variants": ["turquesa", "turquesas"], "contexts": ["turquoise, a blue-green gemstone", "stone from Persia"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Turchois", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "turquesa", "similarity": 0.929}], "id": 3348, "stable_key": "clu_59badf797eeee7d6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Turquoise", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral"}}, {"canonical_name": "Guin\u00e9", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "guiny", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Guiny", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Guiny"], "contexts": ["shore location for compass variation"]}, {"entity_id": "guin\u00e9", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Guin\u00e9", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 5, "variants": ["Guin\u00e9"], "contexts": ["country where bananas are found", "place where figuos are found"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Guiny", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Guin\u00e9", "similarity": 0.912}], "id": 3349, "stable_key": "clu_1fba45c2f95ec449", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Guinea", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Guinea-Bissau", "note": "Early modern usage of 'Guinea' could refer to a broader region, including present-day Guinea, Guinea-Bissau, and surrounding areas."}}, {"canonical_name": "sardonix", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "sardius", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Sardius", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Sardius"], "contexts": ["darker stone"]}, {"entity_id": "sardonix", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Sardonix", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Sardonix"], "contexts": ["darker stone"]}, {"entity_id": "sardonix", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "sardonix", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["sardonix", "sardomx"], "contexts": ["type of stone", "precious stone"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Sardius", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "sardonix", "similarity": 0.883}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Sardonix", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "sardonix", "similarity": 0.986}], "id": 3350, "stable_key": "clu_23a2861773f6c6c4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sardonyx", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q3428905", "wikidata_description": "chalcedony variety"}}, {"canonical_name": "Bay-Tree", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "bay-tree", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Bay-tree", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bay-tree"], "contexts": ["tree blasted by lightning"]}, {"entity_id": "bay-tree", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Bay-Tree", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 5, "variants": ["Bay-tree", "Bay-Tree"], "contexts": ["herb dedicated to Apollo", "well-known tree with many virtues"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Bay-tree", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Bay-Tree", "similarity": 0.88}], "id": 3351, "stable_key": "clu_f885710f727800bc-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bay Laurel", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Laurus nobilis Linnaeus", "family": "Lauraceae", "wikidata_id": "Q26006", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laurus_nobilis", "wikipedia_extract": "Laurus nobilis  is an aromatic evergreen tree or large shrub with green, glabrous (smooth) leaves. It is in the flowering plant family Lauraceae. According to  Muer, Jahn, & Sauerbier, the stem can be 1 metre in diameter and the tree can be as high as 20 metres. It is native to the Mediterranean region and is used as bay leaf for seasoning in cooking. Its common names include bay tree (esp. United Kingdom), bay laurel, sweet bay, true laurel, Grecian laurel, or simply laurel.", "note": "The reference to 'Laurel' in Monardes' medicinal history, and its general use in natural philosophy, points strongly to the common laurel (Laurus nobilis). The mention of 'cinnamon tree' leaf shape suggests a comparison or confusion, but the primary referent for 'laurel' remains."}}, {"canonical_name": "Woodpecker", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "woodpecker", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Woodpecker", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["Woodpecker"], "contexts": ["bird that pecks wood"]}, {"entity_id": "woodpecker", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Woodpecker", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 5, "variants": ["Woodpecker", "woodpecker"], "contexts": ["bird with specialized beak and tongue", "example of adaptation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Woodpecker", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Woodpecker", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3352, "stable_key": "clu_36b09dc27d9b0cd6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Woodpecker", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q11774312", "wikidata_description": "bird that climbs up in trees, probes for insects in tree bark and chisels nest holes in deadwood", "wikipedia_extract": "Woodpeckers are part of the bird family Picidae, which also includes the piculets, wrynecks and sapsuckers. Members of this family are found worldwide, except for Australia, New Guinea, New Zealand, Madagascar and the extreme polar regions. Most species live in forests or woodland habitats, although a few species are known that live in treeless areas, such as rocky hillsides and deserts, and the Gila woodpecker specializes in exploiting cacti. Members of this family are chiefly known for the characteristic behaviour that lent them their common name. Their pecking serves mostly to aid their forage for insect prey in the trunks and branches of trees, and also communication which they achieve by drumming trees with their beaks, producing a reverberatory sound that can be heard at some distance. Some species vary their diet with fruits, birds' eggs, small animals, tree sap, human scraps, and carrion."}}, {"canonical_name": "formiguas", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "formiguas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "formiguas", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["formiguas", "formiga", "formigas"], "contexts": ["large ants that flew and produced lacre", "insect mistaken for the producer of lacca"]}, {"entity_id": "formigas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "formigas", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["formigas", "formiga"], "contexts": ["ants that eat an egg", "insects used in a preparation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "formiguas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "formigas", "similarity": 0.948}], "id": 3353, "stable_key": "clu_4e7dea688d47b5ee", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ants", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "description": "Insects, including flying ants and those used in preparations.", "note": "Contexts suggest common ants and possibly specific species known for producing lacre or used medicinally.", "wikipedia_extract": "Ants are eusocial insects of the family Formicidae and, along with the related wasps and bees, belong to the order Hymenoptera. Ants evolved from vespoid wasp ancestors in the Cretaceous period. More than 13,800 of an estimated total of 22,000 species have been classified. They are easily identified by their geniculate (elbowed) antennae and the distinctive node-like structure that forms their slender waists. Ants form colonies that range in size from a few dozen individuals often living in small natural cavities to highly organised colonies that may occupy large territories with a sizeable nest (or nests) that consist of millions of individuals, in some cases they reach hundreds of millions of individuals in super colonies. Typical colonies consist of various castes of sterile, wingless females, most of which are workers (ergates), as well as soldiers (dinergates) and other specialised groups."}}, {"canonical_name": "Medicines", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "medicines", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Medicines", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Medicines"], "contexts": ["Medicines of higher Natures"]}, {"entity_id": "medicines", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Medicines", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 5, "variants": ["Medicines"], "contexts": ["compounds for treating illness", "compounded of herbs, roots, flowers, seeds"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Medicines", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Medicines", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3354, "stable_key": "clu_3773fd7d57f753a1-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Medicine", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the practice of healing and remedies.", "wikidata_id": "Q12140", "wikidata_description": "substance used to diagnose, cure, treat, or prevent disease", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Substance_dependence", "wikipedia_extract": "Substance dependence, also known as drug dependence, is a biopsychological situation whereby an individual's functionality is dependent on the necessitated re-consumption of a psychoactive substance because of an adaptive state that has developed within the individual from psychoactive substance consumption that results in the experience of withdrawal and that necessitates the re-consumption of the drug. A drug addiction, a distinct concept from substance dependence, is defined as compulsive, out-of-control drug use, despite negative consequences. An addictive drug is a drug which is both rewarding and reinforcing. \u0394FosB, a gene transcription factor, which is now known to be a critical component and common factor in the development of virtually all forms of behavioral and drug addictions, but not dependence. The International Classification of Diseases classifies substance dependence as a mental and behavioural disorder. In the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (DSM-5) (released in 2013), substance abuse and substance dependence were eliminated and replaced with the single diagnosis of substance use disorders."}}, {"canonical_name": "phlegm", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "phlegm", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Phlegm", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Phlegm"], "contexts": ["bodily humor"]}, {"entity_id": "phlegm", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "phlegm", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 5, "variants": ["phlegm", "phlegma ponderofum"], "contexts": ["one of the four humors", "bodily humor"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Phlegm", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "phlegm", "similarity": 0.783}], "id": 3355, "stable_key": "clu_1e84256be13afede", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Phlegm", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to one of the four humors in ancient and medieval medicine, corresponding to watery bodily fluids.", "wikidata_id": "Q17185833", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants"}}, {"canonical_name": "pudenda", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "pudendum", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "pudendum", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["pudendum"], "contexts": ["female external genitalia"]}, {"entity_id": "pudenda", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "pudenda", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 5, "variants": ["pudenda"], "contexts": ["genital parts", "genital area that can swell in dropsy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "pudendum", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "pudenda", "similarity": 0.938}], "id": 3356, "stable_key": "clu_3dcb5092751826be", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pudendum", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "A historical term for external genitalia.", "wikidata_id": "Q2192288", "wikidata_description": "external genital organs of the female mammal"}}, {"canonical_name": "cailloux", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "cailloux", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "cailloux", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 5, "variants": ["cailloux"], "contexts": ["small stones", "pebbles"]}, {"entity_id": "cailloux", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "cailloux", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["cailloux"], "contexts": ["pebbles"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "cailloux", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "cailloux", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3357, "stable_key": "clu_15bd60ea411ac539", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pebbles", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q29726105", "wikidata_description": "protein-coding gene in the species Drosophila melanogaster"}}, {"canonical_name": "Seine", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "seine", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "Seine", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 5, "variants": ["Seine"], "contexts": ["river in Paris", "river in France"]}, {"entity_id": "seine", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Seine", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Seine"], "contexts": ["river banks used for longitude determination"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Seine", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Seine", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3358, "stable_key": "clu_c728017f99097282", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Seine", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "France", "wikidata_id": "Q1471", "wikidata_description": "river in France", "wikipedia_url": "http