"seas", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3475, "stable_key": "clu_499fcff971c37769", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Seas", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "note": "Refers to large bodies of saltwater, a common geographical and geological feature discussed in natural philosophy."}}, {"canonical_name": "St. Lawrence", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "st__lawrence", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "St. Lawrence", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 5, "variants": ["St. Lawrence"], "contexts": ["stream of meteors", "associated with August meteor phenomenon"]}, {"entity_id": "river_st__lawrence", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "river St. Lawrence", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["river St. Lawrence"], "contexts": ["frozen river used for travel"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "St. Lawrence", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "river St. Lawrence", "similarity": 0.884}], "id": 3476, "stable_key": "clu_9f1351de674f6267", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "St. Lawrence River", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Canada", "note": "While the cluster mentions 'stream of meteors' and 'luminous sea observation', the primary referent in the context of place names is the St. Lawrence River and its Gulf.", "wikidata_id": "Q134750", "wikidata_description": "major river in eastern Canada and the United States, flowing into the Gulf of St. Lawrence", "wikipedia_extract": "The St. Lawrence River (French: Fleuve Saint-Laurent, pronounced [fl\u0153v s\u025b\u0303 l\u0254\u0281\u0251\u0303]) is a large international river in the middle latitudes of North America connecting the Great Lakes with the North Atlantic Ocean. Its waters flow in a northeasterly direction from Lake Ontario to the Gulf of St. Lawrence, traversing Ontario and Quebec in Canada and New York in the United States. A portion of the river demarcates the Canada\u2013United States border. As the primary drainage outflow of the Great Lakes Basin, the St.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Lawrence_River"}}, {"canonical_name": "geography of plants", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "geography_of_plants", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "geography of plants", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 5, "variants": ["Geography of Plants", "geography of plants"], "contexts": ["study of plant distribution", "study of plant distribution on Earth"]}, {"entity_id": "botanical_geography", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "botanical geography", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["botanical geography"], "contexts": ["science of plant distribution"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "geography of plants", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "botanical geography", "similarity": 0.908}], "id": 3477, "stable_key": "clu_f750de08e03be4ce", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Biogeography", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Specifically refers to the study of the geographical distribution of plants, which falls under the broader field of biogeography."}}, {"canonical_name": "Biela's comet", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "biela's_comet", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Biela's comet", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 5, "variants": ["Biela's comet"], "contexts": ["comet with shortening period", "comet with a period of 6 3/4th years"]}, {"entity_id": "biela\u2019s_comets", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Biela\u2019s Comets", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Biela\u2019s Comets"], "contexts": ["a specific comet"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Biela's comet", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Biela\u2019s Comets", "similarity": 0.941}], "id": 3478, "stable_key": "clu_8c7c126100861a2a-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Biela's Comet", "confidence": "medium", "type": "concept", "note": "The cluster contains references to 'Biela' and 'Biela\u2019s', which clearly refer to Biela's Comet. However, it also includes 'Beagle', the ship Darwin sailed on. The strong presence of 'Biela' and the context of celestial objects in Humboldt's 'Kosmos' point to the comet as the primary referent, with 'Beagle' being a misattribution or secondary mention within the texts analyzed.", "wikipedia_extract": "Biela's Comet or Comet Biela (official designation: 3D/Biela) was a periodic Jupiter-family comet first recorded in 1772 by Montaigne and Messier and finally identified as periodic in 1826 by Wilhelm von Biela. It was subsequently observed to split in two and has not been seen since 1852. As a result, it is currently considered to have been destroyed, although remnants have survived for some time as a meteor shower, the Andromedids. The comet was first recorded on 8 March 1772 by Jacques Leibax Montaigne; during the same apparition it was independently discovered by Charles Messier. It was also recorded in 1805 by Jean-Louis Pons, but was not recognized as the same object. Around 9 December 1805 comet Biela passed about 0.0366 AU (5.48 million km) from Earth."}}, {"canonical_name": "Benzenberg", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "benzenberg", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Benzenberg", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 5, "variants": ["Benzenberg"], "contexts": ["author of 'Treatise on Shooting Stars'", "ascertained heights and velocity of shooting stars"]}, {"entity_id": "benzenberg", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Benzenberg", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Benzenberg"], "contexts": ["observed heights and velocities of falling stars"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Benzenberg", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Benzenberg", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3479, "stable_key": "clu_9f601b262895bb18", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Johann Friedrich Benzenberg", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1777, "death_year": 1845, "description": "German physicist who studied meteors and shooting stars.", "wikidata_id": "Q75088", "wikidata_description": "German scientist"}}, {"canonical_name": "botany", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "botany", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "botany", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["botany"], "contexts": ["scientific pursuit", "study of plants"]}, {"entity_id": "bot\u00e2nica", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "bot\u00e2nica", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["bot\u00e2nica", "bot\u00e2nico"], "contexts": ["study of plants", "field of study"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "botany", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "bot\u00e2nica", "similarity": 0.955}], "id": 3480, "stable_key": "clu_5f6e6f5d5f00d249", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Botany", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q441", "wikidata_description": "science of plant life", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Botany", "wikipedia_extract": "Botany, also called phytology or plant science, is the branch of natural science and biology that studies plants, especially their anatomy, taxonomy, and ecology. A botanist or plant scientist is a scientist who specialises in this field. \"Plant\" and \"botany\" may be defined more narrowly to include only land plants and their study, which is also known as phytology. Phytologists or botanists (in the strict sense) study approximately 410,000 species of land plants, including some 391,000 species of vascular plants (of which approximately 369,000 are flowering plants) and approximately 20,000 bryophytes. Botany originated as prehistoric herbalism to identify and later cultivate plants that were edible, poisonous, and medicinal, making it one of the first endeavours of human investigation. Medieval physic gardens, often attached to monasteries, contained plants that possibly had medicinal benefits."}}, {"canonical_name": "Schelling", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "schelling", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Schelling", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Schelling"], "contexts": ["philosopher quoted on nature's creative force", "author of 'Bruno'"]}, {"entity_id": "schelling", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Schelling", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Schelling"], "contexts": ["philosopher who enunciated a theory of life", "defined Life as tendency to individuation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Schelling", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Schelling", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3481, "stable_key": "clu_7fdd1566fd5246e3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Friedrich Wilhelm Joseph Schelling", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1775, "death_year": 1854, "description": "German idealist philosopher influential in Romanticism and Idealism.", "wikidata_id": "Q60070", "wikidata_description": "German philosopher (1775\u20131854)"}}, {"canonical_name": "Phillips", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "phillips", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Phillips", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Phillips"], "contexts": ["contributor to Philos. Mag.", "researcher at Monk-wearmouth coal-mine"]}, {"entity_id": "phillips", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Phillips", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Phillips"], "contexts": ["commented on Zayt\u00fan's location", "reflections on location"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Phillips", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Phillips", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3482, "stable_key": "clu_c37f7677071ae90f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "John Phillips", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1800, "death_year": 1874, "description": "British geologist, paleontologist, and meteorologist"}}, {"canonical_name": "Fiedler", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "fiedler", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Fiedler", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Fiedler"], "contexts": ["author of travelogue on Greece", "author of 'Reise durch Griechenland'"]}, {"entity_id": "fiedler", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Fiedler", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Fiedler"], "contexts": ["exhibited fulgorites in London", "exhibited fulgorites"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Fiedler", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Fiedler", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3483, "stable_key": "clu_d2ca3dc934f7a483", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Karl Fiedler", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "German explorer and naturalist, author of a travelogue on Greece", "note": "The specific Karl Fiedler is inferred from the context of travelogue and fulgorites; multiple individuals may share the name."}}, {"canonical_name": "chemical analysis", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "chemical_analysis", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "chemical analysis", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["chemical analysis"], "contexts": ["instrument of chemical analysis", "method for studying atmosphere"]}, {"entity_id": "chemical_analyses", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "chemical analyses", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["chemical analyses"], "contexts": ["method for analyzing substances"]}, {"entity_id": "chemical_analysis", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "chemical analysis", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["chemical analysis"], "contexts": ["method of determining composition", "method for determining molecular constitution"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "chemical analysis", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "chemical analysis", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "chemical analyses", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "chemical analysis", "similarity": 0.939}], "id": 3484, "stable_key": "clu_54f5aa9e99547c86", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Chemical analysis", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q9150363", "wikidata_description": "process of analyzing the chemical composition of a material"}}, {"canonical_name": "Bonn", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "bonn", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Bonn", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Bonn"], "contexts": ["location of tree trunk find", "city in Germany"]}, {"entity_id": "bonn", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Bonn", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Bonn"], "contexts": ["city where Professor Helmholtz was", "German city, location of university"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Bonn", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Bonn", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3485, "stable_key": "clu_0b9096ba5d56ff56", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bonn", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Germany", "note": "Directly refers to the city of Bonn in Germany.", "wikipedia_extract": "The University of Bonn, officially the Rhenish Friedrich Wilhelm University of Bonn (German: Rheinische Friedrich-Wilhelms-Universit\u00e4t Bonn), is a public research university in Bonn, North Rhine-Westphalia, Germany. It was founded in its present form as the Rhein-Universit\u00e4t (English: Rhine University) on 18 October 1818 by Frederick William III, as the linear successor of the Kurk\u00f6lnische Akademie Bonn (English: Academy of the Prince-elector of Cologne) which was founded in 1777. The University of Bonn offers many undergraduate and graduate programs in a range of subjects and has 544 professors. The University of Bonn is a member of the German U15 association of major research-intensive universities in Germany and has the title of \"University of Excellence\" under the German Universities Excellence Initiative. Bonn has 8 Clusters of Excellence, the most of any German university; the Hausdorff Center for Mathematics, the Matter and Light for Quantum Computing cluster, Bonn Center for Dependency and Slavery Studies,  PhenoRob: Research for the Future of Crop Production, the Immune Sensory System cluster, ECONtribute: Markets and Public Policy, Color meets Flavor and Our Dynamic Universe. The University and State Library Bonn (ULB Bonn) is the central university and archive library of the Rheinische Friedrich-Wilhelms-Universit\u00e4t Bonn and North Rhine-Westphalia; it holds more than five million volumes.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Bonn"}}, {"canonical_name": "Molecular attraction", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "molecular_attraction", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "molecular attraction", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["molecular attraction"], "contexts": ["force between molecules"]}, {"entity_id": "molecular_attraction", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Molecular attraction", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["molecular attraction", "Molecular attraction"], "contexts": ["attractive force between molecules", "force within substances affected by temperature"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "molecular attraction", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Molecular attraction", "similarity": 0.973}], "id": 3486, "stable_key": "clu_b0a8c2fc6f41d0ea", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Molecular attraction", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q71931701", "wikidata_description": "journal article; published in Nature on 1916-6"}}, {"canonical_name": "Tangential force", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "tangential_force", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "tangential force", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["tangential force"], "contexts": ["force acting perpendicular to radius"]}, {"entity_id": "tangential_force", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Tangential force", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Tangential force", "tangential force", "Tangential Force"], "contexts": ["force acting along the orbit", "force acting along the tangent of an orbit"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "tangential force", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Tangential force", "similarity": 0.982}], "id": 3487, "stable_key": "clu_b04aa433f0940855", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tangential force", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q91740473", "wikidata_description": "force component tangential to a curve or parallel to a (imaginary) surface"}}, {"canonical_name": "solar atmosphere", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 6, "members": [{"entity_id": "solar_atmosphere", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "solar atmosphere", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["solar atmosphere"], "contexts": ["outer layer of the Sun", "outer layers of the sun"]}, {"entity_id": "solar_atmosphere", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "solar atmosphere", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 4, "variants": ["solar atmosphere"], "contexts": ["visible during eclipses", "gaseous envelope of the sun"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "solar atmosphere", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "solar atmosphere", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3488, "stable_key": "clu_d9