me": "philosophique", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "philosophi", "similarity": 0.929}], "id": 3541, "stable_key": "clu_a29efb545be80340-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Philosophy", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q5891", "wikidata_description": "study of the truths and principles of being, schools of thought, knowledge, or conduct", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philosophy", "note": "Used in the context of academic disciplines and the study of fundamental questions.", "wikipedia_extract": "Philosophy (from Ancient Greek philosoph\u00eda lit. 'love of wisdom') is a systematic study of general and fundamental questions concerning topics like existence, knowledge, mind, reason, language, and value. It is a rational and critical inquiry that reflects on its methods and assumptions. Historically, many of the individual sciences, such as physics and psychology, formed part of philosophy. However, they are considered separate academic disciplines in the modern sense of the term. Influential traditions in the history of philosophy include Western, Arabic\u2013Persian, Indian, and Chinese philosophy."}}, {"canonical_name": "logic", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "logique", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "logique", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["logique"], "contexts": ["principles of valid reasoning"]}, {"entity_id": "logic", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "logic", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["logic"], "contexts": ["principles of valid reasoning", "system of reasoning"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "logique", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "logic", "similarity": 0.966}], "id": 3542, "