james_1890", "target_name": "rational", "similarity": 0.897}], "id": 3550, "stable_key": "clu_64d8a64bd668f3ab-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "rationality", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q938185", "wikidata_description": "quality of being agreeable to reason"}}, {"canonical_name": "Fantasia", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "fantaisie", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "fantaisie", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["fantaisie"], "contexts": ["unfounded medical ideas"]}, {"entity_id": "fantasia", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Fantasia", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Fantasia", "fantasia", "fanta\u017fia"], "contexts": ["faculty of imagination", "resides in the head"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "fantaisie", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Fantasia", "similarity": 0.966}], "id": 3551, "stable_key": "clu_ee42482851d1cba5-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "imagination", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q91048696", "wikidata_description": "vocal track by Bob Dylan; 2017 studio recording; cover version", "wikipedia_extract": "Sociological imagination is a term used in the field of sociology to describe a framework for understanding social reality that places personal experiences within a broader social and historical context. It was coined by American sociologist C. Wright Mills in his 1959 book The Sociological Imagination to describe the type of insight offered by the discipline of sociology. Today, the term is used in many sociology textbooks to explain the nature of sociology and its relevance in daily life. In The Sociological Imagination, Mills attempts to reconcile two different and abstract concepts of social reality: the \"individual\" and the \"society.\" Accordingly, Mills defined sociological imagination as \"the awareness of the relationship between personal experience and the wider society.\" In exercising one's sociological imagination, one seeks to understand situations in one's life by looking at situations in broader society. For example, a single student who fails to keep up with the academic demands of college and ends up dropping out may be perceived to have faced personal difficulties or faults; however, when one considers that around 50% of college students in the United States fail to graduate, we can understand this one student's trajectory as part of a larger social issue.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sociological_imagination"}}, {"canonical_name": "Idolatry", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "idolatry", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Idolatry", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Idolatry"], "contexts": ["worship of idols", "worship of false deities or idols"]}, {"entity_id": "idolatrous", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Idolatrous", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Idolatrous"], "contexts": ["description of Heathens and Papists"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Idolatry", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Idolatrous", "similarity": 0.889}], "id": 3552, "stable_key": "clu_327aa738e043a858", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Idolatry", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q840665", "wikidata_description": "idol worship", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Idolatry", "wikipedia_extract": "Idolatry or idol worship is the worship of an idol as though it were a deity. In Abrahamic religions (namely Judaism, Christianity, and Islam) idolatry connotes the worship of something or someone other than the Abrahamic God as if it were God. In these monotheistic religions, idolatry has been considered as the \"worship of false gods\" and is forbidden by texts such as the Ten Commandments. Other monotheistic religions may apply similar rules. For instance, the phrase false god is a derogatory term used in Abrahamic religions to indicate cult images or deities of non-Abrahamic Pagan religions, as well as other competing entities or objects to which particular importance is attributed. Conversely, followers of animistic and polytheistic religions may regard the gods of various monotheistic religions as \"false gods\" because they do not believe that any real deity possesses the properties ascribed by monotheists to their sole deity."}}, {"canonical_name": "Julian", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "julian", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Julian", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 4, "variants": ["Julian"], "contexts": ["Roman Emperor, known as the Apostate", "Roman Emperor"]}, {"entity_id": "julian_", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Julian.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Julian."], "contexts": ["ancient authority"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Julian", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Julian.", "similarity": 0.787}], "id": 3553, "stable_key": "clu_74e472f97a3f3a2f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Julian the Apostate", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 331, "death_year": 363, "description": "Roman Emperor, known as the Apostate"}}, {"canonical_name": "Empedocles", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "empedocles", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Empedocles", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Empedocles"], "contexts": ["ancient Greek pre-Socratic philosopher", "philosopher whose assertions were rejected by Aristotle"]}, {"entity_id": "empedocles", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Empedocles", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Empedocles"], "contexts": ["early poet of nature"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Empedocles", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Empedocles", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3554, "stable_key": "clu_bbdcf1369be24245", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Empedocles", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": -494, "death_year": -434, "description": "Ancient Greek pre-Socratic philosopher and poet.", "wikipedia_extract": "Empedocles (; Ancient Greek: \u1f18\u03bc\u03c0\u03b5\u03b4\u03bf\u03ba\u03bb\u1fc6\u03c2; c.\u2009494 \u2013 c.\u2009434 BC, fl. 444\u2013443 BC) was a Greek pre-Socratic philosopher and a native citizen of Akragas, a Greek city in Sicily. Empedocles' philosophy is known best for originating the cosmogonic theory of the four classical elements. He also proposed forces he called Love and Strife which would mix and separate the elements, respectively. Empedocles challenged the practice of animal sacrifice and killing animals for food. He developed a distinctive doctrine of reincarnation. He is generally considered the last Greek philosopher to have recorded his ideas in verse."}}, {"canonical_name": "Athenians", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "athenians", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Athenians", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 4, "variants": ["Athenians"], "contexts": ["inhabitants of Athens", "ancient people who believed nation originated from Earth"]}, {"entity_id": "athenians", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Athenians", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Athenians"], "contexts": ["people St. Paul addressed"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Athenians", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Athenians", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3555, "stable_key": "clu_39dea045a199a020", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Athenians", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the people of Athens, not a specific historical person or group.", "wikidata_id": "Q203263", "wikidata_description": "consolidated city-county and county seat of Clarke County, Georgia, United States"}}, {"canonical_name": "Orpheus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "orpheus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Orpheus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 4, "variants": ["Orpheus"], "contexts": ["subject of a fable about his harp", "mythical musician with magical harp"]}, {"entity_id": "orpheus", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Orpheus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Orpheus"], "contexts": ["ancient authority"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Orpheus", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Orpheus", "similarity": 0.828}], "id": 3556, "stable_key": "clu_f4a7843cce27ae81", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Orpheus", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Mythological musician and poet in ancient Greek religion", "wikidata_id": "Q137908594", "wikidata_description": "genus of birds"}}, {"canonical_name": "Augustus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "augustus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Augustus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 4, "variants": ["Augustus"], "contexts": ["first Roman emperor", "Roman emperor who found galls in sacrifice"]}, {"entity_id": "augustus", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Augustus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Augustus"], "contexts": ["Roman emperor, time of marble quarrying"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Augustus", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Augustus", "similarity": 0.842}], "id": 3557, "stable_key": "clu_336652f845792879", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Augustus", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": -63, "death_year": 14, "description": "First Roman emperor, founder of the Roman Principate", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}}, {"canonical_name": "Cocks", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "cocks", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Cocks", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 4, "variants": ["Cocks"], "contexts": ["fail in Cocks and Hens", "male domestic fowl"]}, {"entity_id": "cocks", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "cocks", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["cocks"], "contexts": ["male of domestic fowl"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Cocks", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "cocks", "similarity": 0.895}], "id": 3558, "stable_key": "clu_3d3a23306df55861", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Roosters", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q688973", "wikidata_description": "heraldic animal"}}, {"canonical_name": "Scripture", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "scripture", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Scripture", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Scripture"], "contexts": ["religious text containing accounts", "religious texts"]}, {"entity_id": "scripture", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Scripture", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Scripture"], "contexts": ["basis for sermons", "religious text cited"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Scripture", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Scripture", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3559, "stable_key": "clu_3f7b746f809ca413", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Scripture", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}}, {"canonical_name": "Macrobius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "macrobius", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Macrobius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Macrobius"], "contexts": ["Roman writer", "Roman writer and commentator"]}, {"entity_id": "macrobius", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Macrobius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Macrobius"], "contexts": ["Roman writer", "ancient Roman author"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Macrobius", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Macrobius", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3560, "stable_key": "clu_79b3ada4fc4cf8f4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Macrobius", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Roman writer and commentator on Cicero and Virgil.", "wikidata_id": "Q313934", "wikidata_description": "ancient Roman author (5th century)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Servius_the_Grammarian", "wikipedia_extract": "Servius, distinguished as Servius the Grammarian (Latin: Servius or Seruius Grammaticus), was a late fourth-century and early fifth-century grammarian. He earned a contemporary reputation as the most learned man of his generation in Italy. He authored a set of commentaries on the works of Virgil; these works, In Tria Virgilii Opera Expositio (\"Exposition on Three Works of Virgil\"), Commentarii in Virgilium (\"Commentaries on Virgil\"), Commentarii in Vergilii Opera (\"Commentaries on the Works of Vergil\"), or Vergilii Carmina Commentarii (\"Commentaries on the Poems of Virgil\"), constituted the first incunable to be printed at Florence, by Bernardo Cennini, in 1471. In the Saturnalia of Macrobius, Servius appears as one of the interlocutors; allusions in that work and a letter from Symmachus to Servius indicate that he was not a convert to Christianity. The name Servius also appears as Seruius owing to the unity of the Latin letters V and U from antiquity until as late as the 18th century. Many medieval manuscripts of Servius's commentaries give him the praenomen Marius or Maurus and the cognomen Honoratus."}}, {"canonical_name": "Brimstone", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "brimstone", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Brimstone", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Brimstone"], "contexts": ["sulfur, used for purification", "ingredient of gunpowder"]}, {"entity_id": "brimstone", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Brimstone", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Brimstone"], "contexts": ["mineral mixed with juice", "used with Darnel meal to dissolve knots"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Brimstone", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Brimstone", "similarity": 0.926}], "id": 3561, "stable_key": "clu_2bd325ed697ebcd2-4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sulfur", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "note": "Brimstone is an archaic term for sulfur.", "wikidata_id": "Q1157552", "wikidata_description": "mineral (as opposed to the chemical element)", "wikipedia_extract": "Sulfuric acid (American spelling and the preferred IUPAC name) or sulphuric acid (Commonwealth spelling), known in antiquity as oil of vitriol, is a mineral acid composed of the elements sulfur, oxygen, and hydrogen, with the molecular formula H2SO4. It is a colorless, odorless, and viscous liquid that is miscible with water. Pure sulfuric acid does not occur naturally due to its strong affinity to water vapor; it is hygroscopic and readily absorbs water vapor from the air. Concentrated sulfuric acid is a strong oxidant with powerful dehydrating properties, making it highly corrosive towards other materials, from rocks to metals. Phosphorus pentoxide is a notable exception in that it is not dehydrated by sulfuric acid but, to the contrary, dehydrates sulfuric acid to sulfur trioxide. Upon addition of sulfuric acid to water, a considerable amount of heat is released; thus, the reverse procedure of adding water to the acid is generally avoided since the heat released may boil the solution, spraying droplets of hot acid during the process.", "wikipedia_url": "https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soufre_%28min%C3%A9ral%29"}}, {"canonical_name": "Stars", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "stars", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Stars", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Stars"], "contexts": ["celestial bodies", "celestial bodies considered living"]}, {"entity_id": "stars", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Stars", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Stars"], "contexts": ["look up as high as the stars", "influence on herbs and body"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Stars", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Stars", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3562, "stable_key": "clu_f0a2b1770ab26842", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "stars", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "While the prompt has 'ANIMAL' as the type for 'stars', contextually they are celestial bodies, best represented as an 'object'."}}, {"canonical_name": "Amethyst", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "amethyst", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Amethyst", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["Amethyst"], "contexts": ["mineral with angular figures", "stone to prevent inebriation"]}, {"entity_id": "amethyst", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "amethyst", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["amethyst"], "contexts": ["gemstone transmitting chemical rays", "gemstone composed of quartz layers"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Amethyst", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "amethyst", "similarity": 0.914}], "id": 3563, "stable_key": "clu_8f0ecd389a7dab0f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Amethyst", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q79058", "wikidata_description": "mineral of quartz variety"}}, {"canonical_name": "Moly", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "moly", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Moly", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Moly"], "contexts": ["magical herb from Homer's Odyssey", "magical herb mentioned by Homer"]}, {"entity_id": "moly", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Moly", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Moly", "molo"], "contexts": ["identified as a type of wild rue", "herb used in preparations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Moly", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Moly", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3564, "stable_key": "clu_9c108f8a0e251760", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Moly", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "note": "The exact botanical identification of Moly is uncertain, though it's often associated with *Peganum harmala* or certain *Ruta* species.", "wikipedia_extract": "Allium moly, also known as yellow garlic, golden garlic and lily leek, Is a species of flowering plant in the genus Allium, which also includes the flowering and culinary onions and garlic. A bulbous herbaceous perennial from the Mediterranean. It is edible and used as a medicinal and ornamental plant. Allium moly is a bulb type flower, with the size of the bulb affecting the flowering period and the time of the flowering. With lance-shaped grey-green leaves up to 30 cm long, in early summer it produces masses of star-shaped bright yellow flowers in dense umbels. The cultivar 'Jeannine' has gained the Royal Horticultural Society's Award of Garden Merit.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Allium_moly"}}, {"canonical_name": "quadrupedes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "quadrupedes", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "quadrupedes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["Quadrupedes", "quadrupedes"], "contexts": ["animals with four legs", "four-legged animals with speech advantage"]}, {"entity_id": "quadrupeds", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "quadrupeds", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["quadrupeds"], "contexts": ["four-footed animals", "animals with four feet"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "quadrupedes", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "quadrupeds", "similarity": 0.96}], "id": 3565, "stable_key": "clu_6108ffa24179d595-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Quadruped", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "The terms 'quadrupedes', 'quadrumana', and 'quadrupeds' all refer to four-limbed animals, with 'quadrumana' being a specific subgroup (primates with grasping feet).", "wikipedia_extract": "A tripod stance is a behaviour in which quadruped animals rear up on their hind legs and use their tail to support this position. Several animals use this behaviour to improve observation or surveillance, and during feeding, grooming, thermoregulation, or fighting. The common dwarf mongoose (Helogale parvula) adopts a tripod stance when being vigilant for predators. In a similar mammal, the thirteen-lined ground squirrel (Ictidomys tridecemlineatus), vigilance behaviour includes four postures: (1) quadrupedal alert (all four feet on the ground with head above the horizontal); (2) semiupright alert (on hind feet with a distinctive slouch); (3) upright alert (on hind feet with back straight and mostly perpendicular to the ground); (4) extended upright alert (similar to upright alert except that the squirrel extends its hind legs, (see image of meerkats). In meerkats (Suricata suricatta), the tripod stance may be adopted relatively briefly during foraging, in which case it is termed guarding behaviour, or for substantially longer periods when the animal is not foraging, in which case it is termed sentinel behaviour. Macropods can stand erect on their hind legs, supported by their tail as the third leg of the tripod.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tripod_stance"}}, {"canonical_name": "Nation", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "nations", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Nations", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Nations"], "contexts": ["political and geographical entities", "collectivity that should disclaim falsehood"]}, {"entity_id": "nation", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Nation", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Nation"], "contexts": ["England", "geographic location of the described countries"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Nations", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Nation", "similarity": 0.936}], "id": 3566, "stable_key": "clu_da826a1d4ac64904-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Nation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'nation' and 'country' are used here to refer to political and geographic entities, often in discussions of species distribution or societal structures.", "wikidata_id": "Q3624078", "wikidata_description": "state that has the highest authority over a territory", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sovereign%20state", "wikipedia_extract": "A sovereign state is a state that has the highest authority over a territory. It is commonly understood that a sovereign state is independent. When referring to a specific polity, the term \"country\" may also refer to a constituent country, or a dependent territory. A sovereign state is required to have a permanent population, defined territory, a government not under another, and the capacity to interact with other sovereign states. In actual practice, recognition or non-recognition by other states plays an important role in determining the status of a country. Although the political existence of the sovereign state is independent of recognition by the other states, Unrecognized states often have difficulty engaging in diplomatic relations with other sovereign states."}}, {"canonical_name": "mule", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "mule", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Mule", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Mule"], "contexts": ["Animal whose skin is used in a recipe", "animal with a long lifespan"]}, {"entity_id": "mule", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "mule", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["mule"], "contexts": ["hybrid of ass and horse", "example of a healthy and long-lived hybrid"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Mule", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "mule", "similarity": 0.794}], "id": 3567, "stable_key": "clu_118bedc406973fc1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "mule", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "description": "Hybrid offspring of a male donkey and a female horse.", "wikipedia_extract": "The mule is a domestic equine hybrid between a donkey and a horse. It is the offspring of a male donkey (a jack) and a female horse (a mare). The horse and the donkey are different species, with different numbers of chromosomes; of the two possible first-generation hybrids between them, the mule is easier to obtain and more common than the hinny, which is the offspring of a male horse (a stallion) and a female donkey (a jenny). Mules vary widely in size, and may be of any color seen in horses or donkeys. They are more patient, hardier and longer-lived than horses, and are perceived as less obstinate and more intelligent than donkeys. A female mule is called a molly, a Molly mule, or more formally mare mule.", "wikidata_description": "family name"}}, {"canonical_name": "dysenterias", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "dysenteries", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "dysenteries", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 2, "variants": ["dysenteries"], "contexts": ["disease treated with Crystal", "intestinal inflammation"]}, {"entity_id": "dysenterias", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "dysenterias", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 3, "variants": ["dysenterias", "Dysenterias", "dysenteriam"], "contexts": ["bloody flux", "dysentery"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "dysenteries", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "dysenterias", "similarity": 0.987}], "id": 3568, "stable_key": "clu_a9491023402c61fb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Dysentery", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "An intestinal inflammation causing severe diarrhea with blood and mucus.", "wikipedia_extract": "Dysentery (UK: , US: ), historically known as the bloody flux, is a type of gastroenteritis that results in bloody diarrhea. Other symptoms may include fever, abdominal pain, and a feeling of incomplete defecation. Complications may include dehydration. The cause of dysentery is usually the bacteria from genus Shigella, in which case it is known as shigellosis, or the amoeba Entamoeba histolytica; then it is called amoebiasis. Other causes may include certain chemicals, other bacteria, other protozoa, or parasitic worms. It may spread between people."}}, {"canonical_name": "Hematites", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "h\u00e6matites", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "H\u00e6matites", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["H\u00e6matites"], "contexts": ["iron ore, adheres to lips", "iron ore with medicinal properties"]}, {"entity_id": "hematites", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Hematites", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["Hematites"], "contexts": ["stone used in a remedy", "hematite, a mineral used in a remedy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "H\u00e6matites", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Hematites", "similarity": 0.964}], "id": 3569, "stable_key": "clu_5cda17cbd75a7690", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hematite", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "description": "A common iron oxide mineral, used in remedies and as a pigment.", "note": "While 'pegmatite' is a rock type, 'hematites' clearly points to the mineral hematite.", "wikipedia_extract": "Hematite (), also spelled as haematite, is a common iron oxide compound with the formula Fe2O3 and is widely found in rocks and soils. Hematite crystals belong to the rhombohedral lattice system which is designated the alpha polymorph of Fe2O3. It has the same crystal structure as corundum (Al2O3) and ilmenite (FeTiO3). With this crystal structure geometry it forms a complete solid solution at temperatures above 950 \u00b0C (1,740 \u00b0F). Hematite occurs naturally in black to steel or silver-gray, brown to reddish-brown, or red colors. It is mined as an important ore mineral of iron."}}, {"canonical_name": "flies", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "flies", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Flies", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Flies"], "contexts": ["insects found in amber", "insects that maggots turn into"]}, {"entity_id": "flies", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "flies", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 3, "variants": ["flies"], "contexts": ["insects attacking cattle", "insects destroying horses"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Flies", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "flies", "similarity": 0.91}], "id": 3570, "stable_key": "clu_40d9fa245d9a5b18-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Fly", "confidence": "low", "type": "animal", "note": "The context 'creatures that produce pearls' is highly unusual for flies and suggests a possible misidentification or specific folklore. The 'moUuscos' is likely an OCR error for 'mosquitos' or similar insect, but given the context of pearls, it's highly ambiguous.", "wikidata_id": "Q166111", "wikidata_description": "species of insect in the class \"Muscidae\", sort of fly", "wikipedia_extract": "Flies are insects of the order Diptera, the name being derived from Ancient Greek  \u03b4\u03b9- (di-) 'two' and  \u03c0\u03c4\u03b5\u03c1\u03cc\u03bd (pteron) 'wing'. Insects of this order use only a single pair of wings to fly, the hindwings having evolved into advanced mechanosensory organs known as halteres, which act as high-speed sensors of rotational movement and allow dipterans to perform advanced aerobatics. Diptera is a large order containing more than 150,000 species including horse-flies, crane flies, hoverflies, mosquitoes and others. Flies have a mobile head, with a pair of large compound eyes, and mouthparts designed for piercing and sucking (mosquitoes, black flies and robber flies), or for lapping and sucking in the other groups. Their wing arrangement gives them great manoeuvrability in flight, and claws and pads on their feet enable them to cling to smooth surfaces. Flies undergo complete metamorphosis; the eggs are often laid on the larval food-source and the larvae, which lack true limbs, develop in a protected environment, often inside their food source.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Housefly"}}, {"canonical_name": "sal Armoniaco", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "sal_armoniac", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Sal Armoniac", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Sal Armoniac"], "contexts": ["ammonium chloride, added to gunpowder", "chemical substance, debated reaction with Tartar"]}, {"entity_id": "sal_armoniaco", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "sal Armoniaco", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["sal Armoniaco"], "contexts": ["ingredient in a preparation", "substance with unpleasant smell when mixed"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Sal Armoniac", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "sal Armoniaco", "similarity": 0.884}], "id": 3571, "stable_key": "clu_4a028041d500e56c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ammonium chloride", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "wikidata_id": "Q188543", "wikidata_description": "chemical compound"}}, {"canonical_name": "cows", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "cow", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Cow", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cow"], "contexts": ["female bovine animal"]}, {"entity_id": "cows", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Cows", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cows"], "contexts": ["given mistletoe decoction for after-birth"]}, {"entity_id": "cows", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "cows", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["cows"], "contexts": ["udders developed by milking", "domestic cows with developed teats"]}, {"entity_id": "cow", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "cow", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["cow"], "contexts": ["domestic animal for milk and meat"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Cow", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "cows", "similarity": 0.969}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Cow", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "cow", "similarity": 0.996}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Cows", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "cows", "similarity": 0.996}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Cows", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "cow", "similarity": 0.966}], "id": 3572, "stable_key": "clu_af75f1d6049fe0e9-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cattle", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "The Spanish 'ganado' directly translates to 'livestock' or 'cattle' in this context.", "wikidata_id": "Q133733", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cattleya", "wikipedia_extract": "Cattleya () is a genus of orchids from Costa Rica south to Argentina. The genus is abbreviated C in trade journals. Epiphytic or terrestrial orchids with cylindrical rhizome from which the fleshy noodle-like roots grow. Pseudobulbs can be conical, spindle-shaped or cylindrical; with upright growth; one or two leaves growing from the top of them. The leaves can be oblong, lanceolate or elliptical, somewhat fleshy, with smooth margin. The inflorescence is a terminal raceme with few or several flowers."}}, {"canonical_name": "Philosophy of nature", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "natural_philosophy", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Natural Philosophy", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Natural Philosophy"], "contexts": ["study of the natural world"]}, {"entity_id": "philosophy_of_nature", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Philosophy of nature", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Philosophy of Nature", "philosophy of Nature", "philosophy of nature", "Philosophy of nature"], "contexts": ["discipline studying the natural world", "study of the natural world's principles"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Natural Philosophy", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Philosophy of nature", "similarity": 0.942}], "id": 3573, "stable_key": "clu_dc6b7284fcd174a1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "natural philosophy", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q17791731", "wikidata_description": "university building in City of Edinburgh, Scotland, UK"}}, {"canonical_name": "sue\u00f1o", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "sue\u00f1o", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "sue\u00f1o", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["sue\u00f1o"], "contexts": ["induced by plant root juice", "state of sleep"]}, {"entity_id": "sonho", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "sonho", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sonho"], "contexts": ["dream"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "sue\u00f1o", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "sonho", "similarity": 0.949}], "id": 3574, "stable_key": "clu_891f4ba763ba6f95", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "A dream is a succession of images, dynamic scenes and situations, ideas, emotions, and sensations that usually occur involuntarily in the mind during certain stages of sleep. Humans spend more than two hours dreaming per night, and each dream lasts around 5\u201320 minutes. The content and function of dreams have been topics of scientific, philosophical and religious interest throughout recorded history. Dream interpretation, practiced by the Babylonians in the third millennium BCE and even earlier by the ancient Sumerians, figures prominently in religious texts in several traditions, and has played a lead role in psychotherapy. Dreamwork is similar, but does not seek to conclude with definite meaning. The scientific study of dreams is called oneirology."}}, {"canonical_name": "Montano", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "montanus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Montanus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Montanus"], "contexts": ["both Son and Father"]}, {"entity_id": "montano", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Montano", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Montano", "Montanus"], "contexts": ["early modern author", "ancient medical authority"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Montanus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Montano", "similarity": 0.963}], "id": 3575, "stable_key": "clu_82b4d44ffad83308", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Benito Montano", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Spanish physician and writer on medicine", "note": "Refers to Benito Montano, author of 'Polyanthea medicinal'."}}, {"canonical_name": "lumi\u00e8re", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "light", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "light", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["light"], "contexts": ["positive quality, from good principle"]}, {"entity_id": "lumi\u00e8re", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "lumi\u00e8re", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["lumi\u00e8re"], "contexts": ["element created by God", "physical phenomenon, first formation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "light", "target_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "target_name": "lumi\u00e8re", "similarity": 0.972}], "id": 3576, "stable_key": "clu_99b124e8b2776864", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Light", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q542", "wikidata_description": "sports involving running, jumping, throwing and walking"}}, {"canonical_name": "Brazavolus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "brasavolus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Brasavolus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Brasavolus"], "contexts": ["ancient authority, affirmed crystal is ice"]}, {"entity_id": "brazavolus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Brazavolus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Brazavolus", "Brazavol.", "Brazavola", "Bra\u017favolus"], "contexts": ["ancient medical authority cited", "cited author of medical text"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Brasavolus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Brazavolus", "similarity": 0.948}], "id": 3577, "stable_key": "clu_4c58f545351ce41f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Matthiolus", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Italian physician and botanist, influential in medicine and botany.", "note": "The original canonical name 'Brazavolus' appears to be a misspelling or variant of Italian physician Andrea Matthioli (Lodovico Brazzavola was a contemporary physician, but Matthiolus is more likely given the context of classical medical authority).", "wikipedia_extract": "Pietro Andrea Gregorio Mattioli (Italian: [\u02c8pj\u025b\u02d0tro an\u02c8dr\u025b\u02d0a \u0261re\u02c8\u0261\u0254\u02d0rjo mat\u02c8tj\u0254\u02d0li]; 12 March 1501\u2013 c.\u20091578) was a doctor and naturalist born in Siena. His most important work was a commentary on the medicinal plants of Pedanius Dioscorides first published in 1544 which was translated into several languages and went into thirteen editions in his lifetime. Mattioli was born in Siena to physician Francesco and his wife Lucrezia Buoninsegna. Although belonging to the city elite, the family was economically stressed, with thirteen children. His early studies are unknown but he may have studied in Venice, Siena, and Padua before he received his MD at the University of Padua in 1523, and subsequently practiced the profession in Siena, Rome, Trento and Gorizia, becoming personal physician of Ferdinand II, Archduke of Further Austria in Prague and Ambras Castle, and of Maximilian II, Holy Roman Emperor in Vienna. In 1527 he moved to Trent in the Val di Non."}}, {"canonical_name": "Ironsand", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "ironsand", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Ironsand", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["Ironsand", "Iron-sand", "iron sand"], "contexts": ["mineral substance", "iron-bearing sand"]}, {"entity_id": "iron-stone", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Iron-stone", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Iron-stone", "iron-stone"], "contexts": ["mineral occurring in ordinary form", "mineral presenting a phenomenon"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Ironsand", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Iron-stone", "similarity": 0.896}], "id": 3578, "stable_key": "clu_ff5a6be8051cdffb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Iron sand", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral"}}, {"canonical_name": "Ennius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "ennius", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Ennius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ennius"], "contexts": ["contemporary to Plautus"]}, {"entity_id": "ennius", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Ennius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Ennius"], "contexts": ["early Roman poet", "introduced 'mundus' to Latium"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Ennius", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Ennius", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3579, "stable_key": "clu_29c989e50d0e70b3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Quintus Ennius", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": -239, "death_year": -169, "description": "Influential early Roman poet"}}, {"canonical_name": "pine", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "pine", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Pine", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Pine"], "contexts": ["tree whose gums form concretion"]}, {"entity_id": "pine", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "pine", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 4, "variants": ["pine"], "contexts": ["tree shape used for comparison", "tree not found in South America"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Pine", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "pine", "similarity": 0.947}], "id": 3580, "stable_key": "clu_ae922a7612043f27", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pine tree", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Pinus L.", "family": "Pinaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q59668787", "wikidata_description": "common forest tree"}}, {"canonical_name": "Martialis", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "martial", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Martial", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Martial"], "contexts": ["classical author of epigrams"]}, {"entity_id": "martialis", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Martialis", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Martialis", "Marcial", "Martial"], "contexts": ["cited for medical text", "cited for dulcifying properties"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Martial", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Martialis", "similarity": 0.915}], "id": 3581, "stable_key": "clu_2966e019372b563f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Martial", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 40, "death_year": 104, "description": "Roman poet and epigrammatist, sometimes cited for medical contexts.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}}, {"canonical_name": "Nicolaus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "nicolaus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Nicolaus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Nicolaus"], "contexts": ["associated with a composition for kidney stones"]}, {"entity_id": "nicolaus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Nicolaus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 4, "variants": ["Nicolaus", "NicolausTrevi\u017fanus"], "contexts": ["cited author in the margin", "author of medical text"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Nicolaus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Nicolaus", "similarity": 0.753}], "id": 3582, "stable_key": "clu_4f788c69b03acff9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Nicolaus Salernitanus", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "Medieval physician, associated with kidney stone remedies.", "note": "Likely refers to Nicolaus Salernitanus, a prominent physician of the School of Salerno, known for his treatise on urine, and also for works on polypharmacy."}}, {"canonical_name": "Lacteas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "lacteal_vessels", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Lacteal Vessels", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lacteal Vessels"], "contexts": ["vessels where gold exits alimentary tract"]}, {"entity_id": "lacteas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Lacteas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Lacteas", "lacteas"], "contexts": ["lymphatic vessels", "anatomical structures"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Lacteal Vessels", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Lacteas", "similarity": 0.915}], "id": 3583, "stable_key": "clu_794d79701aad0570", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lacteals", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "Batavia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "batavia", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Batavia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Batavia"], "contexts": ["city from which Dutch Embassadors were sent"]}, {"entity_id": "batavia", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Batavia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Batavia"], "contexts": ["Batavia", "city with observed average temperature"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Batavia", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Batavia", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3584, "stable_key": "clu_0635958a1f94713a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jakarta", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Indonesia", "note": "Batavia was the name of the city (now Jakarta) that served as the capital of the Dutch East Indies. The context of Dutch ambassadors being sent from there confirms this identification.", "wikipedia_extract": "Jakarta, officially the Special Capital Region of Jakarta,  is the de facto capital and largest city of Indonesia and an autonomous region with a status equivalent to that of a province. It is located on the northwest coast of Java, the world's most populous island, bordered by the province of West Java to the south and east and Banten to the west. Its coastline faces the Java Sea to the north, and it shares a maritime border with Lampung to the west. Jakarta's metropolitan area has an estimated population of 41.9 million as of 2025, making it the most populated city in the world and therefore of Southeast Asia and largest urban area in Indonesia. Although Jakarta extends over only 661.23 square kilometres (255.30 square miles) and thus has the smallest area of any Indonesian province, its metropolitan area covers 7,076.31 square kilometres (2,732.18 square miles). Jakarta is the economic, cultural, and political centre of Indonesia, and the ASEAN's second largest economy after Singapore.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jakarta"}}, {"canonical_name": "Orchis", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "orchis_anthropomorphus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Orchis Anthropomorphus", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Orchis Anthropomorphus"], "contexts": ["plant with human-like appearance"]}, {"entity_id": "man-orchis", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Man-Orchis", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Man-Orchis"], "contexts": ["plant with flower representing a man"]}, {"entity_id": "orchis", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Orchis", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["orchis", "Orchis"], "contexts": ["genus with pollen-grain contrivance", "genus of flowering plants"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Orchis Anthropomorphus", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Orchis", "similarity": 0.89}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Man-Orchis", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Orchis", "similarity": 0.892}], "id": 3585, "stable_key": "clu_4b303473ea50650b-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "orchids", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Orchidaceae", "family": "Orchidaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q25308", "wikidata_description": "family of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchid", "wikipedia_extract": "Orchids are plants that belong to the family Orchidaceae (), a diverse and widespread group of flowering plants with blooms that are often colourful and fragrant. Orchids are cosmopolitan plants, living in diverse habitats on every continent except Antarctica. The world's richest diversity of orchid genera and species is in the tropics. Many species are epiphytes, living on trees. The flowers and their pollination mechanisms are highly specialized, attracting insect pollinators by colour, pattern, scent, pheromones, and sometimes by mimicking female insects. Orchids have very small seeds, relying on fungal partners for germination."}}, {"canonical_name": "Liquoris", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "liquorish", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Liquorish", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Liquorish"], "contexts": ["make stridulous noise upon divulsion"]}, {"entity_id": "liquoris", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Liquoris", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Liquoris"], "contexts": ["English plant with woody stalks and leaves", "plant root used for comparison"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Liquorish", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Liquoris", "similarity": 0.935}], "id": 3586, "stable_key": "clu_9f93a5b549367613-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Licorice", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Glycyrrhiza glabra", "family": "Fabaceae", "note": "The description 'make stridulous noise upon divulsion' is unusual for licorice, but the primary identification as 'Liquoris' strongly points to it. It's possible the text refers to a specific preparation or a less common characteristic.", "wikidata_id": "Q15447514", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Licorice_pipe", "wikipedia_extract": "A licorice pipe is a candy made of licorice shaped like a tobacco pipe, often with red sugar balls on the head of the pipe. A pipe may weigh around 16 grams, and the licorice is usually soft and sweet. There are also other varieties, for example with blue or yellow sugar balls, the latter with a taste of sea salt. The first known licorice pipe (Finnish: lakupiippu) was manufactured as early as the 1920s at the Heikki Huhtam\u00e4ki factory in Kokkola, Finland. In January 2010, displaying licorice pipes for sale in stores was banned in Norway. In August 2013, licorice pipes again made headlines because of a forthcoming EU directive on tobacco prevention, with the potential of a ban which could affect the sale of sweets and toys imitating tobacco products."}}, {"canonical_name": "Nutmeg", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "nutmeg", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Nutmeg", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Nutmeg"], "contexts": ["kernel of a fruit from a tree"]}, {"entity_id": "nutmeg", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Nutmeg", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Nutmeg"], "contexts": ["spice added to decoctions", "added to poultice"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Nutmeg", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Nutmeg", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3587, "stable_key": "clu_e62cb317c2b49b83", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "nutmeg", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Myristica fragrans", "family": "Myristicaceae", "description": "A spice obtained from the seed of a tree, used in medicine and cooking.", "note": "'noz no\u017fcada' directly translates to nutmeg, while 'noz' in the other context likely refers to the same commodity.", "wikipedia_extract": "Myristica fragrans, commonly known as the nutmeg tree, is an evergreen species indigenous to the Maluku Islands of Indonesia. This aromatic tree is economically significant as the primary source of two distinct spices: nutmeg, derived from its seed, and mace, obtained from the seed's aril. Valued for centuries in global spice trade, M. fragrans is now widely cultivated throughout tropical regions, including parts of Southeast Asia (Indonesia, Malaysia), South Asia (Kerala in India, Sri Lanka), East Asia (Guangdong and Yunnan in China, Taiwan), the Caribbean (notably Grenada), and South America. Myristica fragrans is an evergreen tree, usually 5\u201315 m (16\u201349 ft) tall, but occasionally reaching 20 m (66 ft) or even 30 m (98 ft) on Tidore. The alternately arranged leaves are dark green, 5\u201315 cm (2.0\u20135.9 in) long by 2\u20137 cm (0.8\u20132.8 in) wide with petioles about 1 cm (0.4 in) long. The species is dioecious, i.e. \"male\" or staminate flowers and \"female\" or carpellate flowers are borne on different plants, although occasional individuals produce both kinds of flower."}}, {"canonical_name": "epilepsy", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "epileptical", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Epileptical", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 1, "variants": ["Epileptical"], "contexts": ["condition mistletoe virtues were expected to help"]}, {"entity_id": "epilepsy", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "epilepsy", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 2, "variants": ["epilepsy"], "contexts": ["neurological disorder causing seizures"]}, {"entity_id": "epileptic", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "epileptic", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "CHRONIC", "count": 2, "variants": ["epileptic"], "contexts": ["man with chronic condition", "potential element in the case"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Epileptical", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "epilepsy", "similarity": 0.916}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Epileptical", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "epileptic", "similarity": 0.937}], "id": 3588, "stable_key": "clu_fe83be69f4d7efaa-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Epilepsy", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Epilepsy", "wikidata_id": "Q41571", "wikidata_description": "human neurological disease causing seizures", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epilepsy", "wikipedia_extract": "Epilepsy is a group of neurological disorders characterized by a tendency for recurrent, unprovoked seizures. A seizure is a sudden burst of abnormal electrical activity in the brain that can cause a variety of symptoms, ranging from brief lapses of awareness or muscle jerks to prolonged convulsions. These episodes can result in physical injuries, either directly, such as broken bones, or through causing accidents. The diagnosis of epilepsy typically requires at least two unprovoked seizures occurring more than 24 hours apart. In some cases, however, it may be diagnosed after a single unprovoked seizure if clinical evidence suggests a high risk of recurrence. Isolated seizures that occur without recurrence risk or are provoked by identifiable causes are not considered indicative of epilepsy."}}, {"canonical_name": "Ivy", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "ivy", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Ivy", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ivy"], "contexts": ["plant that blossoms twice a year"]}, {"entity_id": "ivy", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Ivy", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Ivy"], "contexts": ["plant growing in woods and on walls", "plant used in a cure for wine surfeit"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Ivy", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Ivy", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3589, "stable_key": "clu_7adfd432fcc142ae", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ivy", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Hedera helix", "family": "Araliaceae", "note": "The description 'plant that blossoms twice a year' is not typical for common ivy (Hedera helix), which usually flowers once. This might refer to a specific variety, a misobservation, or a different plant with a similar common name.", "wikidata_id": "Q26354", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hedera%20helix", "wikipedia_extract": "Hedera helix, simply ivy, or common ivy, English ivy, European ivy, King's choice ivy, is a species of flowering plant in the family Araliaceae. It is native to most of Europe and parts of western Asia. Ivy is a clinging evergreen vine that grows on tree trunks, walls, and fences in gardens, waste spaces, and wild habitats. Ivy is popular as an ornamental plant, but escaped plants have become naturalised outside its native range. Ivy has considerable cultural significance and symbolism. Synonyms include Hedera acuta, Hedera arborea ('tree ivy'), Hedera baccifera, and Hedera grandifolia."}}, {"canonical_name": "Gnats", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "gnats", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Gnats", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Gnats"], "contexts": ["insects indicating common corruption"]}, {"entity_id": "gnats", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Gnats", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["Gnats"], "contexts": ["creatures driven away by fern smoke", "insects repelled by smoke"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Gnats", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Gnats", "similarity": 0.911}], "id": 3590, "stable_key": "clu_fdbedab98e268af0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Gnat", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "family": "Culicidae", "description": "Small, biting flying insects, often found near water.", "note": "The singular 'Gnat' is the correct modern identification.", "wikipedia_extract": "A gnat ()  (also knat) is any of many species of tiny flying insects in the dipterid suborder Nematocera, especially those in the families Mycetophilidae, Anisopodidae and Sciaridae. Most often they fly in large numbers, called clouds. \"Gnat\" is a loose descriptive category rather than a phylogenetic or other technical term, so there is no scientific consensus on what constitutes a gnat. Some entomologists consider only non-biting flies to be gnats. Certain universities and institutes also distinguish eye gnats: the Smithsonian Institution describes them as \"non-biting flies, no bigger than a few grains of salt, ... attracted to fluids secreted by your eyes\". As nematoceran flies, adult gnats have antennae with at least six segments that are often long and slender. They are generally slender-bodied with long and narrow wings."}}, {"canonical_name": "rest", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "rest", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "rest", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["rest"], "contexts": ["absence of activity"]}, {"entity_id": "rest", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "rest", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["rest", "Rest"], "contexts": ["absence of motion", "state contrasted with motion"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "rest", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "rest", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3591, "stable_key": "clu_90514970a768c679", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Rest", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q35831", "wikidata_description": "naturally recurring resting state of mind and body", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sleep", "wikipedia_extract": "Sleep is a state of reduced mental and physical activity in which consciousness is altered and certain sensory activity is inhibited. During sleep, there is a marked decrease in muscle activity and interactions with the surrounding environment. While sleep differs from wakefulness in terms of the ability to react to stimuli, it still involves active brain patterns, making it more reactive than a coma or disorders of consciousness. Sleep occurs in repeating periods, during which the body alternates between two distinct modes: rapid eye movement sleep (REM) and non-REM sleep. Although REM stands for \"rapid eye movement\", this mode of sleep has many other aspects, including virtual paralysis of the body. Dreams are a succession of images, ideas, emotions, and sensations that usually occur involuntarily in the mind during certain stages of sleep."}}, {"canonical_name": "Pierre Martyr d'Anghiera", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "pierre_martyr_d'anghiera", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Pierre Martyr d'Anghiera", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Pierre Martyr d'Anghiera"], "contexts": ["recognized existence of chain", "early modern scholar, 16th century"]}, {"entity_id": "peter_martyr_de_aughiera", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Peter Martyr de Aughiera", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Peter Martyr de Aughiera"], "contexts": ["observer of snow limit and friend of Columbus"]}, {"entity_id": "anghiera,_peter_martyr_de", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Anghiera, Peter Martyr de", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Anghiera, Peter Martyr de"], "contexts": ["observed plant associations and snow limit"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Pierre Martyr d'Anghiera", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Peter Martyr de Aughiera", "similarity": 0.864}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Pierre Martyr d'Anghiera", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Anghiera, Peter Martyr de", "similarity": 0.856}], "id": 3592, "stable_key": "clu_a37254071871d883-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Peter Martyr d'Anghiera", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1457, "death_year": 1526, "description": "Italian Renaissance historian and humanist, chronicler of the New World."}}, {"canonical_name": "Alhazen", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "alhazen", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Alhazen", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Alhazen"], "contexts": ["medieval scholar on optics"]}, {"entity_id": "alhazen", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Alhazen", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Alhazen"], "contexts": ["Saracen astronomer of Spain, 9th century", "Saracen astronomer of Spain"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Alhazen", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Alhazen", "similarity": 0.674}], "id": 3593, "stable_key": "clu_26c60ac46c43dee4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ibn al-Haytham", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 965, "death_year": 1040, "description": "Arab polymath, considered a pioneer of optics and scientific method.", "wikidata_id": "Q11104", "wikidata_description": "Persian physicist, mathematician and astronomer (c. 965 \u2013 c. 1040)"}}, {"canonical_name": "Arteria magna", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "great_artery", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "great artery", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["great artery"], "contexts": ["major blood vessel"]}, {"entity_id": "arteria_magna", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Arteria magna", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 4, "variants": ["arteria magna", "Arteria magna", "Arteria Magna"], "contexts": ["great artery", "major artery"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "great artery", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Arteria magna", "similarity": 0.93}], "id": 3594, "stable_key": "clu_4e54e2a4c290fc70", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Aorta", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "The term 'Arteria magna' was commonly used in early modern anatomy for the aorta."}}, {"canonical_name": "stupor", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "stupifies", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "stupifies", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["stupifies"], "contexts": ["causes numbness or paralysis"]}, {"entity_id": "stupor", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "stupor", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 4, "variants": ["stupor", "Stupor", "stuporis"], "contexts": ["state of stupor", "state of reduced consciousness or sensation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "stupifies", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "stupor", "similarity": 0.914}], "id": 3595, "stable_key": "clu_192e8b0d06f22751", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Stupor", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Stupor", "wikidata_id": "Q268594", "wikidata_description": "total or nearly total lack of spontaneous movements (disease)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stupor", "wikipedia_extract": "Stupor is the lack of critical mental function and a level of consciousness, in which an affected person is almost entirely unresponsive and responds only to intense stimuli such as pain. The word derives from the Latin stupor (\"numbness, insensibility\"). Stupor is characterized by impaired reaction to external stimuli. Those in a stuporous state are rigid, mute and only appear to be conscious, as the eyes are open and follow surrounding objects. If not stimulated externally, a patient with stupor will appear to be in a sleepy state most of the time. In some extreme cases of severe depressive disorders the patient can become motionless, lose their appetite and become mute."}}, {"canonical_name": "V\u00e9nus", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "v\u00e9nus", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "V\u00e9nus", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["V\u00e9nus"], "contexts": ["planet in the solar system", "planet influencing Earth's axis obliquity"]}, {"entity_id": "venus", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Venus", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Venus"], "contexts": ["planet with mountainous surfaces"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "V\u00e9nus", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Venus", "similarity": 0.886}], "id": 3596, "stable_key": "clu_a9f381175567c9da", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Venus", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "N/A", "wikidata_id": "Q313", "wikidata_description": "second planet from the Solar System, tellurian and very similar to Earth", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Venus"}}, {"canonical_name": "Pologne", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "pologne", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "Pologne", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Pologne"], "contexts": ["Poland", "country north of the Alps"]}, {"entity_id": "royaume_de_pologne", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "royaume de Pologne", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["royaume de Pologne"], "contexts": ["Included in Russian European area calculation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Pologne", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "royaume de Pologne", "similarity": 0.949}], "id": 3597, "stable_key": "clu_21bfdd7dba5124ea", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Poland", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "European country, historical kingdom and later part of Russian Empire.", "wikipedia_extract": "Poland, officially the Republic of Poland, is a country in Central Europe. It extends from the Baltic Sea in the north to the Sudetes and Carpathian Mountains in the south. Poland borders Kaliningrad Oblast and Lithuania to the north; Belarus and Ukraine to the east; Slovakia and the Czech Republic to the south; and Germany to the west. The territory has a varied landscape, diverse ecosystems, and a temperate climate. Poland is composed of sixteen voivodeships and is the fifth most populous member state of the European Union (EU), with over 38 million people, and the fifth largest EU country by land area, covering 312,696 km2 (120,733 sq mi). The capital and largest city is Warsaw; other major cities include Krak\u00f3w, Wroc\u0142aw, \u0141\u00f3d\u017a, Pozna\u0144, and Gda\u0144sk."}}, {"canonical_name": "pluies", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "pluies", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "pluies", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 4, "variants": ["pluies"], "contexts": ["abstract ideas, theories, mental faculties, and processes", "rains"]}, {"entity_id": "chuvas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "chuvas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["chuvas"], "contexts": ["precipitation administered by winds"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "pluies", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "chuvas", "similarity": 0.98}], "id": 3598, "stable_key": "clu_99867c88ab054c2f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Rain", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'pluies' and 'chuvas' both refer to rain. The contexts suggest precipitation rather than a specific substance.", "wikidata_id": "Q25299795", "wikidata_description": "family name"}}, {"canonical_name": "mati\u00e8res calcaires", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "mati\u00e8res_calcaires", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "mati\u00e8res calcaires", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["mati\u00e8res calcaires"], "contexts": ["calcareous substances", "calcareous materials"]}, {"entity_id": "calcareous_substances", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "calcareous substances", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["calcareous substances"], "contexts": ["calcium-containing materials"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "mati\u00e8res calcaires", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "calcareous substances", "similarity": 0.972}], "id": 3599, "stable_key": "clu_8b397f7cde65deac-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Calcareous formations", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "This refers to geological deposits, often sedimentary rocks, that are rich in calcium carbonate.", "wikidata_id": "Q61748682", "wikidata_description": ""}}, {"canonical_name": "chute des eaux", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "chute_des_eaux", "book_id": "epo