kely referring to animals in general or 'noxious creatures' in a broad sense.", "wikidata_id": "Q431", "wikidata_description": "scientific study of animals", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zoology"}}, {"canonical_name": "Crocus", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "crocus", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Crocus", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Crocus"], "contexts": ["plant whose roots purge phlegm", "plant whose roots are dried"]}, {"entity_id": "crocoma", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Crocoma", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Crocoma", "Croco"], "contexts": ["word in a chapter title", "plant mentioned in text"]}, {"entity_id": "crocus", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Crocus", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Crocus"], "contexts": ["plant that produces saffron"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Crocus", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Crocoma", "similarity": 0.892}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Crocus", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Crocus", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3633, "stable_key": "clu_82c18d14b0d425d0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Crocus", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Crocus L.", "family": "Iridaceae", "note": "Includes references to both the genus and possibly a specific species like Crocus sativus for saffron.", "wikidata_id": "Q157806", "wikidata_description": "genus of flowering plants"}}, {"canonical_name": "arrayan", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "arrach", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Arrach", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Arrach"], "contexts": ["wild and stinking plant"]}, {"entity_id": "arrayan", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "arrayan", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 4, "variants": ["arrayan", "Arrayan"], "contexts": ["myrtle, flower structure", "plant with larger leaves than huitzxochitl"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Arrach", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "arrayan", "similarity": 0.899}], "id": 3634, "stable_key": "clu_1cb9c36c66043191", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "myrtle", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Myrtus communis Linnaeus", "family": "Myrtaceae", "note": "The term 'arrayan' is a common name for myrtle in Spanish.", "wikidata_id": "Q165152", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Myrtus"}}, {"canonical_name": "fumes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "fumes", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Fumes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Fumes"], "contexts": ["hot fumes for easing piles"]}, {"entity_id": "fumes", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "fumes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["fumes"], "contexts": ["inimical to life", "Latin for 'I have been'"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Fumes", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "fumes", "similarity": 0.886}], "id": 3635, "stable_key": "clu_7457b8b2e84e6f9e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Fumes", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to vapors or gases, often with medicinal or harmful connotations."}}, {"canonical_name": "Lupini", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "lupines", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Lupines", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lupines"], "contexts": ["plant used in powder"]}, {"entity_id": "lupini", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Lupini", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 4, "variants": ["lupini", "Lupini"], "contexts": ["lupins for grinding", "lupine seed ingredient"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Lupines", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Lupini", "similarity": 0.943}], "id": 3636, "stable_key": "clu_1bbd0a3bf268f715", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lupins", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Lupinus", "family": "Fabaceae", "description": "Edible seeds or plants of the genus Lupinus.", "note": "Refers to the plant genus Lupinus or its seeds.", "wikipedia_extract": "Lupinus, commonly known as lupin, lupine, or regionally bluebonnet, is a genus of plants in the legume family Fabaceae. The genus includes over 199 species, with centres of diversity in North and South America. Smaller centres occur in North Africa and the Mediterranean. They are widely cultivated both as a food source and as ornamental plants, but are invasive to some areas. For instance, lupine has become an ecological problem in Iceland since the beginning of the 21st century. The species are mostly herbaceous perennial plants 0.3\u20131.5 metres (1\u20135 feet) tall, but some are annual plants and a few are shrubs up to 3 m (10 ft) tall."}}, {"canonical_name": "Thames", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "thames", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Thames", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Thames"], "contexts": ["river where plant grows"]}, {"entity_id": "thames", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Thames", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Thames"], "contexts": ["river with channels filled", "river whose channels are filled by tides"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Thames", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Thames", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3637, "stable_key": "clu_0778cc4264fd119d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "River Thames", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United Kingdom", "wikidata_id": "Q19686", "wikidata_description": "river in southern England", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/River%20Thames", "wikipedia_extract": "The River Thames (  TEMZ), known alternatively in parts as the River Isis, is a river that flows through southern England including London. At 215 miles (346 km), it is the longest river entirely in England and the second-longest in the United Kingdom, after the River Severn. The river rises at Thames Head in Gloucestershire and flows into the North Sea near Tilbury, Essex and Gravesend, Kent, via the Thames Estuary. From the west, it flows through Oxford (where it is sometimes called the Isis), Reading, Henley-on-Thames and Windsor. The Thames also drains the whole of Greater London. The lower reaches of the river are called the Tideway, derived from its long tidal reach up to Teddington Lock."}}, {"canonical_name": "mouse", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "mouse", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Mouse", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mouse"], "contexts": ["small rodent"]}, {"entity_id": "mouse", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "mouse", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["mouse"], "contexts": ["transported by man, wide range", "prey animal"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Mouse", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "mouse", "similarity": 0.798}], "id": 3638, "stable_key": "clu_e782fb00a3d861ac-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mouse", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "The term 'Mice' is consistently used to refer to the common rodent.", "wikidata_id": "Q83310", "wikidata_description": "species of mammal", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House%20mouse", "wikipedia_extract": "The house mouse (Mus musculus) is a small mammal of the rodent family Muridae, characteristically having a pointed snout, large rounded ears, and a long and almost hairless tail. It is one of the most abundant species of the genus Mus. Although a wild animal, the house mouse has benefited significantly from associating with human habitation to the point that truly wild populations are significantly less common than the synanthropic populations near human activity. The house mouse has been domesticated as the pet or fancy mouse, and as the laboratory mouse, which is one of the most important model organisms in biology and medicine. The complete mouse reference genome was sequenced in 2002. House mice have an adult body length (nose to base of tail) of 7.5\u201310 centimetres (3\u20134 in) and a tail length of 5\u201310 cm (2\u20134 in)."}}, {"canonical_name": "domesticated animals", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "domesticated_animals", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "domesticated animals", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["domesticated animals"], "contexts": ["animals bred by humans", "animals with differences in breeds"]}, {"entity_id": "domesticated_animal", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "domesticated animal", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["domesticated animal"], "contexts": ["animal tamed by humans"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "domesticated animals", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "domesticated animal", "similarity": 0.98}], "id": 3639, "stable_key": "clu_855456ca354fc049", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "domesticated animals", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q622852", "wikidata_description": "animal that has been biologically changed by human intervention", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Domesticated%20animal"}}, {"canonical_name": "leg", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "leg", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "leg", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 4, "variants": ["leg"], "contexts": ["limb for locomotion", "lower limb"]}, {"entity_id": "leg", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "leg", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["leg"], "contexts": ["lower limb of a vertebrate"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "leg", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "leg", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3640, "stable_key": "clu_d1ac657d198b440e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Leg", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "Linn\u00e6us", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "linn\u00e6us", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Linn\u00e6us", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Linn\u00e6us"], "contexts": ["calculated plant reproduction rate", "famous naturalist and taxonomist"]}, {"entity_id": "linnaeus", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Linnaeus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Linnaeus"], "contexts": ["botanist, theorist"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Linn\u00e6us", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Linnaeus", "similarity": 0.86}], "id": 3641, "stable_key": "clu_7dd064ac9179e65d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Carl Linnaeus", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1707, "death_year": 1778, "description": "Swedish botanist, physician, and zoologist, formalized binomial nomenclature."}}, {"canonical_name": "Lepidosiren", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "FISH", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "lepidosiren", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Lepidosiren", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "FISH", "count": 4, "variants": ["Lepidosiren"], "contexts": ["anomalous form connecting separated orders", "lungfish, transitional animal"]}, {"entity_id": "lepidosiren", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Lepidosiren", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "FISH", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lepidosiren"], "contexts": ["African animal torpid in dried mud"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Lepidosiren", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Lepidosiren", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3642, "stable_key": "clu_a70012c948ea6d2c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lepidosiren", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "Lepidosiren is a genus of lungfish. Lepidosteus refers to the genus Lepisosteus (gars).", "wikidata_id": "Q1410142", "wikidata_description": "species of chordates", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South%20American%20lungfish", "wikipedia_extract": "The South American lungfish (Lepidosiren paradoxa), also known as the American mud-fish and scaly salamanderfish, is the single species of lungfish found in swamps and slow-moving waters of the Amazon, Paraguay, and lower Paran\u00e1 River basins in South America. Notable as an obligate air-breather, it is the sole member of its family Lepidosirenidae, although some authors also place Protopterus in the family. In Brazil, it is known by the indigenous language Tupi name piramboia, which means \"snake-fish\" (Portuguese pronunciation: [pi\u027e\u0250\u0303\u02c8b\u0254jj\u0250]), and synonyms pirarucu-b\u00f3ia ([pi\u027e\u0250\u027eu\u02c8ku \u02c8b\u0254jj\u0250]), tra\u00edra-b\u00f3ia ([t\u027e\u0250\u02c8i\u027e\u0250 \u02c8b\u0254jj\u0250]), and caramuru ([k\u0250\u027e\u0250mu\u02c8\u027eu]). The South American lungfish is most closely related to the African lungfishes (family Protopteridae), and both families are thought to have diverged during the Early Cretaceous. Some papers suggest classifying both Lepidosiren and Protopterus within Lepidosirenidae, though authorities continue to classify both as distinct families. The immature lungfish is spotted with gold on a black background; in the adult, this fades to a brown or gray color."}}, {"canonical_name": "parent-species", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "parent-species", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "parent-species", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["parent-species"], "contexts": ["original species before hybridization", "original species from which new forms arise"]}, {"entity_id": "parent_organism", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "parent organism", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["parent organism"], "contexts": ["organism containing a germ"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "parent-species", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "parent organism", "similarity": 0.906}], "id": 3643, "stable_key": "clu_e88458ab5e7810a7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "parent species", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "pachyderms", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "pachyderms", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "pachyderms", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["pachyderms", "Pachyderms"], "contexts": ["order of mammals", "thick-skinned mammals like elephants"]}, {"entity_id": "pachydermata", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Pachydermata", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Pachydermata"], "contexts": ["thick-skinned mammals"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "pachyderms", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Pachydermata", "similarity": 0.926}], "id": 3644, "stable_key": "clu_239745a91235046b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pachyderm", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q2735907", "wikidata_description": "skin disease characterized by skin that is loose, hanging, wrinkled and lacking in elasticity; it can also affect connective tissue in other parts of the body, including the heart, blood vessels, joints, intestines and lungs", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cutis%20laxa", "wikipedia_extract": "Pachydermata (meaning 'thick skin', from the Greek \u03c0\u03b1\u03c7\u03cd\u03c2, pachys, 'thick', and \u03b4\u03ad\u03c1\u03bc\u03b1, derma, 'skin') is an obsolete order of mammals described by Gottlieb Storr, Georges Cuvier, and others, at one time recognized by many systematists. The term pachyderm is commonly used to describe elephants, rhinoceroses, hippopotamuses and tapirs. The grouping was determined to be artificial as a biological classification due to genetic studies. Pachydermata is an obsolete order of mammals described by Gottlieb Storr, Georges Cuvier, and others, at one time recognized by many systematists. The grouping is polyphyletic, so the order is no longer in use as a biological classification. Outside strict biological classification, the related term pachyderm is commonly used to describe elephants, rhinoceroses, hippopotamuses and tapirs."}}, {"canonical_name": "dugong", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "dugong", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "dugong", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["Dugong", "dugong"], "contexts": ["marine mammal", "pachydermatous animal with whale resemblance"]}, {"entity_id": "dugong", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "dugong", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["dugong"], "contexts": ["species of marine mammal"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "dugong", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "dugong", "similarity": 0.739}], "id": 3645, "stable_key": "clu_48ab033386e17cd8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Dugong dugon", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal"}}, {"canonical_name": "petals", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "petals", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "petals", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 4, "variants": ["petals"], "contexts": ["often brightly colored parts of a flower", "part of a flower"]}, {"entity_id": "blossoms", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "blossoms", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["blossoms"], "contexts": ["flowers of plants"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "petals", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "blossoms", "similarity": 0.908}], "id": 3646, "stable_key": "clu_f8e048759f64c547", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "petals", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "note": "The term 'petals' refers to a specific part of a flower, while 'blossoms' can refer to flowers in general or the act of flowering. In the given contexts, 'petals' is the more precise term for the botanical structure."}}, {"canonical_name": "forms", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "forms", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "forms", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["forms"], "contexts": ["related varieties or species", "forms dominant in the highest degree"]}, {"entity_id": "form", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "form", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["form"], "contexts": ["shape of an object", "shape attribute noticed by difference"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "forms", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "form", "similarity": 0.874}], "id": 3647, "stable_key": "clu_74fa24e349a64d04", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "form", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'forms' in early modern texts can refer to the Aristotelian concept of 'form' (essence or structure) or the observable shape or appearance of something. Given the contexts, it encompasses both philosophical and descriptive meanings.", "wikidata_id": "Q7386", "wikidata_description": "family of insects"}}, {"canonical_name": "pelvis", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "pelvis", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "pelvis", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 3, "variants": ["pelvis"], "contexts": ["bone structure in birds and humans", "form affected by mountainous country"]}, {"entity_id": "pelvis", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "pelvis", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["pelvis"], "contexts": ["skeletal structure investigated", "part of the skeleton investigated"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "pelvis", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "pelvis", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3648, "stable_key": "clu_043f5ac8c3082676-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Abdomen", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "While 'pelvis' is mentioned, the dominant term and context clearly point to the general abdominal region as the referent.", "wikidata_id": "Q9597", "wikidata_description": "in humans, region of the body between the thorax at the top and the pelvis at the bottom; in insects, segment at the caudal end of the body, following on from the head and thorax", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abdomen", "wikipedia_extract": "An abdomen (also belly or stomach in vertebrates, or metasoma in arthropods)  is the front part of the torso between the thorax (chest) and pelvis in humans and in other vertebrates. The area occupied by the abdomen is called the abdominal cavity. In arthropods, it is the posterior tagma of the body; it follows the thorax or cephalothorax. In humans, the abdomen stretches from the thorax at the thoracic diaphragm to the pelvis at the pelvic brim. The pelvic brim stretches from the lumbosacral joint (the intervertebral disc between L5 and S1) to the pubic symphysis and is the edge of the pelvic inlet. The space above this inlet and under the thoracic diaphragm is termed the abdominal cavity."}}, {"canonical_name": "Pliocene period", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "pliocene_period", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Pliocene period", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Pliocene period"], "contexts": ["geological epoch before the Glacial epoch", "geological time period"]}, {"entity_id": "eocene_period", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Eocene Period", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Eocene Period"], "contexts": ["geological epoch", "geological period for fossil finds"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Pliocene period", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Eocene Period", "similarity": 0.894}], "id": 3649, "stable_key": "clu_d51df38bfa5e070d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "geological epoch", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The cluster includes references to both the Pliocene and Eocene periods, which are specific geological epochs. The overarching concept is 'geological epoch'."}}, {"canonical_name": "external conditions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "external_conditions", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "external conditions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["external conditions"], "contexts": ["environmental factors affecting organisms", "environmental factors influencing organisms"]}, {"entity_id": "external_circumstances", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "external circumstances", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["external circumstances"], "contexts": ["environmental factors influencing beliefs", "environmental and situational factors"]}, {"entity_id": "external_conditions", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "external conditions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["external conditions"], "contexts": ["environmental factors influencing life"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "external conditions", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "external circumstances", "similarity": 0.937}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "external conditions", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "external conditions", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3650, "stable_key": "clu_850d340065ce6b37", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "environmental factors", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The contexts clearly indicate that 'external conditions' and 'external circumstances' refer to the environment and surrounding influences on organisms or beliefs."}}, {"canonical_name": "field of vision", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "vision", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "vision", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["vision"], "contexts": ["sense of sight"]}, {"entity_id": "field_of_vision", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "field of vision", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 3, "variants": ["field of vision"], "contexts": ["area visible to the eye", "area perceived by the eye"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "vision", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "field of vision", "similarity": 0.929}], "id": 3651, "stable_key": "clu_1d1157d35b908d2c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "field of vision", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'vision' in this context, coupled with 'field of vision,' directly points to the area perceived by sight.", "wikidata_id": "Q2364768", "wikidata_description": "total area or space visible in a person's peripheral vision with the eye looking straight forward"}}, {"canonical_name": "Apteryx", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "apteryx", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Apteryx", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 2, "variants": ["Apteryx"], "contexts": ["genus of kiwi birds", "mentioned in text"]}, {"entity_id": "apteryx", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "apteryx", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 2, "variants": ["apteryx"], "contexts": ["living bird species for comparison", "flightless bird native to New Zealand"]}, {"entity_id": "spteryx", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "spteryx", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["spteryx"], "contexts": ["bird allied to dinornis"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Apteryx", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "apteryx", "similarity": 0.977}, {"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Apteryx", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "spteryx", "similarity": 0.903}], "id": 3652, "stable_key": "clu_633062f685c360f4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Apteryx", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q43642", "wikidata_description": "genus of birds", "wikipedia_extract": "Kiwi are flightless birds endemic to New Zealand of the order Apterygiformes. The five extant species fall into the family Apterygidae and genus Apteryx. Approximately the size of a domestic chicken, kiwi are the smallest ratites (which also include ostriches, emus, rheas, cassowaries and the extinct elephant birds and moa). DNA sequence comparisons have yielded the conclusion that kiwi are much more closely related to the extinct Malagasy elephant birds than to the moa with which they shared New Zealand. There are five recognised species, four of which are currently listed as vulnerable, and one of which is near threatened. All species have been negatively affected by historic deforestation, but their remaining habitat is well protected in large forest reserves and national parks."}}, {"canonical_name": "Crustacea", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "crustacea", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Crustacea", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Crustacea"], "contexts": ["water-breathing classes adapted to land", "arthropod subphylum including crabs and lobsters"]}, {"entity_id": "crustacea", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Crustacea", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["Crustacea"], "contexts": ["fossil animal group", "swarming in oceanic waters"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Crustacea", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Crustacea", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3653, "stable_key": "clu_fc17ae855f902eef-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Crustaceans", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "The mention of 'cet\u00e1ceos' (cetaceans) in the context of Garcia de Orta's Colloquios is likely a misidentification or OCR error, as 'cet\u00e1ceos' refers to whales and dolphins, not crustaceans. The dominant theme points to crustaceans.", "wikidata_id": "Q25364", "wikidata_description": "subphylum of arthropods", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crustacean", "wikipedia_extract": "Crustaceans (from Latin word \"crustacea\" meaning: \"those with shells\" or \"crusted ones\") are invertebrate animals that constitute one group of arthropods that are traditionally a part of the subphylum Crustacea (), a large, diverse group of mainly aquatic arthropods including the more familiar decapods (shrimps, prawns, crabs, lobsters and crayfish), seed shrimps, branchiopods, fish lice, krill, remipedes, isopods, barnacles, copepods, opossum shrimps, amphipods and mantis shrimp. The crustacean group can be treated as a subphylum under the clade Mandibulata. It is now well accepted that the hexapods (insects and entognathans) emerged deep in the crustacean group, with the completed pan-group referred to as Pancrustacea. The three classes Cephalocarida, Branchiopoda and Remipedia are more closely related to the hexapods than they are to any of the other crustaceans (oligostracans and multicrustaceans). The 67,000 described species range in size from Stygotantulus stocki at 0.1 mm (0.004 in), to the Japanese spider crab with a leg span of up to 3.8 m (12.5 ft) and a mass of 20 kg (44 lb). Like other arthropods, crustaceans have an exoskeleton, which they moult to grow."}}, {"canonical_name": "sediment", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "sediment", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "sediment", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["sediment"], "contexts": ["material deposited by water", "material derived from erosion"]}, {"entity_id": "sediment", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "sediment", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["sediment"], "contexts": ["material carried by rivers", "distributed by marine currents"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "sediment", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "sediment", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3654, "stable_key": "clu_3ec271596e85fc06", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sediment", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q205768", "wikidata_description": "study of natural sediments and of the processes by which they are formed"}}, {"canonical_name": "United States of America", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "united_states_of_america", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "United States of America", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["United States of America"], "contexts": ["country in North America", "country where White Mountains are located"]}, {"entity_id": "united_states_of_america", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "United States of America", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 3, "variants": ["United States of America"], "contexts": ["nation where New Madrid is located", "country where Cincinnati is located"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "United States of America", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "United States of America", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3655, "stable_key": "clu_9c0afb49427ed09b-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "United States", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United States", "wikidata_id": "Q30", "wikidata_description": "country located primarily in North America", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United%20States", "note": "While 'United States of North America' was a more formal designation, the modern referent is the United States.", "wikipedia_extract": "The United States of America (USA), also known as the United States (U.S.) or America, is a country primarily located in North America. It is a federal republic of 50 states and a federal capital district, Washington, D.C. The 48 contiguous states border Canada to the north and Mexico to the south, with the semi-exclave of Alaska in the northwest and the archipelago of Hawaii in the Pacific Ocean. The United States also asserts sovereignty over five major island territories and various uninhabited islands in Oceania and the Caribbean. It is a megadiverse country, with the world's third-largest land area and third-largest population, exceeding 341 million. Paleo-Indians first migrated from North Asia to North America at least 15,000 years ago, and formed various civilizations."}}, {"canonical_name": "volcanic islands", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "volcanic_islands", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Volcanic islands", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Volcanic islands", "volcanic islands"], "contexts": ["islands formed by volcanoes", "denudation of"]}, {"entity_id": "volcanic_islands", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "volcanic islands", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Volcanic Islands", "volcanic islands"], "contexts": ["book by Darwin on volcanic formations", "book title by Darwin"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Volcanic islands", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "volcanic islands", "similarity": 0.997}], "id": 3656, "stable_key": "clu_a0699c17fbec5228", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Volcanic islands", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "note": "The context mentions a book by Darwin on volcanic formations, which is likely 'Geological Observations on the Volcanic Islands of the Atlantic'."}}, {"canonical_name": "rosa", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "rosa", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Rosa", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rosa"], "contexts": ["genus with polymorphic species"]}, {"entity_id": "rosa", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "rosa", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 4, "variants": ["rosa"], "contexts": ["flower used for its essence and juice", "flower used in preparations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Rosa", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "rosa", "similarity": 0.973}], "id": 3657, "stable_key": "clu_683b94c0b7528ea5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "rose", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Rosa L.", "family": "Rosaceae", "note": "While 'Rosa' can refer to the genus, the context of 'rose' in James's 'Principles of Psychology' might also allude to its use in metaphorical or experimental contexts, but the primary botanical identification is the genus.", "wikidata_id": "Q34687", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants"}}, {"canonical_name": "glottis", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "glottis", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "glottis", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["glottis"], "contexts": ["opening of the larynx"]}, {"entity_id": "glottis", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "glottis", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 4, "variants": ["glottis"], "contexts": ["opening between vocal cords", "part of the larynx"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "glottis", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "glottis", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3658, "stable_key": "clu_84467202063c7f58", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Glottis", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "While 'glottis' refers to an anatomical structure, in these contexts it functions more as a conceptual or descriptive term for a specific opening rather than a distinct entity to be classified as anatomy in the same way as organs.", "wikidata_id": "Q107272795", "wikidata_description": "genus of birds"}}, {"canonical_name": "secondary formations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "secondary_formation", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "secondary formation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["secondary formation"], "contexts": ["geological period"]}, {"entity_id": "secondary_formations", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "secondary formations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 4, "variants": ["secondary formations"], "contexts": ["geological formation designation", "geological time period"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "secondary formation", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "secondary formations", "similarity": 0.961}], "id": 3659, "stable_key": "clu_d6a1d04dc4bfa2d8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Secondary formation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to geological periods or strata, specifically the Mesozoic era in the context of Darwin.", "wikidata_id": "Q57875072", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article"}}, {"canonical_name": "Buckland", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "buckland", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Buckland", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Buckland"], "contexts": ["remarked on fossil classification"]}, {"entity_id": "buckland", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Buckland", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Buckland"], "contexts": ["geologist quoted on coprolite abundance", "geologist"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Buckland", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Buckland", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3660, "stable_key": "clu_7e43973db398a23e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "William Buckland", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1784, "death_year": 1856, "description": "Geologist and paleontologist, known for work on fossils and ancient life."}}, {"canonical_name": "Carolines", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "caroline_archipelagoes", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Caroline Archipelagoes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Caroline Archipelagoes"], "contexts": ["archipelagoes with peculiar bats"]}, {"entity_id": "carolines", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Carolines", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Carolines"], "contexts": ["states mentioned", "region in the United States"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "Caroline Archipelagoes", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Carolines", "similarity": 0.891}], "id": 3661, "stable_key": "clu_a571d4b0d6fb2e64", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Caroline Islands", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Archipelago in the western Pacific Ocean.", "note": "The historical mention in Humboldt likely refers to the Caroline Islands. The reference to 'Carolines' as states in the US is less likely in this context, but the 'Caroline Archipelagoes' strongly confirms the islands.", "wikipedia_extract": "The Caroline Islands (or the Carolines) are a widely scattered archipelago of tiny islands in the western Pacific Ocean, to the north of New Guinea. Politically, they are divided between the Federated States of Micronesia (FSM) in the central and eastern parts of the group, and Palau at the extreme western end. Historically, this area was also called Nuevas Filipinas or New Philippines, because they were part of the Spanish East Indies and were governed from Manila in the Philippines. The Carolines are scattered across a distance of approximately 3,540 kilometres (2,200 mi; 1,910 nmi), from the westernmost island, Tobi, in Palau, to the easternmost island, Kosrae, a state of the FSM. The group consists of about 500 small coral islands, east of the Philippines, in the Pacific Ocean. The distance from Yap (one of the larger Caroline islands) to Manila is 1,200 mi (1,900 km)."}}, {"canonical_name": "te\u017fta", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "teats", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "teats", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["teats"], "contexts": ["developed and rudimentary teats"]}, {"entity_id": "te\u017fta", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "te\u017fta", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 4, "variants": ["te\u017fta"], "contexts": ["head, affected by vapors", "part of the body"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "teats", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "te\u017fta", "similarity": 0.885}], "id": 3662, "stable_key": "clu_522535681d859705", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Testa", "confidence": "medium", "type": "plant", "note": "The term 'te\u017fta' in older texts often refers to the seed coat (testa), but the context 'developed and rudimentary teats' suggests a potential ambiguity with 'teats' (mammal anatomy). Given the 'polyanthea_medicinal' source, it's more likely referring to a plant part. The other context is unclear, but 'head, affected by vapors' is too vague. The 'teats' in Darwin refers to mammalian mammary glands."}}, {"canonical_name": "purpose", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "purpose", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "purpose", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["purpose"], "contexts": ["organ's purpose"]}, {"entity_id": "purpose", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "purpose", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["purpose"], "contexts": ["reason or intention behind action", "the reason for an action"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "source_name": "purpose", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "purpose", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3663, "stable_key": "clu_d067fcaac0c6a736", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "purpose", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "lengua", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "lengua", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "lengua", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 4, "variants": ["lengua"], "contexts": ["tongue, affected by tasting the plant", "body part that can be dry"]}, {"entity_id": "lingua", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "lingua", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["lingua"], "contexts": ["body part used for tasting"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "lengua", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "lingua", "similarity": 0.99}], "id": 3664, "stable_key": "clu_e8d88828fe323b2f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "tongue", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "Refers to the anatomical organ.", "wikidata_id": "Q37258562", "wikidata_description": "family name"}}, {"canonical_name": "cola", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "cola", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "cola", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 4, "variants": ["cola"], "contexts": ["ingredient in Indian oil preparation", "part of an animal used medicinally"]}, {"entity_id": "cola", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "cola", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["cola"], "contexts": ["substance from trees, common in the Indies"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "cola", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "cola", "similarity": 0.663}], "id": 3665, "stable_key": "clu_6a0159c0739b184c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "cola", "confidence": "medium", "type": "plant", "note": "Could refer to various plants from which 'cola' or sticky substances are derived, including species of Cola (kohl) or potentially resins/gums. Given the context of 'Indian oil preparation', it might refer to a plant used for its oily or resinous properties."}}, {"canonical_name": "camara", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "camara", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "camara", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 4, "variants": ["camara"], "contexts": ["symptom related to bowels", "provokes chamber"]}, {"entity_id": "c\u00e2mara", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "c\u00e2mara", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["c\u00e2mara"], "contexts": ["bowel movement"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "camara", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "c\u00e2mara", "similarity": 0.882}], "id": 3666, "stable_key": "clu_a27134923ad4e759", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "bowel movement", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q204015", "wikidata_description": "expulsion of feces from the rectum", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Defecation"}}, {"canonical_name": "xaqueca", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "xaqueca", "book_id": "quatro_libr