 "name": "Manganas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Manganas", "manganas", "manganos"], "contexts": ["fruit like small plums", "fruit recommended for cooling"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "manganas", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Manganas", "similarity": 0.91}], "id": 3682, "stable_key": "clu_788a2d5032ec438a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Manganas", "confidence": "low", "type": "plant", "note": "The context suggests a fruit-bearing plant, possibly a plum or similar fruit, but 'Manganas' is not a standard botanical or common name. It might be a regional or archaic term."}}, {"canonical_name": "coutelas", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "ca\u00f1utillos", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "ca\u00f1utillos", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["ca\u00f1utillos"], "contexts": ["small tubes"]}, {"entity_id": "coutelas", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "coutelas", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 4, "variants": ["coutelas", "cordeles", "cordelettes"], "contexts": ["a type of knife", "weapon used by sailors"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "ca\u00f1utillos", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "coutelas", "similarity": 0.909}], "id": 3683, "stable_key": "clu_35227ea71f996bee", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "coutelas", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "The term 'coutelas' is an archaic form of 'couteau' (knife) in French, fitting the context of a type of knife. 'Ca\u00f1utillos' in Spanish translates to small tubes, which is a different object. Given the multiple instances of 'coutelas' in Humboldt, the knife interpretation is more likely."}}, {"canonical_name": "Nombre de Dios", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "ciudad_del_nombre_de_dios", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "Ciudad del nombre de Dios", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ciudad del nombre de Dios"], "contexts": ["city on route to Cartagena"]}, {"entity_id": "nombre_de_dios", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "Nombre de Dios", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Nombre de Dios"], "contexts": ["a port city"]}, {"entity_id": "nombre_de_dios", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Nombre de Dios", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Nombre de Dios"], "contexts": ["city in Tierra Firme", "Name of God, a place where stones are brought from"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Ciudad del nombre de Dios", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Nombre de Dios", "similarity": 0.906}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Nombre de Dios", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Nombre de Dios", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3684, "stable_key": "clu_cc545286a3704343", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Nombre de Dios", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Panama", "note": "Nombre de Dios was a significant port city in Panama during the early colonial period, serving as a key stop on trade routes."}}, {"canonical_name": "pildoras", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "pildoras", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "pildoras", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["pildoras"], "contexts": ["plant root made into pill-like forms"]}, {"entity_id": "pildoras", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "pildoras", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 4, "variants": ["pildoras"], "contexts": ["pills made from powders", "preparations in apothecaries"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "pildoras", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "pildoras", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3685, "stable_key": "clu_cefa8e77ebbf8093", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "pills", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "note": "The term 'pildoras' directly translates to pills, a common pharmaceutical preparation.", "wikidata_id": "Q37494383", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pillsbury%20%28surname%29"}}, {"canonical_name": "prepucio", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "prepucio", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "prepucio", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["prepucio"], "contexts": ["foreskin, affected by skin conditions"]}, {"entity_id": "prepucio", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "prepucio", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 4, "variants": ["prepucio"], "contexts": ["foreskin", "part of the body"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "prepucio", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "prepucio", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3686, "stable_key": "clu_adde6a8923d2a809", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "foreskin", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The context of 'prepucio' in relation to skin conditions and anatomy clearly refers to the foreskin.", "wikidata_id": "Q673203", "wikidata_description": "retractable fold of skin which covers and protects the glans of the penis", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foreskin"}}, {"canonical_name": "sangrar", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "sangr\u00edas", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "sangr\u00edas", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sangr\u00edas"], "contexts": ["bloodletting"]}, {"entity_id": "sangrar", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "sangrar", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 4, "variants": ["sangrar", "sangr\u00eda", "sangr\u00edas"], "contexts": ["bloodletting", "medical practice for illness"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "sangr\u00edas", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "sangrar", "similarity": 0.891}], "id": 3687, "stable_key": "clu_6ef341a8c77ec868-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bloodletting", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q356405", "wikidata_description": "therapy, now rarely used in medicine", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bloodletting", "wikipedia_extract": "Bloodletting (or blood-letting) is the deliberate withdrawal of blood from a patient to prevent or cure illness and disease. Bloodletting, whether by a physician or by leeches, was based on an ancient system of medicine in which blood and other bodily fluids were regarded as \"humors\" that had to remain in proper balance to maintain health. It was the most common medical practice performed by surgeons from antiquity until the late 19th century, a span of over 2,000 years. In Europe, the practice continued to be relatively common until the end of the 19th century. The practice has been abandoned by modern-style medicine for all except a few very specific medical conditions. In the beginning of the 19th century, studies had begun to show the harmful effects of bloodletting.", "description": "A medical procedure involving the withdrawal of blood."}}, {"canonical_name": "pucho", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "pucha", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "pucha", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["pucha"], "contexts": ["gruel-like preparation"]}, {"entity_id": "pucho", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "pucho", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 4, "variants": ["pucho", "Pucho"], "contexts": ["Malay name for a plant product", "Malay name"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "pucha", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "pucho", "similarity": 0.912}], "id": 3688, "stable_key": "clu_f730c157a13672e4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "pucho", "confidence": "low", "type": "plant", "note": "While 'pucho' is mentioned as a Malay name for a plant product and a gruel-like preparation, it's not readily identifiable as a specific plant or preparation with a clear modern equivalent. It could refer to a variety of processed plant materials."}}, {"canonical_name": "olla", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "VESSEL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "olla", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "olla", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "VESSEL", "count": 1, "variants": ["olla"], "contexts": ["pot for cooking"]}, {"entity_id": "olla", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "olla", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "VESSEL", "count": 4, "variants": ["olla"], "contexts": ["new pot for preparing the root", "pot used for cooking medicine"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "olla", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "olla", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3689, "stable_key": "clu_f58b53377f5bec8c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "pot", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "'Olla' is a common Spanish word for a pot or cauldron, used for cooking and preparing substances.", "wikidata_id": "Q2845", "wikidata_description": "psychoactive herb from the Cannabis plant used for medical or recreational purposes", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cannabis%20%28drug%29"}}, {"canonical_name": "vicio", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "vicios", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "vicios", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["vicios"], "contexts": ["vices or bad habits"]}, {"entity_id": "vicio", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "vicio", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 4, "variants": ["vicio"], "contexts": ["defect or vice", "a vice affecting the body"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "vicios", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "vicio", "similarity": 0.965}], "id": 3690, "stable_key": "clu_ee96c81e6f41732f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "vice", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'vicio' can refer to moral vices or bad habits, as well as a defect or imperfection, fitting the contexts provided."}}, {"canonical_name": "papagayos", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "papagayos", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "papagayos", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["papagayos"], "contexts": ["parrots"]}, {"entity_id": "papagayo", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "papagayo", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["papagayo"], "contexts": ["parrot, a bird"]}, {"entity_id": "papagayos", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "papagayos", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 3, "variants": ["papagayos", "Pagayos"], "contexts": ["parrot meat", "parrot meat eaten"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "papagayos", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "papagayos", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "papagayo", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "papagayos", "similarity": 0.89}], "id": 3691, "stable_key": "clu_f4436c0006ec0fb5", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Papagayos is a village in Chacabuco Department, San Luis Province, Argentina. Among the small towns that are at the feet of the Comechingones Mountains, Papagayos stands out by its particular landscape of caranday palms. It borders Villa Larca to the north, Tilisarao to the west, Villa del Carmen to the south and to the east, C\u00f3rdoba Province. It has around 750 inhabitants. Its climate is mainly dry with a little summer precipitation. There are two theories about Papagayos' name."}}, {"canonical_name": "olho de gato", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "ojos_de_gato", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "ojos de gato", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["ojos de gato"], "contexts": ["cat's eyes stone"]}, {"entity_id": "olho_de_gato", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "olho de gato", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["olho de gato", "Olho de gato", "olhos de gato"], "contexts": ["gemstone with virtues", "gemstone"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "ojos de gato", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "olho de gato", "similarity": 0.95}], "id": 3692, "stable_key": "clu_59710f5a06cf1c99", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cat's Eye", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral"}}, {"canonical_name": "ovo", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "ovos", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "ovos", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["ovos"], "contexts": ["made containers for salt processing"]}, {"entity_id": "ovo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "ovo", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 4, "variants": ["ovo"], "contexts": ["shape comparison", "size comparison for a diamond"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "ovos", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "ovo", "similarity": 0.954}], "id": 3693, "stable_key": "clu_2c07c0216ddd194c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Egg", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "Q9384", "wikidata_description": "internal organ used in the male reproductive system", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Testicle"}}, {"canonical_name": "Pondich\u00e9ry", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "pondich\u00e9ry", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Pondich\u00e9ry", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Pondich\u00e9ry", "Pondicii\u00e9ry"], "contexts": ["independent settlement", "Pondicherry"]}, {"entity_id": "pondich\u00e9ry", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Pondich\u00e9ry", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Pondich\u00e9ry"], "contexts": ["location of cholera epidemics"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Pondich\u00e9ry", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Pondich\u00e9ry", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3694, "stable_key": "clu_a78d89c8f8f81b7d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pondicherry", "confidence": "high", "type": "PLACE", "description": "A city and union territory in India, formerly a French colonial settlement.", "wikipedia_extract": "Pondicherry, officially known as Puducherry, is the capital and most populous city of the Union Territory of Puducherry in India. The city is in the Puducherry district on the southeast coast of India and is surrounded by the Bay of Bengal to the east and the state of Tamil Nadu, with which it shares most of its culture, heritage, and language. Puducherry, formerly known as Pondicherry, gained its significance as \"the French Riviera of the East\" after the advent of French colonialisation in India. Puducherry is the Tamil interpretation of \"new town\" and mainly derives from \"Poduke\", the name of the marketplace or \"port town\" for Roman trade in the 1st century, as mentioned in the Periplus of the Erythraean Sea. The settlement was once an abode of learned scholars versed in the Vedas, hence it was also known as Vedapuri. The history of Puducherry can broadly be classified into two periods: pre-colonial and colonial."}}, {"canonical_name": "r\u00e9gion \u00e9quinoxiale", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "r\u00e9gion_\u00e9quinoxiale", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "r\u00e9gion \u00e9quinoxiale", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 4, "variants": ["r\u00e9gion \u00e9quinoxiale", "R\u00e9gions \u00e9quinoxiales"], "contexts": ["geographic zone of Chandernagor", "equatorial climate zone"]}, {"entity_id": "regions_equinoxiales", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Regions Equinoxiales", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Regions Equinoxiales"], "contexts": ["equatorial regions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "r\u00e9gion \u00e9quinoxiale", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Regions Equinoxiales", "similarity": 0.898}], "id": 3695, "stable_key": "clu_83d0d9cdff3e83cc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Equatorial regions", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Global"}}, {"canonical_name": "Moreau de Jonn\u00e8s", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "moreau_de_jonn\u00e8s", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Moreau de Jonn\u00e8s", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Moreau de Jonn\u00e8s", "Moreau de Jonnes", "Moreau de Jonn\u00e9s"], "contexts": ["mentioned in passage", "author of 'Hist. phys. dct Ant.'"]}, {"entity_id": "moreau_de_jonnes", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Moreau de Jonnes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Moreau de Jonnes"], "contexts": ["author of 'Hist. Phys. des Antilles'"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Moreau de Jonn\u00e8s", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Moreau de Jonnes", "similarity": 0.931}], "id": 3696, "stable_key": "clu_27a48860a32d7fa3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Alexandre Moreau de Jonn\u00e8s", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1778, "death_year": 1870, "description": "French naturalist, physician, and politician; author of works on natural history.", "wikidata_id": "Q2833893", "wikidata_description": "French geologist, officer, physician and politician", "wikipedia_extract": "San Diego de los Ba\u00f1os is a consejo popular (\"popular council\") and urban settlement in Los Palacios, Pinar del R\u00edo Province, Cuba. The consejo popular is in the east of the Pinar del R\u00edo Province. It is located 22 km north-east of Los Palacios, in the Vuelta Abajo area. It borders the consejos populares Entronque de Palacios to the east and Paso Quemado to the south-east. Pinar del R\u00edo is 56 km (by road) to the south-west. It stands near the western end and at the foot of sierra de Rosarios, on the south side of that mountain range.", "wikipedia_url": "https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexandre_Moreau_de_Jonn%C3%A8s"}}, {"canonical_name": "hypersth\u00e9nite", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "hypersth\u00e9nite", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "hypersth\u00e9nite", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["hypersth\u00e9nite", "hypersth\u00e8ne", "hypersth\u00e9ne"], "contexts": ["rock type", "hypersthene mineral"]}, {"entity_id": "hypersthene", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "hypersthene", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["hypersthene"], "contexts": ["mineral component"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "hypersth\u00e9nite", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "hypersthene", "similarity": 0.932}], "id": 3697, "stable_key": "clu_8b55a3e0da0c80b4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hypersthene", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q60706831", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article", "wikipedia_extract": "Hypersthene is a common rock-forming inosilicate mineral belonging to the group of orthorhombic pyroxenes. Its chemical formula is (Mg,Fe)SiO3. It is found in igneous and some metamorphic rocks as well as in stony and iron meteorites. Many references have formally abandoned this term, preferring to categorise this mineral as enstatite or ferrosilite. It forms a solid solution series with the minerals enstatite and ferrosilite, being a mid-way member between the two. Pure enstatite contains no iron, while pure ferrosilite contains no magnesium; hypersthene is the name given to the mineral when a significant amount of both elements are present."}}, {"canonical_name": "livres sterling", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "livres_sterling", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "livres sterling", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 4, "variants": ["livres sterling", "livres sterl."], "contexts": ["currency unit in English colonies", "currency unit for value"]}, {"entity_id": "pounds_sterling", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "pounds sterling", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["pounds sterling"], "contexts": ["more than one hundred pounds sterling"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "livres sterling", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "pounds sterling", "similarity": 0.932}], "id": 3698, "stable_key": "clu_eb3154519a251a74", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "pound sterling", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "Gambey", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "gambey", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Gambey", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Gambey", "Gambon", "M. Gambey"], "contexts": ["instrument maker", "talented instrument maker"]}, {"entity_id": "gambey", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Gambey", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Gambey"], "contexts": ["maker of a declination instrument"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Gambey", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Gambey", "similarity": 0.715}], "id": 3699, "stable_key": "clu_0bd765455c36bf7c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jean-Louis Gambey", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1768, "death_year": 1837, "description": "French instrument maker, known for astronomical instruments"}}, {"canonical_name": "Laet", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "laet", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Laet", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Laet"], "contexts": ["author cited", "author cited in notes"]}, {"entity_id": "laet", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Laet", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Laet"], "contexts": ["cited by Streeter regarding emerald mines"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Laet", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Laet", "similarity": 0.614}], "id": 3700, "stable_key": "clu_8009cd14511344aa", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Johannes de Laet", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1593, "death_year": 1649, "description": "Flemish humanist, geographer, and naturalist", "note": "The citation context regarding emerald mines aligns with his geographical and natural history interests."}}, {"canonical_name": "Quixos", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "quixos", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Quixos", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 4, "variants": ["Quixos"], "contexts": ["city with population 35,000", "province name"]}, {"entity_id": "quixos", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Quixos", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Quixos"], "contexts": ["province"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Quixos", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Quixos", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3701, "stable_key": "clu_346ceb943356430d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Quixos", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Ecuador", "note": "Refers to a historical province and indigenous group in present-day Ecuador."}}, {"canonical_name": "Chiapa", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "chiapa", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Chiapa", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 4, "variants": ["Chiapa"], "contexts": ["province within Guatemala", "Indian city"]}, {"entity_id": "chiapa", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Chiapa", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Chiapa"], "contexts": ["location near Zoyapanco"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Chiapa", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Chiapa", "similarity": 0.793}], "id": 3702, "stable_key": "clu_f1030e06873068e5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Chiapa de Corzo", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Mexico", "note": "The original text refers to 'Chiapa', which historically corresponds to the region encompassing what is now Chiapa de Corzo in Mexico, and has been historically linked to areas within Guatemala."}}, {"canonical_name": "Guancabamba", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "guancabamba", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Guancabamba", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Guancabamba"], "contexts": ["geographic location name", "river entering the Amazon"]}, {"entity_id": "guangamarca", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Guangamarca", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Guangamarca"], "contexts": ["location in South America"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Guancabamba", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Guangamarca", "similarity": 0.881}], "id": 3703, "stable_key": "clu_49720f5ef32eb771", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Huancabamba", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Peru", "note": "The variation 'Guangamarca' is likely an orthographic or transcriptional error for 'Huancabamba', a known location in Peru.", "wikidata_id": "Q18100172", "wikidata_description": "genus of insects"}}, {"canonical_name": "Maryland", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "maryland", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Maryland", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 4, "variants": ["Maryland"], "contexts": ["state mentioned", "confederated state in the United States"]}, {"entity_id": "maryland", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Maryland", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Maryland"], "contexts": ["location where aerolite fell"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Maryland", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Maryland", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3704, "stable_key": "clu_2a367561caca95c2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Maryland", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United States", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_extract": "Maryland (US:   MERR-il-\u0259nd) is a state in the Mid-Atlantic and Southern regions of the United States. It borders the states of Virginia to its south, West Virginia to its west, Pennsylvania to its north, and Delaware to its east, as well as with the Atlantic Ocean to its east, and the national capital and federal district of Washington, D.C. to the southwest. With a total area of 12,407 square miles (32,130 km2), Maryland is the ninth-smallest state by land area, and its population of 6,177,224 ranks it the 19th-most populous state and the fifth-most densely populated. Maryland's capital city is Annapolis, and the state's most populous city is Baltimore. Maryland's coastline was first explored by Europeans in the 16th century. Prior to that, it was inhabited by several Native American tribes, mostly the Algonquian peoples."}}, {"canonical_name": "Astrakhan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "astrakhan", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Astrakhan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Astrakhan"], "contexts": ["government in Piussle", "Wologda et Astrakhan 52"]}, {"entity_id": "astrachan", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Astrachan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Astrachan"], "contexts": ["city with luxuriant fruit"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Astrakhan", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Astrachan", "similarity": 0.917}], "id": 3705, "stable_key": "clu_4ac1e12b8d94a715", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Astrakhan", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Russia", "note": "The spelling 'Astrachan' is an older transliteration of Astrakhan.", "wikidata_id": "Q3927", "wikidata_description": "city in the south of Russia", "wikipedia_extract": "Astrakhan (Russian: \u0410\u0441\u0442\u0440\u0430\u0445\u0430\u043d\u044c, IPA: [\u02c8astr\u0259x\u0259n\u02b2] ) is the largest city and administrative centre of Astrakhan Oblast in southern Russia. The city lies on two banks of the Volga, in the upper part of the Volga Delta, on eleven islands of the Caspian Depression, 100 km (62 mi) from the Caspian Sea, with a population of 475,629 residents at the 2021 Census. At an elevation of 28 meters (92 ft) below sea level, it is the lowest city in Russia. Astrakhan was formerly the capital of the Khanate of Astrakhan (a remnant of the Golden Horde) of the Astrakhan Tatars, and was located on the higher right bank of the Volga, eleven kilometres (6.8 mi) from the present-day city. Situated on caravan and water routes, it developed from a village into a large trading centre, before being conquered by Timur in 1395 and captured by Ivan the Terrible in 1556 and in 1558 it was moved to its present site. The oldest economic and cultural center of the Lower Volga region, it is often called the southernmost outpost of Russia, and the Caspian capital."}}, {"canonical_name": "Guanacas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "guanacas", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Guanacas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Guanacas"], "contexts": ["mountain chain", "Andes passage name"]}, {"entity_id": "guancas", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Guancas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Guancas"], "contexts": ["people of those regions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Guanacas", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Guancas", "similarity": 0.949}], "id": 3706, "stable_key": "clu_cb394adf10013a82", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Guanacas", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Peru", "note": "Likely referring to the Guanacas people or the geographical region associated with them in the Andes, consistent with historical accounts of indigenous populations in South America.", "wikidata_id": "Q690026", "wikidata_description": "province of Costa Rica"}}, {"canonical_name": "Cruces", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "cruces", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Cruces", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Cruces"], "contexts": ["embarcadero", "location on the isthmus"]}, {"entity_id": "cross", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Cross", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cross"], "contexts": ["region in the heavens"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Cruces", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Cross", "similarity": 0.887}], "id": 3707, "stable_key": "clu_ac77099d765f5324", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cruces", "confidence": "medium", "type": "place", "country": "Guatemala", "note": "Contexts suggest multiple meanings. 'Cruces' could refer to a place (likely in the Americas, given Humboldt's work) or astronomical references ('a Crucis'). Without more context, pinpointing a single referent is difficult. The primary referent in the text seems to be a place name."}}, {"canonical_name": "Franconie", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "franconie", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Franconie", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 4, "variants": ["Franconie"], "contexts": ["historical region in Germany", "region containing Ochsenkopf"]}, {"entity_id": "franconia", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Franconia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Franconia"], "contexts": ["region producing wines"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Franconie", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Franconia", "similarity": 0.988}], "id": 3708, "stable_key": "clu_7f64b5502206a3ee", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Franconia", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Historical region in northern Bavaria, Germany.", "note": "'Frangu\u00edstan' appears to be a transliteration of the region.", "wikipedia_extract": "Franconia (German: Franken [\u02c8f\u0281a\u014bkn\u0329] ; East Franconian: Franggn [\u02c8fr\u0251\u014b\u0261\u014b\u030d]; Bavarian: Frankn) is a geographical region of Germany, characterised by its culture and East Franconian dialect (Ostfr\u00e4nkisch). Franconia is made up of the three Regierungsbezirke (governmental districts) of Lower, Middle and Upper Franconia in Bavaria, the adjacent, Franconian-speaking South Thuringia, south of the Thuringian Forest\u2014which constitutes the language boundary between Franconian and Thuringian\u2014and the eastern parts of Heilbronn-Franconia in Baden-W\u00fcrttemberg. Those parts of the Vogtland lying in Saxony (largest city: Plauen) are sometimes regarded as Franconian as well, because the Vogtlandian dialects are mostly East Franconian. The inhabitants of Saxon Vogtland, however, mostly do not consider themselves Franconian. On the other hand, the inhabitants of the Hessian-speaking parts of Lower Franconia west of the Spessart (largest city: Aschaffenburg) do consider themselves Franconian, although not speaking the dialect. Heilbronn-Franconia's largest city of Heilbronn and its surrounding areas are South Franconian-speaking, and therefore only sometimes regarded as Franconian."}}, {"canonical_name": "cent.", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "cent_", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "cent.", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 4, "variants": ["cent."], "contexts": ["temperature unit", "unit of temperature measurement"]}, {"entity_id": "cent_", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Cent.", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cent."], "contexts": ["unit of temperature measurement"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "cent.", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Cent.", "similarity": 0.979}], "id": 3709, "stable_key": "clu_c0363be539cf734b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Celsius", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to a unit of temperature, likely Celsius given the historical context and the mention of temperature measurement.", "wikipedia_extract": "The degree Celsius is the unit of temperature on the Celsius temperature scale (originally known as the centigrade scale in English), one of two temperature scales used in the International System of Units (SI), the other being the closely related Kelvin scale. The degree Celsius (symbol: \u00b0C) can refer to a specific point on the Celsius temperature scale or to a difference or range between two temperatures. It is named after the Swedish astronomer Anders Celsius (1701\u20131744), who proposed the first version of it in 1742. The unit was called centigrade in several languages (from the Latin centum, which means 100, and gradus, which means steps) for many years. In 1948, the International Committee for Weights and Measures renamed it to honor Celsius and also to remove confusion with the term for one hundredth of a gradian in some languages. Most countries use this scale, with the exception of the United States, some island territories, and Liberia, where the Fahrenheit scale is still used."}}, {"canonical_name": "S\u00e9n\u00e9gal", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "s\u00e9n\u00e9gal", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "S\u00e9n\u00e9gal", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 4, "variants": ["S\u00e9n\u00e9gal"], "contexts": ["location of observations", "location of barometric observations"]}, {"entity_id": "senegal", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Senegal", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Senegal"], "contexts": ["river where Bichir is found"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "S\u00e9n\u00e9gal", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Senegal", "similarity": 0.749}], "id": 3710, "stable_key": "clu_f91cf3fb452dbdfb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Senegal", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Senegal", "wikidata_id": "Q3775608", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants"}}, {"canonical_name": "\u00eeles Canaries", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "\u00eeles_canaries", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "\u00eeles Canaries", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["\u00eeles Canaries"], "contexts": ["islands", "islands with low barometric variations"]}, {"entity_id": "iles_canaries", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Iles Canaries", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Iles Canaries"], "contexts": ["islands described in a book"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "\u00eeles Canaries", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Iles Canaries", "similarity": 0.989}], "id": 3711, "stable_key": "clu_b569efb86286465f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Canary Islands", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Spain", "note": "Direct translation of the German name for the archipelago.", "wikidata_id": "Q5813", "wikidata_description": "autonomous community of Spain, and archipelago in the Atlantic Ocean", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canary%20Islands", "wikipedia_extract": "The Canary Islands ( ; Spanish: Canarias [ka\u02c8na\u027ejas] ) or Canaries are an archipelago in the Atlantic Ocean and the southernmost autonomous community of Spain, located about 100 kilometres (60 mi) off the northwest coast of Africa. The archipelago has a population of approximately 2.27 million inhabitants, making it the most populous overseas special territory of the European Union. The seven main islands, listed from largest to smallest by area, are Tenerife, Fuerteventura, Gran Canaria, Lanzarote, La Palma, La Gomera, and El Hierro. The only other inhabited island is La Graciosa, which is administratively dependent on Lanzarote. The archipelago also includes numerous smaller islands and islets, such as Alegranza, Isla de Lobos, Monta\u00f1a Clara, Roque del Oeste, and Roque del Este, as well as various rocks, including Garachico and Anaga. Historically, the island chain was referred to as \"the Fortunate Isles\"."}}, {"canonical_name": "Toulouse", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "toulouse", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Toulouse", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Toulouse"], "contexts": ["city where observations were made", "city with barometric observations"]}, {"entity_id": "toulouse", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Toulouse", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Toulouse"], "contexts": ["location of observatory"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Toulouse", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Toulouse", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3712, "stable_key": "clu_25c87b5338faf59f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Toulouse", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A city in southern France, historically significant for its printing and scientific observations.", "wikipedia_extract": "Toulouse (, too-LOOZ, French: [tuluz] ; Occitan: Tolosa [tu\u02c8luz\u0254] ) is a city in Southern France, the prefecture of the Haute-Garonne department and of the Occitania region. The city is on the banks of the River Garonne, 150 kilometres (93 miles) from the Mediterranean Sea, 230 km (143 mi) from the Atlantic Ocean and 680 km (420 mi) from Paris. It is the fourth-largest city in France after Paris, Marseille, and Lyon, with 511,684 inhabitants within its municipal boundaries (2022); its metropolitan area has a population of 1,513,396 inhabitants (2022). Toulouse is the central city of one of the 22 metropolitan councils of France. Between the 2014 and 2020 censuses, its metropolitan area was the third fastest growing among metropolitan areas larger than 500,000 inhabitants in France. Toulouse is the centre of the European aerospace industry, with the headquarters of Airbus, the SPOT satellite system, ATR and the Aerospace Valley."}}, {"canonical_name": "HOMMES", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "hommes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "HOMMES", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 4, "variants": ["hommes", "HOMMES"], "contexts": ["male enslaved people", "category of people counted"]}, {"entity_id": "humans", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "humans", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["humans"], "contexts": ["human beings, people"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "HOMMES", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "humans", "similarity": 0.916}], "id": 3713, "stable_key": "clu_5dc20acef445f831", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Humans", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "While 'HOMMES' can refer to men, in the context of Humboldt's scientific writings and James's psychology, it refers to people or humankind generally."}}, {"canonical_name": "Wales", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "wales", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Wales", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Wales"], "contexts": ["early modern scholar", "source of published observations"]}, {"entity_id": "welsh", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Welsh", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Welsh"], "contexts": ["made observations during balloon ascents"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Wales", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Welsh", "similarity": 0.909}], "id": 3714, "stable_key": "clu_2d25acfbe960e4c3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "John Wales", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "Early modern scholar who made balloon ascent observations.", "note": "The context points to an individual scholar named Wales, likely English, associated with scientific observations, possibly related to balloon ascents as a historical practice of scientific inquiry."}}, {"canonical_name": "Essai polit, sur la Nouvelle-Espagne", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "essai_polit,_sur_la_nouvelle-espagne", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Essai polit, sur la Nouvelle-Espagne", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Essai polit, sur la Nouvelle-Espagne", "Essai polilique sur la Nouvelle-Espagne"], "contexts": ["title of a work", "political essay title"]}, {"entity_id": "essai_polit__sur_la_nouv__espagne", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Essai Polit. sur la Nouv. Espagne", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Essai Polit. sur la Nouv. Espagne"], "contexts": ["political essay on New Spain"]}, {"entity_id": "essai_politique_sur_la_nouvelle_espagne", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Essai Politique sur la Nouvelle Espagne", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Essai Politique sur la Nouvelle Espagne"], "contexts": ["work by Humboldt on New Spain"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Essai polit, sur la Nouvelle-Espagne", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Essai Polit. sur la Nouv. Espagne", "similarity": 0.904}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Essai polit, sur la Nouvelle-Espagne", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Essai Politique sur la Nouvelle Espagne", "similarity": 0.883}], "id": 3715, "stable_key": "clu_35e4728bd214c33c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Political Essay on the Kingdom of New Spain", "confidence": "high", "type": "text", "note": "This is a direct translation and title of a major work by Alexander von Humboldt."}}, {"canonical_name": "Castille", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "castille", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Castille", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Castille", "Castilla del Oro"], "contexts": ["kingdom in Spain", "unit of weight for gold"]}, {"entity_id": "castalia", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Castalia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Castalia"], "contexts": ["spring at Delphi", "spring at the foot of Phaedriadae"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Castille", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Castalia", "similarity": 0.894}], "id": 3716, "stable_key": "clu_2842128dcd8c23a6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Castile", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Spain", "note": "The context 'spring at Delphi' might refer to Castalia, a spring at Delphi, but the dominant references point to the historical region in Spain, especially given the context of 'kingdom in Spain'. Humboldt's 'Relation historique' and 'Kosmos' would primarily use 'Castille' for the Spanish region.", "wikidata_id": "Q217196", "wikidata_description": "former country", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crown_of_Castile", "wikipedia_extract": "On July 6, 2016, Philando Castile, a 32-year-old African American man, was fatally shot during a traffic stop by police officer Jeronimo Yanez of the St. Anthony police department in the Minneapolis\u2013Saint Paul metropolitan area. About 9 p.m., Castile was driving with his girlfriend, Diamond Reynolds, and her four-year-old daughter when he was pulled over by Yanez and another officer in Falcon Heights, a suburb of Saint Paul, Minnesota. Castile, who was licensed to carry a firearm, told Yanez that he had a firearm. Yanez replied, \"Don't reach for it then\". Castile responded, \"I'm, I, I was reaching for...\", to which Yanez replied, \"Don't pull it out\"."}}, {"canonical_name": "Leonhard", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "leonhard", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Leonhard", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Leonhard", "Leonliard"], "contexts": ["author of rock characteristics", "author of Characteristik der Fctsarten"]}, {"entity_id": "leonhard", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Leonhard", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Leonhard"], "contexts": ["editor of publication", "author of geological works"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Leonhard", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Leonhard", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3717, "stable_key": "clu_29cbf3653b32f537", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Karl Leonhard", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1775, "death_year": 1833, "description": "Geologist and mineralogist, author of 'Rock Characteristics'.", "note": "This refers to Karl Leonhard, a prominent German geologist and mineralogist, known for his classifications and publications.", "wikidata_id": "Q112258", "wikidata_description": "Austrian philosopher", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karl%20Leonhard%20Reinhold", "wikipedia_extract": "Karl Leonhard Reinhold (; Austrian German: [\u02c8ra\u026anh\u0254ld]; 26 October 1757 \u2013 10 April 1823) was an Austrian philosopher who helped to popularise the work of Immanuel Kant in the late 18th century. His \"elementary philosophy\" (Elementarphilosophie) also influenced German idealism, notably Johann Gottlieb Fichte, as a critical system grounded in a fundamental first principle. He was the father of Ernst Christian Gottlieb Reinhold (1793\u20131855), also a philosopher. Reinhold was born in Vienna. In late 1772, at the age of fourteen, he entered the Jesuit (Roman Catholic) seminary St. Anne's Church, Vienna (Jesuitenkollegium St."}}, {"canonical_name": "S. M.C.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "s__m_c_", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "S. M.C.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 3, "variants": ["S. M.C.", "S. M.", "S. M. C."], "contexts": ["His Catholic Majesty", "refers to His Majesty"]}, {"entity_id": "s_a_", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "S.A.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 2, "variants": ["S.A."], "contexts": ["place with gardens of aromatic reed", "his Highness's gardens"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "S. M.C.", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "S.A.", "similarity": 0.862}], "id": 3718, "stable_key": "clu_90a8ecd63eef003c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Philip II of Spain", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1527, "death_year": 1598, "description": "King of Spain, patron of arts and sciences", "note": "The abbreviation 'S. M.C.' (Su Majestad Cat\u00f3lica) and context of 'His Catholic Majesty' strongly suggests Philip II, a prominent patron of exploration and science during the period."}}, {"canonical_name": "San Pedro", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "san_pedro", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "San Pedro", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["San Pedro"], "contexts": ["location within Caracas", "place name in Venezuela"]}, {"entity_id": "rio_de_san_pedro", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Rio de San Pedro", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rio de San Pedro"], "contexts": ["river in Ecuador"]}, {"entity_id": "rio_de_san_pedro", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Rio de San Pedro", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rio de San Pedro"], "contexts": ["river that disappeared and reappeared"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "San Pedro", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Rio de San Pedro", "similarity": 0.883}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Rio de San Pedro", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Rio de San Pedro", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3719, "stable_key": "clu_4ec3e8a16b3235a0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "R\u00edo San Pedro", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Ecuador", "note": "Multiple mentions of 'Rio de San Pedro' in proximity to Ecuador and disappearing/reappearing phenomena point to this specific river."}}, {"canonical_name": "Prague", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "prague", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Prague", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Prague"], "contexts": ["city", "location of herbaria"]}, {"entity_id": "prague", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Prague", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Prague"], "contexts": ["European city observing magnetic disturbance", "location of magnetic storm observation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Prague", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Prague", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3720, "stable_key": "clu_4cb9c4ad79549c34", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Prague", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Czech Republic", "wikidata_id": "Q1085", "wikidata_description": "capital city of the Czech Republic", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prague", "wikipedia_extract": "Prague ( PRAHG; Czech: Praha [\u02c8pra\u0266a] ) is the capital and largest city of the Czech Republic and the historical capital of Bohemia. Prague, located on the Vltava River, has a population of about 1.4 million, while its metropolitan area is home to approximately 2.3 million people. Prague is a historical city with Romanesque, Gothic, Renaissance, and Baroque architecture. It was the capital of the Kingdom of Bohemia and residence of several Holy Roman Emperors, most notably Charles IV (r. 1346\u20131378) and Rudolf II (r. 1575\u20131611)."}}, {"canonical_name": "Texas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "texas", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Texas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 3, "variants": ["Texas"], "contexts": ["region in North America", "lateral chains of Texas"]}, {"entity_id": "texas", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Texas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Texas"], "contexts": ["US state with deserts", "location with deserts causing monsoons"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Texas", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Texas", "similarity": 0.786}], "id": 3721, "stable_key": "clu_f26a8101887238b1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Texas", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United States", "note": "Consistent references to 'Texas' in North America, including desert regions, confirm the modern referent."}}, {"canonical_name": "roches volcaniques", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "roches_volcaniques", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "roches volcaniques", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["roches volcaniques"], "contexts": ["rocks forming the southern edge of terrain", "type of rocks in South America"]}, {"entity_id": "volcanic_eruptive_rocks", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "volcanic eruptive rocks", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["volcanic eruptive rocks"], "contexts": ["rocks from volcanic eruptions"]}, {"entity_id": "volcanic_rock", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "volcanic rock", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["volcanic rock"], "contexts": ["rock formed by volcanic activity"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "roches volcaniques", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "volcanic eruptive rocks", "similarity": 0.927}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "roches volcaniques", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "volcanic rock", "similarity": 0.966}], "id": 3722, "stable_key": "clu_1db14945b8a483d4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Volcanic rocks", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "The terms 'roches volcaniques', 'volcanic eruptive rocks', and 'volcanic rock' all refer to the same category of geological material.", "wikidata_id": "Q57846654", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article by R. Hekinian et al published 1976 in Contributions to Mineralogy and Petrology"}}, {"canonical_name": "janvier", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "janvier", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "janvier", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["janvier", "Janvier"], "contexts": ["driest month with no rain", "month"]}, {"entity_id": "january", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "January", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["January"], "contexts": ["month of observation", "epoch of beginning the year"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "janvier", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "January", "similarity": 0.971}], "id": 3723, "stable_key": "clu_9ff8e0df9762d0f0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "January", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q108", "wikidata_description": "first mo