e_name": "S C", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "S C", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3761, "stable_key": "clu_636f092e86daff7c-4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Spinal cord", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "While 'spinal column' and 'spinal segment' are related, the primary referent is the 'spinal cord' given the frequency and context of 'central nervous system pathway'.", "wikidata_id": "Q2997445", "wikidata_description": "body organ", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anterior%20grey%20column", "description": "The central nerve pathway connecting the brain to the body.", "wikipedia_extract": "The spinal cord is a long, thin, tubular structure made up of nervous tissue that extends from the medulla oblongata in the lower brainstem to the lumbar region of the vertebral column (backbone) of vertebrate animals. The center of the spinal cord is hollow and contains a structure called the central canal, which contains cerebrospinal fluid. The spinal cord is also covered by the meninges and enclosed by the neural arches. Together, the brain and spinal cord make up the central nervous system. In humans, the spinal cord is a continuation of the brainstem and anatomically begins at the occipital bone, passing out of the foramen magnum and then enters the spinal canal at the beginning of the cervical vertebrae. The spinal cord extends down to between the first and second lumbar vertebrae, where it tapers to become the cauda equina."}}, {"canonical_name": "triangle", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "triangles", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "triangles", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["triangles"], "contexts": ["three-sided shapes in a diagram", "geometric shapes with three sides"]}, {"entity_id": "triangle", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "triangle", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["triangle"], "contexts": ["geometric shape used for measurement", "geometric shape used in calculations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "triangles", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "triangle", "similarity": 0.894}], "id": 3762, "stable_key": "clu_79f4f23310ea77d2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "triangle", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "vocal organs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "vocal_organs", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "vocal organs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["vocal organs"], "contexts": ["innervated bilaterally for speech", "organs responsible for producing sound"]}, {"entity_id": "speech-organs", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "speech-organs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["speech-organs"], "contexts": ["organs for producing speech"]}, {"entity_id": "vocal_muscles", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "vocal muscles", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["vocal muscles"], "contexts": ["muscles used for voice production"]}, {"entity_id": "vocal_apparatus", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "vocal apparatus", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["vocal apparatus"], "contexts": ["structures for producing voice"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "vocal organs", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "vocal muscles", "similarity": 0.946}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "s