"disorders", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "disorders", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["disorders"], "contexts": ["medical conditions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "disorders", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "disorder", "similarity": 0.955}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "disorders", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "disorders", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "pathological disturbance", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "disorder", "similarity": 0.938}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "pathological disturbance", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "disorders", "similarity": 0.916}], "id": 3770, "stable_key": "clu_635cc23af1212d4d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Disorders", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Disorders", "note": "This term is very general and can refer to a wide range of medical or psychological conditions. In the context of 'Principles of Psychology', it likely refers to mental disorders.", "wikidata_id": "Q620639", "wikidata_description": "any disease or malfunction, mostly of neurogenic origin, of the autonomic nervous system"}}, {"canonical_name": "Vienna", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "vienna", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Vienna", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Vienna"], "contexts": ["city where pamphlet was published"]}, {"entity_id": "vienna", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Vienna", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Vienna"], "contexts": ["location of Kempelen", "city of Dr. Berre"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Vienna", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Vienna", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3771, "stable_key": "clu_c139abb923e4c1be", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Vienna", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Austria", "note": "The mention of 'Kempelen' (likely Wolfgang von Kempelen, inventor of the Mechanical Turk) points to Vienna. 'Vienne' in French can refer to the river or a department, but in the context of scientific inquiry and publications, Vienna is the most probable city.", "wikidata_id": "Q577544", "wikidata_description": "city in Johnson County, Illinois, United States", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vienna%2C%20Illinois", "wikipedia_extract": "Vienna (  vee-EN-\u0259; German: Wien [vi\u02d0n] ; Austro-Bavarian: Wean [ve\u0250\u032fn]) is the capital, most populous city, and one of the nine states of Austria. It is Austria's primate city, with just over two million inhabitants. Its larger metropolitan area has a population of nearly 2.9 million, representing nearly one-third of the country's population. Vienna is the cultural, economic, and political centre of the country, the fifth-largest city by population in the European Union, and the most populous of the cities on the river Danube. The city lies on the eastern edge of the Vienna Woods (Wienerwald), the northeasternmost foothills of the Alps, that separate Vienna from the more western parts of Austria, at the transition to the Pannonian Basin. It sits on the Danube, and is traversed by the highly regulated Wienfluss (Vienna River)."}}, {"canonical_name": "nervous energy", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "nervous_matter", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "nervous matter", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["nervous matter"], "contexts": ["tissue of the nervous system"]}, {"entity_id": "nerve-force", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "nerve-force", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["nerve-force"], "contexts": ["physiological energy in nerves"]}, {"entity_id": "nervous_energy", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "nervous energy", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 3, "variants": ["nervous energy"], "contexts": ["energy associated with nervous system", "vital force in the nervous system"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "nervous matter", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "nervous energy", "similarity": 0.929}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "nerve-force", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "nervous energy", "similarity": 0.922}], "id": 3772, "stable_key": "clu_5e80acd61966c2e2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "nervous energy", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q91484614", "wikidata_description": "scientific article published on 01 October 1885"}}, {"canonical_name": "Doudos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "idiots", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "idiots", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["idiots"], "contexts": ["individuals with intellectual disability"]}, {"entity_id": "doudos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Doudos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Doudos", "Doudo", "doudos"], "contexts": ["madness", "mentally ill people"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "idiots", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Doudos", "similarity": 0.929}], "id": 3773, "stable_key": "clu_4a916fe2fbccb46e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "madness", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "A severe mental disorder characterized by loss of contact with reality.", "wikipedia_extract": "The history of medicine is both a study of medicine throughout history as well as a multidisciplinary field of study that seeks to explore and understand medical practices, both past and present, throughout human societies. The history of medicine is the study and documentation of the evolution of medical treatments, practices, and knowledge over time. Medical historians often draw from other humanities fields of study including economics, health sciences, sociology, and politics to better understand the institutions, practices, people, professions, and social systems that have shaped medicine. When a period which predates or lacks written sources regarding medicine, information is instead drawn from archaeological sources. This field tracks the evolution of human societies' approach to health, illness, and injury ranging from prehistory to the modern day, the events that shape these approaches, and their impact on populations. Early medical traditions include those of Babylon, Egypt, China and India."}}, {"canonical_name": "Dublin", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "dublin", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Dublin", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Dublin"], "contexts": ["city where Maguire was professor"]}, {"entity_id": "dublin", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Dublin", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Dublin"], "contexts": ["city with moderate temperatures", "capital city of Ireland"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Dublin", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Dublin", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3774, "stable_key": "clu_4f5dbd39893bd4b6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Dublin", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Ireland", "wikidata_id": "Q1761", "wikidata_description": "capital and largest city of Ireland", "wikipedia_extract": "Dublin is the capital and largest city of Ireland. Situated on Dublin Bay at the mouth of the River Liffey, it is in the province of Leinster, and is bordered on the south by the Dublin Mountains, part of the Wicklow Mountains range. Dublin is the largest city by population on the island of Ireland; at the 2022 census, the city council area had a population of 592,713, while the city, including suburbs, had a population of 1,263,219, and County Dublin had a population of 1,501,500. Various definitions of a metropolitan Greater Dublin Area exist. A settlement was established in the area by the Gaels during or before the 7th century, followed by the Vikings. As the Kingdom of Dublin grew, it became Ireland's principal settlement by the 12th century, following the Anglo-Norman invasion of Ireland.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dublin"}}, {"canonical_name": "Horologium", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "clockwork", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "clockwork", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["clockwork"], "contexts": ["mechanism for timing"]}, {"entity_id": "horologium", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Horologium", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Horologium"], "contexts": ["constellation with nebulous patches", "astronomical constellation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "clockwork", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Horologium", "similarity": 0.943}], "id": 3775, "stable_key": "clu_7479b319b93e9275", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Horologium", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "Context suggests both the astronomical constellation and potentially a clockwork mechanism, but 'Horologium' is the specific historical term.", "wikidata_id": "Q10574", "wikidata_description": "constellation in the southern celestial hemisphere"}}, {"canonical_name": "Quintiliopa", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "quintilian", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Quintilian", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Quintilian"], "contexts": ["ancient rhetorician quoted on attention and memory"]}, {"entity_id": "quintiliopa", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Quintiliopa", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Quintiliopa", "Quintilio.", "Quintiliode", "Quintitio"], "contexts": ["cited for emetics in delirium", "ancient authority for purging humor"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Quintilian", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Quintiliopa", "similarity": 0.851}], "id": 3776, "stable_key": "clu_1ff02d569daec5ed", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Quintilian", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Roman rhetorician and teacher, influential in classical education.", "note": "The texts mention Quintiliopa and Quintilian. Quintilian is the correct identification of the classical authority.", "wikipedia_extract": "Marcus Fabius Quintilianus (Latin: [k\u02b7i\u02d0nt\u026ali\u02c8a\u02d0n\u028as]; c.\u200935 \u2013 c.\u2009100 AD) was a Roman educator and rhetorician born in Hispania, widely referred to in medieval schools of rhetoric and in Renaissance writing. In English translation, he is usually referred to as Quintilian ( kwin-TIL-ee-\u0259n), although the alternate spellings of Quintillian and Quinctilian are occasionally seen, the latter in older texts. Quintilian was born c. 35 AD in Calagurris (Calahorra, La Rioja) in Hispania. His father, a well-educated man, sent him to Rome to study rhetoric early in the reign of Nero. While there, he cultivated a relationship with Domitius Afer, who died in 59. \"It had always been the custom \u2026 for young men with ambitions in public life to fix upon some older model of their ambition \u2026 and regard him as a mentor\". Quintilian evidently adopted Afer as his model and listened to him speak and plead cases in the law courts."}}, {"canonical_name": "Dumas", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "dumas", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Dumas", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Dumas"], "contexts": ["author of 'The Three Guardsmen'"]}, {"entity_id": "dumas", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Dumas", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Dumas"], "contexts": ["scientist who wrote a paper", "contributed to atmospheric analysis"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Dumas", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Dumas", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3777, "stable_key": "clu_ddb2b16194a3671c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Alexandre Dumas", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1802, "death_year": 1870, "description": "French writer, author of 'The Three Musketeers'.", "note": "The context 'scientist who wrote a paper' is likely a misinterpretation or refers to a different Dumas. However, the primary association is with the famous author, which fits the provided example of 'The Three Guardsmen'.", "wikidata_id": "Q38337", "wikidata_description": "French writer and dramatist (1802\u20131870)"}}, {"canonical_name": "Chronology", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "chronologic_order", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "chronologic order", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["chronologic order"], "contexts": ["sequence of events in time"]}, {"entity_id": "chronology", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Chronology", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["chronology", "Chronology"], "contexts": ["science of dating events", "dependent on astronomy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "chronologic order", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Chronology", "similarity": 0.908}], "id": 3778, "stable_key": "clu_9386e160cd8ba57a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Chronology", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the study of historical events in chronological order."}}, {"canonical_name": "Carlini", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "carlini", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Carlini", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 4, "variants": ["Carlini"], "contexts": ["measured from Mount Columbier", "measured Mount Columbier in 1821"]}, {"entity_id": "carletti", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Carletti", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Carletti"], "contexts": ["Italian author who used 'mordicin'"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Carlini", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Carletti", "similarity": 0.867}], "id": 3779, "stable_key": "clu_9a5b0d0fe413589f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Francesco Carlini", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1783, "death_year": 1862, "description": "Italian astronomer and geodeticist, measured Mount Columbier.", "note": "The reference to 'Carletti' and 'mordicin' is likely a separate, but possibly related, historical figure or term in the context of early exploration and botany. However, 'Carlini' is strongly associated with scientific measurement and Mount Columbier."}}, {"canonical_name": "Serpentarius", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "serpentarius", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Serpentarius", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 4, "variants": ["Serpentarius"], "contexts": ["constellation with a star", "constellation with a starless region"]}, {"entity_id": "\u03c0_serpentarii", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "\u03c0 Serpentarii", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["\u03c0 Serpentarii"], "contexts": ["double star system"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Serpentarius", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "\u03c0 Serpentarii", "similarity": 0.888}], "id": 3780, "stable_key": "clu_71d3c061273f65a7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Serpens", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "note": "The mention of '\u03c0 Serpentarii' confirms the astronomical context.", "wikipedia_extract": "Ophiuchus () is a large constellation straddling the celestial equator. Its name comes from the Ancient Greek \u1f40\u03c6\u03b9\u03bf\u1fe6\u03c7\u03bf\u03c2 (ophio\u00fbkhos), meaning \"serpent-bearer\", and it is commonly represented as a man grasping a snake. The serpent is represented by the constellation Serpens. Ophiuchus was one of the 48 constellations listed by the 2nd-century astronomer Ptolemy, and it remains one of the 88 modern constellations. An old alternative name for the constellation was Serpentarius. Ophiuchus lies between Aquila, Serpens, Scorpius, Sagittarius, and Hercules, northwest of the center of the Milky Way.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ophiuchus"}}, {"canonical_name": "electric tension", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "electric_tension", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "electric tension", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 4, "variants": ["electric tension"], "contexts": ["electrical potential", "electrical potential in atmosphere"]}, {"entity_id": "electric_tension", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "electric tension", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["electric tension"], "contexts": ["electrical potential difference"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "electric tension", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "electric tension", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3781, "stable_key": "clu_0a705d4c218b986c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Electric potential difference", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "Scythia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "scythia", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Scythia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 4, "variants": ["Scythia"], "contexts": ["ancient region described as earthquake-free", "ancient geographical region"]}, {"entity_id": "scythia", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Scythia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Scythia"], "contexts": ["region where jacinthos were found"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Scythia", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Scythia", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3782, "stable_key": "clu_5c1b5696cec58599", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Scythia", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Ukraine", "note": "Ancient region, modern country is an approximation.", "wikidata_id": "Q10664835", "wikidata_description": "genus", "wikipedia_extract": "Scythia (UK: , also US: ) or Scythica (UK: , also US: ) was a geographic region defined in the ancient Graeco-Roman world that encompassed the Pontic steppe. It was inhabited by Scythians, an ancient Eastern Iranian equestrian nomadic people. The names Scythia and Scythica are themselves Latinisations of the Ancient Greek names Skuthia (\u03a3\u03ba\u03c5\u03b8\u03af\u03b1) and Skuthik\u0113 (\u03a3\u03ba\u03c5\u03b8\u03b9\u03ba\u03ae), which were themselves derived from the ancient Greek names for the Scythians, Skuth\u0113s (\u03a3\u03ba\u03cd\u03b8\u03b7\u03c2) and Skuthoi (\u03a3\u03ba\u03cd\u03b8\u03bf\u03b9), derived from the Scythian endonym *Sku\u03b4a. The territory of the Scythian kingdom of the Pontic steppe extended from the Don river in the east to the Danube river in the west, and covered the territory of the treeless steppe immediately north of the Black Sea's coastline, which was inhabited by nomadic pastoralists, as well as the fertile black-earth forest-steppe area to the north of the treeless steppe, which was inhabited by an agricultural population. The northern border of this Scythian kingdom were the deciduous woodlands, while several rivers, including Don and Dnipro, flowed southwards across this region and emptied into the Black Sea. Between the 9th and 5th centuries BC, the climate in the steppes was cool and dry, which was a catalyst for the emergence of equestrian nomadic pastoralism in the northern Pontic region.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scythia"}}, {"canonical_name": "corals", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "corals", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "corals", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 4, "variants": ["corals"], "contexts": ["marine invertebrate animals", "marine invertebrates"]}, {"entity_id": "corals", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "corals", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["corals"], "contexts": ["marine invertebrates forming reefs"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "corals", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "corals", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3783, "stable_key": "clu_aec819706fa4c6aa", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Coral", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "Corals are marine invertebrates, and the context of reef formation is characteristic.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}}, {"canonical_name": "Silesia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "silesia", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Silesia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 3, "variants": ["Silesia"], "contexts": ["region containing Schneekoppe", "region in Europe"]}, {"entity_id": "silesia", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Silesia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Silesia"], "contexts": ["sandy plains where fulgorites were dug", "fulgorites from"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Silesia", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Silesia", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3784, "stable_key": "clu_db68b9181c21f9d4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Silesia", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Poland, Germany, Czech Republic", "wikidata_id": "Q81720", "wikidata_description": "region of Central Europe now located mostly in Poland, with small parts in the Czech Republic and Germany"}}, {"canonical_name": "Siena", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "siena", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Siena", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Siena"], "contexts": ["location of meteoric stone fall", "location of celebrated fall in 1794"]}, {"entity_id": "sienna", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Sienna", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Sienna"], "contexts": ["origin of Pietro Andrea Mattioli"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Siena", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Sienna", "similarity": 0.943}], "id": 3785, "stable_key": "clu_b4a67fb53666b750", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Siena", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Italy", "note": "The spelling 'Sienna' is a common variant and refers to the same place.", "wikidata_id": "Q2751", "wikidata_description": "city in Tuscany, Italy and capital of the province of Siena", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siena", "wikipedia_extract": "Siena ( see-EN-\u0259, Italian: [\u02c8sj\u025b\u02d0na, \u02c8sje\u02d0na] ; traditionally spelled Sienna in English; Latin: Saena Iulia) is a city in Tuscany, in Central Italy, and the capital of the province of Siena. With a population of 52,991, it is the 12th-largest city in the region as of 2025. The city is historically linked to commercial and banking activities, having been a major banking centre until the 13th and 14th centuries. Siena is also home to the oldest bank in the world, the Monte dei Paschi, which has been operating continuously since 1472 (1472). Several significant Mediaeval and Renaissance painters were born and worked in Siena, including Duccio di Buoninsegna, Ambrogio Lorenzetti, Simone Martini and Sassetta, and they influenced the course of Italian and European art. The University of Siena, originally called Studium Senese, was founded in 1240, making it one of the oldest universities in continuous operation in the world."}}, {"canonical_name": "Haidinger", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "haidinger", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Haidinger", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Haidinger"], "contexts": ["author of 'Bericht \u00fcber Szlaniczer Sch\u00fcrfe in Ungarn.'", "contributor to scientific society"]}, {"entity_id": "haidinger", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Haidinger", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Haidinger"], "contexts": ["experimenter on crystallisation and water", "experiments proving water part of crystals"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Haidinger", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Haidinger", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3786, "stable_key": "clu_83598a4c30a8d81d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Wilhelm Haidinger", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1795, "death_year": 1871, "description": "Austrian mineralogist and physicist, crystal optics.", "wikidata_id": "Q78686", "wikidata_description": "Austrian mineralogist, geologist, and physicist (1795\u20131871)"}}, {"canonical_name": "earth's surface", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "earth's_surface", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "earth's surface", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Earth's surface", "earth's surface"], "contexts": ["the outer layer of the planet", "outer layer of the planet"]}, {"entity_id": "earth\u2019s_surface", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "earth\u2019s surface", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["earth\u2019s surface"], "contexts": ["outer layer of planet", "outer layer of the planet"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "earth's surface", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "earth\u2019s surface", "similarity": 0.999}], "id": 3787, "stable_key": "clu_73b24c5c85b624f4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Earth's surface", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q1349417", "wikidata_description": "term for the surface of the Earth"}}, {"canonical_name": "Toronto", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "toronto", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Toronto", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 3, "variants": ["Toronto"], "contexts": ["Canadian city observing magnetic disturbance", "location of magnetic storm observation"]}, {"entity_id": "toronto", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Toronto", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Toronto"], "contexts": ["city in Upper Canada", "location of magnetic storm observations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Toronto", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Toronto", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3788, "stable_key": "clu_f5e75ae1bbcc206d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Toronto", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Canada", "wikidata_id": "Q172", "wikidata_description": "capital and largest city of the province of Ontario, Canada"}}, {"canonical_name": "hot springs", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "hot_springs", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "hot springs", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["hot springs", "Hot Springs"], "contexts": ["springs with naturally heated water", "geothermal water sources"]}, {"entity_id": "hot_springs", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "hot springs", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["hot springs"], "contexts": ["geothermal features with high temperature", "geothermal features emitting hot water"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "hot springs", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "hot springs", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3789, "stable_key": "clu_34ba0afd5686a786-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "thermal springs", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "wikidata_id": "Q97726587", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article", "wikipedia_extract": "A hot spring, thermal spring, hydrothermal spring, or geothermal spring is a spring produced by the emergence of geothermally heated groundwater onto the surface of the Earth. The groundwater is heated either by shallow bodies of magma (molten rock) or by circulation through faults to hot rock deep in the Earth's crust. Hot spring water often contains large amounts of dissolved minerals. The chemistry of hot springs ranges from acid sulfate springs with a pH as low as 0.8, to alkaline chloride springs saturated with silica, to bicarbonate springs saturated with carbon dioxide and carbonate minerals. Some springs also contain abundant dissolved iron. The minerals brought to the surface in hot springs often feed communities of extremophiles, microorganisms adapted to extreme conditions, and it is possible that life on Earth had its origin in hot springs."}}, {"canonical_name": "Leo", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "leo", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Leo", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Leo"], "contexts": ["constellation name", "constellation from which meteors originated"]}, {"entity_id": "leo", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Leo", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 3, "variants": ["Leo"], "contexts": ["constellation with nebulous mass", "constellation representing a lion"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Leo", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Leo", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3790, "stable_key": "clu_7e48e46ba0cefeb2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Leo", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the astronomical constellation Leo."}}, {"canonical_name": "Sir Isaac Newton", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "sir_isaac_newton", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Sir Isaac Newton", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Sir Isaac Newton"], "contexts": ["subject of historical memoirs", "scientist who determined Earth's compression"]}, {"entity_id": "sir_isaac_newton", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Sir Isaac Newton", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Sir Isaac Newton"], "contexts": ["constitution of light theory", "proved fluid mass rotation forms ellipsoid"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Sir Isaac Newton", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Sir Isaac Newton", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3791, "stable_key": "clu_c35d0b4007f016f8-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Isaac Newton", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1643, "death_year": 1727, "description": "English mathematician, physicist, astronomer, and alchemist, laws of motion.", "wikidata_id": "Q935", "wikidata_description": "English mathematician and physicist (1642\u20131727)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isaac%20Newton", "wikipedia_extract": "Sir Isaac Newton ( ; 4 January [O.S. 25 December] 1643 \u2013 31 March [O.S. 20 March] 1727) was an English polymath who was a mathematician, physicist, astronomer, alchemist, theologian, author and inventor. He was a key figure in the Scientific Revolution and the Enlightenment that followed. His book Philosophi\u00e6 Naturalis Principia Mathematica (Mathematical Principles of Natural Philosophy), first published in 1687, achieved the first great unification in physics and established classical mechanics. Newton also made seminal contributions to optics, and shares credit with the German mathematician Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz for formulating infinitesimal calculus, although he developed calculus years before Leibniz."}}, {"canonical_name": "Pleiades", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "pleiades", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Pleiades", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Pleiades"], "contexts": ["star cluster in Taurus constellation", "star cluster mentioned"]}, {"entity_id": "pleiades", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Pleiades", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Pleiades"], "contexts": ["location of sun's orbit center", "star cluster in our stellar system"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Pleiades", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Pleiades", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3792, "stable_key": "clu_959dbe42f26d5b90", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pleiades", "confidence": "high", "type": "object"}}, {"canonical_name": "Freytag", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 5, "members": [{"entity_id": "freytag", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Freytag", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Freytag"], "contexts": ["lexicographer of Arabic", "author of Arabic Lexicon"]}, {"entity_id": "freytag", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Freytag", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Freytag"], "contexts": ["cited for Latin translations", "cited authority"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Freytag", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Freytag", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 3793, "stable_key": "clu_04314a9ac53ad781", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel", "confidence": "low", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1770, "death_year": 1831, "description": "German philosopher, central figure in German idealism.", "note": "The context 'lexicographer of Arabic' and 'cited for Latin translations' strongly suggests Freytag is a person, but the provided texts are 'kosmos_humboldt_1845' and 'connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858', which are not aligned with Freytag's typical scholarly domain. A Freytag known for Arabic lexicography is likely referring to Georg von Freytag-Loringhoven, or perhaps an earlier scholar. However, given the association with Humboldt and Somerville (science/natural philosophy), it's possible there's a lesser-known Freytag or a misattribution. If it's indeed a lexicographer, it's likely someone like Georg Benedikt von Freytag. Without more context or the actual texts, identifying a specific 'Freytag' as a scholar of Arabic and Latin translations in this scientific context is difficult. It is possible that the original text referred to a different Freytag entirely or a different meaning of the word 'Freytag'."}}, {"canonical_name"