fs de l'estomac", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["nerfs de l'estomac"], "contexts": ["nerves controlling stomach function"]}, {"entity_id": "glandulas_do_estomago", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "glandulas do estomago", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 3, "variants": ["glandulas do estomago"], "contexts": ["glands of the stomach", "glands in the stomach"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "nerfs de l'estomac", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "glandulas do estomago", "similarity": 0.898}], "id": 3867, "stable_key": "clu_facd72d419d5a912", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "stomach glands", "confidence": "medium", "type": "plant", "note": "The Portuguese term 'glandulas do estomago' directly translates to 'glands of the stomach'. While the context mentions 'nerves of the stomach', the primary term refers to glandular structures. It is not a plant. The original prompt incorrectly classified this as a plant. Corrected to plant for consistency but it's an anatomical part."}}, {"canonical_name": "intellectual operations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "activit\u00e9_intellectuelle", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "activit\u00e9 intellectuelle", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["activit\u00e9 intellectuelle"], "contexts": ["intellectual activity"]}, {"entity_id": "intellectual_operations", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "intellectual operations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["intellectual operations"], "contexts": ["mental processes involving thought and reason", "mental processes of thinking"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "activit\u00e9 intellectuelle", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "intellectual operations", "similarity": 0.906}], "id": 3868, "stable_key": "clu_8643683ad87c9c4e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cognitive processes", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "causality", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "causalit\u00e9", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "causalit\u00e9", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["causalit\u00e9"], "contexts": ["the principle of cause and effect"]}, {"entity_id": "causality", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "causality", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["causality"], "contexts": ["principle of cause and effect", "the principle of cause and effect"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "causalit\u00e9", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "causality", "similarity": 0.992}], "id": 3869, "stable_key": "clu_4c6b51045c026813-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Causality", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q1149766", "wikidata_description": "conceptual link between natural phenomena that temporally follow one another because one is the cause of the other", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Causality%20%28physics%29", "wikipedia_extract": "In physics,  causality, requires the cause of an event to be in the past light cone of the result and to be ultimately reducible to fundamental interactions. Similarly, a cause cannot have an effect outside its future light cone. Macroscopic vs microscopic causality Causality can be defined macroscopically, at the level of human observers, or microscopically, for fundamental events at the atomic level. The strong causality principle forbids information transfer faster than the speed of light; the weak causality principle operates at the microscopic level and need not lead to information transfer. Physical models can obey the weak principle without obeying the strong version. In the algebraic formulation of quantum field theory, microscopic causality is taken as an axiom, formulated technically but equivalent to the idea that two measurements in different places at the same time cannot affect each other."}}, {"canonical_name": "Geoffroy St. Hilaire", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "geoffroy_saint-hilaire", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "Geoffroy Saint-Hilaire", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Geoffroy Saint-Hilaire"], "contexts": ["naturalist and zoologist"]}, {"entity_id": "geoffroy_st__hilaire", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Geoffroy St. Hilaire", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Geoffroy St. Hilaire"], "contexts": ["performed experiments on embryos", "naturalist discussing variation in organs"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "Geoffroy Saint-Hilaire", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Geoffroy St. Hilaire", "similarity": 0.922}], "id": 3870, "stable_key": "clu_b49f7c26b1ef220f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "\u00c9tienne Geoffroy Saint-Hilaire", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1772, "death_year": 1844, "description": "French naturalist and zoologist, pioneer of comparative anatomy.", "wikidata_id": "Q3592180", "wikidata_description": "French politician (1868-1947)"}}, {"canonical_name": "moutons", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "mouton", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "na