rs emphasizing transcendental ideas"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "transcendentalists", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "transcendentalists", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "transcendentalist", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "transcendentalists", "similarity": 0.915}], "id": 4148, "stable_key": "clu_5ab7fdbcd2909999", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Transcendentalism", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q127712970", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article"}}, {"canonical_name": "noise", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "noise", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "noise", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["noise"], "contexts": ["unwanted auditory stimulus", "ununderstood auditory sensation"]}, {"entity_id": "noises", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Noises", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Noises"], "contexts": ["audible vibrations"]}, {"entity_id": "noise", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "noise", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["noise"], "contexts": ["sound accompanying earthquakes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "noise", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Noises", "similarity": 0.87}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "noise", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "noise", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4149, "stable_key": "clu_3a66abcbc89364c3-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sound", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q491713", "wikidata_description": "long, relatively wide body of water, connecting two larger parts of the sea", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sound%20%28geography%29", "note": "Context of auditory vibrations and phenomena, including those associated with natural events.", "wikipedia_extract": "In geography, a sound is a smaller body of water usually connected to a sea or an ocean. A sound may be an inlet that is deeper than a bight and wider than a fjord; or  a narrow sea channel or an ocean channel between two land masses, such as a strait; or also a lagoon between a barrier island and the mainland. A sound is often formed by the seas flooding a river valley. This produces a long inlet where the sloping valley hillsides descend to sea-level and continue beneath the water to form a sloping sea floor. These sounds are more appropriately called rias. The Marlborough Sounds in New Zealand are examples of this type of formation."}}, {"canonical_name": "black", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "black", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "black", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["black"], "contexts": ["particular color", "particular color of man"]}, {"entity_id": "negra", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "negra", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["negra"], "contexts": ["black color", "color description"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "black", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "negra", "similarity": 0.942}], "id": 4150, "stable_key": "clu_a77ee063e5d59549", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "black", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This cluster explicitly mentions 'particular color' and 'black color,' confirming it refers to the concept of blackness.", "wikidata_id": "Q97599288", "wikidata_description": "scientific article published on 01 March 2007"}}, {"canonical_name": "skin-spot", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "skin-spot", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "skin-spot", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["skin-spot"], "contexts": ["specific area on the skin", "area on skin for sensation"]}, {"entity_id": "spots", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "spots", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["spots"], "contexts": ["formed by gaseous eruptions", "dark areas on stellar surfaces"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "skin-spot", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "spots", "similarity": 0.89}], "id": 4151, "stable_key": "clu_e9daedd3349b3b29", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Skin discoloration", "confidence": "medium", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'skin-spot' can refer to various phenomena from pigmentation changes to efflorescence. 'Spots' is very general. Given the context of 'gaseous eruptions', it likely refers to an observable mark or eruption on the skin."}}, {"canonical_name": "Lehmann", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "lehmann", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Lehmann", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Dr. Lehmann", "Lehmann"], "contexts": ["experimentalist in Wundt's laboratory", "experimentalist in psychology"]}, {"entity_id": "lehmann", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Lehmann", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lehmann"], "contexts": ["author on comet tails"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Lehmann", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Lehmann", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4152, "stable_key": "clu_c81e7485c807621d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Johann Georg Lehmann", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1815, "death_year": 1876, "description": "German physician, botanist, and chemist", "note": "There were multiple notable individuals named Lehmann in science during this period. Johann Georg Lehmann fits the description of an experimentalist (in chemistry/physiology) and author on scientific topics. For comet tails, Alexander von Humboldt might be a more direct association, but Lehmann also engaged with broader scientific topics."}}, {"canonical_name": "Langelot", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "langley", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Langley", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Langley"], "contexts": ["author of Practical Physiology"]}, {"entity_id": "langelot", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Langelot", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Langelot"], "contexts": ["author cited for a case of gout", "revealed his Panacea for reward"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Langley", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Langelot", "similarity": 0.854}], "id": 4153, "stable_key": "clu_4a149079c8fee582", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jean Fran\u00e7ois Louis Langelot", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "French physician and author of medical texts."}}, {"canonical_name": "spontaneous motion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "spontaneous_motion", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "spontaneous motion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["spontaneous motion"], "contexts": ["motion not caused by external stimulus"]}, {"entity_id": "spontaneous_motion", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "spontaneous motion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["spontaneous motion"], "contexts": ["motion without external cause", "question of inherent movement"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "spontaneous motion", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "spontaneous motion", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4154, "stable_key": "clu_b0af83b8fd8dff0c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Spontaneous Motion", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "In early modern texts, this often refers to motion not caused by external forces, a concept discussed in physics and later in psychology regarding volition and instinct.", "wikidata_id": "Q30528428", "wikidata_description": "scientific article published on November 7, 2012"}}, {"canonical_name": "sensorial excitement", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "sensorial_excitement", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "sensorial excitement", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sensorial excitement"], "contexts": ["stimulation from senses"]}, {"entity_id": "excitements", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "excitements", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["excitements"], "contexts": ["feelings of great enthusiasm and eagerness"]}, {"entity_id": "nervous_excitement", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "nervous excitement", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["nervous excitement"], "contexts": ["stimulation of the nervous system"]}, {"entity_id": "excitement", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "excitement", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["excitement"], "contexts": ["state of heightened emotion"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "sensorial excitement", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "nervous excitement", "similarity": 0.912}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "excitements", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "nervous excitement", "similarity": 0.923}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "excitements", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "excitement", "similarity": 0.963}], "id": 4155, "stable_key": "clu_14e39127154f8276", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sensory stimulation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "Hirth", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "hirsch", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Hirsch", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hirsch"], "contexts": ["observer of reaction times"]}, {"entity_id": "hirth", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Hirth", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 3, "variants": ["Hirth", "F. Hirth", "Hirth (F.)"], "contexts": ["cited source", "modern commentator on Chinese documents"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Hirsch", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Hirth", "similarity": 0.902}], "id": 4156, "stable_key": "clu_39432d8d88b925d7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "August Hirsch", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1817, "death_year": 1885, "description": "German physician and medical historian, known for his work on epidemics.", "note": "The OCR for 'Hirth' in 'coloquios_da_orta_1563' likely represents 'Hirsch', given the context of medical texts and the English spelling 'Hirsch' appearing in a later text.", "wikidata_id": "Q71439", "wikidata_description": "German historian (1817\u20131894)"}}, {"canonical_name": "Fig. 23", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "fig__23", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Fig. 23", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Fig. 23"], "contexts": ["illustration of sphygmograph tracing"]}, {"entity_id": "fig__24", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "FIG. 24", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["FIG. 24"], "contexts": ["illustration for clarity"]}, {"entity_id": "fig__23", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Fig. 23", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Fig. 23"], "contexts": ["illustration of satellite configurations"]}, {"entity_id": "fig__24", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Fig. 24", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Fig. 24"], "contexts": ["illustration for opposition and conjunction"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Fig. 23", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Fig. 23", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Fig. 23", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Fig. 24", "similarity": 0.96}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "FIG. 24", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Fig. 23", "similarity": 0.94}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "FIG. 24", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Fig. 24", "similarity": 0.981}], "id": 4157, "stable_key": "clu_3d55f300ed996329", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Figure 23", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "Refers to specific figures or illustrations within the texts, likely depicting scientific or medical phenomena. 'Figure 23' and 'Figure 24' are distinct but fall under the same type.", "wikidata_id": "Q21550668", "wikidata_description": "process of providing drawings or other pictures intended to elucidate a description, story, or other written material"}}, {"canonical_name": "associated ideas", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "associated_ideas", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "associated ideas", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["associated ideas"], "contexts": ["ideas linked by association"]}, {"entity_id": "associated_ideas", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "associated ideas", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["associated ideas"], "contexts": ["ideas linked together in the mind", "ideas linked by association"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "associated ideas", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "associated ideas", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4158, "stable_key": "clu_d6ac65f9591dc756-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Association of Ideas", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q744778", "wikidata_description": "connection between ideas or mental states due to similarity or proximity in psychology", "note": "This is a foundational concept in psychology, referring to how thoughts and memories are linked."}}, {"canonical_name": "materialistic", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "materialistic", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "materialistic", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["materialistic"], "contexts": ["philosophical view emphasizing matter"]}, {"entity_id": "materialistic", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "materialistic", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["materialistic"], "contexts": ["philosophical view emphasizing matter", "philosophical view of matter as fundamental"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "materialistic", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "materialistic", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4159, "stable_key": "clu_a3b5dce654d8e550-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "materialism", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q7081", "wikidata_description": "form of philosophical monism which holds matter to be the fundamental substance in nature", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Materialism", "wikipedia_extract": "In philosophy and metaphysics, materialism is a form of monism holding that matter is the fundamental substance of nature, so that all things, including mind and consciousness, arise from material interactions and depend on physical processes, including those of the human brain and nervous system. It contrasts with monistic idealism, which treats consciousness as fundamental, and is related to naturalism, the view that only natural laws and forces operate in the universe, and to physicalism, the view that all that exists is ultimately physical. Physicalism extends materialism by including forms of physicality beyond ordinary matter (e.g. spacetime, energy, forces, exotic matter), and some use the terms interchangeably. Alternative philosophies opposed or alternative to materialism or physicalism include idealism, pluralism, dualism, solipsism, panpsychism, and other forms of monism. Materialism is the philosophical doctrine that matter has a primary position in the nature of the world, with mind or consciousness emerging as a secondary, dependent reality or not existing at all."}}, {"canonical_name": "odio", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "hate", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "hate", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["hate"], "contexts": ["cardinal passion of life"]}, {"entity_id": "odio", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "odio", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 3, "variants": ["odio", "odios"], "contexts": ["strong dislike", "hatred that can leave lasting impressions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "hate", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "odio", "similarity": 0.938}], "id": 4160, "stable_key": "clu_d416e29dc95e24b2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "hate", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "inch", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "inch", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "inch", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["inch"], "contexts": ["unit of length"]}, {"entity_id": "inch", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "inch", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["inch"], "contexts": ["unit of length for measurement", "unit of length"]}, {"entity_id": "inches", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "inches", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["inches"], "contexts": ["unit of measurement for pressure"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "inch", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "inch", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "inch", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "inches", "similarity": 0.856}], "id": 4161, "stable_key": "clu_077f6e33fee01322", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Inch", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q21445422", "wikidata_description": "chemical identifier", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/InChIKey"}}, {"canonical_name": "Babel", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "babylon", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Babylon", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Babylon"], "contexts": ["biblical city, symbol of power"]}, {"entity_id": "babel", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Babel", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["Babel"], "contexts": ["original name for Babylon", "biblical city"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Babylon", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Babel", "similarity": 0.881}], "id": 4162, "stable_key": "clu_0597972661b40cb8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Babylon", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "An ancient Mesopotamian city, historically significant.", "note": "'Babil\u00f3nia' is the Portuguese spelling for Babylon.", "wikipedia_extract": "Babylon ( BAB-il-on) was an ancient city located on the lower Euphrates river in southern Mesopotamia, within modern-day Hillah, Iraq, about 85 kilometres (53 miles) south of modern-day Baghdad. Babylon functioned as the main cultural and political centre of the Akkadian-speaking region of Babylonia. Its rulers established two important empires in antiquity, the 19th\u201316th century BC Old Babylonian Empire, and the 7th\u20136th century BC Neo-Babylonian Empire. Babylon was also used as a regional capital of other empires, such as the Achaemenid Empire. Babylon was one of the most important urban centres of the ancient Near East, until its decline during the Hellenistic period. Nearby ancient sites are Kish, Borsippa, Dilbat, and Kutha."}}, {"canonical_name": "heaven", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "heaven", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "heaven", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["heaven"], "contexts": ["alleged temporary dwelling of Lurancy's spirit"]}, {"entity_id": "heaven", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "heaven", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 3, "variants": ["heaven"], "contexts": ["starry vault of heaven", "celestial sphere"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "heaven", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "heaven", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4163, "stable_key": "clu_119b5f457fca94a0-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sky", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "wikidata_id": "Q527", "wikidata_description": "region of the atmosphere and outer space visible from the surface of the Earth", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sky", "wikipedia_extract": "The sky is an unobstructed view upward from the surface of the Earth. It includes the atmosphere and outer space. It may also be considered a place between the ground and outer space, thus distinct from outer space. In the field of astronomy, the sky is also called the celestial sphere. This is an abstract sphere, concentric to the Earth, on which the Sun, Moon, planets, and stars appear to be drifting. The celestial sphere is conventionally divided into designated areas called constellations.", "note": "In this context, 'heaven' refers to the celestial sphere or the sky, not the religious afterlife."}}, {"canonical_name": "attributes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 4, "members": [{"entity_id": "attributes", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "attributes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["attributes"], "contexts": ["qualities composing a concept"]}, {"entity_id": "attribute", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "attribute", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["attribute"], "contexts": ["characteristic of a thing"]}, {"entity_id": "attributes", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "attributes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["attributes"], "contexts": ["qualities or characteristics"]}, {"entity_id": "attribute", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "attribute", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["attribute"], "contexts": ["quality or characteristic"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "attributes", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "attributes", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "attributes", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "attribute", "similarity": 0.922}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "attribute", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "attributes", "similarity": 0.922}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "attribute", "target_book": "first_princ