idence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q29299", "wikidata_description": "excitatory ability that living organisms have to respond to changes in their environment", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Irritability"}}, {"canonical_name": "laws of nature", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "laws_of_nature", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "laws of nature", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["laws of nature"], "contexts": ["immutable, phenomena exist virtually"]}, {"entity_id": "laws_of_nature", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "laws of nature", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 2, "variants": ["laws of nature"], "contexts": ["governing forces of the physical world", "principles governing natural phenomena"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "laws of nature", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "laws of nature", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4335, "stable_key": "clu_10550f3c1ecb86fb-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Laws of nature", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q21191188", "wikidata_description": "1st episode of the 3rd season of Agents of S.H.I.E.L.D.", "wikipedia_extract": "Rights of nature or Earth rights is a legal and jurisprudential theory that describes inherent rights as associated with ecosystems and species, similar to the concept of fundamental human rights. The rights of nature concept challenges twentieth-century laws as generally grounded in a flawed frame of nature as \"resource\" to be owned, used, and degraded. Proponents argue that laws grounded in rights of nature direct humanity to act appropriately and in a way consistent with modern, system-based science, which demonstrates that humans and the natural world are fundamentally interconnected. This school of thought is underpinned by two basic lines of reasoning. First, since the recognition of human rights is based in part on the philosophical belief that those rights emanate from humanity's own existence, logically, so too do inherent rights of the natural world arise from the natural world's own existence. A second and more pragmatic argument asserts that the survival of humans depends on healthy ecosystems, and so protection of nature's rights in turn, advances human rights and well-being.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Physical_law"}}, {"canonical_name": "generalization", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "g\u00e9n\u00e9ralisation", "book_id": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "name": "g\u00e9n\u00e9ralisation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["g\u00e9n\u00e9ralisation"], "contexts": ["process of forming general laws"]}, {"entity_id": "generalization", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "generalization", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["generalization"], "contexts": ["forming general laws from specific instances", "process of forming general principles from specific instances"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "g\u00e9n\u00e9ralisation", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "generalization", "similarity": 0.928}], "id": 4336, "stable_key": "clu_f8aee37eb4402e9d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Generalization", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q6963735", "wikidata_description": "concept on humans' and animals' use of past learning in present situations", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Generalization%20%28learning%29"}}, {"canonical_name": "zoological scale", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2