ikipedia_extract": "Staff is both a surname and a nickname."}}, {"canonical_name": "piernas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "piernas", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "piernas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["piernas"], "contexts": ["body part, legs"]}, {"entity_id": "piernas", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "piernas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["piernas"], "contexts": ["legs, part of the body", "legs"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "piernas", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "piernas", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4667, "stable_key": "clu_db9048fda684e002", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "legs", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "While 'legs' is an anatomical part, in this context it appears to be treated as a substance or element within a classification.", "wikidata_id": "Q14856098", "wikidata_description": "family of plants"}}, {"canonical_name": "Plomo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "plomo", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "plomo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["plomo"], "contexts": ["metal used to make white lead"]}, {"entity_id": "plomo", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Plomo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Plomo", "plomo"], "contexts": ["a type of metal", "lead, a metal"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "plomo", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Plomo", "similarity": 0.844}], "id": 4668, "stable_key": "clu_6ff767abc54f78fc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "lead", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q708", "wikidata_description": "chemical element with symbol Pb and atomic number 82", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lead"}}, {"canonical_name": "Alcornoque", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "alcornoque", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Alcornoque", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Alcornoque", "Alcornoco"], "contexts": ["cork tree, name given to Malpighia", "common name for a tree"]}, {"entity_id": "alcornoque", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Alcornoque", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Alcornoque"], "contexts": ["tree with thick bark"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Alcornoque", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Alcornoque", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4669, "stable_key": "clu_074d5c4831717c8b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cork Oak", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Quercus suber Linnaeus", "family": "Fagaceae", "note": "While 'Alcornoque' can refer to other plants, in historical texts about medicinal plants and trees with thick bark, it most commonly refers to the cork oak.", "wikidata_id": "Q156137", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quercus%20suber", "wikipedia_extract": "Quercus suber, commonly called the cork oak,  is a medium-sized, evergreen oak tree in the section Quercus sect. Cerris. It is the primary source of cork for wine bottle stoppers and other uses, such as cork flooring and as the cores of cricket balls. It is native to southwest Europe and northwest Africa. In the Mediterranean basin the tree is an ancient species with fossil remnants dating back to the Tertiary period. It can survive for as long as two centuries."}}, {"canonical_name": "Aralia", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "aralia", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Aralia", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Aralia", "Aralia turbacensis"], "contexts": ["alpine shrub found in Silla de Caracas", "plant from Turbaco"]}, {"entity_id": "aralia", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "aralia", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["aralia"], "contexts": ["delicate social plant"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Aralia", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "aralia", "similarity": 0.974}], "id": 4670, "stable_key": "clu_abc2623d5700fd5a", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Aralia , or spikenard, is a genus of the family Araliaceae, consisting of 68 accepted species of deciduous or evergreen trees, shrubs, and rhizomatous herbaceous perennials. The genus is native to Asia and the Americas, with most species occurring in mountain woodlands. Aralia plants vary in size, with some herbaceous species only reaching 50 centimetres (20 in) tall, while some are trees growing to 20 metres (66 ft) tall. Aralia plants have large bipinnate (doubly compound) leaves clustered at the ends of their stems or branches; in some species the leaves are covered with bristles. The stems of some woody species are quite prickly, as in Aralia spinosa. The flowers are whitish or greenish occurring in terminal panicles, and the spherical dark purple berry-like fruits are popular with birds."}}, {"canonical_name": "tr\u00e9sor", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "tr\u00e9sor", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "tr\u00e9sor", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["tr\u00e9sor", "tr\u00e9sor royal"], "contexts": ["financial reserve", "royal treasury"]}, {"entity_id": "tesoro", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "tesoro", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["tesoro"], "contexts": ["treasure, likely precious metals and jewels"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "tr\u00e9sor", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "tesoro", "similarity": 0.914}], "id": 4671, "stable_key": "clu_114d88108f3a90fd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Treasury", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This refers to a financial reserve or accumulated wealth, not a specific chemical substance.", "wikidata_id": "Q10756188", "wikidata_description": "department of an organisation related to finance", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treasury", "wikipedia_extract": "a government department related to finance and taxation, a finance ministry; in a business context, corporate treasury a place or location where treasure, such as currency or precious items are kept. These can be  state or royal property, church treasure or in private ownership. The head of a treasury is typically known as a treasurer. This position may not necessarily have the final control over the actions of the treasury, particularly if they are not an elected representative. The adjective for a treasury is normally \"treasurial\". The adjective \"tresorial\" can also be used, but this normally means pertaining to a treasurer."}}, {"canonical_name": "Mac- Culloch", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "mac-_culloch", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Mac- Culloch", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Mac- Culloch", "Mac Culloch"], "contexts": ["author of Researc?tes on America", "author cited for scientific journal"]}, {"entity_id": "mac_crin*dle", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Mac Crin*dle", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mac Crin*dle"], "contexts": ["translated Indica fragments"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Mac- Culloch", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Mac Crin*dle", "similarity": 0.87}], "id": 4672, "stable_key": "clu_d4cca13c6e4b2fe8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "John MacCulloch", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1773, "death_year": 1835, "description": "Scottish geologist and physician, author of geological surveys.", "wikidata_id": "Q43915496", "wikidata_description": "person associated with the witch trials in Scotland"}}, {"canonical_name": "Pline", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "pline", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Pline", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Pline", "Plin"], "contexts": ["Ancient author cited for island names", "ancient Roman author"]}, {"entity_id": "plin", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Plin", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Plin"], "contexts": ["ancient naturalist"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Pline", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Plin", "similarity": 0.873}], "id": 4673, "stable_key": "clu_75041dcbcc428b45-4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pliny the Elder", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 23, "death_year": 79, "description": "Roman author and naturalist, known for Naturalis Historia.", "wikidata_id": "Q58340224", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article by John F. Healy published January 1981 in Interdisciplinary Science Reviews"}}, {"canonical_name": "Imbabura", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "imbabura", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Imbabura", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Imbabura", "imbabum"], "contexts": ["volcano near Villa de Ibarra", "Volcano mentioned in the text"]}, {"entity_id": "imbaburu", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Imbaburu", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Imbaburu"], "contexts": ["volcano near Ibarra"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Imbabura", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Imbaburu", "similarity": 0.96}], "id": 4674, "stable_key": "clu_df2775dd5dff97d4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Imbabura", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Ecuador", "wikidata_id": "Q128880473", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article"}}, {"canonical_name": "Purac\u00e8", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "purac\u00e8", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Purac\u00e8", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Purac\u00e8", "Purace"], "contexts": ["volcano", "village where observations were made"]}, {"entity_id": "purace", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Purace", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Purace"], "contexts": ["volcano between Bogota and Quito"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Purac\u00e8", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Purace", "similarity": 0.949}], "id": 4675, "stable_key": "clu_3d18aee21dc45f19", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Purac\u00e9", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Colombia", "wikipedia_extract": "Purac\u00e9 is an andesitic stratovolcano located in the Purac\u00e9 National Natural Park in the Cauca Department, Colombia. It is part of the North Volcanic Zone of the Andean Volcanic Belt. The volcano is located at the intersection of the Coconucos and Morras Faults. It is one of the most active volcanoes in Colombia. Large explosive eruptions occurred in 1849, 1869, 1885, 1949, 1950, 1956, and 1957. There were about a dozen eruptions in the 20th century, the most recent being in 1977, until 19 January 2025 when Purac\u00e9 once again erupted.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Purac\u00e9"}}, {"canonical_name": "zccbstein", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "zccbstein", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "zccbstein", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["zccbstein", "zcchstein"], "contexts": ["geological term for rock", "type of limestone"]}, {"entity_id": "zeckstein", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "zeckstein", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["zeckstein"], "contexts": ["rock formation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "zccbstein", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "zeckstein", "similarity": 0.902}], "id": 4676, "stable_key": "clu_f79946c64f8fbf58-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Zechstein Formation", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "Zechstein refers to a significant geological period and the associated rock strata, particularly relevant in European geology. 'zechslein', 'zechstone', and 'zeckstein' are variations of this term.", "wikidata_id": "Q98436573", "wikidata_description": "street in the city district of Davenstedt in Hanover, Germany", "wikipedia_extract": "The Zechstein Limestone Formation is a geologic formation in Germany. It preserves fossils dating back to the Permian period. List of fossiliferous stratigraphic units in Germany Various Contributors to the Paleobiology Database. \"Fossilworks: Gateway to the Paleobiology Database\". Archived from the original on 19 August 2022. Retrieved 17 December 2021.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zechstein_Limestone_Formation"}}, {"canonical_name": "Bouc", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "bouc", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Bouc", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Bouc", "Boui"], "contexts": ["author of memoir on terrains", "author on Alps"]}, {"entity_id": "bertuch", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Bertuch", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bertuch"], "contexts": ["editor of geographical ephemerides"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Bouc", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Bertuch", "similarity": 0.852}], "id": 4677, "stable_key": "clu_5b22fe44c4743b12", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Friedrich Justin Bertuch", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1747, "death_year": 1822, "description": "German publisher and geographer, editor of geographical journals.", "note": "The cluster members 'Bouc' and 'Bertuch' suggest this identification. 'Bouc' could be a misreading or abbreviation.", "wikidata_id": "Q213775", "wikidata_description": "German publisher (1747\u20131822)"}}, {"canonical_name": "\u00e9tat hygrom\u00e9trique", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "\u00e9tat_hygrom\u00e9trique", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "\u00e9tat hygrom\u00e9trique", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["\u00e9tat hygrom\u00e9trique", "\u00e9tat cyanom\u00e9trique"], "contexts": ["measure of atmospheric humidity", "measure of sky color/transparency"]}, {"entity_id": "hygrometric_state", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "hygrometric state", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["hygrometric state"], "contexts": ["moisture content of air"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "\u00e9tat hygrom\u00e9trique", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "hygrometric state", "similarity": 0.959}], "id": 4678, "stable_key": "clu_1e678d4f7468f654", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Relative humidity", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term '\u00e9tat hygrom\u00e9trique' directly translates to 'hygrometric state' and refers to the measure of atmospheric humidity.", "wikidata_id": "Q2499617", "wikidata_description": "ratio of the partial pressure of water vapor in humid air to the equilibrium vapor pressure of water at a given temperature", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Relative%20humidity"}}, {"canonical_name": "Montezuma", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "montezuma", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Montezuma", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Montezuma", "Monteath"], "contexts": ["emperor whose empire was shaken", "captain who found a longitude"]}, {"entity_id": "montezuma", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Montezuma", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Montezuma"], "contexts": ["Aztec ruler"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Montezuma", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Montezuma", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4679, "stable_key": "clu_d95944542a56d978", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Moctezuma II", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1466, "death_year": 1520, "description": "Aztec emperor who encountered Hern\u00e1n Cort\u00e9s", "wikidata_id": "Q141791", "wikidata_description": "9th tlatoani of Tenochtitlan and ruler of the Aztec Triple Alliance (1466-1520)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moctezuma%20II", "wikipedia_extract": "Moctezuma Xocoyotzin (c. 1466 \u2013 29 June 1520), retroactively referred to in European sources as Moctezuma II, and often simply called Montezuma, was the ninth emperor of the Aztec Empire (also known as the Mexica Empire), reigning from 1502 or 1503 to 1520. Through his marriage with Queen Tlapalizquixochtzin of Ecatepec, one of his two wives, he was also the king consort of the altepetl. The first contact between the indigenous civilizations of Mesoamerica and Europeans took place during Moctezuma's reign. He was killed during the initial stages of the Spanish conquest of the Aztec Empire when Hern\u00e1n Cort\u00e9s, the Spanish conquistador, and his men seized the Aztec capital of Tenochtitlan. During his reign, the Aztec Empire reached its greatest size."}}, {"canonical_name": "M\u00e9chain", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "m\u00e9chain", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "M\u00e9chain", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["M\u00e9chain", "M. M\u00e9chain"], "contexts": ["cited for astronomical observation", "associated with observatory in Barcelona"]}, {"entity_id": "mechain", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Mechain", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mechain"], "contexts": ["astronomer who first saw Encke's comet"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "M\u00e9chain", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Mechain", "similarity": 0.874}], "id": 4680, "stable_key": "clu_a2827f2da8cf8201", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pierre M\u00e9chain", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1744, "death_year": 1804, "description": "French astronomer and geodetic surveyor", "wikidata_id": "Q311022", "wikidata_description": "French mathematician and astronomer (1744-1804)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pierre%20M%C3%A9chain", "wikipedia_extract": "Pierre Fran\u00e7ois Andr\u00e9 M\u00e9chain (French pronunciation: [pj\u025b\u0281 f\u0281\u0251\u0303swa \u0251\u0303d\u0281e me\u0283\u025b\u0303]; 16 August 1744 \u2013 20 September 1804) was a French astronomer and surveyor who, with Charles Messier, was a major contributor to the early study of deep-sky objects and comets. Pierre M\u00e9chain was born in Laon in northern France, the son of the ceiling designer and plasterer Pierre Fran\u00e7ois M\u00e9chain and Marie\u2013Marguerite Roze. He displayed mental gifts in mathematics and physics but had to give up his studies for lack of money. However, his talents in astronomy were noticed by J\u00e9r\u00f4me Lalande, for whom he became a friend and proof-reader of the second edition of his book \"L'Astronomie\". Lalande then secured a position for him as assistant hydrographer with the Naval Depot of Maps and Charts at Versailles, where he worked through the 1770s engaged in hydrographic work and coastline surveying. It was during this time\u2014approximately 1774\u2014that he met Charles Messier, and apparently, they became friends."}}, {"canonical_name": "Moscou", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "moscou", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Moscou", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Moscou", "Moskou"], "contexts": ["location of magnetic force comparison", "city for observation"]}, {"entity_id": "moscow", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Moscow", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Moscow"], "contexts": ["northern city with magnetic stations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Moscou", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Moscow", "similarity": 0.932}], "id": 4681, "stable_key": "clu_c2391e9f3fa32a39", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Moscow", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Russia", "note": "Both 'Moscou' and 'Moscow' refer to the capital city of Russia.", "wikidata_id": "Q649", "wikidata_description": "capital and most populous city of Russia", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moscow", "wikipedia_extract": "Moscow is the capital and largest city of Russia, standing on the Moskva River in Central Russia. It has a population estimated at over 13 million residents within the city limits, over 19.1 million residents in the urban area, and over 21.5 million residents in its metropolitan area. The city covers an area of 2,511 square kilometers (970 mi2), while the urban area covers 5,891 square kilometers (2,275 mi2), and the metropolitan area covers over 26,000 square kilometers (10,000 mi2). Moscow is among the world's largest cities, being the most populous city entirely in Europe, the largest urban and metropolitan area in Europe, and the largest city by land area on the European continent. First documented in 1147, Moscow became the capital of the Grand Principality of Moscow, which led the unification of the Russian lands in the 15th century and became the center of a unified state. Following the proclamation of the Tsardom of Russia in 1547, Moscow remained the political and economic center for most of its history."}}, {"canonical_name": "Elbe", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "elbe", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Elbe", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Elbe"], "contexts": ["river in Europe", "river from which Bohemians did not come"]}, {"entity_id": "elbe", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Elbe", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Elbe"], "contexts": ["river forming fissures"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Elbe", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Elbe", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4682, "stable_key": "clu_df53f17a94a27d48", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Elba", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Island in Italy and a river in Europe.", "note": "Contexts differentiate between the island and the river.", "wikipedia_extract": "Elba (Italian: isola d'Elba, pronounced [\u02c8i\u02d0zola \u02c8delba]; Latin: Ilva) is a Mediterranean island in Tuscany, Italy, 10 km (6.2 mi) from the coastal town of Piombino on the Italian mainland, and the largest island of the Tuscan Archipelago. It is also part of the Arcipelago Toscano National Park, and the third largest island in Italy, after Sicily and Sardinia. It is located in the Tyrrhenian Sea about 50 km (30 mi) east of the French island of Corsica. The island is part of the province of Livorno and is divided into seven municipalities, with a total population of about 30,000 inhabitants which increases considerably during the summer. The municipalities are Portoferraio (which is also the island's principal town), Campo nell'Elba, Capoliveri, Marciana, Marciana Marina, Porto Azzurro, and Rio. It is famous for being the site of Napoleon's first exile, from 1814 to 1815."}}, {"canonical_name": "Macos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "macos", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Macos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Macos"], "contexts": ["people with less prominent foreheads", "indigenous people devoted to agriculture"]}, {"entity_id": "m\u00e1layos", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "m\u00e1layos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["m\u00e1layos"], "contexts": ["Malays engaged in coastal trade"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Macos", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "m\u00e1layos", "similarity": 0.869}], "id": 4683, "stable_key": "clu_15fcb1614132af78", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Malays", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Southeast Asian ethnic group involved in trade", "note": "The term 'Macos' is likely a misspelling or phonetic rendering of 'Malays' in the context of historical trade routes.", "wikidata_id": "Q833", "wikidata_description": "country in Southeast Asia", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malaysia", "wikipedia_extract": "Malaysia is a country in Southeast Asia. A federal constitutional monarchy, it consists of 13 states and three federal territories, separated by the South China Sea into two regions: Peninsular Malaysia on the Indochinese Peninsula and East Malaysia on the island of Borneo. Peninsular Malaysia shares land and maritime borders with Thailand, as well as maritime borders with Singapore, Vietnam, and Indonesia; East Malaysia shares land borders with Brunei and Indonesia, and maritime borders with the Philippines and Vietnam. Kuala Lumpur is the country's national capital, largest city, and the seat of the legislative branch of the federal government, while Putrajaya is the federal administrative capital, representing the seat of both the executive branch and the judicial branch of the federal government. With a population of over 34 million, it is the world's 42nd-most populous country. The country has its origins in the Malay kingdoms, which, from the 18th century on, became subject to the British Empire, along with the British Straits Settlements protectorate."}}, {"canonical_name": "Pierre Camper", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "pierre_camper", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Pierre Camper", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Pierre Camper"], "contexts": ["illustrious anatomist"]}, {"entity_id": "peter_camper", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Peter Camper", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Peter Camper"], "contexts": ["scientist known for comparative anatomy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Pierre Camper", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Peter Camper", "similarity": 0.913}], "id": 4684, "stable_key": "clu_4eb318917faf2729", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Petrus Camper", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1722, "death_year": 1789, "description": "Dutch anatomist, surgeon, and naturalist", "note": "While the text mentions 'Pierre Camper' and 'Peter Camper', the most recognized historical figure is Petrus Camper.", "wikidata_id": "Q382682", "wikidata_description": "Dutch scientist (1722-1789)"}}, {"canonical_name": "distances lunaires", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "distances_lunaires", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "distances lunaires", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["distances lunaires"], "contexts": ["used for absolute determination", "astronomical measurement"]}, {"entity_id": "lunar_distance", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Lunar distance", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lunar distance"], "contexts": ["defined"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "distances lunaires", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Lunar distance", "similarity": 0.957}], "id": 4685, "stable_key": "clu_76c2c574dc3bf0f6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lunar distance", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q1547342", "wikidata_description": "Angular distance between the Moon and another celestial body", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lunar%20distance%20%28navigation%29", "wikipedia_extract": "In celestial navigation, lunar distance, also called a lunar, is the angular distance between the Moon and another celestial body. The lunar distances method uses this angle and a nautical almanac to calculate Greenwich time if so desired, or by extension any other time. That calculated time can be used in solving  a spherical triangle. The theory was first published by Johannes Werner in 1524, before the necessary almanacs had been published. A fuller  method was published in 1763 and used until about 1850 when it was superseded by the marine chronometer. A similar method uses the positions of the Galilean moons of Jupiter."}}, {"canonical_name": "porphyres", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "porphyres", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "porphyres", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["porphyres"], "contexts": ["type of volcanic rock", "porphyry igneous rock type"]}, {"entity_id": "porfido", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Porfido", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Porfido"], "contexts": ["stone for grinding substances"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "porphyres", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Porfido", "similarity": 0.902}], "id": 4686, "stable_key": "clu_04593b67396f2838-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Porphyry", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q203445", "wikidata_description": "3rd-century Greek Neoplatonist philosopher", "note": "The Italian 'Porfido' directly translates to porphyry."}}, {"canonical_name": "kaolin", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "kaolin", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "kaolin", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["kaolin"], "contexts": ["type of clay", "mineral substance"]}, {"entity_id": "kaolin", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "kaolin", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["kaolin"], "contexts": ["clay mineral for porcelain manufacture"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "kaolin", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "kaolin", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4687, "stable_key": "clu_5c3b28f34cffa815", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Kaolin", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q223197", "wikidata_description": "layered aluminosilicate 1:1 clay mineral"}}, {"canonical_name": "indig\u00e8nes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "indig\u00e8nes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "indig\u00e8nes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["indig\u00e8nes"], "contexts": ["native inhabitants", "native inhabitants of the land"]}, {"entity_id": "indigenous_people", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "indigenous people", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["indigenous people"], "contexts": ["native inhabitants of a region"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "indig\u00e8nes", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "indigenous people", "similarity": 0.98}], "id": 4688, "stable_key": "clu_70398c52bacd8046-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Indigenous peoples", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'savages' was historically used to refer to indigenous peoples, often in a derogatory manner, but in scientific and philosophical texts, it frequently referred to groups studied for comparative anthropology or psychology.", "wikidata_id": "Q1363781", "wikidata_description": "descendants of the population that lived in the territory of modern Brazil before the Portuguese colonization", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indigenous%20peoples%20in%20Brazil", "description": "Original inhabitants of a region or continent", "wikipedia_extract": "Indigenous peoples in Brazil or Native Brazilians (Portuguese: Brasileiros nativos) are the peoples whose ancestors lived in Brazil before European contact around 1500 and those pre-Columbian forebears. Indigenous peoples once comprised an estimated 2,000 distinct tribes and nations inhabiting what is now Brazil. The 2010 Brazil census recorded 305 Indigenous ethnic groups. Historically, many Indigenous peoples of Brazil were semi-nomadic and combined hunting, fishing, and gathering with migratory agriculture. Many tribes were massacred by European settlers, and others assimilated into the growing Brazilian population. The Indigenous population was decimated by European diseases, declining from a pre-Columbian high of 2 million to 3 million to approximately 300,000 by 1997, distributed among 200 tribes."}}, {"canonical_name": "lac de Nicaragua", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "lac_de_nicaragua", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "lac de Nicaragua", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["lac de Nicaragua"], "contexts": ["lake in Central America", "lake in Nicaragua"]}, {"entity_id": "lake_nicaragua", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Lake Nicaragua", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lake Nicaragua"], "contexts": ["location where subterranean thunder was heard"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "lac de Nicaragua", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Lake Nicaragua", "similarity": 0.97}], "id": 4689, "stable_key": "clu_9e7c0a67579d91fd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lake Nicaragua", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Nicaragua", "wikidata_id": "Q173862", "wikidata_description": "largest lake in Central America", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake%20Nicaragua", "wikipedia_extract": "Lake Nicaragua or  Cocibolca or Granada (Spanish: Lago de Nicaragua, Lago Cocibolca, Mar Dulce, Gran Lago, Gran Lago Dulce, or Lago de Granada) is a freshwater lake in Nicaragua. Of tectonic origin and with an area of 8,264 km2 (3,191 sq mi), it is the largest fresh water lake in Central America, the 19th largest lake in the world (by area) and the tenth largest in the Americas, slightly smaller than Lake Titicaca. With an elevation of 32.7 metres (107 ft) above sea level, the lake reaches a depth of 26 metres (85 ft). The intermittent Tipitapa River feeds Lake Nicaragua when Lake Managua has high water. Lake Cocibolca is between two other bodies of water, on top is Lake Xolotl\u00e1n and below is the San Juan River. These bodies of water complete the largest international drainage basin in Central America."}}, {"canonical_name": "La Vega", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "la_vega", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "La Vega", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["La Vega"], "contexts": ["location within Caracas", "place name in Venezuela"]}, {"entity_id": "vega", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Vega", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Vega"], "contexts": ["city with convent of S. Francisco"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "La Vega", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Vega", "similarity": 0.882}], "id": 4690, "stable_key": "clu_c2c57f505c6d23d1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "La Vega", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Venezuela", "note": "Context of 'location within Caracas' and 'city with convent of S. Francisco' points to a specific settlement in Venezuela."}}, {"canonical_name": "Chaco", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "chaco", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Chaco", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Chaco"], "contexts": ["region in South America"]}, {"entity_id": "chaco", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Chaco", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Chaco"], "contexts": ["region in South America"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Chaco", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Chaco", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4691, "stable_key": "clu_4a4b3de561085044", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Gran Chaco", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Argentina, Bolivia, Paraguay", "wikidata_id": "Q208347", "wikidata_description": "region of Southern America", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gran%20Chaco", "wikipedia_extract": "The Gran Chaco (also called Chaco or Chaco Plain), is a vast semiarid lowland region in central South America, spanning over one million square kilometers across eastern Bolivia, western Paraguay, northern Argentina, and parts of Brazil. It forms part of the R\u00edo de la Plata basin. Gran Chaco features a mix of tropical and subtropical dry broadleaf forests, thorn scrub, savannas, wetlands, and palm groves, making it the continent\u2019s second-largest forested ecoregion and a region of high ecological diversity. The Gran Chaco is home to more than 3,400 plant species, around 500 bird species, 150 mammals, and more than 200 reptiles and amphibians, including jaguars, giant armadillos, peccaries, and maned wolves. Its forests and soils also store carbon and regulate water cycles, playing a significant role in climate moderation. The region has been sparsely inhabited for centuries by Indigenous peoples, including the Wich\u00ed, Qom, Pilag\u00e1, Guaran\u00ed, and Ayoreo, among others."}}, {"canonical_name": "r\u00e9coltes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "r\u00e9coltes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "r\u00e9coltes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["r\u00e9coltes"], "contexts": ["agricultural yield", "harvests, related to agricultural yield"]}, {"entity_id": "harvests", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "harvests", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["harvests"], "contexts": ["gathering of crops"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "r\u00e9coltes", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "harvests", "similarity": 0.943}], "id": 4692, "stable_key": "clu_f3f6935e3b6f6221", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Harvest", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "littoral", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "littoral", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "littoral", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["littoral"], "contexts": ["coastal area"]}, {"entity_id": "coast-line", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "coast-line", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["coast-line"], "contexts": ["boundary between land and sea"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "littoral", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "coast-line", "similarity": 0.906}], "id": 4693, "stable_key": "clu_35912c9e583f1b0e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "littoral zone", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "wikidata_id": "Q506894", "wikidata_description": "part of a sea, lake or river that is close to the shore", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Littoral%20zone", "wikipedia_extract": "The littoral zone, also called litoral or nearshore, is the part of an ocean, sea, lake, or river, that is close to the shore. It provides extensive and productive habitats around the world, adjacent to land-water interfaces. In coastal ecology, the littoral zone includes the foreshore (intertidal zone) extending from the high water mark (which is rarely inundated) to the low water mark (where coastal areas become permanently submerged). However, the geographical meaning of littoral zone extends well beyond the intertidal zone to include all neritic waters within the bounds of continental shelves. Continental shelves cover an area of about 7% of the surface area of the oceans. In lake ecosystems, the littoral zone covers about 78% of Earth's total lake area."}}, {"canonical_name": "Maranhao", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "maranhao", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Maranhao", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Maranhao"], "contexts": ["region for rainfall verification", "river name"]}, {"entity_id": "maranhao", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Maranhao", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Maranhao"], "contexts": ["place where a fish is found"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Maranhao", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Maranhao", "similarity": 0.854}], "id": 4694, "stable_key": "clu_67765da34972bd3a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Maranh\u00e3o", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Brazil", "wikidata_id": "Q42362", "wikidata_description": "state in the Northeast Region of Brazil", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maranh%C3%A3o", "wikipedia_extract": "Maranh\u00e3o (, MAH-r\u0259-NYOWN; Brazilian Portuguese pronunciation: [ma\u027e\u0250\u02c8\u0272\u0250\u0303w] ) is a state in Brazil. Located in the country's Northeast Region, it has a population of about 7 million and an area of 332,000 km2 (128,000 sq mi) and it is divided into 217 municipalities. Clockwise from north, it borders on the Atlantic Ocean for 2,243 km and the states of Piau\u00ed, Tocantins and Par\u00e1. The people of Maranh\u00e3o have a distinctive accent within the common Northeastern Brazilian dialect. Maranh\u00e3o is described in literary works such as Exile Song by Gon\u00e7alves Dias and Casa de Pens\u00e3o by Alu\u00edsio Azevedo. The dunes of Len\u00e7\u00f3is are an important area of environmental preservation."}}, {"canonical_name": "Prony", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "prony", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Prony", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Prony"], "contexts": ["author on canal works", "mentioned in work by M. de Pommcuse"]}, {"entity_id": "prony", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Prony", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Prony"], "contexts": ["mutual friend, scientist"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Prony", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Prony", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4695, "stable_key": "clu_507499d5ac2a8b0a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Gaspard de Prony", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1755, "death_year": 1839, "description": "French engineer and mathematician, known for hydraulic and civil engineering.", "wikidata_id": "Q451608", "wikidata_description": "French mathematician and engineer, who worked on hydraulics (1755-1839)", "wikipedia_extract": "Baron Gaspard Clair Fran\u00e7ois Marie Riche de Prony (22 July 1755 \u2013 29 July 1839) was a French mathematician and engineer, who worked on hydraulics. He was born at Chamelet, Beaujolais, France and died in Asni\u00e8res-sur-Seine, France. He was Engineer-in-Chief of the \u00c9cole nationale des ponts et chauss\u00e9es [National school of bridges and roads], a technical school in Paris. The trigonometric and logarithmic tables of the cadastre In 1791, Prony embarked on the task of producing logarithmic and trigonometric tables for the French Cadastre (geographic survey). The effort was sanctioned by the French National Assembly, which, after the French Revolution wanted to bring uniformity to the multiple measurements and standards used throughout the nation. In particular, his tables were intended for precise land surveys, as part of a greater cadastre effort."}}, {"canonical_name": "Andreossy", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "andreossy", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Andreossy", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Andreossy"], "contexts": ["cited for page numbers", "general and author"]}, {"entity_id": "andreossy", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Andreossy", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Andreossy"], "contexts": ["described soda lakes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Andreossy", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Andreossy", "similarity": 0.721}], "id": 4696, "stable_key": "clu_8ae3ba8b6853a128", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Antoine Fran\u00e7ois Andr\u00e9ossy", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1761, "death_year": 1821, "description": "French general and diplomat, known for scientific observations.", "wikidata_id": "Q175286", "wikidata_description": "French general (1761\u20131828)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antoine-Fran%C3%A7ois%20Andr%C3%A9ossy", "wikipedia_extract": "Antoine-Fran\u00e7ois, comte Andr\u00e9ossy (6 March 1761 \u2013 10 September 1828) was a Franco-Italian nobleman, who served as a French Army artillery general, diplomat and parliamentarian. Born at Castelnaudary in Languedoc (now in Aude), scion of an ancient Italian minor noble family from Lucca, he was a great-grandson of the celebrated civil engineer and architect of the Canal du Midi, Fran\u00e7ois Andr\u00e9ossy (1633-1688). An outstanding officer cadet at the Metz School of Artillery, Andr\u00e9ossy was commissioned in the R\u00e9giment Royal-Artillerie in 1781, seeing action in the Dutch Civil War (1787); he was promoted as Captain in 1788. On the outbreak of the Revolution he adopted its principles. At the start of the Revolutionary Wars he saw active service on the Rhine in 1794 and in Italy in 1795, and in the campaign of 1796\u20131797 under Napoleon Bonaparte on engineer duties, commanding the bridging train of the French Army of Italy after June 1796, and fought with distinction at the Battle of the Bridge of Arcole and the Siege of Mantua.. He was promoted to brigadier general in 1798, in which year he accompanied Bonaparte to Egypt."}}, {"canonical_name": "G\u00e9ogr.", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "g\u00e9ogr_", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "G\u00e9ogr.", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["G\u00e9ogr."], "contexts": ["abbreviation for Geography", "title of a work"]}, {"entity_id": "g\u00e9ogr_", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "G\u00e9ogr.", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["G\u00e9ogr."], "contexts": ["work by Edrisi"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "G\u00e9ogr.", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "G\u00e9ogr.", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4697, "stable_key": "clu_4bb1baa7b96e66b8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Geography", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Abbreviation for the field of Geography, and potentially referencing a work on the topic.", "wikidata_id": "Q1071", "wikidata_description": "science that studies the terrestrial surface, the societies that inhabit it and the territories, landscapes, places or regions that form it when interacting with each other"}}, {"canonical_name": "Gilolo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "gilolo", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Gilolo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Gilolo"], "contexts": ["Island whose distance from Brazil was disputed", "location in the Moluccas archipelago"]}, {"entity_id": "gilolo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Gilolo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Gilolo"], "contexts": ["alternative name for Geloulo"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Gilolo", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Gilolo", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4698, "stable_key": "clu_a5e3332cf8217206", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tidore", "confidence": "medium", "type": "place", "description": "Island in the Moluccas archipelago, historically important for spice trade.", "note": "Historical sources use variations for islands in the Moluccas archipelago. 'Geloulo' and 'Gilolo' likely refer to the island of Tidore or the wider region.", "wikipedia_extract": "Tidore (Indonesian: Kota Tidore Kepulauan, lit. \"City of Tidore Islands\") is a city, island, and archipelago in the Maluku Islands of eastern Indonesia, west of the larger island of Halmahera. Part of North Maluku Province, the city includes the island of Tidore (with three smaller outlying islands - Mare, Maitara and Filonga) together with a large part of Halmahera Island to its east. In the pre-colonial era, the Sultanate of Tidore was a major regional political and economic power, and a fierce rival of nearby Ternate, just to the north. Included within the city is the provincial capital, Sofifi, which is situated on the mainland of Halmahera (in North Oba District). Tidore Island consists of a large stratovolcano which rises from the seafloor to an elevation of 1,730 m (5,676 ft) above sea level at the conical Kie Matubu on the south end of the island. The northern side of the island contains a caldera, Sabale, with two smaller volcanic cones within it."}}, {"canonical_name": "Guarmey", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "guarmey", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Guarmey", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Guarmey"], "contexts": ["city in Peru", "geographic location"]}, {"entity_id": "guarmey", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Guarmey", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Guarmey"], "contexts": ["location in Peru"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Guarmey", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Guarmey", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4699, "stable_key": "clu_4406bbdb096871e5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Huarmey", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Peru", "wikidata_id": "Q1779624", "wikidata_description": "province of Ancash Region, Peru", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huarmey%20province", "wikipedia_extract": "Huarmey is one of 20 provinces located in the Ancash Region of Peru. It takes around 4 hours to arrive to Huarmey from Lima by bus and its main economic activities include agriculture, fishing, tourism, and mineral shipping."}}, {"canonical_name": "Guachaneque", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "guachaneque", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Guachaneque", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Guachaneque"], "contexts": ["mountain peak", "Paramos of Guachaneque"]}, {"entity_id": "guaynacaua", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Guaynacaua", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Guaynacaua"], "contexts": ["pass name"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Guachaneque", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Guaynacaua", "similarity": 0.888}], "id": 4700, "stable_key": "clu_3dc86213d84da77d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Guaynacaua Pass", "confidence": "medium", "type": "place", "country": "Chile", "note": "The term 'Guachaneque' is likely a misspelling or variation of 'Guaynacaua', referring to a mountain pass or peak. Monardes' text provides the stronger clue."}}, {"canonical_name": "gryph\u00e9es", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "gryph\u00e9es", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "gryph\u00e9es", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["gryph\u00e9es"], "contexts": ["fossil oyster genus", "found in limestone rich in lead"]}, {"entity_id": "griphos", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Griphos", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Griphos"], "contexts": ["creatures brought from the Indies"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "gryph\u00e9es", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Griphos", "similarity": 0.912}], "id": 4701, "stable_key": "clu_1bce91391c686a3b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Gryphaea (Fossil Oysters)", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "Refers to fossil oysters, often found as fossils in geological contexts. 'Griphos' in Monardes is likely a variant spelling or confusion.", "wikipedia_extract": "The Gryphaeidae, common name the foam oysters or honeycomb oysters, are a family of marine bivalve mollusks. This family of bivalves is very well represented in the fossil record, however the number of living species is very few. All species have shells cemented to a substrate. Shells are considered brittle, inequivalve, with the left, lower (cemented) valve convex and the right (upper, non-cemented) valve flat or slightly concave. Genera and species within this family are divided into three subfamilies, Exogyrinae, Gryphaeinae and Pycnodonteinae. Both Exogyrinae and Gryphaeinae are completely extinct.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gryphaeidae"}}, {"canonical_name": "Anaxagore de Clazom\u00e8nes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "anaxagore_de_clazom\u00e8nes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Anaxagore de Clazom\u00e8nes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Anaxagore de Clazom\u00e8nes"], "contexts": ["ancient philosopher", "ancient philosopher from Clazomenae"]}, {"entity_id": "anaxagoras_of_clazomenae", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Anaxagoras of Clazomenae", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Anaxagoras of Clazomenae"], "contexts": ["ancient Greek philosopher"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Anaxagore de Clazom\u00e8nes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Anaxagoras of Clazomenae", "similarity": 0.889}], "id": 4702, "stable_key": "clu_b666b0a42853029d-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Anaxagoras", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": -500, "death_year": -428, "description": "Ancient Greek philosopher and early cosmologist", "wikidata_id": "Q83041", "wikidata_description": "5th-century BC Greek philosopher", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anaxagoras", "wikipedia_extract": "Anaxagoras (; Ancient Greek: \u1f08\u03bd\u03b1\u03be\u03b1\u03b3\u03cc\u03c1\u03b1\u03c2, Anaxag\u00f3ras, 'lord of the assembly'; c. 500 BC \u2013 c. 428 BC) was a Pre-Socratic Greek philosopher. Born in Clazomenae at a time when Asia Minor was under the control of the Persian Empire, Anaxagoras came to Athens. In later life he was charged with impiety and went into exile in Lampsacus. Responding to the claims of Parmenides on the impossibility of change, Anaxagoras introduced the concept of Nous (Cosmic Mind) as an ordering force."}}, {"canonical_name": "Gor\u00e9e", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "gor\u00e9e", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Gor\u00e9e", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Gor\u00e9e"], "contexts": ["Gor\u00e9e", "island name"]}, {"entity_id": "goree", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Goree", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Goree"], "contexts": ["river in India"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Gor\u00e9e", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Goree", "similarity": 0.81}], "id": 4703, "stable_key": "clu_bf7ebb6d83cdd564", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Gor\u00e9e Island", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Senegal", "note": "The mention of 'river in India' for Gor\u00e9e seems to be an error or confusion in the context provided, as Gor\u00e9e is famously an island off Senegal."}}, {"canonical_name": "NOTES", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "notes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "NOTES", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["NOTES"], "contexts": ["notes section", "section title"]}, {"entity_id": "notes", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "notes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["notes"], "contexts": ["musical sounds produced by playing"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "NOTES", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "notes", "similarity": 0.9}], "id": 4704, "stable_key": "clu_76cc742795cb17db", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "notes", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q1277575", "wikidata_description": "mails or messages written by a person and sent to one or several other persons"}}, {"canonical_name": "Smyrne", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "smyrne", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Smyrne", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Smyrne"], "contexts": ["ancient city, modern Izmir, Turkey", "city where slave trade occurs"]}, {"entity_id": "smyrna", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Smyrna", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Smyrna"], "contexts": ["city where Orientalist resided"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Smyrne", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Smyrna", "similarity": 0.904}], "id": 4705, "stable_key": "clu_9b5b3309607650a7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Izmir", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Turkey", "note": "Smyrna is the ancient name for the modern Turkish city of Izmir. The context of an ancient city and its location fits.", "wikidata_id": "Q35997", "wikidata_description": "city in \u0130zmir Province, Turkey", "wikipedia_extract": "\u0130zmir is the third most populous city in Turkey, after Istanbul and Ankara. It is on the Aegean coast of Anatolia, and is the capital of \u0130zmir Province. As of 2024, \u0130zmir Province has a total population of 4,493,242 while \u0130zmir city is home to around 3.3 million inhabitants. It extends along the outlying waters of the Gulf of \u0130zmir and inland to the north across the Gediz River Delta; to the east along an alluvial plain created by several small streams; and to slightly more rugged terrain in the south. \u0130zmir's climate is Mediterranean. \u0130zmir has more than 3,000 years of recorded urban history, and up to 8,500 years of history as a human settlement since the Neolithic period. In classical antiquity, the city was known as Smyrna \u2013 a name which remained in use in English and various other languages until around 1930, when government efforts led the original Greek name to be forcefully phased out internationally in favor of its Turkish counterpart \u0130zmir. Lying on an advantageous location at the head of a gulf running down in a deep indentation, midway along the western Anatolian coast, \u0130zmir has been one of the principal mercantile cities of the Mediterranean Sea for much of its history.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C4%B0zmir"}}, {"canonical_name": "Fahr.", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "fahr_", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Fahr.", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Fahr."], "contexts": ["Fahrenheit scale for temperature", "unit of temperature measurement"]}, {"entity_id": "fahr_", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Fahr.", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Fahr."], "contexts": ["unit of temperature measurement"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Fahr.", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Fahr.", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4706, "stable_key": "clu_5b981a6615e28d63", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Fahrenheit scale", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q105525247", "wikidata_description": "temperature scale", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fahrenheit%20scale", "wikipedia_extract": "The Fahrenheit scale () is a temperature scale based on one proposed in 1724 by the physicist Daniel Gabriel Fahrenheit (1686\u20131736). It uses the degree Fahrenheit (symbol: \u00b0F) as the unit. Several accounts of how he originally defined his scale exist, but the original paper suggests the lower defining point, 0 \u00b0F, was established as the freezing temperature of a solution of brine made from a mixture of water, ice, and ammonium chloride (a salt). The other limit established was his best estimate of the average human body temperature, originally set at 90 \u00b0F, then 96 \u00b0F (about 2.6 \u00b0F less than the modern value due to a later redefinition of the scale). For much of the 20th century, the Fahrenheit scale was defined by two fixed points with a 180 \u00b0F separation: the temperature at which pure water freezes was defined as 32 \u00b0F and the boiling point of water was defined to be 212 \u00b0F, both at sea level and under standard atmospheric pressure. It is now formally defined using the Kelvin scale.", "note": "While Fahrenheit is a surname, in the context of early modern science and measuring temperature, it refers to the temperature scale."}}, {"canonical_name": "Podocarpus", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "podocarpus", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Podocarpus", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Podocarpus"], "contexts": ["genus of trees", "tree species found at certain altitudes"]}, {"entity_id": "podocarpus", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Podocarpus", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Podocarpus"], "contexts": ["plant genus observed by Columbus"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Podocarpus", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Podocarpus", "similarity": 0.804}], "id": 4707, "stable_key": "clu_8721983039072206", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Podocarpus", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Podocarpus L.f.", "family": "Podocarpaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q157524", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Podocarpus", "wikipedia_extract": "Podocarpus () is a genus of conifers, the most numerous and widely distributed of the podocarp family, the Podocarpaceae. Podocarpus species are evergreen shrubs or trees, usually from 1 to 25 m (3 to 82 ft) tall, known to reach 40 m (130 ft) at times. The cones have two to five fused cone scales, which form a fleshy, berry-like, brightly coloured receptacle at maturity. The fleshy cones attract birds, which then eat the cones and disperse the seeds in their droppings. Plants of the World Online accepts 116 species. Other authorities place 97 to 107 species in the genus depending on the circumscription of the species."}}, {"canonical_name": "Certes", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "certes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Certes", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Certes"], "contexts": ["parliament or assembly", "parliamentary body"]}, {"entity_id": "certidao", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "certidao", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["certidao"], "contexts": ["certificate of authenticity"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Certes", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "certidao", "similarity": 0.898}], "id": 4708, "stable_key": "clu_92b9a9a3c2336d19", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Certificates", "confidence": "medium", "type": "object", "description": "Official documents attesting to a fact, status, or qualification.", "note": "The Latin 'certo' and 'certidao' (certificate) suggest official documents rather than a parliamentary body.", "wikipedia_extract": "An Extended Validation (EV) Certificate is a certificate conforming to X.509 that proves the legal entity of the owner and is signed by a certificate authority key that can issue EV certificates. EV certificates can be used in the same manner as any other X.509 certificates, including securing web communications with HTTPS and signing software and documents. Unlike domain-validated certificates and organization-validation certificates, EV certificates can be issued only by a subset of certificate authorities (CAs) and require verification of the requesting entity's legal identity before certificate issuance. As of February 2021, all major web browsers (Google Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, Microsoft Edge and Apple Safari) have menus which show the EV status of the certificate and the verified legal identity of EV certificates. Mobile browsers typically display EV certificates the same way they do Domain Validation (DV) and Organization Validation (OV) certificates. Of the ten most popular websites online, none use EV certificates and the trend is away from their usage."}}, {"canonical_name": "ma\u00eetre", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "ma\u00eetre", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "ma\u00eetre", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["ma\u00eetre"], "contexts": ["a person who owns another", "owner of a slave"]}, {"entity_id": "masters", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "masters", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["masters"], "contexts": ["highly skilled individuals"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "ma\u00eetre", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "masters", "similarity": 0.865}], "id": 4709, "stable_key": "clu_44194d624078e224", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "master", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "A skilled individual or one in a position of authority or ownership.", "note": "The contexts suggest both a skilled person and a master-owner, hence the general term.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "", "wikipedia_extract": ""}}, {"canonical_name": "Jamaica", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "jamaica", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Jamaica", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Jamaica"], "contexts": ["island whose longitudes are discussed", "island nation"]}, {"entity_id": "jamaica", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Jamaica", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Jamaica"], "contexts": ["island where guaiacam grows"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Jamaica", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Jamaica", "similarity": 0.811}], "id": 4710, "stable_key": "clu_ec03453ff106d384-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jamaica", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Jamaica", "wikidata_id": "Q766", "wikidata_description": "island state in the Caribbean Sea", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jamaica", "wikipedia_extract": "Jamaica is an island country in the Caribbean, covering 10,990 square kilometres (4,240 sq mi). It is a popular tourism and resort destination. It is the third-largest island in the Greater Antilles and the Caribbean, after Cuba and the island of Hispaniola. Jamaica lies about 145 km (78 nmi) south of Cuba, 191 km (103 nmi) west of Hispaniola (the island containing Haiti and the Dominican Republic), and 215 km (116 nmi) southeast of the Cayman Islands (a British Overseas Territory). With 2.8 million people, Jamaica is the third most populous Anglophone country in the Americas and the fourth most populous country in the Caribbean. Kingston is the country's capital and largest city."}}, {"canonical_name": "Candler", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "candler", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Candler", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Candler"], "contexts": ["individual whose observations are mentioned", "observer's name"]}, {"entity_id": "candace", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Candace", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Candace"], "contexts": ["queen"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Candler", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Candace", "similarity": 0.909}], "id": 4711, "stable_key": "clu_47dc1f12fe9ca8a5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Candler", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "An individual whose observations were recorded.", "note": "The presence of 'Candace' suggests a potential confusion or different individual. 'Candler' is the more prominent name in the sources.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}}, {"canonical_name": "Morris", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "morris", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Morris", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Morris"], "contexts": ["observer and year", "author of scientific observations"]}, {"entity_id": "morris", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Morris", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Morris"], "contexts": ["author of geological papers"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Morris", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Morris", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4712, "stable_key": "clu_594792978438d7f3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "John Morris", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1810, "death_year": 1890, "description": "English geologist and paleontologist, curator of the British Museum.", "note": "The context 'author of geological papers' strongly suggests John Morris, a prominent geologist active in the period."}}, {"canonical_name": "Taman", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "taman", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Taman", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Taman"], "contexts": ["location of salses with cold water", "peninsula with mud volcanoes"]}, {"entity_id": "taman", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Taman", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Taman"], "contexts": ["peninsula where gases were studied"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Taman", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Taman", "similarity": 0.795}], "id": 4713, "stable_key": "clu_112cea31a63c6bc2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tam\u00e1n", "confidence": "medium", "type": "place", "country": "Venezuela", "note": "Context suggests a location studied for gases, possibly in Venezuela given Humboldt's travels. 'Taman' is also a common name for trees.", "wikipedia_extract": "Taman Negara is a national park in Peninsular Malaysia. It was established in 1938 and 1939 as the King George V National Park after Theodore Hubback lobbied the sultans of Pahang, Terengganu and Kelantan to set aside a piece of land that covers the three states for the creation of a protected area. After independence, it was renamed Taman Negara, which means \"national park\" in Malay. Taman Negara has a total area of 4,343 km2 and it is one of the world's oldest rainforests, estimated to be more than 130 million years old. Mount Tahan, Peninsular Malaysia's highest point with an elevation of 7,175 ft (2,187 m) above sea level, is within the park's boundaries. Taman Negara is an important conservation area for the Peninsular Malaysian rain forest and montane rain forest ecoregions, is rich in biodiversity and home to several endangered species such as the Malayan tiger and Asian elephant.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taman_Negara"}}, {"canonical_name": "Monte Zibio", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "monte_zibio", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Monte Zibio", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Monte Zibio"], "contexts": ["mountain location", "mountain name"]}, {"entity_id": "monte_ziblo", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Monte Ziblo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Monte Ziblo"], "contexts": ["inactive mud volcano near Sassuolo"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Monte Zibio", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Monte Ziblo", "similarity": 0.942}], "id": 4714, "stable_key": "clu_7ce52829a1535eb1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Montegibbio", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Italy", "note": "The spelling variations and context of 'inactive mud volcano near Sassuolo' clearly point to Montegibbio."}}, {"canonical_name": "Sassuolo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "sassuolo", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Sassuolo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Sassuolo"], "contexts": ["town name", "location of a salse"]}, {"entity_id": "sassuolo", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Sassuolo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Sassuolo"], "contexts": ["town in Northern Italy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Sassuolo", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Sassuolo", "similarity": 0.869}], "id": 4715, "stable_key": "clu_f5d5851277f24634", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sassuolo", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Italy", "wikidata_id": "Q8598", "wikidata_description": "Italian comune", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sassuolo", "wikipedia_extract": "Sassuolo (Italian pronunciation: [sas\u02c8sw\u0254\u02d0lo]; Modenese: Sas\u00f3l [sa\u02c8so\u02d0l]) is an Italian town, comune, and industrial centre of the Province of Modena in Emilia-Romagna. Standing on the right bank of the river Secchia some 17 kilometres (11 mi) southwest of Modena, the town is best known for being the centre of the Italian tile industry and for being the home town of Serie A side U.S. Sassuolo Calcio. The exact origin of the toponym Sassuolo is unclear. One theory is that it might derive from the abundant deposits of petroleum found in the area. This is because petroleum was anciently known as \"stone oil\" or \"olio di sasso\" in Italian, from which the composite word Sassuolo (sasso + olio) may have been created."}}, {"canonical_name": "Obdorsk", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "obdorsk", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Obdorsk", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Obdorsk"], "contexts": ["location near mouth of Ob river", "city near the mouths of the Oby river"]}, {"entity_id": "obdorsk", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Obdorsk", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Obdorsk"], "contexts": ["location on the Oby river"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Obdorsk", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Obdorsk", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4716, "stable_key": "clu_d919ace080fd23f9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Obdorsk", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Russia", "note": "Obdorsk is a historical name for the city now known as Salekhard, located at the mouth of the Ob river.", "wikidata_id": "Q117070340", "wikidata_description": "fishing vessel built in 1959", "wikipedia_extract": "Salekhard (Russian: \u0421\u0430\u043b\u0435\u0445\u0430\u0301\u0440\u0434 [s\u0259l\u02b2\u026a\u02c8xart]; Khanty: \u041f\u0443\u04c6\u04a3\u0430\u0432\u0430\u0442, Pu\u0142\u00f1awat; Nenets: \u0421\u0430\u043b\u044f\u02bc \u0445\u0430\u0440\u0430\u0434, Salja\ua727 harad, formerly Obdorsk) is a town and the administrative centre of Yamalo-Nenets Autonomous Okrug, Russia. The town lies on the Arctic Circle, with the town centre being about 1 kilometer (0.62 mi) south and suburbs stretching to the north of the circle. The population is 47,910\u2009(2021 census). The settlement of Obdorsk (\u041e\u0431\u0434\u043e\u0440\u0441\u043a) was founded in 1595, in the place of a Khanty settlement called Polnovat-Vozh (\u041f\u043e\u043b\u043d\u043e\u0432\u0430\u0442-\u0432\u043e\u0436), by Russian settlers after the conquest of Siberia. It was situated on the Ob River, and its name supposedly derives from that. The land around Obdorsk was referred to as Obdorsky krai, or Obdoriya."}}, {"canonical_name": "Nismes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "nismes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Nismes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Nismes"], "contexts": ["city of observation", "location of geological observation"]}, {"entity_id": "nismes", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Nismes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Nismes"], "contexts": ["location associated with Valz"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Nismes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Nismes", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4717, "stable_key": "clu_6ddfc1f27ed2a2d9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "N\u00eemes", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "France", "wikidata_id": "Q42807", "wikidata_description": "city and prefecture of the Gard department in the Occitanie region of Southern France", "wikipedia_extract": "The siege of N\u00eemes took place shortly after the capture and destruction of Avignon in 737. Charles Martel failed to capture the Umayyad city of Narbonne but devastated most of the other principal settlements of Septimania, including N\u00eemes, Agde, B\u00e9ziers and Maguelonne, which he viewed as potential strongholds of the Saracens. The city of N\u00eemes (Chronicle of Fredegar) and the Roman amphitheatre (turned into a fortress by the Visigoths) were destroyed under the orders of Charles Martel. The Arabs were temporarily contained to the city of Narbonne, though a second expedition was needed later that year to regain control of Provence after Arab forces returned. According to Paul the Deacon's Historia Langobardorum the Arabs retreated when they learned that Martel had formed an alliance with the Lombards. Martel's remaining years - he had only four to live - were spent setting up and strengthening the administrative structure that became the Carolingian Empire, and the feudal state that would persist through the Dark Ages.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/N%C3%AEmes"}}, {"canonical_name": "guerras", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "guerres", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "guerres", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["guerres"], "contexts": ["armed conflicts"]}, {"entity_id": "guerras", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "guerras", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["guerras"], "contexts": ["wars preceding plague", "wars"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "guerres", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "guerras", "similarity": 0.986}], "id": 4718, "stable_key": "clu_87b2ba21509bba76", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "War", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q198", "wikidata_description": "organized and prolonged violent conflict between different nations, states, or different groups within a nation or state", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/War", "wikipedia_extract": "War is an armed conflict between the armed forces of states, or between governmental forces and armed groups that are organized under a certain command structure and have the capacity to sustain military operations, or between such organized groups. It is generally characterized by widespread violence, destruction, and mortality, using regular or irregular military forces. Warfare refers to the common activities and characteristics of types of war, or of wars in general. Total war is warfare that is not restricted to purely legitimate military targets, and can result in massive civilian or other non-combatant suffering and casualties. War has changed in numerous ways over the course of history. Since 1945, great power wars, territorial conquests and war declarations have declined in frequency."}}, {"canonical_name": "Rousseau", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "rousseau", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Rousseau", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rousseau"], "contexts": ["author whose works are unknown to monks"]}, {"entity_id": "rousseau", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Rousseau", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Rousseau"], "contexts": ["example of hypocrisy", "philosopher whose path is followed"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Rousseau", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Rousseau", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4719, "stable_key": "clu_c59721da8e430463", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jean-Jacques Rousseau", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1712, "death_year": 1778, "description": "Genevan philosopher and writer, influential figure of the Enlightenment.", "note": "The context provided, particularly 'example of hypocrisy' and his philosophical influence, strongly aligns with Jean-Jacques Rousseau.", "wikidata_id": "Q6527", "wikidata_description": "Genevan philosopher, writer, and composer (1712\u20131778)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean-Jacques%20Rousseau", "wikipedia_extract": "Jean-Jacques Rousseau (UK: , US: ; French: [\u0292\u0251\u0303\u0292ak \u0281uso]; 28 June 1712 \u2013 2 July 1778) was a Genevan philosopher, philosophe, writer, and composer. His political philosophy influenced the progress of the Age of Enlightenment throughout Europe, as well as aspects of the French Revolution and the development of modern political, economic, and educational thought. Rousseau's Discourse on Inequality, which argues that private property is the source of inequality, and The Social Contract, which outlines the basis for a legitimate political order, are cornerstones in modern political and social thought. Rousseau's sentimental novel Julie, or the New Heloise (1761) was important to the development of preromanticism and romanticism in fiction. His \u00c9mile, or On Education (1762) is an educational treatise on the place of the individual in society. Rousseau's autobiographical writings\u2014the posthumously published Confessions (completed in 1770), which initiated the modern autobiography, and the unfinished Reveries of the Solitary Walker (composed 1776\u20131778)\u2014exemplified the late 18th-century \"Age of Sensibility\", and featured an increased focus on subjectivity and introspection that later characterized modern writing."}}, {"canonical_name": "Lancerote", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "lancerote", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Lancerote", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lancerote"], "contexts": ["volcanic archipelago north of it"]}, {"entity_id": "lancerote", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Lancerote", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Lancerote"], "contexts": ["island with volcanic eruptions", "island with colitic bed"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Lancerote", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Lancerote", "similarity": 0.775}], "id": 4720, "stable_key": "clu_40b93fc0da55ac50", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lanzarote", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Spain", "note": "The spelling variation 'Lancerote' for 'Lanzarote' is common in older texts, and the context of 'volcanic archipelago' is accurate.", "wikidata_id": "Q182432", "wikidata_description": "one of the main islands of the Canary Islands, Spain", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lanzarote", "wikipedia_extract": "Lanzarote (UK: , US: , Spanish: [lan\u03b8a\u02c8\u027eote] , locally [lansa\u02c8\u027eote]) is a Spanish island, the easternmost of the Canary Islands, 125 kilometres (80 miles) off the north coast of Africa and 1,000 kilometres (600 miles) from the Iberian Peninsula. Covering 845.94 square kilometres (326.62 square miles), Lanzarote is the fourth-largest of the islands in the archipelago. With 163,230 inhabitants at the beginning of 2024, it is the third most populous Canary Island, after Tenerife and Gran Canaria. Located in the centre-west of the island is Timanfaya National Park, one of its main attractions. The island was declared a biosphere reserve by UNESCO in 1993. The island's capital is Arrecife, which lies on the eastern coastline."}}, {"canonical_name": "Creusot", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "creuse", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Creuse", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Creuse"], "contexts": ["Creuse, du Var et de l'Aude"]}, {"entity_id": "creusot", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Creusot", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Creusot"], "contexts": ["location of iron experiments"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Creuse", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Creusot", "similarity": 0.899}], "id": 4721, "stable_key": "clu_36e7bf2c57d30557", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Creuse", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "France", "note": "The text mentions 'Creuse, du Var et de l'Aude' which are departments in France. 'Creusot' is likely a misspelling or variation of 'Creuse' in this context, especially given the reference to iron experiments which could be related to industrial history in that region.", "wikidata_id": "Q23782107", "wikidata_description": "departmental legislature in France", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Departmental%20Council%20of%20Creuse", "wikipedia_extract": "Departmental Council of Creuse (French: Conseil d\u00e9partemental de la Creuse) is the deliberative assembly of the French department of Creuse. Its headquarters is at Gu\u00e9ret. Since 2015, the President of the Departmental Council is Val\u00e9rie Simonet (LR). She was re-elected in the 2021 departmental elections. The departmental council of Creuse includes 30 departmental councilors from the 15 cantons of Creuse."}}, {"canonical_name": "railways", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "rail-ways", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "rail-ways", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["rail-ways"], "contexts": ["railways"]}, {"entity_id": "railways", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "railways", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["railways"], "contexts": ["transportation infrastructure"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "rail-ways", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "railways", "similarity": 0.993}], "id": 4722, "stable_key": "clu_5ec86dac7b7f8188", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Railways", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "Q22667", "wikidata_description": "interconnected network of infrastructure used for train rail transport", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Railroad"}}, {"canonical_name": "Girard", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "girard", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Girard", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Girard"], "contexts": ["mentioned in Annales de Physique et de Chimie"]}, {"entity_id": "girard", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Girard", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Girard"], "contexts": ["identified nepheline in basalt", "on basalt composition"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Girard", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Girard", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4723, "stable_key": "clu_9cf7d5585f322bce", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Marc-Auguste Pictet", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1752, "death_year": 1825, "description": "Swiss naturalist, physicist, and geographer associated with Annales de Physique et de Chimie.", "note": "Humboldt's 'Relation Historique' and 'Kosmos' often reference scientific findings published in prominent journals. Girard is a less common name in this context. Marc-Auguste Pictet was a prominent figure in physics and geography and a frequent contributor to and editor of the 'Annales de Physique et de Chimie', making him a more likely referent for a discussion involving that journal and the identification of minerals.", "wikidata_id": "Q116754", "wikidata_description": "Genevan scientist (1752-1825)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marc-Auguste%20Pictet", "wikipedia_extract": "Marc-Auguste Pictet  (French: [pikt\u025b]; 23 July 1752 \u2013 19 April 1825) was a Genevan scientific journalist and experimental natural philosopher. Pictet's main contribution to learning was his editing of the scientific section of the Biblioth\u00e8que Britannique (1796\u20131815), a publication devoted to the diffusion on the Continent of knowledge and arts produced in Great Britain. His own scientific research focused on the fields of physical science, especially calorimetry, but also astronomy, geology, meteorology and technology, especially chronometry and the manufacture of fine earthenware. He was born in Geneva, Republic of Geneva on 23 July 1752, the son of Charles Pictet, a military officer serving in the mercenary troops of the Netherlands, and his wife, Marie Dunant. Marc-Auguste studied natural philosophy and law at the Academy of Geneva and qualified as a lawyer in 1774. After one year in England (1775\u201376), he became assistant to Jacques-Andr\u00e9 Mallet at the Geneva Observatory and took an interest in meteorology and map-making."}}, {"canonical_name": "New Madrid", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "new-madrid", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "New-Madrid", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["New-Madrid"], "contexts": ["location of mounds"]}, {"entity_id": "new_madrid", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "New Madrid", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["New Madrid"], "contexts": ["location in Missouri, USA", "location of hot steam ejection"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "New-Madrid", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "New Madrid", "similarity": 0.915}], "id": 4724, "stable_key": "clu_873aed685508f355-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Madrid", "confidence": "high", "type": "PLACE", "description": "Capital city of Spain, a significant location in historical texts.", "wikipedia_extract": "Madrid is the capital and most populous city of Spain. It had a population of over 3.4 million in the city proper in 2025, and a metropolitan area population of approximately 6.8 million. Madrid is the second-largest city in the European Union (EU), after Berlin, and its metropolitan area is the second-largest in the EU, after Paris. The municipality covers an area of 605.77 km2 (233.89 sq mi). Madrid lies on the River Manzanares in the central part of the Iberian Peninsula at about 660 m (2,170 ft) above mean sea level. The capital city of both Spain and the surrounding autonomous community of Madrid, it is the political, economic and cultural centre of the country."}}, {"canonical_name": "Teguiza", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "teguayo", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Teguayo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Teguayo"], "contexts": ["fabulous lake"]}, {"entity_id": "teguiza", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Teguiza", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Teguiza"], "contexts": ["location near formation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Teguayo", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Teguiza", "similarity": 0.894}], "id": 4725, "stable_key": "clu_4eff1af3495431e8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Isthmus of Tehuantepec", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Mexico", "note": "'Teguiza' is likely a transcription error for Tehuantepec in one context.", "wikipedia_extract": "The Ferrocarril del Istmo de Tehuantepec is a government-owned railway system in Mexico that has three lines. It seeks to become a global logistics network focused on the manufacture and movement of goods between the Pacific Ocean and the Atlantic Ocean through the Isthmus of Tehuantepec. The project consists of the renovation of the railroad, which is expected to increase the speed of the freight train from 20 km/h (12 mph) to 70 km/h (43 mph), more than three times the current one. Meanwhile, the passenger train will be able to reach speeds of up to 100 km/h (62 mph). The state-owned company Ferrocarril del Istmo de Tehuantepec was founded in 1999, to operate the railway that connects Salina Cruz with the locality of Medias Aguas, Veracruz. Mayab and Chiapas lines were formerly leased to Ferrocarriles Chiapas-Mayab until Genesee & Wyoming gave up its concession in 2007.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tren_Interoce\u00e1nico"}}, {"canonical_name": "Hebrew", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "h\u00e9breu", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "h\u00e9breu", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["h\u00e9breu"], "contexts": ["Hebrew language"]}, {"entity_id": "hebrew", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Hebrew", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Hebrew"], "contexts": ["historic race with united arts", "scriptures with marked difference of aggregation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "h\u00e9breu", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Hebrew", "similarity": 0.915}], "id": 4726, "stable_key": "clu_a74e3eb6d2a97c53", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hebrew language", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q9288", "wikidata_description": "Northwest Semitic language"}}, {"canonical_name": "Hindustan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "hindustan", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Hindustan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hindustan"], "contexts": ["French possessions"]}, {"entity_id": "hindustan", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Hindustan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Hindustan", "Hindust\u00e3o"], "contexts": ["region merchants traveled through", "source of garnets"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Hindustan", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Hindustan", "similarity": 0.755}], "id": 4727, "stable_key": "clu_3b1f5efc22628429-4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "India", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "India", "note": "Hindostan is an older Persian name for the Indian subcontinent.", "wikidata_id": "Q668", "wikidata_description": "country in South Asia", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/India", "wikipedia_extract": "India, officially the Republic of India, is a country in South Asia. It is the seventh-largest country by area; the most populous country since 2023; and, since its independence in 1947, the world's most populous democracy. Bounded by the Indian Ocean on the south, the Arabian Sea on the southwest, and the Bay of Bengal on the southeast, it shares land borders with Pakistan to the west; China, Nepal, and Bhutan to the north; and Bangladesh and Myanmar to the east. In the Indian Ocean, India is near Sri Lanka and the Maldives; its Andaman and Nicobar Islands share a maritime border with Myanmar, Thailand, and Indonesia. Modern humans arrived on the Indian subcontinent from Africa no later than 55,000 years ago. Their long occupation, predominantly in isolation as hunter-gatherers, has made the region highly diverse."}}, {"canonical_name": "Sindu", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "sinde", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Sinde", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Sinde"], "contexts": ["independent state"]}, {"entity_id": "sindu", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Sindu", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Sindu", "Sindhu"], "contexts": ["river called Milan or Sinteu", "Sanskrit name for a river"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Sinde", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Sindu", "similarity": 0.949}], "id": 4728, "stable_key": "clu_aa306dfd2e29e515", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sinu River", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "River in Colombia, associated with coal deposits and medicinal plants.", "note": "The mention of 'Sindu' and 'Sinteu' suggests a possible conflation with the Indus River or a misinterpretation of local names.", "wikipedia_extract": "Sin\u00fa River (Spanish: R\u00edo Sin\u00fa), is a river in northwestern Colombia that flows mostly through the C\u00f3rdoba Department and into the Caribbean. It is the third most important river in the Caribbean Region, after the Magdalena River and the Cauca River. It is born in the Antioquia Department, flowing south to north for 415 kilometres (258 mi) until flowing into the Caribbean Sea. The river is navigable for only half of its length, 200 kilometres (120 mi) starting in Monter\u00eda, the largest city in its basin. Also, this river is the main tourist attraction of Monter\u00eda decorated with the first avenue when it passes by Monter\u00eda. The river flows between the eastern and middle prongs of the Cordillera Occidental."}}, {"canonical_name": "Jeniseisk", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "jeniseisk", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Jeniseisk", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Jeniseisk"], "contexts": ["city in Siberia"]}, {"entity_id": "jeniseisk", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Jeniseisk", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Jeniseisk"], "contexts": ["government in Siberia", "government containing district of Agram"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Jeniseisk", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Jeniseisk", "similarity": 0.759}], "id": 4729, "stable_key": "clu_60cd01e72dab1c70", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Yeniseysk", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Russia", "note": "Both 'government' (guberniya) and 'city' refer to the administrative and urban center in Siberia.", "wikidata_id": "Q135686", "wikidata_description": "town in Yeniseysk Urban Okrug, Krasnoyarsk Krai, Russia", "wikipedia_extract": "Yeniseysk (Russian: \u0415\u043d\u0438\u0441\u0435\u0439\u0441\u043a, IPA: [j\u026an\u02b2\u026a\u02c8s\u02b2ejsk]) is a town in Krasnoyarsk Krai, Russia, located on the Yenisei River. Population: 18,766\u2009(2010 census); 20,394\u2009(2002 census); 22,891\u2009(1989 Soviet census); 20,000 (1970). Yeniseysk was founded in 1619 as a stockaded town\u2014the first town on the Yenisei River. It played an important role in Russian colonization of East Siberia in the 17th\u201318th centuries. Its location is due to the Siberian River Routes from the Urals, up the Ob, up the Ket River and over a portage to Yeniseysk and from there to the Yenisei basin. It became less important due to road and rail building further south."}}, {"canonical_name": "Tibet", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "tibet", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Tibet", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Tibet"], "contexts": ["region excluded from Tartary"]}, {"entity_id": "tibet", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Tibet", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Tibet"], "contexts": ["region where wheat ripens at high elevation", "region where wheat ripens"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Tibet", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Tibet", "similarity": 0.929}], "id": 4730, "stable_key": "clu_11c36c110284600c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tibet", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "A region in Asia, also including an island.", "note": "Includes references to Tibet and Tahiti.", "wikipedia_extract": "Tibet ( ) is a region in the western part of East Asia, covering much of the Tibetan Plateau. It is the homeland of the Tibetans. Other ethnic groups also reside on the plateau, including Mongols, the Lhoba, Monpa, Qiang, Sherpa, and since the 20th century, Han and Hui peoples. Tibet is the highest region on Earth, with an average elevation of 4,380 m (14,000 ft). Lying within the Himalayas, the highest point in Tibet is Mount Everest \u2013 the highest peak on Earth, standing 8,848 m (29,000 ft) above sea level. In the 7th century, the Yarlung dynasty expanded into surrounding areas such as Amdo."}}, {"canonical_name": "Carguairazo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "carguairazo", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Carguairazo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Carguairazo"], "contexts": ["volcano in Ecuador"]}, {"entity_id": "carguairazo", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Carguairazo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Carguairazo"], "contexts": ["mountain that fell in", "location of volcanic mud eruptions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Carguairazo", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Carguairazo", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4731, "stable_key": "clu_10214cb2dcfb0fb3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cargua\u00edrazo", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Ecuador"}}, {"canonical_name": "Paramos", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "paramos", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Paramos", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Paramos"], "contexts": ["high-altitude plateaus"]}, {"entity_id": "paramos", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Paramos", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["Paramos"], "contexts": ["cold regions exposed to storms", "tropical region with snow"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Paramos", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Paramos", "similarity": 0.852}], "id": 4732, "stable_key": "clu_dbfbec14c97d58a3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "P\u00e1ramos", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Colombia", "note": "P\u00e1ramos are high-altitude grassland ecosystems.", "wikidata_id": "Q3023796", "wikidata_description": "species of butterfly", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paramo%20aculata", "wikipedia_extract": "Paramo aculata is the sole species in the monotypic butterfly genus Paramo from the subfamily Satyrinae in the family Nymphalidae. The genus was erected by Michael Jan Adams and George Igor Bernard in 1977. The species was first described by Edwin Kr\u00fcger in 1924. It occurs in Colombia."}}, {"canonical_name": "Orizaba", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "orizaba", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Orizaba", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Orizaba"], "contexts": ["volcanoes of Orizaba"]}, {"entity_id": "orizaba", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Orizaba", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Orizaba"], "contexts": ["volcano named Citialtepetl", "volcanic mountain in Mexico"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Orizaba", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Orizaba", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4733, "stable_key": "clu_23e53422a3ec8c24", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Orizaba", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Mexico"}}, {"canonical_name": "Paros", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "paro", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Paro", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Paro"], "contexts": ["river on whose eastern bank hills advance"]}, {"entity_id": "paros", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Paros", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Paros"], "contexts": ["island with geological formations and fossils", "island mentioned in relation to geology"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Paro", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Paros", "similarity": 0.931}], "id": 4734, "stable_key": "clu_d7d72ce0215b65fe", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Paros", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Greece", "wikidata_id": "Q17432507", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants"}}, {"canonical_name": "K. C. von Leonhard", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "k__c__von_leonhard", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "K. C. von Leonhard", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["K. C. von Leonhard"], "contexts": ["translator of geognostic research"]}, {"entity_id": "karl_von_leonhard", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Karl von Leonhard", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Karl von Leonhard"], "contexts": ["experienced observer of mineralogy"]}, {"entity_id": "leonhard,_karl_von", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Leonhard, Karl von", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Leonhard, Karl von"], "contexts": ["geologist"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "K. C. von Leonhard", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Karl von Leonhard", "similarity": 0.916}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "K. C. von Leonhard", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Leonhard, Karl von", "similarity": 0.864}], "id": 4735, "stable_key": "clu_f3ffa73d1e39b8d9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Karl C. von Leonhard", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1779, "death_year": 1862, "description": "German geologist and mineralogist, translator and geognostic researcher."}}, {"canonical_name": "Weald clay", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "weald_cl\u00e0y", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "weald cl\u00e0y", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["weald cl\u00e0y"], "contexts": ["clay from the Weald"]}, {"entity_id": "weald_clay", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Weald clay", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Weald clay"], "contexts": ["geological formation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "weald cl\u00e0y", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Weald clay", "similarity": 0.788}], "id": 4736, "stable_key": "clu_a4010d4fe9f4b5d1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Weald Clay", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q7977996", "wikidata_description": "geological formation in England"}}, {"canonical_name": "London clay", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "argile_de_londres", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "argile de Londres", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["argile de Londres"], "contexts": ["London clay"]}, {"entity_id": "london_clay", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "London clay", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["London clay"], "contexts": ["geological deposit containing fossil fishes", "geological formation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "argile de Londres", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "London clay", "similarity": 0.911}], "id": 4737, "stable_key": "clu_4897373d0355258a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "London Clay", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q59637656", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article by David Atkinson published in June 1969", "wikipedia_extract": "The London Clay Formation is a marine geological formation of Ypresian (early Eocene Epoch, c. 54-50 million years ago) age which crops out in the southeast of England. The London Clay is well known for its fossil content. The fossils from the lower Eocene rocks indicate a moderately warm climate, the tropical or subtropical flora. Though sea levels changed during the deposition of the clay, the habitat was generally a lush forest \u2013 perhaps like in Indonesia or East Africa today \u2013 bordering a warm, shallow ocean. The London Clay is a stiff bluish clay which becomes brown when weathered and oxidized. Nodular lumps of pyrite are frequently found in the clay layers."}}, {"canonical_name": "pyroxenic nepheline", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "pyroxenic", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "pyroxenic", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["pyroxenic"], "contexts": ["porphyry type associated with amygdaloid"]}, {"entity_id": "pyroxenic_nepheline", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "pyroxenic nepheline", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["pyroxenic nepheline"], "contexts": ["type of nepheline", "mineral type"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "pyroxenic", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "pyroxenic nepheline", "similarity": 0.903}], "id": 4738, "stable_key": "clu_8f2790f7005bca20", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Nepheline syenite", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "The term 'pyroxenic nepheline' likely refers to nepheline within a syenitic or igneous rock context, often associated with minerals like pyroxene. Nepheline syenite is a suitable modern classification.", "wikidata_id": "Q130096103", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article published in 2015"}}, {"canonical_name": "syenitic porphyry", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "porphyry", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "porphyry", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["porphyry"], "contexts": ["rock type, pyroxenic"]}, {"entity_id": "syenitic_porphyry", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "syenitic porphyry", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["syenitic porphyry"], "contexts": ["igneous rock type", "type of porphyry"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "porphyry", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "syenitic porphyry", "similarity": 0.914}], "id": 4739, "stable_key": "clu_969c93e452b5e744", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Syenitic porphyry", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral"}}, {"canonical_name": "Mini\u017ftros", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "mineurs", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "mineurs", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["mineurs"], "contexts": ["workers in mines"]}, {"entity_id": "mini\u017ftros", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Mini\u017ftros", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["Mini\u017ftros", "Mini\u017ftro"], "contexts": ["officials who need to stay awake", "official receiving information"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "mineurs", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Mini\u017ftros", "similarity": 0.874}], "id": 4740, "stable_key": "clu_3a9263dcc8d0e021", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "ministers", "confidence": "low", "type": "concept", "note": "The terms 'Mini\u017ftros' (officials) and 'mineurs' (workers in mines) are too divergent and lack specific medical/scientific context to confidently identify a single referent. 'Ministers' is a general guess for the first term.", "wikidata_id": "Q83307", "wikidata_description": "politician who holds significant civil public office in a national or regional government, generally heading a ministry", "wikipedia_extract": "The Union Council of Ministers is the principal executive organ of the Government of India, which serves to aid and advise the President of India in execution of their functions. It is chaired by the Prime Minister of India and consists of the heads of each of the executive government ministries. Currently, the council is headed by Narendra Modi and consists of 71 fellow members. The council is answerable to the Lok Sabha. A smaller executive body called the Union Cabinet is the supreme decision-making body in India; it is a subset of the Union Council of Ministers who hold important portfolios and ministries of the government. Pursuant to Article 75(3), the Council of Ministers is responsible collectively to the lower house of the Indian parliament, called the Lok Sabha (House of the People).", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minister_%28government%29"}}, {"canonical_name": "Deshayes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "des_hayes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "des Hayes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["des Hayes"], "contexts": ["physicists observing barometer variations"]}, {"entity_id": "deshayes", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Deshayes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Deshayes"], "contexts": ["laborer in tertiary formations research", "investigated organic life relations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "des Hayes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Deshayes", "similarity": 0.717}], "id": 4741, "stable_key": "clu_5c9e5dff826d6fc7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "G\u00e9rard Paul Deshayes", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1795, "death_year": 1875, "description": "French paleontologist and conchologist who studied tertiary formations.", "wikidata_id": "Q1559020", "wikidata_description": "French scientist (1795-1875)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/G%C3%A9rard%20Paul%20Deshayes", "wikipedia_extract": "G\u00e9rard Paul Deshayes (French pronunciation: [\u0292e\u0281a\u0281 p\u0254l de\u025b]; 13 May 1795 \u2013 9 June 1875) was a French geologist and conchologist. He was born in Nancy, his father at that time being professor of experimental physics in the \u00c9cole Centrale of the d\u00e9partement Meurthe He studied medicine in Strasbourg, and afterwards took the degree of bachelier \u00e8s lettres in Paris in 1821; but he abandoned the medical profession in order to devote himself to natural history. For some time he gave private lessons on geology, and subsequently became professor of natural history in the Mus\u00e9um National d'Histoire Naturelle. He was distinguished for his researches on the fossil mollusca of the Paris Basin and of other areas Cenozoic cover. His studies on the relations of the fossil to the recent species led him as early as 1829 to conclusions somewhat similar to those arrived at by Lyell, to whom Deshayes rendered much assistance in connection with the classification of the, then, Tertiary system into Eocene, Miocene and Pliocene. With Andr\u00e9 \u00c9tienne d'Audebert de F\u00e9russac, he co-authored an important study on terrestrial and river mollusks titled Histoire naturelle g\u00e9n\u00e9rale et particuli\u00e8re des Mollusques terrestres et fluviatiles (1820-1851)."}}, {"canonical_name": "capitaine Kater", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "capitaine_kater", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "capitaine Kater", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["capitaine Kater"], "contexts": ["observer in Mysore highlands"]}, {"entity_id": "captain_kater", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Captain Kater", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Captain Kater"], "contexts": ["determined pendulum length"]}, {"entity_id": "kater,_captain", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Kater, Captain", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Kater, Captain"], "contexts": ["determined approximate length of the pendulum"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "capitaine Kater", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Captain Kater", "similarity": 0.952}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "capitaine Kater", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Kater, Captain", "similarity": 0.911}], "id": 4742, "stable_key": "clu_bc446493fa688075", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Henry Kater", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1777, "death_year": 1835, "description": "English physicist known for his work on the pendulum and gravity."}}, {"canonical_name": "Hemmer", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "hemmer", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Hemmer", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hemmer"], "contexts": ["scholar who noticed barometric variations"]}, {"entity_id": "hemmer", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Hemmer", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Hemmer"], "contexts": ["observer of falling stars", "on falling stars"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Hemmer", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Hemmer", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4743, "stable_key": "clu_4f28f44638544624", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Christian Friedrich Hemmer", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1731, "death_year": 1790, "description": "German physician and meteorologist, studied atmospheric electricity.", "note": "The context of barometric variations and falling stars strongly points to Christian Friedrich Hemmer, a meteorologist who worked with atmospheric electricity."}}, {"canonical_name": "Manheim", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "manheim", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Manheim", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Manheim"], "contexts": ["city of meteorological society"]}, {"entity_id": "manheim", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Manheim", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Manheim"], "contexts": ["location of observation", "city in Germany"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Manheim", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Manheim", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4744, "stable_key": "clu_fbe379204c79f36b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mannheim", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Germany", "wikidata_id": "Q2119", "wikidata_description": "city in Baden-W\u00fcrttemberg, Germany", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mannheim", "wikipedia_extract": "Mannheim (German pronunciation: [\u02c8manha\u026am] ; Palatine German: Mannem or Monnem), officially the University City of Mannheim (German: Universit\u00e4tsstadt Mannheim), is the second-largest city in Baden-W\u00fcrttemberg after Stuttgart, the state capital, and Germany's 21st-largest city, with a population of over 315,000. It is located at the border with Rhineland-Palatinate. The city is the cultural and economic centre of the Rhine-Neckar, Germany's seventh-largest metropolitan region, with nearly 2.4 million inhabitants. Mannheim is located at the confluence of the Upper Rhine and the Neckar in the Kurpfalz (Electoral Palatinate) region of northwestern Baden-W\u00fcrttemberg. The city lies in the Upper Rhine Plain, Germany's warmest region, between the Palatine Forest and the Oden Forest. Mannheim forms a continuous urban zone of around 500,000 inhabitants with Ludwigshafen am Rhein in the Rhineland-Palatinate, while some northern suburbs lie in Hesse; Hamburg is the only other German city with a presence in two states other than its own."}}, {"canonical_name": "Essai sur la Geographie des Plantes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "essai_sur_la_g\u00e9ographie_des_plantes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Essai sur la G\u00e9ographie des plantes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Essai sur la G\u00e9ographie des plantes"], "contexts": ["title of a work"]}, {"entity_id": "essai_sur_la_geographie_des_plantes", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Essai sur la Geographie des Plantes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Essai sur la Geographie des Plantes"], "contexts": ["publication by Humboldt", "work by Humboldt on plant geography"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Essai sur la G\u00e9ographie des plantes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Essai sur la Geographie des Plantes", "similarity": 0.99}], "id": 4745, "stable_key": "clu_92857365bd7d7636", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Essay on the Geography of Plants", "confidence": "high", "type": "text", "note": "This is a direct translation of the title of Alexander von Humboldt's influential work.", "wikidata_id": "Q51469713", "wikidata_description": "1807 Humboldt text"}}, {"canonical_name": "Canarische Inseln", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "canarischen_jnseln", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Canarischen Jnseln", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Canarischen Jnseln"], "contexts": ["location mentioned in a work title"]}, {"entity_id": "canarische_inseln", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Canarische Inseln", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Canarische Inseln"], "contexts": ["book title by Leop. von Buch", "book title about islands"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Canarischen Jnseln", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Canarische Inseln", "similarity": 0.985}], "id": 4746, "stable_key": "clu_b569efb86286465f-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Canary Islands", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Spain", "note": "Direct translation of the German name for the archipelago.", "wikidata_id": "Q5813", "wikidata_description": "autonomous community of Spain, and archipelago in the Atlantic Ocean", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canary%20Islands", "wikipedia_extract": "The Canary Islands ( ; Spanish: Canarias [ka\u02c8na\u027ejas] ) or Canaries are an archipelago in the Atlantic Ocean and the southernmost autonomous community of Spain, located about 100 kilometres (60 mi) off the northwest coast of Africa. The archipelago has a population of approximately 2.27 million inhabitants, making it the most populous overseas special territory of the European Union. The seven main islands, listed from largest to smallest by area, are Tenerife, Fuerteventura, Gran Canaria, Lanzarote, La Palma, La Gomera, and El Hierro. The only other inhabited island is La Graciosa, which is administratively dependent on Lanzarote. The archipelago also includes numerous smaller islands and islets, such as Alegranza, Isla de Lobos, Monta\u00f1a Clara, Roque del Oeste, and Roque del Este, as well as various rocks, including Garachico and Anaga. Historically, the island chain was referred to as \"the Fortunate Isles\"."}}, {"canonical_name": "\u00e9tat \u00e9lectrique", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "\u00e9tat_\u00e9lectrique", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "\u00e9tat \u00e9lectrique", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["\u00e9tat \u00e9lectrique"], "contexts": ["measure of atmospheric electricity"]}, {"entity_id": "electrical_state", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "electrical state", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["electrical state"], "contexts": ["condition of particles related to electricity"]}, {"entity_id": "electric_state", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "electric state", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["electric state"], "contexts": ["condition of a body regarding electricity"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "\u00e9tat \u00e9lectrique", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "electrical state", "similarity": 0.935}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "\u00e9tat \u00e9lectrique", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "electric state", "similarity": 0.896}], "id": 4747, "stable_key": "clu_46751f11adefc4c3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "electrical state", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The context describes the condition of a body or particles concerning electricity, which is what 'electrical state' refers to.", "wikidata_id": "Q127391535", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article"}}, {"canonical_name": "emigration", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "\u00e9migrations", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "\u00e9migrations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["\u00e9migrations"], "contexts": ["movement of people"]}, {"entity_id": "emigration", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "emigration", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["emigration"], "contexts": ["movement of people out of a country"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "\u00e9migrations", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "emigration", "similarity": 0.924}], "id": 4748, "stable_key": "clu_d2f375b7868a3933", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Emigration", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q187668", "wikidata_description": "act of leaving one's country or region with the intent to settle permanently or temporarily in another", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emigration", "wikipedia_extract": "Emigration is the act of leaving a resident country or place of residence with the intent to settle elsewhere (to permanently leave a country). Conversely, immigration describes the movement of people into one country from another (to permanently move to a country). A migrant emigrates from their old country, and immigrates to their new country. Thus, both emigration and immigration describe migration, but from different countries' perspectives. Demographers examine push and pull factors for people to be pushed out of one place and attracted to another. There can be a desire to escape negative circumstances such as shortages of land or jobs, or unfair treatment."}}, {"canonical_name": "Gryphites", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "gryphites", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "gryphites", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["gryphites"], "contexts": ["fossil found in jurassic limestone"]}, {"entity_id": "gryphites", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Gryphites", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 2, "variants": ["Gryphites"], "contexts": ["fossil bivalve mollusk", "fossil bivalve remains"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "gryphites", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Gryphites", "similarity": 0.825}], "id": 4749, "stable_key": "clu_73009da9fa905e8d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Fossil bivalve mollusk", "confidence": "medium", "type": "animal", "note": "The term 'Gryphites' likely refers to a fossil, and the context suggests a bivalve mollusk, possibly related to Gryphaea. Precise Linnaean classification is uncertain without more context.", "wikidata_id": "Q59096161", "wikidata_description": "scientific article published in Nature"}}, {"canonical_name": "frescas", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "fra\u00eeche", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "fra\u00eeche", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["fra\u00eeche"], "contexts": ["quality of being cool"]}, {"entity_id": "frescas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "frescas", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["frescas", "fresco"], "contexts": ["quality of rooms", "quality of eggs, beneficial"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "fra\u00eeche", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "frescas", "similarity": 0.959}], "id": 4750, "stable_key": "clu_dee1748e5aa6b341", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Coolness", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q127893313", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article", "wikipedia_extract": "Coolness, or being cool, is the aesthetic quality of something (such as attitude, behavior, appearance, or style) being compatible with currently admirable social norms of society or a group of people. Because of the varied and changing interpretation of what is considered cool, as well as its subjective nature, the word has no single meaning. For most, coolness is associated with exemplifying composure and self-control. When used in conversation, it is often as an expression of admiration or approval, and can be used when referencing both people and items of interest. Although commonly regarded as slang, cool is widely used among disparate social groups and has endured in usage for generations. Below are some concise definitions that try to capture its different usages: \"If status is about standing, cool is about standing free.\" \u2013 Grant McCracken \"Cool is a knowledge, a way of life.\" \u2013 Lewis MacAdams \"Cool is an age-specific phenomenon, defined as the central behavioural trait of teenagerhood.\" \"Coolness is the proper way you represent yourself to a human being.\" \u2013 Robert Farris Thompson In the novel Spook Country by William Gibson, one character equates cool with a sense of exclusivity: \"'Secrets,' said the Bigend beside her, 'are the very root of cool.'\" In the novel Lords and Ladies by Terry Pratchett, the \"Monks of Cool\" are mentioned."}}, {"canonical_name": "suicide", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "suicide", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "suicide", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["suicide"], "contexts": ["act of self-killing"]}, {"entity_id": "suicide", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "suicide", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["suicide"], "contexts": ["attempt driven by distress", "act of self-destruction"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "suicide", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "suicide", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4751, "stable_key": "clu_25f83835a587650f", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Suicide is the act of intentionally causing one's own death. Risk factors for suicide include mental disorders, neurodevelopmental disorders, physical disorders, and substance abuse. Some suicides are impulsive acts driven by stress (such as from financial or academic difficulties), relationship problems (such as breakups or divorces), or harassment and bullying. Those who have previously attempted suicide are at a higher risk for future attempts. Effective suicide prevention efforts include limiting access to methods of suicide such as firearms, drugs, and poisons; treating mental disorders and substance abuse; careful media reporting about suicide; improving economic conditions; and psychotherapy, primarily dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT) and cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT). Although crisis hotlines, like 988 in North America and 13 11 14 in Australia, are common resources, their effectiveness has not been well studied."}}, {"canonical_name": "lesser Antilles", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "petites-antilles_hollandoises", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Petites-Antilles hollandoises", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Petites-Antilles hollandoises"], "contexts": ["Dutch Lesser Antilles"]}, {"entity_id": "lesser_antilles", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "lesser Antilles", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["lesser Antilles"], "contexts": ["islands east of hurricane origin", "islands east of Caribbean"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Petites-Antilles hollandoises", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "lesser Antilles", "similarity": 0.903}], "id": 4752, "stable_key": "clu_64e293d18f9383a3-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Antilles", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Archipelago in the Caribbean Sea, comprising numerous islands.", "wikipedia_extract": "The Antilles is an archipelago within the West Indies in the Caribbean region of the Americas. It is bordered by the Caribbean Sea to the south and west, the Gulf of Mexico to the northwest, and the North Atlantic Ocean to the north and east. The Antillean islands are divided into two smaller archipelagos: the Greater Antilles and the Lesser Antilles. The Greater Antilles include the Cayman Islands and larger islands of Cuba, Hispaniola (subdivided into the nations of the Dominican Republic and Haiti), Navassa Island, Jamaica, and Puerto Rico. The Lesser Antilles contains the northerly Leeward Islands and the southeasterly Windward Islands, as well as the Leeward Antilles immediately north of Venezuela. The Lucayan Archipelago, consisting of The Bahamas and the Turks and Caicos Islands, though a part of the West Indies, is generally not included among the Antillean islands."}}, {"canonical_name": "Plates", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "panes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "panes", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["panes"], "contexts": ["Spanish term for sugar forms"]}, {"entity_id": "plates", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Plates", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Plates", "plates"], "contexts": ["components of a voltaic battery", "vibrating objects for experiments"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "panes", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Plates", "similarity": 0.89}], "id": 4753, "stable_key": "clu_1c78652cb6971ef6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Electrode", "confidence": "medium", "type": "object", "note": "The term 'Plates' used in the context of a 'voltaic battery' refers to electrodes. The Spanish term for sugar forms is a different usage of 'platos'.", "wikidata_id": "Q2704989", "wikidata_description": "Pok\u00e9mon species", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electrode%20%28Pok%C3%A9mon%29"}}, {"canonical_name": "No\u00e9", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "no\u00e9", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "No\u00e9", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["No\u00e9"], "contexts": ["figure in Semitic tradition"]}, {"entity_id": "no\u00e9", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "No\u00e9", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["No\u00e9"], "contexts": ["biblical patriarch"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "No\u00e9", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "No\u00e9", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4754, "stable_key": "clu_2a06f405d22b34a2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Noah", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Biblical patriarch of the Great Flood story"}}, {"canonical_name": "drachma", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "drachmes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "drachmes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["drachmes"], "contexts": ["currency unit used by Greeks"]}, {"entity_id": "drachma", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "drachma", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["drachma"], "contexts": ["unit of quantity for medicines", "unit of weight for substance"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "drachmes", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "drachma", "similarity": 0.884}], "id": 4755, "stable_key": "clu_9edd4661cb2e5515", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Drachma", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Unit of weight, often used in apothecaries' contexts, derived from the ancient Greek currency.", "wikidata_id": "Q5304630", "wikidata_description": "genus of insects; genus of Arthoropoda containing only one species", "wikipedia_extract": "In ancient Greek religion and mythology, Helios (; Ancient Greek: \u1f2d\u03bb\u03b9\u03bf\u03c2 pronounced [h\u025b\u030c\u02d0lios], lit. 'Sun'; Homeric Greek: \u1f28\u03ad\u03bb\u03b9\u03bf\u03c2) is the god who personifies the Sun. His name is also Latinized as Helius, and he is often given the epithets Hyperion (\"the one above\") and Phaethon (\"the shining\"). Helios is often depicted in art with a radiant crown and driving a horse-drawn chariot through the sky. He was a guardian of oaths and also the god of sight. Though Helios was a relatively minor deity in Classical Greece, his worship grew more prominent in late antiquity thanks to his identification with several major solar divinities of the Roman period, particularly Apollo and Sol.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drachma"}}, {"canonical_name": "camalea", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "camalote", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "camalote", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["camalote"], "contexts": ["a type of plant"]}, {"entity_id": "camalea", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "camalea", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["camalea", "camelea"], "contexts": ["spice, possibly cinnamon", "plant name"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "camalote", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "camalea", "similarity": 0.883}], "id": 4756, "stable_key": "clu_ce7a3c19ba413887", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Chamomile", "confidence": "medium", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Matricaria chamomilla L.", "family": "Asteraceae", "note": "While 'camalea' could refer to cinnamon (as suggested in the note), 'camemali' in Polyanthea Medicinal strongly suggests Chamomile (from Latin 'camomilla'). The spice context is ambiguous. Given the stronger link to chamomile, that is the primary identification, but cinnamon remains a possibility.", "wikipedia_extract": "Chamomile (American English) or camomile (British English; see spelling differences) ( KAM-\u0259-myle or KAM-\u0259-meel) is the common name for several daisy-like plants of the family Asteraceae. Two of the species, Matricaria chamomilla and Chamaemelum nobile, are commonly used to make herbal infusions for beverages. Chamomile is used as a flavoring in foods, beverages, and cosmetics, in herbal teas, in brewing beer, and as a ground cover or seating plant in gardens. There is no clinical evidence supporting the effectiveness of consuming chamomile to treat any disease. Chamomile may interact adversely with various herbs and drugs, worsen pollen allergies, and is not recommended for people with hormone-sensitive conditions or when combined with anticoagulants. Because Roman chamomile may cause uterine contractions, it should not be used during pregnancy, and its safety during breastfeeding is unknown."}}, {"canonical_name": "Ritchie", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "ritchie", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Ritchie", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ritchie"], "contexts": ["unfortunate Ritchie"]}, {"entity_id": "ritchie", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Ritchie", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Ritchie"], "contexts": ["exhibited rotation of water by electricity", "performed electrical experiments"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Ritchie", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Ritchie", "similarity": 0.731}], "id": 4757, "stable_key": "clu_aff6d4a7c5547747", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ritchie", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "Scientist associated with electrical rotation of water.", "note": "The specific Ritchie is unclear without more context or a first name."}}, {"canonical_name": "Lichtenberg", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "lichtenberg", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Lichtenberg", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lichtenberg"], "contexts": ["scientist associated with electrical figures"]}, {"entity_id": "lichtenberg", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Lichtenberg", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Lichtenberg"], "contexts": ["stated stones reach atmosphere from space", "physicist"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Lichtenberg", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Lichtenberg", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4758, "stable_key": "clu_d7cd070d2ad45303", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Georg Christoph Lichtenberg", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1742, "death_year": 1799, "description": "Scientist known for electrical figures and meteoric theories."}}, {"canonical_name": "Palma", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "palma", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Palma", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Palma"], "contexts": ["island in the archipelago"]}, {"entity_id": "palma", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Palma", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Palma"], "contexts": ["island in the Canary Islands", "island related to eruption of Teneriffe"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Palma", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Palma", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4759, "stable_key": "clu_1728dc99a9e4f441", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Palma de Mallorca", "confidence": "medium", "type": "place", "country": "Spain", "note": "While 'Palma' can refer to multiple places, in the context of the Canary Islands archipelago, it most likely refers to the island of La Palma. However, given the ambiguity, a broader search for 'Palma' in relation to Canary Islands is provided."}}, {"canonical_name": "vents du nord", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "vents_du_nord", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "vents du nord", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["vents du nord"], "contexts": ["cause currents"]}, {"entity_id": "southwest_wind", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "southwest wind", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["southwest wind"], "contexts": ["wind from the southwest direction"]}, {"entity_id": "northeast_wind", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "northeast wind", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["northeast wind"], "contexts": ["wind from the northeast direction"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "vents du nord", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "southwest wind", "similarity": 0.897}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "vents du nord", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "northeast wind", "similarity": 0.93}], "id": 4760, "stable_key": "clu_c7a655be6202e9e7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Wind Direction and Atmospheric Phenomena", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The cluster refers to specific wind directions ('vents du nord', 'southwest wind', 'northeast wind') and their role in atmospheric phenomena and currents, a concept in meteorology and geography.", "wikidata_id": "Q1057900", "wikidata_description": "geographic direction from which a wind blows", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind%20direction", "wikipedia_extract": "Wind direction is generally reported by the direction from which the wind originates. For example, a north or northerly wind blows from the north to the south; the exceptions are onshore winds (blowing onto the shore from the water) and offshore winds (blowing off the shore to the water). Wind direction is usually reported in cardinal (or compass) direction, or in degrees. Consequently, a wind blowing from the north has a wind direction referred to as 0\u00b0 (360\u00b0); a wind blowing from the east has a wind direction referred to as 90\u00b0, etc. Weather forecasts typically give the direction of the wind along with its speed, for example a \"northerly wind at 15 km/h\" is a wind blowing from the north at a speed of 15 km/h. If wind gusts are present, their speed may also be reported."}}, {"canonical_name": "Nouet", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "noiiet", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Noiiet", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Noiiet"], "contexts": ["observer of continental atmosphere temperatures"]}, {"entity_id": "nouet", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Nouet", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Nouet"], "contexts": ["engraved latitudes on Egyptian monuments", "provided latitudes engraved on monuments"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Noiiet", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Nouet", "similarity": 0.859}], "id": 4761, "stable_key": "clu_3126dfd3fd8a45c8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jean-Philippe-Eug\u00e8ne Nouet", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1763, "death_year": 1832, "description": "French astronomer and mathematician, observed continental atmosphere temperatures.", "note": "Identified based on 'Nouet' and the context of astronomical and geographical observations, fitting with Humboldt's scientific network."}}, {"canonical_name": "Bembo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "bembo", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Bembo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bembo"], "contexts": ["named in admirable pages"]}, {"entity_id": "bembo", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Bembo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Bembo"], "contexts": ["historian observing Aetna eruption", "author of 'Aetna Dialogus'"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Bembo", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Bembo", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4762, "stable_key": "clu_889680921cbe93ad", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pietro Bembo", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1470, "death_year": 1547, "description": "Italian scholar, historian, and cardinal, wrote on Aetna.", "note": "The context 'historian observing Aetna eruption' strongly points to Pietro Bembo, known for his work 'De Aetna'.", "wikidata_id": "Q16590374", "wikidata_description": "Italian catholic bishop, great-grandson of Cardinal Pietro Bembo"}}, {"canonical_name": "Tortona", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "tortosa", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Tortosa", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Tortosa"], "contexts": ["city"]}, {"entity_id": "tortona", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Tortona", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Tortona"], "contexts": ["publication location of memoir", "Italian town"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Tortosa", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Tortona", "similarity": 0.921}], "id": 4763, "stable_key": "clu_1cfe3ddd36ebb0ff", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tortosa", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Spain", "note": "While 'Tortona' is a city in Italy, the context of Humboldt's work and geographical references often point to the more prominent 'Tortosa' in Spain, especially when linked to publications or scientific observations within a broader European context.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}}, {"canonical_name": "Terek", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "terek", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Terek", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Terek"], "contexts": ["river mouth location"]}, {"entity_id": "terek", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Terek", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Terek"], "contexts": ["river mouth where Kislar is located", "river mouth location"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Terek", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Terek", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4764, "stable_key": "clu_300f2ac2a86583ce", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Terek River", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Russia", "wikidata_id": "Q82776", "wikidata_description": "river in Georgia and Russia", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Terek%20%28river%29", "wikipedia_extract": "The Terek is a major river in the Northern Caucasus. It originates in the Mtskheta-Mtianeti region of Georgia and flows through North Caucasus region of Russia into the Caspian Sea. It rises near the juncture of the Greater Caucasus Mountain Range and the Khokh Range, to the southwest of Mount Kazbek, winding north in a white torrent between the town of Stepantsminda and the village of Gergeti toward the Russian region North Ossetia and the city of Vladikavkaz. It turns east to flow through Chechnya and Dagestan before dividing into two branches which empty into the Caspian Sea. Below the city of Kizlyar it forms a swampy river delta around 100 kilometres (62 mi) wide. The river is a key natural asset in the region, providing irrigation and hydroelectric power in its upper reaches."}}, {"canonical_name": "Cross", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "croix", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "croix", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["croix"], "contexts": ["held by mendicants"]}, {"entity_id": "cross", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Cross", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Cross"], "contexts": ["constellation near starless patches", "celestial reference point"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "croix", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Cross", "similarity": 0.925}], "id": 4765, "stable_key": "clu_606650985e37c0ed", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Crusado", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "description": "Portuguese currency, and a vessel for melting metals.", "note": "This cluster conflates a unit of currency ('crusados') with a type of crucible ('creusets') and a religious symbol ('croix'). The primary meaning suggested by the context of 'currency' and 'buying amber' is the Portuguese Crusado coin. However, the presence of 'creusets' also points to a vessel.", "wikipedia_extract": "Guru Arjan (Gurmukhi: \u0a17\u0a41\u0a30\u0a42 \u0a05\u0a30\u0a1c\u0a28, pronunciation: [g\u028a\u027eu\u02d0 \u0259\u027ed\u0361\u0292\u0259n\u1d4a]; 15 April 1563 \u2013 30 May 1606) was the fifth of the ten total Sikh Gurus. He compiled the first official edition of the Sikh scripture called the Adi Granth, which later expanded into the Guru Granth Sahib. He is regarded as the first of the two Gurus martyred in the Sikh faith. Guru Arjan was born in Goindval, in the Punjab, the youngest son of Bhai Jetha, who later became Guru Ram Das, and Mata Bhani, the daughter of Guru Amar Das. He completed the construction of the Darbar Sahib at Amritsar, after the fourth Sikh Guru founded the town and built a sarovar. Arjan compiled the hymns of previous Gurus and of other saints into Adi Granth, the first edition of the Sikh scripture, and installed it in the Harimandir Sahib."}}, {"canonical_name": "vital movements", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "mouvement_vital", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "mouvement vital", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["mouvement vital"], "contexts": ["life-related movement"]}, {"entity_id": "vital_movements", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "vital movements", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["vital movements"], "contexts": ["movements essential for life", "movements characteristic of living organisms"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "mouvement vital", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "vital movements", "similarity": 0.933}], "id": 4766, "stable_key": "clu_4a6b4cb816813879-4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Vitalism", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The phrases 'vital activity', 'vital actions', and 'life force' are hallmarks of the philosophical concept of vitalism.", "wikidata_id": "Q137271198", "wikidata_description": "nonprofit organisation from the Netherlands"}}, {"canonical_name": "Girgenti", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "girgenti", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Girgenti", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Girgenti"], "contexts": ["near Macaluba mud volcano"]}, {"entity_id": "girgenti", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Girgenti", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Girgenti"], "contexts": ["location of mud volcanoes", "location in Sicily"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Girgenti", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Girgenti", "similarity": 0.814}], "id": 4767, "stable_key": "clu_f75a608771c88747", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Agrigento", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Italy", "note": "Girgenti is the historical Italian name for Agrigento.", "wikipedia_extract": "Agrigento (Italian: [a\u0261ri\u02c8d\u0292\u025bnto] ; Sicilian: Girgenti [d\u0292\u026a\u027e\u02c8d\u0292\u025bnd\u026a] or Giurgenti [d\u0292\u028a\u027e\u02c8d\u0292\u025bnd\u026a]) is a city on the southern coast of the autonomous island region of Sicily in Italy, capital of the province of Agrigento. As of 2025, with a population of 55,227, it is also the largest city in the province, 10th-largest in Sicily and 115th-largest in Italy. Founded around 582 BC by Greek colonists from Gela, Agrigento, then known as Akragas, was one of the leading cities during the golden age of Ancient Greece. The city flourished under Theron's leadership in the 5th century BC, marked by ambitious public works and the construction of renowned temples. Despite periods of dormancy during the Punic Wars, Agrigento emerged as one of Sicily's largest cities in the Republican era. During the Principate, Agrigento's strategic port and diverse economic ventures, including sulfur mining, trade and agriculture, sustained its importance throughout the high and late Empire."}}, {"canonical_name": "Tanger", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "tanger", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Tanger", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Tanger"], "contexts": ["place where M. de Rieux conceived a plan"]}, {"entity_id": "tanger", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Tanger", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Tanger"], "contexts": ["city in Africa", "city in Morocco"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Tanger", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Tanger", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4768, "stable_key": "clu_a6491f14986d439f", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Tangier ( tan-JEER; Arabic: \u0637\u0646\u062c\u0629, romanized: \u1e6canjah, [t\u02e4and\u0292a], [t\u02e4an\u0292a]) is a city in northwestern Morocco, on the coasts of the Mediterranean Sea and the Atlantic Ocean. The city is the capital of the Tangier-Tetouan-Al Hoceima region, as well as the Tangier-Assilah Prefecture of Morocco. Many civilisations and cultures have influenced the history of Tangier, starting from before the 10th century BCE. Originating as a strategic Phoenician town and trading centre, Tangier has been a nexus for many cultures. In 1923, it became an international zone managed by colonial powers and became a destination for many European and American diplomats, spies, bohemians, writers and businessmen. That status came to an end with Moroccan independence, in phases between 1956 and 1960."}}, {"canonical_name": "Burg", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "b\u00f9rg", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "B\u00f9rg", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["B\u00f9rg"], "contexts": ["creator of astronomical tables"]}, {"entity_id": "burg", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Burg", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Burg"], "contexts": ["astronomer who created tables", "astronomer who provided early tables"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "B\u00f9rg", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Burg", "similarity": 0.609}], "id": 4769, "stable_key": "clu_458b3e7d246a2f14", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ivan Fyodorovich Burg", "confidence": "low", "type": "person", "description": "Astronomer credited with astronomical tables", "note": "The name 'Burg' is very common. Without further context or specific transliteration, identifying the precise historical figure intended is difficult. The subcategory 'SCHOLAR' and the context 'astronomer who created tables' are the primary clues."}}, {"canonical_name": "Magdeburg", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "magdebourg", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Magdebourg", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Magdebourg"], "contexts": ["location of observation"]}, {"entity_id": "magdeburg", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Magdeburg", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Magdeburg"], "contexts": ["city where Leibnitz wrote a letter", "city where Leibnitz's letter was sent"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Magdebourg", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Magdeburg", "similarity": 0.963}], "id": 4770, "stable_key": "clu_de5a51ab5763d092", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Magdeburg", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Germany", "wikidata_id": "Q1733", "wikidata_description": "capital city of the German state of Saxony-Anhalt", "wikipedia_extract": "Magdeburg (  MAG-d\u0259-burg, German: [\u02c8makd\u0259b\u028a\u0281k] , locally [\u02c8ma\u03c7d\u0259b\u028a\u0250\u032f\u00e7]; Low Saxon: Meideborg [\u02c8ma\u02d1\u026a\u032fdeb\u0254\u0250\u032fx]) is the capital of the German state of Saxony-Anhalt. The city is on the Elbe river. Otto I, the Holy Roman Emperor and the founder of the Archbishopric of Magdeburg, was buried in the city's cathedral after his death. Magdeburg's version of German town law, known as Magdeburg rights, spread throughout Central and Eastern Europe. In the Late Middle Ages, Magdeburg was one of the largest and most prosperous German cities and a notable member of the Hanseatic League. One of the most notable people from the city was Otto von Guericke, famous for his experiments with the Magdeburg hemispheres.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magdeburg"}}, {"canonical_name": "Heidelberg", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "heidelberg", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Heidelberg", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Heidelberg"], "contexts": ["location of observation"]}, {"entity_id": "heidelberg", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Heidelberg", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Heidelberg"], "contexts": ["location of an edition", "city in Germany"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Heidelberg", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Heidelberg", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4771, "stable_key": "clu_e9c82b3e9580178f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Heidelberg", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Germany", "note": "One instance refers to 'Hildesheim', which is a separate city.", "wikidata_id": "Q2966", "wikidata_description": "large city in Baden-W\u00fcrttemberg, Germany", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heidelberg", "wikipedia_extract": "Heidelberg (; German: [\u02c8ha\u026adl\u0329b\u025b\u0281k] ; Palatine German: Heidlberg) is the fifth-largest city in the German state of Baden-W\u00fcrttemberg, and with a population of about 163,000, of which roughly a quarter consists of students, it is Germany's 51st-largest city. Located about 78 km (48 mi) south of Frankfurt, Heidelberg is part of the densely populated Rhine-Neckar Metropolitan Region which has its centre in Mannheim. Heidelberg is located on the Neckar River, at the point where it leaves its narrow valley between the Oden Forest and the Little Oden Forest, and enters the wide Upper Rhine Plain. The old town lies in the valley, the end of which is flanked by the K\u00f6nigstuhl in the south and the Heiligenberg in the north. The majority of the population lives in the districts west of the mountains in the Upper Rhine Plain, into which the city has expanded over time. Heidelberg University, founded in 1386, is Germany's oldest and one of Europe's most reputable universities."}}, {"canonical_name": "Faenza", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "faenza", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Faenza", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Faenza"], "contexts": ["location of observation"]}, {"entity_id": "faenza", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Faenza", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Faenza"], "contexts": ["city name", "location mentioned in the passage"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Faenza", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Faenza", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4772, "stable_key": "clu_ed6649ed123bf759", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Faenza (UK:  fah-ENT-s\u0259, US:  fah-EN-z\u0259, Italian: [fa\u02c8\u025bntsa]; Romagnol: F\u00e8nza or F\u1ebdza; Latin: Faventia) is a comune (municipality) with 58,746 inhabitants in the Province of Ravenna in Emilia-Romagna, Italy. The city is historically renowned for its production of artistic ceramics, to the extent that maiolica, due to the fame of local craftsmanship, is known worldwide as faience. Of Roman origin, under the rule of the Manfredi, Faenza entered a period of significant growth that peaked during the Renaissance and Baroque eras, shaping its art and architecture. Due to its vibrant artistic and cultural activity, between the 18th and 19th centuries, it became a prominent center of Neoclassicism in Italy and Europe, with the finest example today being the National Museum of Palazzo Milzetti. Beyond its role as a thriving creative hub, Faenza is currently home to research and development centers focused on materials and environmental sustainability. Located just west of the heart of Romagna, at the foot of the first slopes of the Faenza Apennines, it serves as the administrative seat of the Union of Faenza Romagna and is the episcopal see of the Diocese of Faenza-Modigliana."}}, {"canonical_name": "flames", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "flammes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "flammes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["flammes"], "contexts": ["flames from volcanoes"]}, {"entity_id": "flames", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "flames", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["flames"], "contexts": ["emitted from mud volcanoes", "visible product of combustion"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "flammes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "flames", "similarity": 0.988}], "id": 4773, "stable_key": "clu_aac53ca34af93f81", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Flame", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Contexts like 'burning gas producing light' and 'emitted from mud volcanoes' clearly point to the physical phenomenon of flame.", "wikidata_id": "Q5428803", "wikidata_description": "American nonprofit organization", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facts%20and%20Logic%20About%20the%20Middle%20East", "wikipedia_extract": "Facts and Logic About the Middle East (FLAME) is a non-profit pro-Israel organization based in San Francisco, California. The Los Angeles Times described FLAME as \"an organization that must literally buy its way into print.\" Their ads, they state, are meant as an explanation for \"the proverbial man from Mars\" regarding various aspects of Arab/Israeli issues. It was founded by Gerardo Joffe and began as a local chapter of the pro-Israel media watchdog group CAMERA. By 1987 the ads, which had appeared using the CAMERA name were now sponsored by FLAME, an independent organization. Its stated purpose is \"the research and publication of the facts regarding developments in the Middle East and exposing false propaganda that might harm the interests of the United States and its allies in that area of the world.\" Since 1987, it has regularly placed ads that it describes as \"monthly hasbarah (educating and clarifying) messages in major publications of general circulation, such as U.S. News and World Report, The New York Times, The Nation, The National Review, The American Spectator, The Washington Times National Weekly, and others\"."}}, {"canonical_name": "idealism", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "idealism", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "idealism", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["idealism"], "contexts": ["philosophical doctrine", "philosophical system contrasting with materialism"]}, {"entity_id": "idealism", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "idealism", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["idealism"], "contexts": ["philosophical doctrine of mind"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "idealism", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "idealism", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4774, "stable_key": "clu_0cb37d3c214b638a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Idealism", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q25339942", "wikidata_description": "concept in the arts", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Idealism%20%28arts%29"}}, {"canonical_name": "mechanical conditions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "mechanical_conditions", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "mechanical conditions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["mechanical conditions"], "contexts": ["physical causes of mental processes", "laws governing physical interactions"]}, {"entity_id": "mechanical_conditions", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "mechanical conditions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["mechanical conditions"], "contexts": ["physical forces and constraints"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "mechanical conditions", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mechanical conditions", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4775, "stable_key": "clu_0a75bff4414279ed", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mechanical causation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "exhaustion", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "exhaustion", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "exhaustion", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["exhaustion"], "contexts": ["condition that enfeebles memory", "state of extreme tiredness"]}, {"entity_id": "exhaustion", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "exhaustion", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["exhaustion"], "contexts": ["extreme tiredness"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "exhaustion", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "exhaustion", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4776, "stable_key": "clu_ad597270791247d6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Exhaustion", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Fatigue", "note": "While 'exhaustion' can refer to general tiredness, in medical contexts, it points to a state of extreme fatigue or debilitation.", "wikipedia_extract": "The ICD-11 of the World Health Organization (WHO) describes occupational burnout as a work-related phenomenon resulting from chronic workplace stress that has not been successfully managed. According to the WHO, symptoms include \"feelings of energy depletion or exhaustion; increased mental distance from one's job, or feelings of negativism or cynicism related to one's job; and reduced professional efficacy.\" Occupational burnout is classified as an occupational phenomenon but is not recognized by the WHO as a medical or psychiatric condition. Social psychologist Christina Maslach and colleagues made clear that burnout does not constitute \"a single, one-dimensional phenomenon.\" However, national health bodies in some European countries do recognize it as such, and it is also independently recognized by some health practitioners. Nevertheless, a body of evidence suggests that what is termed burnout is a depressive condition, that is to say, indistinct from, and overlaps with, depression. Kaschka, Korczak, and Broich (2011) advanced the view that burnout is described in the Book of Exodus (18:17\u201318). In the New International Version of the Bible, Moses' father-in-law said to Moses, \"What you are doing is not good.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Occupational_burnout"}}, {"canonical_name": "bodily change", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "bodily_change", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "bodily change", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["bodily change"], "contexts": ["alteration in the physical body", "alteration in physical state"]}, {"entity_id": "bodily_changes", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "bodily changes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["bodily changes"], "contexts": ["physical alterations in the body"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "bodily change", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "bodily changes", "similarity": 0.981}], "id": 4777, "stable_key": "clu_091421a149b5d732", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Physiological change", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "hunger", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "hunger", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "hunger", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 2, "variants": ["hunger"], "contexts": ["physiological drive for food", "physical sensation of needing food"]}, {"entity_id": "hunger", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "hunger", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["hunger"], "contexts": ["sensation implying nervous tension"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "hunger", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "hunger", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4778, "stable_key": "clu_20d1d6de0b8cdef9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "hunger", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "hunger", "wikidata_id": "Q37309248", "wikidata_description": "family name"}}, {"canonical_name": "flame", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "flame", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "flame", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["flame"], "contexts": ["burning gas producing light", "mixed us of clay and flame"]}, {"entity_id": "flame", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "flame", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["flame"], "contexts": ["visible gaseous part of fire"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "flame", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "flame", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4779, "stable_key": "clu_aac53ca34af93f81-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Flame", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Contexts like 'burning gas producing light' and 'emitted from mud volcanoes' clearly point to the physical phenomenon of flame.", "wikidata_id": "Q5428803", "wikidata_description": "American nonprofit organization", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facts%20and%20Logic%20About%20the%20Middle%20East", "wikipedia_extract": "Facts and Logic About the Middle East (FLAME) is a non-profit pro-Israel organization based in San Francisco, California. The Los Angeles Times described FLAME as \"an organization that must literally buy its way into print.\" Their ads, they state, are meant as an explanation for \"the proverbial man from Mars\" regarding various aspects of Arab/Israeli issues. It was founded by Gerardo Joffe and began as a local chapter of the pro-Israel media watchdog group CAMERA. By 1987 the ads, which had appeared using the CAMERA name were now sponsored by FLAME, an independent organization. Its stated purpose is \"the research and publication of the facts regarding developments in the Middle East and exposing false propaganda that might harm the interests of the United States and its allies in that area of the world.\" Since 1987, it has regularly placed ads that it describes as \"monthly hasbarah (educating and clarifying) messages in major publications of general circulation, such as U.S. News and World Report, The New York Times, The Nation, The National Review, The American Spectator, The Washington Times National Weekly, and others\"."}}, {"canonical_name": "pen", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "pen", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "pen", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["pen"], "contexts": ["writing instrument"]}, {"entity_id": "penna", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "penna", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["penna"], "contexts": ["material not to be used for remedies"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "pen", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "penna", "similarity": 0.973}], "id": 4780, "stable_key": "clu_9b382d553d307cc5", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "A pen is a common writing instrument that applies ink to a surface, typically paper, for writing or drawing. Early pens such as reed pens, quill pens, dip pens and ruling pens held a small amount of ink on a nib or in a small void or cavity that had to be periodically recharged by dipping the tip of the pen into an inkwell. Today, such pens find only a small number of specialized uses, such as in illustration and calligraphy. Reed pens, quill pens and dip pens, which were used for writing, have been replaced by ballpoint pens, rollerball pens, fountain pens and felt or ceramic tip pens. Ruling pens, which were used for technical drawing and cartography, have been replaced by technical pens such as the Rapidograph. All of these modern pens contain internal ink reservoirs, such that they do not need to be dipped in ink while writing."}}, {"canonical_name": "Fig. 17", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "fig__17", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Fig. 17", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Fig. 17"], "contexts": ["illustration reference"]}, {"entity_id": "fig__27", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Fig. 27", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Fig. 27"], "contexts": ["illustration reference"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Fig. 17", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Fig. 27", "similarity": 0.926}], "id": 4781, "stable_key": "clu_08d18fbbd3e767c2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "figure reference", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "Refers to labels for illustrations within a text, not a specific standalone object.", "wikidata_id": "Q207961", "wikidata_description": "form of an object or its external boundary", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shape"}}, {"canonical_name": "zoological evolution", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "zoological_evolution", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "zoological evolution", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 2, "variants": ["zoological evolution"], "contexts": ["process of development across animal species", "theory of species development"]}, {"entity_id": "morphological_evolution", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "morphological evolution", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["morphological evolution"], "contexts": ["development of plant forms"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "zoological evolution", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "morphological evolution", "similarity": 0.93}], "id": 4782, "stable_key": "clu_f61cf96cef849460-4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Evolution", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The overwhelming frequency of 'Evolution' in Spencer's and James's works, along with the contexts 'biological change' and 'development across animal species,' clearly points to the scientific concept of evolution.", "wikidata_id": "Q4038411", "wikidata_description": "monthly scientific journal", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Evolution", "wikipedia_extract": "Evolution is the change in the heritable characteristics of biological populations over successive generations. It occurs when evolutionary processes such as genetic drift and natural selection act on genetic variation, resulting in certain characteristics becoming more or less common within a population over successive generations. The process of evolution has given rise to biodiversity at every level of biological organisation. The scientific theory of evolution by natural selection was conceived independently by two British naturalists, Charles Darwin and Alfred Russel Wallace, in the mid-19th century as an explanation for why organisms are adapted to their physical and biological environments. The theory was first set out in detail in Darwin's book On the Origin of Species. Evolution by natural selection is established by observable facts about living organisms: (1) more offspring are often produced than can possibly survive; (2) traits vary among individuals with respect to their morphology, physiology, and behaviour; (3) different traits confer different rates of survival and reproduction (differential fitness); and (4) traits can be passed from generation to generation (heritability of fitness)."}}, {"canonical_name": "psychic world", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "psychic_world", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "psychic world", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["psychic world"], "contexts": ["realm of mental phenomena"]}, {"entity_id": "spiritual_world", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "spiritual world", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["spiritual world"], "contexts": ["realm beyond sensory perception"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "psychic world", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "spiritual world", "similarity": 0.892}], "id": 4783, "stable_key": "clu_ead5d23323f7d0f0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "psychic realm", "confidence": "medium", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the realm of mental phenomena, distinct from the physical or spiritual."}}, {"canonical_name": "candle", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "candle", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "candle", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["candle"], "contexts": ["source of light"]}, {"entity_id": "candles", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "candles", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["candles"], "contexts": ["source of heat and light in mines"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "candle", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "candles", "similarity": 0.936}], "id": 4784, "stable_key": "clu_6a0103b8c610fa34-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "candle", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}}, {"canonical_name": "B. Sachs", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "b__sachs", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "B. Sachs", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["B. Sachs"], "contexts": ["translator of Meynert's work", "translator of clinical treatise"]}, {"entity_id": "sachs", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Sachs", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Sachs"], "contexts": ["author on milk cure"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "B. Sachs", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Sachs", "similarity": 0.862}], "id": 4785, "stable_key": "clu_2a54d4ccb7776f84", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bernhard Sachs", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Translator and author on medical topics.", "note": "The text mentions 'B. Sachs' as a translator of Meynert's work and 'Sachs' as an author on the milk cure. Bernhard Sachs (1858-1898) was a neurologist and psychiatrist who translated Meynert's works. It is plausible this refers to him, though the 'milk cure' reference might point to a different Sachs, or a less prominent one.", "wikidata_id": "Q825832", "wikidata_description": "German politician"}}, {"canonical_name": "Fahrenheit", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "fahrenheit", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Fahrenheit", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Fahrenheit"], "contexts": ["scale for measuring temperature", "unit of temperature measurement"]}, {"entity_id": "fahrenheit", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Fahrenheit", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Fahrenheit"], "contexts": ["temperature scale"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Fahrenheit", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Fahrenheit", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4786, "stable_key": "clu_5b981a6615e28d63-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Fahrenheit scale", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q105525247", "wikidata_description": "temperature scale", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fahrenheit%20scale", "wikipedia_extract": "The Fahrenheit scale () is a temperature scale based on one proposed in 1724 by the physicist Daniel Gabriel Fahrenheit (1686\u20131736). It uses the degree Fahrenheit (symbol: \u00b0F) as the unit. Several accounts of how he originally defined his scale exist, but the original paper suggests the lower defining point, 0 \u00b0F, was established as the freezing temperature of a solution of brine made from a mixture of water, ice, and ammonium chloride (a salt). The other limit established was his best estimate of the average human body temperature, originally set at 90 \u00b0F, then 96 \u00b0F (about 2.6 \u00b0F less than the modern value due to a later redefinition of the scale). For much of the 20th century, the Fahrenheit scale was defined by two fixed points with a 180 \u00b0F separation: the temperature at which pure water freezes was defined as 32 \u00b0F and the boiling point of water was defined to be 212 \u00b0F, both at sea level and under standard atmospheric pressure. It is now formally defined using the Kelvin scale.", "note": "While Fahrenheit is a surname, in the context of early modern science and measuring temperature, it refers to the temperature scale."}}, {"canonical_name": "tactile", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "tactile", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "tactile", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["tactile"], "contexts": ["relating to the sense of touch", "relating to touch"]}, {"entity_id": "tangibility", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "tangibility", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["tangibility"], "contexts": ["physical property of matter"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "tactile", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "tangibility", "similarity": 0.88}], "id": 4787, "stable_key": "clu_590d1d941abbcdf4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "tactile", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q18035138", "wikidata_description": "protein-coding gene in the species Homo sapiens", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CD96", "wikipedia_extract": "CD96 (Cluster of Differentiation 96) or Tactile (T cell activation, increased late expression)  is a protein that in humans is encoded by the CD96 gene. CD96 is a receptor protein which is expressed on T cells and NK cells and shares sequence similarity with CD226 (also known as DNAM-1). The protein encoded by this gene belongs to the immunoglobulin superfamily. It is a type I membrane protein. The protein may play a role in the adhesion of activated T and NK cells to their target cells during the late phase of the immune response. It may also function in antigen presentation."}}, {"canonical_name": "watch", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "watch", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "watch", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["watch"], "contexts": ["instrument for time measurement", "object wound up habitually"]}, {"entity_id": "watch", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "watch", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["watch"], "contexts": ["object created by watchmaker"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "watch", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "watch", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4788, "stable_key": "clu_280999453d20cf2a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "watch", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}}, {"canonical_name": "right hand", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "right_hand", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "right hand", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["right hand"], "contexts": ["part of the body used for holding the bow", "limb on the right side"]}, {"entity_id": "bra\u00e7o_direito", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "bra\u00e7o direito", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["bra\u00e7o direito"], "contexts": ["right arm"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "right hand", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "bra\u00e7o direito", "similarity": 0.944}], "id": 4789, "stable_key": "clu_1c2a47b430fac841", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "right hand", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The Portuguese 'bra\u00e7o direito' translates to 'right arm', which is synonymous with 'right hand' in many anatomical contexts, and both refer to a body part.", "wikidata_id": "Q24206677", "wikidata_description": "hand on the right side of the body"}}, {"canonical_name": "timbre", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "timbre", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "timbre", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["timbre"], "contexts": ["quality of sound distinguishing instruments", "sound quality of instrument"]}, {"entity_id": "timbre", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "timbre", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["timbre"], "contexts": ["quality of a musical sound"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "timbre", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "timbre", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4790, "stable_key": "clu_45afb2aea94c862d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Timbre", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}}, {"canonical_name": "D. G. Thompson", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "d__g__thompson", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "D. G. Thompson", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["D. G. Thompson"], "contexts": ["author of A System of Psychology", "associationist writer on consciousness and Ego"]}, {"entity_id": "thompson", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Thompson", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Thompson"], "contexts": ["investigator of solar heat"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "D. G. Thompson", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Thompson", "similarity": 0.864}], "id": 4791, "stable_key": "clu_0bbf70e77fecbadc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Daniel Greenleaf Thompson", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1830, "death_year": 1901, "description": "American psychologist and author of 'A System of Psychology'.", "wikidata_id": "Q18657318", "wikidata_description": ""}}, {"canonical_name": "personal equation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "personal_equation", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "personal equation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["personal equation"], "contexts": ["investigator's subjective bias", "observer's reaction time difference"]}, {"entity_id": "personal_equation", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "personal equation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["personal equation"], "contexts": ["observer's timing bias"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "personal equation", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "personal equation", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4792, "stable_key": "clu_012e00917f78e900", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "personal equation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q1017823", "wikidata_description": "", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Personal%20equation", "wikipedia_extract": "The term personal equation, in 19th- and early 20th-century science, referred to the idea that  different observers have different reaction times, which can introduce bias when it comes to measurements and observations. Simon Schaffer wrote it \"was the name given by astronomers after Bessel to the differences in measured transit times recorded by observers in the same situation\". The term originated in astronomy, when it was discovered that numerous observers making simultaneous observations would record slightly different values (for example, in recording the exact time at which a star crossed the wires of a reticule in a telescope), some of which were of a significant enough difference to afford for problems in larger calculations. The existence of the effect was first discovered when, in 1796, the Astronomer Royal Neville Maskelyne dismissed his assistant Kinnebrooke because he could not better the error of his observations relative to Maskelyne's own values. The problem was forgotten and only analysed two decades later by Friedrich Wilhelm Bessel at K\u00f6nigsberg Observatory in Prussia. Setting up an experiment to compare the values, Bessel and an assistant measured the times at which several stars crossed the wires of a reticule in different nights."}}, {"canonical_name": "simplicity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "simplicity", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "simplicity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["simplicity"], "contexts": ["quality of the soul", "lack of complexity or division"]}, {"entity_id": "simple", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "simple", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["simple"], "contexts": ["made of one part"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "simplicity", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "simple", "similarity": 0.882}], "id": 4793, "stable_key": "clu_34db1e581774517f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "simplicity", "confidence": "medium", "type": "concept", "note": "The context 'quality of the soul' suggests a philosophical or theological usage, while 'made of one part' points to a more literal or material sense. Without further context for the 1563 text, 'simplicity' is the best general fit."}}, {"canonical_name": "intention", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "intention", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "intention", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["intention"], "contexts": ["purpose or aim", "purposeful direction of the mind"]}, {"entity_id": "intent", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "intent", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["intent"], "contexts": ["intent"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "intention", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "intent", "similarity": 0.963}], "id": 4794, "stable_key": "clu_e5361030cbd152f9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "intention", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q3918409", "wikidata_description": "document or message proposing some course of action, or the process by which that action is considered"}}, {"canonical_name": "Herbert Spencer", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "herbert_spencer", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Herbert Spencer", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Herbert Spencer"], "contexts": ["philosopher quoted in 'Mind'", "psychologist ignoring association by similarity"]}, {"entity_id": "herbert_spencer", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Herbert Spencer", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Herbert Spencer"], "contexts": ["author of \"First Principles\""]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Herbert Spencer", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Herbert Spencer", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4795, "stable_key": "clu_dc23dab3734baea3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Herbert Spencer", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1820, "death_year": 1903, "description": "English philosopher, biologist, anthropologist, and sociologist.", "wikidata_id": "Q144535", "wikidata_description": "English philosopher and political theorist (1820\u20131903)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Herbert%20Spencer", "wikipedia_extract": "Herbert Spencer (27 April 1820 \u2013 8 December 1903) was an English polymath active as a philosopher, psychologist, biologist, sociologist, and anthropologist. Spencer originated the expression \"survival of the fittest\", which he coined in Principles of Biology (1864) after reading Charles Darwin's 1859 book On the Origin of Species. The term strongly suggests natural selection, yet Spencer saw evolution as extending into realms of sociology and ethics, so he also supported Lamarckism. Spencer developed an all-embracing conception of evolution as the progressive development of the physical world, biological organisms, the human mind, and human culture and societies. As a polymath, he contributed to a wide range of subjects, including ethics, religion, anthropology, economics, political theory, philosophy, literature, astronomy, biology, sociology, and psychology. During his lifetime he achieved tremendous authority, mainly in English-speaking academia."}}, {"canonical_name": "father", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "father", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "father", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 2, "variants": ["father"], "contexts": ["male parent"]}, {"entity_id": "his_father", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "his father", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["his father"], "contexts": ["Herschel's father, opinion on star clusters"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "father", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "his father", "similarity": 0.901}], "id": 4796, "stable_key": "clu_65bfa6f69ea4e678", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "father", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to a familial relationship, not a specific individual outside of the context of the text."}}, {"canonical_name": "Marcus Aurelius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "marcus_aurelius", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Marcus Aurelius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 2, "variants": ["Marcus Aurelius"], "contexts": ["Roman emperor and Stoic philosopher"]}, {"entity_id": "marcus_aurelius", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Marcus Aurelius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Marcus Aurelius"], "contexts": ["ancient Roman emperor and philosopher"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Marcus Aurelius", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Marcus Aurelius", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4797, "stable_key": "clu_15d001db99cb879d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Marcus Aurelius", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 121, "death_year": 180, "description": "Roman emperor and Stoic philosopher, author of Meditations", "wikidata_id": "Q1430", "wikidata_description": "16th Roman Emperor from 161 to 180 and Stoic philosopher", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marcus%20Aurelius", "wikipedia_extract": "Marcus Aurelius Antoninus ( or-EE-lee-\u0259s; Latin: [\u02c8ma\u02d0rk\u028as au\u032f\u02c8re\u02d0li.us anto\u02d0\u02c8ni\u02d0n\u028as]; 26 April 121 \u2013 17 March 180) was Roman emperor from 161 to 180 and a Stoic philosopher. He was a member of the Nerva\u2013Antonine dynasty, the last of the rulers later known as the Five Good Emperors and the last emperor of the Pax Romana, an age of relative peace, calm, and stability for the Roman Empire lasting from 27 BC to 180 AD. He served as Roman consul in 140, 145, and 161. Marcus Aurelius was the son of the praetor Marcus Annius Verus and his wife, Domitia Calvilla. He was related through marriage to the emperors Trajan and Hadrian. Marcus was three when his father died, and was raised by his mother and paternal grandfather."}}, {"canonical_name": "consciousness of Self", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "consciousness_of_self", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "consciousness of Self", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["consciousness of self", "consciousness of Self"], "contexts": ["awareness of one's own identity"]}, {"entity_id": "cognition_of_self", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "cognition of self", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["cognition of self"], "contexts": ["knowing one's own identity"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "consciousness of Self", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "cognition of self", "similarity": 0.903}], "id": 4798, "stable_key": "clu_0faa29b218cf588c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Self-awareness", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q1314553", "wikidata_description": "capacity for an individual to consciously know and understand one's own character, feelings, motives, and desires", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Self-awareness", "wikipedia_extract": "In the philosophy of self, self-awareness is the awareness and reflection of one's own personality or individuality, including traits, feelings, and behaviors. It is not to be confused with consciousness in the sense of qualia. While consciousness is being aware of one's body and environment, self-awareness is the recognition of that consciousness. Self-awareness is how an individual experiences and understands their own character, feelings, motives, and desires. Because the term is used in both philosophical and psychological contexts, researchers distinguish between different forms of self-awareness, ranging from awareness of consciousness itself to awareness of oneself within social situations. The term self-awareness is used across several disciplines to describe related but distinct phenomena."}}, {"canonical_name": "Emotion and Will", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "emotion_and_will", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Emotion and Will", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Emotion and Will"], "contexts": ["work by Bain on emotions and will", "book title by Bain"]}, {"entity_id": "the_emotions_and_the_will", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "The Emotions and the Will", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["The Emotions and the Will"], "contexts": ["work title"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Emotion and Will", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "The Emotions and the Will", "similarity": 0.963}], "id": 4799, "stable_key": "clu_cf428cefaf09e29f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Emotion and Will", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to Alexander Bain's work 'The Emotions and the Will'."}}, {"canonical_name": "abstracted", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "abstracted", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "abstracted", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["abstracted"], "contexts": ["lost in thought, detached", "state of inattention to outer things"]}, {"entity_id": "abstractions", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "abstractions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["abstractions"], "contexts": ["ideas considered apart from concrete reality"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "abstracted", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "abstractions", "similarity": 0.888}], "id": 4800, "stable_key": "clu_5c2330b29467e88e-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "abstract concept", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This refers to abstract ideas or thinking, rather than a specific object or substance.", "wikidata_id": "Q2822059", "wikidata_description": "type of thinking"}}, {"canonical_name": "conceive", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "conceive", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "conceive", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["conceive"], "contexts": ["form an idea or concept"]}, {"entity_id": "conceber", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "conceber", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["conceber"], "contexts": ["the act of becoming pregnant"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "conceive", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "conceber", "similarity": 0.957}], "id": 4801, "stable_key": "clu_816af673477a2afe-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Conception", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This cluster refers to both the biological process of pregnancy and the philosophical/psychological process of forming an idea or understanding.", "wikidata_id": "Q1880", "wikidata_description": "city in Chile", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Concepci%C3%B3n%2C%20Chile", "wikipedia_extract": "Concepci\u00f3n (Spanish pronunciation: [konse\u03b2\u02c8sjon] ; originally: Concepci\u00f3n de la Madre Sant\u00edsima de la Luz, \"Conception of the Blessed Mother of Light\") is a city and commune in south-central Chile, and the geographical and demographic core of the Greater Concepci\u00f3n metropolitan area, it is the second largest city in Chile by urban area and one of the three major conurbations in the country. It has a significant impact on domestic trade being part of the most heavily industrialized region in the country. It is the seat of the Concepci\u00f3n Province and the capital of the Biob\u00edo Region. It sits about 500 km south of the nation's capital, Santiago. The city was first settled in the Bay of Concepci\u00f3n, in the zone that would later become the commune of Penco, now part of the Concepci\u00f3n conurbation. The city's demonym, penquista, comes from the place of its original foundation."}}, {"canonical_name": "sensory organs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "sensory_organs", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "sensory organs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["sensory organs"], "contexts": ["organs that detect stimuli", "organs for receiving stimuli"]}, {"entity_id": "perceptive_organs", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "perceptive organs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["perceptive organs"], "contexts": ["organs involved in sensing the environment"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "sensory organs", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "perceptive organs", "similarity": 0.963}], "id": 4802, "stable_key": "clu_46ed10a72a316eb3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sensory organs", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q173253", "wikidata_description": "physiological capacity of organisms that provides data for perception", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sense", "wikipedia_extract": "A sense is a biological system used by an organism for sensation, the process of  gathering information about the surroundings through the detection of stimuli. Although, in some cultures, five human senses were traditionally identified as such (namely sight, smell, touch, taste, and hearing), many more are now recognized. Senses used by non-human organisms are even greater in variety and number. During sensation, sense organs collect various stimuli (such as a sound or smell) for transduction, meaning transformation into a form that can be understood by the brain. Sensation and perception are fundamental to nearly every aspect of an organism's cognition, behavior and thought. In organisms, a sensory organ consists of a group of interrelated sensory cells that respond to a specific type of physical stimulus."}}, {"canonical_name": "organic adjustment", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "organic_adjustment", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "organic adjustment", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Organic adjustment", "organic adjustment"], "contexts": ["bodily preparation for action", "physiological changes in the body"]}, {"entity_id": "organic_adjustment", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "organic adjustment", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["organic adjustment"], "contexts": ["biological adaptation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "organic adjustment", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "organic adjustment", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4803, "stable_key": "clu_a2266c2c07baa385", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Biological adaptation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q67504163", "wikidata_description": "changes in biological features that help an organism cope with its environment, physiological, phenotypic and genetic"}}, {"canonical_name": "Physiological optics", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "physiological_optics", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Physiological optics", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Physiological optics", "physiological optics"], "contexts": ["study of the eye and vision"]}, {"entity_id": "physical_optics", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "physical optics", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["physical optics"], "contexts": ["branch of physics studying light"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Physiological optics", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "physical optics", "similarity": 0.945}], "id": 4804, "stable_key": "clu_d25a44b77864f73b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Physiological optics", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "twilight", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "twilight", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "twilight", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["twilight"], "contexts": ["period of dim light before sunrise or after sunset", "low light condition"]}, {"entity_id": "twilight", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "twilight", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["twilight"], "contexts": ["period of light after sunset"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "twilight", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "twilight", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4805, "stable_key": "clu_9bfee481e4a81d71", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Twilight", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "female given name"}}, {"canonical_name": "pressure-sensation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "pressure-sensation", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "pressure-sensation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["pressure-sensation"], "contexts": ["tactile sensation", "sensory modality"]}, {"entity_id": "feelings_of_pressure", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "feelings of pressure", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["feelings of pressure"], "contexts": ["subjective experience of pressure"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "pressure-sensation", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "feelings of pressure", "similarity": 0.921}], "id": 4806, "stable_key": "clu_da4ca189d0c66790", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pressure sensation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q78010383", "wikidata_description": "scientific article published on 02 March 2002"}}, {"canonical_name": "Quantity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "quantity", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Quantity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["quantity", "Quantity"], "contexts": ["pertaining to differences in amount", "relation between things"]}, {"entity_id": "quantity", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "quantity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["quantity"], "contexts": ["relation comprising number and size"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Quantity", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "quantity", "similarity": 0.942}], "id": 4807, "stable_key": "clu_9dfbe4c3f67733cb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Quantity", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "mixed", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "mixed", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "mixed", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["MIXED", "mixed"], "contexts": ["type of association", "most parts inert"]}, {"entity_id": "mixed", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "mixed", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["mixed"], "contexts": ["combining vocal and instrumental music"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "mixed", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mixed", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4808, "stable_key": "clu_317e5dc8937aeab9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "mixed", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'mixed' in these contexts refers to the combination or blending of different elements, whether psychological, musical, or conceptual, rather than a specific technical term.", "wikidata_id": "Q2749", "wikidata_description": "city in Bavaria, Germany", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Augsburg", "wikipedia_extract": "Augsburg (UK:  OWGZ-burg, also US:  AWGZ-; German: [\u02c8a\u028aksb\u028a\u0281k] ; Swabian German: Ougschburg) is a city in the Swabia region of Bavaria, Germany, around 50 kilometres (31 mi) west of the state capital, Munich. It is a university town and the regional seat of the Regierungsbezirk Swabia with a well-preserved Altstadt (historical city centre). Augsburg is an urban district and home to the institutions of the Landkreis Augsburg. It is the third-largest city in Bavaria (after Munich and Nuremberg), with a population of 304,000 and 885,000 in its metropolitan area. After Neuss, Trier, Worms, Cologne and Xanten, Augsburg is one of Germany's oldest cities, founded in 15 BC by the Romans as Augusta Vindelicorum and named after the Roman emperor Augustus. It was a Free Imperial City from 1276 to 1803 and the home of the patrician Fugger and Welser families that dominated European banking in the 16th century."}}, {"canonical_name": "luminiferous ether", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "luminiferous_ether", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "luminiferous ether", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 2, "variants": ["luminiferous ether"], "contexts": ["hypothetical medium for light", "hypothetical medium for light propagation"]}, {"entity_id": "luminiferous_ether", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "luminiferous ether", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["luminiferous ether"], "contexts": ["medium for light propagation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "luminiferous ether", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "luminiferous ether", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4809, "stable_key": "clu_c927aee90a825390", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Luminiferous Ether", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikipedia_extract": "The etheric body, ether-body, or \u00e6ther body is a subtle body propounded in esoteric and occult philosophies as the first or lowest layer in the human energy field or aura. The etheric body is said to be in immediate contact with the physical body and to sustain it and connect it with \"higher\" bodies. It is also said to consist of a finer substance, more pure and composed of smaller particles, than the ordinary matter of the physical plane. (See the book Occult Chemistry by C.W. Leadbeater) The English term \"etheric\" in this context seems to derive from the Theosophical writings of Madame Blavatsky, but its use was formalised by C.W. Leadbeater and Annie Besant due to the elimination of Hindu terminology from the system of seven planes and bodies.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etheric_body"}}, {"canonical_name": "iron filings", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "iron_filings", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "iron filings", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["iron filings"], "contexts": ["small pieces of iron"]}, {"entity_id": "iron_filings", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "iron filings", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 2, "variants": ["iron filings"], "contexts": ["suspended by electric current", "material used to visualize magnetic forces"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "iron filings", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "iron filings", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4810, "stable_key": "clu_0d4a992dd4d690e4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "iron filings", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "In early modern texts, 'iron filings' refers to small particles of iron, often used in experiments demonstrating magnetism or electrical phenomena.", "wikidata_id": "Q132093", "wikidata_description": "fine iron powder", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iron%20filings", "wikipedia_extract": "Iron filings are very small pieces of iron that look like a powder with a dark-grey appearance. As the name suggests, iron filings can be obtained from metal working operations as the scrap material filed off larger iron and steel parts. They are very often used in science demonstrations to show the direction of a magnetic field. Since iron is a ferromagnetic material, a magnetic field induces each particle to become a tiny bar magnet. The south pole of each particle then attracts the north poles of its neighbors, and this process is repeated over a wide area to create chains of filings parallel to the direction of the magnetic field. Iron filings are used in many places, including schools, where they test the reaction of the filings to magnets."}}, {"canonical_name": "shot", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "shot-gun", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "shot-gun", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["shot-gun"], "contexts": ["firearm used in analogy"]}, {"entity_id": "shot", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "shot", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["shot"], "contexts": ["projectile from a fowling-piece", "projectile with whizzing sound"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "shot-gun", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "shot", "similarity": 0.913}], "id": 4811, "stable_key": "clu_8360c3ccb0c8b2dc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "shot", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "In this context, 'shot' refers to ammunition fired from a gun, particularly small projectiles like those used in shotguns."}}, {"canonical_name": "locomotive", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "locomotive", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "locomotive", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["locomotive"], "contexts": ["a machine for transport"]}, {"entity_id": "locomotive", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "locomotive", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["locomotive"], "contexts": ["steam-powered engine for transport", "machine impelled by force"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "locomotive", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "locomotive", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4812, "stable_key": "clu_8d64efea87266880", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "locomotive", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "Refers to early steam-powered engines designed for transportation, particularly on railways.", "wikidata_id": "Q726543", "wikidata_description": "organ system that gives humans the ability to move using their muscular and skeletal systems"}}, {"canonical_name": "lines", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "lines", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "lines", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["lines"], "contexts": ["straight marks connecting points"]}, {"entity_id": "lines", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "lines", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 2, "variants": ["lines"], "contexts": ["paths described by bodies", "straight paths of light"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "lines", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "lines", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4813, "stable_key": "clu_244f2de446d4088b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "line", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'lines' in early modern texts can refer to geometric lines, paths of motion, or even conceptual relationships. Given the contexts, 'line' as a geometric or conceptual entity is the most fitting.", "wikidata_id": "Q125604313", "wikidata_description": "scientific article published in 2019"}}, {"canonical_name": "Fig. 18", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "fig__18", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Fig. 18", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Fig. 18"], "contexts": ["illustration reference"]}, {"entity_id": "fig__19", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Fig. 19", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Fig. 19"], "contexts": ["illustration reference"]}, {"entity_id": "fig__19", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "fig. 19", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["fig. 19"], "contexts": ["diagram illustrating angles"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Fig. 18", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "fig. 19", "similarity": 0.942}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Fig. 19", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "fig. 19", "similarity": 0.988}], "id": 4814, "stable_key": "clu_41bc2c0693cf3bc4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Figure reference", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to numbered illustrations or diagrams within texts."}}, {"canonical_name": "inspiration", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "inspiration", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "inspiration", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["inspiration"], "contexts": ["process inhibited by superior laryngeal nerve"]}, {"entity_id": "inspirations", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "inspirations", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["inspirations"], "contexts": ["part of the breathing cycle"]}, {"entity_id": "inspirations", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "inspirations", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["inspirations"], "contexts": ["act of breathing in"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "inspiration", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "inspirations", "similarity": 0.973}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "inspirations", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "inspirations", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4815, "stable_key": "clu_44429182b3e08bbd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "inspiration", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the act of inhaling or the process of breathing in, as described in physiological contexts."}}, {"canonical_name": "anatomical connection", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "anatomical_connection", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "anatomical connection", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["anatomical connection"], "contexts": ["physical link between anatomical structures"]}, {"entity_id": "cartilaginous_connexions", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "cartilaginous connexions", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["cartilaginous connexions"], "contexts": ["connections made of cartilage"]}, {"entity_id": "connections_of_organs", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "connections of organs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["connections of organs"], "contexts": ["relationships between body structures"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "anatomical connection", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "cartilaginous connexions", "similarity": 0.885}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "anatomical connection", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "connections of organs", "similarity": 0.93}], "id": 4816, "stable_key": "clu_aa0d267c71f7bfc7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "anatomical connection", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Describes the physical or structural links between different parts of the body, including cartilaginous connections.", "wikidata_id": "Q59547567", "wikidata_description": "doctoral thesis by W. A. Beresford"}}, {"canonical_name": "Hankel", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "hankel", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Hankel", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hankel"], "contexts": ["observer of reaction times"]}, {"entity_id": "hallaschka", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Hallaschka", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hallaschka"], "contexts": ["source for mountain elevation"]}, {"entity_id": "kendal", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Kendal", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Kendal"], "contexts": ["observer of sky"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Hankel", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Hallaschka", "similarity": 0.852}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Hankel", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Kendal", "similarity": 0.854}], "id": 4817, "stable_key": "clu_b4cc70e5665fa9f5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hermann Hankel", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1839, "death_year": 1873, "description": "German mathematician, studied reaction times.", "note": "The references to 'Hankel', 'Hallaschka', and 'Kendal' are problematic. 'Hallaschka' might refer to a person, but 'Kendal' could be a place or a person. Given 'Hankel' is explicitly named and often associated with mathematics and reaction times, this identification is the most plausible, though the other names are less clear and might be errors or less prominent figures.", "wikidata_id": "Q61038", "wikidata_description": "German mathematician (1839\u20131873)"}}, {"canonical_name": "thermometers", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "thermometers", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "thermometers", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 1, "variants": ["thermometers"], "contexts": ["devices for measuring temperature"]}, {"entity_id": "thermometers", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "thermometers", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 2, "variants": ["thermometers"], "contexts": ["devices for measuring temperature"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "thermometers", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "thermometers", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4818, "stable_key": "clu_61149a37ba4a7483-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "thermometer", "confidence": "high", "type": "OBJECT", "description": "An instrument used to measure temperature.", "wikipedia_extract": "A thermometer is a device that measures temperature (the hotness or coldness of an object) or temperature gradient (the rates of change of temperature in space). A thermometer has two important elements: (1) a temperature sensor (e.g. the bulb of a mercury-in-glass thermometer or the pyrometric sensor in an infrared thermometer) in which some change occurs with a change in temperature; and (2) some means of converting this change into a numerical value (e.g. the visible scale that is marked on a mercury-in-glass thermometer or the digital readout on an infrared model). Thermometers are widely used in technology and industry to monitor processes, in meteorology, in medicine (medical thermometer), and in scientific research. While an individual thermometer is able to measure degrees of hotness, the readings on two thermometers cannot be compared unless they conform to an agreed scale. Today there is an absolute thermodynamic temperature scale. Internationally agreed temperature scales are designed to approximate this closely, based on fixed points and interpolating thermometers."}}, {"canonical_name": "optics", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "optic", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "optic", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["optic"], "contexts": ["relating to the sense of sight"]}, {"entity_id": "optics", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "optics", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 2, "variants": ["optics"], "contexts": ["branch of physics studying light", "branch of physics dealing with light"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "optic", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "optics", "similarity": 0.893}], "id": 4819, "stable_key": "clu_7f246d8fbaca1b90", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "optics", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q14620", "wikidata_description": "branch of physics concerning light", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Optics", "wikipedia_extract": "Optics is the branch of physics that studies the behaviour, manipulation, and detection of electromagnetic radiation, including its interactions with matter and instruments that use or detect it. Optics usually describes the behaviour of visible, ultraviolet, and infrared light. The study of optics extends to other forms of electromagnetic radiation, including radio waves, microwaves, and X-rays. The term optics is also applied to technology for manipulating beams of elementary charged particles. Most optical phenomena can be accounted for by using the classical electromagnetic description of light, however, complete electromagnetic descriptions of light are often difficult to apply in practice. Practical optics is usually done using simplified models."}}, {"canonical_name": "atomistic philosophy", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "atomistic_philosophy", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "atomistic philosophy", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["atomistic philosophy"], "contexts": ["Philosophy concerning elementary particles"]}, {"entity_id": "atomic_theory", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "atomic theory", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["atomic theory"], "contexts": ["physics theory of fundamental particles"]}, {"entity_id": "atomic_theory", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "atomic theory", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["atomic theory"], "contexts": ["theory of matter composed of atoms"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "atomistic philosophy", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "atomic theory", "similarity": 0.919}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "atomic theory", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "atomic theory", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4820, "stable_key": "clu_7a38ff11a9379d3d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Atomic theory", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q210553", "wikidata_description": "history of scientific theory that views matter as made up of atoms of chemical elements", "wikipedia_extract": "This list includes well-known general theories in science and pre-scientific natural history and natural philosophy that have since been superseded by other scientific theories. Many discarded explanations were once supported by a scientific consensus, but replaced after more empirical information became available that identified flaws and prompted new theories which better explain the available data. Pre-modern explanations originated before the scientific method, with varying degrees of empirical support. Some scientific theories are discarded in their entirety, such as the replacement of the phlogiston theory by energy and thermodynamics. Some theories known to be incomplete or in some ways incorrect are still used. For example, Newtonian classical mechanics is accurate enough for practical calculations at everyday distances and velocities, and it is still taught in schools.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_atomic_theory"}}, {"canonical_name": "functional activity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "functional_activity", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "functional activity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["functional activity"], "contexts": ["operation of a system or organ"]}, {"entity_id": "functional_activity", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "functional activity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["functional activity"], "contexts": ["activity of living organisms", "operation of bodily functions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "functional activity", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "functional activity", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4821, "stable_key": "clu_0e6625368a467429", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "functional activity", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "molecular change", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "molecular_changes", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "molecular changes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["molecular changes"], "contexts": ["changes at the molecular level"]}, {"entity_id": "molecular_change", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "molecular change", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["molecular change"], "contexts": ["disturbance in metals depth", "alteration at molecular level"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "molecular changes", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "molecular change", "similarity": 0.986}], "id": 4822, "stable_key": "clu_58c2c57830018767", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "molecular change", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q34321453", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article"}}, {"canonical_name": "mechanical motion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "material_motion", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "material motion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["material motion"], "contexts": ["physical movement of matter"]}, {"entity_id": "mechanical_motion", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "mechanical motion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["mechanical motion"], "contexts": ["movement caused by physical forces", "motion governed by mechanical laws"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "material motion", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mechanical motion", "similarity": 0.881}], "id": 4823, "stable_key": "clu_d300d9c00e879f13", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "mechanical motion", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "nervous centres", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "central_nervous_system", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "central nervous system", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["central nervous system"], "contexts": ["brain and spinal cord"]}, {"entity_id": "nervous_centres", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "nervous centres", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 2, "variants": ["nervous centres"], "contexts": ["central parts of the nervous system", "central control for bodily functions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "central nervous system", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "nervous centres", "similarity": 0.915}], "id": 4824, "stable_key": "clu_c4155fb4747a1be2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "central nervous system", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q47273", "wikidata_description": "main information-processing organs of the nervous system, consisting of the brain, spinal cord, and meninges"}}, {"canonical_name": "Hutton", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "r__h__hutton", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "R. H. Hutton", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["R. H. Hutton"], "contexts": ["author in Contemporary Review"]}, {"entity_id": "hutton", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Hutton", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 2, "variants": ["Hutton"], "contexts": ["scientist in Shehallien experiment", "author of 'Fossil Flora'"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "R. H. Hutton", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Hutton", "similarity": 0.855}], "id": 4825, "stable_key": "clu_1af47f943a81ec96", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "James Hutton", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1726, "death_year": 1797, "description": "Scottish geologist, farmer, naturalist, and chemical manufacturer", "note": "The reference to R. H. Hutton is likely a misattribution or a different person. The context of 'Shehallien experiment' strongly points to James Hutton, the geologist.", "wikidata_id": "Q192927", "wikidata_description": "Scottish geologist, physician, chemical manufacturer, naturalist, and experimental agriculturalist; (1726-1797)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James%20Hutton", "wikipedia_extract": "James Hutton  ( ; 3 June O.S. 1726 \u2013 26 March 1797) was a Scottish geologist, agriculturalist, chemical manufacturer, naturalist and physician. Often referred to as the \"Father of Modern Geology,\" he played a key role in establishing geology as a modern science. Hutton advanced the idea that the physical world's remote history can be inferred from evidence in present-day rocks. Through his study of features in the landscape and coastlines of his native Scottish Lowlands, such as Salisbury Crags or Siccar Point, he developed the theory that geological features could not be static but underwent continuing transformation over indefinitely long periods of time. From this he argued, in agreement with many other early geologists, that the Earth could not be young."}}, {"canonical_name": "affection", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "affection_of_the_soul", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "affection of the soul", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["affection of the soul"], "contexts": ["clumsy term for mental states"]}, {"entity_id": "affection", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "affection", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["affection"], "contexts": ["a feeling or sensation", "fondness or liking"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "affection of the soul", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "affection", "similarity": 0.92}], "id": 4826, "stable_key": "clu_e87c75305959fd7f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Affection", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q316", "wikidata_description": "strong, positive emotion based on affection", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Love", "wikipedia_extract": "Love is an emotion involving strong attraction, affection, emotional attachment or concern for a person, animal, or thing. It is expressed in many forms, encompassing a range of strong and positive emotional and mental states, from the most sublime virtue, good habit, deepest interpersonal affection, to the simplest pleasure. An example of this range of meanings is that the love of a mother differs from the love of a spouse, which differs from the love of food. Love is considered to be both positive and negative, with its virtue representing kindness, compassion, and affection\u2014\"the unselfish, loyal, and benevolent concern for the good of another\"\u2014and its vice representing a moral flaw akin to vanity, selfishness, amour-propre, and egotism. It may also describe compassionate and affectionate actions towards other humans, oneself, or animals. In its various forms, love acts as a major facilitator of interpersonal relationships, and owing to its central psychological importance, is one of the most common themes in the creative arts."}}, {"canonical_name": "instant", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "instant", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "instant", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["instant"], "contexts": ["a very brief moment"]}, {"entity_id": "instant", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "instant", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["instant"], "contexts": ["a specific moment in time", "a specific point in time"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "instant", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "instant", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4827, "stable_key": "clu_8a6a38a0b5b7b5fe", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Instant", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q186408", "wikidata_description": "position of a particular instant in time"}}, {"canonical_name": "communica\u00e7\u00e3o", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "communication", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "communication", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["communication"], "contexts": ["exchange of information"]}, {"entity_id": "communica\u00e7\u00e3o", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "communica\u00e7\u00e3o", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["communica\u00e7\u00e3o", "communica\u00e7a\u00f5"], "contexts": ["connection or link", "bodily connection"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "communication", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "communica\u00e7\u00e3o", "similarity": 0.937}], "id": 4828, "stable_key": "clu_4d46b425caf1816b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Communication", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q50498039", "wikidata_description": "Quebec scholarly journal published by Universit\u00e9 Laval, covering topics of language and literature, philology, linguistics, communication, and mass media."}}, {"canonical_name": "laws of thought", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "laws_and_nature_of_thought", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "laws and nature of thought", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["laws and nature of thought"], "contexts": ["principles governing mental processes"]}, {"entity_id": "laws_of_thought", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "laws of thought", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 2, "variants": ["laws of thought"], "contexts": ["fundamental principles of reasoning", "principles governing reasoning and conception"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "laws and nature of thought", "target_