 "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q5043", "wikidata_description": "Abrahamic monotheistic religion founded on the teachings, person and life of Jesus", "note": "Refers to someone adhering to Christianity."}}, {"canonical_name": "ideal world", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "ideal_world", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "ideal world", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["ideal world"], "contexts": ["realm of perfect or spiritual existence"]}, {"entity_id": "ideal_world", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "ideal world", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["ideal world"], "contexts": ["realm of abstract thought", "realm of abstract ideas and thoughts"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "ideal world", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "ideal world", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4835, "stable_key": "clu_97f69d585b05faf5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ideal world", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to a conceptual realm of perfection or abstract thought.", "wikidata_id": "Q5987977", "wikidata_description": "British free-to-view shopping channel"}}, {"canonical_name": "Religious", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "religious", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "religious", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["religious"], "contexts": ["relating to or believing in religion"]}, {"entity_id": "religious", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Religious", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["religious", "Religious"], "contexts": ["relating to religion", "type of society with roughly sketched ends"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "religious", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Religious", "similarity": 0.968}], "id": 4836, "stable_key": "clu_8562eaaa4b081719-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Religion", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q19829980", "wikidata_description": "scholar of religious studies"}}, {"canonical_name": "free-will", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "free-will", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "free-will", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["free-will"], "contexts": ["ability to choose actions"]}, {"entity_id": "free_will", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "free will", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["free will"], "contexts": ["ability to make choices"]}, {"entity_id": "freewill", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "freewill", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["freewill"], "contexts": ["ability to choose actions independently"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "free-will", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "freewill", "similarity": 0.983}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "free will", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "freewill", "similarity": 0.954}], "id": 4837, "stable_key": "clu_7b90a683276327b0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Free will", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q9476", "wikidata_description": "ability of agents to be the ultimate source or originator of their choices, free from external determination", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free%20will", "wikipedia_extract": "Free will is generally understood as the capacity or ability of people to (a) choose between different possible courses of action, (b) exercise control over their actions in a way that is necessary for moral responsibility, or (c) be the ultimate source or originator of their actions. There are different theories as to its nature, and these aspects are often emphasized differently depending on philosophical tradition, with debates focusing on whether and how such freedom can coexist with physical determinism, divine foreknowledge, and other constraints. Free will is closely linked to the concepts of moral responsibility and moral desert, praise, culpability, and other judgements that can logically apply only to actions that are freely chosen. It is also connected with the concepts of advice, persuasion, deliberation, and prohibition. Traditionally, only actions that are freely willed are seen as deserving credit or blame. Whether free will exists and the implications of whether it exists or not constitute some of the longest-running debates of philosophy."}}, {"canonical_name": "mental act", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "mental_act", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "mental act", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["mental act"], "contexts": ["operation of the mind"]}, {"entity_id": "mental_act", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "mental act", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["mental act"], "contexts": ["a process of the mind", "unit of mental operation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "mental act", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "mental act", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4838, "stable_key": "clu_b0cfa054052d96b3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mental act", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "Natural Sciences", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "natural_sciences", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "natural sciences", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["natural sciences"], "contexts": ["disciplines studying nature"]}, {"entity_id": "natural_sciences", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Natural Sciences", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Natural Sciences", "natural sciences"], "contexts": ["field of study", "branches of scientific knowledge"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "natural sciences", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Natural Sciences", "similarity": 0.942}], "id": 4839, "stable_key": "clu_9b04c128bed6bcc9-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Natural science", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q7991", "wikidata_description": "branch of science about the natural world"}}, {"canonical_name": "adjectives", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "adjectives", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "adjectives", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["adjectives"], "contexts": ["words describing nouns"]}, {"entity_id": "adjectives", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "adjectives", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["adjectives"], "contexts": ["words describing nouns"]}, {"entity_id": "adjective", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "adjective", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["adjective"], "contexts": ["modifies nouns"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "adjectives", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "adjectives", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "adjectives", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "adjective", "similarity": 0.935}], "id": 4840, "stable_key": "clu_601992c68d6de3c3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Adjective", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q34698", "wikidata_description": "part of speech that describes a noun or pronoun", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adjective", "wikipedia_extract": "An adjective (abbreviated ADJ) is a word that describes or defines a noun or noun phrase. Its semantic role is to change information given by the noun. The adjective is considered one of the main parts of speech of the English language, although historically they were classed together with nouns. Nowadays, certain words that usually had been classified as adjectives, including the, this, my, etc., typically are classed separately, as determiners. That used to be an immensely funny idea. (Prepositive attributive) Tell me something funny."}}, {"canonical_name": "focus", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "focus", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "focus", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["focus"], "contexts": ["bringing an image into sharp view"]}, {"entity_id": "focus", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "focus", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["focus"], "contexts": ["point of convergence for waves", "point where rays converge"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "focus", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "focus", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4841, "stable_key": "clu_617acca2db715bcc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Focus", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q27723897", "wikidata_description": "scientific journal"}}, {"canonical_name": "inconceivable", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "inconceivable", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "inconceivable", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["inconceivable"], "contexts": ["not able to be imagined"]}, {"entity_id": "inconceivable", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "inconceivable", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 2, "variants": ["inconceivable", "Inconceivable"], "contexts": ["absence of conceivability", "incapable of being thought"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "inconceivable", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "inconceivable", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 4842, "stable_key": "clu_e2b0c5a3ff53dfd3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Inconceivable", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q13732215", "wikidata_description": "episode of Law & Order: Special Victims Unit"}}, {"canonical_name": "Rabier", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 3, "members": [{"entity_id": "rabier", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Rabier", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rabier"], "contexts": ["author of 'Psychologie'"]}, {"entity_id": "rabis", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Rabis", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rabis"], "contexts": ["early modern schol