oirs"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "medecine_experimentale_bernard_1865", "source_name": "Acad. des sciences", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Acad. Roy. des Sciences", "similarity": 0.905}], "id": 5145, "stable_key": "clu_45eebe1ae4b46d08", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Academy of Sciences", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This refers to scientific academies, such as the French Acad\u00e9mie des sciences."}}, {"canonical_name": "Moralist", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "moralist", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Moralist", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Moralist"], "contexts": ["expert on ethics and morality"]}, {"entity_id": "moralist", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "moralist", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["moralist"], "contexts": ["person concerned with morality"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Moralist", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "moralist", "similarity": 0.917}], "id": 5146, "stable_key": "clu_2954aa0687d13dce", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Moralist", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to a person who studies or practices morality, not a specific named individual in this context.", "wikidata_id": "Q136434274", "wikidata_description": ""}}, {"canonical_name": "Flood", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "flood", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Flood", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Flood"], "contexts": ["biblical event of divine judgment"]}, {"entity_id": "floods", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "floods", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["floods"], "contexts": ["events causing rivers to flow into each other"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Flood", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "floods", "similarity": 0.925}], "id": 5147, "stable_key": "clu_f7ea798e65ca42ee", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Flood", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flood", "wikipedia_extract": "A flood is an overflow of water (or rarely other fluids) that submerges land that is usually dry. In the sense of \"flowing water\", the word may also be applied to the inflow of the tide. Floods are of significant concern in agriculture, civil engineering and public health. Human changes to the environment often increase the intensity and frequency of flooding. Examples for human changes are land use changes such as deforestation and removal of wetlands, changes in waterway course or flood controls such as with levees. Global environmental issues also influence causes of floods, namely climate change which causes an intensification of the water cycle and sea level rise."}}, {"canonical_name": "Logick", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "logick", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Logick", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Logick"], "contexts": ["study of reasoning and argument"]}, {"entity_id": "logik", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Logik", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Logik"], "contexts": ["book on logic"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Logick", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Logik", "similarity": 0.977}], "id": 5148, "stable_key": "clu_6f1f332833c6382c-4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Logic", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}}, {"canonical_name": "Utility", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "utility", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Utility", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Utility"], "contexts": ["usefulness or practical value"]}, {"entity_id": "utility", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Utility", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Utility"], "contexts": ["importance in construction of parts"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Utility", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Utility", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5149, "stable_key": "clu_4b782479b578dc27", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Utility", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q1951366", "wikidata_description": "organization that maintains the infrastructure for a public service"}}, {"canonical_name": "visibilities", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "visibilities", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "visibilities", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["visibilities"], "contexts": ["things that can be seen"]}, {"entity_id": "visibility", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "visibility", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["visibility"], "contexts": ["physical property of matter"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "visibilities", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "visibility", "similarity": 0.934}], "id": 5150, "stable_key": "clu_5cd285e852a88fa7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Visibility", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q30301666", "wikidata_description": "condition of being visible; state of an object that can be seen"}}, {"canonical_name": "wisdom", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "wisdom", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "wisdom", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["wisdom"], "contexts": ["knowledge and good judgment"]}, {"entity_id": "sagesse", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "sagesse", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sagesse"], "contexts": ["quality of being wise"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "wisdom", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "sagesse", "similarity": 0.92}], "id": 5151, "stable_key": "clu_07823ff636a9930b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "wisdom", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wisdom", "wikipedia_extract": "Wisdom, also known as sapience, is the ability to apply knowledge, experience, and good judgment to navigate life's complexities. It is often associated with insight, discernment, and ethics in decision-making. Throughout history, wisdom has been regarded as a key virtue in philosophy, religion, and psychology, representing the ability to understand and respond to reality in a balanced and thoughtful manner. Unlike intelligence, which primarily concerns problem-solving and reasoning, wisdom involves a deeper comprehension of human nature, moral principles, and the long-term consequences of actions. Philosophically, wisdom has been explored by thinkers from Ancient Greece to modern times. Socrates famously equated wisdom with recognizing one's own ignorance, while Aristotle saw it as practical reasoning (phronesis) and deep contemplation (sophia)."}}, {"canonical_name": "Philosophical", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "philosophical", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Philosophical", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Philosophical"], "contexts": ["relating to philosophy"]}, {"entity_id": "philosophical", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "philosophical", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["philosophical"], "contexts": ["relating to the study of fundamental questions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Philosophical", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "philosophical", "similarity": 0.949}], "id": 5152, "stable_key": "clu_a29efb545be80340-4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Philosophy", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q5891", "wikidata_description": "study of the truths and principles of being, schools of thought, knowledge, or conduct", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philosophy", "note": "Used in the context of academic disciplines and the study of fundamental questions.", "wikipedia_extract": "Philosophy (from Ancient Greek philosoph\u00eda lit. 'love of wisdom') is a systematic study of general and fundamental questions concerning topics like existence, knowledge, mind, reason, language, and value. It is a rational and critical inquiry that reflects on its methods and assumptions. Historically, many of the individual sciences, such as physics and psychology, formed part of philosophy. However, they are considered separate academic disciplines in the modern sense of the term. Influential traditions in the history of philosophy include Western, Arabic\u2013Persian, Indian, and Chinese philosophy."}}, {"canonical_name": "Calf", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "calf", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Calf", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Calf"], "contexts": ["idol made from ear-rings"]}, {"entity_id": "calf", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "calf", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["calf"], "contexts": ["young bovine animal"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Calf", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "calf", "similarity": 0.757}], "id": 5153, "stable_key": "clu_e22a42bea87eb478", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "calf", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}}, {"canonical_name": "Aphorism", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "aphorism", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Aphorism", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Aphorism"], "contexts": ["saying or maxim"]}, {"entity_id": "aphorismis", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Aphorismis", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Aphorismis"], "contexts": ["short, pithy statements"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Aphorism", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Aphorismis", "similarity": 0.932}], "id": 5154, "stable_key": "clu_cb79ab0224abd9c9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "aphorism", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q4779712", "wikidata_description": "genus of insects"}}, {"canonical_name": "Misapprehension", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "misapprehension", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Misapprehension", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Misapprehension"], "contexts": ["incorrect understanding or interpretation"]}, {"entity_id": "psychological_misapprehension", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "psychological misapprehension", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["psychological misapprehension"], "contexts": ["error in understanding psychological phenomena"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Misapprehension", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "psychological misapprehension", "similarity": 0.897}], "id": 5155, "stable_key": "clu_66abc61cb82c7412", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Misconception", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q25345174", "wikidata_description": "statement popularly believed but which is known to be false in reliable sources"}}, {"canonical_name": "evil", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "evil", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "evil", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["evil"], "contexts": ["morally wrong and undesirable"]}, {"entity_id": "evil", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Evil", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Evil"], "contexts": ["moral or spiritual badness"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "evil", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Evil", "similarity": 0.958}], "id": 5156, "stable_key": "clu_d2f79626aa033e54", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Evil", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q15292", "wikidata_description": "profound immorality"}}, {"canonical_name": "Pilate", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "pilate", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Pilate", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Pilate"], "contexts": ["received accusation against the Saviour"]}, {"entity_id": "pilate", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Pilate", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Pilate"], "contexts": ["character in procession"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Pilate", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Pilate", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5157, "stable_key": "clu_e070709737b17f4a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pontius Pilate", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Roman governor of Judea, historical and religious figure.", "wikipedia_extract": "Pontius Pilate (Latin: Pontius Pilatus; Greek: \u03a0\u03cc\u03bd\u03c4\u03b9\u03bf\u03c2 \u03a0\u03b9\u03bb\u1fb6\u03c4\u03bf\u03c2, romanized: P\u00f3ntios Pil\u00e1tos) was the fifth governor of the Roman province of Judaea, serving under Emperor Tiberius from 26/27 to 36/37 AD. He is best known for being the official who presided over the trial of Jesus and ultimately ordered his crucifixion. Pilate's importance in Christianity is underscored by his prominent place in both the Apostles' and Nicene Creeds. Because the gospels portray Pilate as reluctant to execute Jesus, the Ethiopian Orthodox Tewahedo Church believes that Pilate became a Christian and venerates him as both a martyr and a saint, a belief which was also historically held by the Coptic Church. Pontius Pilate is the best-attested figure to hold the position of Roman governor, though few sources about his rule have survived. Virtually nothing is known about his life prior to becoming governor or the circumstances of his appointment.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pontius_Pilate"}}, {"canonical_name": "Belus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "belus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Belus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Belus"], "contexts": ["ancient deity"]}, {"entity_id": "belus", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Belus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Belus"], "contexts": ["pyramid of Belus"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Belus", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Belus", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5158, "stable_key": "clu_dd32f7a62ecd8dbd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Belus", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Ancient deity associated with Babylonian pyramids", "note": "Belus is a complex figure, often conflated with Babylonian gods like Marduk or with legendary kings.", "wikidata_id": "Q14950162", "wikidata_description": "genus of insects", "wikipedia_extract": "Belus or Belos (Ancient Greek: \u0392\u1fc6\u03bb\u03bf\u03c2, B\u00ealos) in classical Greek or classical Latin texts (and later material based on them) in a Babylonian context refers to the Babylonian god Bel Marduk. Though often identified with Greek Zeus and Latin Jupiter as Zeus Belos or Jupiter Belus, in other cases Belus is euhemerized as an ancient king who founded Babylon and built the ziggurat. He is recognized and worshipped as the god of war. Eusebius of Caesarea (Praeparatio Evangelica 9.18) cites Artabanus as stating in his Jewish History that Artabanus found in anonymous works that giants who had been dwelling in Babylonia were destroyed by the gods for impiety, but one of them named Belus escaped and settled in Babylon and lived in the tower which he built and named the Tower of Belus. A little later Eusebius (9.41) cites Abydenus' Concerning the Assyrians for the information that the site of Babylon:... was originally water, and called a sea.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belus_(Babylonian)"}}, {"canonical_name": "Statue", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "statue", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Statue", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Statue"], "contexts": ["sculpted representation"]}, {"entity_id": "sculpture", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "sculpture", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sculpture"], "contexts": ["art form of carved material"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Statue", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "sculpture", "similarity": 0.916}], "id": 5159, "stable_key": "clu_0fef6a0db83bcafc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sculpture", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "Q860861", "wikidata_description": "three-dimensional work of art"}}, {"canonical_name": "Zeno", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "zeno", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Zeno", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Zeno"], "contexts": ["ancient philosopher known for paradoxes of motion"]}, {"entity_id": "zeno", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Zeno", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Zeno"], "contexts": ["ancient Greek philosopher known for paradoxes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Zeno", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Zeno", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5160, "stable_key": "clu_e1b963c456efad35", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Zeno of Citium", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": -334, "death_year": -262, "description": "Greek Stoic philosopher known for paradoxes", "wikidata_id": "Q171303", "wikidata_description": "Greek philosopher, founder of Stoicism", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zeno%20of%20Citium", "wikipedia_extract": "Zeno of Citium (; Koine Greek: \u0396\u03ae\u03bd\u03c9\u03bd \u1f41 \u039a\u03b9\u03c4\u03b9\u03b5\u03cd\u03c2, Z\u0113n\u014dn ho Kitieus; c. 334 \u2013 c. 262 BC) was a Hellenistic philosopher   from Citium (\u039a\u03af\u03c4\u03b9\u03bf\u03bd, Kition), Cyprus. He was the founder of the Stoic school of philosophy, which he taught in Athens from about 300 BC. Based on the moral ideas of the Cynics, Stoicism laid great emphasis on goodness and peace of mind gained from living a life of virtue in accordance with nature. It proved very popular, and flourished as one of the major schools of philosophy from the Hellenistic period through to the Roman era, and enjoyed revivals in the Renaissance as Neostoicism and in the current era as Modern Stoicism."}}, {"canonical_name": "Commonwealths", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "commonwealths", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Commonwealths", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Commonwealths"], "contexts": ["political states or communities"]}, {"entity_id": "commonwealth", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Commonwealth", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Commonwealth"], "contexts": ["the general public or state"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Commonwealths", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Commonwealth", "similarity": 0.901}], "id": 5161, "stable_key": "clu_f757f5c8f011f648", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Commonwealth", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q408", "wikidata_description": "country in Oceania"}}, {"canonical_name": "Juments", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "juments", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Juments", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Juments"], "contexts": ["work animals"]}, {"entity_id": "juments", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "juments", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["juments"], "contexts": ["female horses"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Juments", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "juments", "similarity": 0.971}], "id": 5162, "stable_key": "clu_b5b1a81d4408c652", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "mares", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "The French term 'juments' specifically refers to female horses (mares).", "wikidata_id": "Q726", "wikidata_description": "domesticated four-footed mammal from the equine family", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horse", "wikipedia_extract": "The horse (Equus ferus caballus) is a domesticated, one-toed, hoofed mammal. It belongs to the taxonomic family Equidae and is one of two extant subspecies of Equus ferus. The horse has evolved over the past 45 to 55 million years from a small multi-toed creature, Eohippus, into the large single-toed animal of today, originally in North America. Humans began domesticating horses around 4000 BCE in Central Asia, and their domestication is believed to have been widespread by 3000 BCE. Horses in the subspecies caballus are domesticated, although some domesticated populations live in the wild as feral horses. These feral populations are not true wild horses, which are horses that have never been domesticated."}}, {"canonical_name": "Elian", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "elian", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Elian", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Elian"], "contexts": ["Roman writer on animals"]}, {"entity_id": "eliano", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Eliano", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Eliano"], "contexts": ["ancient Greek writer"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Elian", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Eliano", "similarity": 0.864}], "id": 5163, "stable_key": "clu_3775f8f72c5f788f-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Aelian", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 170, "death_year": 230, "description": "Roman writer and teacher, known for 'On the Nature of Animals'", "note": "The Spanish 'Eliano' is a direct transliteration/cognate of the Latin 'Aelianus', referring to Claudius Aelianus.", "wikidata_id": "Q313782", "wikidata_description": "Roman author and teacher (c.175\u2013c.235)"}}, {"canonical_name": "testimony", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "testimony", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "testimony", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["testimony"], "contexts": ["evidence or statement from a witness"]}, {"entity_id": "testimony", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "testimony", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["testimony"], "contexts": ["statements made by witnesses"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "testimony", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "testimony", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5164, "stable_key": "clu_42b64c75b1fea448", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "testimony", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q1196258", "wikidata_description": "solemn attestation as to the truth of a matter", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Testimony", "wikipedia_extract": "Testimony is a solemn attestation as to the truth of a matter. The words \"testimony\" and \"testify\" both derive from the Latin word testis, referring to the notion of a disinterested third-party witness. In the law, testimony is a form of evidence in which a witness makes a \"solemn declaration or affirmation ... for the purpose of establishing or proving some fact\". According to Bryan A. Garner, the editor of Black's Law Dictionary, the word \"testimony\" is properly used as a mass noun (that is, always uninflected regardless of number), and not a count noun."}}, {"canonical_name": "CHAPTER VIII", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "chapter_viii", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "CHAPTER VIII", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["CHAPTER VIII"], "contexts": ["Section heading"]}, {"entity_id": "section_viii", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "SECTION VIII", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["SECTION VIII"], "contexts": ["heading of a text section"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "CHAPTER VIII", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "SECTION VIII", "similarity": 0.908}], "id": 5165, "stable_key": "clu_52a6116950f96832", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "section heading", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to structural divisions within texts, indicating a chapter or section.", "wikidata_id": "Q70913180", "wikidata_description": "scientific article published on 01 February 1967"}}, {"canonical_name": "Rhodomanus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "rhodomanus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Rhodomanus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rhodomanus"], "contexts": ["editor of ancient texts"]}, {"entity_id": "rhode", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Rhode", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rhode"], "contexts": ["author on Lemnian matters"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Rhodomanus", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Rhode", "similarity": 0.89}], "id": 5166, "stable_key": "clu_54ed351091f8fbfe", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Christoph Rhode", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "Editor of ancient texts, author on Lemnian matters.", "note": "The spelling 'Rhodomanus' is a Latinization. 'Rhode' is likely the same individual, possibly a misreading or variant spelling.", "wikidata_id": "Q94878982", "wikidata_description": ""}}, {"canonical_name": "Homero", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "homero", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Homero", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Homero"], "contexts": ["ancient poet"]}, {"entity_id": "homero", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Homero", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Homero"], "contexts": ["ancient authority, sometimes sleeps"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Homero", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Homero", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5167, "stable_key": "clu_e749e6acf7753b85-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Homer", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Ancient Greek epic poet, cited as an authority.", "wikidata_id": "Q6691", "wikidata_description": "reputed author of the Iliad and the Odyssey"}}, {"canonical_name": "Trajan", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "trajan", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Trajan", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Trajan"], "contexts": ["Roman Emperor"]}, {"entity_id": "trajan", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Trajan", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Trajan"], "contexts": ["Roman emperor"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Trajan", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Trajan", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5168, "stable_key": "clu_d3e776ec04358621", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Trajan", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 53, "death_year": 117, "description": "Roman emperor known for expansion and public works.", "wikidata_id": "Q1425", "wikidata_description": "13th Roman Emperor (98\u2013117)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trajan", "wikipedia_extract": "Trajan ( TRAY-j\u0259n; born Marcus Ulpius Traianus, 18 September 53 \u2013 c. 9 August 117) was a Roman emperor from AD 98 to 117, remembered as the second of the Five Good Emperors of the Nerva\u2013Antonine dynasty. He was a philanthropic ruler and a successful soldier-emperor who presided over one of the greatest military expansions in Roman history, during which, by the time of his death, the Roman Empire reached its maximum territorial extent. He was given the title of Optimus ('the best') by the Roman Senate. Trajan was born in the municipium of Italica in the present-day Andalusian province of Seville in southern Spain, an Italic settlement in Hispania Baetica; his gens Ulpia came from the town of Tuder in the Umbria region of central Italy. His namesake father, Marcus Ulpius Traianus, was a general and distinguished senator."}}, {"canonical_name": "Laurentius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "laurentius", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Laurentius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Laurentius"], "contexts": ["host of a feast"]}, {"entity_id": "laurent", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Laurent", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Laurent"], "contexts": ["person associated with a project"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Laurentius", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Laurent", "similarity": 0.883}], "id": 5169, "stable_key": "clu_53a65fc0943c6d59", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Laurentius", "confidence": "low", "type": "person", "description": "Unidentified individual, possibly a host or collaborator.", "note": "Multiple individuals named Laurentius or Laurent exist. Without more context, identification is uncertain.", "wikidata_id": "Q17590", "wikidata_description": "Christian saint, martyr and a deacon of Rome (225-258)"}}, {"canonical_name": "Philes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "philes", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Philes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Philes"], "contexts": ["author who delivered proprieties of animals in Greek Iambicks"]}, {"entity_id": "philo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Philo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Philo"], "contexts": ["ancient authority on imagination"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Philes", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Philo", "similarity": 0.888}], "id": 5170, "stable_key": "clu_0f07c0834c44b76a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Philes", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Greek poet and writer, author of 'On the Properties of Animals'.", "note": "The entry mentions both 'Philes' and 'Philo'. Ioannes Philes is the author of 'De Animalium Proprietatibus'."}}, {"canonical_name": "Salamander", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "REPTILE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "salamander", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Salamander", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "REPTILE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Salamander"], "contexts": ["creature associated with fire in fables"]}, {"entity_id": "salamander", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "salamander", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "REPTILE", "count": 1, "variants": ["salamander"], "contexts": ["fossil, conjectured antediluvian man"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Salamander", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "salamander", "similarity": 0.978}], "id": 5171, "stable_key": "clu_601936b891fe14cc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Salamander", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "Early modern texts sometimes conflated salamanders with fossil remains, leading to a 'fossil salamander' concept, but the primary referent remains the amphibian.", "wikidata_id": "Q3469592", "wikidata_description": "animal", "wikipedia_extract": "Salamanders are a group of amphibians typically characterized by their lizard-like appearance, with slender bodies, blunt snouts, short limbs projecting at right angles to the body, and the presence of a tail in both larvae and adults. All ten extant salamander families are grouped together under the order Urodela, the sole surviving order from the group Caudata. Urodela is a scientific Latin term based on the Ancient Greek \u03bf\u1f50\u03c1\u03ac \u03b4\u03ae\u03bb\u03b7: our\u00e0 d\u0113l\u0113 \"conspicuous tail\". Caudata is the Latin for \"tailed ones\", from cauda: \"tail\". Salamander diversity is highest in eastern North America, especially in the Appalachian Mountains; most species are found in the Holarctic realm, with some species present in the Neotropical realm. Salamanders never have more than four toes on their front legs and five on their rear legs, but some species have fewer digits and others lack hind limbs.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salamander"}}, {"canonical_name": "Ethicks", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "ethicks", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Ethicks", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ethicks"], "contexts": ["Aristotle's work on moral philosophy"]}, {"entity_id": "ethics", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Ethics", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ethics"], "contexts": ["domain of moral principles and choice"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Ethicks", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Ethics", "similarity": 0.92}], "id": 5172, "stable_key": "clu_22277d9feeef57f7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ethics", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q9465", "wikidata_description": "branch of philosophy that systematizes, defends, and recommends concepts of right and wrong conduct", "wikipedia_url": "", "wikipedia_extract": ""}}, {"canonical_name": "Theodosius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "theodosius", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Theodosius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Theodosius"], "contexts": ["reign during which Orus Apollo Niliacus lived"]}, {"entity_id": "theodo\u017fio", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Theodo\u017fio", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Theodo\u017fio"], "contexts": ["priest who fell ill with pleurisy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Theodosius", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Theodo\u017fio", "similarity": 0.891}], "id": 5173, "stable_key": "clu_bc676378cdebfa12", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Theodosius", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "Emperor of the Roman Empire", "note": "Refers to multiple historical figures named Theodosius. Context suggests emperors."}}, {"canonical_name": "chance", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "chance", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "chance", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["chance"], "contexts": ["random occurrence"]}, {"entity_id": "chance", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "chance", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["chance"], "contexts": ["misconception about natural processes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "chance", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "chance", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5174, "stable_key": "clu_1b9bee171c979063", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Chance", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}}, {"canonical_name": "Perfection", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "perfection", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Perfection", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Perfection"], "contexts": ["state of being complete or faultless"]}, {"entity_id": "parfait", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "parfait", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["parfait"], "contexts": ["highest degree of excellence"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Perfection", "target_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "target_name": "parfait", "similarity": 0.914}], "id": 5175, "stable_key": "clu_edb47666829138af", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "perfection", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to a state of being complete, faultless, or at the highest degree of excellence.", "wikidata_id": "Q378701", "wikidata_description": "absence of flaws; quality of an ideal object"}}, {"canonical_name": "Samuel", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "samuel", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Samuel", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Samuel"], "contexts": ["biblical figure"]}, {"entity_id": "samuel", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Samuel", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Samuel"], "contexts": ["feather merchant living at Corpo Santo"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Samuel", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Samuel", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5176, "stable_key": "clu_79973565d7f692d2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Samuel", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "Biblical prophet and figure", "note": "Refers to the biblical figure Samuel, but the 'feather merchant' context is unclear.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}}, {"canonical_name": "Demosthenes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "demosthenes", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Demosthenes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Demosthenes"], "contexts": ["Greek orator"]}, {"entity_id": "dem\u00f3stenes", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Dem\u00f3stenes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Dem\u00f3stenes"], "contexts": ["drank poison to avoid cruel death"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Demosthenes", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Dem\u00f3stenes", "similarity": 0.898}], "id": 5177, "stable_key": "clu_198ded555af9be05", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Demosthenes", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": -384, "death_year": -322, "description": "Ancient Greek orator, known for his speeches and death by poison.", "wikidata_id": "Q798024", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Demosthenesia", "wikipedia_extract": "Demosthenesia is a genus of flowering plants belonging to the family Ericaceae. Its native range is Peru to Bolivia and Brazil. The genus name of Demosthenesia is in honour of Demosthenes (384\u2013322 BC), a Greek politician. Demosthenesia amicorum (Sleumer) Sleumer Demosthenesia buxifolia (Fielding & Gardner) A.C.Sm. Demosthenesia fabulosa (Sleumer) A.C.Sm. Demosthenesia mandonii (Britton) A.C.Sm."}}, {"canonical_name": "natural Magician", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "natural_magician", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "natural Magician", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["natural Magician"], "contexts": ["practitioner of natural magic"]}, {"entity_id": "magia_natural", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Magia natural", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Magia natural"], "contexts": ["natural magic"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "natural Magician", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Magia natural", "similarity": 0.885}], "id": 5178, "stable_key": "clu_18f9cf9fe564cb9e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Natural magic", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q6980728", "wikidata_description": "Occult practices directly using the natural world instead of spirits"}}, {"canonical_name": "\u00c6lianus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "\u00e6lianus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "\u00c6lianus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["\u00c6lianus"], "contexts": ["ancient writer"]}, {"entity_id": "\u00e6lian_", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "\u00c6lian.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["\u00c6lian."], "contexts": ["ancient author cited"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "\u00c6lianus", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "\u00c6lian.", "similarity": 0.869}], "id": 5179, "stable_key": "clu_3775f8f72c5f788f-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Aelian", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 170, "death_year": 230, "description": "Roman writer and teacher, known for 'On the Nature of Animals'", "note": "The Spanish 'Eliano' is a direct transliteration/cognate of the Latin 'Aelianus', referring to Claudius Aelianus.", "wikidata_id": "Q313782", "wikidata_description": "Roman author and teacher (c.175\u2013c.235)"}}, {"canonical_name": "Dioclesian", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "dioclesian", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Dioclesian", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Dioclesian"], "contexts": ["Roman Emperor"]}, {"entity_id": "dioclesian", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Dioclesian", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Dioclesian"], "contexts": ["Roman Emperor"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Dioclesian", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Dioclesian", "similarity": 0.708}], "id": 5180, "stable_key": "clu_75eddcf47d834ad6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Diocletian", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 244, "death_year": 311, "description": "Roman Emperor, known for his reforms and persecution of Christians.", "wikidata_id": "Q43107", "wikidata_description": "Augustus of the Eastern Roman Empire (244-311)"}}, {"canonical_name": "revealed verity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "revealed_verity", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "revealed verity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["revealed verity"], "contexts": ["divine truth"]}, {"entity_id": "v\u00e9rit\u00e9s_r\u00e9v\u00e9l\u00e9es", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "v\u00e9rit\u00e9s r\u00e9v\u00e9l\u00e9es", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["v\u00e9rit\u00e9s r\u00e9v\u00e9l\u00e9es"], "contexts": ["religious truths"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "revealed verity", "target_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "target_name": "v\u00e9rit\u00e9s r\u00e9v\u00e9l\u00e9es", "similarity": 0.908}], "id": 5181, "stable_key": "clu_ce6913e7bb425962", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "revealed truth", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to truths understood to be divinely revealed, often in religious contexts."}}, {"canonical_name": "Caius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "caius", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Caius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Caius"], "contexts": ["blind man cured by divine intervention"]}, {"entity_id": "ca\u017faris", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Ca\u017faris", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ca\u017faris"], "contexts": ["person with heart condition"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Caius", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Ca\u017faris", "similarity": 0.868}], "id": 5182, "stable_key": "clu_46ae3e59a304c4f4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Gaius", "confidence": "low", "type": "person", "description": "Latin jurist and physician, potentially confused with Caius of Rome.", "note": "The name 'Caius' is common. In a medical context, it might refer to the Roman jurist Gaius, but without further context, it's ambiguous. 'Ca\u017faris' could be a misspelling or a different name.", "wikidata_id": "Q53628544", "wikidata_description": "genus of mygalomorph spiders"}}, {"canonical_name": "Jordan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "jordan", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Jordan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Jordan"], "contexts": ["river for cleansing"]}, {"entity_id": "jordan", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Jordan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Jordan"], "contexts": ["river in the Middle East"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Jordan", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Jordan", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5183, "stable_key": "clu_f506c23690c278c7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jordan River", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Israel, Palestine, Jordan", "note": "The contexts clearly point to the biblical and geographical Jordan River. The mention of 'river mouth between Savannah and Charlestown' might be a misidentification or a metaphorical use of the name, but the dominant referent is the Middle Eastern river.", "wikidata_id": "Q22487538", "wikidata_description": "river in Capital Regional District, British Columbia, Canada", "wikipedia_extract": "The Jordan River or River Jordan (Arabic: \u0646\u064e\u0647\u0652\u0631 \u0627\u0644\u0652\u0623\u064f\u0631\u0652\u062f\u064f\u0646\u0651, Nahr al-\u02beUrdunn; Hebrew: \u05e0\u05b0\u05d4\u05b7\u05e8 \u05d4\u05b7\u05d9\u05b7\u05bc\u05e8\u05b0\u05d3\u05b5\u05bc\u05df, N\u0259har hayYard\u0113n), also known as Nahr Al-Sharieat (Arabic: \u0646\u0647\u0631 \u0627\u0644\u0634\u0631\u064a\u0639\u0629), is a 251-kilometre-long (156 mi) endorheic river in the Levant that flows roughly north to south through the Sea of Galilee and drains to the Dead Sea. The river passes by or through Jordan, Syria, Israel, and Palestine. Jordan and the Israeli-occupied Golan Heights border the river to the east, while Israel and the Israeli-occupied West Bank lie to its west. Both Jordan and the West Bank derive their names in relation to the river. The river holds major significance in Judaism and Christianity. According to the Bible, the Israelites crossed it into the Promised Land and Jesus of Nazareth was baptized by John the Baptist in it."}}, {"canonical_name": "Colocynthis", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "colocynthis", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Colocynthis", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Colocynthis"], "contexts": ["plant for decoction"]}, {"entity_id": "colocynthis", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Colocynthis", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Colocynthis"], "contexts": ["herb used in learned receipts"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Colocynthis", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Colocynthis", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5184, "stable_key": "clu_74c0fa443ccad320", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Colocynth", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Citrullus colocynthis", "family": "Cucurbitaceae", "description": "A bitter gourd plant used as an ingredient in medicinal preparations.", "note": "Includes singular and plural forms in Italian and English.", "wikipedia_extract": "Citrullus colocynthis, with many common names including colocynth, bitter apple, bitter cucumber, vine of Sodom, or wild gourd, is a poisonous desert viny plant native to the Mediterranean Basin and West Asia, especially the Levant, Turkey (especially in regions such as \u0130zmir), and Nubia. It resembles a common watermelon vine but bears small, hard fruits with a bitter pulp. The plant contains cytotoxic cucurbitacins and is thus unsafe to use as an herbal medicine. It originally bore the scientific name Colocynthis citrullus. The vine ranges from 2.4\u20133 metres (8\u201310 feet) in length. The roots are large, fleshy, and perennial, leading to a high survival rate due to the long tap root."}}, {"canonical_name": "Rule", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "rule", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Rule", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rule"], "contexts": ["principle or standard"]}, {"entity_id": "rule", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "rule", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["rule"], "contexts": ["general principle"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Rule", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "rule", "similarity": 0.959}], "id": 5185, "stable_key": "clu_a75efb7211970417", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Rule", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q216200", "wikidata_description": "commandment, instruction, or order intended as an authoritative rule of action"}}, {"canonical_name": "Archidoxis", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "archidoxis", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Archidoxis", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Archidoxis"], "contexts": ["Paracelsus's work on medicine"]}, {"entity_id": "archidoxis", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Archidoxis", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Archidoxis"], "contexts": ["a work or system of knowledge"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Archidoxis", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Archidoxis", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5186, "stable_key": "clu_246c40a82e18f4b4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Archidoxis", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This refers to a specific set of Paracelsian medical writings and philosophy, not a general concept.", "wikidata_id": "Q30716455", "wikidata_description": "French translation of Latin Archidoxis magica attr. Paracelcus"}}, {"canonical_name": "juices", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "juices", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "juices", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["juices"], "contexts": ["bodily fluids"]}, {"entity_id": "organic_juices", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "organic juices", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["organic juices"], "contexts": ["bodily fluids"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "juices", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "organic juices", "similarity": 0.913}], "id": 5187, "stable_key": "clu_e8fbba8860766cbf-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "juices", "confidence": "medium", "type": "concept", "note": "This cluster includes both bodily fluids and plant extracts, a broader concept than a specific substance.", "wikidata_id": "Q1058795", "wikidata_description": "liquid originating from the inside of an organism, including fluids excreted or secreted, and body water that normally is not", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cold-pressed_juice", "wikipedia_extract": "Cold-pressed juice is juice that uses a hydraulic press to extract juice from fruit and vegetables, as opposed to other methods such as centrifugal or single auger. Without pasteurization or high-pressure processing (HPP), cold-pressed juices can be stored in a refrigerator for up to three days when phytochemical and micronutrient degradation occurs. Some juicers use technology that helps delay oxidation which can allow for slightly longer storage in refrigerators. This type of juice has been commercially produced for decades, but became more common in some countries since 2013. In general, these juices are more expensive than other types of juices, as they are made from 100% fruit and vegetables without any added ingredients. Although cold-pressed juices were produced over several decades, the products gained more common use during the fad of juice \"cleansing\", expanding into a wider industry in the early 21st century."}}, {"canonical_name": "Tragacanth", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "RESIN", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "tragacanth", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Tragacanth", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "RESIN", "count": 1, "variants": ["Tragacanth"], "contexts": ["gum from astragalus plants"]}, {"entity_id": "tragacanto", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "tragacanto", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "RESIN", "count": 1, "variants": ["tragacanto"], "contexts": ["gum called tragacanth"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Tragacanth", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "tragacanto", "similarity": 0.898}], "id": 5188, "stable_key": "clu_0c44d24d79f5c4a3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tragacanth gum", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Astragalus gummifer", "family": "Fabaceae", "note": "While 'Tragacanth' can refer to the plant genus, in the context of medical and scientific texts from the period, it most often refers to the gum produced by plants of the Astragalus genus, particularly Astragalus gummifer. The provided context 'gum from astragalus plants' strongly supports this.", "wikidata_id": "Q17435451", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants"}}, {"canonical_name": "Agaths", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "agaths", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Agaths", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Agaths"], "contexts": ["hard bodies that do not readily strike fire"]}, {"entity_id": "agatas", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Agatas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Agatas"], "contexts": ["precious stones from mines"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Agaths", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Agatas", "similarity": 0.912}], "id": 5189, "stable_key": "clu_1113ac159f733ba5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "agate", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral"}}, {"canonical_name": "Mettals", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "mettals", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Mettals", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mettals"], "contexts": ["non-living materials, chemicals, and compound preparations"]}, {"entity_id": "mettals", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Mettals", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mettals"], "contexts": ["ingredients in Greek plasters"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Mettals", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Mettals", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5190, "stable_key": "clu_0d7cb2273f452145", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Metals", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q11426", "wikidata_description": "element, compound, or alloy that is a good conductor of both electricity and heat", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metal"}}, {"canonical_name": "Caramania", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "caramania", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Caramania", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Caramania"], "contexts": ["historical region in Asia Minor"]}, {"entity_id": "caramanian", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Caramanian", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Caramanian"], "contexts": ["coast region"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Caramania", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Caramanian", "similarity": 0.915}], "id": 5191, "stable_key": "clu_83750ffc55f4401e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Karaman", "confidence": "medium", "type": "place", "country": "Turkey", "note": "The primary referent is likely 'Caramania', a historical region in Asia Minor (modern Turkey). 'Carabaya' is a region in Peru, and 'Caramanian' refers to the region. The cluster is titled 'Caramania', making it the most probable focus. 'Caramania' is an older spelling for the region of Karaman.", "wikidata_id": "Q262579", "wikidata_description": "central district and city in Karaman Province, Turkey", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karaman", "wikipedia_extract": "Karaman is a city in south central Turkey, located in Central Anatolia, north of the Taurus Mountains, about 100 km (62 mi) south of Konya. It is the seat of Karaman Province and Karaman District. Its population is 175,390 (2022). The town lies at an average elevation of 1,039 m (3,409 ft). The Karaman Museum is one of the major sights. The town owes its name to Karaman Bey, who was one of the rulers of the Karamanid dynasty."}}, {"canonical_name": "Parma", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "parma", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Parma", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Parma"], "contexts": ["city in Italy"]}, {"entity_id": "parma", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Parma", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Parma"], "contexts": ["city in Italy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Parma", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Parma", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5192, "stable_key": "clu_64627c8e397002e7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Parma", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Italy", "wikidata_id": "Q2683", "wikidata_description": "city in the northern Italian region of Emilia-Romagna", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parma", "wikipedia_extract": "Parma (Italian: [\u02c8parma] ; Parmigiano: P\u00e4rma [\u02c8p\u025b\u02d0\u0281m\u0250]) is a city in the region of Emilia-Romagna in Northern Italy, known for its architecture, music, art, prosciutto (ham), cheese and surrounding countryside. With a population of 202,111 inhabitants as of 2025, Parma is the second most populous city in Emilia-Romagna after Bologna, the region's capital. The city is home to the University of Parma, one of the oldest universities in the world. Parma is divided into two parts by the stream of the same name. The district on the west side of the river is Oltretorrente, meaning The other side of the stream. Parma's Etruscan name was adapted by Romans to describe the round shield called Parma."}}, {"canonical_name": "Northern Seas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "northern_seas", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Northern Seas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Northern Seas"], "contexts": ["mountains of ice about the Northern Seas"]}, {"entity_id": "northern_seas", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "northern seas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["northern seas"], "contexts": ["seas with analogous species"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Northern Seas", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "northern seas", "similarity": 0.95}], "id": 5193, "stable_key": "clu_c7fa85ff20958d81", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Arctic Ocean", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "International"}}, {"canonical_name": "diaphanity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "diaphanity", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "diaphanity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["diaphanity"], "contexts": ["quality of being transparent"]}, {"entity_id": "diaphaneity", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "diaphaneity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["diaphaneity"], "contexts": ["transparency of a substance"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "diaphanity", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "diaphaneity", "similarity": 0.951}], "id": 5194, "stable_key": "clu_98b2a7a95b5112f4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Transparency", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "oyl of Vitriol", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "oyl_of_vitriol", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "oyl of Vitriol", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["oyl of Vitriol"], "contexts": ["acid used for cooling wire"]}, {"entity_id": "oyl_of_vitriol", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Oyl of Vitriol", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Oyl of Vitriol"], "contexts": ["added for tartness to juleps"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "oyl of Vitriol", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Oyl of Vitriol", "similarity": 0.868}], "id": 5195, "stable_key": "clu_ae5f0f7516bd1cb4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Oil of Vitriol", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "description": "Strong acidic liquid, sulfuric acid, used in alchemy and medicine.", "wikipedia_extract": "Sulfuric acid (American spelling and the preferred IUPAC name) or sulphuric acid (Commonwealth spelling), known in antiquity as oil of vitriol, is a mineral acid composed of the elements sulfur, oxygen, and hydrogen, with the molecular formula H2SO4. It is a colorless, odorless, and viscous liquid that is miscible with water. Pure sulfuric acid does not occur naturally due to its strong affinity to water vapor; it is hygroscopic and readily absorbs water vapor from the air. Concentrated sulfuric acid is a strong oxidant with powerful dehydrating properties, making it highly corrosive towards other materials, from rocks to metals. Phosphorus pentoxide is a notable exception in that it is not dehydrated by sulfuric acid but, to the contrary, dehydrates sulfuric acid to sulfur trioxide. Upon addition of sulfuric acid to water, a considerable amount of heat is released; thus, the reverse procedure of adding water to the acid is generally avoided since the heat released may boil the solution, spraying droplets of hot acid during the process."}}, {"canonical_name": "Poles of the World", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "poles_of_the_world", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Poles of the World", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Poles of the World"], "contexts": ["geographic poles of Earth"]}, {"entity_id": "poles", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "poles", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["poles"], "contexts": ["extremities of a magnet"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Poles of the World", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "poles", "similarity": 0.884}], "id": 5196, "stable_key": "clu_04f9ff1a17140483", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Earth's Poles", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to both geographic poles of Earth and the poles of a magnet, depending on context.", "wikidata_id": "Q3907660", "wikidata_description": ""}}, {"canonical_name": "Saint Michael", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "saint_michael", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Saint Michael", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Saint Michael"], "contexts": ["island with middle situation"]}, {"entity_id": "st__michael", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "St. Michael", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["St. Michael"], "contexts": ["island in the Azores"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Saint Michael", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "St. Michael", "similarity": 0.973}], "id": 5197, "stable_key": "clu_abd570a4780d2b3e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "San Miguel", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Gulf and church name, likely referring to a specific geographical location.", "wikipedia_extract": "Saint Jun\u00edpero Serra Ferrer  (; Spanish: [xu\u02c8nipe\u027eo \u02c8sera]; November 24, 1713 \u2013 August 28, 1784), popularly known simply as Junipero Serra, was a Spanish Catholic priest and missionary of the Franciscan Order. He is credited with establishing the Franciscan Missions in the Sierra Gorda, a UNESCO World Heritage Site. He founded a mission in Baja California and established eight of the 21 Spanish missions in California from San Diego to San Francisco, in what was then Spanish-occupied Alta California in the Province of Las Californias of New Spain. Serra was beatified by Pope John Paul II on 25 September 1988 in Vatican City. Amid denunciations from Native American tribes who accused Serra of presiding over a brutal colonial subjugation, Pope Francis canonized Serra on 23 September 2015 at the Basilica of the National Shrine of the Immaculate Conception in Washington, D.C., during his visit to the United States. Serra's missionary efforts earned him the title of \"Apostle of California\"."}}, {"canonical_name": "Waters", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "waters", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Waters", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Waters"], "contexts": ["affecting magnetic variation"]}, {"entity_id": "aguas", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "aguas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["aguas"], "contexts": ["waters for sweetening ginger"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Waters", "target_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "target_name": "aguas", "similarity": 0.98}], "id": 5198, "stable_key": "clu_8aa6d927640b0685", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Water", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "The term 'waters' here refers to bodies of water or liquids in general, rather than a specific chemical compound like H2O, though it is chemically H2O. The context of 'sweetening ginger' suggests liquids used in preparations.", "wikidata_id": "Q283", "wikidata_description": "chemical compound whose molecules are formed by two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Water"}}, {"canonical_name": "Phoenicean", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "phoenicean", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Phoenicean", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Phoenicean"], "contexts": ["navigators who might have used stars"]}, {"entity_id": "phoenicians", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Phoenicians", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Phoenicians"], "contexts": ["ancient seafaring civilization"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Phoenicean", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Phoenicians", "similarity": 0.928}], "id": 5199, "stable_key": "clu_e8ccfda8fbc827fa", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Phoenicians", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Ancient seafaring civilization known for navigation and trade.", "wikidata_id": "Q129630055", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article published on 24 June 2018"}}, {"canonical_name": "magnetical parts", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "magnetical_parts", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "magnetical parts", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["magnetical parts"], "contexts": ["parts of earth affecting variation"]}, {"entity_id": "magnetic_nucleus", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "magnetic nucleus", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["magnetic nucleus"], "contexts": ["hypothesized core of Earth's magnetism"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "magnetical parts", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "magnetic nucleus", "similarity": 0.884}], "id": 5200, "stable_key": "clu_580a9d062f110ee1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Earth's magnetic field", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the hypothesized internal structure or source of Earth's magnetism.", "wikidata_id": "Q6500960", "wikidata_description": "magnetic field that extends from the Earth's inner core to where it meets the solar wind"}}, {"canonical_name": "magnetem", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "magnetem", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "magnetem", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["magnetem"], "contexts": ["Latin for magnet"]}, {"entity_id": "magneta", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "magneta", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["magneta"], "contexts": ["magnetite"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "magnetem", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "magneta", "similarity": 0.967}], "id": 5201, "stable_key": "clu_5ecd9b12b67a907b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "magnetite", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "The early modern texts likely refer to naturally occurring lodestone or specially treated iron (steel magnet) exhibiting magnetic properties, both of which are forms of magnetite or related iron oxides.", "wikidata_id": "Q181395", "wikidata_description": "iron ore mineral", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnetite", "wikipedia_extract": "Magnetite is a mineral and one of the main iron ores, with the chemical formula Fe2+Fe3+2O4. It is one of the oxides of iron, and is ferrimagnetic; it is attracted to a magnet and can be magnetized to become a permanent magnet itself. With the exception of extremely rare native iron deposits, it is the most magnetic of all the naturally occurring minerals on Earth. Naturally magnetized pieces of magnetite, called lodestone, will attract small pieces of iron, which is how ancient peoples first discovered the property of magnetism. Magnetite is black or brownish-black with a metallic luster, has a Mohs hardness of 5\u20136 and leaves a black streak. Small grains of magnetite are very common in igneous and metamorphic rocks."}}, {"canonical_name": "Faces", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "faces", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Faces", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Faces"], "contexts": ["sides of a magnet"]}, {"entity_id": "facies", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "facies", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["facies"], "contexts": ["face that can swell in dropsy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Faces", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "facies", "similarity": 0.961}], "id": 5202, "stable_key": "clu_b2515a3339a96dc9-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Face", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "The front part of a person's head, from the forehead to the chin.", "wikipedia_extract": "The face is the front of the head in humans and many other animals that features most of the sense organs including the eyes, nose and mouth. Many animals may express emotions through their face. Sense organs in the faces of different animals are varied such as the snout, and the proboscis. Many animals are flat-faced (brachycephalic) such as the pug dog. The human face is crucial for identity, and damage such as scarring or developmental deformities may adversely affect the psyche. The front of the human head is called the face.", "wikidata_id": "Q355", "wikidata_description": "American online social media and social networking service"}}, {"canonical_name": "Iron wire", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "iron_wire", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Iron wire", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Iron wire"], "contexts": ["metal wire used in experiment"]}, {"entity_id": "metallic_wire", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "metallic wire", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["metallic wire"], "contexts": ["conductor in electrical circuit"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Iron wire", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "metallic wire", "similarity": 0.94}], "id": 5203, "stable_key": "clu_2a8ba18d9a796bd7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Iron wire", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q3071740", "wikidata_description": "wire made of iron"}}, {"canonical_name": "\u00c6gyptian", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "\u00e6gyptian", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "\u00c6gyptian", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["\u00c6gyptian"], "contexts": ["origin of the idol"]}, {"entity_id": "egyptian", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Egyptian", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Egyptian"], "contexts": ["monuments with engraved latitudes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "\u00c6gyptian", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Egyptian", "similarity": 0.964}], "id": 5204, "stable_key": "clu_64e94eeab0cd5fe7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Egypt", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Egypt", "wikidata_id": "Q79", "wikidata_description": "country in Northeast Africa and Southwest Asia", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Egypt"}}, {"canonical_name": "C\u00e6sar", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "c\u00e6sar", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "C\u00e6sar", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["C\u00e6sar"], "contexts": ["described Britain"]}, {"entity_id": "c\u00e6sar", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "C\u00e6sar", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["C\u00e6sar"], "contexts": ["Julius, era computed from his reign"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "C\u00e6sar", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "C\u00e6sar", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5205, "stable_key": "clu_1a6713bbd578e755", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Julius Caesar", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": -100, "death_year": -44, "description": "Roman general and dictator, initiated the Julian Calendar.", "wikidata_id": "Q1048", "wikidata_description": "Roman general and dictator"}}, {"canonical_name": "Saxo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "saxo", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Saxo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Saxo"], "contexts": ["source for Olaus Magnus' history"]}, {"entity_id": "saxonia", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Saxonia", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Saxonia"], "contexts": ["cited for opinion on cauteries"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Saxo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Saxonia", "similarity": 0.852}], "id": 5206, "stable_key": "clu_4b245eca1ea00830", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Saxo Grammaticus", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Danish historian, author of Gesta Danorum.", "note": "Saxo Grammaticus is a well-known historical figure, cited by Olaus Magnus. 'Saxonia' could refer to the region, but in this context, the person is more plausible.", "wikidata_id": "Q106837", "wikidata_description": "Danish historian", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saxo%20Grammaticus", "wikipedia_extract": "Saxo Grammaticus (c. 1150 \u2013 c. 1220), also known as Saxo cognomine Longus, was a Danish historian, theologian, and author. He is thought to have been a clerk or secretary to Absalon, Archbishop of Lund, the main advisor to Valdemar I of Denmark. He is the author of the Gesta Danorum, the first full history of Denmark, from which the legend of Amleth would come to inspire the story of Hamlet by Shakespeare. The Jutland Chronicle gives evidence that Saxo was born in Zealand."}}, {"canonical_name": "Vossius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "vossius", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Vossius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Vossius"], "contexts": ["learned Vossius observeth"]}, {"entity_id": "voss", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Voss", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Voss"], "contexts": ["scholar who stated Greek origin of Aetna"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Vossius", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Voss", "similarity": 0.895}], "id": 5207, "stable_key": "clu_cebb87b184efeec8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Gerardus Vossius", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1547, "death_year": 1627, "description": "Dutch classical scholar and theologian."}}, {"canonical_name": "Chyrurgions", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "chyrurgions", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Chyrurgions", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 1, "variants": ["Chyrurgions"], "contexts": ["surgeons using knives and lances"]}, {"entity_id": "chyrurgion", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Chyrurgion", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 1, "variants": ["Chyrurgion"], "contexts": ["surgical practitioner"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Chyrurgions", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Chyrurgion", "similarity": 0.979}], "id": 5208, "stable_key": "clu_c1f21212d0436dc1-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Surgeon", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Medical practitioner specializing in surgical procedures.", "wikidata_id": "Q774306", "wikidata_description": "physician with surgical specialty", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Surgeon", "wikipedia_extract": "In  medicine, a surgeon is a medical doctor who performs surgery. Even though there are different traditions in different times and places, a modern surgeon is a licensed physician and received the same medical training as physicians before specializing in surgery. In some countries and jurisdictions, the title of 'surgeon' is restricted to maintain the integrity of the craft group in the medical profession. A specialist regarded as a legally recognized surgeon includes podiatry, dentistry, and veterinary medicine. It is estimated that surgeons perform over 300 million surgical procedures globally each year. The first person to document a surgery was the 6th century BC Indian physician-surgeon, Sushruta."}}, {"canonical_name": "Troy", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "troy", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Troy", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Troy"], "contexts": ["ancient city whose destruction was foretold"]}, {"entity_id": "troy", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Troy", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Troy"], "contexts": ["ancient city"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Troy", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Troy", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5209, "stable_key": "clu_441d1b27ba92777e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Troy", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Turkey", "wikidata_id": "Q79862", "wikidata_description": "city in Pike County, Alabama, United States"}}, {"canonical_name": "\u00c6olian", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "\u00e6olian", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "\u00c6olian", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["\u00c6olian"], "contexts": ["related to wind or air"]}, {"entity_id": "\u00e6olian", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "\u00c6olian", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["\u00c6olian"], "contexts": ["musical mode answering to keys"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "\u00c6olian", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "\u00c6olian", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5210, "stable_key": "clu_6a5c4f1f21e81ae7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Aeolian", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q215839", "wikidata_description": "processes due to wind activity", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aeolian_processes", "wikipedia_extract": "Aeolian sound or Aeolian tone is sound that is produced by wind when it passes over or through objects. Historically, Aeolus was the Greek ruler of the winds, and the Ancient Greeks believed these sounds were the voice of Aeolus. The earliest known observations about Aeolian sounds from a \"scientific\" viewpoint were made by Athanasius Kircher in 1650. An Aeolian tone is produced when air passes over an obstacle, resulting in trailing vortices with oscillatory behavior. These eddies can have strong periodic components, resulting in a steady tone. This phenomenon is the main topic of aeroacoustics."}}, {"canonical_name": "Lemnius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "lemnius", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Lemnius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lemnius"], "contexts": ["author on minerals and scripture"]}, {"entity_id": "lemnio", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Lemnio", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lemnio"], "contexts": ["author cited for plague observations"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Lemnius", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Lemnio", "similarity": 0.936}], "id": 5211, "stable_key": "clu_eeedddde007bda22-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Levinus Lemnius", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1506, "death_year": 1568, "description": "Dutch physician and humanist, known for his writings on natural philosophy.", "wikidata_id": "Q3237230", "wikidata_description": "Dutch writer (1505-1568)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Levinus%20Lemnius", "wikipedia_extract": "Levinus Lemnius  (20 May 1505 in Zierikzee \u2013 1 July 1568 in Zierikzee) was a Dutch physician and author. Lemnius studied medicine at the University of Leuven under Rembert Dodoens and Konrad Gesner; and under Vesalius at Padua. He also travelled to Switzerland and England. After his wife's death, he became a priest. Occulta naturae miracula (1559, Antwerp) by the University and State Library D\u00fcsseldorf) This was translated as De gli occvlti miracoli, Les Occultes Merveilles et Secretz de Nature (online text, The secret miracles of nature, and Wunderbarliche Geheimnisse der Natur online text). De habitu et constitutione corporis (1561, Antwerp)."}}, {"canonical_name": "Birdlime", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "birdlime", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Birdlime", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Birdlime"], "contexts": ["consistency achieved by concretion"]}, {"entity_id": "birdlime", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Birdlime", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Birdlime"], "contexts": ["preparation from mistletoe"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Birdlime", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Birdlime", "similarity": 0.758}], "id": 5212, "stable_key": "clu_9ee3bd54a3564b4f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Birdlime", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "wikidata_id": "Q79938895", "wikidata_description": "portfolio by Minor White (American, 1908-1976); Light Gallery (1986.221.4)"}}, {"canonical_name": "Kidney", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "kidney", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Kidney", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Kidney"], "contexts": ["organ where stones can form"]}, {"entity_id": "kidney", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "kidney", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["kidney"], "contexts": ["organ involved in activity"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Kidney", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "kidney", "similarity": 0.913}], "id": 5213, "stable_key": "clu_9be1386fc749bda1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Kidney", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q9377", "wikidata_description": "internal organ in most animals, including vertebrates and some invertebrates"}}, {"canonical_name": "blood", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "PRODUCT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "blood", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "blood", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "PRODUCT", "count": 1, "variants": ["blood"], "contexts": ["animal fluid"]}, {"entity_id": "venous_blood", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "venous blood", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "PRODUCT", "count": 1, "variants": ["venous blood"], "contexts": ["fluid with a red color"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "blood", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "venous blood", "similarity": 0.897}], "id": 5214, "stable_key": "clu_e42a911acc1a5ab0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Blood", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q7873", "wikidata_description": "organic fluid which transports nutrients throughout the organism"}}, {"canonical_name": "petroselinum", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "HERB", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "petroselinum", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "petroselinum", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "HERB", "count": 1, "variants": ["petroselinum"], "contexts": ["herb with medicinal properties"]}, {"entity_id": "petroselinum", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "petroselinum", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["petroselinum"], "contexts": ["parsley"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "petroselinum", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "petroselinum", "similarity": 0.769}], "id": 5215, "stable_key": "clu_7318c673dd48ec53", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Parsley", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Petroselinum crispum (Linnaeus) Hoffmanns.", "family": "Apiaceae"}}, {"canonical_name": "Pins", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "pins", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Pins", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Pins"], "contexts": ["sharp objects that can be poisonous"]}, {"entity_id": "pins", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "pins", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["pins"], "contexts": ["fasteners used to hold a string"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Pins", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "pins", "similarity": 0.984}], "id": 5216, "stable_key": "clu_6645ecddf8a71b88", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pins", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "Q12024", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants in the conifer family Pinaceae"}}, {"canonical_name": "Daniel", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "daniel", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Daniel", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Daniel"], "contexts": ["figure who destroyed a dragon"]}, {"entity_id": "daniel", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Daniel", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Daniel"], "contexts": ["Biblical prophet"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Daniel", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Daniel", "similarity": 0.799}], "id": 5217, "stable_key": "clu_bc5f74d7bfb8e12d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Daniel", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Biblical prophet known for destroying a dragon", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}}, {"canonical_name": "figure", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "figure", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "figure", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["figure"], "contexts": ["shape or form of a substance"]}, {"entity_id": "figure", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "figure", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["figure"], "contexts": ["shape or form"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "figure", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "figure", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5218, "stable_key": "clu_9185e93a7ead5e8e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Form", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "In the context of early modern natural philosophy and science, 'figure' often referred to the geometrical shape or the physical form of a substance, distinct from its composition."}}, {"canonical_name": "Dio", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "dio", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Dio", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Dio"], "contexts": ["ancient source on glass malleability"]}, {"entity_id": "diosc_", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Diosc.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Diosc."], "contexts": ["ancient authority on plants"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Dio", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Diosc.", "similarity": 0.902}], "id": 5219, "stable_key": "clu_436167f729fd3013-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Dioscorides", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Ancient Greek authority on materia medica and medicinal plants.", "wikipedia_extract": "Pedanius Dioscorides (Ancient Greek: \u03a0\u03b5\u03b4\u03ac\u03bd\u03b9\u03bf\u03c2 \u0394\u03b9\u03bf\u03c3\u03ba\u03bf\u03c5\u03c1\u03af\u03b4\u03b7\u03c2, Ped\u00e1nios Dioskour\u00edd\u0113s; c. 40\u201390 AD),  \"the father of pharmacognosy\", was a Greek physician, pharmacologist, botanist, and author of De materia medica (in the original Ancient Greek: \u03a0\u03b5\u03c1\u1f76 \u1f55\u03bb\u03b7\u03c2 \u1f30\u03b1\u03c4\u03c1\u03b9\u03ba\u1fc6\u03c2, Peri hul\u0113s iatrik\u0113s, both meaning \"On Medical Material\"), a 5-volume Greek encyclopedic pharmacopeia on herbal medicine and related medicinal substances, that was widely read for more than 1,500 years. For almost two millennia Dioscorides was regarded as the most prominent writer on plants and plant drugs. A native of Anazarbus, Cilicia, Asia Minor, Dioscorides likely studied medicine nearby at the school in Tarsus, which had a pharmacological emphasis, and he dedicated his medical books to Laecanius Arius, a medical practitioner there. Though he writes he lived a \"soldier's life\" or \"soldier-like life\", his pharmacopeia refers almost solely to plants found in the Greek-speaking eastern Mediterranean, making it likely that he served in campaigns, or travelled in a civilian capacity, less widely than supposed. The name Pedanius is Roman, suggesting that an aristocrat of that name sponsored him to become a Roman citizen. Between 50 and 70 AD Dioscorides wrote a five-volume book in his native Greek, \u03a0\u03b5\u03c1\u1f76 \u1f55\u03bb\u03b7\u03c2 \u1f30\u03b1\u03c4\u03c1\u03b9\u03ba\u1fc6\u03c2 (Per\u00ec hyl\u0113s \u00edatrik\u0113s), known in Western Europe more often by its Latin title De materia medica (\"On Medical Material\"), which became the precursor to all modern pharmacopeias.", "birth_year": 40, "death_year": 90}}, {"canonical_name": "infusion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "infusion", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "infusion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["infusion"], "contexts": ["process of steeping a substance in liquid"]}, {"entity_id": "infusion", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Infusion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Infusion"], "contexts": ["method of making syrups"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "infusion", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Infusion", "similarity": 0.985}], "id": 5220, "stable_key": "clu_79bf8f683fd6cfc2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Infusion", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "description": "A method of preparing liquid medicines by steeping ingredients.", "note": "Includes Italian and English terms for the same medicinal practice.", "wikipedia_extract": "Infusion is the process of extracting chemical compounds or flavors from plant material in a solvent such as water, oil, or alcohol, by allowing the material to remain suspended in the solvent over time (a process often called steeping). An infusion is also the name for the resultant liquid. The process of infusion is distinct from both decoction\u2014a method of extraction involving boiling the plant material\u2014and percolation, in which water is passed through the material (as in a coffeemaker). The first recorded use of essential oils was in the 10th or 11th century by the Persian polymath Avicenna, possibly in The Canon of Medicine. Tea is far older than this, dating back to the 10th century BC as the earliest recorded reference. Infusion is a chemical process that uses botanicals (typically dried herbs, flowers or berries) that are volatile and release their active ingredients readily in water, oil, or alcohol."}}, {"canonical_name": "Alphonsus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "alphonsus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Alphonsus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Alphonsus"], "contexts": ["Duke of Ferrara"]}, {"entity_id": "alphonsus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Alphonsus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Alphonsus"], "contexts": ["King of Naples"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Alphonsus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Alphonsus", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5221, "stable_key": "clu_3c7ab0ae13532344", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Alfonso V of Naples", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1400, "death_year": 1458, "description": "King of Naples, patron of arts and sciences.", "note": "The cluster refers to an 'Alphonsus' who was either Duke of Ferrara or King of Naples. Alfonso V of Naples was a significant figure and patron, fitting the context of historical texts. Alfonso II d'Este, Duke of Ferrara, also existed, but Alfonso V of Naples is more commonly referenced in general historical and scientific discourse of the period."}}, {"canonical_name": "Egg-shells", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "PRODUCT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "egg-shells", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Egg-shells", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "PRODUCT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Egg-shells"], "contexts": ["ingredient for dishes"]}, {"entity_id": "egg-shells", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "egg-shells", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "PRODUCT", "count": 1, "variants": ["egg-shells"], "contexts": ["fragments preserved with bones"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Egg-shells", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "egg-shells", "similarity": 0.99}], "id": 5222, "stable_key": "clu_44a1fdf5277b7fbd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Eggshells", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal"}}, {"canonical_name": "Saphyr", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "saphyr", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Saphyr", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Saphyr"], "contexts": ["gem"]}, {"entity_id": "sapphirus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Sapphirus", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Sapphirus"], "contexts": ["blue stone with virtue against carbuncles"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Saphyr", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Sapphirus", "similarity": 0.915}], "id": 5223, "stable_key": "clu_b7f81552a7ac24d3-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sapphire", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q1767374", "wikidata_description": "butterfly genus in family Lycaenidae", "note": "The spelling 'Saphire' is an archaic variation."}}, {"canonical_name": "Thighs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "thighs", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Thighs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Thighs"], "contexts": ["leg parts"]}, {"entity_id": "thigh", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "thigh", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["thigh"], "contexts": ["upper part of the leg"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Thighs", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "thigh", "similarity": 0.913}], "id": 5224, "stable_key": "clu_06b686838f88d3fa", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Thigh", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q1260690", "wikidata_description": "thigh high stockings"}}, {"canonical_name": "Authors ancient or modern", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "authors_ancient_or_modern", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Authors ancient or modern", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Authors ancient or modern"], "contexts": ["sources of information"]}, {"entity_id": "antient_and_modern_writers", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Antient and Modern Writers", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Antient and Modern Writers"], "contexts": ["sources on stomach offensiveness of herb"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Authors ancient or modern", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Antient and Modern Writers", "similarity": 0.852}], "id": 5225, "stable_key": "clu_abf54dbc59e73af7-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Authors", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This cluster refers to the collective body of recognized authorities and writers in a particular field, not specific individuals.", "wikidata_id": "Q230768", "wikidata_description": "in art research, the process of determining the author of a creative work"}}, {"canonical_name": "Nubia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "nubia", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Nubia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Nubia"], "contexts": ["source of poison"]}, {"entity_id": "nubie", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Nubie", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Nubie"], "contexts": ["country with contrasting animal behaviors"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Nubia", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "Nubie", "similarity": 0.959}], "id": 5226, "stable_key": "clu_db1ae7c238737e42", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Nubia", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Egypt/Sudan", "wikidata_id": "Q135028", "wikidata_description": "region along the Nile river, which is located in northern Sudan and southern Egypt", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nubia", "wikipedia_extract": "Nubia ( NEW-bee-uh; Nobiin: Nob\u012bn; Arabic: \u0627\u0644\u0646\u064f\u0648\u0628\u064e\u0629, romanized: an-N\u016bba) is a region along the Nile river encompassing the area between the confluence of the Blue and White Niles (near Khartoum in central Sudan) and the First Cataract (south of Aswan in southern Egypt). It was the seat of one of the earliest civilizations of ancient Africa, the Kerma culture, which lasted from around 2500 BC until its conquest by the New Kingdom of Egypt under Pharaoh Thutmose I around 1500 BC. Egyptian heirs subsequently ruled much of Nubia for the next four centuries. Nubia was home to several empires, most prominently the Kingdom of Kush. This kingdom conquered Egypt in the eighth century BC under Piye, ruling as its 25th Dynasty. From the 3rd century BC to the 3rd century AD, northern Nubia was invaded and annexed by Egypt, then under the rule of the Greeks and later the Romans; this territory was known in the Greco-Roman world as the Dodekaschoinos."}}, {"canonical_name": "Thrushes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "thrushes", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Thrushes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["Thrushes"], "contexts": ["type of bird"]}, {"entity_id": "thrushes", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "thrushes", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["thrushes"], "contexts": ["diving birds"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Thrushes", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "thrushes", "similarity": 0.977}], "id": 5227, "stable_key": "clu_acf171e0cf33b5cb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Thrush", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikipedia_extract": "The song thrush (Turdus philomelos) is a thrush that breeds across the West Palearctic. It has brown upperparts and black-spotted cream or buff underparts and has four recognised subspecies. Its distinctive song, which features repeated musical phrases, has frequently been referenced in poetry. The song thrush breeds in forests, gardens and parks. It is partially migratory, with many birds wintering in southern Europe, North Africa and the Middle East. The species has also been introduced into New Zealand and Australia.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Song_thrush"}}, {"canonical_name": "boughs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "boughs", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "boughs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["boughs"], "contexts": ["branches of a tree"]}, {"entity_id": "boughs", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "boughs", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["boughs"], "contexts": ["large branches of a tree"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "boughs", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "boughs", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5228, "stable_key": "clu_a615b942e7de4aee", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Boughs", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "note": "While 'boughs' are parts of plants, they are too general to specify a Linnaean binomial or family without further context. The contexts suggest general tree branches.", "wikidata_id": "Q2923673", "wikidata_description": "part of a tree; a shoot axis that develops from an axillary bud meristem or from equal divisions of a meristematic apical cell"}}, {"canonical_name": "parasitical Plants", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "parasitical_plants", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "parasitical Plants", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["parasitical Plants"], "contexts": ["plants that live on others"]}, {"entity_id": "parasitical_plants", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "parasitical plants", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["parasitical plants"], "contexts": ["plants that live on other plants"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "parasitical Plants", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "parasitical plants", "similarity": 0.993}], "id": 5229, "stable_key": "clu_ca25a9d702e8a9a9", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Parasitic plants", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "wikidata_id": "Q127498", "wikidata_description": "type of plant that derives some or all of its nutritional requirements from another living plant", "wikipedia_extract": "Plant diseases are diseases in plants caused by pathogens (infectious organisms) and environmental conditions (physiological factors). Organisms that cause infectious disease include fungi, oomycetes, bacteria, viruses, viroids, virus-like organisms, phytoplasmas, protozoa, nematodes and parasitic plants. Not included are ectoparasites like insects, mites, vertebrates, or other pests that affect plant health by eating plant tissues and causing injury that may admit plant pathogens. The study of plant disease is called plant pathology. Most phytopathogenic fungi are Ascomycetes or Basidiomycetes. They reproduce both sexually and asexually via the production of spores and other structures.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parasitic_plant"}}, {"canonical_name": "Furz", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "furz", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Furz", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Furz"], "contexts": ["plant that flowers in winter"]}, {"entity_id": "furze", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Furze", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Furze"], "contexts": ["plant mentioned in text"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Furz", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Furze", "similarity": 0.93}], "id": 5230, "stable_key": "clu_084e06b243619efe", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Gorse", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Ulex europaeus", "family": "Fabaceae", "note": "Both 'Furz' and 'Furze' are common historical names for Gorse, specifically Ulex europaeus.", "wikidata_id": "Q393278", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants"}}, {"canonical_name": "ventricles", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "ventricles", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "ventricles", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["ventricles"], "contexts": ["where spirits insinuate"]}, {"entity_id": "ventricles", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Ventricles", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ventricles"], "contexts": ["cavities within the body, likely heart or brain"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "ventricles", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Ventricles", "similarity": 0.756}], "id": 5231, "stable_key": "clu_1299beb8c8713e53", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ventricles (brain/heart)", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Early modern texts used 'ventricles' to refer to cavities in both the brain and the heart. Context is key for precise identification."}}, {"canonical_name": "Microscopes", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "microscopes", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Microscopes", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Microscopes"], "contexts": ["instruments for viewing microscopic objects"]}, {"entity_id": "microscopes", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "microscopes", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 1, "variants": ["microscopes"], "contexts": ["instruments for viewing small objects"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Microscopes", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "microscopes", "similarity": 0.993}], "id": 5232, "stable_key": "clu_36787e7194fabdf5-2", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "A microscope (from Ancient Greek  \u03bc\u03b9\u03ba\u03c1\u03cc\u03c2 (mikr\u00f3s) 'small' and  \u03c3\u03ba\u03bf\u03c0\u03ad\u03c9 (skop\u00e9\u014d) 'to look (at); examine, inspect') is a laboratory instrument used to examine objects that are too small to be seen by the naked eye. Microscopy is the science of investigating small objects and structures using a microscope. Microscopic means being invisible to the eye unless aided by a microscope. There are many types of microscopes, and they may be grouped in different ways. One way is to describe the method an instrument uses to interact with a sample and produce images, either by sending a beam of light or electrons through a sample in its optical path, by detecting photon emissions from a sample, or by scanning across and a short distance from the surface of a sample using a probe. The most common microscope (and the first to be invented) is the optical microscope, which uses lenses to refract visible light that passed through a thinly sectioned sample to produce an observable image."}}, {"canonical_name": "Johannes Faber", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "johannes_faber", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Johannes Faber", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Johannes Faber"], "contexts": ["scholar who made draughts of seeds"]}, {"entity_id": "petrus_joannes_faber", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Petrus Joannes Faber", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Petrus Joannes Faber"], "contexts": ["author of 'Univer\u017falis Sapientia'"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Johannes Faber", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Petrus Joannes Faber", "similarity": 0.85}], "id": 5233, "stable_key": "clu_ab0f2fbb0022b1b0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Joannes Faber", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1574, "death_year": 1629, "description": "Physician and botanist, known for botanical illustrations.", "wikidata_id": "Q41569344", "wikidata_description": "French jurist-consult (m. 1340?)"}}, {"canonical_name": "Liverwort", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "HERB", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "liverwort", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Liverwort", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "HERB", "count": 1, "variants": ["Liverwort"], "contexts": ["plant with trifoliate leaves"]}, {"entity_id": "liverwort", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Liverwort", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Liverwort"], "contexts": ["plant growing in moist places"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Liverwort", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Liverwort", "similarity": 0.795}], "id": 5234, "stable_key": "clu_e1339fce63ab92e3-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "liverwort", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Marchantiopsida", "family": "Marchantiaceae", "note": "The early modern term 'liverwort' encompasses various plants now classified under different orders and families within the division Bryophyta. Marchantiopsida is a broad class that includes the most common forms referred to by this name historically. The specific family is harder to pinpoint without more context, but Marchantiaceae is a representative example.", "wikidata_id": "Q161050", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anemone%20hepatica", "wikipedia_extract": "Marchantiopsida is a class of liverworts within the phylum Marchantiophyta. The species in this class are known as complex thalloid liverworts. The species in this class are widely distributed and can be found worldwide. Complex oil bodies are only found in the gametophyte. Marchantiidae Engl. 1893 sensu He-Nygr\u00e9n et al. 2006 Marchantiales Limpr. 1877 (complex thalloids) Aytoniaceae Cavers 1911  [Rebouliaceae; Grimaldiaceae] Conocephalaceae M\u00fcll.Frib. ex Grolle 1972 Cyathodiaceae Stotler & Crand.-Stotl. 2000 Exormothecaceae M\u00fcll.Frib.", "description": "A small, non-vascular plant often used for liver ailments."}}, {"canonical_name": "sore Throats", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "sore_throats", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "sore Throats", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 1, "variants": ["sore Throats"], "contexts": ["condition treated by Jews ear"]}, {"entity_id": "sore_throat", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "sore throat", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["sore throat"], "contexts": ["pain or irritation in the throat"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "sore Throats", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "sore throat", "similarity": 0.886}], "id": 5235, "stable_key": "clu_f5a536ade3bcda9d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "sore throat", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "pharyngitis", "wikidata_id": "Q1292082", "wikidata_description": "Pain of the throat", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sore_throat", "wikipedia_extract": "Tonsillectomy is a surgical procedure in which both palatine tonsils are fully removed from the back of the throat. The procedure is mainly performed for recurrent tonsillitis, throat infections and obstructive sleep apnea (OSA). For those with frequent throat infections, surgery results in 0.6 (95% confidence interval: 1.0 to 0.1) fewer sore throats in the following year, but there is no evidence of long term benefits. In children with OSA, it results in improved quality of life. While generally safe, complications may include bleeding, vomiting, dehydration, trouble eating, and trouble talking. Throat pain typically lasts about one to two weeks after surgery."}}, {"canonical_name": "Hollerius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "hollerius", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Hollerius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hollerius"], "contexts": ["scholar who advanced a theory"]}, {"entity_id": "hollerius", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Hollerius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hollerius"], "contexts": ["French physician on scorpion in brain"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Hollerius", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Hollerius", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5236, "stable_key": "clu_da9da0c17c54d56c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Guillaume de Hollerius", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1500, "death_year": 1577, "description": "French physician and anatomist, known for his anatomical works."}}, {"canonical_name": "Anatomia Sambuci", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "anatomia_sambuci", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Anatomia Sambuci", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Anatomia Sambuci"], "contexts": ["work by Galen"]}, {"entity_id": "anatomiasambuci", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Anatomiasambuci", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Anatomiasambuci"], "contexts": ["anatomy of elder tree"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Anatomia Sambuci", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Anatomiasambuci", "similarity": 0.844}], "id": 5237, "stable_key": "clu_8d77965027c5f41d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Anatomia Sambuci", "confidence": "high", "type": "text", "note": "This refers to an anatomical work, likely on plants, not Galen's anatomy."}}, {"canonical_name": "Yew", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "yew", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Yew", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Yew"], "contexts": ["plant and its berries are known to be harmless"]}, {"entity_id": "yews", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "yews", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 1, "variants": ["yews"], "contexts": ["tree species in ancient forests"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Yew", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "yews", "similarity": 0.905}], "id": 5238, "stable_key": "clu_6d1cfc92ee7f7982", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Yew", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Taxus baccata Linnaeus", "family": "Taxaceae", "note": "The note about berries being 'harmless' is historically inaccurate for *Taxus baccata*, as the seeds within the arils are toxic. This reflects early modern knowledge, not the modern understanding of toxicity.", "wikidata_id": "Q8053327", "wikidata_description": "electoral ward of Liverpool City Council", "wikipedia_extract": "Taxus is a genus of coniferous trees or shrubs known as yews in the family Taxaceae. Yews occur around the globe in temperate zones of the northern hemisphere, northernmost in Norway and southernmost in the South Celebes. Some populations exist in tropical highlands. The oldest known fossil species are from the Early Cretaceous. They are relatively slow-growing and can be very long-lived, and reach heights of 2.5\u201320 m (8.2\u201365.6 ft), with trunk girth averaging 5 m (16 ft). They have reddish bark, lanceolate, flat, dark-green leaves 10\u201340 mm (1\u20442\u20131+1\u20442 in) long and 2\u20133 mm (3\u204432\u20131\u20448 in) broad, arranged spirally on the stem, but with the leaf bases twisted to align the leaves in two flat rows either side of the stem.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taxus"}}, {"canonical_name": "Snake", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "REPTILE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "snake", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Snake", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "REPTILE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Snake"], "contexts": ["animal that avoids Ash shade"]}, {"entity_id": "snake", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "snake", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["snake"], "contexts": ["venomous creature"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Snake", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "snake", "similarity": 0.889}], "id": 5239, "stable_key": "clu_e587ddc8978ff591", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Spiders (order Araneae) are air-breathing arthropods that have eight limbs, chelicerae with fangs generally able to inject venom, and spinnerets that extrude silk. They are the largest order of arachnids and rank seventh in total species diversity among all orders of organisms. Spiders are found worldwide on every continent except Antarctica, and have become established in nearly every land habitat. As of January 2026, 53,680 spider species in 139 families have been recorded by taxonomists. However, there has been debate among scientists about how families should be classified, with over 20 different classifications proposed since 1900. Anatomically, spiders (as with all arachnids) differ from other arthropods in that the usual body segments are fused into two tagmata, the cephalothorax or prosoma, and the opisthosoma, or abdomen, and joined by a small, cylindrical pedicel."}}, {"canonical_name": "progression", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "progression", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "progression", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["progression"], "contexts": ["movement or advancement"]}, {"entity_id": "progression", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "progression", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["progression"], "contexts": ["abstract concept in spiritualist philosophy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "progression", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "progression", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5240, "stable_key": "clu_b0ef8f5eeef2f5df", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "progression", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q12559209", "wikidata_description": "sequence in which the next element is derived from the previous element by some rule"}}, {"canonical_name": "Fins", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "fins", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Fins", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Fins"], "contexts": ["appendages for swimming"]}, {"entity_id": "fin", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "fin", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["fin"], "contexts": ["aquatic limb"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Fins", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "fin", "similarity": 0.917}], "id": 5241, "stable_key": "clu_0ffa520d007a839f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "fin", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal"}}, {"canonical_name": "musculous parts", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "musculous_parts", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "musculous parts", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["musculous parts"], "contexts": ["tissue composed of muscles"]}, {"entity_id": "muscular_tissues", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "muscular tissues", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["muscular tissues"], "contexts": ["tissue involved in molecular movements"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "musculous parts", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "muscular tissues", "similarity": 0.954}], "id": 5242, "stable_key": "clu_df61ee2407374878", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "muscle tissue", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "Titius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "titius", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Titius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Titius"], "contexts": ["mythological figure punished in Hades"]}, {"entity_id": "titus", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Titus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Titus"], "contexts": ["person associated with a house"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Titius", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Titus", "similarity": 0.882}], "id": 5243, "stable_key": "clu_b17a93101e9cae0e-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Titus", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 39, "death_year": 81, "description": "Roman Emperor, known for the Flavian dynasty.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "note": "The identification of 'Titius' and 'Titus' is ambiguous. Titus is a known historical figure and a common Roman name. Titius could refer to a lesser-known figure or be an error."}}, {"canonical_name": "Galba", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "galba", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Galba", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Galba"], "contexts": ["Roman Emperor"]}, {"entity_id": "galba", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Galba", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Galba"], "contexts": ["associated with an emplastrum"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Galba", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Galba", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5244, "stable_key": "clu_429df16947c777f2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Galba", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 3, "death_year": 69, "description": "Roman Emperor, associated with a plaster.", "wikidata_id": "Q3075506", "wikidata_description": "genus of molluscs"}}, {"canonical_name": "Curtius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "curtius", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Curtius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Curtius"], "contexts": ["ancient historian"]}, {"entity_id": "curtius", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Curtius", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Curtius"], "contexts": ["professor with notes on spring temperatures"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Curtius", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Curtius", "similarity": 0.746}], "id": 5245, "stable_key": "clu_179753ced54cd082", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Quintus Curtius Rufus", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Ancient historian and possibly a professor.", "note": "While 'Curtius' could refer to other historical figures, Quintus Curtius Rufus is the most likely ancient historian encountered in early modern texts.", "wikidata_id": "Q5959", "wikidata_description": "Roman historian"}}, {"canonical_name": "Henry the third", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "henry_the_third", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Henry the third", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Henry the third"], "contexts": ["received an elephant from Lewis"]}, {"entity_id": "henrique_iii,_de_inglaterra", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Henrique III, de Inglaterra", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Henrique III, de Inglaterra"], "contexts": ["English monarch"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Henry the third", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Henrique III, de Inglaterra", "similarity": 0.864}], "id": 5246, "stable_key": "clu_2080186ed81a85e4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Henry III of England", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1207, "death_year": 1272, "description": "King of England who received an elephant.", "wikidata_id": "Q160311", "wikidata_description": "King of England from 1216 to 1272"}}, {"canonical_name": "Emanuel", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "emanuel", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Emanuel", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Emanuel"], "contexts": ["of Portugal, sent an elephant"]}, {"entity_id": "emanuel", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Emanuel", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Emanuel"], "contexts": ["king whose chronicle was published"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Emanuel", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Emanuel", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5247, "stable_key": "clu_fab955094869351f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Manuel I of Portugal", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1469, "death_year": 1521, "description": "King of Portugal who sent an elephant; his chronicle was published.", "note": "The text refers to 'Emanuel' of Portugal and a king whose chronicle was published. Manuel I fits both descriptions and was a prominent ruler in the period.", "wikidata_id": "Q191231", "wikidata_description": "King of Portugal from 1495 to 1521", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manuel%20I%20of%20Portugal", "wikipedia_extract": "Manuel I (European Portuguese: [m\u0250nu\u02c8\u025bl]; 31 May 1469 \u2013 13 December 1521), known as the Fortunate (Portuguese: O Venturoso), was King of Portugal from 1495 to 1521. A member of the House of Aviz, Manuel was Duke of Beja and Viseu prior to succeeding his cousin, John II of Portugal, as monarch. Manuel ruled over a period of intensive expansion of the Portuguese Empire owing to the numerous Portuguese discoveries made during his reign. His sponsorship of Vasco da Gama led to the Portuguese discovery of the sea route to India in 1498, resulting in the creation of the Portuguese India Armadas, which guaranteed Portugal's monopoly on the spice trade. Manuel began the Portuguese colonization of the Americas and Portuguese India, and oversaw the establishment of a vast trade empire across Africa and Asia. Manuel established the Casa da \u00cdndia, a royal institution that managed Portugal's monopolies and its imperial expansion."}}, {"canonical_name": "clavicles", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "clavicles", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "clavicles", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["clavicles"], "contexts": ["collar bones"]}, {"entity_id": "claviculas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Claviculas", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Claviculas"], "contexts": ["collarbones"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "clavicles", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Claviculas", "similarity": 0.905}], "id": 5248, "stable_key": "clu_1da48588ac1ff04c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Clavicle", "confidence": "high", "type": "anatomy", "wikidata_id": "Q1746068", "wikidata_description": "medical condition"}}, {"canonical_name": "brests", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "brests", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "brests", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["brests"], "contexts": ["chest area"]}, {"entity_id": "brests", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Brests", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Brests"], "contexts": ["female anatomy for milk production"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "brests", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Brests", "similarity": 0.896}], "id": 5249, "stable_key": "clu_e444146ac2b866ef-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Breast", "confidence": "high", "type": "anatomy", "wikidata_id": "Q9103", "wikidata_description": "one of two prominences located on the region of the torso of a female primate, containing the mammary gland"}}, {"canonical_name": "Hugo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "hugo", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Hugo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hugo"], "contexts": ["author consulted on elephant venation"]}, {"entity_id": "hugo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Hugo", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hugo"], "contexts": ["authority cited for prudent reader advice"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Hugo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Hugo", "similarity": 0.712}], "id": 5250, "stable_key": "clu_30f603c459770d75", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hugh of Saint Victor", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1096, "death_year": 1141, "description": "Theologian and philosopher, consulted on nature and prudence.", "note": "The specific 'Hugo' referred to is ambiguous, but Hugh of Saint Victor is a likely candidate for an author cited for prudent advice in philosophical and theological contexts."}}, {"canonical_name": "Witches", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "witches", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Witches", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Witches"], "contexts": ["people associated with speaking animals"]}, {"entity_id": "witch", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Witch", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Witch"], "contexts": ["evil agent"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Witches", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Witch", "similarity": 0.919}], "id": 5251, "stable_key": "clu_b395e51d3eb6985e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Witches", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Individuals accused of practicing magic and associated with supernatural phenomena."}}, {"canonical_name": "membranous substance", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "membranous_substance", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "membranous substance", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["membranous substance"], "contexts": ["thin, skin-like material"]}, {"entity_id": "membrane", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "membrane", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["membrane"], "contexts": ["rudiments of wings"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "membranous substance", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "membrane", "similarity": 0.924}], "id": 5252, "stable_key": "clu_649fab5a90eef467-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Membranes", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "While 'membrane' can refer to anatomical structures, in this context, especially with 'embryonic development', it refers to the conceptual biological structure rather than a specific anatomical part.", "wikidata_id": "Q226697", "wikidata_description": "animal skin processed for writing or painting on", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parchment", "wikipedia_extract": "Parchment is a writing material made from specially prepared untanned skins of animals\u2014primarily sheep, calves and goats. It has been used as a writing medium in West Asia and Europe for more than two millennia. By 400 AD many of the written works intended for preservation in these regions had been transferred from papyrus to parchment. Vellum is a type of fine-quality parchment made from the skins of young animals such as lambs and young calves. The generic term animal membrane is sometimes used by libraries and museums that wish to avoid distinguishing between parchment and vellum. Today the term parchment is often used in non-technical contexts to refer to any animal skin, particularly goat, sheep or cow, that has been scraped or dried under tension."}}, {"canonical_name": "lobes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "lobes", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "lobes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["lobes"], "contexts": ["divisions of the liver"]}, {"entity_id": "lobes", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "lobes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["lobes"], "contexts": ["major divisions of the cerebrum"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "lobes", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "lobes", "similarity": 0.769}], "id": 5253, "stable_key": "clu_2d98b6af84da6ef8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lobe", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to anatomical divisions within organs, such as the liver or brain.", "wikidata_id": "Q158182", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants"}}, {"canonical_name": "bowels", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "bowels", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "bowels", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["bowels"], "contexts": ["intestines"]}, {"entity_id": "bowels", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "bowels", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["bowels"], "contexts": ["process resembles motion down them"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "bowels", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "bowels", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5254, "stable_key": "clu_09b1984512807aa6-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Intestines", "confidence": "high", "type": "anatomy", "description": "The part of the digestive system in animals.", "note": "Refers to the anatomical organs.", "wikipedia_extract": "The gastrointestinal tract (also called the GI tract, digestive tract, and the alimentary canal) is the tract or passageway of the digestive system that leads from the mouth to the anus. The tract is one of the largest of the body's systems. The GI tract contains all the major organs of the digestive system, in humans and other animals, including the esophagus, stomach, and intestines. Food taken in through the mouth is digested to extract nutrients and absorb energy, and the waste expelled at the anus as feces. Gastrointestinal is an adjective meaning of or pertaining to the stomach and intestines. Most animals have a \"through-gut\" or complete digestive tract.", "wikidata_id": "Q9639", "wikidata_description": "alimentary canal of an animal through which food passes after having passed all stomachs"}}, {"canonical_name": "vesicle", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "vesicle", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "vesicle", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["vesicle"], "contexts": ["small sac, like gall bladder"]}, {"entity_id": "vesicular_cavities", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "vesicular cavities", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["vesicular cavities"], "contexts": ["hollow spaces in meteorite structure"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "vesicle", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "vesicular cavities", "similarity": 0.889}], "id": 5255, "stable_key": "clu_293284c12ab18899-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Vesicle", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "A small, fluid-filled sac or cavity, applicable in both biological (e.g., gall bladder) and geological contexts.", "wikidata_id": "Q14860403", "wikidata_description": "cellular transport process in which transported substances are moved in membrane-bounded vesicles"}}, {"canonical_name": "Pheasants", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "pheasants", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Pheasants", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["Pheasants"], "contexts": ["game birds"]}, {"entity_id": "pheasants", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "pheasants", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "BIRD", "count": 1, "variants": ["pheasants"], "contexts": ["game birds"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Pheasants", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "pheasants", "similarity": 0.924}], "id": 5256, "stable_key": "clu_b041af08858d8355", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pheasant", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q25432", "wikidata_description": "species of bird"}}, {"canonical_name": "CHAPTER IV", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "chapter_iv", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "CHAPTER IV", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["CHAPTER IV"], "contexts": ["section heading"]}, {"entity_id": "chapter_iii", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "CHAPTER III", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["CHAPTER III"], "contexts": ["section title"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "CHAPTER IV", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "CHAPTER III", "similarity": 0.903}], "id": 5257, "stable_key": "clu_72eabd277f5d2981", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Chapter heading", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This refers to a structural element of a written work, not a concept in natural philosophy or medicine itself."}}, {"canonical_name": "Ancient Writers", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "ancient_writers", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Ancient Writers", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ancient Writers"], "contexts": ["historical authors of antiquity"]}, {"entity_id": "ancient_author", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "ancient author", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["ancient author"], "contexts": ["writer from antiquity"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Ancient Writers", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "ancient author", "similarity": 0.903}], "id": 5258, "stable_key": "clu_f0b788330a0dc432", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ancient Authors", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to classical writers whose works were cited."}}, {"canonical_name": "Cyrus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "cyrus", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Cyrus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cyrus"], "contexts": ["Persian monarch"]}, {"entity_id": "cyrus", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Cyrus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cyrus"], "contexts": ["historical figure"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Cyrus", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Cyrus", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5259, "stable_key": "clu_b75e82d64c3a3c04", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cyrus", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "death_year": -530, "description": "Founder of the Achaemenid Empire, also known as Cyrus the Great."}}, {"canonical_name": "Male", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "male", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Male", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Male"], "contexts": ["sex with protuberant cods"]}, {"entity_id": "male_sex", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "male sex", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["male sex"], "contexts": ["biological classification of individuals"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Male", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "male sex", "similarity": 0.919}], "id": 5260, "stable_key": "clu_82b1e1573626b441-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Human male", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The cluster encompasses discussions of 'man', 'Male', and 'male' in the context of biological sex and the human species.", "wikidata_id": "Q6581097", "wikidata_description": "to be used in \"sex or gender\" (P21) to indicate that the human subject is a male or \"semantic gender\" (P10339) to indicate that a word refers to a male person"}}, {"canonical_name": "Lobster", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "lobster", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Lobster", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lobster"], "contexts": ["crustacean with a large claw"]}, {"entity_id": "lobster", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "lobster", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["lobster"], "contexts": ["example of longitudinal union in Vertebrata"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Lobster", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "lobster", "similarity": 0.994}], "id": 5261, "stable_key": "clu_fd4cc8f24766f74b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "lobster", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikipedia_extract": "The American lobster (Homarus americanus) is a species of lobster found on the Atlantic coast of North America, chiefly from Labrador to New Jersey. It is also known as Atlantic lobster, Canadian lobster, true lobster, northern lobster, Canadian Reds, or Maine lobster. It can reach a body length of 64 cm (25 in), and a mass of over 20 kilograms (44 lb), making it not only the heaviest crustacean in the world, but also the heaviest of all living arthropod species. Its closest relative is the European lobster Homarus gammarus, which can be distinguished by its coloration and the lack of spines on the underside of the rostrum. American lobsters are usually bluish green to brown with red spines, but several color variations have been observed. Homarus americanus is distributed along the Atlantic coast of North America, from Labrador in the north to Cape Hatteras, North Carolina, in the south.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_lobster"}}, {"canonical_name": "Trent", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "trent", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Trent", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Trent"], "contexts": ["city near Anania valley"]}, {"entity_id": "trent", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Trent", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Trent"], "contexts": ["seat of Cardinal Prince"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Trent", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Trent", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5262, "stable_key": "clu_52eea54176346c84", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Trent", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Italy"}}, {"canonical_name": "Julius Scaliger", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "julius_scaliger", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Julius Scaliger", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Julius Scaliger"], "contexts": ["scholar writing on bear reproduction"]}, {"entity_id": "joseph_scaliger", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Joseph Scaliger", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Joseph Scaliger"], "contexts": ["learned scholar engrossed in Homer"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Julius Scaliger", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Joseph Scaliger", "similarity": 0.954}], "id": 5263, "stable_key": "clu_b695b9595f229f6b-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Julius Caesar Scaliger", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1484, "death_year": 1558, "description": "Italian physician, botanist, poet, and literary critic.", "note": "The cluster includes both Julius Scaliger and Joseph Scaliger. Julius Caesar Scaliger is the most prominent of the two for scholarship in the relevant period and context.", "wikidata_id": "Q441066", "wikidata_description": "Italian scholar, physician and philosopher (1484-1558)", "wikipedia_extract": "Julius Caesar Scaliger (; 23 April  or August 1484 \u2013 21 October 1558), or Giulio Cesare della Scala, was an Italian scholar and physician, who spent a major part of his career in France. He employed the techniques and discoveries of Renaissance humanism to defend Aristotelianism against the New Learning. In spite of his contentious disposition, his contemporary reputation was high. Jacques Auguste de Thou claimed that none of the ancients could be placed above him and that he had no equal in his own time. Scaliger's father, Benedetto Bordone, was a miniaturist and illuminator. Scaliger himself was known in his youth by the family name Bordone, but later insisted that he was a scion of the house of La Scala, for a hundred and fifty years lords of Verona."}}, {"canonical_name": "Senate", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "senate", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Senate", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Senate"], "contexts": ["governing body in Bononia"]}, {"entity_id": "senate", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Senate", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Senate"], "contexts": ["legislative body"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Senate", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Senate", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5264, "stable_key": "clu_9d63d9d5546a14d3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Senate", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'Senate' refers to a deliberative or legislative body, often in historical or governmental contexts."}}, {"canonical_name": "Vulgate", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "vulgate", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Vulgate", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Vulgate"], "contexts": ["Latin translation of the Bible"]}, {"entity_id": "vulgata", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Vulgata", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Vulgata"], "contexts": ["Latin translation of the Bible"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Vulgate", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Vulgata", "similarity": 0.904}], "id": 5265, "stable_key": "clu_210d20a8cc77539d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Vulgate Bible", "confidence": "high", "type": "text", "note": "Refers to the Latin translation of the Bible.", "wikidata_id": "Q7533226", "wikidata_description": "the official Catholic edition of the Latin Vulgate published in 1592 (2nd ed. in 1593, 3rd ed. in 1598) under Pope Clement VIII"}}, {"canonical_name": "Reception", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "reception", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Reception", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Reception"], "contexts": ["theory of sight by reception"]}, {"entity_id": "reception", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Reception", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Reception"], "contexts": ["astrological aspect between Sun and Moon"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Reception", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Reception", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5266, "stable_key": "clu_fe71a22bd22bdb2c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Reception Theory", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The context mentions 'theory of sight by reception' and 'astrological aspect', indicating it refers to a concept of how something is received, whether in optics or astrology.", "wikidata_id": "Q3067311", "wikidata_description": "theory"}}, {"canonical_name": "Poetical", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "poetical", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Poetical", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Poetical"], "contexts": ["origin of a conceit about lifespan"]}, {"entity_id": "poefia", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Poefia", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Poefia"], "contexts": ["art not necessary for healing"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Poetical", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Poefia", "similarity": 0.916}], "id": 5267, "stable_key": "clu_ba008bcf7e3cb85f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "poetry", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "While one term is in Italian ('Poefia'), the context strongly indicates the literary art form of poetry.", "wikidata_id": "Q12106333", "wikidata_description": "collection of poems by a single author published together. See also poetry anthology (Q19357149)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poetry%20collection", "wikipedia_extract": "Poetry (from the Greek word poiesis, \"making\") is a form of literary art that uses aesthetic and often rhythmic qualities of language to evoke meanings in addition to, or in place of, literal or surface-level meanings. Any particular instance of poetry is called a poem and is written by a poet. Poets use a variety of techniques called poetic devices, such as assonance, alliteration, consonance, euphony and cacophony, onomatopoeia, rhythm (via metre), rhyme schemes (patterns in the type and placement of a phoneme group) and sound symbolism, to produce musical or other artistic effects. They also frequently organize these devices into poetic structures, which may be strict or loose, conventional or invented by the poet. Poetic structures vary dramatically by language and cultural convention, but they often rely on rhythmic metre: patterns of syllable stress or syllable (or mora) weight. They may also use repeating patterns of phonemes, phoneme groups, tones, words, or entire phrases."}}, {"canonical_name": "Mules", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "mules", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Mules", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mules"], "contexts": ["animal discussed for gall presence"]}, {"entity_id": "mules", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Mules", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mules"], "contexts": ["striped hybrid animals"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Mules", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Mules", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5268, "stable_key": "clu_118bedc406973fc1-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "mule", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "description": "Hybrid offspring of a male donkey and a female horse.", "wikipedia_extract": "The mule is a domestic equine hybrid between a donkey and a horse. It is the offspring of a male donkey (a jack) and a female horse (a mare). The horse and the donkey are different species, with different numbers of chromosomes; of the two possible first-generation hybrids between them, the mule is easier to obtain and more common than the hinny, which is the offspring of a male horse (a stallion) and a female donkey (a jenny). Mules vary widely in size, and may be of any color seen in horses or donkeys. They are more patient, hardier and longer-lived than horses, and are perceived as less obstinate and more intelligent than donkeys. A female mule is called a molly, a Molly mule, or more formally mare mule.", "wikidata_description": "family name"}}, {"canonical_name": "Glow-worms", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "glow-worms", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Glow-worms", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Glow-worms"], "contexts": ["insects that emit light when alive"]}, {"entity_id": "glow-worm", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "glow-worm", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["glow-worm"], "contexts": ["insect with pale light"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Glow-worms", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "glow-worm", "similarity": 0.918}], "id": 5269, "stable_key": "clu_21b576c5593258b4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Glow-worm", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q3248829", "wikidata_description": "common name for various groups of insect larvae and adult larviform females that glow through bioluminescence", "wikipedia_extract": "Ungoliant photophaga, also known as the glow-worm hunter, is a species of long-legged harvestman in the family Neopilionidae. This species is endemic to New Zealand, found in North Island caves in the vicinity of Waitomo. The name \"photophaga\" comes from their habit of feeding on the luminescent larvae, pupae, and adults of the New Zealand glow-worm Arachnocampa luminosa. Until 2025, it was recovered in the genus Forsteropsalis. Both Aola Richards and Meyer-Rochow and Liddle had previously recorded a species of harvestman in caves at Waitomo feeding on glow-worms which they identified as Megalopsalis tumida (now considered a synonym of Ungoliant fabulosa). Further examination of specimens from the cave revealed this harvestman was in fact two new species of Forsteropsalis.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glowworm"}}, {"canonical_name": "mistion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "mistion", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "mistion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["mistion"], "contexts": ["process of mixing or combining"]}, {"entity_id": "mission", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "mission", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["mission"], "contexts": ["governed by missionary monks"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "mistion", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "mission", "similarity": 0.961}], "id": 5270, "stable_key": "clu_e012cc211dfa5fee", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mixture", "confidence": "medium", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'mistion' likely refers to 'mixture' in a general or alchemical sense. One mention of 'mission' might be a transcription error or refer to a religious context, but given the other cluster member, 'mixture' is the most plausible shared concept.", "wikidata_id": "Q169336", "wikidata_description": "substance formed when two or more constituents are physically combined together"}}, {"canonical_name": "sph\u00e9ro\u00efde", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "sph\u00e9ro\u00efde", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "sph\u00e9ro\u00efde", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["sph\u00e9ro\u