1a0d1e306", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "ages", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q14878485", "wikidata_description": "genus of insects"}}, {"canonical_name": "lapides", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "lapides", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "lapides", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["lapides"], "contexts": ["stones"]}, {"entity_id": "lapides", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "lapides", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["lapides"], "contexts": ["stones falling from the sky"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "lapides", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "lapides", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5279, "stable_key": "clu_fe710bae67c978e5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "stones", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "Refers to both geological stones and meteorites ('stones falling from the sky').", "wikidata_id": "Q12803555", "wikidata_description": "family name"}}, {"canonical_name": "quartzite", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "quartzite", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "quartzite", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["quartzite"], "contexts": ["metamorphosed siliceous rock"]}, {"entity_id": "quartzose", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "quartzose", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["quartzose"], "contexts": ["type of rock"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "quartzite", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "quartzose", "similarity": 0.947}], "id": 5280, "stable_key": "clu_8f456b48926d6796-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Quartz", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q43010", "wikidata_description": "mineral made of silicon and oxygen", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quartz", "note": "The term 'quarz' is a variant spelling. 'quartzose' refers to material composed of quartz.", "wikipedia_extract": "Quartz is a hard mineral composed of silica (silicon dioxide). Its atoms are linked in a continuous framework of SiO4 silicon\u2013oxygen tetrahedra, with each oxygen atom being shared between two tetrahedra, giving an overall chemical formula of SiO2. Therefore, quartz is classified structurally as a framework silicate mineral and compositionally as an oxide mineral. Quartz is the second most common mineral or mineral group in Earth's lithosphere, comprising about 12% by mass. Quartz exists in two forms, the normal \u03b1-quartz and the high-temperature \u03b2-quartz, both of which are chiral. The transformation from \u03b1-quartz to \u03b2-quartz takes place abruptly at 573 \u00b0C (846 K; 1,063 \u00b0F)."}}, {"canonical_name": "Atlantique", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "atlantique", "book_id": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "name": "Atlantique", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Atlantique"], "contexts": ["ocean"]}, {"entity_id": "l'oc\u00e9an_atlantique", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "l'Oc\u00e9an Atlantique", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["l'Oc\u00e9an Atlantique"], "contexts": ["ocean separating continents, appears as narrow canal"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "epoques_nature_buffon_1778", "source_name": "Atlantique", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "l'Oc\u00e9an Atlantique", "similarity": 0.964}], "id": 5281, "stable_key": "clu_c1c6479b638f0b7f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Atlantic Ocean", "confidence": 