 "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["dolores de junturas fr\u00edos"], "contexts": ["cold joint pains cured by herb"]}, {"entity_id": "frios_dolorificos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "frios dolorificos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["frios dolorificos"], "contexts": ["symptom of excess acidity of pancreatic juice"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "dolores de junturas fr\u00edos", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "frios dolorificos", "similarity": 0.938}], "id": 5516, "stable_key": "clu_2e1ee4228b495452-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cold sweats", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "A symptom characterized by cold, clammy perspiration, often associated with illness.", "note": "This cluster includes terms referring to cold pains and cold sweats in Spanish and English.", "wikipedia_extract": "Sweating sickness, also known as the sweats, English sweating sickness, English sweat or sudor anglicus in Latin, was a mysterious and contagious disease that struck England and later continental Europe in a series of epidemics beginning in 1485. Other major outbreaks of the English sweating sickness occurred in 1508, 1517, and 1528, with the last outbreak in 1551, after which the disease apparently vanished. The onset of symptoms was sudden, and death or recovery often occurred within 8\u201310 hours. Sweating sickness epidemics were unique compared with other disease outbreaks of the time: whereas other epidemics were typically urban and long-lasting, cases of sweating sickness spiked and receded very quickly, significantly affecting rural populations. Its cause remains unknown, although it has been suggested that an unknown species of hantavirus was responsible. John Caius was a physician in Shrewsbury in 1551, when an outbreak occurred, and he described the symptoms and signs of the disease in A Boke or Counseill Against the Disease Commonly Called the Sweate, or Sweatyng Sicknesse (1552), which is the main historical source of knowledge of the disease."}}, {"canonical_name": "enfermedades que tienen su origen de fri\u00f3", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "enfermedades_que_tienen_su_origen_de_fri\u00f3", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "enfermedades que tienen su origen de fri\u00f3", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["enfermedades que tienen su origen de fri\u00f3"], "contexts": ["illnesses originating from cold"]}, {"entity_id": "doen\u00e7as_frias", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "doen\u00e7as frias", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["doen\u00e7as frias"], "contexts": ["illnesses characterized by coldness"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "enfermedades que tienen su origen de fri\u00f3", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "doen\u00e7as frias", "similarity": 0.927}], "id": 5517, "stable_key": "clu_76832e712c10b888-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cold", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Common cold", "note": "Early modern texts often grouped various ailments associated with 'coldness' under a broad category. While 'common cold' is a plausible modern referent, the historical usage could encompass a wider range of conditions than the modern specific viral infection.", "wikidata_id": "Q5153310", "wikidata_description": "former unit of the British Medical Research Council", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common%20Cold%20Unit", "wikipedia_extract": "The Common Cold Unit (CCU) or Common Cold Research Unit (CCRU) was a unit of the British Medical Research Council which undertook laboratory and epidemiological research on the common cold between 1946 and 1989 and produced 1,006 papers. The unit studied etiology, epidemiology, prevention, and treatment of common colds. It utilised Harvard Hospital, near Salisbury, a redundant Second World War-duration emergency infectious diseases field-hospital at Harnham Down. Common colds account for a majority of all acute respiratory infections, and the economic costs are substantial in terms of sick leave. The public-facing side of the CCU involved continually recruiting human volunteers, by advertising. Volunteers were housed at the hospital, for typically ten-days while participating in common cold trials."}}, {"canonical_name": "purgatiua", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "purgatiua", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "purgatiua", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["purgatiua"], "contexts": ["purgative property"]}, {"entity_id": "purgatiuas", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "purgatiuas", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["purgatiuas"], "contexts": ["type of medicines the root is not"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "purgatiua", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "purgatiuas", "similarity": 0.91}], "id": 5518, "stable_key": "clu_b492a0bcb0f05fb7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "purgative", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q3410946", "wikidata_description": "preparation to induce evacuation of the bowels. produces a soft formed stool, and relaxes and loosens the bowels, typically used over a protracted period, to relieve constipation"}}, {"canonical_name": "grado", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "grado", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "grado", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["grado"], "contexts": ["degree of quality"]}, {"entity_id": "grados", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "grados", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["grados"], "contexts": ["Units of measurement for demarcation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "grado", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "grados", "similarity": 0.831}], "id": 5519, "stable_key": "clu_dc2c9acc23e7a736", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "degree", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to a measure of intensity or quality.", "wikidata_id": "Q37497718", "wikidata_description": "family name"}}, {"canonical_name": "Paseuaro", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "paseuaro", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "Paseuaro", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Paseuaro"], "contexts": ["cold place in Mechoacan"]}, {"entity_id": "passiloco", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Passiloco", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Passiloco"], "contexts": ["mentioned alongside Somau and Si\u00e3o"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "Paseuaro", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Passiloco", "similarity": 0.899}], "id": 5520, "stable_key": "clu_fa67db582eac6f01", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "P\u00e1tzcuaro", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Mexico", "note": "Hernandez's 'Paseuaro' likely refers to P\u00e1tzcuaro, a significant city in Michoac\u00e1n, Mexico. 'Passiloco' from the other text is harder to place definitively but could be a corruption or a different locality.", "wikidata_id": "Q1101534", "wikidata_description": "species of fish", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/P%C3%A1tzcuaro%20chub"}}, {"canonical_name": "astringcion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "astringcion", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "astringcion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["astringcion"], "contexts": ["property of causing contraction"]}, {"entity_id": "adstric\u00e7\u00e3o", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "adstric\u00e7\u00e3o", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["adstric\u00e7\u00e3o"], "contexts": ["quality of tea"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "astringcion", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "adstric\u00e7\u00e3o", "similarity": 0.885}], "id": 5521, "stable_key": "clu_420e6a6285746725", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "astringency", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q9141413", "wikidata_description": "taste caused by astringent foods", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Astringent%20%28taste%29"}}, {"canonical_name": "Espe\u00f1a", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "espe\u00f1a", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "Espe\u00f1a", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Espe\u00f1a"], "contexts": ["Spain, source of medicines"]}, {"entity_id": "e\u017fpa\u00f1\u00f3les", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "E\u017fpa\u00f1\u00f3les", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["E\u017fpa\u00f1\u00f3les"], "contexts": ["nationality of Spanish Moors"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615