"Hematuria", "wikidata_id": "Q1574609", "wikidata_description": "human disease", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thin%20basement%20membrane%20disease"}}, {"canonical_name": "agudo", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "agudo", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "agudo", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["agudo"], "contexts": ["taste of the plant"]}, {"entity_id": "agudo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "agudo", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["agudo"], "contexts": ["quality of a\u00e7oro and calamo arom\u00e1tico"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "agudo", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "agudo", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5552, "stable_key": "clu_a3ef3d59d3c2ceba", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sharp", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to a sharp or pungent taste/quality.", "wikidata_id": "Q1241160", "wikidata_description": "village in Gloucestershire, England, UK", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sharpness%2C%20Gloucestershire"}}, {"canonical_name": "sutiles", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "sutiles", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "sutiles", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["sutiles"], "contexts": ["quality mixed in sar^aparrilla"]}, {"entity_id": "sutiles", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "sutiles", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["sutiles"], "contexts": ["part of the root's complexion"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "sutiles", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "sutiles", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5553, "stable_key": "clu_30d079fda1cd4844", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Subtle", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to a fine, subtle, or delicate quality, often in humoral theory.", "wikidata_id": "Q49396791", "wikidata_description": "quality of being hard to grasp, not obvious or easily understood, barely noticeable"}}, {"canonical_name": "MONTINENTE", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "montinente", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "MONTINENTE", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["MONTINENTE"], "contexts": ["plant name, also called yerba purgativa"]}, {"entity_id": "montano", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "montano", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["montano"], "contexts": ["herb, likely mountain calamint"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "MONTINENTE", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "montano", "similarity": 0.894}], "id": 5554, "stable_key": "clu_378a7cdb3e1d770e", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Montano in Italian, both Montano and Monta\u00f1o in Spanish, is a surname. Aldo Montano (fencer born 1910), Italian fencer Aldo Montano (fencer born 1978), Italian fencer and grandson of the above fencer Alicia G\u00f3mez Montano (1955\u20132020), Spanish journalist Mario Aldo Montano, Italian fencer and son of the fencer born in 1910 Cesar Montano, multi-awarded Filipino actor and film director Justiniano Montano (1905\u20132005), Filipino politician Linda Montano, American contemporary feminist performance artist Machel Montano (born 1974), Trinidad and Tobago soca singer, record producer and songwriter Mark Montano, American interior designer, artist, writer and TV personality Severino Montano (1915\u20131980), Filipino playwright, writer, director and actor Benito Arias Montano (1527\u20131598) (not Monta\u00f1o), Spanish orientalist Also a governor and a soldier of the same name and same period es:Montano (desambiguaci\u00f3n) rarer Spanish surnames as Montano, no \u00f1."}}, {"canonical_name": "regiones fr\u00edas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "regiones_fr\u00edas", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "regiones fr\u00edas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["regiones fr\u00edas"], "contexts": ["climate where plant grows"]}, {"entity_id": "regi\u00f5es_frias", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "regi\u00f5es frias", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["regi\u00f5es frias"], "contexts": ["cold geographical areas"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "regiones fr\u00edas", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "regi\u00f5es frias", "similarity": 0.984}], "id": 5555, "stable_key": "clu_df45130630d79ded-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Polar regions", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "wikidata_id": "Q60670", "wikidata_description": "regions around the Earth's geographical poles", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polar%20regions%20of%20Earth", "country": "Antarctica, Arctic", "wikipedia_extract": "The polar regions, also called the frigid zones or polar zones, of Earth are Earth's polar ice caps, the regions of the planet that surround its geographical poles (the North Pole and the South Pole), lying within the polar circles. These high latitudes are dominated by floating sea ice covering much of the Arctic Ocean in the north, and by the Antarctic ice sheet on the continent of Antarctica and the Southern Ocean in the south. The Arctic has various definitions, including the region north of the Arctic Circle (currently Epoch 2010 at 66\u00b033'44\" N), or just the region north of 60\u00b0 north latitude, or the region from the North Pole south to the timberline. The Antarctic is usually defined simply as south of 60\u00b0 south latitude, or the continent of Antarctica. The 1959 Antarctic Treaty uses the former definition. The two polar regions are distinguished from the other two climatic and biometric belts of Earth, a tropics belt near the equator, and two middle latitude regions located between the tropics and polar regions."}}, {"canonical_name": "cauterio", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "cauterio", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "cauterio", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["cauterio"], "contexts": ["instrument for burning flesh"]}, {"entity_id": "cauterio", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "cauterio", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["cauterio"], "contexts": ["instrument for cauterization"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "cauterio", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "cauterio", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5556, "stable_key": "clu_237d1f9a5d9b88ad", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cautery", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "Q1136347", "wikidata_description": "burning of part of a body to remove or close off a part of it", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cauterization"}}, {"canonical_name": "lobos marinos", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "lobos_marinos", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "lobos marinos", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["lobos marinos"], "contexts": ["sea wolves, seals"]}, {"entity_id": "lobos_marinos", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "lobos marinos", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["lobos marinos"], "contexts": ["animals that Tiburones fight with"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "lobos marinos", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "lobos marinos", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5557, "stable_key": "clu_3042c3d42ec33196", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sea lion", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q256569", "wikidata_description": "heraldic animal", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sea-lion"}}, {"canonical_name": "cuuierta", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "cuuierta", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "cuuierta", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["cuuierta"], "contexts": ["deck of a ship"]}, {"entity_id": "cutis", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "cutis", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["cutis"], "contexts": ["skin, mentioned in relation to dropsy treatment"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "cuuierta", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cutis", "similarity": 0.91}], "id": 5558, "stable_key": "clu_858214d29ed4fb1e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Skin", "confidence": "low", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'cuuierta' (or 'cutis') is used in two very different contexts: as the deck of a ship and as skin. The latter is the more likely medical context for dropsy treatment. The former appears to be an OCR error or a different meaning of the word.", "wikidata_id": "Q272206", "wikidata_description": "family of reptiles (Reptilia)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skink"}}, {"canonical_name": "monas", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "monas", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "monas", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["monas"], "contexts": ["monkeys, also called 'gatos pahvles'"]}, {"entity_id": "monas", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Monas", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Monas"], "contexts": ["genus of microscopic organisms"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "monas", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Monas", "similarity": 0.718}], "id": 5559, "stable_key": "clu_c806cfa3436db10b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Monkey", "confidence": "medium", "type": "animal", "note": "While 'monas' typically refers to monkeys, the context of microscopic organisms suggests the genus 'Monas' (a genus of protozoa), which is also plausible given early modern scientific classifications. Given the mixed contexts, 'Monkey' is the more direct translation, but 'Monas' (protozoa) is a possible alternate.", "wikidata_id": "Q1367", "wikidata_description": "common idea of an animal of the \"higher primates\" (the simians in English excluding apes)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monkey"}}, {"canonical_name": "thea", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "thea", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "thea", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["thea"], "contexts": ["plant whose smoke is used for ink"]}, {"entity_id": "thea", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "thea", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["thea"], "contexts": ["Ingredient in a preparation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "thea", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "thea", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5560, "stable_key": "clu_75f76ac07789bd8b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tea", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Camellia sinensis", "family": "Theaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q101815", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camellia%20sinensis"}}, {"canonical_name": "indios m\u00e9dicos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "indios_m\u00e9dicos", "book_id": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "name": "indios m\u00e9dicos", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 1, "variants": ["indios m\u00e9dicos"], "contexts": ["indigenous medical practitioners"]}, {"entity_id": "indio_medico", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Indio medico", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 1, "variants": ["Indio medico"], "contexts": ["Indigenous physician who provided method"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "quatro_libros_naturaleza_hernandez_1615", "source_name": "indios m\u00e9dicos", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Indio medico", "similarity": 0.946}], "id": 5561, "stable_key": "clu_bf4e4bb3458029d3-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Indian physicians", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Physicians originating from or practicing in India.", "note": "The cluster contains references to 'Indio medico' (Indigenous physician), 'm\u00e9dicos hindus' (Hindu physicians), and 'm\u00e9dicos indianos' (Indian physicians), all pointing to practitioners from India."}}, {"canonical_name": "lois immuables", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "lois_immuables", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "lois immuables", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["lois immuables"], "contexts": ["unchangeable natural laws"]}, {"entity_id": "unchangeable_laws", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "unchangeable laws", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["unchangeable laws"], "contexts": ["laws that do not change over time"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "lois immuables", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "unchangeable laws", "similarity": 0.934}], "id": 5562, "stable_key": "clu_d517562011a8ae32-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Law of nature", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The context 'constant course of procedure in nature' points to natural law, rather than legal statutes.", "wikidata_id": "Q29524", "wikidata_description": "system of law that is purportedly determined by nature, and is thus universal", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natural_law", "wikipedia_extract": "Rights of nature or Earth rights is a legal and jurisprudential theory that describes inherent rights as associated with ecosystems and species, similar to the concept of fundamental human rights. The rights of nature concept challenges twentieth-century laws as generally grounded in a flawed frame of nature as \"resource\" to be owned, used, and degraded. Proponents argue that laws grounded in rights of nature direct humanity to act appropriately and in a way consistent with modern, system-based science, which demonstrates that humans and the natural world are fundamentally interconnected. This school of thought is underpinned by two basic lines of reasoning. First, since the recognition of human rights is based in part on the philosophical belief that those rights emanate from humanity's own existence, logically, so too do inherent rights of the natural world arise from the natural world's own existence. A second and more pragmatic argument asserts that the survival of humans depends on healthy ecosystems, and so protection of nature's rights in turn, advances human rights and well-being."}}, {"canonical_name": "puret\u00e9", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "puret\u00e9", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "puret\u00e9", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["puret\u00e9"], "contexts": ["state of being pure"]}, {"entity_id": "purity", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "purity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["purity"], "contexts": ["state of being unadulterated"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "puret\u00e9", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "purity", "similarity": 0.922}], "id": 5563, "stable_key": "clu_b4d2ad8de9a83f4c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Purity", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q1401905", "wikidata_description": "mass fraction of fine metal in a precious metal object", "wikipedia_extract": "The fineness of a precious metal object (coin, bar, jewelry, etc.) represents the weight of fine metal therein, in proportion to the total weight which includes alloying base metals and any impurities. Alloy metals are added to increase hardness and durability of coins and jewelry, alter colors, decrease the cost per weight, or avoid the cost of high-purity refinement. For example, copper is added to the precious metal silver to make a more durable alloy for use in coins, housewares and jewelry. Coin silver, which was used for making silver coins in the past, contains 90% silver and 10% copper, by mass. Sterling silver contains 92.5% silver and 7.5% of other metals, usually copper, by mass. Various ways of expressing fineness have been used and two remain in common use: millesimal fineness expressed in units of parts per 1,000 and karats or carats used only for gold.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fineness"}}, {"canonical_name": "Valckenaer", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "valckenaer", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Valckenaer", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Valckenaer"], "contexts": ["author"]}, {"entity_id": "valckenaer", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Valckenaer", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Valckenaer"], "contexts": ["scholar who wrote a diatribe"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Valckenaer", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Valckenaer", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5564, "stable_key": "clu_9a3245125ce3e60e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Valckenaer", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Scholar and author of a diatribe, likely Lodewijk Caspar Valckenaer.", "note": "Lodewijk Caspar Valckenaer was a Dutch classical scholar and philologist. The context suggests a learned individual whose work is being referenced.", "wikidata_id": "Q2155572", "wikidata_description": "Dutch classical scholar (1715\u20131785)"}}, {"canonical_name": "moine franciscain", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "moine_franciscain", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "moine franciscain", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["moine franciscain"], "contexts": ["religious figure"]}, {"entity_id": "franciscan_monk", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Franciscan monk", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Franciscan monk"], "contexts": ["killed by a\u00ebrolite fall"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "moine franciscain", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Franciscan monk", "similarity": 0.911}], "id": 5565, "stable_key": "clu_a83a44024ef1f04f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Franciscan monk", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Member of the Franciscan order, notable for religious roles.", "wikidata_id": "Q90879952", "wikidata_description": "historical religious role, 1209\u20131517"}}, {"canonical_name": "Chamaerops", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "chamaerops", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Chamaerops", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Chamaerops"], "contexts": ["plants in Spain"]}, {"entity_id": "chamaerops_martiana", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Chamaerops martiana", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Chamaerops martiana"], "contexts": ["species of palm"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Chamaerops", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Chamaerops martiana", "similarity": 0.888}], "id": 5566, "stable_key": "clu_8191986965c85d04", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Chamaerops", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Chamaerops humilis Linnaeus", "family": "Arecaceae", "note": "While 'Chamaerops martiana' is mentioned, 'Chamaerops' as a genus is the primary referent. Chamaerops humilis is the type species.", "wikidata_id": "Q3666051", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants"}}, {"canonical_name": "aguas calientes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "aguas_calientes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "aguas calientes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["aguas calientes"], "contexts": ["name for hot springs location"]}, {"entity_id": "agua_das_caldas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "agua das Caldas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["agua das Caldas"], "contexts": ["Water from thermal springs"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "aguas calientes", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "agua das Caldas", "similarity": 0.93}], "id": 5567, "stable_key": "clu_34ba0afd5686a786-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "thermal springs", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "wikidata_id": "Q97726587", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article", "wikipedia_extract": "A hot spring, thermal spring, hydrothermal spring, or geothermal spring is a spring produced by the emergence of geothermally heated groundwater onto the surface of the Earth. The groundwater is heated either by shallow bodies of magma (molten rock) or by circulation through faults to hot rock deep in the Earth's crust. Hot spring water often contains large amounts of dissolved minerals. The chemistry of hot springs ranges from acid sulfate springs with a pH as low as 0.8, to alkaline chloride springs saturated with silica, to bicarbonate springs saturated with carbon dioxide and carbonate minerals. Some springs also contain abundant dissolved iron. The minerals brought to the surface in hot springs often feed communities of extremophiles, microorganisms adapted to extreme conditions, and it is possible that life on Earth had its origin in hot springs."}}, {"canonical_name": "C\u0153salpinia", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "c\u0153salpinia", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "C\u0153salpinia", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["C\u0153salpinia"], "contexts": ["plant used for large logs"]}, {"entity_id": "caesalpinia", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Caesalpinia", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Caesalpinia"], "contexts": ["genus of trees"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "C\u0153salpinia", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Caesalpinia", "similarity": 0.919}], "id": 5568, "stable_key": "clu_9432854defae9d1c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Caesalpinia", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Caesalpinia pulcherrima (Linnaeus) Swartz", "family": "Fabaceae", "note": "The specific species isn't confirmed, but 'Caesalpinia' is a well-established genus.", "wikidata_id": "Q608215", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants"}}, {"canonical_name": "\u00e9lectricit\u00e9 atmosph\u00e9rique", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "\u00e9lectricit\u00e9_atmosph\u00e9rique", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "\u00e9lectricit\u00e9 atmosph\u00e9rique", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["\u00e9lectricit\u00e9 atmosph\u00e9rique"], "contexts": ["scientific phenomenon"]}, {"entity_id": "atmospheric_eletricity", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "atmospheric eletricity", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["atmospheric eletricity"], "contexts": ["electrical phenomena in the atmosphere"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "\u00e9lectricit\u00e9 atmosph\u00e9rique", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "atmospheric eletricity", "similarity": 0.937}], "id": 5569, "stable_key": "clu_af273e941d5cab9f-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Atmospheric electricity", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q427085", "wikidata_description": "electricity in planetary atmospheres"}}, {"canonical_name": "concentration", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "concentration", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "concentration", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["concentration"], "contexts": ["process for treating aluminous schists"]}, {"entity_id": "concentration", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "concentration", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["concentration"], "contexts": ["process of gathering matter"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "concentration", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "concentration", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5570, "stable_key": "clu_cc97b274587ba14d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Concentration", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This refers to the general concept of increasing density or bringing together matter.", "wikidata_id": "Q21157127", "wikidata_description": "collection of entities that are physically or conceptually close to each other"}}, {"canonical_name": "Ficinus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "ficinus", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Ficinus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ficinus"], "contexts": ["cited author in mineralogy"]}, {"entity_id": "facchinus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Facchinus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Facchinus"], "contexts": ["commentator on Rhasis"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Ficinus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Facchinus", "similarity": 0.853}], "id": 5571, "stable_key": "clu_a2dd5b7625e6192d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Paulus Fide (or Facchinus)", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "Italian physician and medical writer, commentator on Rhazes.", "note": "The spelling 'Ficinus' is likely a misspelling or variant of 'Fide' or 'Facchinus', referencing the 15th-century Italian physician and commentator on Rhazes."}}, {"canonical_name": "piles galvaniques", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "piles_galvaniques", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "piles galvaniques", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["piles galvaniques"], "contexts": ["early electrical devices"]}, {"entity_id": "galvanic_pile", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "galvanic pile", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["galvanic pile"], "contexts": ["device for generating electricity"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "piles galvaniques", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "galvanic pile", "similarity": 0.928}], "id": 5572, "stable_key": "clu_aff42e636f047da4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Galvanic pile", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "Refers to early voltaic piles, a precursor to the electric battery.", "wikidata_id": "Q57302444", "wikidata_description": "scientific article published on 01 January 1801"}}, {"canonical_name": "Union", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "union", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Union", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Union"], "contexts": ["Refers to a political entity in America"]}, {"entity_id": "union", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Union", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Union"], "contexts": ["independent Christian states in North America"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Union", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Union", "similarity": 0.821}], "id": 5573, "stable_key": "clu_9c0afb49427ed09b-4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "United States", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United States", "wikidata_id": "Q30", "wikidata_description": "country located primarily in North America", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United%20States", "note": "While 'United States of North America' was a more formal designation, the modern referent is the United States.", "wikipedia_extract": "The United States of America (USA), also known as the United States (U.S.) or America, is a country primarily located in North America. It is a federal republic of 50 states and a federal capital district, Washington, D.C. The 48 contiguous states border Canada to the north and Mexico to the south, with the semi-exclave of Alaska in the northwest and the archipelago of Hawaii in the Pacific Ocean. The United States also asserts sovereignty over five major island territories and various uninhabited islands in Oceania and the Caribbean. It is a megadiverse country, with the world's third-largest land area and third-largest population, exceeding 341 million. Paleo-Indians first migrated from North Asia to North America at least 15,000 years ago, and formed various civilizations."}}, {"canonical_name": "Rio de Lo\u00e2", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "rio_de_lo\u00e2", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Rio de Lo\u00e2", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rio de Lo\u00e2"], "contexts": ["river in South America"]}, {"entity_id": "rio_de_lobos", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Rio de Lobos", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rio de Lobos"], "contexts": ["interpreted meaning of Guadalupe"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Rio de Lo\u00e2", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Rio de Lobos", "similarity": 0.889}], "id": 5574, "stable_key": "clu_8145532ffd4956a0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "R\u00edo de Loa / R\u00edo de Lobos", "confidence": "medium", "type": "place", "country": "Chile / Spain", "note": "There are two possible referents: the R\u00edo de Loa in Chile, and the R\u00edo de Lobos which may be a misinterpretation or a different geographical feature. The context 'river in South America' favors R\u00edo de Loa."}}, {"canonical_name": "lac de Tilicaca", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "lac_de_tilicaca", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "lac de Tilicaca", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["lac de Tilicaca"], "contexts": ["lake in South America"]}, {"entity_id": "lake_of_titicaca", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Lake of Titicaca", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lake of Titicaca"], "contexts": ["lake in Bolivia"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "lac de Tilicaca", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Lake of Titicaca", "similarity": 0.889}], "id": 5575, "stable_key": "clu_69d96da7a892f9d8-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lake Titicaca", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Peru, Bolivia", "wikidata_id": "Q1628083", "wikidata_description": "species of amphibian", "wikipedia_extract": "Waru Waru is a type of raised field agriculture developed by pre-Hispanic people in the Andes region of South America from Ecuador to Bolivia. This regional agricultural technique is also referred to as camellones in Spanish. Functionally similar agricultural techniques have been developed in other parts of the world. This type of altiplano field agriculture consists of parallel canals alternated by raised planting beds, which would be strategically located on floodplains or near a water source so that the fields could be properly irrigated. These flooded fields were composed of soil that was rich in nutrients due to the presence of aquatic plants and other organic materials. Through the process of mounding up this soil to create planting beds, natural, recyclable fertilizer was made available in a region where nitrogen-rich soils were rare.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waru_Waru"}}, {"canonical_name": "Chilo\u00e8", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "chilo\u00e8", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Chilo\u00e8", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Chilo\u00e8"], "contexts": ["gulf in southern Chile"]}, {"entity_id": "chilo\u00eb", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Chilo\u00eb", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Chilo\u00eb"], "contexts": ["South American island"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Chilo\u00e8", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Chilo\u00eb", "similarity": 0.815}], "id": 5576, "stable_key": "clu_9a1f362f75756217", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Chilo\u00e9 Island", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Chile"}}, {"canonical_name": "Bru\u00e9", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "bru\u00e9", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Bru\u00e9", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bru\u00e9"], "contexts": ["cartographer whose maps were consulted"]}, {"entity_id": "bruel", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Bruel", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bruel"], "contexts": ["author of prax. med. theor."]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Bru\u00e9", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Bruel", "similarity": 0.844}], "id": 5577, "stable_key": "clu_5bf8ee0f6843a98f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bru\u00e9", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "Cartographer and author of medical works.", "note": "The spelling variation 'Bruel' is noted, but 'Bru\u00e9' appears more in the primary context."}}, {"canonical_name": "Thibaudia", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "thibaudia", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Thibaudia", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Thibaudia"], "contexts": ["alpine shrub found in Silla de Caracas"]}, {"entity_id": "thibaudia", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "thibaudia", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["thibaudia"], "contexts": ["social plant"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Thibaudia", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "thibaudia", "similarity": 0.988}], "id": 5578, "stable_key": "clu_e363c98cb807cfc6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Thibaudia", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Thibaudia sp.", "family": "Ericaceae", "note": "This is a genus name, specific species not identified.", "wikidata_id": "Q73739", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thibaudia", "wikipedia_extract": "Thibaudia is a genus of flowering plants in the family Ericaceae. They are native to Central and South America with a high concentration of species in the cloud forests of Costa Rica and Ecuador. Species of the genus are known commonly as zagalitas."}}, {"canonical_name": "argentif\u00e8res", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "argentif\u00e8res", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "argentif\u00e8res", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["argentif\u00e8res"], "contexts": ["mineral veins containing silver"]}, {"entity_id": "argentiferous_ores", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "argentiferous ores", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["argentiferous ores"], "contexts": ["ores containing silver"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "argentif\u00e8res", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "argentiferous ores", "similarity": 0.918}], "id": 5579, "stable_key": "clu_74af7d4430abe917", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": null, "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral"}}, {"canonical_name": "manufactures", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "manufactures", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "manufactures", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["manufactures"], "contexts": ["products of industry"]}, {"entity_id": "manufactures", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Manufactures", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Manufactures"], "contexts": ["development from simple to complex"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "manufactures", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Manufactures", "similarity": 0.858}], "id": 5580, "stable_key": "clu_e7dbd3fd8237be52", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": null, "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q106303372", "wikidata_description": "1847 United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland Act of Parliament 10 & 11 Vic c. 91"}}, {"canonical_name": "arts m\u00e9caniques", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "arts_m\u00e9caniques", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "arts m\u00e9caniques", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["arts m\u00e9caniques"], "contexts": ["mechanical arts"]}, {"entity_id": "mechanical_arts", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "mechanical arts", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["mechanical arts"], "contexts": ["skills related to machinery and engineering"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "arts m\u00e9caniques", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "mechanical arts", "similarity": 0.967}], "id": 5581, "stable_key": "clu_fad8535f88ec2313", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": null, "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q707874", "wikidata_description": "medieval concept of ordered practices or skills, often juxtaposed to the traditional seven liberal arts (Artes liberales)"}}, {"canonical_name": "chariots", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "chariots", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "chariots", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["chariots"], "contexts": ["vehicles for transport"]}, {"entity_id": "carriages", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "carriages", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["carriages"], "contexts": ["vehicles for passengers on trains"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "chariots", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "carriages", "similarity": 0.919}], "id": 5582, "stable_key": "clu_c775cefbd27dbca3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "chariot", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "While 'carriages' is mentioned, the primary term and historical context suggest chariots as a more specific, albeit ancient, form of wheeled vehicle. In early modern texts, 'chariot' could also refer to contemporary horse-drawn vehicles.", "wikidata_id": "Q203788", "wikidata_description": "ancient wheeled vehicle"}}, {"canonical_name": "Kiel", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "kiel", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Kiel", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Kiel"], "contexts": ["city connected by a canal"]}, {"entity_id": "kiel", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Kiel", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Kiel"], "contexts": ["city where Brorsen discovered a comet"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Kiel", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Kiel", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5583, "stable_key": "clu_82265306ac1f74b8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Kiel", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Germany", "wikidata_id": "Q1707", "wikidata_description": "German city, capital of Schleswig-Holstein", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kiel", "wikipedia_extract": "THW Kiel is a professional handball club from Kiel, Germany. The club competes in the Handball-Bundesliga and is the record champion with 23 titles. 2007 and 2012 were the most successful years in the club's history, as THW completed the treble, winning the domestic league, the domestic cup, and the EHF Champions League. In 2012, the team won every league game, a first in any top-flight German team sports. They ended the 2019\u201320 season as winners of the EHF Champions League and champions of the Handball-Bundesliga. Since then, they have secured additional league titles in 2021 and 2023, along with the DHB-Pokal in 2025."}}, {"canonical_name": "Oc\u00e9ans", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "oc\u00e9ans", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Oc\u00e9ans", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Oc\u00e9ans"], "contexts": ["large bodies of water to be joined"]}, {"entity_id": "oceans", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "oceans", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["oceans"], "contexts": ["large bodies of saltwater on Earth"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Oc\u00e9ans", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "oceans", "similarity": 0.957}], "id": 5584, "stable_key": "clu_b1b20bdeba2b0163-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ocean", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "wikidata_id": "Q161419", "wikidata_description": "ancient Greek god of the earth-encircling river, Oceanos", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oceanus", "wikipedia_extract": "In Greek mythology, Oceanus or Okeanos was one of the Titans, the children of Uranus (Sky) and Gaia (Earth), the brother and husband of the Titaness Tethys, and the father of the river gods and the Oceanids, as well as being the great river which encircled the entire world. According to M. L. West, the etymology of Oceanus is \"obscure\" and \"cannot be explained from Greek\". The use by Pherecydes of Syros of the form \u014cgen\u00f3s (\u1f68\u03b3\u03b5\u03bd\u03cc\u03c2) for the name lends support for the name being a loanword. However, according to West, no \"very convincing\" foreign models have been found."}}, {"canonical_name": "confiance", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "confiance", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "confiance", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["confiance"], "contexts": ["state of mind"]}, {"entity_id": "confidence", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "confidence", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["confidence"], "contexts": ["feeling of self-assurance"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "confiance", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "confidence", "similarity": 0.911}], "id": 5585, "stable_key": "clu_ed3316e0269b1baa", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "confidence", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "Worcester", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "worcester", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Worcester", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Worcester"], "contexts": ["city in Massachusetts"]}, {"entity_id": "worcester", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Worcester", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Worcester"], "contexts": ["city with establishments"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Worcester", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Worcester", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5586, "stable_key": "clu_c6b84765b6c69c66", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Worcester", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United Kingdom", "note": "Contexts mention both UK and US locations, but \"Worcestershire\" points to UK. Primary context is likely UK.", "wikidata_id": "Q1646181", "wikidata_description": "city and the county town of Worcestershire, England", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Worcester,_Massachusetts", "wikipedia_extract": "Worcester (  WUUST-\u0259r, locally [\u02c8w\u026ast\u0259] ) is a city in the U.S. state of Massachusetts. The principal city of Central Massachusetts, Worcester is both the second-most populous city in the state, and the 113th most populous city in the United States. Named after Worcester, England, the city had 206,518 people at the 2020 census, also making it the second-most populous city in New England, after Boston. Because it is near the geographic center of Massachusetts, Worcester is known as the \"Heart of the Commonwealth\"; a heart is the official symbol of the city. Worcester is the historical seat of Worcester County."}}, {"canonical_name": "marmoris", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "marmoris", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "marmoris", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["marmoris"], "contexts": ["white marble found"]}, {"entity_id": "marmoreum", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "marmoreum", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["marmoreum"], "contexts": ["material of the pavement"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "marmoris", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "marmoreum", "similarity": 0.937}], "id": 5587, "stable_key": "clu_aba4f89f40d67b54-4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Marble", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "Contexts suggest both the rock and potentially a salt derived from it.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marble", "wikipedia_extract": "Marble is a metamorphic rock consisting of carbonate minerals (most commonly calcite  (CaCO3) or dolomite (CaMg(CO3)2) that have recrystallized under the influence of heat and pressure. It has a crystalline texture, and is typically not foliated (layered), although there are exceptions. In geology, the term marble refers to metamorphosed limestone, but its use in stonemasonry more broadly encompasses unmetamorphosed limestone. Pure white marble arises from very clean limestone or rock also called dolomite. However, its signature veins and swirls result from impurities like clay, sand, iron oxides, or chert originally present in the protolith\u2014magnesium-rich varieties may show green tones due to serpentine. These impurities are redistributed and recrystallized during metamorphism."}}, {"canonical_name": "Gaspari", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "gaspari", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Gaspari", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Gaspari"], "contexts": ["co-author of a geography description"]}, {"entity_id": "gasparisguarini", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "GasparisGuarini", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["GasparisGuarini"], "contexts": ["person with memory loss treated"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Gaspari", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "GasparisGuarini", "similarity": 0.892}], "id": 5588, "stable_key": "clu_b34da35a2666dc50", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Gaspar Guarini", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Author and physician, treated memory loss."}}, {"canonical_name": "Mer du Bengal", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "mer_du_bengal", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Mer du Bengal", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mer du Bengal"], "contexts": ["sea south of an island"]}, {"entity_id": "bay_of_bengal", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Bay of Bengal", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bay of Bengal"], "contexts": ["northern part of the Indian Ocean"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Mer du Bengal", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Bay of Bengal", "similarity": 0.938}], "id": 5589, "stable_key": "clu_359c466363f82ffa", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bay of Bengal", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "India", "wikidata_id": "Q107540080", "wikidata_description": "bio-geographic province in MEOW scheme", "wikipedia_extract": "The Tampa Bay Buccaneers (colloquially known as the Bucs) are a professional American football team based in Tampa, Florida. The Buccaneers compete in the National Football League (NFL) as a member of the National Football Conference (NFC) South division. They joined the NFL in 1974 as an expansion team, along with the Seattle Seahawks, and played their first season in 1976 as a member of the American Football Conference (AFC) West division. Before the 1977 season, Tampa Bay switched conferences and divisions with Seattle, becoming a member of the NFC Central division. The Seahawks eventually rejoined the NFC in 2002, meaning the Buccaneers joined the Cleveland Browns, Pittsburgh Steelers, and Baltimore/Indianapolis Colts as the only NFL teams not playing in their original conference. As a result of the league's realignment before the 2002 season, the Buccaneers joined three former NFC West teams to form the NFC South.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tampa_Bay_Buccaneers"}}, {"canonical_name": "Lahore", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "lahore", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Lahore", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lahore"], "contexts": ["independent state"]}, {"entity_id": "lahore", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Lahore", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lahore"], "contexts": ["location of earthquake propagation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Lahore", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Lahore", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5590, "stable_key": "clu_b6548ccc9c746bd4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lahore", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Pakistan", "wikidata_id": "Q11739", "wikidata_description": "capital of the Punjab province, Pakistan", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lahore", "wikipedia_extract": "Lahore is the capital and largest city of the Pakistani province of Punjab. It is the second-largest city in Pakistan, after Karachi, and 27th largest in the world, with a population of over 14 million. Lahore is one of Pakistan's major industrial, educational and economic hubs. It has been the historic capital and cultural centre of the wider Punjab region, and is one of Pakistan's most socially liberal, progressive, and cosmopolitan cities. Lahore's origin dates back to antiquity. The city has been inhabited for around two millennia, although it rose to prominence in the late 10th century with the establishment of the Walled City, its fortified interior."}}, {"canonical_name": "Jakutsk", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "jakutsk", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Jakutsk", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Jakutsk"], "contexts": ["city in Siberia"]}, {"entity_id": "jakutsk", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Jakutsk", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Jakutsk"], "contexts": ["city in northern Asia"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Jakutsk", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Jakutsk", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5591, "stable_key": "clu_9737188088ba1c7f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Yakutsk", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Russia", "wikidata_id": "Q6610", "wikidata_description": "city in eastern Russia, capital of the Republic of Sakha (Yakutia)", "wikipedia_extract": "Yakutsk is the capital and largest city of Sakha, Russia, located about 450 km (280 mi) south of the Arctic Circle. Fueled by the mining industry, Yakutsk has become one of Russia's most rapidly growing regional cities, with a population of 355,443 at the 2021 census. Yakutsk has an average annual temperature of \u22128.0 \u00b0C (17.6 \u00b0F), winter high temperatures consistently well below \u221220 \u00b0C (\u22124 \u00b0F), and a record low of \u221264.4 \u00b0C (\u221283.9 \u00b0F) has been recorded. As a result, Yakutsk is the coldest major city in the world (although a number of smaller towns in that region are slightly colder). Yakutsk is also the largest city located in continuous permafrost; the only other large city is Norilsk, also in Siberia. Yakutsk is in the Central Yakutian Lowland and is a major port on the Lena River.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yakutsk"}}, {"canonical_name": "indo-germanique", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "indo-germanique", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "indo-germanique", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["indo-germanique"], "contexts": ["language family"]}, {"entity_id": "indo-germanic", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Indo-Germanic", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Indo-Germanic"], "contexts": ["tribes inhabiting Turan"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "indo-germanique", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Indo-Germanic", "similarity": 0.954}], "id": 5592, "stable_key": "clu_ded657c450f20333", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Indo-European languages", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'Indo-Germanic' is an older term for the Indo-European language family."}}, {"canonical_name": "hongrois", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "hongrois", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "hongrois", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["hongrois"], "contexts": ["language spoken in Hungary"]}, {"entity_id": "hungarus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Hungarus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hungarus"], "contexts": ["husband of phthisical patient"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "hongrois", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Hungarus", "similarity": 0.93}], "id": 5593, "stable_key": "clu_acc0fcbc0eecef0c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hungarian", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to nationality or people from Hungary, not a specific person.", "wikidata_id": "Q28", "wikidata_description": "country in Central Europe"}}, {"canonical_name": "Saragosse", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "saragosse", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Saragosse", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Saragosse"], "contexts": ["city where treaty was concluded"]}, {"entity_id": "sarago\u00e7a", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Sarago\u00e7a", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Sarago\u00e7a"], "contexts": ["city where contract was celebrated"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Saragosse", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Sarago\u00e7a", "similarity": 0.912}], "id": 5594, "stable_key": "clu_b9c71cf457a429c4", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Zaragoza (Spanish: [\u03b8a\u027ea\u02c8\u0263o\u03b8a] ), traditionally known in English as Saragossa ( SARR-\u0259-GOSS-\u0259), is the capital city of the province of Zaragoza and of the autonomous community of Aragon, Spain. It lies by the Ebro river and its tributaries, the Huerva and the G\u00e1llego, roughly in the centre of both Aragon and the Ebro basin. In the 1st century BCE, Romans founded the colony of Caesaraugusta over the Sedetani settlement of Salduie. The city was the capital of the Upper March of Al-Andalus under Umayyad rule, later thriving as a capital of a rump state ruled by the Banu Hud, the taifa of Zaragoza. It swiftly became the political and spiritual centre of the Kingdom of Aragon after the 1118 Christian conquest, and its inhabitants managed to preserve and consolidate initially granted privileges over the course of the Late Middle Ages. As of 2025, Zaragoza had a population of 699,007 inhabitants, making it the fourth most populous in Spain, on a land area of 973.78 square kilometres (375.98 square miles) and is the centre of a metropolitan area of around 0.76 million."}}, {"canonical_name": "BIake", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "biake", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "BIake", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["BIake"], "contexts": ["confirmed Dbanalagiri measurement"]}, {"entity_id": "biese", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Biese", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Biese"], "contexts": ["author on Aristotle's philosophy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "BIake", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Biese", "similarity": 0.859}], "id": 5595, "stable_key": "clu_0f02d49ac4c88044", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Richard Blake", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "Author on Aristotle, confirmed astronomical measurements.", "note": "The spelling is inconsistent ('BIake', 'Biese'). Richard Blake is a likely candidate given the context of Aristotelian philosophy."}}, {"canonical_name": "lac de Titicaca", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "lac_de_titicaca", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "lac de Titicaca", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["lac de Titicaca"], "contexts": ["lake name"]}, {"entity_id": "lake_titicaca", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Lake Titicaca", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lake Titicaca"], "contexts": ["lake near mountains measured by Pentland"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "lac de Titicaca", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Lake Titicaca", "similarity": 0.982}], "id": 5596, "stable_key": "clu_69d96da7a892f9d8-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lake Titicaca", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Peru, Bolivia", "wikidata_id": "Q1628083", "wikidata_description": "species of amphibian", "wikipedia_extract": "Waru Waru is a type of raised field agriculture developed by pre-Hispanic people in the Andes region of South America from Ecuador to Bolivia. This regional agricultural technique is also referred to as camellones in Spanish. Functionally similar agricultural techniques have been developed in other parts of the world. This type of altiplano field agriculture consists of parallel canals alternated by raised planting beds, which would be strategically located on floodplains or near a water source so that the fields could be properly irrigated. These flooded fields were composed of soil that was rich in nutrients due to the presence of aquatic plants and other organic materials. Through the process of mounding up this soil to create planting beds, natural, recyclable fertilizer was made available in a region where nitrogen-rich soils were rare.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waru_Waru"}}, {"canonical_name": "archipel des Chonos", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "archipel_des_chonos", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "archipel des Chonos", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["archipel des Chonos"], "contexts": ["archipelago of sunken mountains"]}, {"entity_id": "archipelago_'de_los_chonos'", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Archipelago 'de los Chonos'", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Archipelago 'de los Chonos'"], "contexts": ["South American archipelago"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "archipel des Chonos", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Archipelago 'de los Chonos'", "similarity": 0.918}], "id": 5597, "stable_key": "clu_4a443fa12d5750ef", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Chonos Archipelago", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Chile", "wikidata_id": "Q1076210", "wikidata_description": "island group in Chile", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chonos%20Archipelago", "wikipedia_extract": "The Chonos Archipelago is a series of low, mountainous, elongated islands with deep bays, traces of a submerged Chilean Coast Range. Most of the islands are forested with little or no human settlement. The deep Moraleda Channel separates the islands of the Chonos Archipelago from the mainland of Chile and from Magdalena Island. The largest islands are Melchor Island, Benjamin Island, Traigu\u00e9n Island, Riveros Island, Cuptana Island, James Island, Victoria Island, Simpson Island, Level Island, Luz Island. Far out in the Pacific is Guamblin Island with the Isla Guamblin National Park. The National park comprises about 106 km2."}}, {"canonical_name": "Collao", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "collao", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Collao", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Collao"], "contexts": ["high plains in Peru"]}, {"entity_id": "collao", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Collao", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Collao"], "contexts": ["land in Peru with bituminose mountains"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Collao", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Collao", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5598, "stable_key": "clu_16622b1c114d70c7", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Collao is a parish (administrative division) in Siero, a municipality within the province and autonomous community of Asturias, in northern Spain. The parish is located on Highway AS-113. It is 0.27 km2 (0.10 sq mi) in size, and is situated at an elevation of 230 m (750 ft) above sea level. The population is 488 Collao and other nearby parishes, such as Lieres and Carbay\u00edn, are historically associated with Andalusian mining. Collao's patron saint festivities honor San Crist\u00f3bal, and are held every year on the first Sunday of July. The Gazetteer of SADEI identifies five notable sites within the parish: The Cirig\u00fceyu, Cord\u00f3n, Fuentemil, Llori\u00e1n and La Quintana."}}, {"canonical_name": "Hambato", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "hambato", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Hambato", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hambato"], "contexts": ["river basin"]}, {"entity_id": "hambato", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Hambato", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hambato"], "contexts": ["city nearer to earthquake center"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Hambato", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Hambato", "similarity": 0.866}], "id": 5599, "stable_key": "clu_d0f5520e735de1f2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ambato", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Ecuador", "wikidata_id": "Q210161", "wikidata_description": "city in Ecuador, capital of the Tungurahua Province", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ambato%2C_Ecuador", "wikipedia_extract": "Ambato (Spanish pronunciation: [am\u02c8bato]; full form, San Juan de Ambato; Quechua: Ampatu Llaqta) is a city located in the central Andean valley of Ecuador. Lying on the banks of the Ambato River, the city also sits beneath several tall mountains. It is the capital city of the Tungurahua Province, situated at an elevation of 2,577 meters above sea level. It is variously nicknamed \"City of Flowers and Fruits\", \"Land of the Three Juans\", and \"Garden of Ecuador.\" Ambato's inhabitants are called Ambate\u00f1os or Guaytambos (after a type of native peach that the valley is famous for producing). The current mayor of Ambato is Diana Caiza. The city has been fully or partially destroyed by earthquakes several times in its history, most recently on August 5, 1949, when the city and its cathedral were almost completely devastated."}}, {"canonical_name": "Call", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "call", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Call", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Call"], "contexts": ["high plain name"]}, {"entity_id": "dial", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Dial", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Dial"], "contexts": ["name of a river's mouth"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Call", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Dial", "similarity": 0.951}], "id": 5600, "stable_key": "clu_8a4bee1245e267f6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Kall", "confidence": "low", "type": "place", "country": "Switzerland", "note": "The association with 'Dial' (a river's mouth) and 'Call' (a high plain name) makes the exact referent ambiguous. 'Kall' is a Swiss municipality, but its connection to a river mouth or high plain is not immediately clear without more context.", "wikipedia_extract": "Kristina Lee \"Kris\" Halliwell Collins (born July 1, 1996), better known as Kallmekris, is a Canadian social media personality, actress, comedian, and hairdresser from British Columbia. She is known for her skits that range from thirty seconds to a minute long. Collins began posting on TikTok in April 2020. As of February 2026, she has 50.5 million followers, making her the most-followed TikToker from Canada and the 38th overall. Her YouTube channel, which she began uploading to in 2020, has 12.8 million subscribers as of February 2026. In July 2025, Collins made her feature directorial debut with the found footage horror film, House on Eden.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/KallMeKris"}}, {"canonical_name": "Chillo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "chillo", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Chillo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Chillo"], "contexts": ["valley location"]}, {"entity_id": "chillo", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Chillo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Chillo"], "contexts": ["near Quito"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Chillo", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Chillo", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5601, "stable_key": "clu_de4c3bf7679749b2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Chillo Valley", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Ecuador", "wikipedia_extract": "AEK Basketball Club (Greek: \u039a\u0391\u0395 \u0391\u0395\u039a Greek pronunciation: [\u02c8aek]; \u0391\u03b8\u03bb\u03b7\u03c4\u03b9\u03ba\u03ae \u0388\u03bd\u03c9\u03c3\u03b9\u03c2 \u039a\u03c9\u03bd\u03c3\u03c4\u03b1\u03bd\u03c4\u03b9\u03bd\u03bf\u03c5\u03c0\u03cc\u03bb\u03b5\u03c9\u03c2 Athlitik\u00ed \u00c9nosis Konstantinoup\u00f3leos, \"Athletic Union of Constantinople\"), and also known as AEK B.C. or simply AEK, and more commonly known in European competitions as AEK Athens, is a Greek professional basketball club based in Athens, Attica, Greece, part of the major multi-sport club AEK. The club was established in Athens in 1924 by Greek refugees from Constantinople in the wake of the Greco-Turkish War (1919\u20131922). AEK B.C. is considered by as one of the three best Greek clubs in basketball and Greek sports club, as it maintains more than 30 sports' departments. AEK was the first-ever Greek basketball team, not only to reach a European Cup Final, but also to win a European title.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AEK_B.C."}}, {"canonical_name": "Cumacatar", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "cumacatar", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Cumacatar", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cumacatar"], "contexts": ["location of hot springs"]}, {"entity_id": "cumaca", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Cumaca", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cumaca"], "contexts": ["province where Cinnamon grows"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Cumacatar", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Cumaca", "similarity": 0.886}], "id": 5602, "stable_key": "clu_02e8370c484f568e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cumacatar", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Brazil", "note": "Likely refers to a location in the Amazon basin where medicinal plants were found."}}, {"canonical_name": "orographie", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "orographie", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "orographie", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["orographie"], "contexts": ["study of mountains"]}, {"entity_id": "orography", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "orography", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["orography"], "contexts": ["study of mountain ranges"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "orographie", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "orography", "similarity": 0.953}], "id": 5603, "stable_key": "clu_90d046f5ab8b068f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Orography", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q129292457", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article", "wikipedia_extract": "Jabal Hafeet (Arabic: \u062c\u064e\u0628\u064e\u0644 \u062d\u064e\u0641\u0650\u064a\u0652\u062a, romanized: Jabal \u1e24af\u012bt, \"Mount Hafeet\"; variously transcribed Jabel or Jebal and Hafit \u2013 literally \"empty mountain\") is a mountain in the region of Tawam, on the border of the United Arab Emirates and Oman. It is often considered an outlier of the Hajar Mountains in Eastern Arabia due to its proximity to the main range. To the north is the UAE city of Al Ain, in the Eastern Region of the Emirate of Abu Dhabi, and the adjacent Omani town of Al-Buraimi. The sole mountain in the Emirate of Abu Dhabi, and one of the highest mountains in the country, it has given its name to a period in UAE history, the Hafit Period of the Bronze Age (3200 to 2600 BCE), because of the discovery of a cluster of important beehive tombs at its foothills. As of 2017, the mountain is recognised as being part of a national park, and was incorporated into the Sheikh Zayed Network of Protected Areas in 2018. Jebel Hafeet has a hot desert climate (BWh) according to the K\u00f6ppen climate classification.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jebel_Hafeet"}}, {"canonical_name": "Dolomit", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "dolomit", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Dolomit", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Dolomit"], "contexts": ["mineral discussed in a geological work"]}, {"entity_id": "dolomit", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Dolomit", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Dolomit"], "contexts": ["type of rock"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Dolomit", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Dolomit", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5604, "stable_key": "clu_27c45a9aded97ab4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "dolomite", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikipedia_extract": "Limestone is a type of carbonate sedimentary rock which is the main source of the material lime. It is composed mostly of the minerals calcite and aragonite, which are different crystal forms of calcium carbonate CaCO3. Limestone forms when these minerals precipitate out of water containing dissolved calcium. This can take place through both biological and nonbiological processes, though biological processes, such as the accumulation of corals and shells in the sea, have likely been more important for the last 540 million years. Limestone often contains fossils which provide scientists with information on ancient environments and on the evolution of life. About 20% to 25% of sedimentary rock is carbonate rock, and most of this is limestone.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Limestone"}}, {"canonical_name": "Porto Seguro", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "porto_seguro", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Porto Seguro", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Porto Seguro"], "contexts": ["city"]}, {"entity_id": "porto_seguro", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Porto Seguro", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Porto Seguro"], "contexts": ["location north of Os Ilheos"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Porto Seguro", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Porto Seguro", "similarity": 0.863}], "id": 5605, "stable_key": "clu_6a3c563ea4eb60e4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Porto Seguro", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Brazil", "wikidata_id": "Q2596732", "wikidata_description": "microregion in Bahia, Brazil"}}, {"canonical_name": "Saint- Paul", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "saint-_paul", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Saint- Paul", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Saint- Paul"], "contexts": ["city"]}, {"entity_id": "st__paul", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "St. Paul", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["St. Paul"], "contexts": ["city in diamond district"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Saint- Paul", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "St. Paul", "similarity": 0.963}], "id": 5606, "stable_key": "clu_ac6d67d9fcc6db87", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Saint-Paul", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "France", "note": "Given the context of diamonds, this likely refers to Saint-Paul, a commune in the Nord department of France, known for its historical industrial importance, rather than other places named Saint-Paul.", "wikipedia_extract": "Saint Paul (often abbreviated to St. Paul) is the capital city of the U.S. state of Minnesota and the county seat of Ramsey County. As of the 2020 census, the city had a population of 311,527, making it Minnesota's second-most populous city and the 63rd-most populous in the United States. Saint Paul and neighboring Minneapolis form the core of the Twin Cities metropolitan area, the third most populous in the Midwest with around 3.7 million residents. The Minnesota State Capitol and the state government offices sit on a hill next to downtown Saint Paul overlooking a bend in the Mississippi River.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Paul,_Minnesota"}}, {"canonical_name": "Fertentes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "fertentes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Fertentes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Fertentes"], "contexts": ["mountain range connected to Serra do Espinha\u00e7o"]}, {"entity_id": "pregny", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Pregny", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Pregny"], "contexts": ["location near Geneva"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Fertentes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Pregny", "similarity": 0.916}], "id": 5607, "stable_key": "clu_04fe636bdc03adc0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Fermentes", "confidence": "medium", "type": "place", "country": "Brazil", "note": "The text provides two conflicting references: 'Fertentes' in the context of Brazil and 'Pregny' near Geneva. 'Fertentes' is more likely the intended referent given the other Brazilian locations, and may refer to a specific part of the Serra do Espinha\u00e7o."}}, {"canonical_name": "Rhodes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "rhodes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Rhodes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rhodes"], "contexts": ["island"]}, {"entity_id": "rhodes", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Rhodes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rhodes"], "contexts": ["island city, source of spice"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Rhodes", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Rhodes", "similarity": 0.846}], "id": 5608, "stable_key": "clu_cafee4b5b0c6cbf8", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Rhodes ( ; Greek: \u03a1\u03cc\u03b4\u03bf\u03c2, romanized: R\u00f3dos [\u02c8ro\u00f0os]) is the largest of Greece's Dodecanese islands and their historical capital; it is the ninth largest island in the  Mediterranean Sea. Administratively, Rhodes constitutes a separate municipality within the Rhodes regional unit, which is part of the South Aegean administrative region. The principal town of the island and seat of the municipality is the city of Rhodes, home to 50,636 inhabitants according to the 2011 census. By 2022, the island\u2019s population had grown to 125,113 people. Located northeast of Crete and southeast of Athens, Rhodes is often referred to by several nicknames: the \"Island of the Sun\" after its patron sun god Helios; \"The Pearl Island\"; and \"The Island of the Knights\", a reference to the Knights of Saint John of Jerusalem, who ruled the island from 1310 to 1522. Historically, Rhodes was famous for the Colossus of Rhodes, one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World."}}, {"canonical_name": "Vasiva", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "vasiva", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Vasiva", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Vasiva"], "contexts": ["city name"]}, {"entity_id": "vasiva", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Vasiva", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Vasiva"], "contexts": ["Mission where natives use inelegant terms"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Vasiva", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Vasiva", "similarity": 0.666}], "id": 5609, "stable_key": "clu_a3ffd644c747c80b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Vasiva", "confidence": "low", "type": "place", "country": "Brazil", "note": "The primary context suggests a Brazilian location, possibly a mission settlement. 'Vasiva' is likely a place name, though its modern equivalent is uncertain without further textual context."}}, {"canonical_name": "Itacolumite", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "itacolumite", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Itacolumite", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Itacolumite"], "contexts": ["type of rock"]}, {"entity_id": "quartzose_itacolumite", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "quartzose itacolumite", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["quartzose itacolumite"], "contexts": ["rock containing quartz and itacolumite"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Itacolumite", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "quartzose itacolumite", "similarity": 0.891}], "id": 5610, "stable_key": "clu_668299d64a0e7d8b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Itacolumite", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q128241969", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article"}}, {"canonical_name": "formations trap\u00e9ennes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "formations_trap\u00e9ennes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "formations trap\u00e9ennes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["formations trap\u00e9ennes"], "contexts": ["rock formations bordering plains"]}, {"entity_id": "mineral_formations", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "mineral formations", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["mineral formations"], "contexts": ["rocks and minerals"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "formations trap\u00e9ennes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "mineral formations", "similarity": 0.881}], "id": 5611, "stable_key": "clu_a809fce32eb0c2e0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Travertine formations", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "The French 'trap\u00e9ennes' likely refers to travertine, a form of limestone deposited by mineral springs."}}, {"canonical_name": "Allemagne septentrionale", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "allemagne_septentrionale", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Allemagne septentrionale", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Allemagne septentrionale"], "contexts": ["northern Germany"]}, {"entity_id": "southern_germany", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Southern Germany", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Southern Germany"], "contexts": ["geographic location"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Allemagne septentrionale", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Southern Germany", "similarity": 0.914}], "id": 5612, "stable_key": "clu_b7e5d0113531790c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Northern Germany", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Germany", "wikidata_id": "Q46613", "wikidata_description": "geographic region", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Northern_Germany", "wikipedia_extract": "Northern Germany (German: Norddeutschland, [\u02c8n\u0254\u0281td\u0254\u026a\u032ft\u0283lant] ) is a linguistic, geographic, socio-cultural and historic region in the northern part of Germany which includes the coastal states of Schleswig-Holstein, Mecklenburg-Vorpommern and Lower Saxony and the two city-states Hamburg and Bremen. It contrasts with Southern Germany, Western Germany, and Eastern Germany. Northern Germany generally refers to the Sprachraum area north of the Uerdingen and Benrath line isoglosses, where Low German dialects are spoken. These comprise the Low Saxon dialects in the west (including the Westphalian language area up to the Rhineland), the East Low German region along the Baltic coast with Western Pomerania, the Altmark and northern Brandenburg, as well as the North Low German dialects. Although from the 19th century onwards, the use of Standard German was strongly promoted especially by the Prussian administration, Low German dialects are still present in rural areas, with an estimated number of five to eight million active speakers. However, since World War II and the immigration of expellees from the former eastern territories of Germany, its prevalence has steadily reduced."}}, {"canonical_name": "topazes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "topazes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "topazes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["topazes"], "contexts": ["stones found on the coast"]}, {"entity_id": "topazes", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "topazes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["topazes"], "contexts": ["magnificent crystals"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "topazes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "topazes", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5613, "stable_key": "clu_c75b23090a14d63b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Topaz", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q134985", "wikidata_description": "nesosilicate mineral"}}, {"canonical_name": "Th\u00e8bes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "th\u00e8bes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Th\u00e8bes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Th\u00e8bes"], "contexts": ["ancient city in Egypt"]}, {"entity_id": "thebes", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Thebes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Thebes"], "contexts": ["ancient Egyptian city"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Th\u00e8bes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Thebes", "similarity": 0.819}], "id": 5614, "stable_key": "clu_457007ef5fcc5518", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Thebes", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Egypt", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_extract": "Thebes ( THEEBZ; Greek: \u0398\u03ae\u03b2\u03b1, Th\u00edva [\u02c8\u03b8iva]; Ancient Greek: \u0398\u1fc6\u03b2\u03b1\u03b9, Th\u00eabai [t\u02b0\u025b\u0302\u02d0bai\u032f]) is a city in Boeotia, Central Greece, and one of the oldest continuously inhabited cities in the world. It is the largest city in Boeotia and a major center for the region, along with Livadeia and Tanagra. It played an important role in Greek mythology, as the site of the stories of Cadmus, Oedipus, Dionysus, Heracles and others. One myth had the city founded by Agenor, which gave rise to the (now somewhat obscure) name \"Agenorids\" to denote Thebans. Archaeological excavations in and around Thebes have revealed a Mycenaean settlement and clay tablets written in the Linear B script, indicating the importance of the site in the Bronze Age. Thebes was the largest city of the ancient region of Boeotia and was the leader of the Boeotian confederacy.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thebes,_Greece"}}, {"canonical_name": "Gaillard", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "gaillard", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Gaillard", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Gaillard"], "contexts": ["author in Annales de Chimie"]}, {"entity_id": "tillard", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Tillard", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Tillard"], "contexts": ["on the sudden appearance of Sabrina"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Gaillard", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Tillard", "similarity": 0.866}], "id": 5615, "stable_key": "clu_a403b07f7d99630d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jean-Baptiste Gaillard", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1759, "death_year": 1834, "description": "French chemist and editor of Annales de Chimie", "note": "The name 'Tillard' in the second mention is likely a typo or misreading, given the context and the consistent mention of Gaillard in relation to Annales de Chimie.", "wikidata_id": "Q22209598", "wikidata_description": ""}}, {"canonical_name": "br\u00e8che", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "br\u00e8che", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "br\u00e8che", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["br\u00e8che"], "contexts": ["rock composed of fragments"]}, {"entity_id": "breccias", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "breccias", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["breccias"], "contexts": ["rock composed of angular fragments"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "br\u00e8che", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "breccias", "similarity": 0.9}], "id": 5616, "stable_key": "clu_569c5ee3b09b184b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "breccia", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral"}}, {"canonical_name": "m\u00e9sotype", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "m\u00e9sotype", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "m\u00e9sotype", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["m\u00e9sotype"], "contexts": ["mineral found in rocks"]}, {"entity_id": "mesotype", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Mesotype", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mesotype"], "contexts": ["mineral component of basalt"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "m\u00e9sotype", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Mesotype", "similarity": 0.849}], "id": 5617, "stable_key": "clu_950f00ee83c220d5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mesotype", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q6821653", "wikidata_description": "genus of insects", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mesotype", "wikipedia_extract": "Mesotype is a genus of moths in the family Geometridae erected by Jacob H\u00fcbner in 1825. It is sometimes included in Perizoma. Mesotype didymata (Linnaeus, 1758) \u2013 twin-spot carpet Mesotype parallelolineata (Retzius, 1783) Pitkin, Brian & Jenkins, Paul. \"Search results Family: Geometridae\". Butterflies and Moths of the World. Natural History Museum, London."}}, {"canonical_name": "dilatation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "dilatation", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "dilatation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["dilatation"], "contexts": ["process of expanding"]}, {"entity_id": "dilatations", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "dilatations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["dilatations"], "contexts": ["regions of decreased pressure in a fluid"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "dilatation", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "dilatations", "similarity": 0.898}], "id": 5618, "stable_key": "clu_ddf57abc6b673bf1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Dilatation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q73228459", "wikidata_description": "act of dilating as a biomedical investigative technique"}}, {"canonical_name": "Trans.", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "trans_", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Trans.", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Trans."], "contexts": ["abbreviation for publication"]}, {"entity_id": "trans_", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Trans.", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Trans."], "contexts": ["abbreviation for Philosophical Transactions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Trans.", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Trans.", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5619, "stable_key": "clu_9c56e7e02653d2a2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Philosophical Transactions", "confidence": "high", "type": "text"}}, {"canonical_name": "variations journali\u00e8res et p\u00e9riodiques", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "variations_journali\u00e8res_et_p\u00e9riodiques", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "variations journali\u00e8res et p\u00e9riodiques", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["variations journali\u00e8res et p\u00e9riodiques"], "contexts": ["daily and periodic changes"]}, {"entity_id": "daily_variations", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "daily variations", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["daily variations"], "contexts": ["changes occurring within a day"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "variations journali\u00e8res et p\u00e9riodiques", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "daily variations", "similarity": 0.902}], "id": 5620, "stable_key": "clu_e41009b57f818a03", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "diurnal variation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q1469559", "wikidata_description": "pattern that recurs every 24 hours as a result of one full rotation of the Earth with respect to the Sun"}}, {"canonical_name": "Mem. da Academia Real das sciencias", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "mem__da_academia_real_das_sciencias", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Mem. da Academia Real das sciencias", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mem. da Academia Real das sciencias"], "contexts": ["publication containing observations"]}, {"entity_id": "mem__de_l'academie_des_sciences", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Mem. de l'Academie des Sciences", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mem. de l'Academie des Sciences"], "contexts": ["French Academy of Sciences memoirs"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Mem. da Academia Real das sciencias", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Mem. de l'Academie des Sciences", "similarity": 0.886}], "id": 5621, "stable_key": "clu_26c572c2c170c748", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Memoirs of the Royal Academy of Sciences", "confidence": "high", "type": "text", "note": "This refers to the published memoirs of a scientific academy, likely the Portuguese Real Academia das Sci\u00eancias de Lisboa or the French Acad\u00e9mie des Sciences."}}, {"canonical_name": "Bridges", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "bridges", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Bridges", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Bridges"], "contexts": ["author on Negro Slavery"]}, {"entity_id": "br\u00fccke", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Br\u00fccke", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Br\u00fccke"], "contexts": ["noted observation about brainless hen"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Bridges", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Br\u00fccke", "similarity": 0.914}], "id": 5622, "stable_key": "clu_72c84044622b4fda", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Robert Bridges", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "English poet and critic, translator of poets.", "note": "The mention of 'Bridges' in the context of 'Negro Slavery' and 'Br\u00fccke' (meaning bridge in German, potentially a mistranslation or pun) is ambiguous. Robert Bridges is a prominent figure in English literature. The 'brainless hen' observation might be a different Bridges or a misattribution."}}, {"canonical_name": "institutions libres", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "institutions_libres", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "institutions libres", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["institutions libres"], "contexts": ["Free institutions"]}, {"entity_id": "free_institutions", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "free institutions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["free institutions"], "contexts": ["political systems based on liberty"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "institutions libres", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "free institutions", "similarity": 0.979}], "id": 5623, "stable_key": "clu_468498702e851b96", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "free institutions", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This refers to political or social structures that are not subject to arbitrary control and allow for individual liberty."}}, {"canonical_name": "Santo-Domingo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "santo-domingo", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Santo-Domingo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Santo-Domingo"], "contexts": ["colony name"]}, {"entity_id": "santo_domingo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Santo Domingo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Santo Domingo"], "contexts": ["island where it was planted"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Santo-Domingo", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Santo Domingo", "similarity": 0.697}], "id": 5624, "stable_key": "clu_2b02b8216a10fc1d-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Santo Domingo", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Dominican Republic", "note": "The cluster includes references to 'Sancto Domingo' and 'S. Domingos', clearly pointing to the city, which was a significant colonial center and a source of botanical materials.", "wikidata_id": "Q55591", "wikidata_description": "municipality of the Philippines in the province of Nueva Ecija", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Santo%20Domingo%2C%20Nueva%20Ecija", "wikipedia_extract": "Santo Domingo, officially the Municipality of Santo Domingo (Ilocano: Ili ti Santo Domingo; Pangasinan: Baley na Santo Domingo;Tagalog: Bayan ng Santo Domingo), is a municipality in the province of Nueva Ecija, Philippines. According to the 2024 census, it has a population of 63,750 people. The town is named after Saint Dominic, its patron saint. During the last quarter of the 17th century, Ilocos and Bulacan natives and Tagalogs from Cabanatuan migrated to Santo Domingo. They found in the settlement buri palms. Therefore, these people called the site, Pulong Buli."}}, {"canonical_name": "l'\u00eele Saint-Thomas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "l'\u00eele_saint-thomas", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "l'\u00eele Saint-Thomas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["l'\u00eele Saint-Thomas"], "contexts": ["island near magnetic equator"]}, {"entity_id": "island_of_st__thomas", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Island of St. Thomas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Island of St. Thomas"], "contexts": ["location of observed hurricane event"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "l'\u00eele Saint-Thomas", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Island of St. Thomas", "similarity": 0.964}], "id": 5625, "stable_key": "clu_ff2d59a9453283d7-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Saint Thomas", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United States Virgin Islands", "note": "Multiple references to 'Saint-Thomas' and 'St. Thomas' as an island, particularly in the context of Humboldt's works and its location near the magnetic equator, strongly indicate the Caribbean island.", "wikidata_id": "Q20851010", "wikidata_description": "census district of the United States Virgin Islands, includes the Island of Saint Thomas", "wikipedia_extract": "Thomas the Apostle also known as Didymus (Greek: \u0394\u03af\u03b4\u03c5\u03bc\u03bf\u03c2, romanized: D\u00eddymos 'twin'), was one of the Twelve Apostles of Jesus according to the New Testament. Thomas is commonly known as \"doubting Thomas\" because he initially doubted the resurrection of Jesus when he was told of it (as is related in the Gospel of John); he later confessed his faith (\"The lord of me and the God of me\") on seeing the places where the wounds appeared still fresh on the holy body of Jesus after the Crucifixion of Jesus. According to traditional accounts of the Saint Thomas Christians of Kerala, Thomas travelled outside the Roman Empire to preach the Gospel, travelling through southern India in the modern states of Kerala and Tamil Nadu, and eventually reached Muziris (modern-day North Paravur and Kodungalloor) in 52 AD. He started the Church of the East in the region around the Van Province, Edessa, and Hakkari regions of Upper Mesopotamia (modern-day southeastern Turkey), presumably sometime between mid-late 30s and 45, and spent many years evangelizing both Jews and non-christians in the area before heading further east in the late 40s. In 1258, some of the relics were brought to Ortona, in Abruzzo, Italy, where they have been held in the Church of Saint Thomas the Apostle. He is regarded as the patron saint of India among its Christian adherents, and the Feast of Saint Thomas on July 3 is celebrated as Indian Christians' Day.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_the_Apostle"}}, {"canonical_name": "M. de Rossel", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "m__de_rossel", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "M. de Rossel", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["M. de Rossel"], "contexts": ["had oscillation experiments"]}, {"entity_id": "m__de_rossel", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "M. de Rossel", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["M. de Rossel"], "contexts": ["performed oscillation experiments"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "M. de Rossel", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "M. de Rossel", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5626, "stable_key": "clu_30722dba8a56da3e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Jean-Fran\u00e7ois de La Rocque de Roqueburne", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "French naval officer and administrator", "note": "The name 'M. de Rossel' is ambiguous. While Jean-Fran\u00e7ois de La Rocque de Roqueburne is a possibility given the context of exploration and natural philosophy, the specific individual performing oscillation experiments is not definitively established with this name alone in the provided context. Further research into Humboldt's correspondents or scientific circles of the time might be needed."}}, {"canonical_name": "Charles Lyell", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "charles_lyell", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Charles Lyell", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Charles Lyell"], "contexts": ["author cited for geological work"]}, {"entity_id": "charles_lyell", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Charles Lyell", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Charles Lyell"], "contexts": ["author of Principles of Geology"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Charles Lyell", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Charles Lyell", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5627, "stable_key": "clu_122485cab8f60091", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Charles Lyell", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1797, "death_year": 1875, "description": "Geologist, author of Principles of Geology, influenced evolutionary thought.", "wikidata_id": "Q76126037", "wikidata_description": "Peerage person ID=549048"}}, {"canonical_name": "Basse-Egypte", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "basse-egypte", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Basse-Egypte", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Basse-Egypte"], "contexts": ["region with comparable temperature"]}, {"entity_id": "baixo_egipto", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "baixo Egipto", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["baixo Egipto"], "contexts": ["location of Ichneumon mentions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Basse-Egypte", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "baixo Egipto", "similarity": 0.881}], "id": 5628, "stable_key": "clu_c356deec2d8a9976", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lower Egypt", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Egypt", "wikipedia_extract": "In Egyptian history, the Upper and Lower Egypt period (also known as The Two Lands) was the final stage of prehistoric Egypt and directly preceded the unification of the realm. The conception of Egypt as the Two Lands was an example of the dualism in ancient Egyptian culture and frequently appeared in texts and imagery, including in the titles of Egyptian pharaohs. The Egyptian title zm\ua723-t\ua723wj (Egyptological pronunciation sema-tawy) is usually translated as \"Uniter of the Two Lands\" and was depicted as a human trachea entwined with the papyrus and lily plant. The trachea stood for unification, while the papyrus and lily plant represent Lower and Upper Egypt. Standard titles of the pharaoh included the prenomen, quite literally \"Of the Sedge and Bee\" (nswt-bjtj, the symbols of Upper and Lower Egypt) and \"lord of the Two Lands\" (written nb-t\ua723wj). Queens regnant were addressed as pharaohs and male.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Upper_and_Lower_Egypt"}}, {"canonical_name": "courans polaires", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "courans_polaires", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "courans polaires", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["courans polaires"], "contexts": ["ocean currents from polar regions"]}, {"entity_id": "bandes_polaires", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "bandes polaires", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["bandes polaires"], "contexts": ["French for polar bands"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "courans polaires", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "bandes polaires", "similarity": 0.899}], "id": 5629, "stable_key": "clu_678882cc16bef56c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Polar currents", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "Peuchet", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "peuchet", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Peuchet", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Peuchet"], "contexts": ["author of statistical work"]}, {"entity_id": "pouilet", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Pouilet", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Pouilet"], "contexts": ["on atmospheric electricity source"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Peuchet", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Pouilet", "similarity": 0.873}], "id": 5630, "stable_key": "clu_d3b77f0d7429b5c6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Andr\u00e9-Michel Guerry", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1802, "death_year": 1866, "description": "French statistician, studied crime and moral statistics.", "note": "The reference to 'Petiver' and 'Pequet' suggests a possible confusion or multiple figures being referenced. However, 'author of statistical work' strongly points to Guerry, whose foundational work in moral statistics fits the description.", "wikidata_id": "Q441271", "wikidata_description": "French lawyer and statistician (1802-1866)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andr%C3%A9-Michel%20Guerry", "wikipedia_extract": "Andr\u00e9-Michel Guerry (French: [\u0261\u025b\u0281i]; December 24, 1802 \u2013 April 9, 1866) was a French lawyer and amateur statistician. Together with Adolphe Quetelet he may be regarded as the founder of moral statistics which led to the development of criminology, sociology and ultimately, modern social science. Guerry was born in Tours, Indre-et-Loire, the only child of Michel Guerry, a building contractor, whose family had a long history as innkeepers, merchants, farmers and trades-people. About 1817-1820 he studied at the Imperial College of Tours (now the Lyceum Descartes, founded in 1807) and subsequently studied law at the University of Poitiers. About 1824\u20131825 he moved to Paris and was admitted to the bar as a royal advocate. Shortly after, he was employed by the Ministry of Justice."}}, {"canonical_name": "Papoux", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "papoux", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Papoux", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Papoux"], "contexts": ["indigenous people of New Guinea"]}, {"entity_id": "papuan", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Papuan", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Papuan"], "contexts": ["indigenous people of New Guinea"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Papoux", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Papuan", "similarity": 0.883}], "id": 5631, "stable_key": "clu_2187dc54d9f8da99", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Papuans", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Indigenous people of New Guinea", "wikidata_id": "Q213021", "wikidata_description": "species of bird"}}, {"canonical_name": "Documentos", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "documentos", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Documentos", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Documentos"], "contexts": ["collection of documents"]}, {"entity_id": "documentos", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "documentos", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["documentos"], "contexts": ["documents"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Documentos", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "documentos", "similarity": 0.972}], "id": 5632, "stable_key": "clu_9b6424b31e0d9863", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Documents", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "wikidata_id": "Q49848", "wikidata_description": "form for preservation of structured and identified information"}}, {"canonical_name": "catalanes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "catalanes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "catalanes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["catalanes"], "contexts": ["people from Catalonia"]}, {"entity_id": "catalan", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Catalan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Catalan"], "contexts": ["origin of a physician"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "catalanes", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Catalan", "similarity": 0.905}], "id": 5633, "stable_key": "clu_b20e484019766235", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Catalonia", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Spain", "wikidata_id": "Q90400112", "wikidata_description": "Administrative entity of the Kingdom of Spain created to replace the Principality of Catalonia after the Nueva Planta decrees and which lost its civil powers with the reforms of 1835, maintaining the military until its dissolution in 1931"}}, {"canonical_name": "Botanique agricole", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "botanique_agricole", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Botanique agricole", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Botanique agricole"], "contexts": ["study of agricultural plants"]}, {"entity_id": "botany", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Botany", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Botany"], "contexts": ["scientific study of plants"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Botanique agricole", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Botany", "similarity": 0.881}], "id": 5634, "stable_key": "clu_5f6e6f5d5f00d249-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Botany", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q441", "wikidata_description": "science of plant life", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Botany", "wikipedia_extract": "Botany, also called phytology or plant science, is the branch of natural science and biology that studies plants, especially their anatomy, taxonomy, and ecology. A botanist or plant scientist is a scientist who specialises in this field. \"Plant\" and \"botany\" may be defined more narrowly to include only land plants and their study, which is also known as phytology. Phytologists or botanists (in the strict sense) study approximately 410,000 species of land plants, including some 391,000 species of vascular plants (of which approximately 369,000 are flowering plants) and approximately 20,000 bryophytes. Botany originated as prehistoric herbalism to identify and later cultivate plants that were edible, poisonous, and medicinal, making it one of the first endeavours of human investigation. Medieval physic gardens, often attached to monasteries, contained plants that possibly had medicinal benefits."}}, {"canonical_name": "piezas", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "piezas", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "piezas", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["piezas"], "contexts": ["unit of equipment"]}, {"entity_id": "pe\u00e7as", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "pe\u00e7as", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["pe\u00e7as"], "contexts": ["chess pieces"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "piezas", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "pe\u00e7as", "similarity": 0.971}], "id": 5635, "stable_key": "clu_d8cc2aa1af1ee37c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "pieces", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "The context of 'chess pieces' is specific, but 'pieces' is a general term that can refer to various units or parts of equipment in a scientific context.", "wikidata_id": "Q18593264", "wikidata_description": "defines an item, such as an object or document, that is a member of a collection or part of a repository"}}, {"canonical_name": "mine", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "mine", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "mine", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["mine"], "contexts": ["place for extracting minerals"]}, {"entity_id": "mine", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "mine", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["mine"], "contexts": ["excavation for extracting minerals"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "mine", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "mine", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5636, "stable_key": "clu_12f87ca4d30ff1c0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "mine", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "global"}}, {"canonical_name": "grammes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "grammes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "grammes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["grammes"], "contexts": ["unit of mass"]}, {"entity_id": "grams", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "grams", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["grams"], "contexts": ["unit of mass used in experiments"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "grammes", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "grams", "similarity": 0.898}], "id": 5637, "stable_key": "clu_bd7ec5be32db5437", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "The gram (originally gramme; SI unit symbol g) is a unit of mass in the International System of Units (SI) equal to one thousandth of a kilogram. Originally defined in 1795 as \"the absolute weight of a volume of pure water equal to the cube of the hundredth part of a metre [1 cm3], and at the temperature of melting ice\", the defining temperature (0 \u00b0C) was later changed to the temperature of maximum density of water (approximately 4 \u00b0C). Subsequent redefinitions agree with this original definition to within 30 parts per million (0.003%), with the maximum density of water remaining very close to 1 g/cm3, as shown by modern measurements. By the late 19th century, there was an effort to make the base unit the kilogram and the gram a derived unit. In 1960, the new International System of Units defined a gram as one thousandth of a kilogram (i.e., one gram is 1\u00d710\u22123 kg). The kilogram, as of 2019, is defined by the International Bureau of Weights and Measures from the metre, the second, and from the fixed numerical value of the Planck constant (h)."}}, {"canonical_name": "proc\u00e9d\u00e9s chimiques", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "proc\u00e9d\u00e9s_chimiques", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "proc\u00e9d\u00e9s chimiques", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["proc\u00e9d\u00e9s chimiques"], "contexts": ["chemical processes"]}, {"entity_id": "chemical_processes", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "chemical processes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["chemical processes"], "contexts": ["energy generation on the sun's surface"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "proc\u00e9d\u00e9s chimiques", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "chemical processes", "similarity": 0.894}], "id": 5638, "stable_key": "clu_159dc61f51ca1074", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "chemical processes", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "substances alcalines", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "substances_alcalines", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "substances alcalines", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["substances alcalines"], "contexts": ["alkaline substances"]}, {"entity_id": "alkaline", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "alkaline", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["alkaline"], "contexts": ["type of phosphate discussed"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "substances alcalines", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "alkaline", "similarity": 0.921}], "id": 5639, "stable_key": "clu_58560106eca9a584", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Alkaline substances", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical"}}, {"canonical_name": "pueros", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "pueros", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "pueros", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["pueros"], "contexts": ["farm workers"]}, {"entity_id": "puer", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "puer", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["puer"], "contexts": ["child"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "pueros", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "puer", "similarity": 0.845}], "id": 5640, "stable_key": "clu_6e4c523a1876b52f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Boy", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Young male, often referring to a farm laborer or a child.", "wikidata_id": "Q6581097", "wikidata_description": "to be used in \"sex or gender\" (P21) to indicate that the human subject is a male or \"semantic gender\" (P10339) to indicate that a word refers to a male person"}}, {"canonical_name": "bocoy", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "VESSEL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "bocoy", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "bocoy", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "VESSEL", "count": 1, "variants": ["bocoy"], "contexts": ["container for bariles"]}, {"entity_id": "boccie", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "boccie", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "VESSEL", "count": 1, "variants": ["boccie"], "contexts": ["bottles for collecting distillate"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "bocoy", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "boccie", "similarity": 0.91}], "id": 5641, "stable_key": "clu_c2eb1292a81b57e2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Barrel", "confidence": "medium", "type": "object", "note": "The term 'bocoy' in Spanish and Portuguese often refers to a large barrel or cask, particularly for wine or spirits. 'Boccie' in Italian can also refer to a vessel.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}}, {"canonical_name": "fraude", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "fraude", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "fraude", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["fraude"], "contexts": ["illegal activity related to customs"]}, {"entity_id": "fraudentamente", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "fraudentamente", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["fraudentamente"], "contexts": ["deceptive practice"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "fraude", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "fraudentamente", "similarity": 0.896}], "id": 5642, "stable_key": "clu_48024ba01f6a433b-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Falsification", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the act of making something counterfeit or imitation.", "wikidata_id": "Q1332286", "wikidata_description": "process of making, adapting, or imitating objects, statistics, or documents with the intent to deceive", "wikipedia_extract": "Preference falsification is the act of misrepresenting a preference under perceived public pressure. It involves publicly expressing a preference that differs from one's underlying privately held preference (or simply, a public preference at odds with one\u2019s private preference). People frequently convey to each other preferences that differ from what they would communicate privately under credible cover of anonymity (such as in opinion surveys to researchers or pollsters). Techniques such as list experiments can be used to uncover preference falsification. The term was coined by economist and political scientist Timur Kuran in a 1987 article, \"Chameleon voters and public choice.\" On controversial matters that induce preference falsification, he showed there, widely disliked policies may appear popular. The distribution of public preferences, which Kuran defines as public opinion, may differ greatly from private opinion, which is the distribution of private preferences known only to individuals themselves.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forgery"}}, {"canonical_name": "Cabrera", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "cabrera", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Cabrera", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cabrera"], "contexts": ["interpreted indigenous words"]}, {"entity_id": "cabreir", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Cabreir", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cabreir"], "contexts": ["author of Compend. var. remed."]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Cabrera", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Cabreir", "similarity": 0.868}], "id": 5643, "stable_key": "clu_2a656180b921ae2c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cabrera", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Author of medical compendium, interpreter of indigenous words.", "note": "Likely refers to a specific scholar, but the exact individual requires further context from the source texts.", "wikidata_id": "Q5914784", "wikidata_description": "Spanish noble family"}}, {"canonical_name": "fort", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "fort", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "fort", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["fort"], "contexts": ["defensive structure"]}, {"entity_id": "fortaleza", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "fortaleza", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["fortaleza"], "contexts": ["military structure"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "fort", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "fortaleza", "similarity": 0.931}], "id": 5644, "stable_key": "clu_aec5106c43dd5375", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "A fortification (also called a fort, fortress, fastness, or stronghold) is a military construction designed for the defense of territories in warfare, and is used to establish rule in a region during peacetime. The term is derived from Latin fortis (\"strong\") and facere (\"to make\"). From very early history to modern times, defensive walls have often been necessary for cities to survive in an ever-changing world of invasion and conquest. Some settlements in the Indus Valley Civilization were the first small cities to be fortified. In ancient Greece, large cyclopean stone walls fitted without mortar had been built in Mycenaean Greece, such as the ancient site of Mycenae. A Greek phrourion was a fortified collection of buildings used as a military garrison, and is the equivalent of the Roman castellum or fortress."}}, {"canonical_name": "pecado", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "pecado", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "pecado", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["pecado"], "contexts": ["religious concept"]}, {"entity_id": "pecado", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "pecado", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["pecado"], "contexts": ["sin, a moral concept"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "pecado", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "pecado", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5645, "stable_key": "clu_a62441cf47487cf5", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Pecado is a monotypic genus of dwarf spiders containing the single species, Pecado impudicus. It was first described by G. Hormiga & N. Scharff in 2005, and has only been found in Spain, Morocco, and Algeria."}}, {"canonical_name": "peines", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "peines", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "peines", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["peines"], "contexts": ["punishments or suffering"]}, {"entity_id": "punishment", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "punishment", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["punishment"], "contexts": ["consequence for wrongdoing"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "peines", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "punishment", "similarity": 0.889}], "id": 5646, "stable_key": "clu_87396b209347d5fd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "punishment", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'peines' also has a broader meaning of suffering or hardship, but in the context of 'consequence for wrongdoing', punishment is the most fitting.", "wikidata_id": "Q3544090", "wikidata_description": "facet of operant conditioning; change in a human or animal's surroundings that occurs after a given behavior or response which reduces the likelihood of that behavior occurring again in the future"}}, {"canonical_name": "magistrat", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "magistrat", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "magistrat", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["magistrat"], "contexts": ["official to whom a slave can appeal"]}, {"entity_id": "magistrate", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "magistrate", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["magistrate"], "contexts": ["civil authority figure"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "magistrat", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "magistrate", "similarity": 0.99}], "id": 5647, "stable_key": "clu_00db6ea671606faa", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "magistrate", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Civil authority figure, often with judicial or administrative powers.", "wikidata_id": "Q16533", "wikidata_description": "official who presides over court proceedings"}}, {"canonical_name": "gain", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "gain", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "gain", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["gain"], "contexts": ["profit or advantage"]}, {"entity_id": "gain", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "gain", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["gain"], "contexts": ["act of acquiring something"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "gain", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "gain", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5648, "stable_key": "clu_ca862793708183b8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "gain", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q3403879", "wikidata_description": "increase in a person's total body mass"}}, {"canonical_name": "Chio", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "chio", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Chio", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Chio"], "contexts": ["location of massacres"]}, {"entity_id": "chio", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Chio", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Chio"], "contexts": ["island where Almaciga grows"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Chio", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Chio", "similarity": 0.711}], "id": 5649, "stable_key": "clu_725f6c7ababa4811", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Chios", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Greece", "wikidata_id": "Q160483", "wikidata_description": "Greek island situated in the Aegean Sea"}}, {"canonical_name": "Mozambique", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "mozambique", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Mozambique", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mozambique"], "contexts": ["city in Mozambique"]}, {"entity_id": "mozambique", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Mozambique", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mozambique"], "contexts": ["port city, geographical reference"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Mozambique", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Mozambique", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5650, "stable_key": "clu_4d8b2410793434bf", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mozambique", "confidence": "low", "type": "place", "description": "Port city and coastal location, also a river and islands.", "note": "This cluster appears to conflate several distinct geographical locations including the city/country of Mozambique, the Moluccas islands, and a river named Motagua.", "wikipedia_extract": "Mozambique, officially the Republic of Mozambique, is a country located in Southeast Africa bordered by the Indian Ocean to the east, Tanzania to the north, Malawi and Zambia to the northwest, Zimbabwe to the west, and Eswatini and South Africa to the south and southwest. The sovereign state is separated from the Comoros, Mayotte, and Madagascar through the Mozambique Channel to the east. The capital and largest city is Maputo. Between the 7th and 11th centuries, a series of Swahili port towns developed in the area, which contributed to the development of a distinct Swahili culture and dialect. In the late medieval period, these towns were frequented by traders from Somalia, Ethiopia, Egypt, Arabia, Persia, and India. The voyage of Vasco da Gama in 1498 marked the arrival of the Portuguese, who began a gradual process of colonisation and settlement in 1505."}}, {"canonical_name": "Isabelle", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "isabelle", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Isabelle", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Isabelle"], "contexts": ["queen addressed by Columbus"]}, {"entity_id": "queen_isabella", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Queen Isabella", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PATRON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Queen Isabella"], "contexts": ["recipient of Columbus's letter"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Isabelle", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Queen Isabella", "similarity": 0.947}], "id": 5651, "stable_key": "clu_2f713c7980a05454", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Isabella I of Castile", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1451, "death_year": 1504, "description": "Queen of Castile, patron of Columbus's voyages.", "wikidata_id": "Q45859", "wikidata_description": "queen of the Crown of Castile, from 1474 to 1504; first queen of a dynastically-united Spain, from 1479 to 1504 (1451\u20131504)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isabella_I_of_Castile", "wikipedia_extract": "Isabella I (Spanish: Isabel I; also known as Isabella the Catholic Spanish: Isabel la Cat\u00f3lica; 22 April 1451 \u2013 26 November 1504) was Queen of Castile and Le\u00f3n from 1474 until her death in 1504. She was also Queen of Aragon from 1479 until her death as the wife of King Ferdinand II. Reigning together over a dynastically unified Spain, Isabella and Ferdinand are known as the Catholic Monarchs. Her reign marked the end of the Reconquista and also the start of the Spanish Empire, allowing Spain to dominate European politics for the next century. Isabella's marriage to Ferdinand of Aragon in 1469 created the basis of the de facto unification of Spain. With Ferdinand's help, she won the War of the Castilian Succession, securing her position as Queen of Castile."}}, {"canonical_name": "Apis", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "apis", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Apis", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "INSECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Apis"], "contexts": ["genus of bees"]}, {"entity_id": "apis", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Apis", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Apis"], "contexts": ["deity represented by a bull"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Apis", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Apis", "similarity": 0.783}], "id": 5652, "stable_key": "clu_25b12638713658bd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "aphids", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "description": "Small, soft-bodied insects that feed on plant sap.", "note": "'Apis' refers to bees, while 'Aphides' (plural of Aphid) are a distinct group of insects. The context of 'plant-sucking insects' confirms 'aphids'.", "wikipedia_extract": "Aphids are small sap-sucking insects in the family Aphididae. Common names include greenfly and blackfly, although individuals within a species can vary widely in color. The group includes the fluffy white woolly aphids. A typical life cycle involves flightless females giving live birth to female nymphs\u2014who may also be already pregnant, an adaptation scientists call telescoping generations\u2014without the involvement of males. Maturing rapidly, females breed profusely so that the number of these insects multiplies quickly. Winged females may develop later in the season, allowing the insects to colonize new plants."}}, {"canonical_name": "Providence", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "providence", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Providence", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Providence"], "contexts": ["origin of corsairs"]}, {"entity_id": "providence", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Providence", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Providence"], "contexts": ["city where money was drawn"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Providence", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Providence", "similarity": 0.814}], "id": 5653, "stable_key": "clu_f2fe880228b2d138", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Providence", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United States", "note": "The context 'city where money was drawn' strongly suggests Providence, Rhode Island, a significant port.", "wikidata_id": "Q18383", "wikidata_description": "capital city of Rhode Island, United States", "wikipedia_extract": "Providence ( ) is the capital and most populous city of the U.S. state of Rhode Island. It is the third-most populous city in New England with a population of 190,934 at the 2020 census, while the Providence metropolitan area extending into Massachusetts has approximately 1.7 million residents, the 39th-largest metropolitan area in the U.S. It is the county seat of Providence County. Providence is one of the oldest cities in New England, founded in 1636 by Reformed Baptist theologian Roger Williams, a religious exile from the Massachusetts Bay Colony. He named the area in honor of \"God's merciful Providence\" which he believed was responsible for revealing such a haven for him and his followers.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Providence,_Rhode_Island"}}, {"canonical_name": "Angle de hauteur", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "angle_de_hauteur", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Angle de hauteur", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Angle de hauteur"], "contexts": ["measurement of elevation"]}, {"entity_id": "horizontal_angle", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "horizontal angle", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["horizontal angle"], "contexts": ["angle at earth's center"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Angle de hauteur", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "horizontal angle", "similarity": 0.899}], "id": 5654, "stable_key": "clu_6366ffa78eefaf7b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "angle of elevation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "Piazzi", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "piazzi", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Piazzi", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Piazzi"], "contexts": ["astronomer whose catalogue was used"]}, {"entity_id": "piazzi", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Piazzi", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Piazzi"], "contexts": ["measured heat of the moon"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Piazzi", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Piazzi", "similarity": 0.739}], "id": 5655, "stable_key": "clu_c932ba3928f7c7b3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Giuseppe Piazzi", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1746, "death_year": 1826, "description": "Italian astronomer who catalogued stars and studied heat from the moon."}}, {"canonical_name": "Maroc", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "maroc", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Maroc", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Maroc"], "contexts": ["country with sugar consumption"]}, {"entity_id": "marraecos", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Marraecos", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Marraecos"], "contexts": ["kings of Morocco"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Maroc", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Marraecos", "similarity": 0.887}], "id": 5656, "stable_key": "clu_a42b246752e77e5a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Morocco", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Morocco", "wikidata_id": "Q1028", "wikidata_description": "sovereign state in North Africa"}}, {"canonical_name": "zone", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "zone", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "zone", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["zone"], "contexts": ["latitudinal zone"]}, {"entity_id": "zone", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "zone", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["zone"], "contexts": ["stretching from Eta Arg\u00fbs"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "zone", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "zone", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5657, "stable_key": "clu_ad26d79a5641a9fe", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Zone", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to geographical or celestial zones, often related to latitude or temperature."}}, {"canonical_name": "Mexican Sea", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "mexican_sea", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Mexican Sea", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mexican Sea"], "contexts": ["sea"]}, {"entity_id": "mexican_gulf", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Mexican Gulf", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Mexican Gulf"], "contexts": ["Gulf Stream pours from it"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Mexican Sea", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Mexican Gulf", "similarity": 0.909}], "id": 5658, "stable_key": "clu_284b8f0e2efecc13", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Gulf of Mexico", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Mexico, United States, Cuba", "wikidata_id": "Q2673697", "wikidata_description": "species of fish", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gulf%20smooth-hound", "wikipedia_extract": "The Gulf smooth-hound (Mustelus sinusmexicanus) is a houndshark of the family Triakidae, found on the continental shelves of the tropical western central Atlantic. The reproduction of this houndshark is placental viviparous. This species has a long slender body, a plain grey/brown dorsum, pale/white ventrum and a large and rounded dorsal fin. The fins have a pale to white trailing margin fading towards adulthood. The caudal fin is deeply notched; its teeth are flat and pale. This type of shark can be found in the continental shelf between depths of 36 to 229 m (118 to 751 ft)."}}, {"canonical_name": "observations thermo m\u00e9triques", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "observations_thermo_m\u00e9triques", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "observations thermo m\u00e9triques", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["observations thermo m\u00e9triques"], "contexts": ["measurements of temperature"]}, {"entity_id": "thermometrical_measurements", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "thermometrical measurements", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["thermometrical measurements"], "contexts": ["method for measuring temperature"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "observations thermo m\u00e9triques", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "thermometrical measurements", "similarity": 0.887}], "id": 5659, "stable_key": "clu_3f311ec408d994ba", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Thermometric measurements", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "Caliaode Lima", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "caliaode_lima", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Caliaode Lima", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Caliaode Lima"], "contexts": ["part of a voyage route"]}, {"entity_id": "callao_de_lima", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Callao de Lima", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Callao de Lima"], "contexts": ["harbor in Peru"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Caliaode Lima", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Callao de Lima", "similarity": 0.901}], "id": 5660, "stable_key": "clu_44d9068467cd582e-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Callao", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Peru", "wikidata_id": "Q105037", "wikidata_description": "city \u200b\u200band main seaport of Peru", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Callao", "wikipedia_extract": "Callao (Spanish pronunciation: [ka\u02c8\u029da.o]) is a seaside city and constitutional province in Peru. Located next to the Pacific Ocean, it forms part of the Lima metropolitan area alongside Lima province. Its autonomous status dates back to 1836, when it was declared a Littoral Province of the Peru\u2013Bolivian Confederation, after which it was declared a Constitutional Province by the government of Ram\u00f3n Castilla in 1857. Callao is Peru's chief seaport and home to its main airport, Jorge Ch\u00e1vez International Airport. Founded in 1537 by the Spaniards, the city has a long naval history as one of the main ports in Spanish America and the Pacific, having been a vital Spanish town of the Viceroyalty of Peru. Central Callao is about 15 km (9.3 mi) west of the Historic Centre of Lima."}}, {"canonical_name": "Rhizophora Mangle", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "rhizophora_mangle", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Rhizophora Mangle", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "TREE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rhizophora Mangle"], "contexts": ["scientific name for a plant"]}, {"entity_id": "rhizophora_mangle", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Rhizophora mangle", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Rhizophora mangle"], "contexts": ["low bushes found on coasts"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Rhizophora Mangle", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Rhizophora mangle", "similarity": 0.901}], "id": 5661, "stable_key": "clu_0b6633efbf6fd12c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Red Mangrove", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Rhizophora mangle Linnaeus", "family": "Rhizophoraceae", "wikidata_id": "Q853914", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhizophora_mangle", "wikipedia_extract": "Rhizophora mangle, also known as the red mangrove, is a salt-tolerant, small-to-medium sized evergreen tree restricted to coastal, estuarine ecosystems along the southern portions of North America, the Caribbean as well as Central America and tropical West Africa. Its viviparous \"seeds\", in actuality called propagules, become fully mature plants before dropping off the parent tree. These are dispersed by water until eventually embedding in the shallows. Rhizophora mangle grows on aerial prop roots, which arch above the water level, giving stands of this tree the characteristic \"mangrove\" appearance. It is a valuable plant in Florida, Louisiana, and Texas coastal ecosystems. The name refers to the red colour on the inner part of its roots when halved, so it does not display any red colour in its regular appearance."}}, {"canonical_name": "Hydrographie", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "hydrographie", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Hydrographie", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hydrographie"], "contexts": ["field of study for the work"]}, {"entity_id": "hydrography", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "hydrography", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["hydrography"], "contexts": ["study of water bodies"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Hydrographie", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "hydrography", "similarity": 0.887}], "id": 5662, "stable_key": "clu_bd44b08cddac8c16", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hydrography", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "P. speciosa", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "p__speciosa", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "P. speciosa", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["P. speciosa"], "contexts": ["plant species Cluipo"]}, {"entity_id": "s__speciosa", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "S. speciosa", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["S. speciosa"], "contexts": ["flowering plant near snow line"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "P. speciosa", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "S. speciosa", "similarity": 0.954}], "id": 5663, "stable_key": "clu_fc6941ace3ffc0e0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Species Plantarum", "confidence": "medium", "type": "text", "note": "The 'P.' and 'S.' likely refer to abbreviations of a genus, and 'speciosa' is an epithet. Without more context or a known work by Humboldt where he uses such abbreviations, identifying a specific plant is difficult. 'Species Plantarum' is a likely candidate for 'P. speciosa' due to its significance in botany and the abbreviation 'P.' sometimes used for binomial nomenclature.", "wikidata_id": "Q21856070", "wikidata_description": "Second edition of Linnaeus' Species Plantarum", "wikipedia_extract": "Parkia speciosa, the bitter bean, twisted cluster bean, sator bean, stink bean, or petai is a plant of the genus Parkia in the family Fabaceae. It bears long, flat edible beans with bright green seeds the size and shape of plump almonds which have a rather peculiar smell, similar to, but stronger than that of the shiitake mushroom, due to sulfur-containing compounds also found in shiitake, truffles and cabbage. The petai tree can grow to about 30 metres. It bears flowers in a light bulb-shaped mass at the end of long stalks. The flowers secrete a nectar that attracts bats and other pollinators. The fruits emerge as long, twisted, translucent pods in a cluster of seven or eight pods.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parkia_speciosa"}}, {"canonical_name": "solfatares", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "solfatares", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "solfatares", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["solfatares"], "contexts": ["volcanic vents emitting sulfurous gases"]}, {"entity_id": "solfataras", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "solfataras", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["solfataras"], "contexts": ["volcanic vents emitting gases"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "solfatares", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "solfataras", "similarity": 0.923}], "id": 5664, "stable_key": "clu_55191d98e16954ce", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "solfatara", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Italy", "note": "Solfataras are specific geological formations, but the term can also refer to the general phenomenon of volcanic vents emitting sulfurous gases.", "wikipedia_extract": "Pozzuoli (Italian: [pot\u02c8tsw\u0254\u02d0li]; Neapolitan: Pozzule [p\u0259t\u02c8tsu\u02d0l\u0259]; Latin: Puteoli) is a city and comune (municipality) of the Metropolitan City of Naples, in the Italian region of Campania. It is the main city of the Phlegrean Peninsula. Pozzuoli began as the Greek colony of Dicaearchia (Greek: \u0394\u03b9\u03ba\u03b1\u03b9\u03b1\u03c1\u03c7\u03af\u03b1) founded in about 531 BC in Magna Graecia with the consent of nearby Cumae when refugees from Samos escaped from the tyranny of Polycrates. The Samnites occupied Dicaearchia in 421 BC after conquering Cumae and may have changed its name to Fistelia. It enjoyed considerable political and commercial autonomy favoured by the excellent position of its port with the Campanian hinterland. The Roman occupation of Campania after the end of the 1st Samnite War from 341 BC marked the start of the Romanisation of the Greek-Samnite city.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pozzuoli"}}, {"canonical_name": "Gokmali", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "gokmali", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Gokmali", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Gokmali"], "contexts": ["location of an igneous eruption"]}, {"entity_id": "jokmali", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Jokmali", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Jokmali"], "contexts": ["location of a mud volcano"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Gokmali", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Jokmali", "similarity": 0.88}], "id": 5665, "stable_key": "clu_c173b243adb10b02", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Gokmali", "confidence": "low", "type": "place", "country": "Unknown", "note": "The location 'Gokmali' or 'Jokmali' is not readily identifiable in historical or modern geographic records. It may be a transcription error or a very obscure location. Given its association with volcanic activity, it might be a specific volcanic feature."}}, {"canonical_name": "Pena", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "pena", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Pena", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Pena"], "contexts": ["cascade near Mariquita"]}, {"entity_id": "pena", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Pena", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Pena"], "contexts": ["N. Senhora da Ribeyra de Pena"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Pena", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Pena", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5666, "stable_key": "clu_b6869537b30f9e9e", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Penna (originally known as Penneru, also known as Pinakini, Penneru, Penner, Pennar, Pennai) is a river of southern India. After originating from Nandi hills, it flows as two different streams, one in North and South directions. The Penna rises in the Nandi Hills in Chikkaballapur District of Karnataka state, and runs north and east through the states of Karnataka and Andhra Pradesh to empty into bay of bengal in Andhra Pradesh. It is 597 kilometres (371 mi) long, with a drainage basin covering 55,213 km2: 6,937 km2 in Karnataka and 48,276 km2 in Andhra Pradesh. Along with this main stream is another stream south towards Tamil Nadu with the name Then Pennai or south Pennar which further moves towards the east to empty into the Bay of Bengal. The Penna river basin lies in the rain shadow region of Eastern Ghats and receives 500 mm average rainfall annually."}}, {"canonical_name": "administrative", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "administrative", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "administrative", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["administrative"], "contexts": ["Relating to governance"]}, {"entity_id": "administrative", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "administrative", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["administrative"], "contexts": ["relating to the management of public affairs"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "administrative", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "administrative", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5667, "stable_key": "clu_5ee0a299c6012fc6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Administrative", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q5283295", "wikidata_description": "management of organization"}}, {"canonical_name": "alembics", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "alembics", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "alembics", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 1, "variants": ["alembics"], "contexts": ["distillation apparatus"]}, {"entity_id": "alembic", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "alembic", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "INSTRUMENT", "count": 1, "variants": ["alembic"], "contexts": ["implied distillation apparatus"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "alembics", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "alembic", "similarity": 0.955}], "id": 5668, "stable_key": "clu_1acf142c10587a48-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Alambic", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "description": "A type of distillation apparatus used for separating liquids.", "wikipedia_extract": "An alembic (from Arabic: \u0627\u0644\u0625\u0646\u0628\u064a\u0642, romanized: al-inb\u012bq, originating from Ancient Greek: \u1f04\u03bc\u03b2\u03b9\u03be, romanized: ambix, 'cup, beaker') is an alchemical still consisting of two vessels connected by a tube, used for distillation of liquids. The complete distilling apparatus consists of three parts: the \"cucurbit\" (Arabic: \u1e33ar\u02bfa; Greek: \u03b2\u1fd6\u03ba\u03bf\u03c2, b\u00eekos), the still pot containing the liquid to be distilled, which is heated by a flame the \"head\" or \"cap\" (\u0625\u0650\u0646\u0652\u0628\u0650\u064a\u0642\u200e, \u02beinb\u012bq; Greek \u1f04\u03bc\u03b2\u03b9\u03be, ambix) which fits over the mouth of the cucurbit to receive the vapors, with an attached downward-sloping \"tube\" (\u03c3\u03c9\u03bb\u03ae\u03bd, s\u014dl\u0113n) the \"receiver\" (\u0642\u064e\u0627\u0628\u0650\u0644\u064e\u0629, q\u0101bila; \u1f04\u03b3\u03b3\u03bf\u03c2, angos or \u03c6\u03b9\u03ac\u03bb\u03b7, phial\u0113) container In the case of another distilling vessel, the retort, the \"cap\" and the \"cucurbit\" have been combined to form a single vessel. The anbik is also called the ra\u02bes (the Arabic word ra\u02bes means \"head\") of the cucurbit. The liquid in the cucurbit is heated or boiled; the vapour rises into the anbik, where it cools by contact with the walls and condenses, running down the spout into the receiver. A modern descendant of the alembic is the pot still, used to produce distilled beverages. Dioscorides's ambix, described in his De materia medica (c. 50 C.E.), is a helmet-shaped lid for gathering condensed mercury."}}, {"canonical_name": "\u00e9picycles", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "\u00e9picycles", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "\u00e9picycles", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["\u00e9picycles"], "contexts": ["celestial mechanics concept"]}, {"entity_id": "epicycles", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "epicycles", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["epicycles"], "contexts": ["circles used in ancient astronomical models"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "\u00e9picycles", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "epicycles", "similarity": 0.853}], "id": 5669, "stable_key": "clu_663e80ad56c41de7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Epicycles", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "steppe des Kirghises", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "steppe_des_kirghises", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "steppe des Kirghises", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["steppe des Kirghises"], "contexts": ["steppe visited for observations"]}, {"entity_id": "kirghis_steppes", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Kirghis Steppes", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Kirghis Steppes"], "contexts": ["steppe inhabited by Kirghis"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "steppe des Kirghises", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Kirghis Steppes", "similarity": 0.943}], "id": 5670, "stable_key": "clu_a303765edcf7e2a8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Kazakh Steppe", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Kazakhstan", "note": "The term 'steppe des Kirghises' directly translates to 'Steppe of the Kirghiz,' which is historically and geographically synonymous with the vast Kazakh Steppe.", "wikidata_id": "Q602925", "wikidata_description": "Ecoregion of grasslands that encompass most of the eastern segment of the Eurasian steppe", "wikipedia_extract": "The Kazakh Steppe (Kazakh: \u049a\u0430\u0437\u0430\u049b \u0434\u0430\u043b\u0430\u0441\u044b, romanized: Qazaq dalasy [q\u0251\u02c8z\u0251q d\u0251\u026b\u0251\u02c8s\u0259]), also known as the Great Steppe or Great Dala (Kazakh: \u04b0\u043b\u044b \u0434\u0430\u043b\u0430, romanized: \u016aly dala [\u028a\u02c8\u026b\u0264 d\u0251\u02c8\u026b\u0251]), is a vast region of open grassland in Central Asia, covering areas in northern Kazakhstan and adjacent areas of Russia. It lies east of the Pontic\u2013Caspian steppe and west of the Emin Valley steppe, with which it forms the central and western part of the Eurasian steppe. The Kazakh Steppe is an ecoregion of the temperate grasslands, savannas, and shrublands biome in the Palearctic realm. Before the mid-19th century, it was called the Kirghiz steppe, 'Kirghiz' being an old Russian word for the Kazakhs. The steppe extends more than 2,200 km (1,400 mi) from the east of the Caspian Depression and north of the Aral Sea, all the way to the Altai Mountains. It is the largest dry steppe region on earth, covering approximately 804,450 km2 (310,600 sq mi).", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kazakh_Steppe"}}, {"canonical_name": "Morlet", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "morlet", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Morlet", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Morlet"], "contexts": ["scholar who fixed magnetic equator position"]}, {"entity_id": "morlet", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Morlet", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Morlet"], "contexts": ["contributor to Acad. Roy. des Sciences"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Morlet", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Morlet", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5671, "stable_key": "clu_3e253ac876a096cc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Morlet", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Scholar who fixed magnetic equator position, member of Acad. Roy. des Sciences.", "note": "Assuming the 'Morlet' mentioned is the same individual across texts.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}}, {"canonical_name": "Guada\u00efama", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "guada\u00efama", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Guada\u00efama", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Guada\u00efama"], "contexts": ["location of geological observation"]}, {"entity_id": "guacamayo", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Guacamayo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Guacamayo"], "contexts": ["volcano in Quixos province"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Guada\u00efama", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Guacamayo", "similarity": 0.904}], "id": 5672, "stable_key": "clu_05baa05475b9c800", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Guayacan", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Ecuador", "note": "Humboldt's 'Guada\u00efama' and 'Guacamayo' likely refer to the Guayacan area, a region known for its geological features and proximity to volcanoes in the Quijos province of Ecuador."}}, {"canonical_name": "Porphyre schisteux", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "porphyre_schisteux", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Porphyre schisteux", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["Porphyre schisteux"], "contexts": ["type of schistose porphyry rock"]}, {"entity_id": "granitic_porphyry", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "granitic porphyry", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["granitic porphyry"], "contexts": ["porphyry with feldspathic base"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Porphyre schisteux", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "granitic porphyry", "similarity": 0.913}], "id": 5673, "stable_key": "clu_283dc8dc4f00c4ab", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Schistose porphyry", "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "note": "Schistose porphyry is a type of igneous rock with phenocrysts in a fine-grained groundmass, often exhibiting a foliated or schistose texture. 'Granitic porphyry' is a similar classification."}}, {"canonical_name": "Zurich", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "zurich", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Zurich", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Zurich"], "contexts": ["location of observation"]}, {"entity_id": "zurich", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Zurich", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Zurich"], "contexts": ["location for air strata data"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Zurich", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Zurich", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5674, "stable_key": "clu_1041e55061e90485", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Zurich", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Switzerland", "wikidata_id": "Q72", "wikidata_description": "most populous city in Switzerland and capital of the canton of Zurich", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zurich", "wikipedia_extract": "Zurich (German: Z\u00fcrich; Alemannic German: Z\u00fcri; see below) is the largest city in Switzerland and the capital of the canton of Zurich. It is in north-central Switzerland, at the northwestern tip of Lake Zurich. As of the end of 2024, the municipality had a population of 436,551, while the urban area was home to 1.45 million people (2020), while the Zurich metropolitan area had a total population of 2.1 million (2020). Zurich is a hub for railways, roads, and air traffic. Both Zurich Airport and Zurich's main railway station are the largest and busiest in the country. Evidence of early, sparse settlements in the area dates back more than 6,400 years, indicating human presence prior to the establishment of the town."}}, {"canonical_name": "Ust-Kamenogorsk", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "ust-kamenogorsk", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Ust-Kamenogorsk", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ust-Kamenogorsk"], "contexts": ["city for observation"]}, {"entity_id": "ustkamenogorsk", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Ustkamenogorsk", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ustkamenogorsk"], "contexts": ["location on the Irtysch river"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Ust-Kamenogorsk", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Ustkamenogorsk", "similarity": 0.796}], "id": 5675, "stable_key": "clu_9dd8ec76f3c2f18a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Ust-Kamenogorsk", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Kazakhstan", "note": "The spellings 'Ust-Kamenogorsk' and 'Ustkamenogorsk' are variations of the same city located on the Irtysh River in Kazakhstan."}}, {"canonical_name": "d\u00e9bris organiques", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "d\u00e9bris_organiques", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "d\u00e9bris organiques", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["d\u00e9bris organiques"], "contexts": ["remains of living organisms"]}, {"entity_id": "debris", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "debris", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["debris"], "contexts": ["fragmented material"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "d\u00e9bris organiques", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "debris", "similarity": 0.923}], "id": 5676, "stable_key": "clu_666f369f7839ef50", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Organic debris", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This refers to fragmented material derived from living organisms, commonly encountered in geological and ecological contexts.", "wikidata_id": "Q127370609", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article"}}, {"canonical_name": "Hipparque", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "hipparque", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Hipparque", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hipparque"], "contexts": ["ancient astronomer/mathematician"]}, {"entity_id": "hippias", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Hippias", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hippias"], "contexts": ["Ancient Greek sophist and polymath"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Hipparque", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Hippias", "similarity": 0.869}], "id": 5677, "stable_key": "clu_b3e6eaa5e7ac42ff-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hipparchus", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": -190, "death_year": -120, "description": "Ancient Greek astronomer and mathematician, known for star catalogs.", "wikidata_id": "Q1619936", "wikidata_description": "Pythagorean philosopher", "note": "The text also mentions 'Hippias', a different ancient figure, but 'Hipparque' primarily refers to Hipparchus in scientific contexts."}}, {"canonical_name": "thoughts and feelings", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "thoughts_and_feelings", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "thoughts and feelings", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["thoughts and feelings"], "contexts": ["data assumed by psychology"]}, {"entity_id": "thoughts_and_feelings", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "thoughts and feelings", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["thoughts and feelings"], "contexts": ["mental states and emotions"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "thoughts and feelings", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "thoughts and feelings", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5678, "stable_key": "clu_2621fad273b3a96c-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mental states", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q3968640", "wikidata_description": "state of mind of a person", "note": "The term 'thoughts and feelings' directly maps to the concept of mental states in psychology."}}, {"canonical_name": "doctrine of association", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "doctrine_of_association", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "doctrine of association", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["doctrine of association"], "contexts": ["historical development of associationism"]}, {"entity_id": "doctrine_of_correlation", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "doctrine of correlation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["doctrine of correlation"], "contexts": ["implies pre-existence of forces"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "doctrine of association", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "doctrine of correlation", "similarity": 0.897}], "id": 5679, "stable_key": "clu_eedd4fd5e8e57088-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Doctrine of association", "confidence": "medium", "type": "concept", "note": "The cluster links 'doctrine of association' with 'doctrine of correlation'. While both are philosophical/scientific doctrines, 'associationism' is a specific school of thought related to mental connections, whereas 'correlation' is a statistical or scientific relationship. The commonality is the concept of relatedness or connection.", "wikidata_id": "Q18355989", "wikidata_description": "ideology proposed by C. Fourier, asserting the future inevitability of communal associations of people co-working and co-living", "wikipedia_extract": "The motte-and-bailey fallacy (named after the motte-and-bailey castle) is a form of argument and an informal fallacy where an arguer conflates two positions that share similarities: one modest and easy to defend (the \"motte\") and one much more controversial and harder to defend (the \"bailey\"). The arguer advances the controversial position, but when challenged, insists that only the more modest position is being advanced. Upon retreating to the motte, the arguer may claim that the bailey has not been refuted (because the critic refused to attack the motte) or that the critic is unreasonable (by equating an attack on the bailey with an attack on the motte). Philosopher Nicholas Shackel, who coined the term, prefers to speak of a motte-and-bailey doctrine instead of a fallacy. In 2005, Shackel described the reference to medieval castle defense like this: A Motte and Bailey castle is a medieval system of defence in which a stone tower on a mound (the Motte) is surrounded by an area of land (the Bailey) which in turn is encompassed by some sort of a barrier such as a ditch. Being dark and dank, the Motte is not a habitation of choice.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fourierism"}}, {"canonical_name": "desires", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "desires", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "desires", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["desires"], "contexts": ["mental phenomena"]}, {"entity_id": "man\u2019s_desires", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "man\u2019s desires", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["man\u2019s desires"], "contexts": ["human wishes or wants"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "desires", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "man\u2019s desires", "similarity": 0.905}], "id": 5680, "stable_key": "clu_7888562ffdbc5b3d-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Desire", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "note": "The cluster explicitly mentions 'desires' and 'man's desires', directly referring to the psychological concept of desire."}}, {"canonical_name": "wall", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "wall", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "wall", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["wall"], "contexts": ["vertical structure"]}, {"entity_id": "wall", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "wall", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["wall"], "contexts": ["vertical structure"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "wall", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "wall", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5681, "stable_key": "clu_edb2ad66562e2c89", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Wall", "confidence": "high", "type": "object"}}, {"canonical_name": "teleological", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "teleological", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "teleological", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["teleological"], "contexts": ["explanation by purpose or final cause"]}, {"entity_id": "teleological", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Teleological", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Teleological"], "contexts": ["explanation by purpose or end goal"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "teleological", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Teleological", "similarity": 0.936}], "id": 5682, "stable_key": "clu_d3ee5f28bf0e65d7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Teleology", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This cluster refers to the philosophical concept of explaining phenomena by their purpose or final cause.", "wikidata_id": "Q119446245", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article in Science Research Management no. 2, 1996", "wikipedia_extract": "Teleology (from \u03c4\u03ad\u03bb\u03bf\u03c2, telos, 'end', 'aim', or 'goal', and \u03bb\u03cc\u03b3\u03bf\u03c2, logos, 'explanation' or 'reason') or finality is a branch of causality giving the reason or an explanation for something as a function of its end, its purpose, or its goal, as opposed to as a function of its efficient cause. A purpose that is imposed by human use, such as the purpose of a fork to hold food, is called extrinsic. Natural teleology, common in classical philosophy, though controversial today, contends that natural entities also have intrinsic purposes, regardless of human use or opinion. For instance, Aristotle claimed that an acorn's intrinsic telos is to become a fully grown oak tree. Though ancient materialists rejected the notion of natural teleology, teleological accounts of non-personal or non-human nature were explored and often endorsed in ancient and medieval philosophies, but fell into disfavor during the modern era (1600\u20131900). Much of the discussion on teleology revolves around religion and the belief in a Godly, purposeful existence for the world and for humans."}}, {"canonical_name": "nervous machinery", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "nervous_machinery", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "nervous machinery", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["nervous machinery"], "contexts": ["complex network of nerves and centers"]}, {"entity_id": "nervous_apparatus", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "nervous apparatus", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["nervous apparatus"], "contexts": ["structures of the nervous system"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "nervous machinery", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "nervous apparatus", "similarity": 0.963}], "id": 5683, "stable_key": "clu_393fae746481d9dc-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Nervous system", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This refers to the biological system responsible for transmitting signals.", "wikidata_id": "Q3339235", "wikidata_description": "disease of an anatomical entity located in the central or peripheral nervous system"}}, {"canonical_name": "Human Body", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "human_body", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Human Body", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Human Body"], "contexts": ["Book by H. N. Martin"]}, {"entity_id": "corpohumano", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "corpohumano", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["corpohumano"], "contexts": ["human body"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Human Body", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "corpohumano", "similarity": 0.941}], "id": 5684, "stable_key": "clu_7c5c010dd3ffc57c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Human Body", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q23852", "wikidata_description": "entire structure of a human organism"}}, {"canonical_name": "passage", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "passage", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "passage", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["passage"], "contexts": ["movement from one place to another"]}, {"entity_id": "passage", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "passage", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["passage"], "contexts": ["hypothetical connector in language analysis"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "passage", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "passage", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5685, "stable_key": "clu_f027f9e428b9abf6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "transition", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'passage' in the context of James's 'Principles of Psychology' and Spencer's 'First Principles' refers to a conceptual transition or movement between states, ideas, or stages, rather than a physical passage.", "wikidata_id": "Q98775214", "wikidata_description": "Little, Brown paperback edition"}}, {"canonical_name": "catarrhal", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "catarrhal", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "catarrhal", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 1, "variants": ["catarrhal"], "contexts": ["acute inflammation of mucous membranes"]}, {"entity_id": "catarrhos_fuffocativos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "catarrhos fuffocativos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 1, "variants": ["catarrhos fuffocativos"], "contexts": ["respiratory ailment"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "catarrhal", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "catarrhos fuffocativos", "similarity": 0.88}], "id": 5686, "stable_key": "clu_84cfeee475571679", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "catarrh", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "catarrh", "note": "The term 'catarrhal' refers to conditions associated with catarrh, an inflammation of a mucous membrane, typically in the respiratory tract.", "wikidata_id": "Q187958", "wikidata_description": "a species of bird common along the entire coast of the Antarctic continent"}}, {"canonical_name": "crura", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "crura", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "crura", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["crura"], "contexts": ["brain stem part"]}, {"entity_id": "crura", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "crura", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["crura"], "contexts": ["legs that can swell in dropsy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "crura", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "crura", "similarity": 0.766}], "id": 5687, "stable_key": "clu_b3fef97651c59d09", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "crura", "confidence": "high", "type": "anatomical structure", "note": "The term 'crura' can refer to leg-like structures, including parts of the brain stem or, in a more general sense, legs that might swell.", "wikidata_id": "Q133105", "wikidata_description": "weight bearing and locomotive anatomical structure of animals and humans"}}, {"canonical_name": "human embryos", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "human_embryos", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "human embryos", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["human embryos"], "contexts": ["developing humans"]}, {"entity_id": "embryo-organism", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "embryo-organism", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["embryo-organism"], "contexts": ["developing organism before birth"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "human embryos", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "embryo-organism", "similarity": 0.909}], "id": 5688, "stable_key": "clu_4439509e5f04b8d5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Human embryos", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "wikidata_id": "Q964797", "wikidata_description": "process of cell division and cellular differentiation of the embryo that occurs during the early stages of development", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human%20embryonic%20development"}}, {"canonical_name": "Duret", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "duret", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Duret", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Duret"], "contexts": ["tested function restoration in dogs"]}, {"entity_id": "dureto", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Dureto", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Dureto"], "contexts": ["cited author"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Duret", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Dureto", "similarity": 0.925}], "id": 5689, "stable_key": "clu_ef8eb570fe371127", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Claude Duret", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1570, "death_year": 1611, "description": "French surgeon and anatomist, known for early descriptions of nervous system.", "wikidata_id": "Q2977341", "wikidata_description": "14th-century French judge and botanist (1570-1611)"}}, {"canonical_name": "Philos. Transactions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "philos__transactions", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Philos. Transactions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Philos. Transactions"], "contexts": ["journal of the Royal Society"]}, {"entity_id": "philos__transactions", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Philos. Transactions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Philos. Transactions"], "contexts": ["scientific journal"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Philos. Transactions", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Philos. Transactions", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5690, "stable_key": "clu_0543de890d70bb10-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society", "confidence": "high", "type": "text", "wikidata_id": "Q913162", "wikidata_description": "British scientific journal published by the Royal Society", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philosophical%20Transactions%20of%20the%20Royal%20Society", "note": "This refers to the scientific journal published by the Royal Society.", "wikipedia_extract": "Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society is a scientific journal published by the Royal Society. In its earliest days, it was a private venture of the Royal Society's secretary. It was established in 1665, making it the second journal in the world exclusively devoted to science, after the Journal des s\u00e7avans, and therefore also the world's longest-running scientific journal. It became an official society publication in 1752. The use of the word philosophical in the title refers to natural philosophy, which was the equivalent of what would now be generally called science. In 1887 the journal expanded and divided into two separate publications, one serving the physical sciences (Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society A: Mathematical, Physical and Engineering Sciences) and the other focusing on the life sciences (Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B: Biological Sciences)."}}, {"canonical_name": "puppies", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "puppies", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "puppies", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "MAMMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["puppies"], "contexts": ["animals used in experiments"]}, {"entity_id": "puppies", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "puppies", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["puppies"], "contexts": ["animal"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "puppies", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "puppies", "similarity": 0.847}], "id": 5691, "stable_key": "clu_5adce0b0b7f44212", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "A puppy is a juvenile dog, generally one less than 12\u201318 months old. Puppies are markedly underdeveloped and dependent on their mothers at birth (displaying altriciality), but healthy puppies grow quickly and begin walking thereafter. Puppies generally weigh 250\u2013450 grams (8\u201316 oz) shortly after birth, depending on the breed. A puppy's coat colour may change as the puppy grows older, as is commonly seen in breeds such as the Yorkshire Terrier. Puppy refers specifically to young dogs, while pup may be used for other animals such as wolves, seals, giraffes, guinea pigs, rats, or sharks. Dogs go through prenatal development before they are born, just like all animals do."}}, {"canonical_name": "tensions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "tensions", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "tensions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["tensions"], "contexts": ["states of strain or potential energy"]}, {"entity_id": "tensions", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "tensions", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["tensions"], "contexts": ["forces causing stretching or pulling"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "tensions", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "tensions", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5692, "stable_key": "clu_f561be3f367da6a5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tension", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "muscular movement", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "muscular_movement", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "muscular movement", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["muscular movement"], "contexts": ["action of muscles"]}, {"entity_id": "muscular_changes", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "muscular changes", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["muscular changes"], "contexts": ["alterations in muscles"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "muscular movement", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "muscular changes", "similarity": 0.896}], "id": 5693, "stable_key": "clu_02e325c4c7788cbb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Muscle contraction", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "psychic condition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "psychic_condition", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "psychic condition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["psychic condition"], "contexts": ["mental state prerequisite for reflex"]}, {"entity_id": "mental_condition", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "mental condition", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["mental condition"], "contexts": ["influenced by sky clarity"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "psychic condition", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "mental condition", "similarity": 0.939}], "id": 5694, "stable_key": "clu_2621fad273b3a96c-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mental states", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q3968640", "wikidata_description": "state of mind of a person", "note": "The term 'thoughts and feelings' directly maps to the concept of mental states in psychology."}}, {"canonical_name": "nervous tension", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "nervous_tension", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "nervous tension", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["nervous tension"], "contexts": ["heightened state of nerves"]}, {"entity_id": "nervous_tension", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "nervous tension", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["nervous tension"], "contexts": ["state of physiological strain"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "nervous tension", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "nervous tension", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5695, "stable_key": "clu_61e4c25259e50632", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Nervous tension", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q79526330", "wikidata_description": "scientific article published on 01 December 1947"}}, {"canonical_name": "tea", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "tea", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "tea", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["tea"], "contexts": ["intoxicant that shortens reaction time"]}, {"entity_id": "tea", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "tea", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["tea"], "contexts": ["liquid rising in a cup"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "tea", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "tea", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5696, "stable_key": "clu_13dca40096b784fc", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Tea", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Camellia sinensis", "family": "Theaceae", "note": "The context refers to the beverage derived from the leaves of the tea plant."}}, {"canonical_name": "1870", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "1870", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "1870", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["1870"], "contexts": ["year of Schiff's experiments"]}, {"entity_id": "1870", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "1870", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["1870"], "contexts": ["year of botanical description"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "1870", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "1870", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5697, "stable_key": "clu_97688046104221c0-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "year", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The numbers '1867' and '1870' clearly refer to specific years, used in the context of historical dates for scientific works.", "wikidata_id": "Q577", "wikidata_description": "the orbital period of the Earth: the estimated period of time for the Earth's orbit around the Sun and observed at a fixed geographic point (averaging 365.24 days); base later modified to define or adjust various calendars", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Year", "wikipedia_extract": "A year is a unit of time based on how long it takes the Earth to orbit the Sun. In scientific use, the tropical year (approximately 365 solar days, 5 hours, 48 minutes, 45 seconds) and the sidereal year (about 20 minutes longer) are more exact. The modern calendar year, as reckoned according to the Gregorian calendar, approximates the tropical year by using a system of leap years. The term 'year' is also used to indicate other periods of roughly similar duration, such as the lunar year (a roughly 354-day cycle of twelve of the Moon's phases \u2013 see lunar calendar), as well as periods loosely associated with the calendar or astronomical year, such as the seasonal year, the fiscal year, the academic year, etc. Due to the Earth's axial tilt, the course of a year sees the passing of the seasons, marked by changes in weather, the hours of daylight, and, consequently, vegetation and soil fertility. In temperate and subpolar regions around the planet, four seasons are generally recognized: spring, summer, autumn, and winter."}}, {"canonical_name": "nervous force", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "nervous_force", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "nervous force", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["nervous force"], "contexts": ["energy transmitted by nerves"]}, {"entity_id": "nervous_force", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "nervous force", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["nervous force"], "contexts": ["energy transmitted by nerves"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "nervous force", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "nervous force", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5698, "stable_key": "clu_3edded2d0c6faabe", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Nervous energy", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Early modern concept referring to the vital force or energy associated with the nervous system."}}, {"canonical_name": "convulsive affections", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "convulsive_affections", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "convulsive affections", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["convulsive affections"], "contexts": ["conditions involving involuntary muscle contractions"]}, {"entity_id": "convulsions", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "convulsions", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["convulsions"], "contexts": ["sudden violent disturbances"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "convulsive affections", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "convulsions", "similarity": 0.894}], "id": 5699, "stable_key": "clu_c2c2186e526c273e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "convulsive disorders", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "convulsive disorders"}}, {"canonical_name": "mechanical pressures", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "mechanical_pressures", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "mechanical pressures", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["mechanical pressures"], "contexts": ["forces applied by ph