, "confidence": "medium", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to natural underground hollow spaces, not specifically a disease or anatomical part.", "wikidata_id": "Q2660276", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants"}}, {"canonical_name": "Thomas Young", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "thomas_young", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Thomas Young", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Thomas Young"], "contexts": ["made early observations on pendulum vibration"]}, {"entity_id": "thomas_young", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Thomas Young", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Thomas Young"], "contexts": ["polymath, deciphered hieroglyphs"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Thomas Young", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Thomas Young", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5894, "stable_key": "clu_255d4ead803bc14f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Thomas Young", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1773, "death_year": 1829, "description": "British polymath, physician, physicist, and decipherer of hieroglyphs.", "wikidata_id": "Q25820", "wikidata_description": "English polymath (1773-1829)"}}, {"canonical_name": "errors of observation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "errors_of_observation", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "errors of observation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["errors of observation"], "contexts": ["limitations in experimental measurements"]}, {"entity_id": "errors_of_observation", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "errors of observation", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["errors of observation"], "contexts": ["inaccuracies in measurement"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "errors of observation", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "errors of observation", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5895, "stable_key": "clu_be4760bab4ab1a29", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Observational errors", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q196372", "wikidata_description": "difference between a measured quantity value and a reference quantity value"}}, {"canonical_name": "Lipsiae", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "lipsiae", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Lipsiae", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lipsiae"], "contexts": ["location of publication"]}, {"entity_id": "lipsiae", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Lipsiae", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lipsiae"], "contexts": ["location of publication"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Lipsiae", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Lipsiae", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5896, "stable_key": "clu_a9397c8e9400ad21", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Leipzig", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "City in Germany, significant for publication.", "note": "Refers to the city of Leipzig, Germany, a known center for printing and academic work.", "wikipedia_extract": "Leipzig is the most populous city in the German state of Saxony. The city has a population of 628,718 inhabitants as of 2023. It is the eighth-largest city in Germany and is part of the Central German Metropolitan Region. Leipzig is located about 150 km (90 mi) southwest of Berlin, in the southernmost part of the North German Plain (the Leipzig Bay), at the confluence of the White Elster and its tributaries Plei\u00dfe and Parthe. Leipzig has been a trade city since at least the time of the Holy Roman Empire. Via Regia and the Via Imperii, two important medieval trade routes, intersected here, marking the city's economic importance."}}, {"canonical_name": "secular motion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "secular_motion", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "secular motion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["secular motion"], "contexts": ["long-term change in magnetic declination"]}, {"entity_id": "secular_motion", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "secular motion", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["secular motion"], "contexts": ["periodic variations of equinoxes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "secular motion", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "secular motion", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5897, "stable_key": "clu_cccdb70508ed56c8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Secular variation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to long-term changes, particularly in scientific contexts like astronomy or geomagnetism.", "wikidata_id": "Q1763933", "wikidata_description": "long-term non-periodic variation", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Secular%20variation", "wikipedia_extract": "The secular variation of a time series is its long-term, non-periodic variation (see Decomposition of time series). Whether a variation is perceived as secular or not depends on the available timescale: a variation that is secular over a timescale of centuries may be a segment of what is, over a timescale of millions of years, a periodic variation. Natural quantities often have both periodic and secular variations. Secular variation is sometimes called secular trend or secular drift when the emphasis is on a linear long-term trend. The term is used wherever time series are applicable in history, economics, operations research, biological anthropology, and astronomy (particularly celestial mechanics) such as VSOP (planets). The word secular, from the Latin root saecularis (\"of an age, occurring once in an age\"), has two basic meanings: I."}}, {"canonical_name": "Socotora", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "socotora", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Socotora", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Socotora"], "contexts": ["island west of where equator enters Africa"]}, {"entity_id": "socotora", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Socotora", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Socotora"], "contexts": ["island producing excellent aloe"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Socotora", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Socotora", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5898, "stable_key": "clu_f5f23b7c81589d40", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Socotra", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Yemeni island in the Arabian Sea, known for its unique flora and medicinal aloes.", "wikipedia_extract": "Socotra, locally known as Saqatri, is a Yemeni island in the Indian Ocean. It is situated between the Gulf of Aden, the Guardafui Channel and the Arabian Sea, and lies near major shipping routes. The largest of the six islands in the Socotra archipelago, it comprises around 95% of the landmass of the archipelago. It lies 380 kilometres (205 nautical miles) south of the Arabian Peninsula and 232 km (125 nmi) east of the Horn of Africa. The inhabitants of the island are called Socotrans, and they speak Arabic and Soqotri. Socotra is home to a high number of unique species that are endemic."}}, {"canonical_name": "Guinea", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "guinea", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Guinea", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Guinea"], "contexts": ["location of magnetic force observations"]}, {"entity_id": "gu\u00ednea", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Gu\u00ednea", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Gu\u00ednea"], "contexts": ["region where blacks use shells as currency"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Guinea", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Gu\u00ednea", "similarity": 0.813}], "id": 5899, "stable_key": "clu_1fba45c2f95ec449-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Guinea", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Guinea-Bissau", "note": "Early modern usage of 'Guinea' could refer to a broader region, including present-day Guinea, Guinea-Bissau, and surrounding areas."}}, {"canonical_name": "thermo-magnetic", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "thermo-magnetic", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "thermo-magnetic", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["thermo-magnetic"], "contexts": ["related to heat and magnetism"]}, {"entity_id": "thermo-electric", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "thermo-electric", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["thermo-electric"], "contexts": ["relating heat and electricity"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "thermo-magnetic", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "thermo-electric", "similarity": 0.881}], "id": 5900, "stable_key": "clu_02482f178f5e0a66", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Thermo-magnetic and thermo-electric effects", "confidence": "medium", "type": "concept", "note": "The terms 'thermo-magnetic' and 'thermo-electric' are closely related but distinct phenomena. The cluster appears to encompass both or a concept bridging them.", "wikidata_id": "Q129988337", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article published on 12 April 2018"}}, {"canonical_name": "dark red heat", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "dark_red_heat", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "dark red heat", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["dark red heat"], "contexts": ["temperature at which iron manifests magnetism"]}, {"entity_id": "red_heat", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "red heat", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["red heat"], "contexts": ["temperature at which iron loses magnetism"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "dark red heat", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "red heat", "similarity": 0.918}], "id": 5901, "stable_key": "clu_01f6a7a4ce796aa8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Red heat", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Describes a temperature range at which materials, particularly metals, glow visibly red. 'Dark red heat' specifies a lower end of this range.", "wikidata_id": "Q5387937", "wikidata_description": "species of plant"}}, {"canonical_name": "terrestrial stratum", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "terrestrial_stratum", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "terrestrial stratum", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["terrestrial stratum"], "contexts": ["layer of the Earth"]}, {"entity_id": "strata_of_the_earth", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Strata of the earth", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Strata of the earth"], "contexts": ["layers of the earth's crust"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "terrestrial stratum", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Strata of the earth", "similarity": 0.931}], "id": 5902, "stable_key": "clu_c7c29056d48e10e8", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Earth's crust strata", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "While 'terrestrial stratum' could refer to a single layer, the context of 'strata of the earth' and 'layers of the earth's crust' points to the geological concept of stratification."}}, {"canonical_name": "magnetism terrestre", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "magnetism_terrestre", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "magnetism terrestre", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["magnetism terrestre"], "contexts": ["terrestrial magnetism in French"]}, {"entity_id": "planetary_magnetism", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "planetary magnetism", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["planetary magnetism"], "contexts": ["magnetic fields of planets"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "magnetism terrestre", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "planetary magnetism", "similarity": 0.912}], "id": 5903, "stable_key": "clu_2cae0888b5b60d3b-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Earth's magnetic field", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q60703027", "wikidata_description": "", "note": "'Magnetism terrestre' directly translates to terrestrial magnetism, referring to the Earth's magnetic field. 'Planetary magnetism' extends this concept to other celestial bodies."}}, {"canonical_name": "galvanic flame", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "galvanic_flame", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "galvanic flame", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["galvanic flame"], "contexts": ["luminous discharge from battery"]}, {"entity_id": "voltaic_flame", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "voltaic flame", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["voltaic flame"], "contexts": ["spectrum appearance from metals"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "galvanic flame", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "voltaic flame", "similarity": 0.883}], "id": 5904, "stable_key": "clu_fd7b5e4b5a01d91c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Electric arc", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The 'galvanic flame' or 'voltaic flame' refers to the luminous discharge observed when electricity passes through a gap between two electrodes, a phenomenon now known as an electric arc.", "wikidata_id": "Q4785027", "wikidata_description": "unintended arc discharge of electricity"}}, {"canonical_name": "Professor Challis", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "professor_challis", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Professor Challis", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Professor Challis"], "contexts": ["scientist at Cambridge"]}, {"entity_id": "professor_challis", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Professor Challis", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Professor Challis"], "contexts": ["questioned light analysis"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Professor Challis", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Professor Challis", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5905, "stable_key": "clu_f3d044f808a952f0-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "James Challis", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1803, "death_year": 1883, "description": "English astronomer and physicist, Cambridge professor.", "wikidata_id": "Q731321", "wikidata_description": "British astronomer (1803-1882)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James%20Challis", "wikipedia_extract": "James Challis FRS (12 December 1803 \u2013 3 December 1882) was an English clergyman, physicist and astronomer. Plumian Professor of Astronomy and Experimental Philosophy and the director of the Cambridge Observatory, he investigated a wide range of physical phenomena though made few lasting contributions outside astronomy. He is best remembered for his missed opportunity to discover the planet Neptune in 1846. Challis was born in Braintree, Essex where his father, John Challis, was a stonemason. After attending various local schools, he graduated from Trinity College, Cambridge in 1825 as Senior Wrangler and first Smith's prizeman. He was elected a fellow of Trinity in 1826 and was ordained in 1830."}}, {"canonical_name": "Cambridge Observatory", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "cambridge_observatory", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Cambridge Observatory", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cambridge Observatory"], "contexts": ["observatory in Cambridge"]}, {"entity_id": "observatory_at_cambridge", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Observatory at Cambridge", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Observatory at Cambridge"], "contexts": ["astronomical research facility"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Cambridge Observatory", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Observatory at Cambridge", "similarity": 0.975}], "id": 5906, "stable_key": "clu_9ea66ad68c136c8d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cambridge Observatory", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United Kingdom", "wikidata_id": "Q1965699", "wikidata_description": "astronomical observatory at the University of Cambridge in the East of England"}}, {"canonical_name": "Fizeau", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "fizeau", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Fizeau", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Fizeau"], "contexts": ["compared flame to silver and carbon point"]}, {"entity_id": "fizeau", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Fizeau", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Fizeau"], "contexts": ["accomplished experiment on light theory"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Fizeau", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Fizeau", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5907, "stable_key": "clu_347bc8d9ebe22520", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hippolyte Fizeau", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1819, "death_year": 1896, "description": "French physicist known for measuring the speed of light.", "wikidata_id": "Q216814", "wikidata_description": "French physicist (1819\u20131896)"}}, {"canonical_name": "terrestrial physics", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "terrestrial_physics", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "terrestrial physics", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["terrestrial physics"], "contexts": ["physics of the Earth"]}, {"entity_id": "terrestrial_physics", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "terrestrial physics", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["terrestrial physics"], "contexts": ["reasonings of terrestrial physics"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "terrestrial physics", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "terrestrial physics", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5908, "stable_key": "clu_f1f279d6697593b6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Terrestrial physics", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q7703538", "wikidata_description": ""}}, {"canonical_name": "meteorological phenomenon", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "meteorological_phenomenon", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "meteorological phenomenon", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["meteorological phenomenon"], "contexts": ["atmospheric event"]}, {"entity_id": "atmospheric_phenomena", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "atmospheric phenomena", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["atmospheric phenomena"], "contexts": ["observable atmospheric events"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "meteorological phenomenon", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "atmospheric phenomena", "similarity": 0.896}], "id": 5909, "stable_key": "clu_33d58efc7d6ecf6b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Meteorological phenomenon", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q16332653", "wikidata_description": "phenomenon that takes place in the atmosphere"}}, {"canonical_name": "Piedmontese", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "piedmontese", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Piedmontese", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Piedmontese"], "contexts": ["region with valleys"]}, {"entity_id": "piedmont", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "Piedmont", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Piedmont"], "contexts": ["region connected to Savoy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Piedmontese", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "Piedmont", "similarity": 0.899}], "id": 5910, "stable_key": "clu_b5402209f7d21ffb", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Piedmont", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Italy", "wikidata_id": "Q1216", "wikidata_description": "region in North-West Italy", "wikipedia_extract": "Piedmont ( PEED-mont; Italian: Piemonte [pje\u02c8monte]; Piedmontese: Piemont [pje\u02c8m\u028a\u014bt]) is one of the 20 regions of Italy, located in the Northwest of the country. It borders the Liguria region to the south, the Lombardy and Emilia-Romagna regions to the east, and the Aosta Valley region to the northwest. Piedmont also borders Switzerland to the north and France to the west. Piedmont has an area of 25,387.07 km2 (9,802.00 mi2), making it the 2nd-largest region of Italy in area after Sicily. With a population of 4,251,868, it is also the 7th-most populous region as of 2025. The capital of Piedmont is Turin, which was the first capital of the Kingdom of Italy, from 1861 to 1865.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piedmont"}}, {"canonical_name": "waves of sound", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "waves_of_sound", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "waves of sound", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["waves of sound"], "contexts": ["penetrate to us from earth's interior"]}, {"entity_id": "waves_of_sound", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "waves of sound", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["waves of sound"], "contexts": ["propagation of sound through a medium"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "waves of sound", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "waves of sound", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5911, "stable_key": "clu_3238ab434f9b1ce7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Sound waves", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q3629144", "wikidata_description": "measure of the energy loss of sound propagation in media; phenomenon when hearing protectors help reduce acoustic flux from flowing into the ears, is measured in decibels (dBs)"}}, {"canonical_name": "Cyclades", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "cyclades", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Cyclades", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cyclades"], "contexts": ["islands experiencing earthquakes"]}, {"entity_id": "cycladi", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Cycladi", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cycladi"], "contexts": ["islands where Cippero root originates"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Cyclades", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Cycladi", "similarity": 0.935}], "id": 5912, "stable_key": "clu_a91e0f54aac7db19", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Cyclades", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Greece", "wikidata_id": "Q171132", "wikidata_description": "Greek island group in the Aegean Sea", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyclades", "wikipedia_extract": "The Cyclades (; Greek: \u039a\u03c5\u03ba\u03bb\u03ac\u03b4\u03b5\u03c2, romanized: Kykl\u00e1des, IPA: [ci\u02c8kla.\u00f0es]) are an island group in the Aegean Sea, southeast of mainland Greece and a former administrative prefecture of Greece. They are one of the island groups which constitute the Aegean archipelago. The name (\u039a\u03c5\u03ba\u03bb\u03ac\u03b4\u03b5\u03c2 \u03bd\u03ae\u03c3\u03bf\u03b9, Kykl\u00e1des n\u00edsoi, 'encircling islands') refers to the archipelago forming a circle around the sacred island of Delos. The largest island of the Cyclades is Naxos; the most populated, however, is Syros. The significant Late Neolithic and Early Bronze Age Cycladic culture is best known for its schematic, flat sculptures carved out of the islands' pure white marble centuries before the great Middle Bronze Age Minoan civilization arose in Crete to the south. These figures have been looted from burials to satisfy a thriving Cycladic antiquities market since the early 20th century."}}, {"canonical_name": "natives", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "natives", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "natives", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["natives"], "contexts": ["people of a region"]}, {"entity_id": "native_american_nations", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "native American nations", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["native American nations"], "contexts": ["indigenous peoples of America"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "natives", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "native American nations", "similarity": 0.876}], "id": 5913, "stable_key": "clu_70398c52bacd8046-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Indigenous peoples", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'savages' was historically used to refer to indigenous peoples, often in a derogatory manner, but in scientific and philosophical texts, it frequently referred to groups studied for comparative anthropology or psychology.", "wikidata_id": "Q1363781", "wikidata_description": "descendants of the population that lived in the territory of modern Brazil before the Portuguese colonization", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indigenous%20peoples%20in%20Brazil", "description": "Original inhabitants of a region or continent", "wikipedia_extract": "Indigenous peoples in Brazil or Native Brazilians (Portuguese: Brasileiros nativos) are the peoples whose ancestors lived in Brazil before European contact around 1500 and those pre-Columbian forebears. Indigenous peoples once comprised an estimated 2,000 distinct tribes and nations inhabiting what is now Brazil. The 2010 Brazil census recorded 305 Indigenous ethnic groups. Historically, many Indigenous peoples of Brazil were semi-nomadic and combined hunting, fishing, and gathering with migratory agriculture. Many tribes were massacred by European settlers, and others assimilated into the growing Brazilian population. The Indigenous population was decimated by European diseases, declining from a pre-Columbian high of 2 million to 3 million to approximately 300,000 by 1997, distributed among 200 tribes."}}, {"canonical_name": "electric process", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "electric_process", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "electric process", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["electric process"], "contexts": ["electrical phenomena in storms"]}, {"entity_id": "electric_processes", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "electric processes", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["electric processes"], "contexts": ["energy generation on the sun's surface"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "electric process", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "electric processes", "similarity": 0.941}], "id": 5914, "stable_key": "clu_eccbfd338995af8a", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Electrical phenomena", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q56552099", "wikidata_description": "article"}}, {"canonical_name": "mean temperature", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "mean_temperature", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "mean temperature", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["mean temperature"], "contexts": ["average temperature over a period"]}, {"entity_id": "mean_temperature", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "mean temperature", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["mean temperature"], "contexts": ["same in different latitudes of Europe"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "mean temperature", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "mean temperature", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 5915, "stable_key": "clu_f43ab6c01d90403d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mean temperature", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q129363656", "wikidata_description": "scholarly article"}}, {"canonical_name": "Athenaeus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "athenaeus", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Athenaeus", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Athenaeus"], "contexts": ["ancient Greek writer"]}, {"entity_id": "atheneo", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Atheneo", "categor