a work by Galenus"]}, {"entity_id": "simplic__medic_", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Simplic. medic.", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Simplic. medic."], "contexts": ["title of Galen's work"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Simp. medica", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Simplic. medic.", "similarity": 0.91}], "id": 6018, "stable_key": "clu_d51236edea6d8010", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Galen's Simplicia Medica", "confidence": "high", "type": "text"}}, {"canonical_name": "do\u00e7ura", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "do\u00e7ura", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "do\u00e7ura", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["do\u00e7ura"], "contexts": ["quality of taste"]}, {"entity_id": "do\u00e7ura", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "do\u00e7ura", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "QUALITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["do\u00e7ura"], "contexts": ["sweetness"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "do\u00e7ura", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "do\u00e7ura", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 6019, "stable_key": "clu_f93e45e734125675", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "sweetness", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q22981706", "wikidata_description": "vocal track by Lloyd Cole and the Commotions; 1984 studio recording"}}, {"canonical_name": "apagado", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "apagado", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "apagado", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["apagado"], "contexts": ["state of water after boiling gold"]}, {"entity_id": "apagadas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "apagadas", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "PRACTICE", "count": 1, "variants": ["apagadas"], "contexts": ["quenched"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "apagado", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "apagadas", "similarity": 0.889}], "id": 6020, "stable_key": "clu_67acf78141173d39", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "quenching", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the process of cooling a hot object, typically metal, in a liquid, which is then described as 'apagado' or 'apagadas'."}}, {"canonical_name": "letra de forma", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "letra_de_forma", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "letra de forma", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["letra de forma"], "contexts": ["movable type"]}, {"entity_id": "letra", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "letra", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["letra"], "contexts": ["handwriting, used for verification"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "letra de forma", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "letra", "similarity": 0.933}], "id": 6021, "stable_key": "clu_22ad429b6af2c7f6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "movable type", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "In the context of printing, 'letra de forma' refers to movable type, while 'letra' alone can also refer to handwriting, used for verification.", "wikidata_id": "Q426853", "wikidata_description": "system of printing and typography that uses movable components"}}, {"canonical_name": "unguento cetrino", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "unguento_cetrino", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "unguento cetrino", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["unguento cetrino"], "contexts": ["ointment for women's ailments"]}, {"entity_id": "unguento_citrino", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "unguento citrino", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["unguento citrino"], "contexts": ["citrine ointment"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "unguento cetrino", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "unguento citrino", "similarity": 0.92}], "id": 6022, "stable_key": "clu_ed909b4329892024", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "citrine ointment", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "The difference between 'cetrino' and 'citrino' is likely a scribal or printing variation."}}, {"canonical_name": "Ap\u00falia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "ap\u00falia", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Ap\u00falia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ap\u00falia"], "contexts": ["coastline infested by Turkish galleys"]}, {"entity_id": "apulia", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Apulia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Apulia"], "contexts": ["region where tarantulas are found"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Ap\u00falia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Apulia", "similarity": 0.895}], "id": 6023, "stable_key": "clu_82784772fd08c04e", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Ap\u00falia is a town and a former civil parish in the municipality of Esposende, Portugal. In 2013, the parish merged into the new parish Ap\u00falia e F\u00e3o. It has a population of 4,323 inhabitants and a total area of 10.51 km2, population density 411.3. It was a town and a municipality until 1834, when it was incorporated into Esposende. Ap\u00falia is known for its dune beaches and Roman-style folk costumes. It may be related to the region of Apulia in Italy, and a possible migration from there during the Roman Empire."}}, {"canonical_name": "apothecas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "apothecas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "apothecas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["apothecas"], "contexts": ["apothecaries' shops"]}, {"entity_id": "apo\u017fento", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "apo\u017fento", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["apo\u017fento"], "contexts": ["room"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "apothecas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "apo\u017fento", "similarity": 0.885}], "id": 6024, "stable_key": "clu_c20727213f2b07fa", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "apothecary's shop", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Global", "note": "While 'apo\u017fento' can mean room, in the context of apothecaries' shops, it refers to the space where medicines are prepared and sold."}}, {"canonical_name": "tavolas", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "tavolas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "tavolas", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["tavolas"], "contexts": ["ivory backgammon boards"]}, {"entity_id": "tavola", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Tavola", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Tavola"], "contexts": ["table of contents"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "tavolas", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Tavola", "similarity": 0.914}], "id": 6025, "stable_key": "clu_042da52bd47b2157", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "table", "confidence": "medium", "type": "object", "note": "Ambiguous. Could refer to a piece of furniture ('tavola' in Italian often means table) or a table of contents/data ('Tavola' in a scientific/medical context)."}}, {"canonical_name": "contas", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "contas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "contas", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["contas"], "contexts": ["ivory beads"]}, {"entity_id": "contas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Contas", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Contas"], "contexts": ["beads"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "contas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Contas", "similarity": 0.821}], "id": 6026, "stable_key": "clu_5772116dc82bd836", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "beads", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "Refers to small decorative or functional spherical objects, often strung together, such as those used in prayer or for adornment."}}, {"canonical_name": "Villa Nova de Portim\u00e3o", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "villa_nova_de_portim\u00e3o", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Villa Nova de Portim\u00e3o", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Villa Nova de Portim\u00e3o"], "contexts": ["estate owned by nobleman"]}, {"entity_id": "villa_nova_de_portimao", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Villa Nova de Portimao", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Villa Nova de Portimao"], "contexts": ["residence of Melchior Fernandez Marrcyros"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Villa Nova de Portim\u00e3o", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Villa Nova de Portimao", "similarity": 0.829}], "id": 6027, "stable_key": "clu_2bc5ca658d6187b1", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Vila Nova de Portim\u00e3o", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Portugal", "note": "The mention of 'Villa Nova de Portim\u00e3o' in one text and 'Villa-Nova' in another, with context suggesting specific locations, leads to this identification.", "wikidata_id": "Q5176798", "wikidata_description": "noble title in the Kingdom of Portugal", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Count%20of%20Vila%20Nova%20de%20Portim%C3%A3o", "wikipedia_extract": "Count of Vila Nova de Portim\u00e3o (Portuguese: Conde de Vila Nova de Portim\u00e3o) was a Portuguese title of nobility granted on 28 May 1504, by King Manuel I of Portugal to D. Martinho de Castelo Branco, 2nd Lord of Vila Nova de Portim\u00e3o. In 1662, the third count died without issue and this title was inherited by his sister's grandson, Lu\u00eds de Lencastre (who descended from Infante George of Lencastre, Duke of Coimbra). Later, in the 18th century, due to the 5th count's marriage to Maria Sofia de Lencastre (heiress of the House of Abrantes), the family reunited all the estates and honours within the same House, which became a reference among the Portuguese aristocracy. List of counts of Vila Nova de Portim\u00e3o Martinho Castelo Branco (c.1460- ? ); Manuel de Castelo Branco (1550- ?), his grandson; Greg\u00f3rio Taumaturgo de Castelo-Branco (c.1600-1662), his son; Luis de Lancastre (1644-1704), his grand-nephew; Pedro de Lancastre (1697-1752), his son; Jos\u00e9 Maria de Lancastre e Tavora (1742-1771), his grandson; Pedro de Lancastre da Silveira de Castelo-Branco S\u00e1 e Menezes (1762-1862), his son, also 5th Marquis of Abrantes and 12th Count of Penagui\u00e3o; Jos\u00e9 Maria da Piedade de Lancastre (1784-1827), his son, 6th Marquis of Abrantes and 13th Count of Penagui\u00e3o; Pedro Maria da Piedade de Lancastre Almeida S\u00e1 Menezes (1816-1847), 7th Marquis of Abrantes and 14th Count of Penagui\u00e3o, died without issue; Jos\u00e9 Maria da Piedade de Lancastre e Tavora (1819-1870), his younger brother; Jo\u00e3o Maria da Piedade de Lancastre e Tavora (1864-1917), his son, also 8th Marquis of Abrantes and 15th Count of Penagui\u00e3o; Jos\u00e9 Maria da Piedade de Lancastre e Tavora (1887-1961), his son, also 9th Marquis of Abrantes and 16th Count of Penagui\u00e3o; Luis Gonzaga de Lancastre e Tavora (1937-1993), his son, also 10th Marquis of Abrantes and 18th Count of Penagui\u00e3o; Jos\u00e9 Maria da Piedade de Lancastre e Tavora (born 1960), also 11th Marquis of Abrantes and 19th Count of Penagui\u00e3o."}}, {"canonical_name": "visconde de Castilho", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "visconde_de_castilho", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "visconde de Castilho", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["visconde de Castilho"], "contexts": ["friend providing information"]}, {"entity_id": "visconde_da_afleca", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Visconde da Afleca", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Visconde da Afleca"], "contexts": ["patient with ailment"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "visconde de Castilho", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Visconde da Afleca", "similarity": 0.848}], "id": 6028, "stable_key": "clu_0ee01a9ae85701e4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Visconde de Castilho", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Portuguese noble and patron of the arts", "note": "The 'Visconde da Afleca' is likely a conflation or misremembering of 'Visconde de Castilho', a known figure from Portuguese literature and society who was associated with figures like Garrett and Herculano. The context of providing information or being a patient fits.", "wikidata_id": "Q2624029", "wikidata_description": "Portuguese writer (1800\u20131875)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ant%C3%B3nio%20Feliciano%20de%20Castilho"}}, {"canonical_name": "alagoas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "alagoas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "alagoas", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["alagoas"], "contexts": ["bodies of water"]}, {"entity_id": "alagoa", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Alagoa", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Alagoa"], "contexts": ["location of writing"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "alagoas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Alagoa", "similarity": 0.93}], "id": 6029, "stable_key": "clu_66af903b9b33fd66", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Alagoas", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Brazil", "note": "Refers to the state of Alagoas in Brazil, known for its coastline and bodies of water."}}, {"canonical_name": "madre do cravo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "madre_do_cravo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "madre do cravo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["madre do cravo"], "contexts": ["alternative name for clove fruit"]}, {"entity_id": "madredocravo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "madredocravo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["madredocravo"], "contexts": ["clove mother"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "madre do cravo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "madredocravo", "similarity": 0.784}], "id": 6030, "stable_key": "clu_3ddb3095803a392b-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Clove", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Syzygium aromaticum", "family": "Myrtaceae", "description": "Aromatic spice derived from the dried flower buds of a tree.", "wikipedia_extract": "Cloves are the aromatic flower buds of a tree in the family Myrtaceae, Syzygium aromaticum (). They are native to the Maluku Islands, or Moluccas, in Indonesia, and are commonly used as a spice, flavoring, or fragrance in consumer products, such as toothpaste, soaps, or cosmetics. Cloves are available throughout the year owing to different harvest seasons across various countries. The word clove, first used in English in the 15th century, derives via Middle English clow of gilofer, Anglo-French clowes de gilofre and Old French clou de girofle, from the Latin word clavus \"nail\". The related English word gillyflower, originally meaning \"clove\", derives via said Old French girofle and Latin caryophyllon, from the Greek karyophyllon \"clove\", literally \"nut leaf\". The clove tree is an evergreen that grows up to 8\u201312 metres (26\u201339 ft) tall, with large leaves and crimson flowers grouped in terminal clusters.", "note": "The text mentions 'clovers' in the context of bees, which is a different plant (Trifolium). However, 'cloves' in the context of poultices strongly refers to Syzygium aromaticum.", "wikidata_id": "Q26736", "wikidata_description": "species of plant"}}, {"canonical_name": "cominho r\u00fastico", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "cominho_r\u00fastico", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "cominho r\u00fastico", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["cominho r\u00fastico"], "contexts": ["description of ameos"]}, {"entity_id": "cominhos_rusticos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "cominhos rusticos", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["cominhos rusticos"], "contexts": ["ingredient in Papa Benedicto powder"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "cominho r\u00fastico", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cominhos rusticos", "similarity": 0.886}], "id": 6031, "stable_key": "clu_49fd0fc107c93df0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Wild Cumin", "confidence": "medium", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Cuminum cyminum", "family": "Apiaceae", "note": "The identification of 'cominho r\u00fastico' as Wild Cumin is based on the literal translation and context of it being a spice. However, early modern texts sometimes used local names for plants, and further textual analysis might be needed for absolute certainty."}}, {"canonical_name": "Dom Manoel Telo de Menezes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "dom_manoel_telo_de_menezes", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Dom Manoel Telo de Menezes", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Dom Manoel Telo de Menezes"], "contexts": ["person who treated people"]}, {"entity_id": "padre_manoel_de_barros_da_co\u017fta", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Padre Manoel de Barros da Co\u017fta", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Padre Manoel de Barros da Co\u017fta"], "contexts": ["patient being treated"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Dom Manoel Telo de Menezes", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Padre Manoel de Barros da Co\u017fta", "similarity": 0.854}], "id": 6032, "stable_key": "clu_6012fb0ac777fd7b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Manoel Telo de Menezes", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "Treated patients; identified as a medical practitioner or benefactor.", "note": "Two different names are provided for what appears to be the same individual or role within the texts. Further context would be needed to confirm precise identity and roles."}}, {"canonical_name": "agua de ortel\u00e3a", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "agua_de_ortel\u00e3a", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "agua de ortel\u00e3a", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["agua de ortel\u00e3a"], "contexts": ["mixture for vomiting and stomach weakness"]}, {"entity_id": "agua_deorjev\u00e3o", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "agua deOrjev\u00e3o", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["agua deOrjev\u00e3o"], "contexts": ["distilled remedy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "agua de ortel\u00e3a", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "agua deOrjev\u00e3o", "similarity": 0.896}], "id": 6033, "stable_key": "clu_2db934d8a0b5a704", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Mint Water", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "note": "The phrase 'agua de ortel\u00e3a' directly translates to mint water. 'Agua de Orjev\u00e3o' appears to be a corruption or variant spelling, possibly related to oregano or another herb, but 'mint water' is the most direct and plausible referent given the first term."}}, {"canonical_name": "sorva", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "sorva", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "sorva", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["sorva"], "contexts": ["fruit compared to Jaca"]}, {"entity_id": "sorvas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Sorvas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Sorvas"], "contexts": ["plant name"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "sorva", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Sorvas", "similarity": 0.881}], "id": 6034, "stable_key": "clu_b640a92101ca332a-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Strawberry Tree", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Arbutus unedo Linnaeus", "family": "Ericaceae", "note": "The 'sorveira' or 'sorveira' is a Portuguese name for Arbutus unedo, commonly known as the Strawberry Tree. 'Sorvas' could be a variation or related term.", "wikidata_id": "Q329046", "wikidata_description": "species of plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arbutus_unedo", "wikipedia_extract": "Arbutus unedo, commonly known as strawberry tree, also called madrone, is an evergreen shrub or small tree in the family Ericaceae, native to the Mediterranean Basin and Western Europe. The tree is well known for its fruits, the arbutus berry, which bear some resemblance to the strawberry, hence the common name strawberry tree. However, it is not closely related to true strawberries of the genus Fragaria. Its presence in Ireland also lends it the name Irish strawberry tree, or cain, or cane apple (from the Irish name for the tree, caithne), or sometimes Killarney strawberry tree. The strawberry tree is the national tree of Italy because of its green leaves, its white flowers and its red berries, colors that recall the Italian flag. The flower of the strawberry tree is the national flower of Italy."}}, {"canonical_name": "carrascos", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "carrascos", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "carrascos", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["carrascos"], "contexts": ["trees where gr\u00e3a is found"]}, {"entity_id": "carrasco", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "carrasco", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["carrasco"], "contexts": ["plant used in decoction"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "carrascos", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "carrasco", "similarity": 0.918}], "id": 6035, "stable_key": "clu_c9a2dacfa616b760", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Kermes Oak", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Quercus coccifera L.", "family": "Fagaceae", "note": "The term 'carrasco' or 'carrascos' in Portuguese and Spanish refers to Quercus coccifera, known for its association with the kermes insect, used for dye. The context of 'gr\u00e3a' (a dye or pigment) strongly supports this identification."}}, {"canonical_name": "shell lac", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "shell_lac", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "shell lac", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["shell lac"], "contexts": ["processed lacca by fusion"]}, {"entity_id": "shell-lac", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "shell-lac", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["shell-lac"], "contexts": ["resin used in varnish"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "shell lac", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "shell-lac", "similarity": 0.757}], "id": 6036, "stable_key": "clu_a14943995e24e599-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Shellac", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "note": "Shellac is a processed resin produced by the lac insect. The description 'processed lacca by fusion' and 'resin used in varnish' directly aligns with shellac.", "wikidata_id": "Q72507464", "wikidata_description": "chemical compound"}}, {"canonical_name": "Commentary", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "commentary", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Commentary", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Commentary"], "contexts": ["work by Burton"]}, {"entity_id": "commentar__de_pe\u017fte", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Commentar. de Pe\u017fte", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Commentar. de Pe\u017fte"], "contexts": ["commentary on the plague"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Commentary", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Commentar. de Pe\u017fte", "similarity": 0.885}], "id": 6037, "stable_key": "clu_593b77f9a14cf62c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "commentary", "confidence": "high", "type": "text", "wikidata_id": "Q1749541", "wikidata_description": "close explication of a text, focusing on language", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Commentary_%28philology%29", "wikipedia_extract": "In philology, a commentary is a line-by-line or even word-by-word explication usually attached to an edition of a text in the same or an accompanying volume. It may draw on methodologies of close reading and literary criticism, but its primary purpose is to elucidate the language of the text and the specific culture that produced it, both of which may be foreign to the reader. Such a commentary usually takes the form of footnotes, endnotes, or separate text cross-referenced by line, paragraph or page. Means of providing commentary on the language of the text include notes on textual criticism, syntax and semantics, and the analysis of rhetoric, literary tropes, and style. The aim is to remove, lessen or point out linguistic obstacles to reading and understanding the text. If a text is historical, or is produced within a culture assumed to be of limited familiarity to a reader, a broader range of issues may require elucidation."}}, {"canonical_name": "Alcoba\u00e7a", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "alcoba\u00e7a", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Alcoba\u00e7a", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Alcoba\u00e7a"], "contexts": ["library provenance"]}, {"entity_id": "coutos_de_alcoba\u00e7a", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Coutos de Alcoba\u00e7a", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Coutos de Alcoba\u00e7a"], "contexts": ["jurisdiction of Alcoba\u00e7a"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Alcoba\u00e7a", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Coutos de Alcoba\u00e7a", "similarity": 0.882}], "id": 6038, "stable_key": "clu_aa1850a9beb5ea63", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Alcoba\u00e7a", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Portugal", "note": "Alcoba\u00e7a is a well-known town and municipality in Portugal, famous for its monastery. The reference to 'Coutos de Alcoba\u00e7a' further solidifies this as a specific historical jurisdiction.", "wikidata_id": "Q4713216", "wikidata_description": "river in Portugal", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alcoba%C3%A7a_River_%28Portugal%29"}}, {"canonical_name": "Wedel", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "wedel", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Wedel", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Wedel"], "contexts": ["discussed nature of catechu"]}, {"entity_id": "wedel", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Wedel", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Wedel"], "contexts": ["author on hemorrhoids and scurvy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Wedel", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Wedel", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 6039, "stable_key": "clu_09c8ca58cd9a04dd", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Georg Wolfgang Wedel", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1645, "death_year": 1721, "description": "German physician and medical professor, known for his writings.", "note": "Multiple individuals named Wedel existed. This identification is based on the context of medical writings and the likely timeframe."}}, {"canonical_name": "fleum\u00f5es", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "fleum\u00f5es", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "fleum\u00f5es", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["fleum\u00f5es"], "contexts": ["illness caused by fermenting fruits"]}, {"entity_id": "fleym\u00e3o", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "fleym\u00e3o", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["fleym\u00e3o"], "contexts": ["tumor from blood"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "fleum\u00f5es", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "fleym\u00e3o", "similarity": 0.886}], "id": 6040, "stable_key": "clu_6ba44695545ab101", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Phlegm-related conditions", "confidence": "medium", "type": "disease", "note": "The term 'fleum\u00f5es' or 'fleym\u00e3o' in early modern Portuguese medical texts refers to conditions associated with an excess of phlegm, a humor in the humoral theory. This is not a specific modern disease but a category of ailments."}}, {"canonical_name": "Gr\u00e9cia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "gr\u00e9cia", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Gr\u00e9cia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Gr\u00e9cia"], "contexts": ["ancient land of philosophy"]}, {"entity_id": "grecia", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Grecia", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Grecia"], "contexts": ["ancient land of learning"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Gr\u00e9cia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Grecia", "similarity": 0.972}], "id": 6041, "stable_key": "clu_451098d60ff27967-4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Greece", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Southeastern European country, cradle of Western civilization and ancient knowledge.", "note": "Includes references to the Greek language and people.", "wikipedia_extract": "Greece, officially the Hellenic Republic, is a country in Southeast Europe. Located on the southern tip of the Balkan peninsula, it shares land borders with Albania to the northwest, North Macedonia and Bulgaria to the north, and Turkey to the east. The Aegean Sea lies to the east of the mainland, the Ionian Sea to the west, and the Sea of Crete and the Mediterranean Sea to the south. Greece has the longest coastline on the Mediterranean basin, spanning thousands of islands and nine traditional geographic regions. It has a population of over 10 million. Athens is the nation's capital and largest city, followed by Thessaloniki and Patras."}}, {"canonical_name": "Gm.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "gm_", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Gm.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Gm."], "contexts": ["scientific name authority"]}, {"entity_id": "gam_", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Gam.", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "AUTHORITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Gam."], "contexts": ["cited ancient/medieval authority"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Gm.", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Gam.", "similarity": 0.857}], "id": 6042, "stable_key": "clu_c8a199a1310260c6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Gerard van Meeuwen", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "Dutch physician and botanist, cited authority", "note": "The abbreviation 'Gm.' is likely an abbreviation for Gerard van Meeuwen, a Dutch physician and botanist. 'Gam.' is a less common but possible variation."}}, {"canonical_name": "fetos", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "fetos", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "fetos", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["fetos"], "contexts": ["fern fronds"]}, {"entity_id": "feta", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "fetA", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["fetA"], "contexts": ["plant that is viscous with humidity"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "fetos", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "fetA", "similarity": 0.91}], "id": 6043, "stable_key": "clu_7810e16335dafa8f-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Fern", "confidence": "medium", "type": "plant", "note": "The term 'fetos' is ambiguous and could refer to various plants, but 'fern' is a plausible interpretation in a medicinal context, especially when referring to fronds or herbs used in pills. However, without more context, a definitive Linnaean classification is not possible.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_extract": "Neonatal alloimmune thrombocytopenia (NAITP, NAIT, NATP or NAT) is a disease that affects babies in which the platelet count is decreased because the mother's immune system attacks her fetus' or newborn's platelets. A low platelet count increases the risk of bleeding in the fetus and newborn. If the bleeding occurs in the brain, there may be long-term effects. Platelet antigens are inherited from both mother and father. NAIT is caused by antibodies specific for platelet antigens inherited from the father but which are absent in the mother. Fetomaternal transfusions (or fetomaternal hemorrhage) results in the recognition of these antigens by the mother's immune system as non-self, with the subsequent generation of allo-reactive antibodies which cross the placenta.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neonatal_alloimmune_thrombocytopenia"}}, {"canonical_name": "affectus", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "affectus", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "affectus", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["affectus"], "contexts": ["medical conditions"]}, {"entity_id": "cordis_affectus", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Cordis affectus", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cordis affectus"], "contexts": ["affections of the heart"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "affectus", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Cordis affectus", "similarity": 0.886}], "id": 6044, "stable_key": "clu_3d2072e3760642c7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Heart condition", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Cardiac condition", "wikidata_id": "Q49130403", "wikidata_description": "scientific article published on December 21, 1981"}}, {"canonical_name": "Alfandega", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "alfandega", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Alfandega", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Alfandega"], "contexts": ["near the specimen"]}, {"entity_id": "alfandega", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Alfandega", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Alfandega"], "contexts": ["customs house where Jo\u017feph Correa was judge"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Alfandega", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Alfandega", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 6045, "stable_key": "clu_a4ac02faa21a31d3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Customs house", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "Portugal", "wikidata_id": "Q218653", "wikidata_description": "government office building for import and export of goods"}}, {"canonical_name": "ilha", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "ilha", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "ilha", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["ilha"], "contexts": ["island where creatures live"]}, {"entity_id": "ilha", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Ilha", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Ilha"], "contexts": ["Island, a geographic location"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "ilha", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Ilha", "similarity": 0.949}], "id": 6046, "stable_key": "clu_6c5e167e0f18cc73-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Island", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "note": "Includes specific mentions of atolls and general 'islands'.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}}, {"canonical_name": "bichos dom\u00e9sticos", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "bichos_dom\u00e9sticos", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "bichos dom\u00e9sticos", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["bichos dom\u00e9sticos"], "contexts": ["domestic creatures kept by Portuguese"]}, {"entity_id": "bichos_da_\u017feda", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "bichos da \u017feda", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["bichos da \u017feda"], "contexts": ["animal product for head ailment"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "bichos dom\u00e9sticos", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "bichos da \u017feda", "similarity": 0.895}], "id": 6047, "stable_key": "clu_1ef3035db1ecc004", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Domestic animals", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "note": "The cluster mentions 'bichos dom\u00e9sticos' (domestic creatures) and 'bichos da seda' (silkworms), which are both categories of domestic animals or animals utilized by humans.", "wikidata_id": "Q622852", "wikidata_description": "animal that has been biologically changed by human intervention", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Domesticated%20animal"}}, {"canonical_name": "Aristolochia indica", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "aristolochia_indica", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Aristolochia indica", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Aristolochia indica"], "contexts": ["plant cited as possible antidote"]}, {"entity_id": "aristolochia", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Aristolochia", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Aristolochia"], "contexts": ["plant with three species"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Aristolochia indica", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Aristolochia", "similarity": 0.917}], "id": 6048, "stable_key": "clu_47055c8f631269f7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Aristolochia", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Aristolochia Linnaeus", "family": "Aristolochiaceae", "note": "While 'Aristolochia tinbacencis' is mentioned, it's likely a specific species or variety within the genus Aristolochia. The broader genus is the most fitting modern referent given the context of multiple species.", "wikidata_id": "Q156212", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aristolochia", "wikipedia_extract": "Aristolochia californica, the California pipevine, California Dutchman's-pipe, or California snakeroot is a perennial woody vine of western North America. The vine is endemic to northern California. It is native to the Sacramento Valley, northern Sierra Nevada foothills, San Francisco Bay Area, Northern Inner California Coast Ranges, southeastern Klamath Mountains. The plant grows along riparian streambank areas, in chaparral, oak woodland, and mixed evergreen forest habitats. It is found below 700 metres (2,300 ft) in elevation. Aristolochia californica is a deciduous vine."}}, {"canonical_name": "triguo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "triguo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "triguo", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["triguo"], "contexts": ["grain used for weight comparison"]}, {"entity_id": "tritico", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Tritico", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Tritico"], "contexts": ["wheat"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "triguo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Tritico", "similarity": 0.915}], "id": 6049, "stable_key": "clu_436c5219b63bd57e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Wheat", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Triticum aestivum", "family": "Poaceae", "note": "The Portuguese 'triguo' and the Latinized 'Tritico' both refer to wheat, a common grain in the early modern period.", "wikidata_id": "Q12106", "wikidata_description": "genus of plants (focus on taxonomy here, not on agriculture)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Triticum"}}, {"canonical_name": "vinum", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "vinum", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "vinum", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["vinum"], "contexts": ["wine"]}, {"entity_id": "vinum", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "vinum", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["vinum"], "contexts": ["wine"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "vinum", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "vinum", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 6050, "stable_key": "clu_6110a1f26657a473-4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Wine", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "note": "Both 'vino' and 'Vino' are clearly the Italian and Spanish/Portuguese words for wine, a common medicinal ingredient.", "wikidata_id": "Q282", "wikidata_description": "alcoholic drink typically made from grapes through the fermentation process", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wine", "wikipedia_extract": "Wine is an alcoholic drink made from fermented grape juice. It is produced and consumed in many regions around the world, in a wide variety of styles which are influenced by different varieties of grapes, growing environments, viticulture methods, and production techniques. Wine has been produced for thousands of years, the earliest evidence dating from c. 6000 BCE in present-day Georgia. Its popularity spread around the Mediterranean during Classical antiquity, and was sustained in Western Europe by winemaking monks and a secular trade for general drinking. New World wine was established by settler colonies from the 16th century onwards, and the wine trade increased dramatically up to the latter half of the 19th century, when European vineyards were largely destroyed by the invasive pest phylloxera.", "description": "An alcoholic beverage made from fermented grapes."}}, {"canonical_name": "terra arm\u00e9nia", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "terra_arm\u00e9nia", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "terra arm\u00e9nia", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["terra arm\u00e9nia"], "contexts": ["argillaceous earth used in medicine"]}, {"entity_id": "terra_armenia", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "terra Armenia", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["terra Armenia"], "contexts": ["type of medicinal earth"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "terra arm\u00e9nia", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "terra Armenia", "similarity": 0.928}], "id": 6051, "stable_key": "clu_8d1e2bb780282914", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": null, "confidence": "high", "type": "mineral", "wikidata_id": "Q891670", "wikidata_description": "natural mixture of hydrated silicate clays colored with red iron oxide", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armenian%20bole", "wikipedia_extract": "Armenian bole, also known as bolus armenus or bole armoniac, is an earthy clay, usually red, native to Armenia but also found in other places. The term Armenian was later referred to a specific quality of the clay. Originally used in medication, it has also been used as a pigment, as a poliment or base for gilding, and for other uses. It is red due to the presence of iron oxide; the clay also contains hydrous silicates of aluminum and possibly magnesium. Historically, the term bolu or bolus was used only for medicinal earths and Armenian bole was used as an astringent, prescribed against diarrhea, dysentery, and bleeding. References to Armenian bole were made by Theophrastus, Dioscorides (c."}}, {"canonical_name": "dor no estomaguo", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "dor_no_estomaguo", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "dor no estomaguo", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["dor no estomaguo"], "contexts": ["stomach pain"]}, {"entity_id": "asdoresdeestomago", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "asdoresdeestomago", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["asdoresdeestomago"], "contexts": ["excellent for stomach pains"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "dor no estomaguo", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "asdoresdeestomago", "similarity": 0.906}], "id": 6052, "stable_key": "clu_2c4d37aeda3625f3-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Dyspepsia", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Dyspepsia", "note": "The Portuguese term 'de Stomachi dolore' directly translates to stomach pain, a symptom of dyspepsia.", "wikidata_id": "Q653971", "wikidata_description": "human disease", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indigestion", "wikipedia_extract": "Postcholecystectomy syndrome (PCS) describes the presence of abdominal symptoms after a cholecystectomy (gallbladder removal). Symptoms occur in about 5 to 40 percent of patients who undergo cholecystectomy, and can be transient, persistent or lifelong. The chronic condition is diagnosed in approximately 10% of postcholecystectomy cases. The pain associated with postcholecystectomy syndrome is usually ascribed to either sphincter of Oddi dysfunction or to post-surgical adhesions. A recent 2008 study shows that postcholecystectomy syndrome can be caused by biliary microlithiasis. Approximately 50% of cases are due to biliary causes such as remaining stone, biliary injury, dysmotility and choledococyst."}}, {"canonical_name": "Reforma", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "reforma", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Reforma", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Reforma"], "contexts": ["religious movement"]}, {"entity_id": "reform", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Reform", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Reform"], "contexts": ["political ideology of individual liberty"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Reforma", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Reform", "similarity": 0.889}], "id": 6053, "stable_key": "clu_536fd36fa2a92715", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Reformation", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'Reforma' in Portuguese and 'Reform' in English, in this context, refer to the major religious and subsequent political movements.", "wikidata_id": "Q29640250", "wikidata_description": "episode of Holby City (S11 E45)"}}, {"canonical_name": "tintoreiros", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "tintoreiros", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "tintoreiros", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 1, "variants": ["tintoreiros"], "contexts": ["dyers who rejected s\u00e2ndalo"]}, {"entity_id": "tintori", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "tintori", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "PRACTITIONER", "count": 1, "variants": ["tintori"], "contexts": ["dyers"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "tintoreiros", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "tintori", "similarity": 0.971}], "id": 6054, "stable_key": "clu_84f734d435d0c6ec", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Dyers", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Artisans involved in dyeing textiles and other materials.", "wikidata_id": "Q6147194", "wikidata_description": "occupation where a person is involved in dyeing", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dyer%20%28occupation%29"}}, {"canonical_name": "abund\u00e2ncia", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "abund\u00e2ncia", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "abund\u00e2ncia", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["abund\u00e2ncia"], "contexts": ["state of having plenty"]}, {"entity_id": "abundance", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "abundance", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["abundance"], "contexts": ["large quantity"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "abund\u00e2ncia", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "abundance", "similarity": 0.928}], "id": 6055, "stable_key": "clu_2220e5c797f23980", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "abundance", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q336019", "wikidata_description": "ecological quantity", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abundance_%28ecology%29", "wikipedia_extract": "In ecology, local abundance is  the relative representation of a species in a particular ecosystem. It is usually measured as the number of individuals found per sample. The ratio of abundance of one species to one or multiple other species living in an ecosystem is referred to as relative species abundances. Both indicators are relevant for computing biodiversity. A variety of sampling methods are used to measure abundance. For larger animals, these may include spotlight counts, track counts and roadkill counts, as well as presence at monitoring stations."}}, {"canonical_name": "mulas", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "mulas", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "mulas", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["mulas"], "contexts": ["animals that eat the plant"]}, {"entity_id": "mula", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "mula", "category": "ANIMAL", "subcategory": "OTHER_ANIMAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["mula"], "contexts": ["animal involved in illness transfer"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "mulas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "mula", "similarity": 0.892}], "id": 6056, "stable_key": "clu_118bedc406973fc1-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "mule", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "description": "Hybrid offspring of a male donkey and a female horse.", "wikipedia_extract": "The mule is a domestic equine hybrid between a donkey and a horse. It is the offspring of a male donkey (a jack) and a female horse (a mare). The horse and the donkey are different species, with different numbers of chromosomes; of the two possible first-generation hybrids between them, the mule is easier to obtain and more common than the hinny, which is the offspring of a male horse (a stallion) and a female donkey (a jenny). Mules vary widely in size, and may be of any color seen in horses or donkeys. They are more patient, hardier and longer-lived than horses, and are perceived as less obstinate and more intelligent than donkeys. A female mule is called a molly, a Molly mule, or more formally mare mule.", "wikidata_description": "family name"}}, {"canonical_name": "Lyro", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "lyro", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Lyro", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Lyro"], "contexts": ["cited reference"]}, {"entity_id": "lyre", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "lyre", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["lyre"], "contexts": ["stringed musical instrument"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Lyro", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "lyre", "similarity": 0.904}], "id": 6057, "stable_key": "clu_4a26c49cd7eb2341", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lyre", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "The term 'Lyro' in the 1563 text is likely an archaic or variant spelling of 'lyre', a stringed musical instrument.", "wikidata_id": "Q25366088", "wikidata_description": "genus of algae"}}, {"canonical_name": "lat\u00e3o", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "lat\u00e3o", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "lat\u00e3o", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["lat\u00e3o"], "contexts": ["trabalho onde se obtinha espodio"]}, {"entity_id": "lata\u00f3", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "lata\u00f3", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "MINERAL", "count": 1, "variants": ["lata\u00f3"], "contexts": ["brass"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "lat\u00e3o", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "lata\u00f3", "similarity": 0.864}], "id": 6058, "stable_key": "clu_553a795ad1235519-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Brass", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "note": "Includes 'bronzo' and 'bronze', which are often used interchangeably or closely related to brass in historical texts, especially when referring to alloys.", "wikidata_id": "Q177932", "wikidata_description": "plant species grown for its oil-rich seed"}}, {"canonical_name": "dragma", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "dragma", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "dragma", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["dragma"], "contexts": ["ingredient mixed with Raiz da china"]}, {"entity_id": "dragma", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "dragma", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["dragma"], "contexts": ["unit of weight"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "dragma", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "dragma", "similarity": 0.711}], "id": 6059, "stable_key": "clu_9edd4661cb2e5515-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Drachma", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Unit of weight, often used in apothecaries' contexts, derived from the ancient Greek currency.", "wikidata_id": "Q5304630", "wikidata_description": "genus of insects; genus of Arthoropoda containing only one species", "wikipedia_extract": "In ancient Greek religion and mythology, Helios (; Ancient Greek: \u1f2d\u03bb\u03b9\u03bf\u03c2 pronounced [h\u025b\u030c\u02d0lios], lit. 'Sun'; Homeric Greek: \u1f28\u03ad\u03bb\u03b9\u03bf\u03c2) is the god who personifies the Sun. His name is also Latinized as Helius, and he is often given the epithets Hyperion (\"the one above\") and Phaethon (\"the shining\"). Helios is often depicted in art with a radiant crown and driving a horse-drawn chariot through the sky. He was a guardian of oaths and also the god of sight. Though Helios was a relatively minor deity in Classical Greece, his worship grew more prominent in late antiquity thanks to his identification with several major solar divinities of the Roman period, particularly Apollo and Sol.", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drachma"}}, {"canonical_name": "febre latica", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "febre_latica", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "febre latica", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 1, "variants": ["febre latica"], "contexts": ["fever treated with cooked Raiz da china"]}, {"entity_id": "malign\u00e6_febris", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Malign\u00e6 febris", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Malign\u00e6 febris"], "contexts": ["a dangerous fever"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "febre latica", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Malign\u00e6 febris", "similarity": 0.903}], "id": 6060, "stable_key": "clu_d0d9a55bee409e9d", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Malignant fever", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Febrile illness", "note": "'Febre latica' and 'Malign\u00e6 febris' both point to a severe or dangerous type of fever.", "wikidata_id": "Q40934219", "wikidata_description": "scientific article published on March 1, 1970"}}, {"canonical_name": "cidreira", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "cidreira", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "cidreira", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["cidreira"], "contexts": ["plant name"]}, {"entity_id": "ca\u017fquinha_de_cidra", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "ca\u017fquinha de cidra", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["ca\u017fquinha de cidra"], "contexts": ["citron peel, ingredient in remedy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "cidreira", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "ca\u017fquinha de cidra", "similarity": 0.911}], "id": 6061, "stable_key": "clu_9234b21908e78793-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Lemon balm", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Melissa officinalis", "family": "Lamiaceae", "note": "'Cidra' and 'cidreira' both refer to Melissa officinalis, commonly known as lemon balm.", "wikidata_id": "Q148396", "wikidata_description": "lemon balm, a species of plant, herb"}}, {"canonical_name": "pecegos", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "pecegos", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "pecegos", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["pecegos"], "contexts": ["peaches"]}, {"entity_id": "pecego", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "pecego", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["pecego"], "contexts": ["type of fruit to avoid"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "pecegos", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "pecego", "similarity": 0.915}], "id": 6062, "stable_key": "clu_a911a8066ec2cf02", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Peach", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Prunus persica", "family": "Rosaceae", "wikidata_id": "Q13189", "wikidata_description": "species of fruit tree (for the fruit use Q13202263)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peach", "wikipedia_extract": "The peach (Prunus persica) is a deciduous tree that bears edible juicy fruits with various characteristics. Most are simply called peaches, while the glossy-skinned, non-fuzzy varieties are called nectarines. Though from the same species, they are regarded commercially as different fruits. The tree is regarded as handsome and is planted in gardens for its springtime blooms in addition to fruit production. It is relatively short lived, usually not exceeding twenty years of age. Peaches were first domesticated and cultivated in China during the Neolithic period."}}, {"canonical_name": "Franccfco Roflclli", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "franccfco_roflclli", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Franccfco Roflclli", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Franccfco Roflclli"], "contexts": ["Name mentioned in title"]}, {"entity_id": "francifco", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Francifco", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Francifco"], "contexts": ["mentioned in comparison"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Franccfco Roflclli", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Francifco", "similarity": 0.865}], "id": 6063, "stable_key": "clu_0c8897dff9e2bcab", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Francesco Roselli", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "description": "Italian physician and author of medical texts.", "note": "The OCR noise in 'Franccfco Roflclli' and the shortened 'Francifco' clearly point to this individual.", "wikidata_id": "Q60627968", "wikidata_description": "researcher ORCID ID = 0000-0001-9935-6899"}}, {"canonical_name": "Liguria", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "liguria", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Liguria", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Liguria"], "contexts": ["region in Italy with noble herbs"]}, {"entity_id": "liguria", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Liguria", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "REGION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Liguria"], "contexts": ["region in Italy"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Liguria", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Liguria", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 6064, "stable_key": "clu_5efef44a79ce5fad", "ground_truth": {"wikipedia_extract": "Liguria ( lig-YOOR-ee-\u0259, Italian: [li\u02c8\u0261u\u02d0rja]; Ligurian: Lig\u00fbria [li\u02c8\u0261y\u02d0\u027eja]) is a region of north-western Italy; its capital is Genoa. Its territory is crossed by the Alps and the Apennines mountain range and is roughly coextensive with the former territory of the Republic of Genoa. Liguria is bordered by France (Provence-Alpes-C\u00f4te d'Azur) to the west, Piedmont to the north, and Emilia-Romagna and Tuscany to the east. It rests on the Ligurian Sea, and has a population of 1,509,908 as of 2025. The region is part of the Alps\u2013Mediterranean Euroregion. The name Liguria predates Latin and is of obscure origin."}}, {"canonical_name": "cammino", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "cammino", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "cammino", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["cammino"], "contexts": ["hearth"]}, {"entity_id": "camino", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "camino", "category": "OBJECT", "subcategory": "OTHER_OBJECT", "count": 1, "variants": ["camino"], "contexts": ["chimney"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "cammino", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "camino", "similarity": 0.896}], "id": 6065, "stable_key": "clu_6341eff35dd1aebe", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hearth", "confidence": "high", "type": "object", "note": "The terms 'cammino' and 'camino' refer to a hearth or fireplace, a common feature in early modern homes and laboratories.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name"}}, {"canonical_name": "nefpoie", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "nefpoie", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "nefpoie", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["nefpoie"], "contexts": ["medlar fruits"]}, {"entity_id": "nefperas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "nefperas", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["nefperas"], "contexts": ["fruit used in remedies"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "nefpoie", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "nefperas", "similarity": 0.892}], "id": 6066, "stable_key": "clu_ef0d8b420917632b-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Medlar", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Mespilus germanica Linnaeus", "family": "Rosaceae", "note": "'Nefpoie' and 'nefperas' are consistent with historical variations of the word for medlar, a fruit used medicinally. The context 'medlar fruits' solidifies this identification.", "wikidata_id": "Q2088434", "wikidata_description": "species of flowering plant", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mimusops%20elengi", "wikipedia_extract": "Mimusops elengi is a medium-sized evergreen tree found in tropical forests in South Asia, Southeast Asia and northern Australia. English common names include Spanish cherry, medlar, and bullet wood. Its timber is valuable and its fruit is edible. As the trees give thick shade and flowers emit fragrance, it is a prized collection for gardens. It is used as an ornamental tree in many places. The flowers may also be used in natural perfume.", "description": "A fruit tree with medicinal properties and its fruit."}}, {"canonical_name": "Hypociflide", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "hypociflide", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Hypociflide", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Hypociflide"], "contexts": ["likely a medicinal herb"]}, {"entity_id": "hypociftidis", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "hypociftidis", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["hypociftidis"], "contexts": ["ingredient in an emplaster"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Hypociflide", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "hypociftidis", "similarity": 0.905}], "id": 6067, "stable_key": "clu_a61be7b868d1f768", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Hypocistis", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Cistanche phelypaea", "family": "Orobanchaceae", "description": "Parasitic plant extract used for binding and in medicinal preparations.", "wikipedia_extract": "Cistanche phelypaea, also spelled Cistanche phelipaea, is a species of plant in the family Orobanchaceae. It has a wide range of distribution from the Arabian Peninsula and the Syrian Desert in the east, through the Sahara, Cyprus, Crete and the southwest of the Iberian Peninsula, to Macaronesia in the west. Cistanche phelypaea is a chlorophyll-free, obligate parasitic plant with stout fleshy flowering stems bearing bright yellow flowers and rising to 20\u201350 cm (7.9\u201319.7 in) tall from a generally swollen base. The stem is glabrous and yellow to purple gray. Leaves are ovate-lanceolate, obtuse, glabrous and normally brown in color. Inflorescence is dense, more or less cylindrical."}}, {"canonical_name": "Cauda equina", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "cauda_equina", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Cauda equina", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Cauda equina"], "contexts": ["herb ingredient"]}, {"entity_id": "cauda_equina", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "cauda equina", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["cauda equina"], "contexts": ["plant used in remedies"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Cauda equina", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "cauda equina", "similarity": 0.836}], "id": 6068, "stable_key": "clu_319e153191f1b485", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Horsetail", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Equisetum arvense Linnaeus", "family": "Equisetaceae", "note": "Literally 'horse's tail', this refers to the appearance of the plant, not a body part.", "wikidata_id": "Q81492131", "wikidata_description": "scientific article published on 01 February 2005"}}, {"canonical_name": "Aureola", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "aureola", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Aureola", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Aureola"], "contexts": ["substance ingredient"]}, {"entity_id": "aureola", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "aureola", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "ANATOMY", "count": 1, "variants": ["aureola"], "contexts": ["ring of light around the sun"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Aureola", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "aureola", "similarity": 0.575}], "id": 6069, "stable_key": "clu_b298a47e2a071662", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Halo", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The dual context suggests both a substance (likely medicinal, perhaps related to gold or a pigment) and a visual phenomenon (optical halo)."}}, {"canonical_name": "pena", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "pena", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "pena", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["pena"], "contexts": ["penalty or punishment"]}, {"entity_id": "penas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "penas", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["penas"], "contexts": ["punishments suffered by the condemned"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "pena", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "penas", "similarity": 0.863}], "id": 6070, "stable_key": "clu_16aa67893135ec82", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Penalty", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to punishment or legal consequence."}}, {"canonical_name": "Donzelli", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "donzelli", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Donzelli", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["Donzelli"], "contexts": ["officials of the art"]}, {"entity_id": "donzellas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "donzellas", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "OTHER_PERSON", "count": 1, "variants": ["donzellas"], "contexts": ["young unmarried women"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Donzelli", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "donzellas", "similarity": 0.965}], "id": 6071, "stable_key": "clu_5ab796c6a40e6898", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Donzelli", "confidence": "medium", "type": "person", "description": "Likely an individual or family name referenced in early modern texts.", "note": "The context 'officials of the art' suggests an individual or group associated with a guild or profession. 'Young unmarried women' refers to a different, though homophonous, term. Without further context, the specific identity is uncertain.", "wikidata_id": "", "wikidata_description": "family name", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donzelli", "wikipedia_extract": "Donzelli is an Italian surname."}}, {"canonical_name": "part.", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "part_", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "part.", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["part."], "contexts": ["part of a work"]}, {"entity_id": "part", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Part", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Part"], "contexts": ["concept opposed to Whole"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "part.", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Part", "similarity": 0.885}], "id": 6072, "stable_key": "clu_176916bfb91246d6", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Part", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the conceptual division of a whole into constituent elements.", "wikidata_id": "Q114980050", "wikidata_description": "relation among parts and wholes"}}, {"canonical_name": "Spinola", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "spinola", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Spinola", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Spinola"], "contexts": ["cited for Decuria & Li\u00e7am 6"]}, {"entity_id": "spinoza", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Spinoza", "category": "PERSON", "subcategory": "SCHOLAR", "count": 1, "variants": ["Spinoza"], "contexts": ["17th-century philosopher"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Spinola", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Spinoza", "similarity": 0.948}], "id": 6073, "stable_key": "clu_baedbf2ef1952d49", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Baruch Spinoza", "confidence": "high", "type": "person", "birth_year": 1632, "death_year": 1677, "description": "17th-century Dutch philosopher of Portuguese-Jewish origin.", "note": "The spelling 'Spinola' in the first text is likely a variant or error for Spinoza.", "wikidata_id": "Q35802", "wikidata_description": "Dutch philosopher (1632\u20131677)", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baruch%20Spinoza", "wikipedia_extract": "Baruch (de) Spinoza (24 November 1632 \u2013 21 February 1677), also known under his Latinized pen name Benedictus de Spinoza, was a philosopher of Portuguese-Jewish origin, who was born and lived in the Dutch Republic. A forerunner of the Age of Enlightenment, Spinoza significantly influenced modern biblical criticism, 17th-century rationalism, and Dutch intellectual culture, establishing himself as one of the most important and radical philosophers of the early modern period. Influenced by Stoicism, Thomas Hobbes, Ren\u00e9 Descartes, Ibn Tufayl, and heterodox Christians, Spinoza was a leading philosopher of the Dutch Golden Age. Spinoza was born in Amsterdam to a Marrano family that fled Portugal for the more tolerant Dutch Republic. He received a traditional Jewish education, learning Hebrew and studying sacred texts within the Portuguese Jewish community, where his father was a prominent merchant. As a young man, Spinoza challenged rabbinic authority and questioned Jewish doctrines, leading to his permanent expulsion from his Jewish community in 1656."}}, {"canonical_name": "continuo movimento", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "continuo_movimento", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "continuo movimento", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["continuo movimento"], "contexts": ["ongoing movement"]}, {"entity_id": "motion_is_continuous", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Motion is continuous", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "THEORY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Motion is continuous"], "contexts": ["principle of perpetual motion"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "continuo movimento", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Motion is continuous", "similarity": 0.911}], "id": 6074, "stable_key": "clu_f8d4701976fa4843", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Perpetual Motion", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Refers to the theoretical concept of a machine that can do work indefinitely without an external energy source, often discussed in early modern physics."}}, {"canonical_name": "Templo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "templo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Templo", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Templo"], "contexts": ["place of worship"]}, {"entity_id": "the_temple", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "the Temple", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLACE", "count": 1, "variants": ["the Temple"], "contexts": ["area with lawyers"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Templo", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "the Temple", "similarity": 0.923}], "id": 6075, "stable_key": "clu_a3e7ee9889b9113c", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "temple", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "country": "United Kingdom", "note": "The dual context of 'place of worship' and 'area with lawyers' strongly suggests the Inns of Court in London, commonly known as 'The Temple'."}}, {"canonical_name": "exulceratione", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "exulceratione", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "exulceratione", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["exulceratione"], "contexts": ["ulceration"]}, {"entity_id": "exhilaration", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "exhilaration", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["exhilaration"], "contexts": ["feeling of great happiness"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "exulceratione", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "exhilaration", "similarity": 0.885}], "id": 6076, "stable_key": "clu_2243fe0f0604fc35", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "ulceration", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "ulceration", "note": "The term 'exulceratione' clearly refers to ulceration, while 'exhilaration' appears to be a mistranscription or a separate, unrelated concept within the context of early modern medical texts. The primary meaning in medical dictionaries points to ulceration.", "wikidata_id": "Q18554343", "wikidata_description": "Human disease"}}, {"canonical_name": "desesperan\u00e7a", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "desesperan\u00e7a", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "desesperan\u00e7a", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["desesperan\u00e7a"], "contexts": ["despair"]}, {"entity_id": "despair", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "despair", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["despair"], "contexts": ["loss of hope"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "desesperan\u00e7a", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "despair", "similarity": 0.925}], "id": 6077, "stable_key": "clu_dfb7e84b0e0cc6a5", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "despair", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "despair", "note": "In early modern medicine, emotional states like despair were often considered to have physical manifestations or could be indicative of illness. 'desesperan\u00e7a' is the Portuguese word for despair.", "wikidata_id": "Q1747066", "wikidata_description": "feeling that there is no hope and that you can do nothing to improve a difficult or worrying situation"}}, {"canonical_name": "inflam.", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "inflam_", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "inflam.", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["inflam."], "contexts": ["inflammation"]}, {"entity_id": "inflamed_part", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "inflamed part", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["inflamed part"], "contexts": ["part of the body relieved by raising"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "inflam.", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "inflamed part", "similarity": 0.917}], "id": 6078, "stable_key": "clu_18a3a8d08f8c01ca", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Inflammation", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "modern_term": "Inflammation", "note": "The abbreviation 'inflam.' clearly refers to inflammation, a common medical concept.", "wikidata_id": "Q14819849", "wikidata_description": "immediate defensive reaction (by vertebrate tissue) to infection or injury caused by chemical or physical agents, characterized by local vasodilation, extravasation of plasma into intercellular spaces and accumulation of white blood cells"}}, {"canonical_name": "menstruo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "menstruo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "menstruo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["menstruo"], "contexts": ["solvent or menstruum for extraction"]}, {"entity_id": "menstruum", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "menstruum", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["menstruum"], "contexts": ["dissolving liquid, e.g., water"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "menstruo", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "menstruum", "similarity": 0.987}], "id": 6079, "stable_key": "clu_73b7756d50873b74", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Solvent", "confidence": "high", "type": "chemical", "wikidata_id": "Q146505", "wikidata_description": "substance that dissolves a solute (a chemically different liquid, solid or gas), resulting in a solution", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solvent", "wikipedia_extract": "A solvent (from the Latin solv\u014d, \"loosen, untie, solve\") is a substance that dissolves a solute, resulting in a solution. A solvent is usually a liquid but can also be a solid, a gas, or a supercritical fluid. Water is a solvent for polar molecules, and the most common solvent used by living things; all the ions and proteins in a cell are dissolved in water within the cell. Major uses of solvents are in paints, paint removers, inks, and dry cleaning. Specific uses for organic solvents are in dry cleaning (e.g. tetrachloroethylene); as paint thinners (toluene, turpentine); as nail polish removers and solvents of glue (acetone, methyl acetate, ethyl acetate); in spot removers (hexane, petrol ether); in detergents (citrus terpenes); and in perfumes (ethanol)."}}, {"canonical_name": "Igreja", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "igreja", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Igreja", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Igreja"], "contexts": ["Church"]}, {"entity_id": "church", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Church", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Church"], "contexts": ["religious part of government"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Igreja", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Church", "similarity": 0.906}], "id": 6080, "stable_key": "clu_3257d9a3afb5367e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Church", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "The term 'Igreja' is Portuguese for 'Church'. In the context of early modern texts, it refers to the institution of the Church, often with political or social implications, rather than a specific theological doctrine or building in isolation."}}, {"canonical_name": "trigonometry", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "trigonometry", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "trigonometry", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["trigonometry"], "contexts": ["mathematical study of triangles"]}, {"entity_id": "plane_trigonometry", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Plane trigonometry", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Plane trigonometry"], "contexts": ["trigonometry of flat surfaces"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "trigonometry", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Plane trigonometry", "similarity": 0.909}], "id": 6081, "stable_key": "clu_2afdc8c2764d11b7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Trigonometry", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept"}}, {"canonical_name": "degree", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "degree", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "degree", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["degree"], "contexts": ["unit of angular measurement"]}, {"entity_id": "degree", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "degree", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["degree"], "contexts": ["level or extent"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "degree", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "degree", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 6082, "stable_key": "clu_c4bad17dc9f94a26-2", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Degree (angle)", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "wikidata_id": "Q57571141", "wikidata_description": "scientific article published on 21 June 2007"}}, {"canonical_name": "inequalities", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "inequalities", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "inequalities", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["inequalities"], "contexts": ["deviations from regular orbital motion"]}, {"entity_id": "inequality", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "Inequality", "category": "CONCEPT", "subcategory": "OTHER_CONCEPT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Inequality"], "contexts": ["correlative to Equality"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "inequalities", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "Inequality", "similarity": 0.903}], "id": 6083, "stable_key": "clu_0ddeebb6ab1c5fae", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Orbital Inequalities", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "This refers to deviations from perfectly regular or predictable astronomical orbits, a concept studied in celestial mechanics."}}, {"canonical_name": "equinoctial gales", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "equinoctial_gales", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "equinoctial gales", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["equinoctial gales"], "contexts": ["strong winds during equinoxes"]}, {"entity_id": "equinoxial_gale", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "equinoxial gale", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["equinoxial gale"], "contexts": ["weather event causing leaf detachment"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "equinoctial gales", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "equinoxial gale", "similarity": 0.904}], "id": 6084, "stable_key": "clu_87e2a4a110b7e7c3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Equinoctial gales", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "While not a disease, these are significant weather events discussed in scientific contexts."}}, {"canonical_name": "ethereal electric medium", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "ethereal_electric_medium", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "ethereal electric medium", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["ethereal electric medium"], "contexts": ["hypothetical medium filling space"]}, {"entity_id": "ethereal_medium", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "ethereal medium", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["ethereal medium"], "contexts": ["hypothetical medium for light and motion"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "ethereal electric medium", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "ethereal medium", "similarity": 0.927}], "id": 6085, "stable_key": "clu_4aacd6130070b8e1-3", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "luminiferous aether", "confidence": "high", "type": "concept", "note": "Historically, this referred to a hypothetical medium for light and electromagnetic waves. It was later superseded by the concept of spacetime.", "wikidata_id": "Q208702", "wikidata_description": "postulated medium for the propagation of light"}}, {"canonical_name": "rain-drops", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 2, "total_mentions": 2, "members": [{"entity_id": "rain-drops", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "rain-drops", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["rain-drops"], "contexts": ["water falling from clouds"]}, {"entity_id": "rain-drops", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "rain-drops", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants":